{"notes":[{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10609961/10609862/10610364.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"yu|adapting_for_calibration_disturbances_a_neural_uncalibrated_visual_servoing_policy"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongxiang_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Anzhe_Chen:","~Kechun_Xu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dashun_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yufei_Wei:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhongxiang_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuebo_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yue_Wang_0020:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rong_Xiong:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA57147.2024.10610364"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/YuCXGWZZ0X24,\n  author={Hongxiang Yu and Anzhe Chen and Kechun Xu and Dashun Guo and Yufei Wei and Zhongxiang Zhou and Xuebo Zhang and Yue Wang and Rong Xiong},\n  title={Adapting for Calibration Disturbances: A Neural Uncalibrated Visual Servoing Policy},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={17417-17423},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA57147.2024.10610364},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Visual servoing (VS) is a widely used technique in industries where there are hundreds of robots, but it requires accurate camera calibration including camera intrinsic and extrinsic parameters. However, it is labour-intensive to calibrate robots one-by-one in practical use. In this paper, we propose a neural uncalibrated VS policy (NUVS) that can adapt to calibration disturbances with an adaption mechanism and a control-oriented guidance. It bridges the disturbance adaption of classical VS methods and the large convergence of learning-based VS methods. NUVS estimates the calibration embedding from past observations and servos to the desired pose under the supervision of a PBVS that can access the ground truth in simulation. With this adaption mechanism, NUVS outperforms the classical IBUVS algorithm when facing large initial camera pose offsets under the calibration disturbance. Supplementary material in: https://sites.google.com/view/neural-uncalibrated-vs"},"title":{"value":"Adapting for Calibration Disturbances: A Neural Uncalibrated Visual Servoing Policy"},"authors":{"value":["Hongxiang Yu","Anzhe Chen","Kechun Xu","Dashun Guo","Yufei Wei","Zhongxiang Zhou","Xuebo Zhang","Yue Wang","Rong Xiong"]}},"tmdate":1738602383169,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1738602375124,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kechun_Xu1"],"forum":"BxTNERw4ai","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":290881,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738602383169,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"BxTNERw4ai","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.03056v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"herau|moisst_multimodal_optimization_of_implicit_scene_for_spatiotemporal_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Quentin_Herau:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nathan_Piasco:","~Moussab_Bennehar1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luis_Roldão:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dzmitry_Tsishkou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cyrille_Migniot:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pascal_Vasseur:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cédric_Demonceaux:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2303.03056"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2303-03056,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Quentin Herau and Nathan Piasco and Moussâb Bennehar and Luis Roldão and Dzmitry Tsishkou and Cyrille Migniot and Pascal Vasseur and Cédric Demonceaux},\n  title={MOISST: Multi-modal Optimization of Implicit Scene for SpatioTemporal calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2303.03056},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2303.03056}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With the recent advances in autonomous driving and the decreasing cost of LiDARs, the use of multimodal sensor systems is on the rise. However, in order to make use of the information provided by a variety of complimentary sensors, it is necessary to accurately calibrate them. We take advantage of recent advances in computer graphics and implicit volumetric scene representation to tackle the problem of multi-sensor spatial and temporal calibration. Thanks to a new formulation of the Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) optimization, we are able to jointly optimize calibration parameters along with scene representation based on radiometric and geometric measurements. Our method enables accurate and robust calibration from data captured in uncontrolled and unstructured urban environments, making our solution more scalable than existing calibration solutions. We demonstrate the accuracy and robustness of our method in urban scenes typically encountered in autonomous driving scenarios."},"title":{"value":"MOISST: Multi-modal Optimization of Implicit Scene for SpatioTemporal calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Quentin Herau","Nathan Piasco","Moussâb Bennehar","Luis Roldão","Dzmitry Tsishkou","Cyrille Migniot","Pascal Vasseur","Cédric Demonceaux"]}},"tmdate":1734536572393,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1734536560356,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Moussab_Bennehar1"],"forum":"4CxBUTCMbd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":258550,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1734536572393,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4CxBUTCMbd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IGARSS 2012"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6334512/6350328/06350435.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IGARSS/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"vega|calibration_of_the_nasa_dualfrequency_dualpolarized_doppler_radar"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Manuel_Vega:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:V._Chandrasekar_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cuong_Nguyen_0002:","~Kumar_Vijay_Mishra2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:James_R._Carswell:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS.2012.6350435"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/igarss/VegaCNMC12,\n  author={Manuel Vega and V. Chandrasekar and Cuong Nguyen and Kumar Vijay Mishra and James R. Carswell},\n  title={Calibration of the NASA Dual-Frequency, Dual-Polarized, Doppler Radar},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  pages={4625-4628},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS.2012.6350435},\n  booktitle={IGARSS},\n  crossref={conf/igarss/2012}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper summarizes part of the work performed on the calibration and characterization of the Dual-frequency, Dual-polarized, Doppler Radar (D3R) system. The D3R makes use of pulse compression combined with a three pulse frequency separated waveform to achieve it's sensitivity while still maintaining a blind range similar to that of a single pulse weather radar. A slightly modified receiver calibration procedure to the one used on conventional weather radar was required to accommodate the use of multiple sub-channels. The use of the calibration loop in both transceivers is described and transmitter output power is shown for a twenty four hour period. Antenna beam (Ku to Ka) co-alignment verification using solar scans is also presented followed by sphere calibration results for the Ku-band radar. Finally, Ku-band reflectivity plots for two events during May 6th, 2012 and May 7th, 2012 are presented."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of the NASA Dual-Frequency, Dual-Polarized, Doppler Radar"},"authors":{"value":["Manuel Vega","V. Chandrasekar","Cuong Nguyen","Kumar Vijay Mishra","James R. Carswell"]}},"tmdate":1727458406894,"pdate":1325376000000,"tcdate":1727454710528,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kumar_Vijay_Mishra2"],"forum":"wScBOIS1T7","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":89126,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727458406894,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"wScBOIS1T7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Int. J. Comput. Assist. Radiol. Surg. 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11548-014-0995-y.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CARS/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|videoguided_calibration_of_an_augmented_reality_mobile_carm"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Chen:","~Hemal_Naik1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lejing_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nassir_Navab:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pascal_Fallavollita:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11548-014-0995-y"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/cars/ChenNWNF14,\n  author={Xin Chen and Hemal Naik and Lejing Wang and Nassir Navab and Pascal Fallavollita},\n  title={Video-guided calibration of an augmented reality mobile C-arm},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  journal={Int. J. Comput. Assist. Radiol. Surg.},\n  volume={9},\n  number={6},\n  pages={987-996},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s11548-014-0995-y}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The augmented reality (AR) fluoroscope augments an X-ray image by video and provides the surgeon with a real-time in situ overlay of the anatomy. The overlay alignment is crucial for diagnostic and intra-operative guidance, so precise calibration of the AR fluoroscope is required. The first and most complex step of the calibration procedure is the determination of the X-ray source position. Currently, this is achieved using a biplane phantom with movable metallic rings on its top layer and fixed X-ray opaque markers on its bottom layer. The metallic rings must be moved to positions where at least two pairs of rings and markers are isocentric in the X-ray image. The current “trial and error” calibration process currently requires acquisition of many X-ray images, a task that is both time consuming and radiation intensive. An improved process was developed and tested for C-arm calibration."},"title":{"value":"Video-guided calibration of an augmented reality mobile C-arm"},"authors":{"value":["Xin Chen","Hemal Naik","Lejing Wang","Nassir Navab","Pascal Fallavollita"]}},"tmdate":1717420041324,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1717420035653,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hemal_Naik1"],"forum":"XRNz2EPVmY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":23829,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1717420041324,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"XRNz2EPVmY","version":2},{"content":{"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d0fd2f0a5387d7575de4f08b472d6a36a1372be7.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|code_llama_open_foundation_models_for_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jingyu_Liu6","~Baptiste_Roziere1","~Jonas_Gehring1","~Fabian_Gloeckle1","~Sten_Sootla1","~Itai_Gat1","~Xiaoqing_Tan1","~Yossi_Adi1","~Tal_Remez2","~Jérémy_Rapin1","~Ivan_Evtimov2","~Joanna_Bitton1","~Manish_Bhatt1","~Cristian_Canton_Ferrer1","~Wenhan_Xiong1","~Alexandre_Défossez1","~Jade_Copet1","~Hugo_Touvron1","~Louis_Martin1","~Nicolas_Usunier1","~Thomas_Scialom1","~Gabriel_Synnaeve1"]},"abstract":{"value":"We release Code Llama, a family of large language models for code based on Llama 2 providing state-of-the-art performance among open models, infilling capabilities, support for large input contexts, and zero-shot instruction following ability for programming tasks. We provide multiple flavors to cover a wide range of applications: foundation models (Code Llama), Python specializations (Code Llama - Python), and instruction-following models (Code Llama - Instruct) with 7B, 13B, 34B and 70B parameters each. All models are trained on sequences of 16k tokens and show improvements on inputs with up to 100k tokens. 7B, 13B and 70B Code Llama and Code Llama - Instruct variants support infilling based on surrounding content. Code Llama reaches state-of-the-art performance among open models on several code benchmarks, with scores of up to 67% and 65% on HumanEval and MBPP, respectively. Notably, Code Llama - Python 7B outperforms Llama 2 70B on HumanEval and MBPP, and all our models outperform every other publicly available model on MultiPL-E. We release Code Llama under a permissive license that allows for both research and commercial use."},"title":{"value":"Code Llama: Open Foundation Models for Code"},"authors":{"value":["Jingyu Liu","Baptiste Roziere","Jonas Gehring","Fabian Gloeckle","Sten Sootla","Itai Gat","Xiaoqing Tan","Yossi Adi","Tal Remez","Jérémy Rapin","Ivan Evtimov","Joanna Bitton","Manish Bhatt","Cristian Canton Ferrer","Wenhan Xiong","Alexandre Défossez","Jade Copet","Hugo Touvron","Louis Martin","Nicolas Usunier","Thomas Scialom","Gabriel Synnaeve"]}},"tmdate":1707293702391,"pdate":1692897295415,"tcdate":1707293702391,"writers":["~Jingyu_Liu6","~Baptiste_Roziere1","~Jonas_Gehring1","~Fabian_Gloeckle1","~Sten_Sootla1","~Itai_Gat1","~Xiaoqing_Tan1","~Yossi_Adi1","~Tal_Remez2","~Jérémy_Rapin1","~Ivan_Evtimov2","~Joanna_Bitton1","~Manish_Bhatt1","~Cristian_Canton_Ferrer1","~Wenhan_Xiong1","~Alexandre_Défossez1","~Jade_Copet1","~Hugo_Touvron1","~Louis_Martin1","~Nicolas_Usunier1","~Thomas_Scialom1","~Gabriel_Synnaeve1"],"signatures":["~Jingyu_Liu6"],"forum":"TYStgO3Nte","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":25054,"cdate":1707293702391,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1707293702391,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"TYStgO3Nte","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.17407v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|posthoc_reward_calibration_a_case_study_on_length_bias"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zeyu_Huang1","~Zihan_Qiu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zili_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Edoardo_M._Ponti:","~Ivan_Titov1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.17407"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2409-17407,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zeyu Huang and Zihan Qiu and Zili Wang and Edoardo M. Ponti and Ivan Titov},\n  title={Post-hoc Reward Calibration: A Case Study on Length Bias},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2409.17407},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.17407}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback aligns the outputs of Large Language Models with human values and preferences. Central to this process is the reward model (RM), which translates human feedback into training signals for optimising LLM behaviour. However, RMs can develop biases by exploiting spurious correlations in their training data, such as favouring outputs based on length or style rather than true quality. These biases can lead to incorrect output rankings, sub-optimal model evaluations, and the amplification of undesirable behaviours in LLMs alignment. This paper addresses the challenge of correcting such biases without additional data and training, introducing the concept of Post-hoc Reward Calibration. We first propose an intuitive approach to estimate the bias term and, thus, remove it to approximate the underlying true reward. We then extend the approach to a more general and robust form with the Locally Weighted Regression. Focusing on the prevalent length bias, we validate our proposed approaches across three experimental settings, demonstrating consistent improvements: (1) a 3.11 average performance gain across 33 reward models on the RewardBench dataset; (2) enhanced alignment of RM rankings with GPT-4 evaluations and human preferences based on the AlpacaEval benchmark; and (3) improved Length-Controlled win rate of the RLHF process in multiple LLM--RM combinations. Our method is computationally efficient and generalisable to other types of bias and RMs, offering a scalable and robust solution for mitigating biases in LLM alignment. Our code and results are available at https://github.com/ZeroYuHuang/Reward-Calibration."},"title":{"value":"Post-hoc Reward Calibration: A Case Study on Length Bias"},"authors":{"value":["Zeyu Huang","Zihan Qiu","Zili Wang","Edoardo M. Ponti","Ivan Titov"]}},"tmdate":1753907451771,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747291834545,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zihan_Qiu1"],"forum":"PNAOwKwLau","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":475085,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1753907451771,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"PNAOwKwLau","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2024 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["network compression"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/e093f0f7a0d4532889bd5b347eea82062f9d0f3d.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"optimization"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Post-training quantization (PTQ) converts a pre-trained full-precision (FP) model into a quantized model in a training-free manner. Determining suitable quantization parameters, such as scaling factors and weight rounding, is the primary strategy for mitigating the impact of quantization noise (calibration) and restoring the performance of the quantized models. However, the existing activation calibration methods have never considered information degradation between pre- (FP) and post-quantized activations. In this study, we introduce a well-defined distributional metric from information theory, mutual information, into PTQ calibration. We aim to calibrate the quantized activations by maximizing the mutual information between the pre- and post-quantized activations. To realize this goal, we establish a contrastive learning (CL) framework for the PTQ calibration, where the quantization parameters are optimized through a self-supervised proxy task. Specifically, by leveraging CL during the PTQ process, we can benefit from pulling the positive pairs of quantized and FP activations collected from the same input samples, while pushing negative pairs from different samples. Thanks to the ingeniously designed critic function, we avoid the unwanted but often-encountered collision solution in CL, especially in calibration scenarios where the amount of calibration data is limited. Additionally, we provide a theoretical guarantee that minimizing our designed loss is equivalent to maximizing the desired mutual information. Consequently, the quantized activations retain more information, which ultimately enhances the performance of the quantized network. Experimental results show that our method can effectively serve as an add-on module to existing SoTA PTQ methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nshang2024clcalib,\ntitle={{CL}-Calib: Enhancing Post-training Quantization Calibration through Contrastive Learning},\nauthor={Yuzhang Shang and Gaowen Liu and Ramana Rao Kompella and Yan Yan},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Wxyyc2vvGd}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CL-Calib: Enhancing Post-training Quantization Calibration through Contrastive Learning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3149347004eabd40cde7325623965906d5f55ab5.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"shang|clcalib_enhancing_posttraining_quantization_calibration_through_contrastive_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yuzhang_Shang1","~Gaowen_Liu4","~Ramana_Rao_Kompella1","~Yan_Yan6"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Yuzhang Shang","Gaowen Liu","Ramana Rao Kompella","Yan Yan"]}},"tmdate":1711385473951,"tcdate":1695482837529,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission7689/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission7689/Authors"],"forum":"Wxyyc2vvGd","number":7689,"cdate":1695482837529,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission7689/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1711385473951,"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"Wxyyc2vvGd","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Post-training improves accuracy but can degrade confidence reliability; calibration is a moving target across evaluation pools, while a small amount of target-pool data enables effective recalibration."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Models; Confidence Calibration; Uncertainty Estimation; Post-Training; Distribution Shift; Model Reliability"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models typically achieve higher predictive accuracy after post-training, but a more fundamental question is whether this improvement in accuracy is accompanied by better confidence quality. To investigate this question, we examine the changes induced by post-training from two aspects of confidence: reliability and discrimination. Across 47 model–task cells comprising 16 base/tuned model pairs, 6 post-training settings, and 3 multiple-choice tasks, we find that in the 35-cell alignment cohort, post-training improves average accuracy by 1.1 percentage points while increasing the Brier reliability component by 0.047, with reliability degrading in 32 of 35 cells; discrimination remains largely stable. We refer to this phenomenon as the reliability tax of post-training. To mitigate this reliability loss, we further evaluate several confidence calibration methods and find that simple temperature scaling can substantially reduce calibration error, but its effectiveness is not fixed: when the model, task, and prompt template are held constant while the evaluation pool changes, the median temperature required for harder pools is approximately 5 times that for easier pools, and the excess ECE induced by calibration transfer is strongly correlated with the accuracy gap between pools. Further experiments show that only 20 target labels can substantially improve calibration on a new evaluation pool, reducing held-out ECE by approximately 83.4% relative to the uncalibrated state. Overall, our study reveals the reliability tax introduced by post-training and demonstrates that calibration is strongly dependent on the evaluation pool, while a small amount of target data can support effective recalibration on new evaluation pools."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026the,\ntitle={The Post-Training Tax: The Moving Target of Calibration},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=jTY0LTIiES},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"The Post-Training Tax: The Moving Target of Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/96e02147a06b8945437d34025255f29186e451fb.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791231427837,"tcdate":1789652027452,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission31729/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission31729/Authors"],"forum":"jTY0LTIiES","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":31729,"cdate":1789652027452,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission31729/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing"],"mdate":1791231427837,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"jTY0LTIiES","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["VLA","Temporal Difference","Calibration","Brier Score","Safety","Robotics","Probability Forcast"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nfrancis-meretzki2026temporal,\ntitle={Temporal Difference Calibration in Sequential Tasks: Application to Vision-Language-Action Models},\nauthor={Shelly Francis-Meretzki and Mirco Mutti and Yaniv Romano and Aviv Tamar},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=TIru4KuhcB}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Temporal Difference Calibration in Sequential Tasks: Application to Vision-Language-Action Models"},"paperhash":{"value":"francismeretzki|temporal_difference_calibration_in_sequential_tasks_application_to_visionlanguageaction_models"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/c9ebb26928e66d0c3fcce80223b470a8510e6a98.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"reinforcement_learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent advances in vision-language-action (VLA) models for robotics have highlighted the importance of reliable uncertainty quantification in sequential tasks. However, assessing and improving calibration in such settings remains mostly unexplored, especially when only partial trajectories are observed. In this work, we formulate *sequential calibration* for episodic tasks, where task-success confidence is produced along an episode, while success is determined at the end of it. We introduce a sequential extension of the Brier score and show that, for binary outcomes, its risk minimizer coincides with the VLA policy’s value function. This connection bridges uncertainty calibration and reinforcement learning, enabling the use of temporal-difference (TD) value estimation as a principled calibration mechanism over time. \nWe empirically show that TD calibration improves performance relative to the state-of-the-art on simulated and real-robot data. Interestingly, we show that when calibrated using TD, the VLA's single-step action probabilities can yield competitive uncertainty estimates, in contrast to recent findings that employed different calibration techniques."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://github.com/shellytechnion/TDQC"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a2b1e18c91a87885912f2c5a346285bedbaea5d1.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Recent advances in vision-language-action (VLA) models for robotics have highlighted the importance of reliable uncertainty quantification and calibration in sequential tasks. However, assessing and improving calibration in such settings remains mostly unexplored, especially when only partial trajectories are observed. Our work provides the first formal framework for calibration in sequential tasks. We establish a novel connection between sequential calibration and RL value function prediction, enabling use of value estimation methods. We demonstrate that black-box calibration, using only action probabilities output by a VLA model, can match or outperform white-box methods, that require access to model's internal features, making it useful for proprietary models. The approach requires no VLA fine-tuning and operates as a lightweight MLP on top of the model's output. Finally, we show the added value of the calibrated success predictor both for early stopping and for improving the task success rates."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shelly_Francis-Meretzki1","~Mirco_Mutti1","~Yaniv_Romano1","~Aviv_Tamar2"]},"authors":{"value":["Shelly Francis-Meretzki","Mirco Mutti","Yaniv Romano","Aviv Tamar"]}},"tmdate":1790067335039,"pdate":1777576347243,"tcdate":1768925134152,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission10131/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission10131/Authors"],"forum":"TIru4KuhcB","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":10131,"cdate":1768925134152,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission10131/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission10131/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067335039,"odate":1782341937168,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"TIru4KuhcB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IGARSS 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9553015/9553016/09555066.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IGARSS/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"imai|lunar_calibration_and_its_validation_for_a_multispectral_sensor_onboard_risesat_microsatellite"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","~Toshinori_Kuwahara1","","","","","","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS47720.2021.9555066"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/igarss/ImaiKKK0SSHYST21,\n  author={Masataka Imai and Junichi Kurihara and Toru Kouyama and Toshinori Kuwahara and Shinya Fujita and Yuji Sakamoto and Sei-Ichi Saitoh and Takafumi Hirata and Hirokazu Yamamoto and Yuji Sato and Yukihiro Takahashi},\n  title={Lunar Calibration and its Validation for a Multispectral Sensor Onboard Risesat Microsatellite},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={7775-7778},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS47720.2021.9555066},\n  booktitle={IGARSS},\n  crossref={conf/igarss/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Radiometric calibration with the Moon (called the lunar calibration) is a promising method for evaluating the performance of instruments onboard satellites. In particular, the lunar calibration can provide inflight radiometric calibration opportunities for nano/microsatellites after the launch without any special equipment. In this study, we have conducted more than one year of Moon observations with a microsatellite named RISESAT. The performance of an onboard multispectral sensor OOC is investigated by using the ROLO and SELENE/SP Moon models. Our lunar calibration revealed the dependence of the sensor sensitivity on the instrument temperature, and no sensitivity degradation (< 1 %) can be confirmed for all four channels after the correction of the temperature dependence. Lunar calibration also revealed the bluing trend in the OOC’ s inter-band ratio, which indicates a 15% alternation in the sensitivity at maximum. This bluing trend was validated by the Railroad Valley Playa observations as comparing the Landsat-8/OLI data."},"title":{"value":"Lunar Calibration and its Validation for a Multispectral Sensor Onboard Risesat Microsatellite"},"authors":{"value":["Masataka Imai","Junichi Kurihara","Toru Kouyama","Toshinori Kuwahara","Shinya Fujita","Yuji Sakamoto","Sei-Ichi Saitoh","Takafumi Hirata","Hirokazu Yamamoto","Yuji Sato","Yukihiro Takahashi"]}},"tmdate":1772355359837,"pdate":1640908800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/igarss/ImaiKKK0SSHYST21"],"tcdate":1772355350422,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Toshinori_Kuwahara1"],"forum":"w8Hp0zl0YE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":840744,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772355359837,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"w8Hp0zl0YE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICCV 2025 Wild3D"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/9b15b018e56553bac9b9db4832a246f782d8d999.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Multimodal SpatioTemporal Calibration","Periodic Activation","Gaussian Splatting"]},"venueid":{"value":"thecvf.com/ICCV/2025/Workshop/Wild3D"},"paperhash":{"value":"park|fast_and_precise_multimodal_spatiotemporal_calibration_via_periodicactivated_2d_gaussian_splatting"},"authorids":{"value":["~Hongbeen_Park1","~Minjeong_Park1","~Sunpil_Kim1","~Jinkyu_Kim1","~Jung_Hyun_Lee3"]},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate spatiotemporal calibration between heterogeneous sensors such as cameras and LiDAR is essential for robust performance in tasks like localization, mapping, and object detection. Traditional calibration methods rely on physical targets and manual procedures, limiting scalability and applicability in dynamic or real-time environments. Recent advances in targetless and scene reconstruction-based calibration have improved spatial alignment but often neglect temporal synchronization and are computationally intensive. In this work, we propose PACS (Periodic Activation with 2D Gaussian Splatting for Multimodal SpatioTemporal Calibration), a novel and efficient calibration framework that jointly estimates spatial and temporal alignment across sensors. PACS uses a sine-activated multilayer perceptron (MLP) to effectively capture high-frequency scene details, enhancing convergence and representational power. Leveraging an anchor-based decoding scheme, our method significantly accelerates training while maintaining robust scene reconstruction. Furthermore, we employ 2D Gaussian splatting to render scenes with improved alignment between visual and LiDAR-based geometry. Experimental results demonstrate that PACS achieves accurate, efficient, and robust calibration across multimodal sensor configurations."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\npark2025fast,\ntitle={Fast and Precise Multimodal Spatiotemporal Calibration via Periodic-Activated 2D Gaussian Splatting},\nauthor={Hongbeen Park and Minjeong Park and Sunpil Kim and Jinkyu Kim and Jung Hyun Lee},\nbooktitle={ICCV 2025 Workshop on Wild 3D: 3D Modeling, Reconstruction, and Generation in the Wild},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Gx4GXvh2y9}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Fast and Precise Multimodal Spatiotemporal Calibration via Periodic-Activated 2D Gaussian Splatting"},"authors":{"value":["Hongbeen Park","Minjeong Park","Sunpil Kim","Jinkyu Kim","Jung Hyun Lee"]}},"tmdate":1760407886523,"pdate":1757875626827,"tcdate":1756847603165,"writers":["thecvf.com/ICCV/2025/Workshop/Wild3D","thecvf.com/ICCV/2025/Workshop/Wild3D/Submission39/Authors"],"signatures":["thecvf.com/ICCV/2025/Workshop/Wild3D/Submission39/Authors"],"forum":"Gx4GXvh2y9","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":39,"cdate":1756847603165,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["thecvf.com/ICCV/2025/Workshop/Wild3D/-/Submission","thecvf.com/ICCV/2025/Workshop/Wild3D/-/Post_Submission","thecvf.com/ICCV/2025/Workshop/Wild3D/-/Edit","thecvf.com/ICCV/2025/Workshop/Wild3D/Submission39/-/Revision"],"mdate":1760407886523,"odate":1760388419181,"domain":"thecvf.com/ICCV/2025/Workshop/Wild3D","id":"Gx4GXvh2y9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.06645v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"popordanoska|beyond_classification_definition_and_densitybased_estimation_of_calibration_in_object_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Teodora_Popordanoska:","~Aleksei_Tiulpin1","~Matthew_B._Blaschko1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2312.06645"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2312-06645,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Teodora Popordanoska and Aleksei Tiulpin and Matthew B. Blaschko},\n  title={Beyond Classification: Definition and Density-based Estimation of Calibration in Object Detection},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2312.06645},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2312.06645}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite their impressive predictive performance in various computer vision tasks, deep neural networks (DNNs) tend to make overly confident predictions, which hinders their widespread use in safety-critical applications. While there have been recent attempts to calibrate DNNs, most of these efforts have primarily been focused on classification tasks, thus neglecting DNN-based object detectors. Although several recent works addressed calibration for object detection and proposed differentiable penalties, none of them are consistent estimators of established concepts in calibration. In this work, we tackle the challenge of defining and estimating calibration error specifically for this task. In particular, we adapt the definition of classification calibration error to handle the nuances associated with object detection, and predictions in structured output spaces more generally. Furthermore, we propose a consistent and differentiable estimator of the detection calibration error, utilizing kernel density estimation. Our experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of our estimator against competing train-time and post-hoc calibration methods, while maintaining similar detection performance."},"title":{"value":"Beyond Classification: Definition and Density-based Estimation of Calibration in Object Detection"},"authors":{"value":["Teodora Popordanoska","Aleksei Tiulpin","Matthew B. Blaschko"]}},"tmdate":1752649730024,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1729753878844,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Matthew_Blaschko1"],"forum":"cdN7E8Bv5Q","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":158897,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1752649730024,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"cdN7E8Bv5Q","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"WACV 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10483279/10483282/10484015.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/WACV/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"popordanoska|beyond_classification_definition_and_densitybased_estimation_of_calibration_in_object_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Teodora_Popordanoska:","~Aleksei_Tiulpin1","~Matthew_B._Blaschko1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV57701.2024.00064"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/wacv/PopordanoskaTB24,\n  author={Teodora Popordanoska and Aleksei Tiulpin and Matthew B. Blaschko},\n  title={Beyond Classification: Definition and Density-based Estimation of Calibration in Object Detection},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={574-583},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV57701.2024.00064},\n  booktitle={WACV},\n  crossref={conf/wacv/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite their impressive predictive performance in various computer vision tasks, deep neural networks (DNNs) tend to make overly confident predictions, which hinders their widespread use in safety-critical applications. While there have been recent attempts to calibrate DNNs, most of these efforts have primarily been focused on classification tasks, thus neglecting DNN-based object detectors. Although several recent works addressed calibration for object detection and proposed differentiable penalties, none of them are consistent estimators of established concepts in calibration. In this work, we tackle the challenge of defining and estimating calibration error specifically for this task. In particular, we adapt the definition of classification calibration error to handle the nuances associated with object detection, and predictions in structured output spaces more generally. Furthermore, we propose a consistent and differentiable estimator of the detection calibration error, utilizing kernel density estimation. Our experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of our estimator against competing train-time and post-hoc calibration methods, while maintaining similar detection performance."},"title":{"value":"Beyond Classification: Definition and Density-based Estimation of Calibration in Object Detection"},"authors":{"value":["Teodora Popordanoska","Aleksei Tiulpin","Matthew B. Blaschko"]}},"tmdate":1729753877093,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727774802434,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Aleksei_Tiulpin1"],"forum":"9ppFOHsFGw","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":129693,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1729753877093,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9ppFOHsFGw","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Under review for TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent studies have argued normatively, on synthetic data, that evaluating survival models\nby discrimination alone (i.e., via concordance index) produces model comparisons that are\nsystematically misleading, because the metric ignores calibration and time-dependent accu-\nracy. Whether this matters for real, published, non-clinical models has not been tested. We\nreproduce three published survival-ML models across three structurally distinct domains –\nhard-drive failure prediction, peer-to-peer credit default, and user disengagement on digital\nplatforms – validate our evaluation instrument against the anchor paper’s own synthetic ex-\nperiment, and test five pre-registered hypotheses under a Holm-corrected family-wise error\nrate. Three of five reject (though this count is contingent on one pre-registered thresh-\nold that clears by a narrow margin). A model that reproduces the published literature’s\ndiscrimination almost exactly (C = 0.9595 vs. 0.958 reported) fails a formal calibration\ntest at p < 0.001; a broad feature-ablation search finds no single attribute responsible for\nthis discrimination, indicating the calibration failure is not an artifact of a trivial short-\ncut. A lender’s estimated default risk is biased upward by roughly two percentage points,\ngrowing to nearly four points in the riskiest segment, when loan prepayment is treated as\nnon-informative censoring rather than as a competing risk. A platform’s churn model shows\nprobability estimates that degrade with the prediction horizon even as its global discrim-\nination remains within the pre-registered C-index band. A direct test of whether metric\nchoice inverts which model would be preferred does not reject, though with limited statis-\ntical power given only two to three models per domain; the failure mode we document is\nbetter characterized as misplaced confidence in a chosen model than as choice of the wrong\none. We release a reusable, pre-registered evaluation harness, together with full code and a\nsingle annotated notebook, so that these results can be independently verified and the audit\nextended to further domains and models"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026the,\ntitle={The C-index illusion: discrimination without calibration in published survival models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=I4ijZQ67XR},\nnote={Under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"The C-index illusion: discrimination without calibration in published survival models"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"We revised the manuscript in response to the reviewers comments and made the main claims more closely match the evidence.\n\nFirst, we removed H_meta as a formal omnibus inference. The five hypotheses are now reported separately with Holm-adjusted p-values. Under the literal H3 rule, H2, H3, and H5 reject; under the proportional H3 sensitivity, only H2 and H5 reject. The resulting $3/5$ and $2/5$ counts are reported only as descriptive summaries, with no global p-value or overall reject/non-reject conclusion.\n\nSecond, we revised the interpretation of H5. Higher raw Brier scores across 12, 24, and 36 months are now interpreted as higher absolute prediction error at later horizons, not as evidence that the model itself degrades. We added a post-hoc comparison with a training-set Kaplan-Meier reference. The Brier Skill Scores were $0.1353$, $0.1322$, and $0.1454$ at 12, 24, and 36 months, respectively. The paired-bootstrap contrast was $BSS(36)-BSS(12)=0.0101$, with 95% CI $[-0.0082, 0.0276]$. These results do not support relative deterioration. The temporal-AUC sensitivity analysis is also non-monotonic and does not support temporal decline.\n\nThird, we revised the calibration claims. The manuscript now states explicitly that D-Calibration is interpreted conditional on non-informative censoring and that the Domain 1 result is an in-sample goodness-of-fit result, not out-of-sample predictive validation. We added fixed-horizon calibration analyses at 12, 24, and 36 months, including predicted risk, observed risk, observed-minus-predicted differences, and ICI. The observed-minus-predicted differences are $0.0117$, $0.0134$, and $0.0214$, respectively. We also moderated the wording throughout the manuscript so that the conclusions do not go beyond these assumptions.\n\nFourth, we clarified the role of H3. H3 is now described as an audit of the cumulative-incidence estimator used under competing risks, not as a direct test of the audited Cox model predictions. The manuscript now states explicitly that the observed difference between the naive estimator and the Aalen-Johansen estimator does not show that the Cox models are defective or miscalibrated. We also retained the preregistration ambiguity transparently: the frozen rule used an absolute count of at least three strata, while the observed Bondora data contain seven rating strata. The literal and proportional interpretations are both reported.\n\nWe also improved the clarity of several figures and made small consistency edits across the abstract, methods, results, discussion, conclusion, and protocol appendix. In particular, we softened calibration wording, clarified that downstream discrepancies may arise from models, data, or evaluation procedures, and restored the literal frozen H3 wording in the appendix while keeping the denominator mismatch disclosure.\n\nOverall, the revised manuscript now presents a narrower conclusion: in the evaluated published models and domains, the global C-index does not capture every decision-relevant dimension examined. Calibration analyses, competing-risk-aware evaluation, and horizon-specific proper scores provide complementary information, subject to their assumptions."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d2e5e81073f2fdb925554d71876f3e979465dea8.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Under_Review"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Jiwei_Zhao1"}},"tmdate":1789093408963,"tcdate":1784662591174,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper10715/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper10715/Authors"],"forum":"I4ijZQ67XR","number":10715,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1784662591174,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper10715/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review"],"mdate":1789093408963,"odate":1786375179485,"domain":"TMLR","id":"I4ijZQ67XR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SPAC 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6971853/6982642/06982658.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/SPAC/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"zheng|an_efficient_and_practical_calibration_method_for_roadside_camera_using_two_vanishing_points"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuan_Zheng_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhenyu_He_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wei-Guo_Yang:","~Xiaofeng_Zhang1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/SPAC.2014.6982658"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/spac/ZhengHYZ14,\n  author={Yuan Zheng and Zhenyu He and Wei-Guo Yang and Xiaofeng Zhang},\n  title={An efficient and practical calibration method for roadside camera using two vanishing points},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={65-69},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/SPAC.2014.6982658},\n  booktitle={SPAC},\n  crossref={conf/spac/2014}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrating roadside camera is essential and indispensable for intelligent traffic surveillance systems. Due to the characteristics of the traffic scenes, the traditional camera calibration methods based on calibration patterns are no longer suitable, since there are generally no calibration patterns (e.g. checkerboard) in traffic scenes. In this paper, we propose a simple and practical calibration method for roadside camera, where the vanishing point in the traffic road direction and the vertical vanishing point are employed that can be easily obtained from most traffic scenes. By making full use of video information, the multiple observations of two vanishing points are available. In order to obtain more accurate calibration results, we present a dynamic calibration method that employs these observations to correct camera parameters and substitutes least squares optimization for closed-form computation. The experimental results on real traffic images demonstrate the effectiveness and practicability of the proposed calibration method."},"title":{"value":"An efficient and practical calibration method for roadside camera using two vanishing points"},"authors":{"value":["Yuan Zheng","Zhenyu He","Wei-Guo Yang","Xiaofeng Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1744196278901,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1744196269906,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiaofeng_Zhang1"],"forum":"Wx6VyjjBmV","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":386191,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744196278901,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Wx6VyjjBmV","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.11915v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"tonini|two_new_calibration_techniques_of_lumpedparameter_mathematical_models_for_the_cardiovascular_system"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrea_Tonini:","~Francesco_Regazzoni1","~Matteo_Salvador1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luca_Dede':","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Roberto_Scrofani:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Laura_Fusini:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chiara_Cogliati:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gianluca_Pontone:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Christian_Vergara:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alfio_Quarteroni:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.11915"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2405-11915,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Andrea Tonini and Francesco Regazzoni and Matteo Salvador and Luca Dede' and Roberto Scrofani and Laura Fusini and Chiara Cogliati and Gianluca Pontone and Christian Vergara and Alfio Quarteroni},\n  title={Two new calibration techniques of lumped-parameter mathematical models for the cardiovascular system},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2405.11915},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.11915}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Cardiocirculatory mathematical models serve as valuable tools for investigating physiological and pathological conditions of the circulatory system. To investigate the clinical condition of an individual, cardiocirculatory models need to be personalized by means of calibration methods. In this study we propose a new calibration method for a lumped-parameter cardiocirculatory model. This calibration method utilizes the correlation matrix between parameters and model outputs to calibrate the latter according to data. We test this calibration method and its combination with L-BFGS-B (Limited memory Broyden - Fletcher - Goldfarb - Shanno with Bound constraints) comparing them with the performances of L-BFGS-B alone. We show that the correlation matrix calibration method and the combined one effectively reduce the loss function of the associated optimization problem. In the case of in silico generated data, we show that the two new calibration methods are robust with respect to the initial guess of parameters and to the presence of noise in the data. Notably, the correlation matrix calibration method achieves the best results in estimating the parameters in the case of noisy data and it is faster than the combined calibration method and L-BFGS-B. Finally, we present real test case where the two new calibration methods yield results comparable to those obtained using L-BFGS-B in terms of minimizing the loss function and estimating the clinical data. This highlights the effectiveness of the new calibration methods for clinical applications."},"title":{"value":"Two new calibration techniques of lumped-parameter mathematical models for the cardiovascular system"},"authors":{"value":["Andrea Tonini","Francesco Regazzoni","Matteo Salvador","Luca Dede'","Roberto Scrofani","Laura Fusini","Chiara Cogliati","Gianluca Pontone","Christian Vergara","Alfio Quarteroni"]}},"tmdate":1778066337402,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1746878290675,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Francesco_Regazzoni1"],"forum":"SBWtjCkY7L","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":415543,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1778066337402,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SBWtjCkY7L","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"TASCT improves existing time series forecasters through temporal–spectral input adaptation and level-preserving output calibration, with additional gains when combined with other forecasting plugins."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["time series forecasting","temporal-spectral adaptation","forecast calibration","mean-preserving correction"]},"primary_area":{"value":"learning on time series and dynamical systems"},"abstract":{"value":"We introduce Temporal--Spectral Adaptation and Shape Calibration for Time Series Forecasting (TASCT), which combines temporal--spectral input adaptation with level-preserving forecast calibration.\nIts Temporal--Spectral Adapter (TSA) combines centered temporal corrections with frequency-grouped input adaptation, preserving the historical mean.\nIts Level-Preserving Calibrator (LPC) combines centered contextual and consistency corrections, preserving the pre-calibration forecast mean.\nAcross five backbones, seven datasets, and four forecasting horizons, TASCT improves three-seed mean MSE in 130 of 140 settings, with an average relative reduction of 4.17\\%.\nExperiments with LIFT and CCM further show that TASCT can provide additional gains on top of existing forecasting plugins.\nThese results demonstrate individual and combined gains from input adaptation and output calibration."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026tasct,\ntitle={{TASCT}: Temporal{\\textendash}Spectral Adaptation and Shape Calibration for Time Series Forecasting},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=E91M11aur5},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"TASCT: Temporal–Spectral Adaptation and Shape Calibration for Time Series Forecasting"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8ca92f8540eefb77afbf7dc88b2cfa176a922e13.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794132150487,"tcdate":1789611599751,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission27641/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission27641/Authors"],"forum":"E91M11aur5","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":27641,"cdate":1789611599751,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission27641/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission27641/-/Rebuttal_Revision"],"mdate":1794132150487,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"E91M11aur5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IST 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11268367/11268353/11268387.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"song|a_recalibration_method_for_object_detection_with_multimodal_alignment_bias_in_autonomous_driving"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IST66504.2025.11268387"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ist/SongYMPYZ25a,\n  author={Zhihang Song and Dingyi Yao and Ruibo Ming and Lihui Peng and Danya Yao and Yi Zhang},\n  title={A Re-Calibration Method for Object Detection with Multimodal Alignment Bias in Autonomous Driving},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={1-6},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IST66504.2025.11268387},\n  booktitle={IST},\n  crossref={conf/ist/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-modal object detection in autonomous driving has achieved great breakthroughs due to the usage of fusing complementary information from different sensors. The calibration in fusion between sensors such as LiDAR and camera was always supposed to be precise in previous work. However, in reality, calibration matrices are fixed when the vehicles leave the factory, but mechanical vibration, road bumps, and data lags may cause calibration bias. As there is relatively limited research on the impact of calibration on fusion detection performance, multi-sensor detection methods with flexible calibration dependency have remained a key objective. In this paper, we systematically evaluate the sensitivity of the SOTA EPNet++ detection framework and prove that even slight bias on calibration can reduce the performance seriously. To address this vulnerability, we propose a re-calibration model to re-calibrate the misalignment in detection tasks. This model integrates LiDAR point cloud, camera image, and initial calibration matrix as inputs, generating re-calibrated bias through semantic segmentation guidance and a tailored loss function design. The re-calibration model can operate with existing detection algorithms, enhancing both robustness against calibration bias and overall object detection performance. Our approach establishes a foundational methodology for maintaining reliability in multi-modal perception systems under real-world calibration uncertainties."},"title":{"value":"A Re-Calibration Method for Object Detection with Multimodal Alignment Bias in Autonomous Driving"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhihang Song","username":""},{"fullname":"Dingyi Yao","username":"~Dingyi_Yao1"},{"fullname":"Ruibo Ming","username":"~Ruibo_Ming1"},{"fullname":"Lihui Peng","username":""},{"fullname":"Danya Yao","username":""},{"fullname":"Yi Zhang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1789979114091,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/ist/SongYMPYZ25a"],"tcdate":1779787645623,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Ruibo_Ming1"],"forum":"UKRt8ddWTL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":28187,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1789979114091,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"UKRt8ddWTL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICME 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5997811/6011826/06011885.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICMCS/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"chiu|novel_projector_calibration_approaches_of_multiresolution_display"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICME.2011.6011885"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icmcs/ChiuLCYH11,\n  author={Po-Hsun Chiu and Shih-Yao Lin and Li-Wei Chan and Neng-Hao Yu and Yi-Ping Hung},\n  title={Novel projector calibration approaches of multi-resolution display},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={1-6},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICME.2011.6011885},\n  booktitle={ICME},\n  crossref={conf/icmcs/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes convenient and useful approaches to automatically calibrate the projectors of a multi-resolution display. The proposed approaches estimate both the keystone effect and misalignment of the projections with an assistance of a color camera. Structured light patterns are employed to construct the geometric relationship between projectors and the projection surface, and then pre-warp the images so that they appear undistorted as a result. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed approaches successfully reduce the human-effort and lower the calibration time of multi-resolution display calibration task."},"title":{"value":"Novel projector calibration approaches of multi-resolution display"},"authors":{"value":["Po-Hsun Chiu","Shih-Yao Lin","Li-Wei Chan","Neng-Hao Yu","Yi-Ping Hung"]}},"tmdate":1772225676341,"pdate":1325289600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icmcs/ChiuLCYH11"],"tcdate":1772225549508,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shih-Yao_Lin2"],"forum":"Mvm43KRkko","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":834232,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772225676341,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Mvm43KRkko","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Human Vision and Electronic Imaging 1996"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.spiedigitallibrary.org/conference-proceedings-of-spie/2657/0000/Halftone-independent-calibration-of-black-and-white-printers/10.1117/12.238739.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/HVEI/1996"},"paperhash":{"value":"knox|halftoneindependent_calibration_of_blackandwhite_printers"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Keith_T._Knox:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Charles_M._Hains:","~Gaurav_Sharma3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1117/12.238739"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/hvei/KnoxH096,\n  author={Keith T. Knox and Charles M. Hains and Gaurav Sharma},\n  title={Halftone-independent calibration of black-and-white printers},\n  year={1996},\n  cdate={820454400000},\n  pages={432-436},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1117/12.238739},\n  booktitle={Human Vision and Electronic Imaging},\n  crossref={conf/hvei/1996}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Halftone calibration of a black and white printer is a known process that involves printing and measuring patches for many different halftone levels. It is a tedious process that has to be repeated for every halftone dot or algorithm to be used. A new calibration procedure is described that uses a halftone-independent characterization of the printer and a pixel overlap model to predict the tone response of any halftone algorithm. This enables all halftone dots and algorithms to be calibrated with only one set of printer measurements."},"title":{"value":"Halftone-independent calibration of black-and-white printers"},"authors":{"value":["Keith T. Knox","Charles M. Hains","Gaurav Sharma"]}},"tmdate":1740107125571,"pdate":820454400000,"tcdate":1740106890795,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Gaurav_Sharma3"],"forum":"id63vx4zvh","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":337355,"cdate":820454400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740107125571,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"id63vx4zvh","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Model learns when to trust each analyzer on LLM-generated code and powers a conformal, risk-controlled CI gate capping escaped vulns at alpha. On a multi-language benchmark with dynamic tests+SAST, it beats union/unanimity and generalizes OOD."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Desk Rejected Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["conformal risk control","CI/CD","LLM-generated code","code security","analyzer fusion","noisy annotators","instance-dependent reliability","vulnerability detection","CWE","dynamic testing","SAST cross-validation","calibration","OOD generalization"]},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Security tools frequently disagree on vulnerabilities in LLM-generated code, leaving CI pipelines to trade off developer friction against escaped defects. We introduce a risk-controlled CI framework that learns when to trust which tool and provides finite-sample guarantees on the escaped-vulnerability rate. First, we propose a Latent Vulnerability Model that fuses code representations with instance-dependent reliabilities of multiple analyzers (e.g., CodeQL, Semgrep, Bandit), estimated from a small gold set. The model outputs calibrated p(vuln∣x) and per-CWE reliability maps. Second, we derive a cost-aware CI policy and wrap it with Conformal Risk Control, yielding a deployment-time certificate that the EVR is less than or equal to alpha without distributional assumptions. To drive reproducibility, we release a multi-language benchmark (Python, JavaScript, and a typed language), comprising LLM-generated code across model families, dynamic tests and SAST cross-validation for ground truth, and loaders/CLI for evaluation. Across languages and unseen model families, our method dominates union/unanimity and stacking baselines at matched break-rates, maintains its EVR guarantees under stratified OOD calibration, and improves downstream repair ROI in a small CI pilot. Our results position risk-controlled tool fusion as a practical path to measurably safer LLM code generation in real engineering pipelines."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nanonymous2025riskcontrolled,\ntitle={Risk-Controlled {CI} Gating for {LLM} Code via Noisy-Analyzer Fusion},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=WpkxQOcrZg}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Risk-Controlled CI Gating for LLM Code via Noisy-Analyzer Fusion"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d8d610ce9b903151e2d5b1c76417dc5fe0a58d7a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Desk_Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"shukla|riskcontrolled_ci_gating_for_llm_code_via_noisyanalyzer_fusion"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shivani_Shukla1","~Himanshu_Joshi2"]},"authors":{"value":["Shivani Shukla","Himanshu Joshi"]}},"tmdate":1770907901402,"tcdate":1758344659232,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23502/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23502/Authors"],"forum":"WpkxQOcrZg","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":23502,"cdate":1758344659232,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23502/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Desk_Rejected_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770907901402,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"WpkxQOcrZg","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.17446v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"ni|l2ceval_evaluating_languagetocode_generation_capabilities_of_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ansong_Ni:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pengcheng_Yin:","~Yilun_Zhao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Martin_Riddell:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Troy_Feng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rui_Shen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Stephen_Yin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ye_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Semih_Yavuz:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Caiming_Xiong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shafiq_Joty:","~Yingbo_Zhou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dragomir_Radev:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Arman_Cohan:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2309.17446"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2309-17446,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ansong Ni and Pengcheng Yin and Yilun Zhao and Martin Riddell and Troy Feng and Rui Shen and Stephen Yin and Ye Liu and Semih Yavuz and Caiming Xiong and Shafiq Joty and Yingbo Zhou and Dragomir Radev and Arman Cohan},\n  title={L2CEval: Evaluating Language-to-Code Generation Capabilities of Large Language Models},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2309.17446},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2309.17446}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recently, large language models (LLMs), especially those that are pretrained on code, have demonstrated strong capabilities in generating programs from natural language inputs in a few-shot or even zero-shot manner. Despite promising results, there is a notable lack of a comprehensive evaluation of these models language-to-code generation capabilities. Existing studies often focus on specific tasks, model architectures, or learning paradigms, leading to a fragmented understanding of the overall landscape. In this work, we present L2CEval, a systematic evaluation of the language-to-code generation capabilities of LLMs on 7 tasks across the domain spectrum of semantic parsing, math reasoning and Python programming, analyzing the factors that potentially affect their performance, such as model size, pretraining data, instruction tuning, and different prompting methods. In addition to assessing model performance, we measure confidence calibration for the models and conduct human evaluations of the output programs. This enables us to identify and analyze the typical failure modes across various tasks and models. L2CEval offers a comprehensive understanding of the capabilities and limitations of LLMs in language-to-code generation. We also release the evaluation framework and all model outputs, hoping to lay the groundwork for further future research in this domain."},"title":{"value":"L2CEval: Evaluating Language-to-Code Generation Capabilities of Large Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Ansong Ni","Pengcheng Yin","Yilun Zhao","Martin Riddell","Troy Feng","Rui Shen","Stephen Yin","Ye Liu","Semih Yavuz","Caiming Xiong","Shafiq Joty","Yingbo Zhou","Dragomir Radev","Arman Cohan"]}},"tmdate":1753907494012,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1713455217066,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yingbo_Zhou1"],"forum":"HR8D9BKP4E","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":1585,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1753907494012,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"HR8D9BKP4E","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["Bayesian Optimization; Goal-Oriented Calibration; Gaussian Processes; Expected Improvement"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\npion2026goaloriented,\ntitle={Goal-Oriented Lower-Tail Calibration of Gaussian Processes for Bayesian Optimization},\nauthor={Aur{\\'e}lien Pion and Emmanuel Vazquez},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=O0gI00yRnm}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Goal-Oriented Lower-Tail Calibration of Gaussian Processes for Bayesian Optimization"},"paperhash":{"value":"pion|goaloriented_lowertail_calibration_of_gaussian_processes_for_bayesian_optimization"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/b11708a3fcda047d2836001364b662b755817c5f.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"We define spatial calibration below a threshold, introduce a post-hoc lower-tail calibration method for Gaussian-process predictive distributions, and study its impact on Bayesian optimization with expected improvement."},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic_methods->gaussian_processes"},"abstract":{"value":"Gaussian process (GP) predictive distributions are commonly used in Bayesian\noptimization (BO) to guide the selection of evaluation points for expensive\nobjective functions. \nThe choice of kernel and hyperparameters has a strong influence on the\nexploration--exploitation trade-off. \nFor minimization, sampling criteria such as expected improvement (EI) depend on\nboth the probability mass below the current best value and the shape of the\npredictive distribution in this region. \nThis article studies goal-oriented calibration of GP predictive\ndistributions below a low threshold $t$ in the noiseless setting, for\nstandard GP models with hyperparameters selected by maximum\nlikelihood. We consider two complementary forms of calibration below $t$ for inputs\ndistributed according to a reference measure $\\mu$: occurrence calibration over\nthe design space and thresholded $\\mu$-calibration on sublevel sets of the form\n$\\lbrace x\\in\\mathbb{X}, f(x)\\le t \\rbrace$. We propose tcGP, a post-hoc method that combines\nthese two forms of calibration for GP predictive distributions below $t$. With\nfixed GP hyperparameters, the exact EI\nsampling criterion based on tcGP generates a sequence of evaluation\npoints that is dense in the design space. Experiments on standard\nbenchmarks show improved lower-tail calibration and BO performance\nrelative to standard GP models and globally calibrated GP models."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/5315975109c0e54c4c66e5366d86359416cc921b.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Many scientific and engineering tasks require finding the best setting of a system, such as the shape of a component, the parameters of a simulation, or the settings of a machine learning model. Testing one setting can be slow or expensive, so Bayesian optimization tries to choose each new test carefully. To do this, it builds a model that predicts both the likely result of a test and how uncertain that prediction is. In practice, these uncertainty estimates can be unreliable, especially in the most promising regions where low objective values are expected.\n \nWe study how to make these predictions more reliable specifically in those promising low-value regions. We introduce a way to measure this targeted reliability and propose a calibration method called tcGP that adjusts Gaussian process predictions with this goal in mind. Experiments on standard optimization problems show that this targeted approach improves both prediction reliability and optimization performance. By helping Bayesian optimization focus its tests more effectively, this method can reduce the number of expensive evaluations needed in practice."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Aurélien_Pion1","~Emmanuel_Vazquez1"]},"authors":{"value":["Aurélien Pion","Emmanuel Vazquez"]}},"tmdate":1790067417736,"pdate":1777576770809,"tcdate":1769189942970,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission26918/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission26918/Authors"],"forum":"O0gI00yRnm","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","number":26918,"cdate":1769189942970,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission26918/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission26918/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067417736,"odate":1782342003106,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"O0gI00yRnm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"LifeTech 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9754591/9754592/09754875.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/LIFETECH/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"miyahara|camera_calibration_using_street_surveillance_camera_for_detecting_wandering_elderly"},"authorids":{"value":["~Natsuki_Miyahara1","~Takahiro_Yoshioka1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Takeshi_Konno:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LifeTech53646.2022.9754875"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/lifetech/MiyaharaYK22,\n  author={Natsuki Miyahara and Takahiro Yoshioka and Takeshi Konno},\n  title={Camera Calibration Using Street Surveillance Camera for Detecting Wandering Elderly},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={314-315},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LifeTech53646.2022.9754875},\n  booktitle={LifeTech},\n  crossref={conf/lifetech/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this study, we propose a camera calibration method intended for detecting wandering elderlies using surveillance cameras. Using camera calibration, it is possible to obtain various characteristics of the elderly automatically, such as physical information and the size of belongings. Because the previous method has only shown its effectiveness for different situations from surveillance cameras, we propose a method to achieve high reliability for camera calibration towards dataset of possible shooting conditions in surveillance cameras. Furthermore, in the previous method, it was assumed that multiple people were shown in the video, which contributed to the construction of the geometric relationship in the estimation process. Hence, it is difficult to achieve high reliability of camera calibration for a video showing only one person, although wandering incident could occur without anyone around the elderly. Therefore, we propose a method for increasing constraints in the construction of geometric relationships by assigning multiple IDs instead of assigning one ID to the person. As a result, our method achieved a minimum camera height estimation error of 3.2 cm. We will continue this research for a more reliable camera calibration, and we further aim to construct a detection system for wandering elderlies to contribute to a safe and secure society."},"title":{"value":"Camera Calibration Using Street Surveillance Camera for Detecting Wandering Elderly"},"authors":{"value":["Natsuki Miyahara","Takahiro Yoshioka","Takeshi Konno"]}},"tmdate":1773797446872,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731307210896,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Natsuki_Miyahara1"],"forum":"bsmJzpyqQC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":179652,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1773797446872,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bsmJzpyqQC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.12412v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"rachman|endtoend_lidarcamera_selfcalibration_for_autonomous_vehicles"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Arya_Rachman:","~Jürgen_Seiler1","~Andre_Kaup1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2304.12412"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2304-12412,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Arya Rachman and Jürgen Seiler and André Kaup},\n  title={End-to-End Lidar-Camera Self-Calibration for Autonomous Vehicles},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2304.12412},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2304.12412}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Autonomous vehicles are equipped with a multi-modal sensor setup to enable the car to drive safely. The initial calibration of such perception sensors is a highly matured topic and is routinely done in an automated factory environment. However, an intriguing question arises on how to maintain the calibration quality throughout the vehicle's operating duration. Another challenge is to calibrate multiple sensors jointly to ensure no propagation of systemic errors. In this paper, we propose CaLiCa, an end-to-end deep self-calibration network which addresses the automatic calibration problem for pinhole camera and Lidar. We jointly predict the camera intrinsic parameters (focal length and distortion) as well as Lidar-Camera extrinsic parameters (rotation and translation), by regressing feature correlation between the camera image and the Lidar point cloud. The network is arranged in a Siamese-twin structure to constrain the network features learning to a mutually shared feature in both point cloud and camera (Lidar-camera constraint). Evaluation using KITTI datasets shows that we achieve 0.154 {\\deg} and 0.059 m accuracy with a reprojection error of 0.028 pixel with a single-pass inference. We also provide an ablative study of how our end-to-end learning architecture offers lower terminal loss (21% decrease in rotation loss) compared to isolated calibration"},"title":{"value":"End-to-End Lidar-Camera Self-Calibration for Autonomous Vehicles"},"authors":{"value":["Arya Rachman","Jürgen Seiler","André Kaup"]}},"tmdate":1739881456929,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731573805220,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Andre_Kaup1"],"forum":"zWX7gWBGx6","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":241070,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1739881456929,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zWX7gWBGx6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["calibration","online calibration","lower bounds","kernel calibration","online learning","approachability"]},"primary_area":{"value":"learning theory"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrated forecasting in online adversarial settings has been widely studied and occupies a central place in online learning theory. Yet, fundamental gaps still remain in our understanding of minimax rates for a number of key notions of online calibration. In particular, tight online rates for the central \\emph{smooth calibration} and \\emph{calibration distance} notions have remained open, with a substantial gap between the known $\\Omega(T^{1/3})$ and $O(\\sqrt T)$ bounds. In this work, we settle the minimax rate for both notions at $\\Theta(\\sqrt{T})$, with the tight lower bound witnessed by a single \\emph{oblivious} adversary. To do so, we develop a simple and general lower-bounding \\emph{two-test principle}, which forces the forecaster to incur $\\Omega(\\sqrt{T})$ calibration error via a single carefully constructed pair of calibration tests.\n\nWe next demonstrate the wide applicability of our two-test principle. As an example, we settle the minimax rate of the above calibration notions under delayed feedback. Next, we apply our principle to online \\emph{kernel calibration}, which admits Vovk's classical $O(\\sqrt{T})$ upper bound. We obtain the first matching $\\Omega(\\sqrt{T})$ lower bounds for a wide class of kernels, including the Gaussian and Laplace ones.\nYet, we then show that the broader kernel calibration landscape is far too nuanced to permit kernel-agnostic $\\sqrt{T}$-type rates: instead, kernels that permit various degrees of inter-prediction \\emph{cancellation} can be exploited by the learner to obtain faster rates of $\\Theta(\\log T)$ or even $\\Theta(1)$. This opens up an investigation of minimax calibration rates for different kernel geometries."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026sharp,\ntitle={Sharp Rates in Online Calibration: Two-Test Lower Bounds and Kernel Geometry},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=yUBw8ooX1o},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Sharp Rates in Online Calibration: Two-Test Lower Bounds and Kernel Geometry"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c146cfc373e69f0a54a237427235babe07a5be76.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791234318819,"tcdate":1789771781877,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission49910/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission49910/Authors"],"forum":"yUBw8ooX1o","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":49910,"cdate":1789771781877,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission49910/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791234318819,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"yUBw8ooX1o","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SUM 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-319-67582-4_30.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/SUM/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"minary|evidential_joint_calibration_of_binary_svm_classifiers_using_logistic_regression"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pauline_Minary:","~Frédéric_Pichon1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:David_Mercier:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Eric_Lefevre:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Benjamin_Droit:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-67582-4_30"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/sum/MinaryPMLD17,\n  author={Pauline Minary and Frédéric Pichon and David Mercier and Eric Lefevre and Benjamin Droit},\n  title={Evidential Joint Calibration of Binary SVM Classifiers Using Logistic Regression},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  pages={405-411},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-67582-4_30},\n  booktitle={SUM},\n  crossref={conf/sum/2017}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In a context of multiple classifiers, a calibration step based on logistic regression is usually used to independently transform each classifier output into a probability distribution, to be then able to combine them. This calibration has been recently refined, using the evidence theory, to better handle uncertainties. In this paper, we propose to use this logistic-based calibration in a multivariable scenario, i.e., to consider jointly all the outputs returned by the classifiers, and to extend this approach to the evidential framework. Our evidential approach was tested on generated and real datasets and presents several advantages over the probabilistic version."},"title":{"value":"Evidential Joint Calibration of Binary SVM Classifiers Using Logistic Regression"},"authors":{"value":["Pauline Minary","Frédéric Pichon","David Mercier","Eric Lefevre","Benjamin Droit"]}},"tmdate":1732718282855,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1732718270283,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Frédéric_Pichon1"],"forum":"ehnSXvBNyT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":251591,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1732718282855,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ehnSXvBNyT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["RLVR","Model Calibration","Over-confidence","Decoupled Estimation"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/42811b99d41f485c1f510abd576f729f763a81b8.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nma2026decoupling,\ntitle={Decoupling Reasoning and Confidence: Resurrecting Calibration in Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards},\nauthor={Zhengzhao Ma and Xueru Wen and Boxi Cao and Yaojie Lu and Hongyu Lin and Jinglin Yang and Min He and Xianpei Han and Le Sun},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=3V1p2bJugq}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Decoupling Reasoning and Confidence: Resurrecting Calibration in Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards"},"paperhash":{"value":"ma|decoupling_reasoning_and_confidence_resurrecting_calibration_in_reinforcement_learning_from_verifiable_rewards"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/3fad3682e55f23d6b8015c4d3ab921ad5b440b24.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"deep_learning->large_language_models"},"abstract":{"value":"Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) significantly enhances large language models (LLMs) reasoning but severely suffers from calibration degeneration, where models become excessively over-confident in incorrect answers.\nPrevious studies devote to directly incorporating calibration objective into existing optimization target.\nHowever, our theoretical analysis demonstrates that there exists a fundamental gradient conflict between the optimization for maximizing policy accuracy and minimizing calibration error.\nBuilding on this insight, we propose DCPO, a simple yet effective framework that systematically decouples reasoning and calibration objectives.\nExtensive experiments demonstrate that \nour DCPO not only preserves accuracy on par with GRPO but also achieves the best calibration performance and substantially mitigates the over-confidence issue.\nOur study provides valuable insights and practical solution for more reliable LLM deployment."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://github.com/icip-cas/DCPO"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fc6c7f7fac2bee25bf0a187b0ad95dce8e595a16.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Modern AI systems trained with reinforcement learning are becoming increasingly capable at solving complex problems through reinforcement learning. However, these systems often become overly confident in their answers, even when they are wrong. This overconfidence is especially concerning in high-stakes applications such as healthcare, finance, and law, where users may rely on AI predictions without realizing their uncertainty.\n\nIn this work, we study why reinforcement learning causes this problem and show that improving reasoning accuracy and improving confidence reliability can directly interfere with each other during training. Based on this insight, we propose a new training framework called DCPO that separates these two objectives instead of optimizing them together.\n\nOur method teaches models to generate both an answer and an explicit confidence estimate, while training reasoning and confidence signals independently. This allows the model to maintain strong reasoning ability while producing more trustworthy confidence estimates.\n\nExperiments on mathematical reasoning and code generation tasks show that DCPO substantially reduces overconfidence while preserving the performance gains brought by reinforcement learning. Our findings contribute toward building more reliable and trustworthy AI systems."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhengzhao_Ma1","~Xueru_Wen1","~Boxi_Cao1","~Yaojie_Lu1","~Hongyu_Lin1","~Jinglin_Yang2","~Min_He7","~Xianpei_Han1","~Le_Sun1"]},"authors":{"value":["Zhengzhao Ma","Xueru Wen","Boxi Cao","Yaojie Lu","Hongyu Lin","Jinglin Yang","Min He","Xianpei Han","Le Sun"]}},"tmdate":1790067396345,"pdate":1777576629791,"tcdate":1769151509455,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission21631/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission21631/Authors"],"forum":"3V1p2bJugq","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":21631,"cdate":1769151509455,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission21631/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission21631/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067396345,"odate":1782341982874,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"3V1p2bJugq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.09117v3"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"ma|decoupling_reasoning_and_confidence_resurrecting_calibration_in_reinforcement_learning_from_verifiable_rewards"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.09117"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2603-09117,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhengzhao Ma and Xueru Wen and Boxi Cao and Yaojie Lu and Hongyu Lin and Jinglin Yang and Min He and Xianpei Han and Le Sun},\n  title={Decoupling Reasoning and Confidence: Resurrecting Calibration in Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards},\n  year={2026},\n  month={March},\n  cdate={1772323200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2603.09117},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.09117}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) significantly enhances large language models (LLMs) reasoning but severely suffers from calibration degeneration, where models become excessively over-confident in incorrect answers. Previous studies devote to directly incorporating calibration objective into existing optimization target. However, our theoretical analysis demonstrates that there exists a fundamental gradient conflict between the optimization for maximizing policy accuracy and minimizing calibration error. Building on this insight, we propose DCPO, a simple yet effective framework that systematically decouples reasoning and calibration objectives. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our DCPO not only preserves accuracy on par with GRPO but also achieves the best calibration performance and substantially mitigates the over-confidence issue. Our study provides valuable insights and practical solution for more reliable LLM deployment."},"title":{"value":"Decoupling Reasoning and Confidence: Resurrecting Calibration in Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhengzhao Ma","username":"~Zhengzhao_Ma1"},{"fullname":"Xueru Wen","username":""},{"fullname":"Boxi Cao","username":""},{"fullname":"Yaojie Lu","username":""},{"fullname":"Hongyu Lin","username":""},{"fullname":"Jinglin Yang","username":""},{"fullname":"Min He","username":""},{"fullname":"Xianpei Han","username":""},{"fullname":"Le Sun","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784633694590,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2603-09117"],"tcdate":1784633691159,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Zhengzhao_Ma1"],"forum":"zIdNUe54QJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":82485,"cdate":1772323200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784633694590,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"zIdNUe54QJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRL 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v229/fu23a/fu23a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CORL/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"fu|batch_differentiable_pose_refinement_for_inthewild_cameralidar_extrinsic_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lanke_Frank_Tarimo_Fu:","~Maurice_F._Fallon1"]},"html":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v229/fu23a.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/corl/FuF23,\n  author={Lanke Frank Tarimo Fu and Maurice F. Fallon},\n  title={Batch Differentiable Pose Refinement for In-The-Wild Camera/LiDAR Extrinsic Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1362-1377},\n  url={https://proceedings.mlr.press/v229/fu23a.html},\n  booktitle={CoRL},\n  crossref={conf/corl/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate camera to LiDAR (Light Detection and Ranging) extrinsic calibration is important for robotic tasks carrying out tight sensor fusion — such as target tracking and odometry. Calibration is typically performed before deployment in controlled conditions using calibration targets, however, this limits scalability and subsequent recalibration. We propose a novel approach for target-free camera-LiDAR calibration using end-to-end direct alignment which doesn’t need calibration targets. Our batched formulation enhances sample efficiency during training and robustness at inference time. We present experimental results, on publicly available real-world data, demonstrating 1.6cm/$0.07^{\\circ}$ median accuracy when transferred to unseen sensors from held-out data sequences. We also show state-of-the-art zero-shot transfer to unseen cameras, LiDARs, and environments."},"title":{"value":"Batch Differentiable Pose Refinement for In-The-Wild Camera/LiDAR Extrinsic Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Lanke Frank Tarimo Fu","Maurice F. Fallon"]}},"tmdate":1731495122463,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731491568176,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Maurice_Fallon1"],"forum":"Exx9ukSeEO","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":221811,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731495122463,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Exx9ukSeEO","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Open-weight LLM confidence on African-language multiple-choice tasks is strongly model-dependent and can change substantially under simple prompt perturbations."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM calibration","uncertainty quantification","multilingual language models","African languages","large language models","prompt robustness","confidence estimation","evaluation","responsible AI"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/605f7f23f4f8dfd02fc09f2f49ba5fb3d6f7f2a7.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Research question: Are open-weight LLMs well-calibrated on African-language multiple-choice questions, does their calibration survive prompt perturbations, and at what compute cost? Answer: Mostly no. The failure is model-specific in ways aggregate benchmarks hide, and the full audit costs under 40 USD. We audit four open-weight instruct models (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B) on 422,000 MCQ items spanning 24 Global-MMLU languages (9 African, 15 European) and 18 afrimmlu configurations, under 5 prompt perturbations, for a total cost of 39.48 USD on serverless A100s. Accuracy drops 16-31 points from European to African language cells for every model. The calibration gap, measured as Expected Calibration Error (ECE), is real but model-dependent at baseline: large for Qwen (+0.140 ECE [0.099, 0.177]) and Gemma (+0.133 [0.109, 0.159]), absent for Llama, and reversed for Mistral. Perturbation effects flip sign across models; a format that calibrates one model can break another, and Gemma loses 20 accuracy points in African language cells simply when asked to \"answer with a single letter\", with a 26.1% fallback rate across evaluated African cells that reaches 100% in some format_A afrimmlu cells. Miscalibration is largely repairable in-distribution: regularized logistic regression fit on approximately 250 items per cell cuts held-out ECE to 0.04-0.10 depending on region. We provide code, parsed outputs, and an executable reproduction notebook."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026when,\ntitle={When Confidence Doesn{\\textquoteright}t Travel: Calibration of Open-Weight {LLM}s on African-Language {MCQ}s Under Prompt Perturbations},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=AUmfeRoT2j},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"When Confidence Doesn’t Travel: Calibration of Open-Weight LLMs on African-Language MCQs Under Prompt Perturbations"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6ad620b01ad0e00f922b86d08121243584dfb2ba.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791228962869,"tcdate":1789413504791,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission18918/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission18918/Authors"],"forum":"AUmfeRoT2j","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":18918,"cdate":1789413504791,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing"],"mdate":1791228962869,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"AUmfeRoT2j","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10654794/10654797/10657684.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"qian|from_a_birds_eye_view_to_see_joint_camera_and_subject_registration_without_the_camera_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zekun_Qian:","~Ruize_Han1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wei_Feng_0005:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Song_Wang_0002:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52733.2024.00088"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/QianH0024,\n  author={Zekun Qian and Ruize Han and Wei Feng and Song Wang},\n  title={From a Bird's Eye View to See: Joint Camera and Subject Registration without the Camera Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={863-873},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52733.2024.00088},\n  booktitle={CVPR},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We tackle a new problem of multi-view camera and sub-ject registration in the bird’ s eye view (BEV) without pregiven camera calibration, which promotes the multi-view subject registration problem to a new calibration-free stage. This greatly alleviates the limitation in many practical applications. However, this is a very challenging problem since its only input is several RGB images from different first-person views (FPVs), without the BEV image and the calibration of the FPVs, while the output is a unified plane aggregated from all views with the positions and orientations of both the subjects and cameras in a BEV. For this purpose, we propose an end-to-end framework solving cam-era and subject registration together by taking advantage of their mutual dependence, whose main idea is as below: i) creating a subject view-transform module (VTM) to project each pedestrian from FPV to a virtual BEV, ii) deriving a multi-view geometry-based spatial alignment module (SAM) to estimate the relative camera pose in a unified BEV, iii) selecting and refining the subject and camera registration results within the unified BEV. We collect a new large-scale synthetic dataset with rich annotations for training and evaluation. Additionally, we also collect a real dataset for cross-domain evaluation. The experimental results show the remarkable effectiveness of our method. The code and proposed datasets are available at BEVSee."},"title":{"value":"From a Bird's Eye View to See: Joint Camera and Subject Registration without the Camera Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Zekun Qian","Ruize Han","Wei Feng","Song Wang"]}},"tmdate":1731289570109,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731289565229,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ruize_Han1"],"forum":"OlNRiJVObH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":178573,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731289570109,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"OlNRiJVObH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICIC (1) 2007"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-540-74171-8_135.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICIC/2007"},"paperhash":{"value":"fan|fcode_an_optimized_mds_array_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianbo_Fan:","~Lidan_Shou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinxiang_Dong:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-74171-8_135"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icic/FanSD07,\n  author={Jianbo Fan and Lidan Shou and Jinxiang Dong},\n  title={F-Code: An Optimized MDS Array Code},\n  year={2007},\n  cdate={1167609600000},\n  pages={1325-1336},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-74171-8_135},\n  booktitle={ICIC (1)},\n  crossref={conf/icic/2007-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Based on the research of MDS array code of size n(n in distributed storage system, in this paper, we present a novel encoding scheme called the F-code and prove that the column distance of the F-code is 3, i.e. F-code is a MDS array code given that odd number n is greater than 3 and does not include factor 3. And we also implement a novel decoding algorithm of the F-code. The algorithm only needs two decoding chains in each linear equation group and is able to recover all unknown symbols on two erasure columns. The analysis of F-code shows that our method extends the range of number n in n×n MDS array code and gets lower/reduction algorithmic complexity. Therefore, the reliability of a distributed storage system that features the F-code can be effectively reinforced."},"title":{"value":"F-Code: An Optimized MDS Array Code"},"authors":{"value":["Jianbo Fan","Lidan Shou","Jinxiang Dong"]}},"tmdate":1768968087802,"pdate":1199059200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icic/FanSD07"],"tcdate":1768967323553,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lidan_Shou1"],"forum":"vwh2MLphrb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":775791,"cdate":1167609600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768968087802,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"vwh2MLphrb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"UAI 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v216/matsubara23a/matsubara23a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/UAI/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"matsubara|tce_a_testbased_approach_to_measuring_calibration_error"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Takuo_Matsubara:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Niek_Tax:","~Richard_Mudd1","~Ido_Guy1"]},"html":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v216/matsubara23a.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/uai/MatsubaraTMG23,\n  author={Takuo Matsubara and Niek Tax and Richard Mudd and Ido Guy},\n  title={TCE: A Test-Based Approach to Measuring Calibration Error},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1390-1400},\n  url={https://proceedings.mlr.press/v216/matsubara23a.html},\n  booktitle={UAI},\n  crossref={conf/uai/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes a new metric to measure the calibration error of probabilistic binary classifiers, called test-based calibration error (TCE). TCE incorporates a novel loss function based on a statistical test to examine the extent to which model predictions differ from probabilities estimated from data. It offers (i) a clear interpretation, (ii) a consistent scale that is unaffected by class imbalance, and (iii) an enhanced visual representation with respect to the standard reliability diagram. In addition, we introduce an optimality criterion for the binning procedure of calibration error metrics based on a minimal estimation error of the empirical probabilities. We provide a novel computational algorithm for optimal bins under bin-size constraints. We demonstrate properties of TCE through a range of experiments, including multiple real-world imbalanced datasets and ImageNet 1000."},"title":{"value":"TCE: A Test-Based Approach to Measuring Calibration Error"},"authors":{"value":["Takuo Matsubara","Niek Tax","Richard Mudd","Ido Guy"]}},"tmdate":1768372063365,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1738769778783,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Richard_Mudd1"],"forum":"k4Eo0l0Be1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":294591,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768372063365,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"k4Eo0l0Be1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.14343v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"matsubara|tce_a_testbased_approach_to_measuring_calibration_error"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Takuo_Matsubara:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Niek_Tax:","~Richard_Mudd1","~Ido_Guy1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2306.14343"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2306-14343,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Takuo Matsubara and Niek Tax and Richard Mudd and Ido Guy},\n  title={TCE: A Test-Based Approach to Measuring Calibration Error},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2306.14343},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2306.14343}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes a new metric to measure the calibration error of probabilistic binary classifiers, called test-based calibration error (TCE). TCE incorporates a novel loss function based on a statistical test to examine the extent to which model predictions differ from probabilities estimated from data. It offers (i) a clear interpretation, (ii) a consistent scale that is unaffected by class imbalance, and (iii) an enhanced visual representation with repect to the standard reliability diagram. In addition, we introduce an optimality criterion for the binning procedure of calibration error metrics based on a minimal estimation error of the empirical probabilities. We provide a novel computational algorithm for optimal bins under bin-size constraints. We demonstrate properties of TCE through a range of experiments, including multiple real-world imbalanced datasets and ImageNet 1000."},"title":{"value":"TCE: A Test-Based Approach to Measuring Calibration Error"},"authors":{"value":["Takuo Matsubara","Niek Tax","Richard Mudd","Ido Guy"]}},"tmdate":1768372062579,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1738769778818,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Richard_Mudd1"],"forum":"Jg9rYRSSG0","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":294595,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768372062579,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Jg9rYRSSG0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MMSP 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6937466/6958782/06958798.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MMSP/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"shen|extrinsic_calibration_for_widebaseline_rgbd_camera_network"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ju_Shen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wanxin_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ying_Luo_0008:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Po-Chang_Su:","~Sen-ching_Samson_Cheung1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/MMSP.2014.6958798"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/mmsp/ShenXLSC14,\n  author={Ju Shen and Wanxin Xu and Ying Luo and Po-Chang Su and Sen-ching Samson Cheung},\n  title={Extrinsic calibration for wide-baseline RGB-D camera network},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={1-6},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/MMSP.2014.6958798},\n  booktitle={MMSP},\n  crossref={conf/mmsp/2014}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In the recent years, color and depth camera systems have attracted intensive attention because of its wide applications in image-based rendering, 3D model reconstruction, and human tracking and pose estimation. These applications often require multiple color and depth cameras to be placed with wide separation so as to capture the scene objects from different prospectives. The difference in modality and the wide baseline make calibration a challenging problem. In this paper, we present an algorithm that simultaneously and automatically calibrates the extrinsics across multiple color and depth cameras across the network. Rather than using the standard checkerboard, we use a sphere as a calibration object to identify the correspondences across different views. We experimentally demonstrate that our calibration framework can seamlessly integrate different views with wide baselines that outperforms other techniques in the literature."},"title":{"value":"Extrinsic calibration for wide-baseline RGB-D camera network"},"authors":{"value":["Ju Shen","Wanxin Xu","Ying Luo","Po-Chang Su","Sen-ching Samson Cheung"]}},"tmdate":1752865887561,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1752865871846,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sen-ching_Samson_Cheung1"],"forum":"9cXrtXCiRi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":575046,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1752865887561,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9cXrtXCiRi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/17/6/1204/pdf?version=1495713049"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"darwish|a_new_calibration_method_for_commercial_rgbd_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Walid_Darwish:","~Shengjun_Tang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenbin_Li_0010:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wu_Chen_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s17061204"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/DarwishTLC17,\n  author={Walid Darwish and Shengjun Tang and Wenbin Li and Wu Chen},\n  title={A New Calibration Method for Commercial RGB-D Sensors},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={17},\n  number={6},\n  pages={1204},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s17061204}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Commercial RGB-D sensors such as Kinect and Structure Sensors have been widely used in the game industry, where geometric fidelity is not of utmost importance. For applications in which high quality 3D is required, i.e., 3D building models of centimeter-level accuracy, accurate and reliable calibrations of these sensors are required. This paper presents a new model for calibrating the depth measurements of RGB-D sensors based on the structured light concept. Additionally, a new automatic method is proposed for the calibration of all RGB-D parameters, including internal calibration parameters for all cameras, the baseline between the infrared and RGB cameras, and the depth error model. When compared with traditional calibration methods, this new model shows a significant improvement in depth precision for both near and far ranges."},"title":{"value":"A New Calibration Method for Commercial RGB-D Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Walid Darwish","Shengjun Tang","Wenbin Li","Wu Chen"]}},"tmdate":1744100448322,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1744100443269,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shengjun_Tang1"],"forum":"heMzMOMRzN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":381535,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744100448322,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"heMzMOMRzN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICCV Workshops 2009"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5453386/5457418/05457546.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICCVW/2009"},"paperhash":{"value":"salazargaribay|direct_approach_to_the_selfcalibration_of_omnidirectional_cameras"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Adan_Salazar-Garibay:","~Ezio_Malis1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCVW.2009.5457546"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iccvw/Salazar-Garibay09,\n  author={Adan Salazar-Garibay and Ezio Malis},\n  title={Direct approach to the self-calibration of omnidirectional cameras},\n  year={2009},\n  cdate={1230768000000},\n  pages={2149-2155},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCVW.2009.5457546},\n  booktitle={ICCV Workshops},\n  crossref={conf/iccvw/2009}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Omnidirectional cameras are a popular choice of visual sensors in robotics because their large field of view is well suited for motion estimation and obstacle avoidance. However their practical use is often burdened by the calibration phase that can be time consuming and require an experienced user. The contribution of this work is a simplification of calibration phase by providing a direct approach to the calibration of this kind of sensor. This approach tracks a planar region in the scene to find the camera's intrinsic parameters. The only requirement for the user is to select a plane in the first image of the sequence. The algorithm needs no prior knowledge about extrinsic, camera, lens or mirror parameters. In order to assess the performance of the proposed method, we perform experiments with synthetic and real data."},"title":{"value":"Direct approach to the self-calibration of omnidirectional cameras"},"authors":{"value":["Adan Salazar-Garibay","Ezio Malis"]}},"tmdate":1730304669573,"pdate":1230768000000,"tcdate":1730304650346,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ezio_Malis1"],"forum":"wVmqTR09Fa","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":164787,"cdate":1230768000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730304669573,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"wVmqTR09Fa","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IV 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10186382/10186383/10186786.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IVS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"damicantonio|homography_estimation_for_camera_calibration_in_complex_topological_scenes"},"authorids":{"value":["~Giacomo_D'Amicantonio1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Egor_Bondarau:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peter_H._N._de_With:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IV55152.2023.10186786"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ivs/DAmicantonioBW23,\n  author={Giacomo D'Amicantonio and Egor Bondarau and Peter H. N. de With},\n  title={Homography Estimation for Camera Calibration in Complex Topological Scenes},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1-8},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IV55152.2023.10186786},\n  booktitle={IV},\n  crossref={conf/ivs/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Surveillance videos and images are used for a broad set of applications, ranging from traffic analysis to crime detection. Extrinsic camera calibration data is important for most analysis applications. However, security cameras are susceptible to environmental conditions and small camera movements, resulting in a need for an automated re-calibration method that can account for these varying conditions. In this paper, we present an automated camera-calibration process leveraging a dictionary-based approach that does not require prior knowledge on any camera settings. The method consists of a custom implementation of a Spatial Transformer Network (STN) and a novel topological loss function. Experiments reveal that the proposed method improves the IoU metric by up to 12% w.r.t. a state-of-the-art model across five synthetic datasets and the World Cup 2014 dataset."},"title":{"value":"Homography Estimation for Camera Calibration in Complex Topological Scenes"},"authors":{"value":["Giacomo D'Amicantonio","Egor Bondarau","Peter H. N. de With"]}},"tmdate":1727789187192,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727789184332,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Giacomo_D'Amicantonio1"],"forum":"R4RNeNHESS","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":132081,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727789187192,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"R4RNeNHESS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"WACV 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10483279/10483282/10483610.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/WACV/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"song|mscc_multiscale_transformers_for_camera_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xu_Song:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hao_Kang:","~Atsunori_Moteki1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Genta_Suzuki:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yoshie_Kobayashi:","~Zhiming_Tan2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV57701.2024.00323"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/wacv/SongKMSKT24,\n  author={Xu Song and Hao Kang and Atsunori Moteki and Genta Suzuki and Yoshie Kobayashi and Zhiming Tan},\n  title={MSCC: Multi-Scale Transformers for Camera Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={3250-3259},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV57701.2024.00323},\n  booktitle={WACV},\n  crossref={conf/wacv/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration is very important for some vision tasks, like rendering 3D scenes, environment reconstruction, and self-localization, etc. In this paper, we propose a framework of multi-scale transformers for camera calibration. With the input of a single image, the multi-scale features output from the model’s backbone are utilized to estimate camera parameters. At the same time, we show that the way of coarse-to-fine is effective to locate global structures and detailed features in the image, by studying the attention response of horizon line estimation. Moreover, deep supervision is proven to get more precise results and accelerated training. Our method outperforms all the state-of-the-art methods by objective and subjective experiments on Google Street View dataset and Pano360."},"title":{"value":"MSCC: Multi-Scale Transformers for Camera Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Xu Song","Hao Kang","Atsunori Moteki","Genta Suzuki","Yoshie Kobayashi","Zhiming Tan"]}},"tmdate":1747359869859,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1739826888649,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Atsunori_Moteki1"],"forum":"7fJFIMYIJf","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":324503,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747359869859,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"7fJFIMYIJf","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.17411v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"cheon|pretraining_with_random_noise_for_uncertainty_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jeonghwan_Cheon1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Se-Bum_Paik:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.17411"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-17411,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jeonghwan Cheon and Se-Bum Paik},\n  title={Pretraining with random noise for uncertainty calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2412.17411},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.17411}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Uncertainty calibration is crucial for various machine learning applications, yet it remains challenging. Many models exhibit hallucinations - confident yet inaccurate responses - due to miscalibrated confidence. Here, we show that the common practice of random initialization in deep learning, often considered a standard technique, is an underlying cause of this miscalibration, leading to excessively high confidence in untrained networks. Our method, inspired by developmental neuroscience, addresses this issue by simply pretraining networks with random noise and labels, reducing overconfidence and bringing initial confidence levels closer to chance. This ensures optimal calibration, aligning confidence with accuracy during subsequent data training, without the need for additional pre- or post-processing. Pre-calibrated networks excel at identifying \"unknown data,\" showing low confidence for out-of-distribution inputs, thereby resolving confidence miscalibration."},"title":{"value":"Pretraining with random noise for uncertainty calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Jeonghwan Cheon","Se-Bum Paik"]}},"tmdate":1747081490681,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747080438411,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jeonghwan_Cheon1"],"forum":"n0wxja97mN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":421742,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747081490681,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"n0wxja97mN","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We turn the conditional D-calibration criterion into a covariate-dependent self-correction map for survival models; it is exact without censoring, contracts under censoring, and its one-step kernel estimate improves conditional calibration."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["survival analysis","conditional calibration","post-hoc calibration","D-calibration","right censoring"]},"primary_area":{"value":"other topics in machine learning (i.e., none of the above)"},"abstract":{"value":"We propose a post-hoc $\\it{self-correction}$ procedure that moves a fitted survival model toward conditional calibration by transforming its estimated conditional cumulative distribution function (CDF) separately for each covariate value. The transformation is a nonparametric calibration map built from the conditional D-calibration criterion in survival analysis. At the population level, a single application recovers the true conditional CDF when there is no censoring. Under right censoring, each application reduces the discrepancy from the true conditional CDF by a factor determined by the censoring mechanism, and repeated applications converge to the true conditional CDF under conditions on the time horizon. We estimate the map using kernel weighting in a reduced covariate space. Experiments on ten datasets and six survival models show that a single application of the estimated map improves conditional calibration and the integrated Brier score in most pairwise comparisons with existing post-hoc methods, while maintaining comparable discrimination."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026posthoc,\ntitle={Post-Hoc Self-Correction for Conditional Calibration of Survival Models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=dwaowKs9r5},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Post-Hoc Self-Correction for Conditional Calibration of Survival Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/154f2207fe8f8045c09ee2a9408813647bf3e282.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791235150926,"tcdate":1789802353069,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission56443/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission56443/Authors"],"forum":"dwaowKs9r5","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":56443,"cdate":1789802353069,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission56443/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791235150926,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"dwaowKs9r5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM-as-a-judge","selective evaluation","online calibration","risk control","distribution-free guarantees"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/bc51a31f4b4f333a8fa10d556c680a472e02e4f4.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as automated judges, motivating selective evaluation methods that certify human agreement by accepting only sufficiently confident predictions. \nHowever, with limited calibration data, noisy estimates of the confidence distribution can destabilize the certified threshold and degrade the coverage–agreement trade-off. \nWe introduce Online Confidence Calibration (OCC), which continuously refines the empirical confidence distribution using confidence scores observed during deployment. \nOCC maps confidence scores to their empirical percentiles, re-scores the fixed calibration set, and re-certifies the acceptance threshold without requiring additional human labels. \nWe prove that the resulting threshold converges to its population counterpart, achieving finite-time exact recovery with high probability and eventual almost-sure lock-in, while preserving the human-agreement guarantee at every round. \nAcross six benchmarks, eleven LLM judges, and four confidence functions, OCC consistently improves threshold stability and calibration quality, certifies stronger agreement at matched coverage than label-free baselines, approaches the population-CDF oracle."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026trust,\ntitle={Trust the Stream: Online Confidence Calibration for Selective {LLM} Judges},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=jcUCndqioW},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Trust the Stream: Online Confidence Calibration for Selective LLM Judges"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/4aed8fe957a1f2a37fd0141b90622b6dd2d871c8.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791232682797,"tcdate":1789717835137,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission39131/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission39131/Authors"],"forum":"jcUCndqioW","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":39131,"cdate":1789717835137,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission39131/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791232682797,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"jcUCndqioW","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"This paper proposes capability calibration, a new confidence evaluation framework for LLMs, that is useful in several scenarios."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Confidence Calibration","Confidence Estimator Evaluation","Large Language Models"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/e9f8488c153ad8fe53e238e982a99ffa966438cc.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate confidence estimation is critical for reliable use of large language models (LLMs). Prior work on LLM calibration largely focuses on response-level confidence, which estimates the correctness of a single generated output. However, this formulation is misaligned with many practical settings where the central question is how likely a model is to solve a query overall. We show that this mismatch results from the stochastic nature of modern LLM decoding, under which single-response correctness fails to reflect underlying model capability. To address this issue, we introduce **capability calibration, a new evaluation framework** for measuring how well query-level confidence aligns with a model’s expected accuracy on individual queries. We formally distinguish capability calibration (CC) from response calibration (RC) and show that the two differ both theoretically and empirically. We further show that CC is better suited than RC to applications like pass@k prediction and inference budget allocation. Finally, we evaluate common confidence estimation methods to understand the practical feasibility of CC."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026on,\ntitle={On Calibration of Large Language Models: From Response To Capability},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=if53FcawpU},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"On Calibration of Large Language Models: From Response To Capability"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3d299348cc38a8f6cbb200d530c16feb592f023a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791227781996,"tcdate":1789354466537,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission16677/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission16677/Authors"],"forum":"if53FcawpU","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":16677,"cdate":1789354466537,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission16677/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791227781996,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"if53FcawpU","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.25884v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"cao|qcaleval_benchmarking_visionlanguage_models_for_quantum_calibration_plot_understanding"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.25884"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2604-25884,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shuxiang Cao and Zijian Zhang and Abhishek Agarwal and Grace Bratrud and Niyaz R. Beysengulov and Daniel C. Cole and Alejandro Gómez Frieiro and Elena O. Glen and Hao Hsu and Gang Huang and Raymond Jow and Greshma Shaji and Tom Lubowe and Ligeng Zhu and Luis Mantilla Calderón and Nicola Pancotti and Joel Pendleton and Brandon Severin and Charles Etienne Staub and Sara Sussman and Antti Vepsäläinen and Neel Rajeshbhai Vora and Yilun Xu and Varinia Bernales and Daniel Bowring and Elica Kyoseva and Ivan Rungger and Giulia Semeghini and Sam Stanwyck and Timothy Costa and Alán Aspuru-Guzik and Krysta Svore},\n  title={QCalEval: Benchmarking Vision-Language Models for Quantum Calibration Plot Understanding},\n  year={2026},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1775001600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2604.25884},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.25884}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Quantum computing calibration depends on interpreting experimental data, and calibration plots provide the most universal human-readable representation for this task, yet no systematic evaluation exists of how well vision-language models (VLMs) interpret them. We introduce QCalEval, the first VLM benchmark for quantum calibration plots: 243 samples across 87 scenario types from 22 experiment families, spanning superconducting qubits and neutral atoms, evaluated on six question types in both zero-shot and in-context learning settings. The best general-purpose zero-shot model reaches a mean score of 72.3, and many open-weight models degrade under multi-image in-context learning, whereas frontier closed models improve substantially. A supervised fine-tuning ablation at the 9-billion-parameter scale shows that SFT improves zero-shot performance but cannot close the multimodal in-context learning gap. As a reference case study, we release NVIDIA Ising Calibration 1, an open-weight model based on Qwen3.5-35B-A3B that reaches 74.7 zero-shot average score."},"title":{"value":"QCalEval: Benchmarking Vision-Language Models for Quantum Calibration Plot Understanding"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Shuxiang Cao","username":""},{"fullname":"Zijian Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Abhishek Agarwal","username":""},{"fullname":"Grace Bratrud","username":""},{"fullname":"Niyaz R. Beysengulov","username":""},{"fullname":"Daniel C. Cole","username":""},{"fullname":"Alejandro Gómez Frieiro","username":""},{"fullname":"Elena O. Glen","username":""},{"fullname":"Hao Hsu","username":""},{"fullname":"Gang Huang","username":""},{"fullname":"Raymond Jow","username":""},{"fullname":"Greshma Shaji","username":""},{"fullname":"Tom Lubowe","username":""},{"fullname":"Ligeng Zhu","username":""},{"fullname":"Luis Mantilla Calderón","username":""},{"fullname":"Nicola Pancotti","username":""},{"fullname":"Joel Pendleton","username":""},{"fullname":"Brandon Severin","username":""},{"fullname":"Charles Etienne Staub","username":""},{"fullname":"Sara Sussman","username":""},{"fullname":"Antti Vepsäläinen","username":""},{"fullname":"Neel Rajeshbhai Vora","username":""},{"fullname":"Yilun Xu","username":""},{"fullname":"Varinia Bernales","username":""},{"fullname":"Daniel Bowring","username":""},{"fullname":"Elica Kyoseva","username":""},{"fullname":"Ivan Rungger","username":""},{"fullname":"Giulia Semeghini","username":""},{"fullname":"Sam Stanwyck","username":""},{"fullname":"Timothy Costa","username":""},{"fullname":"Alán Aspuru-Guzik","username":"~Alan_Aspuru-Guzik2"},{"fullname":"Krysta Svore","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1785254176668,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2604-25884"],"tcdate":1785254144504,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Alan_Aspuru-Guzik2"],"forum":"CSREfR5Lls","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":110049,"cdate":1775001600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785254176668,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"CSREfR5Lls","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Aerosp. Electron. Syst. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/7/11031113/10856450.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TAES/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|calibration_of_rotation_memsimudvl_system_based_on_fgo"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hanwen_Zhou:","~Xiufen_Ye1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Meng_Luo:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAES.2025.3535476"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/taes/ZhouYL25a,\n  author={Hanwen Zhou and Xiufen Ye and Meng Luo},\n  title={Calibration of Rotation MEMS-IMU/DVL System Based on FGO},\n  year={2025},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1748736000000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Aerosp. Electron. Syst.},\n  volume={61},\n  number={3},\n  pages={7412-7421},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TAES.2025.3535476}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Traditional underwater navigation relies on a costly fiber optic gyroscope (FOG) inertial navigation system (INS)/Doppler velocity log (DVL) system. This study aims to address the challenges of applying rotation INS technology within MEMS-IMU/DVL systems, potentially enabling them to replace FOG systems—something that nonrotating MEMS-IMUs cannot achieve. While integrating rotation INS techniques enhances accuracy, it also introduces system errors that affect precision and are difficult to calibrate in field swaying conditions. To facilitate calibration in these situations, we incorporate an external nonrotating MEMS-IMU alongside the rotating MEMS-IMU, forming a dual-IMU system for more reliable calibration. We also design a specialized factor graph optimization structure that estimates the INS state using maximum a posteriori estimation and employs maximum likelihood estimation for constant calibration parameters, effectively minimizing the impact of inaccurate prior information in standard estimation methods. After calibration and compensation, the compact MEMS-IMU/DVL system achieves high positional accuracy and delivers smoother heading results."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of Rotation MEMS-IMU/DVL System Based on FGO"},"authors":{"value":["Hanwen Zhou","Xiufen Ye","Meng Luo"]}},"tmdate":1767686858756,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/taes/ZhouYL25a"],"tcdate":1767686846079,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiufen_Ye1"],"forum":"bNnif0fYlc","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":718585,"cdate":1748736000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1767686858756,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bNnif0fYlc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"WACV 2012"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6156698/6162991/06163030.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/WACV/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"puwein|ptz_camera_network_calibration_from_moving_people_in_sports_broadcasts"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jens_Puwein:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Remo_Ziegler:","~Luca_Ballan2","~Marc_Pollefeys1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV.2012.6163030"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/wacv/PuweinZBP12,\n  author={Jens Puwein and Remo Ziegler and Luca Ballan and Marc Pollefeys},\n  title={PTZ camera network calibration from moving people in sports broadcasts},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  pages={25-32},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV.2012.6163030},\n  booktitle={WACV},\n  crossref={conf/wacv/2012}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In sports broadcasts, networks consisting of pan-tilt-zoom (PTZ) cameras usually exhibit very wide baselines, making standard matching techniques for camera calibration very hard to apply. If, additionally, there is a lack of texture, finding corresponding image regions becomes almost impossible. However, such networks are often set up to observe dynamic scenes on a ground plane. Corresponding image trajectories produced by moving objects need to fulfill specific geometric constraints, which can be leveraged for camera calibration. We present a method which combines image trajectory matching with the self-calibration of rotating and zooming cameras, effectively reducing the remaining degrees of freedom in the matching stage to a 2D similarity transformation. Additionally, lines on the ground plane are used to improve the calibration. In the end, all extrinsic and intrinsic camera parameters are refined in a final bundle adjustment. The proposed algorithm was evaluated both qualitatively and quantitatively on four different soccer sequences."},"title":{"value":"PTZ camera network calibration from moving people in sports broadcasts"},"authors":{"value":["Jens Puwein","Remo Ziegler","Luca Ballan","Marc Pollefeys"]}},"tmdate":1762679696983,"pdate":1325376000000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/wacv/PuweinZBP12"],"tcdate":1762331089072,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Luca_Ballan2"],"forum":"298Zdmf5nQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":657708,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762679696983,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"298Zdmf5nQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Expert Syst. Appl. 2010"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ESWA/2010"},"paperhash":{"value":"vong|casebased_adaptation_for_automotive_engine_electronic_control_unit_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chi-Man_Vong2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pak-Kin_Wong_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2009.09.063"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/eswa/VongW10,\n  author={Chi-Man Vong and Pak-Kin Wong},\n  title={Case-based adaptation for automotive engine electronic control unit calibration},\n  year={2010},\n  cdate={1262304000000},\n  journal={Expert Syst. Appl.},\n  volume={37},\n  number={4},\n  pages={3184-3194},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2009.09.063}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The automotive engine performance is greatly affected by the calibration of its electronic control unit (ECU). The method for ECU calibration is traditionally done by trial-and-error. This traditional method consumes a large amount of time and money. To resolve this problem, case-based reasoning (CBR) is employed, so that an existing and effective ECU setup can be adapted to fit another similar class of engines. The adaptation procedure is done through a more sophisticated step called case-based adaptation (CBA) (Craw et al., 2001, Craw et al., 2006, Leake et al., 1996, Leake et al., 1997). CBA is an effective knowledge management tool, which can interactively learn the expert adaptation knowledge. The paper briefly reviews the methodologies of CBR and CBA. Then the application to ECU calibration is described via a case study. With CBR and CBA, the efficiency of calibrating an ECU can be enhanced. A prototype system has also been developed to verify the usefulness of CBR in ECU calibration."},"title":{"value":"Case-based adaptation for automotive engine electronic control unit calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Chi-Man Vong","Pak-Kin Wong"]}},"tmdate":1747108304051,"pdate":1262304000000,"tcdate":1747108220786,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chi_Man_VONG1"],"forum":"DHZIvhS7xY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":434251,"cdate":1262304000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747108304051,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"DHZIvhS7xY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICPRAM 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICPRAM/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"chamseddine|caracto_robust_cameraradar_extrinsic_calibration_with_triple_constraint_optimization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Mahdi_Chamseddine1","~Jason_R._Rambach1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Didier_Stricker:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.5220/0012369700003654"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icpram/ChamseddineRS24,\n  author={Mahdi Chamseddine and Jason R. Rambach and Didier Stricker},\n  title={CaRaCTO: Robust Camera-Radar Extrinsic Calibration with Triple Constraint Optimization},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={534-545},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.5220/0012369700003654},\n  booktitle={ICPRAM},\n  crossref={conf/icpram/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The use of cameras and radar sensors is well established in various automation and surveillance tasks. The multimodal nature of the data captured by those two sensors allows for a myriad of applications where one covers for the shortcomings of the other. While cameras can capture high resolution color data, radar can capture the depth and velocity of targets. Calibration is a necessary step before applying fusion algorithms to the data. In this work, a robust extrinsic calibration algorithm is developed for camera-radar setups. The standard geometric constraints used in calibration are extended with elevation constraints to improve the optimization. Furthermore, the method does not rely on any external measurements beyond the camera and radar data, and does not require complex targets unlike existing work. The calibration is done in 3D thus allowing for the estimation of the elevation information that is lost when using 2D radar. The results are evaluated against a sub-millimeter gro"},"title":{"value":"CaRaCTO: Robust Camera-Radar Extrinsic Calibration with Triple Constraint Optimization"},"authors":{"value":["Mahdi Chamseddine","Jason R. Rambach","Didier Stricker"]}},"tmdate":1731406898255,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1730799236853,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jason_Rambach1"],"forum":"Ac8YjAkyqn","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":170613,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1731406898255,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Ac8YjAkyqn","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Two new code embeddings with SOTA performance, derived from code generation models"},"venue":{"value":"DL4C @ NeurIPS 2025 Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ed07da5ce7830d719a9d9fe0811ab2f44f0cf604.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["code embeddings; retrieval; embedding models"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C"},"paperhash":{"value":"kryvosheieva|efficient_code_embeddings_from_code_generation_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Daria_Kryvosheieva1","~Saba_Sturua1","~Michael_Günther1","~Scott_Martens1","~Han_Xiao8"]},"abstract":{"value":"jina-code-embeddings is a novel code embedding model suite designed to retrieve code from natural language queries, perform technical question-answering, and identify semantically similar code snippets across programming languages. It makes innovative use of an autoregressive backbone pre-trained on both text and code, generating embeddings via last-token pooling. We outline the training methodology and demonstrate state-of-the-art performance despite the relatively small size of the models, validating this approach to code embedding model construction."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nkryvosheieva2025efficient,\ntitle={Efficient Code Embeddings from Code Generation Models},\nauthor={Daria Kryvosheieva and Saba Sturua and Michael G{\\\"u}nther and Scott Martens and Han Xiao},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2025 Fourth Workshop on Deep Learning for Code},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=smR7RPdWZs}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Efficient Code Embeddings from Code Generation Models"},"authors":{"value":["Daria Kryvosheieva","Saba Sturua","Michael Günther","Scott Martens","Han Xiao"]}},"tmdate":1764073174994,"pdate":1758551725483,"tcdate":1756322801121,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission52/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission52/Authors"],"forum":"smR7RPdWZs","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":52,"cdate":1756322801121,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission52/-/Revision"],"mdate":1764073174994,"odate":1764073174981,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C","id":"smR7RPdWZs","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Robotics 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8860/9556521/09390394.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TROB/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"pedrosa|a_general_approach_to_handeye_calibration_through_the_optimization_of_atomic_transformations"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Eurico_Farinha_Pedrosa:","~Miguel_Oliveira2","~Nuno_Lau1","~Vítor_M._F._Santos2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TRO.2021.3062306"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/trob/PedrosaOLS21,\n  author={Eurico Farinha Pedrosa and Miguel Oliveira and Nuno Lau and Vítor M. F. Santos},\n  title={A General Approach to Hand-Eye Calibration Through the Optimization of Atomic Transformations},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Robotics},\n  volume={37},\n  number={5},\n  pages={1619-1633},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TRO.2021.3062306}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This article proposes a general approach to solve the hand–eye calibration problem. The system is general since it is able to calibrate any number of cameras and, moreover, is able to simultaneously perform the calibration of several instances of the two common hand–eye calibration use cases: eye-on-hand and eye-to-base. The calibration is solved with a nonlinear least squares method, and the reprojection error is used as a metric to guide the optimization procedure. Our approach is seamlessly integrated with the robot operating system framework and allows for the interactive positioning of sensors and labeling of data, facilitating both the data acquisition and labeling and the calibration procedures. Results show that the proposed approach is able to handle any calibration use case with a minimal initial configuration. The approach is compared with several other state-of-the-art hand–eye calibration algorithms. Results show that the proposed approach produces very accurate calibrations when compared to the state of the art."},"title":{"value":"A General Approach to Hand-Eye Calibration Through the Optimization of Atomic Transformations"},"authors":{"value":["Eurico Farinha Pedrosa","Miguel Oliveira","Nuno Lau","Vítor M. F. Santos"]}},"tmdate":1753454280978,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1729171452005,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Nuno_Lau1"],"forum":"ZaeiXiNE8q","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":153909,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1753454280978,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ZaeiXiNE8q","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CCNC 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10454139/10454627/10454850.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CCNC/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"sone|calibration_of_realtime_lidar_data_with_static_3d_image"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuta_Sone:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Katsuki_Teraoka:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kenta_Azuma:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ryoichi_Shinkuma:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gabriele_Trovato:","~Koichi_Nihei1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Takanori_Iwai:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CCNC51664.2024.10454850"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ccnc/SoneTASTNI24,\n  author={Yuta Sone and Katsuki Teraoka and Kenta Azuma and Ryoichi Shinkuma and Gabriele Trovato and Koichi Nihei and Takanori Iwai},\n  title={Calibration of Real-Time LIDAR Data with Static 3D Image},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={1072-1073},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CCNC51664.2024.10454850},\n  booktitle={CCNC},\n  crossref={conf/ccnc/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes a system to improve the visibility of real-time Light-Detection-and-Ranging (LIDAR) data by enabling calibration with pre-created three-dimensional (3D) images. The proposed system automatically selects suitable frames to periodically maintain calibration, and we demonstrate its effectiveness through experiments."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of Real-Time LIDAR Data with Static 3D Image"},"authors":{"value":["Yuta Sone","Katsuki Teraoka","Kenta Azuma","Ryoichi Shinkuma","Gabriele Trovato","Koichi Nihei","Takanori Iwai"]}},"tmdate":1757390693088,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/ccnc/SoneTASTNI24"],"tcdate":1757390683848,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Koichi_Nihei1"],"forum":"vivYKaG60z","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":621362,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1757390693088,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"vivYKaG60z","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICASSP 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10445798/10445803/10448017.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICASSP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|featuredistribution_perturbation_and_calibration_for_generalized_reid"},"authorids":{"value":["~Qilei_Li1","~Jiabo_Huang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jian_Hu_0002:",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP48485.2024.10448017"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icassp/LiH0G24,\n  author={Qilei Li and Jiabo Huang and Jian Hu and Shaogang Gong},\n  title={Feature-Distribution Perturbation and Calibration for Generalized Reid},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={2880-2884},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP48485.2024.10448017},\n  booktitle={ICASSP},\n  crossref={conf/icassp/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Person Re-identification (ReID) has been advanced remarkably over the last 10 years. However, the i.i.d. (independent and identically distributed) assumption is somewhat non-applicable to ReID considering its objective to identify images of the same pedestrian across cameras at different locations. In this work, we propose a Feature-Distribution Perturbation and Calibration (PECA) method to derive generic feature representations for person ReID. Specifically, we perform per-domain feature-distribution perturbation to refrain the model from overfitting to the domain-biased distribution of each source (seen) domain by enforcing feature invariance to distribution shifts caused by perturbation. Furthermore, we design a global calibration mechanism to align feature distributions across all the source domains to improve the model’s generalization capacity by eliminating domain bias. These local perturbation and global calibration are conducted simultaneously, which share the same principle to avoid models overfitting by regularization respectively on the perturbed and the original distributions. Extensive experiments were conducted and the proposed PECA model outperformed the state-of-the-art competitors by significant margins."},"title":{"value":"Feature-Distribution Perturbation and Calibration for Generalized Reid"},"authors":{"value":["Qilei Li","Jiabo Huang","Jian Hu","Shaogang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1784037860004,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1737124282050,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jiabo_Huang1"],"forum":"s4xvJmXKg3","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":267155,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784037860004,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"s4xvJmXKg3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.20605v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"beaudet|optimizationbased_calibration_for_intravascular_ultrasound_volume_reconstruction"},"authorids":{"value":["","","~Philippe_C._Cattin1","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.20605"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2508-20605,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Karl-Philippe Beaudet and Sidaty El Hadramy and Philippe C. Cattin and Juan Verde and Stéphane Cotin},\n  title={Optimization-Based Calibration for Intravascular Ultrasound Volume Reconstruction},\n  year={2025},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1754006400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2508.20605},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.20605}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Intraoperative ultrasound images are inherently challenging to interpret in liver surgery due to the limited field of view and complex anatomical structures. Bridging the gap between preoperative and intraoperative data is crucial for effective surgical guidance. 3D IntraVascular UltraSound (IVUS) offers a potential solution by enabling the reconstruction of the entire organ, which facilitates registration between preoperative computed tomography (CT) scans and intraoperative IVUS images. In this work, we propose an optimization-based calibration method using a 3D-printed phantom for accurate 3D Intravascular Ultrasound volume reconstruction. Our approach ensures precise alignment of tracked IVUS data with preoperative CT images, improving intraoperative navigation. We validated our method using in vivo swine liver images, achieving a calibration error from 0.88 to 1.80 mm and a registration error from 3.40 to 5.71 mm between the 3D IVUS data and the corresponding CT scan. Our method provides a reliable and accurate means of calibration and volume reconstruction. It can be used to register intraoperative ultrasound images with preoperative CT images in the context of liver surgery, and enhance intraoperative guidance."},"title":{"value":"Optimization-Based Calibration for Intravascular Ultrasound Volume Reconstruction"},"authors":{"value":["Karl-Philippe Beaudet","Sidaty El Hadramy","Philippe C. Cattin","Juan Verde","Stéphane Cotin"]}},"tmdate":1772257736979,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2508-20605"],"tcdate":1772257718706,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Philippe_C._Cattin1"],"forum":"Yva4RWrmrT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":837822,"cdate":1754006400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772257736979,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Yva4RWrmrT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ASMUS@MICCAI 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-032-06329-8_6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MICCAI/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"beaudet|optimizationbased_calibration_for_intravascular_ultrasound_volume_reconstruction"},"authorids":{"value":["","","~Philippe_C._Cattin1","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-06329-8_6"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/miccai/BeaudetHCVC25,\n  author={Karl-Philippe Beaudet and Sidaty El Hadramy and Philippe C. Cattin and Juan Verde and Stéphane Cotin},\n  title={Optimization-Based Calibration for Intravascular Ultrasound Volume Reconstruction},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={53-62},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-06329-8_6},\n  booktitle={ASMUS@MICCAI},\n  crossref={conf/miccai/2025asmus}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Intraoperative ultrasound images are inherently challenging to interpret in liver surgery due to the limited field of view and complex anatomical structures. Bridging the gap between preoperative and intraoperative data is crucial for effective surgical guidance. 3D IntraVascular UltraSound (IVUS) offers a potential solution by enabling the reconstruction of the entire organ, which facilitates registration between preoperative computed tomography (CT) scans and intraoperative IVUS images. In this work, we propose an optimization-based calibration method using a 3D-printed phantom for accurate 3D Intravascular Ultrasound volume reconstruction. Our approach ensures precise alignment of tracked IVUS data with preoperative CT images, improving intraoperative navigation. We validated our method using in vivo swine liver images, achieving a calibration error from 0.88 to 1.80 mm and a registration error from 3.40 to 5.71 mm between the 3D IVUS data and the corresponding CT scan. Our method provides a reliable and accurate means of calibration and volume reconstruction. It can be used to register intraoperative ultrasound images with preoperative CT images in the context of liver surgery, and enhance intraoperative guidance."},"title":{"value":"Optimization-Based Calibration for Intravascular Ultrasound Volume Reconstruction"},"authors":{"value":["Karl-Philippe Beaudet","Sidaty El Hadramy","Philippe C. Cattin","Juan Verde","Stéphane Cotin"]}},"tmdate":1772257724874,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/miccai/BeaudetHCVC25"],"tcdate":1772257718544,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Philippe_C._Cattin1"],"forum":"4mJlvPp1hs","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":837816,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772257724874,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4mJlvPp1hs","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Cybern. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6221036/10542154/10113157.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TCYB/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"yu|engine_calibration_with_surrogateassisted_bilevel_evolutionary_algorithm"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xunzhao_Yu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yan_Wang_0075:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ling_Zhu_0001:","~Dimitar_P._Filev1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Yao_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TCYB.2023.3267454"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tcyb/YuWZFY24,\n  author={Xunzhao Yu and Yan Wang and Ling Zhu and Dimitar P. Filev and Xin Yao},\n  title={Engine Calibration With Surrogate-Assisted Bilevel Evolutionary Algorithm},\n  year={2024},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1717200000000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Cybern.},\n  volume={54},\n  number={6},\n  pages={3832-3845},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TCYB.2023.3267454}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Engine calibration problems are black-box optimization problems which are evaluation costly and most of them are constrained in the objective space. In these problems, decision variables may have different impacts on objectives and constraints, which could be detected by sensitivity analysis. Most existing surrogate-assisted evolutionary algorithms do not analyze variable sensitivity, thus, useless effort may be made on some less sensitive variables. This article proposes a surrogate-assisted bilevel evolutionary algorithm to solve a real-world engine calibration problem. Principal component analysis is performed to investigate the impact of variables on constraints and to divide decision variables into lower-level and upper-level variables. The lower-level aims at optimizing lower-level variables to make candidate solutions feasible, and the upper-level focuses on adjusting upper-level variables to optimize the objective. In addition, an ordinal-regression-based surrogate is adapted to estimate the ordinal landscape of solution feasibility. Computational studies on a gasoline engine model demonstrate that our algorithm is efficient in constraint handling and also achieves a smaller fuel consumption value than other state-of-the-art calibration methods."},"title":{"value":"Engine Calibration With Surrogate-Assisted Bilevel Evolutionary Algorithm"},"authors":{"value":["Xunzhao Yu","Yan Wang","Ling Zhu","Dimitar P. Filev","Xin Yao"]}},"tmdate":1762356222998,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727714782052,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xunzhao_Yu1"],"forum":"qmOKNkx82x","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":121185,"cdate":1717200000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762356222998,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"qmOKNkx82x","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.08586v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"popordanoska|estimating_calibration_error_under_label_shift_without_labels"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Teodora_Popordanoska:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gorjan_Radevski:","~Tinne_Tuytelaars1","~Matthew_B._Blaschko1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2312.08586"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2312-08586,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Teodora Popordanoska and Gorjan Radevski and Tinne Tuytelaars and Matthew B. Blaschko},\n  title={Estimating calibration error under label shift without labels},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2312.08586},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2312.08586}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In the face of dataset shift, model calibration plays a pivotal role in ensuring the reliability of machine learning systems. Calibration error (CE) is an indicator of the alignment between the predicted probabilities and the classifier accuracy. While prior works have delved into the implications of dataset shift on calibration, existing CE estimators assume access to labels from the target domain, which are often unavailable in practice, i.e., when the model is deployed and used. This work addresses such challenging scenario, and proposes a novel CE estimator under label shift, which is characterized by changes in the marginal label distribution $p(Y)$, while keeping the conditional $p(X|Y)$ constant between the source and target distributions. Our contribution is an approach, which, by leveraging importance re-weighting of the labeled source distribution, provides consistent and asymptotically unbiased CE estimation with respect to the shifted target distribution. Empirical results across diverse real-world datasets, under various conditions and label-shift intensities, demonstrate the effectiveness and reliability of the proposed estimator."},"title":{"value":"Estimating calibration error under label shift without labels"},"authors":{"value":["Teodora Popordanoska","Gorjan Radevski","Tinne Tuytelaars","Matthew B. Blaschko"]}},"tmdate":1747079546493,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1729753878980,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Matthew_Blaschko1"],"forum":"MgXOOtfPJc","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":158902,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747079546493,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"MgXOOtfPJc","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Efficient Methods for NLP,Multilingualism and Cross-Lingual NLP"},"keywords":{"value":["Post-Training Quantization","Calibration Dataset","Calibration Language","Multilinguality","Low-Bit","Mixed Precision","Cross-Linguality"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English,Chinese,Indonesian,Tamil,Swahili"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"N/A"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"Yes"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix C Experiment Setup"},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"No"},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"No"},"A2_elaboration":{"value":"Ethical Considerations"},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix C Experiment Setup. Yes it is transparant that we use single run, while we will increase it if necessary."},"B2_elaboration":{"value":"All dataset, model, and code in this work are publicly available and not used outside research. Moreover, we cite every artifact that we use."},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3 Experimental Setup and Appendix C Experiment Setup"},"C4_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix C Experiment Setup"},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"Yes"},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"Subsection 3.2 Model, Calibration Dataset, and Languages. As for total computational budget for reproducible we dont intent to include it, it is quite astronomical, On estimation: quantizing the model needs at least 360 GPU-hours (105 quantization, while the most demanding SliMLLM takes around 6 GPU-hours each), while evaluation, it should be around 200-300 GPU-hours (108 models 7 benchmark). As for computing infrastructure, mostly done on DGXA100 and any online GPU provider."},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3 Experimental Setup, Appendix C Experiment Setup"},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"Ethical Considerations. We use Gemini and ChatGPT to proofreading and correcting: grammar, word choice, and awkward phrasing."},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"N/A"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026multilinguality,\ntitle={Multilinguality after Quantization: Low-Bits, Calibration Languages, Mixed-Precisions, and Cross-Linguality},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=2yfaHHH2aX},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Multilinguality after Quantization: Low-Bits, Calibration Languages, Mixed-Precisions, and Cross-Linguality"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"Yes"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Approaches to low-resource settings","Approaches to low-compute settings (efficiency)","Data analysis"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"N/A"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"N/A"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"There are inconsistent conclusions about the impact of calibration data on Post-Training Quantization (PTQ) across studies, specifically regarding the factors that make the method effective, such as language that we explore in this work.  We investigate this question by evaluating \\texttt{GPTQ} and its mixed-precision variants across three low-bit settings, using language-targeted calibration sets across five language families. By evaluating performance across both perplexity and downstream tasks, we find that selecting an appropriate calibration language is generally beneficial, though model sensitivity varies with bit-width, target language, and task difficulty.\nWe further show that mixed-precision techniques expand the representational capacity of low-bit models, occasionally allowing 2-bit models to approach 3-bit performance when paired with language-targeted calibration. Furthermore, we explore the correlation between these factors and observe that the calibration set sensitivity is influenced by different combinations of factors for each controlled variable, which can be explained by the downstream task results. Finally, while the benefits of language-targeted calibration transfer to sibling languages within the same family, the magnitude of this transfer is not reliably predicted by closeness in family alone. Together, these findings show calibration data selection as a deliberate design choice in multilingual PTQ rather than an incidental one."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"N/A"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/428888be93050edcc4653750c5fbbb03cdd0d60e.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Efficient Methods for NLP"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"N/A"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"no"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"N/A"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1790696978023,"tcdate":1779728389204,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission9056/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission9056/Authors"],"forum":"2yfaHHH2aX","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":9056,"cdate":1779728389204,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1790696978023,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"2yfaHHH2aX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IJCNN 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9533266/9533267/09534411.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IJCNN/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"datta|calnet_jointly_learning_classification_and_calibration_on_imbalanced_binary_classification_tasks"},"authorids":{"value":["~Arghya_Datta2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Noah_R._Flynn:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:S._Joshua_Swamidass:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IJCNN52387.2021.9534411"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ijcnn/DattaFS21,\n  author={Arghya Datta and Noah R. Flynn and S. Joshua Swamidass},\n  title={Cal-Net: Jointly Learning Classification and Calibration On Imbalanced Binary Classification Tasks},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={1-8},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IJCNN52387.2021.9534411},\n  booktitle={IJCNN},\n  crossref={conf/ijcnn/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Datasets in critical domains are often class imbalanced, with a minority class far rarer than the majority class, and classification models face challenges to produce calibrated predictions on these datasets. A common approach to address this issue is to train classification models in the first step and subsequently use post-processing parametric or non-parametric calibration techniques to re-scale the model's outputs in the second step without tuning any underlying parameters in the model to improve calibration. In this study, we have shown that these common approaches are vulnerable to class imbalanced data, often producing unstable results that do not jointly optimize classification or calibration performance. We have introduced Cal-Net, a “self-calibrating” neural network architecture that simultaneously optimizes classification and calibration performances for class imbalanced datasets in a single training phase, thereby eliminating the need for any post-processing procedure for confidence calibration. Empirical results have shown that Cal-Net outperforms far more complex neural networks and post-processing calibration techniques in both classification and calibration performances on four synthetic and four benchmark class imbalanced binary classification datasets. Furthermore, Cal-Net can readily be extended to more complicated learning tasks, online learning and can be incorporated in more complex architectures as the final state."},"title":{"value":"Cal-Net: Jointly Learning Classification and Calibration On Imbalanced Binary Classification Tasks"},"authors":{"value":["Arghya Datta","Noah R. Flynn","S. Joshua Swamidass"]}},"tmdate":1747337236850,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1747337233572,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Arghya_Datta2"],"forum":"maIGMSW6qt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":496691,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747337236850,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"maIGMSW6qt","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We show that LLMs can be semantically calibrated, and we develop theory for when and why."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["calibration","LLM","semantic","uncertainty","theory"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) often lack meaningful confidence estimates for the semantic content of their outputs. While base LLMs are known to exhibit next-token calibration, it remains unclear whether they can assess confidence in the actual meaning of their responses beyond the token level. We find that, when using a certain sampling-based notion of semantic calibration, base LLMs are remarkably well-calibrated: they can meaningfully assess confidence in various open-ended question-answering tasks, despite training only on next-token prediction. To formalize this phenomenon, we introduce \"$B$-calibration,\" a notion of calibration parameterized by the choice of equivalence classes. Our main theoretical contribution establishes a mechanism for why semantic calibration emerges in base LLMs, leveraging a recent connection between calibration and local loss optimality. This theoretical mechanism leads to a testable prediction: base LLMs will be semantically calibrated when they can easily predict their own distribution over semantic answer classes before generating a response. We state three implications of this prediction, which we validate through experiments: (1) Base LLMs are semantically calibrated across question-answering tasks, (2) instruction-tuning procedures systematically break this calibration, and (3) chain-of-thought reasoning breaks calibration (intuitively because models cannot predict their final answers before completing their generation). To our knowledge, our work provides the first principled explanation of when and why semantic calibration emerges in LLMs."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nnakkiran2026trained,\ntitle={Trained on Tokens, Calibrated on Concepts: The Emergence of Semantic Calibration in {LLM}s},\nauthor={Preetum Nakkiran and Arwen Bradley and Adam Golinski and Eugene Ndiaye and Michael Kirchhof and Sinead Williamson},\nbooktitle={The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=0sCyk9Tr5J}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Trained on Tokens, Calibrated on Concepts: The Emergence of Semantic Calibration in LLMs"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6cd63883b6f1271c277f7b5d1d7ca64d47709f8d.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"nakkiran|trained_on_tokens_calibrated_on_concepts_the_emergence_of_semantic_calibration_in_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["~Preetum_Nakkiran1","~Arwen_Bradley1","~Adam_Golinski1","~Eugene_Ndiaye1","~Michael_Kirchhof1","~Sinead_Williamson1"]},"authors":{"value":["Preetum Nakkiran","Arwen Bradley","Adam Golinski","Eugene Ndiaye","Michael Kirchhof","Sinead Williamson"]}},"tmdate":1775877017784,"pdate":1769435928518,"tcdate":1758069984795,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission8146/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission8146/Authors"],"forum":"0sCyk9Tr5J","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":8146,"cdate":1758069984795,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission8146/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission8146/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission8146/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1775877017784,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"0sCyk9Tr5J","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.04869v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"nakkiran|trained_on_tokens_calibrated_on_concepts_the_emergence_of_semantic_calibration_in_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Preetum_Nakkiran:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Arwen_Bradley:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Adam_Golinski:","~Eugene_Ndiaye1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Michael_Kirchhof_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sinead_Williamson:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.04869"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2511-04869,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Preetum Nakkiran and Arwen Bradley and Adam Golinski and Eugène Ndiaye and Michael Kirchhof and Sinead Williamson},\n  title={Trained on Tokens, Calibrated on Concepts: The Emergence of Semantic Calibration in LLMs},\n  year={2025},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1761955200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2511.04869},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.04869}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) often lack meaningful confidence estimates for their outputs. While base LLMs are known to exhibit next-token calibration, it remains unclear whether they can assess confidence in the actual meaning of their responses beyond the token level. We find that, when using a certain sampling-based notion of semantic calibration, base LLMs are remarkably well-calibrated: they can meaningfully assess confidence in open-domain question-answering tasks, despite not being explicitly trained to do so. Our main theoretical contribution establishes a mechanism for why semantic calibration emerges as a byproduct of next-token prediction, leveraging a recent connection between calibration and local loss optimality. The theory relies on a general definition of \"B-calibration,\" which is a notion of calibration parameterized by a choice of equivalence classes (semantic or otherwise). This theoretical mechanism leads to a testable prediction: base LLMs will be semantically calibrated when they can easily predict their own distribution over semantic answer classes before generating a response. We state three implications of this prediction, which we validate through experiments: (1) Base LLMs are semantically calibrated across question-answering tasks, (2) RL instruction-tuning systematically breaks this calibration, and (3) chain-of-thought reasoning breaks calibration. To our knowledge, our work provides the first principled explanation of when and why semantic calibration emerges in LLMs."},"title":{"value":"Trained on Tokens, Calibrated on Concepts: The Emergence of Semantic Calibration in LLMs"},"authors":{"value":["Preetum Nakkiran","Arwen Bradley","Adam Golinski","Eugène Ndiaye","Michael Kirchhof","Sinead Williamson"]}},"tmdate":1769531801368,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2511-04869"],"tcdate":1769531797088,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Eugene_Ndiaye1"],"forum":"sO4S3iDYjZ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":808416,"cdate":1761955200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769531801368,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"sO4S3iDYjZ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Virtual Real. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10055-024-01089-7.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/VR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"vanherck|projectorcamera_calibration_with_nonoverlapping_fields_of_view_using_a_planar_mirror"},"authorids":{"value":["~Joni_Vanherck1","~Lode_Jorissen1","~Brent_Zoomers1","~Nick_Michiels1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10055-024-01089-7"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/vr/VanherckJZM25,\n  author={Joni Vanherck and Lode Jorissen and Brent Zoomers and Nick Michiels},\n  title={Projector-camera calibration with non-overlapping fields of view using a planar mirror},\n  year={2025},\n  month={March},\n  cdate={1740787200000},\n  journal={Virtual Real.},\n  volume={29},\n  number={1},\n  pages={19},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s10055-024-01089-7}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Projector-camera systems have numerous applications across diverse domains. Accurate calibration of both intrinsic and extrinsic parameters is crucial for these systems. Intrinsic parameters include focal length, distortion parameters, and the principal point, while extrinsic parameters encompass the position and orientation of the projector and camera. A non-overlapping projector-camera system is required in certain scenarios due to practical limitations, physical arrangements, or specific application requirements. These systems pose a more complex calibration challenge because the devices have no direct correspondences or overlapping fields of view, necessitating intermediate objects or methods. This paper proposes a calibration method for non-overlapping projector-camera systems using a planar mirror. The method involves a straightforward process that requires a calibrated camera and a separate mirror calibration step. In this setup, the projector displays a pattern on a planar calibration board, and the camera has an indirect view of this calibration board through the mirror. Using homography, 3D-2D correspondences of the projector are established, enabling the calibration of the system. This method is empirically evaluated using real-world setups and quantitatively assessed in a synthetic environment. The results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves precise calibration across various setups, proving its effectiveness. This approach is an easy-to-use and accessible calibration process for non-overlapping projector-camera systems, made possible by a mirror."},"title":{"value":"Projector-camera calibration with non-overlapping fields of view using a planar mirror"},"authors":{"value":["Joni Vanherck","Lode Jorissen","Brent Zoomers","Nick Michiels"]}},"tmdate":1762936111323,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/vr/VanherckJZM25"],"tcdate":1762180386188,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Joni_Vanherck1"],"forum":"hbHKVpWUQB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":650329,"cdate":1740787200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762936111323,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"hbHKVpWUQB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Int. J. Comput. Vis. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11263-023-01779-w.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJCV/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"long|bicalibration_networks_for_weaklysupervised_video_representation_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fuchen_Long:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ting_Yao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhaofan_Qiu:","~Xinmei_Tian1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiebo_Luo_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_Mei_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11263-023-01779-w"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijcv/LongYQTLM23,\n  author={Fuchen Long and Ting Yao and Zhaofan Qiu and Xinmei Tian and Jiebo Luo and Tao Mei},\n  title={Bi-calibration Networks for Weakly-Supervised Video Representation Learning},\n  year={2023},\n  month={July},\n  cdate={1688169600000},\n  journal={Int. J. Comput. Vis.},\n  volume={131},\n  number={7},\n  pages={1704-1721},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s11263-023-01779-w}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The leverage of large volumes of web videos paired with the query (short phrase for searching the video) or surrounding text (long textual description, e.g., video title) offers an economic and extensible alternative to supervised video representation learning. Nevertheless, modeling such weakly visual-textual connection is not trivial due to query polysemy (i.e., many possible meanings for a query) and text isomorphism (i.e., same syntactic structure of different text). In this paper, we introduce a new design of mutual calibration between query and text to achieve more reliable visual-textual supervision for video representation learning. Specifically, we present Bi-Calibration Networks (BCN) that novelly couples two calibrations to learn the correction from text to query and vice versa. Technically, BCN executes clustering on all the titles of the videos searched by an identical query and takes the centroid of each cluster as a text prototype. All the queries constitute the query set. The representation learning of BCN is then formulated as video classification over text prototypes and queries, with text-to-query and query-to-text calibrations. A selection scheme is also devised to balance the two calibrations. Two large-scale web video datasets paired with query and title, named YOVO-3M and YOVO-10M, are newly collected for weakly-supervised video feature learning. The video features of BCN with ResNet backbone learnt on YOVO-3M (3M YouTube videos) obtain superior results under linear protocol on action recognition. More remarkably, BCN trained on the larger set of YOVO-10M (10M YouTube videos) with further fine-tuning leads to 1.3% gain in top-1 accuracy on Kinetics-400 dataset over the state-of-the-art TAda2D method with ImageNet pre-training. Source code and datasets are available at https://github.com/FuchenUSTC/BCN."},"title":{"value":"Bi-calibration Networks for Weakly-Supervised Video Representation Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Fuchen Long","Ting Yao","Zhaofan Qiu","Xinmei Tian","Jiebo Luo","Tao Mei"]}},"tmdate":1737854960182,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1737854948643,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xinmei_Tian1"],"forum":"BzyXReVtxx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":279623,"cdate":1688169600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1737854960182,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"BzyXReVtxx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Eng. Appl. Artif. Intell. 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/EAAI/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|feature_transformation_and_statistical_calibration_for_crossdomain_fewshot_classification"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiafan_Liu:","~Jin_Deng2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinrong_Cui:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wei_Luo_0006:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.engappai.2025.111181"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/eaai/LiuDCL25,\n  author={Jiafan Liu and Jin Deng and Jinrong Cui and Wei Luo},\n  title={Feature transformation and statistical calibration for cross-domain few-shot classification},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={Eng. Appl. Artif. Intell.},\n  volume={157},\n  pages={111181},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.engappai.2025.111181}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The limited labeled training samples in target tasks, combined with the distribution discrepancy between the target and the model-learning source datasets, create a significant challenge in Remote Sensing Scene Classification (RSSC). Existing work attacks this problem from the perspective of feature generalizability while ignoring model bias. In this paper, we address this issue in the framework of Cross-Domain Few-Shot Classification (CDFSC) and propose an overfitting-resistant and debiased learning method, FEature Transformation and stAtistical Calibration (FETAC), to tackle this problem. In FETAC, linear and residual modules are incorporated to the backbone to transform features layer-wisely for model overfitting-resistant learning at the task level. Meanwhile, a parameter-free Lifted Laplacian Activation (LLA) unit is proposed to encourage balanced activations of features across tasks on the target domain at the dataset level. This property of balanced activations prevents the model from using only a small number of features for adaptation, thus achieving feature calibration statistically for an unbiased cross-domain feature transformation. In model architecture, FETAC is designed as a plug-and-play module on the backbone and is optimized on the target few-shot task at test time on the fly. Experiments on five distinct RSSC target domains validate the superior performance of FETAC for CDFSC tasks. Furthermore, the excellent performance exhibited by FETAC on the 1-shot CDFSC tasks further demonstrates its robust applicability in RSSC under extremely scarce labeled examples. Code will be available at https://github.com/JiaHg/fetac."},"title":{"value":"Feature transformation and statistical calibration for cross-domain few-shot classification"},"authors":{"value":["Jiafan Liu","Jin Deng","Jinrong Cui","Wei Luo"]}},"tmdate":1752568146171,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1752568138128,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jin_Deng2"],"forum":"cewQAK6UVb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":568644,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1752568146171,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"cewQAK6UVb","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"code models, retrieval-augmented generation, prompting, code generation and understanding, chunking"},"keywords":{"value":["repository-level code completion","code completion","code generation","code chunking","retrieval-augmented code generation"]},"languages_studied":{"value":"Python, Java, C#"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 January Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026late,\ntitle={Late Code Chunking: A Code Chunking Strategy for Repository-Level Code Completion},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - January 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=hY1USmqQou},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Late Code Chunking: A Code Chunking Strategy for Repository-Level Code Completion"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment"]},"abstract":{"value":"This paper introduces Late Code Chunking (LC$^2$), a chunking strategy designed to improve the semantic understanding of code segments for Large Language Models (LLMs). Repository-level code completion requires predicting the subsequent code for unfinished code by leveraging cross-file context spread across a repository. However, when retrieved fragments have missing semantics—the loss of structural or behavioral information during chunking—LLMs struggle to interpret the target code. To address this, LC$^2$ bifurcates chunks into a \"Code Retrieval Context\" optimized for similarity-based search and a \"Code Comprehension Context\" enriched via context expanding and augmentation. This dual-context design reduces information loss due to chunking and enhances the ability of LLMs to utilize retrieved code. Additionally, we introduce an Asymmetric Query-Chunk Sizing strategy to further enhance retrieval quality by minimizing query noise. Our experiments demonstrate that LC$^2$ provides robust performance enhancements, achieving a statistically significant 19.7\\% improvement in Exact Match accuracy on the CrossCodeEval benchmark compared to the best existing chunking method."},"paper_type":{"value":"Short"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d974c19b811d6a9e6e25728bb16a9390f12db552.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Code Models"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/Submission"}},"tmdate":1780805056624,"tcdate":1767510161999,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/Submission3138/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/Submission3138/Authors"],"forum":"hY1USmqQou","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":3138,"cdate":1767510161999,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1780805056624,"odate":1774007940523,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January","id":"hY1USmqQou","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.02772v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"kolodziej|efficient_numerical_calibration_of_water_delivery_network_using_shortburst_hydrant_trials"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.02772"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2410-02772,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Katarzyna Kolodziej and Michal Cholewa and Przemyslaw Glomb and Wojciech Koral and Michal Romaszewski},\n  title={Efficient Numerical Calibration of Water Delivery Network Using Short-Burst Hydrant Trials},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2410.02772},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.02772}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration is a critical process for reducing uncertainty in Water Distribution Network Hydraulic Models (WDN HM). However, features of certain WDNs, such as oversized pipelines, lead to shallow pressure gradients under normal daily conditions, posing a challenge for effective calibration. This study proposes a calibration methodology using short hydrant trials conducted at night, which increase the pressure gradient in the WDN. The data is resampled to align with hourly consumption patterns. In a unique real-world case study of a WDN zone, we demonstrate the statistically significant superiority of our method compared to calibration based on daily usage. The experimental methodology, inspired by a machine learning cross-validation framework, utilises two state-of-the-art calibration algorithms, achieving a reduction in absolute error of up to 45% in the best scenario."},"title":{"value":"Efficient Numerical Calibration of Water Delivery Network Using Short-Burst Hydrant Trials"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Katarzyna Kolodziej","username":""},{"fullname":"Michal Cholewa","username":"~Michal_Cholewa1"},{"fullname":"Przemyslaw Glomb","username":""},{"fullname":"Wojciech Koral","username":""},{"fullname":"Michal Romaszewski","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1787571662367,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2410-02772"],"tcdate":1787571656819,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Michał_Cholewa1"],"forum":"bWfpnFj4fY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":140067,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1787571662367,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"bWfpnFj4fY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Speech Audio Process. 2013"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10376/6631496/06584746.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TASLP/2013"},"paperhash":{"value":"mandasari|quality_measure_functions_for_calibration_of_speaker_recognition_systems_in_various_duration_conditions"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Miranti_Indar_Mandasari:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rahim_Saeidi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mitchell_McLaren:","~David_A._van_Leeuwen1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TASL.2013.2279332"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/taslp/MandasariSML13,\n  author={Miranti Indar Mandasari and Rahim Saeidi and Mitchell McLaren and David A. van Leeuwen},\n  title={Quality Measure Functions for Calibration of Speaker Recognition Systems in Various Duration Conditions},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Speech Audio Process.},\n  volume={21},\n  number={11},\n  pages={2425-2438},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TASL.2013.2279332}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper investigates the effect of utterance duration to the calibration of a modern i-vector speaker recognition system with probabilistic linear discriminant analysis (PLDA) modeling. A calibration approach to deal with these effects using quality measure functions (QMFs) is proposed to include duration in the calibration transformation. Extensive experiments are performed in order to evaluate the robustness of the proposed calibration approach for unseen conditions in the training of calibration parameters. Using the latest NIST corpora for evaluation, results highlight the importance of considering the quality metrics like duration in calibrating the scores for automatic speaker recognition systems."},"title":{"value":"Quality Measure Functions for Calibration of Speaker Recognition Systems in Various Duration Conditions"},"authors":{"value":["Miranti Indar Mandasari","Rahim Saeidi","Mitchell McLaren","David A. van Leeuwen"]}},"tmdate":1716457642203,"pdate":1356998400000,"tcdate":1716457615286,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~David_A._van_Leeuwen1"],"forum":"6xMBS1djDv","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":20627,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1716457642203,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"6xMBS1djDv","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Int. J. Robotics Res. 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/reader/10.1177/02783649221107703"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJRR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"luo|hybrid_sparse_monocular_visual_odometry_with_online_photometric_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongting_Luo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yan_Zhuang:","~Sen_Wang7"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1177/02783649221107703"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijrr/LuoZW22,\n  author={Dongting Luo and Yan Zhuang and Sen Wang},\n  title={Hybrid sparse monocular visual odometry with online photometric calibration},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={Int. J. Robotics Res.},\n  volume={41},\n  number={11-12},\n  pages={993-1021},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1177/02783649221107703}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Most monocular visual Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (vSLAM) and visual odometry (VO) algorithms focus on either feature-based methods or direct methods. Hybrid (semi-direct) approach is less studied although it is equally important. In this paper, a hybrid sparse visual odometry (HSO) algorithm with online photometric calibration is proposed for monocular vision. HSO introduces two novel measures, that is, direct image alignment with adaptive mode selection and image photometric description using ratio factors, to enhance the robustness against dramatic image intensity changes and motion blur. Moreover, HSO is able to establish pose constraints between keyframes far apart in time and space by using KLT tracking enhanced with a local-global brightness consistency. The convergence speed of candidate map points is adopted as the basis for keyframe selection, which strengthens the coordination between the front end and the back end. Photometric calibration is elegantly integrated into the VO system working in tandem: (1) Photometric interference from the camera, such as vignetting and changes in exposure time, is accurately calibrated and compensated in HSO, thereby improving the accuracy and robustness of VO. (2) On the other hand, VO provides pre-calculated data for the photometric calibration algorithm, which reduces resource consumption and improves the estimation accuracy of photometric parameters. Extensive experiments are performed on various public datasets to evaluate the proposed HSO against the state-of-the-art monocular vSLAM/VO and online photometric calibration methods. The results show that the proposed HSO achieves superior performance on VO and photometric calibration in terms of accuracy, robustness, and efficiency, being comparable with the state-of-the-art VO/vSLAM systems. We open source HSO for the benefit of the community."},"title":{"value":"Hybrid sparse monocular visual odometry with online photometric calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Dongting Luo","Yan Zhuang","Sen Wang"]}},"tmdate":1765901651168,"pdate":1640995200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/ijrr/LuoZW22"],"tcdate":1765901619993,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sen_Wang7"],"forum":"96LZz0E4T8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":709540,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1765901651168,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"96LZz0E4T8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.07441v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"han|autocalibration_method_using_stop_signs_for_urban_autonomous_driving_applications"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yunhai_Han1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuhan_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:David_Paz:","~Henrik_I._Christensen2"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.07441"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2010-07441,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yunhai Han and Yuhan Liu and David Paz and Henrik I. Christensen},\n  title={Auto-calibration Method Using Stop Signs for Urban Autonomous Driving Applications},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2010.07441},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.07441}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration of sensors is fundamental to robust performance for intelligent vehicles. In natural environments, disturbances can easily challenge calibration. One possibility is to use natural objects of known shape to recalibrate sensors. An approach based on recognition of traffic signs, such as stop signs, and use of them for recalibration of cameras is presented. The approach is based on detection, geometry estimation, calibration, and recursive updating. Results from natural environments are presented that clearly show convergence and improved performance."},"title":{"value":"Auto-calibration Method Using Stop Signs for Urban Autonomous Driving Applications"},"authors":{"value":["Yunhai Han","Yuhan Liu","David Paz","Henrik I. Christensen"]}},"tmdate":1741236917734,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1724536733763,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunhai_Han1"],"forum":"KCrsnhxCAR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":72157,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1741236917734,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KCrsnhxCAR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"VISIGRAPP (2): VISAPP 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"schulze|classifier_ensemble_for_efficient_uncertainty_calibration_of_deep_neural_networks_for_image_classification"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.5220/0013129000003912"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/visigrapp/SchulzeERW25,\n  author={Michael Schulze and Nikolas Ebert and Laurenz Reichardt and Oliver Wasenmüller},\n  title={Classifier Ensemble for Efficient Uncertainty Calibration of Deep Neural Networks for Image Classification},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={316-323},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.5220/0013129000003912},\n  booktitle={VISIGRAPP (2): VISAPP},\n  crossref={conf/visigrapp/2025-2}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper investigates novel classifier ensemble techniques for uncertainty calibration applied to various deep neural networks for image classification. We evaluate both accuracy and calibration metrics, focusing on Expected Calibration Error (ECE) and Maximum Calibration Error (MCE). Our work compares different methods for building simple yet efficient classifier ensembles, including majority voting and several metamodel-based approaches. Our evaluation reveals that while state-of-the-art deep neural networks for image classification achieve high accuracy on standard datasets, they frequently suffer from significant calibration errors. Basic ensemble techniques like majority voting provide modest improvements, while metamodel-based ensembles consistently reduce ECE and MCE across all architectures. Notably, the largest of our compared metamodels demonstrate the most substantial calibration improvements, with minimal impact on accuracy. Moreover, classifier ensembles with metamodel"},"title":{"value":"Classifier Ensemble for Efficient Uncertainty Calibration of Deep Neural Networks for Image Classification"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Michael Schulze","username":""},{"fullname":"Nikolas Ebert","username":""},{"fullname":"Laurenz Reichardt","username":"~Laurenz_Reichardt1"},{"fullname":"Oliver Wasenmüller","username":"~Oliver_Wasenmüller1"}]}},"tmdate":1781851217502,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/visigrapp/SchulzeERW25"],"tcdate":1781847811228,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Laurenz_Reichardt1"],"forum":"5EEkxrj0jE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":43756,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1781851217502,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"5EEkxrj0jE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Machine Learning"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10994-024-06652-6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"kängsepp|on_the_usefulness_of_the_fitontest_view_on_evaluating_calibration_of_classifiers"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10994-024-06652-6"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrated uncertainty estimates are essential for classifiers used in safety-critical applications. If a classifier is uncalibrated, then there is a unique way to calibrate its uncertainty using the idealistic true calibration map corresponding to this classifier. Although the true calibration map is typically unknown in practice, it can be estimated with many post-hoc calibration methods which fit some family of potential calibration functions on a validation dataset. This paper examines the connection between such post-hoc calibration methods and calibration evaluation. Despite the negative connotations of fitting on test data in machine learning, we claim that fitting calibration maps on test data as part of the calibration evaluation process is a method worth considering, and we refer to this view as fit-on-test. This view enables the usage of any post-hoc calibration method as an evaluation measure, unlocking missed opportunities in development of evaluation methods. We prove that even ECE, which is the most common calibration evaluation method, is actually a fit-on-test measure. This observation leads us to a new method of tuning the number of bins in ECE with cross-validation. Fitting on test data can lead to test-time overfitting, and therefore, we discuss the limitations and concerns with the fit-on-test view. Our contributions also include: (1) enhancement of reliability diagrams with diagonal filling; (2) development of new calibration map families PL and PL3; and (3) an experimental study of which families perform strongly both as post-hoc calibrators and calibration evaluators."},"title":{"value":"On the usefulness of the fit-on-test view on evaluating calibration of classifiers"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Markus Kängsepp","username":"~Markus_Kängsepp1"},{"fullname":"Kaspar Valk"},{"fullname":"Meelis Kull"}]}},"tmdate":1789092340096,"pdate":1743465600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1007/s10994-024-06652-6"],"tcdate":1767694625892,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Markus_Kängsepp1"],"forum":"GLW7U9KnyK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":22451,"cdate":1740484532658,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789092340096,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"GLW7U9KnyK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Mach. Learn. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10994-024-06652-6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ML/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"kängsepp|on_the_usefulness_of_the_fitontest_view_on_evaluating_calibration_of_classifiers"},"authorids":{"value":["~Markus_Kängsepp1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaspar_Valk:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Meelis_Kull:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10994-024-06652-6"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ml/KangseppVK25,\n  author={Markus Kängsepp and Kaspar Valk and Meelis Kull},\n  title={On the usefulness of the fit-on-test view on evaluating calibration of classifiers},\n  year={2025},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1743465600000},\n  journal={Mach. Learn.},\n  volume={114},\n  number={4},\n  pages={105},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s10994-024-06652-6}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrated uncertainty estimates are essential for classifiers used in safety-critical applications. If a classifier is uncalibrated, then there is a unique way to calibrate its uncertainty using the idealistic true calibration map corresponding to this classifier. Although the true calibration map is typically unknown in practice, it can be estimated with many post-hoc calibration methods which fit some family of potential calibration functions on a validation dataset. This paper examines the connection between such post-hoc calibration methods and calibration evaluation. Despite the negative connotations of fitting on test data in machine learning, we claim that fitting calibration maps on test data as part of the calibration evaluation process is a method worth considering, and we refer to this view as fit-on-test. This view enables the usage of any post-hoc calibration method as an evaluation measure, unlocking missed opportunities in development of evaluation methods. We prove that even ECE, which is the most common calibration evaluation method, is actually a fit-on-test measure. This observation leads us to a new method of tuning the number of bins in ECE with cross-validation. Fitting on test data can lead to test-time overfitting, and therefore, we discuss the limitations and concerns with the fit-on-test view. Our contributions also include: (1) enhancement of reliability diagrams with diagonal filling; (2) development of new calibration map families PL and PL3; and (3) an experimental study of which families perform strongly both as post-hoc calibrators and calibration evaluators."},"title":{"value":"On the usefulness of the fit-on-test view on evaluating calibration of classifiers"},"authors":{"value":["Markus Kängsepp","Kaspar Valk","Meelis Kull"]}},"tmdate":1747306170439,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1747306167078,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Markus_Kängsepp1"],"forum":"lkJ5nUxihz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":482653,"cdate":1743465600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747306170439,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"lkJ5nUxihz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11245651/11245652/11247371.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|robust_online_calibration_for_uwbaided_visualinertial_navigation_with_bias_correction"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yizhi_Zhou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiawei_Xia:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zechen_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weizi_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuan_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS60139.2025.11247371"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/ZhouXXHLW25,\n  author={Yizhi Zhou and Jie Xu and Jiawei Xia and Zechen Hu and Weizi Li and Xuan Wang},\n  title={Robust Online Calibration for UWB-Aided Visual-Inertial Navigation with Bias Correction},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={17956-17963},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS60139.2025.11247371},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents a novel robust online calibration framework for Ultra-Wideband (UWB) anchors in UWB-aided Visual-Inertial Navigation Systems (VINS). Accurate anchor positioning, a process known as calibration, is crucial for integrating UWB ranging measurements into state estimation. While several prior works have demonstrated satisfactory results by using robot-aided systems to autonomously calibrate UWB systems, there are still some limitations: 1) these approaches assume accurate robot localization during the initialization step, ignoring localization errors that can compromise calibration robustness, and 2) the calibration results are highly sensitive to the initial guess of the UWB anchors’ positions, reducing the practical applicability of these methods in real-world scenarios. Our approach addresses these challenges by explicitly incorporating the impact of robot localization uncertainties into the calibration process, ensuring robust initialization. To further enhance the robustness of the calibration results against initialization errors, we propose a tightly-coupled Schmidt Kalman Filter (SKF)-based online refinement method, making the system suitable for practical applications. Simulations and real-world experiments validate the improved accuracy and robustness of our approach."},"title":{"value":"Robust Online Calibration for UWB-Aided Visual-Inertial Navigation with Bias Correction"},"authors":{"value":["Yizhi Zhou","Jie Xu","Jiawei Xia","Zechen Hu","Weizi Li","Xuan Wang"]}},"tmdate":1768999180879,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iros/ZhouXXHLW25"],"tcdate":1768999167581,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yizhi_Zhou1"],"forum":"1cRHqZWM9C","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":784126,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768999180879,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"1cRHqZWM9C","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.10999v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|robust_online_calibration_for_uwbaided_visualinertial_navigation_with_bias_correction"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yizhi_Zhou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiawei_Xia:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zechen_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weizi_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuan_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.10999"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2508-10999,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yizhi Zhou and Jie Xu and Jiawei Xia and Zechen Hu and Weizi Li and Xuan Wang},\n  title={Robust Online Calibration for UWB-Aided Visual-Inertial Navigation with Bias Correction},\n  year={2025},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1754006400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2508.10999},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.10999}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents a novel robust online calibration framework for Ultra-Wideband (UWB) anchors in UWB-aided Visual-Inertial Navigation Systems (VINS). Accurate anchor positioning, a process known as calibration, is crucial for integrating UWB ranging measurements into state estimation. While several prior works have demonstrated satisfactory results by using robot-aided systems to autonomously calibrate UWB systems, there are still some limitations: 1) these approaches assume accurate robot localization during the initialization step, ignoring localization errors that can compromise calibration robustness, and 2) the calibration results are highly sensitive to the initial guess of the UWB anchors' positions, reducing the practical applicability of these methods in real-world scenarios. Our approach addresses these challenges by explicitly incorporating the impact of robot localization uncertainties into the calibration process, ensuring robust initialization. To further enhance the robustness of the calibration results against initialization errors, we propose a tightly-coupled Schmidt Kalman Filter (SKF)-based online refinement method, making the system suitable for practical applications. Simulations and real-world experiments validate the improved accuracy and robustness of our approach."},"title":{"value":"Robust Online Calibration for UWB-Aided Visual-Inertial Navigation with Bias Correction"},"authors":{"value":["Yizhi Zhou","Jie Xu","Jiawei Xia","Zechen Hu","Weizi Li","Xuan Wang"]}},"tmdate":1768999177388,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2508-10999"],"tcdate":1768999168061,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yizhi_Zhou1"],"forum":"F7zFw6Kdru","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":784135,"cdate":1754006400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768999177388,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"F7zFw6Kdru","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.04451v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|appearancebased_gaze_estimation_via_gaze_decomposition_and_single_gaze_point_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhaokang_Chen1","~Bertram_E._Shi1"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1905.04451"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1905-04451,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhaokang Chen and Bertram E. Shi},\n  title={Appearance-Based Gaze Estimation via Gaze Decomposition and Single Gaze Point Calibration},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1905.04451},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1905.04451}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Appearance-based gaze estimation provides relatively unconstrained gaze tracking. However, subject-independent models achieve limited accuracy partly due to individual variations. To improve estimation, we propose a novel gaze decomposition method and a single gaze point calibration method, motivated by our finding that the inter-subject squared bias exceeds the intra-subject variance for a subject-independent estimator. We decompose the gaze angle into a subject-dependent bias term and a subject-independent term between the gaze angle and the bias. The subject-independent term is estimated by a deep convolutional network. For calibration-free tracking, we set the subject-dependent bias term to zero. For single gaze point calibration, we estimate the bias from a few images taken as the subject gazes at a point. Experiments on three datasets indicate that as a calibration-free estimator, the proposed method outperforms the state-of-the-art methods by up to $10.0\\%$. The proposed calibration method is robust and reduces estimation error significantly (up to $35.6\\%$), achieving state-of-the-art performance for appearance-based eye trackers with calibration."},"title":{"value":"Appearance-Based Gaze Estimation via Gaze Decomposition and Single Gaze Point Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Zhaokang Chen","Bertram E. Shi"]}},"tmdate":1740705231089,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1731476342421,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bertram_E._Shi1"],"forum":"Vi8vf1WNwK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":202079,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1740705231089,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Vi8vf1WNwK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICASSP 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7943262/7951776/07952545.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICASSP/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"nadeau|insitu_calibration_of_accelerometers_in_bodyworn_sensors_using_quiescent_gravity"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrew_Nadeau:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Karthik_Dinesh:","~Gaurav_Sharma3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mulin_Xiong:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP.2017.7952545"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icassp/NadeauDSX17,\n  author={Andrew Nadeau and Karthik Dinesh and Gaurav Sharma and Mulin Xiong},\n  title={In-situ calibration of accelerometers in body-worn sensors using quiescent gravity},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  pages={2192-2196},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP.2017.7952545},\n  booktitle={ICASSP},\n  crossref={conf/icassp/2017}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As the cost, size and power required by sensor devices decrease, an increasing range of applications are possible. We focus on the application of tracking accelerometer data from body worn sensors over long durations in health monitoring applications. Body worn sensors must be compact for the convenience of the patient and low power to support extended operation, but these benefits can come at the cost of accuracy. To mitigate this loss of accuracy we first examine the ability of calibration to improve accuracy. Next we propose an in-situ method for calibrating the accelerometers using the quiescent acceleration due to gravity as a calibration signal. This in-situ calibration uses only the stored data from the duration the sensor is worn by the patient and does not require any extra procedures or measurements from the physical sensor devices. Compared to a manual three-axis calibration technique, the proposed calibration can be applied to data without the need for specific calibration procedures or even access to the original sensor and provides comparable or better accuracy."},"title":{"value":"In-situ calibration of accelerometers in body-worn sensors using quiescent gravity"},"authors":{"value":["Andrew Nadeau","Karthik Dinesh","Gaurav Sharma","Mulin Xiong"]}},"tmdate":1740106898450,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1740106883775,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Gaurav_Sharma3"],"forum":"DQxFGNpKr1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":337219,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740106898450,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"DQxFGNpKr1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ASPLOS (2) 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ASPLOS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"tan|qufem_fast_and_accurate_quantum_readout_calibration_using_the_finite_element_method"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Siwei_Tan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liqiang_Lu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hanyu_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jia_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Congliang_Lang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yongheng_Shang:","~Xinkui_Zhao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mingshuai_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yun_Liang_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianwei_Yin:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3620665.3640380"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/asplos/TanLZYLSZC0Y24,\n  author={Siwei Tan and Liqiang Lu and Hanyu Zhang and Jia Yu and Congliang Lang and Yongheng Shang and Xinkui Zhao and Mingshuai Chen and Yun Liang and Jianwei Yin},\n  title={QuFEM: Fast and Accurate Quantum Readout Calibration Using the Finite Element Method},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={948-963},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3620665.3640380},\n  booktitle={ASPLOS (2)},\n  crossref={conf/asplos/2024-2}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Quantum readout noise turns out to be the most significant source of error, which greatly affects the measurement fidelity. Matrix-based calibration has been demonstrated to be effective in various quantum platforms. However, existing methodologies are fundamentally limited in either scalability or accuracy. Inspired by the classical finite element method (FEM), a formal method to model the complex interaction between elements, we present our calibration framework named QuFEM. First, we apply a divide-and-conquer strategy that formulates the calibration as a series of tensor products with noise matrices. This matrices are iteratively characterized together with the calibrated probability distribution, aiming to capture the inherent locality of qubit interactions. Then, to accelerate the end-to-end calibration, we propose a sparse tensor-product engine to exploit the sparsity in the intermediate values. Our experiments show that QuFEM achieves 2.5×103× speedup in the 136-qubit calibration compared to the state-of-the-art matrix-based calibration technique [50], and provides 1.2× and 1.4× fidelity improvement on the 18-qubit and 36-qubit real-world quantum devices."},"title":{"value":"QuFEM: Fast and Accurate Quantum Readout Calibration Using the Finite Element Method"},"authors":{"value":["Siwei Tan","Liqiang Lu","Hanyu Zhang","Jia Yu","Congliang Lang","Yongheng Shang","Xinkui Zhao","Mingshuai Chen","Yun Liang","Jianwei Yin"]}},"tmdate":1738805560229,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1738805556664,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xinkui_Zhao1"],"forum":"avs0vucfEA","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":298210,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738805560229,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"avs0vucfEA","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/sandruskyi/SocratesLoss"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep neural networks, despite their high accuracy, often exhibit poor confidence calibration, limiting their reliability in high-stakes applications. Current ad-hoc confidence calibration methods attempt to fix this during training but face a fundamental trade-off: two-phase training methods achieve strong classification performance at the cost of training instability and poorer confidence calibration, while single-loss methods are stable but underperform in classification. This paper addresses and mitigates this stability-performance trade-off. We propose Socrates Loss, a novel, unified loss function that explicitly leverages uncertainty by incorporating an auxiliary unknown class, whose predictions directly influence the loss function and a dynamic uncertainty penalty. This unified objective allows the model to be optimized for both classification and confidence calibration simultaneously, without the instability of complex, scheduled losses. We provide theoretical guarantees that our method regularizes the model to prevent miscalibration and overfitting. Across four benchmark datasets and multiple architectures, our comprehensive experiments demonstrate that Socrates Loss consistently improves training stability while achieving more favorable accuracy-calibration trade-off, often converging faster than existing methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\ng{\\'o}mez-g{\\'a}lvez2026socrates,\ntitle={Socrates Loss: Unifying Confidence Calibration and Classification by Leveraging the Unknown},\nauthor={Sandra G{\\'o}mez-G{\\'a}lvez and Tobias Olenyi and Gillian Dobbie and Katerina Taskova},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=DONqw1KhHq},\nnote={}\n}"},"video":{"value":"https://youtu.be/7WuSkC-aWW8?si=9fgq5ZN7euIyGZGU"},"title":{"value":"Socrates Loss: Unifying Confidence Calibration and Classification by Leveraging the Unknown"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"Small rewording in the last paragraph of the Introduction, last paragraph of the Abstract, and in the Conclusion to improve readability."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/48165d4348bc9e1e1acf838e1d93d1720293ccb6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"gómezgálvez|socrates_loss_unifying_confidence_calibration_and_classification_by_leveraging_the_unknown"},"authorids":{"value":["~Sandra_Gómez-Gálvez1","~Tobias_Olenyi1","~Gillian_Dobbie1","~Katerina_Taskova1"]},"previous_TMLR_submission_url":{"value":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=S9DGZaDYJP"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Jose_Dolz1"},"authors":{"value":["Sandra Gómez-Gálvez","Tobias Olenyi","Gillian Dobbie","Katerina Taskova"]}},"tmdate":1775842374138,"pdate":1775842373419,"tcdate":1764706293062,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper6786/Authors"],"forum":"DONqw1KhHq","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6786,"cdate":1764706293062,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper6786/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper6786/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1775842374138,"odate":1765208285141,"domain":"TMLR","id":"DONqw1KhHq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"J. Comput. Inf. Sci. Eng. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://asmedigitalcollection.asme.org/computingengineering/article-pdf/23/1/011006/6916389/jcise_23_1_011006.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/JCISE/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"kapusuzoglu|multilevel_bayesian_calibration_of_a_multicomponent_dynamic_system_model"},"authorids":{"value":["~Berkcan_Kapusuzoglu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sankaran_Mahadevan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shunsaku_Matsumoto:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yoshitomo_Miyagi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daigo_Watanabe:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1115/1.4055315"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/jcise/KapusuzogluMMMW23,\n  author={Berkcan Kapusuzoglu and Sankaran Mahadevan and Shunsaku Matsumoto and Yoshitomo Miyagi and Daigo Watanabe},\n  title={Multi-Level Bayesian Calibration of a Multi-Component Dynamic System Model},\n  year={2023},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1675209600000},\n  journal={J. Comput. Inf. Sci. Eng.},\n  volume={23},\n  number={1},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1115/1.4055315}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes a multi-level Bayesian calibration approach that fuses information from heterogeneous sources and accounts for uncertainties in modeling and measurements for time-dependent multi-component systems. The developed methodology has two elements: quantifying the uncertainty at component and system levels, by fusing all available information, and corrected model prediction. A multi-level Bayesian calibration approach is developed to estimate component-level and system-level parameters using measurement data that are obtained at different time instances for different system components. Such heterogeneous data are consumed in a sequential manner, and an iterative strategy is developed to calibrate the parameters at the two levels. This calibration strategy is implemented for two scenarios: offline and online. The offline calibration uses data that is collected over all the time-steps, whereas online calibration is performed in real-time as new measurements are obtained at each time-step. Analysis models and observation data for the thermo-mechanical behavior of gas turbine engine rotor blades are used to analyze the effectiveness of the proposed approach."},"title":{"value":"Multi-Level Bayesian Calibration of a Multi-Component Dynamic System Model"},"authors":{"value":["Berkcan Kapusuzoglu","Sankaran Mahadevan","Shunsaku Matsumoto","Yoshitomo Miyagi","Daigo Watanabe"]}},"tmdate":1737731143569,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1737731131687,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Berkcan_Kapusuzoglu1"],"forum":"KtatDmfZEI","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":271147,"cdate":1675209600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1737731143569,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KtatDmfZEI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.14380v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|deep_domain_adaptation_regression_for_force_calibration_of_optical_tactile_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhuo_Chen:","~Ni_Ou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiaqi_Jiang:","~Shan_Luo1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.14380"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2407-14380,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhuo Chen and Ni Ou and Jiaqi Jiang and Shan Luo},\n  title={Deep Domain Adaptation Regression for Force Calibration of Optical Tactile Sensors},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2407.14380},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.14380}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Optical tactile sensors provide robots with rich force information for robot grasping in unstructured environments. The fast and accurate calibration of three-dimensional contact forces holds significance for new sensors and existing tactile sensors which may have incurred damage or aging. However, the conventional neural-network-based force calibration method necessitates a large volume of force-labeled tactile images to minimize force prediction errors, with the need for accurate Force/Torque measurement tools as well as a time-consuming data collection process. To address this challenge, we propose a novel deep domain-adaptation force calibration method, designed to transfer the force prediction ability from a calibrated optical tactile sensor to uncalibrated ones with various combinations of domain gaps, including marker presence, illumination condition, and elastomer modulus. Experimental results show the effectiveness of the proposed unsupervised force calibration method, with lowest force prediction errors of 0.102N (3.4\\% in full force range) for normal force, and 0.095N (6.3\\%) and 0.062N (4.1\\%) for shear forces along the x-axis and y-axis, respectively. This study presents a promising, general force calibration methodology for optical tactile sensors."},"title":{"value":"Deep Domain Adaptation Regression for Force Calibration of Optical Tactile Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Zhuo Chen","Ni Ou","Jiaqi Jiang","Shan Luo"]}},"tmdate":1731508248877,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1729126494288,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ni_Ou1"],"forum":"4AZ8rCTsZh","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":153577,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1731508248877,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4AZ8rCTsZh","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"arxiv"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d1ea1996e221e89c71c8d9405f8b3b8ac01c3fcd.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"pershin|calibrationgated_llm_pseudoobservations_for_online_contextual_bandits"},"authorids":{"value":["~Maksim_Pershin1","ivan.golovanov@phystech.edu","psbaltabaev@edu.hse.ru","nattrankova@gmail.com"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.14961"},"abstract":{"value":"Contextual bandit algorithms suffer from high regret during cold-start, when the learner has insufficient data to distinguish good arms from bad. We propose augmenting Disjoint LinUCB with LLM pseudo-observations: after each round, a large language model predicts counterfactual rewards for the unplayed arms, and these predictions are injected into the learner as weighted pseudo-observations. The injection weight is controlled by a calibration-gated decay schedule that tracks the LLM's prediction accuracy on played arms via an exponential moving average; high calibration error suppresses the LLM's influence, while accurate predictions receive higher weight during the critical early rounds. We evaluate on two contextual bandit environments - UCI Mushroom (2-arm, asymmetric rewards) and MIND-small (5-arm news recommendation) - and find that when equipped with a task-specific prompt, LLM pseudo-observations reduce cumulative regret by 19% on MIND relative to pure LinUCB. However, generic counterfactual prompt framing increases regret on both environments, demonstrating that prompt design is the dominant factor, more important than the choice of decay schedule or calibration gating parameters. We analyze the failure modes of calibration gating on domains with small prediction errors and provide a theoretical motivation for the bias-variance trade-off governing pseudo-observation weight."},"title":{"value":"Calibration-Gated LLM Pseudo-Observations for Online Contextual Bandits"},"authors":{"value":["Maksim Pershin","Ivan Golovanov","Pavel Baltabaev","Natalia Trankova"]}},"tmdate":1780697154359,"pdate":1776312000000,"tcdate":1780697154359,"writers":["~Maksim_Pershin1","ivan.golovanov@phystech.edu","psbaltabaev@edu.hse.ru","nattrankova@gmail.com"],"signatures":["~Maksim_Pershin1"],"forum":"xAxSfmJJki","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":50976,"cdate":1780697154359,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1780697154359,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"xAxSfmJJki","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVIP (2) 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-11349-9_35.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVIP/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"hazra|two_novel_methods_for_multiple_kinect_v2_sensor_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","","~Rafał_Scherer1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-11349-9_35"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvip/HazraPPNS21,\n  author={Sumit Hazra and Manasa Pisipati and Amrit Puhan and Anup Nandy and Rafal Scherer},\n  title={Two Novel Methods for Multiple Kinect v2 Sensor Calibration},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={403-414},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-11349-9_35},\n  booktitle={CVIP (2)},\n  crossref={conf/cvip/2021-2}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration is an essential step for measuring an instrument’s accuracy by using its parameters. In this paper, we propose two methods for calibrating eight Kinect v2.0 sensors, namely, pairwise and simultaneous single-camera calibration. In the first method, an experimental setup is managed so that the eight Kinect cameras can view a 3D object on a treadmill in consecutive pairs. The novelty of this method infers pairwise calibration of eight Kinects using six steps, specifically: precalibration process, acquiring images from Kinect, exporting the parameters, generation of independent live point clouds, feeding exported parameters for point cloud matching and ensuring successful calibration through merged point clouds. In the second method, a novel octagonal model is developed to calibrate each of the eight cameras on the same experimental setup. We obtain an overall mean reprojection error of 1.37 pixels and 0.42 pixels for the first method and second method, respectively. The smallest reprojection error for the first method is reported to be 0.63 pixels and 0.27 pixels for the second method. The efficiency of the proposed methods is compared with the state-of-art techniques using the root mean square and the reprojection error metrics. We observed that the proposed methods outperform the existing techniques."},"title":{"value":"Two Novel Methods for Multiple Kinect v2 Sensor Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Sumit Hazra","Manasa Pisipati","Amrit Puhan","Anup Nandy","Rafal Scherer"]}},"tmdate":1774437618334,"pdate":1640908800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/cvip/HazraPPNS21"],"tcdate":1774437572526,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rafał_Scherer1"],"forum":"ZtMBDvqnit","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":859259,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1774437618334,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ZtMBDvqnit","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVIP (2) 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-11349-9_35.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVIP/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"hazra|two_novel_methods_for_multiple_kinect_v2_sensor_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sumit_Hazra:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Manasa_Pisipati:","~Amrit_Puhan1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Anup_Nandy:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rafal_Scherer:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-11349-9_35"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvip/HazraPPNS21,\n  author={Sumit Hazra and Manasa Pisipati and Amrit Puhan and Anup Nandy and Rafal Scherer},\n  title={Two Novel Methods for Multiple Kinect v2 Sensor Calibration},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={403-414},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-11349-9_35},\n  booktitle={CVIP (2)},\n  crossref={conf/cvip/2021-2}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration is an essential step for measuring an instrument’s accuracy by using its parameters. In this paper, we propose two methods for calibrating eight Kinect v2.0 sensors, namely, pairwise and simultaneous single-camera calibration. In the first method, an experimental setup is managed so that the eight Kinect cameras can view a 3D object on a treadmill in consecutive pairs. The novelty of this method infers pairwise calibration of eight Kinects using six steps, specifically: precalibration process, acquiring images from Kinect, exporting the parameters, generation of independent live point clouds, feeding exported parameters for point cloud matching and ensuring successful calibration through merged point clouds. In the second method, a novel octagonal model is developed to calibrate each of the eight cameras on the same experimental setup. We obtain an overall mean reprojection error of 1.37 pixels and 0.42 pixels for the first method and second method, respectively. The smallest reprojection error for the first method is reported to be 0.63 pixels and 0.27 pixels for the second method. The efficiency of the proposed methods is compared with the state-of-art techniques using the root mean square and the reprojection error metrics. We observed that the proposed methods outperform the existing techniques."},"title":{"value":"Two Novel Methods for Multiple Kinect v2 Sensor Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Sumit Hazra","Manasa Pisipati","Amrit Puhan","Anup Nandy","Rafal Scherer"]}},"tmdate":1715785305484,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1715785302907,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Amrit_Puhan1"],"forum":"fy5v9CkxPC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":13755,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1715785305484,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"fy5v9CkxPC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.21990v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"oliveira|understanding_code_understandability_improvements_in_code_reviews"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Delano_Oliveira:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Reydne_Santos:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Benedito_de_Oliveira:","~Martin_Monperrus2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fernando_Castor:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fernanda_Madeiral:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.21990"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2410-21990,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Delano Oliveira and Reydne Santos and Benedito de Oliveira and Martin Monperrus and Fernando Castor and Fernanda Madeiral},\n  title={Understanding Code Understandability Improvements in Code Reviews},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2410.21990},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.21990}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Motivation: Code understandability is crucial in software development, as developers spend 58% to 70% of their time reading source code. Improving it can improve productivity and reduce maintenance costs. Problem: Experimental studies often identify factors influencing code understandability in controlled settings but overlook real-world influences like project culture, guidelines, and developers' backgrounds. Ignoring these factors may yield results with limited external validity. Objective: This study investigates how developers enhance code understandability through code review comments, assuming that code reviewers are specialists in code quality. Method and Results: We analyzed 2,401 code review comments from Java open-source projects on GitHub, finding that over 42% focus on improving code understandability. We further examined 385 comments specifically related to this aspect and identified eight categories of concerns, such as inadequate documentation and poor identifiers. Notably, 83.9% of suggestions for improvement were accepted and integrated, with fewer than 1% later reverted. We identified various types of patches that enhance understandability, from simple changes like removing unused code to context-dependent improvements such as optimizing method calls. Additionally, we evaluated four well-known linters for their ability to flag these issues, finding they cover less than 30%, although many could be easily added as new rules. Implications: Our findings encourage the development of tools to enhance code understandability, as accepted changes can serve as reliable training data for specialized machine-learning models. Our dataset supports this training and can inform the development of evidence-based code style guides. Data Availability: Our data is publicly available at https://codeupcrc.github.io."},"title":{"value":"Understanding Code Understandability Improvements in Code Reviews"},"authors":{"value":["Delano Oliveira","Reydne Santos","Benedito de Oliveira","Martin Monperrus","Fernando Castor","Fernanda Madeiral"]}},"tmdate":1747140873984,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747140823989,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Martin_Monperrus2"],"forum":"mu2C6Bh7aY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":446834,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747140873984,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"mu2C6Bh7aY","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Rounding the conformal cutoff onto a shared grid in the units of a public score distribution makes independent analysts deploy the identical classifier, at a coverage-mass price that no method can beat in order."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["conformal prediction","replicability","calibration","uncertainty quantification","reproducibility","distribution-free inference","learning theory"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/8f4187c388b54a1cbbed289593381ddfc2358cc2.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"learning theory"},"abstract":{"value":"Two laboratories that calibrate the same prediction model on their own data deploy different prediction sets. The reason is that the calibrated cutoff is computed from continuous scores, so two calibrations never produce the same cutoff, even when both are correct on average. As a result, predictions stored by one laboratory cannot be reused by another, and an auditor cannot reproduce a vendor's prediction sets. We introduce replicable split-conformal calibration. It rounds each calibrated cutoff up to the nearest of a few values that both laboratories agree on in advance, so that both deploy identical prediction sets with a probability they choose, while each keeps its own coverage guarantee. The cost is larger prediction sets. How large depends on what the laboratories share to choose these values, and we prove three results about it. First, without shared information, identical prediction sets are impossible. Second, a shared random seed makes them possible, but at a cost that no seed-based threshold rule can avoid in the worst case. Third, a small public sample from the same population removes the need for a seed, lowers the cost, and gives guarantees that hold for every continuous score distribution. We implement our method on ImageNet, CIFAR-100 and four language models, GPT-2, Pythia, Qwen and Llama, and confirm the theory. On ImageNet, the chance of identical prediction sets rises from zero to above ninety percent, for prediction sets about a fifth larger. On CIFAR-100, with six times less calibration data, the same pattern holds at a higher cost. On the language models, a shared seed reaches the chosen probability of identical prediction sets in all 42 settings, and its cost falls as the calibration data grow. In a question-answering service built on Llama, two laboratories whose stored predictions differ on more than a third of the questions under standard calibration end up with identical stored predictions ninety-five percent of the time, at the cost of storing larger prediction sets."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026replicable,\ntitle={Replicable Split-Conformal Calibration in Coverage Units},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Ptuhr3yDfH},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Replicable Split-Conformal Calibration in Coverage Units"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/adb3bdf1478fea2da2ae3be28255f0ed00e210d0.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791232457150,"tcdate":1789715547361,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission38638/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission38638/Authors"],"forum":"Ptuhr3yDfH","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":38638,"cdate":1789715547361,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission38638/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791232457150,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"Ptuhr3yDfH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2024 Poster"},"abstract":{"value":"Discrimination and calibration represent two important properties of survival analysis, with the former assessing the model's ability to accurately rank subjects and the latter evaluating the alignment of predicted outcomes with actual events. With their distinct nature, it is hard for survival models to simultaneously optimize both of them especially as many previous results found improving calibration tends to diminish discrimination performance. This paper introduces a novel approach utilizing *conformal regression* that can improve a model's calibration without degrading discrimination. We provide theoretical guarantees for the above claim, and rigorously validate the efficiency of our approach across 11 real-world datasets, showcasing its practical applicability and robustness in diverse scenarios."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nqi2024conformalized,\ntitle={Conformalized Survival Distributions: A Generic Post-Process to Increase Calibration},\nauthor={Shi-ang Qi and Yakun Yu and Russell Greiner},\nbooktitle={Forty-first International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=jr0W36wOBx}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Conformalized Survival Distributions: A Generic Post-Process to Increase Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/66c329536fb46f07e6e970a99dfc2b8cd274e745.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2024/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"qi|conformalized_survival_distributions_a_generic_postprocess_to_increase_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shi-ang_Qi1","~Yakun_Yu1","~Russell_Greiner2"]},"authors":{"value":["Shi-ang Qi","Yakun Yu","Russell Greiner"]}},"tmdate":1719287238835,"pdate":1714610326462,"tcdate":1706596659707,"writers":["ICML.cc/2024/Conference","ICML.cc/2024/Conference/Submission3026/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2024/Conference/Submission3026/Authors"],"forum":"jr0W36wOBx","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":3026,"cdate":1706596659707,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2024/Conference/Submission3026/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1719287238835,"odate":1717693046171,"domain":"ICML.cc/2024/Conference","id":"jr0W36wOBx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Calibration","Category Discovery"]},"primary_area":{"value":"unsupervised, self-supervised, semi-supervised, and supervised representation learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Generalized Category Discovery (GCD) aims to recognize old classes while discovering novel ones in unlabeled data, yet the practical value of such discoveries hinges on whether the model's confidence can be trusted. Existing GCD methods optimize clustering accuracy alone, leaving their predictions on novel classes markedly miscalibrated. We argue that this deficiency cannot be resolved by conventional post-hoc calibration. First, miscalibration in GCD is inherently cluster-level, whereas conventional methods apply a single sample-level mapping to all clusters. Second, novel classes provide no labels for calibrating confidence. To address this, we formulate Calibrated Category Discovery (CalCD) and introduce a cluster-conditional expected calibration error (cluECE) that assesses calibration within each predicted cluster, preventing the deviations of different clusters from cancelling as they do in the standard expected calibration error. We further propose Known-to-Novel calIbration Transfer (KNIT), grounded in the premise that the trustworthiness of a cluster may follow a class-agnostic pattern. KNIT characterizes cluster reliability through the model's self-assessment and an independent semantic check by a vision-language model, estimates this pattern on labeled old-class clusters, and transfers it to novel-class clusters, correcting confidence without altering predictions. Extensive experiments comprising 220 comparisons across 4 GCD methods, 11 datasets spanning generic, fine-grained and multi-domain images, and 5 calibration baselines demonstrate that KNIT significantly reduces cluECE for both novel and old classes."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026calibrated,\ntitle={Calibrated Category Discovery: Knitting Calibration from the Known into the Novel},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=kVDcjySgTG},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibrated Category Discovery: Knitting Calibration from the Known into the Novel"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c3970ebf05f7288e302ff9e7d8b923caa5423fd1.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791233930659,"tcdate":1789755819317,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission47231/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission47231/Authors"],"forum":"kVDcjySgTG","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":47231,"cdate":1789755819317,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission47231/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791233930659,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"kVDcjySgTG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE/ASME Transactions on Mechatronics"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"dai|highaccuracy_calibration_for_a_multiview_microscopic_3d_measurement_system"},"authorids":{"value":["~Rong_DAI2","~Wenpan_Li1","fxytang@gmail.com","707998032@qq.com","yhliu@cuhk.edu.hk"]},"html":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/10358363"},"abstract":{"value":"This article proposes a novel calibration framework for a multiview microscopic 3-D measurement system comprising multiple tilted-cameras with the Scheimpflug condition and one vertical projector. Conventionally, the accurate and independent point correspondences between images and planar targets achieve successful calibration. However, affected by our system's large imaging magnification and anisotropic perspectivity, targets’ poses must be limited in the shallow common focus volume to ensure correspondences’ extracting accuracy of all views. This imaging constraint will lead to insufficiently independent correspondences in calibration computation, where constraints will be insufficient in parameter estimation (under-constraint problems) and bundle adjustment (falling in local optima caused by ambiguous models). To this end, our method first relies on insufficiently independent correspondences to tackle the above problems. In calibrating multiple tilted-cameras, the proposed initialization method incorporates the lens equation as an additional constraint to solve the under-constraint problem. The subsequent multicamera bundle adjustment disambiguates by stereo constraints. Moreover, for the vertical projector, another novel unconventional calibration method utilizes geometric constraints to determine its projection matrix. The experiments prove that the estimation and optimization of parameters for multiple tilted-cameras are significantly enhanced with the smallest reprojection error near 0.1 pixel. In addition, the 3-D measurement result of the standard concentric cylinders has also been applied to demonstrate the accuracy of the vertical projector calibration and the whole calibration framework."},"title":{"value":"High-Accuracy Calibration for a Multiview Microscopic 3-D Measurement System"},"authors":{"value":["Rong DAI","Wenpan Li","Xueyan Tang","Yucheng Li","Yun-Hui Liu"]}},"tmdate":1741165917743,"pdate":1702396800000,"tcdate":1741165917743,"writers":["~Rong_DAI2","~Wenpan_Li1","fxytang@gmail.com","707998032@qq.com","yhliu@cuhk.edu.hk"],"signatures":["~Rong_DAI2"],"forum":"OUtJLQ3Dsd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":34173,"cdate":1741165917743,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1741165917743,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"OUtJLQ3Dsd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2025 Poster"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Content Moderation","LLM-based Guard Models","Calibration","Safety"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/17ccc5704e03149dccbd16ce866959a1cbb525f8.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) pose significant risks due to the potential for generating harmful content or users attempting to evade guardrails. Existing studies have developed LLM-based guard models designed to moderate the input and output of threat LLMs, ensuring adherence to safety policies by blocking content that violates these protocols upon deployment. However, limited attention has been given to the reliability and calibration of such guard models. In this work, we empirically conduct comprehensive investigations of confidence calibration for 9 existing LLM-based guard models on 12 benchmarks in both user input and model output classification. Our findings reveal that current LLM-based guard models tend to 1) produce overconfident predictions, 2) exhibit significant miscalibration when subjected to jailbreak attacks, and 3) demonstrate limited robustness to the outputs generated by different types of response models. Additionally, we assess the effectiveness of post-hoc calibration methods to mitigate miscalibration. We demonstrate the efficacy of temperature scaling and, for the first time, highlight the benefits of contextual calibration for confidence calibration of guard models, particularly in the absence of validation sets. Our analysis and experiments underscore the limitations of current LLM-based guard models and provide valuable insights for the future development of well-calibrated guard models toward more reliable content moderation. We also advocate for incorporating reliability evaluation of confidence calibration when releasing future LLM-based guard models."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nliu2025on,\ntitle={On Calibration of {LLM}-based Guard Models for Reliable Content Moderation},\nauthor={Hongfu Liu and Hengguan Huang and Xiangming Gu and Hao Wang and Ye Wang},\nbooktitle={The Thirteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=wUbum0nd9N}\n}"},"title":{"value":"On Calibration of LLM-based Guard Models for Reliable Content Moderation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/5619950285fc49ddd5796c719c9636310b9dd984.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|on_calibration_of_llmbased_guard_models_for_reliable_content_moderation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Hongfu_Liu4","~Hengguan_Huang1","~Xiangming_Gu1","~Hao_Wang3","~Ye_Wang3"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Hongfu Liu","Hengguan Huang","Xiangming Gu","Hao Wang","Ye Wang"]}},"tmdate":1740890359711,"pdate":1737562964981,"tcdate":1727494764294,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission13206/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission13206/Authors"],"forum":"wUbum0nd9N","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":13206,"cdate":1727494764294,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission13206/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission13206/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission13206/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1740890359711,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"wUbum0nd9N","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.10414v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|on_calibration_of_llmbased_guard_models_for_reliable_content_moderation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongfu_Liu_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hengguan_Huang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hao_Wang_0014:","~Xiangming_Gu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ye_Wang_0007:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.10414"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2410-10414,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Hongfu Liu and Hengguan Huang and Hao Wang and Xiangming Gu and Ye Wang},\n  title={On Calibration of LLM-based Guard Models for Reliable Content Moderation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2410.10414},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.10414}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) pose significant risks due to the potential for generating harmful content or users attempting to evade guardrails. Existing studies have developed LLM-based guard models designed to moderate the input and output of threat LLMs, ensuring adherence to safety policies by blocking content that violates these protocols upon deployment. However, limited attention has been given to the reliability and calibration of such guard models. In this work, we empirically conduct comprehensive investigations of confidence calibration for 9 existing LLM-based guard models on 12 benchmarks in both user input and model output classification. Our findings reveal that current LLM-based guard models tend to 1) produce overconfident predictions, 2) exhibit significant miscalibration when subjected to jailbreak attacks, and 3) demonstrate limited robustness to the outputs generated by different types of response models. Additionally, we assess the effectiveness of post-hoc calibration methods to mitigate miscalibration. We demonstrate the efficacy of temperature scaling and, for the first time, highlight the benefits of contextual calibration for confidence calibration of guard models, particularly in the absence of validation sets. Our analysis and experiments underscore the limitations of current LLM-based guard models and provide valuable insights for the future development of well-calibrated guard models toward more reliable content moderation. We also advocate for incorporating reliability evaluation of confidence calibration when releasing future LLM-based guard models."},"title":{"value":"On Calibration of LLM-based Guard Models for Reliable Content Moderation"},"authors":{"value":["Hongfu Liu","Hengguan Huang","Hao Wang","Xiangming Gu","Ye Wang"]}},"tmdate":1736500828344,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1736500826237,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiangming_Gu1"],"forum":"Nf2q44Bf9U","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":261170,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1736500828344,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Nf2q44Bf9U","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SafeGenAi Oral"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6f1b5278fa9ddb17efc2f12c527d980a67f9d79c.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Content Moderation","LLM-based Guard Models","Calibration","Reliability"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Workshop/SafeGenAi"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|on_calibration_of_llmbased_guard_models_for_reliable_content_moderation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Hongfu_Liu4","~Hengguan_Huang1","~Hao_Wang3","~Xiangming_Gu1","~Ye_Wang3"]},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) pose significant risks due to the potential for generating harmful content or users attempting to evade guardrails. Existing studies have developed LLM-based guard models designed to moderate the input and output of threat LLMs, ensuring adherence to safety policies by blocking content that violates these protocols upon deployment. However, limited attention has been given to the reliability and calibration of such guard models. In this work, we empirically conduct comprehensive investigations of confidence calibration for 9 existing LLM-based guard models on 12 benchmarks in both user input and model output classification. Our findings reveal that current LLM-based guard models tend to 1) produce overconfident predictions, 2) exhibit significant miscalibration when subjected to jailbreak attacks, and 3) demonstrate limited robustness to the outputs generated by different types of response models. Additionally, we assess the effectiveness of post-hoc calibration methods to mitigate miscalibration. We demonstrate the efficacy of temperature scaling and, for the first time, highlight the benefits of contextual calibration for confidence calibration of guard models, particularly in the absence of validation sets. Our analysis and experiments underscore the limitations of current LLM-based guard models and provide valuable insights for the future development of well-calibrated guard models toward more reliable content moderation. We also advocate for incorporating reliability evaluation of confidence calibration when releasing future LLM-based guard models."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nliu2024on,\ntitle={On Calibration of {LLM}-based Guard Models for Reliable Content Moderation},\nauthor={Hongfu Liu and Hengguan Huang and Hao Wang and Xiangming Gu and Ye Wang},\nbooktitle={Neurips Safe Generative AI Workshop 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=WariAWh7Ih}\n}"},"title":{"value":"On Calibration of LLM-based Guard Models for Reliable Content Moderation"},"authors":{"value":["Hongfu Liu","Hengguan Huang","Hao Wang","Xiangming Gu","Ye Wang"]}},"tmdate":1731623832946,"pdate":1728768797906,"tcdate":1727939425261,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Workshop/SafeGenAi","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Workshop/SafeGenAi/Submission178/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Workshop/SafeGenAi/Submission178/Authors"],"forum":"WariAWh7Ih","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":178,"cdate":1727939425261,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Workshop/SafeGenAi/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Workshop/SafeGenAi/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Workshop/SafeGenAi/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Workshop/SafeGenAi/Submission178/-/Revision"],"mdate":1731623832946,"odate":1728768797906,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Workshop/SafeGenAi","id":"WariAWh7Ih","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Intelligent Transportation Systems"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6979/10304348/10196359.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"ren|trajmatch_toward_automatic_spatiotemporal_calibration_for_roadside_lidars_through_trajectory_matching"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TITS.2023.3295757"},"abstract":{"value":"Recently, deploying sensors such as LiDARs on the roadside to monitor the passing traffic and assist autonomous vehicle perception has become popular. However, unlike autonomous vehicle systems, roadside sensor systems involve sensors from different subsystems, resulting in a lack of synchronization in both time and space between the sensors. Calibration is a critical technology that enables the central server to fuse data generated by different location infrastructures, which vastly improves sensing range and detection robustness. Regrettably, existing calibration algorithms frequently assume that LiDARs have significant overlap or that temporal calibration has already been achieved. However, since these assumptions do not always hold in real-world scenarios, the calibration results obtained from existing algorithms are frequently unsatisfactory. In this paper, we propose TrajMatch - the first system that can automatically calibrate roadside LiDARs in both time and space. The main idea is to automatically calibrate the sensors based on the result of the detection/tracking task, rather than relying on extracting special features. Furthermore, we propose a novel mechanism for evaluating calibration parameters that align with our algorithm, and we demonstrate its effectiveness through experiments. This mechanism can also guide parameter iterations for multiple calibrations, further enhancing the accuracy and efficiency of our calibration method. Finally, to evaluate the performance of TrajMatch, we collected two datasets, one simulated dataset LiDARnet-sim 1.0 and one real-world dataset. The experimental results show that TrajMatch can achieve a spatial calibration error of less than  $10cm$  and a temporal calibration error of less than  $1.5ms$ ."},"title":{"value":"TrajMatch: Toward Automatic Spatio-Temporal Calibration for Roadside LiDARs Through Trajectory Matching"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Haojie Ren"},{"fullname":"Sha Zhang"},{"fullname":"Sugang Li"},{"fullname":"Yao Li"},{"fullname":"Xinchen Li"},{"fullname":"Jianmin Ji"},{"fullname":"Yu Zhang","username":"~Yu_Zhang51"},{"fullname":"Yanyong Zhang"}]}},"tmdate":1789091301888,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tits.2023.3295757"],"tcdate":1772440770974,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yu_Zhang51"],"forum":"XXIcEb7E8Q","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":51749,"cdate":1690481993927,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091301888,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"XXIcEb7E8Q","version":2},{"content":{"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ed5f106b89b877831ff3bd8887e3e3965e4294c7.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"ren|trajmatch_toward_automatic_spatiotemporal_calibration_for_roadside_lidars_through_trajectory_matching"},"authorids":{"value":["~Haojie_Ren2","~Sha_Zhang1","~Yao_Li8","~Jianmin_Ji1","~Yanyong_Zhang2"]},"html":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/stamp/stamp.jsp?tp=&arnumber=10196359"},"abstract":{"value":"Recently, deploying sensors such as LiDARs on the\nroadside to monitor the passing traffic and assist autonomous\nvehicle perception has become popular. However, unlike\nautonomous vehicle systems, roadside sensor systems involve\nsensors from different subsystems, resulting in a lack of synchronization in both time and space between the sensors. Calibration\nis a critical technology that enables the central server to fuse\ndata generated by different location infrastructures, which vastly\nimproves sensing range and detection robustness. Regrettably,\nexisting calibration algorithms frequently assume that LiDARs\nhave significant overlap or that temporal calibration has already\nbeen achieved. However, since these assumptions do not always\nhold in real-world scenarios, the calibration results obtained from\nexisting algorithms are frequently unsatisfactory. In this paper,\nwe propose TrajMatch - the first system that can automatically\ncalibrate roadside LiDARs in both time and space. The main\nidea is to automatically calibrate the sensors based on the result\nof the detection/tracking task, rather than relying on extracting\nspecial features. Furthermore, we propose a novel mechanism for\nevaluating calibration parameters that align with our algorithm,\nand we demonstrate its effectiveness through experiments. This\nmechanism can also guide parameter iterations for multiple\ncalibrations, further enhancing the accuracy and efficiency of\nour calibration method. Finally, to evaluate the performance\nof TrajMatch , we collected two datasets, one simulated dataset\nLiDARnet-sim 1.0 and one real-world dataset. The experimental\nresults show that TrajMatch can achieve a spatial calibration\nerror of less than 10cm and a temporal calibration error of less\nthan 1.5ms."},"title":{"value":"TrajMatch: Toward Automatic Spatio-Temporal Calibration for Roadside LiDARs Through Trajectory Matching"},"authors":{"value":["Haojie Ren","Sha Zhang","Yao Li","Jianmin Ji","Yanyong Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1778208132445,"pdate":1683475200000,"tcdate":1778208132445,"writers":["~Haojie_Ren2","~Sha_Zhang1","~Yao_Li8","~Jianmin_Ji1","~Yanyong_Zhang2"],"signatures":["~Haojie_Ren2"],"forum":"AV3n3iepSi","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":50026,"cdate":1778208132445,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1778208132445,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"AV3n3iepSi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Intell. Transp. Syst. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6979/10304348/10196359.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TITS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"ren|trajmatch_toward_automatic_spatiotemporal_calibration_for_roadside_lidars_through_trajectory_matching"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haojie_Ren:","~Sha_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sugang_Li:","~Yao_Li8","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinchen_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianmin_Ji:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu_Zhang_0086:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yanyong_Zhang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TITS.2023.3295757"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tits/RenZLLLJZZ23,\n  author={Haojie Ren and Sha Zhang and Sugang Li and Yao Li and Xinchen Li and Jianmin Ji and Yu Zhang and Yanyong Zhang},\n  title={TrajMatch: Toward Automatic Spatio-Temporal Calibration for Roadside LiDARs Through Trajectory Matching},\n  year={2023},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1698796800000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Intell. Transp. Syst.},\n  volume={24},\n  number={11},\n  pages={12549-12559},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TITS.2023.3295757}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recently, deploying sensors such as LiDARs on the roadside to monitor the passing traffic and assist autonomous vehicle perception has become popular. However, unlike autonomous vehicle systems, roadside sensor systems involve sensors from different subsystems, resulting in a lack of synchronization in both time and space between the sensors. Calibration is a critical technology that enables the central server to fuse data generated by different location infrastructures, which vastly improves sensing range and detection robustness. Regrettably, existing calibration algorithms frequently assume that LiDARs have significant overlap or that temporal calibration has already been achieved. However, since these assumptions do not always hold in real-world scenarios, the calibration results obtained from existing algorithms are frequently unsatisfactory. In this paper, we propose TrajMatch - the first system that can automatically calibrate roadside LiDARs in both time and space. The main idea is to automatically calibrate the sensors based on the result of the detection/tracking task, rather than relying on extracting special features. Furthermore, we propose a novel mechanism for evaluating calibration parameters that align with our algorithm, and we demonstrate its effectiveness through experiments. This mechanism can also guide parameter iterations for multiple calibrations, further enhancing the accuracy and efficiency of our calibration method. Finally, to evaluate the performance of TrajMatch, we collected two datasets, one simulated dataset LiDARnet-sim 1.0 and one real-world dataset. The experimental results show that TrajMatch can achieve a spatial calibration error of less than $10cm$ and a temporal calibration error of less than $1.5ms$ ."},"title":{"value":"TrajMatch: Toward Automatic Spatio-Temporal Calibration for Roadside LiDARs Through Trajectory Matching"},"authors":{"value":["Haojie Ren","Sha Zhang","Sugang Li","Yao Li","Xinchen Li","Jianmin Ji","Yu Zhang","Yanyong Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1731461923982,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1715133222432,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yao_Li8"],"forum":"eEc8BA8Iu7","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":7421,"cdate":1698796800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1731461923982,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"eEc8BA8Iu7","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"The paper proposes a novel train-time calibration method which is based on multiclass alignment of confidences with the gradually softened target occurrences."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2024 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Model calibration","network calibration","calibration"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/0b225a92a4f4d38fcf4ecc5f3823f9eccc5f6cc8.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"general machine learning (i.e., none of the above)"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"In spite of delivering remarkable predictive accuracy across many domains, including computer vision and medical imaging, Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) are susceptible to making overconfident predictions. This could potentially limit their utilization and adoption in many real-world applications, especially involving security-sensitive decision making. Among existing approaches to model calibration, post-hoc based techniques are simple and effective, however, they require a separate hold-out data. Lately, train-time calibration has emerged as an alternate paradigm, in which the recent methods have shown state-of-the-art calibration results. Inspired by the train-time calibration direction, in this paper, we propose a novel train-time calibration method at the core of which is an auxiliary loss formulation, namely multiclass alignment of confidences with the gradually softened ground truth occurrences (MACSO). It is developed on the intuition that, for a class, the gradually softened ground truth occurrences distribution is a suitable non-zero entropy signal whose better alignment with the predicted confidences distribution is positively correlated with reducing the model calibration error. In our train-time approach, besides simply aligning the two distributions, e.g., via their means or KL divergence, we propose to quantify the linear correlation between the two distributions which preserves the relations among them, thereby further improving the calibration performance. Extensive results on several challenging datasets, featuring in and out-of-domain scenarios, class imbalanced problem, and a medical image classification task, validate the efficacy of our method against state-of-the-art train-time calibration methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nkugathasan2024multiclass,\ntitle={Multiclass Alignment of Confidences and Softened Target Occurrences for Train-time Calibration},\nauthor={Vinith Kugathasan and Honglu Zhou and Zachary Izzo and Gayal Kuruppu and Sejong Yoon and Muhammad Haris Khan},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=UZmKxjvsyo}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Multiclass Alignment of Confidences and Softened Target Occurrences for Train-time Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/72b13e4e5f1986ec537bfa18ad8bb386bd66868e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"kugathasan|multiclass_alignment_of_confidences_and_softened_target_occurrences_for_traintime_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Vinith_Kugathasan1","~Honglu_Zhou1","~Zachary_Izzo1","~Gayal_Kuruppu1","~Sejong_Yoon2","~Muhammad_Haris_Khan3"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Vinith Kugathasan","Honglu Zhou","Zachary Izzo","Gayal Kuruppu","Sejong Yoon","Muhammad Haris Khan"]}},"tmdate":1711385485276,"tcdate":1695346394345,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission4370/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission4370/Authors"],"forum":"UZmKxjvsyo","number":4370,"cdate":1695346394345,"mdate":1711385485276,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission4370/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"UZmKxjvsyo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Multim. Tools Appl. 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11042-018-7105-z.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|a_novel_monocular_calibration_method_for_underwater_vision_measurement"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11042-018-7105-z"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/mta/ChenWJZFW19,\n  author={Zhe Chen and Ruili Wang and Wanting Ji and Ming Zong and Tanghuai Fan and Huibin Wang},\n  title={A novel monocular calibration method for underwater vision measurement},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={Multim. Tools Appl.},\n  volume={78},\n  number={14},\n  pages={19437-19455},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s11042-018-7105-z}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Vision measurement systems have a reliable performance on ground, but it remains a challenge to apply commonly-used vision measurement systems (i.e. multi-camera systems and laser systems) in underwater environments. One of the most challenging issues is the transformation from an unscaled measurement to a scaled result, which is achieved by a calibration method and determinate the strategy used for underwater vision measurement. This paper proposes a novel monocular underwater calibration method underlying a simple underwater vision measurement system. Underwater unscaled measurement results are calculated by the dark channel prior model. These results are then processed by our calibration method, transforming the unscaled measurements to accurately scaled results. These measurement results finally are used to estimate the scaled 3D structure of underwater objects. Experimental results under natural open water show that our proposed method is reliable and efficient."},"title":{"value":"A novel monocular calibration method for underwater vision measurement"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhe Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Ruili Wang","username":"~Ruili_Wang1"},{"fullname":"Wanting Ji","username":"~Wanting_Ji1"},{"fullname":"Ming Zong","username":""},{"fullname":"Tanghuai Fan","username":""},{"fullname":"Huibin Wang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1791770521848,"pdate":1577750400000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/mta/ChenWJZFW19"],"tcdate":1786508729540,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Ruili_Wang1"],"forum":"syXiv2TXwt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":130918,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1791770521848,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"syXiv2TXwt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Auton. Robots 1999"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1023/A:1008891612854.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"garcia|fully_visionbased_calibration_of_a_handeye_robot"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1008891612854"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/arobots/Garcia99,\n  author={Christophe Garcia},\n  title={Fully Vision-based Calibration of a Hand-Eye Robot},\n  year={1999},\n  cdate={915148800000},\n  journal={Auton. Robots},\n  volume={6},\n  number={2},\n  pages={223-238},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1008891612854}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This article is concerned with calibrating an anthropomorphic two-armed robot equipped with a stereo-camera vision system, that is estimating the different geometric relationships involved in the model of the robot. The calibration procedure that is presented is fully vision-based: the relationships between each camera and the neck and between each arm and the neck are determined using visual measurements. The online calculation of all the relationships involved in the model of the robot is obtained with satisfactory precision and, above all, without expensive calibration mechanisms. For this purpose, two new main algorithms have been developed. The first one implements a non-linear optimization method using quaternions for camera calibration from 2D to 3D point or line correspondences. The second one implements a real-time camera pose estimation method based on the iterative use of a paraperspective camera model."},"title":{"value":"Fully Vision-based Calibration of a Hand-Eye Robot"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Christophe Garcia","username":"~Christophe_Garcia3"}]}},"tmdate":1787692802659,"pdate":946598400000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/arobots/Garcia99"],"tcdate":1787692769083,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Christophe_Garcia3"],"forum":"xfGm6gIf7c","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":142479,"cdate":915148800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1787692802659,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"xfGm6gIf7c","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"31st European Signal Processing Conference, EUSIPCO 2023 - Proceedings"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"larsson|efficient_timeofarrival_selfcalibration_using_source_implicitization"},"html":{"value":"https://portal.research.lu.se/en/publications/5ca8d583-a071-4a1c-910c-a949185e2f0b"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper we revisit the Time-of-Arrival self-calibration problem. In particular we focus on imbalanced problem instances where there are significantly more sources compared to the number of receivers, which is a common configuration in real applications. Using an implicit representation, we are able to re-parameterize the sensor node self-calibration problem using only the parameters of the receiver positions. Making the source positions implicit, we show that it is possible to linearize the maximum-likelihood error around the measured distances, resulting in a Sampson-like approximation. Given four unknown receiver positions and a large number of unknown sender positions, we show that our formulation leads to algorithms for robust calibration, with significant speed-up compared to running the full optimization over all unknowns. The proposed method is tested on both synthetic and real data."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{5ca8d583a0714a1c910ca949185e2f0b,\n  title     = \"Efficient Time-of-Arrival Self-Calibration using Source Implicitization\",\n  abstract  = \"In this paper we revisit the Time-of-Arrival self-calibration problem. In particular we focus on imbalanced problem instances where there are significantly more sources compared to the number of receivers, which is a common configuration in real applications. Using an implicit representation, we are able to re-parameterize the sensor node self-calibration problem using only the parameters of the receiver positions. Making the source positions implicit, we show that it is possible to linearize the maximum-likelihood error around the measured distances, resulting in a Sampson-like approximation. Given four unknown receiver positions and a large number of unknown sender positions, we show that our formulation leads to algorithms for robust calibration, with significant speed-up compared to running the full optimization over all unknowns. The proposed method is tested on both synthetic and real data.\",\n  keywords  = \"robust optimization, Sensor node calibration, Time-of-Arrival\",\n  author    = \"Malte Larsson and Viktor Larsson and Magnus Oskarsson\",\n  year      = \"2023\",\n  doi       = \"10.23919/EUSIPCO58844.2023.10289933\",\n  language  = \"English\",\n  series    = \"European Signal Processing Conference\",\n  publisher = \"European Signal Processing Conference, EUSIPCO\",\n  pages     = \"1644--1648\",\n  booktitle = \"31st European Signal Processing Conference, EUSIPCO 2023 - Proceedings\",\n  address   = \"Belgium\",\n  note      = \"31st European Signal Processing Conference, EUSIPCO 2023 ; Conference date: 04-09-2023 Through 08-09-2023\",\n}"},"title":{"value":"Efficient Time-of-Arrival Self-Calibration using Source Implicitization"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Malte Larsson","username":"https://orcid.org/0009-0008-0904-6001"},{"fullname":"Viktor Larsson","username":"~Viktor_Larsson1"},{"fullname":"Magnus Oskarsson","username":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1789-8094"}]}},"tmdate":1786525736034,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.23919/eusipco58844.2023.10289933"],"tcdate":1786525735437,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Viktor_Larsson3"],"forum":"G2Pl02t3SD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":97604,"cdate":1782523453821,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1786525736034,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"G2Pl02t3SD","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Beyond the well-known benefits on improving generalization, SAM is also found to be surprisingly effective in model calibration."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["deep neural networks","stochastic gradient descent","sharpness-aware minimization"]},"primary_area":{"value":"optimization"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep neural networks have been increasingly used in safety-critical applications such as medical diagnosis and autonomous driving. However, many studies suggest that they are prone to being poorly calibrated and have a propensity for overconfidence, which may have disastrous consequences. In this paper, unlike standard training such as stochastic gradient descent, we show that the recently proposed sharpness-aware minimization (SAM) counteracts this tendency towards overconfidence. The theoretical analysis suggests that SAM allows us to learn models that are already well-calibrated by implicitly maximizing the entropy of the predictive distribution. Inspired by this finding, we further propose a variant of SAM, coined as CSAM, to ameliorate model calibration. Extensive experiments on various datasets, including ImageNet-1K, demonstrate the benefits of SAM in reducing calibration error. Meanwhile, CSAM performs even better than SAM and consistently achieves lower calibration error than other approaches."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\ntan2026towards,\ntitle={Towards Understanding The Calibration Benefits of Sharpness-Aware Minimization},\nauthor={Chengli Tan and Yubo Zhou and Haishan Ye and Guang Dai and Junmin Liu and Zengjie Song and Jiangshe Zhang and Zixiang Zhao and Yunda Hao and Yong Xu},\nbooktitle={The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=c0ERcCz6lD}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Towards Understanding The Calibration Benefits of Sharpness-Aware Minimization"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3c8541be8a5d29da1acda5f42b2c3272673eab58.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"tan|towards_understanding_the_calibration_benefits_of_sharpnessaware_minimization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chengli_Tan1","~Yubo_Zhou4","~Haishan_Ye2","~Guang_Dai1","~Junmin_Liu1","~Zengjie_Song1","~Jiangshe_Zhang1","~Zixiang_Zhao1","~Yunda_Hao1","~Yong_Xu19"]},"authors":{"value":["Chengli Tan","Yubo Zhou","Haishan Ye","Guang Dai","Junmin Liu","Zengjie Song","Jiangshe Zhang","Zixiang Zhao","Yunda Hao","Yong Xu"]}},"tmdate":1775877196029,"pdate":1769436228454,"tcdate":1758244793280,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14862/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14862/Authors"],"forum":"c0ERcCz6lD","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":14862,"cdate":1758244793280,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14862/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14862/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14862/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1775877196029,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"c0ERcCz6lD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ETRA 2019"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ETRA/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"abdrabou|calibrationfree_text_entry_using_smooth_pursuit_eye_movements"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yasmeen_Abdrabou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mariam_Mostafa:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohamed_Khamis:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Amr_El_Mougy:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3314111.3319838"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/etra/AbdrabouMKE19,\n  author={Yasmeen Abdrabou and Mariam Mostafa and Mohamed Khamis and Amr El Mougy},\n  title={Calibration-free text entry using smooth pursuit eye movements},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  pages={35:1-35:5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3314111.3319838},\n  booktitle={ETRA},\n  crossref={conf/etra/2019}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we propose a calibration-free gaze-based text entry system that uses smooth pursuit eye movements. We report on our implementation, which improves over prior work on smooth pursuit text entry by 1) eliminating the need of calibration using motion correlation, 2) increasing input rate from 3.34 to 3.41 words per minute, 3) featuring text suggestions that were trained on 10,000 lexicon sentences recommended in the literature. We report on a user study (N=26) which shows that users are able to eye type at 3.41 words per minutes without calibration and without user training. Qualitative feedback also indicates that users positively perceive the system. Our work is of particular benefit for disabled users and for situations when voice and tactile input are not feasible (e.g., in noisy environments or when the hands are occupied)."},"title":{"value":"Calibration-free text entry using smooth pursuit eye movements"},"authors":{"value":["Yasmeen Abdrabou","Mariam Mostafa","Mohamed Khamis","Amr El Mougy"]}},"tmdate":1758268254947,"pdate":1546300800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/etra/AbdrabouMKE19"],"tcdate":1758268227731,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yasmeen_Abdrabou1"],"forum":"oH91KBi5a3","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":625082,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1758268254947,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"oH91KBi5a3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Big Data 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10824975/10824942/10825694.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/BIGDATACONF/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"kang|overcoming_data_imbalance_in_federated_learning_with_calibration_weighting"},"authorids":{"value":["~Nathaniel_Kang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yugyeong_Lim:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jongho_Im:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/BigData62323.2024.10825694"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/bigdataconf/KangLI24,\n  author={Nathaniel Kang and Yugyeong Lim and Jongho Im},\n  title={Overcoming Data Imbalance in Federated Learning with Calibration Weighting},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={7832-7841},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/BigData62323.2024.10825694},\n  booktitle={IEEE Big Data},\n  crossref={conf/bigdataconf/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Federated Learning (FL) is an essential technique for enabling machine learning on decentralized data while preserving privacy. A major challenge in FL is data imbalance, where skewed class distributions across clients lead to biased global models and reduced performance. This paper presents Federated Learning with Feature Calibration and Client Resampling (FL-FCR), an approach tailored to address this challenge. FL-FCR integrates a calibrated loss function during client training and employs calibration-based resampling at the server level, effectively aligning model training with the true data distributions. Extensive experiments on various datasets, including MNIST, FMNIST, CIFAR-10, and CIFAR-100, demonstrate that FL-FCR consistently outperforms existing FL methods in terms of accuracy, particularly in highly skewed data scenarios. These results highlight the potential of calibration techniques in FL, providing a robust solution for effective distributed machine learning."},"title":{"value":"Overcoming Data Imbalance in Federated Learning with Calibration Weighting"},"authors":{"value":["Nathaniel Kang","Yugyeong Lim","Jongho Im"]}},"tmdate":1747291231581,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747291198025,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Nathaniel_Kang1"],"forum":"CbkKUGjWAN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":474480,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747291231581,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"CbkKUGjWAN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EUSIPCO 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10289698/10289713/10289933.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EUSIPCO/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"larsson|efficient_timeofarrival_selfcalibration_using_source_implicitization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Malte_Larsson1","~Viktor_Larsson1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Magnus_Oskarsson:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.23919/EUSIPCO58844.2023.10289933"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/eusipco/LarssonLO23,\n  author={Malte Larsson and Viktor Larsson and Magnus Oskarsson},\n  title={Efficient Time-of-Arrival Self-Calibration using Source Implicitization},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1644-1648},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.23919/EUSIPCO58844.2023.10289933},\n  booktitle={EUSIPCO},\n  crossref={conf/eusipco/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper we revisit the Time-of-Arrival self-calibration problem. In particular we focus on imbalanced problem instances where there are significantly more sources compared to the number of receivers, which is a common configuration in real applications. Using an implicit representation, we are able to re-parameterize the sensor node self-calibration problem using only the parameters of the receiver positions. Making the source positions implicit, we show that it is possible to linearize the maximum-likelihood error around the measured distances, resulting in a Sampson-like approximation. Given four unknown receiver positions and a large number of unknown sender positions, we show that our formulation leads to algorithms for robust calibration, with significant speed-up compared to running the full optimization over all unknowns. The proposed method is tested on both synthetic and real data."},"title":{"value":"Efficient Time-of-Arrival Self-Calibration using Source Implicitization"},"authors":{"value":["Malte Larsson","Viktor Larsson","Magnus Oskarsson"]}},"tmdate":1741165150269,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1737100140577,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Malte_Larsson1"],"forum":"nHCefyMrZz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":266854,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1741165150269,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"nHCefyMrZz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CCCV (3) 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-981-10-7305-2_34.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CCCV/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"duan|imaging_model_and_calibration_of_lytro_light_field_camera"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Huixian_Duan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Song:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Na_Liu_0013:","~Lin_Mei2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-10-7305-2_34"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cccv/DuanWSLM17,\n  author={Huixian Duan and Jun Wang and Lei Song and Na Liu and Lin Mei},\n  title={Imaging Model and Calibration of Lytro Light Field Camera},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  pages={389-400},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-10-7305-2_34},\n  booktitle={CCCV (3)},\n  crossref={conf/cccv/2017-3}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Light field cameras with microlens array are being paid more and more attention, due to their light gathering and post-capture processing. Calibrating light field cameras is still an open and challenging problem. In this paper, we present a imaging model and a calibration method for Lytro light field camera. Firstly, based on the image formation for pinhole camera and the theory of light propagation, we model the imaging process of Lytro light field, and drive a homogenous intrinsic matrix, which represents the relationship between each pixel on the image plane and its corresponding light field. Then, based on the derived intrinsic matrix, a calibration algorithm using the checkerboard grid for Lytro light field camera is proposed. At last, extensive experimental results have demonstrated the correctness of the imaging model of Lytro light field camera and the effectiveness of our calibration algorithm."},"title":{"value":"Imaging Model and Calibration of Lytro Light Field Camera"},"authors":{"value":["Huixian Duan","Jun Wang","Lei Song","Na Liu","Lin Mei"]}},"tmdate":1731487214701,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1731483718405,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lin_Mei2"],"forum":"rTT7DY1feR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":213650,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731487214701,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rTT7DY1feR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2302.09834v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|transductive_matrix_completion_with_calibration_for_multitask_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hengfang_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yasi_Zhang:","~Xiaojun_Mao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhonglei_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2302.09834"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2302-09834,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Hengfang Wang and Yasi Zhang and Xiaojun Mao and Zhonglei Wang},\n  title={Transductive Matrix Completion with Calibration for Multi-Task Learning},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2302.09834},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2302.09834}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-task learning has attracted much attention due to growing multi-purpose research with multiple related data sources. Moreover, transduction with matrix completion is a useful method in multi-label learning. In this paper, we propose a transductive matrix completion algorithm that incorporates a calibration constraint for the features under the multi-task learning framework. The proposed algorithm recovers the incomplete feature matrix and target matrix simultaneously. Fortunately, the calibration information improves the completion results. In particular, we provide a statistical guarantee for the proposed algorithm, and the theoretical improvement induced by calibration information is also studied. Moreover, the proposed algorithm enjoys a sub-linear convergence rate. Several synthetic data experiments are conducted, which show the proposed algorithm out-performs other existing methods, especially when the target matrix is associated with the feature matrix in a nonlinear way."},"title":{"value":"Transductive Matrix Completion with Calibration for Multi-Task Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Hengfang Wang","Yasi Zhang","Xiaojun Mao","Zhonglei Wang"]}},"tmdate":1716718275881,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1716718267676,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mao_Xiaojun1"],"forum":"J9DFk8q786","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":22528,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1716718275881,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"J9DFk8q786","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICASSP 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10094559/10094560/10096691.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICASSP/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|transductive_matrix_completion_with_calibration_for_multitask_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hengfang_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yasi_Zhang:","~Xiaojun_Mao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhonglei_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP49357.2023.10096691"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icassp/WangZMW23,\n  author={Hengfang Wang and Yasi Zhang and Xiaojun Mao and Zhonglei Wang},\n  title={Transductive Matrix Completion with Calibration for Multi-Task Learning},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1-5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP49357.2023.10096691},\n  booktitle={ICASSP},\n  crossref={conf/icassp/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-task learning has attracted much attention due to growing multi-purpose research with multiple related data sources. More- over, transduction with matrix completion is a useful method in multi-label learning. In this paper, we propose a transductive matrix completion algorithm that incorporates a calibration constraint for the features under the multi-task learning framework. The proposed algorithm recovers the incomplete feature matrix and target matrix simultaneously. Fortunately, the calibration information improves the completion results. In particular, we provide a statistical guarantee for the proposed algorithm, and the theoretical improvement induced by calibration information is also studied. Moreover, the proposed algorithm enjoys a sub-linear convergence rate. Several synthetic data experiments are conducted, which show the proposed algorithm out-performs other methods, especially when the target matrix is associated with the feature matrix in a nonlinear way."},"title":{"value":"Transductive Matrix Completion with Calibration for Multi-Task Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Hengfang Wang","Yasi Zhang","Xiaojun Mao","Zhonglei Wang"]}},"tmdate":1716718275583,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1716718267657,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mao_Xiaojun1"],"forum":"CHwJ9US5ZG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":22527,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1716718275583,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"CHwJ9US5ZG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"APSEC (2) 2013"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6734090/6754325/06754347.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/APSEC/2013"},"paperhash":{"value":"yamamoto|seamless_code_reuse_with_source_code_corpus"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tetsuo_Yamamoto:","~Norihiro_Yoshida1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yoshiki_Higo:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/APSEC.2013.108"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/apsec/YamamotoYH13,\n  author={Tetsuo Yamamoto and Norihiro Yoshida and Yoshiki Higo},\n  title={Seamless Code Reuse with Source Code Corpus},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  pages={31-36},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/APSEC.2013.108},\n  booktitle={APSEC (2)},\n  crossref={conf/apsec/2013}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code reuse is attracting much attention as a promising technique for efficient software development. However, code reuse itself requires human resources: for example, searching and opening source files including code fragments that users would like to reuse, or considering keywords in using code search systems. The present paper proposes a novel technique that hardly requires such reuse cost. In the proposed technique, what programmers have to do for obtaining reusable code is just inputting a trigger key for code reuse on their development environments. Also, this paper describes some applications on OSS with a prototype tool working on Eclipse."},"title":{"value":"Seamless Code Reuse with Source Code Corpus"},"authors":{"value":["Tetsuo Yamamoto","Norihiro Yoshida","Yoshiki Higo"]}},"tmdate":1753024481285,"pdate":1356998400000,"tcdate":1753024462651,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Norihiro_Yoshida1"],"forum":"PAO5D5x2R5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":577466,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753024481285,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"PAO5D5x2R5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["Supervised fine-tuning","Token weighting","Token Entropy","Relative rank","Learning dynamics"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/032b39a906b43e2039a54f3658b41d3c954f86b0.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nyu2026probabilityentropy,\ntitle={Probability-Entropy Calibration: An Elastic Indicator for Adaptive Fine-tuning},\nauthor={Wenhao Yu and Shaohang Wei and Jiahong Liu and Yifan Li and Minda Hu and Aiwei Liu and Hao Zhang and Irwin King},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=lYBn5XSfEv}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Probability-Entropy Calibration: An Elastic Indicator for Adaptive Fine-tuning"},"paperhash":{"value":"yu|probabilityentropy_calibration_an_elastic_indicator_for_adaptive_finetuning"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/9f214275a58a6c248beb3805ea410e06b8d15ee8.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"deep_learning->large_language_models"},"abstract":{"value":"Token-level reweighting is a simple yet effective mechanism for controlling supervised fine-tuning, but common indicators are largely one-dimensional: the ground-truth probability reflects downstream alignment, while token entropy reflects intrinsic uncertainty induced by the pre-training prior. Ignoring entropy can misidentify noisy or easily replaceable tokens as learning-critical, while ignoring probability fails to reflect target-specific alignment. RankTuner introduces a probability--entropy calibration signal, the **Relative Rank Indicator**, by comparing the rank of the ground-truth token with its expected rank under the predictiondiction distribution. The inverse indicator is used as a token-wise **Relative Scale** to reweight the fine-tuning objective, focusing updates on truly under-learned tokens without over-penalizing intrinsically uncertain positions. Experiments on multiple backbones show consistent improvements on mathematical reasoning benchmarks, transfer gains on out-of-distribution reasoning, and pre code generation performance over probability- or entropy-only reweighting baselines."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://github.com/LvAoAo/Ranktuner_VERL/"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3b09923f835a151baf19dc1c62c075af1d0e9b06.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"When a language model is trained to improve at math reasoning, it learns from many individual words and\n  symbols in each solution. But some parts of a solution matter much more than others: a wrong number or\n  equation step can break the answer, while a filler word or interchangeable phrase usually does not. Many\n  current fine-tuning methods struggle to tell these cases apart because they look only at how confident the\n  model is in the correct word, or only at how uncertain the model is overall.\n\n  RankTuner gives the model a better sense of what deserves attention. For each token, it compares the rank of\n   the correct answer token with the rank we would expect from the model’s own uncertainty. If the model makes\n   a surprising mistake in a clear situation, RankTuner strengthens the learning signal; if the token is\n  ambiguous or noisy, it weakens that signal. Experiments on multiple math reasoning benchmarks show that this\n   helps models learn more reliably than standard fine-tuning and previous token-weighting methods."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Wenhao_Yu5","~Shaohang_Wei1","~Jiahong_Liu1","~Yifan_Li12","~Minda_Hu1","~Aiwei_Liu1","~Hao_Zhang108","~Irwin_King1"]},"authors":{"value":["Wenhao Yu","Shaohang Wei","Jiahong Liu","Yifan Li","Minda Hu","Aiwei Liu","Hao Zhang","Irwin King"]}},"tmdate":1790067386892,"pdate":1777576593073,"tcdate":1769137317136,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19895/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19895/Authors"],"forum":"lYBn5XSfEv","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":19895,"cdate":1769137317136,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19895/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19895/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067386892,"odate":1782341977061,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"lYBn5XSfEv","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Under review for TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Mixed-precision quantization (MPQ) aims to identify optimal bit-widths for layers to quantize\na model. On the other hand, zero-shot post-training quantization (ZSQ) performs postquantization\nwithout training data by generating a synthetic calibration set. While it is\nintuitive that there exists inherent correlation between the quality of the generated calibration\ndataset and the bit allocation to the model’s layers, existing approaches generally optimize\nthese two problems separately. This paper proposes a novel method that alternately optimizes\nboth the calibration set and the bit-width of each layer in the context of zero-shot posttraining\nquantization. First, we propose a novel data optimization framework that preserves\nthe internal feature statistics encoded in the pretrained model while improving the diversity of\nreconstruction behaviors required for quantization. To achieve this, we construct an expanded\nstatistic-consistent distribution and distill its higher-order reconstruction-gradient structure\ninto a compact calibration set through a k-order Gram-gradient matching objective. Second,\nwe formulate mixed-precision bit allocation as a principled quadratic optimization problem\nderived from the quantization objective while remaining computationally efficient. These\nsteps are integrated into a single framework to alternately optimize both the calibration data\nand the bit allocation to the model’s layers. Experimental results on ImageNet benchmarks\ndemonstrate that the proposed method consistently outperforms existing zero-shot posttraining\nquantization methods for CNNs under comparable model size settings."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026calibbits,\ntitle={CalibBits: Zero-Shot Calibration and Mixed-Precision Quantization for {CNN}s},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=UfMP6FuCrG},\nnote={Under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CalibBits: Zero-Shot Calibration and Mixed-Precision Quantization for CNNs"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/4ded90c5a5df80786078216b9fa64c35f45d0e19.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Under_Review"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Sinno_Pan1"}},"tmdate":1794279276018,"tcdate":1793219389957,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper13840/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper13840/Authors"],"forum":"UfMP6FuCrG","number":13840,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1793219389957,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review"],"mdate":1794279276018,"odate":1794279274659,"domain":"TMLR","id":"UfMP6FuCrG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.19705v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"sun|cafeq_calibrationfree_quantization_via_learned_transformations_and_adaptive_rounding"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.19705"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2511-19705,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ziteng Sun and Adrian Benton and Samuel Kushnir and Asher Trockman and Vikas Singh and Suhas N. Diggavi and Ananda Theertha Suresh},\n  title={CafeQ: Calibration-free Quantization via Learned Transformations and Adaptive Rounding},\n  year={2025},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1761955200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2511.19705},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.19705}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Post-training quantization is an effective method for reducing the serving cost of large language models, where the standard approach is to use a round-to-nearest quantization level scheme. However, this often introduces large errors due to outliers in the weights. Proposed mitigation mechanisms include applying adaptive rounding, random rotation transformations or committing to a post-training target using calibration data. Unfortunately, this reliance on calibration data can be severely limiting in some real-world scenarios as such data may be unavailable or subject to privacy regulations. In this paper, we propose algorithms to optimize transformations and adaptive rounding without access to any calibration data. The optimization is achieved by designing a suitable proxy function for the quantization loss without calibration data. To maintain inference efficiency, we perform structured matrix transformations for single matrices. For paired weights that interact directly in the computation graph, we use dual matrix transformations and adaptive rounding methods. We conduct experiments on Gemma 2 models, and observe consistent improvement over the baselines. For Gemma 2 9B quantization, our method improves the average benchmark score from 61.9 to 62.4 for 4-bit quantization and from 52.0 to 60.6 for 3-bit quantization, while adding less than 3% of computation overhead. Furthermore, our method achieves performance comparable to the commonly used GPTQ method, which requires calibration data."},"title":{"value":"CafeQ: Calibration-free Quantization via Learned Transformations and Adaptive Rounding"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Ziteng Sun","username":""},{"fullname":"Adrian Benton","username":"~Adrian_Benton1"},{"fullname":"Samuel Kushnir","username":""},{"fullname":"Asher Trockman","username":""},{"fullname":"Vikas Singh","username":""},{"fullname":"Suhas N. Diggavi","username":""},{"fullname":"Ananda Theertha Suresh","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1792163410445,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2511-19705"],"tcdate":1792163407636,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Adrian_Benton1"],"forum":"LqHnHLcVCl","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":194586,"cdate":1761955200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1792163410445,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"LqHnHLcVCl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"International Journal of Applied Earth Observation and Geoinformation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/5d0eb955c74be294ae05937fbe0a3a456484eae0.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|calibration_and_uncertainty_quantification_for_deep_learningbased_drought_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["~Mengxue_Zhang1","~Miguel-Ángel_Fernández-Torres1","~Kai-Hendrik_Cohrs1","~Gustau_Camps-Valls1"]},"html":{"value":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1569843225002109"},"abstract":{"value":"Droughts are hydrometeorological extreme events influenced by highly intricate land-atmosphere feedback mechanisms and climate variability. Deep learning models have recently succeeded in detecting extreme climate events and promise to uncover and understand droughts further. There are two main challenges of reliability and trustworthiness limiting their applications: miscalibration and inherent uncertainty. However, they remain rarely explored because deep learning models are overparameterized and seldom tractable. To address this shortcoming, we introduce methodologies for model calibration and entropy-based uncertainty quantification for deep learning-based drought detection. The calibration algorithm can deal with calibration errors by reducing distributional shifts and alleviating overconfident predictions. The uncertainty framework, in turn, decomposes and quantifies the total uncertainty according to several components: data uncertainty, procedural variability, parametric variability, and latent variability. Thus, our method identifies uncertain predictions and supports robust evaluations, benefiting the credibility of the decision-making process. Empirical evidence of performance in a wide range of European drought events is given, justifying the effectiveness of our approach. The calibration methodology yields the lowest expected calibration error (0.31\\%) and the precision of the uncertainty-based decision-making is improved from 72.27\\% to 74.06\\% and 76.59\\%, based on ensemble predictions and rejecting the predictions for the top 20\\% uncertain negative samples, respectively. In summary, our approach significantly enhances drought detection's reliability and classification accuracy, constituting a key step toward more trustworthy and actionable climate decision-making."},"title":{"value":"Calibration and uncertainty quantification for deep learning-based drought detection"},"authors":{"value":["Mengxue Zhang","Miguel-Ángel Fernández-Torres","Kai-Hendrik Cohrs","Gustau Camps-Valls"]}},"tmdate":1788161130319,"pdate":1748728800000,"tcdate":1788161130319,"writers":["~Mengxue_Zhang1","~Miguel-Ángel_Fernández-Torres1","~Kai-Hendrik_Cohrs1","~Gustau_Camps-Valls1"],"signatures":["~Kai-Hendrik_Cohrs1"],"forum":"RHWoWpCxv7","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":53567,"cdate":1788161130319,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1788161130319,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"RHWoWpCxv7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/10367905/10005563.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zou|robust_equipmentfree_calibration_of_lowcost_inertial_measurement_units"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zuhao_Zou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liang_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiangcheng_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yilong_Zhu:","~Bohuan_XUE1","~Jin_Wu5","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ming_Liu_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2023.3234081"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/ZouLHZXWL24,\n  author={Zuhao Zou and Liang Li and Xiangcheng Hu and Yilong Zhu and Bohuan XUE and Jin Wu and Ming Liu},\n  title={Robust Equipment-Free Calibration of Low-Cost Inertial Measurement Units},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={73},\n  pages={1-12},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2023.3234081}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Low-cost inertial measurement units (IMUs) are ubiquitously used in the attitude estimation of cell phones and robots. Accurate and robust IMU calibration is required to ensure attitude estimation accuracy. This article proposes an accurate and robust equipment-free IMU calibration method. We do not assume gyroscope biases are invariant during calibration compared with conventional methods because low-cost IMUs like MPU6000 have large gyroscope biases instability. We propose introducing biases removal and outlier-aware optimization to alleviate the impact of variant gyroscope biases. Furthermore, we introduce a multiresolution analysis (MRA)-based static detector to detect subtle IMU motion in real data collection. Our detector can detect 84% subtle motions (1° rotations) present in the simulated calibration data while the conventional variance thresholding detector can only detect 31% of them. In addition, we derive a proper data collection method to guide the user to collect data effectively. We benchmark our method with another existing equipment-free method with synthetic and real datasets. The results in synthetic datasets show that our method is 25% more accurate and robust than the existing method. The results in real datasets vouch that our method achieves an estimation of IMU intrinsic parameters comparable to the ground truth. Furthermore, the roll and pitch estimation of MPU6000 using our calibration method are close to ( $< 0.15$ ° in ±30°) that of an expensive factory-calibrated IMU in real testing."},"title":{"value":"Robust Equipment-Free Calibration of Low-Cost Inertial Measurement Units"},"authors":{"value":["Zuhao Zou","Liang Li","Xiangcheng Hu","Yilong Zhu","Bohuan XUE","Jin Wu","Ming Liu"]}},"tmdate":1786474923305,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tim/ZouLHZXWL24"],"tcdate":1767873375966,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bohuan_XUE1"],"forum":"DRNTpZqLcp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":734477,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1786474923305,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"DRNTpZqLcp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["large language model; post-training quantization"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Post-training quantization is an effective method for reducing the serving cost of large language models, and the standard approach is to use a round-to-nearest quantization level scheme. But this often suffers from large errors due to certain outliers in the weights. \nProposed mitigation mechanisms include applying adaptive rounding, random rotation transformations or committing to a post-training target using calibration data. Unfortunately, this reliance on calibration data can be severely limiting in many real-world scenarios as such data may be unavailable or subject to privacy regulations.  In this paper, we propose algorithms to optimize transformations and adaptive rounding without access to any calibration data. The optimization is achieved by designing a suitable proxy function for the quantization loss without calibration data. To maintain inference efficiency, we perform structured matrix transformations for single matrices. For paired weights that interact directly in the computation graph, we use dual matrix transformations and adaptive rounding methods. We conduct experiments on Gemma 2 models, and observe consistent improvement over the baselines. For Gemma 2 9B quantization, our method improves the average benchmark score from 61.9 to 62.4 for 4-bit quantization and from 52.0 to 60.6 for 3-bit quantization, while adding less than 3\\% of computation overhead. Furthermore, our method achieves performance comparable to the commonly used GPTQ method, which needs calibration data."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nsun2026cafeq,\ntitle={CafeQ: Calibration-free Quantization via Learned Transformations and Adaptive Rounding},\nauthor={Ziteng Sun and Adrian Benton and Samuel Kushnir and Asher Trockman and Vikas Singh and Suhas Diggavi and Ananda Theertha Suresh},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=r6A7bNvBjC}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CafeQ: Calibration-free Quantization via Learned Transformations and Adaptive Rounding"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/534c414672493c1a5a0373ef1bb0aaf6ef12f318.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"sun|cafeq_calibrationfree_quantization_via_learned_transformations_and_adaptive_rounding"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ziteng_Sun1","~Adrian_Benton1","~Samuel_Kushnir2","~Asher_Trockman1","~Vikas_Singh1","~Suhas_Diggavi1","~Ananda_Theertha_Suresh1"]},"authors":{"value":["Ziteng Sun","Adrian Benton","Samuel Kushnir","Asher Trockman","Vikas Singh","Suhas Diggavi","Ananda Theertha Suresh"]}},"tmdate":1767302244523,"tcdate":1758228661894,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14118/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14118/Authors"],"forum":"r6A7bNvBjC","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":14118,"cdate":1758228661894,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14118/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1767302244523,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"r6A7bNvBjC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ROBIO 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7062021/7090291/07090442.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ROBIO/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|fisheye_omnidirectional_camera_calibration_pinhole_or_spherical_model"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ke_Wang_0028:","~Lijun_Zhao3","~Ruifeng_Li2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBIO.2014.7090442"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/robio/WangZL14,\n  author={Ke Wang and Lijun Zhao and Ruifeng Li},\n  title={Fisheye omnidirectional camera calibration - Pinhole or spherical model?},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={873-877},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBIO.2014.7090442},\n  booktitle={ROBIO},\n  crossref={conf/robio/2014}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we investigate two camera models for fisheye camera, namely, the pinhole camera model and spherical projection model. The former one has been successfully applied in calibration modeling of monocular vision system. However, for fisheye camera calibration modeling, we demonstrate that the spherical projection model is more appreciate for nonlinear image system. Moreover, to deal with the high distortion introduced by the camera lens, we use local Hough Transform to detect sub-pixel corners in stead of some classical corner detectors, and two optimization methods are proposed to estimate the camera parameters. Experimental results validate the approach."},"title":{"value":"Fisheye omnidirectional camera calibration - Pinhole or spherical model?"},"authors":{"value":["Ke Wang","Lijun Zhao","Ruifeng Li"]}},"tmdate":1784539423501,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1741407996298,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~lijun_zhao2"],"forum":"gI8B8Ywc4C","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":366744,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784539423501,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"gI8B8Ywc4C","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"UAI 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v216/glaser23a/glaser23a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/UAI/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"glaser|fast_and_scalable_scorebased_kernel_calibration_tests"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pierre_Glaser:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:David_Widmann:","~Fredrik_Lindsten1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Arthur_Gretton:"]},"html":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v216/glaser23a.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/uai/GlaserWLG23,\n  author={Pierre Glaser and David Widmann and Fredrik Lindsten and Arthur Gretton},\n  title={Fast and scalable score-based kernel calibration tests},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={691-700},\n  url={https://proceedings.mlr.press/v216/glaser23a.html},\n  booktitle={UAI},\n  crossref={conf/uai/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We introduce the Kernel Calibration Conditional Stein Discrepancy test (KCCSD test), a nonparametric, kernel-based test for assessing the calibration of probabilistic models with well-defined scores. In contrast to previous methods, our test avoids the need for possibly expensive expectation approximations while providing control over its type-I error. We achieve these improvements by using a new family of kernels for score-based probabilities that can be estimated without probability density samples, and by using a Conditional Goodness of Fit criterion for the KCCSD test’s U-statistic. We demonstrate the properties of our test on various synthetic settings."},"title":{"value":"Fast and scalable score-based kernel calibration tests"},"authors":{"value":["Pierre Glaser","David Widmann","Fredrik Lindsten","Arthur Gretton"]}},"tmdate":1756904863870,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/uai/GlaserWLG23"],"tcdate":1756904840385,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fredrik_Lindsten1"],"forum":"Zcrug2GUQR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":620089,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1756904863870,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Zcrug2GUQR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CSNDSP 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6913114/6923783/06923932.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CSNDSP/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"fakhir|inferring_dimensions_from_an_image_using_automatic_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:M._M._Fakhir:","~Wai_Lok_Woo1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Satnam_Singh_Dlay:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CSNDSP.2014.6923932"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/csndsp/FakhirWD14,\n  author={M. M. Fakhir and Wai Lok Woo and Satnam Singh Dlay},\n  title={Inferring dimensions from an image using automatic calibration},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={776-780},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CSNDSP.2014.6923932},\n  booktitle={CSNDSP},\n  crossref={conf/csndsp/2014}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We propose a new technique to infer the dimensions of a real object using an image via the automatic calibration of a camera. This method utilises different geometric shapes to make comparisons for calibration and thus decrease the number of errors. Various square, rectangular and triangular shapes are used as the origin by which to calibrate the camera. Since the position from one of the geometrical shapes is known, thus measurement can be inferred for the objects or plane in with it lies. In this paper we provide several ways to calibrate the camera leading to a higher accuracy of measurement."},"title":{"value":"Inferring dimensions from an image using automatic calibration"},"authors":{"value":["M. M. Fakhir","Wai Lok Woo","Satnam Singh Dlay"]}},"tmdate":1753446144725,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1753446058729,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wai_Lok_Woo1"],"forum":"kIPOycBJiG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":588627,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753446144725,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"kIPOycBJiG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACM Trans. Sens. Networks 2006"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TOSN/2006"},"paperhash":{"value":"devarajan|distributed_metric_calibration_of_ad_hoc_camera_networks"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dhanya_Devarajan:","~Richard_J._Radke2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haeyong_Chung:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1167935.1167939"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tosn/DevarajanRC06,\n  author={Dhanya Devarajan and Richard J. Radke and Haeyong Chung},\n  title={Distributed metric calibration of ad hoc camera networks},\n  year={2006},\n  cdate={1136073600000},\n  journal={ACM Trans. Sens. Networks},\n  volume={2},\n  number={3},\n  pages={380-403},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/1167935.1167939}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We discuss how to automatically obtain the metric calibration of an ad hoc network of cameras with no centralized processor. We model the set of uncalibrated cameras as nodes in a communication network, and propose a distributed algorithm in which each camera performs a local, robust bundle adjustment over the camera parameters and scene points of its neighbors in an overlay “vision graph.” We analyze the performance of the algorithm on both simulated and real data, and show that the distributed algorithm results in a fairer allocation of messages per node while achieving comparable calibration accuracy to centralized bundle adjustment."},"title":{"value":"Distributed metric calibration of ad hoc camera networks"},"authors":{"value":["Dhanya Devarajan","Richard J. Radke","Haeyong Chung"]}},"tmdate":1741194419090,"pdate":1136073600000,"tcdate":1741194404794,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Richard_Radke1"],"forum":"ij13W7ltEo","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":354854,"cdate":1136073600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1741194419090,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ij13W7ltEo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.11167v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|heterogeneous_multisensor_calibration_based_on_graph_optimization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongyu_Chen:","~Sören_Schwertfeger1"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1905.11167"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1905-11167,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Hongyu Chen and Sören Schwertfeger},\n  title={Heterogeneous Multi-sensor Calibration based on Graph Optimization},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1905.11167},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1905.11167}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Many robotics and mapping systems contain multiple sensors to perceive the environment. Extrinsic parameter calibration, the identification of the position and rotation transform between the frames of the different sensors, is critical to fuse data from different sensors. When obtaining multiple camera to camera, lidar to camera and lidar to lidar calibration results, inconsistencies are likely. We propose a graph-based method to refine the relative poses of the different sensors. We demonstrate our approach using our mapping robot platform, which features twelve sensors that are to be calibrated. The experimental results confirm that the proposed algorithm yields great performance."},"title":{"value":"Heterogeneous Multi-sensor Calibration based on Graph Optimization"},"authors":{"value":["Hongyu Chen","Sören Schwertfeger"]}},"tmdate":1724431426112,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1724431402301,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sören_Schwertfeger1"],"forum":"zFWgeiTslY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":71968,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1724431426112,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zFWgeiTslY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"RCAR 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9034064/9043918/09044031.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/RCAR/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|heterogeneous_multisensor_calibration_based_on_graph_optimization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongyu_Chen:","~Sören_Schwertfeger1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/RCAR47638.2019.9044031"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/rcar/ChenS19,\n  author={Hongyu Chen and Sören Schwertfeger},\n  title={Heterogeneous Multi-sensor Calibration based on Graph Optimization},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  pages={158-163},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/RCAR47638.2019.9044031},\n  booktitle={RCAR},\n  crossref={conf/rcar/2019}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Many robotics and mapping systems contain multiple sensors to perceive the environment. Extrinsic parameter calibration, the identification of the position and rotation transform between the frames of the different sensors, is critical to fuse data from different sensors. When obtaining multiple camera to camera, lidar to camera and lidar to lidar calibration results, inconsistencies are likely. We propose a graph-based method to refine the relative poses of the different sensors. We demonstrate our approach using our mapping robot platform, which features twelve sensors that are to be calibrated. The experimental results confirm that the proposed algorithm yields great performance."},"title":{"value":"Heterogeneous Multi-sensor Calibration based on Graph Optimization"},"authors":{"value":["Hongyu Chen","Sören Schwertfeger"]}},"tmdate":1724431418108,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1724431402291,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sören_Schwertfeger1"],"forum":"zHS4bMnpH2","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":71962,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1724431418108,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zHS4bMnpH2","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ECCV (42) 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-032-37422-6_19.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"qin|unmaskingtime_visual_calibration_for_hallucination_mitigation_in_multimodal_discrete_diffusion_language_models"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-37422-6_19"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/eccv/QinCZW26,\n  author={Tian Qin and Junzhe Chen and Tianshu Zhang and Lijie Wen},\n  title={Unmasking-Time Visual Calibration for Hallucination Mitigation in Multimodal Discrete Diffusion Language Models},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={333-350},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-37422-6_19},\n  booktitle={ECCV (42)},\n  crossref={conf/eccv/2026-42}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite the recent success of multimodal discrete diffusion language models (multimodal dLLMs) in generating text through iterative demasking with bidirectional attention, object hallucination remains a critical challenge. Existing hallucination mitigation methods are designed for autoregressive generation and encounter fundamental mismatches when applied to multimodal dLLMs, either doubling inference cost through contrastive decoding or suffering from ambiguous step and position alignment under bidirectional context. To address this, we propose Unmasking-Time Visual Calibration (UVC), a lightweight, training-free framework that mitigates hallucinations natively within the demasking process. UVC extracts multi-granularity contrastive activation shift vectors offline by comparing model responses to clean and visually degraded inputs, identifies the most visually informative attention heads via per-head AUC scoring, and injects the pre-computed calibration signals exclusively at still-masked positions during inference, requiring no additional forward passes. Extensive experiments on POPE and MME with two representative multimodal dLLMs demonstrate that UVC consistently reduces hallucinations by a substantial margin while introducing less than one percent inference overhead. Additional evaluation on open-ended captioning with the CHAIR metric further confirms that UVC substantially lowers hallucination rates without affecting generation length, and generalizes effectively across benchmarks without retraining. Code is available at https://github.com/THU-BPM/UVC."},"title":{"value":"Unmasking-Time Visual Calibration for Hallucination Mitigation in Multimodal Discrete Diffusion Language Models"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Tian Qin","username":"~Tian_Qin13"},{"fullname":"Junzhe Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Tianshu Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Lijie Wen","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1793876828915,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/eccv/QinCZW26"],"tcdate":1793876815209,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Tian_Qin13"],"forum":"OvcTTzrJyi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":224033,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1793876828915,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"OvcTTzrJyi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9635848/9635849/09636258.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|underwater_visual_acoustic_slam_with_extrinsic_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shida_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tomasz_Luczynski:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jonatan_Scharff_Willners:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ziyang_Hong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaicheng_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yvan_R._Petillot:","~Sen_Wang7"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS51168.2021.9636258"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/XuLWHZPW21,\n  author={Shida Xu and Tomasz Luczynski and Jonatan Scharff Willners and Ziyang Hong and Kaicheng Zhang and Yvan R. Petillot and Sen Wang},\n  title={Underwater Visual Acoustic SLAM with Extrinsic Calibration},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={7647-7652},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS51168.2021.9636258},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Underwater scenarios are challenging for visual Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) due to limited visibility and intermittently losing structures in image views. In this paper, we propose a visual acoustic bundle adjustment system which fuses a camera and a Doppler Velocity Log (DVL) in a graph SLAM framework for reliable underwater localization and mapping. In order to fuse the vision with the acoustic measurements, an calibration algorithm is also designed to estimate extrinsic parameters between a camera and a DVL using features detected in scenes. Experimental results in a tank and an offshore wind farm show the proposed method can achieve better robustness and localization accuracy than pure visual SLAM, especially in visually challenging scenarios, and the extrinsic calibration parameters can be accurately estimated, even when initialized with a random guess."},"title":{"value":"Underwater Visual Acoustic SLAM with Extrinsic Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Shida Xu","Tomasz Luczynski","Jonatan Scharff Willners","Ziyang Hong","Kaicheng Zhang","Yvan R. Petillot","Sen Wang"]}},"tmdate":1765901641191,"pdate":1609459200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iros/XuLWHZPW21"],"tcdate":1765901620771,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sen_Wang7"],"forum":"aTR5jFBl2M","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":709592,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1765901641191,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"aTR5jFBl2M","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICPR 2018"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8527858/8545020/08546204.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICPR/2018"},"paperhash":{"value":"fasogbon|automatic_feature_extraction_for_wideangle_and_fisheye_camera_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peter_Fasogbon:","~Lixin_Fan1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPR.2018.8546204"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icpr/FasogbonF18a,\n  author={Peter Fasogbon and Lixin Fan},\n  title={Automatic Feature Extraction for Wide-angle and Fish-eye Camera Calibration},\n  year={2018},\n  cdate={1514764800000},\n  pages={2947-2952},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPR.2018.8546204},\n  booktitle={ICPR},\n  crossref={conf/icpr/2018}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The increase need to have 360 degree perception of world environment has led to the combination of various cameras in the varieties of narrow and wide-angle field of view. Current state of art methods do not provide an automatic means for fish-eye calibration, which is indispensable in an industrial environment where many lenses are to be calibrated in a relative short time. As automatic feature extraction is the key issue for fully automatic calibration framework, we address this issue to remove any form of human intervention during the calibration process. Unlike state-of-the-art methods, the proposed framework is completely automatic and not prone to detection errors. We evaluate the proposed feature extraction using state-of-the-art generic calibration model with both real and synthetic images."},"title":{"value":"Automatic Feature Extraction for Wide-angle and Fish-eye Camera Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Peter Fasogbon","Lixin Fan"]}},"tmdate":1731487717864,"pdate":1514764800000,"tcdate":1731484370328,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lixin_Fan1"],"forum":"2z7OyAAlCu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":214264,"cdate":1514764800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731487717864,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2z7OyAAlCu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Image Process. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/83/10346232/10555516.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"salah|ecalib_a_fast_robust_and_accurate_calibration_toolbox_for_event_cameras"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohammed_Salah:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abdulla_Ayyad:","~Muhammad_Humais1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daniel_Gehrig:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abdelqader_Abusafieh:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lakmal_D._Seneviratne:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Davide_Scaramuzza_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yahya_H._Zweiri:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2024.3410673"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tip/SalahAHGASSZ24,\n  author={Mohammed Salah and Abdulla Ayyad and Muhammad Humais and Daniel Gehrig and Abdelqader Abusafieh and Lakmal D. Seneviratne and Davide Scaramuzza and Yahya H. Zweiri},\n  title={E-Calib: A Fast, Robust, and Accurate Calibration Toolbox for Event Cameras},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Image Process.},\n  volume={33},\n  pages={3977-3990},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2024.3410673}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Event cameras triggered a paradigm shift in the computer vision community delineated by their asynchronous nature, low latency, and high dynamic range. Calibration of event cameras is always essential to account for the sensor intrinsic parameters and for 3D perception. However, conventional image-based calibration techniques are not applicable due to the asynchronous, binary output of the sensor. The current standard for calibrating event cameras relies on either blinking patterns or event-based image reconstruction algorithms. These approaches are difficult to deploy in factory settings and are affected by noise and artifacts degrading the calibration performance. To bridge these limitations, we present E-Calib, a novel, fast, robust, and accurate calibration toolbox for event cameras utilizing the asymmetric circle grid, for its robustness to out-of-focus scenes. E-Calib introduces an efficient reweighted least squares (eRWLS) method for feature extraction of the calibration pattern circles with sub-pixel accuracy and robustness to noise. In addition, a modified hierarchical clustering algorithm is devised to detect the calibration grid apart from the background clutter. The proposed method is tested in a variety of rigorous experiments for different event camera models, on circle grids with different geometric properties, on varying calibration trajectories and speeds, and under challenging illumination conditions. The results show that our approach outperforms the state-of-the-art in detection success rate, reprojection error, and pose estimation accuracy."},"title":{"value":"E-Calib: A Fast, Robust, and Accurate Calibration Toolbox for Event Cameras"},"authors":{"value":["Mohammed Salah","Abdulla Ayyad","Muhammad Humais","Daniel Gehrig","Abdelqader Abusafieh","Lakmal D. Seneviratne","Davide Scaramuzza","Yahya H. Zweiri"]}},"tmdate":1730739487584,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1730739481188,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Muhammad_Ahmed_Humais1"],"forum":"Vub8P045PO","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":170044,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730739487584,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Vub8P045PO","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Crossref"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhu|exploring_and_improving_code_completion_for_test_code"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3643916.3644421"},"abstract":{"value":"Code completion is an important feature in Integrated Development Environments (IDEs). These years, researchers have been making efforts for intelligent code completion. However, existing work on intelligent code completion either only considered production code, or did not distinguish between production code and test code. It is unclear how effective existing completion models are for test code completion, nor whether we can further improve it. In this work, we focus on the completion of test code. We first find through experiments that completion models for production code are suboptimal for test code completion. Then we analyze the specific characteristics of test code, and observe that test code has inter- and intra-project similarities, and a strong relationship with its focal class and other production classes depending on the focal class (i.e., focal-related code). By incorporating test code from other projects to fine-tune existing models, we leverage the inter-project similarity of test code to improve the completion of tokens specific to test code. By introducing a local component and constructing existing test code as well as the focal-related code in the project as references, we enhance existing code completion models with the intra-project similarity and the focal-related code of test code. Experiments show that each characteristic of test code we exploit can bring substantial improvement to test code completion and our integrated framework outperforms other baseline frameworks. Compared to the base completion model, on token-level completion, our optimal model for test code completion relatively improves all-token and identifier completion accuracy by 7.68% and 19.96%, respectively; on line-level completion, it relatively improves edit-distance similarity and exact-match metrics by 8.89% and 22.82%, respectively. Moreover, we perform error analysis and point out potential directions for future work."},"title":{"value":"Exploring and Improving Code Completion for Test Code"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Tingwei Zhu","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Tingwei%20Zhu"},{"fullname":"Zhongxin Liu","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Zhongxin%20Liu"},{"fullname":"Tongtong Xu","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Tongtong%20Xu"},{"fullname":"Ze Tang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Ze%20Tang"},{"fullname":"Tian Zhang","username":"~Tian_Zhang8"},{"fullname":"Minxue Pan","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Minxue%20Pan"},{"fullname":"Xin Xia","username":"~Xin_Xia11"}]}},"tmdate":1791773712712,"pdate":1713139200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1145/3643916.3644421"],"tcdate":1778200733524,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Tian_Zhang8"],"forum":"Jkcqththmm","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":69057,"cdate":1718285716145,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1791773712712,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Jkcqththmm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Cryosphere"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"verjans|bayesian_calibration_of_firn_densification_models"},"html":{"value":"https://research.lancaster-university.uk/en/publications/cb0cd044-5704-4054-869b-e43febb65c0d"},"abstract":{"value":"Firn densification modelling is key to understanding ice sheet mass balance, ice sheet surface elevation change, and the age difference between ice and the air in enclosed air bubbles. This has resulted in the development of many firn models, all relying to a certain degree on parameter calibration against observed data. We present a novel Bayesian calibration method for these parameters and apply it to three existing firn models. Using an extensive dataset of firn cores from Greenland and Antarctica, we reach optimal parameter estimates applicable to both ice sheets. We then use these to simulate firn density and evaluate against independent observations. Our simulations show a significant decrease (24 % and 56 %) in observation–model discrepancy for two models and a smaller increase (15 %) for the third. As opposed to current methods, the Bayesian framework allows for robust uncertainty analysis related to parameter values. Based on our results, we review some inherent model assumptions and demonstrate how firn model choice and uncertainties in parameter values cause spread in key model outputs."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{cb0cd04457044054869be43febb65c0d,\n  title     = \"Bayesian calibration of firn densification models\",\n  abstract  = \"Firn densification modelling is key to understanding ice sheet mass balance, ice sheet surface elevation change, and the age difference between ice and the air in enclosed air bubbles. This has resulted in the development of many firn models, all relying to a certain degree on parameter calibration against observed data. We present a novel Bayesian calibration method for these parameters and apply it to three existing firn models. Using an extensive dataset of firn cores from Greenland and Antarctica, we reach optimal parameter estimates applicable to both ice sheets. We then use these to simulate firn density and evaluate against independent observations. Our simulations show a significant decrease (24 \\% and 56 \\%) in observation–model discrepancy for two models and a smaller increase (15 \\%) for the third. As opposed to current methods, the Bayesian framework allows for robust uncertainty analysis related to parameter values. Based on our results, we review some inherent model assumptions and demonstrate how firn model choice and uncertainties in parameter values cause spread in key model outputs.\",\n  author    = \"Vincent Verjans and Amber Leeson and Christopher Nemeth and Stevens, \\{C. Max\\} and Munneke, \\{Peter Kuipers\\} and Brice No{\\\"e}l and Wessem, \\{Jan Melchior van\\}\",\n  year      = \"2020\",\n  month     = sep,\n  day       = \"15\",\n  doi       = \"10.5194/tc-14-3017-2020\",\n  language  = \"English\",\n  volume    = \"14\",\n  pages     = \"3017--3032\",\n  journal   = \"Cryosphere\",\n  issn      = \"1994-0416\",\n  publisher = \"Copernicus Publications\",\n}"},"title":{"value":"Bayesian calibration of firn densification models"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Vincent Verjans","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Vincent%20Verjans"},{"fullname":"Amber Leeson","username":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8720-9808"},{"fullname":"Christopher Nemeth","username":"~Christopher_Nemeth1"},{"fullname":"C. Max Stevens","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=C.%20Max%20Stevens"},{"fullname":"Peter Kuipers Munneke","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Peter%20Kuipers%20Munneke"},{"fullname":"Brice Noël","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Brice%20No%C3%ABl"},{"fullname":"Jan Melchior van Wessem","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Jan%20Melchior%20van%20Wessem"}]}},"tmdate":1794055547280,"pdate":1600128000000,"externalIds":["doi:10.5194/tc-14-3017-2020"],"tcdate":1794055547064,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Christopher_Nemeth1"],"forum":"Q91kt36qG7","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":146881,"cdate":1606183797192,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1794055547280,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Q91kt36qG7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Neural SDEs","Functional Calibration","Time-series"]},"primary_area":{"value":"learning on time series and dynamical systems"},"abstract":{"value":"Neural Stochastic Differential Equations (Neural SDEs) provide flexible\ncontinuous-time generative models, but generic neural drift and diffusion\nnetworks are costly to simulate on long horizons and can give unstable\ngradients when the training signal is a path functional rather than a\npointwise observation. We introduce SLiSDE, a family of Neural SDE\nmodels built from structured linear stochastic layers. Parallel-in-time\nsimulation is obtained at the layer level, while expressivity is recovered\nby gated in-flow stacking: previous-layer paths modulate the next\nlayer's latent flow through learned gates. For\nfunctional calibration tasks in which rare paths dominate the loss, we add\nan optional Girsanov tilt that acts as a learned importance\nsampler with an exact likelihood-ratio correction. We prove\nwell-posedness, a discretisation error bound, validity of the change of\nmeasure, and a universality result: the terminal laws of the gated stack\nare dense in the space of square-integrable laws. Experiments on\nfunctional calibration benchmarks show that the structured model\noutperforms fully neural SDE baselines while retaining parallel-time\nsimulation and stable importance weights."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026structured,\ntitle={Structured Neural {SDE}s for Functional Calibration},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=MfCjnGNkqh},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Structured Neural SDEs for Functional Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/958df40a6cbec8ab211f7db950722251b0e42875.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791235548776,"tcdate":1789815509506,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission60566/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission60566/Authors"],"forum":"MfCjnGNkqh","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":60566,"cdate":1789815509506,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission60566/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791235548776,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"MfCjnGNkqh","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.19906v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"su|calidrop_kv_cache_compression_with_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Su_0006:","~Quantong_Qiu1","~Yuechi_Zhou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Juntao_Li_0005:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qingrong_Xia:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ping_Li_0016:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinyu_Duan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhe-Feng_Wang_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Min_Zhang_0005:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.19906"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2507-19906,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yi Su and Quantong Qiu and Yuechi Zhou and Juntao Li and Qingrong Xia and Ping Li and Xinyu Duan and Zhe-Feng Wang and Min Zhang},\n  title={CaliDrop: KV Cache Compression with Calibration},\n  year={2025},\n  month={July},\n  cdate={1751328000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2507.19906},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.19906}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) require substantial computational resources during generation. While the Key-Value (KV) cache significantly accelerates this process by storing attention intermediates, its memory footprint grows linearly with sequence length, batch size, and model size, creating a bottleneck in long-context scenarios. Various KV cache compression techniques, including token eviction, quantization, and low-rank projection, have been proposed to mitigate this bottleneck, often complementing each other. This paper focuses on enhancing token eviction strategies. Token eviction leverages the observation that the attention patterns are often sparse, allowing for the removal of less critical KV entries to save memory. However, this reduction usually comes at the cost of notable accuracy degradation, particularly under high compression ratios. To address this issue, we propose \\textbf{CaliDrop}, a novel strategy that enhances token eviction through calibration. Our preliminary experiments show that queries at nearby positions exhibit high similarity. Building on this observation, CaliDrop performs speculative calibration on the discarded tokens to mitigate the accuracy loss caused by token eviction. Extensive experiments demonstrate that CaliDrop significantly improves the accuracy of existing token eviction methods."},"title":{"value":"CaliDrop: KV Cache Compression with Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Yi Su","Quantong Qiu","Yuechi Zhou","Juntao Li","Qingrong Xia","Ping Li","Xinyu Duan","Zhe-Feng Wang","Min Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1787407274664,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2507-19906"],"tcdate":1767602404335,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Quantong_Qiu1"],"forum":"3bQECSnp5k","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":714854,"cdate":1751328000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1787407274664,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"3bQECSnp5k","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/ade7947a48c624f423b2790ed68af16cdfc1254d.zip"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep learning models are often trained to approximate dynamical systems that can be modeled using differential equations. Many of these models are optimized to predict one step ahead; such approaches produce calibrated one-step predictions if the predictive model can quantify uncertainty, such as Deep Ensembles. At inference time, multi-step predictions are generated via autoregression, which needs a sound uncertainty propagation method to produce calibrated multi-step predictions. This work introduces an alternative Predictor-Corrector approach named HopCast that uses Modern Hopfield Networks (MHN) to learn the errors of a deterministic Predictor that approximates the dynamical system. The Corrector predicts a set of errors for the Predictor's output based on a context state at any timestep during autoregression. The set of errors creates sharper and well-calibrated prediction intervals with higher predictive accuracy compared to baselines without uncertainty propagation. The calibration and prediction performances are evaluated across a set of dynamical systems. This work is also the first to benchmark existing uncertainty propagation methods based on calibration errors."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nshahid2025hopcast,\ntitle={HopCast: Calibration of Autoregressive Dynamics Models},\nauthor={Muhammad Bilal Shahid and Cody Fleming},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=wsO6nxvGof},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"HopCast: Calibration of Autoregressive Dynamics Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ebc0e9a70c51dd7bb39bfdc6c82679963caa3d3f.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"shahid|hopcast_calibration_of_autoregressive_dynamics_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Muhammad_Bilal_Shahid1","~Cody_Fleming2"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Zheng_Wen1"},"authors":{"value":["Muhammad Bilal Shahid","Cody Fleming"]}},"tmdate":1766928430489,"pdate":1766928430437,"tcdate":1755811750861,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper5698/Authors"],"forum":"wsO6nxvGof","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":5698,"cdate":1755811750861,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper5698/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper5698/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1766928430489,"odate":1756346094030,"domain":"TMLR","id":"wsO6nxvGof","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2024 poster"},"TLDR":{"value":"This paper formulates domain calibration as a cooperative MARL problem to improve efficiency and fidelity."},"keywords":{"value":["Multi-agent reinforcement learning","domain transfer"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/f7147abb1fcca64f57370a364827a07957e2179a.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"reinforcement_learning"},"abstract":{"value":"The shift in dynamics results in significant performance degradation of policies trained in the source domain when deployed in a different target domain, posing a challenge for the practical application of reinforcement learning (RL) in real-world scenarios. Domain transfer methods aim to bridge this dynamics gap through techniques such as domain adaptation or domain calibration. While domain adaptation involves refining the policy through extensive interactions in the target domain, it may not be feasible for sensitive fields like healthcare and autonomous driving. On the other hand, offline domain calibration utilizes only static data from the target domain to adjust the physics parameters of the source domain (e.g., a simulator) to align with the target dynamics, enabling the direct deployment of the trained policy without sacrificing performance, which emerges as the most promising for policy deployment. However, existing techniques primarily rely on evolution algorithms for calibration, resulting in low sample efficiency.\nTo tackle this issue, we propose a novel framework Madoc (\\textbf{M}ulti-\\textbf{a}gent \\textbf{do}main \\textbf{c}alibration). Firstly, we formulate a bandit RL objective to match the target trajectory distribution by learning a couple of classifiers. We then address the challenge of a large domain parameter space by modeling domain calibration as a cooperative multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) problem. Specifically, we utilize a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) to automatically cluster physics parameters with similar effects on the dynamics, grouping them into distinct agents. These grouped agents train calibration policies coordinately to adjust multiple parameters using MARL.\nOur empirical evaluation on 21 offline locomotion tasks in D4RL and NeoRL benchmarks showcases the superior performance of our method compared to strong existing offline model-based RL, offline domain calibration, and hybrid offline-and-online RL baselines."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\njiang2024multiagent,\ntitle={Multi-Agent Domain Calibration with a Handful of Offline Data},\nauthor={Tao Jiang and Lei Yuan and Lihe Li and Cong Guan and Zongzhang Zhang and Yang Yu},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-eighth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=hkBhX5ABjk}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Multi-Agent Domain Calibration with a Handful of Offline Data"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/5656a2f21c1001a3a55e7683e5a3d1a20e36023e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"jiang|multiagent_domain_calibration_with_a_handful_of_offline_data"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tao_Jiang21","~Lei_Yuan2","~Lihe_Li1","~Cong_Guan1","~Zongzhang_Zhang1","~Yang_Yu5"]},"authors":{"value":["Tao Jiang","Lei Yuan","Lihe Li","Cong Guan","Zongzhang Zhang","Yang Yu"]}},"tmdate":1730873895624,"pdate":1727287823683,"tcdate":1715603148911,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission6727/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission6727/Authors"],"forum":"hkBhX5ABjk","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6727,"cdate":1715603148911,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission6727/-/Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission6727/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1730873895624,"odate":1730873895607,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference","id":"hkBhX5ABjk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.10914v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"aryabumi|to_code_or_not_to_code_exploring_impact_of_code_in_pretraining"},"authorids":{"value":["~Viraat_Aryabumi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yixuan_Su:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Raymond_Ma:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Adrien_Morisot:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ivan_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Acyr_Locatelli:","~Marzieh_Fadaee2","~Ahmet_Üstün1","~Sara_Hooker1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.10914"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2408-10914,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Viraat Aryabumi and Yixuan Su and Raymond Ma and Adrien Morisot and Ivan Zhang and Acyr Locatelli and Marzieh Fadaee and Ahmet Üstün and Sara Hooker},\n  title={To Code, or Not To Code? Exploring Impact of Code in Pre-training},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2408.10914},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.10914}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Including code in the pre-training data mixture, even for models not specifically designed for code, has become a common practice in LLMs pre-training. While there has been anecdotal consensus among practitioners that code data plays a vital role in general LLMs' performance, there is only limited work analyzing the precise impact of code on non-code tasks. In this work, we systematically investigate the impact of code data on general performance. We ask \"what is the impact of code data used in pre-training on a large variety of downstream tasks beyond code generation\". We conduct extensive ablations and evaluate across a broad range of natural language reasoning tasks, world knowledge tasks, code benchmarks, and LLM-as-a-judge win-rates for models with sizes ranging from 470M to 2.8B parameters. Across settings, we find a consistent results that code is a critical building block for generalization far beyond coding tasks and improvements to code quality have an outsized impact across all tasks. In particular, compared to text-only pre-training, the addition of code results in up to relative increase of 8.2% in natural language (NL) reasoning, 4.2% in world knowledge, 6.6% improvement in generative win-rates, and a 12x boost in code performance respectively. Our work suggests investments in code quality and preserving code during pre-training have positive impacts."},"title":{"value":"To Code, or Not To Code? Exploring Impact of Code in Pre-training"},"authors":{"value":["Viraat Aryabumi","Yixuan Su","Raymond Ma","Adrien Morisot","Ivan Zhang","Acyr Locatelli","Marzieh Fadaee","Ahmet Üstün","Sara Hooker"]}},"tmdate":1759843102599,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727701084882,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ahmet_Üstün1"],"forum":"o8pksjUFVR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":111859,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1759843102599,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"o8pksjUFVR","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Exploring the impact of code data in pre-training"},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2025 Poster"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["code data","pre-training","code pre-training"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Including code in the pre-training data mixture, even for models not specifically designed for code, has become a common practice in LLMs pre-training. While there has been anecdotal consensus among practitioners that code data plays a vital role in general LLMs' performance, there is only limited work analyzing the precise impact of code on non-code tasks. In this work, we systematically investigate the impact of code data on general performance. We ask “what is the impact of code data used in pre-training on a large variety of downstream tasks beyond code generation”. We conduct extensive ablations and evaluate across a broad range of natural language reasoning tasks, world knowledge tasks, code benchmarks, and LLM-as-a-judge win-rates for models with sizes ranging from 470M to 2.8B parameters. Across settings, we find a consistent results that code is a critical building block for generalization far beyond coding tasks and improvements to code quality have an outsized impact across all tasks. In particular, compared to text-only pre-training, the addition of code results in up to relative increase of 8.2% in natural language (NL) reasoning, 4.2% in world knowledge, 6.6% improvement in generative win-rates, and a 12x boost in code performance respectively. Our work suggests investments in code quality and preserving code during pre-training have positive impacts."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\naryabumi2025to,\ntitle={To Code or Not To Code? Exploring Impact of Code in Pre-training},\nauthor={Viraat Aryabumi and Yixuan Su and Raymond Ma and Adrien Morisot and Ivan Zhang and Acyr Locatelli and Marzieh Fadaee and Ahmet {\\\"U}st{\\\"u}n and Sara Hooker},\nbooktitle={The Thirteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=zSfeN1uAcx}\n}"},"title":{"value":"To Code or Not To Code? Exploring Impact of Code in Pre-training"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6ae475c968a726fc5ae017d75d7d601677e4b550.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"aryabumi|to_code_or_not_to_code_exploring_impact_of_code_in_pretraining"},"authorids":{"value":["~Viraat_Aryabumi1","~Yixuan_Su1","~Raymond_Ma1","~Adrien_Morisot1","~Ivan_Zhang1","~Acyr_Locatelli1","~Marzieh_Fadaee2","~Ahmet_Üstün1","~Sara_Hooker2"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Viraat Aryabumi","Yixuan Su","Raymond Ma","Adrien Morisot","Ivan Zhang","Acyr Locatelli","Marzieh Fadaee","Ahmet Üstün","Sara Hooker"]}},"tmdate":1740675670044,"pdate":1737562498250,"tcdate":1727357241048,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission6951/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission6951/Authors"],"forum":"zSfeN1uAcx","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6951,"cdate":1727357241048,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission6951/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission6951/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1740675670044,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"zSfeN1uAcx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP (Tutorial Abstracts) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-tutorials.4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhuo|nlpcode_code_intelligence_in_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","~Wasi_Uddin_Ahmad1","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-tutorials.4"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/Zhuo00AHA25,\n  author={Terry Yue Zhuo and Qian Liu and Zijian Wang and Wasi Uddin Ahmad and Binyuan Hui and Loubna Ben Allal},\n  title={NLP+Code: Code Intelligence in Language Models},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={9-11},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-tutorials.4},\n  booktitle={EMNLP (Tutorial Abstracts)},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2025t}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Language models (LMs) like GPT and Claude have shown impressive abilities in a range of natural language processing (NLP) tasks. Among these tasks, code understanding and generation have quickly become one of the most popular applications of LMs, given its nature of executable logic forms. However, there is a practical understanding of how programming knowledge can be combined with natural language to automate software development. Moreover, recent studies also empirically demonstrate that code can be a better form for complex reasoning and agentic task automation, but they do not indicate their significance. In this tutorial, we deem such superior capabilities brought by code modeling as Code Intelligence, and aim to provide a coherent overview of recent advances in this topic. We will start by first providing preliminaries of training foundation models on code and their common practices. We will then focus on downstream tasks in the domain of code and their evaluations. Then, we will cover how code can contribute to advancements in general tasks, and the opportunities of future research on Code Intelligence."},"title":{"value":"NLP+Code: Code Intelligence in Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Terry Yue Zhuo","Qian Liu","Zijian Wang","Wasi Uddin Ahmad","Binyuan Hui","Loubna Ben Allal"]}},"tmdate":1773343653381,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/Zhuo00AHA25"],"tcdate":1773343640789,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wasi_Uddin_Ahmad1"],"forum":"81ktGssaen","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":851365,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1773343653381,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"81ktGssaen","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Code Generation, Code Language Models, Code Preference, Preference Learning, Machine Learning for Code"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English, Python"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2025 May Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025do,\ntitle={Do {LLM}s Understand Code Preference? Training Code Preference Models via Synthetic Code Evolution},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=R9z7phkARD},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Do LLMs Understand Code Preference? Training Code Preference Models via Synthetic Code Evolution"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment","Data resources"]},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently demonstrated remarkable coding capabilities. However, assessing code generation from verifiable properties and aligning it with developer preferences remains a challenge. In this paper, we explore two key questions under the new challenge of code preference learning: (i) How to train models to predict meaningful preferences for code; and (ii) how do code preferences based on verifiers, human, and neural models align with each other? To this end, we introduce **CodeFavor**, an open recipe to train pairwise code preference models using synthetic code evolution, including code commits and code critiques. We evaluate code preferences via **CodePrefBench**, a new benchmark with 1364 rigorously curated code preference tasks to cover three verifiable properties: correctness, efficiency, and security, along with human preference. Our evaluation shows that CodeFavor holistically improves model-based code preferences by up to $28.8%$. Our comprehensive controlled experiments also validate the design choices in CodeFavor. Furthermore, we quantified the cost and limitations of human-based code preference: (i) Despite spending 23 person-minutes per task, $15\\sim 40%$ of tasks remain unsolved; and (ii) human preference is the most accurate on code correctness while underperforming model-based preferences on non-functional objectives."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/5e048666dc8ec008d53468d65e9a50f08c9789b8.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Language Modeling"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782962240978,"tcdate":1747740096417,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission7327/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission7327/Authors"],"forum":"R9z7phkARD","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":7327,"cdate":1747740096417,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782962240978,"odate":1753766020949,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","id":"R9z7phkARD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2002"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/7916/21827/01013611.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2002"},"paperhash":{"value":"neubert|robust_active_stereo_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jeremiah_Neubert1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nicola_J._Ferrier:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2002.1013611"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/NeubertF02,\n  author={Jeremiah Neubert and Nicola J. Ferrier},\n  title={Robust Active Stereo Calibration},\n  year={2002},\n  cdate={1009843200000},\n  pages={2525-2531},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2002.1013611},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2002}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present a calibration procedure to determine the kinematic parameters of an active stereo system in a robot-centric frame of reference. Our goal was to obtain a solution of sufficient accuracy that the kinematic model information can be used to estimate scene structure given the measured motion/position of the eye and target object's image location. We formulate our problem using canonical coordinates of the rotation group. which enables a particularly simple closed form solution. Additionally, this formulation and solution provides quantitative measures of the resulting solutions. Experiments verify that the solutions are accurate, 3D structure can be estimated from the kinematic models, and the algorithm can indicate when image errors are large enough to produce unreliable results."},"title":{"value":"Robust Active Stereo Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Jeremiah Neubert","Nicola J. Ferrier"]}},"tmdate":1762465355905,"pdate":1009843200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icra/NeubertF02"],"tcdate":1762465344766,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jeremiah_J_Neubert1"],"forum":"LA165qaKKt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":667651,"cdate":1009843200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762465355905,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"LA165qaKKt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1709.02012v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"pleiss|on_fairness_and_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Geoff_Pleiss:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Manish_Raghavan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Felix_Wu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jon_M._Kleinberg:","~Kilian_Q._Weinberger1"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1709.02012"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1709-02012,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Geoff Pleiss and Manish Raghavan and Felix Wu and Jon M. Kleinberg and Kilian Q. Weinberger},\n  title={On Fairness and Calibration},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1709.02012},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1709.02012}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The machine learning community has become increasingly concerned with the potential for bias and discrimination in predictive models. This has motivated a growing line of work on what it means for a classification procedure to be \"fair.\" In this paper, we investigate the tension between minimizing error disparity across different population groups while maintaining calibrated probability estimates. We show that calibration is compatible only with a single error constraint (i.e. equal false-negatives rates across groups), and show that any algorithm that satisfies this relaxation is no better than randomizing a percentage of predictions for an existing classifier. These unsettling findings, which extend and generalize existing results, are empirically confirmed on several datasets."},"title":{"value":"On Fairness and Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Geoff Pleiss","Manish Raghavan","Felix Wu","Jon M. Kleinberg","Kilian Q. Weinberger"]}},"tmdate":1731508586462,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1731508406143,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kilian_Q_Weinberger1"],"forum":"1q9qbk1DdH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":229740,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731508586462,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"1q9qbk1DdH","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Calibration in VQA Tasks"},"venue":{"value":"AIR-FM Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Calibration","VQA","Fine-tuning"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/c9305d2232e52d76e0cabe5351496e2df4ee9e34.zip"},"confirmation":{"value":"I have read and agree with the workshop's policy on behalf of myself and my co-authors."},"abstract":{"value":"In the context of Visual Question Answering (VQA) and Agentic AI, calibration refers to how closely an AI system's confidence in its answers reflects their actual correctness. While modern VQA systems, powered by advanced vision-language models (VLMs), are increasingly used in high-stakes domains like medical diagnostics and autonomous navigation due to their improved accuracy, the reliability of their confidence estimates remains under-examined. Particularly, these systems often produce overconfident responses. To address this, we introduce AlignVQA, a debate-based multi-agent framework, in which diverse specialized VLM -- each following distinct prompting strategies -- generate candidate answers and then engage in two-stage interaction: generalist agents critique, refine and aggregate these proposals. Furthermore, we introduce a novel differentiable calibration-aware loss function called AlignCal designed to fine-tune the specialized agents by minimizing an upper bound on the calibration error. This objective explicitly improves the fidelity of each agent’s confidence estimates. Empirical results across multiple benchmark VQA datasets substantiate the efficacy of our approach, demonstrating substantial reductions in calibration discrepancies."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\npandey2026alignvqa,\ntitle={Align{VQA}:  Debate-Driven Multi-Agent Calibration for Vision Language Models},\nauthor={Ayush Pandey and Jai Bardhan and Ramya S Hebbalaguppe},\nbooktitle={AAAI 2026 Workshop on Assessing and Improving Reliability of Foundation Models in the Real World},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=w0Ai70K5Wk}\n}"},"title":{"value":"AlignVQA:  Debate-Driven Multi-Agent Calibration for Vision Language Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ae855ee07e76219d7ee695791e24a3baa1c55145.pdf"},"submission_track":{"value":"Workshop Paper Track"},"venueid":{"value":"AAAI.org/2026/Workshop/AIR-FM"},"paperhash":{"value":"pandey|alignvqa_debatedriven_multiagent_calibration_for_vision_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ayush_Pandey3","~Jai_Bardhan1","~Ramya_S_Hebbalaguppe1"]},"authors":{"value":["Ayush Pandey","Jai Bardhan","Ramya S Hebbalaguppe"]}},"tmdate":1769523240616,"pdate":1762437467520,"tcdate":1760499600225,"writers":["AAAI.org/2026/Workshop/AIR-FM","AAAI.org/2026/Workshop/AIR-FM/Submission11/Authors"],"signatures":["AAAI.org/2026/Workshop/AIR-FM/Submission11/Authors"],"forum":"w0Ai70K5Wk","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":11,"cdate":1760499600225,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["AAAI.org/2026/Workshop/AIR-FM/-/Submission","AAAI.org/2026/Workshop/AIR-FM/-/Post_Submission","AAAI.org/2026/Workshop/AIR-FM/-/Edit","AAAI.org/2026/Workshop/AIR-FM/Submission11/-/Camera-Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1769523240616,"odate":1762437467520,"domain":"AAAI.org/2026/Workshop/AIR-FM","id":"w0Ai70K5Wk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10609961/10609862/10611119.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|lidarcamera_extrinsic_calibration_with_hierachical_and_iterative_feature_matching"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuzhong_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zaipeng_Duan:","~Junfeng_Ding1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhe_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiao_Huang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Ma_0003:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA57147.2024.10611119"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/HuDDZHM24,\n  author={Xuzhong Hu and Zaipeng Duan and Junfeng Ding and Zhe Zhang and Xiao Huang and Jie Ma},\n  title={LiDAR-Camera Extrinsic Calibration with Hierachical and Iterative Feature Matching},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={16691-16697},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA57147.2024.10611119},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In autonomous driving, the LiDAR-Camera system plays a crucial role in a vehicle’s perception of 3D environments. To effectively fuse information from both camera and LiDAR, extrinsic calibration is indispensable. Recently, some researchers have proposed deep learning-based methods that utilize convolutional networks to automatically extract features from LiDAR depth images and RGB images for calibration. However, these features do not sufficiently interact during feature matching, which limits the calibration accuracy. To this end, we introduce a novel extrinsic calibration network (HIFM-Net) in this paper. It establishes a comprehensive connection between camera and LiDAR features by calculating a globally-aware map-to-map cost volume and hierachical point-to-map cost volumes. The former is used to regress large extrinsic offsets. The latter is employed to iteratively fine-tune extrinsic parameters, while the rigidity of LiDAR points is considered in each iteration to enhance regression robustness. Extensive experiments on the KITTI-odometry dataset demonstrate the superior performance of our HIFMNet compared to other state-of-the-art learning-based methods."},"title":{"value":"LiDAR-Camera Extrinsic Calibration with Hierachical and Iterative Feature Matching"},"authors":{"value":["Xuzhong Hu","Zaipeng Duan","Junfeng Ding","Zhe Zhang","Xiao Huang","Jie Ma"]}},"tmdate":1744825893226,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1744825890664,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Junfeng_Ding1"],"forum":"Q6s5qPnTd5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":399468,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744825893226,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Q6s5qPnTd5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MFI 2008"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/4634656/4648003/04648067.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MFI/2008"},"paperhash":{"value":"florez|extrinsic_calibration_between_a_multilayer_lidar_and_a_camera"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sergio_Alberto_Rodriguez_Florez:","~Vincent_Frémont1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Philippe_Bonnifait:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/MFI.2008.4648067"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/mfi/FlorezFB08,\n  author={Sergio Alberto Rodriguez Florez and Vincent Frémont and Philippe Bonnifait},\n  title={Extrinsic calibration between a multi-layer lidar and a camera},\n  year={2008},\n  cdate={1199145600000},\n  pages={214-219},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/MFI.2008.4648067},\n  booktitle={MFI},\n  crossref={conf/mfi/2008}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we present a novel approach for solving the extrinsic calibration between a camera and a multi-layer laser range finder. Our approach is oriented for intelligent vehicle applications, where the separation distance between sensors frames are frequently very important. For this purpose, we use a circle-based calibration object because its geometry allows us to obtain not only an accurate estimation pose by taking advantage of the 3D multi-layer laser range finder perception but also a simultaneous estimation of the pose in the camera frame and the camera intrinsic parameters. These advantages simplify the calibration task in outdoor environments. The method determines the relative position of the sensors by estimating sets of corresponded features and by solving the classical absolute orientation problem. The proposed method is evaluated by using different synthetics environments and real data. An error propagation analysis is made in order to estimate the calibration accuracy and the confidence intervals. Finally, we present a laser data projection into images to validate the consistency of the results."},"title":{"value":"Extrinsic calibration between a multi-layer lidar and a camera"},"authors":{"value":["Sergio Alberto Rodriguez Florez","Vincent Frémont","Philippe Bonnifait"]}},"tmdate":1730366113944,"pdate":1199145600000,"tcdate":1730366084609,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~FREMONT_Vincent1"],"forum":"h7jJ9HkQVJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":165392,"cdate":1199145600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730366113944,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"h7jJ9HkQVJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ECCV (40) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-73661-2_1.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ECCV/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"veicht|geocalib_learning_singleimage_calibration_with_geometric_optimization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Alexander_Veicht1","~Paul-Edouard_Sarlin1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Philipp_Lindenberger:","~Marc_Pollefeys1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-73661-2_1"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/eccv/VeichtSLP24,\n  author={Alexander Veicht and Paul-Edouard Sarlin and Philipp Lindenberger and Marc Pollefeys},\n  title={GeoCalib: Learning Single-Image Calibration with Geometric Optimization},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={1-20},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-73661-2_1},\n  booktitle={ECCV (40)},\n  crossref={conf/eccv/2024-40}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"From a single image, visual cues can help deduce intrinsic and extrinsic camera parameters like the focal length and the gravity direction. This single-image calibration can benefit various downstream applications like image editing and 3D mapping. Current approaches to this problem are based on either classical geometry with lines and vanishing points or on deep neural networks trained end-to-end. The learned approaches are more robust but struggle to generalize to new environments and are less accurate than their classical counterparts. We hypothesize that they lack the constraints that 3D geometry provides. In this work, we introduce GeoCalib, a deep neural network that leverages universal rules of 3D geometry through an optimization process. GeoCalib is trained end-to-end to estimate camera parameters and learns to find useful visual cues from the data. Experiments on various benchmarks show that GeoCalib is more robust and more accurate than existing classical and learned approaches. Its internal optimization estimates uncertainties, which help flag failure cases and benefit downstream applications like visual localization. The code and trained models are publicly available at https://github.com/cvg/GeoCalib."},"title":{"value":"GeoCalib: Learning Single-Image Calibration with Geometric Optimization"},"authors":{"value":["Alexander Veicht","Paul-Edouard Sarlin","Philipp Lindenberger","Marc Pollefeys"]}},"tmdate":1772114836307,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/eccv/VeichtSLP24"],"tcdate":1762245100049,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Paul-Edouard_Sarlin1"],"forum":"fWBwcAIlCO","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":651341,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772114836307,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"fWBwcAIlCO","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.06704v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"veicht|geocalib_learning_singleimage_calibration_with_geometric_optimization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alexander_Veicht:","","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Philipp_Lindenberger:","~Marc_Pollefeys1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.06704"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2409-06704,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Alexander Veicht and Paul-Edouard Sarlin and Philipp Lindenberger and Marc Pollefeys},\n  title={GeoCalib: Learning Single-image Calibration with Geometric Optimization},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2409.06704},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.06704}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"From a single image, visual cues can help deduce intrinsic and extrinsic camera parameters like the focal length and the gravity direction. This single-image calibration can benefit various downstream applications like image editing and 3D mapping. Current approaches to this problem are based on either classical geometry with lines and vanishing points or on deep neural networks trained end-to-end. The learned approaches are more robust but struggle to generalize to new environments and are less accurate than their classical counterparts. We hypothesize that they lack the constraints that 3D geometry provides. In this work, we introduce GeoCalib, a deep neural network that leverages universal rules of 3D geometry through an optimization process. GeoCalib is trained end-to-end to estimate camera parameters and learns to find useful visual cues from the data. Experiments on various benchmarks show that GeoCalib is more robust and more accurate than existing classical and learned approaches. Its internal optimization estimates uncertainties, which help flag failure cases and benefit downstream applications like visual localization. The code and trained models are publicly available at https://github.com/cvg/GeoCalib."},"title":{"value":"GeoCalib: Learning Single-image Calibration with Geometric Optimization"},"authors":{"value":["Alexander Veicht","Paul-Edouard Sarlin","Philipp Lindenberger","Marc Pollefeys"]}},"tmdate":1762679665233,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1730552968781,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Paul-Edouard_Sarlin1"],"forum":"QuDeJucVhe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":168222,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762679665233,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"QuDeJucVhe","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"To appear in Journal of Machine Learning Research"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"derr|three_types_of_calibration_with_properties_and_their_semantic_and_formal_relationships"},"authorids":{"value":["~Rabanus_Derr1","~Jessica_Finocchiaro1","~Robert_Williamson1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.18395"},"abstract":{"value":"Fueled by discussions around “trustworthiness” and algorithmic fairness, calibra-\ntion of predictive systems has regained scholars attention. The vanilla definition and\nunderstanding of calibration is, simply put, on all days on which the rain probability\nhas been predicted to be p, the actual frequency of rain days was p. However, the\nincreased attention has led to an immense variety of new notions of “calibration.”\nSome of the notions are incomparable, serve different purposes, or imply each other.\nIn this work, we provide two accounts which motivate calibration: self-realization of\nforecasted properties and precise estimation of incurred losses of the decision makers\nrelying on forecasts. We substantiate the former via the reflection principle and the\nlatter by actuarial fairness. For both accounts we formulate prototypical definitions\nvia properties Γ of outcome distributions, e.g., the mean or median. The proto-\ntypical definition for self-realization, which we call Γ-calibration, is equivalent to a\ncertain type of swap regret under certain conditions. These implications are strongly\nconnected to the omniprediction learning paradigm. The prototypical definition for\nprecise loss estimation is a modification of decision calibration adopted from Zhao\net al. [73]. For binary outcome sets both prototypical definitions coincide under\nappropriate choices of reference properties. For higher-dimensional outcome sets,\nboth prototypical definitions can be subsumed by a natural extension of the binary\ndefinition, called distribution calibration with respect to a property. We conclude by\ncommenting on the role of groupings in both accounts of calibration often used to\nobtain multicalibration. In sum, this work provides a semantic map of calibration in\norder to navigate a fragmented terrain of notions and definitions."},"title":{"value":"Three Types of Calibration with Properties and their Semantic and Formal Relationships"},"authors":{"value":["Rabanus Derr","Jessica Finocchiaro","Robert Williamson"]}},"tmdate":1777896705242,"pdate":1770177600000,"tcdate":1777896705242,"writers":["~Rabanus_Derr1","~Jessica_Finocchiaro1","~Robert_Williamson1"],"signatures":["~Jessica_Finocchiaro1"],"forum":"qAWbKuIUFR","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":47854,"cdate":1777896705242,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1777896705242,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"qAWbKuIUFR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2015"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/15/10/25919/pdf?version=1444728612"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2015"},"paperhash":{"value":"lüken|multisensor_calibration_of_lowcost_magnetic_angular_rate_and_gravity_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["~Markus_J._Lüken1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Berno_J._E._Misgeld:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daniel_Rüschen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Steffen_Leonhardt:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s151025919"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/LukenMRL15,\n  author={Markus J. Lüken and Berno J. E. Misgeld and Daniel Rüschen and Steffen Leonhardt},\n  title={Multi-Sensor Calibration of Low-Cost Magnetic, Angular Rate and Gravity Systems},\n  year={2015},\n  cdate={1420070400000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={15},\n  number={10},\n  pages={25919-25936},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s151025919}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present a new calibration procedure for low-cost nine degrees-of-freedom (9DOF) magnetic, angular rate and gravity (MARG) sensor systems, which relies on a calibration cube, a reference table and a body sensor network (BSN). The 9DOF MARG sensor is part of our recently-developed “Integrated Posture and Activity Network by Medit Aachen” (IPANEMA) BSN. The advantage of this new approach is the use of the calibration cube, which allows for easy integration of two sensor nodes of the IPANEMA BSN. One 9DOF MARG sensor node is thereby used for calibration; the second 9DOF MARG sensor node is used for reference measurements. A novel algorithm uses these measurements to further improve the performance of the calibration procedure by processing arbitrarily-executed motions. In addition, the calibration routine can be used in an alignment procedure to minimize errors in the orientation between the 9DOF MARG sensor system and a motion capture inertial reference system. A two-stage experimental study is conducted to underline the performance of our calibration procedure. In both stages of the proposed calibration procedure, the BSN data, as well as reference tracking data are recorded. In the first stage, the mean values of all sensor outputs are determined as the absolute measurement offset to minimize integration errors in the derived movement model of the corresponding body segment. The second stage deals with the dynamic characteristics of the measurement system where the dynamic deviation of the sensor output compared to a reference system is corrected. In practical validation experiments, this procedure showed promising results with a maximum RMS error of 3.89 ° ."},"title":{"value":"Multi-Sensor Calibration of Low-Cost Magnetic, Angular Rate and Gravity Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Markus J. Lüken","Berno J. E. Misgeld","Daniel Rüschen","Steffen Leonhardt"]}},"tmdate":1752858296928,"pdate":1420070400000,"tcdate":1752858274227,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Markus_Lueken1"],"forum":"M5FtzKoaxu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":574993,"cdate":1420070400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1752858296928,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"M5FtzKoaxu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Int. J. Hum. Comput. Stud. 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJMMS/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zeng|contractmind_trustcalibration_interaction_design_for_ai_contract_review_tools"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jian_Zeng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaixin_Chen_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ruiqi_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yilong_Li:","~Mingming_Fan2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaishun_Wu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoke_Qi:","~Lu_Wang12"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhcs.2024.103411"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijmms/ZengCWLFWQW25,\n  author={Jian Zeng and Kaixin Chen and Ruiqi Wang and Yilong Li and Mingming Fan and Kaishun Wu and Xiaoke Qi and Lu Wang},\n  title={ContractMind: Trust-calibration interaction design for AI contract review tools},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={Int. J. Hum. Comput. Stud.},\n  volume={196},\n  pages={103411},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhcs.2024.103411}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•Develop an AI contract review tool called ContractMind using a context-centered and interactive design approach.•Collaborate with Attorneys to create 7 components that enhance trust calibration.•Users prefer seeking evidence over explanations, especially from shared knowledge bases.•Findings inform the development of future Human–AI collaborative decision-making tools for enhancing trust calibration."},"title":{"value":"ContractMind: Trust-calibration interaction design for AI contract review tools"},"authors":{"value":["Jian Zeng","Kaixin Chen","Ruiqi Wang","Yilong Li","Mingming Fan","Kaishun Wu","Xiaoke Qi","Lu Wang"]}},"tmdate":1760534746019,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1747748107965,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mingming_Fan2"],"forum":"UJEOIebX0Z","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":537571,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1760534746019,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UJEOIebX0Z","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICDAR (2) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-70536-6_9.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICDAR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ayllon|analysis_of_the_calibration_of_handwriting_text_recognition_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Eric_Ayllon:","~Francisco_J._Castellanos1","~Jorge_Calvo-Zaragoza1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-70536-6_9"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icdar/AyllonCC24,\n  author={Eric Ayllon and Francisco J. Castellanos and Jorge Calvo-Zaragoza},\n  title={Analysis of the Calibration of Handwriting Text Recognition Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={139-155},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-70536-6_9},\n  booktitle={ICDAR (2)},\n  crossref={conf/icdar/2024-2}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Current neural approaches for Handwritten Text Recognition (HTR) have proven to be successful in many settings, but their performance can be unpredictable when facing new data. In this context, it is essential to correctly estimate an approximate error of the target predictions. To achieve this, the model must be well calibrated, meaning that the confidence values are sufficiently representative of the expected accuracy. Calibration is a crucial aspect in practical applications of HTR, but the topic remains overly underexplored. In this paper, we fill this gap by studying calibration in state-of-the-art HTR models, along with specific techniques for this purpose. We perform thorough experiments on several datasets, both in a classic setting and in cross-collection scenarios. Our results report interesting conclusions about the calibration of HTR, highlighting their strengths, weaknesses, and the extent to which the considered strategies improve results."},"title":{"value":"Analysis of the Calibration of Handwriting Text Recognition Models"},"authors":{"value":["Eric Ayllon","Francisco J. Castellanos","Jorge Calvo-Zaragoza"]}},"tmdate":1771093766389,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731476003325,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jorge_Calvo-Zaragoza1"],"forum":"0BuSwD8Poi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":200695,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1771093766389,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0BuSwD8Poi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICINCO 2021"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICINCO/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"rosenbauer|an_evolutionary_calibration_approach_for_touch_interface_filter_chains"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lukas_Rosenbauer:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Johannes_Maier:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daniel_Gerber:","~Anthony_Stein1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jörg_Hähner:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.5220/0010512900290038"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icinco/RosenbauerMGSH21,\n  author={Lukas Rosenbauer and Johannes Maier and Daniel Gerber and Anthony Stein and Jörg Hähner},\n  title={An Evolutionary Calibration Approach for Touch Interface Filter Chains},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={29-38},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.5220/0010512900290038},\n  booktitle={ICINCO},\n  crossref={conf/icinco/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Touch interfaces are human machine interface (HMI) that can be found in a wide range of products ranging from mobile phones over cars to home appliances. Many of these HMIs measure digital signals which are used to detect touch events. These signals are processed using filters in order to decide whether there is a touch event or not. The filterchain must be functional even if the signal contains heavy noise. Thus a precise calibration of the individual filters is necessary. We employ a genetic algorithm (GA) to choose the filter parameters automatically. We evaluate our approach in a series of experiments which includes simulated as well as real data. We additionally compare our GA with manually calibrated parameters and thereby show the superiority of our method in terms of the accuracy of the calibration provided. A cost-intensive manual calibration can thus be avoided."},"title":{"value":"An Evolutionary Calibration Approach for Touch Interface Filter Chains"},"authors":{"value":["Lukas Rosenbauer","Johannes Maier","Daniel Gerber","Anthony Stein","Jörg Hähner"]}},"tmdate":1738620654168,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1738620628178,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Anthony_Stein1"],"forum":"YmZhcnejB5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":291036,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738620654168,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"YmZhcnejB5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (Findings) 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.1079.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"xiao|faq_mitigating_quantization_error_via_regenerating_calibration_data_with_familyaware_quantization"},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.1079/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/XiaoLGJLZ26,\n  author={Haiyang Xiao and Weiqing Li and Jinyue Guo and Guochao Jiang and Guohua Liu and Yuewei Zhang},\n  title={FAQ: Mitigating Quantization Error via Regenerating Calibration Data with Family-Aware Quantization},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={21441-21460},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.1079/},\n  booktitle={ACL (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2026f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Although post-training quantization (PTQ) provides an efficient numerical compression scheme for deploying large language models (LLMs) on resource-constrained devices, the representativeness and universality of calibration data remain a core bottleneck in determining the accuracy of quantization parameters. Traditional PTQ methods typically rely on limited samples, making it difficult to capture the activation distribution during the inference phase, leading to biases in quantization parameters. To address this, we propose **FAQ** (Family-Aware Quantization), a calibration data regeneration framework that leverages prior knowledge from LLMs of the same family to generate high-fidelity calibration samples. Specifically, FAQ first inputs the original calibration samples into a larger LLM from the same family as the target model, regenerating a series of high-fidelity calibration data using a highly consistent knowledge system. Subsequently, this data, carrying Chain-of-Thought reasoning and conforming to the expected activation distribution, undergoes group competition under expert guidance to select the best samples, which are then re-normalized to enhance the effectiveness of standard PTQ. Experiments on multiple model series, including Qwen3-8B, show that FAQ reduces accuracy loss by up to 28.5% compared to the baseline with original calibration data, demonstrating its powerful potential and contribution."},"title":{"value":"FAQ: Mitigating Quantization Error via Regenerating Calibration Data with Family-Aware Quantization"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Haiyang Xiao","username":""},{"fullname":"Weiqing Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Jinyue Guo","username":""},{"fullname":"Guochao Jiang","username":"~Guochao_Jiang1"},{"fullname":"Guohua Liu","username":""},{"fullname":"Yuewei Zhang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784182517241,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/XiaoLGJLZ26"],"tcdate":1784182513478,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Guochao_Jiang1"],"forum":"4Skmi00yFL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":58812,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784182517241,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"4Skmi00yFL","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"quantization, distillation,data augmentation,NLP in resource-constrained settings"},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Models","Post-Training Quantization","Calibration Data","Quantization Error","Family Quantization","In-Family LLMs","Model Compression"]},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 January Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026faq,\ntitle={{FAQ}: Mitigating Quantization Error via Regenerating Calibration Data with Family-Aware Quantization},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - January 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=KKqFtVG7Qa},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"FAQ: Mitigating Quantization Error via Regenerating Calibration Data with Family-Aware Quantization"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Approaches to low-resource settings"]},"abstract":{"value":"Although post-training quantization (PTQ) provides an efficient numerical compression scheme for deploying large language models (LLMs) on resource-constrained devices, the representativeness and universality of calibration data remain a core bottleneck in determining the accuracy of quantization parameters. Traditional PTQ methods typically rely on limited samples, making it difficult to capture the activation distribution during the inference phase, leading to biases in quantization parameters. To address this, we propose ***FAQ*** (Family-Aware Quantization), a calibration data regeneration framework that leverages prior knowledge from LLMs of the same family to generate high-fidelity calibration samples. Specifically, FAQ first inputs the original calibration samples into a larger LLM from the same family as the target model, regenerating a series of high-fidelity calibration data using a highly consistent knowledge system. Subsequently, this data, carrying Chain-of-Thought reasoning and conforming to the expected activation distribution, undergoes group competition under expert guidance to select the best samples, which are then re-normalized to enhance the effectiveness of standard PTQ. Experiments on multiple model series, including Qwen3-8B, show that FAQ reduces accuracy loss by up to 28.5\\% compared to the baseline with original calibration data, demonstrating its powerful potential and contribution."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/92ebf130d8941608df618270ac1a29dcf393e0c7.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Low-resource Methods for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/Submission"}},"tmdate":1780805199646,"tcdate":1767696723677,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/Submission9783/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/Submission9783/Authors"],"forum":"KKqFtVG7Qa","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":9783,"cdate":1767696723677,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1780805199646,"odate":1774007940523,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January","id":"KKqFtVG7Qa","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SPECOM (1) 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-48309-7_13.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/SPECOM/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"novoselov|investigation_of_different_calibration_methods_for_deep_speaker_embedding_based_verification_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["~Sergey_Novoselov1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Galina_Lavrentyeva:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Vladimir_Volokhov:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marina_Volkova:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nikita_Khmelev:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Artem_Akulov:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-48309-7_13"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/specom/NovoselovLVVKA23,\n  author={Sergey Novoselov and Galina Lavrentyeva and Vladimir Volokhov and Marina Volkova and Nikita Khmelev and Artem Akulov},\n  title={Investigation of Different Calibration Methods for Deep Speaker Embedding Based Verification Systems},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={159-168},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-48309-7_13},\n  booktitle={SPECOM (1)},\n  crossref={conf/specom/2023-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep speaker embedding extractors have already become new state-of-the-art systems in the speaker verification field. However, the problem of verification score calibration for such systems often remains out of focus. An irrelevant score calibration leads to serious issues, especially in the case of unknown acoustic conditions, even if we use a strong speaker verification system in terms of threshold-free metrics. This paper presents an investigation over several methods of score calibration: a classical approach based on the logistic regression model; the recently presented magnitude estimation network MagNetO that uses activations from the pooling layer of the trained deep speaker extractor and generalization of such approach based on separate scale and offset prediction neural networks. An additional focus of this research is to estimate the impact of score normalization on the calibration performance of the system. The obtained results demonstrate that there are no serious problems if in-domain development data are used for calibration tuning. Otherwise, a trade-off between good calibration performance and threshold-free system quality arises. In most cases using adaptive s-norm helps to stabilize score distributions and to improve system performance."},"title":{"value":"Investigation of Different Calibration Methods for Deep Speaker Embedding Based Verification Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Sergey Novoselov","Galina Lavrentyeva","Vladimir Volokhov","Marina Volkova","Nikita Khmelev","Artem Akulov"]}},"tmdate":1768984961187,"pdate":1703980800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/specom/NovoselovLVVKA23"],"tcdate":1768984956546,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sergey_Novoselov1"],"forum":"ILU1qRKofW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":782351,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768984961187,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ILU1qRKofW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"J. Intell. Fuzzy Syst. 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/reader/10.3233/JIFS-191567"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/JIFS/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|bench_calibration_method_for_automotive_electric_motors_based_on_deep_reinforcement_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yafu_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hantao_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Linhui_Li:","~Jing_Lian3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3233/JIFS-191567"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/jifs/ZhouWLL20,\n  author={Yafu Zhou and Hantao Wang and Linhui Li and Jing Lian},\n  title={Bench calibration method for automotive electric motors based on deep reinforcement learning},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={J. Intell. Fuzzy Syst.},\n  volume={39},\n  number={1},\n  pages={607-626},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3233/JIFS-191567}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The efficiency and control accuracy of Interior Permanent Magnet Synchronous Motor (IPMSM) are the main factors affecting performance. Manual calibration has the disadvantage of high work intensity, long calibration period and high technical requirement, which leads to low calibration accuracy and motor efficiency. Thus, a novel calibration method based on Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (DDPG) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) is proposed. By constructing a deep reinforcement learning network, the self-optimization of the optimal working point under any working condition is realized, and the MAP for IPMSM in full speed-torque range is obtained. The method can be used to quickly realize the optimal matching of d-q axis current with arbitrary stator current. It focuses on solving the problem of motor overheating caused by long adjustment time of manually calibrated MAP when the motor is overloaded, to realize fast calibration in overload area. Moreover, the method reduces the dependence on the motor parameters and increases the adaptability of the calibration MAP data to the operating conditions. The simulation and bench test indicate that the method can meet the response requirements of motor torque, and results reveal that the motor efficiency is greatly improved."},"title":{"value":"Bench calibration method for automotive electric motors based on deep reinforcement learning"},"authors":{"value":["Yafu Zhou","Hantao Wang","Linhui Li","Jing Lian"]}},"tmdate":1762600635495,"pdate":1577836800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/jifs/ZhouWLL20"],"tcdate":1762600631524,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lian_Jing1"],"forum":"bfRaY08A8f","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":678994,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762600635495,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bfRaY08A8f","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"DASFAA (4) 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-30678-5_25.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/DASFAA/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"ma|twostage_interest_calibration_network_for_reranking_hotels"},"authorids":{"value":["~Denghao_Ma1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiajia_Sun:","~Yueguo_Chen2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liang_Shen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Genliang_Yi:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-30678-5_25"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/dasfaa/MaSCSY23,\n  author={Denghao Ma and Jiajia Sun and Yueguo Chen and Liang Shen and Genliang Yi},\n  title={Two-stage Interest Calibration Network for Reranking Hotels},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={325-335},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-30678-5_25},\n  booktitle={DASFAA (4)},\n  crossref={conf/dasfaa/2023-4}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As one key task of hotel search, interest modelling is to capture users’ interests over their historical clicks. To this end, it is important yet challenging to understand the searching intention behind each click (interest focus). Besides, the query input by a user represents the user’s interests to some extent. The differences between historical queries and the current query result in the gap between historical interests and the current interests (interest gap). Because historical clicks stem from historical interests, using them to model the current interests inevitably suffers from the interest gap challenge. To capture the interest focus and address the interest gap, we propose the two-stage interest calibration network (TCN), i.e., search-internal and search-external. In the search-internal calibration, we propose new insights of using the divergences among clicks and unclicks to model interest focus, and then develop a divergence-based calibration network. In the search-external calibration, inspired by the smoothing techniques for language models, we propose the interest smoothing principle to bridge interest gap: the interests learnt from historical clicks \\(+\\) smoothing factor \\(\\approx \\) current interests. To implement this principle, we develop an interest smoothing network by reusing the query data. In the network, the interest domination unit is developed to learn user’s interests from historical clicks, and the interest smoothing unit is developed to construct the smoothing factor. Extensive offline experiments and online A/B testing are performed and show that TCN significantly outperforms the state-of-the-art baselines. Besides, our model has been deployed in a hotel e-commerce platform and brought \\(2.90\\%\\) CTCVR and \\(1.53\\%\\) CTR lifts."},"title":{"value":"Two-stage Interest Calibration Network for Reranking Hotels"},"authors":{"value":["Denghao Ma","Jiajia Sun","Yueguo Chen","Liang Shen","Genliang Yi"]}},"tmdate":1747293533931,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731969371551,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Denghao_Ma1"],"forum":"PuyKJUVj3n","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":250249,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747293533931,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"PuyKJUVj3n","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Desk Rejected Submission"},"TLDR":{"value":"EmoReward uses reward traces from a completed training run to adaptively and self-evolving calibrate emotional evidence in subsequent video generation RL without retraining reward models."},"keywords":{"value":["Self-Evolving Agent","Reward System","Reinforcement Learning","Video Generation"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/9447d3831f8626a264137eee1bdf137ebab7213d.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"reinforcement learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) has advanced generative model post-training in verifiable domains such as code and mathematics, but evaluating video quality, especially emotional expression, often depends on subjective aesthetics and has no uniquely correct target.\nExisting approaches typically rely on fixed rewards for visual quality, motion quality, and text alignment that poorly capture emotional intent and can invite reward hacking, while reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) is difficult to scale and subject to individual aesthetic differences that complicate the calibration of facial, body motion, and chromatic cues across narrative contexts.\nWe introduce **EmoReward**, a new self-evolving training mechanism that distills evidence reliability from reward traces collected during an actual training run to adaptively guide multi-reward weight allocation in subsequent training by determining which emotional evidence to trust and how much to weight it.\nEmoReward first plans specific grounding cues from the emotional prompt to guide evaluation, then uses heterogeneous signal primitives to score the generated video during the act stage. *Reflective Reward Gating* calibrates their contributions through instance-level gates for phase, face confidence, conflict, and subtlety, then renormalizes weights over the active set to produce the sample's fused reward.\n*RewardMemory* then estimates each primitive's reliability from rollout scores and outcomes within each emotional regime in the finished training run, then fixes bounded multipliers that calibrate fusion weights in the next training run without updating reward models.\nExperiments on HunyuanVideo1.5 show improvements across multiple RL algorithms and compare EmoReward with other agentic reward methods under SAGE-GRPO. Ablations and transfer experiments on SD3.5 and FLUX further support adaptive reward calibration for visual generation."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nanonymous2026emoreward,\ntitle={EmoReward: Self-Evolving Reward Calibration for Emotional Video Generation {RL}},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=z409Nct6AS}\n}"},"title":{"value":"EmoReward: Self-Evolving Reward Calibration for Emotional Video Generation RL"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/df269cf77c24023fba4d267daadef6330fccdb5d.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Desk_Rejected_Submission"}},"tmdate":1792254720605,"tcdate":1787215667540,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission1381/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission1381/Authors"],"forum":"z409Nct6AS","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1381,"cdate":1787215667540,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission1381/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Desk_Rejected_Submission"],"mdate":1792254720605,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"z409Nct6AS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"TLDR":{"value":"BindGauge learns which tool and which metric to trust for RBP-RNA affinity ranking, using 145 interpretable parameters and self-evolving memory to outperform tested black-box baselines."},"keywords":{"value":["Self-Evolving Agents","Interpretable Calibration","Heterogeneous Tool Aggregation","Computational Biology","RBP-RNA Binding Affinity"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/d83f70f4b123bae5c39b7db1880e1dba051676a9.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"applications to physical sciences (physics, chemistry, biology, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Ranking RNA-binding protein (RBP)–RNA pairs by binding affinity is a foundational step toward characterizing post-transcriptional regulatory specificity, yet it remains difficult because the available computational evidence is heterogeneous, noisy, and context-dependent. Sequence-based predictors, physics-based energy functions, and structure-confidence metrics each supply useful but incomplete signals whose raw scores are not comparable across pairs. Conventional pipelines compound this by optimizing a single global objective without revisiting the ranking errors they produce, so recurrent failure patterns are discarded rather than used to refine subsequent calibration. The central challenge is therefore not to collect more scores, but to decide how much to trust each one, and when. We introduce BindGauge, a self-evolving agent that represents its knowledge as a compact hierarchy of interpretable importance weights over tools and over metrics within each tool, and that aggregates calibrated evidence through multi-round residual deliberation with cross-tool interaction terms. To make calibration adaptive, BindGauge maintains a Ranking Experience Memory that records past ranking outcomes, diagnoses recurrent failure signatures, and distills per-metric reliability profiles. This closed loop, separated from standard parameter training, enables BindGauge to evolve from a uniform aggregator into a task-specialized ranker while preserving full interpretability. With 145 interpretable scalars, BindGauge attains Spearman ρ = 0.832 on PRAB and 0.317 on KD on two homology-controlled benchmarks, against 0.500 (Random Forest) and 0.216 (SVM) for the strongest learned baselines on each, and it exposes per-round tool weights and failure-signature clusters that render each ranking decision auditable. Both test splits are small, so we report bootstrap intervals alongside point estimates. Our code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/BindGauge-4F71/."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026selfevolving,\ntitle={Self-Evolving Hierarchical Trust Calibration for {RBP}-{RNA} Affinity Ranking},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=c2RLlv02WP},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Self-Evolving Hierarchical Trust Calibration for RBP-RNA Affinity Ranking"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ccb7f48786cdbb5899487f2cdbf85d3c9ddc98d9.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791219858546,"tcdate":1787490512825,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission3562/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission3562/Authors"],"forum":"c2RLlv02WP","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":3562,"cdate":1787490512825,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission3562/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791219858546,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"c2RLlv02WP","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"pruning, LLM efficiency, efficient models, sparse models"},"keywords":{"value":["depth pruning","layer pruning","large language models","LLM compression","model compression"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"N/A"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"Yes"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4.1 specifies calibration data; Section 4.3 specifies pruning budgets (M=7,9); Section 4.4 mentions 64 calibration samples."},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"No"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B5_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4.1, 4.4 describe calibration data and evaluation benchmarks."},"B4_elaboration":{"value":"All datasets are publicly released standard benchmarks that do not contain personally identifying information or targeted offensive content."},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"Our study spans a 7 (search algorithms) × 3 (models) × 2 (calibration setups) × 2 (pruning budgets) grid, totaling 84 (model, setup, search, budget) cells. Running multiple seeds per cell was infeasible under our compute budget (NVIDIA A40 48GB), so we report a single fixed random seed per cell (Section 4.4) and acknowledge this in the Limitations section."},"A2_elaboration":{"value":"Discussed in the Limitations and Ethics Statement section."},"B3_elaboration":{"value":"All models/datasets used for research evaluation consistent with their intended use."},"B2_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix B (Licenses and Terms of Use) lists licenses for all models (LLaMA3/3.1 8B, Qwen3 8B) and datasets (C4, Commonsense 170k, WikiText-2, LAMBADA, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, ARC, PIQA, BoolQ)."},"C4_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4.2 specifies the hyperparameters for each search algorithm. Default settings are used for other standard libraries unless otherwise specified."},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4.2 reports all hyperparameters (beam width B=5, GA population 16, elitism 0.2, mutation 0.15, 10 generations, BO 200 trials with 10 random inits, etc.). These values were fixed following prior work rather than tuned via hyperparameter search."},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"Yes"},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4.4 reports computing infrastructure (A40 48GB GPUs). Table 3 reports per-search wall-clock time."},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4.1, 4.4. All models and datasets are cited at first mention."},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"AI assistance used for linguistic editing and grammar checking only."},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"N/A"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026rethinking,\ntitle={Rethinking Layer Redundancy: Calibration Matters More Than Search in {LLM} Depth Pruning},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=68P2xV1J5q},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Rethinking Layer Redundancy: Calibration Matters More Than Search in LLM Depth Pruning"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"No"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","NLP engineering experiment","Approaches to low-resource settings"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"No"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"Yes"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"Depth pruning improves the inference efficiency of large language models by removing Transformer blocks. Prior work typically treats layer redundancy as an inherent structural property of pretrained networks, emphasizing importance criteria and search algorithms to identify removable layers. In this study, we empirically investigate depth pruning from a functional perspective. Evaluating representative LLM families across diverse calibration configurations and multiple search algorithms, we show that different configurations produce different pruning patterns. Furthermore, under a fixed calibration configuration, complex search algorithms yield marginal performance improvements over simple one-shot methods, converging to similar pruned subsets. Overall, our results suggest that the calibration configuration plays a substantially larger role than the choice of search algorithm in shaping pruning patterns and calibration perplexity, while contributing comparably to variance in downstream reasoning accuracy. This indicates that future pruning efforts may benefit from prioritizing the calibration configuration over search complexity."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"N/A"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8758a2726a18e064bbe497c29db62ecad7b8fbff.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Efficient Methods for NLP"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"no"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"N/A"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1790696904454,"tcdate":1779085847993,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission1475/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission1475/Authors"],"forum":"68P2xV1J5q","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1475,"cdate":1779085847993,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1790696904454,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"68P2xV1J5q","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.660.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|discal_distributionaware_calibration_for_mathematical_reasoning_under_characterlevel_noisy_inputs"},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.660/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/ZhangZGHHZCLYGW26,\n  author={Bo Zhang and Jiawei Zhang and Cong Gao and Bingxu Han and Minghao Hu and Jun Zhang and Yunbo Cao and Zhunchen Luo and Wen Yao and Guotong Geng and Zhong Wang},\n  title={DisCal: Distribution-Aware Calibration for Mathematical Reasoning Under Character-Level Noisy Inputs},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={14484-14507},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.660/},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2026-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Although large reasoning models (LRMs) exhibit exceptional mathematical reasoning capabilities on clean inputs, their reasoning accuracy drops substantially in the presence of character-level noise such as typographical errors. Critically, their confidence estimates fail to reflect the corresponding decline in reasoning accuracy. While confidence calibration offers a principled solution, existing methods predominantly target clean inputs, leaving noisy scenarios largely unexplored. To address this gap, we propose DisCal (Distribution-aware Calibration), a confidence calibration framework for character-level noisy inputs. DisCal extracts uncertainty signals from both the empirical answer distribution and the model’s predictive distribution, and integrates them via a learned calibrator to produce well-calibrated confidence. Experiments across multiple mathematical reasoning benchmarks demonstrate that DisCal consistently outperforms existing calibration methods under noisy inputs, reducing Expected Calibration Error (ECE) by up to 39.21% and improving Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUROC) by up to 31.44%."},"title":{"value":"DisCal: Distribution-Aware Calibration for Mathematical Reasoning Under Character-Level Noisy Inputs"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Bo Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Jiawei Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Cong Gao","username":""},{"fullname":"Bingxu Han","username":""},{"fullname":"Minghao Hu","username":"~Minghao_Hu2"},{"fullname":"Jun Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Yunbo Cao","username":""},{"fullname":"Zhunchen Luo","username":""},{"fullname":"Wen Yao","username":""},{"fullname":"Guotong Geng","username":""},{"fullname":"Zhong Wang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784642069376,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/ZhangZGHHZCLYGW26"],"tcdate":1784642065570,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Minghao_Hu2"],"forum":"V2XjbaNrYs","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":83861,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784642069376,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"V2XjbaNrYs","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/file/e55edcdb01ac45c839a602f96e09fbcb-Paper-Conference.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/NIPS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"haghtalab|a_unifying_perspective_on_multicalibration_game_dynamics_for_multiobjective_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Nika_Haghtalab2","~Michael_I._Jordan2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Eric_Zhao_0003:"]},"html":{"value":"http://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/e55edcdb01ac45c839a602f96e09fbcb-Abstract-Conference.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/nips/HaghtalabJ023,\n  author={Nika Haghtalab and Michael I. Jordan and Eric Zhao},\n  title={A Unifying Perspective on Multi-Calibration: Game Dynamics for Multi-Objective Learning},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  url={http://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/e55edcdb01ac45c839a602f96e09fbcb-Abstract-Conference.html},\n  booktitle={NeurIPS},\n  crossref={conf/nips/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We provide a unifying framework for the design and analysis of multi-calibrated predictors. By placing the multi-calibration problem in the general setting of multi-objective learning---where learning guarantees must hold simultaneously over a set of distributions and loss functions---we exploit connections to game dynamics to achieve state-of-the-art guarantees for a diverse set of multi-calibration learning problems. In addition to shedding light on existing multi-calibration guarantees and greatly simplifying their analysis, our approach also yields improved guarantees, such as error tolerances that scale with the square-root of group size versus the constant tolerances guaranteed by prior works, and improving the complexity of $k$-class multi-calibration by an exponential factor of $k$ versus Gopalan et al.. Beyond multi-calibration, we use these game dynamics to address emerging considerations in the study of group fairness and multi-distribution learning."},"title":{"value":"A Unifying Perspective on Multi-Calibration: Game Dynamics for Multi-Objective Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Nika Haghtalab","Michael I. Jordan","Eric Zhao"]}},"tmdate":1779859527403,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1747115805114,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Nika_Haghtalab2"],"forum":"gt25rqb1qq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":437470,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1779859527403,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"gt25rqb1qq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.544.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"williams|on_the_impact_of_calibration_data_in_posttraining_quantization_and_pruning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Miles_Williams1","~Nikolaos_Aletras1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.544"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/WilliamsA24,\n  author={Miles Williams and Nikolaos Aletras},\n  title={On the Impact of Calibration Data in Post-training Quantization and Pruning},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={10100-10118},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.544},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2024-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Quantization and pruning form the foundation of compression for neural networks, enabling efficient inference for large language models (LLMs). Recently, various quantization and pruning techniques have demonstrated remarkable performance in a post-training setting. They rely upon calibration data, a small set of unlabeled examples that are used to generate layer activations. However, no prior work has systematically investigated how the calibration data impacts the effectiveness of model compression methods. In this paper, we present the first extensive empirical study on the effect of calibration data upon LLM performance. We trial a variety of quantization and pruning methods, datasets, tasks, and models. Surprisingly, we find substantial variations in downstream task performance, contrasting existing work that suggests a greater level of robustness to the calibration data. Finally, we make a series of recommendations for the effective use of calibration data in LLM quantization and pruning."},"title":{"value":"On the Impact of Calibration Data in Post-training Quantization and Pruning"},"authors":{"value":["Miles Williams","Nikolaos Aletras"]}},"tmdate":1768988936880,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1732650714086,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Miles_Williams1"],"forum":"F4PP8ar0w8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":251470,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768988936880,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"F4PP8ar0w8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/10012124/10177789.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"he|a_unified_and_accurate_subpixel_detector_of_control_points_for_camera_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qing_He:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhiwen_Ji:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peng_Yu:",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2023.3293870"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/HeJYC23,\n  author={Qing He and Zhiwen Ji and Peng Yu and Dongyue Chen},\n  title={A Unified and Accurate Subpixel Detector of Control Points for Camera Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={72},\n  pages={1-12},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2023.3293870}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration is a necessary step in 3-D computer vision, and the accuracy of calibration is largely dependent on the localization precision of the control points of the calibration patterns. However, the sub-pixel localization methods for the commonly used two types of control points, corners of the squares and centers of the circles, are markedly different from each other. In this work, we propose a unified approach to sub-pixel localization for the two types of control points. The approach is derived from theoretical analysis and consists of a closed-form solution, followed by algorithms for practical implementation. Both synthetic datasets and real calibration experiments have been used to test the proposed approach and good results have been obtained. Compared with state-of-the-art techniques, the proposed approach provides a unified and high-precision sub-pixel localization method of the control points for the commonly used calibration patterns."},"title":{"value":"A Unified and Accurate Subpixel Detector of Control Points for Camera Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Qing He","Zhiwen Ji","Peng Yu","Dongyue Chen"]}},"tmdate":1731484999395,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731479856123,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dongyue_Chen1"],"forum":"mO9eLdRFRq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":208243,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1731484999395,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"mO9eLdRFRq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2023 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["multicalibration","multi-objective learning","learning theory","calibration","fairness","games"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/133400983e6f6d1d0d8ec8290e993ef2f8fe9b81.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhaghtalab2023a,\ntitle={A Unifying Perspective on Multi-Calibration: Game Dynamics for Multi-Objective Learning},\nauthor={Nika Haghtalab and Michael Jordan and Eric Zhao},\nbooktitle={Thirty-seventh Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ysqlhW0v26}\n}"},"title":{"value":"A Unifying Perspective on Multi-Calibration: Game Dynamics for Multi-Objective Learning"},"paperhash":{"value":"haghtalab|a_unifying_perspective_on_multicalibration_game_dynamics_for_multiobjective_learning"},"TLDR":{"value":"We use learning in game dynamics as a unifying framework for the design and analysis of multicalibrated predictors with guarantees improving on past results."},"abstract":{"value":"We provide a unifying framework for the design and analysis of multi-calibrated predictors. By placing the multi-calibration problem in the general setting of multi-objective learning---where learning guarantees must hold simultaneously over a set of distributions and loss functions---we exploit connections to game dynamics to achieve state-of-the-art guarantees for a diverse set of multi-calibration learning problems. In addition to shedding light on existing multi-calibration guarantees and greatly simplifying their analysis, our approach also yields improved guarantees, such as error tolerances that scale with the square-root of group size versus the constant tolerances guaranteed by prior works, and improving the complexity of $k$-class multi-calibration by an exponential factor of $k$ versus Gopalan et al.. Beyond multi-calibration, we use these game dynamics to address emerging considerations in the study of group fairness and multi-distribution learning."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/4fc0a02b81ccdac5a5f0ee76f12af77ce9166a7a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Nika_Haghtalab2","~Michael_Jordan1","~Eric_Zhao1"]},"authors":{"value":["Nika Haghtalab","Michael Jordan","Eric Zhao"]}},"tmdate":1698949792469,"pdate":1695326172264,"tcdate":1683832255978,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission15173/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission15173/Authors"],"forum":"ysqlhW0v26","number":15173,"cdate":1683832255978,"mdate":1698949792469,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission15173/-/Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission15173/-/Supplementary_Material_Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission15173/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"odate":1698949792452,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference","id":"ysqlhW0v26","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.11169v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"pandey|refine_and_align_confidence_calibration_through_multiagent_interaction_in_vqa"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ayush_Pandey:","~Jai_Bardhan1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ishita_Jain:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ramya_S._Hebbalaguppe:","~Rohan_Raju_Dhanakshirur1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lovekesh_Vig:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.11169"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2511-11169,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ayush Pandey and Jai Bardhan and Ishita Jain and Ramya S. Hebbalaguppe and Rohan Raju Dhanakshirur and Lovekesh Vig},\n  title={Refine and Align: Confidence Calibration through Multi-Agent Interaction in VQA},\n  year={2025},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1761955200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2511.11169},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.11169}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In the context of Visual Question Answering (VQA) and Agentic AI, calibration refers to how closely an AI system's confidence in its answers reflects their actual correctness. This aspect becomes especially important when such systems operate autonomously and must make decisions under visual uncertainty. While modern VQA systems, powered by advanced vision-language models (VLMs), are increasingly used in high-stakes domains like medical diagnostics and autonomous navigation due to their improved accuracy, the reliability of their confidence estimates remains under-examined. Particularly, these systems often produce overconfident responses. To address this, we introduce AlignVQA, a debate-based multi-agent framework, in which diverse specialized VLM -- each following distinct prompting strategies -- generate candidate answers and then engage in two-stage interaction: generalist agents critique, refine and aggregate these proposals. This debate process yields confidence estimates that more accurately reflect the model's true predictive performance. We find that more calibrated specialized agents produce better aligned confidences. Furthermore, we introduce a novel differentiable calibration-aware loss function called aligncal designed to fine-tune the specialized agents by minimizing an upper bound on the calibration error. This objective explicitly improves the fidelity of each agent's confidence estimates. Empirical results across multiple benchmark VQA datasets substantiate the efficacy of our approach, demonstrating substantial reductions in calibration discrepancies. Furthermore, we propose a novel differentiable calibration-aware loss to fine-tune the specialized agents and improve the quality of their individual confidence estimates based on minimising upper bound calibration error."},"title":{"value":"Refine and Align: Confidence Calibration through Multi-Agent Interaction in VQA"},"authors":{"value":["Ayush Pandey","Jai Bardhan","Ishita Jain","Ramya S. Hebbalaguppe","Rohan Raju Dhanakshirur","Lovekesh Vig"]}},"tmdate":1790344130737,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2511-11169"],"tcdate":1769347178470,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jai_Bardhan1"],"forum":"i66fdjZfW9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":797817,"cdate":1761955200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1790344130737,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"i66fdjZfW9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/24/16/5228/pdf?version=1723529457"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"dai|nmc3d_nonoverlapping_multicamera_calibration_based_on_sparse_3d_map"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Changshuai_Dai:","~Ting_Han4","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Luo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mengyi_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guorong_Cai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinhe_Su:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zheng_Gong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Niansheng_Liu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s24165228"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/DaiHLWCSGL24,\n  author={Changshuai Dai and Ting Han and Yang Luo and Mengyi Wang and Guorong Cai and Jinhe Su and Zheng Gong and Niansheng Liu},\n  title={NMC3D: Non-Overlapping Multi-Camera Calibration Based on Sparse 3D Map},\n  year={2024},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1722470400000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={24},\n  number={16},\n  pages={5228},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s24165228}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With the advancement of computer vision and sensor technologies, many multi-camera systems are being developed for the control, planning, and other functionalities of unmanned systems or robots. The calibration of multi-camera systems determines the accuracy of their operation. However, calibration of multi-camera systems without overlapping parts is inaccurate. Furthermore, the potential of feature matching points and their spatial extent in calculating the extrinsic parameters of multi-camera systems has not yet been fully realized. To this end, we propose a multi-camera calibration algorithm to solve the problem of the high-precision calibration of multi-camera systems without overlapping parts. The calibration of multi-camera systems is simplified to the problem of solving the transformation relationship of extrinsic parameters using a map constructed by multiple cameras. Firstly, the calibration environment map is constructed by running the SLAM algorithm separately for each camera in the multi-camera system in closed-loop motion. Secondly, uniformly distributed matching points are selected among the similar feature points between the maps. Then, these matching points are used to solve the transformation relationship between the multi-camera external parameters. Finally, the reprojection error is minimized to optimize the extrinsic parameter transformation relationship. We conduct comprehensive experiments in multiple scenarios and provide results of the extrinsic parameters for multiple cameras. The results demonstrate that the proposed method accurately calibrates the extrinsic parameters for multiple cameras, even under conditions where the main camera and auxiliary cameras rotate 180°."},"title":{"value":"NMC3D: Non-Overlapping Multi-Camera Calibration Based on Sparse 3D Map"},"authors":{"value":["Changshuai Dai","Ting Han","Yang Luo","Mengyi Wang","Guorong Cai","Jinhe Su","Zheng Gong","Niansheng Liu"]}},"tmdate":1744865548901,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1744865545182,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ting_Han4"],"forum":"RBe80aUwJQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":402082,"cdate":1722470400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744865548901,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"RBe80aUwJQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 October Submission"},"research_area_keywords":{"value":"calibration/uncertainty"},"keywords":{"value":["Confidence calibration","Large language models","Lightweight calibrators"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026effconf,\ntitle={EffConf: Efficient Confidence Calibration for Large Language Models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - October 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Dg6BS7B77e},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"EffConf: Efficient Confidence Calibration for Large Language Models"},"languages_studied":{"value":["eng"]},"contribution_types":{"value":"NLP engineering experiment"},"abstract":{"value":"Enabling large language models (LLMs) to accurately express confidence in their answers is essential for reliable decision-making, yet current LLMs often verbalize high confidence in incorrect answers. Confidence calibration aims to address this mismatch, but a practical calibrator should also be (1) efficient, adding negligible cost to every query, and (2) broadly applicable to both white-box and black-box LLMs, since many of the most capable models are accessible only through APIs. Existing calibration methods do not jointly satisfy these properties: they either probe or fine-tune the LLM, which requires white-box access, or rely on multiple inference passes per query. We propose EffConf, an efficient calibration framework that leverages signals extracted when the LLM generates its verbalized confidence token: the confidence logit vector or hidden state for white-box LLMs, and the confidence log-probability vector returned by APIs for black-box LLMs. These signals come from standard generation, allowing EffConf to operate without fine-tuning or additional inference passes. Experiments across five LLMs and five question-answering benchmarks show that EffConf generally outperforms strong baselines, remains effective with limited training data, and generalizes well to out-of-distribution datasets."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/755976a48987d051b75996f4e16a3bb4e642a970.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Interpretability and Analysis of Models for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission"}},"tmdate":1793696787382,"tcdate":1791800306971,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission5759/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission5759/Authors"],"forum":"Dg6BS7B77e","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":5759,"cdate":1791800306971,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Responsible_NLP_Checklist_Status","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1793696787382,"odate":1791894541772,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","id":"Dg6BS7B77e","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/7083369/10638067/10659118.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhi|unifying_representation_and_calibration_with_3d_foundation_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Weiming_Zhi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haozhan_Tang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianyi_Zhang_0014:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Matthew_Johnson-Roberson:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3451396"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/ZhiTZJ24,\n  author={Weiming Zhi and Haozhan Tang and Tianyi Zhang and Matthew Johnson-Roberson},\n  title={Unifying Representation and Calibration With 3D Foundation Models},\n  year={2024},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1727740800000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={9},\n  number={10},\n  pages={8953-8960},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3451396}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Representing the environment is a central challenge in robotics, and is essential for effective decision-making. Traditionally, before capturing images with a manipulator-mounted camera, users need to calibrate the camera using a specific external marker, such as a checkerboard or AprilTag. However, recent advances in computer vision have led to the development of 3D foundation models. These are large, pre-trained neural networks that can establish fast and accurate multi-view correspondences with very few images, even in the absence of rich visual features. This paper advocates for the integration of 3D foundation models into scene representation approaches for robotic systems equipped with manipulator-mounted RGB cameras. Specifically, we propose the Joint Calibration and Representation (JCR) method. JCR uses RGB images, captured by a manipulator-mounted camera, to simultaneously construct an environmental representation and calibrate the camera relative to the robot's end-effector, in the absence of specific calibration markers. The resulting 3D environment representation is aligned with the robot's coordinate frame and maintains physically accurate scales. We demonstrate that JCR can build effective scene representations using a low-cost RGB camera attached to a manipulator, without prior calibration."},"title":{"value":"Unifying Representation and Calibration With 3D Foundation Models"},"authors":{"value":["Weiming Zhi","Haozhan Tang","Tianyi Zhang","Matthew Johnson-Roberson"]}},"tmdate":1763254437196,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/ral/ZhiTZJ24"],"tcdate":1763254426779,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Weiming_Zhi1"],"forum":"LY0az4NiU9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":704140,"cdate":1727740800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1763254437196,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"LY0az4NiU9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CLIP/FAIMI/EPIMI@MICCAI 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-45249-9_13.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CLIP-WS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"lara|towards_unraveling_calibration_biases_in_medical_image_analysis"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:María_Agustina_Ricci_Lara:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Candelaria_Mosquera:","~Enzo_Ferrante1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rodrigo_Echeveste:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-45249-9_13"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/clip-ws/LaraMFE23,\n  author={María Agustina Ricci Lara and Candelaria Mosquera and Enzo Ferrante and Rodrigo Echeveste},\n  title={Towards Unraveling Calibration Biases in Medical Image Analysis},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={132-141},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-45249-9_13},\n  booktitle={CLIP/FAIMI/EPIMI@MICCAI},\n  crossref={conf/clip-ws/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In recent years the development of artificial intelligence (AI) for medical image analysis has gained enormous momentum. At the same time, a large body of work has shown that AI systems can systematically and unfairly discriminate against certain populations in various application scenarios, motivating the emergence of algorithmic fairness studies. Most research on healthcare algorithmic fairness to date has focused on the assessment of biases in terms of classical discrimination metrics such as AUC and accuracy. Potential biases in terms of model calibration, however, have only recently begun to be evaluated. This is especially important when working with clinical decision support systems, as predictive uncertainty is key to optimally evaluate and combine multiple sources of information. Here we study discrimination and calibration biases in models trained for automatic detection of malignant dermatological conditions from skin lesions images. Importantly, we show how several typically employed calibration metrics are systematically biased with respect to sample sizes, and how this can lead to erroneous conclusions if not taken into consideration. This is of particular relevance to fairness studies, where data imbalance results in drastic sample size differences between demographic sub-groups, which could act as confounders."},"title":{"value":"Towards Unraveling Calibration Biases in Medical Image Analysis"},"authors":{"value":["María Agustina Ricci Lara","Candelaria Mosquera","Enzo Ferrante","Rodrigo Echeveste"]}},"tmdate":1731510128956,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731510116285,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Enzo_Ferrante1"],"forum":"bxnrmMDTvD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":230575,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731510128956,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bxnrmMDTvD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Instrumentation and Measurement"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/19/10764799/11237201.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|defocused_camera_calibration_with_selfencoded_phaseshifting_circular_grating_arrays"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2025.3629829"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration plays an important role in machine vision systems as it directly impacts the measurement accuracy. However, traditional calibration methods usually perform poorly when calibrating defocused cameras. To overcome this issue, this article proposes an effective defocused camera calibration method that enables feature detection with high accuracy from blurred target images. This study designs a new calibration target called the “self-encoded phase-shifting circular grating (PCG) arrays.” Phase shifts of each PCG are used to encode its index, and a robust decoding algorithm is developed to identify each PCG. The phase map is recovered via phase-shifting algorithms, and the PCG centers are extracted using minimum projection and least-square fitting algorithms. Based on these PCG centers and their indices, the projection mapping between the 3-D world and 2-D image can be established for accurate estimation of internal parameters. Compared with traditional methods, our method uses more valuable phase points to precisely locate the PCG centers and uses phase shifts to correctly identify the PCG indices. Simulations and experiments have been conducted to validate the proposed method, demonstrating its superior performance over traditional calibration methods."},"title":{"value":"Defocused Camera Calibration With Self-Encoded Phase-Shifting Circular Grating Arrays"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yuwei Wang"},{"fullname":"Yu Wang"},{"fullname":"Mengze Du"},{"fullname":"Fei Wang"},{"fullname":"Kun Li"},{"fullname":"Lu Liu","username":"~Lu_Liu36"}]}},"tmdate":1789089539389,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tim.2025.3629829"],"tcdate":1768459142659,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Lu_Liu36"],"forum":"xFax2vXwxI","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":29053,"cdate":1762802190385,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789089539389,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"xFax2vXwxI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"obdrálek|autocalibration_of_camera_intrinsics_and_extrinsics_using_lidar_and_motion"},"authorids":{"value":["~Štěpán_Obdržálek1","~Jiri_Matas3"]},"html":{"value":"https://cmp.felk.cvut.cz/~xobdrzal/publications/obdrzalek25iros.pdf"},"abstract":{"value":"A novel camera autocalibration method is presented. Any camera model can be calibrated, and no calibration targets like checkerboards are used. The method requires the camera to be mounted on a lidar-equipped moving platform travelling through a structured environment along a known path.\n    The primary reason for cross-modal camera calibration is not to solve the sensor fusion problem, but to tap the huge amount of accurate metric data points available from the lidar. The amount of measurements is easily four orders of magnitude higher than in checkerboard based methods. This leads to improved estimation accuracy, especially of higher-order distortion coefficients. \n    In a multi-camera setup, the lidar additionally defines a common reference coordinate system for all cameras.\nCompared to the majority of published methods on camera-lidar autocalibration, (i) our calibration procedure relies on motion features, (ii) the hard-to-obtain-accurately lidar-lidar and lidar-image feature correspondences are not required, and (iii) both camera extrinsics and intrinsics, including complex distortion models, are autocalibrated.\n    Experiments show that the calibration accuracy reaches or exceeds the accuracy of methods relying on calibration targets."},"title":{"value":"Auto-calibration of Camera Intrinsics and Extrinsics using Lidar and Motion"},"authors":{"value":["Štěpán Obdržálek","Jiri Matas"]}},"tmdate":1763038138783,"pdate":1760911200000,"tcdate":1763038138783,"writers":["~Štěpán_Obdržálek1","~Jiri_Matas3"],"signatures":["~Štěpán_Obdržálek1"],"forum":"32cb5V9Jkj","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":41250,"cdate":1763038138783,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1763038138783,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"32cb5V9Jkj","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.04187v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"persic|spatiotemporal_multisensor_calibration_based_on_gaussian_processes_moving_object_tracking"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Juraj_Persic:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luka_Petrovic:","~Ivan_Marković1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ivan_Petrovic:"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1904.04187"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1904-04187,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Juraj Persic and Luka Petrovic and Ivan Markovic and Ivan Petrovic},\n  title={Spatio-Temporal Multisensor Calibration Based on Gaussian Processes Moving Object Tracking},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1904.04187},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1904.04187}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Perception is one of the key abilities of autonomous mobile robotic systems, which often relies on fusion of heterogeneous sensors. Although this heterogeneity presents a challenge for sensor calibration, it is also the main prospect for reliability and robustness of autonomous systems. In this paper, we propose a method for multisensor calibration based on Gaussian processes (GPs) estimated moving object trajectories, resulting with temporal and extrinsic parameters. The appealing properties of the proposed temporal calibration method are: coordinate frame invariance, thus avoiding prior extrinsic calibration, theoretically grounded batch state estimation and interpolation using GPs, computational efficiency with O(n) complexity, leveraging data already available in autonomous robot platforms, and the end result enabling 3D point-to-point extrinsic multisensor calibration. The proposed method is validated both in simulations and real-world experiments. For real-world experiment we evaluated the method on two multisensor systems: an externally triggered stereo camera, thus having temporal ground truth readily available, and a heterogeneous combination of a camera and motion capture system. The results show that the estimated time delays are accurate up to a fraction of the fastest sensor sampling time."},"title":{"value":"Spatio-Temporal Multisensor Calibration Based on Gaussian Processes Moving Object Tracking"},"authors":{"value":["Juraj Persic","Luka Petrovic","Ivan Markovic","Ivan Petrovic"]}},"tmdate":1731497111755,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1731497060129,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ivan_Marković1"],"forum":"2vDVKUQwn6","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":225239,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731497111755,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2vDVKUQwn6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.04187v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"persic|spatiotemporal_multisensor_calibration_based_on_gaussian_processes_moving_object_tracking"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Juraj_Persic:","~Luka_Petrovic1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ivan_Markovic:","~Ivan_Petrovic1"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1904.04187"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1904-04187,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Juraj Persic and Luka Petrovic and Ivan Markovic and Ivan Petrovic},\n  title={Spatio-Temporal Multisensor Calibration Based on Gaussian Processes Moving Object Tracking},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1904.04187},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1904.04187}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Perception is one of the key abilities of autonomous mobile robotic systems, which often relies on fusion of heterogeneous sensors. Although this heterogeneity presents a challenge for sensor calibration, it is also the main prospect for reliability and robustness of autonomous systems. In this paper, we propose a method for multisensor calibration based on Gaussian processes (GPs) estimated moving object trajectories, resulting with temporal and extrinsic parameters. The appealing properties of the proposed temporal calibration method are: coordinate frame invariance, thus avoiding prior extrinsic calibration, theoretically grounded batch state estimation and interpolation using GPs, computational efficiency with O(n) complexity, leveraging data already available in autonomous robot platforms, and the end result enabling 3D point-to-point extrinsic multisensor calibration. The proposed method is validated both in simulations and real-world experiments. For real-world experiment we evaluated the method on two multisensor systems: an externally triggered stereo camera, thus having temporal ground truth readily available, and a heterogeneous combination of a camera and motion capture system. The results show that the estimated time delays are accurate up to a fraction of the fastest sensor sampling time."},"title":{"value":"Spatio-Temporal Multisensor Calibration Based on Gaussian Processes Moving Object Tracking"},"authors":{"value":["Juraj Persic","Luka Petrovic","Ivan Markovic","Ivan Petrovic"]}},"tmdate":1731488282587,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1731193101122,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Luka_Petrovic1"],"forum":"IJ0xyIWDW2","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":177370,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1731488282587,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"IJ0xyIWDW2","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"DL4C @ ICLR 2025"},"keywords":{"value":["Code Generation","Large Language Model","Code Preference"]},"Funding":{"value":"Yes, the presenting author of this submission falls under ICLR’s funding aims, and funding would significantly impact their ability to attend the workshop in person."},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently demonstrated remarkable coding capabilities. However, assessing code generation from verifiable properties and aligning it with developer preferences remains a challenge. In this paper, we explore two key questions under the new challenge of code preference learning: \\textit{(i)} How to train models to predict meaningful preferences for code; and \\textit{(ii)} how do code preferences based on verifiers, human, and neural models align with each other? To this end, we introduce \\textsc{CodeFavor}, an open recipe to train pairwise code preference models using synthetic code evolution, including code commits and code critiques. We evaluate code preferences via \\textsc{CodePrefBench}, a new benchmark with 1364 rigorously curated code preference tasks to cover three verifiable properties: correctness, efficiency, and security, along with human preference. Our evaluation shows that \\textsc{CodeFavor} holistically improves model-based code preferences by up to $28.8%$. Our comprehensive controlled experiments also validate the design choices in \\textsc{CodeFavor}. Furthermore, we quantified the cost and limitations of human-based code preference: \\textit{(i)} Despite spending 23 person-minutes per task, $15\\sim 40%$ of tasks remain unsolved; and \\textit{(ii)} human preference is the most accurate on code correctness while underperforming model-based preferences on non-functional objectives."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nliu2025do,\ntitle={Do {LLM}s Understand Code Preference? Training Code Preference Models via Synthetic Code Evolution},\nauthor={Jiawei Liu and Thanh V Nguyen and Mingyue Shang and Hantian Ding and Xiaopeng Li and Yu Yu and Varun Kumar and Zijian Wang},\nbooktitle={ICLR 2025 Third Workshop on Deep Learning for Code},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=eSwlZCBGV4}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Do LLMs Understand Code Preference? Training Code Preference Models via Synthetic Code Evolution"},"Anonymization":{"value":"This submission has been anonymized for double-blind review via the removal of identifying information such as names, affiliations, and identifying URLs."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/09d4ec5ded0d42a5e00bad599ccb4d54066deb70.pdf"},"Format":{"value":"Yes, the presenting author will definitely attend in person because they attending ICLR for other complementary reasons."},"Presenter":{"value":"~Zijian_Wang1"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|do_llms_understand_code_preference_training_code_preference_models_via_synthetic_code_evolution"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jiawei_Liu11","~Thanh_V_Nguyen1","~Mingyue_Shang1","~Hantian_Ding1","~Xiaopeng_Li1","~Yu_Yu3","~Varun_Kumar3","~Zijian_Wang1"]},"Track":{"value":"long paper (up to 9 pages)"},"authors":{"value":["Jiawei Liu","Thanh V Nguyen","Mingyue Shang","Hantian Ding","Xiaopeng Li","Yu Yu","Varun Kumar","Zijian Wang"]}},"tmdate":1741234620982,"pdate":1741234620970,"tcdate":1739428668279,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission54/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission54/Authors"],"forum":"eSwlZCBGV4","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":54,"cdate":1739428668279,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Edit"],"mdate":1741234620982,"odate":1741234620970,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C","id":"eSwlZCBGV4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 1991"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel2/561/4454/00174464.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/1991"},"paperhash":{"value":"dias|improving_camera_calibration_by_using_multiple_frames_in_handeye_robotic_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jorge_Dias_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Aníbal_T._de_Almeida:","~Helder_J._Araujo1","~Jorge_Batista1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.1991.174464"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/DiasAAB91,\n  author={Jorge Dias and Aníbal T. de Almeida and Helder Araújo and Jorge Batista},\n  title={Improving camera calibration by using multiple frames in hand-eye robotic systems},\n  year={1991},\n  cdate={662688000000},\n  pages={285-291},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.1991.174464},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/1991}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Addresses the geometric modelling aspects related with the use of a pair of video cameras mounted on a six degrees-of-freedom manipulator in an hand-eye configuration. The information obtained by these sensors shall be used to generate the depth map of the environment where the manipulator works. The procedure to determining the image formation parameters is known by camera calibration and a method to optimize these calibration parameters is presented. After presenting the calibration technique used, the authors develop a calibration matrix invariant to movement. The optimization process is applied to this matrix to obtain a better performance in the numerical data.<>"},"title":{"value":"Improving camera calibration by using multiple frames in hand-eye robotic systems"},"authors":{"value":["Jorge Dias","Aníbal T. de Almeida","Helder Araújo","Jorge Batista"]}},"tmdate":1785102923481,"pdate":662688000000,"tcdate":1752675015931,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Helder_J._Araujo1"],"forum":"1yDSviqJmq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":569988,"cdate":662688000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1785102923481,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"1yDSviqJmq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/9195928/09064831.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|error_correctable_handeye_calibration_for_stripelaser_visionguided_robotics"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liang_Zhang_0037:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jian-Zhou_Zhang:","~Xiaoyi_Jiang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Biao_Liang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2020.2987492"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/ZhangZJL20,\n  author={Liang Zhang and Jian-Zhou Zhang and Xiaoyi Jiang and Biao Liang},\n  title={Error Correctable Hand-Eye Calibration for Stripe-Laser Vision-Guided Robotics},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={69},\n  number={10},\n  pages={8314-8327},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2020.2987492}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The stripe-laser vision guidance technology has received more and more attention in industry and academia. The hand-eye calibration technology plays an important role in the applications of the stripe-laser vision-guided robotics. Most existing hand-eye calibration methods impose orthogonality constraints. However, the orthogonality in hand-eye relationship may not be true when the stripe-laser sensor has errors. This makes the final guiding error inevitable. In this article, an error correctable (EC) hand-eye calibration method based on the linear properties of the central projection for the stripe-laser vision sensor guidance system is proposed. In this method, the measurements of a vision sensor with errors can be corrected to the right tool center point coordinates. Experiments involving synthetic and real data confirm that the proposed hand-eye calibration method is robust to the vision sensor errors."},"title":{"value":"Error Correctable Hand-Eye Calibration for Stripe-Laser Vision-Guided Robotics"},"authors":{"value":["Liang Zhang","Jian-Zhou Zhang","Xiaoyi Jiang","Biao Liang"]}},"tmdate":1754258747223,"pdate":1577836800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tim/ZhangZJL20"],"tcdate":1754258691533,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiaoyi_Jiang2"],"forum":"5D5xk297d6","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":612987,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1754258747223,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5D5xk297d6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Greeks in AI 2025 Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/690d075acbd235cd19559d790129160fbb8b0a9f.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Automatic calibration","deep Q-learning","parameter estimation","reinforcement learning"]},"venueid":{"value":"greeksin.ai/Greeks_in_AI/2025/Symposium"},"paperhash":{"value":"dimitriou|efficient_deep_qlearning_for_industrial_equipment_calibration_in_elevator_manufacturing"},"authorids":{"value":["~Nikolaos_Dimitriou1","~Paschalis_Charalampous1","~Theofilos_D._Mastos1","~Dimitris_Tzovaras1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Industrial equipment calibration is an essential element for the proper functioning of any production plant. Without frequent and proper calibration, the quality and efficiency of the overall production process are threatened. Despite its significance, it is often performed manually based on qualitative measures that may lead to suboptimal behavior. In this article, we propose a deep reinforcement learning (RL)-based methodology to automate the calibration process and evaluate it in an elevator control use case. Moreover, to overcome data scarcity we develop a simulation environment based on generative modeling that creates synthetic RL episodes. The proposed methodology relies on a minimal set of sensors and actuators, i.e., a webcam, an Arduino board, three stepper motors, and an edge computational unit. Nevertheless, experimental evaluations indicate that it can perform in real-time applications achieving accurate calibration results."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\ndimitriou2025efficient,\ntitle={Efficient Deep Q-Learning for Industrial Equipment Calibration in Elevator Manufacturing},\nauthor={Nikolaos Dimitriou and Paschalis Charalampous and Theofilos D. Mastos and Dimitris Tzovaras},\nbooktitle={Greeks in AI Symposium 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=nKznBIdUFg}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Efficient Deep Q-Learning for Industrial Equipment Calibration in Elevator Manufacturing"},"authors":{"value":["Nikolaos Dimitriou","Paschalis Charalampous","Theofilos D. Mastos","Dimitris Tzovaras"]}},"tmdate":1750690429264,"pdate":1750690429232,"tcdate":1748343656320,"writers":["greeksin.ai/Greeks_in_AI/2025/Symposium","greeksin.ai/Greeks_in_AI/2025/Symposium/Submission70/Authors"],"signatures":["greeksin.ai/Greeks_in_AI/2025/Symposium/Submission70/Authors"],"forum":"nKznBIdUFg","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":70,"cdate":1748343656320,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["greeksin.ai/Greeks_in_AI/2025/Symposium/-/Submission","greeksin.ai/Greeks_in_AI/2025/Symposium/-/Post_Submission","greeksin.ai/Greeks_in_AI/2025/Symposium/-/Edit"],"mdate":1750690429264,"odate":1750690429232,"domain":"greeksin.ai/Greeks_in_AI/2025/Symposium","id":"nKznBIdUFg","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ITSC 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10420842/10420843/10422384.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ITSC/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"dreissig|on_the_calibration_of_uncertainty_estimation_in_lidarbased_semantic_segmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mariella_Dreissig:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Florian_Piewak:","~Joschka_Boedecker1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC57777.2023.10422384"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/itsc/DreissigPB23,\n  author={Mariella Dreissig and Florian Piewak and Joschka Boedecker},\n  title={On the Calibration of Uncertainty Estimation in LiDAR-Based Semantic Segmentation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={4798-4805},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC57777.2023.10422384},\n  booktitle={ITSC},\n  crossref={conf/itsc/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The confidence calibration of deep learning-based perception models plays a crucial role in their reliability. Especially in the context of autonomous driving, downstream tasks like prediction and planning depend on accurate confidence estimates. In the point-wise multiclass classification task of sematic segmentation the model naturally has to deal with heavy class imbalances. Due to their underrepresentation, the confidence calibration of classes with smaller instances is challenging but essential, especially for safety reasons. We propose a metric to measure the confidence calibration quality of a semantic segmentation model with respect to individual classes. It utilizes the computation of sparsification curves based on uncertainty estimates. The insights provided by a careful analysis using the proposed metric uncover potential improvements of any semantic segmentation model and allow for a specific improvement of the model's classification and calibration performance."},"title":{"value":"On the Calibration of Uncertainty Estimation in LiDAR-Based Semantic Segmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Mariella Dreissig","Florian Piewak","Joschka Boedecker"]}},"tmdate":1744374273618,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1744374254962,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Joschka_Bödecker1"],"forum":"4p1M2HP5lb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":392318,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744374273618,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4p1M2HP5lb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"J. Syst. Softw. 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/JSS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"tuna|do_code_reviews_lead_to_fewer_code_smells"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Erdem_Tuna:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Carolyn_Seaman:","~Eray_Tüzün1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jss.2024.112101"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/jss/TunaST24,\n  author={Erdem Tuna and Carolyn Seaman and Eray Tüzün},\n  title={Do code reviews lead to fewer code smells?},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={J. Syst. Softw.},\n  volume={215},\n  pages={112101},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jss.2024.112101}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•Code review process smells show weak correlation with code smells.•Code smell density of a file is not influenced by smelly code reviews.•Focus group participants reinforced that code reviews should have a positive influence on internal code quality."},"title":{"value":"Do code reviews lead to fewer code smells?"},"authors":{"value":["Erdem Tuna","Carolyn Seaman","Eray Tüzün"]}},"tmdate":1752997332640,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1752997319018,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Eray_Tüzün1"],"forum":"zTMlqeIDa4","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":576417,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1752997332640,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zTMlqeIDa4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Intelligent Transportation Systems"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6979/10139322/10070382.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|globally_optimal_robust_radar_calibration_in_intelligent_transportation_systems"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TITS.2023.3251183"},"abstract":{"value":"Radar is among the most popular sensors in modern Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITSs), enabling weather-robust perception. The orientation and position of the traffic radar relative to the ITS coordinate system are necessary for the perception fusion in ITSs. However, due to the unknown target association, sparseness and noisiness of traffic radar measurements, the robust and accurate extrinsic calibration of traffic radar is challenging. In this paper, we propose a targetless traffic radar calibration method based on GPS to overcome the inconvenience during ITS operation, because the installation of a dedicated calibration target on the highway is impractical and dangerous. On the other hand, the high-precision GPS device installed on the moving vehicle can provide traffic radar with accurate positioning information of the detection target. Furthermore, during the optimization process of extrinsic calibration, we propose a globally optimal registration method, which is robust to noise and outliers in radar measurements, and is called Gaussian Mixture Robust Branch and Bound (GMRBnB). Specifically, we first construct the robust objective function by utilizing the Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM). Then, we derive novel relaxation bounds and present the GMRBnB algorithm that overcomes the susceptibility to local minima and the dependence on initialization of traditional optimization methods. Compared with existing methods, extensive experiments in synthetic and real-world data demonstrate that our method is not only globally optimal, but also more accurate and robust."},"title":{"value":"Globally Optimal Robust Radar Calibration in Intelligent Transportation Systems"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Xinyi Li","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Xinyi%20Li"},{"fullname":"Yinlong Liu","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Yinlong%20Liu"},{"fullname":"Venkatnarayanan Lakshminarasimhan","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Venkatnarayanan%20Lakshminarasimhan"},{"fullname":"Hu Cao","username":"~Hu_Cao1"},{"fullname":"Feihu Zhang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Feihu%20Zhang"},{"fullname":"Alois Knoll","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Alois%20Knoll"}]}},"tmdate":1781578079115,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tits.2023.3251183"],"tcdate":1781578074900,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Hu_Cao1"],"forum":"kNXluQDu0b","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":76435,"cdate":1678816286362,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1781578079115,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"kNXluQDu0b","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Intell. Transp. Syst. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6979/10139322/10070382.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TITS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|globally_optimal_robust_radar_calibration_in_intelligent_transportation_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinyi_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yinlong_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Venkatnarayanan_Lakshminarasimhan:","~Hu_Cao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Feihu_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alois_C._Knoll:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TITS.2023.3251183"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tits/LiLLCZK23,\n  author={Xinyi Li and Yinlong Liu and Venkatnarayanan Lakshminarasimhan and Hu Cao and Feihu Zhang and Alois C. Knoll},\n  title={Globally Optimal Robust Radar Calibration in Intelligent Transportation Systems},\n  year={2023},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1685577600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Intell. Transp. Syst.},\n  volume={24},\n  number={6},\n  pages={6082-6095},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TITS.2023.3251183}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Radar is among the most popular sensors in modern Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITSs), enabling weather-robust perception. The orientation and position of the traffic radar relative to the ITS coordinate system are necessary for the perception fusion in ITSs. However, due to the unknown target association, sparseness and noisiness of traffic radar measurements, the robust and accurate extrinsic calibration of traffic radar is challenging. In this paper, we propose a targetless traffic radar calibration method based on GPS to overcome the inconvenience during ITS operation, because the installation of a dedicated calibration target on the highway is impractical and dangerous. On the other hand, the high-precision GPS device installed on the moving vehicle can provide traffic radar with accurate positioning information of the detection target. Furthermore, during the optimization process of extrinsic calibration, we propose a globally optimal registration method, which is robust to noise and outliers in radar measurements, and is called Gaussian Mixture Robust Branch and Bound (GMRBnB). Specifically, we first construct the robust objective function by utilizing the Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM). Then, we derive novel relaxation bounds and present the GMRBnB algorithm that overcomes the susceptibility to local minima and the dependence on initialization of traditional optimization methods. Compared with existing methods, extensive experiments in synthetic and real-world data demonstrate that our method is not only globally optimal, but also more accurate and robust."},"title":{"value":"Globally Optimal Robust Radar Calibration in Intelligent Transportation Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Xinyi Li","Yinlong Liu","Venkatnarayanan Lakshminarasimhan","Hu Cao","Feihu Zhang","Alois C. Knoll"]}},"tmdate":1733566452307,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1733566442820,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hu_Cao1"],"forum":"JU3bDNJl3X","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":253728,"cdate":1685577600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1733566452307,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"JU3bDNJl3X","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10160211/10160212/10161153.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhao|insitu_mechanical_calibration_for_visionbased_tactile_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Can_Zhao:","~Jieji_Ren1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hexi_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daolin_Ma:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA48891.2023.10161153"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/ZhaoRYM23,\n  author={Can Zhao and Jieji Ren and Hexi Yu and Daolin Ma},\n  title={In-situ Mechanical Calibration for Vision-based Tactile Sensors},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={10387-10393},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA48891.2023.10161153},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes a novel approach to conduct routine calibration for the changing mechanical parameters over time of a vision-based tactile sensor, without disassembling its overall structure, i.e., in-situ mechanical calibration. Calibration for mechanical parameters, Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio, of a tactile sensor's sensing elastomer, is crucial for its force perception capabilities. However, there are few methods that can retrieve values of these parameters both accurately and conveniently. To address this problem, we propose an in-situ approach to calibrate mechanical parameters other than the verbose traditional evaluation process. This method incorporates the deformation sensing capability of the sensor, the accurate force sensing capability of a force/torque sensor, and most importantly, the deformation-force relation-ship for an indentation with embedded mechanical parameters of the elastomers. We also present the indentation test setup and the complete pipeline to extract Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio from experimental results. We validate the method by comparing the indentation depths simulated through finite element analysis (FEA) using the cali-brated parameters with the indentation depths measured in real experiments. Furthermore, superior contact force distribution can be achieved with the accurate mechanical parameters. The proposed method provides the theoretical basis for accurate, lifelong routine calibration, whether weekly or even daily, which can enhance the applications of tactile sensors in real manipulation scenarios."},"title":{"value":"In-situ Mechanical Calibration for Vision-based Tactile Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Can Zhao","Jieji Ren","Hexi Yu","Daolin Ma"]}},"tmdate":1730990522747,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1730990514441,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jieji_Ren1"],"forum":"9tgOFlcrDP","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":173967,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730990522747,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9tgOFlcrDP","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICUIMC 2013"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"park|detecting_source_code_similarity_using_code_abstraction"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2448556.2448630"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icuimc/ParkKCHCC13,\n  author={Seongsoo Park and Seungcheol Ko and Jungsik Choi and Hwansoo Han and Seong-je Cho and Jongmoo Choi},\n  title={Detecting source code similarity using code abstraction},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  pages={74},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/2448556.2448630},\n  booktitle={ICUIMC},\n  crossref={conf/icuimc/2013}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Various approaches have been proposed to develop effective methods to measure program similarity. Even commercial tools and freeware tools are available for measuring program similarity based on source code comparison. These tools are quite useful to handle small to middle scale software products, but limited for large scale software products. In addition, these tools may report similarity measures with less credentials for the source code either obfuscated by malicious users or generated by automatic program template generation tools. To handle large scale software, more drastic measures should be provided. In this paper, we propose an automatic abstraction method to summarize source code. We eliminate a large portion of source code which is less relevant to similarity comparison. With this abstraction, our similarity comparison method can provide more robust measures for obfuscation and automatic code generation. We evaluate our abstraction method by running through source comparison tool --- MOSS, a web-based similarity detection tool. According to our experiment with multiple versions of Apache HTTP server, Putty SSH client, and Lighttpd server, our abstraction method reports quite reliable results with abstracted source code, which are only 23--35% of original source code. As the execution time for pattern match is linearly proportional to the length of the source code, our method can reduce the execution time as much as the percentage of source code reduction."},"title":{"value":"Detecting source code similarity using code abstraction"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Seongsoo Park","username":""},{"fullname":"Seungcheol Ko","username":""},{"fullname":"Jungsik Choi","username":""},{"fullname":"Hwansoo Han","username":"~Hwansoo_Han1"},{"fullname":"Seong-je Cho","username":""},{"fullname":"Jongmoo Choi","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1778142261013,"pdate":1388448000000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icuimc/ParkKCHCC13"],"tcdate":1778142222635,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Hwansoo_Han1"],"forum":"WXqXHmGvgP","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":11426,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1778142261013,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"WXqXHmGvgP","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.06844v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|dynamic_correlation_learning_and_regularization_for_multilabel_confidence_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tianshui_Chen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weihang_Wang_0008:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_Pu_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinghui_Qin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhijing_Yang:","~Jie_Liu33","~Liang_Lin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.06844"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2407-06844,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Tianshui Chen and Weihang Wang and Tao Pu and Jinghui Qin and Zhijing Yang and Jie Liu and Liang Lin},\n  title={Dynamic Correlation Learning and Regularization for Multi-Label Confidence Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2407.06844},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.06844}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Modern visual recognition models often display overconfidence due to their reliance on complex deep neural networks and one-hot target supervision, resulting in unreliable confidence scores that necessitate calibration. While current confidence calibration techniques primarily address single-label scenarios, there is a lack of focus on more practical and generalizable multi-label contexts. This paper introduces the Multi-Label Confidence Calibration (MLCC) task, aiming to provide well-calibrated confidence scores in multi-label scenarios. Unlike single-label images, multi-label images contain multiple objects, leading to semantic confusion and further unreliability in confidence scores. Existing single-label calibration methods, based on label smoothing, fail to account for category correlations, which are crucial for addressing semantic confusion, thereby yielding sub-optimal performance. To overcome these limitations, we propose the Dynamic Correlation Learning and Regularization (DCLR) algorithm, which leverages multi-grained semantic correlations to better model semantic confusion for adaptive regularization. DCLR learns dynamic instance-level and prototype-level similarities specific to each category, using these to measure semantic correlations across different categories. With this understanding, we construct adaptive label vectors that assign higher values to categories with strong correlations, thereby facilitating more effective regularization. We establish an evaluation benchmark, re-implementing several advanced confidence calibration algorithms and applying them to leading multi-label recognition (MLR) models for fair comparison. Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate the superior performance of DCLR over existing methods in providing reliable confidence scores in multi-label scenarios."},"title":{"value":"Dynamic Correlation Learning and Regularization for Multi-Label Confidence Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Tianshui Chen","Weihang Wang","Tao Pu","Jinghui Qin","Zhijing Yang","Jie Liu","Liang Lin"]}},"tmdate":1779777819226,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727508595183,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Tianshui_Chen1"],"forum":"7ycUJ2TyGc","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":100213,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1779777819226,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"7ycUJ2TyGc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Image Process. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/83/10346232/10663362.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|dynamic_correlation_learning_and_regularization_for_multilabel_confidence_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tianshui_Chen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weihang_Wang_0008:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_Pu_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinghui_Qin:","~Zhijing_Yang1","~Jie_Liu33","~Liang_Lin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2024.3448248"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tip/ChenWPQYLL24,\n  author={Tianshui Chen and Weihang Wang and Tao Pu and Jinghui Qin and Zhijing Yang and Jie Liu and Liang Lin},\n  title={Dynamic Correlation Learning and Regularization for Multi-Label Confidence Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Image Process.},\n  volume={33},\n  pages={4811-4823},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2024.3448248}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Modern visual recognition models often display overconfidence due to their reliance on complex deep neural networks and one-hot target supervision, resulting in unreliable confidence scores that necessitate calibration. While current confidence calibration techniques primarily address single-label scenarios, there is a lack of focus on more practical and generalizable multi-label contexts. This paper introduces the Multi-Label Confidence Calibration (MLCC) task, aiming to provide well-calibrated confidence scores in multi-label scenarios. Unlike single-label images, multi-label images contain multiple objects, leading to semantic confusion and further unreliability in confidence scores. Existing single-label calibration methods, based on label smoothing, fail to account for category correlations, which are crucial for addressing semantic confusion, thereby yielding sub-optimal performance. To overcome these limitations, we propose the Dynamic Correlation Learning and Regularization (DCLR) algorithm, which leverages multi-grained semantic correlations to better model semantic confusion for adaptive regularization. DCLR learns dynamic instance-level and prototype-level similarities specific to each category, using these to measure semantic correlations across different categories. With this understanding, we construct adaptive label vectors that assign higher values to categories with strong correlations, thereby facilitating more effective regularization. We establish an evaluation benchmark, re-implementing several advanced confidence calibration algorithms and applying them to leading multi-label recognition (MLR) models for fair comparison. Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate the superior performance of DCLR over existing methods in providing reliable confidence scores in multi-label scenarios."},"title":{"value":"Dynamic Correlation Learning and Regularization for Multi-Label Confidence Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Tianshui Chen","Weihang Wang","Tao Pu","Jinghui Qin","Zhijing Yang","Jie Liu","Liang Lin"]}},"tmdate":1779777817963,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727508595113,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Tianshui_Chen1"],"forum":"SP4akyj9wN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":100212,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1779777817963,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SP4akyj9wN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISVC (1) 2007"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-540-76858-6_23.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISVC/2007"},"paperhash":{"value":"wu|robust_selfcalibration_from_single_image_using_ransac"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qi_Wu_0014:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Te-Chin_Shao:","~Tsuhan_Chen3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-76858-6_23"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/isvc/WuSC07,\n  author={Qi Wu and Te-Chin Shao and Tsuhan Chen},\n  title={Robust Self-calibration from Single Image Using RANSAC},\n  year={2007},\n  cdate={1167609600000},\n  pages={230-237},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-76858-6_23},\n  booktitle={ISVC (1)},\n  crossref={conf/isvc/2007-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, a novel approach for the self-calibration of single image is proposed. Unlike most existing methods, we can obtain the intrinsic and extrinsic parameters based on the information of restricted image points from single image. First, we show how the vanishing point, vanishing line and foot-to-head plane homology can be used to obtain the calibration parameters and then we show our approach how to efficiently adopt RANSAC to estimate them. In addition, noise reduction is proposed to handle the measurement uncertainties of input points. Results in synthetic and real scenes are presented to evaluate the performance of the proposed method."},"title":{"value":"Robust Self-calibration from Single Image Using RANSAC"},"authors":{"value":["Qi Wu","Te-Chin Shao","Tsuhan Chen"]}},"tmdate":1747303991363,"pdate":1167609600000,"tcdate":1747303924091,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Tsuhan_Chen3"],"forum":"EuKSPpqx4u","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":481953,"cdate":1167609600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747303991363,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"EuKSPpqx4u","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"2021 International Symposium on Medical Robotics (ISMR)"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/5db2daf913f12d9737a0773f6de0d0ecc1e8f720.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"song|towards_automatic_robotic_calibration_system_for_flexible_needles_with_fbg_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kefan_Song2","dlezcan1@jhu.edu","~Ge_Sun1","jkim115@jhu.edu","~Iulian_Iordachita1"]},"abstract":{"value":"There has been much research exploring the use of fiber Bragg grating (FBG)-sensorized needles in the prostate biopsy procedure, but all FBG needles used in the research need to be calibrated, which is time consuming and prone to human errors. In this work, a semi-automatic robotic system was developed to perform FBG needle calibration. Compared to manual calibration results, the robotic system is able to calibrate FBG needles with the similar level of accuracy as achieved by an experienced manual operator, thus reducing the time cost during the needle calibration process."},"title":{"value":"Towards Automatic Robotic Calibration System for Flexible Needles with FBG Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Kefan Song","Dimitri A. Lezcano","Ge Sun","Jin Seob Kim","Iulian Iordachita"]}},"tmdate":1749481570258,"pdate":1641182400000,"tcdate":1749481570258,"writers":["~Kefan_Song2","dlezcan1@jhu.edu","~Ge_Sun1","jkim115@jhu.edu","~Iulian_Iordachita1"],"signatures":["~Kefan_Song2"],"forum":"7cZp0m9Ilf","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":37367,"cdate":1749481570258,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1749481570258,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"7cZp0m9Ilf","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IbPRIA (1) 2005"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/11492429_21.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IBPRIA/2005"},"paperhash":{"value":"martins|a_calibration_algorithm_for_poxslits_camera"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nuno_Martins:","~Helder_J._Araujo1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/11492429_21"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ibpria/MartinsA05,\n  author={Nuno Martins and Helder Araújo},\n  title={A Calibration Algorithm for POX-Slits Camera},\n  year={2005},\n  cdate={1104537600000},\n  pages={168-175},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/11492429_21},\n  booktitle={IbPRIA (1)},\n  crossref={conf/ibpria/2005-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent developments have suggested alternative multiperspective camera models potentially advantageous for the analysis of the scene structure. Two-slit cameras are one such case. These cameras collect all rays passing through two lines. The projection model for these cameras is non-linear, and in this model every 3D point is projected by a line that passes through that point and intersects two slits. In this paper we propose a robust non-iterative linear method for the calibration of this type of cameras. For that purpose a calibrating object with known dimensions is required. A solution for the calibration can be obtained using at least thirteen world to image correspondences. To achieve a higher level of accuracy data normalization and a non-linear technique based on the maximum likelihood criterion can be used to refine the estimated solution."},"title":{"value":"A Calibration Algorithm for POX-Slits Camera"},"authors":{"value":["Nuno Martins","Helder Araújo"]}},"tmdate":1752675075847,"pdate":1104537600000,"tcdate":1752675015574,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Helder_J._Araujo1"],"forum":"Dhhuy8dZRm","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":569971,"cdate":1104537600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1752675075847,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Dhhuy8dZRm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"WSC 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10406299/10407113/10408337.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/WSC/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|microscopic_vehicular_traffic_simulation_toward_online_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yulong_Wang:","~John_A._Miller1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Casey_Bowman:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/WSC60868.2023.10408337"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/wsc/WangMB23,\n  author={Yulong Wang and John A. Miller and Casey Bowman},\n  title={Microscopic Vehicular Traffic Simulation: Toward Online Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={2638-2649},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/WSC60868.2023.10408337},\n  booktitle={WSC},\n  crossref={conf/wsc/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The modern world requires accurate and efficient traffic modeling to facilitate commerce and ensure citizens’ safety. Traffic simulations play an important role in this endeavor by allowing traffic engineers to test traffic systems and policies before implementing them. This requires traffic simulation models that have the ability to accurately represent real-world traffic systems, and which are also capable of re-calibrating model parameters when needed through online calibration. This work presents four contributions toward this endeavor. The data science system SCALATION was extended with agent-based modeling and makes use of virtual threads for each vehicle, which improves the efficiency of simulations. The modeling, simulating, and data loading schema were all optimized to enhance the system performance as well. Additionally, a new arrival model strategy was implemented improving the accuracy of the model calibration phase."},"title":{"value":"Microscopic Vehicular Traffic Simulation: Toward Online Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Yulong Wang","John A. Miller","Casey Bowman"]}},"tmdate":1738782238240,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1738782152943,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~John_A._Miller1"],"forum":"zuS0nn6Y11","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":296484,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738782238240,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zuS0nn6Y11","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"language-to-code generation, code-to-language generation, code models for NLP tasks"},"keywords":{"value":["code language models","code completion","multi-language code models","evaluation of code models","prompting"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"Arabic, Bulgarian, German, Greek, English, Spanish, French, Hindi, Russian, Swahili, Thai, Turkish, Urdu, Vietnamese, Chinese"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"Yes"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"Yes"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"Sec. 3.1"},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_elaboration":{"value":"Sec. Ethic Statements"},"B5_elaboration":{"value":"Sec. 3"},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"Yes"},"A2_elaboration":{"value":"Sec. Limitations"},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"Sec. 3.1, 3.4, 4, Appendix B"},"B2_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix A"},"B3_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix A"},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"Sec. 3.3"},"C4_elaboration":{"value":"Sec. 3.3"},"D3_elaboration":{"value":"Some old data in one of the evaluated dataset (HCC-Staging) was collected before we started the study."},"D4_elaboration":{"value":"Sec. Ethic Statements"},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"No"},"D1_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix D"},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix A"},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"Sec. 3"},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"D2_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix D"},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"No"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026codeprompt,\ntitle={Code-Prompt: Evaluating Code-{LLM}s for Natural Language Tasks via Code Aligned Prompts},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=HDxvvvONp3},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Code-Prompt: Evaluating Code-LLMs for Natural Language Tasks via Code Aligned Prompts"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"Yes"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment","Data resources"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"Yes"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"Yes"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"N/A"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models pre-trained on code (Code-LLMs) have achieved remarkable performance on coding tasks. Despite their success of processing programming language, their application to natural language tasks has largely been limited to narrow tasks and ad-hoc model selection.\nIn this paper, we present Code-Prompt, a comprehensive prompt-based framework to benchmark Code-LLMs over ten NLP tasks.\nWe evaluate Code-Prompt on 13 Code-LLMs using code-aligned prompts and compare them with their parallel natural language LLMs (NL-LLMs) using natural language prompts.\n\nOur results show that Code-LLMs perform on par with NL-LLMs across a range of NLP tasks, while producing more format-consistent generation with less redundancy. Our cross-lingual experiments further indicate that knowledge in Code-LLMs is well shared among different programming languages, whereas performance across different human languages still varies significantly. All our code and data will be made publicly available at *anonymous_url*."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"Yes"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fa885294b2623273362c33a034b2b3c553572621.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"NLP and Code Models"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"no"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"Yes"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1790696938332,"tcdate":1779613575389,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission4990/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission4990/Authors"],"forum":"HDxvvvONp3","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4990,"cdate":1779613575389,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission4990/-/Blind_Submission_License_Agreement","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1790696938332,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"HDxvvvONp3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote. Sens. 2001"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/36/19582/00905254.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TGRS/2001"},"paperhash":{"value":"nakhkash|an_improved_calibration_technique_for_freespace_measurement_of_complex_permittivity"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mansor_Nakhkash:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Huang_0001:","~Waleed_Al-Nuaimy1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:M._T._C._Fang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/36.905254"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tgrs/NakhkashHAF01,\n  author={Mansor Nakhkash and Yi Huang and Waleed Al-Nuaimy and M. T. C. Fang},\n  title={An improved calibration technique for free-space measurement of complex permittivity},\n  year={2001},\n  cdate={978307200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={39},\n  number={2},\n  pages={453-455},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/36.905254}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"A calibration technique for free-space measurement of the permittivity and conductivity has been developed, taking account of antenna-medium coupling. Thus, the accuracy is improved compared to previously used calibration. Two examples are given to illustrate this improvement."},"title":{"value":"An improved calibration technique for free-space measurement of complex permittivity"},"authors":{"value":["Mansor Nakhkash","Yi Huang","Waleed Al-Nuaimy","M. T. C. Fang"]}},"tmdate":1763381459341,"pdate":978307200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tgrs/NakhkashHAF01"],"tcdate":1763381441863,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Waleed_Al-Nuaimy1"],"forum":"lpU5xDnSN2","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":705160,"cdate":978307200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1763381459341,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"lpU5xDnSN2","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["Graph neural networks","Confidence calibration","Uncertainty estimation"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/68e5376d404995f1891bf2205ab90b904b42045d.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"learning on graphs and other geometries & topologies"},"abstract":{"value":"Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) have demonstrated remarkable effectiveness on graph-based tasks. However, their predictive confidence is often miscalibrated, typically exhibiting _under-confidence_, which harms the reliability of their decisions. Existing calibration methods for GNNs normally introduce additional calibration components, which fail to capture the intrinsic relationship between the model and the prediction confidence, resulting in limited theoretical guarantees and increased computational overhead. To address this issue, we propose a simple yet efficient graph calibration method. We establish a unified theoretical framework revealing that model confidence is jointly governed by class-centroid-level and node-level calibration at the final layer. Based on this insight, we theoretically show that reducing the weight decay of the final-layer parameters alleviates GNN under-confidence by acting on the class-centroid level, while node-level calibration acts as a finer-grained complement to class-centroid level calibration, which encourages each test node to be closer to its predicted class centroid at the final-layer representations."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nhuang2026the,\ntitle={The Final Layer Holds the Key: A Unified and Efficient {GNN} Calibration Framework},\nauthor={Jincheng Huang and Jie Xu and Xiaoshuang Shi and Ping Hu and Lei Feng and Xiaofeng Zhu},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=F0XWYqYWJC}\n}"},"title":{"value":"The Final Layer Holds the Key: A Unified and Efficient GNN Calibration Framework"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/870dcf7da357d68f5c6e93221967f22aac33d282.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|the_final_layer_holds_the_key_a_unified_and_efficient_gnn_calibration_framework"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jincheng_Huang1","~Jie_Xu8","~Xiaoshuang_Shi1","~Ping_Hu3","~Lei_Feng1","~Xiaofeng_Zhu7"]},"authors":{"value":["Jincheng Huang","Jie Xu","Xiaoshuang Shi","Ping Hu","Lei Feng","Xiaofeng Zhu"]}},"tmdate":1770804979345,"tcdate":1758207841555,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12439/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12439/Authors"],"forum":"F0XWYqYWJC","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":12439,"cdate":1758207841555,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12439/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12439/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770804979345,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"F0XWYqYWJC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AISTATS 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v258/main/assets/panda25a/panda25a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AISTATS/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"panda|copula_based_trainable_calibration_error_estimator_of_multilabel_classification_with_label_interdependencies"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Arkapal_Panda:","~Utpal_Garain1"]},"html":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v258/panda25a.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aistats/PandaG25,\n  author={Arkapal Panda and Utpal Garain},\n  title={Copula Based Trainable Calibration Error Estimator of Multi-Label Classification with Label Interdependencies},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={3745-3753},\n  url={https://proceedings.mlr.press/v258/panda25a.html},\n  booktitle={AISTATS},\n  crossref={conf/aistats/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"A key challenge in calibrating Multi-Label Classification(MLC) problems is to consider the interdependencies among labels. To address this, in this research we propose an unbiased, differentiable, trainable calibration error estimator for MLC problems by using Copula. Unlike other methods for calibrating MLC tasks that focus on marginal calibration, this novel estimator takes label interdependencies into account and enables us to tackle the strictest notion of calibration that is canonical calibration. To design the estimator, we begin by leveraging the kernel trick to construct a continuous distribution from the discrete label space. Then we take a semiparametric approach to construct the estimator where the marginals are modeled non-parametrically and the Copula is modeled parametrically. Theoretically we show that our estimator is unbiased and converges to true $L^p$ calibration error. We also use our estimator as a regularizer at the time of training and observe that it reduces calibration error on test datasets significantly. Experiments on a well established dataset endorses our claims."},"title":{"value":"Copula Based Trainable Calibration Error Estimator of Multi-Label Classification with Label Interdependencies"},"authors":{"value":["Arkapal Panda","Utpal Garain"]}},"tmdate":1762948134217,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/aistats/PandaG25"],"tcdate":1762948132360,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Utpal_Garain1"],"forum":"Sk4qUoefxu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":695599,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762948134217,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Sk4qUoefxu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AISTATS 2025 Poster"},"full_paper":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v258/panda25a.html"},"abstract":{"value":"A key challenge in calibrating Multi-Label Classification(MLC) problems is to consider the interdependencies among labels. To address this, in this research we propose an unbiased, differentiable, trainable calibration error estimator for MLC problems by using Copula. Unlike other methods for calibrating MLC tasks that focus on marginal calibration, this novel estimator takes label interdependencies into account and enables us to tackle the strictest notion of calibration that is canonical calibration. To design the estimator, we begin by leveraging the kernel trick to construct a continuous distribution from the discrete label space. Then we take a semiparametric approach to construct the estimator where the marginals are modeled non-parametrically and the Copula is modeled parametrically. Theoretically we show that our estimator is unbiased and converges to true $L^p$ calibration error. We also use our estimator as a regularizer at the time of training and observe that it reduces calibration error on test datasets significantly. Experiments on a well established dataset endorses our claims."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\npanda2025copula,\ntitle={Copula Based Trainable Calibration Error Estimator of Multi-Label Classification with Label Interdependencies},\nauthor={Arkapal Panda and Utpal Garain},\nbooktitle={The 28th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=B86T1rjmff}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Copula Based Trainable Calibration Error Estimator of Multi-Label Classification with Label Interdependencies"},"venueid":{"value":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"panda|copula_based_trainable_calibration_error_estimator_of_multilabel_classification_with_label_interdependencies"},"authorids":{"value":["~Arkapal_Panda1","~Utpal_Garain1"]},"authors":{"value":["Arkapal Panda","Utpal Garain"]}},"tmdate":1759485704040,"pdate":1737538863705,"tcdate":1727975280684,"writers":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference","aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference/Submission1401/Authors"],"signatures":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference/Submission1401/Authors"],"forum":"B86T1rjmff","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1401,"cdate":1727975280684,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference/-/Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference/-/Edit","aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference/Submission1401/-/Full_Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference/Submission1401/-/Supplementary_Material","aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference/Submission1401/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1759485704040,"odate":1738917494350,"domain":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2025/Conference","id":"B86T1rjmff","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.08037v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"koh|safeecgmatch_calibrationaware_joint_frequency_and_time_space_semisupervised_learning_for_openset_ecg_classification"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.08037"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2606-08037,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Hongkyu Koh and Ikbeom Jang},\n  title={SafeECGMatch: Calibration-Aware Joint Frequency and Time Space Semi-Supervised Learning for Open-Set ECG Classification},\n  year={2026},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1780272000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2606.08037},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.08037}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Electrocardiogram (ECG) classification models often suffer from severe label scarcity, making semi-supervised learning (SSL) an attractive strategy for reducing annotation costs. In clinical settings, however, unlabeled pools frequently contain out-of-distribution (OOD) anomalies or diagnostic groups absent from the labeled set. Standard SSL forces incorrect pseudo-labels onto these unseen classes, producing overconfident predictions. To address this, we propose SafeECGMatch, a calibration-aware safe SSL framework for single-label ECG classification under label distribution mismatch. Methodologically, SafeECGMatch employs a dual-branch architecture extracting time-frequency latent representations via ECG-specific augmentations. Crucially, it dynamically aligns confidence with empirical accuracy through adaptive label smoothing and temperature scaling, calibrating both the multiclass classifier and the OOD detector across temporal and spectral domains. This joint optimization allows trustworthy OOD rejection and reliable pseudo-labeling. Evaluated on the PTB-XL and PhysioNet/CinC Challenge benchmarks, SafeECGMatch achieves state-of-the-art accuracy and calibration, advancing reliable knowledge discovery in physiological time-series. Code is available at https://github.com/labhai/SafeECGMatch."},"title":{"value":"SafeECGMatch: Calibration-Aware Joint Frequency and Time Space Semi-Supervised Learning for Open-Set ECG Classification"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Hongkyu Koh","username":""},{"fullname":"Ikbeom Jang","username":"~Ikbeom_Jang1"}]}},"tmdate":1783442163227,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2606-08037"],"tcdate":1783442156080,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Ikbeom_Jang1"],"forum":"kJp9XHIYDU","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":50352,"cdate":1780272000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1783442163227,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"kJp9XHIYDU","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ITSC 2008"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/4721641/4732517/04732548.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ITSC/2008"},"paperhash":{"value":"kosmatopoulos|aft2_an_automated_maintenance_and_calibration_tool_for_traffic_management_control_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Elias_B._Kosmatopoulos:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Markos_Papageorgiou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yibing_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ioannis_Papamichail:","~Anastasios_Kouvelas1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC.2008.4732548"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/itsc/KosmatopoulosPW08,\n  author={Elias B. Kosmatopoulos and Markos Papageorgiou and Yibing Wang and Ioannis Papamichail and Anastasios Kouvelas},\n  title={AFT2: An Automated Maintenance and Calibration Tool for Traffic Management & Control Systems},\n  year={2008},\n  cdate={1199145600000},\n  pages={97-104},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC.2008.4732548},\n  booktitle={ITSC},\n  crossref={conf/itsc/2008}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Currently, a tremendous amount of human effort and time is spent for maintenance and calibration of operations of Transport Management & Control Systems (TMCSs). TMCS maintenance and calibration is usually performed by experienced personnel in the lack of an automated and systematic approach with no guarantee that the overall maintenance procedure will end-up successfully. Severe congestion, delay and safety problems may occur during manually-based maintenance and calibration activities, which usually take from several months to few years until completed. AFT2 is aiming at replacing the manually-based TMCS maintenance and calibration by a fully-automated procedure applicable to general TMCSs. The approach of AFT2 is based on a recently introduced Adaptive Optimization (AO) methodology which was proven - using rigorous mathematical arguments - to provide with safe and reliable, efficient and rapid maintenance and calibration of general TMCSs. The objective of the present paper is to demonstrate AFT2 efficiency through its application - in simulation - to three different complex, large-scale road TMCSs."},"title":{"value":"AFT2: An Automated Maintenance and Calibration Tool for Traffic Management & Control Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Elias B. Kosmatopoulos","Markos Papageorgiou","Yibing Wang","Ioannis Papamichail","Anastasios Kouvelas"]}},"tmdate":1747130696847,"pdate":1199145600000,"tcdate":1747130692212,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Anastasios_Kouvelas1"],"forum":"72IDZOI47t","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":444681,"cdate":1199145600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747130696847,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"72IDZOI47t","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["multimodal evaluation","code synthesis","execution-based evaluation","visual grounding","item response theory","adaptive testing"]},"primary_area":{"value":"datasets and benchmarks"},"abstract":{"value":"Visual code generation couples interpretation of a reference artifact with program synthesis, yet artifact fidelity and calibrated capability are distinct measurement problems. We introduce MetroBench, an execution-grounded framework and diagnostic study toward their joint evaluation. The framework specifies Construct, Reflect, and Repair protocols, and separates a four-component chart scorer from an independently audited psychometric layer. On 200 procedural chart items and 1,000 program variants, mean fidelity declines from 1.000 to 0.658 across five perturbation levels; 197 item profiles decrease strictly, while three exhibit local reversals. Removing plotted-data agreement reduces the strictly ordered profiles to 145, exposing sensitivity to the scoring rubric. In a separate synthetic-response experiment with 20 repetitions, 40 Fisher-selected items yield held-out ability RMSE 0.104 versus 0.110 for random selection and 0.090 for the 200-item full test; the paired 40-item gain is uncertain. A matched-bank study shows that anchor linkage can recover scale despite nearly unchanged rank correlation. However, nominal 95% plug-in ability intervals cover only 45.8% of held-out abilities, compared with 95.8% when item parameters are known. These findings demonstrate why execution grounding, adaptive efficiency, and uncertainty calibration require separate evidence. The present studies evaluate procedural scoring and synthetic measurement, not model capabilities; a validated common scale across multimodal perception and code synthesis remains an open empirical requirement."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nli2026metrobench,\ntitle={MetroBench: Execution-Grounded Measurement for Visual Code Generation},\nauthor={Yuanhao Li and Xiaotang Shang and Ping Wang and Shaochen Lin and Wei Tan and Xunzhu Tang},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=tD9z10fpsY}\n}"},"title":{"value":"MetroBench: Execution-Grounded Measurement for Visual Code Generation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7aa8fe12e7eb7c3c8825a28307e1b07fddd1366d.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|metrobench_executiongrounded_measurement_for_visual_code_generation"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Yuanhao_Li1","fullname":"Yuanhao Li","institutions":[{"name":"Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications","domain":"bupt.edu.cn","country":"CN"}]},{"username":"~Xiaotang_Shang1","fullname":"Xiaotang Shang","institutions":[{"name":"Beijing University of Post and Telecommunications","domain":"bupt.edu.cn","country":"CN"}]},{"username":"~Ping_Wang20","fullname":"Ping Wang","institutions":[{"name":"Beijing University of Post and Telecommunications","domain":"bupt.edu.cn","country":"CN"}]},{"username":"~Shaochen_Lin1","fullname":"Shaochen Lin","institutions":[{"name":"Beijing University of Post and Telecommunications","domain":"bupt.edu.cn","country":"CN"}]},{"username":"~Wei_Tan7","fullname":"Wei Tan","institutions":[{"name":"University of Bristol","domain":"bristol.ac.uk","country":"GB"}]},{"username":"~Xunzhu_Tang1","fullname":"Xunzhu Tang","institutions":[{"name":"University of Luxemburg","domain":"uni.lu","country":"LU"}]}]}},"tmdate":1794124501057,"tcdate":1789798424463,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55475/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55475/Authors"],"forum":"tD9z10fpsY","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":55475,"cdate":1789798424463,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55475/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1794124501057,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"tD9z10fpsY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.545.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|decocal_decoding_with_calibration_in_diffusion_large_language_models"},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.545/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/XuZW26,\n  author={Fan Xu and Huixuan Zhang and Xiaojun Wan},\n  title={DecoCal: Decoding with Calibration in Diffusion Large Language Models},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={11866-11880},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.545/},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2026-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Diffusion Large Language Models (DLLMs) generate text via iterative masked-token denoising, supporting parallel prediction and bidirectional context modeling. Despite these advantages, decoding remains challenging: many tokens appear predictable early, yet single-step predictions are often unstable, exhibiting temporal oscillations or overconfidence, making it difficult to determine which tokens can be safely committed. To address these challenges, we propose DecoCal, a Decoding framework that explicitly performs Calibration of token-level confidence across diffusion steps and leverages the calibrated results to guide decoding decisions. Specifically, DecoCal aggregates historical predictions to maintain calibrated confidence, triggering unmasking only when a token is sufficiently stable, while a remasking mechanism allows revision of premature commitments. This calibration-based design enables early decoding of reliably converged tokens while deferring or correcting unstable ones, balancing reliability and speed. Experiments on multiple DLLMs and benchmarks show that DecoCal improves generation accuracy compared to existing strategies. Our results highlight the importance of temporal calibration in unlocking the full potential of diffusion-based language generation."},"title":{"value":"DecoCal: Decoding with Calibration in Diffusion Large Language Models"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Fan Xu","username":"~Fan_Xu1"},{"fullname":"Huixuan Zhang","username":"~Huixuan_Zhang1"},{"fullname":"Xiaojun Wan","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1793001362430,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/XuZW26"],"tcdate":1784970445828,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Fan_Xu1"],"forum":"CIkco07YDh","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":104166,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1793001362430,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"CIkco07YDh","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"This paper frames honesty alignment as a two-stage learning problem and proposes an annotation-efficient training framework called Elicitation-Then-Calibration (EliCal)"},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Trustworthy LLMs","Alignment for Honesty"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Honesty alignment—the ability of large language models (LLMs) to recognize their knowledge boundaries and express calibrated confidence—is essential for trustworthy deployment. Existing methods either rely on training-free confidence estimation (e.g., token probabilities, self-consistency) or training-based calibration with correctness annotations. While effective, the latter demands costly, large-scale labeling.\nWe introduce Elicitation-Then-Calibration (EliCal), a two-stage framework that first elicits internal confidence using inexpensive self-consistency supervision, then calibrates this confidence with a small set of correctness annotations. This design substantially reduces annotation requirements while improving generalization across tasks. To support a large-scale study, we release HonestyBench, a benchmark covering ten free-form QA datasets with 560k training and 70k evaluation instances annotated with correctness and self-consistency signals.\nExperiments show that EliCal achieves near-optimal alignment with only 1k correctness annotations ($\\sim$0.18\\% of full supervision) and better alignment performance on unseen MMLU tasks than the calibration-only baseline, offering a scalable solution toward universal honesty alignment in LLMs."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nni2026annotationefficient,\ntitle={Annotation-Efficient Honesty Alignment via Confidence Elicitation and Calibration},\nauthor={Shiyu Ni and Keping Bi and Jiafeng Guo and Minghao Tang and Jingtong wu and Zengxin Han and Xueqi Cheng},\nbooktitle={The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=cW6oDsPobl}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Annotation-Efficient Honesty Alignment via Confidence Elicitation and Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f5133f147bb546ea6b7eb2cdffeee8b1db214926.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"ni|annotationefficient_honesty_alignment_via_confidence_elicitation_and_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shiyu_Ni1","~Keping_Bi1","~Jiafeng_Guo1","~Minghao_Tang2","~Jingtong_wu2","~Zengxin_Han2","~Xueqi_Cheng1"]},"authors":{"value":["Shiyu Ni","Keping Bi","Jiafeng Guo","Minghao Tang","Jingtong wu","Zengxin Han","Xueqi Cheng"]}},"tmdate":1775877364550,"pdate":1769436649854,"tcdate":1758347025415,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23676/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23676/Authors"],"forum":"cW6oDsPobl","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":23676,"cdate":1758347025415,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23676/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23676/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1775877364550,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"cW6oDsPobl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"WACV 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10030081/10030084/10030585.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/WACV/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"theiner|tvcalib_camera_calibration_for_sports_field_registration_in_soccer"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jonas_Theiner:","~Ralph_Ewerth2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV56688.2023.00122"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/wacv/TheinerE23,\n  author={Jonas Theiner and Ralph Ewerth},\n  title={TVCalib: Camera Calibration for Sports Field Registration in Soccer},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1166-1175},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV56688.2023.00122},\n  booktitle={WACV},\n  crossref={conf/wacv/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Sports field registration in broadcast videos is typically interpreted as the task of homography estimation, which provides a mapping between a planar field and the corresponding visible area of the image. In contrast to previous approaches, we consider the task as a camera calibration problem. First, we introduce a differentiable objective function that is able to learn the camera pose and focal length from segment correspondences (e.g., lines, point clouds), based on pixel-level annotations for segments of a known calibration object. The calibration module iteratively minimizes the segment reprojection error induced by the estimated camera parameters. Second, we propose a novel approach for 3D sports field registration from broadcast soccer images. Compared to the typical solution, which subsequently refines an initial estimation, our solution does it in one step. The proposed method is evaluated for sports field registration on two datasets and achieves superior results compared to two state-of-the-art approaches."},"title":{"value":"TVCalib: Camera Calibration for Sports Field Registration in Soccer"},"authors":{"value":["Jonas Theiner","Ralph Ewerth"]}},"tmdate":1769251282786,"pdate":1703980800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/wacv/TheinerE23"],"tcdate":1769251272116,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ralph_Ewerth2"],"forum":"sg6YSP2eCJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":793955,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769251282786,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"sg6YSP2eCJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Probabilistic Drediction","Calibration Attention","Model Calibration"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/d2b408be24a1defd313e53633bd0bf6c7dc3d7b4.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration is essential for deploying Vision Transformers (ViTs) in risk-sensitive settings. While post-hoc temperature scaling fits a single global scalar on a validation split, it can degrade under distribution shift, as it ignores input-dependent uncertainty. We introduce Calibration Attention (CalAttn), a lightweight plug-in head that that dynamically learns an adaptive, per-instance temperature directly from the ViT’s CLS token. On CIFAR-10/100, MNIST, Tiny-ImageNet and ImageNet-1K with ViT/DeiT/Swin backbones, CalAttn reduces ECE by 2.02 pp (57.2\\%) pre-TS and 1.18 pp (56.6\\%) post-TS on average, while adding $<$0.1\\% parameters. Learned temperatures concentrate near 1.0 on in-distribution data, limiting distortion when the model is already calibrated, yet adapt on harder examples. Extensive experiments confirm robustness across datasets, while comparisons highlight CalAttn’s efficiency over Dirichlet heads (3× params) and logit-temperature scaling baselines. Calibration Attention thus offers a simple, efficient strategy for producing trustworthy predictions in state-of-the-art Vision Transformers."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nliang2025calibration,\ntitle={Calibration Attention: Instance-wise Temperature Scaling for Vision Transformers},\nauthor={Wenhao Liang and Wei Emma Zhang and Lin Yue and Miao Xu and Olaf Maennel and Weitong Chen},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=16dklduerp}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration Attention: Instance-wise Temperature Scaling for Vision Transformers"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/b53deea797986da2b4131d153256d1530a45eedc.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"liang|calibration_attention_instancewise_temperature_scaling_for_vision_transformers"},"authorids":{"value":["~Wenhao_Liang1","~Wei_Emma_Zhang1","~Lin_Yue3","~Miao_Xu3","~Olaf_Maennel1","~Weitong_Chen2"]},"authors":{"value":["Wenhao Liang","Wei Emma Zhang","Lin Yue","Miao Xu","Olaf Maennel","Weitong Chen"]}},"tmdate":1762926438286,"tcdate":1757895901216,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5300/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5300/Authors"],"forum":"16dklduerp","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":5300,"cdate":1757895901216,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5300/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1762926438286,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"16dklduerp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Image Process. 2005"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/83/31487/01468201.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIP/2005"},"paperhash":{"value":"bala|twodimensional_transforms_for_device_color_correction_and_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Raja_Bala3","~Gaurav_Sharma3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Vishal_Monga:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jean-Pierre_Van_de_Capelle:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2005.851678"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tip/BalaSMC05,\n  author={Raja Bala and Gaurav Sharma and Vishal Monga and Jean-Pierre Van de Capelle},\n  title={Two-dimensional transforms for device color correction and calibration},\n  year={2005},\n  cdate={1104537600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Image Process.},\n  volume={14},\n  number={8},\n  pages={1172-1186},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2005.851678}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Color device calibration is traditionally performed using one-dimensional (1-D) per-channel tone-response corrections (TRCs). While 1-D TRCs are attractive in view of their low implementation complexity and efficient real-time processing of color images, their use severely restricts the degree of control that can be exercised along various device axes. A typical example is that per separation (or per-channel), TRCs in a printer can be used to either ensure gray balance along the C=M=Y axis or to provide a linear response in delta-E units along each of the individual (C, M, and Y) axis, but not both. This paper proposes a novel two-dimensional color correction architecture that enables much greater control over the device color gamut with a modest increase in implementation cost. Results show significant improvement in calibration accuracy and stability when compared to traditional 1-D calibration. Superior cost quality tradeoffs (over 1-D methods) are also achieved for emulation of one color device on another."},"title":{"value":"Two-dimensional transforms for device color correction and calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Raja Bala","Gaurav Sharma","Vishal Monga","Jean-Pierre Van de Capelle"]}},"tmdate":1762725858873,"pdate":1104537600000,"tcdate":1740106888533,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Gaurav_Sharma3"],"forum":"FC9TJbMUUE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":337306,"cdate":1104537600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762725858873,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"FC9TJbMUUE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Signal Process. 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/78/7562579/07533436.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TSP/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|optimal_sample_complexity_for_blind_gain_and_phase_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yanjun_Li_0001:","~Kiryung_Lee1","~Yoram_Bresler1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSP.2016.2598311"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tsp/LiLB16,\n  author={Yanjun Li and Kiryung Lee and Yoram Bresler},\n  title={Optimal Sample Complexity for Blind Gain and Phase Calibration},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Signal Process.},\n  volume={64},\n  number={21},\n  pages={5549-5556},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TSP.2016.2598311}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Blind gain and phase calibration (BGPC) is a structured bilinear inverse problem, which arises in many applications, including inverse rendering in computational relighting (albedo estimation with unknown lighting), blind phase and gain calibration in sensor array processing, and multichannel blind deconvolution. The fundamental question of the uniqueness of the solutions to such problems has been addressed only recently. In a previous paper, we proposed studying the identifiability in bilinear inverse problems up to transformation groups. In particular, we studied several special cases of blind gain and phase calibration, including the cases of subspace and joint sparsity models on the signals, and gave sufficient and necessary conditions for identifiability up to certain transformation groups. However, there were gaps between the sample complexities in the sufficient conditions and the necessary conditions. In this paper, under a mild assumption that the signals and models are generic, we bridge the gaps by deriving tight sufficient conditions with optimal or near optimal sample complexities."},"title":{"value":"Optimal Sample Complexity for Blind Gain and Phase Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Yanjun Li","Kiryung Lee","Yoram Bresler"]}},"tmdate":1747368210178,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1741218222630,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yoram_Bresler1"],"forum":"FNz6KVNBnB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":356118,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747368210178,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"FNz6KVNBnB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2015"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1512.07293v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2015"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|optimal_sample_complexity_for_blind_gain_and_phase_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yanjun_Li_0001:","~Kiryung_Lee1","~Yoram_Bresler1"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1512.07293"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/LiLB15c,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yanjun Li and Kiryung Lee and Yoram Bresler},\n  title={Optimal Sample Complexity for Blind Gain and Phase Calibration},\n  year={2015},\n  cdate={1420070400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1512.07293},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1512.07293}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Blind gain and phase calibration (BGPC) is a structured bilinear inverse problem, which arises in many applications, including inverse rendering in computational relighting (albedo estimation with unknown lighting), blind phase and gain calibration in sensor array processing, and multichannel blind deconvolution. The fundamental question of the uniqueness of the solutions to such problems has been addressed only recently. In a previous paper, we proposed studying the identifiability in bilinear inverse problems up to transformation groups. In particular, we studied several special cases of blind gain and phase calibration, including the cases of subspace and joint sparsity models on the signals, and gave sufficient and necessary conditions for identifiability up to certain transformation groups. However, there were gaps between the sample complexities in the sufficient conditions and the necessary conditions. In this paper, under a mild assumption that the signals and models are generic, we bridge the gaps by deriving tight sufficient conditions with optimal sample complexities."},"title":{"value":"Optimal Sample Complexity for Blind Gain and Phase Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Yanjun Li","Kiryung Lee","Yoram Bresler"]}},"tmdate":1747368210102,"pdate":1420070400000,"tcdate":1741218220350,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yoram_Bresler1"],"forum":"Rr4kUwZBXf","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":356077,"cdate":1420070400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747368210102,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Rr4kUwZBXf","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"calibration/uncertainty, code generation and understanding, language model, Relational Algebra Tree, SQL"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2024 June Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2024coarse,\ntitle={Coarse and Fine-grained Confidence Calibration of {LLM}-based Text-to-{SQL} Generation},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - June 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=J9vbZnEoxi},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Coarse and Fine-grained Confidence Calibration of LLM-based Text-to-SQL Generation"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","NLP engineering experiment"]},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration plays a crucial role as LLMs are increasingly deployed to convert natural language questions into SQL over commercial databases. In this work, we study the calibration of the confidence attached to both the whole query, and for the first time, to sub-parts of the query.  For whole queries, we demonstrate that the simple baseline of deriving confidence from model assigned whole sequence probability yields the best calibration surpassing recent self-check and verbalization methods. For fine-grained calibration, we propose a novel method of assigning confidence to nodes of a logical relational algebra tree representation of the SQL string. We present an extensive comparison spanning two popular Text-to-SQL benchmarks on multiple LLMs, and draw interesting insights about various calibration methods."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2b1a621acb99eef3da7a7b3e1cd76e0f05d4847e.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Interpretability and Analysis of Models for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794065377669,"tcdate":1718539203204,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission5862/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission5862/Authors"],"forum":"J9vbZnEoxi","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":5862,"cdate":1718539203204,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794065377669,"odate":1719930600391,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June","id":"J9vbZnEoxi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":" Computer Vision – ECCV 2020"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"larsson|calibrationfree_structurefrommotion_with_calibrated_radial_trifocal_tensors"},"html":{"value":"https://portal.research.lu.se/en/publications/816a2bf9-58d8-4e07-80d4-9984a08d5c12"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper we consider the problem of Structure-from-Motion from images with unknown intrinsic calibration. Instead of estimating the internal camera parameters through some self-calibration procedure, we propose to use a subset of the reprojection constraints that is invariant to radial displacement. This allows us to recover metric 3D reconstructions without explicitly estimating the cameras’ focal length or radial distortion parameters. The weaker projection model makes initializing the reconstruction especially difficult. To handle this additional challenge we propose two novel minimal solvers for radial trifocal tensor estimation. We evaluate our approach on real images and show that even for extreme optical systems, such as fisheye or catadioptric, we are able to get accurate reconstructions without performing any calibration."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{816a2bf958d84e0780d49984a08d5c12,\n  title     = \"Calibration-free structure-from-motion with calibrated radial trifocal tensors\",\n  abstract  = \"In this paper we consider the problem of Structure-from-Motion from images with unknown intrinsic calibration. Instead of estimating the internal camera parameters through some self-calibration procedure, we propose to use a subset of the reprojection constraints that is invariant to radial displacement. This allows us to recover metric 3D reconstructions without explicitly estimating the cameras{\\textquoteright} focal length or radial distortion parameters. The weaker projection model makes initializing the reconstruction especially difficult. To handle this additional challenge we propose two novel minimal solvers for radial trifocal tensor estimation. We evaluate our approach on real images and show that even for extreme optical systems, such as fisheye or catadioptric, we are able to get accurate reconstructions without performing any calibration.\",\n  author    = \"Viktor Larsson and Nicolas Zobernig and Kasim Taskin and Marc Pollefeys\",\n  year      = \"2020\",\n  doi       = \"10.1007/978-3-030-58558-7\\_23\",\n  language  = \"English\",\n  isbn      = \"978-3-030-58451-1\",\n  series    = \"Lecture Notes in Computer Science\",\n  publisher = \"Springer\",\n  pages     = \"382--399\",\n  booktitle = \"Computer Vision – ECCV 2020\",\n  address   = \"Germany\",\n  note      = \"16th European Conference on Computer Vision, ECCV 2020 ; Conference date: 23-08-2020 Through 28-08-2020\",\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration-free structure-from-motion with calibrated radial trifocal tensors"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Viktor Larsson","username":"~Viktor_Larsson1"},{"fullname":"Nicolas Zobernig","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Nicolas%20Zobernig"},{"fullname":"Kasim Taskin","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Kasim%20Taskin"},{"fullname":"Marc Pollefeys","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Marc%20Pollefeys"}]}},"tmdate":1786525738724,"pdate":1577836800000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1007/978-3-030-58558-7_23"],"tcdate":1786525735998,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Viktor_Larsson3"],"forum":"2Z4gfPfQK1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":97667,"cdate":1782523453093,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1786525738724,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"2Z4gfPfQK1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.16133v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ye|uncovering_llmgenerated_code_a_zeroshot_synthetic_code_detector_via_code_rewriting"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tong_Ye:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yangkai_Du:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tengfei_Ma_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lingfei_Wu_0001:","~Xuhong_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shouling_Ji:","~Wenhai_Wang3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.16133"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2405-16133,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Tong Ye and Yangkai Du and Tengfei Ma and Lingfei Wu and Xuhong Zhang and Shouling Ji and Wenhai Wang},\n  title={Uncovering LLM-Generated Code: A Zero-Shot Synthetic Code Detector via Code Rewriting},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2405.16133},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.16133}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable proficiency in generating code. However, the misuse of LLM-generated (synthetic) code has raised concerns in both educational and industrial contexts, underscoring the urgent need for synthetic code detectors. Existing methods for detecting synthetic content are primarily designed for general text and struggle with code due to the unique grammatical structure of programming languages and the presence of numerous ''low-entropy'' tokens. Building on this, our work proposes a novel zero-shot synthetic code detector based on the similarity between the original code and its LLM-rewritten variants. Our method is based on the observation that differences between LLM-rewritten and original code tend to be smaller when the original code is synthetic. We utilize self-supervised contrastive learning to train a code similarity model and evaluate our approach on two synthetic code detection benchmarks. Our results demonstrate a significant improvement over existing SOTA synthetic content detectors, with AUROC scores increasing by 20.5% on the APPS benchmark and 29.1% on the MBPP benchmark."},"title":{"value":"Uncovering LLM-Generated Code: A Zero-Shot Synthetic Code Detector via Code Rewriting"},"authors":{"value":["Tong Ye","Yangkai Du","Tengfei Ma","Lingfei Wu","Xuhong Zhang","Shouling Ji","Wenhai Wang"]}},"tmdate":1768968742610,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747383791912,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wenhai_Wang3"],"forum":"hruKg4FJBO","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":508422,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768968742610,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"hruKg4FJBO","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/32082/34237"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AAAI/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"ye|uncovering_llmgenerated_code_a_zeroshot_synthetic_code_detector_via_code_rewriting"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tong_Ye:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yangkai_Du:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tengfei_Ma_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lingfei_Wu_0001:","~Xuhong_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shouling_Ji:","~Wenhai_Wang3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i1.32082"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aaai/YeD000JW25,\n  author={Tong Ye and Yangkai Du and Tengfei Ma and Lingfei Wu and Xuhong Zhang and Shouling Ji and Wenhai Wang},\n  title={Uncovering LLM-Generated Code: A Zero-Shot Synthetic Code Detector via Code Rewriting},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={968-976},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i1.32082},\n  booktitle={AAAI},\n  crossref={conf/aaai/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable proficiency in generating code. However, the misuse of LLM-generated (synthetic) code has raised concerns in both educational and industrial contexts, underscoring the urgent need for synthetic code detectors. Existing methods for detecting synthetic content are primarily designed for general text and struggle with code due to the unique grammatical structure of programming languages and the presence of numerous ``low-entropy'' tokens. Building on this, our work proposes a novel zero-shot synthetic code detector based on the similarity between the original code and its LLM-rewritten variants. Our method is based on the observation that differences between LLM-rewritten and original code tend to be smaller when the original code is synthetic. We utilize self-supervised contrastive learning to train a code similarity model and evaluate our approach on two synthetic code detection benchmarks. Our results demonstrate a significant improvement over existing SOTA synthetic content detectors, delivering notable gains in both performance and robustness on the APPS and MBPP benchmarks."},"title":{"value":"Uncovering LLM-Generated Code: A Zero-Shot Synthetic Code Detector via Code Rewriting"},"authors":{"value":["Tong Ye","Yangkai Du","Tengfei Ma","Lingfei Wu","Xuhong Zhang","Shouling Ji","Wenhai Wang"]}},"tmdate":1768968741389,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1747383789945,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wenhai_Wang3"],"forum":"1Vq0CL6cIz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":508378,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768968741389,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"1Vq0CL6cIz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.10864v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ba|fill_in_the_gaps_model_calibration_and_generalization_with_synthetic_data"},"authorids":{"value":["","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Michelle_V._Mancenido:","~Rong_Pan2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.10864"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2410-10864,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yang Ba and Michelle V. Mancenido and Rong Pan},\n  title={Fill In The Gaps: Model Calibration and Generalization with Synthetic Data},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2410.10864},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.10864}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As machine learning models continue to swiftly advance, calibrating their performance has become a major concern prior to practical and widespread implementation. Most existing calibration methods often negatively impact model accuracy due to the lack of diversity of validation data, resulting in reduced generalizability. To address this, we propose a calibration method that incorporates synthetic data without compromising accuracy. We derive the expected calibration error (ECE) bound using the Probably Approximately Correct (PAC) learning framework. Large language models (LLMs), known for their ability to mimic real data and generate text with mixed class labels, are utilized as a synthetic data generation strategy to lower the ECE bound and improve model accuracy on real test data. Additionally, we propose data generation mechanisms for efficient calibration. Testing our method on four different natural language processing tasks, we observed an average up to 34\\% increase in accuracy and 33\\% decrease in ECE."},"title":{"value":"Fill In The Gaps: Model Calibration and Generalization with Synthetic Data"},"authors":{"value":["Yang Ba","Michelle V. Mancenido","Rong Pan"]}},"tmdate":1769577685727,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2410-10864"],"tcdate":1753981059197,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rong_Pan2"],"forum":"vseSDPmnOq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":605461,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1769577685727,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"vseSDPmnOq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.955.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ba|fill_in_the_gaps_model_calibration_and_generalization_with_synthetic_data"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yang_Ba1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Michelle_Mancenido:","~Rong_Pan2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.955"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/BaMP24,\n  author={Yang Ba and Michelle Mancenido and Rong Pan},\n  title={Fill In The Gaps: Model Calibration and Generalization with Synthetic Data},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={17211-17225},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.955},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As machine learning models continue to swiftly advance, calibrating their performance has become a major concern prior to practical and widespread implementation. Most existing calibration methods often negatively impact model accuracy due to the lack of diversity of validation data, resulting in reduced generalizability. To address this, we propose a calibration method that incorporates synthetic data without compromising accuracy. We derive the expected calibration error (ECE) bound using the Probably Approximately Correct (PAC) learning framework. Large language models (LLMs), known for their ability to mimic real data and generate text with mixed class labels, are utilized as a synthetic data generation strategy to lower the ECE bound and improve model accuracy on real test data. Additionally, we propose data generation mechanisms for efficient calibration. Testing our method on four different natural language processing tasks, we observed an average up to 34% increase in accuracy and 33% decrease in ECE."},"title":{"value":"Fill In The Gaps: Model Calibration and Generalization with Synthetic Data"},"authors":{"value":["Yang Ba","Michelle Mancenido","Rong Pan"]}},"tmdate":1769577537009,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/BaMP24"],"tcdate":1753981059039,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rong_Pan2"],"forum":"yRaN5tv3cH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":605463,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1769577537009,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"yRaN5tv3cH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Signal Process. Lett. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/97/10380231/10545598.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SPL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ge|geometry_calibration_for_deformable_linear_microphone_arrays_with_bézier_curve_fitting"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuhai_Ge:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weixin_Meng:","~Xiaodong_Li15","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chengshi_Zheng:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LSP.2024.3408605"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/spl/GeMLZ24,\n  author={Yuhai Ge and Weixin Meng and Xiaodong Li and Chengshi Zheng},\n  title={Geometry Calibration for Deformable Linear Microphone Arrays With Bézier Curve Fitting},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Signal Process. Lett.},\n  volume={31},\n  pages={1620-1624},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LSP.2024.3408605}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Geometry calibration of microphone arrays is an essential preprocessing step for many applications, e.g., beamforming in deformable arrays. However, most existing geometry calibration methods involve the use of non-convex cost functions, suffering from the local minimum problem and low stability. To overcome these drawbacks, we introduce a novel approach that leverages the geometry feature of deformable linear arrays (DLAs) as an additional constraint. The proposed method employs Bézier curve fitting, utilizing the characteristics of Bézier curves to model the geometry feature. Specifically, we first introduce the general form of the geometry calibration problem, and an alternative approach is then proposed for a specific scenario where quadratic Bézier curves are used to fit the array shape. Finally, an additional scale modification is adopted to improve the performance of the proposed method in real scenarios. Simulations and real experiments validate the effectiveness of the proposed method for geometry calibration of DLAs."},"title":{"value":"Geometry Calibration for Deformable Linear Microphone Arrays With Bézier Curve Fitting"},"authors":{"value":["Yuhai Ge","Weixin Meng","Xiaodong Li","Chengshi Zheng"]}},"tmdate":1753437211177,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1753437172201,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiaodong_Li15"],"forum":"rPR160oDpC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":588224,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753437211177,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rPR160oDpC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.10864v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ba|fill_in_the_gaps_model_calibration_and_generalization_with_synthetic_data"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Ba:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Michelle_V._Mancenido:","~Rong_Pan2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.10864"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2410-10864,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yang Ba and Michelle V. Mancenido and Rong Pan},\n  title={Fill In The Gaps: Model Calibration and Generalization with Synthetic Data},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2410.10864},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.10864}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As machine learning models continue to swiftly advance, calibrating their performance has become a major concern prior to practical and widespread implementation. Most existing calibration methods often negatively impact model accuracy due to the lack of diversity of validation data, resulting in reduced generalizability. To address this, we propose a calibration method that incorporates synthetic data without compromising accuracy. We derive the expected calibration error (ECE) bound using the Probably Approximately Correct (PAC) learning framework. Large language models (LLMs), known for their ability to mimic real data and generate text with mixed class labels, are utilized as a synthetic data generation strategy to lower the ECE bound and improve model accuracy on real test data. Additionally, we propose data generation mechanisms for efficient calibration. Testing our method on four different natural language processing tasks, we observed an average up to 34\\% increase in accuracy and 33\\% decrease in ECE."},"title":{"value":"Fill In The Gaps: Model Calibration and Generalization with Synthetic Data"},"authors":{"value":["Yang Ba","Michelle V. Mancenido","Rong Pan"]}},"tmdate":1738770334897,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1738770310989,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rong_Pan2"],"forum":"KhBS7QbXqD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":294652,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738770334897,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KhBS7QbXqD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.955.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ba|fill_in_the_gaps_model_calibration_and_generalization_with_synthetic_data"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Ba:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Michelle_Mancenido:","~Rong_Pan2"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.955"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/BaMP24,\n  author={Yang Ba and Michelle Mancenido and Rong Pan},\n  title={Fill In The Gaps: Model Calibration and Generalization with Synthetic Data},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={17211-17225},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.955},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As machine learning models continue to swiftly advance, calibrating their performance has become a major concern prior to practical and widespread implementation. Most existing calibration methods often negatively impact model accuracy due to the lack of diversity of validation data, resulting in reduced generalizability. To address this, we propose a calibration method that incorporates synthetic data without compromising accuracy. We derive the expected calibration error (ECE) bound using the Probably Approximately Correct (PAC) learning framework. Large language models (LLMs), known for their ability to mimic real data and generate text with mixed class labels, are utilized as a synthetic data generation strategy to lower the ECE bound and improve model accuracy on real test data. Additionally, we propose data generation mechanisms for efficient calibration. Testing our method on four different natural language processing tasks, we observed an average up to 34% increase in accuracy and 33% decrease in ECE."},"title":{"value":"Fill In The Gaps: Model Calibration and Generalization with Synthetic Data"},"authors":{"value":["Yang Ba","Michelle Mancenido","Rong Pan"]}},"tmdate":1738770318794,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1738770311854,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rong_Pan2"],"forum":"1DgxH8OAMe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":294660,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738770318794,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"1DgxH8OAMe","version":2},{"content":{"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"ba|fill_in_the_gaps_model_calibration_and_generalization_with_synthetic_data"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yang_Ba1","~Michelle_V_Mancenido1","~Rong_Pan2"]},"abstract":{"value":"As machine learning models continue to swiftly advance, calibrating their performance has become a major concern prior to practical and widespread implementation. Most existing calibration methods often negatively impact model accuracy due to the lack of diversity of validation data, resulting in reduced generalizability. To address this, we propose a calibration method that incorporates synthetic data without compromising accuracy. We derive the expected calibration error (ECE) bound using the Probably Approximately Correct (PAC) learning framework. Large language models (LLMs), known for their ability to mimic real data and generate text with mixed class labels, are utilized as a synthetic data generation strategy to lower the ECE bound and improve model accuracy on real test data. Additionally, we propose data generation mechanisms for efficient calibration. Testing our method on four different natural language processing tasks, we observed an average up to 34% increase in accuracy and 33% decrease in ECE."},"title":{"value":"Fill In The Gaps: Model Calibration and Generalization with Synthetic Data"},"authors":{"value":["Yang Ba","Michelle V Mancenido","Rong Pan"]}},"tmdate":1727505089385,"pdate":1726813868001,"tcdate":1727505089385,"writers":["~Yang_Ba1","~Michelle_V_Mancenido1","~Rong_Pan2"],"signatures":["~Yang_Ba1"],"forum":"vmdrfUMFNg","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":28894,"cdate":1727505089385,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1727505089385,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"vmdrfUMFNg","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.15106v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"lavrentyeva|investigation_of_different_calibration_methods_for_deep_speaker_embedding_based_verification_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Galina_Lavrentyeva:","~Sergey_Novoselov1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrey_Shulipa:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marina_Volkova:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Aleksandr_Kozlov:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2203.15106"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2203-15106,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Galina Lavrentyeva and Sergey Novoselov and Andrey Shulipa and Marina Volkova and Aleksandr Kozlov},\n  title={Investigation of Different Calibration Methods for Deep Speaker Embedding based Verification Systems},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2203.15106},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2203.15106}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep speaker embedding extractors have already become new state-of-the-art systems in the speaker verification field. However, the problem of verification score calibration for such systems often remains out of focus. An irrelevant score calibration leads to serious issues, especially in the case of unknown acoustic conditions, even if we use a strong speaker verification system in terms of threshold-free metrics. This paper presents an investigation over several methods of score calibration: a classical approach based on the logistic regression model; the recently presented magnitude estimation network MagnetO that uses activations from the pooling layer of the trained deep speaker extractor and generalization of such approach based on separate scale and offset prediction neural networks. An additional focus of this research is to estimate the impact of score normalization on the calibration performance of the system. The obtained results demonstrate that there are no serious problems if in-domain development data are used for calibration tuning. Otherwise, a trade-off between good calibration performance and threshold-free system quality arises. In most cases using adaptive s-norm helps to stabilize score distributions and to improve system performance. Meanwhile, some experiments demonstrate that novel approaches have their limits in score stabilization on several datasets."},"title":{"value":"Investigation of Different Calibration Methods for Deep Speaker Embedding based Verification Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Galina Lavrentyeva","Sergey Novoselov","Andrey Shulipa","Marina Volkova","Aleksandr Kozlov"]}},"tmdate":1768984964395,"pdate":1672444800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2203-15106"],"tcdate":1768984956743,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sergey_Novoselov1"],"forum":"UAaHgh6OX1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":782356,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768984964395,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UAaHgh6OX1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/9717300/09831993.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|closedloop_kinematic_calibration_of_robots_using_a_sixpoint_measuring_device"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ying_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhenghao_Zhuang:",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2022.3191707"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/LiuZL22,\n  author={Ying Liu and Zhenghao Zhuang and Yuwen Li},\n  title={Closed-Loop Kinematic Calibration of Robots Using a Six-Point Measuring Device},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={71},\n  pages={1-12},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2022.3191707}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The low absolute positioning accuracy of robots has limited their applications for many precision tasks. Although the robot accuracy can be improved through kinematic calibration, traditional calibration approaches need either expensive external measurement devices or tedious manual intervention. To overcome these issues, this work proposes a closed-loop kinematic calibration method for robots using a six-point measuring device. This device consists of six displacement sensors to determine the pose of a gauge block as a reference workpiece mounted on the robot end-effector. A comprehensive error model is derived to calibrate the kinematic parameters, including the Denavit–Hartenberg (D–H) parameters of the robot geometry and the pose of the robot base. Then, the Levenberg–Marquard (LM) algorithm is adopted to identify these parameters. An experimental study has been conducted on a UR10 robot, combining a coordinate measuring machine (CMM) to verify the calibration results. It is found that the maximum and average distance errors of the robot movement could be significantly decreased from over 5.60 and 2.50 mm to approximately 0.50 and 0.30 mm, respectively. Benefited from the fact that only six displacement sensors are used, the proposed method is cost-effective. Also, the proposed calibration process can be completed without tedious manual teaching and, thus, easy to achieve autonomous calibration."},"title":{"value":"Closed-Loop Kinematic Calibration of Robots Using a Six-Point Measuring Device"},"authors":{"value":["Ying Liu","Zhenghao Zhuang","Yuwen Li"]}},"tmdate":1747369118074,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1747367594088,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yuwen_Li3"],"forum":"G32mYNekEe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":503803,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747369118074,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"G32mYNekEe","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10654794/10654797/10657158.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|fcs_feature_calibration_and_separation_for_nonexemplar_class_incremental_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Qiwei_Li1","~Yuxin_Peng1","~Jiahuan_Zhou1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52733.2024.02692"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/LiPZ24,\n  author={Qiwei Li and Yuxin Peng and Jiahuan Zhou},\n  title={FCS: Feature Calibration and Separation for Non-Exemplar Class Incremental Learning},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={28495-28504},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52733.2024.02692},\n  booktitle={CVPR},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Non-Exemplar Class Incremental Learning (NECIL) involves learning a classification model on a sequence of data without access to exemplars from previously encountered old classes. Such a stringent constraint always leads to catastrophic forgetting of the learned knowledge. Currently, existing methods either employ knowledge distillation techniques or preserved class prototypes to sustain prior knowledge. However, two critical issues still persist. On the one hand, as the model is continually updated, the preserved prototypes of old classes will inevitably derive from the suitable location in the feature space of the new model. On the other hand, due to the lack of exemplars, the features of new classes will take the place of similar old classes which breaks the classification boundary. To address these challenges, we propose a Feature Calibration and Separation (FCS) method for NECIL. Our approach comprises a Feature Calibration Network (FCN) that adapts prototypes of old classes to the new model via optimal transport learning, approximating the drift of prototypes caused by model evolution. Additionally, we also propose a Prototype-Involved Contrastive Loss (PIC) that enhances feature separation among different classes. Specifically, to mitigate the boundary distortion arising from the interplay of classes from different learning stages, prototypes are involved in pushing the feature of new classes away from the old classes. Extensive experiments on three datasets with different settings have demonstrated the superiority of our FCS method against the state-of-the-art class incremental learning approaches. Code is available at https://github.com/zhoujiahuan1991/CVPR2024-FCS."},"title":{"value":"FCS: Feature Calibration and Separation for Non-Exemplar Class Incremental Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Qiwei Li","Yuxin Peng","Jiahuan Zhou"]}},"tmdate":1757917239667,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731474440168,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yuxin_Peng1"],"forum":"Amz1Dyy768","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":197854,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1757917239667,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Amz1Dyy768","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Neural Comput. Appl. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s00521-024-10748-4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/NCA/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"mohammed|machine_learningbased_models_for_predicting_calibration_cost_of_flowmeters_in_the_oil_industry"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Awsan_Mohammed:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abdullah_Javaid:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ahmed_M._Ghaithan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Osamah_AlDhafer:","~Maged_Saeed1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Adel_Alshibani:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00521-024-10748-4"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/nca/MohammedJGAAA25,\n  author={Awsan Mohammed and Abdullah Javaid and Ahmed M. Ghaithan and Osamah AlDhafer and Maged Saeed AlShaibani and Adel Alshibani},\n  title={Machine learning-based models for predicting calibration cost of flowmeters in the oil industry},\n  year={2025},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1738368000000},\n  journal={Neural Comput. Appl.},\n  volume={37},\n  number={4},\n  pages={2537-2556},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s00521-024-10748-4}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate measurement of liquid volume in pipelines is a crucial and sensitive process in the petroleum industry. Flowmeter is a common device used to measure the volume of the liquid passing through the pipeline and to invoice clients. Improving flowmeter calibration preserves functionality, identifies issues, and ensures accurate market valuation and pricing. Estimating the cost of third-party calibration is a challenging task for decision-makers in the petroleum industry. Thus, this paper proposes machine-learning algorithms to predict and analyze the cost of flowmeter calibration in the petroleum industry. These techniques include artificial neural networks, SGDRegressor, XGBoost, AdaBoost, support vector machine, K-nearest neighbors, and random forest. Prior to developing the model, the factors impacting the cost calibration of the flowmeters are identified from the literature and finalized by oil and gas experts. In addition, the relationship between the input factors and the output is examined to ensure the quality of the data. The analysis indicated that the most crucial factors affecting the calibration cost are the flowmeter size, flange class, flowmeter type, calibration method, and calibration factor. Furthermore, the developed machine learning-based models are validated by using 153 new additional data. The results revealed that the random forest is the best technique for estimating calibration costs, with an accuracy of 99%, followed by AdaBoost with an accuracy of 97%, and artificial neural network with an accuracy of 96%. The proposed models provide superior accuracy and efficiency which will significantly contribute to estimating calibration costs, assisting decision-makers in establishing a budget for flowmeter calibration."},"title":{"value":"Machine learning-based models for predicting calibration cost of flowmeters in the oil industry"},"authors":{"value":["Awsan Mohammed","Abdullah Javaid","Ahmed M. Ghaithan","Osamah AlDhafer","Maged Saeed AlShaibani","Adel Alshibani"]}},"tmdate":1767788903859,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/nca/MohammedJGAAA25"],"tcdate":1767788900588,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Maged_Saeed1"],"forum":"B8K6puw6k0","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":726753,"cdate":1738368000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1767788903859,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"B8K6puw6k0","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Under review for TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent progress in supervised multi-label abnormality classification for three-dimensional (3D) computed tomography (CT) has been driven by increasingly powerful visual encoders. \nHowever, current evaluation remains largely limited to discriminative and ranking-based metrics, overlooking the reliability of predicted probabilities. \nCalibration, the alignment between predicted confidence and true outcome likelihood, is critical for clinical deployment, where probabilities inform decision-making and risk assessment. \nIn this work, we present an evaluation of calibration in multi-label abnormality classification from 3D CT scans. We benchmark representative architectures spanning convolutional, transformer-based, and hybrid CT visual encoders under a unified training protocol across two public datasets, and extend our analysis to CT foundation models spanning diverse supervision paradigms and training data domains.\nOur results show that architectures achieving the best performance on standard predictive metrics are not consistently the best calibrated, and that calibration is observed to further degrade under distribution shift. We further identify that miscalibration is structured and data-dependent, increasing with abnormality prevalence and volumetric size, varying across anatomical systems, and exacerbated by abnormality co-occurrence. These findings highlight fundamental limitations of current evaluation practices and emphasize the need for calibration-aware modeling in medical image analysis."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026beyond,\ntitle={Beyond Discrimination: Evaluating the Calibration of Computed Tomography Visual Encoders},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=iIHVJCnvfS},\nnote={Under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Beyond Discrimination: Evaluating the Calibration of Computed Tomography Visual Encoders"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e774be4c489658ce38ca74e7e539745e215d5bb4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Under_Review"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Wenrui_Dai1"}},"tmdate":1784603881662,"tcdate":1783621484030,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper10405/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper10405/Authors"],"forum":"iIHVJCnvfS","number":10405,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1783621484030,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review"],"mdate":1784603881662,"odate":1784603875696,"domain":"TMLR","id":"iIHVJCnvfS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2207.10454v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|temporal_and_spatial_online_integrated_calibration_for_camera_and_lidar"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shouan_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinyu_Zhang_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:GuiPeng_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yijin_Xiong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ganglin_Tian:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shichun_Guo:","~Jun_Li25"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2207.10454"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2207-10454,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shouan Wang and Xinyu Zhang and GuiPeng Zhang and Yijin Xiong and Ganglin Tian and Shichun Guo and Jun Li},\n  title={Temporal and Spatial Online Integrated Calibration for Camera and LiDAR},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2207.10454},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2207.10454}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"While camera and LiDAR are widely used in most of the assisted and autonomous driving systems, only a few works have been proposed to associate the temporal synchronization and extrinsic calibration for camera and LiDAR which are dedicated to online sensors data fusion. The temporal and spatial calibration technologies are facing the challenges of lack of relevance and real-time. In this paper, we introduce the pose estimation model and environmental robust line features extraction to improve the relevance of data fusion and instant online ability of correction. Dynamic targets eliminating aims to seek optimal policy considering the correspondence of point cloud matching between adjacent moments. The searching optimization process aims to provide accurate parameters with both computation accuracy and efficiency. To demonstrate the benefits of this method, we evaluate it on the KITTI benchmark with ground truth value. In online experiments, our approach improves the accuracy by 38.5\\% than the soft synchronization method in temporal calibration. While in spatial calibration, our approach automatically corrects disturbance errors within 0.4 second and achieves an accuracy of 0.3-degree. This work can promote the research and application of sensor fusion."},"title":{"value":"Temporal and Spatial Online Integrated Calibration for Camera and LiDAR"},"authors":{"value":["Shouan Wang","Xinyu Zhang","GuiPeng Zhang","Yijin Xiong","Ganglin Tian","Shichun Guo","Jun Li"]}},"tmdate":1731561322632,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731561298365,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jun_Li25"],"forum":"N9yD0lxjoc","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":237745,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731561322632,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"N9yD0lxjoc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ITSC 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9921415/9921739/09921858.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ITSC/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|temporal_and_spatial_online_integrated_calibration_for_camera_and_lidar"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shouan_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinyu_Zhang_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:GuiPeng_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yijin_Xiong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ganglin_Tian:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shichun_Guo:","~Jun_Li25","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pingping_Lu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junqing_Wei:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Tian:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC55140.2022.9921858"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/itsc/WangZZXTGLLWT22,\n  author={Shouan Wang and Xinyu Zhang and GuiPeng Zhang and Yijin Xiong and Ganglin Tian and Shichun Guo and Jun Li and Pingping Lu and Junqing Wei and Lei Tian},\n  title={Temporal and Spatial Online Integrated Calibration for Camera and LiDAR},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={3016-3022},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC55140.2022.9921858},\n  booktitle={ITSC},\n  crossref={conf/itsc/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"While camera and LiDAR are widely used in most of the assisted and autonomous driving systems, only a few works have been proposed to associate the temporal synchro-nization and extrinsic calibration for camera and LiDAR which are dedicated to online sensors data fusion. The temporal and spatial calibration technologies are facing the challenges of lack of relevance and real-time. In this paper, we introduce the pose estimation model and environmental robust line features extraction to improve the relevance of data fusion and instant online ability of correction. Dynamic targets eliminating aims to seek optimal policy considering the correspondence of point cloud matching between adjacent moments. The searching optimization process aims to provide accurate parameters with both computation accuracy and efficiency. To demonstrate the benefits of this method, we evaluate it on the KITTI benchmark with ground truth value. In online experiments, our approach improves the accuracy by 38.5 % than the soft synchronization method in temporal calibration. While in spatial calibration, our approach automatically corrects disturbance errors within 0.4 second and achieves an accuracy of 0.3-degree. This work can promote the research and application of sensor fusion."},"title":{"value":"Temporal and Spatial Online Integrated Calibration for Camera and LiDAR"},"authors":{"value":["Shouan Wang","Xinyu Zhang","GuiPeng Zhang","Yijin Xiong","Ganglin Tian","Shichun Guo","Jun Li","Pingping Lu","Junqing Wei","Lei Tian"]}},"tmdate":1731561318142,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731561298214,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jun_Li25"],"forum":"Z31Ow8jAEy","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":237737,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731561318142,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Z31Ow8jAEy","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"This work demonstrates that VLMs are persistently overconfident in medical VQA regardless of scale or prompting, shows that post-hoc calibration effectively reduces this, and proposes hallucination-aware calibration for better discrimination."},"venue":{"value":"COLM 2026"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the COLM Code of Ethics on https://colmweb.org/CoE.html"},"LLM_usage":{"value":"In accordance with the Policy on the use of Large Language Models in the Call for Papers https://colmweb.org/cfp.html, I certify that my submission discloses any substantive use of LLMs in the research and paper-writing process."},"keywords":{"value":["Medical Visual Question Answering","Confidence Calibration"]},"abstract":{"value":"As vision-language models (VLMs) are increasingly deployed in clinical decision support, more than accuracy is required: knowing when to trust their predictions is equally critical. Yet, a comprehensive and systematic investigation into the overconfidence of these models remains notably scarce in the medical domain. We address this gap through a comprehensive empirical study of confidence calibration in VLMs, spanning three model families (Qwen3-VL, InternVL3, LLaVA-NeXT), three model scales (2B--38B), and multiple confidence estimation prompting strategies, across three medical visual question answering (VQA) benchmarks. Our study yields three key findings: First, overconfidence persists across model families and is not resolved by scaling or prompting, such as chain-of-thought and verbalized confidence variants. Second, simple post-hoc calibration approaches, such as Platt scaling, reduce calibration error and consistently outperform the prompt-based strategy. Third, due to their (strict) monotonicity, these post-hoc calibration methods are inherently limited in improving the discriminative quality of predictions, leaving AUROC at the same level. Motivated by these findings, we investigate hallucination-aware calibration (HAC), which incorporates vision-grounded hallucination detection signals as complementary inputs to refine confidence estimates. We find that leveraging these hallucination signals improves both calibration and AUROC, with the largest gains on open-ended questions. On closed-ended questions, format-specific signals such as the logit margin show more informative. Overall, our findings suggest post-hoc calibration as standard practice for medical VLM deployment over raw confidence estimates, and highlight the practical usefulness of hallucination signals to enable more reliable use of VLMs in medical VQA."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nbyun2026overconfidence,\ntitle={Overconfidence and Calibration in Medical {VQA}: Empirical Findings and Hallucination-Aware Mitigation},\nauthor={Ji Young Byun and Young-Jin Park and Jean-Philippe Corbeil and Asma Ben Abacha},\nbooktitle={Third Conference on Language Modeling},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=JH5MAXhj4v}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Overconfidence and Calibration in Medical VQA: Empirical Findings and Hallucination-Aware Mitigation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2b18f45393b6b90c1d379409f62ecd9a1d5286b3.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"byun|overconfidence_and_calibration_in_medical_vqa_empirical_findings_and_hallucinationaware_mitigation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ji_Young_Byun1","~Young-Jin_Park1","~Jean-Philippe_Corbeil1","~Asma_Ben_Abacha1"]},"author_guide":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://colmweb.org/AuthorGuide.html"},"authors":{"value":["Ji Young Byun","Young-Jin Park","Jean-Philippe Corbeil","Asma Ben Abacha"]}},"tmdate":1787537168888,"pdate":1783543379799,"tcdate":1774493248854,"writers":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission1525/Authors"],"signatures":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission1525/Authors"],"forum":"JH5MAXhj4v","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1525,"cdate":1774493248854,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission1525/-/Full_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Edit","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission1525/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1787537168888,"odate":1787537168832,"domain":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","id":"JH5MAXhj4v","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ROBIO 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7861993/7866265/07866370.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ROBIO/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"su|toward_flexible_calibration_of_headmounted_gaze_trackers_with_parallax_error_compensation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dan_Su_0001:","~Youfu_Li1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBIO.2016.7866370"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/robio/SuL16,\n  author={Dan Su and Youfu Li},\n  title={Toward flexible calibration of head-mounted gaze trackers with parallax error compensation},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  pages={491-496},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBIO.2016.7866370},\n  booktitle={ROBIO},\n  crossref={conf/robio/2016}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Although the mobile head-mounted gaze tracker (HMGT) has gained its great success in human-machine interactions, the real implementation of HMGT still poses several significant challenges. The parallax error and the tedious calibration procedure, as two of these challenges, will be addressed in our proposed two-step calibration method. In the first step, instead of fixating at several pre-defined calibration points successively, the user is only required to change his or her head pose while gazing at one calibration marker with allowance of short-period distractions. Users can continuously change head poses or keep each of their head poses for 1~2 seconds before changing to the next head pose. Then the traditional Gaussian process (GP) regression considering the dominant linear trend and a sparse GP regression using pseudo-inputs are applied for above two kinds of head motions to register input space of glint-pupil vectors with output space of image gaze points, respectively. Besides, the heat map, the correlation test and blob analysis are made use of to detect users' distractions and eye blinks during the calibration phase. The second step involves recovering the epipolar geometry set up by HMGT and eyeballs. To this end, a nonlinear optimization problem is formulated to search for the geometry parameter set which could well explain the detected parallax errors resulted from varying the depth of the calibration point. Then the real image gaze point can be straightforwardly estimated as the intersection of two epipolar lines calculated from binocular data. The simulation and experimental results validate the effectiveness of our proposed calibration method."},"title":{"value":"Toward flexible calibration of head-mounted gaze trackers with parallax error compensation"},"authors":{"value":["Dan Su","Youfu Li"]}},"tmdate":1747450068105,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1747450048763,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Youfu_Li1"],"forum":"Eu7a3GHQOQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":518542,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747450068105,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Eu7a3GHQOQ","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"venue":{"value":"EWRL 2026 Poster"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["reinforcement learning","theoretical reinforcement learning","Bellman regression","value-based reinforcement learning","surrogate losses","Bellman calibration","Bregman divergence","proper scoring rules","misspecification geometry","finite-horizon MDPs"]},"abstract":{"value":"We study which surrogate losses are admissible for Bellman regression in finite-horizon reinforcement learning. We define \\emph{Bellman-composability}: a loss and decoder are Bellman-composable if every Bayes-optimal fit to any Bellman target law decodes to its Bellman mean. In the scalar regime, we prove that Bellman-composability is equivalent to strict mean-consistency and hence, under standard regularity assumptions, to strict generalized Bregman mean scoring. We then establish a dynamic Bellman-calibration theorem: stagewise surrogate excess risk controls one-step Bellman residuals and therefore propagates to sup-norm \\(Q\\)-error and greedy-policy suboptimality. In the scalar Bregman regime, we show that excess surrogate risk is exactly a Bregman divergence to the Bellman mean, yielding exact calibration formulas and a universal scalar barrier: every smooth scalar Bellman-composable loss has worst-case calibration modulus of order \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt{\\varepsilon})\\), and this order is witnessed by a one-step control lower bound. Finally, we prove a local misspecification-geometry theorem showing that admissible losses induce curvature-weighted projection operators; consequently, losses that agree on the target and on worst-case calibration can nevertheless exhibit different first-order misspecification amplification. Together, these results separate admissibility, calibration, and approximation geometry, and provide a theory-first foundation for loss design in value-based reinforcement learning."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nsaravanan2026bellmanadmissible,\ntitle={Bellman-Admissible Scalar Losses: Dynamic Calibration, Fundamental Limits, and Misspecification Geometry},\nauthor={Manoj Saravanan},\nbooktitle={Nineteenth European Workshop on Reinforcement Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=TQ7DxflQMm}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Bellman-Admissible Scalar Losses: Dynamic Calibration, Fundamental Limits, and Misspecification Geometry"},"oral_presentation":{"value":"No, we do not want to be considered."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6e9c680cc926adc9662a76e19e71ab807563a659.pdf"},"fast_track":{"value":"No"},"venueid":{"value":"EWRL/2026/Workshop"},"paperhash":{"value":"saravanan|bellmanadmissible_scalar_losses_dynamic_calibration_fundamental_limits_and_misspecification_geometry"},"authorids":{"value":["~Manoj_Saravanan1"]},"authors":{"value":["Manoj Saravanan"]}},"tmdate":1791064077900,"pdate":1791064075048,"tcdate":1779887075142,"writers":["EWRL/2026/Workshop","EWRL/2026/Workshop/Submission64/Authors"],"signatures":["EWRL/2026/Workshop/Submission64/Authors"],"forum":"TQ7DxflQMm","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":64,"cdate":1779887075142,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["EWRL/2026/Workshop/-/Submission","EWRL/2026/Workshop/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","EWRL/2026/Workshop/Submission64/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","EWRL/2026/Workshop/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1791064077900,"odate":1791064075048,"domain":"EWRL/2026/Workshop","id":"TQ7DxflQMm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["Conformal prediction","Robustness","Uncertainty quantification"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/7dcbea05f3f3aaabdc1de2cf35282fd716602611.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzargarbashi2026frontloaded,\ntitle={Front-Loaded Robust Conformal Prediction: Heavy Calibration, Minimal Test-Time Cost},\nauthor={Soroush H. Zargarbashi and Mohammad Sadegh Akhondzadeh and Aleksandar Bojchevski},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=OfXpAituJ9}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Front-Loaded Robust Conformal Prediction: Heavy Calibration, Minimal Test-Time Cost"},"paperhash":{"value":"zargarbashi|frontloaded_robust_conformal_prediction_heavy_calibration_minimal_testtime_cost"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/e8df9543f2176c614db735c6611518c18d3381b3.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"Leveraging the computation in pre-processing calibration step, we propose a much more efficient robust conformal prediction that still benefits from single-sample (and very low sampling) inference during test-time."},"primary_area":{"value":"deep_learning->robustness"},"abstract":{"value":"Robust conformal prediction (RCP) extends conformal prediction (CP) to noisy inputs by producing prediction sets with guaranteed coverage, ensuring that the true label is contained in the set with a user-specified probability even under worst-case perturbations. Recent works use randomized smoothing, as it provides robustness for black-box models at larger radii. Currently, there exist two setups for smoothing-based RCP: one requires extensive Monte Carlo sampling at calibration and test time but results in smaller prediction sets; the other setup produces larger prediction sets but uses a single sample at both stages. In deployment, calibration—as a one-time pre-processing step—can accommodate substantially higher computational overhead than inference. Inspired by this observation, we introduce an RCP framework that strikes a balance between the two extremes of this trade-off: we increase the sample rate at calibration time while keeping it either one or very low during test time. This calibration-time sampling opens the possibility of reducing the size of the prediction sets. In production, where the number of test predictions typically far exceeds the size of the calibration set, our Front-Loaded RCP matches the computational complexity of the state of the art while producing considerably smaller prediction sets at larger radii."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://github.com/soroushzargar/RCP1"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/41cd4236920053d4778c1de01dde28c6a8a50637.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"AI systems used in high-stakes settings—such as medical diagnosis or autonomous driving—must communicate not just a prediction but a reliable measure of their uncertainty. Conformal prediction as a representation of such uncertainty returns a set of plausible answers guaranteed to contain the correct solution with high probability. However, this guarantee collapses when inputs are slightly corrupted by noise or adversarial manipulation. Robust conformal prediction addresses this issue.\nAmong existing approaches, a fundamental trade-off exists: methods that run the model hundreds of times per input produce small, informative sets but are too slow for real-time use, while methods that use a single model evaluation are fast but return larger, less informative sets. We resolve this tension by observing that the one-time setup phase before deployment—calibration—can absorb high computational cost without affecting response time. Concentrating all heavy computation in calibration while keeping inference to a single model evaluation\nWe additionally address a separate limitation of single-evaluation methods: their outputs are inherently random, meaning the same input can yield unpredictably large sets across runs. By accounting for (low-rate) repeated sampling at inference time we extend our method to produce near-deterministic, consistently sized sets without sacrificing the robustness guarantee. Importantly, both methods require no knowledge of the model's internal architecture, training procedure, or data distribution, making them directly applicable to any existing AI system as a post-hoc wrapper."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Soroush_H._Zargarbashi1","~Mohammad_Sadegh_Akhondzadeh1","~Aleksandar_Bojchevski1"]},"authors":{"value":["Soroush H. Zargarbashi","Mohammad Sadegh Akhondzadeh","Aleksandar Bojchevski"]}},"tmdate":1790067329794,"pdate":1777576331000,"tcdate":1768902522064,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission9483/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission9483/Authors"],"forum":"OfXpAituJ9","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":9483,"cdate":1768902522064,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission9483/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission9483/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067329794,"odate":1782341934266,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"OfXpAituJ9","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"CONCORD trains LLMs to output calibrated verbalized confidence from rollout consistency alone, eliminating the need for labels while preserving single-response inference."},"venue":{"value":"COLM 2026"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the COLM Code of Ethics on https://colmweb.org/CoE.html"},"LLM_usage":{"value":"In accordance with the Policy on the use of Large Language Models in the Call for Papers https://colmweb.org/cfp.html, I certify that my submission discloses any substantive use of LLMs in the research and paper-writing process."},"keywords":{"value":["confidence calibration","large language models","verbalized confidence","uncertainty estimation","label-free learning","semantic consistency"]},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrated confidence, where a model's stated probability matches its actual expected accuracy, is often essential in contexts where confidence scores are relied upon by humans to make decisions or serve as inputs to downstream agents. Existing approaches for training models to verbalize calibrated confidence typically require ground-truth correctness labels, which are costly to collect and unavailable for many prompt distributions. Multi-pass consistency methods achieve good calibration but significantly increase inference costs.\nWe introduce CONCORD, a label-free method for training language models to improve the calibration of verbalized confidence scores without adding a dedicated calibration stage. Adapting models with Group Relative Policy Optimization already yields multiple rollouts per prompt; we repurpose these rollouts as a calibration signal by deriving confidence targets from semantic agreement among sampled answers, using either Natural Language Inference-based answer clustering or semantic entropy. The resulting reward encourages the model to align its stated confidence with empirical semantic agreement, without requiring ground-truth labels. During inference, the model generates a reasoning trace, an answer, and a better calibrated confidence score in a single pass. Across six benchmarks and four models, CONCORD substantially reduces expected calibration error over unadapted verbalized confidence and over label-dependent adaptation baselines, while maintaining competitive AUROC."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nschall2026concord,\ntitle={{CONCORD}: Label-Free Calibration of Verbalized {LLM} Confidence via Rollout Consistency},\nauthor={Maximilian Schall and Gerard de Melo and Padhraic Smyth},\nbooktitle={Third Conference on Language Modeling},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ToJd6W2TJc}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CONCORD: Label-Free Calibration of Verbalized LLM Confidence via Rollout Consistency"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/42a9dea72ab32ec37b32e919aac5834fdf444565.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"schall|concord_labelfree_calibration_of_verbalized_llm_confidence_via_rollout_consistency"},"authorids":{"value":["~Maximilian_Schall1","~Gerard_de_Melo3","~Padhraic_Smyth1"]},"author_guide":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://colmweb.org/AuthorGuide.html"},"authors":{"value":["Maximilian Schall","Gerard de Melo","Padhraic Smyth"]}},"tmdate":1787537174003,"pdate":1783543389558,"tcdate":1774546511747,"writers":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission2148/Authors"],"signatures":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission2148/Authors"],"forum":"ToJd6W2TJc","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2148,"cdate":1774546511747,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission2148/-/Full_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Edit","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission2148/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1787537174003,"odate":1787537173991,"domain":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","id":"ToJd6W2TJc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/9058729/08763999.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"chi|a_novel_multicamera_global_calibration_method_for_gaze_tracking_system"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jiannan_Chi1","","","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2019.2922754"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/ChiYZLW20,\n  author={Jiannan Chi and Zuoyun Yang and Guosheng Zhang and Tongbo Liu and Zhiliang Wang},\n  title={A Novel Multi-Camera Global Calibration Method for Gaze Tracking System},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={69},\n  number={5},\n  pages={2093-2104},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2019.2922754}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In a 3-D gaze tracking system, the system calibration is used to determine the positions of the light sources and the screen in the camera coordinate system, which plays an important role in the estimation of the 3-D gaze and the point of regard (PoR). The traditional way for the system calibration is the mirror-based method, in which a mirror is used to make the light sources and the screen can be captured by the camera through specular reflection. However, the mirror-based method is imprecise and complex. In this paper, a novel system-calibration method for the 3-D gaze tracking system is proposed. On the basis of the system camera, a calibration camera is placed on the opposite of the screen to constitute a multi-camera system. The images are captured by the two cameras to estimate the light sources and the screen of the system camera coordinate frame. Simulations and experiments were carried out to illustrate the validation of the method. This method solves the problem that the light sources and the screen are not in view of the system camera. In addition, it is easy to implement while ensuring high accuracy and providing a new method for other similar calibration systems."},"title":{"value":"A Novel Multi-Camera Global Calibration Method for Gaze Tracking System"},"authors":{"value":["Jiannan Chi","Zuoyun Yang","Guosheng Zhang","Tongbo Liu","Zhiliang Wang"]}},"tmdate":1774446459822,"pdate":1609372800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tim/ChiYZLW20"],"tcdate":1774446456190,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jiannan_Chi1"],"forum":"PKBkDPfA62","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":859497,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1774446459822,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"PKBkDPfA62","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/19/10367905/10726792.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"mao|joint_deformation_compensation_algorithm_for_robust_kinematic_calibration_of_serial_robots"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Yuhua_Zheng1","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2024.3480196"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/MaoZXL24,\n  author={Chentao Mao and Yuhua Zheng and Zihao Xing and Shuai Li},\n  title={Joint Deformation Compensation Algorithm for Robust Kinematic Calibration of Serial Robots},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={73},\n  pages={1-11},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2024.3480196}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration is a vital method for improving the performance of robots. While the Denavit-Hartenberg (DH) model is a widely used framework in robot calibration, challenges remain in accurately compensating for joint deformations caused by the robot’s weight or end payload, which negatively impact absolute positioning accuracy. In addition, conventional methods can encounter convergence issues during optimization. This article proposes a robust calibration algorithm that addresses these challenges by leveraging the unique characteristics of the modified DH (MDH) model, considering joint deformation, and the separable nonlinear least squares (SNLS) algorithm. The method utilizes the MDH model’s ability to separate linear and nonlinear parameters, transforming the calibration task into an SNLS optimization problem. This enhances robustness during parameter identification by eliminating partial linear model parameters. The SNLS algorithm also reduces the number of nonlinear parameters to be identified, resulting in fewer iterations and faster convergence, significantly decreasing dependence on initial values. Numerical and experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed SNLS algorithm. The method significantly improves robot positioning accuracy, particularly under varying load conditions, from 9.43 to 0.71 mm, a 92.47% improvement. These findings highlight the substantial advances and importance of the proposed calibration algorithm in enhancing robot performance and reliability."},"title":{"value":"Joint Deformation Compensation Algorithm for Robust Kinematic Calibration of Serial Robots"},"authors":{"value":["Chentao Mao","Yuhua Zheng","Zihao Xing","Shuai Li"]}},"tmdate":1772443143587,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tim/MaoZXL24"],"tcdate":1772443141756,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yuhua_Zheng1"],"forum":"PdZ7K9jLg8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":844663,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772443143587,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"PdZ7K9jLg8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Comput. Environ. Urban Syst. 2012"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/URBAN/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"yue|exploratory_calibration_of_a_spatial_interaction_model_using_taxi_gps_trajectories"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yang_Yue6","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Handong_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bo_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qingquan_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuguang_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Anthony_Gar-On_Yeh:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compenvurbsys.2011.09.002"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/urban/YueWHLLY12,\n  author={Yang Yue and Handong Wang and Bo Hu and Qingquan Li and Yuguang Li and Anthony Gar-On Yeh},\n  title={Exploratory calibration of a spatial interaction model using taxi GPS trajectories},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  journal={Comput. Environ. Urban Syst.},\n  volume={36},\n  number={2},\n  pages={140-153},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compenvurbsys.2011.09.002}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Model calibration is the cornerstone of spatial interaction models in many geographic, transportation and marketing analysis. Conventional questionnaire approaches that collect data for model calibration are both labor-intensive and time-consuming, and generally show a poor response rate. This study takes advantage of increasingly available vehicle GPS trajectory data to conduct spatial interaction model calibration. A Huff model for retail trading area analysis was used as an example. Model calibration and parameter validation were conducted based on over 63,000 taxi GPS trajectories for seven major shopping centers in Wuhan, a large city in China. The results were positive and in general showed satisfactory descriptive and predictive capability. This study demonstrated the feasibility of using the emerging technology to calibrate spatial interaction models (and also showed the potential for use in other related studies). The main advantage of using these new data sources is that they allow efficient use of increasingly available positioning data, which is easier to collect than conventional customer surveys, and usually with larger data sizes. It also allows inferences to be made about distance-decay rates based on accurate computation of travel time and distance. This could save both time and expense in many related areas of research, while achieving high quality model calibration results."},"title":{"value":"Exploratory calibration of a spatial interaction model using taxi GPS trajectories"},"authors":{"value":["Yang Yue","Handong Wang","Bo Hu","Qingquan Li","Yuguang Li","Anthony Gar-On Yeh"]}},"tmdate":1740747653034,"pdate":1325376000000,"tcdate":1740747624620,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yang_Yue6"],"forum":"8hGmVDCDqf","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":342342,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740747653034,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"8hGmVDCDqf","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/PhenetOs/CalibrationAttack"},"abstract":{"value":"In this work, we highlight and perform a comprehensive study on calibration attacks, a form of adversarial attacks that aim to trap victim models to be heavily miscalibrated without altering their predicted labels, hence endangering the trustworthiness of the models and follow-up decision making based on their confidence. We propose four typical forms of calibration attacks: underconfidence, overconfidence, maximum miscalibration, and random confidence attacks, conducted in both the black-box and white-box setups. We demonstrate that the attacks are highly effective on both convolutional and attention-based models: with a small number of queries, they seriously skew confidence without changing the predictive performance. Given the potential danger, we further investigate the effectiveness of a wide range of adversarial defence and recalibration methods, including our proposed defences specifically designed for calibration attacks to mitigate the harm. From the ECE and KS scores, we observe that there are still significant limitations in handling calibration attacks. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first dedicated study that provides a comprehensive investigation on calibration-focused attacks. We hope this study helps attract more attention to these types of attacks and hence hamper their potential serious damages. To this end, this work also provides detailed analyses to understand the characteristics of the attacks."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nobadinma2024calibration,\ntitle={Calibration Attacks: A Comprehensive Study of Adversarial Attacks on Model Confidence},\nauthor={Stephen Obadinma and Xiaodan Zhu and Hongyu Guo},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=TXzz9xwdpv},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration Attacks: A Comprehensive Study of Adversarial Attacks on Model Confidence"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"Dear Action Editor, \n\nFirst of all, we thank you for your comments. We have implemented all your suggested changes in our revision. Specifically, below please kindly find a summary of our revision.  \n\n**AE comment 1**: add discussion of the significance of the problem, in Section 3.4 or elsewhere. This can be a summary of the response to Reviewer 9Y8b.\n\nThank you. We have added into Section 3.4 a summary of the response to Reviewer 9Y8b and adjusted the original content of Section 3.4 to accommodate the change. In addition, the detailed, full-version discussion has been included in Appendix B, which has been referenced in Section 3.4 in case the readers want to read more details.\n\n**AE comment 2**: Add discussion on the focus of ECE versus downstream metrics.\n\nWe have added a discussion in Section 4.1 to describe the focus on intrinsic metrics (ECE and KS) versus downstream metrics, and why we focus on the former in our research. Specifically, the following passage has been added under the “Metrics” part in Section 4.1.\n\n“Same as in the previous work (Guo et al., 2017; Kumar et al., 2018; Minderer et al., 2021), we focus on intrinsic evaluation metrics in this research, which isolate our analysis from downstream tasks to abstain from task-specific conclusions, particularly considering the downstream tasks here are very diverse. In addition, the harm from miscalibration in downstream applications has been well studied in prior work, as detailed in Appendix B.”\n\n**AE comment 3**: add experimental results on the transferrability of the attacks across models, per the response to Reviewer 9Y8b.\n\nThank you. We have added the suggested experimental results on the transferability of the attacks across models in Section 4.7, titled “Attack Transferability”. Specifically, the following content has been added.\n\n“Determining the transferability of calibration attacks across different model architectures is of interest since although one can effectively optimize attacks against a single model, using an ensemble model could potentially be a form of defence if transferability is limited. We investigate how our attacks transfer to different victim models. Specifically in our experiments, the attacks are constructed on CIFAR-100 images with regular settings based on ViT but used on (i) ResNet and (ii) ensemble victim models combining prediction of both ResNet-50 and ViT (Table 4). We focus on the transfer of the UCA attack, given that OCA has little room to skew confidence due to the already high accuracy of victim models. The attacks show transferability, although in general they become less effective when applied on different architectures. This is expected because the attacks are constructed without considering the target victim models’ properties. Most notably, the simple ensemble of two typical victim models (ViT and ResNet) is still greatly subject to the attacks (the output distributions of ResNet and ViT are averaged to get the ensemble), so using ensembles as a defence does not fully protect against calibration attacks. To further explore transferability, attack methods need to be explicitly redesigned (Xie et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2021), which could be further studied in the future.”\n\n**AE comment 4**: add experimental results on Swin Transformers, per the response to Reviewer nBsh.\n\nWe have summarized and added the observation on Swin transformer in the first paragraph of Section 4.2, confirming it shows similar trends as those presented in Table 1. The detailed results have been added into appendix H.8, considering ResNet and ViT are regarded as the most typical frameworks for convolution and attention-based models, respectively. The Swin transformer is an extension of ViT, and the experiments results on it agree with those on ViT. Specifically, the following description has been added into the first paragraph of Section 4.2.\n\n“While ResNet and ViT are the most representative frameworks for convolution and attention-based models respectively, in Appendix H.8 we include additional experiments on the Swin Transformer (Liu et al., 2021), which is a famous extension of ViT. The results show similar trends as those presented in Table 1. Again, Figure 1 in the introduction section demonstrates the attack effects using calibration diagrams, visually showing the severity of miscalibration.”\n\n**AE comment 5**: typos\n\nThank you. We have fixed them. We have also read the paper multiple more times.\n\n**AE comment 6**: Fig. 1-2 font sizes\n\nWe have increased font sizes of x-axis in Fig 1 and legends in Fig 2.\n\n**AE comment 7**: format of Eq 3.\n\nThank you. We have used the \\rm font.\n\n**Additional Changes**: In addition, we have added section 4.8 “Influence of Model Scale on Attack Effectiveness” to include results that we provided in the rebuttal period, to address the suggestions from the reviewer about the effect of model scale.\n\nFinally, we really appreciate your time and the reviewers' time. Thank you!\n\nBest regards,"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/0c7af0e8b8d3985245dc708e913e478047e3bab6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"obadinma|calibration_attacks_a_comprehensive_study_of_adversarial_attacks_on_model_confidence"},"authorids":{"value":["~Stephen_Obadinma1","~Xiaodan_Zhu1","~Hongyu_Guo1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Aditya_Menon1"},"authors":{"value":["Stephen Obadinma","Xiaodan Zhu","Hongyu Guo"]}},"tmdate":1732241430926,"pdate":1732241430860,"tcdate":1715899760243,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper2705/Authors"],"forum":"TXzz9xwdpv","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2705,"cdate":1715899760243,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper2705/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper2705/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1732241430926,"odate":1716307500229,"domain":"TMLR","id":"TXzz9xwdpv","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"2022 IEEE/SICE International Symposium on System Integration, SII 2022"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"madsen|aurt_a_tool_for_dynamics_calibration_of_robot_manipulators"},"html":{"value":"https://pure.au.dk/portal/en/publications/8bd92c41-159b-4dff-bc8e-1205752425af"},"abstract":{"value":"We introduce AURT, an open-source software for modeling and calibration of robot manipulator dynamics. AURT includes rigid-body dynamics and a selection of joint dynamics models. The separation of the robot dynamics into rigid-body dynamics and joint dynamics allows for fast recalibration of the robot dynamics using different joint dynamics models. We evaluate the calibration performance using a Universal Robots UR5e robot manipulator. We also demonstrate how the joint dynamics models embedded in AURT outperforms the standard approach of the state of the art."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{8bd92c41159b4dffbc8e1205752425af,\n  title     = \"AURT: A Tool for Dynamics Calibration of Robot Manipulators\",\n  abstract  = \"We introduce AURT, an open-source software for modeling and calibration of robot manipulator dynamics. AURT includes rigid-body dynamics and a selection of joint dynamics models. The separation of the robot dynamics into rigid-body dynamics and joint dynamics allows for fast recalibration of the robot dynamics using different joint dynamics models. We evaluate the calibration performance using a Universal Robots UR5e robot manipulator. We also demonstrate how the joint dynamics models embedded in AURT outperforms the standard approach of the state of the art.\",\n  author    = \"Emil Madsen and Daniella Tola and Carlos Hansen and Cl{\\'a}udio Gomes and Larsen, \\{Peter Gorm\\}\",\n  year      = \"2022\",\n  month     = jan,\n  doi       = \"10.1109/SII52469.2022.9708769\",\n  language  = \"English\",\n  pages     = \"190--195\",\n  booktitle = \"2022 IEEE/SICE International Symposium on System Integration, SII 2022\",\n  publisher = \"IEEE\",\n  note      = \"2022 IEEE/SICE International Symposium on System Integration (SII) ; Conference date: 09-01-2022 Through 12-01-2022\",\n  url       = \"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/xpl/conhome/9708598/proceeding\",\n}"},"title":{"value":"AURT: A Tool for Dynamics Calibration of Robot Manipulators"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Emil Madsen"},{"fullname":"Daniella Tola"},{"fullname":"Carlos Hansen"},{"fullname":"Cláudio Gomes"},{"fullname":"Peter Gorm Larsen","username":"~Peter_Gorm_Larsen1"}]}},"tmdate":1789091873298,"pdate":1640995200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/sii52469.2022.9708769"],"tcdate":1769062729401,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Peter_Gorm_Larsen1"],"forum":"NwEfZbyyYG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":34879,"cdate":1646871045219,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091873298,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"NwEfZbyyYG","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"quantization, distillation, LLM efficiency"},"keywords":{"value":["calibration","model compression","quantization","knowledge distillation","large language models","expected calibration error","temperature scaling","uncertainty estimation","model deployment","empirical evaluation"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"N/A"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"Yes"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"§5"},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"No"},"B5_elaboration":{"value":"§4"},"A2_elaboration":{"value":"§10"},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"§5."},"B2_elaboration":{"value":"§10"},"B3_elaboration":{"value":"§10"},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"§5."},"C4_elaboration":{"value":"§5"},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"Yes"},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"§5."},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"§5"},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"§10"},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"N/A"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026compression,\ntitle={Compression Does Not Equally Hurt: Characterizing Calibration Drift in Quantized and Distilled {LLM}s},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=HR794qUqea},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Compression Does Not Equally Hurt: Characterizing Calibration Drift in Quantized and Distilled LLMs"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"Yes"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","NLP engineering experiment","Approaches to low-compute settings (efficiency)"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"N/A"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"Yes"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"Quantization and knowledge distillation are the dominant methods for compressing large language models before deployment. Their effects on accuracy are well documented; their effects on calibration — whether model confidence tracks empirical accuracy — are not. We evaluate calibration drift across a grid of 77 cells measuring Expected Calibration Error before and after compression.¹ Of these, 53 are clean non-baseline cells — compressed cells passing our anomaly criteria — on which all CCD-based claims rest. The headline finding is that compression’s calibration effect is sign-mixed rather than uniformly harmful: 32 of 53 cells show improved calibration, with the negative-CCD fraction varying by task (50% on NLI/CSQA, 64% on QA, 82% on XSum). We introduce the Compression Calibration Degradation (CCD) metric and propose an interpretive framework: overconfident baselines soften under logit perturbation; well-calibrated ones accumulate noise. The framework predicts a sign-flip in CCD as a function of baseline overconfidence and that the signed effect is detectable only on tasks with sufficient baseline-ECE variance. Both predictions are consistent with the data (pooled β₁ = −2.005, p < 10⁻⁸; per-task slopes negative on 3 of 4 tasks). At fixed deployment size, sequence-level distillation matches or improves same-size quantization on the joint accuracy-calibration frontier in 3 of 4 tasks after temperature scaling."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"N/A"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/332f9fc0042245033b8479c0be71260f77a71e5d.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Efficient Methods for NLP"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"yes"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"N/A"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1790696972287,"tcdate":1779720763399,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission8454/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission8454/Authors"],"forum":"HR794qUqea","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":8454,"cdate":1779720763399,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission8454/-/Blind_Submission_License_Agreement"],"mdate":1790696972287,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"HR794qUqea","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/93de5908cde62829956ccad817d20987f0b2aa9e.zip"},"abstract":{"value":"Offline deep reinforcement learning (offline DRL) has attracted considerable attention across various domains due to its ability to learn effective policies without direct environmental interaction. Although highly effective, the trustworthiness of agent concerns the community. Offline DRL can be categorized into three principal paradigms: model-based algorithms, model-free algorithms, and trajectory optimization. While extant research predominantly concentrates on calibration enhancement of model-based and model-free algorithms, calibration of trajectory optimization remains a rather rare topic. In this paper, we introduce the concept of Expected Agent Calibration Error (EACE), a novel metric designed to assess agent calibration. Furthermore, we rigorously prove its theoretical relationship to the state-action marginal distribution distance. Subsequently, we introduce the Calibration Enhanced Decision Maker (CEDM), which employs a binning executor to process feature distribution histograms as input for the large sequence model, thereby minimizing the state-action marginal distribution distance and enhancing the agent's calibration. A series of in-depth case studies of CEDM are carried out, with application on Decision Transformer, Decision ConvFormer, and Decision Mamba. Empirical results substantiate the robustness of EACE and demonstrate the effectiveness of CEDM in enhancing agent calibration, thereby offering valuable insights for future research on trustworthy sequential decision-making."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nsun2026calibration,\ntitle={Calibration Enhanced Decision Maker: Towards Trustworthy Sequential Decision-Making with Large Sequence Models},\nauthor={Haoyuan Sun and Bo Xia and Yifu Luo and Tiantian Zhang and Xueqian Wang},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=b6WcxPEb48},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration Enhanced Decision Maker: Towards Trustworthy Sequential Decision-Making with Large Sequence Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/21330c043922579d83428f5a250019fdedc16547.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"sun|calibration_enhanced_decision_maker_towards_trustworthy_sequential_decisionmaking_with_large_sequence_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Haoyuan_Sun2","~Bo_Xia1","~Yifu_Luo1","~Tiantian_Zhang2","~Xueqian_Wang1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Shaofeng_Zou1"},"authors":{"value":["Haoyuan Sun","Bo Xia","Yifu Luo","Tiantian Zhang","Xueqian Wang"]}},"tmdate":1768239560990,"pdate":1768239560931,"tcdate":1761384352116,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper6307/Authors"],"forum":"b6WcxPEb48","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6307,"cdate":1761384352116,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/Paper6307/-/Revision","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper6307/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1768239560990,"odate":1762533066356,"domain":"TMLR","id":"b6WcxPEb48","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2024 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Model calibration","Softmax loss","Gradient decay","PID controller","Supervised learning"]},"primary_area":{"value":"optimization_for_deep_networks"},"abstract":{"value":"Modern deep learning models often exhibit overconfident predictions, inadequately capturing uncertainty. During model optimization, the expected calibration error tends to overfit  earlier than classification accuracy, indicating distinct optimization objectives for classification error and calibration error. To ensure consistent optimization of both model accuracy and model calibration, we propose a novel method incorporating a probability-dependent gradient decay coefficient into loss function. This coefficient exhibits a strong correlation with the overall confidence level.  To maintain model calibration during optimization, we utilize a proportional-integral-derivative (PID) controller to dynamically adjust this gradient decay rate, where the adjustment relies on the proposed relative calibration error feedback in each epoch, thereby preventing the model from exhibiting over-confidence or under-confidence. Within the PID control system framework, the proposed relative calibration error serves as the control system output, providing an indication of the overall confidence level, while the gradient decay rate functions as the controlled variable. Moreover, recognizing the impact of gradient amplitude of adaptive decay rates, we implement an adaptive learning rate mechanism for gradient compensation to prevent inadequate learning of over-small or over-large gradient. Empirical experiments validate the efficacy of our PID-based adaptive gradient decay rate approach, ensuring consistent optimization of model calibration and model accuracy."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhang2024a,\ntitle={A {PID} Controller Approach for Adaptive Probability-dependent Gradient Decay in Model Calibration},\nauthor={Siyuan Zhang and Linbo Xie},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-eighth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=fAnubdSFpn}\n}"},"title":{"value":"A PID Controller Approach for Adaptive Probability-dependent Gradient Decay in Model Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7a0287f0d64f3b5142f6e9bf773c8521ed202538.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|a_pid_controller_approach_for_adaptive_probabilitydependent_gradient_decay_in_model_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Siyuan_Zhang6","~Linbo_Xie1"]},"authors":{"value":["Siyuan Zhang","Linbo Xie"]}},"tmdate":1730873887138,"pdate":1727287791599,"tcdate":1715564632791,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5676/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5676/Authors"],"forum":"fAnubdSFpn","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":5676,"cdate":1715564632791,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5676/-/Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5676/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1730873887138,"odate":1730873887114,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference","id":"fAnubdSFpn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Robotics Auton. Syst. 2002"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAS/2002"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|modelling_and_calibration_of_the_laser_beamscanning_triangulation_measurement_system"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guoyu_Wang:","~Bing_Zheng1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zweitze_Houkes:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Paul_P._L._Regtien:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0921-8890(02)00247-6"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ras/WangZLHR02,\n  author={Guoyu Wang and Bing Zheng and Xin Li and Zweitze Houkes and Paul P. L. Regtien},\n  title={Modelling and calibration of the laser beam-scanning triangulation measurement system},\n  year={2002},\n  cdate={1009843200000},\n  journal={Robotics Auton. Syst.},\n  volume={40},\n  number={4},\n  pages={267-277},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/S0921-8890(02)00247-6}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present an approach of modelling and calibration of an active laser beam-scanning triangulation measurement system. The system works with the pattern of two-dimensional beam-scanning illumination and one-dimensional slit-scanning detection with a photo-multiplier tube instead of a CCD camera. By modelling the system-fixed coordinate, we describe the formulation of 3D computation and propose a calibration method in terms of LSE using a planar fitting algorithm. As a sensor-dependent solution, the estimation is refined in the domain of sensing variables. Result of calibration of the real system and a brief analysis of systematic errors are given."},"title":{"value":"Modelling and calibration of the laser beam-scanning triangulation measurement system"},"authors":{"value":["Guoyu Wang","Bing Zheng","Xin Li","Zweitze Houkes","Paul P. L. Regtien"]}},"tmdate":1731564066104,"pdate":1009843200000,"tcdate":1731563967353,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bing_Zheng1"],"forum":"SJezZ3LPyM","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":238325,"cdate":1009843200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731564066104,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SJezZ3LPyM","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.19121v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"jiang|code_comparison_tuning_for_code_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yufan_Jiang:","~Qiaozhi_He1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaomin_Zhuang:","~Zhihua_Wu3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.19121"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2403-19121,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yufan Jiang and Qiaozhi He and Xiaomin Zhuang and Zhihua Wu},\n  title={Code Comparison Tuning for Code Large Language Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2403.19121},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.19121}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present Code Comparison Tuning (CCT), a simple and effective tuning method for code large language models (Code LLMs) to better handle subtle code errors. Specifically, we integrate the concept of comparison into instruction tuning, both at the token and sequence levels, enabling the model to discern even the slightest deviations in code. To compare the original code with an erroneous version containing manually added code errors, we use token-level preference loss for detailed token-level comparisons. Additionally, we combine code segments to create a new instruction tuning sample for sequence-level comparisons, enhancing the model's bug-fixing capability. Experimental results on the HumanEvalFix benchmark show that CCT surpasses instruction tuning in pass@1 scores by up to 4 points across diverse code LLMs, and extensive analysis demonstrates the effectiveness of our method."},"title":{"value":"Code Comparison Tuning for Code Large Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Yufan Jiang","Qiaozhi He","Xiaomin Zhuang","Zhihua Wu"]}},"tmdate":1760522465560,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731659652454,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhihua_Wu3"],"forum":"AdS18BLTn6","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":248032,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1760522465560,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"AdS18BLTn6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 DeLTa Workshop Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8de761163371d6e54eb432111389049848d975f1.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Mixture-of-Experts","LLMs","Quantization","Heavy Tails"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/DeLTa"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|alphaq_calibrationfree_bit_allocation_for_mixtureofexperts_quantization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Wanqi_Yang4","~Yuexiao_Ma1","~Alexander_Conzelmann1","~Xiawu_Zheng1","~Michael_W._Mahoney1","~T._Konstantin_Rusch1","~Shiwei_Liu2"]},"abstract":{"value":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures scale computation via sparse expert activations, yet they remain memory-bound because all expert weights must reside in memory. Mixed-precision quantization can substantially reduce this footprint, but existing quantization methods estimate expert importance and assign bits based on calibration data. For frontier MoE LLMs, however, the original training data (and thus its true training distribution) is proprietary and inaccessible. Thus, any calibration set is at best a surrogate and may yield a biased or incomplete view of expert utilization, leading to suboptimal bit allocation. To address these problems, we propose AlphaQ, a novel calibration-free bit-allocation method for MoE quantization. AlphaQ is inspired by Heavy-Tailed Self-Regularization (HT-SR) theory; and it is based on a simple but effective principle: experts with more heavy-tailed weight spectra tend to be better trained, and therefore merit higher bits, and vice versa. We find that different MoE variants can exhibit substantial cross-expert quality variability, calling for a nuanced bit allocation, that is difficult to achieve with limited/biased calibration data. By leveraging HT-SR theory, AlphaQ incorporates expert-wise spectral heavy-tailedness and formulates mixed-precision quantization as a budget-constrained optimization problem that minimizes total quantization error under a global bit-budget constraint. Empirical results on DeepSeekV2-Lite, Qwen1.5-MoE, and Mixtral-8$\\times$7B show that AlphaQ consistently outperforms calibration-based baselines under matched bit budgets. Notably, on Qwen1.5-MoE, AlphaQ achieves near full-precision accuracy, with an average expert precision of 3.5 bits, while delivering more than 4$\\times$ memory compression."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nyang2026alphaq,\ntitle={AlphaQ: Calibration-Free Bit Allocation for Mixture-of-Experts Quantization},\nauthor={Wanqi Yang and Yuexiao Ma and Alexander Conzelmann and Xiawu Zheng and Michael W. Mahoney and T. Konstantin Rusch and Shiwei Liu},\nbooktitle={ICLR 2026 2nd Workshop on Deep Generative Model in Machine Learning: Theory, Principle and Efficacy},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=rbE8Pxs8vx}\n}"},"title":{"value":"AlphaQ: Calibration-Free Bit Allocation for Mixture-of-Experts Quantization"},"authors":{"value":["Wanqi Yang","Yuexiao Ma","Alexander Conzelmann","Xiawu Zheng","Michael W. Mahoney","T. Konstantin Rusch","Shiwei Liu"]}},"tmdate":1775534459031,"pdate":1772506968016,"tcdate":1770297041300,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/DeLTa","ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/DeLTa/Submission48/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/DeLTa/Submission48/Authors"],"forum":"rbE8Pxs8vx","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":48,"cdate":1770297041300,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/DeLTa/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/DeLTa/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/DeLTa/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/DeLTa/Submission48/-/Revision"],"mdate":1775534459031,"odate":1772676371758,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/DeLTa","id":"rbE8Pxs8vx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Zenodo"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"sounack|tripodcode"},"abstract":{"value":"Code for reproducing the results in - Code Sharing In Prediction Model Research: A Scoping Review"},"title":{"value":"TRIPOD-Code"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Thomas Sounack","username":"https://orcid.org/0009-0002-5548-2695"},{"fullname":"Raffaele Giancotti","username":"https://orcid.org/0009-0000-3906-9214"},{"fullname":"Lasai Barreñada","username":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8020-0210"},{"fullname":"Tom Pollard","username":"~Tom_Pollard1"}]}},"tmdate":1778180609781,"pdate":1774656000000,"externalIds":["doi:10.5281/zenodo.18897513"],"tcdate":1778180609254,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Tom_Pollard1"],"forum":"RNvOgMAiJ8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":68752,"cdate":1773682982259,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1778180609781,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"RNvOgMAiJ8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.02379v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"amer|single_image_calibration_using_knowledge_distillation_approaches"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Khadidja_Ould_Amer:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Oussama_Hadjerci:","~Mohamed_Abbas_Hedjazi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Antoine_Letienne:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2212.02379"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2212-02379,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Khadidja Ould Amer and Oussama Hadjerci and Mohamed Abbas Hedjazi and Antoine Letienne},\n  title={Single image calibration using knowledge distillation approaches},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2212.02379},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2212.02379}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Although recent deep learning-based calibration methods can predict extrinsic and intrinsic camera parameters from a single image, their generalization remains limited by the number and distribution of training data samples. The huge computational and space requirement prevents convolutional neural networks (CNNs) from being implemented in resource-constrained environments. This challenge motivated us to learn a CNN gradually, by training new data while maintaining performance on previously learned data. Our approach builds upon a CNN architecture to automatically estimate camera parameters (focal length, pitch, and roll) using different incremental learning strategies to preserve knowledge when updating the network for new data distributions. Precisely, we adapt four common incremental learning, namely: LwF , iCaRL, LU CIR, and BiC by modifying their loss functions to our regression problem. We evaluate on two datasets containing 299008 indoor and outdoor images. Experiment results were significant and indicated which method was better for the camera calibration estimation."},"title":{"value":"Single image calibration using knowledge distillation approaches"},"authors":{"value":["Khadidja Ould Amer","Oussama Hadjerci","Mohamed Abbas Hedjazi","Antoine Letienne"]}},"tmdate":1746603500788,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1746603493312,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mohamed_Abbas_Hedjazi1"],"forum":"fWq0AMd4a6","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":412118,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1746603500788,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"fWq0AMd4a6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Comput. Vis. Image Underst. 2015"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CVIU/2015"},"paperhash":{"value":"galego|uncertainty_analysis_of_the_dltlines_calibration_algorithm_for_cameras_with_radial_distortion"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ricardo_Galego:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Agustin_Alberto_Ortega_Jimenez:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ricardo_Ferreira_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alexandre_Bernardino:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Juan_Andrade-Cetto:","~José_António_Gaspar1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cviu.2015.05.015"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/cviu/GalegoJFBAG15,\n  author={Ricardo Galego and Agustin Alberto Ortega Jimenez and Ricardo Ferreira and Alexandre Bernardino and Juan Andrade-Cetto and José António Gaspar},\n  title={Uncertainty analysis of the DLT-Lines calibration algorithm for cameras with radial distortion},\n  year={2015},\n  cdate={1420070400000},\n  journal={Comput. Vis. Image Underst.},\n  volume={140},\n  pages={115-126},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cviu.2015.05.015}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•Uncertainty analysis for the Direct Linear Transformation based camera calibration.•Direct Linear Transformation (DLT) specialized to cameras with square pixels.•Flexible DLT by combining more constraints based on a Gauss–Newton formulation.•Calibration uncertainty back-projection to the ground plane.•Using indoor or outdoor scene data instead of conventional calibration patterns."},"title":{"value":"Uncertainty analysis of the DLT-Lines calibration algorithm for cameras with radial distortion"},"authors":{"value":["Ricardo Galego","Agustin Alberto Ortega Jimenez","Ricardo Ferreira","Alexandre Bernardino","Juan Andrade-Cetto","José António Gaspar"]}},"tmdate":1729261110221,"pdate":1420070400000,"tcdate":1729261090907,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~José_António_Gaspar1"],"forum":"CFZ8iGZyvb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":154570,"cdate":1420070400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1729261110221,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"CFZ8iGZyvb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.21109v2"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"mao|anomalyinformed_confidence_calibration_for_visionbased_safety_prediction"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.21109"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2605-21109,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhenjiang Mao and Jiawen Wu and Gabriel Wagner and Zhongzheng Zhang and Ivan Ruchkin},\n  title={Anomaly-Informed Confidence Calibration for Vision-Based Safety Prediction},\n  year={2026},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1777593600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2605.21109},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.21109}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Reliable confidence estimates are important for safely deploying vision-based controllers in autonomous racing, where safety predictions must be derived from camera images, yet modern predictors become dangerously overconfident under test-time distribution shifts. A commonly assumed perception-dynamics gap holds that observation-space anomaly signals, such as autoencoder reconstruction error, should miss dynamics anomalies (e.g., actuation bias, latency) that originate outside the image. We interrogate this gap with an Anomaly-Informed Test-Time Calibration approach that, without retraining any model component, fuses a perceptual score (reconstruction error) and a disentangled dynamics score (epistemic uncertainty and control-stream statistics) from a world model to condition a lightweight temperature-scaling calibrator, aided by test-time augmentation. On a physical DonkeyCar under four real-world anomaly protocols unseen during training (darkness, blur, actuation bias, processing latency), it cuts average expected calibration error from 0.184 to 0.116, a 37% improvement over the best baseline, without modifying the base predictor. Our central finding is that in a tightly coupled closed loop, the perception-dynamics gap is not prominent. Specifically, a dynamics fault degrades the trajectory until the observation is itself out-of-distribution, so the perceptual score alone already captures most dynamics anomalies and accounts for the calibration gains. As a result, the disentangled dynamics score adds little further calibration but remains a direct, interpretable signal that aids out-of-distribution detection of dynamics and control faults."},"title":{"value":"Anomaly-Informed Confidence Calibration for Vision-Based Safety Prediction"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhenjiang Mao","username":"~Zhenjiang_Mao1"},{"fullname":"Jiawen Wu","username":""},{"fullname":"Gabriel Wagner","username":""},{"fullname":"Zhongzheng Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Ivan Ruchkin","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1792019426037,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2605-21109"],"tcdate":1792019414166,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Zhenjiang_Mao1"],"forum":"5e3ev8UvPn","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":185910,"cdate":1777593600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1792019426037,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"5e3ev8UvPn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"COPA 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/56133bd7d7b3be4d2ae3043b33f69e4e92b65a1a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"azizi|conformal_calibration_for_multimodal_regression_with_missing_modalities"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ilia_Azizi1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Prediction intervals for multi-modal regression with tabular variables, text, images, or other input sources are difficult to calibrate when those sources disagree or one is missing. A single global quantile averages these regimes together instead of calibrating to the modality pattern observed at test time. We address this through a modality-aware conformal calibration layer. The layer trains or reuses one predictor per modality, computes a disagreement score from their predictions, and uses that score in split conformal calibration under a strict split protocol. We use the score in two complementary ways. First, a continuous disagreement-scaled method reallocates interval width across examples while preserving the usual marginal split-conformal guarantee. Second, a Mondrian (stratified) method calibrates within groups defined by disagreement or modality availability fixed before calibration, giving group guarantees under joint exchangeability of the calibration and test examples. Across four multi-modal datasets, the disagreement-scaled layer matches or improves the marginal conformal baseline in 59 of 60 paired runs for interval continuous ranked probability score (CRPS) and in 52 of 60 for interval width, while keeping empirical coverage near the 95\\% target. In stress tests with missing modalities, mask-matched recalibration recovers up to 19.5 percentage points of coverage in the hardest fixed-mask regime. The result is a simple, model-agnostic reliability layer for multi-modal regression systems. A project page is available at https://unco3892.github.io/modality-aware-conformal."},"title":{"value":"Conformal Calibration for Multi-Modal Regression with Missing Modalities"},"authors":{"value":["Ilia Azizi"]}},"tmdate":1789984131731,"pdate":1788300000000,"tcdate":1789983703750,"writers":["~Ilia_Azizi1"],"signatures":["~Ilia_Azizi1"],"forum":"oCwN502Mre","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":54446,"cdate":1789983703750,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1789984131731,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"oCwN502Mre","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MIDL 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v172/kock22a/kock22a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MIDL/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"kock|confidence_histograms_for_model_reliability_analysis_and_temperature_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Farina_Kock:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Felix_Thielke:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Grzegorz_Chlebus:","~Hans_Meine1"]},"html":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v172/kock22a.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/midl/KockTCM22,\n  author={Farina Kock and Felix Thielke and Grzegorz Chlebus and Hans Meine},\n  title={Confidence Histograms for Model Reliability Analysis and Temperature Calibration},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={741-759},\n  url={https://proceedings.mlr.press/v172/kock22a.html},\n  booktitle={MIDL},\n  crossref={conf/midl/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Proper estimation of uncertainty may help the adoption of deep learning-based solutions in clinical practice, when measurements can take error bounds into account and out-of-distribution situations can be reliably detected. Therefore, a variety of approaches have been proposed already, with varying requirements and computational effort. Uncertainty estimation is complicated by the fact that typical neural networks are overly confident; this effect is particularly prominent with the Dice loss, which is commonly used for image segmentation. Therefore, various methods for model calibration have been proposed to reduce the discrepancy between classifier confidence and the observed accuracy. In this work, we focus on the simple calibration method of introducing a temperature parameter for the softmax operation. This approach is not only appealing because of its mathematical simplicity, it also appears to be well-suited for countering the main distortion of the classifier output confidence levels. Finally, it comes at literally zero extra cost, because the necessary multiplications can be integrated into the previous layer’s weights after calibration, and a scalar temperature does not affect the classification at all. Our contributions are as follows: We thoroughly evaluate the confidence behavior of several models with different architectures, different numbers of output classes, different loss functions, and different segmentation tasks. In order to do so, we propose an efficient intermediate representation and some adaptations of reliability diagrams to semantic segmentation. We investigate different calibration measures and their optimal temperatures for these diverse models."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Histograms for Model Reliability Analysis and Temperature Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Farina Kock","Felix Thielke","Grzegorz Chlebus","Hans Meine"]}},"tmdate":1765638021739,"pdate":1640995200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/midl/KockTCM22"],"tcdate":1765638003942,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hans_Meine1"],"forum":"5y0BYNgTWt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":709024,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1765638021739,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5y0BYNgTWt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MASS 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7814392/7814995/07815029.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MASS/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"kleunen|calibrationfree_signalstrength_localization_using_productmoment_correlation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wouter_A._P._van_Kleunen:","~Duc_Viet_Le1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Paul_J._M._Havinga:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/MASS.2016.035"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/mass/KleunenLH16,\n  author={Wouter A. P. van Kleunen and Duc Viet Le and Paul J. M. Havinga},\n  title={Calibration-Free Signal-Strength Localization Using Product-Moment Correlation},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  pages={210-218},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/MASS.2016.035},\n  booktitle={MASS},\n  crossref={conf/mass/2016}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Localization, a process of determining the position of a blind node, can be used in various applications. Signal-strength localization provides a low-cost and low-power solution to positioning. Signal-strength positioning approaches using fingerprinting or calibrated approaches require a time-consuming calibration phase. Existing self-calibrating approaches, which do not require a priori calibration, use a least-squares fitting model to determine both the position of the blind node as well as the optimal environmental parameters. In this paper, we propose an approach using the Product-Moment correlation between the measured signal strength and the estimated signal strengths. Such approach does not require estimation of the environmental parameters or prior calibration and outperforms existing self-calibrating least-squares approaches. We compare our approach to existing least-squares calibration-free positioning approaches. Moreover, we look at the Cramer-Rao Bound (CRB) of signal-strength localization and using simulations we show that the product-moment correlation outperforms least-squares approaches and follows the CRB closely. Simulation and evaluation using a real-world experiment dataset show the product-moment approach significantly outperforms least-squares approaches. The product-moment approach follows the CRB much more closely and achieves up to twice more accurate positions in certain scenarios. When the error ratio increases and the number of reference positions stays fixed at 6, the product-moment approach scores 20% more accurate positions. In the cooperative localization scenario, the product-moment correlation performs 40% better."},"title":{"value":"Calibration-Free Signal-Strength Localization Using Product-Moment Correlation"},"authors":{"value":["Wouter A. P. van Kleunen","Duc Viet Le","Paul J. M. Havinga"]}},"tmdate":1724638790966,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1724638772667,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Duc_Viet_Le1"],"forum":"Vnd30VJoJp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":73535,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1724638790966,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Vnd30VJoJp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISCIS 2006"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/11902140_39.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISCIS/2006"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|applying_natural_neighbor_coordinates_for_electromagnetic_tracker_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weidong_Chen_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongming_Lu:","~Lei_Zhao3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/11902140_39"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iscis/LiCLZ06,\n  author={Yi Li and Weidong Chen and Dongming Lu and Lei Zhao},\n  title={Applying Natural Neighbor Coordinates for Electromagnetic Tracker Calibration},\n  year={2006},\n  cdate={1136073600000},\n  pages={353-362},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/11902140_39},\n  booktitle={ISCIS},\n  crossref={conf/iscis/2006}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we use the natural neighbor coordinates and its properties as a tool for electromagnetic tracker calibration. We have established a framework for computing the three-dimensional natural neighbor coordinates and used it for the calibration process for which we wish to eliminate the distortion error presented in the electromagnetic field. Our work comprises of two main parts. The first is about the computational model of the natural neighbor coordinates while the second is a discussion on the result we get after calibrating the electromagnetic tracker distortion. Our result has shown that the natural neighbor coordinates can be a promising method for approximating the distortion in the electromagnetic field and for calibrating the distorted field data."},"title":{"value":"Applying Natural Neighbor Coordinates for Electromagnetic Tracker Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Yi Li","Weidong Chen","Dongming Lu","Lei Zhao"]}},"tmdate":1727829275106,"pdate":1136073600000,"tcdate":1727829268012,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lei_Zhao3"],"forum":"MtJVQl90fD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":134644,"cdate":1136073600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727829275106,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"MtJVQl90fD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2002"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/7916/21828/01014226.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2002"},"paperhash":{"value":"desouza|a_worldindependent_approach_for_the_calibration_of_mobile_robotics_active_stereo_heads"},"authorids":{"value":["~Guilherme_N._DeSouza1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrew_H._Jones:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Avinash_C._Kak:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2002.1014226"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/DeSouzaJK02,\n  author={Guilherme N. DeSouza and Andrew H. Jones and Avinash C. Kak},\n  title={A World-Independent Approach for the Calibration of Mobile Robotics Active Stereo Heads},\n  year={2002},\n  cdate={1009843200000},\n  pages={3336-3341},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2002.1014226},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2002}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper describes an approach for calibrating active stereo heads for which no prior knowledge of the world coordinate system is required. During an automated procedure derived from this approach, the mobile robot can be loosely positioned in front of a calibration pattern. Through our procedure, both the camera calibration and the neck-eye calibration are obtained. With this set of calibration matrices, the XYZ coordinates of a landmark can be determined with respect to the robot coordinate frame. The so calculated XYZ coordinates are accurate regardless of the orientation of the stereo head, even for orientations that were not used for calibration. This paper validates our approach by presenting results as accurate as a few millimeters in the calculation of the XYZ coordinates of objects at 1.5m from the stereo head."},"title":{"value":"A World-Independent Approach for the Calibration of Mobile Robotics Active Stereo Heads"},"authors":{"value":["Guilherme N. DeSouza","Andrew H. Jones","Avinash C. Kak"]}},"tmdate":1758292512158,"pdate":1009843200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icra/DeSouzaJK02"],"tcdate":1758292499207,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Guilherme_N._DeSouza1"],"forum":"L9irPEvO6Z","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":625831,"cdate":1009843200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1758292512158,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"L9irPEvO6Z","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Statistical inverse problems; Calibration; Semiparametric efficiency;"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/7d63d7150f8f3aff5057cac8cc5ad606cbbd98c7.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"learning theory"},"abstract":{"value":"Many learning problems combine observations that contain the target with auxiliary measurements that do not observe the target directly, but instead reveal a shared nuisance variable that also affects those observations. When, and by how much, can such auxiliary measurements improve target recovery? We study this question in a Gaussian inverse problem with an acquisition channel, in which target and nuisance effects are mixed, and a calibration channel, which observes only the nuisance. We show that the value of calibration is determined not by the quality of either channel alone, but by how the nuisance effects in the two channels are paired. This pairing gives an exact formula for the information about the target that remains after the nuisance is eliminated, even when calibration measures the nuisance only in a weaker norm. The formula separates a structural loss that no calibration budget can remove from the loss due to a finite calibration budget. For unrestricted nuisance and compact convex target classes, we prove that the minimax risk equals that of the Gaussian model left after the nuisance is eliminated. For restricted nuisance classes, we give a condition under which this equality remains valid. Under a Hilbert-scale condition, we derive matching minimax rates and the amount of calibration data needed to reach the rate with known nuisance. If calibration follows acquisition in heat-equation models, this amount grows exponentially instead of polynomially, and restricting the nuisance can change the minimax rate itself. The same pairing also yields target-oriented calibration allocation, which can differ from nuisance-optimal allocation. Experiments support these predictions and show that learning the nuisance effect from passively observed data suffers errors-in-variables attenuation. On fastMRI scans with a fixed number of sampled lines, an intermediate calibration width minimizes the error at moderate acceleration."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026when,\ntitle={When Does Auxiliary Calibration Improve Target Recovery? Nuisance Geometry, Sample Complexity, and Timing},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=GpB5sFHnTJ},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"When Does Auxiliary Calibration Improve Target Recovery? Nuisance Geometry, Sample Complexity, and Timing"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/092b6a5293b039edb162b18331afdcc683415ffc.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794266219296,"tcdate":1788914322843,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission11794/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission11794/Authors"],"forum":"GpB5sFHnTJ","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":11794,"cdate":1788914322843,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission11794/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission11794/-/Rebuttal_Revision"],"mdate":1794266219296,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"GpB5sFHnTJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Crossref"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-032-37490-5_14.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"lee|rethinking_detection_calibration_a_coordinate_and_direction_perspective"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-37490-5_14"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep learning based object detectors require trustworthiness beyond competitive detection performance, but deep neural networks are prone to overconfident predictions, assigning high confidence scores to predictions that are likely to be inaccurate. To improve the alignment between confidence scores and prediction accuracy, existing methods calibrate confidence scores based on box-level localization, such as precision or intersection over union with the ground truth bounding box. However, box-level localization reflects only a measure of agreement between the predicted box and the ground truth, resulting in calibrated confidence scores for box-level accuracy failing to capture the localization accuracy of coordinates of box. To tackle this issue, we propose a novel post-hoc calibration framework, rethinking detection calibration (ReDC), which provides reliable coordinate-level confidence scores, including directional information. The proposed framework defines coordinate-wise alignment and deviation direction between predictions and ground truth. Based on the alignment measure, confidence re-encoding produces reliable coordinate-level confidence scores, while directional displacement estimation predicts coordinate-wise deviation directions. Extensive experiments under in-domain and out-domain scenarios demonstrate that the proposed approach expresses the coordinate-wise localization of detected objects more precisely than existing methods. Furthermore, our method covers the representational scope of prior calibration approaches by aggregating coordinate-level confidence scores into box-level localization."},"title":{"value":"Rethinking Detection Calibration: A Coordinate and Direction Perspective"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Juyong Lee","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Juyong%20Lee"},{"fullname":"Seungjin Jung","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Seungjin%20Jung"},{"fullname":"Jungmin Lee","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Jungmin%20Lee"},{"fullname":"Sunju Lee","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Sunju%20Lee"},{"fullname":"Jongwon Choi","username":"~Jongwon_Choi1"}]}},"tmdate":1792201896697,"pdate":1767225600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1007/978-3-032-37490-5_14"],"tcdate":1792201891207,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Jongwon_Choi1"],"forum":"k8oN0YP83a","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":130101,"cdate":1789461472649,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1792201896697,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"k8oN0YP83a","version":2},{"content":{"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/64aced08674df9f1079bc81fb89f003d9836e1cf.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"tang|bimanual_joint_camera_calibration_and_scene_representation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Haozhan_Tang1","~Tianyi_Zhang14","~Matthew_Johnson-Roberson3","~Weiming_Zhi1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Robot manipulation, especially bimanual manipulation, often requires setting up multiple cameras on multiple robot manipulators. Before robot manipulators can generate motion or even build representations of their environments, the cameras rigidly mounted to the robot need to be calibrated. Camera calibration is a cumbersome process involving collecting a set of images, with each capturing a pre-determined marker. In this work, we introduce the Bi-Manual Joint Calibration and Representation Framework (Bi-JCR). Bi-JCR enables multiple robot manipulators, each with cameras mounted, to circumvent taking images of calibration markers. By leveraging 3D foundation models for dense, marker-free multi-view correspondence, Bi-JCR jointly estimates: (i) the extrinsic transformation from each camera to its end-effector, (ii) the inter-arm relative poses between manipulators, and (iii) a unified, scale-consistent 3D representation of the shared workspace, all from the same captured RGB image sets. The representation, jointly constructed from images captured by cameras on both manipulators, lives in a common coordinate frame and supports collision checking and semantic segmentation to facilitate downstream bimanual coordination tasks. We empirically evaluate the robustness of Bi-JCR on a variety of tabletop environments, and demonstrate its applicability on a variety of downstream tasks."},"title":{"value":"Bi-Manual Joint Camera Calibration and Scene Representation"},"authors":{"value":["Haozhan Tang","Tianyi Zhang","Matthew Johnson-Roberson","Weiming Zhi"]}},"tmdate":1778016250968,"pdate":1769867640000,"tcdate":1778016250968,"writers":["~Haozhan_Tang1","~Tianyi_Zhang14","~Matthew_Johnson-Roberson3","~Weiming_Zhi1"],"signatures":["~Haozhan_Tang1"],"forum":"6PgNEnBR1p","license":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license","number":48476,"cdate":1778016250968,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1778016250968,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"6PgNEnBR1p","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.24819v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"tang|bimanual_joint_camera_calibration_and_scene_representation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haozhan_Tang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianyi_Zhang_0014:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Matthew_Johnson-Roberson:","~Weiming_Zhi1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.24819"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-24819,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Haozhan Tang and Tianyi Zhang and Matthew Johnson-Roberson and Weiming Zhi},\n  title={Bi-Manual Joint Camera Calibration and Scene Representation},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2505.24819},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.24819}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Robot manipulation, especially bimanual manipulation, often requires setting up multiple cameras on multiple robot manipulators. Before robot manipulators can generate motion or even build representations of their environments, the cameras rigidly mounted to the robot need to be calibrated. Camera calibration is a cumbersome process involving collecting a set of images, with each capturing a pre-determined marker. In this work, we introduce the Bi-Manual Joint Calibration and Representation Framework (Bi-JCR). Bi-JCR enables multiple robot manipulators, each with cameras mounted, to circumvent taking images of calibration markers. By leveraging 3D foundation models for dense, marker-free multi-view correspondence, Bi-JCR jointly estimates: (i) the extrinsic transformation from each camera to its end-effector, (ii) the inter-arm relative poses between manipulators, and (iii) a unified, scale-consistent 3D representation of the shared workspace, all from the same captured RGB image sets. The representation, jointly constructed from images captured by cameras on both manipulators, lives in a common coordinate frame and supports collision checking and semantic segmentation to facilitate downstream bimanual coordination tasks. We empirically evaluate the robustness of Bi-JCR on a variety of tabletop environments, and demonstrate its applicability on a variety of downstream tasks."},"title":{"value":"Bi-Manual Joint Camera Calibration and Scene Representation"},"authors":{"value":["Haozhan Tang","Tianyi Zhang","Matthew Johnson-Roberson","Weiming Zhi"]}},"tmdate":1763254433625,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2505-24819"],"tcdate":1763254425894,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Weiming_Zhi1"],"forum":"803QvEhsNj","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":704122,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1763254433625,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"803QvEhsNj","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.05101v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"lara|towards_unraveling_calibration_biases_in_medical_image_analysis"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:María_Agustina_Ricci_Lara:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Candelaria_Mosquera:","~Enzo_Ferrante1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rodrigo_Echeveste:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2305.05101"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2305-05101,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={María Agustina Ricci Lara and Candelaria Mosquera and Enzo Ferrante and Rodrigo Echeveste},\n  title={Towards unraveling calibration biases in medical image analysis},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2305.05101},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2305.05101}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In recent years the development of artificial intelligence (AI) systems for automated medical image analysis has gained enormous momentum. At the same time, a large body of work has shown that AI systems can systematically and unfairly discriminate against certain populations in various application scenarios. These two facts have motivated the emergence of algorithmic fairness studies in this field. Most research on healthcare algorithmic fairness to date has focused on the assessment of biases in terms of classical discrimination metrics such as AUC and accuracy. Potential biases in terms of model calibration, however, have only recently begun to be evaluated. This is especially important when working with clinical decision support systems, as predictive uncertainty is key for health professionals to optimally evaluate and combine multiple sources of information. In this work we study discrimination and calibration biases in models trained for automatic detection of malignant dermatological conditions from skin lesions images. Importantly, we show how several typically employed calibration metrics are systematically biased with respect to sample sizes, and how this can lead to erroneous fairness analysis if not taken into consideration. This is of particular relevance to fairness studies, where data imbalance results in drastic sample size differences between demographic sub-groups, which, if not taken into account, can act as confounders."},"title":{"value":"Towards unraveling calibration biases in medical image analysis"},"authors":{"value":["María Agustina Ricci Lara","Candelaria Mosquera","Enzo Ferrante","Rodrigo Echeveste"]}},"tmdate":1731510126091,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731510116281,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Enzo_Ferrante1"],"forum":"XG4aIl8FuZ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":230573,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731510126091,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"XG4aIl8FuZ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Graph Foundation Models","Retrieval-Augmented Generation","Utility-Aligned Retrieval","Few-Shot Learning"]},"primary_area":{"value":"learning on graphs and other geometries & topologies"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper studies the problem of few-shot adaptation for graph foundation models (GFMs), which aims to transfer pre-trained GFMs to unseen domains with limited support sets. Previous approaches usually introduce learnable task-aware prompts to adapt GFMs to new domains. Despite the progress, these approaches primarily exploit parametric knowledge in pre-trained models while neglecting rich contextual knowledge embedded in source graphs. \nTowards this end, we propose a novel approach named Complementary Context Retrieval with Utility Calibration (COLA) for graph foundation model adaptation. The core of our COLA is to retrieve relevant graph candidates from complementary knowledge sources and then refine them into fine-grained contexts guided by their estimated utility for graph representation adaptation. In particular, we first build a graph semantics database and a graph structure database from source domains to capture complementary knowledge. Given a target query, we retrieve relevant graph candidates from these two databases using graph representations and graph kernels, respectively. More importantly, we leverage reinforcement learning with pseudo-target calibration to train a lightweight policy model, which estimates the utility of retrieved graphs to guide fine-grained context selection from abundant candidates. The refined complementary contexts are then incorporated into target graph representations under the guidance of null-aware routing for domain adaptation. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets validate the effectiveness of the proposed COLA in comparison with extensive baselines. Our code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/COLA-D0DC."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026cola,\ntitle={{COLA}: Complementary Context Retrieval with Utility Calibration for Graph Foundation Model Adaptation},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=VhLccdYrzX},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"COLA: Complementary Context Retrieval with Utility Calibration for Graph Foundation Model Adaptation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3b45119d3756be0caa386ba49c28b8454b61f20c.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791230445758,"tcdate":1789565303177,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission25447/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission25447/Authors"],"forum":"VhLccdYrzX","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":25447,"cdate":1789565303177,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission25447/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791230445758,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"VhLccdYrzX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.11335v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|the_final_layer_holds_the_key_a_unified_and_efficient_gnn_calibration_framework"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jincheng_Huang_0005:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Xu_0044:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoshuang_Shi:","~Ping_Hu2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Feng_0006:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaofeng_Zhu_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.11335"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-11335,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jincheng Huang and Jie Xu and Xiaoshuang Shi and Ping Hu and Lei Feng and Xiaofeng Zhu},\n  title={The Final Layer Holds the Key: A Unified and Efficient GNN Calibration Framework},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2505.11335},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.11335}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) have demonstrated remarkable effectiveness on graph-based tasks. However, their predictive confidence is often miscalibrated, typically exhibiting under-confidence, which harms the reliability of their decisions. Existing calibration methods for GNNs normally introduce additional calibration components, which fail to capture the intrinsic relationship between the model and the prediction confidence, resulting in limited theoretical guarantees and increased computational overhead. To address this issue, we propose a simple yet efficient graph calibration method. We establish a unified theoretical framework revealing that model confidence is jointly governed by class-centroid-level and node-level calibration at the final layer. Based on this insight, we theoretically show that reducing the weight decay of the final-layer parameters alleviates GNN under-confidence by acting on the class-centroid level, while node-level calibration acts as a finer-grained complement to class-centroid level calibration, which encourages each test node to be closer to its predicted class centroid at the final-layer representations. Extensive experiments validate the superiority of our method."},"title":{"value":"The Final Layer Holds the Key: A Unified and Efficient GNN Calibration Framework"},"authors":{"value":["Jincheng Huang","Jie Xu","Xiaoshuang Shi","Ping Hu","Lei Feng","Xiaofeng Zhu"]}},"tmdate":1762610667416,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2505-11335"],"tcdate":1762610658219,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ping_Hu3"],"forum":"SPbc4oxEAl","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":679242,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762610667416,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SPbc4oxEAl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.10981v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wirth|automatic_spatial_calibration_of_nearfield_mimo_radar_with_respect_to_optical_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Vanessa_Wirth_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Johanna_Bräunig:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Danti_Khouri:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Florian_Gutsche:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Martin_Vossiek:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tim_Weyrich:","~Marc_Stamminger1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.10981"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2403-10981,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Vanessa Wirth and Johanna Bräunig and Danti Khouri and Florian Gutsche and Martin Vossiek and Tim Weyrich and Marc Stamminger},\n  title={Automatic Spatial Calibration of Near-Field MIMO Radar With Respect to Optical Sensors},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2403.10981},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.10981}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite an emerging interest in MIMO radar, the utilization of its complementary strengths in combination with optical depth sensors has so far been limited to far-field applications, due to the challenges that arise from mutual sensor calibration in the near field. In fact, most related approaches in the autonomous industry propose target-based calibration methods using corner reflectors that have proven to be unsuitable for the near field. In contrast, we propose a novel, joint calibration approach for optical RGB-D sensors and MIMO radars that is designed to operate in the radar's near-field range, within decimeters from the sensors. Our pipeline consists of a bespoke calibration target, allowing for automatic target detection and localization, followed by the spatial calibration of the two sensor coordinate systems through target registration. We validate our approach using two different depth sensing technologies from the optical domain. The experiments show the efficiency and accuracy of our calibration for various target displacements, as well as its robustness of our localization in terms of signal ambiguities."},"title":{"value":"Automatic Spatial Calibration of Near-Field MIMO Radar With Respect to Optical Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Vanessa Wirth","Johanna Bräunig","Danti Khouri","Florian Gutsche","Martin Vossiek","Tim Weyrich","Marc Stamminger"]}},"tmdate":1723818840506,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1723818829110,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Marc_Stamminger1"],"forum":"an7cqbEQFo","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":69072,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1723818840506,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"an7cqbEQFo","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"Two retained records show that low calibration error is insufficient for confidence-based routing when errors are scarce, coverage varies, or correctness cannot be verified."},"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2026 TAE Poster"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/1fd2e45304ba803387e18da450e88f935bc75a8f.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["verbalized confidence","calibration","selective risk","agent routing","missing data","artifact auditing"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE"},"paperhash":{"value":"hernandez|when_calibration_records_cannot_support_routing_a_denominator_and_artifact_audit"},"abstract":{"value":"Low calibration error does not establish that verbalized confidence can support routing. We audit the evidence needed for that decision in two retained records. The first is nearly perfectly accurate and has low aggregate calibration error, but it contains only one observed error and its joint answer-confidence coverage varies sharply by endpoint. It can describe average confidence on this finite record, but cannot establish discrimination or selective risk. The second record omits item text, answers, gold labels, raw outputs, and verification evidence. Reproducible arithmetic on its supplied correctness field therefore does not become a verified behavioral estimate. Together, the cases change the operational decision from choosing a confidence threshold to redesigning the items, interface, and evidence record before any routing evaluation. We provide a compact machine-readable audit covering denominators, artifact status, calibration, discrimination, selective risk, uncertainty, and unidentified outcomes. The contribution is a bounded workflow for deciding what a calibration record can support, not a validated benchmark or a general claim about verbalized confidence."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhernandez2026when,\ntitle={When Calibration Records Cannot Support Routing: A Denominator and Artifact Audit},\nauthor={Carlos Toxtli Hernandez and Manuel Delaflor},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2026 Trust-AI-Eval Workshop},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=uPtp2butrm}\n}"},"title":{"value":"When Calibration Records Cannot Support Routing: A Denominator and Artifact Audit"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Carlos_Toxtli_Hernandez2","fullname":"Carlos Toxtli Hernandez","institutions":[{"name":"Clemson University","domain":"clemson.edu","country":"US"}]},{"username":"~Manuel_Delaflor1","fullname":"Manuel Delaflor","institutions":[{"name":"Universidad Nacional Autónoma de México","domain":"unam.mx","country":"MX"}]}]}},"tmdate":1793028251011,"pdate":1790555917247,"tcdate":1788058042530,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/Submission317/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/Submission317/Authors"],"forum":"uPtp2butrm","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":317,"cdate":1788058042530,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/-/Accepted_Submission_Release","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/Submission317/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/-/Edit"],"mdate":1793028251011,"odate":1790555917247,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE","id":"uPtp2butrm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CACRE 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9834148/9834101/09834162.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CACRE/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|a_fast_and_straightforward_handeye_calibration_method_using_stereo_camera"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongcen_Xu:","~Cong_Wang15","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yixuan_Tong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuyang_Dong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liangqing_Huo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yiping_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qifeng_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xisheng_Feng:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CACRE54574.2022.9834162"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cacre/XuWTDHLZF22,\n  author={Dongcen Xu and Cong Wang and Yixuan Tong and Xuyang Dong and Liangqing Huo and Yiping Li and Qifeng Zhang and Xisheng Feng},\n  title={A fast and straightforward hand-eye calibration method using stereo camera},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={374-381},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CACRE54574.2022.9834162},\n  booktitle={CACRE},\n  crossref={conf/cacre/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Robot hand-eye calibration is crucial for a robot to transform the target 3D position from the camera coordinate system into the robot coordinate system. The conventional method is to use a single-lens camera and calibration board and then solve the AX=XB problem. Recently with the popularization of stereo camera in robot industry, we design a fast and straightforward way to accomplish hand-eye calibration using the point cloud information from the stereo camera and orthogonal projection. We also investigate the number of pairs of data required for calculation in this procedure. We conclude that normally a minimal of 6 points are required for obtaining satisfying result and more pairs of data can provide better result. The procedure is demonstrated and tested on an electric robot arm, and further validated on a full ocean depth hydraulic robot arm. The procedure takes little time to set up and the formulas are concise. It can be applied easily to most robot arms for hand-eye calibration."},"title":{"value":"A fast and straightforward hand-eye calibration method using stereo camera"},"authors":{"value":["Dongcen Xu","Cong Wang","Yixuan Tong","Xuyang Dong","Liangqing Huo","Yiping Li","Qifeng Zhang","Xisheng Feng"]}},"tmdate":1727703656893,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1727701425589,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Cong_Wang15"],"forum":"st594pZsyd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":113271,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727703656893,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"st594pZsyd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/21/17/5799/pdf?version=1630390817"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"xue|twostage_calibration_scheme_for_magnetic_measurement_system_on_guided_munition"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yuyang_Xue1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoming_Zhang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s21175799"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/XueZ21,\n  author={Yuyang Xue and Xiaoming Zhang},\n  title={Two-Stage Calibration Scheme for Magnetic Measurement System on Guided Munition},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={21},\n  number={17},\n  pages={5799},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s21175799}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In order to calibrate the magnetic measurement system used in guided munition on site, a two-stage calibration (TSC) scheme without reference is proposed in this paper. Analyzing the interfering magnetic field in the projectile and misalignment angles between the projectile coordinate system and measurement coordinate system establishes a proper mathematical equivalent model and derives a calibration method. The first stage is ellipsoid fitting to obtain the equivalent zero-offset, equivalent sensitivity and equivalent non-orthogonal angles of the sensor; the second stage is to calibrate the misalignment angles between the projectile coordinate system and the measurement coordinate system with the three-position calibration (TPC) method. Complete calibration is convenient to operate and does not need an additional reference, which has wide applicability. The simulation results show that the deviation in the measured value after compensation is within 100 nT. The experiment proves that the error of compensated magnetic value is about 150 nT, which meets the accuracy of requirements in guided munitions."},"title":{"value":"Two-Stage Calibration Scheme for Magnetic Measurement System on Guided Munition"},"authors":{"value":["Yuyang Xue","Xiaoming Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1715614851713,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1715614836713,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yuyang_Xue1"],"forum":"PLDxVxQr1m","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":9761,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1715614851713,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"PLDxVxQr1m","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Ghent University Academic Bibliography"},"pdf":{"value":"https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/8060629/file/8060649.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"verhoeven|reducing_bci_calibration_time_with_transfer_learning_a_shrinkage_approach"},"html":{"value":"https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/8060629"},"abstract":{"value":"Introduction: A brain-computer interface system (BCI) allows subjects to make use of neural control signals to drive a computer application. Therefor a BCI is generally equipped with a decoder to differentiate between types of responses recorded in the brain. For example, an application giving feedback to the user can benefit from recognizing the presence or absence of a so-called error potential (Errp), elicited in the brain of the user when this feedback is perceived as being ‘wrong’, a mistake of the system. Due to the high inter- and intra- subject variability in these response signals, calibration data needs to be recorded to train the decoder. This calibration session is exhausting and demotivating for the subject. Transfer Learning is a general name for techniques in which data from previous subjects is used as additional information to train a decoder for a new subject, thereby reducing the amount of subject specific data that needs to be recorded during calibration. In this work we apply transfer learning to an Errp detection task by applying single-target shrinkage to Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA), a method originally proposed by Höhne et. al. to improve accuracy by compensating for inter-stimuli differences in an ERP-speller [1].\r\n\r\nMaterial, Methods and Results: For our study we used the error potential dataset recorded by Perrin et al. in [2]. For 26 subjects each, 340 Errp/nonErrp responses were recorded with a #Errp to #nonErrp ratio of 0.41 to 0.94. 272 responses were available for training the decoder and the remaining 68 responses were left out for testing. For every subject separately we built three different decoders. First, a subject specific LDA decoder was built solely making use of the subject’s own train data. Second, we added the train data of the other 25 subjects to train a global LDA decoder, naively ignoring the difference between subjects. Finally, the single-target-shrinkage method (STS) [1] is used to regularize the parameters of the subject specific decoder towards those of the global decoder. Making use of cross validation this method assigns an optimal weight to the subject specific data and data from previous subjects to be used for training. Figure 1 shows the performance of the three decoders on the test data in terms of AUC as a function of the amount of subject specific calibration data used.\r\n\r\nDiscussion: The subject specific decoder in Figure 1 shows how sensitive the decoding performance is to the amount of calibration data provided. Using data from previously recorded subjects the amount of calibration data, and as such the calibration time, can be reduced as shown by the global decoder. A certain amount of quality is however sacrificed. Making an optimal compromise between the subject specific and global decoder, the single-target-shrinkage decoder allows the calibration time to be reduced by 20% without any change in decoder quality (confirmed by a paired sample t-test giving p=0.72).\r\n\r\nSignificance: This work serves as a first proof of concept in the use of shrinkage LDA as a transfer learning method. More specific, the error potential decoder built with reduced calibration time boosts the opportunity for error correcting methods in BCI."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{8060629,\n  abstract     = {{Introduction: A brain-computer interface system (BCI) allows subjects to make use of neural control signals to drive a computer application. Therefor a BCI is generally equipped with a decoder to differentiate between types of responses recorded in the brain. For example, an application giving feedback to the user can benefit from recognizing the presence or absence of a so-called error potential (Errp), elicited in the brain of the user when this feedback is perceived as being ‘wrong’, a mistake of the system. Due to the high inter- and intra- subject variability in these response signals, calibration data needs to be recorded to train the decoder. This calibration session is exhausting and demotivating for the subject. Transfer Learning is a general name for techniques in which data from previous subjects is used as additional information to train a decoder for a new subject, thereby reducing the amount of subject specific data that needs to be recorded during calibration. In this work we apply transfer learning to an Errp detection task by applying single-target shrinkage to Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA), a method originally proposed by Höhne et. al. to improve accuracy by compensating for inter-stimuli differences in an ERP-speller [1].\r\n\r\nMaterial, Methods and Results: For our study we used the error potential dataset recorded by Perrin et al. in [2]. For 26 subjects each, 340 Errp/nonErrp responses were recorded with a #Errp to #nonErrp ratio of 0.41 to 0.94. 272 responses were available for training the decoder and the remaining 68 responses were left out for testing. For every subject separately we built three different decoders. First, a subject specific LDA decoder was built solely making use of the subject’s own train data. Second, we added the train data of the other 25 subjects to train a global LDA decoder, naively ignoring the difference between subjects. Finally, the single-target-shrinkage method (STS) [1] is used to regularize the parameters of the subject specific decoder towards those of the global decoder. Making use of cross validation this method assigns an optimal weight to the subject specific data and data from previous subjects to be used for training. Figure 1 shows the performance of the three decoders on the test data in terms of AUC as a function of the amount of subject specific calibration data used.\r\n\r\nDiscussion: The subject specific decoder in Figure 1 shows how sensitive the decoding performance is to the amount of calibration data provided. Using data from previously recorded subjects the amount of calibration data, and as such the calibration time, can be reduced as shown by the global decoder. A certain amount of quality is however sacrificed. Making an optimal compromise between the subject specific and global decoder, the single-target-shrinkage decoder allows the calibration time to be reduced by 20% without any change in decoder quality (confirmed by a paired sample t-test giving p=0.72).\r\n\r\nSignificance: This work serves as a first proof of concept in the use of shrinkage LDA as a transfer learning method. More specific, the error potential decoder built with reduced calibration time boosts the opportunity for error correcting methods in BCI.}},\n  articleno    = {{133}},\n  author       = {{Verhoeven, Thibault and Kindermans, Pieter-Jan and Vandenberghe, Stefaan and Dambre, Joni}},\n  booktitle    = {{Proceedings of the Sixth International Brain-Computer Interface Meeting: BCI Past, Present, and Future}},\n  editor       = {{Müller-Putz, Gernot R. and Huggins, Jane E. and Steyrl, David}},\n  isbn         = {{978-3-85125-467-9}},\n  keywords     = {{Machine Learning,Brain-Computer Interface,EEG,Error Potential}},\n  language     = {{eng}},\n  location     = {{Asilomar Conference Center in Pacific Grove, California, USA}},\n  publisher    = {{Technischen Universität Graz}},\n  title        = {{Reducing BCI calibration time with transfer learning: a shrinkage approach}},\n  url          = {{http://doi.org/10.3217/978-3-85125-467-9-133}},\n  year         = {{2016}},\n}\n\n"},"title":{"value":"Reducing BCI calibration time with transfer learning: a shrinkage approach"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Thibault Verhoeven","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Thibault%20Verhoeven"},{"fullname":"Pieter-Jan Kindermans","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Pieter-Jan%20Kindermans"},{"fullname":"Stefaan Vandenberghe","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Stefaan%20Vandenberghe"},{"fullname":"Joni Dambre","username":"~Joni_Dambre2"},{"fullname":"Gernot R. Müller-Putz","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Gernot%20R.%20M%C3%BCller-Putz"},{"fullname":"Jane E. Huggins","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Jane%20E.%20Huggins"},{"fullname":"David Steyrl","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=David%20Steyrl"}]}},"tmdate":1785855684044,"pdate":1451606400000,"externalIds":["doi:10.3217/978-3-85125-467-9-133"],"tcdate":1785855663770,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Joni_Dambre2"],"forum":"pf6YVUXsRA","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":92425,"cdate":1704896836157,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785855684044,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"pf6YVUXsRA","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.18065v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"hao|on_calibration_of_speech_classification_models_insights_from_energybased_model_investigations"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yaqian_Hao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chenguang_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yingying_Gao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shilei_Zhang:","~Junlan_Feng2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2406.18065"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2406-18065,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yaqian Hao and Chenguang Hu and Yingying Gao and Shilei Zhang and Junlan Feng},\n  title={On Calibration of Speech Classification Models: Insights from Energy-Based Model Investigations},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2406.18065},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2406.18065}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"For speech classification tasks, deep learning models often achieve high accuracy but exhibit shortcomings in calibration, manifesting as classifiers exhibiting overconfidence. The significance of calibration lies in its critical role in guaranteeing the reliability of decision-making within deep learning systems. This study explores the effectiveness of Energy-Based Models in calibrating confidence for speech classification tasks by training a joint EBM integrating a discriminative and a generative model, thereby enhancing the classifiers calibration and mitigating overconfidence. Experimental evaluations conducted on three speech classification tasks specifically: age, emotion, and language recognition. Our findings highlight the competitive performance of EBMs in calibrating the speech classification models. This research emphasizes the potential of EBMs in speech classification tasks, demonstrating their ability to enhance calibration without sacrificing accuracy."},"title":{"value":"On Calibration of Speech Classification Models: Insights from Energy-Based Model Investigations"},"authors":{"value":["Yaqian Hao","Chenguang Hu","Yingying Gao","Shilei Zhang","Junlan Feng"]}},"tmdate":1732260591375,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1732260563742,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Junlan_Feng3"],"forum":"Qt37SdYUSq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":250935,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1732260591375,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Qt37SdYUSq","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Effi-Code generates high-quality, efficient code samples, which are then used for instruction tuning to significantly improve both code correctness and efficiency across various LLMs."},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2025"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Large Langugae Models","Code Generation","Program Synthesis","Efficient Method","Alignment"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/b32096aea4de8eceaa0571716cb3c488282b656c.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"As the use of large language models (LLMs) for code generation becomes more prevalent in software development, it is critical to enhance both the efficiency and correctness of the generated code. \nExisting methods and models primarily focus on the correctness of LLM-generated code, ignoring the efficiency. \nIn this work, we present Effi-Code, an approach to enhancing code generation in LLMs that can improve both efficiency and correctness. We introduce a Self-Optimization process based on Overhead Profiling that leverages open-source LLMs to generate a high-quality dataset of correct and efficient code samples. This dataset is then used to fine-tune various LLMs. Our method involves iterative refinement of generated code, guided by runtime performance metrics and correctness checks. Extensive experiments demonstrate that models fine-tuned on the Effi-Code significantly improve code correctness and efficiency across task types. \nFor example, the pass@1 of DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Instruct generated cod increases from **43.3\\%** to **76.8\\%**, and the average execution time for the same correct tasks decreases by **30.5\\%**.\nEffi-Code offers a scalable and generalizable approach to improving code generation in AI systems, with potential applications in software development, algorithm design, and computational problem-solving."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nhuang2025efficode,\ntitle={Effi-Code: Unleashing Code Efficiency in Language Models},\nauthor={Dong HUANG and Guangtao Zeng and Jianbo Dai and Meng Luo and Han Weng and Yuhao QING and Heming Cui and Zhijiang Guo and Jie Zhang},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ulXCYmvVg6}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Effi-Code: Unleashing Code Efficiency in Language Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c9bb7d3759444078137c53f4b6de7713cdcd4238.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|efficode_unleashing_code_efficiency_in_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Dong_HUANG4","~Guangtao_Zeng1","~Jianbo_Dai1","~Meng_Luo2","~Han_Weng2","~Yuhao_QING1","~Heming_Cui1","~Zhijiang_Guo2","~Jie_Zhang21"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Dong HUANG","Guangtao Zeng","Jianbo Dai","Meng Luo","Han Weng","Yuhao QING","Heming Cui","Zhijiang Guo","Jie Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1738735646937,"tcdate":1726891726360,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2292/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2292/Authors"],"forum":"ulXCYmvVg6","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2292,"cdate":1726891726360,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2292/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2292/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738735646937,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"ulXCYmvVg6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"World Haptics 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5935427/5945436/05945472.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/HAPTICS/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"knoerlein|comparison_of_trackerbased_to_trackerless_haptic_device_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Benjamin_Knoerlein:","~Matthias_Harders1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/WHC.2011.5945472"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/haptics/KnoerleinH11,\n  author={Benjamin Knoerlein and Matthias Harders},\n  title={Comparison of tracker-based to tracker-less haptic device calibration},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={119-124},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/WHC.2011.5945472},\n  booktitle={World Haptics},\n  crossref={conf/haptics/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In our current work we explore the feasibility of using visuo-haptic augmented reality techniques for training and remote collaboration. Accurate calibration of system components is a key factor in this context. In this paper we deal with the calibration of the kinematic parameters of the haptic device. We compare a previously developed technique using an external optical tracker to a new approach not relying on additional devices. Simulated and real experiments are carried out to investigate the performance of the new technique. As will be shown, both methods yield reasonable results with average errors in the workspace below 2mm. It is found that the trackerless technique is an acceptable alternative to using a tracker for calibration, with only slightly reduced accuracy. It is straightforward to apply and allows to improve rendering fidelity."},"title":{"value":"Comparison of tracker-based to tracker-less haptic device calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Benjamin Knoerlein","Matthias Harders"]}},"tmdate":1762417706033,"pdate":1293840000000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/haptics/KnoerleinH11"],"tcdate":1762417668782,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Matthias_Harders1"],"forum":"zGa4XKNrqQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":664874,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762417706033,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zGa4XKNrqQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICASSP 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10445798/10445803/10448417.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICASSP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"yin|autocali_enhancing_aoabased_indoor_localization_through_automatic_phase_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pengfei_Yin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongheng_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianyu_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuai_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guanzhong_Wang:","~Yang_Hu3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yan_Chen_0007:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP48485.2024.10448417"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icassp/YinZZYWHC24,\n  author={Pengfei Yin and Dongheng Zhang and Tianyu Zhang and Shuai Yang and Guanzhong Wang and Yang Hu and Yan Chen},\n  title={AutoCali: Enhancing AoA-based Indoor Localization through Automatic Phase Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={9046-9050},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP48485.2024.10448417},\n  booktitle={ICASSP},\n  crossref={conf/icassp/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent advancements in WiFi indoor localization have demonstrated the potential for achieving decimeter-level accuracy based on Angle of Arrival (AoA). However, existing commercial WiFi Access Points (APs) suffer from phase offset across different antennas, which significantly degrade the performance of AoA-based methods in practical deployment. Previous work either relied on labor-intensive manual calibration or involved inaccurate and non-robust automatic calibration. In this paper, we propose AutoCali, an accurate and robust automatic phase offset calibration system. The key insight is to utilize the binary nature of phase offsets and the property that triangulation exhibits higher convergence when the correct combination of phase offsets is employed. Extensive experiments demonstrate that AutoCali outperforms state-of-the-art methods by 22.1% in median localization error for simple scenarios and by 37.1% for complex multipath scenarios."},"title":{"value":"AutoCali: Enhancing AoA-based Indoor Localization through Automatic Phase Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Pengfei Yin","Dongheng Zhang","Tianyu Zhang","Shuai Yang","Guanzhong Wang","Yang Hu","Yan Chen"]}},"tmdate":1736561508259,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1736561505698,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yang_Hu3"],"forum":"BlUmzKTTvX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":263938,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1736561508259,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"BlUmzKTTvX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2005"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/10495/33250/01570714.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2005"},"paperhash":{"value":"ammi|flexible_microscope_calibration_using_virtual_pattern_for_3d_telemicromanipulation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mehdi_Ammi:","~Vincent_Frémont1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Antoine_Ferreira:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2005.1570714"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/AmmiFF05,\n  author={Mehdi Ammi and Vincent Frémont and Antoine Ferreira},\n  title={Flexible Microscope Calibration using Virtual Pattern for 3-D Telemicromanipulation},\n  year={2005},\n  cdate={1104537600000},\n  pages={3888-3893},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2005.1570714},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2005}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In the context of virtualized reality based telemicromanipulation, we present in this paper a visual calibration technique for optical microscope coupled with a CCD camera. In contrast to previous approaches, a virtual calibration pattern is constructed using the micromanipulator with a sub-pixel localization in the image. We also present a new camera calibration algorithm based on Parallel Single-Plane properties. The proposed procedure leads to a linear system from which the solution gives directly both intrinsic and extrinsic parameters of the geometrical model. Both computer simulation and real data have been used to test the proposed technique, and very good results have been obtained. Compared with classical techniques, our method provides an alternative technical solution, easy to use and flexible in the context of micromanipulation and virtual reality."},"title":{"value":"Flexible Microscope Calibration using Virtual Pattern for 3-D Telemicromanipulation"},"authors":{"value":["Mehdi Ammi","Vincent Frémont","Antoine Ferreira"]}},"tmdate":1730366122136,"pdate":1104537600000,"tcdate":1730366084791,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~FREMONT_Vincent1"],"forum":"myvFwkA0Bq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":165410,"cdate":1104537600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730366122136,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"myvFwkA0Bq","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Investigation of how noise perturbations impact neural network calibration and generalisation, identifying which perturbations are helpful and when."},"venue":{"value":"SCIS 2023 Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/55cdf5d5619d928901592272bf574426da01719f.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Neural networks","noise perturbation","calibration","uncertainty quantification"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/SCIS"},"abstract":{"value":"Noise injection and data augmentation strategies have been effective for enhancing the generalisation and robustness of neural networks (NNs). Certain types of noise such as label smoothing and MixUp have also been shown to improve calibration. \nSince noise can be added in various stages of the NN's training, it motivates the question of when and where the noise is the most effective. \nWe study a variety of noise types to determine how much they improve calibration and generalisation, and under what conditions. \nMore specifically we evaluate various noise-injection strategies in both in-distribution (ID) and out-of-distribution (OOD) scenarios.\nThe findings highlight that activation noise was the most transferable and effective in improving generalisation, while input augmentation noise was prominent in improving calibration on OOD but not necessarily ID data."},"title":{"value":"Impact of Noise on Calibration and Generalisation of Neural Networks"}},"tmdate":1690513101087,"pdate":1687228756075,"tcdate":1684665212736,"writers":["ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/SCIS","ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/SCIS/Submission8/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/SCIS/Submission8/Authors"],"forum":"QzlN0rUJVi","number":8,"cdate":1684665212736,"mdate":1690513101087,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/SCIS/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/SCIS/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/SCIS/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/SCIS/Submission8/-/Post-Decision_Revision"],"domain":"ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/SCIS","id":"QzlN0rUJVi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MICCAI (11) 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-032-38179-8_56.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|trajectoryaware_crossparadigm_transformers_for_efficient_system_matrix_calibration_in_magnetic_particle_imaging"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-38179-8_56"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/miccai/LiZHHLSHG26,\n  author={Jintao Li and Lizhi Zhang and Xiaowei He and Shuaishuai He and Shuangchen Li and Bo Shi and Yuqing Hou and Hongbo Guo},\n  title={Trajectory-Aware Cross-Paradigm Transformers for Efficient System Matrix Calibration in Magnetic Particle Imaging},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={583-593},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-38179-8_56},\n  booktitle={MICCAI (11)},\n  crossref={conf/miccai/2026-11}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Magnetic particle imaging (MPI) is an emerging medical imaging modality that enables highly sensitive visualization of magnetic nanoparticles with high temporal resolution. However, variations in magnetic particle materials and field-free point trajectories require repeated system matrix recalibration, which is time-consuming and limits the efficiency and flexibility of MPI in practical applications. To address this challenge, we propose a trajectory-aware cross-paradigm transformer (TACPT) for efficient and accurate SM calibration. Leveraging the trajectory-dependent distribution of SM signals relative to FFPs, we introduce a trajectory-based encoding mechanism that embeds physically grounded positional priors derived from FFP trajectories into the transformer backbone. This design guides the network to focus on trajectory-relevant and information-dense regions. To further accommodate the complex structure of SM data, we develop a cross-paradigm feature interaction module that integrates FFT-based inter-channel modeling with spatial structure learning. These complementary representations are adaptively fused through a dynamic sparsity-gated mechanism, suppressing redundant responses and producing compact, discriminative SM representations. Experimental results demonstrate superior SM calibration and MPI reconstruction performance over existing methods, while reducing recalibration overhead without compromising fidelity to improve MPI system efficiency and flexibility. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/learning-TL/TACPTnet."},"title":{"value":"Trajectory-Aware Cross-Paradigm Transformers for Efficient System Matrix Calibration in Magnetic Particle Imaging"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jintao Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Lizhi Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Xiaowei He","username":"~Xiaowei_He1"},{"fullname":"Shuaishuai He","username":""},{"fullname":"Shuangchen Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Bo Shi","username":""},{"fullname":"Yuqing Hou","username":""},{"fullname":"Hongbo Guo","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1793711325653,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/miccai/LiZHHLSHG26"],"tcdate":1793711322748,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Xiaowei_He1"],"forum":"aYJaScXD9D","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":222195,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1793711325653,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"aYJaScXD9D","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Neural Systems and Rehabilitation Engineering"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7333/9216637/09177172.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wong|inter_and_intrasubject_transfer_reduces_calibration_effort_for_highspeed_ssvepbased_bcis"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TNSRE.2020.3019276"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{Wong_2020,\n\tdoi = {10.1109/tnsre.2020.3019276},\n\turl = {https://doi.org/10.1109%2Ftnsre.2020.3019276},\n\tyear = 2020,\n\tmonth = {oct},\n\tpublisher = {Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers ({IEEE})},\n\tvolume = {28},\n\tnumber = {10},\n\tpages = {2123--2135},\n\tauthor = {Chi Man Wong and Ze Wang and Boyu Wang and Ka Fai Lao and Agostinho Rosa and Peng Xu and Tzyy-Ping Jung and C. L. Philip Chen and Feng Wan},\n\ttitle = {Inter- and Intra-Subject Transfer Reduces Calibration Effort for High-Speed {SSVEP}-Based {BCIs}},\n\tjournal = {{IEEE} Transactions on Neural Systems and Rehabilitation Engineering}\n}"},"abstract":{"value":"Objective: Steady-state visual evoked potential (SSVEP)-based brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) that can deliver a high information transfer rate (ITR) usually require subject’s calibration data to learn the class- and subject-specific model parameters (e.g. the spatial filters and SSVEP templates). Normally, the amount of the calibration data for learning is proportional to the number of classes (or visual stimuli), which could be huge and consequently lead to a time-consuming calibration. This study presents a transfer learning scheme to substantially reduce the calibration effort. Methods: Inspired by the parameter-based and instance-based transfer learning techniques, we propose a subject transfer based canonical correlation analysis (stCCA) method which utilizes the knowledge within subject and between subjects, thus requiring few calibration data from a new subject. Results: The evaluation study on two SSVEP datasets (from Tsinghua and UCSD) shows that the stCCA method performs well with only a small amount of calibration data, providing an ITR at 198.18±59.12 (bits/min) with 9 calibration trials in the Tsinghua dataset and 111.04±57.24 (bits/min) with 3 trials in the UCSD dataset. Such performances are comparable to those from using the multi-stimulus CCA (msCCA) and the ensemble task-related component analysis (eTRCA) methods with the minimally required calibration data (i.e., at least 40 trials in the Tsinghua dataset and at least 12 trials in the UCSD dataset), respectively. Conclusion: Inter- and intra-subject transfer helps the recognition method achieve high ITR with extremely little calibration effort. Significance: The proposed approach saves much calibration effort without sacrificing the ITR, which would be significant for practical SSVEP-based BCIs."},"title":{"value":"Inter- and Intra-Subject Transfer Reduces Calibration Effort for High-Speed SSVEP-Based BCIs"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Chi Man Wong"},{"fullname":"Ze Wang"},{"fullname":"Boyu Wang"},{"fullname":"Ka Fai Lao"},{"fullname":"Agostinho Rosa"},{"fullname":"Peng Xu"},{"fullname":"Tzyy-Ping Jung"},{"fullname":"C. L. Philip Chen"},{"fullname":"Feng Wan","username":"~Feng_Wan2"}]}},"tmdate":1789091819599,"pdate":1601510400000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tnsre.2020.3019276"],"tcdate":1767435616701,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Feng_Wan2"],"forum":"Onwj7Wn5zE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":21367,"cdate":1602099852164,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091819599,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Onwj7Wn5zE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Remote. Sens. 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/13/14/2832/pdf?version=1626754942"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/REMOTESENSING/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|a_spliced_satellite_optical_camera_geometric_calibration_method_based_on_interchip_geometry_constraints"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_Wang_0075:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yan_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yongsheng_Zhang:","~Zhenchao_Zhang1","~Xiongwu_Xiao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ying_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Longhui_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/rs13142832"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/remotesensing/WangZZZXYW21,\n  author={Tao Wang and Yan Zhang and Yongsheng Zhang and Zhenchao Zhang and Xiongwu Xiao and Ying Yu and Longhui Wang},\n  title={A Spliced Satellite Optical Camera Geometric Calibration Method Based on Inter-Chip Geometry Constraints},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={13},\n  number={14},\n  pages={2832},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/rs13142832}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"When in orbit, spliced satellite optical cameras are affected by various factors that degrade the actual image stitching precision and the accuracy of their data products. This is a major bottleneck in the current remote sensing technology. Previous geometric calibration research has mostly focused on stitched satellite images and has largely ignored the inter-chip relationship among original multi-chip images, resulting in accuracy loss in geometric calibration and subsequent image products. Therefore, in this paper, a novel geometric calibration method is proposed for spliced satellite optical cameras. The integral geometric calibration model was developed on inter-chip geometry constraints among multi-chip images, including the corresponding external and internal calibration models. The proposed approach improves uncontrolled geopositioning accuracy and enhances mosaic precision at the same time. For evaluation, images from the optical butting satellite ZiYuan-3 (ZY-3) and mechanical interleaving satellite Tianhui-1 (TH-1) were used for the experiments. Multiple sets of satellite data of the Songshan Calibration field and other regions were used to evaluate the reliability, stability, and applicability of the calibration parameters. The experiment results found that the proposed method obtains reliable camera alignment angles and interior calibration parameters and generates high-precision seamless mosaic images. The calibration scheme is not only suitable for mechanical interleaving cameras with large geometric displacement among multi-chip images but is also effective for optical butting cameras with minor chip offset. It also significantly improves uncontrolled geopositioning accuracy for both types of spliced satellite images. Moreover, the proposed calibration procedure results in multi-chip satellite images being seamlessly stitched together and mosaic errors within one pixel."},"title":{"value":"A Spliced Satellite Optical Camera Geometric Calibration Method Based on Inter-Chip Geometry Constraints"},"authors":{"value":["Tao Wang","Yan Zhang","Yongsheng Zhang","Zhenchao Zhang","Xiongwu Xiao","Ying Yu","Longhui Wang"]}},"tmdate":1744378387530,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1743990110075,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhenchao_Zhang1"],"forum":"izIDlDPHAK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":376771,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1744378387530,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"izIDlDPHAK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2025 Conference Desk Rejected Submission"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Code completion","LLMs","Benchmark","RAG","Code snippets","GitHub files","Exact match metric"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/4a6d97649b6382b6ba1ec9ac9656012fa306b6f5.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"generative models"},"reciprocal_reviewing":{"value":"I understand the reciprocal reviewing requirement as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/CallForPapers. If none of the authors are registered as a reviewer, it may result in a desk rejection at the discretion of the program chairs. To request an exception, please complete this form at https://forms.gle/Huojr6VjkFxiQsUp6."},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"The purpose of the code completion task is to continue the following line of the source code. This feature helps developers to write code faster and with fewer errors by providing suggestions for completing the current line of code based on the context and the available libraries and functions.  One common but resource-expensive approach to improve model performance on the code completion task is to fine-tune Large Language Models (LLMs). This paper introduces a technique that involves feeding the additional input with ranked portions of code to the LLMs without additional fine-tuning. The proposed approach aims to replicate the development process of programmers by scanning project files for the required code snippets, making the process intuitive and efficient. The paper also discusses the lack of metrics for the task and puts forward a novel metric ClickScore, as well as a new code benchmark RealCode for the code completion task. The paper compares the insertion technique of code snippets with the existing state-of-the-art methods using standard and proposed metrics and demonstrates the approach's effectiveness."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nkamkia2024code,\ntitle={Code snippets insertion approach for code completion task},\nauthor={Nina Kamkia and Dmitry Stanishevskiy and Alena Fenogenova and Vitaly Kadulin and Denis Kokosinskii and Dmitry Zmitrovich},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=sQa6VrK6tm}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Code snippets insertion approach for code completion task"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/78472ec74af6893ede655ab305d8034076cc356e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Desk_Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"kamkia|code_snippets_insertion_approach_for_code_completion_task"},"authorids":{"value":["~Nina_Kamkia1","~Dmitry_Stanishevskiy1","~Alena_Fenogenova1","~Vitaly_Kadulin1","~Denis_Kokosinskii1","~Dmitry_Zmitrovich1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Nina Kamkia","Dmitry Stanishevskiy","Alena Fenogenova","Vitaly Kadulin","Denis Kokosinskii","Dmitry Zmitrovich"]}},"tmdate":1766786236809,"tcdate":1727438187479,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission10119/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission10119/Authors"],"forum":"sQa6VrK6tm","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":10119,"cdate":1727438187479,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission10119/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Desk_Rejected_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1766786236809,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"sQa6VrK6tm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"KDD 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/KDD/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|beyond_binary_preference_leveraging_bayesian_approaches_for_joint_optimization_of_ranking_and_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chang_Liu_0078:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qiwei_Wang:","~Wenqing_Lin1","~Yue_Ding2","~Hongtao_Lu1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3637528.3671577"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/kdd/LiuWL0L24,\n  author={Chang Liu and Qiwei Wang and Wenqing Lin and Yue Ding and Hongtao Lu},\n  title={Beyond Binary Preference: Leveraging Bayesian Approaches for Joint Optimization of Ranking and Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={5442-5453},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3637528.3671577},\n  booktitle={KDD},\n  crossref={conf/kdd/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Predicting click-through rate (CTR) is a critical task in recommendation systems, where the models are optimized with pointwise loss to infer the probability of items being clicked. In industrial practice, applications also require ranking items based on these probabilities. Existing solutions primarily combine the ranking-based loss, i.e., pairwise and listwise loss, with CTR prediction. However, they can hardly calibrate or generalize well in CTR scenarios where the clicks reflect the binary preference. This is because the binary click feedback leads to a large number of ties, which renders high data sparsity. In this paper, we propose an effective data augmentation strategy, named Beyond Binary Preference (BBP) training framework, to address this problem. Our key idea is to break the ties by leveraging Bayesian approaches, where the beta distribution models click behavior as probability distributions in the training data that naturally break ties. Therefore, we can obtain an auxiliary training label that generates more comparable pairs and improves the ranking performance. Besides, BBP formulates ranking and calibration as a multi-task framework to optimize both objectives simultaneously. Through extensive offline experiments and online tests on various datasets, we demonstrate that BBP significantly outperforms state-of-the-art methods in both ranking and calibration capabilities, showcasing its effectiveness in addressing the limitations of existing methods. Our code is available at https://github.com/AlvinIsonomia/BBP."},"title":{"value":"Beyond Binary Preference: Leveraging Bayesian Approaches for Joint Optimization of Ranking and Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Chang Liu","Qiwei Wang","Wenqing Lin","Yue Ding","Hongtao Lu"]}},"tmdate":1791986414684,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1737924434482,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yue_Ding2"],"forum":"Ot3WeKfG5t","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":281903,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1791986414684,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Ot3WeKfG5t","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.03238v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"pan|longtailed_medical_diagnosis_with_relationaware_representation_learning_and_iterative_classifier_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Li_Pan4","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yupei_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qiushi_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tan_Li_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhen_Chen_0013:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.03238"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2502-03238,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Li Pan and Yupei Zhang and Qiushi Yang and Tan Li and Zhen Chen},\n  title={Long-tailed Medical Diagnosis with Relation-aware Representation Learning and Iterative Classifier Calibration},\n  year={2025},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1738368000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2502.03238},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.03238}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recently computer-aided diagnosis has demonstrated promising performance, effectively alleviating the workload of clinicians. However, the inherent sample imbalance among different diseases leads algorithms biased to the majority categories, leading to poor performance for rare categories. Existing works formulated this challenge as a long-tailed problem and attempted to tackle it by decoupling the feature representation and classification. Yet, due to the imbalanced distribution and limited samples from tail classes, these works are prone to biased representation learning and insufficient classifier calibration. To tackle these problems, we propose a new Long-tailed Medical Diagnosis (LMD) framework for balanced medical image classification on long-tailed datasets. In the initial stage, we develop a Relation-aware Representation Learning (RRL) scheme to boost the representation ability by encouraging the encoder to capture intrinsic semantic features through different data augmentations. In the subsequent stage, we propose an Iterative Classifier Calibration (ICC) scheme to calibrate the classifier iteratively. This is achieved by generating a large number of balanced virtual features and fine-tuning the encoder using an Expectation-Maximization manner. The proposed ICC compensates for minority categories to facilitate unbiased classifier optimization while maintaining the diagnostic knowledge in majority classes. Comprehensive experiments on three public long-tailed medical datasets demonstrate that our LMD framework significantly surpasses state-of-the-art approaches. The source code can be accessed at https://github.com/peterlipan/LMD."},"title":{"value":"Long-tailed Medical Diagnosis with Relation-aware Representation Learning and Iterative Classifier Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Li Pan","Yupei Zhang","Qiushi Yang","Tan Li","Zhen Chen"]}},"tmdate":1769478196535,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2502-03238"],"tcdate":1769478191181,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Li_Pan4"],"forum":"pwZkalbE5G","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":805855,"cdate":1738368000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769478196535,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"pwZkalbE5G","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Int. J. Intell. Robotics Appl. 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s41315-017-0034-6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJIRA/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|global_homography_calibration_for_monocular_visionbased_pose_measurement_of_mobile_robots"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuebo_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Congyuan_Wang:","~Yongchun_Fang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Han_Lu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiang_Chen_0011:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s41315-017-0034-6"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijira/ZhangWFLC17,\n  author={Xuebo Zhang and Congyuan Wang and Yongchun Fang and Han Lu and Xiang Chen},\n  title={Global homography calibration for monocular vision-based pose measurement of mobile robots},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  journal={Int. J. Intell. Robotics Appl.},\n  volume={1},\n  number={4},\n  pages={372-382},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s41315-017-0034-6}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Compared with the size of a calibration board, the inspected area within the camera field of view is much bigger in many applications such as visual surveillance systems. In this paper, we present a global image-to-ground homography calibration method to obtain the mapping between the image and the planar scene lying in the whole camera field of view. The calibration approach is proposed by fusing multiple local homography matrices, with each of them only reflecting the relationship between a small calibration board and its corresponding small image part. By further calibrating a height-related homography, visual measurement can be extended for arbitrary planar surfaces with known heights. The proposed method presents such merits as no requirement for camera internal parameters, high calibration accuracy, and ease of implementation. Experimental results show that (1) the proposed global homography method achieves more accurate measurement results than the local homography method; (2) the height-related homography is calibrated with high accuracy; (3) the proposed approach can be used for mobile robot localization with accuracy close to the performance limit of a monocular camera."},"title":{"value":"Global homography calibration for monocular vision-based pose measurement of mobile robots"},"authors":{"value":["Xuebo Zhang","Congyuan Wang","Yongchun Fang","Han Lu","Xiang Chen"]}},"tmdate":1748236024866,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1748235990533,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yongchun_Fang1"],"forum":"K2NBOevZF5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":551543,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1748236024866,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"K2NBOevZF5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"BIC-TA 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-981-99-1549-1_12.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/BIC-TA/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|multistage_objective_function_optimized_handeye_selfcalibration_of_robot_in_autonomous_environment"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaibo_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wangli_Zheng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Huasong_Min:","~Yunhan_Lin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-99-1549-1_12"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/bic-ta/LiuZML22,\n  author={Kaibo Liu and Wangli Zheng and Huasong Min and Yunhan Lin},\n  title={Multi-stage Objective Function Optimized Hand-Eye Self-calibration of Robot in Autonomous Environment},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={144-157},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-99-1549-1_12},\n  booktitle={BIC-TA},\n  crossref={conf/bic-ta/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In the application scenario of robot autonomous tasks, the robot needs to be able to complete calibration online and automatically to achieve self-maintenance, which differs from traditional robot hand-eye calibration in that the traditional robot hand-eye calibration requires a dedicated calibration board to assist offline completion. Aiming at the problem that the existing self-calibration methods cannot be optimized as a whole, which leads to low accuracy and instability of the solution, a multi-stage objective function optimization self-calibration algorithm is proposed, which describes the solution of hand-eye self-calibration as a minimization objective function problem involving multiple stages. An optimization method based on the minimization of re-projection error is designed to compensate for the results, which uses an efficient Oriented fast and rotated brief (ORB) feature extraction algorithm and introduces a scoring mechanism to retain more correct matching points in the feature matching stage. Two different types of experiments were designed to validate our method. One is a single camera dataset experiment, which shows that our method is more accurate and robust than the existing self-calibration method; the other is an application platform experiment, which verifies the feasibility and availability of our method."},"title":{"value":"Multi-stage Objective Function Optimized Hand-Eye Self-calibration of Robot in Autonomous Environment"},"authors":{"value":["Kaibo Liu","Wangli Zheng","Huasong Min","Yunhan Lin"]}},"tmdate":1749114129327,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1749114122900,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lin_Yunhan1"],"forum":"KtycRXUvFQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":555648,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1749114129327,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KtycRXUvFQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.21454v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"yao|4dcaal_4d_radarcamera_calibration_and_autolabeling_for_autonomous_driving"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.21454"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2601-21454,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shanliang Yao and Zhuoxiao Li and Runwei Guan and Kebin Cao and Meng Xia and Fuping Hu and Sen Xu and Yong Yue and Xiaohui Zhu and Weiping Ding and Ryan Wen Liu},\n  title={4D-CAAL: 4D Radar-Camera Calibration and Auto-Labeling for Autonomous Driving},\n  year={2026},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2601.21454},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.21454}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"4D radar has emerged as a critical sensor for autonomous driving, primarily due to its enhanced capabilities in elevation measurement and higher resolution compared to traditional 3D radar. Effective integration of 4D radar with cameras requires accurate extrinsic calibration, and the development of radar-based perception algorithms demands large-scale annotated datasets. However, existing calibration methods often employ separate targets optimized for either visual or radar modalities, complicating correspondence establishment. Furthermore, manually labeling sparse radar data is labor-intensive and unreliable. To address these challenges, we propose 4D-CAAL, a unified framework for 4D radar-camera calibration and auto-labeling. Our approach introduces a novel dual-purpose calibration target design, integrating a checkerboard pattern on the front surface for camera detection and a corner reflector at the center of the back surface for radar detection. We develop a robust correspondence matching algorithm that aligns the checkerboard center with the strongest radar reflection point, enabling accurate extrinsic calibration. Subsequently, we present an auto-labeling pipeline that leverages the calibrated sensor relationship to transfer annotations from camera-based segmentations to radar point clouds through geometric projection and multi-feature optimization. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method achieves high calibration accuracy while significantly reducing manual annotation effort, thereby accelerating the development of robust multi-modal perception systems for autonomous driving."},"title":{"value":"4D-CAAL: 4D Radar-Camera Calibration and Auto-Labeling for Autonomous Driving"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Shanliang Yao","username":""},{"fullname":"Zhuoxiao Li","username":"~Zhuoxiao_Li3"},{"fullname":"Runwei Guan","username":""},{"fullname":"Kebin Cao","username":""},{"fullname":"Meng Xia","username":""},{"fullname":"Fuping Hu","username":"~Fuping_Hu1"},{"fullname":"Sen Xu","username":""},{"fullname":"Yong Yue","username":""},{"fullname":"Xiaohui Zhu","username":""},{"fullname":"Weiping Ding","username":""},{"fullname":"Ryan Wen Liu","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784774354960,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2601-21454"],"tcdate":1784311764916,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~ZhuoxiaoLi1"],"forum":"hyOkc7lQT5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":62293,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1784774354960,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"hyOkc7lQT5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IPSJ Trans. Comput. Vis. Appl. 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ipsjcva.springeropen.com/counter/pdf/10.1186/s41074-017-0032-5"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IPSJTCVA/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"fürsattel|accurate_laser_scanner_to_camera_calibration_with_application_to_range_sensor_evaluation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peter_Fürsattel:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Claus_Plank:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andreas_K._Maier:","~Christian_Riess1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s41074-017-0032-5"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ipsjtcva/FursattelPMR17,\n  author={Peter Fürsattel and Claus Plank and Andreas K. Maier and Christian Riess},\n  title={Accurate laser scanner to camera calibration with application to range sensor evaluation},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  journal={IPSJ Trans. Comput. Vis. Appl.},\n  volume={9},\n  pages={21},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1186/s41074-017-0032-5}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-modal sensory data plays an important role in many computer vision and robotics tasks. One popular multi-modal pair is cameras and laser scanners. To overlay and jointly use the data from both modalities, it is necessary to calibrate the sensors, i.e., to obtain the spatial relation between the sensors. Computing such a calibration is challenging as both sensors provide quite different data: cameras yield color or brightness information, laser scanners yield 3-D points. However, several laser scanners additionally provide reflectances, which turn out to make calibration to a camera well feasible. To this end, we first estimate a rough alignment of the coordinate systems of both modalities. Then, we use the laser scanner reflectances to compute a virtual image of the scene. Stereo calibration on the virtual image and the camera image are then used to compute a refined, high-accuracy calibration. It is encouraging that the accuracies in our experiments are comparable to camera-camera stereo setups and outperform another of other target-based calibration approach. This shows that the proposed algorithm reliably integrates the point cloud with the intensity image. As an example application, we use the calibration results to obtain ground-truth distance images for range cameras. Furthermore, we utilize this data to investigate the accuracy of the Microsoft Kinect V2 time-of-flight and the Intel RealSense R200 structured light camera."},"title":{"value":"Accurate laser scanner to camera calibration with application to range sensor evaluation"},"authors":{"value":["Peter Fürsattel","Claus Plank","Andreas K. Maier","Christian Riess"]}},"tmdate":1750671736845,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1750671669517,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Christian_Riess1"],"forum":"JO0KzbUXKE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":564484,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1750671736845,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"JO0KzbUXKE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SIGIR 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|selfimproving_teacher_cultivates_better_student_distillation_calibration_for_multimodal_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xinwei_Li1","~Li_Lin2","~Shuai_Wang17","~Chen_Qian1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3626772.3657692"},"abstract":{"value":"Knowledge transfer from multimodal large language models to compact student models remains expensive and can constrain student performance. We present Multimodal Distillation Calibration (MmDC), a framework that improves student learning through iterative refinement of the teacher's supervision. MmDC combines multimodal feature alignment with a calibration procedure that identifies student weaknesses and constructs challenging training examples. Parameter-efficient adaptation supports alignment across modalities, while repeated distillation calibration improves the quality of the transferred knowledge. Experimental results demonstrate improvements across multimodal benchmarks. After three rounds of calibration, a 7B student model surpasses LLaVA-13B on ScienceQA and LLaVA Test and achieves competitive zero-shot results."},"title":{"value":"Self-Improving Teacher Cultivates Better Student: Distillation Calibration for Multimodal Large Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Xinwei Li","Li Lin","Shuai Wang","Chen Qian"]}},"tmdate":1791881785667,"pdate":1720627200000,"tcdate":1791881785667,"writers":["~Xinwei_Li1","~Li_Lin2","~Shuai_Wang17","~Chen_Qian1"],"signatures":["~Xinwei_Li1"],"forum":"VHBpCAL9bC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":55815,"cdate":1791881785667,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1791881785667,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"VHBpCAL9bC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2013"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6679723/6696319/06696592.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2013"},"paperhash":{"value":"heng|camodocal_automatic_intrinsic_and_extrinsic_calibration_of_a_rig_with_multiple_generic_cameras_and_odometry"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lionel_Heng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bo_Li_0018:","~Marc_Pollefeys1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.2013.6696592"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/HengLP13,\n  author={Lionel Heng and Bo Li and Marc Pollefeys},\n  title={CamOdoCal: Automatic intrinsic and extrinsic calibration of a rig with multiple generic cameras and odometry},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  pages={1793-1800},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.2013.6696592},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2013}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multiple cameras are increasingly prevalent on robotic and human-driven vehicles. These cameras come in a variety of wide-angle, fish-eye, and catadioptric models. Furthermore, wheel odometry is generally available on the vehicles on which the cameras are mounted. For robustness, vision applications tend to use wheel odometry as a strong prior for camera pose estimation, and in these cases, an accurate extrinsic calibration is required in addition to an accurate intrinsic calibration. To date, there is no known work on automatic intrinsic calibration of generic cameras, and more importantly, automatic extrinsic calibration of a rig with multiple generic cameras and odometry. We propose an easy-to-use automated pipeline that handles both intrinsic and extrinsic calibration; we do not assume that there are overlapping fields of view. At the begining, we run an intrinsic calibration for each generic camera. The intrinsic calibration is automatic and requires a chessboard. Subsequently, we run an extrinsic calibration which finds all camera-odometry transforms. The extrinsic calibration is unsupervised, uses natural features, and only requires the vehicle to be driven around for a short time. The intrinsic parameters are optimized in a final bundle adjustment step in the extrinsic calibration. In addition, the pipeline produces a globally-consistent sparse map of landmarks which can be used for visual localization. The pipeline is publicly available as a standalone C++ package."},"title":{"value":"CamOdoCal: Automatic intrinsic and extrinsic calibration of a rig with multiple generic cameras and odometry"},"authors":{"value":["Lionel Heng","Bo Li","Marc Pollefeys"]}},"tmdate":1762679713805,"pdate":1356998400000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iros/HengLP13"],"tcdate":1762679695830,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Marc_Pollefeys2"],"forum":"uTt5CSWHve","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":681025,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762679713805,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"uTt5CSWHve","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IGARSS 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9323073/9323063/09323468.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IGARSS/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"imai|inflight_radiometric_calibration_for_a_multiband_sensor_onboard_risesat_with_the_moon"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","~Toshinori_Kuwahara1","","","","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS39084.2020.9323468"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/igarss/ImaiKKK0SSHT20,\n  author={Masataka Imai and Toru Kouyama and Junichi Kurihara and Toshinori Kuwahara and Shinya Fujita and Yuji Sakamoto and Sei-Ichi Saitoh and Takafumi Hirata and Yukihiro Takahashi},\n  title={Inflight Radiometric Calibration for a Multi-Band Sensor Onboard Risesat with the Moon},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={6222-6225},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS39084.2020.9323468},\n  booktitle={IGARSS},\n  crossref={conf/igarss/2020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Radiometric calibration with the Moon (called the lunar calibration) is a promising method for evaluating the performance of instruments onboard satellites orbiting around the Earth. In particular, the lunar calibration can provide inflight radiometric calibration opportunities for nano/microsatellites whose priorities are technical demonstrations and scientific observations during rather a short lifetime. In this study, we have applied the lunar calibration to a recently launched microsatellite named RISESAT. RISESAT had observed the Moon every month for a half year after the launch with an onboard instrument OOC. By simulating the Moon brightness for each observation based on the ROLO and SELENE/SP Moon models, we succeeded to measure <; 2 % of a small degradation in four months. Further comparison of the observation and the simulation irradiance of the Moon revealed the bluing trend in the OOC's inter-band ratio."},"title":{"value":"Inflight Radiometric Calibration for a Multi-Band Sensor Onboard Risesat with the Moon"},"authors":{"value":["Masataka Imai","Toru Kouyama","Junichi Kurihara","Toshinori Kuwahara","Shinya Fujita","Yuji Sakamoto","Sei-Ichi Saitoh","Takafumi Hirata","Yukihiro Takahashi"]}},"tmdate":1772355362783,"pdate":1609372800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/igarss/ImaiKKK0SSHT20"],"tcdate":1772355350421,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Toshinori_Kuwahara1"],"forum":"bCPX8uy3J4","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":840743,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772355362783,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bCPX8uy3J4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICSME 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9240597/9240598/09240627.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|learning_codequery_interaction_for_enhancing_code_searches"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSME46990.2020.00021"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icsm/LiQYSC20,\n  author={Wei Li and Haozhe Qin and Shuhan Yan and Beijun Shen and Yuting Chen},\n  title={Learning Code-Query Interaction for Enhancing Code Searches},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={115-126},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSME46990.2020.00021},\n  booktitle={ICSME},\n  crossref={conf/icsm/2020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code search plays an important role in software development and maintenance. In recent years, deep learning (DL) has achieved a great success in this domain-several DL-based code search methods, such as DeepCS and UNIF, have been proposed for exploring deep, semantic correlations between code and queries; each method usually embeds source code and natural language queries into real vectors followed by computing their vector distances representing their semantic correlations. Meanwhile, deep learning-based code search still suffers from three main problems, i.e., the OOV (Out of Vocabulary) problem, the independent similarity matching problem, and the small training dataset problem. To tackle the above problems, we propose CQIL, a novel, deep learning-based code search method. CQIL learns code-query interactions and uses a CNN (Convolutional Neural Network) to compute semantic correlations between queries and code snippets. In particular, CQIL employs a hybrid representation to model code-query correlations, which solves the OOV problem. CQIL also deeply learns the code-query interaction for enhancing code searches, which solves the independent similarity matching and the small training dataset problems. We evaluate CQIL on two datasets (CODEnn and CosBench). The evaluation results show the strengths of CQIL-it achieves the MAP@1 values, 0.694 and 0.574, on CODEnn and CosBench, respectively. In particular, it outperforms DeepCS and UNIF, two state-of-the-art code search methods, by 13.6% and 18.1% in MRR, respectively, when the training dataset is insufficient."},"title":{"value":"Learning Code-Query Interaction for Enhancing Code Searches"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Wei Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Haozhe Qin","username":""},{"fullname":"Shuhan Yan","username":""},{"fullname":"Beijun Shen","username":"~Beijun_Shen2"},{"fullname":"Yuting Chen","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1780122312155,"pdate":1609372800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icsm/LiQYSC20"],"tcdate":1780122300632,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Beijun_Shen2"],"forum":"aKCLr8dfmy","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":38957,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1780122312155,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"aKCLr8dfmy","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICASSP 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7465907/7471614/07472271.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"le|experimental_validation_of_toabased_methods_for_microphones_array_positions_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP.2016.7472271"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icassp/LeONDR16,\n  author={Trung-Kien Le and Nobutaka Ono and Thibault Nowakowski and Laurent Daudet and Julien de Rosny},\n  title={Experimental validation of TOA-based methods for microphones array positions calibration},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  pages={3216-3220},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP.2016.7472271},\n  booktitle={ICASSP},\n  crossref={conf/icassp/2016}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This study is an experimental validation of a new closed-form method for automatic array position calibration, based on time of arrival (TOA) measurements between sources and sensors. An experiment with a large array composed of 121 microphones and a dozen of sources has been set up. We first show that, when considering the whole array, this calibration method gives results on par with a reference state-of-the-art acoustic method. We then show experimentally that the new method provides significantly better results when the number of sources and microphones decreases, confirming numerical simulations. We conclude the paper with a discussion on methodological issues for array position calibration."},"title":{"value":"Experimental validation of TOA-based methods for microphones array positions calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Trung-Kien Le","username":""},{"fullname":"Nobutaka Ono","username":"~Nobutaka_Ono2"},{"fullname":"Thibault Nowakowski","username":""},{"fullname":"Laurent Daudet","username":""},{"fullname":"Julien de Rosny","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1778152243442,"pdate":1483142400000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icassp/LeONDR16"],"tcdate":1778152117757,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Nobutaka_Ono2"],"forum":"KTz5FBUyFF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":11760,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1778152243442,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"KTz5FBUyFF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.21804v2"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"patel|is_agent_code_less_maintainable_than_human_code"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.21804"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2606-21804,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shaswat Patel and Betty Li Hou and Arun Purohit and Kai Xu and Jane Pan and He He and Valerie Chen},\n  title={Is Agent Code Less Maintainable Than Human Code?},\n  year={2026},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1780272000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2606.21804},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.21804}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Maintainability is a core dimension of software engineering, shaping how code is written, reviewed, and developed over time. While coding agents have demonstrated strong performance on single-issue tasks, it remains unclear how maintainable their code is when future agents build on top of it, potentially leading to compounding downstream effects. We investigate how agent code compares to human code in these maintenance settings, presenting CodeThread, a framework to construct controlled experiments from repository-level coding benchmarks. Applying CodeThread to four frontier coding agents and four benchmarks, we find that agents are less effective at resolving tasks when building on agent code compared to human code, with task resolve rate drops of up to 13.1%. Regression analysis reveals that many traditional software engineering maintainability metrics do not explain this difference. Instead, the clearest signals are subtler behavioral differences in agent code, such as changes to input validation and error handling, along with differences in downstream code size and task difficulty. These findings highlight the need to evaluate these systems not only by immediate task resolution but also by code maintainability, and point to potential sources of downstream errors introduced by agent code."},"title":{"value":"Is Agent Code Less Maintainable Than Human Code?"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Shaswat Patel","username":""},{"fullname":"Betty Li Hou","username":""},{"fullname":"Arun Purohit","username":""},{"fullname":"Kai Xu","username":""},{"fullname":"Jane Pan","username":"~Jane_Pan1"},{"fullname":"He He","username":""},{"fullname":"Valerie Chen","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1792102203748,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2606-21804"],"tcdate":1792102200567,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Jane_Pan1"],"forum":"SoTLUI0Oir","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":190930,"cdate":1780272000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1792102203748,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"SoTLUI0Oir","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce \\textbf{CodeThread}, a framework for comparing the maintainability of agent and human code, and find that agents resolve tasks less effectively when building on agent code than on human code."},"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 AIWILD"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["coding agents","large language models","maintainability","evaluation","software engineering"]},"abstract":{"value":"Maintainability is a core dimension of software engineering, shaping how code is written, reviewed, and developed over time. While coding agents have demonstrated strong performance on single-issue tasks, it remains unclear how maintainable their code is when future agents build on top of it, potentially leading to compounding downstream effects. We investigate how agent code compares to human code in these maintenance settings, presenting \\textbf{CodeThread}, a framework to construct controlled experiments from repository-level coding benchmarks. Applying CodeThread to four frontier coding agents and four benchmarks, we find that agents are less effective at resolving tasks when building on agent code compared to human code, with task resolve rate drops of up to 13.1%. Regression analysis reveals that many traditional software engineering maintainability metrics do not explain this difference. Instead, the clearest signals are subtler behavioral differences in agent code, such as changes to input validation and error handling, along with differences in downstream code size and task difficulty. These findings highlight the need to evaluate these systems not only by immediate task resolution but also by code maintainability, and point to potential sources of downstream errors introduced by agent code."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\npatel2026is,\ntitle={Is Agent Code Less Maintainable Than Human Code?},\nauthor={Shaswat P Patel and Betty Li Hou and Arun Purohit and Kai Xu and Jane Pan and He He and Valerie Chen},\nbooktitle={Second Workshop on Agents in the Wild: Safety, Security, and Beyond},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=2BeQnNnfps}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Is Agent Code Less Maintainable Than Human Code?"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/81d543495ff1314bc1459b834ed0beb96806d7c3.pdf","readers":["everyone"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD"},"paperhash":{"value":"patel|is_agent_code_less_maintainable_than_human_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shaswat_P_Patel1","~Betty_Li_Hou1","~Arun_Purohit1","~Kai_Xu28","~Jane_Pan1","~He_He2","~Valerie_Chen2"]},"track":{"value":"Regular Paper (9 pages)"},"authors":{"value":["Shaswat P Patel","Betty Li Hou","Arun Purohit","Kai Xu","Jane Pan","He He","Valerie Chen"]}},"tmdate":1783293466822,"pdate":1779518192432,"tcdate":1778218703886,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/Submission143/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/Submission143/Authors"],"forum":"2BeQnNnfps","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":143,"cdate":1778218703886,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/-/Submission_Release","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/Submission143/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1783293466822,"odate":1779518192432,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD","id":"2BeQnNnfps","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce \\textbf{CodeThread}, a framework for comparing the maintainability of agent and human code, and find that agents resolve tasks less effectively when building on agent code than on human code."},"venue":{"value":"SCALE@ICML2026 Poster"},"LLM_usage":{"value":"Both ideation and writing, the authors took help of LLMs/agents in refining the idea as well as for writing for the draft."},"keywords":{"value":["coding agents","large language models","maintainability","evaluation","software engineering"]},"Funding":{"value":"No, the presenting author of this submission does *not*  fall under ICML’s funding aims, or has sufficient alternate funding."},"abstract":{"value":"Maintainability is a core dimension of software engineering, shaping how code is written, reviewed, and developed over time. While coding agents have demonstrated strong performance on single-issue tasks, it remains unclear how maintainable their code is when future agents build on top of it, potentially leading to compounding downstream effects. We investigate how agent code compares to human code in these maintenance settings, presenting \\textbf{CodeThread}, a framework to construct controlled experiments from repository-level coding benchmarks. Applying CodeThread to four frontier coding agents and four benchmarks, we find that agents are less effective at resolving tasks when building on agent code compared to human code, with task resolve rate drops of up to 13.1\\%. Regression analysis reveals that many traditional software engineering maintainability metrics do not explain this difference. Instead, the clearest signals are subtler behavioral differences in agent code, such as changes to input validation and error handling, along with differences in downstream code size and task difficulty. These findings highlight the need to evaluate these systems not only by immediate task resolution but also by code maintainability, and point to potential sources of downstream errors introduced by agent code."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\npatel2026is,\ntitle={Is Agent Code Less Maintainable Than Human Code?},\nauthor={Shaswat P Patel and Betty Li Hou and Arun Purohit and Kai Xu and Jane Pan and He He and Valerie Chen},\nbooktitle={ICML 2026 Workshop on Multimodal AI Agents},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=muNtkMS4qQ}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Is Agent Code Less Maintainable Than Human Code?"},"Anonymization":{"value":"This submission has been anonymized for double-blind review via the removal of identifying information such as names, affiliations, and identifying URLs."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a7f81a154da31345019481856f147bf371d2648e.pdf"},"Format":{"value":"Yes, the presenting author will definitely attend in person because they attending ICML for other complementary reasons."},"Presenter":{"value":"~Betty_Li_Hou1"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/SCALE"},"paperhash":{"value":"patel|is_agent_code_less_maintainable_than_human_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shaswat_P_Patel1","~Betty_Li_Hou1","~Arun_Purohit1","~Kai_Xu28","~Jane_Pan1","~He_He2","~Valerie_Chen2"]},"Track":{"value":"Main track (up to 7 pages excluding references and appendix)"},"authors":{"value":["Shaswat P Patel","Betty Li Hou","Arun Purohit","Kai Xu","Jane Pan","He He","Valerie Chen"]}},"tmdate":1783264827778,"pdate":1778875850709,"tcdate":1777441506368,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/SCALE","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/SCALE/Submission81/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/SCALE/Submission81/Authors"],"forum":"muNtkMS4qQ","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":81,"cdate":1777441506368,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/SCALE/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/SCALE/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/SCALE/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/SCALE/Submission81/-/Camera-Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1783264827778,"odate":1783264827754,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/SCALE","id":"muNtkMS4qQ","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce \\textbf{CodeThread}, a framework for comparing the maintainability of agent and human code, and find that agents resolve tasks less effectively when building on agent code than on human code."},"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 Workshop DL4C Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/53f9c7b4814a6ebad968dcff5a78a3b6fe96754d.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["coding agents","large language models","maintainability","evaluation","software engineering"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C"},"paperhash":{"value":"patel|is_agent_code_less_maintainable_than_human_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shaswat_P_Patel1","~Betty_Li_Hou1","~Arun_Purohit1","~Kai_Xu28","~Jane_Pan1","~He_He2","~Valerie_Chen2"]},"abstract":{"value":"Maintainability is a core dimension of software engineering, shaping how code is written, reviewed, and developed over time. While coding agents have demonstrated strong performance on single-issue tasks, it remains unclear how maintainable their code is when future agents build on top of it, potentially leading to compounding downstream effects. We investigate how agent code compares to human code in these maintenance settings, presenting \\textbf{CodeThread}, a framework to construct controlled experiments from repository-level coding benchmarks. Applying CodeThread to four frontier coding agents and four benchmarks, we find that agents are less effective at resolving tasks when building on agent code compared to human code, with task resolve rate drops of up to 13.1%. Regression analysis reveals that many traditional software engineering maintainability metrics do not explain this difference. Instead, the clearest signals are subtler behavioral differences in agent code, such as changes to input validation and error handling, along with differences in downstream code size and task difficulty. These findings highlight the need to evaluate these systems not only by immediate task resolution but also by code maintainability, and point to potential sources of downstream errors introduced by agent code."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\npatel2026is,\ntitle={Is Agent Code Less Maintainable Than Human Code?},\nauthor={Shaswat P Patel and Betty Li Hou and Arun Purohit and Kai Xu and Jane Pan and He He and Valerie Chen},\nbooktitle={Deep Learning for Code: Towards Human-Centered Coding Agents},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=V4qjdZLmtk}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Is Agent Code Less Maintainable Than Human Code?"},"authors":{"value":["Shaswat P Patel","Betty Li Hou","Arun Purohit","Kai Xu","Jane Pan","He He","Valerie Chen"]}},"tmdate":1783060328437,"pdate":1781618625536,"tcdate":1778776739879,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/Submission55/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/Submission55/Authors"],"forum":"V4qjdZLmtk","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","number":55,"cdate":1778776739879,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C/Submission55/-/Revision"],"mdate":1783060328437,"odate":1781618625536,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/DL4C","id":"V4qjdZLmtk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"LECTURE NOTES IN COMPUTER SCIENCE"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"massinissa|extrinsic_calibration_framework_for_cameralidar_fusion_using_recurrent_residual_network"},"authorids":{"value":["~Aouragh_Massinissa1","~László_Gulyás1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Combining the use of 3D LiDAR’s and 2D cameras is getting increasingly popular in sensor suits for perception tasks, making them two important sets of sensors for 3D object detection. Fusing the data of these two sensors results in a highly descriptive environment. However, the combination into a single representation is not straightforward due to the difference in signal characteristics and distribution. Thus the robustness of such system is highly dependent on calibration. In case of most methods the image quality is also a predominant condition for performance. This paper proposes a calibration framework in PyTorch for both KITTI and nuScenes. CalibRRNet takes monocular images and 3D depth information as input and outputs a 6 DoF rigid body transformation. Using similar architecture to ResNet18, leveraging the advantage of jumping connection, we add a recurrent network on the last stage to keep track of the previously predicted transformations. Training CalibRRNet is against photometric consistency and point cloud distance. CalibRRNet solves the geometric problem and predicts the extrinsic calibration parameters. The application of the proposed framework is not limited to only pure calibration tasks. It can also be used as a preprocessing module for camera-lidar fusion models to alleviate the need for an accurate calibration to ensure performance. Our experiments results confirm the validity of the proposed approach, with primary improvements observed on translation, but also on rotation."},"title":{"value":"Extrinsic Calibration Framework for Camera-Lidar Fusion Using Recurrent Residual Network"},"authors":{"value":["Aouragh Massinissa","László Gulyás"]}},"tmdate":1774452668741,"pdate":1694556000000,"tcdate":1774452668741,"writers":["~Aouragh_Massinissa1","~László_Gulyás1"],"signatures":["~László_Gulyás1"],"forum":"o1PGeelrIE","license":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license","number":46341,"cdate":1774452668741,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1774452668741,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"o1PGeelrIE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"RO-MAN 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8951224/8956239/08956356.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/RO-MAN/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"ishikawa|dynamic_calibration_between_a_mobile_robot_and_slam_device_for_navigation"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Takeshi_Oishi1",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/RO-MAN46459.2019.8956356"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ro-man/IshikawaOI19,\n  author={Ryoichi Ishikawa and Takeshi Oishi and Katsushi Ikeuchi},\n  title={Dynamic Calibration between a Mobile Robot and SLAM Device for Navigation},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  pages={1-6},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/RO-MAN46459.2019.8956356},\n  booktitle={RO-MAN},\n  crossref={conf/ro-man/2019}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we propose a dynamic calibration between a mobile robot and a device using simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) technology, which we termed as the SLAM device, for a robot navigation system. The navigation framework assumes loose mounting of SLAM device for easy use and requires an online adjustment to remove localization errors. The online adjustment method dynamically corrects not only the calibration errors between the SLAM device and the part of the robot to which the device is attached but also the robot encoder errors by calibrating the whole body of the robot. The online adjustment assumes that the information of the external environment and shape information of the robot are consistent. In addition to the online adjustment, we also present an offline calibration between a robot and device. The offline calibration is motion-based and we clarify the most efficient method based on the number of degrees-of-freedom of the robot movement. Our method can be easily used for various types of robots with sufficiently precise localization for navigation. In the experiments, we confirm the parameters obtained via two types of offline calibration based on the degree of freedom of robot movement. We also validate the effectiveness of the online adjustment method by plotting localized position errors during a robots intense movement. Finally, we demonstrate the navigation using a SLAM device."},"title":{"value":"Dynamic Calibration between a Mobile Robot and SLAM Device for Navigation"},"authors":{"value":["Ryoichi Ishikawa","Takeshi Oishi","Katsushi Ikeuchi"]}},"tmdate":1772365207008,"pdate":1577750400000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/ro-man/IshikawaOI19"],"tcdate":1772365199961,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Takeshi_Oishi1"],"forum":"25kSdhfsMl","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":840953,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772365207008,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"25kSdhfsMl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"HPEC 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10938401/10938415/10938445.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/HPEC/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"reddy|quantum_computing_for_data_calibration_in_parallel_magnetic_resonance_imaging_reconstruction"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Girish_Babu_Reddy:","~Gulfam_Ahmed_Saju1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Liu_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuchou_Chang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/HPEC62836.2024.10938445"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/hpec/ReddySLC24,\n  author={Girish Babu Reddy and Gulfam Ahmed Saju and Yi Liu and Yuchou Chang},\n  title={Quantum Computing for Data Calibration in Parallel Magnetic Resonance Imaging Reconstruction},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={1-6},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/HPEC62836.2024.10938445},\n  booktitle={HPEC},\n  crossref={conf/hpec/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Parallel imaging techniques, such as GeneRalized Autocalibrating Partially Parallel Acquisitions (GRAPPA) play an important role in Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) by significantly reducing scan times and enhancing patient comfort without compromising image quality. GRAPPA's algorithmic framework involving calibration and synthesis stages is critical in reconstructing high-quality images. However, the computational load of the calibration stage, especially with large convolutional kernel sizes or an increased number of receiver coils, poses a significant bottleneck and limits its efficiency and applicability in clinical settings. In this paper, we introduce an approach by proposing a hybrid software architecture that integrates quantum computing into the GRAPPA reconstruction process. Our method exploits the computational capabilities of quantum computing to accelerate the calibration phase, thereby enabling real-time processing speeds. Through experimentation, we demonstrate that the quantum-enhanced approach can expedite the calibration process to around 10–20 milliseconds of Quantum Processing Unit (QPU) programming time for each Linear Time-Invariant (LTI) system solved. The method maintains the integrity of calibration outcomes, achieving results on par with conventional central processing unit (CPU)-based processes. The result represents the progress towards real-time MRI reconstruction by reducing clinical MRI workflows times, improving patient throughput, and potentially enabling new diagnostic capabilities. Solving LTI system with more attributes on D-Wave quantum computer will be studied to show advantages of QPU to CPU in the future work."},"title":{"value":"Quantum Computing for Data Calibration in Parallel Magnetic Resonance Imaging Reconstruction"},"authors":{"value":["Girish Babu Reddy","Gulfam Ahmed Saju","Yi Liu","Yuchou Chang"]}},"tmdate":1770924527958,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/hpec/ReddySLC24"],"tcdate":1770924526353,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Gulfam_Ahmed_Saju1"],"forum":"yjrtuJ2boE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":822765,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770924527958,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"yjrtuJ2boE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/22/22/8900/pdf?version=1668687197"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"eichler|spatiotemporal_calibration_of_multiple_kinect_cameras_using_3d_human_pose"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nadav_Eichler:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hagit_Hel-Or:","~Ilan_Shimshoni1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s22228900"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/EichlerHS22,\n  author={Nadav Eichler and Hagit Hel-Or and Ilan Shimshoni},\n  title={Spatio-Temporal Calibration of Multiple Kinect Cameras Using 3D Human Pose},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={22},\n  number={22},\n  pages={8900},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s22228900}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"RGB and depth cameras are extensively used for the 3D tracking of human pose and motion. Typically, these cameras calculate a set of 3D points representing the human body as a skeletal structure. The tracking capabilities of a single camera are often affected by noise and inaccuracies due to occluded body parts. Multiple-camera setups offer a solution to maximize coverage of the captured human body and to minimize occlusions. According to best practices, fusing information across multiple cameras typically requires spatio-temporal calibration. First, the cameras must synchronize their internal clocks. This is typically performed by physically connecting the cameras to each other using an external device or cable. Second, the pose of each camera relative to the other cameras must be calculated (Extrinsic Calibration). The state-of-the-art methods use specialized calibration session and devices such as a checkerboard to perform calibration. In this paper, we introduce an approach to the spatio-temporal calibration of multiple cameras which is designed to run on-the-fly without specialized devices or equipment requiring only the motion of the human body in the scene. As an example, the system is implemented and evaluated using Microsoft Azure Kinect. The study shows that the accuracy and robustness of this approach is on par with the state-of-the-art practices."},"title":{"value":"Spatio-Temporal Calibration of Multiple Kinect Cameras Using 3D Human Pose"},"authors":{"value":["Nadav Eichler","Hagit Hel-Or","Ilan Shimshoni"]}},"tmdate":1733194052043,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1733193979032,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ilan_Shimshoni3"],"forum":"rBqhftDYb1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":252201,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1733194052043,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rBqhftDYb1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.08727v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"gui|calibrationcompatible_listwise_distillation_of_privileged_features_for_ctr_prediction"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoqiang_Gui:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yueyao_Cheng:","~Xiang-Rong_Sheng1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yunfeng_Zhao:","~Guoxian_Yu1","~Shuguang_Han1","~Yuning_Jiang4","~Jian_Xu1","~Bo_Zheng5"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2312.08727"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2312-08727,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Xiaoqiang Gui and Yueyao Cheng and Xiang-Rong Sheng and Yunfeng Zhao and Guoxian Yu and Shuguang Han and Yuning Jiang and Jian Xu and Bo Zheng},\n  title={Calibration-compatible Listwise Distillation of Privileged Features for CTR Prediction},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2312.08727},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2312.08727}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In machine learning systems, privileged features refer to the features that are available during offline training but inaccessible for online serving. Previous studies have recognized the importance of privileged features and explored ways to tackle online-offline discrepancies. A typical practice is privileged features distillation (PFD): train a teacher model using all features (including privileged ones) and then distill the knowledge from the teacher model using a student model (excluding the privileged features), which is then employed for online serving. In practice, the pointwise cross-entropy loss is often adopted for PFD. However, this loss is insufficient to distill the ranking ability for CTR prediction. First, it does not consider the non-i.i.d. characteristic of the data distribution, i.e., other items on the same page significantly impact the click probability of the candidate item. Second, it fails to consider the relative item order ranked by the teacher model's predictions, which is essential to distill the ranking ability. To address these issues, we first extend the pointwise-based PFD to the listwise-based PFD. We then define the calibration-compatible property of distillation loss and show that commonly used listwise losses do not satisfy this property when employed as distillation loss, thus compromising the model's calibration ability, which is another important measure for CTR prediction. To tackle this dilemma, we propose Calibration-compatible LIstwise Distillation (CLID), which employs carefully-designed listwise distillation loss to achieve better ranking ability than the pointwise-based PFD while preserving the model's calibration ability. We theoretically prove it is calibration-compatible. Extensive experiments on public datasets and a production dataset collected from the display advertising system of Alibaba further demonstrate the effectiveness of CLID."},"title":{"value":"Calibration-compatible Listwise Distillation of Privileged Features for CTR Prediction"},"authors":{"value":["Xiaoqiang Gui","Yueyao Cheng","Xiang-Rong Sheng","Yunfeng Zhao","Guoxian Yu","Shuguang Han","Yuning Jiang","Jian Xu","Bo Zheng"]}},"tmdate":1768973855901,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727490433589,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Guoxian_Yu1"],"forum":"bgTJ8VEhQL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":97822,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768973855901,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bgTJ8VEhQL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"WSDM 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/WSDM/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"gui|calibrationcompatible_listwise_distillation_of_privileged_features_for_ctr_prediction"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoqiang_Gui:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yueyao_Cheng:","~Xiang-Rong_Sheng1","~Yunfeng_Zhao3","~Guoxian_Yu1","~Shuguang_Han1","~Yuning_Jiang4","~Jian_Xu1","~Bo_Zheng5"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3616855.3635810"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/wsdm/GuiCSZYHJXZ24,\n  author={Xiaoqiang Gui and Yueyao Cheng and Xiang-Rong Sheng and Yunfeng Zhao and Guoxian Yu and Shuguang Han and Yuning Jiang and Jian Xu and Bo Zheng},\n  title={Calibration-compatible Listwise Distillation of Privileged Features for CTR Prediction},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={247-256},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3616855.3635810},\n  booktitle={WSDM},\n  crossref={conf/wsdm/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In machine learning systems, privileged features refer to the features that are available during offline training but inaccessible for online serving. Previous studies have recognized the importance of privileged features and explored ways to tackle online-offline discrepancies. A typical practice is privileged features distillation (PFD): train a teacher model using all features (including privileged ones) and then distill the knowledge from the teacher model using a student model (excluding the privileged features), which is then employed for online serving. In practice, the pointwise cross-entropy loss is often adopted for PFD. However, this loss is insufficient to distill the ranking ability for CTR prediction. First, it does not consider the non-i.i.d. characteristic of the data distribution, i.e., other items on the same page significantly impact the click probability of the candidate item. Second, it fails to consider the relative item order ranked by the teacher model's predictions, which is essential to distill the ranking ability. To address these issues, we first extend the pointwise-based PFD to the listwise-based PFD. We then define the calibration-compatible property of distillation loss and show that commonly used listwise losses do not satisfy this property when employed as distillation loss, thus compromising the model's calibration ability, which is another important measure for CTR prediction. To tackle this dilemma, we propose Calibration-compatible LIstwise Distillation (CLID), which employs carefully-designed listwise distillation loss to achieve better ranking ability than the pointwise-based PFD while preserving the model's calibration ability. We theoretically prove it is calibration-compatible. Extensive experiments on public datasets and a production dataset collected from the display advertising system of Alibaba further demonstrate the effectiveness of CLID."},"title":{"value":"Calibration-compatible Listwise Distillation of Privileged Features for CTR Prediction"},"authors":{"value":["Xiaoqiang Gui","Yueyao Cheng","Xiang-Rong Sheng","Yunfeng Zhao","Guoxian Yu","Shuguang Han","Yuning Jiang","Jian Xu","Bo Zheng"]}},"tmdate":1768558296769,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1722991513939,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunfeng_Zhao3"],"forum":"GX10tFWmj1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":58147,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768558296769,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"GX10tFWmj1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"KDD 2023"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/KDD/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"wu|towards_reliable_rare_category_analysis_on_graphs_via_individual_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Longfeng_Wu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bowen_Lei:","~Dongkuan_Xu2","~Dawei_Zhou1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3580305.3599525"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/kdd/WuLX023,\n  author={Longfeng Wu and Bowen Lei and Dongkuan Xu and Dawei Zhou},\n  title={Towards Reliable Rare Category Analysis on Graphs via Individual Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={2629-2638},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3580305.3599525},\n  booktitle={KDD},\n  crossref={conf/kdd/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Rare categories abound in a number of real-world networks and play a pivotal role in a variety of high-stakes applications, including financial fraud detection, network intrusion detection, and rare disease diagnosis. Rare category analysis (RCA) refers to the task of detecting, characterizing, and comprehending the behaviors of minority classes in a highly-imbalanced data distribution. While the vast majority of existing work on RCA has focused on improving the prediction performance, a few fundamental research questions heretofore have received little attention and are less explored: How confident or uncertain is a prediction model in rare category analysis? How can we quantify the uncertainty in the learning process and enable reliable rare category analysis? To answer these questions, we start by investigating miscalibration in existing RCA methods. Empirical results reveal that state-of-the-art RCA methods are mainly over-confident in predicting minority classes and under-confident in predicting majority classes. Motivated by the observation, we propose a novel individual calibration framework, named CALIRARE, for alleviating the unique challenges of RCA, thus enabling reliable rare category analysis. In particular, to quantify the uncertainties in RCA, we develop a node-level uncertainty quantification algorithm to model the overlapping support regions with high uncertainty; to handle the rarity of minority classes in miscalibration calculation, we generalize the distribution-based calibration metric to the instance level and propose the first individual calibration measurement on graphs named Expected Individual Calibration Error (EICE). We perform extensive experimental evaluations on real-world datasets, including rare category characterization and model calibration tasks, which demonstrate the significance of our proposed framework."},"title":{"value":"Towards Reliable Rare Category Analysis on Graphs via Individual Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Longfeng Wu","Bowen Lei","Dongkuan Xu","Dawei Zhou"]}},"tmdate":1738814130801,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1713482573937,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dongkuan_Xu2"],"forum":"YEvOzsJFTG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":1847,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1738814130801,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"YEvOzsJFTG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.09858v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"wu|towards_reliable_rare_category_analysis_on_graphs_via_individual_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Longfeng_Wu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bowen_Lei:","~Dongkuan_Xu2","~Dawei_Zhou1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.09858"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2307-09858,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Longfeng Wu and Bowen Lei and Dongkuan Xu and Dawei Zhou},\n  title={Towards Reliable Rare Category Analysis on Graphs via Individual Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2307.09858},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.09858}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Rare categories abound in a number of real-world networks and play a pivotal role in a variety of high-stakes applications, including financial fraud detection, network intrusion detection, and rare disease diagnosis. Rare category analysis (RCA) refers to the task of detecting, characterizing, and comprehending the behaviors of minority classes in a highly-imbalanced data distribution. While the vast majority of existing work on RCA has focused on improving the prediction performance, a few fundamental research questions heretofore have received little attention and are less explored: How confident or uncertain is a prediction model in rare category analysis? How can we quantify the uncertainty in the learning process and enable reliable rare category analysis? To answer these questions, we start by investigating miscalibration in existing RCA methods. Empirical results reveal that state-of-the-art RCA methods are mainly over-confident in predicting minority classes and under-confident in predicting majority classes. Motivated by the observation, we propose a novel individual calibration framework, named CALIRARE, for alleviating the unique challenges of RCA, thus enabling reliable rare category analysis. In particular, to quantify the uncertainties in RCA, we develop a node-level uncertainty quantification algorithm to model the overlapping support regions with high uncertainty; to handle the rarity of minority classes in miscalibration calculation, we generalize the distribution-based calibration metric to the instance level and propose the first individual calibration measurement on graphs named Expected Individual Calibration Error (EICE). We perform extensive experimental evaluations on real-world datasets, including rare category characterization and model calibration tasks, which demonstrate the significance of our proposed framework."},"title":{"value":"Towards Reliable Rare Category Analysis on Graphs via Individual Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Longfeng Wu","Bowen Lei","Dongkuan Xu","Dawei Zhou"]}},"tmdate":1738814130800,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1713482574006,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dongkuan_Xu2"],"forum":"WBvHdOvxeb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":1863,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1738814130800,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"WBvHdOvxeb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.07072v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"rai|label_calibration_in_source_free_domain_adaptation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shivangi_Rai1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rini_Smita_Thakur:","~Kunal_Jangid1","~Vinod_K._Kurmi1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.07072"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-07072,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shivangi Rai and Rini Smita Thakur and Kunal Jangid and Vinod K. Kurmi},\n  title={Label Calibration in Source Free Domain Adaptation},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2501.07072},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.07072}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Source-free domain adaptation (SFDA) utilizes a pre-trained source model with unlabeled target data. Self-supervised SFDA techniques generate pseudolabels from the pre-trained source model, but these pseudolabels often contain noise due to domain discrepancies between the source and target domains. Traditional self-supervised SFDA techniques rely on deterministic model predictions using the softmax function, leading to unreliable pseudolabels. In this work, we propose to introduce predictive uncertainty and softmax calibration for pseudolabel refinement using evidential deep learning. The Dirichlet prior is placed over the output of the target network to capture uncertainty using evidence with a single forward pass. Furthermore, softmax calibration solves the translation invariance problem to assist in learning with noisy labels. We incorporate a combination of evidential deep learning loss and information maximization loss with calibrated softmax in both prior and non-prior target knowledge SFDA settings. Extensive experimental analysis shows that our method outperforms other state-of-the-art methods on benchmark datasets."},"title":{"value":"Label Calibration in Source Free Domain Adaptation"},"authors":{"value":["Shivangi Rai","Rini Smita Thakur","Kunal Jangid","Vinod K. Kurmi"]}},"tmdate":1791955349260,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2501-07072"],"tcdate":1762245713604,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kunal_Jangid1"],"forum":"mRyqeE8GQY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":651532,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1791955349260,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"mRyqeE8GQY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/35485/37640"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AAAI/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|noisy_label_calibration_for_multiview_classification"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shilin_Xu2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuan_Sun_0016:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xingfeng_Li_0004:","~Siyuan_Duan1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhenwen_Ren:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zheng_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dezhong_Peng:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i20.35485"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aaai/Xu00DRLP25,\n  author={Shilin Xu and Yuan Sun and Xingfeng Li and Siyuan Duan and Zhenwen Ren and Zheng Liu and Dezhong Peng},\n  title={Noisy Label Calibration for Multi-View Classification},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={21797-21805},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i20.35485},\n  booktitle={AAAI},\n  crossref={conf/aaai/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In recent years, multi-view learning has aroused extensive research passion. Most existing multi-view learning methods often rely on well-annotations to improve decision accuracy. However, noise labels are ubiquitous in multi-view data due to imperfect annotations. To deal with this problem, we propose a novel noisy label calibration method (NLC) for multi-view classification to resist the negative impact of noisy labels. Specifically, to capture consensus information from multiple views, we employ max-margin rank loss to reduce the heterogeneous gap. Subsequently, we evaluate the confidence scores to enrich predictions associated with noise instances according to all reliable neighbors. Further, we propose Label Noise Detection (LND) to separate multi-view data into a clean or noisy subset, and propose Label Calibration Learning (LCL) to correct noisy instances. Finally, we adopt the cross-entropy loss to achieve multi-view classification. Extensive experiments on six datasets validate that our method outperforms eight state-of-the-art methods."},"title":{"value":"Noisy Label Calibration for Multi-View Classification"},"authors":{"value":["Shilin Xu","Yuan Sun","Xingfeng Li","Siyuan Duan","Zhenwen Ren","Zheng Liu","Dezhong Peng"]}},"tmdate":1784541839863,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1753542621902,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shilin_Xu2"],"forum":"HXQPYAz8lC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":593846,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784541839863,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"HXQPYAz8lC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICCVW 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11373940/11374285/11374346.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICCV/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"nakano|optic_illusion_patterns_for_accurate_camera_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Gaku_Nakano1",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCVW69036.2025.00022"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iccv/NakanoK25,\n  author={Gaku Nakano and Takenobu Kiyama},\n  title={Optic Illusion Patterns for Accurate Camera Calibration},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={149-158},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCVW69036.2025.00022},\n  booktitle={ICCVW},\n  crossref={conf/iccv/2025w}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes a camera calibration pattern that leverages optical illusions. An optical illusion pattern is a texture that appears geometrically distorted but looks flat when viewed from the camera's perspective. The proposed method initially displays a conventional checkerboard pattern on a monitor to estimate the homography matrix between the pattern and the camera, along with lens distortion. Subsequently, the inverse homography transformation is applied to the pattern image to display an optical illusion pattern, and corner points on the illusion pattern are detected. As the captured image of the illusion pattern appears front-facing, the localization accuracy of the corner points is enhanced. The proposed method addresses the “chicken-and-egg” problem of needing to tilt the pattern for stable vanishing point estimation while equalizing localization accuracy of corner points at different depths. Simulations and real image experiments report that the proposed method outperforms conventional calibration methods for both narrow-angle and wide-angle lenses."},"title":{"value":"Optic Illusion Patterns for Accurate Camera Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Gaku Nakano","Takenobu Kiyama"]}},"tmdate":1773752937505,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iccv/NakanoK25"],"tcdate":1773752931646,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Gaku_Nakano1"],"forum":"J7aI163MwF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":853153,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1773752937505,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"J7aI163MwF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 October Submission"},"research_area_keywords":{"value":"tool use, environment interaction"},"keywords":{"value":["Terminal Agent","Synthetic Data"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026calibforge,\ntitle={CalibForge: Adversarial Solver Calibration for Scaling Learnable Terminal Tasks},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - October 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=fjo78nuHE6},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CalibForge: Adversarial Solver Calibration for Scaling Learnable Terminal Tasks"},"languages_studied":{"value":["eng"]},"contribution_types":{"value":"NLP engineering experiment"},"abstract":{"value":"Training terminal agents requires executable and verifiable tasks that are not merely solvable, but appropriately challenging for learning. Executable validation establishes feasibility, yet does not reveal how a task behaves relative to a given solver setting. In this paper, we present CalibForge, an autonomous terminal-task synthesis system that uses verified solver behavior to revise candidate tasks through adversarial solver calibration. Multi-solver calibration targets disagreement within a heterogeneous solver pool, whereas contrastive solver calibration targets a designated strong-pass/weak-fail relation; both operationalize a solver-relative learnable zone anchored in demonstrated solvability. Using CalibForge, we construct 5,431 calibrated terminal tasks. Our ablations show that both strategies yield more effective supervision than authoring and validation alone or ordinary single-solver feedback. Models trained on the full collection achieve 31.84% and 47.57% on Terminal-Bench 2.1. The largest improvements over the corresponding base model reach 23.97 percentage points on Terminal-Bench 2.1, 26.54 points on SWE-bench Pro, and 30.04 points on Doc2Repo. Together, these results support solver-relative learnability as a practical target for constructing effective and transferable agent training data."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/906c40d4573ccc27e9bebd53c2bf0b85c79e5daa.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"LLM agents for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission"}},"tmdate":1793759096080,"tcdate":1791696138417,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission3553/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission3553/Authors"],"forum":"fjo78nuHE6","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":3553,"cdate":1791696138417,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Responsible_NLP_Checklist_Status","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1793759096080,"odate":1791894541772,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","id":"fjo78nuHE6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2025 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Multimodal data","Modality imbalance","modality valuation","Sample Calibration","Encoder Calibration"]},"primary_area":{"value":"deep_learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Multimodal Intention Recognition (MIR) plays a critical role in applications such as intelligent assistants, service robots, and autonomous systems. However, in real-world settings, different modalities often vary significantly in informativeness, reliability, and noise levels. This leads to modality imbalance, where models tend to over-rely on dominant modalities, thereby limiting generalization and robustness. While existing methods attempt to alleviate this issue at either the sample or model level, most overlook its multi-level nature. To address this, we propose Adaptive Re-calibration Learning (ARL), a novel dual-path framework that models modality importance from both sample-wise and structural perspectives. ARL incorporates two key mechanisms: Contribution-Inverse Sample Calibration (CISC), which dynamically masks overly dominant modalities at the sample level to encourage attention to underutilized ones; and Weighted Encoder Calibration (WEC), which adjusts encoder weights based on global modality contributions to prevent overfitting. Experimental results on multiple MIR benchmarks demonstrate that ARL significantly outperforms existing methods in both accuracy and robustness, particularly under noisy or modality-degraded conditions."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nyang2025adaptive,\ntitle={Adaptive Re-calibration Learning for Balanced Multimodal Intention Recognition},\nauthor={Qu Yang and Xiyang Li and Fu Lin and Mang Ye},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=k71nsscO9b}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Adaptive Re-calibration Learning for Balanced Multimodal Intention Recognition"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/915fe2d60c5433861623d4de60039dbf065c564c.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|adaptive_recalibration_learning_for_balanced_multimodal_intention_recognition"},"authorids":{"value":["~Qu_Yang2","~Xiyang_Li1","~Fu_Lin2","~Mang_Ye1"]},"authors":{"value":["Qu Yang","Xiyang Li","Fu Lin","Mang Ye"]}},"tmdate":1783627324144,"pdate":1758217263253,"tcdate":1746950809531,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission20109/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission20109/Authors"],"forum":"k71nsscO9b","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":20109,"cdate":1746950809531,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission20109/-/Full_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission20109/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1783627324144,"odate":1761704990540,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference","id":"k71nsscO9b","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.18764v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|task_calibration_calibrating_large_language_models_on_inference_tasks"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.18764"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2410-18764,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yingjie Li and Yun Luo and Xiaotian Xie and Yue Zhang},\n  title={Task Calibration: Calibrating Large Language Models on Inference Tasks},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2410.18764},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.18764}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) have exhibited impressive zero-shot performance on inference tasks. However, LLMs may suffer from spurious correlations between input texts and output labels, which limits LLMs' ability to reason based purely on general language understanding. In other words, LLMs may make predictions primarily based on premise or hypothesis, rather than both components. To address this problem that may lead to unexpected performance degradation, we propose task calibration (TC), a zero-shot and inference-only calibration method inspired by mutual information which recovers LLM performance through task reformulation. TC encourages LLMs to reason based on both premise and hypothesis, while mitigating the models' over-reliance on individual premise or hypothesis for inference. Experimental results show that TC achieves a substantial improvement on 13 inference tasks in the zero-shot setup. We further validate the effectiveness of TC in few-shot setups and various natural language understanding tasks. Further analysis indicates that TC is also robust to prompt templates and has the potential to be integrated with other calibration methods."},"title":{"value":"Task Calibration: Calibrating Large Language Models on Inference Tasks"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yingjie Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Yun Luo","username":"~Yun_Luo1"},{"fullname":"Xiaotian Xie","username":""},{"fullname":"Yue Zhang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1780123313950,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2410-18764"],"tcdate":1780123310529,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yun_Luo1"],"forum":"93sKaS5sPH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":39045,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1780123313950,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"93sKaS5sPH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISMAR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10316350/10316351/10316458.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISMAR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"dong|adaptive_color_structured_light_for_calibration_and_shape_reconstruction"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Dong:","~Haibin_Ling1","~Bingyao_Huang1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ISMAR59233.2023.00141"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ismar/DongLH23,\n  author={Xin Dong and Haibin Ling and Bingyao Huang},\n  title={Adaptive Color Structured Light for Calibration and Shape Reconstruction},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1240-1249},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ISMAR59233.2023.00141},\n  booktitle={ISMAR},\n  crossref={conf/ismar/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Color structured light (SL) plays an important role in spatial augmented reality and shape reconstruction. Compared to traditional non-color multi-shot SL, it has the advantage of fewer projections, and can even achieve single-shot. However, distortions caused by ambient light and imaging devices limit color SL’s applicability and accuracy. A common solution is to apply color adaptation techniques to cancel the disturbances. Previous studies focus on either robust fixed color patterns or adaptation approaches that may require preliminary geometric calibrations. In this paper, we propose an approach that can efficiently adapt color SL to arbitrary ambient light and imaging devices’ color responses, without device response function calibration or geometric calibration. First, we design a novel algorithm to quickly find the most distinct colors that are easily separable under a new environment and device setup. Then, we design a maximum a posteriori (MAP)-based color detection algorithm that can utilize ambient light and device priors to robustly detect the SL colors. In experiments, our adaptive color SL outperforms previous methods in both calibration and shape reconstruction tasks across a variety of setups."},"title":{"value":"Adaptive Color Structured Light for Calibration and Shape Reconstruction"},"authors":{"value":["Xin Dong","Haibin Ling","Bingyao Huang"]}},"tmdate":1753008830805,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1744184461789,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bingyao_Huang2"],"forum":"yHBom6rl9I","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":385802,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1753008830805,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"yHBom6rl9I","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2025"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["large language model","zero-shot learning","model calibration","natural language inference"]},"primary_area":{"value":"applications to computer vision, audio, language, and other modalities"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) have exhibited impressive zero-shot performance on inference tasks. However, LLMs may suffer from spurious correlations between input texts and output labels, which limits LLMs' ability to reason based purely on general language understanding. In other words, LLMs may make predictions primarily based on premise or hypothesis, rather than both components. To address this problem that may lead to unexpected performance degradation, we propose task calibration (TC), a zero-shot and inference-only calibration method inspired by mutual information which recovers LLM performance through task reformulation. TC encourages LLMs to reason based on both premise and hypothesis, while mitigating the models' over-reliance on individual premise or hypothesis for inference. Experimental results show that TC achieves a substantial improvement on 13 inference tasks in the zero-shot setup. We further validate the effectiveness of TC in few-shot setups and various natural language understanding tasks. Further analysis indicates that TC is also robust to prompt templates and has the potential to be integrated with other calibration methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nli2025task,\ntitle={Task Calibration: Calibrating Large Language Models on Inference Tasks},\nauthor={Yingjie Li and Yun Luo and Xiaotian Xie and Yue Zhang},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=8LZ1D1yqeg}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Task Calibration: Calibrating Large Language Models on Inference Tasks"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/61d83d7207a397c9c86f103376ee3c25dfd7f643.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|task_calibration_calibrating_large_language_models_on_inference_tasks"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yingjie_Li2","~Yun_Luo1","~Xiaotian_Xie1","~Yue_Zhang7"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Yingjie Li","Yun Luo","Xiaotian Xie","Yue Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1738735803284,"tcdate":1727433964271,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission9946/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission9946/Authors"],"forum":"8LZ1D1yqeg","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":9946,"cdate":1727433964271,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission9946/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission9946/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738735803284,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"8LZ1D1yqeg","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2010"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5501116/5509124/05509331.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2010"},"paperhash":{"value":"malti|robust_handeye_calibration_for_computer_aided_medical_endoscopy"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abed_Malti:","~João_Pedro_Barreto1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2010.5509331"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/MaltiB10,\n  author={Abed Malti and João Pedro Barreto},\n  title={Robust hand-eye calibration for computer aided medical endoscopy},\n  year={2010},\n  cdate={1262304000000},\n  pages={5543-5549},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2010.5509331},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2010}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Endoscopic camera for surgical navigation and 3D visualization requires precise and stable estimates of the calibration parameters. The estimation of the hand-eye transform between the camera frame and the opto-tracked body of the endoscope is an important issue of the calibration. This paper presents a new stable method for the hand-eye calibration problem. The most popular method estimates the transform directly in the special euclidean group SE(3) by computing separately the rotation and the translation. The second famous approach formulates the problem in the dual quaternion space and estimates jointly the rotation and the translation. In a first glance, the simultaneous estimation seems to be always advantageous. However, and according to the experiments, this is not the case for the rotation estimation that is affected by the noise in translation. Our approach takes advantage of the both methods and uses the dual quaternion to estimate separately the rotation and the translation. We show experimentally that our algorithm is more stable with minimal number and small amplitude of motions."},"title":{"value":"Robust hand-eye calibration for computer aided medical endoscopy"},"authors":{"value":["Abed Malti","João Pedro Barreto"]}},"tmdate":1729846816619,"pdate":1262304000000,"tcdate":1729846787017,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Joao_Barreto1"],"forum":"D5Nq1oi1XN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":160469,"cdate":1262304000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1729846816619,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"D5Nq1oi1XN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2010"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5501116/5509124/05509331.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2010"},"paperhash":{"value":"malti|robust_handeye_calibration_for_computer_aided_medical_endoscopy"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abed_Malti:","~João_Pedro_Barreto1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2010.5509331"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/MaltiB10,\n  author={Abed Malti and João Pedro Barreto},\n  title={Robust hand-eye calibration for computer aided medical endoscopy},\n  year={2010},\n  cdate={1262304000000},\n  pages={5543-5549},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2010.5509331},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2010}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Endoscopic camera for surgical navigation and 3D visualization requires precise and stable estimates of the calibration parameters. The estimation of the hand-eye transform between the camera frame and the opto-tracked body of the endoscope is an important issue of the calibration. This paper presents a new stable method for the hand-eye calibration problem. The most popular method estimates the transform directly in the special euclidean group SE(3) by computing separately the rotation and the translation. The second famous approach formulates the problem in the dual quaternion space and estimates jointly the rotation and the translation. In a first glance, the simultaneous estimation seems to be always advantageous. However, and according to the experiments, this is not the case for the rotation estimation that is affected by the noise in translation. Our approach takes advantage of the both methods and uses the dual quaternion to estimate separately the rotation and the translation. We show experimentally that our algorithm is more stable with minimal number and small amplitude of motions."},"title":{"value":"Robust hand-eye calibration for computer aided medical endoscopy"},"authors":{"value":["Abed Malti","João Pedro Barreto"]}},"tmdate":1729846789448,"pdate":1262304000000,"tcdate":1729846781168,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Joao_Barreto1"],"forum":"l9tcUxfknd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":160424,"cdate":1262304000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1729846789448,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"l9tcUxfknd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2010"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5501116/5509124/05509331.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2010"},"paperhash":{"value":"malti|robust_handeye_calibration_for_computer_aided_medical_endoscopy"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abed_Malti:","~João_Pedro_Barreto1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2010.5509331"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/MaltiB10,\n  author={Abed Malti and João Pedro Barreto},\n  title={Robust hand-eye calibration for computer aided medical endoscopy},\n  year={2010},\n  cdate={1262304000000},\n  pages={5543-5549},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2010.5509331},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2010}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Endoscopic camera for surgical navigation and 3D visualization requires precise and stable estimates of the calibration parameters. The estimation of the hand-eye transform between the camera frame and the opto-tracked body of the endoscope is an important issue of the calibration. This paper presents a new stable method for the hand-eye calibration problem. The most popular method estimates the transform directly in the special euclidean group SE(3) by computing separately the rotation and the translation. The second famous approach formulates the problem in the dual quaternion space and estimates jointly the rotation and the translation. In a first glance, the simultaneous estimation seems to be always advantageous. However, and according to the experiments, this is not the case for the rotation estimation that is affected by the noise in translation. Our approach takes advantage of the both methods and uses the dual quaternion to estimate separately the rotation and the translation. We show experimentally that our algorithm is more stable with minimal number and small amplitude of motions."},"title":{"value":"Robust hand-eye calibration for computer aided medical endoscopy"},"authors":{"value":["Abed Malti","João Pedro Barreto"]}},"tmdate":1729846781953,"pdate":1262304000000,"tcdate":1729846770156,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Joao_Barreto1"],"forum":"UJhfRi0gfD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":160408,"cdate":1262304000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1729846781953,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UJhfRi0gfD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"undefined 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://drum.lib.umd.edu/bitstreams/d2e152d3-57a4-4ef7-a45d-cc8ed0cc5ca4/download"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/phd/BASESEARCH/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|reliable_machine_learning_robustness_calibration_and_reproducibility"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chihuang_Liu1"]},"html":{"value":"http://hdl.handle.net/1903/28694"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@phdthesis{DBLP:phd/basesearch/Liu21g,\n  author={Chihuang Liu},\n  title={Reliable Machine Learning: robustness, calibration, and reproducibility},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  url={http://hdl.handle.net/1903/28694},\n  school={University of Maryland, College Park, MD, USA}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Modern machine learning (ML) algorithms are being applied today to a rapidly increasing number of tasks that affect the lives and well-being of people across the globe. Despite all the successes of artificial intelligence (AI), these methods are not always reliable and in fact are often quite brittle. It has been shown that a wide range of recent ML algorithms are vulnerable to adversarial attacks, and are over-confident even when they are not accurate. In this dissertation, we focus on the overall goal of making machine learning algorithms more reliable in terms of adversarial robustness, confidence calibration, and reproducibility. In the first part of the thesis, we explore novel approaches to improve the adversarial robustness of a deep neural network. We present a method that involves feature regularization and attention-based feature prioritization to motivate the model to only learn and rely on robust features that are not manipulated by the adversarial perturbation. We show that the resulting model is significantly more robust than other existing methods. In the second part of the thesis, we discover that the current training scheme of using one-hot labels under cross-entropy loss is a major cause of the over-confident behavior of deep neural networks. We propose a generalized definition of confidence calibration that requires the entire output to be calibrated. This approach leads to a novel form of the smooth labeling algorithm, called \\textit{class-similarity based label smoothing}, which tries to approximate a distribution that is optimal for generalized confidence calibration. We show that a model trained with the proposed smooth labels is significantly better calibrated than all existing methods. In the third part of the thesis, we propose an approach that can improve the calibration performance of robust models. We first learn a representation space using prototypical learning which bases its classification on the distances between the representation of a sample and the representations of each class prototype. We then use the distance information to train a confidence prediction network to encourage the model to make calibrated predictions. We demonstrate through extensive experiments that our method can improve the calibration performance of a model while maintaining comparable accuracy and adversarial robustness levels. In the fourth part of the thesis, we tackle the problem of determining reproducible, large-scale functional patterns for the whole brain from a group of fMRI subjects. Because of the non-linear nature of the signals and significant inter-subject variability, how to reliably extract patterns that are reproducible across subjects has been a challenging task. We propose a group-level model, called LEICA, that uses Laplacian eigenmaps as the main data reduction step to preserve the correlation information in the original data as best as possible in a certain rigorous sense. The nonlinear map is robust relative to noise in the data and inter-subject variability. We show that LEICA detects functionally cohesive maps that are much more reproducible than the state-of-the-art methods."},"title":{"value":"Reliable Machine Learning: robustness, calibration, and reproducibility"},"authors":{"value":["Chihuang Liu"]}},"tmdate":1738817800846,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1738817791791,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chihuang_Liu1"],"forum":"UzaD623ts6","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":300789,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738817800846,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UzaD623ts6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICIAR (1) 2012"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-31295-3_37.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICIAR/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"almeida|3d2d_laser_range_finder_calibration_using_a_conic_based_geometry_shape"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Miguel_Almeida:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Paulo_Dias:","~Miguel_Oliveira2","~Vítor_M._F._Santos2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-31295-3_37"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iciar/AlmeidaDOS12,\n  author={Miguel Almeida and Paulo Dias and Miguel Oliveira and Vítor M. F. Santos},\n  title={3D-2D Laser Range Finder Calibration Using a Conic Based Geometry Shape},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  pages={312-319},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-31295-3_37},\n  booktitle={ICIAR (1)},\n  crossref={conf/iciar/2012-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The AtlasCar is a prototype that is being developed at the University of Aveiro to research advanced driver assistance systems. The car is equipped with several sensors: 3D and 2D laser scanners, a stereo camera, inertial sensors and GPS. The combination of all these sensor data in useful representations is essential. Therefore, calibration is one of the first problems to tackle. This paper focuses on 3D/2D laser calibration. The proposed method uses a 3D Laser Range Finder (LRF) to produce a reference 3D point cloud containing a known calibration object. Manual input from the user and knowledge of the object geometry are used to register the 3D point cloud with the 2D Lasers. Experimental results with simulated and real data demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed calibration method."},"title":{"value":"3D-2D Laser Range Finder Calibration Using a Conic Based Geometry Shape"},"authors":{"value":["Miguel Almeida","Paulo Dias","Miguel Oliveira","Vítor M. F. Santos"]}},"tmdate":1753454281267,"pdate":1325376000000,"tcdate":1753450773838,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Miguel_Riem_Oliveira1"],"forum":"ldoVXcdkbZ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":588878,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1753454281267,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ldoVXcdkbZ","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Controlled rotated-cache comparisons reveal a calibration-timing effect on Llama and severe model-dependent four-bit failures."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["KV cache","quantization","calibration","language models","reproducibility"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/0da5a4ba79fbeeb9a791db0900ab55651c304daf.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"infrastructure, software libraries, hardware, systems, etc."},"abstract":{"value":"Does a rotated uniform KV-cache quantizer require locally fitted scales? A comparison can conflate scale granularity with calibration timing and metadata cost. We audit these factors with non-overlapping finite windows, a dense non-evicted reference, and one-write quantize–dequantize simulation. Following development experiments, a frozen 80-condition comparison crosses model, dataset, rotation seed, calibration timing, granularity, and symmetry, with eight-bit controls. On Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, four-bit per-write global calibration yields post-prefill perplexities of 7.508–7.557 on WikiText-2 test (reference 7.404), whereas prefill-frozen global configurations yield 125.414–39,255.244. The timing effect persists on SQuAD contexts and for two new rotations. Grouped per-write scales reduce error further but incur more metadata, preventing an equal-total-budget claim. Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct behaves differently: all tested four-bit configurations fail severely across both datasets and rotations, while eight-bit controls remain close to their dense references. Unquantized cache and rotation-roundtrip controls also remain close on a diagnostic window. These results support calibration- and model-specific conclusions rather than universal necessity of local scales. They do not establish packed-kernel speed, cross-domain generalization, or a unique mechanism for the Qwen low-bit failure."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026calibration,\ntitle={Calibration Timing and Model Sensitivity in Rotated {KV}-Cache Quantization},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=AYT3Kmxq8y},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration Timing and Model Sensitivity in Rotated KV-Cache Quantization"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/64b88a4e3321a8a5b32e55161d2de655bec6aff2.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791221246208,"tcdate":1788087442377,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission6395/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission6395/Authors"],"forum":"AYT3Kmxq8y","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6395,"cdate":1788087442377,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission6395/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791221246208,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"AYT3Kmxq8y","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2024 R2-FM Workshop Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ce60286dfa57dec366ea9e3f0877971c4f6c5cd7.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["In-Context Learning","Calibration","Ensembling"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/R2-FM"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|can_large_language_models_achieve_calibration_with_incontext_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chengzu_Li1","~Han_Zhou4","~Goran_Glavaš1","~Anna_Korhonen1","~Ivan_Vulić1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Following the standard supervised fine-tuning (SFT) paradigm, in-context learning (ICL) has become an efficient approach propelled by the recent advancements in large language models (LLMs), yielding promising performance across various tasks in few-shot data setups. \nHowever, both paradigms are prone to suffer from the critical problem of overconfidence (i.e., miscalibration), especially in such limited data setups. \nIn this work, we deliver an in-depth analysis of the behavior across different choices of learning methods from the perspective of both performance and calibration, as well as their interplay.\nThrough extensive controlled experiments, we find that simultaneous gains for both task performance and calibration are difficult to achieve, and the miscalibration problem exists across all learning methods in low-resource setups.\nTo address this challenging trade-off between performance and calibration, we then study the potential of self-ensembling techniques applied at different modeling stages (e.g., variations of in-context examples or variations in prompts or different ensembling strategies). We justify the feasibility of self-ensembling on SFT in addition to ICL, to make the predictions more calibrated and have comparable or even better performance. Our work sheds light on which learning paradigm to choose and how to enhance both task performance and calibration of LLMs."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nli2024can,\ntitle={Can Large Language Models Achieve Calibration with In-Context Learning?},\nauthor={Chengzu Li and Han Zhou and Goran Glava{\\v{s}} and Anna Korhonen and Ivan Vuli{\\'c}},\nbooktitle={ICLR 2024 Workshop on Reliable and Responsible Foundation Models},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=8rfsSokbeq}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Can Large Language Models Achieve Calibration with In-Context Learning?"},"authors":{"value":["Chengzu Li","Han Zhou","Goran Glavaš","Anna Korhonen","Ivan Vulić"]}},"tmdate":1715151140295,"pdate":1709677575519,"tcdate":1707491616564,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/R2-FM","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/R2-FM/Submission52/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/R2-FM/Submission52/Authors"],"forum":"8rfsSokbeq","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":52,"cdate":1707491616564,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/R2-FM/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/R2-FM/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/R2-FM/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/R2-FM/Submission52/-/Camera-Ready_Version"],"mdate":1715151140295,"odate":1715151140078,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/R2-FM","id":"8rfsSokbeq","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We analyze rejection sampling as an efficient approach to calibrate any statistic, by allowing efficient query access to the exponentially tilted model."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Calibration","Entropy Calibration","Rejection Sampling"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/9cc1489555bdb20b81e2ce977d6eecd0ecb5d914.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Exponential Tilting (ET) is a classical technique for model calibration, alignment and reward maximization. For a given statistic (reward) function $f$, ET transforms a base model distribiution $Q$ via the multiplicative update rule $Q^\\alpha \\propto Q\\cdot \\exp(\\alpha \\cdot f)$. Braverman et al. (ICML 2020) showed that fitting $\\alpha$ to minimize cross-entropy loss against some reference distribution, ensures the tilted model does not lose quality, while maximizing the \\emph{average} value of $f$. Unfortunately, ET has a prohibitive computational cost of  computing the normalizing partition function, requiring multiple foward passes to evaluate $f$ on the full support of tokens, which renders this technique impreactical in the autoregressive LLM setup. \n\nWe propose a natural, highly efficient algorithm for implicitly sampling from the tilted distribution via rejection-sampling, which dramatically reduces the query access to the tilted model and avoids computing the normalizing partition function altogether. We demonstrate the efficiency of our technique in theory and practice, by analyzing the expected query count and upper bounding it in terms of the cross-entropy distance to the base model and the ``variance\" of $f$. We then corroborate our bounds on small-size LLMs (Qwen2.5, Qwen3 and SmolLM3), applying our approach to the substantive case study of \\emph{entropy calibration} of LLMs (Cao et al., NIPS 2025). Our experiments show improvement in entropy miscalibration in base models, while incurring 15--25\\% additional sampling attempts due to rejections. We expect this overhead to improve when moving to more capable and larger models."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026efficient,\ntitle={Efficient Calibration via Rejection Sampling},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=o1lQx8CVTn},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Efficient Calibration via Rejection Sampling"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/03f1338ebcff60569585828b5ff9dbcdde67fda9.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791235189204,"tcdate":1789803742008,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission56827/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission56827/Authors"],"forum":"o1lQx8CVTn","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":56827,"cdate":1789803742008,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission56827/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791235189204,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"o1lQx8CVTn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/7509710/07456267.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"schopp|selfcalibration_of_accelerometer_arrays"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Patrick_Schopp:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hagen_Graf:","~Wolfram_Burgard3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yiannos_Manoli:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2016.2549758"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/SchoppGBM16,\n  author={Patrick Schopp and Hagen Graf and Wolfram Burgard and Yiannos Manoli},\n  title={Self-Calibration of Accelerometer Arrays},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={65},\n  number={8},\n  pages={1913-1925},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2016.2549758}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"A gyroscope-free inertial measurement unit employs solely accelerometers to capture the motion of a body in the form of its linear and angular acceleration as well as its angular velocity. For that, multiple transducers are fixed at distinct locations of the body that together form an accelerometer array. To accurately estimate the motion, the poses of the sensors, i.e., their positions and orientations, must be known precisely. Unfortunately, these parameters are typically hard to assess. Current state-of-the-art calibration methods are able to reconstruct the geometrical sensor configuration based on a set of motion data and corresponding acceleration measurements. However, to impose a reference motion on the sensor array and to capture that motion with the necessary accuracy requires sophisticated laboratory equipment. In this paper, we present a method to estimate the transducer poses using only their own measurements without depending on reference motion data. It is based on an iterative graph optimization that considers both the sensor poses and the motion as target variables. Initially, this results in infinitely many solutions. We reduce the solutions to only one global optimum by explicitly modeling the used triple-axis accelerometers as sensor triads and furthermore taking the temporal dependence of the acceleration samples into account. We compare our method to the conventional calibration using reference data in terms of its estimation accuracy. Furthermore, we analyze the convergence properties of our method by evaluating its tolerance to initial pose deviations. For both, we use synthetic and experimental data recorded on a 3-D rotation table."},"title":{"value":"Self-Calibration of Accelerometer Arrays"},"authors":{"value":["Patrick Schopp","Hagen Graf","Wolfram Burgard","Yiannos Manoli"]}},"tmdate":1746122142338,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1746122005142,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wolfram_Burgard3"],"forum":"9U7iKSmOJX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":409015,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1746122142338,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9U7iKSmOJX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Comput. Vis. Image Underst. 2013"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CVIU/2013"},"paperhash":{"value":"perdigoto|calibration_of_mirror_position_and_extrinsic_parameters_in_axial_noncentral_catadioptric_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luis_Perdigoto:","~Helder_J._Araujo1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cviu.2013.04.001"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/cviu/PerdigotoA13,\n  author={Luis Perdigoto and Helder Araújo},\n  title={Calibration of mirror position and extrinsic parameters in axial non-central catadioptric systems},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  journal={Comput. Vis. Image Underst.},\n  volume={117},\n  number={8},\n  pages={909-921},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cviu.2013.04.001}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•We propose a novel calibration method for non-central catadioptric systems.•We assume an axial symmetrical mirror and a pinhole camera placed on the mirror axis.•The calibration estimates the camera/mirror position and the extrinsic parameters.•The procedure requires a single image of a (possibly planar) calibration object.•The Direct-Linear-Transformation algorithm and cross-ratio are used."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of mirror position and extrinsic parameters in axial non-central catadioptric systems"},"authors":{"value":["Luis Perdigoto","Helder Araújo"]}},"tmdate":1752675131494,"pdate":1356998400000,"tcdate":1752675016128,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Helder_J._Araujo1"],"forum":"toOCc6zBg7","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":570012,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1752675131494,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"toOCc6zBg7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2025 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Models","Machine Learning for Code Editing","Code Generation","Reasoning"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nli2025editlord,\ntitle={EditLord: Learning Code Transformation Rules for Code Editing},\nauthor={Weichen Li and Albert Jan and Baishakhi Ray and Junfeng Yang and Chengzhi Mao and Kexin Pei},\nbooktitle={Forty-second International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=CxQPqcxRnx}\n}"},"title":{"value":"EditLord: Learning Code Transformation Rules for Code Editing"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|editlord_learning_code_transformation_rules_for_code_editing"},"TLDR":{"value":"The paper introduces EditLord, a novel framework that enhances LLMs for code editing by introducing discrete and explicit procedural steps, leading to improved performance on various code editing tasks and better robustness."},"primary_area":{"value":"applications->everything_else"},"abstract":{"value":"Code editing is a foundational task in software development, where its effectiveness depends on whether it introduces desired code property changes without changing the original code's intended functionality. \nExisting approaches often formulate code editing as an implicit end-to-end task, omitting the fact that code-editing procedures inherently consist of discrete and explicit steps, and thus suffer from suboptimal performance and lack of robustness and generalization. \nWe introduce EditLord, a code editing framework that makes the code transformation steps explicit. \nOur key insight is to employ a language model (LM) as an inductive learner to extract code editing rules from the training code pairs as concise meta-rule sets.\nSuch rule sets will be manifested for each training sample to augment them for finetuning or assist in prompting- and iterative-based code editing.\nEditLord outperforms the state-of-the-art by an average of 22.7% in editing performance and 58.1% in robustness while achieving 20.2% higher functional correctness, across critical software engineering and security applications, LM models, and editing modes."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3d6f0dfb6ca348bde814ad64a3d850bbd27fbe37.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Editing code is a common but complex part of software development. \nMost current tools try to do this automatically, but they often treat the process as one big step, which can make the results unreliable or hard to generalize.\n\nWe created a new tool called EditLord that takes a more structured approach. \nInstead of treating code editing as a black box, EditLord breaks the process down into clear steps. \nEditLord uses a powerful language model to learn simple editing rules from examples of how code was changed. \nThese rules can then help guide future edits, whether the model is being fine-tuned or used directly.\n\nEditLord consistently outperforms other tools across various real-world programming tasks, e.g., performance optimization, readability improvement, and security hardening.\nIt makes more accurate edits, handles different situations better, and maintains the original function of the code more reliably."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2025/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Weichen_Li1","~Albert_Jan1","~Baishakhi_Ray2","~Junfeng_Yang1","~Chengzhi_Mao2","~Kexin_Pei1"]},"authors":{"value":["Weichen Li","Albert Jan","Baishakhi Ray","Junfeng Yang","Chengzhi Mao","Kexin Pei"]}},"tmdate":1755139227599,"pdate":1746105682073,"tcdate":1737691190509,"writers":["ICML.cc/2025/Conference","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission14642/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission14642/Authors"],"forum":"CxQPqcxRnx","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":14642,"cdate":1737691190509,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission14642/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission14642/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1755139227599,"odate":1750231507489,"domain":"ICML.cc/2025/Conference","id":"CxQPqcxRnx","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"The paper introduces EditLord, a novel framework that enhances LLMs for code editing by introducing discrete and explicit procedural steps, leading to improved performance on various code editing tasks and better robustness."},"venue":{"value":"ICML 2025 Workshop PRAL"},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Models","Machine Learning for Code Editing","Code Generation","Reasoning"]},"abstract":{"value":"Code editing is a foundational task in software development, where its effectiveness depends on whether it introduces desired code property changes without changing the original code's intended functionality. \nExisting approaches often formulate code editing as an implicit end-to-end task, omitting the fact that code-editing procedures inherently consist of discrete and explicit steps, and thus suffer from suboptimal performance and lack of robustness and generalization. \nWe introduce EditLord, a code editing framework that makes the code transformation steps explicit. \nOur key insight is to employ a language model (LM) as an inductive learner to extract code editing rules from the training code pairs as concise meta-rule sets.\nSuch rule sets will be manifested for each training sample to augment them for finetuning or assist in prompting- and iterative-based code editing.\nEditLord outperforms the state-of-the-art by an average of 22.7% in editing performance and 58.1% in robustness while achieving 20.2% higher functional correctness, across critical software engineering and security applications, LM models, and editing modes."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nli2025editlord,\ntitle={EditLord: Learning Code Transformation Rules for Code Editing},\nauthor={Weichen Li and Albert Jan and Baishakhi Ray and Junfeng Yang and Chengzhi Mao and Kexin Pei},\nbooktitle={ICML 2025 Workshop on Programmatic Representations for Agent Learning},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=rCUJFqn9YO}\n}"},"title":{"value":"EditLord: Learning Code Transformation Rules for Code Editing"},"Anonymization":{"value":"This submission has been anonymized for double-blind review via the removal of identifying information such as names, affiliations, and identifying URLs."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f3e2c144b918a9adfcc2d9112bb171d4d191c002.pdf"},"Format":{"value":"We have read the camera-ready instructions, and our paper is formatted with the provided template."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/PRAL"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|editlord_learning_code_transformation_rules_for_code_editing"},"authorids":{"value":["~Weichen_Li1","~Albert_Jan1","~Baishakhi_Ray2","~Junfeng_Yang1","~Chengzhi_Mao2","~Kexin_Pei1"]},"De-Anonymization":{"value":"This submission has been de-anonymized."},"track":{"value":"Long Paper (up to 9 pages)"},"authors":{"value":["Weichen Li","Albert Jan","Baishakhi Ray","Junfeng Yang","Chengzhi Mao","Kexin Pei"]}},"tmdate":1752898593245,"pdate":1749884844699,"tcdate":1748593033284,"writers":["ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/PRAL","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/PRAL/Submission25/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/PRAL/Submission25/Authors"],"forum":"rCUJFqn9YO","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":25,"cdate":1748593033284,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/PRAL/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/PRAL/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/PRAL/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/PRAL/Submission25/-/Camera_Ready_Submission"],"mdate":1752898593245,"odate":1752898296526,"domain":"ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/PRAL","id":"rCUJFqn9YO","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose an adaptive pseudo-label refinement and calibration framework that enables accurate, biologically consistent long-term survival prediction despite heavy censoring and limited follow-up."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Survival Analysis","Pseudo-Label Refinement","Calibration","Censoring","Long-Term Survival Prediction","Deep Ensemble Learning","Weibull–Kaplan–Meier Initialization","Clinical Reliability","Oncology","Healthcare AI"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/cefa4980faddbddd7b6a2fcb9c0087e6babaede6.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate survival prediction is critical for oncology, public health, and reliability engineering, yet existing methods remain constrained by limited follow-up, heavy censoring, and static pseudo-labeling practices. In many clinical datasets, including Our reconstructed cohort of $N = 50{,}155$ patients with observed follow-up of only 74.742 months (58.8\\% deceased, 41.2\\% censored), long-term outcomes remain unobserved, preventing reliable 10-year (120-month) survival estimation. We address this gap by introducing a dynamic pseudo-label refinement and calibration framework that transforms incomplete follow-up into extended, biologically consistent survival trajectories. Starting from a hybrid Weibull–Kaplan–Meier initialization, pseudo-labels are iteratively corrected under survival-theoretic constraints and clinical plausibility rules, including enforcing zero survival beyond death and monotonic survival probabilities for censored patients. These refined labels are propagated through a deep ensemble trained with variance-penalizing objectives and monitored via diagnostic feedback for stability and uncertainty calibration. This process enables survival labels to evolve adaptively, rather than remain static preprocessing artifacts, and produces clinically plausible estimates well beyond the observed horizon. We applied to the 50{,}155-patient cohort, the framework achieved rapid convergence and outstanding predictive performance ($R^2 = 0.9964$, MAE = 0.0066, C-index = 0.9915), with predictions tightly calibrated, biologically consistent, and robust under long-term censoring. We validated Our proposed framework on two public datasets of N = 2509 \\& N = 205  available in \\cite{Metabric_Kaggle} (Metabric) \\& \\cite{finalfit2023survival} (Malignant Melanoma) achieved remarkable results ($R^2 = 0.9924$ \\& $0.9781$, MAE = 0.0142 \\& 0.0247, C-index = 0.9633 \\& 0.8459) by follow-up to 480 \\& 240 months respectively. Thus, by bridging the 74.742-month follow-up limit with reliable 120-month projections on Our dataset, Our work establishes adaptive pseudo-label refinement as a principled foundation for long-horizon, interpretable, and clinically reliable survival modeling. Moreover, we are going to publicly publish Our dataset and code at \\url{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17163267} and \\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/Dynamic-Pseudo-Labeling-D2AB/} respectively for the research community."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nsher2026sparc,\ntitle={{SPARC}: {SURVIVAL} {PSEUDO}-{LABEL} {ADAPTIVE} {RE}- {FINEMENT} {AND} {CALIBRATION}},\nauthor={Tahir Sher and Abdul Rehman and Heungjo Kim and Hongjae Lee and Min Jung Chang and Chanwoo Kim},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=KBXrByWLcb}\n}"},"title":{"value":"SPARC: SURVIVAL PSEUDO-LABEL ADAPTIVE RE- FINEMENT AND CALIBRATION"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/95906b885552fe9decdc2e06c9248e85309a722b.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"sher|sparc_survival_pseudolabel_adaptive_re_finement_and_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tahir_Sher1","~Abdul_Rehman7","~Heungjo_Kim1","~Hongjae_Lee2","~Min_Jung_Chang1","~Chanwoo_Kim7"]},"authors":{"value":["Tahir Sher","Abdul Rehman","Heungjo Kim","Hongjae Lee","Min Jung Chang","Chanwoo Kim"]}},"tmdate":1767779900555,"tcdate":1758361279078,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission24861/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission24861/Authors"],"forum":"KBXrByWLcb","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":24861,"cdate":1758361279078,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission24861/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1767779900555,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"KBXrByWLcb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["calibration","adaptive data analysis","sample complexity"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrating a multiclass predictor, that outputs a distribution over labels, is particularly challenging due to the exponential number of possible prediction values. In this work, we propose a new definition of calibration error that interpolates between two established calibration error notions, one with known exponential sample complexity and one with polynomial sample complexity for calibrating a given predictor. Our algorithm can calibrate any given predictor for the entire range of interpolation, except for one endpoint, using only a polynomial number of samples. At the other endpoint, we achieve nearly optimal dependence on the error parameter, improving upon previous work. A key technical contribution is a novel application of adaptive data analysis with high adaptivity but only logarithmic overhead in the sample complexity."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nbairaktari2026sampleefficient,\ntitle={Sample-efficient Multiclass Calibration under \\${\\textbackslash}ell\\_\\{p\\}\\$ Error},\nauthor={Konstantina Bairaktari and Huy Nguyen},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=jwv2Lsh8Wo}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Sample-efficient Multiclass Calibration under $\\ell_{p}$ Error"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e35332a76a5d108988619d068fe7cc47f52746f6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"bairaktari|sampleefficient_multiclass_calibration_under_\\ell_p_error"},"authorids":{"value":["~Konstantina_Bairaktari1","~Huy_Nguyen1"]},"authors":{"value":["Konstantina Bairaktari","Huy Nguyen"]}},"tmdate":1770805103424,"tcdate":1758295583313,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19355/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19355/Authors"],"forum":"jwv2Lsh8Wo","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":19355,"cdate":1758295583313,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19355/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19355/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770805103424,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"jwv2Lsh8Wo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/6287639/10820123/10870225.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ACCESS/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"nguyen|on_calibration_of_prompt_learning_using_temperature_scaling"},"authorids":{"value":["~Khanh-Binh_Nguyen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chae_Jung_Park:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3538617"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/NguyenP25,\n  author={Khanh-Binh Nguyen and Chae Jung Park},\n  title={On Calibration of Prompt Learning Using Temperature Scaling},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={13},\n  pages={31171-31182},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3538617}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt learning has emerged as a valuable technique for enhancing vision-language models (VLMs) for downstream tasks in specific domains, resulting in high performance on such tasks. However, existing prompt learning methods place too much focus on increasing complexity, overlooking the natural issue of learning with limited data, which leads to overfitting. In this study, we address this issue by applying temperature scaling (TS) during training to improve confidence calibration. By sharpening predictions, TS reduces overfitting and enhances generalization across diverse datasets. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach improves the calibration of CLIP-based methods without compromising accuracy. Our method is model-agnostic and applicable to broader tasks, offering a simple yet effective solution for zero-shot and few-shot learning."},"title":{"value":"On Calibration of Prompt Learning Using Temperature Scaling"},"authors":{"value":["Khanh-Binh Nguyen","Chae Jung Park"]}},"tmdate":1763419841963,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/access/NguyenP25"],"tcdate":1763419839822,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Khanh-Binh_Nguyen1"],"forum":"KFj6evwZRh","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":705486,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1763419841963,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KFj6evwZRh","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Commun. Lett. 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/4234/9945064/09869869.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ICL/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|wideband_blind_calibration_for_uca_oam_communication_system"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoyu_Sun:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiaqiong_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Feng_Chen_0030:","~Shengquan_Li1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LCOMM.2022.3202822"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/icl/ZhangSZCL22,\n  author={Yi Zhang and Xiaoyu Sun and Jiaqiong Zhou and Feng Chen and Shengquan Li},\n  title={Wideband Blind Calibration for UCA OAM Communication System},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={IEEE Commun. Lett.},\n  volume={26},\n  number={11},\n  pages={2596-2600},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LCOMM.2022.3202822}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This letter presents a wideband blind calibration method for uniform circular array (UCA) orbital angular momentum (OAM) systems without the prior knowledge of the positions and attitudes of the transmit and receive UCAs. The basic idea is to rewrite the maximum-likelihood estimation (MLE) for all parameters as an optimization problem over a Lie group which can represent the positions and attitudes of the UCAs. Then an accelerated Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm is implemented to solve this optimization problem by utilizing the sparsity in the iteration process. Simulation results show that the proposed wideband blind calibration method can estimate the gain/phase errors and the positions and attitudes of the transmit and receive UCAs simultaneously and improve the achievable spectral efficiency of the UCA OAM communication system efficiently."},"title":{"value":"Wideband Blind Calibration for UCA OAM Communication System"},"authors":{"value":["Yi Zhang","Xiaoyu Sun","Jiaqiong Zhou","Feng Chen","Shengquan Li"]}},"tmdate":1753774641471,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1753774632875,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shengquan_Li1"],"forum":"2KoB6hY5ge","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":599268,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753774641471,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2KoB6hY5ge","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.12801v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"bandyopadhyay|effect_of_personalized_calibration_on_gaze_estimation_using_deeplearning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nairit_Bandyopadhyay:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sébastien_Riou:","~Didier_Schwab1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.12801"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2109-12801,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Nairit Bandyopadhyay and Sébastien Riou and Didier Schwab},\n  title={Effect Of Personalized Calibration On Gaze Estimation Using Deep-Learning},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2109.12801},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.12801}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With the increase in computation power and the development of new state-of-the-art deep learning algorithms, appearance-based gaze estimation is becoming more and more popular. It is believed to work well with curated laboratory data sets, however it faces several challenges when deployed in real world scenario. One such challenge is to estimate the gaze of a person about which the Deep Learning model trained for gaze estimation has no knowledge about. To analyse the performance in such scenarios we have tried to simulate a calibration mechanism. In this work we use the MPIIGaze data set. We trained a multi modal convolutional neural network and analysed its performance with and without calibration and this evaluation provides clear insights on how calibration improved the performance of the Deep Learning model in estimating gaze in the wild."},"title":{"value":"Effect Of Personalized Calibration On Gaze Estimation Using Deep-Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Nairit Bandyopadhyay","Sébastien Riou","Didier Schwab"]}},"tmdate":1728632053199,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1728631874890,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Didier_Schwab1"],"forum":"vizJRprfOf","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":147867,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1728632053199,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"vizJRprfOf","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.12801v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"bandyopadhyay|effect_of_personalized_calibration_on_gaze_estimation_using_deeplearning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nairit_Bandyopadhyay:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sébastien_Riou:","~Didier_Schwab1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.12801"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2109-12801,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Nairit Bandyopadhyay and Sébastien Riou and Didier Schwab},\n  title={Effect Of Personalized Calibration On Gaze Estimation Using Deep-Learning},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2109.12801},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.12801}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With the increase in computation power and the development of new state-of-the-art deep learning algorithms, appearance-based gaze estimation is becoming more and more popular. It is believed to work well with curated laboratory data sets, however it faces several challenges when deployed in real world scenario. One such challenge is to estimate the gaze of a person about which the Deep Learning model trained for gaze estimation has no knowledge about. To analyse the performance in such scenarios we have tried to simulate a calibration mechanism. In this work we use the MPIIGaze data set. We trained a multi modal convolutional neural network and analysed its performance with and without calibration and this evaluation provides clear insights on how calibration improved the performance of the Deep Learning model in estimating gaze in the wild."},"title":{"value":"Effect Of Personalized Calibration On Gaze Estimation Using Deep-Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Nairit Bandyopadhyay","Sébastien Riou","Didier Schwab"]}},"tmdate":1728631935495,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1728631851507,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Didier_Schwab1"],"forum":"8IS8m3B3bd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":147816,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1728631935495,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"8IS8m3B3bd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1608.04509v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|unconstrained_twoparallelplane_model_for_focused_plenoptic_cameras_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chunping_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhe_Ji:","~Qing_Wang3"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1608.04509"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/ZhangJW16,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Chunping Zhang and Zhe Ji and Qing Wang},\n  title={Unconstrained Two-parallel-plane Model for Focused Plenoptic Cameras Calibration},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1608.04509},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1608.04509}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The plenoptic camera can capture both angular and spatial information of the rays, enabling 3D reconstruction by single exposure. The geometry of the recovered scene structure is affected by the calibration of the plenoptic camera significantly. In this paper, we propose a novel unconstrained two-parallel-plane (TPP) model with 7 parameters to describe a 4D light field. By reconstructing scene points from ray-ray association, a 3D projective transformation is deduced to establish the relationship between the scene structure and the TPP parameters. Based on the transformation, we simplify the focused plenoptic camera as a TPP model and calibrate its intrinsic parameters. Our calibration method includes a close-form solution and a nonlinear optimization by minimizing re-projection error. Experiments on both simulated data and real scene data verify the performance of the calibration on the focused plenoptic camera."},"title":{"value":"Unconstrained Two-parallel-plane Model for Focused Plenoptic Cameras Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Chunping Zhang","Zhe Ji","Qing Wang"]}},"tmdate":1727530965110,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1727530950679,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Qing_Wang3"],"forum":"7nDztBvmrM","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":102273,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727530965110,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"7nDztBvmrM","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["Graph Anomaly Detection","Semi-supervised Learning","Graph Representation Learning","Anomaly Detection"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/7ca018cff5371be592a4d8160f7d067c5d1e46f6.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzeng2026normality,\ntitle={Normality Calibration in Semi-supervised Graph Anomaly Detection},\nauthor={Guolei Zeng and Hezhe Qiao and Guoguo Ai and Jinsong Guo and Guansong Pang},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ifjUMvvY05}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Normality Calibration in Semi-supervised Graph Anomaly Detection"},"paperhash":{"value":"zeng|normality_calibration_in_semisupervised_graph_anomaly_detection"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/a3ab32f0c9702dec519b452de14eb6c65e64b885.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"We propose GraphNC, a graph normality calibration framework that leverages both labeled and unlabeled data to calibrate the normality from a teacher model."},"primary_area":{"value":"deep_learning->graph_neural_networks"},"abstract":{"value":"Semi-supervised graph anomaly detection (GAD), which assumes a subset of labeled normal nodes for training, is widely studied. However, existing methods learn normality only from these labeled nodes, often overfitting their patterns and causing high detection errors, such as many false positives. To overcome this limitation, we propose $GraphNC$, a graph normality calibration framework that leverages both labeled and unlabeled data to calibrate the normality from a teacher (a pre-trained semi-supervised GAD model) jointly in anomaly score and representation spaces. GraphNC includes two main components, anomaly score distribution alignment ($ScoreDA$) and perturbation-based normality regularization ($NormReg$). ScoreDA optimizes our model’s anomaly scores by aligning them with the teacher’s score distribution. Because the teacher provides accurate scores for most normal nodes and some anomalies, this alignment pulls the scores of the two classes toward opposite ends, making them more separable. To reduce the impact of inaccurate teacher scores, NormReg regularizes normality in the representation space, making normal node representations more compact via a perturbation-guided consistency loss applied only to the labeled nodes. Comprehensive experiments on six benchmarks demonstrate that GraphNC (1) consistently and substantially enhances the performance of teacher models from different GAD methods (2) achieves new state-of-the-art performance. Our code is available at https://github.com/mala-lab/GraphNC."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://github.com/mala-lab/GraphNC"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a42bec4174b850c8ae143f8efcd397b5d19caf75.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Many real-world systems, such as social networks and financial transaction networks, can be represented as graphs, where each item is connected to others. Detecting unusual items in these graphs is important for tasks such as finding spam accounts or fraud. However, abnormal cases are often rare and costly to label, while normal cases are easier to obtain.\n\nThis paper proposes $GraphNC$, a method for improving graph anomaly detection when only a small set of normal examples is labeled. Existing methods often rely too heavily on these few labeled normal examples, which can cause them to mistake unfamiliar but normal items for anomalies. GraphNC addresses this by learning from both labeled normal data and unlabeled data. It first uses the results of an existing detector as guidance, then refines this guidance by encouraging known normal items to remain consistently represented even after small changes.\n\nExperiments on six real-world datasets show that GraphNC improves several existing methods and achieves the best overall performance. These results suggest that GraphNC can make anomaly detection more reliable in practical settings where abnormal examples are difficult to collect."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Guolei_Zeng2","~Hezhe_Qiao1","~Guoguo_Ai1","~Jinsong_Guo2","~Guansong_Pang1"]},"authors":{"value":["Guolei Zeng","Hezhe Qiao","Guoguo Ai","Jinsong Guo","Guansong Pang"]}},"tmdate":1790067350528,"pdate":1777576420763,"tcdate":1769039222153,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission13055/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission13055/Authors"],"forum":"ifjUMvvY05","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":13055,"cdate":1769039222153,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission13055/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission13055/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/PC_Revision"],"mdate":1790067350528,"odate":1782341948529,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"ifjUMvvY05","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Circuits and Systems for Video Technology"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/76/4358651/11165433.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wen|rulesemantic_generative_calibration_blur_detection_for_uav_imagery"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TCSVT.2025.3610689"},"abstract":{"value":"Blur detection is a novel method for evaluating image quality in the context of Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) surveillance and monitoring activities. However, existing methods lack multi-scenario adaptability due to the overreliance of deep learning models on learned priors from limited datasets, reducing their adaptability to unfamiliar conditions. To address this issue, the Denoising Diffusion Implicit Model (DDIM) is integrated with a paradigm named Rule-based Semantic Calibration (RSC) to create Rule-Semantic Generative Calibration Blur Detection (RSGC-BD). This approach generates robust blur detection maps through an iterative calibration process that enhances generalization capabilities. Unlike current Blur Detection (BD) methods, which categorize pixels as blurred or unblurred with a single forward propagation, the suggested approach employs the DDIM-based generative model to create and refine a BD map iteratively. By utilizing the iterative calibration process through RSC to integrate rule-based blur masks into generative semantic results at each step, this model ensures high-precision blur prediction, enhanced multi-scenario adaptability, and significantly improved inference speed. Furthermore, we propose a conversion module, namely the Adaptive RGB-to-Grayscale Conversion Cascade (ARGC-Cascade), to convert RGB images to grayscale through adaptive integration, highlighting blurred regions and improving detection accuracy. This enhancement of blur features is achieved by balancing the spectral channel weights during image conversion. The superior performance of the proposed RSGC-BD approach is validated by extensive tests on four high-resolution BD datasets, including the newly introduced UAV-BD. Source codes are available at: https://github.com/udrs/RSGCBD."},"title":{"value":"Rule-Semantic Generative Calibration Blur Detection for UAV Imagery"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yihan Wen"},{"fullname":"Zhuo Zhang"},{"fullname":"Xianping Ma","username":"~Xianping_Ma1"},{"fullname":"Peipei Zhu"},{"fullname":"Jinglei Li"},{"fullname":"Guanchong Niu"},{"fullname":"Qiguang Miao"}]}},"tmdate":1789089736989,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tcsvt.2025.3610689"],"tcdate":1762322725642,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Xianping_Ma1"],"forum":"vniGBwegbX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":5559,"cdate":1758052389833,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789089736989,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"vniGBwegbX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ECCV (78) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-73229-4_18.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ECCV/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"amininaieni|instant_uncertainty_calibration_of_nerfs_using_a_metacalibrator"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Niki_Amini-Naieni:","~Tomas_Jakab1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrea_Vedaldi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ronald_Clark:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-73229-4_18"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/eccv/AminiNaieniJVC24,\n  author={Niki Amini-Naieni and Tomas Jakab and Andrea Vedaldi and Ronald Clark},\n  title={Instant Uncertainty Calibration of NeRFs Using a Meta-calibrator},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={309-324},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-73229-4_18},\n  booktitle={ECCV (78)},\n  crossref={conf/eccv/2024-78}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) have markedly improved novel view synthesis, but accurate uncertainty quantification in their image predictions remains an open problem. The prevailing methods for estimating uncertainty, including the state-of-the-art Density-aware NeRF Ensembles (DANE) [29], quantify uncertainty without calibration. This frequently leads to over- or under-confidence in image predictions, which can undermine their real-world applications. In this paper, we propose a method which, for the first time, achieves calibrated uncertainties for NeRFs. To accomplish this, we overcome a significant challenge in adapting existing calibration techniques to NeRFs: a need to hold out ground truth images from the target scene, reducing the number of images left to train the NeRF. This issue is particularly problematic in sparse-view settings, where we can operate with as few as three images. To address this, we introduce the concept of a meta-calibrator that performs uncertainty calibration for NeRFs with a single forward pass without the need for holding out any images from the target scene. Our meta-calibrator is a neural network that takes as input the NeRF images and uncalibrated uncertainty maps and outputs a scene-specific calibration curve that corrects the NeRF’s uncalibrated uncertainties. We show that the meta-calibrator can generalize on unseen scenes and achieves well-calibrated and state-of-the-art uncertainty for NeRFs, significantly beating DANE and other approaches. This opens opportunities to improve applications that rely on accurate NeRF uncertainty estimates such as next-best view planning and potentially more trustworthy image reconstruction for medical diagnosis."},"title":{"value":"Instant Uncertainty Calibration of NeRFs Using a Meta-calibrator"},"authors":{"value":["Niki Amini-Naieni","Tomas Jakab","Andrea Vedaldi","Ronald Clark"]}},"tmdate":1757083580782,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/eccv/AminiNaieniJVC24"],"tcdate":1757083571387,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Tomas_Jakab1"],"forum":"g161QWRTm9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":620699,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1757083580782,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"g161QWRTm9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ECCV 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"amininaieni|instant_uncertainty_calibration_of_nerfs_using_a_metacalibrator"},"authorids":{"value":["~Niki_Amini-Naieni2","~Tomas_Jakab1","~Andrea_Vedaldi1","~Ronald_Clark2"]},"html":{"value":"https://www.ecva.net/papers/eccv_2024/papers_ECCV/papers/10061.pdf"},"abstract":{"value":"Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) have markedly improved novel view synthesis, but accurate uncertainty quantification in their image predictions remains an open problem. The prevailing methods for estimating uncertainty, including the state-of-the-art Density-aware NeRF Ensembles (DANE), quantify uncertainty without calibration. This frequently leads to over- or under-confidence in image predictions, which can undermine their real-world applications. In this paper, we propose a method which, for the first time, achieves calibrated uncertainties for NeRFs. To accomplish this, we overcome a significant challenge in adapting existing calibration techniques to NeRFs: a need to hold out ground truth images from the target scene, reducing the number of images left to train the NeRF. This issue is particularly problematic in sparse-view settings, where we can operate with as few as three images. To address this, we introduce the concept of a meta-calibrator that performs uncertainty calibration for NeRFs with a single forward pass without the need for holding out any images from the target scene. Our meta-calibrator is a neural network that takes as input the NeRF images and uncalibrated uncertainty maps and outputs a scene-specific calibration curve that corrects the NeRF’s uncalibrated uncertainties. We show that the meta-calibrator can generalize on unseen scenes and achieves well-calibrated and state-of-the-art uncertainty for NeRFs, significantly beating DANE and other approaches. This opens opportunities to improve applications that rely on accurate NeRF uncertainty estimates such as next-best view planning and potentially more trustworthy image reconstruction for medical diagnosis."},"title":{"value":"Instant Uncertainty Calibration of NeRFs Using a Meta-Calibrator"},"authors":{"value":["Niki Amini-Naieni","Tomas Jakab","Andrea Vedaldi","Ronald Clark"]}},"tmdate":1730323809829,"pdate":1730246400000,"tcdate":1730323809829,"writers":["~Niki_Amini-Naieni2","~Tomas_Jakab1","~Andrea_Vedaldi1","~Ronald_Clark2"],"signatures":["~Niki_Amini-Naieni1"],"forum":"YjhYLWqWpB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":30948,"cdate":1730323809829,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1730323809829,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"YjhYLWqWpB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"DFTS 2015"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7304347/7315124/07315132.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/DFT/2015"},"paperhash":{"value":"teravainen|softwarebased_onchip_thermal_sensor_calibration_for_dvfsenabled_manycore_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sami_Teravainen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohammad_Hashem_Haghbayan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Amir-Mohammad_Rahmani:","~Pasi_Liljeberg1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hannu_Tenhunen:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/DFT.2015.7315132"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/dft/TeravainenHRLT15,\n  author={Sami Teravainen and Mohammad Hashem Haghbayan and Amir-Mohammad Rahmani and Pasi Liljeberg and Hannu Tenhunen},\n  title={Software-based on-chip thermal sensor calibration for DVFS-enabled many-core systems},\n  year={2015},\n  cdate={1420070400000},\n  pages={35-40},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/DFT.2015.7315132},\n  booktitle={DFTS},\n  crossref={conf/dft/2015}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Due to increase in power density and temperature gradient in modern chips, multiple thermal sensors are deployed on the chip area to provide realtime temperature feedback for fine-grained dynamic thermal management (DTM) techniques. Thermal sensor accuracy is extremely prone to intra-die process variation and aging phenomena, and its report gradually drifts from the nominal value. This necessitates efficient calibration techniques to be applied before the sensor values are used. In addition, in modern many-core systems which are often enabled with dynamic voltage and frequency scaling (DVFS), thermal sensors located on cores are sensitive to the core's current voltage-frequency (VF) level, meaning that dedicated calibration is needed for each VF level. In this paper, we propose a general-purpose software-based auto-calibration strategy for thermal sensors without using any hardware infrastructures for DVFS-enabled many-core systems. We adopt a 2-point calibration method for calculating the calibration constants of each thermal sensor at each VF level. We demonstrate the efficiency of the proposed calibration strategy on a many-core platform, Intel's Single-chip Cloud Computer (SCC), covering all voltage and frequency combinations on the platform."},"title":{"value":"Software-based on-chip thermal sensor calibration for DVFS-enabled many-core systems"},"authors":{"value":["Sami Teravainen","Mohammad Hashem Haghbayan","Amir-Mohammad Rahmani","Pasi Liljeberg","Hannu Tenhunen"]}},"tmdate":1731509121040,"pdate":1420070400000,"tcdate":1731509022018,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Pasi_Liljeberg1"],"forum":"iH1MrpBAkL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":230153,"cdate":1420070400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731509121040,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"iH1MrpBAkL","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a reformulation of confidence calibration as a binary problem to significantly improve the performance of existing calibration methods for classifiers with many classes."},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2024"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Calibration","Image Classification","Deep Learning","Neural Networks"]},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Most classifiers based on deep neural networks associate their class prediction with a probability known as the confidence score. This score is often a by-product of the learning step and may not correctly estimate the classification accuracy, which impacts real-world usage. To be reliably used, the confidence score requires a post-processing calibration step. Data-driven methods have been proposed to calibrate the confidence score of already-trained classifiers. Still, many methods fail when the number of classes is high and per-class calibration data is scarce. To deal with a large number of classes, we propose to reformulate the confidence calibration of multiclass classifiers as a single binary classification problem. Our top-versus-all reformulation allows the use of the binary cross-entropy loss for scaling calibration methods. Contrary to the standard one-versus-all reformulation, it also allows the application of binary calibration methods to multiclass classifiers with efficient use of scarce per-class calibration data and without degradation of the accuracy. Additionally, we solve the problem of scaling methods overfitting the calibration set by introducing a regularization loss term during optimization. We evaluate our approach on an extensive list of deep networks and standard image classification datasets (CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and ImageNet). We show that it significantly improves the performance of existing calibration methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\ncoz2024efficient,\ntitle={Efficient calibration as a binary top-versus-all problem for classifiers with many classes},\nauthor={Adrien Le Coz and St{\\'e}phane Herbin and Faouzi Adjed},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=5HpZZbgdeK}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Efficient calibration as a binary top-versus-all problem for classifiers with many classes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ed503c7c1dab627d3899cd869130b50980b23206.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"coz|efficient_calibration_as_a_binary_topversusall_problem_for_classifiers_with_many_classes"},"authorids":{"value":["~Adrien_Le_Coz1","~Stéphane_Herbin1","~Faouzi_Adjed1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Adrien Le Coz","Stéphane Herbin","Faouzi Adjed"]}},"tmdate":1707625727962,"tcdate":1695396995650,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5749/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5749/Authors"],"forum":"5HpZZbgdeK","number":5749,"cdate":1695396995650,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5749/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5749/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1707625727962,"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"5HpZZbgdeK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Expert Syst. Appl. 2021"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ESWA/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"aguiar|a_camera_to_lidar_calibration_approach_through_the_optimization_of_atomic_transformations"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:André_Silva_Pinto_de_Aguiar:","~Miguel_Armando_Riem_de_Oliveira1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Eurico_Farinha_Pedrosa:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Filipe_Baptista_Neves_dos_Santos:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2021.114894"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/eswa/AguiarOPS21,\n  author={André Silva Pinto de Aguiar and Miguel Armando Riem de Oliveira and Eurico Farinha Pedrosa and Filipe Baptista Neves dos Santos},\n  title={A Camera to LiDAR calibration approach through the optimization of atomic transformations},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={Expert Syst. Appl.},\n  volume={176},\n  pages={114894},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2021.114894}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•Camera to 3D laser calibration through the optimization of atomic transformations.•Novel consideration of the calibration of multiple sensors simultaneously.•Support for multiple sensor modalities.•Interactive, visual and user-oriented Robot Operating System implementation."},"title":{"value":"A Camera to LiDAR calibration approach through the optimization of atomic transformations"},"authors":{"value":["André Silva Pinto de Aguiar","Miguel Armando Riem de Oliveira","Eurico Farinha Pedrosa","Filipe Baptista Neves dos Santos"]}},"tmdate":1753450822166,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1753450816021,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Miguel_Riem_Oliveira1"],"forum":"NL5eTxu6uJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":588886,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753450822166,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"NL5eTxu6uJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/6287639/10380310/10693433.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"jeon|biobridge_unified_bioembedding_with_bridging_modality_in_codeswitched_emr"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2024.3467251"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/JeonCLLK24,\n  author={Jangyeong Jeon and Sangyeon Cho and Dongjoon Lee and Changhee Lee and Junyeong Kim},\n  title={BioBridge: Unified Bio-Embedding With Bridging Modality in Code-Switched EMR},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={12},\n  pages={141866-141877},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2024.3467251}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Pediatric Emergency Department (PED) overcrowding presents a significant global challenge, prompting the need for efficient solutions. This paper introduces the BioBridge framework, a novel approach that applies Natural Language Processing (NLP) to Electronic Medical Records (EMRs) in written free-text form to enhance decision-making in PED. In non-English speaking countries, such as South Korea, EMR data is often written in a Code-Switching(CS) format that mixes the native language with English, with most code-switched English words having clinical significance. The BioBridge framework consists of two core modules: “bridging modality in context” and “unified bio-embedding.” The “bridging modality in context” module improves the contextual understanding of bilingual and code-switched EMRs. In the “unified bio-embedding” module, the knowledge of the model trained in the medical domain is injected into the encoder-based model to bridge the gap between the medical and general domains. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed BioBridge significantly performance traditional machine learning and pre-trained encoder-based models on several metrics, including F1 score, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC), area under the precision-recall Curve (AUPRC), and Brier score. Specifically, BioBridge-XLM achieved enhancements of 0.85% in F1 score, 0.75% in AUROC, and 0.76% in AUPRC, along with a notable 3.04% decrease in the Brier score, demonstrating marked improvements in accuracy, reliability, and prediction calibration over the baseline XLM model. The source code will be made publicly available at https://github.com/jjy961228/BioBridge."},"title":{"value":"BioBridge: Unified Bio-Embedding With Bridging Modality in Code-Switched EMR"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jangyeong Jeon","username":""},{"fullname":"Sangyeon Cho","username":"~Sangyeon_Cho2"},{"fullname":"Dongjoon Lee","username":""},{"fullname":"Changhee Lee","username":"~Changhee_Lee1"},{"fullname":"Junyeong Kim","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1789366363469,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/access/JeonCLLK24"],"tcdate":1780123656252,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Changhee_Lee1"],"forum":"5DmWTq3awy","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":39073,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1789366363469,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"5DmWTq3awy","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/6287639/10380310/10693433.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"jeon|biobridge_unified_bioembedding_with_bridging_modality_in_codeswitched_emr"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2024.3467251"},"abstract":{"value":"Pediatric Emergency Department (PED) overcrowding presents a significant global challenge, prompting the need for efficient solutions. This paper introduces the BioBridge framework, a novel approach that applies Natural Language Processing (NLP) to Electronic Medical Records (EMRs) in written free-text form to enhance decision-making in PED. In non-English speaking countries, such as South Korea, EMR data is often written in a Code-Switching(CS) format that mixes the native language with English, with most code-switched English words having clinical significance. The BioBridge framework consists of two core modules: “bridging modality in context” and “unified bio-embedding.” The “bridging modality in context” module improves the contextual understanding of bilingual and code-switched EMRs. In the “unified bio-embedding” module, the knowledge of the model trained in the medical domain is injected into the encoder-based model to bridge the gap between the medical and general domains. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed BioBridge significantly performance traditional machine learning and pre-trained encoder-based models on several metrics, including F1 score, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC), area under the precision-recall Curve (AUPRC), and Brier score. Specifically, BioBridge-XLM achieved enhancements of 0.85% in F1 score, 0.75% in AUROC, and 0.76% in AUPRC, along with a notable 3.04% decrease in the Brier score, demonstrating marked improvements in accuracy, reliability, and prediction calibration over the baseline XLM model. The source code will be made publicly available at https://github.com/jjy961228/BioBridge."},"title":{"value":"BioBridge: Unified Bio-Embedding With Bridging Modality in Code-Switched EMR"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jangyeong Jeon","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Jangyeong%20Jeon"},{"fullname":"Sangyeon Cho","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Sangyeon%20Cho"},{"fullname":"Dongjoon Lee","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Dongjoon%20Lee"},{"fullname":"Changhee Lee","username":"~Changhee_Lee1"},{"fullname":"Junyeong Kim","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Junyeong%20Kim"}]}},"tmdate":1779785087510,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/access.2024.3467251"],"tcdate":1779785083769,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Changhee_Lee1"],"forum":"87MUvFkrXn","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":72691,"cdate":1727294038321,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1779785087510,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"87MUvFkrXn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MFI 2012"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6335102/6342994/06343010.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MFI/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|extrinsic_calibration_between_a_stereoscopic_system_and_a_lidar_with_sensor_noise_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:You_Li_0005:","~Yassine_Ruichek2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cindy_Cappelle:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/MFI.2012.6343010"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/mfi/LiRC12,\n  author={You Li and Yassine Ruichek and Cindy Cappelle},\n  title={Extrinsic calibration between a stereoscopic system and a LIDAR with sensor noise models},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  pages={484-489},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/MFI.2012.6343010},\n  booktitle={MFI},\n  crossref={conf/mfi/2012}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Visual sensors and depth sensors, such as camera and LIDAR (Light Detection and Ranging) are more and more used together in current perception systems of intelligent vehicles. Fusing information obtained separately from these heterogeneous sensors always requires extrinsic calibration of vision sensors and LIDARs. In this paper, we propose an optimal extrinsic calibration algorithm between a binocular stereo vision system and a 2D LIDAR. The extrinsic calibration problem is solved by 3D reconstruction of a chessboard and geometric constraints between the views from the stereovision system and the LIDAR. The proposed approach takes sensor noise models into account that it provides optimal results under Mahalanobis distance constraints. Experiments based on both computer simulation and real data sets are presented and analyzed to evaluate the performance of the calibration method. A comparison with a popular camera/LIDAR calibration method is also proposed to show the benefits of our method."},"title":{"value":"Extrinsic calibration between a stereoscopic system and a LIDAR with sensor noise models"},"authors":{"value":["You Li","Yassine Ruichek","Cindy Cappelle"]}},"tmdate":1752847898743,"pdate":1325376000000,"tcdate":1752847815469,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yassine_RUICHEK1"],"forum":"hYNpsEBFeZ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":574748,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1752847898743,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"hYNpsEBFeZ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Measurement"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0263224125020305/pdfft?md5=1765b9b20ee46753ae3107b5b199096c&pid=1-s2.0-S0263224125020305-main.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"lin|rescaunet_residual_coordinate_attention_enhanced_unet_for_nonlinear_ct_geometric_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.measurement.2025.118671"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•A 2D nonlinear calibration workflow is introduced to address perturbations from mechanical vibrations in CT systems, transforming geometric calibration into a view-by-view 2D nonlinear projection registration process.•ResCA-UNet, a neural network model, is developed to accelerate projection registration speed and enhance accuracy, utilizing directional average pooling to capture global information in both horizontal and vertical directions.•The proposed workflow significantly improves the accuracy and robustness of CT system calibration by incorporating advanced neural network models, enhancing the registration process for more reliable imaging."},"title":{"value":"ResCA-UNet: Residual coordinate attention enhanced UNet for nonlinear CT geometric calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Tong Lin"},{"fullname":"Xinyun Zhong"},{"fullname":"Li Chen"},{"fullname":"Xu Ji"},{"fullname":"Yikun Zhang"},{"fullname":"Shipeng Xie","username":"~Shipeng_Xie4"},{"fullname":"Tianling Lyu","username":"~Tianling_Lyu1"},{"fullname":"Yan Xi"},{"fullname":"Jian Yang"},{"fullname":"Yang Chen"}]}},"tmdate":1794277343699,"pdate":1767225600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1016/j.measurement.2025.118671"],"tcdate":1762955528189,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Shipeng_Xie4"],"forum":"czxzQZvUKB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":15279,"cdate":1755662970931,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1794277343699,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"czxzQZvUKB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.18851v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"kim|marking_code_without_breaking_it_code_watermarking_for_detecting_llmgenerated_code"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Shinwoo_Park1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yo-Sub_Han:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.18851"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2502-18851,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jungin Kim and Shinwoo Park and Yo-Sub Han},\n  title={Marking Code Without Breaking It: Code Watermarking for Detecting LLM-Generated Code},\n  year={2025},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1738368000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2502.18851},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.18851}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code watermarking identifies AI-generated code by embedding patterns into the code during generation. Effective watermarking requires meeting two key conditions: the watermark should be reliably detectable, and the code should retain its original functionality. However, existing methods often modify tokens that are critical for program logic, such as keywords in conditional expressions or operators in arithmetic computations. These modifications can cause syntax errors or functional failures, limiting the practical use of watermarking. We present STONE, a method that preserves functional integrity by selectively inserting watermarks only into non-syntax tokens. By excluding tokens essential for code execution, STONE minimizes the risk of functional degradation. In addition, we introduce CWEM, a comprehensive evaluation metric that evaluates watermarking techniques based on correctness, detectability, and naturalness. While correctness and detectability have been widely used, naturalness remains underexplored despite its importance. Unnatural patterns can reveal the presence of a watermark, making it easier for adversaries to remove. We evaluate STONE using CWEM and compare its performance with the state-of-the-art approach. The results show that STONE achieves an average improvement of 7.69% in CWEM across Python, C++, and Java. Our code is available in https://github.com/inistory/STONE-watermarking/."},"title":{"value":"Marking Code Without Breaking It: Code Watermarking for Detecting LLM-Generated Code"},"authors":{"value":["Jungin Kim","Shinwoo Park","Yo-Sub Han"]}},"tmdate":1791450474205,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1747377166048,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shinwoo_Park1"],"forum":"H961QPPkqt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":506217,"cdate":1738368000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1791450474205,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"H961QPPkqt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Robust Conformal","Anomaly Detection","Calibration Poisoning"]},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Conformal prediction is routinely used to wrap an anomaly detector in a\ndistribution-free coverage guarantee. That guarantee is about normal samples\nonly, and we show it can be satisfied by a detector that has stopped working.\nWhen an attacker places a small fraction of high-scoring points into the\ncalibration set, the conformal threshold moves past the range of plausible\nnormal scores, so coverage on normal data is met or exceeded while recall on\ntrue anomalies falls to near zero. We give a closed-form account: the operating\nlevel of standard split conformal under upper-tail contamination is\n$\\min\\{(1-\\alpha)/(1-\\epsilon),\\,1\\}$, so at exactly $\\epsilon=\\alpha$ the\ndetector stops raising false alarms and keeps only whatever power comes from\nanomalies scoring beyond the clean support, a collapse point predictable before\nany experiment is run. We then analyze a trimmed-quantile calibration rule with the level correction\n$\\alpha'=\\alpha(1+2\\epsilon)$. Writing its threshold as an order statistic of\nthe full calibration sample reduces the analysis to two short concentration\narguments and yields a two-sided bracket on the operating level whose upward\ndrift is $O(\\alpha\\epsilon^{2})$ rather than $O(\\epsilon)$, a power bound, and\nan expression for attainable coverage that shows where a symmetric trim can be\ndriven and motivates a one-sided variant needing no contamination parameter. Across 5 tabular benchmarks, 5\ndetectors, 3 attacker models, and frozen CLIP and MiniLM embeddings, standard\nconformal retains 12 to 19 percent of its clean power at $\\epsilon=0.10$ and 4\nto 7 percent at $\\epsilon=0.30$, and $0.1$ percent on an autoencoder, while the\ntrimmed rule stays above 97 percent everywhere."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nali2026robust,\ntitle={Robust Conformal Anomaly Detection under Calibration Poisoning},\nauthor={Sarwan Ali},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=FaBcqf2W1c}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Robust Conformal Anomaly Detection under Calibration Poisoning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f32f507a6129041b824e0322614d5f39b6bbc4c5.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"ali|robust_conformal_anomaly_detection_under_calibration_poisoning"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Sarwan_Ali1","fullname":"Sarwan Ali","institutions":[{"name":"Columbia University","domain":"cumc.columbia.edu","country":"US"}]}]}},"tmdate":1794163913394,"tcdate":1789780239682,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission51322/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission51322/Authors"],"forum":"FaBcqf2W1c","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":51322,"cdate":1789780239682,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission51322/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1794163913394,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"FaBcqf2W1c","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2025 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["language models","calibration","entropy","sampling","language model inference"]},"primary_area":{"value":"generative models"},"reciprocal_reviewing":{"value":"I understand the reciprocal reviewing requirement as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/CallForPapers. If none of the authors are registered as a reviewer, it may result in a desk rejection at the discretion of the program chairs. To request an exception, please complete this form at https://forms.gle/Huojr6VjkFxiQsUp6."},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Language models are trained with teacher forcing but are used autoregressively, so errors accumulate as more tokens are generated. This issue is well-studied but remains a fundamental problem that harms generation quality. Building on past work, we take the perspective that error accumulation is reflected in the model's entropy, so we can better understand and address it through the lens of entropy calibration. A language model is entropy calibrated if its entropy over generations, i.e. its confidence, matches the log loss it incurs on actual text. First, we find that models are indeed miscalibrated in practice: for base models across a range of sizes, entropy per step increases as more tokens are generated, leading to generations becoming incoherent over time. On the other hand, after instruction tuning, the largest models now have too little entropy (i.e. are overconfident), leading to a lack of diversity in model outputs. From a theoretical perspective, entropy calibration is difficult to attain because it is a global property of the entire generation process, which has an exponentially large output space. Per-step adjustments are tractable but fail to preserve the model's log loss, while global adjustments preserve log loss but are intractable. Our main theoretical contribution is to propose future entropy scaling, an adjustment to the next token probabilities that uses information about the future entropy of each token, i.e. the average entropy of continuations from that token. With additional assumptions, we prove that this adjustment calibrates the model while preserving log loss. While future entropy estimation is expensive, this result suggests that calibration and stabilization of the entropy should be possible without trading off model quality."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\ncao2024on,\ntitle={On the Entropy Calibration of Language Models},\nauthor={Steven Cao and Gregory Valiant and Percy Liang},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ZpQ2SqQNXf}\n}"},"title":{"value":"On the Entropy Calibration of Language Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/b6a37663da98cedc57098b447bd9d528013be612.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"cao|on_the_entropy_calibration_of_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Steven_Cao1","~Gregory_Valiant1","~Percy_Liang1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Steven Cao","Gregory Valiant","Percy Liang"]}},"tmdate":1731640363784,"tcdate":1727405888654,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission8708/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission8708/Authors"],"forum":"ZpQ2SqQNXf","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":8708,"cdate":1727405888654,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission8708/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1731640363784,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"ZpQ2SqQNXf","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Long submission (more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Under review for TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Gesture detection algorithms working on time-series measurements obtained from an inertial measurement unit (IMU) offer a privacy-preserving and user-friendly alternative to vision-based systems. Yet, adoption remains limited in applications where accurate calibration of the predicted probabilities and robustness against out-of-distribution (OOD) scenarios is necessary. To address these challenges, we propose Uncertainty-Aware Calibration (UAC), a framework designed to improve calibration and robustness in IMU-based gesture recognition in both in- and out-of-distribution scenarios. First, we introduce an uncertainty-aware gesture detection neural network that jointly predicts gesture probabilities and their associated uncertainties; the latter are used to calibrate the probabilities of each gesture prediction. Second, we propose an entropy-weighted expectation mechanism that aggregates predictions over multiple IMU time-series windows to improve accuracy while preserving calibration of the probabilities. We evaluate UAC on three public IMU gesture datasets, benchmarking against classical methods (SVM, random forest) and state-of-the-art neural network calibration techniques (temperature scaling, entropy maximization, Laplace approximation). Results demonstrate that UAC simultaneously improves accuracy, expected calibration error (ECE), and negative log-likelihood (NLL)---both in-distribution and OOD---whereas existing calibration methods fail to do so."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026uac,\ntitle={{UAC}: Uncertainty-Aware Calibration of Neural Networks for {IMU} Time Series Classification of Gestures},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=9X5WSC2OBu},\nnote={Under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"UAC: Uncertainty-Aware Calibration of Neural Networks for IMU Time Series Classification of Gestures"},"changes_since_last_submission":{"value":"To strengthen our claim that, especially under OOD shift, uncertainty prediction and uncertainty-aware entropy weighting improve calibration and reduce the impact of domain shift, we have made three changes to the paper:\n\n1. We have added paired t-tests over seeds for every comparison (all tables), together with the number of runs and the definition of the error bars. All reported error bars are standard deviations across 9 runs with different random seeds. To address the concern that improvements may be small relative to run-to-run variation, we added paired t-tests comparing our method to each baseline on every metric, with a significance threshold of p = 0.05. In the OOD setting, UAC significantly outperforms the ablation variants on both accuracy and NLL (Table 2). In the ID setting, the differences are smaller, but the calibration benefit of the uncertainty prediction is clear: $M_u$ achieves better ECE and NLL than $UAC_{\\neg \\sigma}$. Hence, although low entropy is not a perfect proxy for reliability, it is a better reliability indicator in our method than in the baselines.\n\n2. Comparison with alternative aggregation strategies. We have added two training-free alternatives applied to the same frozen window predictions: Max (use the window with the highest class probability) and Margin (use the window with the largest gap between its two highest probabilities). The results (Table 2) show a split:\n    1. In ID, Max and Margin are better calibrated than UAC but less accurate. Notably, **they also outperform the state-of-the-art calibration baselines (Table 1)**, since UAC itself outperforms those baselines.\n    2. In OOD, Max and Margin degrade to ECE = 0.21–0.28 and NLL = 1.4–2.4, while UAC maintains ECE = 0.07–0.10 and NLL = 0.7–1.7 with higher accuracy (all differences significant at p < 0.01). Max and Margin rely on raw confidence, which is miscalibrated under distribution shift; UAC's use of the predicted aleatoric uncertainty instead of raw confidence preserves both accuracy and calibration.\n\nWe are currently training the missing USCHAD configurations and will update the paper as soon as those results are available (expected shortly).\n\nWe have expanded the description of the IMU inputs in Sec. 3.1 (IMU Data Pre-Processing) and of the datasets in Sec. 4.2, addressing each requested addition:\n\n- We now explain that each time step of an IMU recording yields a $D$-dimensional feature vector composed of the 3-axis linear acceleration ($a_x$, $a_y$, $a_z$, in m/s², including the gravitational acceleration) recorded by the accelerometer and the 3-axis angular velocity ($\\omega_x$, $\\omega_y$, $\\omega_z$, in rad/s) recorded by the gyroscope, plus the 3-axis orientation for SAMOSA ($D = 9$; $D = 6$ for WISDM and USCHAD).\n- Each sample $x_i$ extracted by the sliding window is a matrix of shape $m \\times D$ ($m = 100$ time steps; $D = 6$ or $9$), i.e., $x_i \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times D}$, and a mini-batch of $B$ samples has shape $B \\times m \\times D$.\n- Fig. 3(a) shows all 6 IMU channels of one window of 100 time steps (5 s at 20 Hz) from a real WISDM smartwatch recording of a drinking gesture.\n- Fig. 3(b) shows how a gesture sequence is divided into overlapping windows of $m = 100$ time steps extracted every 10 time steps (0.5 s at 20 Hz), with the window length, stride, and the first and last windows annotated.\n\nWe have rewritten the uncertainty formulation in Sec. 3.2 to address each point of the review, clarifying the implementation of the input-dependent Gaussian over the logits and the i.i.d. assumption.\n\nWe added more information about the datasets in the main text. The datasets and splits we used are balanced: each activity is repeated the same number of times by each participant:\n\n* [WISDM](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/507/wisdm+smartphone+and+smartwatch+activity+and+biometrics+dataset): Raw accelerometer and gyroscope data collected from the smartphone and smartwatch at 20 Hz while 51 participants performed 18 activities for 3 minutes each.\n* [SAMOSA](https://github.com/cmusmashlab/SAMoSA/tree/main): 20 participants, 26 activities in 4 contexts, with each activity repeated 3 times.\n* [USCHAD](https://sipi.usc.edu/had/): 14 participants, 12 activities, 5 trials per activity.\n\nFinal decisions are obtained by taking the argmax of the final predicted probabilities.\n\nWe also extended the conclusion and future work to take in account remarks from the reviewers concerning possible future work and interesting directions."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/9cfa13ca68fbadae25418a4d525123aca55a3a85.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Under_Review"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Matias_Alejandro_Valdenegro-Toro1"}},"tmdate":1792533925855,"tcdate":1784563470404,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper10680/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper10680/Authors"],"forum":"9X5WSC2OBu","number":10680,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1784563470404,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper10680/-/Revision"],"mdate":1792533925855,"odate":1785802198438,"domain":"TMLR","id":"9X5WSC2OBu","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"This paper studies post-training quantization of reasoning LLMs via the lens of ternary quantization"},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Desk Rejected Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["large language model","post-training quantization","quantization-aware training","ternary quantization","reasoning tasks"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we present ScaleQ-1.58, a ternary post-training quantization (PTQ) framework for reasoning LLMs. Its core insight stems from an empirical finding: although modern LLMs are typically trained to have chain-of-thought reasoning capabilities, in the PTQ regime, even recently proposed differentiable ternarization still leads to performance collapse on challenging mathematics and coding tasks. We reveal that this is caused by conventional calibration schemes that ignore the model's reasoning process. Motivated by this, we present Attend to Your Own Thoughts (AYOT), a new and simple calibration method, which constitutes the core contribution of this paper. Given a pre-trained target LLM, AYOT uses its self-generated reasoning traces and final answers on a proper set of calibration samples, together with the corresponding questions, as context inputs during ternarization. ScaleQ-1.58 is built by combining AYOT with differentiable ternarization, and demonstrates several favorable scaling properties. With only 4M calibration tokens: (1) Qwen3-1.7B ternarized by ScaleQ-1.58 reaches over 90.52% of the performance of the prior best BitNet b1.58 2B4T averaged over 4 mathematics and coding tasks, and our ternary Qwen3-4B shows an absolute gain of 8.97%, while requiring 1,000,000$\\times$ fewer calibration tokens for ternarization; (2) ScaleQ-1.58 generalizes well to 8 dense and MoE architectures, with performance significantly improving as model scale increases from 1.7B to 235B parameters; (3) ScaleQ-1.58 exhibits strong generalization across 13 tasks of varying difficulty levels, including challenging mathematics, coding and scientific logic reasoning, as well as basic commonsense reasoning and language generation. Intriguingly, ScaleQ-1.58 shows continuously improving performance as the number of calibration tokens increases. Besides, AYOT generalizes well beyond 1.58-bit quantization to other bit-width settings. With the default 4M calibration tokens, ScaleQ-1.58 requires only 4 to 240 hours on 8 A100-80GB GPUs to ternarize 1.7B to 235B reasoning LLMs."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nanonymous2026attend,\ntitle={Attend to Your Own Thoughts: Calibration Matters for 1.58-Bit Reasoning {LLM}s},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=UuAAvOn17o}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Attend to Your Own Thoughts: Calibration Matters for 1.58-Bit Reasoning LLMs"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/72c255bce33869ef285cdd417161a247e5f65236.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Desk_Rejected_Submission"}},"tmdate":1792254765094,"tcdate":1789350891992,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission16568/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission16568/Authors"],"forum":"UuAAvOn17o","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":16568,"cdate":1789350891992,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission16568/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Desk_Rejected_Submission"],"mdate":1792254765094,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"UuAAvOn17o","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.13990v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"campos|qcore_dataefficient_ondevice_continual_calibration_for_quantized_models_extended_version"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:David_Campos_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bin_Yang_0002:","~Tung_Kieu1","~Miao_Zhang4","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chenjuan_Guo:","~Christian_S._Jensen1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.13990"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2404-13990,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={David Campos and Bin Yang and Tung Kieu and Miao Zhang and Chenjuan Guo and Christian S. Jensen},\n  title={QCore: Data-Efficient, On-Device Continual Calibration for Quantized Models - Extended Version},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2404.13990},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.13990}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We are witnessing an increasing availability of streaming data that may contain valuable information on the underlying processes. It is thus attractive to be able to deploy machine learning models on edge devices near sensors such that decisions can be made instantaneously, rather than first having to transmit incoming data to servers. To enable deployment on edge devices with limited storage and computational capabilities, the full-precision parameters in standard models can be quantized to use fewer bits. The resulting quantized models are then calibrated using back-propagation and full training data to ensure accuracy. This one-time calibration works for deployments in static environments. However, model deployment in dynamic edge environments call for continual calibration to adaptively adjust quantized models to fit new incoming data, which may have different distributions. The first difficulty in enabling continual calibration on the edge is that the full training data may be too large and thus not always available on edge devices. The second difficulty is that the use of back-propagation on the edge for repeated calibration is too expensive. We propose QCore to enable continual calibration on the edge. First, it compresses the full training data into a small subset to enable effective calibration of quantized models with different bit-widths. We also propose means of updating the subset when new streaming data arrives to reflect changes in the environment, while not forgetting earlier training data. Second, we propose a small bit-flipping network that works with the subset to update quantized model parameters, thus enabling efficient continual calibration without back-propagation. An experimental study, conducted with real-world data in a continual learning setting, offers insight into the properties of QCore and shows that it is capable of outperforming strong baseline methods."},"title":{"value":"QCore: Data-Efficient, On-Device Continual Calibration for Quantized Models - Extended Version"},"authors":{"value":["David Campos","Bin Yang","Tung Kieu","Miao Zhang","Chenjuan Guo","Christian S. Jensen"]}},"tmdate":1784598760277,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1722951609436,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Tung_Kieu1"],"forum":"LRm7O7MuUI","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":54957,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784598760277,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"LRm7O7MuUI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Models","Machine Learning for Code Editing","Code Generation","Reasoning","Reinforcement Learning"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/6037bf16ef4f2f0bde1cddb675ffe1b914ceebbd.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nli2026semrep,\ntitle={SemRep : Generative Code Representation Learning with Code Transformations},\nauthor={Weichen Li and Jiamin Song and Bogdan Alexandru Stoica and Arav Dhoot and Gabriel Ryan and Shengyu Fu and Kexin Pei},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=d81PBCmA4s}\n}"},"title":{"value":"SemRep : Generative Code Representation Learning with Code Transformations"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|semrep_generative_code_representation_learning_with_code_transformations"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/346226362dbe59689900b56973621a64ad00dc21.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"applications->everything_else"},"abstract":{"value":"Code transformation is a foundational capability in the software development process, where its effectiveness relies on constructing a high-quality code representation to characterize the input code semantics and guide the transformation.\n    Existing approaches treat code transformation as an end-to-end learning task, leaving the construction of the representation needed for semantic reasoning implicit in model weights or relying on rigid compiler-level abstractions.\n    We present SemRep, a framework that improves code transformation through *generative code representation learning*.\n    Our key insight is to employ the semantics-preserving transformations as the intermediate representation, which serves as both a generative mid-training task and the guidance for subsequent instruction-specific code transformations.\n    Across general code editing and optimization tasks (e.g., GPU kernel optimization), SemRep outperforms the extensively finetuned baselines with strictly the same training budget by 6.9\\% in correctness, 1.1$\\times$ in performance, 13.9\\% in generalization, and 6.7\\% in robustness.\n    With the improved exploration of diverse code transformations, SemRep is particularly amenable to evolutionary search.\n    Combined with an evolutionary coding agent, SemRep finds optimizations that 685B larger-weight baselines fail to discover while achieving the same performance with 25\\% less inference compute."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a250932c84df6c3b4aef7e6df82f2efbd3dbe8ca.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Code transformation, e.g., editing code to fix bugs, add features, or improve performance, is a core task in software engineering. \nCurrent large language models attempt these edits end-to-end, jumping directly from input code to the desired output. \nThis often leads to errors because the model never explicitly reasons about what the original code does before changing it.\n\nSemRep addresses this by disentangling semantic understanding from task-specific code editing. The model is first trained to generate semantically equivalent rewrites of the input code — programs that look different but behave identically, verified through test execution. This serves as a form of generative representation learning, forcing the model to internalize code semantics as explicit, human-readable intermediate programs rather than latent weight parameters. The model is then finetuned for the specific editing task. Crucially, both training phases share a fixed total budget, so the approach introduces no additional training cost over standard finetuning.\n\nDuring inference, SemRep allows the model to explore semantically equivalent variants of the input before applying the requested transformation. This naturally supports test-time scaling through evolutionary search, where diverse equivalent rewrites serve as stepping stones toward better solutions.\n\nExperiments on GPU kernel optimization (KernelBench) and real-world code editing (EditBench) show that SemRep enables a 32B model to match or exceed commercial systems and open-weight models up to 12× larger. \nIt improves correctness by up to 43% over the previous state of the art, generalizes 13.9% better to unseen hardware, and is 6.7% more robust to surface-level code perturbations. \nWhen integrated with an evolutionary coding agent, SemRep discovers optimizations that 685B-parameter models fail to find while using 25% less inference compute."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Weichen_Li1","~Jiamin_Song4","~Bogdan_Alexandru_Stoica1","~Arav_Dhoot1","~Gabriel_Ryan1","~Shengyu_Fu1","~Kexin_Pei1"]},"authors":{"value":["Weichen Li","Jiamin Song","Bogdan Alexandru Stoica","Arav Dhoot","Gabriel Ryan","Shengyu Fu","Kexin Pei"]}},"tmdate":1790067349680,"pdate":1777576414281,"tcdate":1769027083358,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12780/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12780/Authors"],"forum":"d81PBCmA4s","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":12780,"cdate":1769027083358,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12780/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12780/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067349680,"odate":1782341947641,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"d81PBCmA4s","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Continual Learning","Class-Incremental Learning","Calibration"]},"primary_area":{"value":"transfer learning, meta learning, and lifelong learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Reliable class-incremental learning requires confidence estimates that remain informative after sequential training, because downstream decisions such as selective prediction and human referral depend on per-sample confidence rather than on accuracy alone. Calibration is typically summarized by aggregate scores, yet such averages can hide substantial errors within groups of classes introduced at different times during the training schedule. In this paper, we show that global temperature scaling can improve overall calibration while leaving large errors in specific schedule-age groups, and that the direction of these group-level changes differs across learners. Aggregate improvement therefore does not by itself certify uniformly reliable confidence. To make this discrepancy visible, we introduce a group-aware audit that evaluates aggregate and group-level calibration jointly, together with the direction of confidence bias, the source of calibration data, and the scale of the scores being calibrated. Measured against random groupings that keep classes intact, the residual is specific to schedule age in every tested learner whose forgetting concentrates errors on older classes. On the primary benchmark, age-informed fitting also reduces it far more than budget-matched random groupings. In the tested high-accuracy pretrained models the residual is smaller and not consistently larger than ordinary heterogeneity among classes. At fixed checkpoints, the temperature search range and the data used for fitting can change the measured calibration by about an order of magnitude. These findings support reporting group-level calibration alongside aggregate scores whenever continual learners are compared or deployed."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026the,\ntitle={The Illusion of Global Calibration: Group-Aware Risk in Class-Incremental Learning},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=yeZnoRHXLd},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"The Illusion of Global Calibration: Group-Aware Risk in Class-Incremental Learning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6c61acc64904864d3ee5426f3d138b6b0772f045.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794284794937,"tcdate":1789356141531,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission16742/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission16742/Authors"],"forum":"yeZnoRHXLd","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":16742,"cdate":1789356141531,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission16742/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission16742/-/Rebuttal_Revision"],"mdate":1794284794937,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"yeZnoRHXLd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9981026/9981028/09981558.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"wei|croon_automatic_multilidar_calibration_and_refinement_method_in_road_scene"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pengjin_Wei:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guohang_Yan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yikang_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kun_Fang_0004:","~Xinyu_Cai2","~Jie_Yang84","~Wei_Liu38"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS47612.2022.9981558"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/WeiYLFCYL22,\n  author={Pengjin Wei and Guohang Yan and Yikang Li and Kun Fang and Xinyu Cai and Jie Yang and Wei Liu},\n  title={CROON: Automatic Multi-LiDAR Calibration and Refinement Method in Road Scene},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={12857-12863},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS47612.2022.9981558},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Sensor-based environmental perception is a crucial part of the autonomous driving system. In order to get an excellent perception of the surrounding environment, an intelligent system would configure multiple LiDARs (3D Light Detection and Ranging) to cover the distant and near space of the car. The precision of perception relies on the quality of sensor calibration. This research aims at developing an accurate, automatic, and robust calibration strategy for multiple LiDAR systems in the general road scene. We thus propose CROON (automatic multi-LiDAR Calibration and Refinement methOd in rOad sceNe), a two-stage method including rough and refinement calibration. The first stage can calibrate the sensor from an arbitrary initial pose, and the second stage is able to precisely calibrate the sensor iteratively. Specifically, CROON utilize the nature characteristics of road scene so that it is independent and easy to apply in large-scale conditions. Experimental results on real-world and simulated data sets demonstrate the reliability and accuracy of our method. All the related data sets and codes are open-sourced on the Github website https://github.com/OpenCalib/LiDAR2LiDAR."},"title":{"value":"CROON: Automatic Multi-LiDAR Calibration and Refinement Method in Road Scene"},"authors":{"value":["Pengjin Wei","Guohang Yan","Yikang Li","Kun Fang","Xinyu Cai","Jie Yang","Wei Liu"]}},"tmdate":1791950971417,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1715747124780,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wei_Liu38"],"forum":"l7RXfpE3dH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":12550,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1791950971417,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"l7RXfpE3dH","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"venue":{"value":"BSN 2026"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["Brain-computer interface","steady-state visual evoked potential","calibration-free","spatio-temporal filtering"]},"nominate_reviewer":{"value":"Yue Zhang, yz4604@bath.ac.uk, University of Bath"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration-free steady-state visual evoked potential (SSVEP)-based brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) aim to provide plug-and-play target recognition without subject-specific training data. Although existing online adaptive methods improve calibration-free performance by learning from incoming trials, most of them focus mainly on spatial adaptation and do not explicitly model local temporal-delay characteristics of SSVEP responses. This paper proposes online adaptive spatio-temporal canonical correlation analysis (OAST-CCA), which incorporates delay-embedded spatio-temporal discriminant features into calibration-free online adaptation. OAST-CCA constructs augmented EEG and template representations using delay embedding, learns a spatio-temporal global spatial filter from pseudo-labeled online trials, and combines reference-driven, online adaptive, and spatio-temporal matching features for target recognition. Experiments on a public 40-target SSVEP benchmark dataset with 35 subjects show that OAST-CCA outperforms filter bank CCA (FBCCA) and online adaptive CCA (OACCA) across different time windows. Feature ablation results further show the complementary contribution of the proposed feature set. These findings demonstrate the potential of local temporal-delay structures for improving calibration-free SSVEP decoding."},"confirmation":{"value":"I have read and agree with the IEEE BSN 2026 conference submission's policy on behalf of myself and my co-authors."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhang2026online,\ntitle={Online Adaptive Spatio-Temporal {CCA} for Calibration-Free {SSVEP}-Based {BCI}s},\nauthor={Yue Zhang},\nbooktitle={IEEE-EMBS International Conference on Body Sensor Networks 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=2dcrEoX8Zo}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Online Adaptive Spatio-Temporal CCA for Calibration-Free SSVEP-Based BCIs"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/da1f534deab5acdd8c8edd7cd1c24d3ce9ad8b61.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|online_adaptive_spatiotemporal_cca_for_calibrationfree_ssvepbased_bcis"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yue_Zhang98"]},"track":{"value":"8. Signal processing, machine learning, deep learning, and decision-support algorithms"},"authors":{"value":["Yue Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1785927438609,"pdate":1785447366281,"tcdate":1781546382209,"writers":["IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference","IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference/Submission158/Authors"],"signatures":["IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference/Submission158/Authors"],"forum":"2dcrEoX8Zo","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":158,"cdate":1781546382209,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference/-/Submission","IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference/-/Submission_Release","IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference/Submission158/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785927438609,"odate":1785447366281,"domain":"IEEE.org/EMBS/BSN/2026/Conference","id":"2dcrEoX8Zo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.05888v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"shalaby|calibration_and_uncertainty_characterization_for_ultrawideband_twowayranging_measurements"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohammed_Ayman_Shalaby:","~Charles_Champagne_Cossette1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:James_Richard_Forbes:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jerome_Le_Ny:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2210.05888"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2210-05888,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Mohammed Ayman Shalaby and Charles Champagne Cossette and James Richard Forbes and Jerome Le Ny},\n  title={Calibration and Uncertainty Characterization for Ultra-Wideband Two-Way-Ranging Measurements},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2210.05888},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2210.05888}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Ultra-Wideband (UWB) systems are becoming increasingly popular for indoor localization, where range measurements are obtained by measuring the time-of-flight of radio signals. However, the range measurements typically suffer from a systematic error or bias that must be corrected for high-accuracy localization. In this paper, a ranging protocol is proposed alongside a robust and scalable antenna-delay calibration procedure to accurately and efficiently calibrate antenna delays for many UWB tags. Additionally, the bias and uncertainty of the measurements are modelled as a function of the received-signal power. The full calibration procedure is presented using experimental training data of 3 aerial robots fitted with 2 UWB tags each, and then evaluated on 2 test experiments. A localization problem is then formulated on the experimental test data, and the calibrated measurements and their modelled uncertainty are fed into an extended Kalman filter (EKF). The proposed calibration is shown to yield an average of 46% improvement in localization accuracy. Lastly, the paper is accompanied by an open-source UWB-calibration Python library, which can be found at https://github.com/decargroup/uwb_calibration."},"title":{"value":"Calibration and Uncertainty Characterization for Ultra-Wideband Two-Way-Ranging Measurements"},"authors":{"value":["Mohammed Ayman Shalaby","Charles Champagne Cossette","James Richard Forbes","Jerome Le Ny"]}},"tmdate":1762733023807,"pdate":1640995200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2210-05888"],"tcdate":1762733014645,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Charles_Cossette1"],"forum":"YcuYCbF0Xj","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":684136,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762733023807,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"YcuYCbF0Xj","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10160211/10160212/10160769.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"shalaby|calibration_and_uncertainty_characterization_for_ultrawideband_twowayranging_measurements"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohammed_Ayman_Shalaby:","~Charles_Champagne_Cossette1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:James_Richard_Forbes:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jerome_Le_Ny:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA48891.2023.10160769"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/ShalabyCFN23,\n  author={Mohammed Ayman Shalaby and Charles Champagne Cossette and James Richard Forbes and Jerome Le Ny},\n  title={Calibration and Uncertainty Characterization for Ultra-Wideband Two-Way-Ranging Measurements},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={4128-4134},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA48891.2023.10160769},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Ultra-Wideband (UWB) systems are becoming increasingly popular for indoor localization, where range measurements are obtained by measuring the time-of-flight of radio signals. However, the range measurements typically suffer from a systematic error or bias that must be corrected for high-accuracy localization. In this paper, a ranging protocol is proposed alongside a robust and scalable antenna-delay calibration procedure to accurately and efficiently calibrate antenna delays for many UWB tags. Additionally, the bias and uncertainty of the measurements are modelled as a function of the received-signal power. The full calibration procedure is presented using experimental training data of 3 aerial robots fitted with 2 UWB tags each, and then evaluated on 2 test experiments. A localization problem is then formulated on the experimental test data, and the calibrated measurements and their modelled uncertainty are fed into an extended Kalman filter (EKF). The proposed calibration is shown to yield an average of 46% improvement in localization accuracy. Lastly, the paper is accompanied by an open-source UWB-calibration Python library, which can be found at https://github.com/decargroup/uwb_calibration."},"title":{"value":"Calibration and Uncertainty Characterization for Ultra-Wideband Two-Way-Ranging Measurements"},"authors":{"value":["Mohammed Ayman Shalaby","Charles Champagne Cossette","James Richard Forbes","Jerome Le Ny"]}},"tmdate":1762733016984,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icra/ShalabyCFN23"],"tcdate":1762733014211,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Charles_Cossette1"],"forum":"EKSxHVoxDo","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":684121,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762733016984,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"EKSxHVoxDo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph. 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/2945/9721431/09200793.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TVCG/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|a_probability_densitybased_visual_analytics_approach_to_forecast_bias_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Renpei_Huang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Quan_Li:","~Li_Chen7","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoru_Yuan:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2020.3025072"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tvcg/HuangLCY22,\n  author={Renpei Huang and Quan Li and Li Chen and Xiaoru Yuan},\n  title={A Probability Density-Based Visual Analytics Approach to Forecast Bias Calibration},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph.},\n  volume={28},\n  number={4},\n  pages={1732-1744},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2020.3025072}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Biases inevitably occur in numerical weather prediction (NWP) due to an idealized numerical assumption for modeling chaotic atmospheric systems. Therefore, the rapid and accurate identification and calibration of biases is crucial for NWP in weather forecasting. Conventional approaches, such as various analog post-processing forecast methods, have been designed to aid in bias calibration. However, these approaches fail to consider the spatiotemporal correlations of forecast bias, which can considerably affect calibration efficacy. In this article, we propose a novel bias pattern extraction approach based on forecasting-observation probability density by merging historical forecasting and observation datasets. Given a spatiotemporal scope, our approach extracts and fuses bias patterns and automatically divides regions with similar bias patterns. Termed BicaVis , our spatiotemporal bias pattern visual analytics system is proposed to assist experts in drafting calibration curves on the basis of these bias patterns. To verify the effectiveness of our approach, we conduct two case studies with real-world reanalysis datasets. The feedback collected from domain experts confirms the efficacy of our approach."},"title":{"value":"A Probability Density-Based Visual Analytics Approach to Forecast Bias Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Renpei Huang","Quan Li","Li Chen","Xiaoru Yuan"]}},"tmdate":1731687036000,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731687016052,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Li_Chen7"],"forum":"iQqsgElP7q","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":249047,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731687036000,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"iQqsgElP7q","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9981026/9981028/09981558.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"wei|croon_automatic_multilidar_calibration_and_refinement_method_in_road_scene"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pengjin_Wei:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guohang_Yan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yikang_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kun_Fang_0004:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinyu_Cai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Yang_0002:","~Wei_Liu38"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS47612.2022.9981558"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/WeiYLFCYL22,\n  author={Pengjin Wei and Guohang Yan and Yikang Li and Kun Fang and Xinyu Cai and Jie Yang and Wei Liu},\n  title={CROON: Automatic Multi-LiDAR Calibration and Refinement Method in Road Scene},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={12857-12863},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS47612.2022.9981558},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Sensor-based environmental perception is a crucial part of the autonomous driving system. In order to get an excellent perception of the surrounding environment, an intelligent system would configure multiple LiDARs (3D Light Detection and Ranging) to cover the distant and near space of the car. The precision of perception relies on the quality of sensor calibration. This research aims at developing an accurate, automatic, and robust calibration strategy for multiple LiDAR systems in the general road scene. We thus propose CROON (automatic multi-LiDAR Calibration and Refinement methOd in rOad sceNe), a two-stage method including rough and refinement calibration. The first stage can calibrate the sensor from an arbitrary initial pose, and the second stage is able to precisely calibrate the sensor iteratively. Specifically, CROON utilize the nature characteristics of road scene so that it is independent and easy to apply in large-scale conditions. Experimental results on real-world and simulated data sets demonstrate the reliability and accuracy of our method. All the related data sets and codes are open-sourced on the Github website https://github.com/OpenCalib/LiDAR2LiDAR."},"title":{"value":"CROON: Automatic Multi-LiDAR Calibration and Refinement Method in Road Scene"},"authors":{"value":["Pengjin Wei","Guohang Yan","Yikang Li","Kun Fang","Xinyu Cai","Jie Yang","Wei Liu"]}},"tmdate":1715747124966,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1715747118229,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wei_Liu38"],"forum":"xe2FZ6tNPB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":12535,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1715747124966,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xe2FZ6tNPB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2024"},"TLDR":{"value":"Calibration error serves as an effective criterion for selecting teachers in KD, and employing calibration methods can further enhance KD performance."},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["knowledge distillation","Calibration"]},"primary_area":{"value":"general machine learning (i.e., none of the above)"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Knowledge distillation (KD) is one of the successful deep learning compression methods for edge devices, transferring the knowledge from a large model, known as the *teacher*, to a smaller model, referred to as the *student*. KD has demonstrated remarkable performance since its first introduction. However, recent research in KD reveals that using a higher-performance teacher network does not guarantee better performance of the student network. This naturally leads to a question about the criterion for choosing an appropriate teacher. In this paper, we reveal that there is a strong correlation between the calibration error of the teacher and the accuracy of the student. Therefore, we claim that the calibration error of the teacher model can be a selection criterion for knowledge distillation. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the performance of KD can be improved by simply applying a temperature-based calibration method that reduces the teacher's calibration error. Our algorithm can be easily applied to other methods, and when applied on top of the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) model, it achieves a new SOTA performance."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nkim2024unlocking,\ntitle={Unlocking the Potential of Knowledge Distillation: The Role of Teacher Calibration},\nauthor={Suyoung Kim and SeongUk Park and JunHoo Lee and Nojun Kwak},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=TQWXWtJSda}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Unlocking the Potential of Knowledge Distillation: The Role of Teacher Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c7081bf5a53d9776296796773ec0fe51d6327e49.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"kim|unlocking_the_potential_of_knowledge_distillation_the_role_of_teacher_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Suyoung_Kim2","~SeongUk_Park1","~JunHoo_Lee1","~Nojun_Kwak1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Suyoung Kim","SeongUk Park","JunHoo Lee","Nojun Kwak"]}},"tmdate":1707625666893,"tcdate":1695010776450,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1117/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1117/Authors"],"forum":"TQWXWtJSda","number":1117,"cdate":1695010776450,"mdate":1707625666893,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1117/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"TQWXWtJSda","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.23744v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"shihab|calibrationpreserving_pruning_compression_as_a_reliability_contract"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.23744"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2608-23744,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ibne Farabi Shihab and Adria Binte Habib and Anuj Sharma},\n  title={Calibration-Preserving Pruning: Compression as a Reliability Contract},\n  year={2026},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1785542400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2608.23744},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.23744}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Split conformal prediction, not the pruning rule, supplies finite-sample marginal coverage once a pruned model is fixed independently of the conformal calibration split. We study the separate efficiency problem: can pruning preserve score geometry well enough to obtain smaller valid prediction sets? Calibration-Preserving Pruning (CPP) augments a base pruning score with nonconformity-gradient saliency and uses disjoint pruning, validation-selection, conformal-calibration, and test splits. Bounded score perturbations imply bounded conformal-quantile shifts and controlled set inflation, but do not make the generic coverage theorem CPP-specific. Final five-seed Qwen2.5-1.5B results at 50\\% sparsity show the largest gains on large-label tasks. On DBpedia-14, CPP-SparseGPT reduces mean set size from \\(10.1\\) to \\(8.6\\) while changing accuracy from \\(0.347\\) to \\(0.366\\); CPP-Wanda reduces \\(11.2\\) to \\(9.0\\) with an accuracy trade-off from \\(0.310\\) to \\(0.295\\). Across 15 dataset--sparsity cells, CPP-SparseGPT produces smaller sets in 13 and higher accuracy in 11. Matched controls show that generic supervised gradients explain much of the gain: true-label CPP is not statistically resolved from matched Wanda+SNIP, whereas threshold-aware candidate-label CPP reaches \\(7.8\\) mean set size at explicit accuracy and offline-compute costs. RoBERTa-base and Llama-3-8B diagnostics support transfer, but our claims remain limited to reliability-sensitive classification."},"title":{"value":"Calibration-Preserving Pruning: Compression as a Reliability Contract"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Ibne Farabi Shihab","username":""},{"fullname":"Adria Binte Habib","username":""},{"fullname":"Anuj Sharma","username":"~Anuj_Sharma1"}]}},"tmdate":1791746075623,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2608-23744"],"tcdate":1791746066359,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Anuj_Sharma1"],"forum":"mVJh579EtB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":171171,"cdate":1785542400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791746075623,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"mVJh579EtB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Journal of Physics: Conference Series"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"batalo|statistical_moments_for_simulation_calibration_with_modelbridge"},"authorids":{"value":["~Bojan_Batalo1","~Lincon_Souza1","~Keisuke_Yamazaki1"]},"html":{"value":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1742-6596/2701/1/012047"},"abstract":{"value":"Computer simulations are actively used for analyzing complex phenomena, especially in fields where access to their real-world counterparts is not feasible, such as physics, chemistry, material science, and others. The key to executing successful simulations is making sure that the parameters of a simulator reflect real-world scenarios, a tedious and error-prone effort addressed through simulation calibration. Recently, several methods have been proposed to automatize this task by learning from previously calibrated simulations using a model-bridge paradigm: a complex simulation is replaced by a simpler surrogate model, which can then be bridged to the calibrated simulation parameters. However, designing the surrogate model is a non-trivial problem involving trade-offs between simplicity of representation, interpretability and calibration accuracy, as well as the complexity of the bridge model required to map the surrogate to calibrated parameters. Further, while effective, such approaches can be non-intuitive for practitioners due to their distance from the simulation. In this paper, we view a simulation as a distribution of output variables, which can be easily represented by statistical moments. This yields a very simple and interpretable surrogate that can be bridged to calibrated parameters with a simple linear regression. We show that our method outperforms previous approaches, in terms of calibration accuracy and time, through experiments on simulations of turbulent flow dynamics and synthetic signals."},"title":{"value":"Statistical moments for simulation calibration with model-bridge"},"authors":{"value":["Bojan Batalo","Lincon Souza","Keisuke Yamazaki"]}},"tmdate":1751517022018,"pdate":1708873200000,"tcdate":1751517022018,"writers":["~Bojan_Batalo1","~Lincon_Souza1","~Keisuke_Yamazaki1"],"signatures":["~Bojan_Batalo1"],"forum":"YgwfB3SINo","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","number":37537,"cdate":1751517022018,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1751517022018,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"YgwfB3SINo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.05005v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|a_highaccuracy_calibration_method_of_transient_tseps_for_power_semiconductor_devices"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.05005"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-05005,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Qinghao Zhang and Wenrui Li and Pinjia Zhang},\n  title={A High-accuracy Calibration Method of Transient TSEPs for Power Semiconductor Devices},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2501.05005},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.05005}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The thermal sensitive electrical parameter (TSEP) method is crucial for enhancing the reliability of power devices through junction temperature monitoring. The TSEP method comprises three key processes: calibration, regression, and application. While significant efforts have been devoted to improving regression algorithms and increasing TSEP sensitivity to enhance junction temperature monitoring accuracy, these approaches have reached a bottleneck. In reality, the calibration method significantly influences monitoring accuracy, an aspect often overlooked in conventional TSEP methods. To address this issue, we propose a high-accuracy calibration method for transient TSEPs. First, a temperature compensation strategy based on thermal analysis is introduced to mitigate the temperature difference caused by load current during dual pulse tests. Second, the impact of stray parameters is analyzed to identify coupled parameters, which are typically neglected in existing methods. Third, it is observed that random errors follow a logarithm Gaussian distribution, covering a hidden variable. A neural network is used to obtain the junction temperature predictive model. The proposed calibration method is experimental validated in threshold voltage as an example. Compared with conventional calibration methods, the mean absolute error is reduced by over 30%. Moreover, this method does not require additional hardware cost and has good generalization."},"title":{"value":"A High-accuracy Calibration Method of Transient TSEPs for Power Semiconductor Devices"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Qinghao Zhang","username":"~Qinghao_Zhang7"},{"fullname":"Wenrui Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Pinjia Zhang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1792860538015,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2501-05005"],"tcdate":1792860529191,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~QingHao_Zhang1"],"forum":"hatz6lrVoI","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":207926,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1792860538015,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"hatz6lrVoI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.10232v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"lin|beyond_static_calibration_the_impact_of_user_preference_dynamics_on_calibrated_recommendation"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.10232"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2405-10232,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Kun Lin and Masoud Mansoury and Farzad Eskandanian and Milad Sabouri and Bamshad Mobasher},\n  title={Beyond Static Calibration: The Impact of User Preference Dynamics on Calibrated Recommendation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2405.10232},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.10232}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration in recommender systems is an important performance criterion that ensures consistency between the distribution of user preference categories and that of recommendations generated by the system. Standard methods for mitigating miscalibration typically assume that user preference profiles are static, and they measure calibration relative to the full history of user's interactions, including possibly outdated and stale preference categories. We conjecture that this approach can lead to recommendations that, while appearing calibrated, in fact, distort users' true preferences. In this paper, we conduct a preliminary investigation of recommendation calibration at a more granular level, taking into account evolving user preferences. By analyzing differently sized training time windows from the most recent interactions to the oldest, we identify the most relevant segment of user's preferences that optimizes the calibration metric. We perform an exploratory analysis with datasets from different domains with distinctive user-interaction characteristics. We demonstrate how the evolving nature of user preferences affects recommendation calibration, and how this effect is manifested differently depending on the characteristics of the data in a given domain. Datasets, codes, and more detailed experimental results are available at: https://github.com/nicolelin13/DynamicCalibrationUMAP."},"title":{"value":"Beyond Static Calibration: The Impact of User Preference Dynamics on Calibrated Recommendation"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Kun Lin","username":""},{"fullname":"Masoud Mansoury","username":""},{"fullname":"Farzad Eskandanian","username":""},{"fullname":"Milad Sabouri","username":""},{"fullname":"Bamshad Mobasher","username":"~Bamshad_Mobasher1"}]}},"tmdate":1784562353526,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2405-10232"],"tcdate":1784562338518,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Bamshad_Mobasher1"],"forum":"lyYNsLtpM4","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":75794,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784562353526,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"lyYNsLtpM4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.583.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|enhancing_language_model_factuality_via_activationbased_confidence_calibration_and_guided_decoding"},"authorids":{"value":["","","~Lu_Wang1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.583"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/LiuBW24,\n  author={Xin Liu and Farima Fatahi Bayat and Lu Wang},\n  title={Enhancing Language Model Factuality via Activation-Based Confidence Calibration and Guided Decoding},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={10436-10448},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.583},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrating language models (LMs) aligns their generation confidence with the actual likelihood of answer correctness, which can inform users about LMs’ reliability and mitigate hallucinated content. However, prior calibration methods, such as self-consistency-based and logit-based approaches, are either limited in inference-time efficiency or fall short of providing informative signals. Moreover, simply filtering out low-confidence responses reduces the LM’s helpfulness when the answers are correct. Therefore, effectively using calibration techniques to enhance an LM’s factuality remains an unsolved challenge. In this paper, we first propose an activation-based calibration method, ActCab, which trains a linear layer on top of the LM’s last-layer activations that can better capture the representations of knowledge. Built on top of ActCab, we further propose CoDec, a confidence-guided decoding strategy to elicit truthful answers with high confidence from LMs. By evaluating on five popular QA benchmarks, ActCab achieves superior calibration performance than all competitive baselines, e.g., by reducing the average expected calibration error (ECE) score by up to 39%. Further experiments on CoDec show consistent improvements in several LMs’ factuality on challenging QA datasets, such as TruthfulQA, highlighting the value of confidence signals in enhancing the factuality."},"title":{"value":"Enhancing Language Model Factuality via Activation-Based Confidence Calibration and Guided Decoding"},"authors":{"value":["Xin Liu","Farima Fatahi Bayat","Lu Wang"]}},"tmdate":1773331202961,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/LiuBW24"],"tcdate":1773331177527,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lu_Wang9"],"forum":"U7sYzGjS0c","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":851283,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1773331202961,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"U7sYzGjS0c","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.13230v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|enhancing_language_model_factuality_via_activationbased_confidence_calibration_and_guided_decoding"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Liu:","~Farima_Fatahi_Bayat1","~Lu_Wang1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2406.13230"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2406-13230,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Xin Liu and Farima Fatahi Bayat and Lu Wang},\n  title={Enhancing Language Model Factuality via Activation-Based Confidence Calibration and Guided Decoding},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2406.13230},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2406.13230}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrating language models (LMs) aligns their generation confidence with the actual likelihood of answer correctness, which can inform users about LMs' reliability and mitigate hallucinated content. However, prior calibration methods, such as self-consistency-based and logit-based approaches, are either limited in inference-time efficiency or fall short of providing informative signals. Moreover, simply filtering out low-confidence responses reduces the LM's helpfulness when the answers are correct. Therefore, effectively using calibration techniques to enhance an LM's factuality remains an unsolved challenge. In this paper, we first propose an activation-based calibration method, ActCab, which trains a linear layer on top of the LM's last-layer activations that can better capture the representations of knowledge. Built on top of ActCab, we further propose CoDec, a confidence-guided decoding strategy to elicit truthful answers with high confidence from LMs. By evaluating on five popular QA benchmarks, ActCab achieves superior calibration performance than all competitive baselines, e.g., by reducing the average expected calibration error (ECE) score by up to 39%. Further experiments on CoDec show consistent improvements in several LMs' factuality on challenging QA datasets, such as TruthfulQA, highlighting the value of confidence signals in enhancing factuality."},"title":{"value":"Enhancing Language Model Factuality via Activation-Based Confidence Calibration and Guided Decoding"},"authors":{"value":["Xin Liu","Farima Fatahi Bayat","Lu Wang"]}},"tmdate":1773331178481,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1725896324396,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Farima_Fatahi_Bayat1"],"forum":"JBjl0W9gSl","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":79717,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1773331178481,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"JBjl0W9gSl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6287639/8600701/08867930.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ACCESS/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|linebased_geometric_consensus_rectification_and_calibration_from_single_distorted_manhattan_image"},"authorids":{"value":["~Mi_Zhang3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiangyun_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jian_Yao_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Like_Zhao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiancheng_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianya_Gong:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2947177"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/ZhangHYZLG19,\n  author={Mi Zhang and Xiangyun Hu and Jian Yao and Like Zhao and Jiancheng Li and Jianya Gong},\n  title={Line-Based Geometric Consensus Rectification and Calibration From Single Distorted Manhattan Image},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={7},\n  pages={156400-156412},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2947177}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent advances in single image rectification and intrinsic calibration has been addressed by employing line information on the distorted image. The core issues of this technique are the separation of rectification and calibration procedures, and the suffering of geometric nonconformity. In this work, we propose a novel Geometric Consensus Rectification and Calibration algorithm, which we refer to as GCRC framework. We show how the geometric consensus rectification and calibration can be performed in a unified framework and solve the above issues. The proposed GCRC not only guarantees the geometrical consensus on the rectified images, but allows us to perform the robust intrinsic parameters estimation with the grouped circular arcs. Through “grouping by voting” in a unified framework, the geometric consensus rectification and calibration are robustly conducted on single distorted Manhattan images. Experiments on a number of distorted images, including the simulated YorkUrbanDB dataset, Panoramic Fisheye dataset, checkerboard image, and Internet images, demonstrate that the GCRC significantly improve the performance of geometrically consensus rectification and intrinsic parameters estimation. In particular, the GCRC shows relatively small variations with a different number of lines, which outperforms various previous approaches."},"title":{"value":"Line-Based Geometric Consensus Rectification and Calibration From Single Distorted Manhattan Image"},"authors":{"value":["Mi Zhang","Xiangyun Hu","Jian Yao","Like Zhao","Jiancheng Li","Jianya Gong"]}},"tmdate":1740970070154,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1740970062534,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mi_Zhang3"],"forum":"rVdCtdzSZL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":344481,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740970070154,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rVdCtdzSZL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7083369/9831196/09834080.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|learningbased_distortion_correction_and_feature_detection_for_high_precision_and_robust_camera_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2022.3192610"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{Zhang_2022,\n\tdoi = {10.1109/lra.2022.3192610},\n\turl = {https://doi.org/10.1109%2Flra.2022.3192610},\n\tyear = 2022,\n\tmonth = {oct},\n\tpublisher = {Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers ({IEEE})},\n\tvolume = {7},\n\tnumber = {4},\n\tpages = {10470--10477},\n\tauthor = {Yesheng Zhang and Xu Zhao and Dahong Qian},\n\ttitle = {Learning-Based Distortion Correction and Feature Detection for High Precision and Robust Camera Calibration},\n\tjournal = {{IEEE} Robotics and Automation Letters}\n}"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration is a crucial technique which significantly influences the performance of many robotic systems. Robustness and high precision have always been the pursuit of diverse calibration methods. State-of-the-art calibration techniques, however, still suffer from inexact corner detection, radial lens distortion and unstable parameter estimation. Therefore, in this paper, we improve the precision and robustness of calibration by widening these bottlenecks. In particular, effective distortion correction is performed by a learning-based method. Then, accurate sub-pixel feature location is achieved by the combination of robust learning detection, exact refinement and complete post-processing. To obtain stable parameter estimation, an image-level RANSAC-based calibration procedure is proposed. Ultimately, we assemble these methods into a novel and practical calibration framework. Compared with state-of-art methods, experiment results on both real and synthetic datasets under noise, bad lighting and distortion manifest the better robustness and higher precision of the proposed framework."},"title":{"value":"Learning-Based Distortion Correction and Feature Detection for High Precision and Robust Camera Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yesheng Zhang","username":"~Yesheng_Zhang2"},{"fullname":"Xu Zhao"},{"fullname":"Dahong Qian"}]}},"tmdate":1789091732861,"pdate":1664582400000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/lra.2022.3192610"],"tcdate":1773391083461,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yesheng_Zhang2"],"forum":"QBkDQ2uIfE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":56174,"cdate":1658365705207,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091732861,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"QBkDQ2uIfE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"UNSURE@MICCAI 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-16749-2_9.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MICCAI/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"carse|calibration_of_deep_medical_image_classifiers_an_empirical_comparison_using_dermatology_and_histopathology_datasets"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jacob_Carse:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andres_Alvarez_Olmo:","~Stephen_J._McKenna1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-16749-2_9"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/miccai/CarseOM22,\n  author={Jacob Carse and Andres Alvarez Olmo and Stephen J. McKenna},\n  title={Calibration of Deep Medical Image Classifiers: An Empirical Comparison Using Dermatology and Histopathology Datasets},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={89-99},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-16749-2_9},\n  booktitle={UNSURE@MICCAI},\n  crossref={conf/miccai/2022unsure}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As deep learning classifiers become ever more widely deployed for medical image analysis tasks, issues of predictive calibration need to be addressed. Mis-calibration is the deviation between predictive probability (confidence) and classification correctness. Well-calibrated classifiers enable cost-sensitive and selective decision-making. This paper presents an empirical investigation of calibration methods on two medical image datasets (multi-class dermatology and binary histopathology image classification). We show the effect of temperature scaling with temperature optimized using various measures of calibration replacing the standard negative log-likelihood. We do so not only for networks trained using one-hot encoding and cross-entropy loss, but also using focal loss and label smoothing. We compare these with two Bayesian methods. Results suggest little or no advantage to the use of alternative calibration metrics for tuning temperature. Temperature scaling of networks trained using focal loss (with appropriate hyperparameters) provided strong results in terms of both calibration and accuracy across both datasets."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of Deep Medical Image Classifiers: An Empirical Comparison Using Dermatology and Histopathology Datasets"},"authors":{"value":["Jacob Carse","Andres Alvarez Olmo","Stephen J. McKenna"]}},"tmdate":1762519607140,"pdate":1640995200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/miccai/CarseOM22"],"tcdate":1762519403543,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Stephen_J_Mckenna1"],"forum":"MlFKtzjOlK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":675420,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762519607140,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"MlFKtzjOlK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/23/8/3929/pdf?version=1681382511"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|an_automatic_calibration_method_for_kappa_angle_based_on_a_binocular_gaze_constraint"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Jiannan_Chi1",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s23083929"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/LiuCS23,\n  author={Jiahui Liu and Jiannan Chi and Hang Sun},\n  title={An Automatic Calibration Method for Kappa Angle Based on a Binocular Gaze Constraint},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={23},\n  number={8},\n  pages={3929},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s23083929}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Kappa-angle calibration shows its importance in gaze tracking due to the special structure of the eyeball. In a 3D gaze-tracking system, after the optical axis of the eyeball is reconstructed, the kappa angle is needed to convert the optical axis of the eyeball to the real gaze direction. At present, most of the kappa-angle-calibration methods use explicit user calibration. Before eye-gaze tracking, the user needs to look at some pre-defined calibration points on the screen, thereby providing some corresponding optical and visual axes of the eyeball with which to calculate the kappa angle. Especially when multi-point user calibration is required, the calibration process is relatively complicated. In this paper, a method that can automatically calibrate the kappa angle during screen browsing is proposed. Based on the 3D corneal centers and optical axes of both eyes, the optimal objective function of the kappa angle is established according to the coplanar constraint of the visual axes of the left and right eyes, and the differential evolution algorithm is used to iterate through kappa angles according to the theoretical angular constraint of the kappa angle. The experiments show that the proposed method can make the gaze accuracy reach 1.3 ° in the horizontal plane and 1.34 ° in the vertical plane, both of which are within the acceptable margins of gaze-estimation error. The demonstration of explicit kappa-angle calibration is of great significance to the realization of the instant use of gaze-tracking systems."},"title":{"value":"An Automatic Calibration Method for Kappa Angle Based on a Binocular Gaze Constraint"},"authors":{"value":["Jiahui Liu","Jiannan Chi","Hang Sun"]}},"tmdate":1774446461147,"pdate":1703980800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/sensors/LiuCS23"],"tcdate":1774446456203,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jiannan_Chi1"],"forum":"sTbDFEeMPu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":859498,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1774446461147,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"sTbDFEeMPu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Journal of Physics: Conference Series"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"batalo|random_fourier_surrogate_for_simulation_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Bojan_Batalo1","maha_mahyub@cvlab.cs.tsukuba.ac.jp"]},"html":{"value":"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1742-6596/2701/1/012048"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper we propose Random Fourier Surrogate (RFS) as a method for simulation calibration. Computer simulations are actively used in various fields to model and analyze complex real-world phenomena, enabling practitioners to explore situations inaccessible by other means. To create a simulation that accurately reflects the desired real-world scenario requires calibrating the simulation parameters, a non-trivial process that requires domain expertise and iterative trial-and-error. The model-bridge paradigm has been proposed to automatize this process, where a more computationally efficient surrogate model is used in lieu of the real simulation, and a bridge model is used to map the noninterpretable surrogate parameters to calibrated simulation parameters, which are physically interpretable. Recently an efficient solution called tangent slope-intercept (TSI) descriptors has been proposed, where tangent lines of a principal curve are used to represent the simulation in low dimensionality. However, we find that it has two key problems: it is prone to overfitting and is sensitive to initialization. We address these issues using Random Fourier Features to construct a surrogate model, thereby reducing calibration time by avoiding complex computations, while retaining high accuracy. We evaluate the effectiveness of our method with different experiments on synthetic signal simulations and physical simulations of turbulent flow dynamics."},"title":{"value":"Random Fourier Surrogate for simulation calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Bojan Batalo","Maha Mahyub"]}},"tmdate":1751517163053,"pdate":1708873200000,"tcdate":1751517163053,"writers":["~Bojan_Batalo1","maha_mahyub@cvlab.cs.tsukuba.ac.jp"],"signatures":["~Bojan_Batalo1"],"forum":"WLNXQiORsl","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","number":37538,"cdate":1751517163053,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1751517163053,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"WLNXQiORsl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP (Findings) 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-emnlp.152.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"jiang|generative_calibration_for_incontext_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhongtao_Jiang1","~Yuanzhe_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cao_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Zhao_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kang_Liu_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.findings-emnlp.152"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/JiangZLZL23,\n  author={Zhongtao Jiang and Yuanzhe Zhang and Cao Liu and Jun Zhao and Kang Liu},\n  title={Generative Calibration for In-context Learning},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={2312-2333},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.findings-emnlp.152},\n  booktitle={EMNLP (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2023f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As one of the most exciting features of large language models (LLMs), in-context learning is a mixed blessing. While it allows users to fast-prototype a task solver with only a few training examples, the performance is generally sensitive to various configurations of the prompt such as the choice or order of the training examples. In this paper, we for the first time theoretically and empirically identify that such a paradox is mainly due to the label shift of the in-context model to the data distribution, in which LLMs shift the label marginal p(y) while having a good label conditional p(x|y). With this understanding, we can simply calibrate the in-context predictive distribution by adjusting the label marginal, which is estimated via Monte-Carlo sampling over the in-context model, i.e., generation of LLMs. We call our approach as generative calibration. We conduct exhaustive experiments with 12 text classification tasks and 12 LLMs scaling from 774M to 33B, generally find that the proposed method greatly and consistently outperforms the ICL as well as state-of-the-art calibration methods, by up to 27% absolute in macro-F1. Meanwhile, the proposed method is also stable under different prompt configurations."},"title":{"value":"Generative Calibration for In-context Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Zhongtao Jiang","Yuanzhe Zhang","Cao Liu","Jun Zhao","Kang Liu"]}},"tmdate":1718678571263,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1718678200053,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yuanzhe_Zhang1"],"forum":"fXWMOyOR1o","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":32151,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1718678571263,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"fXWMOyOR1o","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.10266v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"jiang|generative_calibration_for_incontext_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhongtao_Jiang1","~Yuanzhe_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cao_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Zhao_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kang_Liu_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.10266"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2310-10266,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhongtao Jiang and Yuanzhe Zhang and Cao Liu and Jun Zhao and Kang Liu},\n  title={Generative Calibration for In-context Learning},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2310.10266},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.10266}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As one of the most exciting features of large language models (LLMs), in-context learning is a mixed blessing. While it allows users to fast-prototype a task solver with only a few training examples, the performance is generally sensitive to various configurations of the prompt such as the choice or order of the training examples. In this paper, we for the first time theoretically and empirically identify that such a paradox is mainly due to the label shift of the in-context model to the data distribution, in which LLMs shift the label marginal $p(y)$ while having a good label conditional $p(x|y)$. With this understanding, we can simply calibrate the in-context predictive distribution by adjusting the label marginal, which is estimated via Monte-Carlo sampling over the in-context model, i.e., generation of LLMs. We call our approach as generative calibration. We conduct exhaustive experiments with 12 text classification tasks and 12 LLMs scaling from 774M to 33B, generally find that the proposed method greatly and consistently outperforms the ICL as well as state-of-the-art calibration methods, by up to 27% absolute in macro-F1. Meanwhile, the proposed method is also stable under different prompt configurations."},"title":{"value":"Generative Calibration for In-context Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Zhongtao Jiang","Yuanzhe Zhang","Cao Liu","Jun Zhao","Kang Liu"]}},"tmdate":1718678571256,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1718678200213,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yuanzhe_Zhang1"],"forum":"B24cj56BMu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":32164,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1718678571256,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"B24cj56BMu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2023 Findings"},"Submission_Track_2":{"value":"Language Modeling and Analysis of Language Models"},"keywords":{"value":["Calibration","In-context Learning"]},"abstract":{"value":"As one of the most exciting features of large language models (LLMs), in-context learning is a mixed blessing. While it allows users to fast-prototype a task solver with only a few training examples, the performance is generally sensitive to various configurations of the prompt such as the choice or order of the training examples. In this paper, we for the first time theoretically and empirically identify that such a paradox is mainly due to the label shift of the in-context model to the data distribution, in which LLMs shift the label marginal $p(y)$ while having a good label conditional $p(x|y)$. With this understanding, we can simply calibrate the in-context predictive distribution by adjusting the label marginal, which is estimated via Monte-Carlo sampling over the in-context model, i.e., generation of LLMs. We call our approach as generative calibration. We conduct exhaustive experiments with 12 text classification tasks and 12 LLMs scaling from 774M to 33B, generally find that the proposed method greatly and consistently outperforms the ICL as well as state-of-the-art calibration methods, by up to 27% absolute in macro-F1. Meanwhile, the proposed method is also stable under different prompt configurations."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\njiang2023generative,\ntitle={Generative Calibration for In-context Learning},\nauthor={Zhongtao Jiang and Yuanzhe Zhang and Cao Liu and Jun Zhao and Kang Liu},\nbooktitle={The 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=sxJU7X2ZG0}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Generative Calibration for In-context Learning"},"Submission_Type":{"value":"Regular Long Paper"},"pdf":{"value":"/attachment/72bc6cc5b627f846e6cb5eb5fbb56295302f0a24.pdf"},"Submission_Track":{"value":"Theme Track: Large Language Models and the Future of NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"EMNLP/2023/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"jiang|generative_calibration_for_incontext_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhongtao_Jiang1","~Yuanzhe_Zhang1","~Cao_Liu1","~Jun_Zhao4","~Kang_Liu1"]},"authors":{"value":["Zhongtao Jiang","Yuanzhe Zhang","Cao Liu","Jun Zhao","Kang Liu"]}},"tmdate":1701459779316,"pdate":1696709298325,"tcdate":1686992381674,"writers":["EMNLP/2023/Conference","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission5523/Authors"],"signatures":["EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission5523/Authors"],"forum":"sxJU7X2ZG0","number":5523,"cdate":1686992381674,"mdate":1701459779316,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Submission","EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Post_Submission","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission5523/-/Revision","EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Edit","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission5523/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"odate":1701459779302,"domain":"EMNLP/2023/Conference","id":"sxJU7X2ZG0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"J. Math. Imaging Vis. 2007"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10851-006-9699-4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/JMIV/2007"},"paperhash":{"value":"espuny|a_new_linear_method_for_camera_selfcalibration_with_planar_motion"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ferran_Espuny1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10851-006-9699-4"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/jmiv/Espuny07,\n  author={Ferran Espuny},\n  title={A New Linear Method for Camera Self-Calibration with Planar Motion},\n  year={2007},\n  cdate={1167609600000},\n  journal={J. Math. Imaging Vis.},\n  volume={27},\n  number={1},\n  pages={81-88},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s10851-006-9699-4}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We consider the self-calibration (affine and metric reconstruction) problem from images acquired with a camera with unchanging internal parameters undergoing planar motion. The general self-calibration methods (modulus constraint, Kruppa equations) are known to fail with this camera motion. In this paper we give two novel linear constraints on the coordinates of the plane at infinity in a projective reconstruction for any camera motion. In the planar case, we show that the two constraints are equivalent and easy to compute, giving us a linear version of the quartic modulus constraint. Using this fact, we present a new linear method to solve the self-calibration problem with planar motion of the camera from three or more images."},"title":{"value":"A New Linear Method for Camera Self-Calibration with Planar Motion"},"authors":{"value":["Ferran Espuny"]}},"tmdate":1776632194815,"pdate":1199059200000,"tcdate":1776632183837,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ferran_Espuny-Pujol2"],"forum":"mMEv8Wl0Z9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":868182,"cdate":1167609600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1776632194815,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"mMEv8Wl0Z9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.17077v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"cabezas|cp4sbi_local_conformal_calibration_of_credible_sets_in_simulationbased_inference"},"authorids":{"value":["","","~Thiago_Ramos1","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.17077"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2508-17077,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Luben M. C. Cabezas and Vagner S. Santos and Thiago Ramos and Pedro L. C. Rodrigues and Rafael Izbicki},\n  title={CP4SBI: Local Conformal Calibration of Credible Sets in Simulation-Based Inference},\n  year={2025},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1754006400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2508.17077},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.17077}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Current experimental scientists have been increasingly relying on simulation-based inference (SBI) to invert complex non-linear models with intractable likelihoods. However, posterior approximations obtained with SBI are often miscalibrated, causing credible regions to undercover true parameters. We develop $\\texttt{CP4SBI}$, a model-agnostic conformal calibration framework that constructs credible sets with local Bayesian coverage. Our two proposed variants, namely local calibration via regression trees and CDF-based calibration, enable finite-sample local coverage guarantees for any scoring function, including HPD, symmetric, and quantile-based regions. Experiments on widely used SBI benchmarks demonstrate that our approach improves the quality of uncertainty quantification for neural posterior estimators using both normalizing flows and score-diffusion modeling."},"title":{"value":"CP4SBI: Local Conformal Calibration of Credible Sets in Simulation-Based Inference"},"authors":{"value":["Luben M. C. Cabezas","Vagner S. Santos","Thiago Ramos","Pedro L. C. Rodrigues","Rafael Izbicki"]}},"tmdate":1772388455495,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2508-17077"],"tcdate":1772388452279,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Thiago_Rodrigo_Ramos1"],"forum":"Ahbgdu1Bxb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":842050,"cdate":1754006400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772388455495,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Ahbgdu1Bxb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Arxiv"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7dbf5a573ef166f9cc1e9cc0dd581e0fc1ffc3be.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"cabezas|cp4sbi_local_conformal_calibration_of_credible_sets_in_simulationbased_inference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Luben_Miguel_Cruz_Cabezas1","~Vagner_Silva_Santos1","~Thiago_Ramos1","~Pedro_L._C._Rodrigues1","~Rafael_Izbicki1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.17077"},"abstract":{"value":"Current experimental scientists have been increasingly relying on simulation-based inference (SBI) to invert complex non-linear models with intractable likelihoods. However, posterior approximations obtained with SBI are often miscalibrated, causing credible regions to undercover true parameters. We develop CP4SBI, a model-agnostic conformal calibration framework that constructs credible sets with local Bayesian coverage. Our two proposed variants, namely local calibration via regression trees and CDF-based calibration, enable finite-sample local coverage guarantees for any scoring function, including HPD, symmetric, and quantile-based regions. Experiments on widely used SBI benchmarks demonstrate that our approach improves the quality of uncertainty quantification for neural posterior estimators using both normalizing flows and score-diffusion modeling."},"title":{"value":"CP4SBI: Local Conformal Calibration of Credible Sets in Simulation-Based Inference"},"authors":{"value":["Luben Miguel Cruz Cabezas","Vagner Silva Santos","Thiago Ramos","Pedro L. C. Rodrigues","Rafael Izbicki"]}},"tmdate":1761144351794,"pdate":1755972780000,"tcdate":1761144351794,"writers":["~Luben_Miguel_Cruz_Cabezas1","~Vagner_Silva_Santos1","~Thiago_Ramos1","~Pedro_L._C._Rodrigues1","~Rafael_Izbicki1"],"signatures":["~Luben_M._C._Cabezas1"],"forum":"TaY2ZJwPbt","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":39898,"cdate":1761144351794,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1761144351794,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"TaY2ZJwPbt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.02539v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"garciadurso|markerbased_extrinsic_calibration_method_for_accurate_multicamera_3d_reconstruction"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nahuel_E._Garcia-D'Urso:","~Bernabe_Sanchez-Sos1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jorge_Azorín_López:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrés_Fuster_Guilló:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Antonio_Maciá-Lillo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Higinio_Mora_Mora:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.02539"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-02539,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Nahuel E. Garcia-D'Urso and Bernabe Sanchez-Sos and Jorge Azorín López and Andrés Fuster Guilló and Antonio Maciá-Lillo and Higinio Mora Mora},\n  title={Marker-Based Extrinsic Calibration Method for Accurate Multi-Camera 3D Reconstruction},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2505.02539},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.02539}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate 3D reconstruction using multi-camera RGB-D systems critically depends on precise extrinsic calibration to achieve proper alignment between captured views. In this paper, we introduce an iterative extrinsic calibration method that leverages the geometric constraints provided by a three-dimensional marker to significantly improve calibration accuracy. Our proposed approach systematically segments and refines marker planes through clustering, regression analysis, and iterative reassignment techniques, ensuring robust geometric correspondence across camera views. We validate our method comprehensively in both controlled environments and practical real-world settings within the Tech4Diet project, aimed at modeling the physical progression of patients undergoing nutritional treatments. Experimental results demonstrate substantial reductions in alignment errors, facilitating accurate and reliable 3D reconstructions."},"title":{"value":"Marker-Based Extrinsic Calibration Method for Accurate Multi-Camera 3D Reconstruction"},"authors":{"value":["Nahuel E. Garcia-D'Urso","Bernabe Sanchez-Sos","Jorge Azorín López","Andrés Fuster Guilló","Antonio Maciá-Lillo","Higinio Mora Mora"]}},"tmdate":1755027440845,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2505-02539"],"tcdate":1755027434329,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bernabe_Sanchez-Sos1"],"forum":"lwNJCJDGlu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":616451,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1755027440845,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"lwNJCJDGlu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"RISEx Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/0d545d0962a244fd5dc32756168917b65ac9d3b2.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["IMU/ RGB-D camera Extrinsic calibration","Sensor fusion","Ground plane segmentation","Pose estimation","RANSAC","Target-less calibration","Uncrewed aerial manipulator"]},"venueid":{"value":"albertarobotics.ca/RISEx/2025/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"hokmabadi|ground_planeaided_extrinsic_calibration_of_inertial_and_rgbd_sensors_for_uncrewed_aerial_vehicles"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ilyar_Asl_Sabbaghian_Hokmabadi1","~Mahdis_Bisheban1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate extrinsic calibration between inertial measurement units (IMUs) and RGB-D cameras is essential for trajectory estimation in multi-sensor robotic manipulators. Target-based calibration methods, such as Kalibr, are not suitable for online calibration, while state-of-the-art target-less techniques often fail to achieve high accuracy under poor illumination. This study proposes a ground plane-based extrinsic calibration method for uncrewed aerial vehicles (UAVs) and, more generally, other multi-sensor robotic systems. The proposed method employs a deep neural network to extract the floor segment from RGB images. The corresponding depth pixels are then back-projected to fit a plane. The normal of this plane, and the reliable gravity vector detection from IMU measurements, is used to estimate the extrinsic calibration parameters. The trained deep neural network achieves up to 0.96 precision and recall. Furthermore, comparative experiments demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms MATLAB’s target-based calibration toolbox. These results highlight the effectiveness of floor-segmentation-based calibration for UAV applications."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhokmabadi2025ground,\ntitle={{GROUND} {PLANE}-{AIDED} {EXTRINSIC} {CALIBRATION} {OF} {INERTIAL} {AND} {RGB}-D {SENSORS} {FOR} {UNCREWED} {AERIAL} {VEHICLES}},\nauthor={Ilyar Asl Sabbaghian Hokmabadi and Mahdis Bisheban},\nbooktitle={Robotics {\\&} Intelligent Systems Expo},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Q4moqWxEkC}\n}"},"title":{"value":"GROUND PLANE-AIDED EXTRINSIC CALIBRATION OF INERTIAL AND RGB-D SENSORS FOR UNCREWED AERIAL VEHICLES"},"authors":{"value":["Ilyar Asl Sabbaghian Hokmabadi","Mahdis Bisheban"]}},"tmdate":1771032562170,"pdate":1759353877212,"tcdate":1757815739507,"writers":["albertarobotics.ca/RISEx/2025/Conference","albertarobotics.ca/RISEx/2025/Conference/Submission28/Authors"],"signatures":["albertarobotics.ca/RISEx/2025/Conference/Submission28/Authors"],"forum":"Q4moqWxEkC","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":28,"cdate":1757815739507,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["albertarobotics.ca/RISEx/2025/Conference/-/Submission","albertarobotics.ca/RISEx/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","albertarobotics.ca/RISEx/2025/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1771032562170,"odate":1762999591612,"domain":"albertarobotics.ca/RISEx/2025/Conference","id":"Q4moqWxEkC","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Whether a wildfire risk model is calibrated at all, not which strategy calibrated it, determines who gets protected, and that finding only survived once tested with proper statistical power."},"venue":{"value":"AI4GOOD Workshop @ NeurIPS 2026 Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["wildfire risk modeling","model calibration","active sensing","resource allocation","trustworthy AI","statistical rigor"]},"abstract":{"value":"Wildfire spread models increasingly inform which locations receive scarce protection resources: firefighting crews, evacuation support, aerial retardant drops. These models are built on physical parameters (fuel response, wind coupling, ember-spotting rate) that are never known exactly, and practitioners calibrate them against field data under real time and budget constraints. We argue that treating this calibration step as an afterthought is a trustworthiness failure with a direct, measurable harm channel: an uncalibrated model does not just have a worse loss, it silently determines who is protected and who is left exposed. We compare three ways of spending a limited field-observation budget to calibrate a fire model: an active, uncertainty-seeking sensing strategy, a random baseline, and a fixed monitoring-network sweep. We evaluate calibration quality not by parameter error alone but by its direct effect on a downstream resource-allocation decision. Across 12 independent simulated fires, we find that whether a model is calibrated at all dominates which sampling strategy calibrated it: all three strategies converge to statistically indistinguishable decision quality ($\\approx 0.93$--$0.96$ AUC, paired Wilcoxon $p>0.4$ at every tested budget) once the budget passes roughly a dozen observations, while an uncalibrated nominal model stays far behind throughout. In a concrete allocation exercise with a fixed 60-resource budget, recalibrating a plausible but wrong nominal model raises the fraction of allocated resources that actually reach a burning location from 47% to 83%, regardless of which of the three (statistically equivalent) strategies produced the calibration data. We use these results to argue for a reporting norm: risk models deployed for resource allocation should disclose their calibration budget and report a decision-relevant accuracy metric, not only headline predictive accuracy. We caution against assuming that more sophisticated (adaptive) data collection is automatically worth its added operational cost without checking, as we did here, whether it actually moves the decision-relevant number."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nsenan2026budgetconstrained,\ntitle={Budget-Constrained Sensing Strategies for Wildfire Model Calibration},\nauthor={Rhea Senan},\nbooktitle={Trustworthy AI for Good (AI4GOOD) Workshop @ NeurIPS 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=AkQDwZlba4}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Budget-Constrained Sensing Strategies for Wildfire Model Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/b65ed841214b5463d15143827ee5afafc1f3b88e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD"},"paperhash":{"value":"senan|budgetconstrained_sensing_strategies_for_wildfire_model_calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Rhea_Senan1","fullname":"Rhea Senan","institutions":[{"name":"California Institute of Technology","domain":"caltech.edu","country":"US"}]}]}},"tmdate":1790905911097,"pdate":1790905900160,"tcdate":1788349436709,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/Submission418/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/Submission418/Authors"],"forum":"AkQDwZlba4","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":418,"cdate":1788349436709,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1790905911097,"odate":1790905900160,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD","id":"AkQDwZlba4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6287639/8600701/08698871.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ACCESS/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|highprecision_measurement_of_binocular_telecentric_vision_system_with_novel_calibration_and_matching_methods"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shengfu_Zhang_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bo_Li_0038:","~Fuji_Ren2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rong_Dong:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2913181"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/ZhangLRD19,\n  author={Shengfu Zhang and Bo Li and Fuji Ren and Rong Dong},\n  title={High-Precision Measurement of Binocular Telecentric Vision System With Novel Calibration and Matching Methods},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={7},\n  pages={54682-54692},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2913181}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In stereo vision-based three-dimensional measurements, calibration, and stereo matching are the most challenging tasks for accurate three-dimensional reconstruction. The traditional binocular vision algorithm has low precision, and we propose a binocular vision system using a telecentric lens. In this paper, we propose a calibration and matching algorithm for the telecentric binocular vision system, which collects only two pictures of the calibration plate to complete the system calibration. The algorithm is a special application of 3D reconstruction. In this paper, if the measurement points are selected as points on the grid, the appropriate extension can be used for high-precision 3D reconstruction, which will be explained in detail in the experiments. In order to reduce the calibration error and obtain high maneuverability, the binocular vision system is only calibrated once with a two-dimensional calibration board, and the depth information is obtained through the parallax of the detection process, so as to effectively simplify the calibration process and reduce the errors introduced in the calibration process. For the matching of images in the right and left cameras, the polar theory of telecentric binocular vision system is constructed according to the characteristics of telecentric lens imaging model based on the traditional binocular vision system theory. The matching algorithm only needs to apply the two-dimensional calibration information and then calculates the results according to the parallax combined with the polar line matching algorithm, which simplifies the algorithm flow and reduces the error. In this paper, experiments show that the algorithm proposed is simple and has high precision, good stability, and high-practical value."},"title":{"value":"High-Precision Measurement of Binocular Telecentric Vision System With Novel Calibration and Matching Methods"},"authors":{"value":["Shengfu Zhang","Bo Li","Fuji Ren","Rong Dong"]}},"tmdate":1770280051188,"pdate":1577750400000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/access/ZhangLRD19"],"tcdate":1770280033113,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fuji_Ren2"],"forum":"d8NGRKb7fG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":819665,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770280051188,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"d8NGRKb7fG","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"This work explores transfer learning for BCIs with a focus on realistic limited-data settings relevant to online model calibration"},"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2025 Workshop BrainBodyFM"},"keywords":{"value":["Brain Computer Interface","Riemannian Geometry","EEG"]},"abstract":{"value":"The practical deployment of motor imagery brain-computer interfaces (MI-BCIs) is bottlenecked by their dependence on large amounts of subject-specific calibration data. To overcome this, we introduce Riemannian Transfer CSP (RTCSP), a transfer learning method that aligns EEG covariance matrices from previous users to a new target subject using Riemannian geometry. This process generates robust spatial filters that perform well even when the target subject's calibration data is severely limited. Our method enables efficient and accurate calibration with minimal data, directly addressing a key challenge for practical MI-BCI systems."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\ngunasar2025efficient,\ntitle={Efficient Calibration in Motor Imagery {BCI}s Under Data Constraints via Subject Transfer},\nauthor={Tekin Gunasar and Virginia R. de Sa},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Foundation Models for the Brain and Body},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=MQNOt7fCiH}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Efficient Calibration in Motor Imagery BCIs Under Data Constraints via Subject Transfer"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/cf72e22c760bc0952f81e37361abb75b91db928e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/BrainBodyFM"},"paperhash":{"value":"gunasar|efficient_calibration_in_motor_imagery_bcis_under_data_constraints_via_subject_transfer"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tekin_Gunasar1","~Virginia_R._de_Sa2"]},"authors":{"value":["Tekin Gunasar","Virginia R. de Sa"]}},"tmdate":1763957747251,"pdate":1758593470354,"tcdate":1756442942469,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/BrainBodyFM","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/BrainBodyFM/Submission38/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/BrainBodyFM/Submission38/Authors"],"forum":"MQNOt7fCiH","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":38,"cdate":1756442942469,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/BrainBodyFM/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/BrainBodyFM/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/BrainBodyFM/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/BrainBodyFM/Submission38/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1763957747251,"odate":1760020513155,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/BrainBodyFM","id":"MQNOt7fCiH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ROBOT (1) 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-030-35990-4_17.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ROBOT/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"oliveira|a_general_approach_to_the_extrinsic_calibration_of_intelligent_vehicles_using_ros"},"authorids":{"value":["~Miguel_Oliveira2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Afonso_Castro:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tiago_Madeira:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Paulo_Dias:","~Vítor_M._F._Santos2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-35990-4_17"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/robot/OliveiraCMDS19,\n  author={Miguel Oliveira and Afonso Castro and Tiago Madeira and Paulo Dias and Vítor M. F. Santos},\n  title={A General Approach to the Extrinsic Calibration of Intelligent Vehicles Using ROS},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  pages={203-215},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-35990-4_17},\n  booktitle={ROBOT (1)},\n  crossref={conf/robot/2019-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Intelligent vehicles are complex systems which often accommodate several sensors of different modalities. This paper proposes a general approach to the problem of extrinsic calibration of multiple sensors of varied modalities. Our approach is seamlessly integrated with the Robot Operating System (ROS) framework, and allows for the interactive positioning of sensors and labelling of data, facilitating the calibration process. The calibration problem is formulated as a simultaneous optimization for all sensors, in which the objective function accounts for the various sensor modalities. Results show that the proposed procedure produces accurate calibrations."},"title":{"value":"A General Approach to the Extrinsic Calibration of Intelligent Vehicles Using ROS"},"authors":{"value":["Miguel Oliveira","Afonso Castro","Tiago Madeira","Paulo Dias","Vítor M. F. Santos"]}},"tmdate":1753454280859,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1753450773086,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Miguel_Riem_Oliveira1"],"forum":"53QYPpKSY4","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":588855,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1753454280859,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"53QYPpKSY4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.17159v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"neo|maxent_loss_constrained_maximum_entropy_for_calibration_under_outofdistribution_shift"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dexter_Neo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Stefan_Winkler_0001:","~Tsuhan_Chen3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.17159"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2310-17159,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Dexter Neo and Stefan Winkler and Tsuhan Chen},\n  title={MaxEnt Loss: Constrained Maximum Entropy for Calibration under Out-of-Distribution Shift},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2310.17159},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.17159}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present a new loss function that addresses the out-of-distribution (OOD) calibration problem. While many objective functions have been proposed to effectively calibrate models in-distribution, our findings show that they do not always fare well OOD. Based on the Principle of Maximum Entropy, we incorporate helpful statistical constraints observed during training, delivering better model calibration without sacrificing accuracy. We provide theoretical analysis and show empirically that our method works well in practice, achieving state-of-the-art calibration on both synthetic and real-world benchmarks."},"title":{"value":"MaxEnt Loss: Constrained Maximum Entropy for Calibration under Out-of-Distribution Shift"},"authors":{"value":["Dexter Neo","Stefan Winkler","Tsuhan Chen"]}},"tmdate":1747303939596,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1747303922005,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Tsuhan_Chen3"],"forum":"TeVdOWtliw","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":481879,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747303939596,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"TeVdOWtliw","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.16742v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ramachandran|texttosql_calibration_no_need_to_ask_just_rescale_model_probabilities"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ashwin_Ramachandran1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sunita_Sarawagi:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2411.16742"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2411-16742,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ashwin Ramachandran and Sunita Sarawagi},\n  title={Text-to-SQL Calibration: No Need to Ask - Just Rescale Model Probabilities},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2411.16742},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2411.16742}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration is crucial as large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed to convert natural language queries into SQL for commercial databases. In this work, we investigate calibration techniques for assigning confidence to generated SQL queries. We show that a straightforward baseline -- deriving confidence from the model's full-sequence probability -- outperforms recent methods that rely on follow-up prompts for self-checking and confidence verbalization. Our comprehensive evaluation, conducted across two widely-used Text-to-SQL benchmarks and multiple LLM architectures, provides valuable insights into the effectiveness of various calibration strategies."},"title":{"value":"Text-to-SQL Calibration: No Need to Ask - Just Rescale Model Probabilities"},"authors":{"value":["Ashwin Ramachandran","Sunita Sarawagi"]}},"tmdate":1747290585860,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747290579104,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ashwin_Ramachandran1"],"forum":"XTnLtUkazi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":473952,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747290585860,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"XTnLtUkazi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.10209v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|efficode_unleashing_code_efficiency_in_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dong_Huang_0005:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guangtao_Zeng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianbo_Dai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Meng_Luo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Han_Weng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuhao_Qing:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Heming_Cui:","~Zhijiang_Guo1","~Jie_M._Zhang1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.10209"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2410-10209,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Dong Huang and Guangtao Zeng and Jianbo Dai and Meng Luo and Han Weng and Yuhao Qing and Heming Cui and Zhijiang Guo and Jie M. Zhang},\n  title={Effi-Code: Unleashing Code Efficiency in Language Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2410.10209},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.10209}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As large language models (LLMs) play an increasingly important role in code generation, enhancing both correctness and efficiency has become crucial. Current methods primarily focus on correctness, often overlooking efficiency. To address this gap, we introduce \\dataset to improve both aspects by fine-tuning LLMs on a high-quality dataset comprising correct and efficient code samples. Our methodology involves leveraging multiple LLMs to generate diverse candidate code solutions for various tasks across different programming languages. We then evaluate these solutions by directly measuring their execution time and memory usage through local execution. The code solution with the lowest execution time and memory consumption is selected as the final output for each task. Experimental results demonstrate significant improvements when fine-tuning with \\dataset. For instance, Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct's pass@1 score increases from 44.8\\% to 57.7\\%, while the average execution time for correct tasks decreases by 48.4\\%. \\dataset offers a scalable and effective solution for advancing AI-driven code generation, benefiting both software development and computational problem-solving. The source code of Effi-Code was released in https://github.com/huangd1999/Effi-Code."},"title":{"value":"Effi-Code: Unleashing Code Efficiency in Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Dong Huang","Guangtao Zeng","Jianbo Dai","Meng Luo","Han Weng","Yuhao Qing","Heming Cui","Zhijiang Guo","Jie M. Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1747276992836,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1746026819516,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jie_Zhang21"],"forum":"pC04RKIGKx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":408369,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747276992836,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"pC04RKIGKx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"2018 IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Workshops (CVPRW)"},"pdf":{"value":"https://vbn.aau.dk/files/299483563/Pedersen_Camera_Calibration_for_CVPR_2018_paper.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"pedersen|camera_calibration_for_underwater_3d_reconstruction_based_on_ray_tracing_using_snells_law"},"html":{"value":"https://vbn.aau.dk/en/publications/f143b60d-9d55-4f8f-961f-0cc8e78c1dbd"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurately estimating the 3D position of underwater objects is of great interest when doing research on marine animals. An inherent problem of 3D reconstruction of underwater positions is the presence of refraction which invalidates the assumption of a single viewpoint. Three ways of performing 3D reconstruction on underwater objects are compared in this work: an approach relying solely on in-air camera calibration, an approach with the camera calibration performed under water and an approach based on ray tracing with Snell’s law. As expected, the in-air camera calibration showed to be the most inaccurate as it does not take refraction into account. The precision of the estimated 3D positions based on the underwater camera calibration and the ray tracing based approach were, on the other hand, almost identical. However, the ray tracing based approach is found to be advantageous as it is far more flexible in terms of the calibration procedure due to the decoupling of the intrinsic and extrinsic camera parameters."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{f143b60d9d554f8f961f0cc8e78c1dbd,\n  title     = \"Camera Calibration for Underwater 3D Reconstruction Based on Ray Tracing using Snell{\\textquoteright}s Law\",\n  abstract  = \"Accurately estimating the 3D position of underwater objects is of great interest when doing research on marine animals. An inherent problem of 3D reconstruction of underwater positions is the presence of refraction which invalidates the assumption of a single viewpoint. Three ways of performing 3D reconstruction on underwater objects are compared in this work: an approach relying solely on in-air camera calibration, an approach with the camera calibration performed under water and an approach based on ray tracing with Snell{\\textquoteright}s law. As expected, the in-air camera calibration showed to be the most inaccurate as it does not take refraction into account. The precision of the estimated 3D positions based on the underwater camera calibration and the ray tracing based approach were, on the other hand, almost identical. However, the ray tracing based approach is found to be advantageous as it is far more flexible in terms of the calibration procedure due to the decoupling of the intrinsic and extrinsic camera parameters.\",\n  author    = \"Malte Pedersen and Bengtson, \\{Stefan Hein\\} and Rikke Gade and Niels Madsen and Moeslund, \\{Thomas B.\\}\",\n  year      = \"2018\",\n  month     = dec,\n  day       = \"13\",\n  doi       = \"10.1109/CVPRW.2018.00190\",\n  language  = \"English\",\n  isbn      = \"978-1-5386-6101-7\",\n  series    = \"IEEE Computer Society Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Workshops\",\n  publisher = \"IEEE (Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers)\",\n  pages     = \"1410--1417\",\n  booktitle = \"2018 IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Workshops (CVPRW)\",\n  address   = \"United States\",\n  note      = \"31st Meeting of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Workshops, CVPRW 2018 ; Conference date: 18-06-2018 Through 22-06-2018\",\n}"},"title":{"value":"Camera Calibration for Underwater 3D Reconstruction Based on Ray Tracing using Snell’s Law"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Malte Pedersen"},{"fullname":"Stefan Hein Bengtson","username":"~Stefan_Hein_Bengtson1"},{"fullname":"Rikke Gade"},{"fullname":"Niels Madsen"},{"fullname":"Thomas B. Moeslund"}]}},"tmdate":1789090903337,"pdate":1544659200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/cvprw.2018.00190"],"tcdate":1772181280819,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Stefan_Hein_Bengtson1"],"forum":"eeodiKoq5i","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":48258,"cdate":1557293448311,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789090903337,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"eeodiKoq5i","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2024 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Code Evaluation"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/aac336734a57f8e92cd8b3d457a8f121dff5a05e.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"general machine learning (i.e., none of the above)"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"A proper code evaluation metric (CEM) profoundly impacts the evolution of code generation, which is an important research field in NLP and software engineering. Prevailing match-based CEMs (e.g., BLEU, Accuracy, and CodeBLEU) suffer from two significant drawbacks. \n1. They primarily measure the surface differences between codes without considering their functional equivalence. However, functional equivalence is pivotal in evaluating the effectiveness of code generation, as different codes can perform identical operations.\n2. They are predominantly designed for the Ref-only input format. However, code evaluation necessitates versatility in input formats. Aside from Ref-only, there are NL-only and Ref\\&NL formats, which existing match-based CEMs cannot effectively accommodate.\n\nIn this paper, we propose CodeScore, a large language model (LLM)-based CEM, which estimates the functional correctness of generated code on three input types. To acquire CodeScore, we present UniCE, a unified code generation learning framework, for LLMs to learn code execution (i.e., learning PassRatio and Executability of generated code) with unified input. Extensive experimental results on multiple code evaluation datasets demonstrate that CodeScore absolutely improves up to 58.87% correlation with functional correctness compared to other CEMs, achieves state-of-the-art performance, and effectively handles three input formats."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\ndong2024codescore,\ntitle={CodeScore: Evaluating Code Generation by Learning Code Execution},\nauthor={Yihong Dong and jiazheng ding and Ge Li and Xue Jiang and Zhuo Li and Zhi Jin},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=OeOxLvDUuW}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CodeScore: Evaluating Code Generation by Learning Code Execution"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e6e11b18ee748e3bdbad68e8b1a200fa1721c8ca.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"dong|codescore_evaluating_code_generation_by_learning_code_execution"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yihong_Dong1","~jiazheng_ding1","~Ge_Li4","~Xue_Jiang5","~Zhuo_Li10","~Zhi_Jin1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Yihong Dong","jiazheng ding","Ge Li","Xue Jiang","Zhuo Li","Zhi Jin"]}},"tmdate":1711385509214,"tcdate":1695341462710,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission4263/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission4263/Authors"],"forum":"OeOxLvDUuW","number":4263,"cdate":1695341462710,"mdate":1711385509214,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission4263/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"OeOxLvDUuW","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We learn shared history-dependent response models for soft tactile sensors and calibrate only their output combination at new sensing locations using a few initial force calibrations."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Sensor calibration","Tactile sensing","Transfer learning","Hysteresis modeling","Force estimation"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/e63d93eb32a1b6689e046d7818db3d2148bddcb4.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"transfer learning, meta learning, and lifelong learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Maintaining accurate force estimation continuously after calibration is challenging for soft tactile sensors, whose responses vary across sensors and depend on loading history. For large-scale manufacturing, the challenge is to capture these history-dependent responses with expressive models while keeping local calibration effort low. We present a few-shot calibration framework that separates shared response modeling from local output calibration. We use four response models: static, path-dependent, temporal relaxation, and hysteresis. Each predicts normal contact force through a compact neural readout. We train the models independently on source recordings, using initial segments for calibration and later segments to optimize force prediction. At each new sensing location, we freeze the models and use a few initial force references to calibrate their output combination. After calibration, all model and calibration parameters remain fixed, while local memory states update causally from the incoming electrical data stream, enabling force estimation without further force references. We evaluate the framework at fourteen sensing locations across three tactile sensor arrays excluded from training, each containing 48 sensing elements. With three fitting references selected from dense initial force recordings, our framework achieves an overall RMSE of 0.375 N under full-window calibration, outperforming the evaluated external baselines. These results suggest that shared response models can support accurate force estimation under changing loading conditions while limiting local learning to output calibration."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026touch,\ntitle={Touch Has Memory: Learning Shared Response Models for Few-Shot Calibration of Soft Tactile Sensors},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=X8lwrN8OlG},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Touch Has Memory: Learning Shared Response Models for Few-Shot Calibration of Soft Tactile Sensors"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/aa8191ca220d1ab87b024ff495ada4e2e2babb74.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791234880044,"tcdate":1789792565638,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission54156/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission54156/Authors"],"forum":"X8lwrN8OlG","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":54156,"cdate":1789792565638,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission54156/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791234880044,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"X8lwrN8OlG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.16237v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"cui|origenenhancing_rtl_code_generation_with_codetocode_augmentation_and_selfreflection"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fan_Cui:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chenyang_Yin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kexing_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Youwei_Xiao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guangyu_Sun_0003:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qiang_Xu_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qipeng_Guo:","~Demin_Song1","~Dahua_Lin1","~Xingcheng_Zhang3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yun_Liang_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.16237"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2407-16237,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Fan Cui and Chenyang Yin and Kexing Zhou and Youwei Xiao and Guangyu Sun and Qiang Xu and Qipeng Guo and Demin Song and Dahua Lin and Xingcheng Zhang and Yun Liang},\n  title={OriGen:Enhancing RTL Code Generation with Code-to-Code Augmentation and Self-Reflection},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2407.16237},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.16237}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent studies have demonstrated the significant potential of Large Language Models (LLMs) in generating Register Transfer Level (RTL) code, with notable advancements showcased by commercial models such as GPT-4 and Claude3-Opus. However, these proprietary LLMs often raise concerns regarding privacy and security. While open-source LLMs offer solutions to these concerns, they typically underperform commercial models in RTL code generation tasks, primarily due to the scarcity of high-quality open-source RTL datasets. To address this challenge, we introduce OriGen , a fully open-source framework that incorporates self-reflection capabilities and a novel dataset augmentation methodology for generating high-quality, large-scale RTL code. Our approach employs a code-tocode augmentation technique to enhance the quality of open-source RTL code datasets. Furthermore, OriGen can rectify syntactic errors through a self-reflection process that leverages compiler feedback. Experimental results demonstrate that OriGen significantly outperforms other open-source alternatives in RTL code generation. It surpasses the previous best-performing open-source LLM by 12.8% and even exceeds GPT-4 Turbo in the pass@1 metric on the VerilogEval-Human benchmark. Moreover, OriGen exhibits superior capabilities in self-reflection and error correction, outperforming GPT-4 by 19.9% on a benchmark designed to evaluate self-reflection capabilities."},"title":{"value":"OriGen:Enhancing RTL Code Generation with Code-to-Code Augmentation and Self-Reflection"},"authors":{"value":["Fan Cui","Chenyang Yin","Kexing Zhou","Youwei Xiao","Guangyu Sun","Qiang Xu","Qipeng Guo","Demin Song","Dahua Lin","Xingcheng Zhang","Yun Liang"]}},"tmdate":1747806263324,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731149309835,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xingcheng_ZHANG2"],"forum":"wgwtNIHB7p","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":176466,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747806263324,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"wgwtNIHB7p","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.10098v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"pascarella|on_the_effect_of_code_review_on_code_smells"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luca_Pascarella:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Davide_Spadini:","~Fabio_Palomba1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alberto_Bacchelli:"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1912.10098"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1912-10098,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Luca Pascarella and Davide Spadini and Fabio Palomba and Alberto Bacchelli},\n  title={On The Effect Of Code Review On Code Smells},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1912.10098},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1912.10098}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code smells are symptoms of poor design quality. Since code review is a process that also aims at improving code quality, we investigate whether and how code review influences the severity of code smells. In this study, we analyze more than 21,000 code reviews belonging to seven Java open-source projects; we find that active and participated code reviews have a significant influence on the likelihood of reducing the severity of code smells. This result seems to confirm the expectations around code review's influence on code quality. However, by manually investigating 365 cases in which the severity of a code smell in a file was reduced with a review, we found that-in 95% of the cases-the reduction was a side effect of changes that reviewers requested on matters unrelated to code smells. Data and materials [https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3588501]."},"title":{"value":"On The Effect Of Code Review On Code Smells"},"authors":{"value":["Luca Pascarella","Davide Spadini","Fabio Palomba","Alberto Bacchelli"]}},"tmdate":1752557682697,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1752557548774,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fabio_Palomba1"],"forum":"JH84dxZ9Zx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":568498,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1752557682697,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"JH84dxZ9Zx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"APSEC 2009"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5358473/5358474/05358762.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/APSEC/2009"},"paperhash":{"value":"fukushima|code_clone_graph_metrics_for_detecting_diffused_code_clones"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yoshihiko_Fukushima:","~Raula_Gaikovina_Kula1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shinji_Kawaguchi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kyohei_Fushida:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Masataka_Nagura:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hajimu_Iida:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/APSEC.2009.53"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/apsec/FukushimaKKFNI09,\n  author={Yoshihiko Fukushima and Raula Gaikovina Kula and Shinji Kawaguchi and Kyohei Fushida and Masataka Nagura and Hajimu Iida},\n  title={Code Clone Graph Metrics for Detecting Diffused Code Clones},\n  year={2009},\n  cdate={1230768000000},\n  pages={373-380},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/APSEC.2009.53},\n  booktitle={APSEC},\n  crossref={conf/apsec/2009}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code clones (duplicated source code in a software system) are one of the major factors in decreasing maintainability. Many code clone detection methods have been proposed to find code clones automatically from large-scale software. However, it is still hard to find harmful code clones to improve maintainability because there are many code clones that should remain. Thus, to help find harmful code clones, we propose a code clone visualization method and a metrics application on the visualized information. Our method enables the location of harmful code clones diffused in a software system. We apply our method to three open source software programs and visualize their code clone information."},"title":{"value":"Code Clone Graph Metrics for Detecting Diffused Code Clones"},"authors":{"value":["Yoshihiko Fukushima","Raula Gaikovina Kula","Shinji Kawaguchi","Kyohei Fushida","Masataka Nagura","Hajimu Iida"]}},"tmdate":1754372937613,"pdate":1230768000000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/apsec/FukushimaKKFNI09"],"tcdate":1754372824386,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Raula_Gaikovina_Kula1"],"forum":"Mpq4uCBedT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":614431,"cdate":1230768000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1754372937613,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Mpq4uCBedT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7083369/9647862/09714838.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"xiao|cameraimu_extrinsic_calibration_quality_monitoring_for_autonomous_ground_vehicles"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2022.3151970"},"abstract":{"value":"Highly accurate sensor extrinsic calibration is critical for data fusion from multiple sensors, such as camera and Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU) sensor suit. A pre-calibrated extrinsics, however, may no longer be accurate due to external disturbances, e.g., vehicle vibration, which will lead to significant performance deterioration of autonomous vehicles. Existing approaches rely on online recalibration at a fixed frequency regardless of whether the extrinsics have actually been changed or recalibration is needed, which is computationally inefficient. In this letter, we present an approach to monitor extrinsic calibration quality for camera-IMU sensor suite to determine when recalibration is actually necessary. We propose an efficient algorithm to detect robust road image features, utilize IMU data to capture the mismatches of those features, and quantify extrinsic calibration error through three commonly-used error metrics. Our algorithm is demonstrated to be effective using both simulated and real-world data."},"title":{"value":"Camera-IMU Extrinsic Calibration Quality Monitoring for Autonomous Ground Vehicles"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Xuesu Xiao"},{"fullname":"Yulin Zhang"},{"fullname":"Haifeng Li","username":"~Haifeng_Li8"},{"fullname":"Hongpeng Wang"},{"fullname":"Binbin Li"}]}},"tmdate":1789090310874,"pdate":1648771200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/lra.2022.3151970"],"tcdate":1775217198490,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Haifeng_Li8"],"forum":"p65ilOq0ZT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":61219,"cdate":1646096776094,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789090310874,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"p65ilOq0ZT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2018"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.05781v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wu|active_semisupervised_transfer_learning_astl_for_offline_bci_calibration"},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1805.05781"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1805-05781,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Dongrui Wu},\n  title={Active Semi-supervised Transfer Learning (ASTL) for Offline BCI Calibration},\n  year={2018},\n  cdate={1514764800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1805.05781},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1805.05781}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Single-trial classification of event-related potentials in electroencephalogram (EEG) signals is a very important paradigm of brain-computer interface (BCI). Because of individual differences, usually some subject-specific calibration data are required to tailor the classifier for each subject. Transfer learning has been extensively used to reduce such calibration data requirement, by making use of auxiliary data from similar/relevant subjects/tasks. However, all previous research assumes that all auxiliary data have been labeled. This paper considers a more general scenario, in which part of the auxiliary data could be unlabeled. We propose active semi-supervised transfer learning (ASTL) for offline BCI calibration, which integrates active learning, semi-supervised learning, and transfer learning. Using a visual evoked potential oddball task and three different EEG headsets, we demonstrate that ASTL can achieve consistently good performance across subjects and headsets, and it outperforms some state-of-the-art approaches in the literature."},"title":{"value":"Active Semi-supervised Transfer Learning (ASTL) for Offline BCI Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Dongrui Wu","username":"~Dongrui_Wu1"}]}},"tmdate":1784557231808,"pdate":1546214400000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-1805-05781"],"tcdate":1784557196666,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Dongrui_Wu1"],"forum":"H0UPWijJtC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":74733,"cdate":1514764800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784557231808,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"H0UPWijJtC","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"venue":{"value":"CTB@ICML 2026"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["Adversarial Attack","conformal prediction","robustness"]},"abstract":{"value":"Conformal prediction (CP) provides distribution-free, finite-sample coverage guarantees but critically relies on exchangeability, a condition often violated under distribution shift. We study the robustness of split conformal prediction under adversarial perturbations at test time, focusing on both coverage validity and the resulting prediction {efficiency}. Our theoretical analysis characterizes how the strength of adversarial perturbations during calibration affects {the coverage gap relative to the nominal coverage level} under adversarial test conditions. We further examine the impact of adversarial training at the model-training stage. Experiments support our theory: (i) Prediction coverage varies monotonically with the calibration-time attack strength, enabling the use of nonzero calibration-time attack to predictably control coverage under adversarial tests; (ii) the marginal coverage can remain within a user-specified tolerance band around the nominal coverage level and (iii) adversarial training at the training stage produces tighter prediction sets that improve efficiency while maintaining coverage validity."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nqian2026ensuring,\ntitle={Ensuring Calibration Robustness in Split Conformal Prediction Under Adversarial Attacks},\nauthor={Xunlei Qian and Yue Xing},\nbooktitle={ICML 2026 Workshop on Combining Theory and Benchmarks: Towards A Virtuous Cycle to Understand and Guarantee Foundation Model Performance},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=TUUWndAMYk}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Ensuring Calibration Robustness in Split Conformal Prediction Under Adversarial Attacks"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/0b427872deca6e832a0316c6edb3882bfa10f6f6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB"},"paperhash":{"value":"qian|ensuring_calibration_robustness_in_split_conformal_prediction_under_adversarial_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xunlei_Qian1","~Yue_Xing1"]},"authors":{"value":["Xunlei Qian","Yue Xing"]}},"tmdate":1781429835021,"pdate":1779714420438,"tcdate":1778127970009,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/Submission94/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/Submission94/Authors"],"forum":"TUUWndAMYk","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":94,"cdate":1778127970009,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Submission_Release","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/Submission94/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Edit"],"mdate":1781429835021,"odate":1779714420438,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB","id":"TUUWndAMYk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7083369/9647862/09714838.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"xiao|cameraimu_extrinsic_calibration_quality_monitoring_for_autonomous_ground_vehicles"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuesu_Xiao:","~Yulin_Zhang2","~Haifeng_Li8","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongpeng_Wang:","~Binbin_Li2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2022.3151970"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/XiaoZLWL22,\n  author={Xuesu Xiao and Yulin Zhang and Haifeng Li and Hongpeng Wang and Binbin Li},\n  title={Camera-IMU Extrinsic Calibration Quality Monitoring for Autonomous Ground Vehicles},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={7},\n  number={2},\n  pages={4614-4621},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2022.3151970}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highly accurate sensor extrinsic calibration is critical for data fusion from multiple sensors, such as camera and Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU) sensor suit. A pre-calibrated extrinsics, however, may no longer be accurate due to external disturbances, e.g., vehicle vibration, which will lead to significant performance deterioration of autonomous vehicles. Existing approaches rely on online recalibration at a fixed frequency regardless of whether the extrinsics have actually been changed or recalibration is needed, which is computationally inefficient. In this letter, we present an approach to monitor extrinsic calibration quality for camera-IMU sensor suite to determine when recalibration is actually necessary. We propose an efficient algorithm to detect robust road image features, utilize IMU data to capture the mismatches of those features, and quantify extrinsic calibration error through three commonly-used error metrics. Our algorithm is demonstrated to be effective using both simulated and real-world data."},"title":{"value":"Camera-IMU Extrinsic Calibration Quality Monitoring for Autonomous Ground Vehicles"},"authors":{"value":["Xuesu Xiao","Yulin Zhang","Haifeng Li","Hongpeng Wang","Binbin Li"]}},"tmdate":1775217147367,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1737129542150,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yulin_Zhang2"],"forum":"2rSPXSkiv9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":267417,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1775217147367,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2rSPXSkiv9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"TLDR":{"value":"CentriQ removes the per-token mean from diffusion transformer activations before Hadamard rotation and adds it back exactly as a rank-1 term, letting 4-bit (and even 2-bit) quantization work without any calibration data."},"keywords":{"value":["Diffusion Models","Quantization","Calibration-free","DiTs","Image Generation"]},"primary_area":{"value":"generative models"},"abstract":{"value":"Diffusion transformers (DiTs) achieve state-of-the-art image generation, but their sampling cost limits deployment. Quantizing both weights and activations to 4 bits reduces this cost, yet existing methods fall short in one of two ways. Calibration-based methods are tied to a specific checkpoint and prompt distribution, whereas data-free Hadamard rotation, effective for LLMs, loses quality on DiTs. We show that this loss has a structural cause. Adaptive layer-norm conditioning adds a per-token mean to the activations, and at the widths of the evaluated DiTs, the Hadamard rotations used by data-free methods cannot spread this mean uniformly across coordinates. A single dominant direction therefore survives the rotation and sets the quantization range. We introduce CentriQ, a calibration-free quantizer that centers each token before rotation and restores the mean exactly through a rank-1 full-precision branch, so that per-token scales follow in closed form without data. Weights are fitted under a robust $\\ell_p$ objective that tracks the dense mode of each group and discounts heavy tails. Across three DiTs, CentriQ matches the quality of calibrated SVDQuant at 4 bits, whereas calibration-free weight quantizers with plain per-token activation quantization collapse or degrade substantially. CentriQ outperforms the strongest calibration-free method reported to date at 2-bit weights. It is also the first calibration-free method to retain usable image quality at 2-bit activations."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026centriq,\ntitle={CentriQ: Calibration-Free Quantization of Diffusion Transformers via Exact Mean Centering},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=y5hvGgjglI},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CentriQ: Calibration-Free Quantization of Diffusion Transformers via Exact Mean Centering"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2b2068657f5572653fae521571380994ee9ee90d.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791233619936,"tcdate":1789745317219,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission44994/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission44994/Authors"],"forum":"y5hvGgjglI","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":44994,"cdate":1789745317219,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission44994/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791233619936,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"y5hvGgjglI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MMSP 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9733399/9733432/09733556.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MMSP/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"fachada|a_calibration_method_for_subaperture_views_of_plenoptic_20_camera_arrays"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Armand_Losfeld1","","~Gauthier_Lafruit3",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/MMSP53017.2021.9733556"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/mmsp/FachadaLSLT21,\n  author={Sarah Fachada and Armand Losfeld and Takanori Senoh and Gauthier Lafruit and Mehrdad Teratani},\n  title={A Calibration Method for Subaperture Views of Plenoptic 2.0 Camera Arrays},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={1-6},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/MMSP53017.2021.9733556},\n  booktitle={MMSP},\n  crossref={conf/mmsp/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present a novel methodology to precisely calibrate the subaperture views of an array of plenoptic 2.0 cameras. Such cameras consist of a micro lens array, and the image captured through them is a lenslet image that can be converted to a dense set of pinhole views, the so-called subaperture images. This cam-era array provides several dense multiview images at some sparse points of 3D space. To find the relative position of those views, simply using structure-from-motion creates misalignments due to the small disparities within each set. Additionally, a traditional calibration using calibration patterns will also fail due to the complicated objectives of plenoptic 2.0 cameras and artifacts when they are converted to subaperture views. In this paper, we propose two calibration steps (a) to register the sparse central subaperture views using Structure-from-Motion which makes it robust to artifacts in the subaperture views, and (b) to register all dense multiview sets per plenoptic camera using camera’s lenses specifications, disparity and distance to the scene. These two steps are followed by a novel merging process of the former registrations, to achieve precise calibration parameters for all the subaperture views of the multi-plenoptic array. Experimental results objectively and subjectively demonstrate high accuracy of the calibration. We show a 10% smaller reprojection error than using a naive structure-from-motion approach and verify that our method is suitable for high precision view synthesis applications such as virtual reality and holography."},"title":{"value":"A Calibration Method for Subaperture Views of Plenoptic 2.0 Camera Arrays"},"authors":{"value":["Sarah Fachada","Armand Losfeld","Takanori Senoh","Gauthier Lafruit","Mehrdad Teratani"]}},"tmdate":1772393900024,"pdate":1640908800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/mmsp/FachadaLSLT21"],"tcdate":1772138807279,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Armand_Losfeld1"],"forum":"7Ee3LjJlpR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":828867,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772393900024,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"7Ee3LjJlpR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.19862v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"kong|calibration_without_ground_truth"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.19862"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2601-19862,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yuqing Kong and Mingyu Song and Yizhou Wang and Yifan Wu},\n  title={Calibration without Ground Truth},\n  year={2026},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2601.19862},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.19862}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Villalobos et al. [2024] predict that publicly available human text will be exhausted within the next decade. Thus, improving models without access to ground-truth labels becomes increasingly important. We propose a label-free post-processing framework that improves a strong but miscalibrated model using a weaker yet better-calibrated reference. Our framework guarantees a strict performance improvement under any proper loss. Our approach is based on a characterization of when strict improvement is possible: when the strong and reference models are not mutually calibrated. We formalize this condition, connect it to arbitrage and no-trade results from economics, and develop an efficient Bregman projection algorithm that guarantees worst-case loss reduction without labels. Experiments on representative LLMs across varying scales demonstrate that our label-free method significantly reduces proper losses and calibration errors, achieving performance competitive with supervised baselines."},"title":{"value":"Calibration without Ground Truth"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yuqing Kong","username":"~Yuqing_Kong1"},{"fullname":"Mingyu Song","username":""},{"fullname":"Yizhou Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Yifan Wu","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1793017297720,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2601-19862"],"tcdate":1793017292165,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yuqing_Kong1"],"forum":"To7nik9wuG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":211265,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1793017297720,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"To7nik9wuG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Remote. Sens. 2018"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/10/1/120/pdf?version=1516206299"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/REMOTESENSING/2018"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|vicarious_radiometric_calibration_of_the_hyperspectral_imaging_microsatellites_spark01_and_02_over_dunhuang_china"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hao_Zhang_0014:","~Bing_Zhang6","~Zhengchao_Chen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhihua_Huang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/rs10010120"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/remotesensing/ZhangZCH18,\n  author={Hao Zhang and Bing Zhang and Zhengchao Chen and Zhihua Huang},\n  title={Vicarious Radiometric Calibration of the Hyperspectral Imaging Microsatellites SPARK-01 and -02 over Dunhuang, China},\n  year={2018},\n  cdate={1514764800000},\n  journal={Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={10},\n  number={1},\n  pages={120},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/rs10010120}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Two wide-swath hyperspectral imaging microsatellites, SPARK-01 and -02, were launched on 22 December 2016. Radiometric calibration coefficients were determined for these two satellites via a calibration experiment performed from the end of February to the beginning of March 2017 at the high-altitude, homogenous Dunhuang calibration site in the Gobi Desert in China. In-situ measurements, including ground reflectance, direct transmittance, diffuse-to-global irradiance ratio, and radiosonde vertical profile, were acquired. A unique relative calibration procedure was developed using actual satellite images. This procedure included dark current computation and non-uniform correction processes. The former was computed by averaging multiple lines of long strip imagery acquired over open oceans during nighttime, while the latter was computed using images acquired after the adjustment of the satellite yaw angle to 90°. This technique was shown to be suitable for large-swath satellite image relative calibration. After relative calibration, reflectance, irradiance, and improved irradiance-based methods were used to conduct absolute radiometric calibrations in order to predict the top-of-atmosphere (TOA) radiance. The SPARK-01 and -02 satellites passed over the calibration site on 7 March and 28 February 2017, during which time fair and non-ideal weather occurred, respectively. Thus, the SPARK-01 calibration coefficient was derived using reflectance- and irradiance-based methods, while that of SPARK -02 was derived using reflectance- and improved irradiance-based methods. The sources of calibration uncertainty, which include aerosol-type assumptions, transmittance measurements, water vapor content retrieval, spectral wavelength shift and satellite image misregistration, were explored in detail for different calibration methods. Using the reflectance and irradiance-based methods, the total uncertainty for SPARK-01 was estimated to be 4.7% and 4.1%, respectively, in the <1000 nm spectral range. For SPARK-02, total uncertainties of 8.1% and of 5.9% were estimated using the reflectance- and improved irradiance-based methods, respectively. The calibration methods were also verified using MODIS images, which confirmed that the calibration accuracies were within the expected range. These in-situ measurements, analyses, and results provide a basis for in-orbit radiometric calibration of the SPARK-01 and -02 satellites. These experiments strongly support the use of diffuse-to-global ratio measurements in in-situ vicarious calibration experiments and the addition of spectrally continuous measurements for direct transmittance, which is important for hyperspectral satellite sensors."},"title":{"value":"Vicarious Radiometric Calibration of the Hyperspectral Imaging Microsatellites SPARK-01 and -02 over Dunhuang, China"},"authors":{"value":["Hao Zhang","Bing Zhang","Zhengchao Chen","Zhihua Huang"]}},"tmdate":1779951372448,"pdate":1514764800000,"tcdate":1730954374279,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bing_Zhang6"],"forum":"OTSJ7XAGCk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":173035,"cdate":1514764800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1779951372448,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"OTSJ7XAGCk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACC 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7951530/7962914/07963501.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AMCC/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|gyroaided_cameraodometer_online_calibration_and_localization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongxuan_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kevin_Eckenhoff:","~Kanzhi_Wu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yue_Wang_0020:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rong_Xiong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guoquan_Huang_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.23919/ACC.2017.7963501"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/amcc/LiEWWXH17,\n  author={Dongxuan Li and Kevin Eckenhoff and Kanzhi Wu and Yue Wang and Rong Xiong and Guoquan Huang},\n  title={Gyro-aided camera-odometer online calibration and localization},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  pages={3579-3586},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.23919/ACC.2017.7963501},\n  booktitle={ACC},\n  crossref={conf/amcc/2017}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we introduce a novel factor-graph based multi-sensor fusion algorithm that optimally estimates camera-odometer extrinsic calibration (6 DOF rigid body transformation between the two sensors) and localization aided by MEMS gyroscope measurements. In particular, we rigorously derive the camera-odometer calibration node and factors from the camera and odometer measurements. Moreover, due to the significantly higher frequency of the gyro than those of the camera and odometer, we develop analytical gyro preintegration using unit quaternions to provide a relative-rotation factor (constraint) between consecutive camera/odometer keyframes. Note that in our proposed gyro preintegration theory, the time-varying bias is addressed systematically (instead of treated in ad-hoc way, e.g., simply assumed to be constant as in most literature). The proposed approach is broadly corroborated in real-world experiments."},"title":{"value":"Gyro-aided camera-odometer online calibration and localization"},"authors":{"value":["Dongxuan Li","Kevin Eckenhoff","Kanzhi Wu","Yue Wang","Rong Xiong","Guoquan Huang"]}},"tmdate":1762438387463,"pdate":1483228800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/amcc/LiEWWXH17"],"tcdate":1762438363150,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kanzhi_Wu1"],"forum":"6BgVLqfW7I","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":666774,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762438387463,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"6BgVLqfW7I","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Pattern Anal. Mach. Intell. 2004"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/34/28218/01262199.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/PAMI/2004"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|error_analysis_of_pure_rotationbased_selfcalibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Wang:","~Sing_Bing_Kang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Heung-Yeung_Shum:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guangyou_Xu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2004.1262199"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/pami/WangKSX04,\n  author={Lei Wang and Sing Bing Kang and Heung-Yeung Shum and Guangyou Xu},\n  title={Error Analysis of Pure Rotation-Based Self-Calibration},\n  year={2004},\n  cdate={1072915200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Pattern Anal. Mach. Intell.},\n  volume={26},\n  number={2},\n  pages={275-280},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2004.1262199}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Self-calibration using pure rotation is a well-known technique and has been shown to be a reliable means for recovering intrinsic camera parameters. However, in practice, it is virtually impossible to ensure that the camera motion for this type of self-calibration is a pure rotation. In this paper, we present an error analysis of recovered intrinsic camera parameters due to the presence of translation. We derived closed-form error expressions for a single pair of images with nondegenerate motion; for multiple rotations for which there are no closed-form solutions, analysis was done through repeated experiments. Among others, we show that translation-independent solutions do exist under certain practical conditions. Our analysis can be used to help choose the least error-prone approach (if multiple approaches exist) for a given set of conditions."},"title":{"value":"Error Analysis of Pure Rotation-Based Self-Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Lei Wang","Sing Bing Kang","Heung-Yeung Shum","Guangyou Xu"]}},"tmdate":1743870394234,"pdate":1072915200000,"tcdate":1743870355402,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sing_Bing_Kang1"],"forum":"S0eFVw6cOe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":376514,"cdate":1072915200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1743870394234,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"S0eFVw6cOe","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICCV Workshops 2009"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5453386/5457418/05457556.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICCVW/2009"},"paperhash":{"value":"shabayek|a_novel_noncentral_catadioptric_autocalibration_approach"},"authorids":{"value":["~Abd_El_Rahman_Shabayek1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:David_Fofi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Olivier_Morel:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCVW.2009.5457556"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iccvw/ShabayekFM09,\n  author={Abd El Rahman Shabayek and David Fofi and Olivier Morel},\n  title={A novel non-central catadioptric auto-calibration approach},\n  year={2009},\n  cdate={1230768000000},\n  pages={2228-2235},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCVW.2009.5457556},\n  booktitle={ICCV Workshops},\n  crossref={conf/iccvw/2009}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration is the determination of coordinates of all pixels' rays in some common coordinate system. It enables to compute a 3D ray, along which light travels, for every image pixel. In this paper we propose a novel non-central catadioptric system auto-calibration approach. It uses polarization imaging to know the specular surface shape in order to estimate the catacaustics of the catadioptric system which geometrically fully describe the imaging system. The catacaustic is a locus of viewpoints. Each pixel in the image maps to a point on the caustic surface on which every point maps to a unique light ray from the scene. Polarization imaging helps to widen the range of catadioptric systems to include all types of specular surfaces. Our novel approach helps to auto-calibrate any combination of specular surfaces and lenses."},"title":{"value":"A novel non-central catadioptric auto-calibration approach"},"authors":{"value":["Abd El Rahman Shabayek","David Fofi","Olivier Morel"]}},"tmdate":1723537273356,"pdate":1230768000000,"tcdate":1723537269277,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Abd_El_Rahman_Shabayek1"],"forum":"KMvvwcEltY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":66436,"cdate":1230768000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1723537273356,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KMvvwcEltY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1606.04317v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"leeuwen|calibration_of_phone_likelihoods_in_automatic_speech_recognition"},"authorids":{"value":["~David_A._van_Leeuwen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Joost_van_Doremalen:"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1606.04317"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/LeeuwenD16,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={David A. van Leeuwen and Joost van Doremalen},\n  title={Calibration of Phone Likelihoods in Automatic Speech Recognition},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1606.04317},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1606.04317}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper we study the probabilistic properties of the posteriors in a speech recognition system that uses a deep neural network (DNN) for acoustic modeling. We do this by reducing Kaldi's DNN shared pdf-id posteriors to phone likelihoods, and using test set forced alignments to evaluate these using a calibration sensitive metric. Individual frame posteriors are in principle well-calibrated, because the DNN is trained using cross entropy as the objective function, which is a proper scoring rule. When entire phones are assessed, we observe that it is best to average the log likelihoods over the duration of the phone. Further scaling of the average log likelihoods by the logarithm of the duration slightly improves the calibration, and this improvement is retained when tested on independent test data."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of Phone Likelihoods in Automatic Speech Recognition"},"authors":{"value":["David A. van Leeuwen","Joost van Doremalen"]}},"tmdate":1716457637900,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1716457615426,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~David_A._van_Leeuwen1"],"forum":"RJ9YmBG38O","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":20631,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1716457637900,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"RJ9YmBG38O","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Desk Rejected Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Interactive World Model","3D Representation","Video Diffusion Model"]},"primary_area":{"value":"generative models"},"abstract":{"value":"Existing interactive world models accumulate geometric inconsistencies during long-sequence generation, making it difficult to maintain coherence over thousands of frames. Recent methods mostly provide visual guidance using RGB conditions rendered from 3D representations, but they still suffer from imperfect rendering and reconstruction conflicts that hinder effective geometric conditioning. We find that directly embedding 3D representations can provide geometric guidance while bypassing errors introduced during RGB rendering. Building on this finding, we propose PointWorld that explicitly uses 3D point clouds as geometric memory, rather than relying on rendered RGB proxies, for long-horizon consistency. The core is an explicit 3D representation calibration mechanism that selects relevant points from dynamically reconstructed point clouds, encodes their geometric information, and directly injects the resulting features into the video generation model. To provide reliable 3D conditioning, PointWorld further incorporates a spatiotemporal retrieval strategy into dynamic point cloud reconstruction to mitigate reconstruction conflicts. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PointWorld extends consistent interactive generation, maintaining long-term consistency across sequences of up to 2,000 frames and outperforming recent methods in both quantitative and qualitative comparisons. Our code will be made publicly available."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nanonymous2026pointworld,\ntitle={PointWorld: Explicit 3D Representation Calibration for Interactive World Models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=aNqPkhXJaJ}\n}"},"title":{"value":"PointWorld: Explicit 3D Representation Calibration for Interactive World Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ff108b80ad9b011d4f3981eb5e83c72dcbb3251f.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Desk_Rejected_Submission"}},"tmdate":1792254728211,"tcdate":1787659484732,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission4504/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission4504/Authors"],"forum":"aNqPkhXJaJ","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4504,"cdate":1787659484732,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission4504/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Desk_Rejected_Submission"],"mdate":1792254728211,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"aNqPkhXJaJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.07062v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zong|dirichletbased_prediction_calibration_for_learning_with_noisy_labels"},"authorids":{"value":["","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ye-Wen_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ming-Kun_Xie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sheng-Jun_Huang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2401.07062"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2401-07062,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Chen-Chen Zong and Ye-Wen Wang and Ming-Kun Xie and Sheng-Jun Huang},\n  title={Dirichlet-Based Prediction Calibration for Learning with Noisy Labels},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2401.07062},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2401.07062}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Learning with noisy labels can significantly hinder the generalization performance of deep neural networks (DNNs). Existing approaches address this issue through loss correction or example selection methods. However, these methods often rely on the model's predictions obtained from the softmax function, which can be over-confident and unreliable. In this study, we identify the translation invariance of the softmax function as the underlying cause of this problem and propose the \\textit{Dirichlet-based Prediction Calibration} (DPC) method as a solution. Our method introduces a calibrated softmax function that breaks the translation invariance by incorporating a suitable constant in the exponent term, enabling more reliable model predictions. To ensure stable model training, we leverage a Dirichlet distribution to assign probabilities to predicted labels and introduce a novel evidence deep learning (EDL) loss. The proposed loss function encourages positive and sufficiently large logits for the given label, while penalizing negative and small logits for other labels, leading to more distinct logits and facilitating better example selection based on a large-margin criterion. Through extensive experiments on diverse benchmark datasets, we demonstrate that DPC achieves state-of-the-art performance. The code is available at https://github.com/chenchenzong/DPC."},"title":{"value":"Dirichlet-Based Prediction Calibration for Learning with Noisy Labels"},"authors":{"value":["Chen-Chen Zong","Ye-Wen Wang","Ming-Kun Xie","Sheng-Jun Huang"]}},"tmdate":1747224545155,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1726547986781,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chenchen_Zong1"],"forum":"Rj8oHBf1nN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":81509,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747224545155,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Rj8oHBf1nN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/29672/31149"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AAAI/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zong|dirichletbased_prediction_calibration_for_learning_with_noisy_labels"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chen-Chen_Zong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ye-Wen_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ming-Kun_Xie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sheng-Jun_Huang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v38i15.29672"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aaai/ZongWXH24,\n  author={Chen-Chen Zong and Ye-Wen Wang and Ming-Kun Xie and Sheng-Jun Huang},\n  title={Dirichlet-Based Prediction Calibration for Learning with Noisy Labels},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={17254-17262},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v38i15.29672},\n  booktitle={AAAI},\n  crossref={conf/aaai/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Learning with noisy labels can significantly hinder the generalization performance of deep neural networks (DNNs). Existing approaches address this issue through loss correction or example selection methods. However, these methods often rely on the model's predictions obtained from the softmax function, which can be over-confident and unreliable. In this study, we identify the translation invariance of the softmax function as the underlying cause of this problem and propose the \\textit{Dirichlet-based Prediction Calibration} (DPC) method as a solution. Our method introduces a calibrated softmax function that breaks the translation invariance by incorporating a suitable constant in the exponent term, enabling more reliable model predictions. To ensure stable model training, we leverage a Dirichlet distribution to assign probabilities to predicted labels and introduce a novel evidence deep learning (EDL) loss. The proposed loss function encourages positive and sufficiently large logits for the given label, while penalizing negative and small logits for other labels, leading to more distinct logits and facilitating better example selection based on a large-margin criterion. Through extensive experiments on diverse benchmark datasets, we demonstrate that DPC achieves state-of-the-art performance. The code is available at https://github.com/chenchenzong/DPC."},"title":{"value":"Dirichlet-Based Prediction Calibration for Learning with Noisy Labels"},"authors":{"value":["Chen-Chen Zong","Ye-Wen Wang","Ming-Kun Xie","Sheng-Jun Huang"]}},"tmdate":1726547993396,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1726547986804,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chenchen_Zong1"],"forum":"Ko3tldYZdR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":81512,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1726547993396,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Ko3tldYZdR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Scientific Reports"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6c2360eae005ef9a40182ac81a7c3ce9e3d1e19f.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|excs_accelerating_code_search_with_code_expansion"},"authorids":{"value":["~Siwei_Huang1","~Bo_Cai2","~Yaoxiang_Yu1","~jianluo1"]},"html":{"value":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-73907-6.pdf"},"abstract":{"value":"Efficiently searching and reusing code from expansive codebases is pivotal for enhancing developers' productivity. In recent times, the emergence of deep learning-driven neural ranking models, characterized by their vast dimensions and intricate interaction mechanisms, has been noteworthy. Yet, these models, in real-world scenarios, pose computational challenges due to their high dimensionality. Moreover, models rooted in interaction necessitate querying every piece of code within a voluminous corpus. While these methodologies offer superior accuracy, their online retrieval process is considerably more time-consuming compared to traditional Information Retrieval (IR) techniques. Addressing this, we introduce \"ExCS\", an innovative code search tool designed to expedite the code search process without compromising on accuracy. ExCS innovatively employs code expansion in its offline phase, leveraging predictions on potential queries for specific codes, thereby enriching the code's semantic depth. During online retrieval, ExCS prioritizes IR-based methods to pinpoint a concise set of persuasive candidates. Our evaluations, conducted on the Java dataset from CodeSearchNet, reveal that ExCS achieves a remarkable 90% reduction in retrieval duration while maintaining an impressive 99% retrieval accuracy."},"title":{"value":"ExCS: accelerating code search with code expansion"},"authors":{"value":["Siwei Huang","Bo Cai","Yaoxiang Yu","jianluo"]}},"tmdate":1769089037656,"pdate":1732464000000,"tcdate":1769089037656,"writers":["~Siwei_Huang1","~Bo_Cai2","~Yaoxiang_Yu1","~jianluo1"],"signatures":["~Siwei_Huang1"],"forum":"iLUo0LCZul","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":42323,"cdate":1769089037656,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1769089037656,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"iLUo0LCZul","version":2},{"content":{"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d4c5b6fcc85c95c15f031c7804b0b44a88c990aa.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|cost_calibration_learning_for_imbalanced_classification_with_dynamic_cost_uncertainty"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jincheng_Zhang4"]},"abstract":{"value":"Traditional cost-sensitive learning paradigms heavily rely on the idealized assumption that both the predicted conditional probabilities and the misclassification cost matrix are precisely specified and static. However, in real-world applications, deep neural networks often suffer from severe miscalibration, exhibiting overconfidence in their outputs. Concurrently, the operational cost structures provided by domain experts are frequently imprecise, ambiguous, and subject to dynamic fluctuations, leading to the challenge of cost miscalibration. To resolve the joint degradation caused by these two phenomena, this paper proposes a novel framework named Cost Calibration Learning (CCAL).\n\nUnlike conventional approaches that focus solely on probability alignment or static thresholding, CCAL introduces a dual-calibration mechanism. At its core, a dedicated Cost Calibration Module (CCM) is formulated to adaptively rectify the nominal, initial cost matrix based on the instance-level feature representations and the real-time classifier status. By employing a continuous, differentiable approximation of the empirical decision loss, the entire framework—comprising the primary classification network, the temperature scaling factor, and the parameters of the Cost Calibration Module—can be optimized in an end-to-end, joint training fashion. Theoretical derivations demonstrate that CCAL effectively establishes an optimized, instance-specific Bayesian decision boundary under severe uncertainty. The proposed framework provides a robust, cost-effective, and theoretically grounded solution for deploying deep learning models in cost-critical and class-imbalanced environments."},"title":{"value":"Cost Calibration Learning for Imbalanced Classification with Dynamic Cost Uncertainty"},"authors":{"value":["Jincheng Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1784198472370,"pdate":1784198457500,"tcdate":1784198472370,"writers":["~Jincheng_Zhang4"],"signatures":["~Jincheng_Zhang4"],"forum":"WqaJsU5vnS","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":51989,"cdate":1784198472370,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1784198472370,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"WqaJsU5vnS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"WACV 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5707201/5711474/05711521.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/WACV/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"puwein|robust_multiview_camera_calibration_for_widebaseline_camera_networks"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jens_Puwein:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Remo_Ziegler:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Julia_Vogel:","~Marc_Pollefeys1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV.2011.5711521"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/wacv/PuweinZVP11,\n  author={Jens Puwein and Remo Ziegler and Julia Vogel and Marc Pollefeys},\n  title={Robust multi-view camera calibration for wide-baseline camera networks},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={321-328},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV.2011.5711521},\n  booktitle={WACV},\n  crossref={conf/wacv/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Real-world camera networks are often characterized by very wide baselines covering a wide range of viewpoints. We describe a method not only calibrating each camera sequence added to the system automatically, but also taking advantage of multi-view correspondences to make the entire calibration framework more robust. Novel camera sequences can be seamlessly integrated into the system at any time, adding to the robustness of future computations. One of the challenges consists in establishing correspondences between cameras. Initializing a bag of features from a calibrated frame, correspondences between cameras are established in a two-step procedure. First, affine invariant features of camera sequences are warped into a common coordinate frame and a coarse matching is obtained between the collected features and the incrementally built and updated bag of features. This allows us to warp images to a common view. Second, scale invariant features are extracted from the warped images. This leads to both more numerous and more accurate correspondences. Finally, the parameters are optimized in a bundle adjustment. Adding the feature descriptors and the optimized 3D positions to the bag of features, we obtain a feature-based scene abstraction, allowing for the calibration of novel sequences and the correction of drift in single-view calibration tracking. We demonstrate that our approach can deal with wide baselines. Novel sequences can seamlessly be integrated in the calibration framework."},"title":{"value":"Robust multi-view camera calibration for wide-baseline camera networks"},"authors":{"value":["Jens Puwein","Remo Ziegler","Julia Vogel","Marc Pollefeys"]}},"tmdate":1762679714268,"pdate":1293840000000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/wacv/PuweinZVP11"],"tcdate":1762679696859,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Marc_Pollefeys2"],"forum":"sLnzbaiSWB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":681047,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762679714268,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"sLnzbaiSWB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Comput. Vis. Image Underst. 2006"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CVIU/2006"},"paperhash":{"value":"sinha|pantiltzoom_camera_calibration_and_highresolution_mosaic_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sudipta_N._Sinha:","~Marc_Pollefeys1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cviu.2006.06.002"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/cviu/SinhaP06,\n  author={Sudipta N. Sinha and Marc Pollefeys},\n  title={Pan-tilt-zoom camera calibration and high-resolution mosaic generation},\n  year={2006},\n  cdate={1136073600000},\n  journal={Comput. Vis. Image Underst.},\n  volume={103},\n  number={3},\n  pages={170-183},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cviu.2006.06.002}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we discuss the problem of estimating parameters of a calibration model for active pan–tilt–zoom cameras. The variation of the intrinsic parameters of each camera over its full range of zoom settings is estimated through a two step procedure. We first determine the intrinsic parameters at the camera’s lowest zoom setting very accurately by capturing an extended panorama. The camera intrinsics and radial distortion parameters are then determined at discrete steps in a monotonically increasing zoom sequence that spans the full zoom range of the camera. Our model incorporates the variation of radial distortion with camera zoom. Both calibration phases are fully automatic and do not assume any knowledge of the scene structure. High-resolution calibrated panoramic mosaics are also computed during this process. These fully calibrated panoramas are represented as multi-resolution pyramids of cube-maps. We describe a hierarchical approach for building multiple levels of detail in panoramas, by aligning hundreds of images captured within a 1–12× zoom range. Results are shown from datasets captured from two types of pan–tilt–zoom cameras placed in an uncontrolled outdoor environment. The estimated camera intrinsics model along with the cube-maps provides a calibration reference for images captured on the fly by the active pan–tilt–zoom camera under operation making our approach promising for active camera network calibration."},"title":{"value":"Pan-tilt-zoom camera calibration and high-resolution mosaic generation"},"authors":{"value":["Sudipta N. Sinha","Marc Pollefeys"]}},"tmdate":1762679712629,"pdate":1136073600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/cviu/SinhaP06"],"tcdate":1762679697468,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Marc_Pollefeys2"],"forum":"YdAZGvlNJb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":681075,"cdate":1136073600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762679712629,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"YdAZGvlNJb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Wirel. Commun. 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7693/9426619/09320525.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TWC/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|effect_of_signal_propagation_model_calibration_on_localization_performance_limits_for_wireless_sensor_networks"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bingpeng_Zhou:","~Hing_Cheung_So1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shahid_Mumtaz:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TWC.2020.3048744"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/twc/ZhouSM21,\n  author={Bingpeng Zhou and Hing Cheung So and Shahid Mumtaz},\n  title={Effect of Signal Propagation Model Calibration on Localization Performance Limits for Wireless Sensor Networks},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Wirel. Commun.},\n  volume={20},\n  number={5},\n  pages={3254-3268},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TWC.2020.3048744}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we focus on wireless sensor network-based localization for user devices (UDs). Prior to UD localization, signal propagation model (SPM) is required to calibrate using training samples from a number of location grids. However, SPM usually suffers from measurement noise and inevitable error in calibration grid (CG) locations. This will significantly degrade UD localization performance. Nevertheless, the impact of CG location error, CG layout and the number of CGs on SPM calibration performance has not been characterized. Furthermore, the effect of SPM calibration error on UD localization performance has not been developed. In this paper, we aim to provide a unified framework for performance analysis of SPM calibration and UD localization. Firstly, we establish a closed-form Cramér-Rao lower bound on SPM calibration error and UD localization error, respectively. Secondly, the impact of measurement noise, CG location error and the number of CGs on SPM calibration performance is revealed. Thirdly, the influence of SPM calibration error, CG location error and measurement noise on UD localization performance is studied. The effect of modeling mismatch is also studied. The obtained analysis framework builds a theoretical basis for the design of efficient system optimization strategies, including resource allocation and CG deployment optimization, for UD localization performance enhancement."},"title":{"value":"Effect of Signal Propagation Model Calibration on Localization Performance Limits for Wireless Sensor Networks"},"authors":{"value":["Bingpeng Zhou","Hing Cheung So","Shahid Mumtaz"]}},"tmdate":1747925337081,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1747925227476,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hing_Cheung_So1"],"forum":"29APLccO8N","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":548866,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747925337081,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"29APLccO8N","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose an automated pipeline to clean code data"},"venue":{"value":"SyntheticData4ML 2023 Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/437d2a0fb0dd71d35f0cfd29d4a0c154204f1ba2.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Code Generation","Data Quality","Synthetic Datasets"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/SyntheticData4ML"},"paperhash":{"value":"jain|improving_code_style_for_accurate_code_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Naman_Jain2","~Tianjun_Zhang1","~Wei-Lin_Chiang1","~Joseph_E._Gonzalez1","~Koushik_Sen2","~Ion_Stoica1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Natural language to code generation is an important application area of LLMs and has received wide attention from the community. \nThe majority of relevant studies have exclusively concentrated on increasing the quantity and functional correctness of training sets while disregarding other stylistic elements of programs. More recently, data quality has garnered a lot of interest and multiple works have showcased its importance for improving performance. In this work, we investigate data quality for code and find that making the code more structured and readable leads to improved code generation performance of the system. We build a novel data-cleaning pipeline that uses these principles to transform existing programs by 1.) renaming variables, 2.) modularizing and decomposing complex code into smaller helper sub-functions, and 3.) inserting natural-language based planning annotations. We evaluate our approach on two challenging algorithmic code generation benchmarks and find that fine-tuning CodeLLaMa-7B on our transformed programs improves the performance by up to \\textbf{30\\%} compared to fine-tuning on the original dataset. Additionally, we demonstrate improved performance from using a smaller amount of higher-quality data, finding that a model fine-tuned on the entire original dataset is outperformed by a model trained on one-eighth of our cleaned dataset. Even in comparison to closed-source models, our models outperform the much larger AlphaCode models."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\njain2023improving,\ntitle={Improving Code Style for Accurate Code Generation},\nauthor={Naman Jain and Tianjun Zhang and Wei-Lin Chiang and Joseph E. Gonzalez and Koushik Sen and Ion Stoica},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2023 Workshop on Synthetic Data Generation with Generative AI},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=uc9sYHRX6O}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Improving Code Style for Accurate Code Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Naman Jain","Tianjun Zhang","Wei-Lin Chiang","Joseph E. Gonzalez","Koushik Sen","Ion Stoica"]}},"tmdate":1701372947176,"pdate":1698695511167,"tcdate":1695985197154,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/SyntheticData4ML","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/SyntheticData4ML/Submission40/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/SyntheticData4ML/Submission40/Authors"],"forum":"uc9sYHRX6O","number":40,"cdate":1695985197154,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/SyntheticData4ML/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/SyntheticData4ML/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/SyntheticData4ML/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/SyntheticData4ML/Submission40/-/Revision"],"mdate":1701372947176,"odate":1701372947163,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/SyntheticData4ML","id":"uc9sYHRX6O","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.02827v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"peng|coffe_a_code_efficiency_benchmark_for_code_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yun_Peng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Wan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yichen_Li:","~Xiaoxue_Ren1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.02827"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2502-02827,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yun Peng and Jun Wan and Yichen Li and Xiaoxue Ren},\n  title={COFFE: A Code Efficiency Benchmark for Code Generation},\n  year={2025},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1738368000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2502.02827},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.02827}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code generation has largely improved development efficiency in the era of large language models (LLMs). With the ability to follow instructions, current LLMs can be prompted to generate code solutions given detailed descriptions in natural language. Many research efforts are being devoted to improving the correctness of LLM-generated code, and many benchmarks are proposed to evaluate the correctness comprehensively. Despite the focus on correctness, the time efficiency of LLM-generated code solutions is under-explored. Current correctness benchmarks are not suitable for time efficiency evaluation since their test cases cannot well distinguish the time efficiency of different code solutions. Besides, the current execution time measurement is not stable and comprehensive, threatening the validity of the time efficiency evaluation. To address the challenges in the time efficiency evaluation of code generation, we propose COFFE, a code generation benchmark for evaluating the time efficiency of LLM-generated code solutions. COFFE contains 398 and 358 problems for function-level and file-level code generation, respectively. To improve the distinguishability, we design a novel stressful test case generation approach with contracts and two new formats of test cases to improve the accuracy of generation. For the time evaluation metric, we propose efficienct@k based on CPU instruction count to ensure a stable and solid comparison between different solutions. We evaluate 14 popular LLMs on COFFE and identify four findings. Based on the findings, we draw some implications for LLM researchers and software practitioners to facilitate future research and usage of LLMs in code generation."},"title":{"value":"COFFE: A Code Efficiency Benchmark for Code Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Yun Peng","Jun Wan","Yichen Li","Xiaoxue Ren"]}},"tmdate":1753188857858,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1753188849819,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiaoxue_Ren1"],"forum":"Lilyn7Hd5M","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":581663,"cdate":1738368000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753188857858,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Lilyn7Hd5M","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.09310v3"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"simeone|conformal_calibration_ensuring_the_reliability_of_blackbox_ai_in_wireless_systems"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.09310"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-09310,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Osvaldo Simeone and Sangwoo Park and Matteo Zecchin},\n  title={Conformal Calibration: Ensuring the Reliability of Black-Box AI in Wireless Systems},\n  year={2025},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1743465600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2504.09310},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.09310}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"AI is poised to revolutionize telecommunication networks by boosting efficiency, automation, and decision-making. However, the black-box nature of most AI models introduces substantial risk, possibly deterring adoption by network operators. These risks are not addressed by the current prevailing deployment strategy, which typically follows a best-effort train-and-deploy paradigm. This paper reviews conformal calibration, a general framework that moves beyond the state of the art by adopting computationally lightweight, advanced statistical tools that offer formal reliability guarantees without requiring further training or fine-tuning. Conformal calibration encompasses pre-deployment calibration via uncertainty quantification or hyperparameter selection; online monitoring to detect and mitigate failures in real time; and counterfactual post-deployment performance analysis to address \"what if\" diagnostic questions after deployment. By weaving conformal calibration into the AI model lifecycle, network operators can establish confidence in black-box AI models as a dependable enabling technology for wireless systems."},"title":{"value":"Conformal Calibration: Ensuring the Reliability of Black-Box AI in Wireless Systems"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Osvaldo Simeone","username":""},{"fullname":"Sangwoo Park","username":""},{"fullname":"Matteo Zecchin","username":"~Matteo_Zecchin1"}]}},"tmdate":1791442293514,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2504-09310"],"tcdate":1791442283806,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Matteo_Zecchin1"],"forum":"ZoZItK8hUO","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":166567,"cdate":1743465600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791442293514,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"ZoZItK8hUO","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["LLMs","Uncertainty","Calibration","Reasoning"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/b350bb8c23c4266ca0ef64277489097f1ff16ae8.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\njang2026confidence,\ntitle={Confidence is Not Universal: Task-Dependent Calibration and Emergent Behavior in {LLM}s},\nauthor={Chaeyun Jang and Moonseok Choi and Yegon Kim and Seungyoo Lee and Juho Lee and Hyungi Lee},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=1dvDUYc8nm}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Confidence is Not Universal: Task-Dependent Calibration and Emergent Behavior in LLMs"},"paperhash":{"value":"jang|confidence_is_not_universal_taskdependent_calibration_and_emergent_behavior_in_llms"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/3d2f57aa23134a9ab4085d1d6c98f338f35cdc8a.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"Verbalized confidence cannot be universally calibrated with a single scalar across tasks; instead, separating it into reasoning uncertainty and evidence-localization uncertainty restores cross-task generalization without sacrificing accuracy."},"primary_area":{"value":"deep_learning->large_language_models"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) increasingly support human decision-making, rendering human-interpretable confidence essential. However, it remains unclear whether verbalized confidence calibration generalizes across heterogeneous tasks without degrading accuracy. We show that universal confidence calibration fails. Across diverse benchmarks, we identify two incompatible task families with distinct confidence semantics. In reasoning-centric tasks, confidence supervision transfers within the family, often improving calibration while preserving or even improving accuracy, and induces emergent behaviors such as confidence-dependent reasoning length and self-verification. Retrieval- and copy-oriented tasks also exhibit within-family transfer, but fail to generalize to reasoning tasks, with cross-family supervision degrading both calibration and accuracy. Motivated by this finding, we disentangle confidence into reasoning uncertainty and evidence localization uncertainty. This simple decomposition restores cross-family generalization using supervised fine-tuning alone, suggesting that effective confidence alignment requires task-aware semantics rather than a universal scalar notion."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c445c67db1257b6d8cf2ba64bf7d44d903b8c447.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"AI language models are increasingly used to help people make important decisions, from solving math problems to answering questions about long documents. For these tools to be trustworthy, they should not only give an answer but also report how confident they are, so that people know when to double check.\n\nMany researchers have assumed that a model could be trained once to report its confidence reliably across every kind of task. We show that this assumption does not hold. Confidence turns out to carry two different meanings. In some cases a model is unsure because its step by step reasoning might be wrong, as in a math problem. In other cases it is unsure because it cannot locate the right piece of information inside a long text. Training a model to express one kind of confidence makes it worse at the other.\n\nWe also observed that when a model was trained to judge its confidence on reasoning problems, it began to think longer and check its own work whenever it was unsure, which led to more accurate answers.\n\nOur solution is simple. We let the model report two separate confidence scores, one for its reasoning and one for finding information. This restores reliable confidence across all task types, without any complex training."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chaeyun_Jang1","~Moonseok_Choi1","~Yegon_Kim1","~Seungyoo_Lee3","~Juho_Lee2","~Hyungi_Lee1"]},"authors":{"value":["Chaeyun Jang","Moonseok Choi","Yegon Kim","Seungyoo Lee","Juho Lee","Hyungi Lee"]}},"tmdate":1790067443807,"pdate":1777576930350,"tcdate":1769235274325,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission31858/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission31858/Authors"],"forum":"1dvDUYc8nm","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":31858,"cdate":1769235274325,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission31858/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission31858/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067443807,"odate":1782342035707,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"1dvDUYc8nm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Remote. Sens. 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/14/15/3521/pdf?version=1658986762"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/REMOTESENSING/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|a_quadrifocal_tensor_sfm_photogrammetry_positioning_and_calibration_technique_for_hofs_aerial_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_Wang_0075:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yan_Zhang:","~Yongsheng_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ying_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shaocong_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiang_Zhao:","~Zhenchao_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Longhui_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/rs14153521"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/remotesensing/WangZZYLLZZW22,\n  author={Tao Wang and Yan Zhang and Yongsheng Zhang and Ying Yu and Lei Li and Shaocong Liu and Xiang Zhao and Zhenchao Zhang and Longhui Wang},\n  title={A Quadrifocal Tensor SFM Photogrammetry Positioning and Calibration Technique for HOFS Aerial Sensors},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={14},\n  number={15},\n  pages={3521},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/rs14153521}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Nowadays, the integration between photogrammetry and structure from motion (SFM) has become much closer, and many attempts have been made to combine the two approaches to realize the positioning, calibration, and 3D reconstruction of a large number of images. For the positioning and calibration of high oblique frame sweep (HOFS) aerial cameras, a quadrifocal tensor SFM photogrammetry technique is proposed to resolve the positioning and calibration task of such cameras. It adopts the quadrifocal tensor idea into the OpenMVG SFM pipeline to solve the complexity problem caused by the small single-viewing imaging area and the high image overlapping ratio. It also integrates the photogrammetry iteration idea into the OpenMVG SFM pipeline to enhance the positioning and calibration accuracy, which includes a coarse to fine three-stage Bundle Adjustment (BA) processing approach. In this paper, the overall workflow of the proposed technique was first introduced in detail, from feature extraction and image matching, relative rotation and translation estimation, global rotation and translation estimation, and the quadrifocal tensor model construction to the three-stage BA process and calibration. Then, experiments were carried out in the Zhengzhou area, implementing four types of adjustment methods. The results suggest that the proposed quadrifocal tensor SFM photogrammetry is suitable for large tilt frame sweep camera positioning and calibration without prior information on detailed camera intrinsic parameters and structure. The modifications made to the OpenMVG SFM pipeline enhanced the precision of image positioning and calibration and provided the precision level of professional photogrammetry software."},"title":{"value":"A Quadrifocal Tensor SFM Photogrammetry Positioning and Calibration Technique for HOFS Aerial Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Tao Wang","Yan Zhang","Yongsheng Zhang","Ying Yu","Lei Li","Shaocong Liu","Xiang Zhao","Zhenchao Zhang","Longhui Wang"]}},"tmdate":1744851719120,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1743990110262,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhenchao_Zhang1"],"forum":"icbwB2rrUS","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":376778,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1744851719120,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"icbwB2rrUS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.17541v4"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"yoshida|towards_understanding_variants_of_invariant_risk_minimization_through_the_lens_of_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kotaro_Yoshida:","~Hiroki_Naganuma1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2401.17541"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2401-17541,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Kotaro Yoshida and Hiroki Naganuma},\n  title={Towards Understanding Variants of Invariant Risk Minimization through the Lens of Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2401.17541},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2401.17541}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Machine learning models traditionally assume that training and test data are independently and identically distributed. However, in real-world applications, the test distribution often differs from training. This problem, known as out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization, challenges conventional models. Invariant Risk Minimization (IRM) emerges as a solution that aims to identify invariant features across different environments to enhance OOD robustness. However, IRM's complexity, particularly its bi-level optimization, has led to the development of various approximate methods. Our study investigates these approximate IRM techniques, using the consistency and variance of calibration across environments as metrics to measure the invariance aimed for by IRM. Calibration, which measures the reliability of model prediction, serves as an indicator of whether models effectively capture environment-invariant features by showing how uniformly over-confident the model remains across varied environments. Through a comparative analysis of datasets with distributional shifts, we observe that Information Bottleneck-based IRM achieves consistent calibration across different environments. This observation suggests that information compression techniques, such as IB, are potentially effective in achieving model invariance. Furthermore, our empirical evidence indicates that models exhibiting consistent calibration across environments are also well-calibrated. This demonstrates that invariance and cross-environment calibration are empirically equivalent. Additionally, we underscore the necessity for a systematic approach to evaluating OOD generalization. This approach should move beyond traditional metrics, such as accuracy and F1 scores, which fail to account for the model's degree of over-confidence, and instead focus on the nuanced interplay between accuracy, calibration, and model invariance."},"title":{"value":"Towards Understanding Variants of Invariant Risk Minimization through the Lens of Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Kotaro Yoshida","Hiroki Naganuma"]}},"tmdate":1747570429756,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747570425731,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hiroki_Naganuma1"],"forum":"PgjZrYoZ7n","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":521249,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747570429756,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"PgjZrYoZ7n","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Accepted by TMLR"},"code":{"value":"https://github.com/katoro8989/IRM_Variants_Calibration"},"abstract":{"value":"Machine learning models traditionally assume that training and test data are independently and identically distributed. However, in real-world applications, the test distribution often differs from training. This problem, known as out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization, challenges conventional models. Invariant Risk Minimization (IRM) emerges as a solution that aims to identify invariant features across different environments to enhance OOD robustness. However, IRM's complexity, particularly its bi-level optimization, has led to the development of various approximate methods. Our study investigates these approximate IRM techniques, using the consistency and variance of calibration across environments as metrics to measure the invariance aimed for by IRM. Calibration, which measures the reliability of model prediction, serves as an indicator of whether models effectively capture environment-invariant features by showing how uniformly over-confident the model remains across varied environments. Through a comparative analysis of datasets with distributional shifts, we observe that Information Bottleneck-based IRM achieves consistent calibration across different environments. This observation suggests that information compression techniques, such as IB, are potentially effective in achieving model invariance. Furthermore, our empirical evidence indicates that models exhibiting consistent calibration across environments are also well-calibrated. This demonstrates that invariance and cross-environment calibration are empirically equivalent. Additionally, we underscore the necessity for a systematic approach to evaluating OOD generalization. This approach should move beyond traditional metrics, such as accuracy and F1 scores, which fail to account for the model’s degree of over-confidence, and instead focus on the nuanced interplay between accuracy, calibration, and model invariance."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nyoshida2024towards,\ntitle={Towards Understanding Variants of Invariant Risk Minimization through the Lens of Calibration},\nauthor={Kotaro Yoshida and Hiroki Naganuma},\njournal={Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nissn={2835-8856},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=9YqacugDER},\nnote={}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Towards Understanding Variants of Invariant Risk Minimization through the Lens of Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e2804db67a26b06931fd3f25d9f301c70b9829e0.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR"},"paperhash":{"value":"yoshida|towards_understanding_variants_of_invariant_risk_minimization_through_the_lens_of_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kotaro_Yoshida1","~Hiroki_Naganuma1"]},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Jeffrey_Pennington1"},"authors":{"value":["Kotaro Yoshida","Hiroki Naganuma"]}},"tmdate":1726599881436,"pdate":1718713752568,"tcdate":1713430093315,"writers":["TMLR"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper2544/Authors"],"forum":"9YqacugDER","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2544,"cdate":1713430093315,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/Paper2544/-/Revision","TMLR/Paper2544/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","TMLR/-/Accepted"],"mdate":1726599881436,"odate":1714481602468,"domain":"TMLR","id":"9YqacugDER","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.14615v2"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|calibanyview_beyond_singleview_camera_calibration_in_the_wild"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.14615"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2605-14615,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Boying Li and Cheng Zhang and Weirong Chen and Daniel Cremers and Ian D. Reid and Hamid Rezatofighi},\n  title={CalibAnyView: Beyond Single-View Camera Calibration in the Wild},\n  year={2026},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1777593600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2605.14615},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.14615}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration is fundamental to reliable geometric perception, yet classical approaches rely on dedicated targets, successful reconstruction, or dense view coverage, which casually captured imagery rarely satisfies. Recent learning-based single-image methods lift these requirements by exploiting visual cues, and extend calibration beyond intrinsics to gravity estimation. Yet in the common multi-view case, they predict each view independently and combine the estimates only afterwards, lacking full use of cross-view consistency. We bridge this gap with CalibAnyView, a framework that unifies single- and sparse multi-view calibration by enforcing that consistency inside the network: a transformer with cross-view attention predicts camera-model-agnostic perspective fields, followed by a multi-view optimization that fuses them into shared intrinsics and per-view gravity directions, covering camera models from pinhole to severely distorted lenses. To support this, we construct a large-scale in-the-wild multi-view video dataset spanning diverse camera models, dynamic scenes, realistic motion trajectories, and heterogeneous lens distortions. Extensive experiments show that CalibAnyView outperforms state-of-the-art methods in both sparse multi-view and single-view settings spanning pinhole to distorted optics."},"title":{"value":"CalibAnyView: Beyond Single-View Camera Calibration in the Wild"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Boying Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Cheng Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Weirong Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Daniel Cremers","username":""},{"fullname":"Ian D. Reid","username":""},{"fullname":"Hamid Rezatofighi","username":"~Hamid_Rezatofighi1"}]}},"tmdate":1792096619068,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2605-14615"],"tcdate":1792096612501,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Hamid_Rezatofighi1"],"forum":"rEQTH7ElAx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":190437,"cdate":1777593600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1792096619068,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"rEQTH7ElAx","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"This paper proposes CoCal (Companion Confidence Calibration), which uses a separate companion module to learn confidence calibration alongside capability improvement during RLVR."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM Honesty","Confidence Calibration","RLVR"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Reliable self-assessment is essential for large language models (LLMs), yet they often remain highly confident when their answers are wrong. We study \\emph{concurrent confidence calibration}, where confidence is learned alongside capability improvement rather than calibrated only after training. Reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards (RLVR) provides a natural setting for this paradigm, as it continuously produces responses paired with verifiable correctness feedback. Existing concurrent methods, however, learn both capability and confidence through reinforcement learning within shared policy parameters, potentially coupling two fundamentally different learning problems. We instead propose \\emph{shared experience but separate learning}: capability and confidence learn from the same trajectories, but through separate optimization mechanisms and parameters. Based on this principle, we introduce \\textbf{CoCal (Companion Confidence Calibration)}, which trains a lightweight companion from rollout hidden states and verifier-derived correctness supervision while leaving task optimization unchanged. Experiments on Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-14B show that CoCal improves confidence estimation without sacrificing task performance, outperforming both RL-based concurrent methods and matched post-hoc calibration. The learned companion further generalizes across domains and policy shifts, while the benefits of CoCal persist at both scales."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026shared,\ntitle={Shared Experience, Separate Learning: Companion Confidence Calibration for {LLM}s},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=1DRfBQuVyz},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Shared Experience, Separate Learning: Companion Confidence Calibration for LLMs"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2c7fcf2db35983e3120801ebfa434c03ddf5cfa2.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791232419410,"tcdate":1789715203694,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission38551/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission38551/Authors"],"forum":"1DRfBQuVyz","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":38551,"cdate":1789715203694,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission38551/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791232419410,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"1DRfBQuVyz","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We analyze conformal calibration with response-free balancing weights under covariate shift, deriving coverage bounds and showing why spectral resolution relative to regularization matters beyond weight concentration."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["conformal prediction","covariate shift","uncertainty quantification","distributional balancing","kernel mean matching","spectral analysis"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/e763579bb4d2232b4438a25da850b1098b8d4e31.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Conformal prediction provides distribution-free marginal coverage under exchangeability. Under covariate shift, covariate distributions change while the conditional response distribution remains unchanged. Weighted conformal prediction corrects this mismatch using known density ratios, which can be difficult to estimate. We propose conformal calibration with distributional balancing weights, learned to align source calibration and unlabeled target covariate distributions without using calibration or target responses. Characteristic function distance provides a common framework for kernel-based and energy-based balancing. We establish a finite-sample error bound for estimating the target score distribution, yielding approximate marginal coverage guarantees. The bound separates covariate imbalance, approximation of conditional score probabilities, target-sample uncertainty, and weight concentration. We connect this bound to weight optimization and develop a validation rule for selecting weights without responses. A spectral analysis shows that nearly uniform fitted weights can miss increasingly fine-scale shifts and yield persistent undercoverage despite bounded density ratios. Controlled synthetic and semi-synthetic experiments reproduce this failure and show that tuning bandwidth and regularization can restore near-nominal coverage. Together, these results highlight the importance of kernel choice and regularization beyond controlling weight concentration alone."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026distributional,\ntitle={Distributional Balancing Weights for Conformal Calibration under Covariate Shift},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=YZY46VxTHS},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Distributional Balancing Weights for Conformal Calibration under Covariate Shift"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f2a77708b004a4ce14e1c30f5fd3dbbea445c430.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791230633901,"tcdate":1789584275928,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission26638/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission26638/Authors"],"forum":"YZY46VxTHS","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":26638,"cdate":1789584275928,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission26638/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791230633901,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"YZY46VxTHS","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"CLAWS makes activation sparsity practical by combining Fisher-calibrated routing with hardware-friendly row-sparse masks, improving Gemma-4-E2B-IT MMLU at 50% FFN density while delivering a real ARM MLP speedup."},"venue":{"value":"AdaptFM Poster"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["Sparse neural networks","model pruning","knowledge distillation","efficient inference","transformer compression","activation sparsity","parameter-efficient learning","large language models"]},"abstract":{"value":"Modern LLMs contain gated MLPs whose per-token activations are highly skewed, a natural axis for dynamic top-K\nsparsification. Practical sparsity needs both accurate routing and a mask the inference kernel can execute efficiently.\nCATS (Lee et al., 2024) gives the hardware-friendly row-wise mask but routes on local gate information alone, while\nLaRoSA (Liu et al., 2025) improves routing through rotating the basis with calibration data, but induces a column-sparse\npattern poorly aligned with quantized row-major layouts. We introduce CLAWS, a gradient-saliency calibration method for\ngated-MLP sparsification that keeps the CATS row-sparse execution pattern and multiplies the runtime gate score by a\nstatic per-neuron saliency constant, so the top-K mask still maps directly to quantized kernels. We also explain why\nthis multiplier reorders the native gate score but barely moves LaRoSA's rotated-input score, isolating where\ncalibration helps. On Gemma-4-E2B-IT at 50\\% FFN density, CLAWS recovers dense-level Avg5 and surpasses matched-recipe\nCATS with LoRA by 6.8pp MMLU (49.6 vs. 42.8), and a custom ARM kernel delivers a $\\times 1.24$ wall-clock speedup on the full\nMLP block."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\ncylich2026claws,\ntitle={{CLAWS}: Calibration-Aware Activation Sparsity for Instruction-Tuned {LLM}s},\nauthor={Noah Cylich and Karen Mosoyan and Henry Ndubuaku and Roman Shemet},\nbooktitle={AdaptFM: Resource-Adaptive Foundation Model Inference},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=kdZbHVOjd6}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CLAWS: Calibration-Aware Activation Sparsity for Instruction-Tuned LLMs"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/1868135757b6643e1d86916843263343942a4d63.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AdaptFM"},"paperhash":{"value":"cylich|claws_calibrationaware_activation_sparsity_for_instructiontuned_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["~Noah_Cylich1","~Karen_Mosoyan1","~Henry_Ndubuaku1","~Roman_Shemet1"]},"authors":{"value":["Noah Cylich","Karen Mosoyan","Henry Ndubuaku","Roman Shemet"]}},"tmdate":1781154202715,"pdate":1780353776095,"tcdate":1778324313883,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AdaptFM","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AdaptFM/Submission213/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AdaptFM/Submission213/Authors"],"forum":"kdZbHVOjd6","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":213,"cdate":1778324313883,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AdaptFM/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AdaptFM/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AdaptFM/-/Submission_Release","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AdaptFM/Submission213/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AdaptFM/-/Edit"],"mdate":1781154202715,"odate":1780353776095,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AdaptFM","id":"kdZbHVOjd6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Neurocomputing 2022"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJON/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhao|episodebased_personalization_network_for_gaze_estimation_without_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoyu_Zhao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yaping_Huang:","~Yi_Tian3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mei_Tian:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2022.09.050"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijon/ZhaoHTT22,\n  author={Xiaoyu Zhao and Yaping Huang and Yi Tian and Mei Tian},\n  title={Episode-based personalization network for gaze estimation without calibration},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={Neurocomputing},\n  volume={513},\n  pages={36-45},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2022.09.050}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Appearance-based methods employing deep convolutional architectures have achieved impressive performance for unconstrained gaze estimation. However, due to inter-personal anatomical differences, existing methods always get biased results for person-independent tasks. Generally, improvements can be achieved by adapting the models to a specified person with few calibration samples. Nevertheless, the calibration-based strategies are likely to collapse to high errors as over-parameterized neural networks easily fall into over-fitting. In this paper, we propose an Episode-based Personalization Network (EbPN) for person-independent gaze estimation, which does not rely on any calibration samples. Firstly, inspired by multi-task learning, we propose a two-branch gaze estimation network, including appearance-aware network and personalization-aware network. Secondly, during training, our model is optimized with a collection of episodes, each of which is designed to imitate a few-shot gaze estimation task. Through multiple episodes, our EbPN model progressively accumulates the experience on capturing appearance and personalized information of each image, which would help our model get lower errors and generalize well on any new persons. Experiments on GazeCapture, MPIIGaze and ETH-XGaze datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of our method."},"title":{"value":"Episode-based personalization network for gaze estimation without calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Xiaoyu Zhao","Yaping Huang","Yi Tian","Mei Tian"]}},"tmdate":1744364503507,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1744364492035,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yi_Tian3"],"forum":"8ENaFcIN92","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":391530,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744364503507,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"8ENaFcIN92","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9560720/9560666/09561216.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|linebased_automatic_extrinsic_calibration_of_lidar_and_camera"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinyu_Zhang_0001:","~Shifan_Zhu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shichun_Guo:","~Jun_Li25","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Huaping_Liu_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA48506.2021.9561216"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/ZhangZGLL21,\n  author={Xinyu Zhang and Shifan Zhu and Shichun Guo and Jun Li and Huaping Liu},\n  title={Line-based Automatic Extrinsic Calibration of LiDAR and Camera},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={9347-9353},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA48506.2021.9561216},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Reliable real-time extrinsic parameters of 3D Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) and camera are a key component of multi-modal perception systems. However, extrinsic transformation may drift gradually during operation, which can result in decreased accuracy of perception system. To solve this problem, we propose a line-based method that enables automatic online extrinsic calibration of LiDAR and camera in real-world scenes. Herein, the line feature is selected to constrain the extrinsic parameters for its ubiquity. Initially, the line features are extracted and filtered from point clouds and images. Afterwards, an adaptive optimization is utilized to provide accurate extrinsic parameters. We demonstrate that line features are robust geometric features that can be extracted from point clouds and images, thus contributing to the extrinsic calibration. To demonstrate the benefits of this method, we evaluate it on KITTI benchmark with ground truth value. The experiments verify the accuracy of the calibration approach. In online experiments on hundreds of frames, our approach automatically corrects miscalibration errors and achieves an accuracy of 0.2 degrees, which verifies its applicability in various scenarios. This work can provide basis for perception systems and further improve the performance of other algorithms that utilize these sensors."},"title":{"value":"Line-based Automatic Extrinsic Calibration of LiDAR and Camera"},"authors":{"value":["Xinyu Zhang","Shifan Zhu","Shichun Guo","Jun Li","Huaping Liu"]}},"tmdate":1731718104913,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1731561298395,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jun_Li25"],"forum":"yPoVHmctwQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":237754,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1731718104913,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"yPoVHmctwQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.19950v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"du|confidence_calibration_for_multimodal_llms_an_empirical_study_through_medical_vqa"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.19950"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2606-19950,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yuetian Du and Yucheng Wang and Ming Kong and Tian Liang and Qiang Long and Bingdi Chen and Qiang Zhu},\n  title={Confidence Calibration for Multimodal LLMs: An Empirical Study through Medical VQA},\n  year={2026},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1780272000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2606.19950},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.19950}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) show great potential in medical tasks, but their elicited confidence often misaligns with actual accuracy, potentially leading to misdiagnosis or overlooking correct advice. This study presents the first comprehensive analysis of the relationship between accuracy and confidence in medical MLLMs. It proposes a novel method that combines Multi-Strategy Fusion-Based Interrogation (MS-FBI) with auxiliary expert LLM assessment, aiming to improve confidence calibration in Medical Visual Question Answering (VQA). Experiments demonstrate that our method reduces the Expected Calibration Error (ECE) by an average of 40\\% across three Medical VQA datasets, significantly enhancing MLLMs' reliability. The findings highlight the importance of domain-specific calibration for MLLMs in healthcare, offering a more trustworthy solution for AI-assisted diagnosis."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration for Multimodal LLMs: An Empirical Study through Medical VQA"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yuetian Du","username":"~Yuetian_Du1"},{"fullname":"Yucheng Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Ming Kong","username":""},{"fullname":"Tian Liang","username":""},{"fullname":"Qiang Long","username":""},{"fullname":"Bingdi Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Qiang Zhu","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1785072798287,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2606-19950"],"tcdate":1785072795401,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yuetian_Du1"],"forum":"6rlj02br4I","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":106547,"cdate":1780272000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785072798287,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"6rlj02br4I","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MICCAI (6) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-032-04978-0_9.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"du|confidence_calibration_for_multimodal_llms_an_empirical_study_through_medical_vqa"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-04978-0_9"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/miccai/DuWKLLCZ25,\n  author={Yuetian Du and Yucheng Wang and Ming Kong and Tian Liang and Qiang Long and Bingdi Chen and Qiang Zhu},\n  title={Confidence Calibration for Multimodal LLMs: An Empirical Study Through Medical VQA},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={89-99},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-04978-0_9},\n  booktitle={MICCAI (6)},\n  crossref={conf/miccai/2025-6}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) show great potential in medical tasks, but their elicited confidence often misaligns with actual accuracy, potentially leading to misdiagnosis or overlooking correct advice. This study presents the first comprehensive analysis of the relationship between accuracy and confidence in medical MLLMs. It proposes a novel method that combines Multi-Strategy Fusion-Based Interrogation (MS-FBI) with auxiliary expert LLM assessment, aiming to improve confidence calibration in Medical Visual Question Answering (VQA). Experiments demonstrate that our method reduces the Expected Calibration Error (ECE) by an average of 40% across three Medical VQA datasets, significantly enhancing MLLMs’ reliability. The findings highlight the importance of domain-specific calibration for MLLMs in healthcare, offering a more trustworthy solution for AI-assisted diagnosis."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration for Multimodal LLMs: An Empirical Study Through Medical VQA"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yuetian Du","username":"~Yuetian_Du1"},{"fullname":"Yucheng Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Ming Kong","username":"~Ming_Kong1"},{"fullname":"Tian Liang","username":""},{"fullname":"Qiang Long","username":""},{"fullname":"Bingdi Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Qiang Zhu","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1785072795475,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/miccai/DuWKLLCZ25"],"tcdate":1779779402886,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Ming_Kong1"],"forum":"qDJ4XV05UP","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":27158,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1785072795475,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"qDJ4XV05UP","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Geosci. Remote. Sens. Lett. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8859/10034981/10102530.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/LGRS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"ge|modified_adaptive_2d_calibration_algorithm_for_airborne_multichannel_sargmti"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Beibei_Ge:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daoxiang_An:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinyuan_Liu_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dong_Feng:","~Leping_Chen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhimin_Zhou:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/lgrs.2023.3267148"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/lgrs/GeALFCZ23,\n  author={Beibei Ge and Daoxiang An and Jinyuan Liu and Dong Feng and Leping Chen and Zhimin Zhou},\n  title={Modified Adaptive 2-D Calibration Algorithm for Airborne Multichannel SAR-GMTI},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={IEEE Geosci. Remote. Sens. Lett.},\n  volume={20},\n  pages={1-5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/lgrs.2023.3267148}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The performance of multichannel synthetic aperture radar (SAR)-based ground moving target indication (GMTI) is inevitably restricted by channel imperfections and imbalances, and channel calibration procedure is incorporated into the scheme accordingly. The adaptive 2-D calibration (A2DC) algorithm is sensitive to outliers and suffers from correction errors. To solve the problem, a modified A2DC (MA2DC) algorithm is proposed in this letter. By improving the Doppler calibration model, the proposed MA2DC is more robust than the A2DC algorithm. It can effectively suppress clutter while maintaining detailed features of moving targets, after which the detection capability of moving targets in SAR-GMTI is improved. Its performance is demonstrated by the Gotcha data, which is publicly released by Air Force Research Laboratory."},"title":{"value":"Modified Adaptive 2-D Calibration Algorithm for Airborne Multichannel SAR-GMTI"},"authors":{"value":["Beibei Ge","Daoxiang An","Jinyuan Liu","Dong Feng","Leping Chen","Zhimin Zhou"]}},"tmdate":1762941994092,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/lgrs/GeALFCZ23"],"tcdate":1762941295524,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Leping_Chen1"],"forum":"64ljiI394n","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":693421,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762941994092,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"64ljiI394n","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/9717300/09895291.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"yu|statistical_analysis_of_infield_magnetometer_calibration_for_two_representative_methods"},"authorids":{"value":["~Hairong_Yu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ying_Guo_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lin_Ye:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Steven_Weidong_Su:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2022.3207818"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/YuGYS22,\n  author={Hairong Yu and Ying Guo and Lin Ye and Steven Weidong Su},\n  title={Statistical Analysis of In-Field Magnetometer Calibration for Two Representative Methods},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={71},\n  pages={1-8},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2022.3207818}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this article, we propose a procedure for the investigation of the feasibility and effectiveness of recently developed in-field magnetometer calibration methods. Extensive numerical analyses under different noise levels are conducted to identify the proper measurement conditions of each method. The variance analysis is also presented to study the observability of model parameters based on the information matrix under different inclination angles. In accordance with the analysis, several important conclusions are drawn including the condition to ensure the validity of the parameter estimation and the approach to improve the performance of the calibration results. Numerical simulation and experimental validation support our analytical findings. Accordingly, this study provides an in-depth exploration of the limitations of typical in-field magnetometer calibration methods."},"title":{"value":"Statistical Analysis of In-Field Magnetometer Calibration for Two Representative Methods"},"authors":{"value":["Hairong Yu","Ying Guo","Lin Ye","Steven Weidong Su"]}},"tmdate":1749118204178,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1749118200772,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hairong_Yu1"],"forum":"BHQPddO2P1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":555929,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1749118204178,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"BHQPddO2P1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sci. China Inf. Sci. 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11432-018-9726-3.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CHINAF/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|parameter_estimation_survey_for_multijoint_robot_dynamic_calibration_case_study"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shaolin_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuo_Wang_0001:","~Fengshui_Jing1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Min_Tan_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11432-018-9726-3"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/chinaf/ZhangWJT19,\n  author={Shaolin Zhang and Shuo Wang and Fengshui Jing and Min Tan},\n  title={Parameter estimation survey for multi-joint robot dynamic calibration case study},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={Sci. China Inf. Sci.},\n  volume={62},\n  number={10},\n  pages={202203:1-202203:15},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s11432-018-9726-3}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate model parameters are the basis of robot dynamics. Many linear and nonlinear models have been proposed to calibrate the inertial parameters and friction parameters of multi-joint robots. However, methods of choosing a model and calculating its parameters still have few summaries. This paper reviews typical linear/nonlinear models and different calculation methods for robot dynamic calibration. Through simulations, the features of different methods are analyzed, including torque error, parameter error, model adaptability, solution time, and anti-interference ability of the calibration results. Finally, an experiment performed on a six-degree-of-freedom industrial manipulator is used as an example to illustrate how to select the model for a specified robot. These comparisons and experiments provide references for the parameter calibration of multi-joint robots."},"title":{"value":"Parameter estimation survey for multi-joint robot dynamic calibration case study"},"authors":{"value":["Shaolin Zhang","Shuo Wang","Fengshui Jing","Min Tan"]}},"tmdate":1740981251898,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1740981239772,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fengshui_Jing1"],"forum":"GUymZUFWxX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":344533,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740981251898,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"GUymZUFWxX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ITSC 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9921415/9921739/09922234.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ITSC/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"ye|efficient_calibration_of_agentbased_traffic_simulation_using_variational_autoencoder"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peijun_Ye:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fenghua_Zhu:","~Yisheng_Lv1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiao_Wang_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuanyuan_Chen:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC55140.2022.9922234"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/itsc/YeZLWC22,\n  author={Peijun Ye and Fenghua Zhu and Yisheng Lv and Xiao Wang and Yuanyuan Chen},\n  title={Efficient Calibration of Agent-Based Traffic Simulation Using Variational Auto-Encoder},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={3077-3082},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC55140.2022.9922234},\n  booktitle={ITSC},\n  crossref={conf/itsc/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In agent-based traffic simulation, calibration is an essential stage before the models applied to reproduce the individual/group travel behaviors. While traditional methods suffer from a high computational complexity, this paper proposes an improved method to alleviate the computational burden for large-scaled simulations. Specifically, we introduce variational auto-encoder to compress the original agent state vector into a lower dimensional hidden space, where the state transfer probability is calculated fast. Then the probability is mapped into the original space through a decoder, to achieve the agent travel parameters. The dynamic calibration method is tested with other baselines in urban travel demand analysis. Experiment results demonstrate that our method brings about 19% elevation of efficiency with the same accuracy of calibration."},"title":{"value":"Efficient Calibration of Agent-Based Traffic Simulation Using Variational Auto-Encoder"},"authors":{"value":["Peijun Ye","Fenghua Zhu","Yisheng Lv","Xiao Wang","Yuanyuan Chen"]}},"tmdate":1731533255481,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731478368587,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yisheng_Lv1"],"forum":"XadYn2BEor","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":205810,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731533255481,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"XadYn2BEor","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Signal Process. 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/78/7453252/07398163.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TSP/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"houssineau|a_unified_approach_for_multiobject_triangulation_tracking_and_camera_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jeremie_Houssineau1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daniel_E._Clark:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Spela_Ivekovic:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chee_Sing_Lee_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jose_Franco:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSP.2016.2523454"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tsp/HoussineauCILF16,\n  author={Jeremie Houssineau and Daniel E. Clark and Spela Ivekovic and Chee Sing Lee and Jose Franco},\n  title={A Unified Approach for Multi-Object Triangulation, Tracking and Camera Calibration},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Signal Process.},\n  volume={64},\n  number={11},\n  pages={2934-2948},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TSP.2016.2523454}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Object triangulation, 3-D object tracking, feature correspondence, and camera calibration are key problems for estimation from camera networks. This paper addresses these problems within a unified Bayesian framework for joint multi-object tracking and camera calibration, based on the finite set statistics methodology. In contrast to the mainstream approaches, an alternative parametrization is investigated for triangulation, called disparity space. The approach for feature correspondence is based on the probability hypothesis density (phd) filter, and hence inherits the ability to handle the initialization of new tracks as well as the discrimination between targets and clutter within a Bayesian paradigm. The phd filtering approach then forms the basis of a camera calibration method from static or moving objects. Results are shown on simulated and real data."},"title":{"value":"A Unified Approach for Multi-Object Triangulation, Tracking and Camera Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Jeremie Houssineau","Daniel E. Clark","Spela Ivekovic","Chee Sing Lee","Jose Franco"]}},"tmdate":1727747949847,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1727747929264,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jeremie_Houssineau1"],"forum":"lCTiJyk4tR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":125933,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727747949847,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"lCTiJyk4tR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10341341/10341342/10341911.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"dang|multiplanar_selfcalibration_for_mobile_cobot_3d_object_manipulation_using_2d_detectors_and_depth_estimation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tuan_Dang1","~Khang_Nguyen3","~Manfred_Huber1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS55552.2023.10341911"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/Dang0H23,\n  author={Tuan Dang and Khang Nguyen and Manfred Huber},\n  title={Multiplanar Self-Calibration for Mobile Cobot 3D Object Manipulation Using 2D Detectors and Depth Estimation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1782-1788},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS55552.2023.10341911},\n  booktitle={IROS}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration is the first and foremost step in dealing with sensor displacement errors that can appear during extended operation and off-time periods to enable robot object manipulation with precision. In this paper, we present a novel multiplanar self-calibration between the camera system and the robot's end-effector for 3D object manipulation. Our approach first takes the robot end-effector as ground truth to calibrate the camera's position and orientation while the robot arm moves the object in multiple planes in 3D space, and a 2D state-of-the-art vision detector identifies the object's center in the image coordinates system. The transformation between world coordinates and image coordinates is then computed using 2D pixels from the detector and 3D known points obtained by robot kinematics. Next, an integrated stereo-vision system estimates the distance between the camera and the object, resulting in 3D object localization. We test our proposed method on the Baxter robot with two 7-DOF arms and a 2D detector that can run in real time on an onboard GPU. After self-calibrating, our robot can localize objects in 3D using an RGB camera and depth image. The source code is available at https://github.com/tuantdang/calib_cobot."},"title":{"value":"Multiplanar Self-Calibration for Mobile Cobot 3D Object Manipulation Using 2D Detectors and Depth Estimation"},"authors":{"value":["Tuan Dang","Khang Nguyen","Manfred Huber"]}},"tmdate":1762252757637,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1716489804019,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Manfred_Huber1"],"forum":"IZyNQ7EODs","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":20884,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762252757637,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"IZyNQ7EODs","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICCV 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10376473/10376477/10377733.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICCV/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|drtune_improving_finetuning_of_pretrained_visual_models_by_distribution_regularization_with_semantic_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Nan_Zhou1","~Jiaxin_Chen2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Di_Huang_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCV51070.2023.00149"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iccv/ZhouC023,\n  author={Nan Zhou and Jiaxin Chen and Di Huang},\n  title={DR-Tune: Improving Fine-tuning of Pretrained Visual Models by Distribution Regularization with Semantic Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1547-1556},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCV51070.2023.00149},\n  booktitle={ICCV},\n  crossref={conf/iccv/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The visual models pretrained on large-scale benchmarks encode general knowledge and prove effective in building more powerful representations for downstream tasks. Most existing approaches follow the fine-tuning paradigm, either by initializing or regularizing the downstream model based on the pretrained one. The former fails to retain the knowledge in the successive fine-tuning phase, thereby prone to be over-fitting, and the latter imposes strong constraints to the weights or feature maps of the downstream model without considering semantic drift, often incurring insufficient optimization. To deal with these issues, we propose a novel fine-tuning framework, namely distribution regularization with semantic calibration (DR-Tune). It employs distribution regularization by enforcing the downstream task head to decrease its classification error on the pretrained feature distribution, which prevents it from over-fitting while enabling sufficient training of downstream encoders. Furthermore, to alleviate the interference by semantic drift, we develop the semantic calibration (SC) module to align the global shape and class centers of the pretrained and downstream feature distributions. Extensive experiments on widely used image classification datasets show that DR-Tune consistently improves the performance when combing with various backbones under different pretraining strategies. Code is available at: https://github.com/weeknan/DR-Tune."},"title":{"value":"DR-Tune: Improving Fine-tuning of Pretrained Visual Models by Distribution Regularization with Semantic Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Nan Zhou","Jiaxin Chen","Di Huang"]}},"tmdate":1731472944686,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727701553693,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jiaxin_Chen2"],"forum":"7iVPrDTmsg","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":113444,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1731472944686,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"7iVPrDTmsg","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.188.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"xia|influences_on_llm_calibration_a_study_of_response_agreement_loss_functions_and_prompt_styles"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Pedro_Henrique_Luz_de_Araujo1","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.188/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/XiaAZ025,\n  author={Yuxi Xia and Pedro Henrique Luz de Araujo and Klim Zaporojets and Benjamin Roth},\n  title={Influences on LLM Calibration: A Study of Response Agreement, Loss Functions, and Prompt Styles},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={3740-3761},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.188/},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2025-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration, the alignment between model confidence and prediction accuracy, is critical for the reliable deployment of large language models (LLMs). Existing works neglect to measure the generalization of their methods to other prompt styles and different sizes of LLMs. To address this, we define a controlled experimental setting covering 12 LLMs and four prompt styles. We additionally investigate if incorporating the response agreement of multiple LLMs and an appropriate loss function can improve calibration performance. Concretely, we build Calib-n, a novel framework that trains an auxiliary model for confidence estimation that aggregates responses from multiple LLMs to capture inter-model agreement. To optimize calibration, we integrate focal and AUC surrogate losses alongside binary cross-entropy. Experiments across four datasets demonstrate that both response agreement and focal loss improve calibration from baselines. We find that few-shot prompts are the most effective for auxiliary model-based methods, and auxiliary models demonstrate robust calibration performance across accuracy variations, outperforming LLMs’ internal probabilities and verbalized confidences. These insights deepen the understanding of influence factors in LLM calibration, supporting their reliable deployment in diverse applications."},"title":{"value":"Influences on LLM Calibration: A Study of Response Agreement, Loss Functions, and Prompt Styles"},"authors":{"value":["Yuxi Xia","Pedro Henrique Luz de Araujo","Klim Zaporojets","Benjamin Roth"]}},"tmdate":1775656016224,"pdate":1767139200000,"tcdate":1775656012123,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Pedro_Henrique_Luz_de_Araujo1"],"forum":"oQnqZdXQmW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":865383,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1775656016224,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"oQnqZdXQmW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10801246/10801290/10801705.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wirth|automatic_spatial_calibration_of_nearfield_mimo_radar_with_respect_to_optical_depth_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["~Vanessa_Wirth1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Johanna_Bräunig:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Danti_Khouri:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Florian_Gutsche:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Martin_Vossiek:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tim_Weyrich:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marc_Stamminger:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS58592.2024.10801705"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/0001BKGVWS24,\n  author={Vanessa Wirth and Johanna Bräunig and Danti Khouri and Florian Gutsche and Martin Vossiek and Tim Weyrich and Marc Stamminger},\n  title={Automatic Spatial Calibration of Near-Field MIMO Radar With Respect to Optical Depth Sensors},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={8322-8329},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS58592.2024.10801705},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite an emerging interest in MIMO radar, the utilization of its complementary strengths in combination with optical depth sensors has so far been limited to far-field applications, due to the challenges that arise from mutual sensor calibration in the near field. In fact, most related approaches in the autonomous industry propose target-based calibration methods using corner reflectors that have proven to be unsuitable for the near field. In contrast, we propose a novel, joint calibration approach for optical RGB-D sensors and MIMO radars that is designed to operate in the radar’s near-field range, within decimeters from the sensors. Our pipeline consists of a bespoke calibration target, allowing for automatic target detection and localization, followed by the spatial calibration of the two sensor coordinate systems through target registration. We validate our approach using two different depth sensing technologies from the optical domain. The experiments show the efficiency and accuracy of our calibration for various target displacements, as well as its robustness of our localization in terms of signal ambiguities."},"title":{"value":"Automatic Spatial Calibration of Near-Field MIMO Radar With Respect to Optical Depth Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Vanessa Wirth","Johanna Bräunig","Danti Khouri","Florian Gutsche","Martin Vossiek","Tim Weyrich","Marc Stamminger"]}},"tmdate":1762933868887,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iros/0001BKGVWS24"],"tcdate":1762933841646,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Vanessa_Wirth1"],"forum":"KV7CLdrBA0","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":690437,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762933868887,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KV7CLdrBA0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.03991v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"xia|influences_on_llm_calibration_a_study_of_response_agreement_loss_functions_and_prompt_styles"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yuxi_Xia1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pedro_Henrique_Luz_de_Araujo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Klim_Zaporojets:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Benjamin_Roth_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.03991"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-03991,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yuxi Xia and Pedro Henrique Luz de Araujo and Klim Zaporojets and Benjamin Roth},\n  title={Influences on LLM Calibration: A Study of Response Agreement, Loss Functions, and Prompt Styles},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2501.03991},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.03991}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration, the alignment between model confidence and prediction accuracy, is critical for the reliable deployment of large language models (LLMs). Existing works neglect to measure the generalization of their methods to other prompt styles and different sizes of LLMs. To address this, we define a controlled experimental setting covering 12 LLMs and four prompt styles. We additionally investigate if incorporating the response agreement of multiple LLMs and an appropriate loss function can improve calibration performance. Concretely, we build Calib-n, a novel framework that trains an auxiliary model for confidence estimation that aggregates responses from multiple LLMs to capture inter-model agreement. To optimize calibration, we integrate focal and AUC surrogate losses alongside binary cross-entropy. Experiments across four datasets demonstrate that both response agreement and focal loss improve calibration from baselines. We find that few-shot prompts are the most effective for auxiliary model-based methods, and auxiliary models demonstrate robust calibration performance across accuracy variations, outperforming LLMs' internal probabilities and verbalized confidences. These insights deepen the understanding of influence factors in LLM calibration, supporting their reliable deployment in diverse applications."},"title":{"value":"Influences on LLM Calibration: A Study of Response Agreement, Loss Functions, and Prompt Styles"},"authors":{"value":["Yuxi Xia","Pedro Henrique Luz de Araujo","Klim Zaporojets","Benjamin Roth"]}},"tmdate":1742766884928,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1742766882488,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yuxi_Xia1"],"forum":"R3Vor8f9QF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":372062,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1742766884928,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"R3Vor8f9QF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6287639/10005208/10190560.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ACCESS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"kim|automatic_extrinsic_calibration_of_a_camera_and_a_2d_lidar_with_pointline_correspondences"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jae-Yeul_Kim1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jong-Eun_Ha:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2023.3298055"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/KimH23a,\n  author={Jae-Yeul Kim and Jong-Eun Ha},\n  title={Automatic Extrinsic Calibration of a Camera and a 2D LiDAR With Point-Line Correspondences},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={11},\n  pages={76904-76912},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2023.3298055}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Extrinsic calibration of a 2D camera and a 2D LiDAR is necessary to fuse information from two sensors by representing the information under the same frame. Various geometric constraints such as point-plane, point-line, and point-point are used for the extrinsic calibration. Usually, these require a manual step, including control points selection for camera calibration and LiDAR points. We propose a new algorithm for automatic extrinsic calibration with point-line correspondences. A calibration structure with two perpendicular planes having a chessboard on both sides is used for the extrinsic calibration. First, we use predefined colors at specific locations on a chessboard to quickly find the origin of the coordinate system. Second, we robustly detect three control points on LiDAR raw data using a geometric constraint that two end points among three control points should lie on the same line. The initial linear solution is obtained by using a point-line constraint. Finally, it is refined by nonlinear minimization, which gives a 15.3% improvement compared to the linear solution. Experimental results show the feasibility of the proposed algorithm."},"title":{"value":"Automatic Extrinsic Calibration of a Camera and a 2D LiDAR With Point-Line Correspondences"},"authors":{"value":["Jae-Yeul Kim","Jong-Eun Ha"]}},"tmdate":1741148450054,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1741148447282,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jaeyeul_Kim1"],"forum":"zhWD06JiZ0","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":351660,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1741148450054,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zhWD06JiZ0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS)"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/857ddbd96d074245ed9947c1857896abf70cb61e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"wirth|automatic_spatial_calibration_of_nearfield_mimo_radar_with_respect_to_optical_depth_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["~Vanessa_Wirth1","johanna.braeunig@fau.de","danti.khouri@fau.de","florian.gutsche@fau.de","~Martin_Vossiek1","~Tim_Weyrich1","~Marc_Stamminger1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.10981"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite an emerging interest in MIMO radar, the utilization of its complementary strengths in combination with optical depth sensors has so far been limited to far-field applications, due to the challenges that arise from mutual sensor calibration in the near field. In fact, most related approaches in the autonomous industry propose target-based calibration methods using corner reflectors that have proven to be unsuitable for the near field. In contrast, we propose a novel, joint calibration approach for optical RGB-D sensors and MIMO radars that is designed to operate in the radar's near-field range, within decimeters from the sensors. Our pipeline consists of a bespoke calibration target, allowing for automatic target detection and localization, followed by the spatial calibration of the two sensor coordinate systems through target registration. We validate our approach using two different depth sensing technologies from the optical domain. The experiments show the efficiency and accuracy of our calibration for various target displacements, as well as its robustness of our localization in terms of signal ambiguities."},"title":{"value":"Automatic Spatial Calibration of Near-Field MIMO Radar With Respect to Optical Depth Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Vanessa Wirth","Johanna Bräunig","Danti Khouri","Florian Gutsche","Martin Vossiek","Tim Weyrich","Marc Stamminger"]}},"tmdate":1730728861963,"pdate":1727733600000,"tcdate":1730728861963,"writers":["~Vanessa_Wirth1","johanna.braeunig@fau.de","danti.khouri@fau.de","florian.gutsche@fau.de","~Martin_Vossiek1","~Tim_Weyrich1","~Marc_Stamminger1"],"signatures":["~Vanessa_Wirth1"],"forum":"KjYaQCE8TM","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":31118,"cdate":1730728861963,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1730728861963,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"KjYaQCE8TM","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Int. J. Comput. Vis. 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11263-010-0335-9.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJCV/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"espuny|generic_selfcalibration_of_central_cameras_from_two_rotational_flows"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ferran_Espuny1",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11263-010-0335-9"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijcv/EspunyG11,\n  author={Ferran Espuny and José I. Burgos Gil},\n  title={Generic Self-calibration of Central Cameras from Two Rotational Flows},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  journal={Int. J. Comput. Vis.},\n  volume={91},\n  number={2},\n  pages={131-145},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s11263-010-0335-9}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We address the self-calibration of a smooth generic central camera from only two dense rotational flows produced by rotations of the camera about two unknown linearly independent axes passing through the camera centre. We give a closed-form theoretical solution to this problem, and we prove that it can be solved exactly up to a linear orthogonal transformation ambiguity. Using the theoretical results, we propose an algorithm for the self-calibration of a generic central camera from two rotational flows."},"title":{"value":"Generic Self-calibration of Central Cameras from Two Rotational Flows"},"authors":{"value":["Ferran Espuny","José I. Burgos Gil"]}},"tmdate":1776632194482,"pdate":1325289600000,"tcdate":1776632183747,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ferran_Espuny-Pujol2"],"forum":"9DAFEOKpKE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":868180,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1776632194482,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9DAFEOKpKE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["model merging","mixture-of-experts","routing"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/ae10f5e5fdf504221dbd9ef6a53f0b6120aad8fc.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhuang2026when,\ntitle={When Model Merging Breaks Routing: Training-Free Calibration for MoE},\nauthor={Canbin Huang and Tianyuan Shi and Xiaojun Quan and Jingang Wang and Jianfei Zhang and Qifan Wang},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=1ORZJtadQl}\n}"},"title":{"value":"When Model Merging Breaks Routing: Training-Free Calibration for MoE"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|when_model_merging_breaks_routing_trainingfree_calibration_for_moe"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/5e334496531a7240b860edf4d077e486b1cef274.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"deep_learning->large_language_models"},"abstract":{"value":"Model merging has emerged as a cost-effective approach for consolidating the capabilities of multiple LLMs without retraining. However, existing merging techniques, largely based on linear parameter arithmetic or optimization, struggle when applied to Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures. We identify a critical failure mode in MoE merging, termed *routing breakdown*, in which the merged router fails to dispatch tokens to suitable experts. Routing breakdown stems from the sensitivity of the non-linear softmax and discrete Top-$k$ routing mechanisms to parameter perturbations from merging, a sensitivity further amplified by load-balancing constraints imposed during MoE pretraining. Because fine-tuned experts exhibit distinct specializations, even modest misrouting can cause severe performance degradation. To address this issue, we propose Hessian-Aware Router Calibration (HARC), a training-free framework that leverages second-order curvature information to realign the merged router. This approach admits a closed-form solution that can be efficiently solved using a matrix-free conjugate gradient method. Experiments on mathematical reasoning and code generation tasks show that HARC effectively mitigates routing breakdown across diverse MoE merging baselines and leads to substantial performance improvements."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://github.com/huangcb01/HARC"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a649d7eb04d5fd4325c60f8be0ec94d7cbb69dad.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Building powerful AI models is expensive. A popular cost-saving shortcut is “model merging”—combining several specialized AI models into a single, smarter one without the high cost of training from scratch. However, this shortcut fails when applied to a popular AI architecture called “Mixture-of-Experts” (MoE). We found that standard merging methods confuse the router, causing it to assign tasks to the wrong experts, which severely degrades the model’s performance. To fix this “routing breakdown,” we developed HARC (Hessian-Aware Router Calibration). HARC is a training-free method that recalibrates the router after merging, ensuring tasks are directed to the correct experts. Unlike other solutions, it requires no expensive retraining. In experiments on mathematical reasoning and coding tasks, HARC successfully fixed the routing errors and significantly boosted the merged model’s performance. Our work makes it possible to reliably combine these complex AI models, saving both computational resources and energy."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Canbin_Huang1","~Tianyuan_Shi1","~Xiaojun_Quan1","~Jingang_Wang1","~Jianfei_Zhang2","~Qifan_Wang2"]},"authors":{"value":["Canbin Huang","Tianyuan Shi","Xiaojun Quan","Jingang Wang","Jianfei Zhang","Qifan Wang"]}},"tmdate":1790067402427,"pdate":1777576673346,"tcdate":1769164651069,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23532/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23532/Authors"],"forum":"1ORZJtadQl","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":23532,"cdate":1769164651069,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23532/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission23532/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067402427,"odate":1782341989707,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"1ORZJtadQl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["AI-Generated Image Detection","Synthetic Image Detection","Digital Forensics","Forgery Detection","Commercial AIGC Detection"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhou2026pgc,\ntitle={{PGC}: Peak-Guided Calibration for Generalizable {AI}-Generated Image Detection},\nauthor={Xiaoyu Zhou and Jianwei Fei and Peipeng Yu and Jingchang Xie and Chong Cheng and Zhihua Xia},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=yEjix8H6Dw}\n}"},"title":{"value":"PGC: Peak-Guided Calibration for Generalizable AI-Generated Image Detection"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|pgc_peakguided_calibration_for_generalizable_aigenerated_image_detection"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/106ef1f5ddd0da4522d17de282b4ea7c6acaa40b.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"applications->computer_vision"},"abstract":{"value":"The rapid evolution of generative AI, from GANs to modern diffusion models, has resulted in increasingly subtle discriminative clues. These fine-grained signals are often overshadowed by dominant, high-fidelity image content (e.g., the main subject), limiting the reliability of existing detectors that predominantly rely on global representations. To address this challenge, we propose the Peak-Guided Calibration (PGC) framework. PGC introduces a novel strategy that aggregates salient features via a peak-focusing mechanism. Specifically, by employing a peak-sensitive aggregation that accentuates the most discriminative local clues, PGC leverages these critical signals to calibrate the global decision. This approach recovers subtle patterns that would otherwise be submerged in the global context. Furthermore, to better simulate real-world threats, we introduce the CommGen15 dataset, a challenging benchmark comprising samples from 15 commercial models. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PGC achieves state-of-the-art performance. Specifically, it improves mean accuracy by +12.3% on our CommGen15 dataset, and sets new records on standard benchmarks, including GenImage (+2.1%), AIGI (+3.5%), and UniversalFakeDetect (+1.7%). Code is available at https://github.com/xiaoyu6868/PGC."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://github.com/xiaoyu6868/PGC"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/977743c191fd20e29128a854452b3c94e0f61f03.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"As AI image generators improve, their outputs become increasingly difficult for both people and existing detectors to distinguish from real images. This makes detection challenging because useful clues may be subtle and localized to certain regions. Many existing detectors summarize an image globally, so these local clues can be weakened by the realistic main content of the image.\n\nWe propose Peak-Guided Calibration, a method that identifies the most informative local regions and uses them to adjust the detector’s overall decision. Rather than treating all parts of an image as equally important, the method gives more weight to regions that provide stronger evidence for whether an image is AI-generated. We also introduce CommGen15, a benchmark containing images and video frames from 15 commercial generation models, to better reflect current real-world generators.\n\nAcross several benchmarks, our method improves detection accuracy, especially for images produced by commercial models. These results suggest that focusing on localized evidence can make AI-generated image detection more reliable."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xiaoyu_Zhou6","~Jianwei_Fei1","~Peipeng_Yu1","~Jingchang_Xie1","~Chong_Cheng3","~Zhihua_Xia1"]},"authors":{"value":["Xiaoyu Zhou","Jianwei Fei","Peipeng Yu","Jingchang Xie","Chong Cheng","Zhihua Xia"]}},"tmdate":1790067340986,"pdate":1777576377021,"tcdate":1768983904940,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission11378/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission11378/Authors"],"forum":"yEjix8H6Dw","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":11378,"cdate":1768983904940,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission11378/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission11378/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067340986,"odate":1782341941821,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"yEjix8H6Dw","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IJCNN 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10190990/10190992/10191763.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IJCNN/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"tsiligkaridis|diverse_gaussian_noise_consistency_regularization_for_robustness_and_uncertainty_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Theodoros_Tsiligkaridis1","~Athanasios_Tsiligkaridis1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IJCNN54540.2023.10191763"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ijcnn/TsiligkaridisT23,\n  author={Theodoros Tsiligkaridis and Athanasios Tsiligkaridis},\n  title={Diverse Gaussian Noise Consistency Regularization for Robustness and Uncertainty Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1-8},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IJCNN54540.2023.10191763},\n  booktitle={IJCNN},\n  crossref={conf/ijcnn/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep neural networks achieve high prediction accuracy when the train and test distributions coincide. In practice though, various types of corruptions occur which deviate from this setup and cause severe performance degradations. Few methods have been proposed to address generalization in the presence of unforeseen domain shifts. In particular, digital noise corruptions arise commonly in practice during the image acquisition stage and present a significant challenge for current methods. In this paper, we propose a diverse Gaussian noise consistency regularization method for improving robustness of image classifiers under a variety of corruptions while still maintaining high clean accuracy. We derive bounds to motivate and understand the behavior of our Gaussian noise consistency regularization using a local loss landscape analysis. Our approach improves robustness against unforeseen noise corruptions by 4.2-18.4 % over adversarial training and other strong diverse data augmentation baselines across several benchmarks. Furthermore, it improves robustness and uncertainty calibration by 3.7% and 5.5 %, respectively, against all common corruptions (weather, digital, blur, noise) when combined with state-of-the-art diverse data augmentations. Code is available at https://github.com/TheoT1/DiGN."},"title":{"value":"Diverse Gaussian Noise Consistency Regularization for Robustness and Uncertainty Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Theodoros Tsiligkaridis","Athanasios Tsiligkaridis"]}},"tmdate":1774414829991,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727789930722,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Theodoros_Tsiligkaridis1"],"forum":"EQ3TZfkfLu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":132129,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1774414829991,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"EQ3TZfkfLu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EWSN 2017"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EWSN/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"fang|using_multiparameters_for_calibration_of_lowcost_sensors_in_urban_environment"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xinwei_Fang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Iain_Bate:"]},"html":{"value":"http://dl.acm.org/citation.cfm?id=3108011"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ewsn/FangB17,\n  author={Xinwei Fang and Iain Bate},\n  title={Using Multi-parameters for Calibration of Low-cost Sensors in Urban Environment},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  pages={1-11},\n  url={http://dl.acm.org/citation.cfm?id=3108011},\n  booktitle={EWSN},\n  crossref={conf/ewsn/2017}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Measurements that were taken at appropriate spatial and temporal resolution are important for understanding urban environment. However, due to cost issues, most of current monitoring sensors are sparsely deployed and are not able to provide sufficient spatial resolution. As an alternative solution, low-cost sensors that cost several orders less than the current sensors have been exploited, providing much higher spatial resolution with relatively low cost. However, the data from low-cost sensors are widely reported to be deficient, resulting in the calibration of low-cost sensors being more difficult. In this work, key challenges of calibration of low-cost sensors were identified, and the limitations of current calibration methods were discussed. A multi-parameter calibration that not only utilises cross-sensitive parameters but also considers other relevant parameters was proposed. The stepwise regression method with interaction term was then proposed to systematically select optimal parameters for the calibration. The evaluations that were carried out in both city centre and outside of city centre have shown a great advantage of using the proposed method. It shows significantly better result than the existing methods, in terms of improved root mean square errors and better linearity between the calibrated trace and the reference."},"title":{"value":"Using Multi-parameters for Calibration of Low-cost Sensors in Urban Environment"},"authors":{"value":["Xinwei Fang","Iain Bate"]}},"tmdate":1759523983567,"pdate":1483228800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/ewsn/FangB17"],"tcdate":1759523975053,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xinwei_Fang1"],"forum":"wKz15S7OWD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":634587,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1759523983567,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"wKz15S7OWD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.05688v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|ekalibr_dynamic_intrinsic_calibration_for_event_cameras_from_first_principles_of_events"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuolong_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xingxing_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liu_Yuan:","~Ziao_Liu2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.05688"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-05688,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shuolong Chen and Xingxing Li and Liu Yuan and Ziao Liu},\n  title={eKalibr: Dynamic Intrinsic Calibration for Event Cameras From First Principles of Events},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2501.05688},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.05688}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The bio-inspired event camera has garnered extensive research attention in recent years, owing to its significant potential derived from its high dynamic range and low latency characteristics. Similar to the standard camera, the event camera requires precise intrinsic calibration to facilitate further high-level visual applications, such as pose estimation and mapping. While several calibration methods for event cameras have been proposed, most of them are either (i) engineering-driven, heavily relying on conventional image-based calibration pipelines, or (ii) inconvenient, requiring complex instrumentation. To this end, we propose an accurate and convenient intrinsic calibration method for event cameras, named eKalibr, which builds upon a carefully designed event-based circle grid pattern recognition algorithm. To extract target patterns from events, we perform event-based normal flow estimation to identify potential events generated by circle edges, and cluster them spatially. Subsequently, event clusters associated with the same grid circles are matched and grouped using normal flows, for subsequent time-varying ellipse estimation. Fitted ellipse centers are time-synchronized, for final grid pattern recognition. We conducted extensive experiments to evaluate the performance of eKalibr in terms of pattern extraction and intrinsic calibration. The implementation of eKalibr is open-sourced at (https://github.com/Unsigned-Long/eKalibr) to benefit the research community."},"title":{"value":"eKalibr: Dynamic Intrinsic Calibration for Event Cameras From First Principles of Events"},"authors":{"value":["Shuolong Chen","Xingxing Li","Liu Yuan","Ziao Liu"]}},"tmdate":1744857885860,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1744857883206,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ziao_Liu2"],"forum":"P7td14rBgy","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":401137,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744857885860,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"P7td14rBgy","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Handbook of Mathematical Models in Computer Vision 2006"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/0-387-28831-7_24.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/books/daglib/P/2006"},"paperhash":{"value":"pollefeys|3d_from_image_sequences_calibration_motion_and_shape_recovery"},"authorids":{"value":["~Marc_Pollefeys1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/0-387-28831-7_24"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@incollection{DBLP:books/daglib/p/Pollefeys06,\n  author={Marc Pollefeys},\n  title={3D from Image Sequences: Calibration, Motion and Shape Recovery},\n  year={2006},\n  cdate={1136073600000},\n  pages={389-403},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/0-387-28831-7_24},\n  booktitle={Handbook of Mathematical Models in Computer Vision},\n  crossref={books/daglib/0015194}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this chapter we discuss how to recover the motion and calibration of a camera and the shape of a static object from an image sequence. The problem can be split into four subproblems: (1) computing the geometric relation between neighboring images, (2) estimating the motion and calibration of the camera, (3) computing a dense set of correspondences between neighboring images, (4) reconstruction of the 3D object shape. The approach we present here is fully automatic and can deal with photo or video sequences acquired with an uncalibrated hand-held camera. The different algorithms can also be used to provide solutions for other applications."},"title":{"value":"3D from Image Sequences: Calibration, Motion and Shape Recovery"},"authors":{"value":["Marc Pollefeys"]}},"tmdate":1762679711588,"pdate":1136073600000,"externalIds":["dblp:books/daglib/p/Pollefeys06"],"tcdate":1762679697434,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Marc_Pollefeys2"],"forum":"UNiiJnxSlS","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":681074,"cdate":1136073600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762679711588,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UNiiJnxSlS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICPR (1) 2004"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/9258/29385/01334018.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICPR/2004"},"paperhash":{"value":"cao|simple_calibration_without_metric_information_using_an_isoceles_trapezoid"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xiaochun_Cao3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hassan_Foroosh:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPR.2004.1334018"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icpr/CaoF04,\n  author={Xiaochun Cao and Hassan Foroosh},\n  title={Simple Calibration Without Metric Information Using an Isoceles Trapezoid},\n  year={2004},\n  cdate={1072915200000},\n  pages={104-107},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPR.2004.1334018},\n  booktitle={ICPR (1)},\n  crossref={conf/icpr/2004}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper addresses the problem of calibrating a pin-hole camera from images of an isoceles trapezoid. Assuming a unit aspect ratio and zero skew, we introduce a novel and simple camera calibration approach. The key features of the proposed technique are its simplicity and the lack of need for 3D coordinate information about the calibrating object - i.e. the isosceles trapezoid. By utilizing the symmetry of such trapezoid, we show that one can obtain both the internal and the external camera parameters. To demonstrate the effectiveness of the algorithm, we present the processing results on synthetic and real images, and compare our results to Zhang's flexible calibration method."},"title":{"value":"Simple Calibration Without Metric Information Using an Isoceles Trapezoid"},"authors":{"value":["Xiaochun Cao","Hassan Foroosh"]}},"tmdate":1736561860448,"pdate":1072915200000,"tcdate":1736561796884,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiaochun_Cao3"],"forum":"kIka2keUYa","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":264099,"cdate":1072915200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1736561860448,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"kIka2keUYa","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.17290v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"rabenseifner|calibration_strategies_for_robust_causal_estimation_theoretical_and_empirical_insights_on_propensity_score_based_estimators"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jan_Rabenseifner1","~Sven_Klaassen1","~Jannis_Kueck1","~Philipp_Bach1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.17290"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2503-17290,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jan Rabenseifner and Sven Klaassen and Jannis Kueck and Philipp Bach},\n  title={Calibration Strategies for Robust Causal Estimation: Theoretical and Empirical Insights on Propensity Score Based Estimators},\n  year={2025},\n  month={March},\n  cdate={1740787200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2503.17290},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.17290}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The partitioning of data for estimation and calibration critically impacts the performance of propensity score based estimators like inverse probability weighting (IPW) and double/debiased machine learning (DML) frameworks. We extend recent advances in calibration techniques for propensity score estimation, improving the robustness of propensity scores in challenging settings such as limited overlap, small sample sizes, or unbalanced data. Our contributions are twofold: First, we provide a theoretical analysis of the properties of calibrated estimators in the context of DML. To this end, we refine existing calibration frameworks for propensity score models, with a particular emphasis on the role of sample-splitting schemes in ensuring valid causal inference. Second, through extensive simulations, we show that calibration reduces variance of inverse-based propensity score estimators while also mitigating bias in IPW, even in small-sample regimes. Notably, calibration improves stability for flexible learners (e.g., gradient boosting) while preserving the doubly robust properties of DML. A key insight is that, even when methods perform well without calibration, incorporating a calibration step does not degrade performance, provided that an appropriate sample-splitting approach is chosen."},"title":{"value":"Calibration Strategies for Robust Causal Estimation: Theoretical and Empirical Insights on Propensity Score Based Estimators"},"authors":{"value":["Jan Rabenseifner","Sven Klaassen","Jannis Kueck","Philipp Bach"]}},"tmdate":1747385370856,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1747298229440,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jannis_Kueck1"],"forum":"FErG4rPE2C","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":479605,"cdate":1740787200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747385370856,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"FErG4rPE2C","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["calibration","Hierarchical classificatoin","multicalibration","utility","uncertainty quantification"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/72d16070cab70c700f705ef0238d88707c919af2.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"In multiclass probabilistic prediction, Utility Calibration (UC), which concentrates auditing on specified utilities, has recently received attention as a way to guarantee downstream decisions while controlling computational and sample burdens. At the same time, some multiclass problems have meaningful label hierarchies that play important roles in medicine and image classification, yet it remains insufficiently understood how UC evaluates utility in a hierarchy. We show that the difference between realized utility and predicted mean utility admits an exact decomposition into a sum of contributions from the internal nodes of the label tree in the hierarchical structure. This decomposition shows that positive and negative contributions from different nodes can cancel, and that even when UC is small, the utility errors remaining in parts of the hierarchy need not be small. To address this problem, we propose Hierarchical Utility Calibration (HUC), which evaluates each node contribution before summation while retaining the same target utility, subgroup, and predicted-utility interval. We further provide a finite-sample evaluation over all predicted-utility intervals and propose HUC Boost, which updates only violated internal nodes, with theoretical guarantees."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026hierarchical,\ntitle={Hierarchical Utility Calibration for Structured Multiclass Decisions},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=xoMVx0wQSt},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Hierarchical Utility Calibration for Structured Multiclass Decisions"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c747956c00d26305b9d3ad30a31c39a0344ccda4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791229612507,"tcdate":1789459489937,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission20302/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission20302/Authors"],"forum":"xoMVx0wQSt","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":20302,"cdate":1789459489937,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission20302/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791229612507,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"xoMVx0wQSt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICCMA 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8969006/8988591/08988631.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICCMA/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"astad|vive_for_robotics_rapid_robot_cell_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Morten_Andre_Astad:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mathias_Hauan_Arbo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Esten_Ingar_Grøtli:","~Jan_Tommy_Gravdahl1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCMA46720.2019.8988631"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iccma/AstadAGG19,\n  author={Morten Andre Astad and Mathias Hauan Arbo and Esten Ingar Grøtli and Jan Tommy Gravdahl},\n  title={Vive for Robotics: Rapid Robot Cell Calibration},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  pages={151-156},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCMA46720.2019.8988631},\n  booktitle={ICCMA},\n  crossref={conf/iccma/2019}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The use of an HTC Vive; a virtual reality (VR) system and its innovative tracking technology is explored in order to create an approximate one-to-one mapping to the virtual representation of a robot cell. The mapping is found by performing hand-eye calibration, establishing a spatial relationship between the inertial frames of the robot cell and the tracking system. One of the main contributions of this article is the development of an open-source Robotic Operating System (ROS) package for VR devices such as the Vive. The package includes automated calibration procedures such that the devices gives a centimetric measurement error in the robot cell. The calibrated system has problems that are related to specific issues of the tracking technology. This article outlines these issues, their cause, and potential fixes in a concise manner. A simple assembly scenario is presented, where the outline of objects in the robot cell are defined by registering points with a Vive tracker. The potential use cases of the calibrated system are limited by its accuracy, and depends on the required tolerances."},"title":{"value":"Vive for Robotics: Rapid Robot Cell Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Morten Andre Astad","Mathias Hauan Arbo","Esten Ingar Grøtli","Jan Tommy Gravdahl"]}},"tmdate":1747082624309,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1747081828541,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jan_Tommy_Gravdahl1"],"forum":"4R0DrXMUIz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":423176,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747082624309,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4R0DrXMUIz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICME 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9428049/9428068/09428389.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICMCS/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|automatic_checkerboard_detection_for_robust_camera_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ben_Chen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuyao_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Caihua_Xiong:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICME51207.2021.9428389"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icmcs/ChenLX21,\n  author={Ben Chen and Yuyao Liu and Caihua Xiong},\n  title={Automatic Checkerboard Detection for Robust Camera Calibration},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={1-6},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICME51207.2021.9428389},\n  booktitle={ICME},\n  crossref={conf/icmcs/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate checkerboard recognition is vital for basic camera calibration in many machine vision tasks. However, existing detectors usually encounter two problems. First, X-corner location precision is easily affected by undesired factors such as noise and distortion. Then, the pattern recovery requires manual input of the dimensions of the real corner matrix, and most recovery schemes can’t deal well with the pattern with occlusion or missing corners. In this paper we propose a novel CNN-based checkerboard detection framework to address these problems. This framework consists of three sub-modules: 1) an X-corner detection network to identify as many real corners as possible. 2) a sub-pixel refinement technique obtained from the geometric analysis of the image response map and grayscale to find precise corner locations. 3) a pattern recovery scheme to find the regular checkerboard layout even with high distortion and partial occlusion. Quantitative experimental results show that the proposed approach has higher accuracy and stronger robustness than state-of-the-art methods to both synthetic images and real-world camera calibration scenarios."},"title":{"value":"Automatic Checkerboard Detection for Robust Camera Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Ben Chen","Yuyao Liu","Caihua Xiong"]}},"tmdate":1739780224795,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1739780215812,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ben_Chen1"],"forum":"xl030CZVNF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":324011,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1739780224795,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xl030CZVNF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/21/4/1091/pdf?version=1613972527"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"crombrugge|extrinsic_camera_calibration_with_linelaser_projection"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Izaak_Van_Crombrugge:","~Rudi_Penne1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Steve_Vanlanduit:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s21041091"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/CrombruggePV21,\n  author={Izaak Van Crombrugge and Rudi Penne and Steve Vanlanduit},\n  title={Extrinsic Camera Calibration with Line-Laser Projection},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={21},\n  number={4},\n  pages={1091},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s21041091}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Knowledge of precise camera poses is vital for multi-camera setups. Camera intrinsics can be obtained for each camera separately in lab conditions. For fixed multi-camera setups, the extrinsic calibration can only be done in situ. Usually, some markers are used, like checkerboards, requiring some level of overlap between cameras. In this work, we propose a method for cases with little or no overlap. Laser lines are projected on a plane (e.g., floor or wall) using a laser line projector. The pose of the plane and cameras is then optimized using bundle adjustment to match the lines seen by the cameras. To find the extrinsic calibration, only a partial overlap between the laser lines and the field of view of the cameras is needed. Real-world experiments were conducted both with and without overlapping fields of view, resulting in rotation errors below 0.5°. We show that the accuracy is comparable to other state-of-the-art methods while offering a more practical procedure. The method can also be used in large-scale applications and can be fully automated."},"title":{"value":"Extrinsic Camera Calibration with Line-Laser Projection"},"authors":{"value":["Izaak Van Crombrugge","Rudi Penne","Steve Vanlanduit"]}},"tmdate":1731503524208,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1731503520613,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rudi_Penne1"],"forum":"nwIf2Bx7rF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":227345,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731503524208,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"nwIf2Bx7rF","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"calibration/uncertainty,"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2024 June Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2024factlevel,\ntitle={Fact-Level Confidence Calibration: Empowering Confidence-Guided {LLM} Self-Correction},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - June 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=bCAxEwwmBr},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Fact-Level Confidence Calibration: Empowering Confidence-Guided LLM Self-Correction"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability"]},"abstract":{"value":"Confidence calibration in LLMs, i.e., aligning their self-assessed confidence with the actual accuracy of their responses, enabling them to self-evaluate the correctness of their outputs. However, current calibration methods for LLMs typically estimate two scalars to represent overall response confidence and correctness, which is inadequate for long-form generation where the response includes multiple atomic facts and may be partially confident and correct. These methods also overlook the relevance of each fact to the query. To address these challenges, we propose a Fact-Level Calibration framework that operates at a finer granularity, calibrating confidence to relevance-weighted correctness at the fact level. Furthermore, comprehensive analysis under the framework inspired the development of Confidence-guided Fact-level self-correction (ConFact), which uses high-confidence facts within a response as additional knowledge to improve low-confidence ones. Extensive experiments across four datasets and six models demonstrate that ConFact effectively mitigates hallucinations without requiring external knowledge sources such as retrieval systems."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/0c80381df1260c821bd261aad72edc3f6282d901.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Interpretability and Analysis of Models for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794065368330,"tcdate":1718530380205,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission4668/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission4668/Authors"],"forum":"bCAxEwwmBr","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4668,"cdate":1718530380205,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794065368330,"odate":1719930600391,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June","id":"bCAxEwwmBr","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"Excluding normal nodes near known anomalies from conformal calibration shifts node degree and can break FDR control for degree-sensitive graph anomaly detectors."},"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2026 TAE Poster"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/439db34a2482323eefafb0ecd71313dbf24feb34.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["conformal prediction","false discovery rate","graph anomaly detection","calibration","covariate shift","exchangeability"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE"},"paperhash":{"value":"khan|calibration_selection_breaks_conformal_fdr_control_in_graph_anomaly_detection"},"abstract":{"value":"We study a failure mode of conformal false discovery rate (FDR) control for graph anomaly detection in which the guarantee is violated without any visible sign in the procedure's output. The standard precaution against calibration contamination, restricting calibration to normal nodes with no anomalous neighbors, is itself a covariate filter. On a large fraud-detection graph, this filter shifts calibration toward a far lower degree than the population under test. When a detector's score depends on degree, the guarantee breaks: realized false discovery rate reaches 0.936 against a nominal target of 0.10, and the resulting discovery set is indistinguishable in its output from a valid one of the same size. Holding the graph fixed and varying only the detector shows that the failure tracks a property of the detector, not the graph, replicating on a second graph and disappearing on a third where the underlying covariate relationship does not hold. These results indicate that a defense against one failure mode does not provide evidence against another."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nkhan2026calibration,\ntitle={Calibration Selection Breaks Conformal {FDR} Control in Graph Anomaly Detection},\nauthor={Suhaan Khan and Suhaan Gopal},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2026 Trust-AI-Eval Workshop},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=I614HtAuvJ}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration Selection Breaks Conformal FDR Control in Graph Anomaly Detection"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Suhaan_Khan1","fullname":"Suhaan Khan","institutions":[{"name":"University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign","domain":"illinois.edu","country":"US"}]},{"username":"~Suhaan_Gopal1","fullname":"Suhaan Gopal","institutions":[{"name":"McMurry University","domain":"mcm.edu","country":"US"}]}]}},"tmdate":1793418259094,"pdate":1790555917247,"tcdate":1788067802996,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/Submission343/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/Submission343/Authors"],"forum":"I614HtAuvJ","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":343,"cdate":1788067802996,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/-/Accepted_Submission_Release","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/Submission343/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE/-/Edit"],"mdate":1793418259094,"odate":1790555917247,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/TAE","id":"I614HtAuvJ","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"QCalEval evaluates vision-language models on quantum calibration plots and releases a trained open-weight reference model for this task."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Desk Rejected Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["vision-language models","scientific plot understanding","quantum calibration","multimodal in-context learning"]},"primary_area":{"value":"datasets and benchmarks"},"abstract":{"value":"Quantum computing calibration depends on interpreting experimental data, and calibration plots provide the most universal human-readable representation for this task, yet no systematic evaluation exists of how well vision-language models (VLMs) interpret them. We introduce QCalEval, the first VLM benchmark for quantum calibration plots: 243 samples across 87 scenario types from 22 experiment families, spanning superconducting qubits and neutral atoms, evaluated on six question types in both zero-shot and in-context learning settings. The best general-purpose zero-shot model reaches a mean score of 72.3, and many open weight models degrade under multi-image in-context learning, whereas frontier closed models improve substantially. A supervised fine-tuning ablation at the 9-billion-parameter scale shows that SFT improves zero-shot performance but cannot close the multimodal in-context learning gap. As a reference case study, we release our model, an open-weight model based on Qwen3.5-35B-A3B that reaches 74.7 zero-shot average score."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nanonymous2026qcaleval,\ntitle={{QC}alEval: Benchmarking Vision-Language Models for Quantum Calibration Plot Understanding},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=rEMgfF1A2Q}\n}"},"title":{"value":"QCalEval: Benchmarking Vision-Language Models for Quantum Calibration Plot Understanding"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/28b4538e29f7c73200d305d781bb50bb695cea08.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Desk_Rejected_Submission"}},"tmdate":1791218484227,"tcdate":1788738417114,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission10219/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission10219/Authors"],"forum":"rEMgfF1A2Q","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":10219,"cdate":1788738417114,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Desk_Rejected_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791218484227,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"rEMgfF1A2Q","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["diffusion models","generative models","inference acceleration","feature caching"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/6807ae284f651538ce207157c4ef19e12bce8b36.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhuang2026learnibridge,\ntitle={LearniBridge: Learnable Calibration of Feature Caching for Diffusion Models Acceleration},\nauthor={Xuyue Huang and Zhe Chen and Wang Shen and Xiao-Ping Zhang},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=8sD74Krbw7}\n}"},"title":{"value":"LearniBridge: Learnable Calibration of Feature Caching for Diffusion Models Acceleration"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|learnibridge_learnable_calibration_of_feature_caching_for_diffusion_models_acceleration"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/dceb0c48d3eeaaa2fdcc989f0903eb4b989e229a.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"LearniBridge introduces a lightweight LoRA-based learnable calibration of feature caching for accelerating diffusion models, enabling high acceleration ratios while preserving high generation quality."},"primary_area":{"value":"applications->computer_vision"},"abstract":{"value":"Diffusion Transformers (DiTs) have driven substantial progress in image and video generation but suffer from prohibitive computational costs.\nFeature caching accelerates inference by reusing intermediate representations. \nExisting methods rely on historical features for implementation simplicity, \nyet suffer from severe error accumulation at high acceleration ratios.\nTo address this limitation, we investigate the nature of the requisite feature correction.\nWe demonstrate that the optimal calibration update is characterized by a shared low-rank subspace across diverse prompts.\nGuided by this structural insight, we propose LearniBridge, a learnable calibration mechanism for feature caching that bridges multiple timesteps through lightweight LoRA updates.\nThis mechanism enables effective calibration requiring only $3-5$ training samples.\nExtensive experiments on image and video generation show that LearniBridge achieves up to $5.87\\times$, $5.75\\times$, and $4.10\\times$ acceleration\non FLUX, HunyuanVideo, and WAN 2.1, respectively. On WAN 2.1, it improves VBench by 1.28\\% over the previous SOTA at $4.10\\times$ acceleration."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://github.com/Iiiiiiirene/LearniBridge"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/9105db3b4513fbce17141e5d523450f83144911f.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Diffusion-based AI models have become very powerful tools for generating high-quality images and videos, but they usually require a large amount of computation and are therefore slow and expensive to run. A common way to speed them up is to reuse information computed in earlier steps, rather than recalculating everything from scratch. However, when this reuse is too aggressive, small errors can build up and noticeably reduce the quality of the generated results.\nIn this paper, we study how to correct these accumulated errors efficiently. We find that the needed corrections share a simple underlying structure across many different text prompts. Based on this observation, we propose LearniBridge, a lightweight method that learns how to adjust reused features so that the generation process remains accurate while becoming much faster. Importantly, LearniBridge only needs a few training examples to work effectively.\nExperiments on both image and video generation models show that LearniBridge can greatly reduce the computation needed for generation while maintaining strong visual quality. It achieves significant speedups on several widely used models and improves over previous methods on video generation benchmarks. This makes high-quality image and video generation more efficient and practical."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xuyue_Huang1","~Zhe_Chen23","~Wang_Shen3","~Xiao-Ping_Zhang1"]},"authors":{"value":["Xuyue Huang","Zhe Chen","Wang Shen","Xiao-Ping Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1790067304936,"pdate":1777576246391,"tcdate":1768556887496,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5888/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5888/Authors"],"forum":"8sD74Krbw7","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":5888,"cdate":1768556887496,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5888/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission5888/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067304936,"odate":1782341919267,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"8sD74Krbw7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2207.11709v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"theiner|keypointless_camera_calibration_for_sports_field_registration_in_soccer"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jonas_Theiner:","~Ralph_Ewerth2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2207.11709"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2207-11709,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jonas Theiner and Ralph Ewerth},\n  title={Keypoint-less Camera Calibration for Sports Field Registration in Soccer},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2207.11709},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2207.11709}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Sports field registration in broadcast videos is typically interpreted as the task of homography estimation, which provides a mapping between a planar field and the corresponding visible area of the image. In contrast to previous approaches, we consider the task as a camera calibration problem. First, we introduce a differentiable objective function that is able to learn the camera pose and focal length from segment correspondences (e.g., lines, point clouds), based on pixel-level annotations for segments of a known calibration object. The calibration module iteratively minimizes the segment reprojection error induced by the estimated camera parameters. Second, we propose a novel approach for 3D sports field registration from broadcast soccer images. Compared to the typical solution, which subsequently refines an initial estimation, our solution does it in one step. The proposed method is evaluated for sports field registration on two datasets and achieves superior results compared to two state-of-the-art approaches."},"title":{"value":"Keypoint-less Camera Calibration for Sports Field Registration in Soccer"},"authors":{"value":["Jonas Theiner","Ralph Ewerth"]}},"tmdate":1769251306713,"pdate":1672444800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2207-11709"],"tcdate":1769251273151,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ralph_Ewerth2"],"forum":"3TLtxVqjSu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":793968,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769251306713,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"3TLtxVqjSu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISVC (2) 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-030-64559-5_15.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISVC/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"palmer|simple_camerato2dlidar_calibration_method_for_general_use"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrew_H._Palmer:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chris_Peterson_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Janelle_Blankenburg:","~David_Feil-Seifer1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Monica_N._Nicolescu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-64559-5_15"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/isvc/PalmerPBFN20,\n  author={Andrew H. Palmer and Chris Peterson and Janelle Blankenburg and David Feil-Seifer and Monica N. Nicolescu},\n  title={Simple Camera-to-2D-LiDAR Calibration Method for General Use},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={193-206},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-64559-5_15},\n  booktitle={ISVC (2)},\n  crossref={conf/isvc/2020-2}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As systems that utilize computer vision move into the public domain, methods of calibration need to become easier to use. Though multi-plane LiDAR systems have proven to be useful for vehicles and large robotic platforms, many smaller platforms and low-cost solutions still require 2D LiDAR combined with RGB cameras. Current methods of calibrating these sensors make assumptions about camera and laser placement and/or require complex calibration routines. In this paper we propose a new method of feature correspondence in the two sensors and an optimization method capable of using a calibration target with unknown lengths in its geometry. Our system is designed with an inexperienced layperson as the intended user, which has led us to remove as many assumptions about both the target and laser as possible. We show that our system is capable of calibrating the 2-sensor system from a single sample in configurations other methods are unable to handle."},"title":{"value":"Simple Camera-to-2D-LiDAR Calibration Method for General Use"},"authors":{"value":["Andrew H. Palmer","Chris Peterson","Janelle Blankenburg","David Feil-Seifer","Monica N. Nicolescu"]}},"tmdate":1749850484498,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1749850446593,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~David_Feil-Seifer1"],"forum":"WaKzJtLBgb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":562387,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1749850484498,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"WaKzJtLBgb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"BMEI 2009"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5301643/5301644/05305685.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/BMEI/2009"},"paperhash":{"value":"lv|visual_model_based_carm_system_calibration_and_image_correction"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shaojie_Lv:","~Cai_Meng1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fugen_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bo_Liu_0027:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaojun_Zhou:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/BMEI.2009.5305685"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/bmei/LvMZLZ09,\n  author={Shaojie Lv and Cai Meng and Fugen Zhou and Bo Liu and Xiaojun Zhou},\n  title={Visual Model Based C-Arm System Calibration and Image Correction},\n  year={2009},\n  cdate={1230768000000},\n  pages={1-5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/BMEI.2009.5305685},\n  booktitle={BMEI},\n  crossref={conf/bmei/2009}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In traditional methods, the C-Arm system calibration was performed based on the result of C-Arm image correction. When correcting images, high-order polynomial was used to fit relating distorted pixels to ideal image pixel. However accurate ideal image could not be established easily, that heavily restricted the precision of correction. In this paper, a novel method is presented based on visual model which does not depend on ideal image and integrates correction and calibration as one process. In this method, the Tsai's calibration method is used to calculate the internal and external parameters of C-Arm system with markers in the center region of the image. Then, nonlinear optimization is applied to get more precise C-Arm imaging model with all markers. Finally, the distortion image is corrected using the distortion parameters of internal parameters. To verify this method, known distances between points are reconstructed and the experimental results show that the maximum error is less than 1 mm."},"title":{"value":"Visual Model Based C-Arm System Calibration and Image Correction"},"authors":{"value":["Shaojie Lv","Cai Meng","Fugen Zhou","Bo Liu","Xiaojun Zhou"]}},"tmdate":1747299071656,"pdate":1230768000000,"tcdate":1747299037162,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Cai_Meng1"],"forum":"4RuPxfOePt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":480221,"cdate":1230768000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747299071656,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4RuPxfOePt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Ad Hoc Networks 2019"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ADHOC/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"simonjan|decentralized_and_resourceefficient_selfcalibration_of_visual_sensor_networks"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jennifer_Simonjan:","~Bernhard_Rinner2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adhoc.2019.01.007"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/adhoc/SimonjanR19,\n  author={Jennifer Simonjan and Bernhard Rinner},\n  title={Decentralized and resource-efficient self-calibration of visual sensor networks},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={Ad Hoc Networks},\n  volume={88},\n  pages={112-128},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.adhoc.2019.01.007}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Many multi-camera applications rely on the knowledge of the spatial relationship among the individual nodes. However, establishing such a network-wide calibration is typically a time-consuming task and requires user interaction. In this paper we present a decentralized and resource-aware algorithm for estimating the poses of all network nodes without any user interaction. This self-calibration of the network is achieved in two steps: First, overlapping camera pairs estimate relative positions and orientations by exchanging locally measured distances and angles to detected objects. Second, calibration information of overlapping cameras is spread throughout the network such that poses of non-overlapping cameras can also be estimated. Our approach does not rely on a priori topological information and delivers the extrinsic camera parameters with respect to a common coordinate system. In a simulation study we analyze the performance of our approach concerning the achieved spatial accuracy and computational effort considering noisy measurements and different communication schemes."},"title":{"value":"Decentralized and resource-efficient self-calibration of visual sensor networks"},"authors":{"value":["Jennifer Simonjan","Bernhard Rinner"]}},"tmdate":1731564473084,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1731564459148,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bernhard_Rinner2"],"forum":"UCZwPRr1Q0","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":238442,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731564473084,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UCZwPRr1Q0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MMSP 2010"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/5656074/5661979/05661986.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MMSP/2010"},"paperhash":{"value":"valente|geometric_calibration_of_distributed_microphone_arrays_from_acoustic_source_correspondences"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:S._Daniele_Valente:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marco_Tagliasacchi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fabio_Antonacci:","~Paolo_Bestagini1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Augusto_Sarti:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Stefano_Tubaro:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/MMSP.2010.5661986"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/mmsp/ValenteTABST10,\n  author={S. Daniele Valente and Marco Tagliasacchi and Fabio Antonacci and Paolo Bestagini and Augusto Sarti and Stefano Tubaro},\n  title={Geometric calibration of distributed microphone arrays from acoustic source correspondences},\n  year={2010},\n  cdate={1262304000000},\n  pages={13-18},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/MMSP.2010.5661986},\n  booktitle={MMSP},\n  crossref={conf/mmsp/2010}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes a method that solves the problem of geometric calibration of microphone arrays. We consider a distributed system, in which each array is controlled by separate acquisition devices that do not share a common synchronization clock. Given a set of probing sources, e.g. loudspeakers, each array computes an estimate of the source locations using a conventional TDOA-based algorithm. These observations are fused together by the proposed method, in order to estimate the position and pose of one array with respect to the other. Unlike previous approaches, we explicitly consider the anisotropic distribution of localization errors. As such, the proposed method is able to address the problem of geometric calibration when the probing sources are located both in the near- and far-field of the microphone arrays. Experimental results demonstrate that the improvement in terms of calibration accuracy with respect to state-of-the-art algorithms can be substantial, especially in the far-field."},"title":{"value":"Geometric calibration of distributed microphone arrays from acoustic source correspondences"},"authors":{"value":["S. Daniele Valente","Marco Tagliasacchi","Fabio Antonacci","Paolo Bestagini","Augusto Sarti","Stefano Tubaro"]}},"tmdate":1731495891115,"pdate":1262304000000,"tcdate":1731495529804,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Paolo_Bestagini1"],"forum":"noaJRTOt6K","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":224326,"cdate":1262304000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731495891115,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"noaJRTOt6K","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICASSP 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10094559/10094560/10096594.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICASSP/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"mi|dual_meta_calibration_mix_for_improving_generalization_in_metalearning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ze-Yu_Mi:","~Yu-Bin_Yang3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP49357.2023.10096594"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icassp/MiY23,\n  author={Ze-Yu Mi and Yu-Bin Yang},\n  title={Dual Meta Calibration Mix for Improving Generalization in Meta-Learning},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1-5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP49357.2023.10096594},\n  booktitle={ICASSP},\n  crossref={conf/icassp/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Meta-learning has achieved remarkable success as a powerful paradigm for transferring learned knowledge from previous tasks. However, the lack of a large number of diverse and qualified tasks is the bottleneck of current meta-learning, which can easily lead to overfitting and therefore seriously hurt the generalization ability. In this paper, to address this challenge, we proposed Dual Meta Calibration Mix (DMCM) to improve the diversity and quality of tasks with \"more data\". Concretely, we designed a dual augmentation framework and meta calibration mix. The dual augmentation framework augments individual tasks and pairs of tasks by linearly combining samples and labels from both support and query sets, respectively. The meta calibration mix generates new samples by linearly combining image patches and corresponding labels based on the calibrated mixing matrix and calibrated label. Extensive experiments show that our proposed method significantly improves the generalization of meta-learning algorithms and consistently outperforms other state-of-the-art regularization meta-learning methods."},"title":{"value":"Dual Meta Calibration Mix for Improving Generalization in Meta-Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Ze-Yu Mi","Yu-Bin Yang"]}},"tmdate":1731297651405,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731297643445,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yu-Bin_Yang3"],"forum":"ho4mdPqf2O","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":179055,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731297651405,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ho4mdPqf2O","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/20/10/2898/pdf?version=1590397917"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"garcíagómez|geometric_model_and_calibration_method_for_a_solidstate_lidar"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pablo_García-Gómez:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Santiago_Royo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Noel_Rodrigo:","~Josep_R._Casas1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s20102898"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/Garcia-GomezRRC20,\n  author={Pablo García-Gómez and Santiago Royo and Noel Rodrigo and Josep R. Casas},\n  title={Geometric Model and Calibration Method for a Solid-State LiDAR},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={20},\n  number={10},\n  pages={2898},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s20102898}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents a novel calibration method for solid-state LiDAR devices based on a geometrical description of their scanning system, which has variable angular resolution. Determining this distortion across the entire Field-of-View of the system yields accurate and precise measurements which enable it to be combined with other sensors. On the one hand, the geometrical model is formulated using the well-known Snell’s law and the intrinsic optical assembly of the system, whereas on the other hand the proposed method describes the scanned scenario with an intuitive camera-like approach relating pixel locations with scanning directions. Simulations and experimental results show that the model fits with real devices and the calibration procedure accurately maps their variant resolution so undistorted representations of the observed scenario can be provided. Thus, the calibration method proposed during this work is applicable and valid for existing scanning systems improving their precision and accuracy in an order of magnitude."},"title":{"value":"Geometric Model and Calibration Method for a Solid-State LiDAR"},"authors":{"value":["Pablo García-Gómez","Santiago Royo","Noel Rodrigo","Josep R. Casas"]}},"tmdate":1730371581801,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1730371579330,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Josep_R._Casas1"],"forum":"JP9YSy9mNV","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":165554,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730371581801,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"JP9YSy9mNV","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"NLP Applications,Language Modeling"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English,programming"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2025 May Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025code,\ntitle={Code to Think, Think to Code: A Survey on Code-Enhanced Reasoning and Reasoning-Driven Code Intelligence in {LLM}s},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=SD51Pw1NCW},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Code to Think, Think to Code: A Survey on Code-Enhanced Reasoning and Reasoning-Driven Code Intelligence in LLMs"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Surveys"]},"abstract":{"value":"Code and reasoning recently exhibit a mutually reinforcing relationship in large language models (LLMs): Code is abstract, modular, highly structured and has strong logic, guiding reasoning in training and inference. While reasoning translates high-level goals into small executable steps, enable more sophisticated code intellignece, solving real-world challenging software development problems. In this study, we examine how code serves as a structured medium for enhancing reasoning - providing verifiable execution paths, enforcing logical decomposition, and enabling runtime validation, and how advances in reasoning have transformed code intelligence from basic completion to sophisticated agent - enabling models to tackle complex software engineering tasks through deliberate planning and systematic debugging. Finally, we identify key challenges and propose future research directions may deepen the synergy, ultimately advancing LLM performance in both complex reasoning and code intelligence."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ddbc6cacf62a5a155d8b26ab9ec3bc837d810bed.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"NLP Applications"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782962036111,"tcdate":1747347974689,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission882/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission882/Authors"],"forum":"SD51Pw1NCW","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":882,"cdate":1747347974689,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782962036111,"odate":1753766020949,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","id":"SD51Pw1NCW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.130.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|code_to_think_think_to_code_a_survey_on_codeenhanced_reasoning_and_reasoningdriven_code_intelligence_in_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","~Antoine_Simoulin1","","","","","","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.130"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/YangLZSLCTQYLM25,\n  author={Dayu Yang and Tianyang Liu and Daoan Zhang and Antoine Simoulin and Xiaoyi Liu and Yuwei Cao and Zhaopu Teng and Xin Qian and Grey Yang and Jiebo Luo and Julian J. McAuley},\n  title={Code to Think, Think to Code: A Survey on Code-Enhanced Reasoning and Reasoning-Driven Code Intelligence in LLMs},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={2586-2616},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.130},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code and reasoning recently exhibit a mutually reinforcing relationship in large language models (LLMs): Code is abstract, modular, highly structured and has strong logic, guiding reasoning in training and inference. While reasoning translates high-level goals into small executable steps, enable more sophisticated code intellignece, solving real-world challenging software development problems. In this study, we examine how code serves as a structured medium for enhancing reasoning - providing verifiable execution paths, enforcing logical decomposition, and enabling runtime validation, and how advances in reasoning have transformed code intelligence from basic completion to sophisticated agent - enabling models to tackle complex software engineering tasks through deliberate planning and systematic debugging. Finally, we identify key challenges and propose future research directions may deepen the synergy, ultimately advancing LLM performance in both complex reasoning and code intelligence."},"title":{"value":"Code to Think, Think to Code: A Survey on Code-Enhanced Reasoning and Reasoning-Driven Code Intelligence in LLMs"},"authors":{"value":["Dayu Yang","Tianyang Liu","Daoan Zhang","Antoine Simoulin","Xiaoyi Liu","Yuwei Cao","Zhaopu Teng","Xin Qian","Grey Yang","Jiebo Luo","Julian J. McAuley"]}},"tmdate":1772232531013,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/YangLZSLCTQYLM25"],"tcdate":1772232527333,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Antoine_Simoulin1"],"forum":"cHOrituSgv","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":836214,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772232531013,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"cHOrituSgv","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.24008v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"shihab|coveragebased_calibration_for_posttraining_quantization_via_weighted_set_cover_over_outlier_channels"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.24008"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2604-24008,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ibne Farabi Shihab and Sanjeda Akter and Anuj Sharma},\n  title={Coverage-Based Calibration for Post-Training Quantization via Weighted Set Cover over Outlier Channels},\n  year={2026},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1775001600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2604.24008},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.24008}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Post-Training Quantization (PTQ) compresses large language models to low bit-widths using a small calibration set, and its quality depends strongly on which samples are chosen. We identify a failure mode in which calibration samples fail to activate outlier channels, hidden dimensions with unusually large activations, causing the quantizer to underestimate their dynamic range and producing per-channel reconstruction errors that dominate layer-wise loss. Motivated by this observation, we argue that PTQ calibration quality is governed more by weighted outlier-channel coverage than by generic sample representativeness, and formulate calibration selection as a weighted set cover problem over outlier channels. The objective is monotone submodular, and the greedy algorithm, COVERCAL, operates on pre-computed activation statistics and requires no GPU time at selection. We further show that the weight choice is internally consistent: under a stylized clipping model, missed weighted coverage upper-bounds surrogate loss, justifying the weighted coverage objective as principled rather than purely empirical. Across LLaMA-2, LLaMA-3, and Mistral, under AWQ and GPTQ backends and five downstream evaluations, COVERCAL improves over random, max-perplexity, max-activation-variance, and stratified baselines, with the largest gains at small calibration budgets. At INT4 with 128 samples, COVERCAL improves MMLU by 1.2 to 1.5 points over random calibration and reduces perplexity degradation by 15 to 30\\%; with 64 samples, it matches or exceeds random calibration at 256. The contribution is not a new PTQ backend but a formulation of calibration selection as weighted outlier coverage, with a simple, efficient algorithm and a surrogate-based justification."},"title":{"value":"Coverage-Based Calibration for Post-Training Quantization via Weighted Set Cover over Outlier Channels"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Ibne Farabi Shihab","username":""},{"fullname":"Sanjeda Akter","username":""},{"fullname":"Anuj Sharma","username":"~Anuj_Sharma1"}]}},"tmdate":1791746083989,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2604-24008"],"tcdate":1791746066000,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Anuj_Sharma1"],"forum":"s2E72clv7W","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":171167,"cdate":1775001600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791746083989,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"s2E72clv7W","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE J. Sel. Top. Appl. Earth Obs. Remote. Sens. 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/4609443/9314330/09364670.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/STAEORS/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"hasheminasab|multiscale_image_matching_for_automated_calibration_of_uavbased_frame_and_line_camera_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Seyyed_Meghdad_Hasheminasab:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tian_Zhou_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lisa_LaForest:","~Ayman_Habib1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/JSTARS.2021.3062573"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/staeors/HasheminasabZLH21,\n  author={Seyyed Meghdad Hasheminasab and Tian Zhou and Lisa LaForest and Ayman Habib},\n  title={Multiscale Image Matching for Automated Calibration of UAV-Based Frame and Line Camera Systems},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={IEEE J. Sel. Top. Appl. Earth Obs. Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={14},\n  pages={3133-3150},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/JSTARS.2021.3062573}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) equipped with integrated global navigation satellite systems/inertial navigation systems together with frame and/or line cameras are used for a variety of applications. Geometric system calibration is crucial for delivering accurate products from UAV-based imaging systems. This article presents automated geometric calibration strategies for UAV-based frame and line camera systems to estimate accurate system calibration parameters without the need for ground control points or manual measurements of tie points. The matching strategy used in this article to establish conjugate features among overlapping frame camera images is based on a traditional Structure from Motion technique augmented with several layers of matching outlier removal. On the other hand, a new strategy relying on ortho-rectified images is introduced for automated feature matching in line camera scenes. Then, a general bundle adjustment procedure with system calibration capabilities for frame and line cameras is presented, where the derived automated tie points are used for estimating accurate geometric system calibration parameters. The proposed approach is evaluated using four datasets-two datasets captured by frame cameras and two datasets captured by line cameras. The results show that the developed automated calibration strategy is capable of producing the same level of absolute accuracy when compared to using manually measured tie points for both frame camera and line camera systems. Results also indicate that the presented automated system calibration approach can be applied to systems even with significant deviation of actual system parameters from their nominal values, and still produce accurate estimates of calibration parameters."},"title":{"value":"Multiscale Image Matching for Automated Calibration of UAV-Based Frame and Line Camera Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Seyyed Meghdad Hasheminasab","Tian Zhou","Lisa LaForest","Ayman Habib"]}},"tmdate":1731600254414,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1731600243943,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ayman_Habib1"],"forum":"57vi7D1yP2","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":244061,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731600254414,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"57vi7D1yP2","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Neural Networks 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/NN/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|saccade_and_purify_task_adapted_multiview_feature_calibration_network_for_few_shot_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jing_Zhang_0041:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yunzuo_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinzhou_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mingzhe_Chen:","~Zhe_Wang15"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neunet.2025.107482"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/nn/ZhangHZCW25,\n  author={Jing Zhang and Yunzuo Hu and Xinzhou Zhang and Mingzhe Chen and Zhe Wang},\n  title={Saccade and purify: Task adapted multi-view feature calibration network for few shot learning},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={Neural Networks},\n  volume={188},\n  pages={107482},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neunet.2025.107482}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Current few-shot image classification methods encounter challenges in extracting multi-view features that can complement each other and selecting optimal features for classification in a specific task. To address this problem, we propose a novel Task-adapted Multi-view feature Calibration Network (TMCN) inspired by the different saccade patterns observed in the human visual system. The TMCN is designed to “saccade” for extracting complementary multi-view features and “purify” multi-view features in a task-adapted manner. To capture more representative features, we propose a multi-view feature extraction method that simulates the voluntary saccades and scanning saccades in the human visual system, which generates global, local grid, and randomly sampled multi-view features. To purify and obtain the most appropriate features, we employ a global local feature calibration module to calibrate global and local grid features for achieving more stable non-local image features. Furthermore, a sampling feature fusion method is proposed to fuse the randomly sampled features from classes to obtain better prototypes, and a multi-view feature calibrating module is proposed to adaptively fuse purified multi-view features based on the task information obtained from the task feature extracting module. Extensive experiments conducted on three widely used public datasets prove that our proposed TMCN can achieve excellent performance and surpass state-of-the-art methods. The code is available at the following address: https://github.com/huyunzuo/TMCN."},"title":{"value":"Saccade and purify: Task adapted multi-view feature calibration network for few shot learning"},"authors":{"value":["Jing Zhang","Yunzuo Hu","Xinzhou Zhang","Mingzhe Chen","Zhe Wang"]}},"tmdate":1753015539319,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1753015532661,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhe_Wang15"],"forum":"Xqjny88BCD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":577151,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753015539319,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Xqjny88BCD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Desk Rejected Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Confidence Calibration; LLMs; Temperature Scaling"]},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"The importance of reliable uncertainty estimation in large language models (LLMs) has become increasingly pronounced, yet modern models remain poorly calibrated and frequently overconfident. In this study, we address this by proposing an efficient method for LLM classifier calibration using the Asymmetric Duos framework. Our approach combines a strong base model with a smaller sidekick model via logit-space fusion, utilizing global scaling parameters optimized on a validation set. Across diverse benchmarks, this Asymmetric LLM Duo consistently achieves best or second-best performance on expected calibration error and negative log-likelihood, without degrading predictive accuracy. More importantly, these gains are realized at a fraction of the computational cost of more complex baselines, placing our method on the calibration-compute Pareto frontier. We further demonstrate that our method acts orthogonal to various established calibration-aware techniques, meaning it can be easily combined with them to consistently boost their performance. Finally, our analysis of the logit-space behavior illustrates how asymmetric fusion successfully mitigates base model overconfidence. Overall, our findings demonstrate that the benefits of an asymmetric fusion successfully transfer to the language domain, providing a simple, highly cost-efficient approach to improving uncertainty estimation in LLM classifiers."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nanonymous2026advancing,\ntitle={Advancing the Calibration-Compute Frontier of {LLM} Classifiers via Asymmetric Duos},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=r1zsoUu97b}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Advancing the Calibration-Compute Frontier of LLM Classifiers via Asymmetric Duos"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/608d067ae2af9716aac28deaff1999c35d6b58f2.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Desk_Rejected_Submission"}},"tmdate":1792347511467,"tcdate":1787172511622,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission192/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission192/Authors"],"forum":"r1zsoUu97b","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":192,"cdate":1787172511622,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission192/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Desk_Rejected_Submission"],"mdate":1792347511467,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"r1zsoUu97b","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"TLDR":{"value":"With frozen foundation models, cross-entropy readouts often approach noise-aware methods in accuracy under moderate label noise yet remain miscalibrated; we analyze this gap and evaluate post-hoc calibration without clean validation labels."},"keywords":{"value":["Learning with Noisy Labels","Frozen Foundation Models","Probability Calibration","Linear Probing","Temperature Scaling"]},"primary_area":{"value":"unsupervised, self-supervised, semi-supervised, and supervised representation learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Foundation-model encoders change the starting point for learning from noisy downstream labels: a visual representation is available before the corrupted supervision is observed. We ask how much accuracy headroom remains for noise-specific training when the encoder is immutable, and whether accurate readouts also yield reliable probabilities. Linear probes serve as matched diagnostics over the same frozen representation, not as substitutes for representation learning. Across the evaluated benchmarks, cross-entropy probing often approaches a matched clean-label readout and specialized noisy-label learning rules under moderate noise, while severe or structured corruption leaves meaningful accuracy headroom. Absolute accuracy also varies across encoders, so this finding is conditional on the tested representations. Yet similar top-1 accuracy can coexist with markedly different calibration errors. A population analysis separates changes in Bayes decisions from changes in posterior probabilities. We then evaluate Anchor-based Efficient Calibration (AEC), a post-hoc temperature-matching intervention that uses training logits rather than clean calibration labels. AEC often improves ECE and representative proper scores without changing predictions, but its fixed target can also worsen calibration. These results position decision quality and probability reliability as distinct objectives in noisy-label learning with frozen foundation models."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026frozen,\ntitle={Frozen Foundation Models and Noisy Labels: Accuracy Headroom and Calibration Gaps},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=tJplle7VSx},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Frozen Foundation Models and Noisy Labels: Accuracy Headroom and Calibration Gaps"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/89f42b6a55ae8785913e5b0fdef5db36b5976854.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791226948144,"tcdate":1789209748128,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission15074/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission15074/Authors"],"forum":"tJplle7VSx","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":15074,"cdate":1789209748128,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission15074/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791226948144,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"tJplle7VSx","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"quantization"},"keywords":{"value":["quantization","llms","calibration-free","transformation-free","post-training quantization"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"N/A"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"No"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"We follow standard evaluation protocols on widely used benchmark datasets and refer to the original works for detailed dataset statistics and splits."},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"No"},"B5_elaboration":{"value":"We use widely established benchmark datasets and follow standard evaluation protocols. Detailed documentation of these datasets is available in the original references."},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"No"},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"We report single-run performance metrics for all experiments and do not perform repeated trials or statistical analysis such as variance or confidence intervals, as our focus is on comparing quantization methods under consistent evaluation settings."},"B2_elaboration":{"value":"We use standard publicly available datasets and models consistent with prior work, and refer readers to the original publications for licensing and usage details."},"B3_elaboration":{"value":"We use standard publicly available datasets and models strictly for research and evaluation purposes, consistent with common practice in the literature."},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"4"},"C4_elaboration":{"value":"We rely on standard implementations and evaluation pipelines commonly used in prior work but do not explicitly document full package details, versions, or parameter settings, as our focus is on the proposed quantization methods."},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"Yes"},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"We report representative runtime comparisons and partial hardware details, but do not provide a complete accounting of computational budgets or infrastructure configurations, as the focus of this work is on algorithmic evaluation rather than system-level efficiency."},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"4.1.1"},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"AI assistants were only used for minor language editing and phrasing improvements, therefore, no explicit disclosure was included."},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"No"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"N/A"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026calibration,\ntitle={Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only {LLM}s Quantization via Dynamic Grouping},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=sUz6nboClw},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration and Transformation-Free Weight-Only LLMs Quantization via Dynamic Grouping"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"No"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Approaches to low-resource settings"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"No"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"N/A"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"No"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"N/A"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"No"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) deliver strong performance but are difficult to deploy under tight memory and compute constraints. Low-bit post-training quantization (PTQ) is a promising direction; however, it typically relies on calibration data, auxiliary transformations, and GPU tools.\nTo address these limitations, we propose Multi Scale Binary (MSB), a calibration-free and transformation-free PTQ method that generalizes binary quantization to multi-bit settings. \nMSB optimizes a variance-based dynamic grouping objective that assigns shared scales to groups of weights, yielding a multi-scale binary representation. This formulation applies consistently across quantization granularities, from per-tensor to block-wise settings, without requiring calibration data or intermediate transformations.\nWe implement the optimization in a CPU based solver for the quantization step and evaluate using standard bfloat16 execution without low-bit packing.\nOn Llama 3.2 3B, MSB achieves 8.43 perplexity on WikiText-2 under 4-bit weight only block-wise quantization, compared to 7.81 in full precision and 12.23 with GPTQ in its default setup. Overall, MSB provides a new optimization perspective for low-bit PTQ while simplifying the pipeline by removing calibration and transformations."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"N/A"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d1fed4f1488359e49dbc91b0dad8240da8cf8c39.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Efficient Methods for NLP"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"No"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"no"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"N/A"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1790696931133,"tcdate":1779546359994,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission4194/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission4194/Authors"],"forum":"sUz6nboClw","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4194,"cdate":1779546359994,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1790696931133,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"sUz6nboClw","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Nature Machine Intelligence"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-026-01215-x.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"cheon|braininspired_warmup_training_with_random_noise_for_uncertainty_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s42256-026-01215-x"},"abstract":{"value":"Uncertainty calibration, the alignment of predictive confidence with accuracy, is essential for the reliable deployment of machine learning systems in real-world applications. However, current models often fail to achieve this goal, generating responses that are overconfident, inaccurate or even fabricated. Here we show that the widely adopted initialization method in deep learning—long regarded as standard practice—is, in fact, a primary source of overconfidence. To address this problem, we introduce a neurodevelopment-inspired warm-up strategy that inherently resolves uncertainty-related issues without requiring pre- or post-processing. In our approach, networks are first briefly trained on random noise and random labels before being exposed to real data. This warm-up phase yields optimal calibration, ensuring that confidence remains well aligned with accuracy throughout subsequent training. Moreover, the resulting networks demonstrate high proficiency in the identification of ‘unknown’ inputs, providing a robust solution for uncertainty calibration in both in-distribution and out-of-distribution contexts. Cheon and Paik show that overconfidence in deep neural networks arises from standard initialization practices, and that brief warm-up training with random noise improves uncertainty calibration and meta-cognitive recognition of unknown inputs."},"title":{"value":"Brain-inspired warm-up training with random noise for uncertainty calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jeonghwan Cheon","username":"~Jeonghwan_Cheon1"},{"fullname":"Se-Bum Paik","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Se-Bum%20Paik"}]}},"tmdate":1781531579401,"pdate":1775692800000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1038/s42256-026-01215-x"],"tcdate":1781531573060,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Jeonghwan_Cheon1"],"forum":"Fm7Lv1pUyk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":76327,"cdate":1781525584301,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1781531579401,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Fm7Lv1pUyk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.07874v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"giacomini|calib_simple_and_accurate_lidarrgb_calibration_using_small_common_markers"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Emanuele_Giacomini:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Leonardo_Brizi:","","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Omar_Salem:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Patrizio_Perugini:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Giorgio_Grisetti:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2309.07874"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2309-07874,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Emanuele Giacomini and Leonardo Brizi and Luca Di Giammarino and Omar Salem and Patrizio Perugini and Giorgio Grisetti},\n  title={CaLib: Simple and Accurate LiDAR-RGB Calibration using Small Common Markers},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2309.07874},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2309.07874}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In many fields of robotics, knowing the relative position and orientation between two sensors is a mandatory precondition to operate with multiple sensing modalities. In this context, the pair LiDAR-RGB cameras offer complementary features: LiDARs yield sparse high quality range measurements, while RGB cameras provide a dense color measurement of the environment. Existing techniques often rely either on complex calibration targets that are expensive to obtain, or extracted virtual correspondences that can hinder the estimate's accuracy. In this paper we address the problem of LiDAR-RGB calibration using typical calibration patterns (i.e. A3 chessboard) with minimal human intervention. Our approach exploits the planarity of the target to find correspondences between the sensors measurements, leading to features that are robust to LiDAR noise. Moreover, we estimate a solution by solving a joint non-linear optimization problem. We validated our approach by carrying on quantitative and comparative experiments with other state-of-the-art approaches. Our results show that our simple schema performs on par or better than other approches using complex calibration targets. Finally, we release an open-source C++ implementation at \\url{https://github.com/srrg-sapienza/ca2lib}"},"title":{"value":"CaLib: Simple and Accurate LiDAR-RGB Calibration using Small Common Markers"},"authors":{"value":["Emanuele Giacomini","Leonardo Brizi","Luca Di Giammarino","Omar Salem","Patrizio Perugini","Giorgio Grisetti"]}},"tmdate":1762937253452,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1740935896951,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Luca_Di_Giammarino1"],"forum":"XVViQMmoVW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":344316,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762937253452,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"XVViQMmoVW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.07874v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"giacomini|calib_simple_and_accurate_lidarrgb_calibration_using_small_common_markers"},"authorids":{"value":["~Emanuele_Giacomini1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Leonardo_Brizi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luca_Di_Giammarino:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Omar_Salem:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Patrizio_Perugini:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Giorgio_Grisetti:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2309.07874"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2309-07874,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Emanuele Giacomini and Leonardo Brizi and Luca Di Giammarino and Omar Salem and Patrizio Perugini and Giorgio Grisetti},\n  title={CaLib: Simple and Accurate LiDAR-RGB Calibration using Small Common Markers},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2309.07874},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2309.07874}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In many fields of robotics, knowing the relative position and orientation between two sensors is a mandatory precondition to operate with multiple sensing modalities. In this context, the pair LiDAR-RGB cameras offer complementary features: LiDARs yield sparse high quality range measurements, while RGB cameras provide a dense color measurement of the environment. Existing techniques often rely either on complex calibration targets that are expensive to obtain, or extracted virtual correspondences that can hinder the estimate's accuracy. In this paper we address the problem of LiDAR-RGB calibration using typical calibration patterns (i.e. A3 chessboard) with minimal human intervention. Our approach exploits the planarity of the target to find correspondences between the sensors measurements, leading to features that are robust to LiDAR noise. Moreover, we estimate a solution by solving a joint non-linear optimization problem. We validated our approach by carrying on quantitative and comparative experiments with other state-of-the-art approaches. Our results show that our simple schema performs on par or better than other approches using complex calibration targets. Finally, we release an open-source C++ implementation at \\url{https://github.com/srrg-sapienza/ca2lib}"},"title":{"value":"CaLib: Simple and Accurate LiDAR-RGB Calibration using Small Common Markers"},"authors":{"value":["Emanuele Giacomini","Leonardo Brizi","Luca Di Giammarino","Omar Salem","Patrizio Perugini","Giorgio Grisetti"]}},"tmdate":1758731323001,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2309-07874"],"tcdate":1758731317866,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Emanuele_Giacomini1"],"forum":"iRUXDQXp49","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":627966,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1758731323001,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"iRUXDQXp49","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Geosci. Remote. Sens. Lett. 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8859/9268492/08966472.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/LGRS/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|atmospheric_refraction_calibration_of_geometric_positioning_for_optical_remote_sensing_satellite"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yanli_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ying_Zhu_0002:","~Mi_Wang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuying_Jin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qilong_Rao:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LGRS.2019.2963626"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/lgrs/WangZWJR20,\n  author={Yanli Wang and Ying Zhu and Mi Wang and Shuying Jin and Qilong Rao},\n  title={Atmospheric Refraction Calibration of Geometric Positioning for Optical Remote Sensing Satellite},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={IEEE Geosci. Remote. Sens. Lett.},\n  volume={17},\n  number={12},\n  pages={2130-2134},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LGRS.2019.2963626}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Owing to the effects of atmospheric refraction, the path of light propagation is bent, making the three-point collinear principle inapplicable and influencing the geometric accuracy of high-resolution optical satellite geometric positioning. This letter presents a novel geometric positioning method with atmospheric refraction calibration for optical remote sensing satellites. The atmospheric ellipsoid model is established using the measured atmospheric parameters and accurately describes the shape and characteristics of the real atmosphere. With iterative processing of geometric positioning and atmospheric refraction calibration, the path of light propagation in atmospheric ellipsoids is calibrated, and the real coordinates of the ground object are positioned accurately. With the advantages of simplicity and independence of sensors, the rational function model with atmospheric refraction calibration is proposed to achieve geometric positioning with higher geometric accuracy. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed model can calibrate atmospheric refraction error and improve the geometric accuracy of the optical imagery with a large view angle. Furthermore, compared with the refraction index using the measurement data, it is proven that atmospheric refraction calibration should be implemented according to the measured atmospheric parameters during imaging instead of using the empirical model."},"title":{"value":"Atmospheric Refraction Calibration of Geometric Positioning for Optical Remote Sensing Satellite"},"authors":{"value":["Yanli Wang","Ying Zhu","Mi Wang","Shuying Jin","Qilong Rao"]}},"tmdate":1741241073673,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1741241026103,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mi_Wang1"],"forum":"5X2KDOlC1X","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":358131,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1741241073673,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5X2KDOlC1X","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.12461v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|towards_tracing_code_provenance_with_code_watermarking"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wei_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Borui_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yujie_Sun:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Suyu_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ziyun_Song:","~Liyao_Xiang1","~Xinbing_Wang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chenghu_Zhou:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2305.12461"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2305-12461,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Wei Li and Borui Yang and Yujie Sun and Suyu Chen and Ziyun Song and Liyao Xiang and Xinbing Wang and Chenghu Zhou},\n  title={Towards Tracing Code Provenance with Code Watermarking},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2305.12461},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2305.12461}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent advances in large language models have raised wide concern in generating abundant plausible source code without scrutiny, and thus tracing the provenance of code emerges as a critical issue. To solve the issue, we propose CodeMark, a watermarking system that hides bit strings into variables respecting the natural and operational semantics of the code. For naturalness, we novelly introduce a contextual watermarking scheme to generate watermarked variables more coherent in the context atop graph neural networks. Each variable is treated as a node on the graph and the node feature gathers neighborhood (context) information through learning. Watermarks embedded into the features are thus reflected not only by the variables but also by the local contexts. We further introduce a pre-trained model on source code as a teacher to guide more natural variable generation. Throughout the embedding, the operational semantics are preserved as only variable names are altered. Beyond guaranteeing code-specific properties, CodeMark is superior in watermarking accuracy, capacity, and efficiency due to a more diversified pattern generated. Experimental results show CodeMark outperforms the SOTA watermarking systems with a better balance of the watermarking requirements."},"title":{"value":"Towards Tracing Code Provenance with Code Watermarking"},"authors":{"value":["Wei Li","Borui Yang","Yujie Sun","Suyu Chen","Ziyun Song","Liyao Xiang","Xinbing Wang","Chenghu Zhou"]}},"tmdate":1768482033426,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727614182300,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Liyao_Xiang1"],"forum":"gWfVLYS8My","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":106335,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768482033426,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"gWfVLYS8My","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IbPRIA 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-36616-1_27.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IBPRIA/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"moravec|stocamo_online_calibration_monitoring_for_stereo_cameras"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jaroslav_Moravec1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Radim_Sára:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-36616-1_27"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ibpria/MoravecS23,\n  author={Jaroslav Moravec and Radim Sára},\n  title={StOCaMo: Online Calibration Monitoring for Stereo Cameras},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={336-350},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-36616-1_27},\n  booktitle={IbPRIA},\n  crossref={conf/ibpria/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Cameras are the prevalent sensors used for perception in autonomous robotic systems, but initial calibration may degrade over time due to dynamic factors. This may lead to the failure of the downstream tasks, such as simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) or object recognition. Hence, a computationally light process that detects the decalibration is of interest."},"title":{"value":"StOCaMo: Online Calibration Monitoring for Stereo Cameras"},"authors":{"value":["Jaroslav Moravec","Radim Sára"]}},"tmdate":1762943341022,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/ibpria/MoravecS23"],"tcdate":1762943336960,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jaroslav_Moravec1"],"forum":"IJ0P53ZjBZ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":694232,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762943341022,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"IJ0P53ZjBZ","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"CALIBUDGET turns train-internal calibration statistics into exact, capacity-aware source quotas under a fixed data budget, improving source-balanced metrics across three matched LLaMA-2-7B LoRA+ seeds while revealing a coverage–retention trade-off."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["source allocation","data mixture","instruction tuning","large language models","fixed-budget adaptation","data selection","multi-source learning","parameter-efficient fine-tuning","LoRA","calibration-guided allocation"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/eda4b0bd7509a1d3c654b32a3431311cf103f92b.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Fixed-budget adaptation from heterogeneous data sources requires deciding not only how much data to use, but how much exposure each source receives. Size-proportional rules can crowd out small sources, whereas difficulty-only rules can chase noisy estimates or allocate residual budget to nearly saturated pools. We introduce CALIBUDGET, a floor-protected, reliability-aware integer allocator that treats source exposure as an explicit adaptation variable. From small train-internal calibration splits, it combines model need, post-floor availability, and bootstrap stability, then produces exact capacity-respecting quotas without changing the model, objective, or total budget. In a controlled setting combining mathematical and commonsense data, CALIBUDGET improves CommonAvg, FragileAvg, and MacroAvg over validation-error-with-floor, the strongest matched comparator, in all three paired LLaMA-2-7B LoRA+ runs. The respective mean gains are 0.56, 0.46, and 0.41 percentage points (pp). Overall increases by 0.18 pp, whereas MathAvg decreases by 0.20 pp, exposing a coverage–retention boundary rather than a uniform gain. CALIBUDGET changes only 1.14–1.42% of the source budget but improves performance in 15 of 24 comparisons across commonsense tasks and seeds. These results suggest that small changes in source quotas can matter; example-level selection can then determine which examples fill each quota. The code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/calibudget-anonymous-code-7470/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026calibudget,\ntitle={{CALIBUDGET}: Calibration-Guided Source Allocation for Fixed-Budget Mixed-Reasoning Adaptation},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=3tKmi5QgQ7},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CALIBUDGET: Calibration-Guided Source Allocation for Fixed-Budget Mixed-Reasoning Adaptation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ba0a5908eb7aa19a3ebf30544991da366c7fb415.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791219713066,"tcdate":1787308140235,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission2668/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission2668/Authors"],"forum":"3tKmi5QgQ7","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2668,"cdate":1787308140235,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission2668/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791219713066,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"3tKmi5QgQ7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACM Comput. Surv. 2023"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CSUR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"grazia|code_search_a_survey_of_techniques_for_finding_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Luca_Di_Grazia1","~Michael_Pradel1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3565971"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/csur/GraziaP23,\n  author={Luca Di Grazia and Michael Pradel},\n  title={Code Search: A Survey of Techniques for Finding Code},\n  year={2023},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1698796800000},\n  journal={ACM Comput. Surv.},\n  volume={55},\n  number={11},\n  pages={220:1-220:31},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3565971}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The immense amounts of source code provide ample challenges and opportunities during software development. To handle the size of code bases, developers commonly search for code, e.g., when trying to find where a particular feature is implemented or when looking for code examples to reuse. To support developers in finding relevant code, various code search engines have been proposed. This article surveys 30 years of research on code search, giving a comprehensive overview of challenges and techniques that address them. We discuss the kinds of queries that code search engines support, how to preprocess and expand queries, different techniques for indexing and retrieving code, and ways to rank and prune search results. Moreover, we describe empirical studies of code search in practice. Based on the discussion of prior work, we conclude the article with an outline of challenges and opportunities to be addressed in the future."},"title":{"value":"Code Search: A Survey of Techniques for Finding Code"},"authors":{"value":["Luca Di Grazia","Michael Pradel"]}},"tmdate":1767880641885,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/csur/GraziaP23"],"tcdate":1764672197970,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Luca_Di_Grazia1"],"forum":"uqT8bHo6D8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706885,"cdate":1698796800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1767880641885,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"uqT8bHo6D8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.02765v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"grazia|code_search_a_survey_of_techniques_for_finding_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Luca_Di_Grazia1","~Michael_Pradel1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2204.02765"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2204-02765,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Luca Di Grazia and Michael Pradel},\n  title={Code Search: A Survey of Techniques for Finding Code},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2204.02765},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2204.02765}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The immense amounts of source code provide ample challenges and opportunities during software development. To handle the size of code bases, developers commonly search for code, e.g., when trying to find where a particular feature is implemented or when looking for code examples to reuse. To support developers in finding relevant code, various code search engines have been proposed. This article surveys 30 years of research on code search, giving a comprehensive overview of challenges and techniques that address them. We discuss the kinds of queries that code search engines support, how to preprocess and expand queries, different techniques for indexing and retrieving code, and ways to rank and prune search results. Moreover, we describe empirical studies of code search in practice. Based on the discussion of prior work, we conclude the article with an outline of challenges and opportunities to be addressed in the future."},"title":{"value":"Code Search: A Survey of Techniques for Finding Code"},"authors":{"value":["Luca Di Grazia","Michael Pradel"]}},"tmdate":1767880640450,"pdate":1640995200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2204-02765"],"tcdate":1764672198040,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Luca_Di_Grazia1"],"forum":"g73lHlLBOT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706889,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1767880640450,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"g73lHlLBOT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["Calibration","trustworthiness","interpretability","actionable interpretability","mechanistic interpretability"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nsubramani2026the,\ntitle={The {ACUTE} Protocol: Operationalizing Language Model Activations for Better Calibration, Utility, and Trust},\nauthor={Nishant Subramani and Palash Goyal and Yiwen Song and Mani Malek and Yuan Xue and Tomas Pfister and Hamid Palangi},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=67lpPXbYf6}\n}"},"title":{"value":"The ACUTE Protocol: Operationalizing Language Model Activations for Better Calibration, Utility, and Trust"},"paperhash":{"value":"subramani|the_acute_protocol_operationalizing_language_model_activations_for_better_calibration_utility_and_trust"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/3e9163a41dc5fc1dfeba194b0162fbb297135934.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"We propose an activation-based confidence, utility, and trust estimation protocol (ACUTE) that we apply to LLMs to improve calibration and decision-making utility across different tasks."},"primary_area":{"value":"social_aspects->accountability_transparency_and_interpretability"},"abstract":{"value":"As language models improve and become increasingly deployed to solve a variety of tasks, trustworthiness becomes essential. Calibration is a good proxy for trust: well-calibrated confidence estimates help inform the risk versus reward trade-off when trusting a specific model output. Unfortunately, even as models improve, they remain poorly calibrated, often biasing towards overconfidence. Additionally, calibration can be gamed: a policy that always predicts the base rate is perfectly calibrated, but completely uninformative. To resolve this, we develop a new metric, **expected utility renormalized by the oracle (EURO)**, that balances calibration and informativeness. We also propose a general-purpose **activation-based confidence, utility, and trust estimation protocol (ACUTE)** to appropriately adjudicate uncertainty. The ACUTE protocol provides flexible, sample-efficient, and compute-efficient confidence estimators for 3 tasks including multiple choice question answering, tool-calling, and scientific document summarization across 6 models from 4 model families. ACUTE outperforms strong baselines on EURO, while maintaining low calibration error. Taken together, our work shows that equipping LLMs with the ACUTE protocol can improve calibration, utility, and trustworthiness in numerous settings."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f55f02f95227f3bfcd3d0398cf379c0d0d384f7e.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Large language models are being used by many users in many different areas, even though automatically estimating whether a user should trust the language model's output is hard. The question of whether to trust the output of an LM is often decided by directly looking at the probability that the LM itself assigns to whether that output was correct and going with that. However, these estimates rarely reflect whether the outputs are correct. In this work, we use the internal representations of a language model to decide whether to trust the model output and find that across tasks and language models, using these internal representations is beneficial."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Nishant_Subramani1","~Palash_Goyal1","~Yiwen_Song1","~Mani_Malek1","~Yuan_Xue5","~Tomas_Pfister1","~Hamid_Palangi1"]},"authors":{"value":["Nishant Subramani","Palash Goyal","Yiwen Song","Mani Malek","Yuan Xue","Tomas Pfister","Hamid Palangi"]}},"tmdate":1790067431509,"pdate":1777576852928,"tcdate":1769210184079,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission29119/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission29119/Authors"],"forum":"67lpPXbYf6","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":29119,"cdate":1769210184079,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission29119/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission29119/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067431509,"odate":1782342023683,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"67lpPXbYf6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.07098v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"das|extrinsic_calibration_and_verification_of_multiple_nonoverlapping_field_of_view_lidar_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sandipan_Das:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Navid_Mahabadi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Addi_Djikic:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cesar_Nassir:","~Saikat_Chatterjee1","~Maurice_F._Fallon1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2205.07098"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2205-07098,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Sandipan Das and Navid Mahabadi and Addi Djikic and Cesar Nassir and Saikat Chatterjee and Maurice F. Fallon},\n  title={Extrinsic Calibration and Verification of Multiple Non-overlapping Field of View Lidar Sensors},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2205.07098},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2205.07098}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We demonstrate a multi-lidar calibration framework for large mobile platforms that jointly calibrate the extrinsic parameters of non-overlapping Field-of-View (FoV) lidar sensors, without the need for any external calibration aid. The method starts by estimating the pose of each lidar in its corresponding sensor frame in between subsequent timestamps. Since the pose estimates from the lidars are not necessarily synchronous, we first align the poses using a Dual Quaternion (DQ) based Screw Linear Interpolation. Afterward, a Hand-Eye based calibration problem is solved using the DQ-based formulation to recover the extrinsics. Furthermore, we verify the extrinsics by matching chosen lidar semantic features, obtained by projecting the lidar data into the camera perspective after time alignment using vehicle kinematics. Experimental results on the data collected from a Scania vehicle [$\\sim$ 1 Km sequence] demonstrate the ability of our approach to obtain better calibration parameters than the provided vehicle CAD model calibration parameters. This setup can also be scaled to any combination of multiple lidars."},"title":{"value":"Extrinsic Calibration and Verification of Multiple Non-overlapping Field of View Lidar Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Sandipan Das","Navid Mahabadi","Addi Djikic","Cesar Nassir","Saikat Chatterjee","Maurice F. Fallon"]}},"tmdate":1789838095231,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731491567635,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Maurice_Fallon1"],"forum":"L1Hy3tCj5I","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":221782,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1789838095231,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"L1Hy3tCj5I","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9811522/9811357/09811704.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"das|extrinsic_calibration_and_verification_of_multiple_nonoverlapping_field_of_view_lidar_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sandipan_Das:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Navid_Mahabadi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Addi_Djikic:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cesar_Nassir:","~Saikat_Chatterjee1","~Maurice_F._Fallon1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA46639.2022.9811704"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/DasMDNCF22,\n  author={Sandipan Das and Navid Mahabadi and Addi Djikic and Cesar Nassir and Saikat Chatterjee and Maurice F. Fallon},\n  title={Extrinsic Calibration and Verification of Multiple Non-overlapping Field of View Lidar Sensors},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={919-925},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA46639.2022.9811704},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We demonstrate a multi-lidar calibration frame-work for large mobile platforms that jointly calibrate the extrinsic parameters of non-overlapping Field-of-View (FoV) lidar sensors, without the need for any external calibration aid. The method starts by estimating the pose of each lidar in its corresponding sensor frame in between subsequent timestamps. Since the pose estimates from the lidars are not necessarily synchronous, we first align the poses using a Dual Quaternion (DQ) based Screw Linear Interpolation. Afterward, a Hand-Eye based calibration problem is solved using the DQ-based formulation to recover the extrinsics. Furthermore, we verify the extrinsics by matching chosen lidar semantic features, obtained by projecting the lidar data into the camera perspective after time alignment using vehicle kinematics. Experimental results on the data collected from a Scania vehicle [~ 1 Km sequence] demonstrate the ability of our approach to obtain better calibration parameters than the provided vehicle CAD model calibration parameters. This setup can also be scaled to any combination of multiple lidars."},"title":{"value":"Extrinsic Calibration and Verification of Multiple Non-overlapping Field of View Lidar Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Sandipan Das","Navid Mahabadi","Addi Djikic","Cesar Nassir","Saikat Chatterjee","Maurice F. Fallon"]}},"tmdate":1789838095173,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731491568230,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Maurice_Fallon1"],"forum":"4j9h9MaAEL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":221817,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1789838095173,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4j9h9MaAEL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.01457v3"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhan|language_as_prior_vision_as_calibration_metric_scale_recovery_for_monocular_depth_estimation"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.01457"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2601-01457,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Mingxia Zhan and Li Zhang and Beibei Wang and Yingjie Wang and Zenglin Shi},\n  title={Language as Prior, Vision as Calibration: Metric Scale Recovery for Monocular Depth Estimation},\n  year={2026},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2601.01457},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.01457}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Relative-depth foundation models transfer well, yet monocular metric depth remains ill-posed due to unidentifiable global scale and heightened domain-shift sensitivity. Under a frozen-backbone calibration setting, we recover metric depth via an image-specific affine transform in inverse depth and train only lightweight calibration heads while keeping the relative-depth backbone and the CLIP text encoder fixed. Since captions provide coarse but noisy scale cues that vary with phrasing and missing objects, we use language to predict an uncertainty-aware envelope that bounds feasible calibration parameters in an unconstrained space, rather than committing to a text-only point estimate. We then use pooled multi-scale frozen visual features to select an image-specific calibration within this envelope. During training, a closed-form least-squares oracle in inverse depth provides per-image supervision for learning the envelope and the selected calibration. Experiments on NYUv2 and KITTI improve in-domain accuracy, while zero-shot transfer to SUN-RGBD and DDAD demonstrates improved robustness over strong language-only baselines."},"title":{"value":"Language as Prior, Vision as Calibration: Metric Scale Recovery for Monocular Depth Estimation"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Mingxia Zhan","username":""},{"fullname":"Li Zhang","username":"~Li_Zhang81"},{"fullname":"Beibei Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Yingjie Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Zenglin Shi","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1785053252940,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2601-01457"],"tcdate":1785053247129,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Zhang_Li3"],"forum":"9AOvttPc0e","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":105945,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785053252940,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"9AOvttPc0e","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose an activation based confidence estimation protocol and a new metric that balances calibration and decision-making utility called EURO that we apply to LLMs to improve trustworthiness and reliability across different tasks."},"venue":{"value":"Mech Interp Workshop ICML 2026 viafasttrackPoster"},"keywords":{"value":["Applications of interpretability","Methods (probing, steering, causal interventions)"]},"abstract":{"value":"As language models improve and become increasingly deployed to solve a variety of tasks, trustworthiness becomes essential. Calibration is a good proxy for trust: well-calibrated confidence estimates help inform the risk versus reward tradeoff when trusting a specific model output. Unfortunately, even as models improve, they remain poorly calibrated, often biasing towards overconfidence. Additionally, calibration can be gamed: a policy that always predicts the base rate is perfectly calibrated, but completely uninformative. To resolve this, we develop a new metric, **expected utility renormalized by the oracle (EURO)**, that balances calibration and informativeness. We also propose a **general-purpose activation-based confidence, utility, and trust estimation protocol (ACUTE)** to appropriately adjudicate uncertainty. The ACUTE protocol provides *flexible, sample-efficient, and compute-efficient* confidence estimators for 3 tasks including multiple choice question answering, tool-calling, and scientific document summarization across 6 models from 4 model families. ACUTE outperforms strong baselines on EURO, while maintaining low calibration error. Taken together, our work shows that equipping LLMs with the ACUTE protocol can improve calibration, utility, and trustworthiness in numerous settings."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nsubramani2026the,\ntitle={The {ACUTE} Protocol: Operationalizing Language Model Activations for Better Calibration, Utility, and Trust},\nauthor={Nishant Subramani and Palash Goyal and Yiwen Song and Mani Malek and Yuan Xue and Tomas Pfister and Hamid Palangi},\nbooktitle={Mechanistic Interpretability Workshop at ICML 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=52dEECHGDN}\n}"},"title":{"value":"The ACUTE Protocol: Operationalizing Language Model Activations for Better Calibration, Utility, and Trust"},"other_keywords":{"value":"Calibration, trustworthiness, interpretability, actionable interpretability, mechanistic interpretability"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3cf7c3d6f878deb57d4f8a3f940c0a3166663e9e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp"},"paperhash":{"value":"subramani|the_acute_protocol_operationalizing_language_model_activations_for_better_calibration_utility_and_trust"},"authorids":{"value":["~Nishant_Subramani1","~Palash_Goyal1","~Yiwen_Song1","~Mani_Malek1","~Yuan_Xue5","~Tomas_Pfister1","~Hamid_Palangi1"]},"authors":{"value":["Nishant Subramani","Palash Goyal","Yiwen Song","Mani Malek","Yuan Xue","Tomas Pfister","Hamid Palangi"]}},"tmdate":1781186359026,"pdate":1781186329999,"tcdate":1778265874067,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/Submission393/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/Submission393/Authors"],"forum":"52dEECHGDN","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":393,"cdate":1778265874067,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1781186359026,"odate":1781186329999,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp","id":"52dEECHGDN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/7083369/10683798/10694691.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"petek|automatic_targetless_cameralidar_calibration_from_motion_and_deep_point_correspondences"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kürsat_Petek:","~Niclas_Vödisch1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Johannes_Meyer:","~Daniele_Cattaneo1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abhinav_Valada:","~Wolfram_Burgard3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3468090"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/PetekVMCVB24,\n  author={Kürsat Petek and Niclas Vödisch and Johannes Meyer and Daniele Cattaneo and Abhinav Valada and Wolfram Burgard},\n  title={Automatic Target-Less Camera-LiDAR Calibration From Motion and Deep Point Correspondences},\n  year={2024},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1730419200000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={9},\n  number={11},\n  pages={9978-9985},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3468090}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Sensor setups of robotic platforms commonly include both camera and LiDAR as they provide complementary information. However, fusing these two modalities typically requires a highly accurate calibration between them. In this letter, we propose MDPCalib which is a novel method for camera-LiDAR calibration that requires neither human supervision nor any specific target objects. Instead, we utilize sensor motion estimates from visual and LiDAR odometry as well as deep learning-based 2D-pixel-to-3D-point correspondences that are obtained without in-domain retraining. We represent camera-LiDAR calibration as an optimization problem and minimize the costs induced by constraints from sensor motion and point correspondences. In extensive experiments, we demonstrate that our approach yields highly accurate extrinsic calibration parameters and is robust to random initialization. Additionally, our approach generalizes to a wide range of sensor setups, which we demonstrate by employing it on various robotic platforms including a self-driving perception car, a quadruped robot, and a UAV."},"title":{"value":"Automatic Target-Less Camera-LiDAR Calibration From Motion and Deep Point Correspondences"},"authors":{"value":["Kürsat Petek","Niclas Vödisch","Johannes Meyer","Daniele Cattaneo","Abhinav Valada","Wolfram Burgard"]}},"tmdate":1775725879575,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1746121995792,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wolfram_Burgard3"],"forum":"1cx81D1MJh","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":408825,"cdate":1730419200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1775725879575,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"1cx81D1MJh","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We investigated whether a collection of LLM inference-time uncertainty measures show evidence of human similarity and calibration, with positive results for both."},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["large language models","alignment","uncertainty quantification","calibration"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/0716a95b6cc31e99f6d9e6f4733a13db30682ede.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"There has been much recent interest in evaluating large language models for uncertainty calibration to facilitate model control and modulate user trust. Inference time uncertainty, which may provide a real-time signal to the model or external control modules, is particularly important for applying these concepts to improve the large language model user experience. While many of the existing papers consider model calibration, comparatively little work has sought to evaluate how closely model uncertainty aligns to human uncertainty. In this work, we evaluate a collection of inference-time uncertainty measures, using both established metrics and novel variations, to determine how closely they align with both human group-level uncertainty and traditional notions of model calibration. We find that numerous measures show evidence of strong alignment to human uncertainty, even despite the lack of alignment to human answer preference. For those successful metrics, we find moderate to strong evidence of model calibration in terms of both correctness correlation and distributional analysis."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nmoore2026humanalignment,\ntitle={Human-Alignment and Calibration of Inference-Time Uncertainty in Large Language Models},\nauthor={Kyle Moore and Jesse Roberts and Daryl Watson},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=EDz39N1Crc}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Human-Alignment and Calibration of Inference-Time Uncertainty in Large Language Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7b1fa1bc73beffec0b7bf04af9e42549cef9bdfb.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"moore|humanalignment_and_calibration_of_inferencetime_uncertainty_in_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kyle_Moore1","~Jesse_Roberts1","~Daryl_Watson1"]},"authors":{"value":["Kyle Moore","Jesse Roberts","Daryl Watson"]}},"tmdate":1770805168011,"tcdate":1758337024074,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission22914/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission22914/Authors"],"forum":"EDz39N1Crc","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":22914,"cdate":1758337024074,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission22914/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770805168011,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"EDz39N1Crc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.14118v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"he|beyond_code_generation_assessing_code_llm_maturity_with_postconditions"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fusen_He:","~Juan_Zhai1","~Minxue_Pan1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.14118"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2407-14118,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Fusen He and Juan Zhai and Minxue Pan},\n  title={Beyond Code Generation: Assessing Code LLM Maturity with Postconditions},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2407.14118},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.14118}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Most existing code Large Language Model (LLM) benchmarks, e.g., EvalPlus, focus on the code generation tasks. Namely, they contain a natural language description of a problem and ask the LLM to write code to solve the problem. We argue that they do not capture all capabilities needed to assess the quality of a code LLM. In this paper, we propose a code LLM maturity model, based on the postcondition generation problem, to access a more complete set of code LLM capabilities. We choose the postcondition generation problem as it requires the code LLM to understand the code including semantics, natural language, and also have the capability to generate unambiguous postconditions in programming languages (i.e., the generation capablity). Moreover, postconditions have various types, requiring different levels of these capabilities, making it suitable to evaluate the maturity of the code LLM. Based on our designed maturity model, we augment the EvalPlus dataset to a postcondition testing benchmark, and evaluated several open-sourced models. Our results highlight the necessary improvements needed for better LLMs for code. Code: https://github.com/MatureModel/PostcondGen"},"title":{"value":"Beyond Code Generation: Assessing Code LLM Maturity with Postconditions"},"authors":{"value":["Fusen He","Juan Zhai","Minxue Pan"]}},"tmdate":1747745932605,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747294518521,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Minxue_Pan1"],"forum":"D9zJz5n3Zx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":477018,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747745932605,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"D9zJz5n3Zx","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"OrthoGrad constrains optimization geometry in a way that reduces overconfidence without sacrificing accuracy."},"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2025 Workshop"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f40007cb313917304be2186d9c27be53b7fa882c.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Neural Calibration","Orthogonal Gradient Descent","Geometry-Aware Optimization","Intrinsic Calibration Methods"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT"},"paperhash":{"value":"hedges|orthograd_improves_neural_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~C._Evans_Hedges2"]},"abstract":{"value":"We study $\\perp$Grad, a geometry-aware modification to gradient-based optimization that constrains descent directions to address overconfidence, a key limitation of standard optimizers in uncertainty-critical applications. By enforcing orthogonality between gradient updates and weight vectors, $\\perp$Grad alters optimization trajectories without architectural changes. On CIFAR-10 with 10\\% labeled data, $\\perp$Grad matches SGD in accuracy while achieving statistically significant improvements in test loss ($p=0.05$), predictive entropy ($p=0.001$), and confidence measures. These effects show consistent trends across corruption levels and architectures. $\\perp$Grad is optimizer-agnostic, incurs minimal overhead, and remains compatible with post-hoc calibration techniques.\n\nTheoretically, we characterize convergence and stationary points for a simplified $\\perp$Grad variant, revealing that orthogonalization constrains loss reduction pathways to avoid confidence inflation and encourage decision-boundary improvements. Our findings suggest that geometric interventions in optimization can improve predictive uncertainty estimates at low computational cost."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhedges2025orthograd,\ntitle={OrthoGrad Improves Neural Calibration},\nauthor={C. Evans Hedges},\nbooktitle={OPT 2025: Optimization for Machine Learning},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=vr2nSrnhYY}\n}"},"title":{"value":"OrthoGrad Improves Neural Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["C. Evans Hedges"]}},"tmdate":1764597312451,"pdate":1758568911482,"tcdate":1754093342161,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/Submission3/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/Submission3/Authors"],"forum":"vr2nSrnhYY","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":3,"cdate":1754093342161,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/Submission3/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1764597312451,"odate":1764597312369,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT","id":"vr2nSrnhYY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IGARSS 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8891871/8897702/08898163.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IGARSS/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|a_rigorous_onorbit_geometric_calibration_method_for_highresolution_optical_sensor_of_chinese_mapping_satellite"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kun_Hu10","~Yongjun_Zhang4","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xu_Huang_0005:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS.2019.8898163"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/igarss/Hu0019,\n  author={Kun Hu and Yongjun Zhang and Xu Huang},\n  title={A Rigorous on-orbit Geometric Calibration Method for High-Resolution Optical Sensor of Chinese Mapping Satellite},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  pages={8526-8529},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS.2019.8898163},\n  booktitle={IGARSS},\n  crossref={conf/igarss/2019}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Internationally, the technology of optical earth observation satellite is developing in the direction of miniaturization, high-resolution and high maneuverability. High-precision on-orbit geometric calibration of the imaging sensors is the most important guarantee of interior geometric quality and direct positioning accuracy of the satellite images. The high-resolution camera of Chinese Mapping Satellite-1 is a multi-chip TDICCD optical sensor in triangular mechanical staggered stitching structure. To improve the accuracy and robustness of its geometric calibration while reducing the correlation between calibration parameters, this paper proposed a new multi-chip pointing angle model for internal calibration and an optimized imaging model for external calibration. The overlapping connection relation between multi-chip TDICCDs of an image and multi-scene images are fully utilized for constructing the calibration error equations with constraints. Then, the internal and external calibration are iteratively solved through relaxation block adjustment method. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed rigorous on-orbit geometric calibration method can effectively and stably eliminate various system errors of the imaging system, thus improving the interior and exterior geometric quality of image production."},"title":{"value":"A Rigorous on-orbit Geometric Calibration Method for High-Resolution Optical Sensor of Chinese Mapping Satellite"},"authors":{"value":["Kun Hu","Yongjun Zhang","Xu Huang"]}},"tmdate":1758326221217,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1731480910348,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yongjun_Zhang4"],"forum":"IOuHkUoKJD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":209320,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1758326221217,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"IOuHkUoKJD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.02821v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"lv|targetless_intrinsics_and_extrinsic_calibration_of_multiple_lidars_and_cameras_with_imu_using_continuoustime_estimation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuezhang_Lv:","~Yunzhou_Zhang2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chao_Lu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiajun_Zhu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Song_Wu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.02821"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-02821,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yuezhang Lv and Yunzhou Zhang and Chao Lu and Jiajun Zhu and Song Wu},\n  title={Targetless Intrinsics and Extrinsic Calibration of Multiple LiDARs and Cameras with IMU using Continuous-Time Estimation},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2501.02821},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.02821}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate spatiotemporal calibration is a prerequisite for multisensor fusion. However, sensors are typically asynchronous, and there is no overlap between the fields of view of cameras and LiDARs, posing challenges for intrinsic and extrinsic parameter calibration. To address this, we propose a calibration pipeline based on continuous-time and bundle adjustment (BA) capable of simultaneous intrinsic and extrinsic calibration (6 DOF transformation and time offset). We do not require overlapping fields of view or any calibration board. Firstly, we establish data associations between cameras using Structure from Motion (SFM) and perform self-calibration of camera intrinsics. Then, we establish data associations between LiDARs through adaptive voxel map construction, optimizing for extrinsic calibration within the map. Finally, by matching features between the intensity projection of LiDAR maps and camera images, we conduct joint optimization for intrinsic and extrinsic parameters. This pipeline functions in texture-rich structured environments, allowing simultaneous calibration of any number of cameras and LiDARs without the need for intricate sensor synchronization triggers. Experimental results demonstrate our method's ability to fulfill co-visibility and motion constraints between sensors without accumulating errors."},"title":{"value":"Targetless Intrinsics and Extrinsic Calibration of Multiple LiDARs and Cameras with IMU using Continuous-Time Estimation"},"authors":{"value":["Yuezhang Lv","Yunzhou Zhang","Chao Lu","Jiajun Zhu","Song Wu"]}},"tmdate":1740928138977,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1740928135106,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunzhou_Zhang2"],"forum":"55Q6TJz4CL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":344224,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740928138977,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"55Q6TJz4CL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICASSP 2012"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6268628/6287775/06288477.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICASSP/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"gribonval|blind_calibration_for_compressed_sensing_by_convex_optimization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Rémi_Gribonval1","~Gilles_Chardon1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Laurent_Daudet:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP.2012.6288477"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icassp/GribonvalCD12,\n  author={Rémi Gribonval and Gilles Chardon and Laurent Daudet},\n  title={Blind calibration for compressed sensing by convex optimization},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  pages={2713-2716},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP.2012.6288477},\n  booktitle={ICASSP},\n  crossref={conf/icassp/2012}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We consider the problem of calibrating a compressed sensing measurement system under the assumption that the decalibration consists in unknown gains on each measure. We focus on blind calibration, using measures performed on a few unknown (but sparse) signals. A naive formulation of this blind calibration problem, using ℓ1 minimization, is reminiscent of blind source separation and dictionary learning, which are known to be highly non-convex and riddled with local minima. In the considered context, we show that in fact this formulation can be exactly expressed as a convex optimization problem, and can be solved using off-the-shelf algorithms. Numerical simulations demonstrate the effectiveness of the approach even for highly uncalibrated measures, when a sufficient number of (unknown, but sparse) calibrating signals is provided. We observe that the success/failure of the approach seems to obey sharp phase transitions."},"title":{"value":"Blind calibration for compressed sensing by convex optimization"},"authors":{"value":["Rémi Gribonval","Gilles Chardon","Laurent Daudet"]}},"tmdate":1793625933848,"pdate":1325376000000,"tcdate":1747258373790,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rémi_Gribonval1"],"forum":"SSerlfkm55","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":466851,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1793625933848,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SSerlfkm55","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2024 poster"},"TLDR":{"value":"This paper offers a comprehensive analysis of the expected calibration error using the information-theoretic generalization analysis."},"keywords":{"value":["information thery","information-theoretic generalization error analysis","generalization error","expected calibration error","calibration error","binning"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/b2543ac5e3637d8a5c66cd924fec0a9177dcec00.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"learning_theory"},"abstract":{"value":"While the expected calibration error (ECE), which employs binning, is widely adopted to evaluate the calibration performance of machine learning models, theoretical understanding of its estimation bias is limited. In this paper, we present the first comprehensive analysis of the estimation bias in the two common binning strategies, uniform mass and uniform width binning.\nOur analysis establishes upper bounds on the bias, achieving an improved convergence rate. Moreover, our bounds reveal, for the first time, the optimal number of bins to minimize the estimation bias. We further extend our bias analysis to generalization error analysis based on the information-theoretic approach, deriving upper bounds that enable the numerical evaluation of how small the ECE is for unknown data. Experiments using deep learning models show that our bounds are nonvacuous thanks to this information-theoretic generalization analysis approach."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nfutami2024informationtheoretic,\ntitle={Information-theoretic Generalization Analysis for Expected Calibration Error},\nauthor={Futoshi Futami and Masahiro Fujisawa},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-eighth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=yltJAlwtW9}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Information-theoretic Generalization Analysis for Expected Calibration Error"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c4a25ce5ac23050bc6d164b9b2269d2890c8bde3.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"futami|informationtheoretic_generalization_analysis_for_expected_calibration_error"},"authorids":{"value":["~Futoshi_Futami1","~Masahiro_Fujisawa1"]},"authors":{"value":["Futoshi Futami","Masahiro Fujisawa"]}},"tmdate":1730873928021,"pdate":1727287936298,"tcdate":1715692912527,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission10327/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission10327/Authors"],"forum":"yltJAlwtW9","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":10327,"cdate":1715692912527,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission10327/-/Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference/Submission10327/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1730873928021,"odate":1730873928008,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Conference","id":"yltJAlwtW9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.15709v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"futami|informationtheoretic_generalization_analysis_for_expected_calibration_error"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Futoshi_Futami:","~Masahiro_Fujisawa1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.15709"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2405-15709,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Futoshi Futami and Masahiro Fujisawa},\n  title={Information-theoretic Generalization Analysis for Expected Calibration Error},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2405.15709},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.15709}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"While the expected calibration error (ECE), which employs binning, is widely adopted to evaluate the calibration performance of machine learning models, theoretical understanding of its estimation bias is limited. In this paper, we present the first comprehensive analysis of the estimation bias in the two common binning strategies, uniform mass and uniform width binning. Our analysis establishes upper bounds on the bias, achieving an improved convergence rate. Moreover, our bounds reveal, for the first time, the optimal number of bins to minimize the estimation bias. We further extend our bias analysis to generalization error analysis based on the information-theoretic approach, deriving upper bounds that enable the numerical evaluation of how small the ECE is for unknown data. Experiments using deep learning models show that our bounds are nonvacuous thanks to this information-theoretic generalization analysis approach."},"title":{"value":"Information-theoretic Generalization Analysis for Expected Calibration Error"},"authors":{"value":["Futoshi Futami","Masahiro Fujisawa"]}},"tmdate":1728226252842,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1728226244610,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Masahiro_Fujisawa1"],"forum":"ggsmpUNRJJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":140525,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1728226252842,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ggsmpUNRJJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"GeoAI@SIGSPATIAL 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/GEOAI/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"gramacki|evaluation_of_code_llms_on_geospatial_code_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Piotr_Gramacki1","~Bruno_Martins1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Piotr_Szymanski:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3687123.3698286"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/geoai/GramackiMS24,\n  author={Piotr Gramacki and Bruno Martins and Piotr Szymanski},\n  title={Evaluation of Code LLMs on Geospatial Code Generation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={54-62},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3687123.3698286},\n  booktitle={GeoAI@SIGSPATIAL},\n  crossref={conf/geoai/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Software development support tools have been studied for a long time, with recent approaches using Large Language Models (LLMs) for code generation. These models can generate Python code for data science and machine learning applications. LLMs are helpful for software engineers because they increase productivity in daily work. An LLM can also serve as a \"mentor\" for inexperienced software developers, and be a viable learning support. High-quality code generation with LLMs can also be beneficial in geospatial data science. However, this domain poses different challenges, and code generation LLMs are typically not evaluated on geospatial tasks. Here, we show how we constructed an evaluation benchmark for code generation models, based on a selection of geospatial tasks. We categorised geospatial tasks based on their complexity and required tools. Then, we created a dataset with tasks that test model capabilities in spatial reasoning, spatial data processing, and geospatial tools usage. The dataset consists of specific coding problems that were manually created for high quality. For every problem, we proposed a set of test scenarios that make it possible to automatically check the generated code for correctness. In addition, we tested a selection of existing code generation LLMs for code generation in the geospatial domain. We share our dataset and reproducible evaluation code on a public GitHub repository1, arguing that this can serve as an evaluation benchmark for new LLMs in the future. Our dataset will hopefully contribute to the development new models capable of solving geospatial coding tasks with high accuracy. These models will enable the creation of coding assistants tailored for geospatial applications."},"title":{"value":"Evaluation of Code LLMs on Geospatial Code Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Piotr Gramacki","Bruno Martins","Piotr Szymanski"]}},"tmdate":1768377036762,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1752141316746,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Piotr_Gramacki1"],"forum":"gUZsQ7mkoB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":566694,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768377036762,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"gUZsQ7mkoB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Internet Things J. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6488907/10551641/10477246.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IOTJ/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|bevsoc_selfsupervised_contrastive_learning_for_calibrationfree_bev_3d_object_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yongqing_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nanyu_Li:","~Dandan_Zhu3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Charles_C._Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhuhua_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yong_Bai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Yan_0009:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/JIOT.2024.3379471"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/iotj/ChenLZZHBY24,\n  author={Yongqing Chen and Nanyu Li and Dandan Zhu and Charles C. Zhou and Zhuhua Hu and Yong Bai and Jun Yan},\n  title={BEVSOC: Self-Supervised Contrastive Learning for Calibration-Free BEV 3-D Object Detection},\n  year={2024},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1717200000000},\n  journal={IEEE Internet Things J.},\n  volume={11},\n  number={12},\n  pages={22167-22182},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/JIOT.2024.3379471}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"3-D object detection based on multiview cameras and bird’s-eye view (BEV) representation is a key task for autonomous driving, as it enables the perception systems to understand the surrounding scenes. However, most existing BEV representation methods rely on the projection matrix of camera intrinsic and extrinsic parameters, which requires a complex and time-consuming calibration process that may introduce errors and degrade the detection performance. Moreover, the calibration results may vary due to environmental changes and affect the stability of the detection system. To address this problem, we propose a calibration-free 3-D object detection method that leverages a group-equivariant convolutional network to extract features from multiview images and a projection network module to learn the implicit 3D-to-2D projection relationship for obtaining BEV representation. Furthermore, we employ contrastive learning (CL) to pretrain the projection network module without using manually annotated data. By exploiting the multiview camera data through CL, our proposed method eliminates the need for tedious calibration, avoids calibration errors, and reduces the dependence on a large amount of annotated data for calibration-free 3-D object detection. We evaluate our method on the nuScenes data set and demonstrate its competitive performance. Our method improves the stability and reliability of 3-D object detection in long-term autonomous driving."},"title":{"value":"BEVSOC: Self-Supervised Contrastive Learning for Calibration-Free BEV 3-D Object Detection"},"authors":{"value":["Yongqing Chen","Nanyu Li","Dandan Zhu","Charles C. Zhou","Zhuhua Hu","Yong Bai","Jun Yan"]}},"tmdate":1748083452529,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747104361732,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dandan_Zhu3"],"forum":"SRH2sSFoc8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":432757,"cdate":1717200000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1748083452529,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SRH2sSFoc8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICDL 2023"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"seyler|novel_methods_inspired_by_reinforcement_learning_actorcritic_mechanism_for_eyeinhand_calibration_in_robotics"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jan_R._Seyler1","~Chenxing_Li2","~Yinlong_Liu1","~Shahram_Eivazi2","fabian.schreier@festo.com","ge82reh@mytum.de"]},"html":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/10364560"},"abstract":{"value":"Eye-in-hand camera calibration is an essential step in robotic vision systems. Traditional methods perform the calibration via geometry computation methods where the calibration points are acquired by moving the robot arm while capturing images of a static object (often a checkerboard) of known geometry. Our work aims to explore deep reinforcement learning (DRL) techniques for solving common calibration optimization problems. Inspired by the actor-critic mechanism of DRL, we propose a novel approach to perform eye-in-hand camera calibration on the robot arm. Numerical and real-world experiments prove the feasibility of the proposed method as such here we show the potential of the reinforcement learning method to solve the camera calibration problem."},"title":{"value":"Novel Methods Inspired by Reinforcement Learning Actor-Critic Mechanism for Eye-in-Hand Calibration in Robotics"},"authors":{"value":["Jan R. Seyler","Chenxing Li","Yinlong Liu","Shahram Eivazi","Fabian Schreier","Yingbai Hu"]}},"tmdate":1726126830363,"pdate":1687678824141,"tcdate":1726126830363,"writers":["~Jan_R._Seyler1","~Chenxing_Li2","~Yinlong_Liu1","~Shahram_Eivazi2","fabian.schreier@festo.com","ge82reh@mytum.de"],"signatures":["~Jan_R._Seyler1"],"forum":"oMi423lJt7","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","number":28278,"cdate":1726126830363,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1726126830363,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"oMi423lJt7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.268.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"lee|who_wrote_this_code_watermarking_for_code_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Taehyun_Lee:","~Seokhee_Hong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jaewoo_Ahn:","~Ilgee_Hong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hwaran_Lee:","~Sangdoo_Yun1","~Jamin_Shin1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gunhee_Kim:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.268"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/Lee0AHLYSK24,\n  author={Taehyun Lee and Seokhee Hong and Jaewoo Ahn and Ilgee Hong and Hwaran Lee and Sangdoo Yun and Jamin Shin and Gunhee Kim},\n  title={Who Wrote this Code? Watermarking for Code Generation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={4890-4911},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.268},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2024-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Since the remarkable generation performance of large language models raised ethical and legal concerns, approaches to detect machine-generated text by embedding watermarks are being developed.However, we discover that the existing works fail to function appropriately in code generation tasks due to the task’s nature of having low entropy.Extending a logit-modifying watermark method, we propose Selective WatErmarking via Entropy Thresholding (SWEET), which enhances detection ability and mitigates code quality degeneration by removing low-entropy segments at generating and detecting watermarks.Our experiments show that SWEET significantly improves code quality preservation while outperforming all baselines, including post-hoc detection methods, in detecting machine-generated code text.Our code is available inhttps://github.com/hongcheki/sweet-watermark."},"title":{"value":"Who Wrote this Code? Watermarking for Code Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Taehyun Lee","Seokhee Hong","Jaewoo Ahn","Ilgee Hong","Hwaran Lee","Sangdoo Yun","Jamin Shin","Gunhee Kim"]}},"tmdate":1772437602716,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747629495192,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Seokhee_Hong1"],"forum":"D7bvuc5mqF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":524718,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772437602716,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"D7bvuc5mqF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7083369/10409903/10387679.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"murai|distributed_simultaneous_localisation_and_autocalibration_using_gaussian_belief_propagation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Riku_Murai1","~Ignacio_Alzugaray1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Paul_H._J._Kelly:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrew_J._Davison:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3352361"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/MuraiAKD24,\n  author={Riku Murai and Ignacio Alzugaray and Paul H. J. Kelly and Andrew J. Davison},\n  title={Distributed Simultaneous Localisation and Auto-Calibration Using Gaussian Belief Propagation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={9},\n  number={3},\n  pages={2136-2143},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3352361}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present a novel scalable, fully distributed, and online method for simultaneous localisation and extrinsic calibration for multi-robot setups. Individual a priori unknown robot poses are probabilistically inferred as robots sense each other while simultaneously calibrating their sensors and markers extrinsic using Gaussian Belief Propagation. In the presented experiments, we show how our method not only yields accurate robot localisation and auto-calibration but also is able to perform under challenging circumstances such as highly noisy measurements, significant communication failures or limited communication range."},"title":{"value":"Distributed Simultaneous Localisation and Auto-Calibration Using Gaussian Belief Propagation"},"authors":{"value":["Riku Murai","Ignacio Alzugaray","Paul H. J. Kelly","Andrew J. Davison"]}},"tmdate":1762403170375,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1741257572457,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ignacio_Alzugaray1"],"forum":"OT1RIXXWFo","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":361334,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762403170375,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"OT1RIXXWFo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.15036v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"murai|distributed_simultaneous_localisation_and_autocalibration_using_gaussian_belief_propagation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Riku_Murai1","~Ignacio_Alzugaray1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Paul_H._J._Kelly:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrew_J._Davison:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2401.15036"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2401-15036,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Riku Murai and Ignacio Alzugaray and Paul H. J. Kelly and Andrew J. Davison},\n  title={Distributed Simultaneous Localisation and Auto-Calibration using Gaussian Belief Propagation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2401.15036},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2401.15036}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present a novel scalable, fully distributed, and online method for simultaneous localisation and extrinsic calibration for multi-robot setups. Individual a priori unknown robot poses are probabilistically inferred as robots sense each other while simultaneously calibrating their sensors and markers extrinsic using Gaussian Belief Propagation. In the presented experiments, we show how our method not only yields accurate robot localisation and auto-calibration but also is able to perform under challenging circumstances such as highly noisy measurements, significant communication failures or limited communication range."},"title":{"value":"Distributed Simultaneous Localisation and Auto-Calibration using Gaussian Belief Propagation"},"authors":{"value":["Riku Murai","Ignacio Alzugaray","Paul H. J. Kelly","Andrew J. Davison"]}},"tmdate":1762403170231,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1741257572452,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ignacio_Alzugaray1"],"forum":"zwiiPs13OP","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":361332,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762403170231,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zwiiPs13OP","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"35th IEEE Intelligent Vehicles Symposium (IV 2023)"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|l2v2t2calib_automatic_and_unified_extrinsic_calibration_toolbox_for_different_3d_lidar_visual_camera_and_thermal_camera"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jun_Zhang18"]},"abstract":{"value":"Extrinsic calibration between LiDAR-Camera and LiDAR-LiDAR has been researched extensively, because it is the foundation for sensor fusion. Meanwhile, many projects are open-sourced and significantly promote related research. However, limited solutions can unify the calibration between repetitive scanning and non-repetitive scanning 3D LiDAR, sparse and dense 3D LiDAR, visual and thermal camera. Currently, to achieve that, we normally need to use different targets and extract different features for different sensor combinations. Sometimes, human intervention is required to locate the target. It is inconvenient and time-consuming. In this paper, L 2 V 2 T 2 Calib is introduced and open-sourced as a trial to unify the calibration. 1). A four-circular-holes board is adopted for all sensors. The four circle centers can be detected by all the sensors, thus are ideal common features. Previous works also use this target, but the algorithms don't consider non-repetitive scanning LiDARs, thus cannot be directly applied. 2). To unify the process, an important step is to automatically and robustly detect the target from different types of LiDARs. However, this does not receive enough attention. We propose a method based on template matching. It is simple, but effective and general to different depth sensors. 3). We provide two types of output, minimizing 2D re-projection error (Min2D) and minimizing 3D matching error (Min3D), for different users. And their performance is compared. Extensive experiments conducted in both simulation and real environment demonstrate L2V2T2Calib is accurate, robust, more importantly, unified. The code will be open-sourced to promote related research at: https://github.com/Clothooo/lvt2calib"},"title":{"value":"L2V2T2Calib: Automatic and Unified Extrinsic Calibration Toolbox for Different 3D LiDAR, Visual Camera and Thermal Camera"},"authors":{"value":["Jun Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1761098001499,"pdate":1687363200000,"tcdate":1761097759939,"writers":["~Jun_Zhang18"],"signatures":["~Jun_Zhang18"],"forum":"Wph5dlGcYm","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":39884,"cdate":1761097759939,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1761098001499,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"Wph5dlGcYm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Remote. Sens. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/15/1/104/pdf?version=1672821455"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/REMOTESENSING/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|quick_quality_assessment_and_radiometric_calibration_of_csar01_satellite_using_flexible_automatic_corner_reflector"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qiqi_Huang:","~Fengli_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lu_Li_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaochen_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yanan_Jiao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinzhe_Yuan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Huirong_Li:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/rs15010104"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/remotesensing/HuangZLLJYL23,\n  author={Qiqi Huang and Fengli Zhang and Lu Li and Xiaochen Liu and Yanan Jiao and Xinzhe Yuan and Huirong Li},\n  title={Quick Quality Assessment and Radiometric Calibration of C-SAR/01 Satellite Using Flexible Automatic Corner Reflector},\n  year={2023},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={15},\n  number={1},\n  pages={104},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/rs15010104}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"C-SAR/01, the successor of China’s Gaofen-3 Satellite, which launched on 23 November 2021, is the first synthetic aperture radar (SAR) satellite to be launched in China’s civil space infrastructure plan and has served as an invaluable data resource. Radiometric calibration and validation are prerequisites for the quantitative application of SAR data. In this study, the radiometric calibration experiments of C-SAR/01 data of the ultra-fine strip (UFS) and fine strip I (FSI) modes were conducted applying flexible automatic triangular trihedral corner reflectors deployed in Xilinhot SAR satellite calibration and validation site. Accordingly, the image quality and radiometric calibration accuracy were evaluated. The results show that the spatial resolution, peak sidelobe ratio, and integrated sidelobe ratio of UFS and FSI mode data of C-SAR/01 are better than those of the design indexes, and the calibration results from the integral method are more stable than those from the peak method. Furthermore, the standard deviation of the calibration constant for UFS mode data is 0.234 dB, with the relative and absolute calibration accuracies obtained as 0.233 and 0.532 dB, respectively, whereas the standard deviation calibration constant for FSI mode data is 0.198 dB, with its relative and absolute calibration accuracies evaluated as 0.199 and 0.333 dB, respectively."},"title":{"value":"Quick Quality Assessment and Radiometric Calibration of C-SAR/01 Satellite Using Flexible Automatic Corner Reflector"},"authors":{"value":["Qiqi Huang","Fengli Zhang","Lu Li","Xiaochen Liu","Yanan Jiao","Xinzhe Yuan","Huirong Li"]}},"tmdate":1731640435703,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731640432309,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fengli_Zhang1"],"forum":"tm1oUbHawU","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":246172,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731640435703,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"tm1oUbHawU","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.16441v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"sun|unicoder_scaling_code_large_language_model_via_universal_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_Sun:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Linzheng_Chai:","~Jian_Yang10","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuwei_Yin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongcheng_Guo:","~Jiaheng_Liu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bing_Wang:","~Liqun_Yang2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhoujun_Li_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2406.16441"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2406-16441,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Tao Sun and Linzheng Chai and Jian Yang and Yuwei Yin and Hongcheng Guo and Jiaheng Liu and Bing Wang and Liqun Yang and Zhoujun Li},\n  title={UniCoder: Scaling Code Large Language Model via Universal Code},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2406.16441},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2406.16441}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Intermediate reasoning or acting steps have successfully improved large language models (LLMs) for handling various downstream natural language processing (NLP) tasks. When applying LLMs for code generation, recent works mainly focus on directing the models to articulate intermediate natural-language reasoning steps, as in chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting, and then output code with the natural language or other structured intermediate steps. However, such output is not suitable for code translation or generation tasks since the standard CoT has different logical structures and forms of expression with the code. In this work, we introduce the universal code (UniCode) as the intermediate representation. It is a description of algorithm steps using a mix of conventions of programming languages, such as assignment operator, conditional operator, and loop. Hence, we collect an instruction dataset UniCoder-Instruct to train our model UniCoder on multi-task learning objectives. UniCoder-Instruct comprises natural-language questions, code solutions, and the corresponding universal code. The alignment between the intermediate universal code representation and the final code solution significantly improves the quality of the generated code. The experimental results demonstrate that UniCoder with the universal code significantly outperforms the previous prompting methods by a large margin, showcasing the effectiveness of the structural clues in pseudo-code."},"title":{"value":"UniCoder: Scaling Code Large Language Model via Universal Code"},"authors":{"value":["Tao Sun","Linzheng Chai","Jian Yang","Yuwei Yin","Hongcheng Guo","Jiaheng Liu","Bing Wang","Liqun Yang","Zhoujun Li"]}},"tmdate":1792297480608,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731469425902,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jiaheng_Liu1"],"forum":"tK8jEm2PEK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":194234,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1792297480608,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"tK8jEm2PEK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.100.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"sun|unicoder_scaling_code_large_language_model_via_universal_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_Sun:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Linzheng_Chai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jian_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuwei_Yin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongcheng_Guo:","~Jiaheng_Liu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bing_Wang:","~Liqun_Yang2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhoujun_Li:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.100"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/SunCYYGLWYL24,\n  author={Tao Sun and Linzheng Chai and Jian Yang and Yuwei Yin and Hongcheng Guo and Jiaheng Liu and Bing Wang and Liqun Yang and Zhoujun Li},\n  title={UniCoder: Scaling Code Large Language Model via Universal Code},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={1812-1824},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.100},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2024-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Intermediate reasoning or acting steps have successfully improved large language models (LLMs) for handling various downstream natural language processing (NLP) tasks.When applying LLMs for code generation, recent works mainly focus on directing the models to articulate intermediate natural-language reasoning steps, as in chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting, and then output code with the natural language or other structured intermediate steps. However, such output is not suitable for code translation or generation tasks since the standard CoT has different logical structures and forms of expression with the code. In this work, we introduce the universal code (UniCode) as the intermediate representation. It is a description of algorithm steps using a mix of conventions of programming languages, such as assignment operator, conditional operator, and loop. Hence, we collect an instruction dataset UniCoder-Instruct to train our model UniCoder on multi-task learning objectives. UniCoder-Instruct comprises natural-language questions, code solutions, and the corresponding universal code. The alignment between the intermediate universal code representation and the final code solution significantly improves the quality of the generated code. The experimental results demonstrate that UniCoder with the universal code significantly outperforms the previous prompting methods by a large margin, showcasing the effectiveness of the structural clues in pseudo-code."},"title":{"value":"UniCoder: Scaling Code Large Language Model via Universal Code"},"authors":{"value":["Tao Sun","Linzheng Chai","Jian Yang","Yuwei Yin","Hongcheng Guo","Jiaheng Liu","Bing Wang","Liqun Yang","Zhoujun Li"]}},"tmdate":1792297480365,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731469425877,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jiaheng_Liu1"],"forum":"r4xWNfkRgv","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":194232,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1792297480365,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"r4xWNfkRgv","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a KS-based calibration method for survival models that avoids discretization and improves calibration while preserving predictive accuracy across real-world datasets and models."},"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2025 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["survival analysis","calibration","Kolmogorov-Smirnov metric","post-processing"]},"primary_area":{"value":"general_machine_learning"},"abstract":{"value":"We propose a new calibration method for survival models based on the Kolmogorov–Smirnov (KS) metric. Existing approaches—including conformal prediction, D-calibration, and Kaplan–Meier (KM)-based methods—often rely on heuristic binning or additional nonparametric estimators, which undermine their adaptability to continuous-time settings and complex model outputs. To address these limitations, we introduce a streamlined $\\textit{KS metric-based post-processing}$ framework (KSP) that calibrates survival predictions without relying on discretization or KM estimation. This design enhances flexibility and broad applicability. We conduct extensive experiments on diverse real-world datasets using a variety of survival models. Empirical results demonstrate that our method consistently improves calibration performance over existing methods while maintaining high predictive accuracy. We also provide a theoretical analysis of the KS metric and discuss extensions to in-processing settings."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\npark2025ksp,\ntitle={{KSP}: Kolmogorov-Smirnov metric-based Post-Hoc Calibration for Survival Analysis},\nauthor={Jeongho Park and Daheen Kim and Cheoljun Kim and Hyungbin Park and Sangwook Kang and Gwangsu Kim},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=39PoZNT4XX}\n}"},"title":{"value":"KSP: Kolmogorov-Smirnov metric-based Post-Hoc Calibration for Survival Analysis"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/bbd9e5261e2937c13e74561bb86350119596c69c.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"park|ksp_kolmogorovsmirnov_metricbased_posthoc_calibration_for_survival_analysis"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jeongho_Park2","~Daheen_Kim1","~Cheoljun_Kim1","~Hyungbin_Park3","~Sangwook_Kang2","~Gwangsu_Kim1"]},"authors":{"value":["Jeongho Park","Daheen Kim","Cheoljun Kim","Hyungbin Park","Sangwook Kang","Gwangsu Kim"]}},"tmdate":1783627312182,"pdate":1758217321578,"tcdate":1746971812214,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission21598/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission21598/Authors"],"forum":"39PoZNT4XX","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","number":21598,"cdate":1746971812214,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission21598/-/Full_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission21598/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1783627312182,"odate":1761705013324,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference","id":"39PoZNT4XX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2025 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Calibration","multicalibration","Venn-Abers","conformal prediction","isotonic calibration","distribution-free","epistemic uncertainty"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nlaan2025generalized,\ntitle={Generalized Venn and Venn-Abers Calibration with Applications in Conformal Prediction},\nauthor={Lars van der Laan and Ahmed Alaa},\nbooktitle={Forty-second International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=kl2SA1N03E}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Generalized Venn and Venn-Abers Calibration with Applications in Conformal Prediction"},"paperhash":{"value":"laan|generalized_venn_and_vennabers_calibration_with_applications_in_conformal_prediction"},"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce a unified framework for Venn and Venn-Abers calibration for generic prediction tasks and loss functions, including conformal prediction as a special case."},"primary_area":{"value":"general_machine_learning->everything_else"},"abstract":{"value":"Ensuring model calibration is critical for reliable prediction, yet popular distribution-free methods such as histogram binning and isotonic regression offer only asymptotic guarantees. We introduce a unified framework for Venn and Venn-Abers calibration that extends Vovk's approach beyond binary classification to a broad class of prediction tasks defined by generic loss functions. Our method transforms any perfectly in-sample calibrated predictor into a set-valued predictor that, in finite samples, outputs at least one marginally calibrated point prediction. These set predictions shrink asymptotically and converge to a conditionally calibrated prediction, capturing epistemic uncertainty. We further propose Venn multicalibration, a new approach for achieving finite-sample calibration across subpopulations. For quantile loss, our framework recovers group-conditional and multicalibrated conformal prediction as special cases and yields novel prediction intervals with quantile-conditional coverage."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://github.com/Larsvanderlaan/VennCalibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/21910c45bf4576f4b1688a6c4f1c644e405b733c.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"To be trustworthy, machine learning models must be well-calibrated—that is, when a model predicts an 80\\% chance of an event, that event should occur about 80\\% of the time. Traditional calibration methods, such as histogram binning and isotonic regression, adjust model outputs to align with observed outcomes, but they only guarantee reliable performance when applied to large datasets. We introduce a new approach that generalizes Vovk’s method—originally developed for binary classification—to a broad class of prediction problems. Specifically, our framework extends calibration to any elicitable property defined via the minimization of a loss function, including but not limited to probabilities and quantiles. Instead of producing a single point prediction, our method outputs a set of possible predictions for each input. This set is guaranteed to contain at least one prediction that is well-calibrated, even in small-sample settings. As more data becomes available, the prediction sets shrink, eventually converging to a single, conditionally calibrated prediction. Moreover, we extend this approach to ensure calibration holds across different subpopulations, ensuring fairness and reliability. Overall, our method provides a unified and practical solution for capturing and communicating uncertainty in model predictions, especially when data is limited or decisions are high-stakes."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2025/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Lars_van_der_Laan1","~Ahmed_Alaa1"]},"authors":{"value":["Lars van der Laan","Ahmed Alaa"]}},"tmdate":1753292860105,"pdate":1746105219173,"tcdate":1737391032433,"writers":["ICML.cc/2025/Conference","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission3932/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission3932/Authors"],"forum":"kl2SA1N03E","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":3932,"cdate":1737391032433,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission3932/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission3932/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1753292860105,"odate":1750231056614,"domain":"ICML.cc/2025/Conference","id":"kl2SA1N03E","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"UbiComp/ISWC Adjunct 2018"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/HUC/2018"},"paperhash":{"value":"fürst|automated_wireless_localization_data_acquisition_and_calibration_with_6dof_image_localization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jonathan_Fürst1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaifei_Chen:","~Gürkan_Solmaz2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ernö_Kovacs:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3267305.3274186"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/huc/FurstCSK18,\n  author={Jonathan Fürst and Kaifei Chen and Gürkan Solmaz and Ernö Kovacs},\n  title={Automated Wireless Localization Data Acquisition and Calibration with 6DOF Image Localization},\n  year={2018},\n  cdate={1514764800000},\n  pages={1372-1381},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3267305.3274186},\n  booktitle={UbiComp/ISWC Adjunct},\n  crossref={conf/huc/2018ap}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Radio frequency (RF) signals have been used extensively to enable (indoor) localization and proximity detection based on Received Signal Strength Indication (RSSI). However, localization systems often suffer from large data collection and calibration overhead, especially when being deployed in a new environment. RSSI fingerprinting based localization systems require the construction of a fingerprinting database. This localization data acquisition is a hindrance for the proliferation of localization systems in practice. Similarly, RSSI proximity applications require an RSSI calibration for the receiver hardware and the deployment environment. To overcome these problems, we propose the usage of visual 3D models which enable 6DOF localization and distance measurement with high accuracy. We then fuse this physical knowledge with RF data: (1) for automated acquisition of fingerprinting data and (2) easy calibration of a RF propagation model for proximity estimation."},"title":{"value":"Automated Wireless Localization Data Acquisition and Calibration with 6DOF Image Localization"},"authors":{"value":["Jonathan Fürst","Kaifei Chen","Gürkan Solmaz","Ernö Kovacs"]}},"tmdate":1739953679949,"pdate":1514764800000,"tcdate":1739953175014,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jonathan_Fuerst1"],"forum":"UkOdkq7eaU","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":329991,"cdate":1514764800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1739953679949,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UkOdkq7eaU","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICAC 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6076822/6084889/06084895.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICONAC/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"parkinson|representing_the_process_of_machine_tool_calibration_on_firstorder_logic"},"authorids":{"value":["~Simon_Parkinson1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrew_Longstaff:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrew_Crampton:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Simon_Fletcher:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:G._Allen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alan_Myers:"]},"html":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/6084895/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iconac/ParkinsonLCFAM11,\n  author={Simon Parkinson and Andrew Longstaff and Andrew Crampton and Simon Fletcher and G. Allen and Alan Myers},\n  title={Representing the process of machine tool calibration on first-order logic},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={22-27},\n  url={https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/6084895/},\n  booktitle={ICAC},\n  crossref={conf/iconac/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Machine tool calibration requires a wide range of measurement techniques that can be carried out in many different sequences. Planning a machine tool calibration is typically performed by a subject expert with a great understanding of International standards and industrial best-practice guides. However, it is often the case that the planned sequence of measurements is not the optimal. Therefore, in an attempt to improve the process, intelligent computing methods can be designed for plan suggestion. As a starting point, this paper presents a way of converting expert knowledge into first-order logic that can be expressed in the PROLOG language. It then shows how queries can be executed against the logic to construct a knowledge-base of all the different measurements that can be performed during machine tool calibration."},"title":{"value":"Representing the process of machine tool calibration on first-order logic"},"authors":{"value":["Simon Parkinson","Andrew Longstaff","Andrew Crampton","Simon Fletcher","G. Allen","Alan Myers"]}},"tmdate":1730454342907,"pdate":1293840000000,"tcdate":1730454324305,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Simon_Parkinson1"],"forum":"slWjHV2RVV","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":167080,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730454342907,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"slWjHV2RVV","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Conformal prediction","Prediction set efficiency","Representation geometry"]},"primary_area":{"value":"unsupervised, self-supervised, semi-supervised, and supervised representation learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Conformal prediction endows any classifier with a distribution-free coverage guarantee, yet under limited calibration data the guarantee is nearly vacuous: the returned prediction sets routinely grow to almost the size of the full label space, eroding the very utility they are meant to provide.  We trace this failure to a structural blind spot shared by every published conformity score: each reads the model solely through the post-softmax probability vector---a bottleneck that discards the class-conditional geometry the encoder has already computed, and which often separates the classes more cleanly than the linear head that projects them.  We show that this geometry can be handed back to the calibration pipeline at negligible cost: because every encoder already computes a hidden state alongside its logits, a simple per-class distance to training-set centroids recovers exactly the structure the softmax throws away. \\methodlong (\\method) builds on this observation---it blends the centroid distance with the familiar rank-regularised score on temperature-scaled logits, and a holdout slice of the calibration data picks the blend for each deployment---so the score leans on geometry where it helps and safely falls back to the softmax ranking where it does not.  The guarantee survives this adaptivity on one condition, a data-handling discipline: the geometric statistics must be estimated on \\emph{training} data---estimating them on the calibration set breaks exchangeability and inflates set size by $30.9\\%$.  Across $18$ cells of an LLM-style sequence-classification benchmark, \\method delivers the smallest prediction set in $5/6$ settings and the smallest average against nine published baselines, reducing average set size by $\\mathbf{44.3\\%}$ over APS, $\\mathbf{12.3\\%}$ over RAPS, and $\\mathbf{4.9\\%}$ over the strongest competing baseline, while preserving the requested marginal coverage to within $\\pm 0.02$ in every evaluated cell."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026mahalanobissmoothed,\ntitle={Mahalanobis-Smoothed Trust Conformal Prediction under Limited Calibration Data},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=aek99ufuhP},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Mahalanobis-Smoothed Trust Conformal Prediction under Limited Calibration Data"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a7061333e83558a6570027ba66e98302e5e0e407.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791230623577,"tcdate":1789582040329,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission26533/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission26533/Authors"],"forum":"aek99ufuhP","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":26533,"cdate":1789582040329,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission26533/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791230623577,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"aek99ufuhP","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks and Learning Systems"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/5962385/11151745/11038829.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"dong|a_survey_on_confidence_calibration_of_deep_learningbased_classification_models_under_class_imbalance_data"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TNNLS.2025.3565159"},"abstract":{"value":"Confidence calibration in classification models is a vital technique for accurately estimating the posterior probabilities of predicted results, which is crucial for assessing the likelihood of correct decisions in real-world applications. Class imbalance data, which biases the model’s learning and subsequently skews predicted posterior probabilities, makes confidence calibration more challenging. Especially for underrepresented classes, which are often more important and tend to have higher uncertainty, confidence calibration is more complex and essential. Unlike previous surveys that typically separately investigate confidence calibration or class imbalance, this article comprehensively investigates confidence calibration methods for deep learning-based classification models under class imbalance. First, the problem of confidence calibration under class imbalance data is outlined. Second, this article explores the impact of class imbalance data on confidence calibration in theory, providing some explanations for empirical findings in existing studies. Third, this article reviews 60 state-of-the-art confidence calibration methods under class imbalance data, divides these methods into six groups according to method differences, and systematically compares seven properties to evaluate their superiority. Then, some commonly used and emerging evaluation methodology are summarized, including public datasets and evaluation metrics. Subsequently, this article performs necessary comparative experiments to provide better guidelines and insights to the readership. Finally, we discuss several application fields and promising research directions that serve as a guideline for future studies."},"title":{"value":"A Survey on Confidence Calibration of Deep Learning-Based Classification Models Under Class Imbalance Data"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jinzong Dong"},{"fullname":"Zhaohui Jiang"},{"fullname":"Dong Pan"},{"fullname":"Zhiwen Chen"},{"fullname":"Qingyi Guan"},{"fullname":"Hongbin Zhang"},{"fullname":"Gui Gui","username":"~Gui_Gui1"},{"fullname":"Weihua Gui"}]}},"tmdate":1789092520963,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tnnls.2025.3565159"],"tcdate":1762526808964,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Gui_Gui1"],"forum":"DIX89J3kSx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":10957,"cdate":1750270241331,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789092520963,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"DIX89J3kSx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.19411v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|code_to_think_think_to_code_a_survey_on_codeenhanced_reasoning_and_reasoningdriven_code_intelligence_in_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["~Dayu_Yang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianyang_Liu_0003:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daoan_Zhang:","~Antoine_Simoulin1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoyi_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuwei_Cao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhaopu_Teng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Qian:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Grey_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiebo_Luo_0001:","~Julian_J._McAuley1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.19411"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2502-19411,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Dayu Yang and Tianyang Liu and Daoan Zhang and Antoine Simoulin and Xiaoyi Liu and Yuwei Cao and Zhaopu Teng and Xin Qian and Grey Yang and Jiebo Luo and Julian J. McAuley},\n  title={Code to Think, Think to Code: A Survey on Code-Enhanced Reasoning and Reasoning-Driven Code Intelligence in LLMs},\n  year={2025},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1738368000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2502.19411},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.19411}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In large language models (LLMs), code and reasoning reinforce each other: code offers an abstract, modular, and logic-driven structure that supports reasoning, while reasoning translates high-level goals into smaller, executable steps that drive more advanced code intelligence. In this study, we examine how code serves as a structured medium for enhancing reasoning: it provides verifiable execution paths, enforces logical decomposition, and enables runtime validation. We also explore how improvements in reasoning have transformed code intelligence from basic completion to advanced capabilities, enabling models to address complex software engineering tasks through planning and debugging. Finally, we identify key challenges and propose future research directions to strengthen this synergy, ultimately improving LLM's performance in both areas."},"title":{"value":"Code to Think, Think to Code: A Survey on Code-Enhanced Reasoning and Reasoning-Driven Code Intelligence in LLMs"},"authors":{"value":["Dayu Yang","Tianyang Liu","Daoan Zhang","Antoine Simoulin","Xiaoyi Liu","Yuwei Cao","Zhaopu Teng","Xin Qian","Grey Yang","Jiebo Luo","Julian J. McAuley"]}},"tmdate":1772232527589,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2502-19411"],"tcdate":1768501531117,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Julian_McAuley1"],"forum":"uQTTKcQ02B","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":765944,"cdate":1738368000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772232527589,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"uQTTKcQ02B","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.20170v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ahn|realtime_calibration_model_for_lowcost_sensor_in_finegrained_time_series"},"authorids":{"value":["","","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sungbok_Shin:","~Young-Duk_Seo1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.20170"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-20170,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Seokho Ahn and Hyungjin Kim and Sungbok Shin and Young-Duk Seo},\n  title={Real-time Calibration Model for Low-cost Sensor in Fine-grained Time series},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2412.20170},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.20170}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Precise measurements from sensors are crucial, but data is usually collected from low-cost, low-tech systems, which are often inaccurate. Thus, they require further calibrations. To that end, we first identify three requirements for effective calibration under practical low-tech sensor conditions. Based on the requirements, we develop a model called TESLA, Transformer for effective sensor calibration utilizing logarithmic-binned attention. TESLA uses a high-performance deep learning model, Transformers, to calibrate and capture non-linear components. At its core, it employs logarithmic binning to minimize attention complexity. TESLA achieves consistent real-time calibration, even with longer sequences and finer-grained time series in hardware-constrained systems. Experiments show that TESLA outperforms existing novel deep learning and newly crafted linear models in accuracy, calibration speed, and energy efficiency."},"title":{"value":"Real-time Calibration Model for Low-cost Sensor in Fine-grained Time series"},"authors":{"value":["Seokho Ahn","Hyungjin Kim","Sungbok Shin","Young-Duk Seo"]}},"tmdate":1762267945094,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1741358305430,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Young-Duk_Seo1"],"forum":"xd0VMSNHE1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":365072,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762267945094,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xd0VMSNHE1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/31974/34129"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AAAI/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"ahn|realtime_calibration_model_for_lowcost_sensor_in_finegrained_time_series"},"authorids":{"value":["~Seokho_Ahn1","~Hyungjin_Kim1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sungbok_Shin:","~Young-Duk_Seo1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i1.31974"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aaai/Ahn0SS25,\n  author={Seokho Ahn and Hyungjin Kim and Sungbok Shin and Young-Duk Seo},\n  title={Real-Time Calibration Model for Low-Cost Sensor in Fine-Grained Time Series},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={3-11},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i1.31974},\n  booktitle={AAAI},\n  crossref={conf/aaai/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Precise measurements from sensors are crucial, but data is usually collected from low-cost, low-tech systems, which are often inaccurate. Thus, they require further calibrations. To that end, we first identify three requirements for effective calibration under practical low-tech sensor conditions. Based on the requirements, we develop a model called TESLA, Transformer for effective sensor calibration utilizing logarithmic-binned attention. TESLA uses a high-performance deep learning model, Transformers, to calibrate and capture non-linear components. At its core, it employs logarithmic binning, to minimize attention complexity. TESLA achieves consistent real-time calibration, even with longer sequences and finer-grained time series in hardware-constrained systems. Experiments show that TESLA outperforms existing novel deep learning and newly crafted linear models in accuracy, calibration speed, and energy efficiency."},"title":{"value":"Real-Time Calibration Model for Low-Cost Sensor in Fine-Grained Time Series"},"authors":{"value":["Seokho Ahn","Hyungjin Kim","Sungbok Shin","Young-Duk Seo"]}},"tmdate":1762267679865,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/aaai/Ahn0SS25"],"tcdate":1761550535502,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hyungjin_Kim1"],"forum":"aYV1X7zA3x","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":648606,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762267679865,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"aYV1X7zA3x","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 1998"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel4/5876/15681/00727271.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/1998"},"paperhash":{"value":"malis|2d_12_visual_servoing_stability_analysis_with_respect_to_camera_calibration_errors"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ezio_Malis1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:François_Chaumette:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sylvie_Boudet:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.1998.727271"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/MalisCB98,\n  author={Ezio Malis and François Chaumette and Sylvie Boudet},\n  title={2D 1/2 visual servoing stability analysis with respect to camera calibration errors},\n  year={1998},\n  cdate={883612800000},\n  pages={691-697},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.1998.727271},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/1998}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, the robustness of a new visual servoing scheme with respect to camera calibration errors is analyzed. This scheme, called 2D 1/2 visual servoing, is based on the estimation of the partial camera displacement from the current to the desired camera poses at each iteration of the control law. Visual features and data extracted from the partial displacement allow as to design a decoupled control law controlling the six camera DOF. The necessary and sufficient conditions for local asymptotic stability in presence of camera calibration errors are easily obtained. Then, thanks to the simple structure of the system, sufficient conditions for global asymptotic stability are proposed. Finally, experimental results show the validity of our approach and its robustness not only with respect camera calibration errors but also to robot calibration errors."},"title":{"value":"2D 1/2 visual servoing stability analysis with respect to camera calibration errors"},"authors":{"value":["Ezio Malis","François Chaumette","Sylvie Boudet"]}},"tmdate":1730304664706,"pdate":883612800000,"tcdate":1730304649897,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ezio_Malis1"],"forum":"uqEPwk7vPV","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":164774,"cdate":883612800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730304664706,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"uqEPwk7vPV","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IJCNN 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9891857/9889787/09892324.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IJCNN/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"jouet|a_novel_gradient_accumulation_method_for_calibration_of_named_entity_recognition_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Grégor_Jouet:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Clément_Duhart:","~Jacopo_Staiano2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Francis_Rousseaux:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cyril_de_Runz:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IJCNN55064.2022.9892324"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ijcnn/JouetDSRR22,\n  author={Grégor Jouet and Clément Duhart and Jacopo Staiano and Francis Rousseaux and Cyril de Runz},\n  title={A Novel Gradient Accumulation Method for Calibration of Named Entity Recognition Models},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={1-8},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IJCNN55064.2022.9892324},\n  booktitle={IJCNN},\n  crossref={conf/ijcnn/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The adoption of deep learning models has brought significant performance improvements across several research fields, such as computer vision and natural language processing. However, their “black-box” nature yields the downside of poor explainability: in particular, several real-world applications require - to varying extents - reliable confidence scores associated to a model's prediction. The relation between a model's accuracy and confidence is typically referred to as calibration. In this work, we propose a novel calibration method based on gradient accumulation in conjunction with existing loss regularization techniques. Our experiments on the Named Entity Recognition task show an improvement of the performance/calibration ratio compared to the current methods."},"title":{"value":"A Novel Gradient Accumulation Method for Calibration of Named Entity Recognition Models"},"authors":{"value":["Grégor Jouet","Clément Duhart","Jacopo Staiano","Francis Rousseaux","Cyril de Runz"]}},"tmdate":1727613322601,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1727613313624,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jacopo_Staiano2"],"forum":"AXLDgIAiIK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":106237,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727613322601,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"AXLDgIAiIK","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"Under verified answer-token visibility, top-k truncation contributes nothing measurable to MCQA calibration; model x provider heterogeneity, aggregator choice, benchmark shift, and calibration-repair transfer dominate."},"venue":{"value":"SafeAI 2026"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["equivalence testing","TOST","expected calibration error","LLM calibration","multiple-choice QA","API logprobs","measurement-channel auditing"]},"abstract":{"value":"The result that surprises practitioners about top-k API truncation in forced single-token multiple-choice QA is the null result: under verified answer-token visibility the truncated and full distributions yield exactly the same calibration metrics. We provide the formal equivalence test that establishes this claim. For four-choice MCQA where confidence is renormalized over admissible answer tokens {A,B,C,D}, a short analytical argument shows that ECE, Brier, and AUROC depend on the truncation level only through whether the answer set is contained in the returned top-k. Two One-Sided Tests at a pre-specified margin Delta_ECE = 0.02 confirm the prediction empirically: across four self-hosted vLLM model-benchmark conditions, top-5 ECE is statistically equivalent to a ~99K-token reference at p_TOST < 0.001, with |Delta ECE| <= 6 x 10^-5. Three other channels move calibration measurably across the audited conditions. Across sixteen model-provider conditions ECE spans 0.066-0.564 (a 9x spread); accuracy explains 63% of the variance, and the bimodal residual outliers (LFM2-24B, DeepSeek-V3.1) do not improve under accuracy control. Across nine matched conditions in our corpus, transferred calibration repair (temperature scaling, Platt, isotonic) fit on CalProbe-167 and applied to GPQA Diamond improved target ECE in 7-8 of 9 cases per method but worsened it on at least one, with residual ECE above 0.10 on 8/9. Confidence-aggregator choice for multi-token answers shifts ECE by 0.15-0.29 without changing model outputs, an order of magnitude larger than any truncation effect we observe. The contribution is methodological: a pre-specified equivalence-testing audit protocol that locates which channel of the LLM measurement pipeline distorts calibration. Code, the CalProbe-167 stress set, and item-level manifests are released as supplementary material."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nsneiderman2026when,\ntitle={When Truncation Is Not the Bottleneck: An Equivalence-Testing Audit of {API} Logprob Calibration in Forced Multiple-Choice {QA}},\nauthor={Rob Sneiderman},\nbooktitle={Second Workshop on Safe AI},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=yqU7gOZhfU}\n}"},"title":{"value":"When Truncation Is Not the Bottleneck: An Equivalence-Testing Audit of API Logprob Calibration in Forced Multiple-Choice QA"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3c0c2d50a5c2a8547abc80fb1aae1f9b685c7c28.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"auai.org/UAI/2026/Workshop/SafeAI"},"paperhash":{"value":"sneiderman|when_truncation_is_not_the_bottleneck_an_equivalencetesting_audit_of_api_logprob_calibration_in_forced_multiplechoice_qa"},"authorids":{"value":["~Rob_Sneiderman1"]},"authors":{"value":["Rob Sneiderman"]}},"tmdate":1786363286277,"pdate":1786363282291,"tcdate":1779893849236,"writers":["auai.org/UAI/2026/Workshop/SafeAI","auai.org/UAI/2026/Workshop/SafeAI/Submission29/Authors"],"signatures":["auai.org/UAI/2026/Workshop/SafeAI/Submission29/Authors"],"forum":"yqU7gOZhfU","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":29,"cdate":1779893849236,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["auai.org/UAI/2026/Workshop/SafeAI/-/Submission","auai.org/UAI/2026/Workshop/SafeAI/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","auai.org/UAI/2026/Workshop/SafeAI/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","auai.org/UAI/2026/Workshop/SafeAI/Submission29/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","auai.org/UAI/2026/Workshop/SafeAI/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1786363286277,"odate":1786363282291,"domain":"auai.org/UAI/2026/Workshop/SafeAI","id":"yqU7gOZhfU","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.04212v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"guo|code_fingerprints_disentangled_attribution_of_llmgenerated_code"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.04212"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2603-04212,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jiaxun Guo and Ziyuan Yang and Mengyu Sun and Hui Wang and Jingfeng Lu and Yi Zhang},\n  title={Code Fingerprints: Disentangled Attribution of LLM-Generated Code},\n  year={2026},\n  month={March},\n  cdate={1772323200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2603.04212},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.04212}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The rapid adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) has transformed modern software development by enabling automated code generation at scale. While these systems improve productivity, they introduce new challenges for software governance, accountability, and compliance. Existing research primarily focuses on distinguishing machine-generated code from human-written code; however, many practical scenarios--such as vulnerability triage, incident investigation, and licensing audits--require identifying which LLM produced a given code snippet. In this paper, we study the problem of model-level code attribution, which aims to determine the source LLM responsible for generated code. Although attribution is challenging, differences in training data, architectures, alignment strategies, and decoding mechanisms introduce model-dependent stylistic and structural variations that serve as generative fingerprints. Leveraging this observation, we propose the Disentangled Code Attribution Network (DCAN), which separates Source-Agnostic semantic information from Source-Specific stylistic representations. Through a contrastive learning objective, DCAN isolates discriminative model-dependent signals while preserving task semantics, enabling multi-class attribution across models and programming languages. To support systematic evaluation, we construct the first large-scale benchmark dataset comprising code generated by four widely used LLMs (DeepSeek, Claude, Qwen, and ChatGPT) across four programming languages (Python, Java, C, and Go). Experimental results demonstrate that DCAN achieves reliable attribution performance across diverse settings, highlighting the feasibility of model-level provenance analysis in software engineering contexts. The dataset and implementation are publicly available at https://github.com/mtt500/DCAN."},"title":{"value":"Code Fingerprints: Disentangled Attribution of LLM-Generated Code"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jiaxun Guo","username":""},{"fullname":"Ziyuan Yang","username":""},{"fullname":"Mengyu Sun","username":""},{"fullname":"Hui Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Jingfeng Lu","username":""},{"fullname":"Yi Zhang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784321038959,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2603-04212"],"tcdate":1784320534316,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Jingfeng_Lu1"],"forum":"W2BS3k5NEH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":62410,"cdate":1772323200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Author_Removal"],"mdate":1784321038959,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"W2BS3k5NEH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.24938v3"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"kim|rethinking_layer_redundancy_in_large_language_models_calibration_objectives_and_search_for_depth_pruning"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.24938"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2604-24938,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Minkyu Kim and Vincent-Daniel Yun and Youngrae Kim and Youngjin Heo and Suin Cho and Seong-hun Kim and Woosang Lim and Gaeul Kwon},\n  title={Rethinking Layer Redundancy in Large Language Models: Calibration Objectives and Search for Depth Pruning},\n  year={2026},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1775001600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2604.24938},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.24938}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Depth pruning improves the inference efficiency of large language models by removing Transformer blocks. Prior work typically treats layer redundancy as an inherent structural property of pretrained networks, emphasizing importance criteria and search algorithms to identify removable layers. In this study, we empirically investigate depth pruning from a functional perspective. Evaluating representative LLM families across diverse calibration configurations and multiple search algorithms, we show that different configurations produce different pruning patterns. Furthermore, under a fixed calibration configuration, complex search algorithms yield marginal performance improvements over simple one-shot methods, converging to similar pruned subsets. Overall, our results suggest that the calibration configuration plays a substantially larger role than the choice of search algorithm in shaping pruning patterns and calibration perplexity, while contributing comparably to variance in downstream reasoning accuracy. This indicates that future pruning efforts may benefit from prioritizing the calibration configuration over search complexity."},"title":{"value":"Rethinking Layer Redundancy in Large Language Models: Calibration Objectives and Search for Depth Pruning"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Minkyu Kim","username":""},{"fullname":"Vincent-Daniel Yun","username":""},{"fullname":"Youngrae Kim","username":"~Youngrae_Kim1"},{"fullname":"Youngjin Heo","username":""},{"fullname":"Suin Cho","username":""},{"fullname":"Seong-hun Kim","username":""},{"fullname":"Woosang Lim","username":"~Woosang_Lim3"},{"fullname":"Gaeul Kwon","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1791790693490,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2604-24938"],"tcdate":1782191481334,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Youngrae_Kim2"],"forum":"OIifswgRTg","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":46069,"cdate":1775001600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1791790693490,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"OIifswgRTg","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"A montage-agnostic layer interpolating learned channel embeddings and a lightweight head turn frozen EEG transformers into cross-dataset, calibration-free segmentors for precise event segmentation, outperforming all baselines on diverse datasets."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Electroencephalography","brain-computer interface","montage invariance","foundation models","segmentation"]},"primary_area":{"value":"applications to neuroscience & cognitive science"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate segmentation and detection of events in continuous electroencephalography (EEG) signals are critical for advancing brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) and understanding neural dynamics, but generalization across diverse recording montages and datasets remains a major challenge. Traditional methods for EEG analysis have largely relied on manual segmentation or time-locked experiments, which struggle with real-world scenarios that involve irregularly timed stimuli triggering or endogeneous events from the BCI operator. Although progress has been made in segmentation tasks such as sleep staging, those approaches primarily address longer, well-defined segments and do not generalize to short-duration stimuli events, such as events typically used in BCI control. In this work, we introduce a novel montage-agnostic framework with spatial interpolation of learned channel embeddings. This approach enables high temporal resolution segmentation in continuous EEG that operates without training on the target recording montage, requiring no subject-specific calibration. Validated across diverse datasets and different paradigms spanning P300, SSVEP and motor imagery, our model consistently outperforms the original foundation models including BIOT and EEGPT, demonstrating superior cross-dataset and montage-agnostic generalization. By removing montage dependency, our framework lays the groundwork for future real-time applications in neuroscience research, clinical diagnostics, and the development of brain-computer interfaces. The code and pre-processed datasets are available at: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/BN00FFgN2H1mhYhVC10J-4505"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nma2025montageagnostic,\ntitle={Montage-Agnostic and Calibration-Free {EEG} Event Segmentation},\nauthor={Jun Ma and Tuukka Ruotsalo},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=SO4bEELWVL}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Montage-Agnostic and Calibration-Free EEG Event Segmentation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/761f0fb0adf15e3d72c99ac24cf2d22070a7d106.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"ma|montageagnostic_and_calibrationfree_eeg_event_segmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jun_Ma8","~Tuukka_Ruotsalo2"]},"authors":{"value":["Jun Ma","Tuukka Ruotsalo"]}},"tmdate":1764780548502,"tcdate":1758297310819,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19566/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19566/Authors"],"forum":"SO4bEELWVL","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":19566,"cdate":1758297310819,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19566/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1764780548502,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"SO4bEELWVL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.07618v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|proposal_distribution_calibration_for_fewshot_object_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["~Bohao_Li1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chang_Liu_0030:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mengnan_Shi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaozhong_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiangyang_Ji:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qixiang_Ye:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2212.07618"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2212-07618,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Bohao Li and Chang Liu and Mengnan Shi and Xiaozhong Chen and Xiangyang Ji and Qixiang Ye},\n  title={Proposal Distribution Calibration for Few-Shot Object Detection},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2212.07618},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2212.07618}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Adapting object detectors learned with sufficient supervision to novel classes under low data regimes is charming yet challenging. In few-shot object detection (FSOD), the two-step training paradigm is widely adopted to mitigate the severe sample imbalance, i.e., holistic pre-training on base classes, then partial fine-tuning in a balanced setting with all classes. Since unlabeled instances are suppressed as backgrounds in the base training phase, the learned RPN is prone to produce biased proposals for novel instances, resulting in dramatic performance degradation. Unfortunately, the extreme data scarcity aggravates the proposal distribution bias, hindering the RoI head from evolving toward novel classes. In this paper, we introduce a simple yet effective proposal distribution calibration (PDC) approach to neatly enhance the localization and classification abilities of the RoI head by recycling its localization ability endowed in base training and enriching high-quality positive samples for semantic fine-tuning. Specifically, we sample proposals based on the base proposal statistics to calibrate the distribution bias and impose additional localization and classification losses upon the sampled proposals for fast expanding the base detector to novel classes. Experiments on the commonly used Pascal VOC and MS COCO datasets with explicit state-of-the-art performances justify the efficacy of our PDC for FSOD. Code is available at github.com/Bohao-Lee/PDC."},"title":{"value":"Proposal Distribution Calibration for Few-Shot Object Detection"},"authors":{"value":["Bohao Li","Chang Liu","Mengnan Shi","Xiaozhong Chen","Xiangyang Ji","Qixiang Ye"]}},"tmdate":1762327567707,"pdate":1640995200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2212-07618"],"tcdate":1762327561817,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Li_Bohao1"],"forum":"zZ1GY2deDk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":657413,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762327567707,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zZ1GY2deDk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Neural Networks Learn. Syst. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/5962385/10832116/10323593.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TNN/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|proposal_distribution_calibration_for_fewshot_object_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["~Bohao_Li1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chang_Liu_0047:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mengnan_Shi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaozhong_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiangyang_Ji:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qixiang_Ye:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TNNLS.2023.3331648"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tnn/LiLSCJY25,\n  author={Bohao Li and Chang Liu and Mengnan Shi and Xiaozhong Chen and Xiangyang Ji and Qixiang Ye},\n  title={Proposal Distribution Calibration for Few-Shot Object Detection},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Neural Networks Learn. Syst.},\n  volume={36},\n  number={1},\n  pages={1911-1918},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TNNLS.2023.3331648}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Adapting object detectors learned with sufficient supervision to novel classes under low data regimes is charming yet challenging. In few-shot object detection (FSOD), the two-step training paradigm is widely adopted to mitigate the severe sample imbalance, i.e., holistic pre-training on base classes, then partial fine-tuning in a balanced setting with all classes. Since unlabeled instances are suppressed as backgrounds in the base training phase, the learned region proposal network (RPN) is prone to produce biased proposals for novel instances, resulting in dramatic performance degradation. Unfortunately, the extreme data scarcity aggravates the proposal distribution bias, hindering the region of interest (RoI) head from evolving toward novel classes. In this brief, we introduce a simple yet effective proposal distribution calibration (PDC) approach to neatly enhance the localization and classification abilities of the RoI head by recycling its localization ability endowed in base training and enriching high-quality positive samples for semantic fine-tuning. Specifically, we sample proposals based on the base proposal statistics to calibrate the distribution bias and impose additional localization and classification losses upon the sampled proposals for fast expanding the base detector to novel classes. Experiments on the commonly used Pascal VOC and MS COCO datasets with explicit state-of-the-art performances justify the efficacy of our PDC for FSOD. Code is available at github.com/Bohao-Lee/PDC."},"title":{"value":"Proposal Distribution Calibration for Few-Shot Object Detection"},"authors":{"value":["Bohao Li","Chang Liu","Mengnan Shi","Xiaozhong Chen","Xiangyang Ji","Qixiang Ye"]}},"tmdate":1762327567468,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tnn/LiLSCJY25"],"tcdate":1762327561867,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Li_Bohao1"],"forum":"HZOww0mFp9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":657414,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762327567468,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"HZOww0mFp9","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"calibration/uncertainty, robustness, optimization methods, prompting, chain-of-thought"},"keywords":{"value":["prompt optimization","large language models","reasoning"]},"languages_studied":{"value":"english"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 March Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026think,\ntitle={Think Before You Optimize: Calibration-Aware Prompt Optimization for Robust {LLM} Reasoning},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - March 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=pkMrXNGJS9},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Think Before You Optimize: Calibration-Aware Prompt Optimization for Robust LLM Reasoning"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","Data resources"]},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt optimization methods iteratively refine instructions to improve large language model performance, yet they frequently introduce regressions---an optimizer that raises accuracy on one task may confidently produce wrong answers on another. We trace this fragility to two root causes: *overconfidence amplification*, where optimization reinforces the model's existing biases, and *unconstrained regression*, where iterative refinement converges to worse prompts without safety checks. We propose CAPO (Calibration-Aware Prompt Optimization), a two-phase framework that addresses both failure modes. Phase 1 performs calibration-aware search: a calibration prefix forces the model to reconsider its initial intuitions during every evaluation, while a regression guard rejects any candidate that degrades accuracy on a held-out safety set, and convergence-based stopping halts the search before over-optimization occurs. Phase 2 selects the best calibration prefix from a small library as a decoupled post-processing step. On six BIG-Bench Hard reasoning tasks with Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, our method achieves 89.1\\% average accuracy---outperforming the strongest baseline (ACE, 73.6\\%) by +15.5 points and the unoptimized model (61.3\\%) by +27.8 points. Ablations confirm that both calibration-aware evaluation and regression guards contribute complementary gains, with benefits transferring across model families. These results demonstrate that principled architectural choices, rather than brute-force iteration, are key to reliable prompt optimization---establishing our approach as a foundation for robust LLM instruction tuning."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/949dbcac4f7d3907f2b0adbaea8dce3a7935f9ca.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"NLP Applications"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/Submission"}},"tmdate":1780804834788,"tcdate":1773740856089,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/Submission2073/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/Submission2073/Authors"],"forum":"pkMrXNGJS9","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2073,"cdate":1773740856089,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1780804834788,"odate":1780804780244,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March","id":"pkMrXNGJS9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11127273/11127223/11127688.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"yuan|largescale_uwb_anchor_calibration_and_oneshot_localization_using_gaussian_process"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shenghai_Yuan:","~Boyang_Lou2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Thien-Minh_Nguyen:","~Pengyu_Yin1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Muqing_Cao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinghang_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianping_Li_0004:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Siyu_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lihua_Xie_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA55743.2025.11127688"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/YuanLNYCXLXCX25,\n  author={Shenghai Yuan and Boyang Lou and Thien-Minh Nguyen and Pengyu Yin and Muqing Cao and Xinghang Xu and Jianping Li and Jie Xu and Siyu Chen and Lihua Xie},\n  title={Large-Scale UWB Anchor Calibration and One-Shot Localization Using Gaussian Process},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={3132-3138},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA55743.2025.11127688},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Ultra-wideband (UWB) is gaining popularity with devices like AirTags for precise home item localization but faces significant challenges when scaled to large environments like seaports. The main challenges are calibration and localization under obstructed conditions, which are common in logistics environments. Traditional calibration methods, dependent on line-of-sight (LoS), are slow, costly, and unreliable in seaports and warehouses, making large-scale localization a significant pain point in the industry. To overcome these challenges, we propose a one-shot calibration and localization framework based on UWB-LiDAR fusion. Our method uses Gaussian processes to estimate the anchor position from continuous-time LiDAR Inertial Odometry with sampled UWB ranges. This approach ensures accurate and reliable calibration with only one round of sampling in large-scale areas, i.e., $600 \\times 450 ~\\mathrm{m}^{2}$. With LoS issues, UWB-only localization can be problematic, even when anchor positions are known. We demonstrate that by applying a UWB-range filter, the search range for LiDAR loop closure descriptors is significantly reduced, improving both accuracy and speed. This concept can be applied to other loop closure detection methods, enabling cost-effective localization in large-scale warehouses and seaports. It significantly improves precision in challenging environments where the UWB-only and LiDAR-Inertial methods fail, as shown in the video https://https://youtu.be/oY8jQKdM7lU. We will open-source our datasets and calibration codes for community use."},"title":{"value":"Large-Scale UWB Anchor Calibration and One-Shot Localization Using Gaussian Process"},"authors":{"value":["Shenghai Yuan","Boyang Lou","Thien-Minh Nguyen","Pengyu Yin","Muqing Cao","Xinghang Xu","Jianping Li","Jie Xu","Siyu Chen","Lihua Xie"]}},"tmdate":1772614197667,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icra/YuanLNYCXLXCX25"],"tcdate":1762324439970,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~boyang_lou1"],"forum":"4Z9yMDVLjv","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":656572,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772614197667,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4Z9yMDVLjv","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.16880v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"yuan|largescale_uwb_anchor_calibration_and_oneshot_localization_using_gaussian_process"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shenghai_Yuan:","~Boyang_Lou2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Thien-Minh_Nguyen:","~Pengyu_Yin1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Muqing_Cao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinghang_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianping_Li_0004:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Siyu_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lihua_Xie_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.16880"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-16880,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shenghai Yuan and Boyang Lou and Thien-Minh Nguyen and Pengyu Yin and Muqing Cao and Xinghang Xu and Jianping Li and Jie Xu and Siyu Chen and Lihua Xie},\n  title={Large-Scale UWB Anchor Calibration and One-Shot Localization Using Gaussian Process},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2412.16880},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.16880}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Ultra-wideband (UWB) is gaining popularity with devices like AirTags for precise home item localization but faces significant challenges when scaled to large environments like seaports. The main challenges are calibration and localization in obstructed conditions, which are common in logistics environments. Traditional calibration methods, dependent on line-of-sight (LoS), are slow, costly, and unreliable in seaports and warehouses, making large-scale localization a significant pain point in the industry. To overcome these challenges, we propose a UWB-LiDAR fusion-based calibration and one-shot localization framework. Our method uses Gaussian Processes to estimate anchor position from continuous-time LiDAR Inertial Odometry with sampled UWB ranges. This approach ensures accurate and reliable calibration with just one round of sampling in large-scale areas, I.e., 600x450 square meter. With the LoS issues, UWB-only localization can be problematic, even when anchor positions are known. We demonstrate that by applying a UWB-range filter, the search range for LiDAR loop closure descriptors is significantly reduced, improving both accuracy and speed. This concept can be applied to other loop closure detection methods, enabling cost-effective localization in large-scale warehouses and seaports. It significantly improves precision in challenging environments where UWB-only and LiDAR-Inertial methods fall short, as shown in the video (https://youtu.be/oY8jQKdM7lU). We will open-source our datasets and calibration codes for community use."},"title":{"value":"Large-Scale UWB Anchor Calibration and One-Shot Localization Using Gaussian Process"},"authors":{"value":["Shenghai Yuan","Boyang Lou","Thien-Minh Nguyen","Pengyu Yin","Muqing Cao","Xinghang Xu","Jianping Li","Jie Xu","Siyu Chen","Lihua Xie"]}},"tmdate":1772614197614,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2412-16880"],"tcdate":1762324439950,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~boyang_lou1"],"forum":"utc93VEl7D","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":656571,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772614197614,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"utc93VEl7D","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7083369/10287676/10288387.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|robust_and_adaptive_calibration_of_uwbaided_vision_navigation_system_for_uavs"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinwen_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yiming_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yifei_Lei:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhao_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mingwei_Lv:","~Junwei_Han3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2023.3326385"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/HuLLXLH23,\n  author={Jinwen Hu and Yiming Li and Yifei Lei and Zhao Xu and Mingwei Lv and Junwei Han},\n  title={Robust and Adaptive Calibration of UWB-Aided Vision Navigation System for UAVs},\n  year={2023},\n  month={December},\n  cdate={1701388800000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={8},\n  number={12},\n  pages={8247-8254},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2023.3326385}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This letter proposes an adaptive calibration scheme that integrates Ultra-Wideband (UWB) anchor position estimation and adaptive path planning for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs). A closed-form solution, independent of the initial guess of the UWB anchor positions, is rigorously derived, in the UWB anchor position estimation algorithm. Additionally, the covariance of the localization error is provided to adaptively evaluate accuracy. Based on the derived covariance, a path planning strategy is designed for the calibration phase. This strategy enables the UAV to adaptively plan its next best sampling point by optimizing the norm of the covariance matrix, with the convergence of the norm indicating the completion of the calibration phase. Simulation and experiments are carried out to verify the proposed method. Simulations with various parameters are implemented to compare the performance of different methods. In the experiment, a prototype system consisting of an easily deployable multi-UWB anchor base and a vision navigation enabled UAV is designed and realized. The system is then applied in the performance evaluation for the entire UWB-aided vision navigation task. The results of the simulations and experiments validate the effectiveness of the proposed adaptive calibration scheme. The integration of UWB anchor position estimation, path planning, and the vision navigation system leads to improved localization accuracy, efficient calibration completion, and enhanced performance in UWB-aided vision navigation tasks."},"title":{"value":"Robust and Adaptive Calibration of UWB-Aided Vision Navigation System for UAVs"},"authors":{"value":["Jinwen Hu","Yiming Li","Yifei Lei","Zhao Xu","Mingwei Lv","Junwei Han"]}},"tmdate":1731490727756,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731487368037,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Junwei_Han3"],"forum":"Dnj40v9nHt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":217978,"cdate":1701388800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731490727756,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Dnj40v9nHt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2002.10838v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"dekel|optimal_leastsquares_solution_to_the_handeye_calibration_problem"},"authorids":{"value":["~Amit_Dekel1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Linus_Härenstam-Nielsen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sergio_Caccamo:"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.10838"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2002-10838,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Amit Dekel and Linus Härenstam-Nielsen and Sergio Caccamo},\n  title={Optimal least-squares solution to the hand-eye calibration problem},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2002.10838},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.10838}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We propose a least-squares formulation to the noisy hand-eye calibration problem using dual-quaternions, and introduce efficient algorithms to find the exact optimal solution, based on analytic properties of the problem, avoiding non-linear optimization. We further present simple analytic approximate solutions which provide remarkably good estimations compared to the exact solution. In addition, we show how to generalize our solution to account for a given extrinsic prior in the cost function. To the best of our knowledge our algorithm is the most efficient approach to optimally solve the hand-eye calibration problem."},"title":{"value":"Optimal least-squares solution to the hand-eye calibration problem"},"authors":{"value":["Amit Dekel","Linus Härenstam-Nielsen","Sergio Caccamo"]}},"tmdate":1747405313855,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1747405308554,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Amit_Dekel1"],"forum":"KUdzZHkBWj","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":516062,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747405313855,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KUdzZHkBWj","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"GraphSieve extends similarity pruning to edge addition under attack-agnostic calibration, with no significant loss to RGCN or GCN-SVD and none under an adaptive attack; exploratory ablations show the same calibration lifts the baselines."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["graph neural networks","adversarial robustness","structural poisoning attacks","graph purification","calibration","hyperparameter selection","attack-agnostic defense","homophily","pre-registered study","node classification"]},"primary_area":{"value":"learning on graphs and other geometries & topologies"},"abstract":{"value":"Structural poisoning attacks degrade node classification in graph neural networks by editing edges before training. Purification defenses respond by editing the observed graph, and their test accuracy depends on both the editing rule and the procedure that selects its hyperparameters. We study that selection when it must be made without access to the evaluation attack instances. We introduce GraphSieve, a purification defense for homophilous graphs that prunes low-similarity edges and adds high-similarity absent edges. Its attack-agnostic calibration selects configurations by mean validation accuracy over a trusted clean graph and label-blind random perturbations at prespecified severities. The same procedure calibrates GraphSieve and the purification baselines, separating calibration from graph editing. A prespecified evaluation on three citation graphs identifies gains in attacked-graph test accuracy over the undefended GCN, RGCN, and GCN-SVD, with no statistically significant loss to RGCN or GCN-SVD. An attack that adapts to the published editing criterion leaves GraphSieve ahead of the undefended GCN in 20 of 36 cells, with no loss. Against equally calibrated GCN-Jaccard, which applies the pruning rule alone, the evaluation finds both gains and losses. In exploratory ablations, calibration also lifts the baselines, raising GCN-Jaccard's mean attacked-graph accuracy over clean-only tuning by +0.44 and +4.18 percentage points on the fresh and reproduction profiles, and the benefit extends to GCN-SVD and GraphSieve. Single-severity comparisons show that a selection rule must be reported with its severity distribution. A follow-up replication, run after the prespecified evaluation with new attack graphs and model seeds, reproduces the augmentation gains in the Cora deletion-Metattack conditions, where calibration disables pruning. The gains are +1.21 and +0.93 percentage points over calibrated GCN-Jaccard at deletion budgets of 15 and 25 percent, respectively. These gains identify a replicated use case for augmentation rather than a general benefit from edge addition. Recovery diagnostics show that most added edges had never been deleted. These results motivate a common calibration protocol for purification defenses and distinguish predictive augmentation from reconstruction of the original topology."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026graphsieve,\ntitle={GraphSieve: Graph Purification via Attack-Agnostic Calibration across Perturbation Severities},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=rsNOwpAWjx},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"GraphSieve: Graph Purification via Attack-Agnostic Calibration across Perturbation Severities"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/db4cd8e352d29ff626aa9037bbaab15d03cd18cf.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791231851137,"tcdate":1789680625370,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission34658/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission34658/Authors"],"forum":"rsNOwpAWjx","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":34658,"cdate":1789680625370,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission34658/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791231851137,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"rsNOwpAWjx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote. Sens. 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/36/9633014/09406186.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TGRS/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"lu|an_automatic_radiometric_crosscalibration_method_for_wideangle_mediumresolution_multispectral_satellite_sensor_using_landsat_data"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Lu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_He_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shunlin_Liang:","~Yongjun_Zhang4"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2021.3067672"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tgrs/LuHLZ22,\n  author={Jun Lu and Tao He and Shunlin Liang and Yongjun Zhang},\n  title={An Automatic Radiometric Cross-Calibration Method for Wide-Angle Medium-Resolution Multispectral Satellite Sensor Using Landsat Data},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={60},\n  pages={1-11},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2021.3067672}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Radiometric calibration of the medium-resolution satellite data is critical for monitoring and quantifying changes in the Earth’s environment and resources. Many medium-resolution satellite sensors have irregular revisits and, sometimes, have a large difference in illumination viewing geometry compared with a reference sensor, posing a great challenge for routine cross-calibration practices. To overcome these issues, this study proposed a cross-calibration method to calibrate medium-resolution multispectral data. The Chinese Gaofen-4 (GF-4) panchromatic and multispectral sensor (PMS) data with large viewing angles were used as the test data, and Landsat-8 operational land imager (OLI) data were used as the reference data. A bidirectional reflectance distribution function (BRDF) correction method was proposed to eliminate the effects of differences in illumination viewing geometry between GF-4 and Landsat-8. The validation using concurrent image shows that the mean relative error (MRE) of cross calibration is less than 6.65%. Validation using ground measurements shows that our calibration results have an improvement of around 14.8% compared with the official released calibration coefficients. The time series cross calibration reveals that, without the requirements of simultaneous nadir observations (SNOs), our calibration activities can be carried out more often in practice. Gradual and continuous radiometric sensor degradation is identified with the monthly updated calibration coefficients, demonstrating the reliability and importance of the timely cross calibration. Besides, the cross-calibration approach does not rely on any specific calibration site, and the difference in illumination viewing geometry can be well considered. Thus, it can be easily adapted and applied to other optical satellite data."},"title":{"value":"An Automatic Radiometric Cross-Calibration Method for Wide-Angle Medium-Resolution Multispectral Satellite Sensor Using Landsat Data"},"authors":{"value":["Jun Lu","Tao He","Shunlin Liang","Yongjun Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1731483759086,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731480910060,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yongjun_Zhang4"],"forum":"ZsjMSDHzeA","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":209299,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731483759086,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ZsjMSDHzeA","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/6287639/10820123/11250616.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ACCESS/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"faustini|empirical_calibration_and_metric_differential_privacy_in_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Pedro_Faustini1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Natasha_Fernandes:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Annabelle_McIver:","~Mark_Dras1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3633551"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/FaustiniFMD25,\n  author={Pedro Faustini and Natasha Fernandes and Annabelle McIver and Mark Dras},\n  title={Empirical Calibration and Metric Differential Privacy in Language Models},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={13},\n  pages={196024-196039},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3633551}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"NLP models trained with differential privacy (DP) usually adopt the DP-SGD framework that applies a Gaussian mechanism for privacy, and privacy guarantees are often reported in terms of the privacy budget  $\\epsilon $ . The problem is that  $\\epsilon $  does not have any intrinsic meaning, and it is generally not possible to compare across variants of the framework (such as Rényi DP or Concentrated DP). Work in image processing has therefore explored how to empirically calibrate noise across frameworks using Membership Inference Attacks (MIAs). However, this kind of calibration has not been established for NLP. As the first finding of this paper, we show that MIAs offer little help in calibrating privacy, whereas reconstruction attacks are a more useful potential calibration solution. As a use case, we define a novel kind of directional privacy based on the von Mises-Fisher (VMF) distribution, a metric DP mechanism that perturbs angular distance rather than adding (isotropic) Gaussian noise, and apply this to NLP architectures. We show that, even though formal guarantees are incomparable (that is, the Gaussian mechanism’s privacy parameter  $\\epsilon $  cannot be directly linked to the VMF’s parameter), empirical privacy calibration permits a comparison and reveals that each mechanism has different areas of strength with respect to utility-privacy trade-offs."},"title":{"value":"Empirical Calibration and Metric Differential Privacy in Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Pedro Faustini","Natasha Fernandes","Annabelle McIver","Mark Dras"]}},"tmdate":1792276366653,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/access/FaustiniFMD25"],"tcdate":1768965345393,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mark_Dras1"],"forum":"WGSQjowz5d","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":773157,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1792276366653,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"WGSQjowz5d","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Ethics, Bias, and Fairness"},"keywords":{"value":["Normative–Descriptive Reasoning;Fairness Evaluation; Responsible AI; Demographic Evaluation"]},"A1_potential_risks":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"B3_data_contains_personally_identifying_info":{"value":"N/A"},"checklist_separator":{"readers":["everyone"]},"B4_data_contains_offensive_content":{"value":"N/A"},"A1_elaboration":{"value":"Section 7"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 August Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026beyond,\ntitle={Beyond Demographic Discrimination: Benchmarking Normative{\\textendash}Descriptive Calibration in {LLM}},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - August 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=WEhvEd8nhz},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Beyond Demographic Discrimination: Benchmarking Normative–Descriptive Calibration in LLM"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","Data resources"]},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in socially consequential settings, where they must distinguish between normative reasoning (what should happen) and descriptive reasoning (what is likely to happen). Existing fairness benchmarks evaluate demographic discrimination under a single reasoning objective but do not assess whether models distinguish when demographic differentiation is contextually appropriate. We introduce normative-descriptive calibration as a complementary dimension of LLM alignment, operationalized through the creation of Normative-Descriptive Calibration Benchmark (NDCB) and three evaluation metrics: Normative Engagement Rate (NER), Normative Leakage Rate (NLR), and Normative-Descriptive Separation (NDS). Evaluating six LLMs reveals substantial differences not captured by existing benchmarks; we discover that under our matched-pair framing, models exhibit substantial and consistent rank-order differences in normative–descriptive separation that are not captured by BOLD or BBQ. Absolute separation magnitudes are partly framing-dependent, but rank orderings and taxonomic classifications are robust across prompt variations. Claude Sonnet 4.6 achieves the strongest separation (average NDS = +0.55), whereas GPT-5.5 exhibits inverted separation (average NDS = -0.14) due to high normative leakage despite strong normative engagement. These findings show that fairness evaluation has conflated two distinct behaviors, faithfully \\emph{describing} disparities and improperly \\emph{prescribing} them, and establish normative–descriptive calibration as a previously unevaluated, complementary dimension  of alignment."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/9317c3acfd3d0bf36ddb533b1355afe577338264.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Ethics, Bias, and Fairness"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/August/Submission"},"D5_annotator_population":{"value":"N/A"}},"tmdate":1787830603928,"tcdate":1785260379212,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/August","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/August/Submission360/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/August/Submission360/Authors"],"forum":"WEhvEd8nhz","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":360,"cdate":1785260379212,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/August/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/August/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/August/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1787830603928,"odate":1785846543432,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/August","id":"WEhvEd8nhz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"BESC (1) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-981-95-7138-3_16.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"sun|dcsn_dynamic_calibration_and_contrastive_sharpening_for_multimodal_recommendation"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-95-7138-3_16"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/besc/SunXL25,\n  author={Jicheng Sun and Shuai Xu and Yicong Li},\n  title={DCSN: Dynamic Calibration and Contrastive Sharpening for MultiModal Recommendation},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={236-251},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-95-7138-3_16},\n  booktitle={BESC (1)},\n  crossref={conf/besc/2025-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multimodal Recommender Systems (MMRS) alleviate data sparsity and enhance item understanding by integrating auxiliary information, such as images and text, making them a prominent research direction. However, they face two limitations. Traditional models typically statically concatenate an item’s modal and behavioral features, restricting the effective utilization of its key physical attributes for improving recommendation accuracy. Additionally, graph propagation can induce representation over-smoothing; after several rounds of information aggregation, the representations of modally-similar items homogenize. This process masks critical personalized features that attract specific users, thereby diminishing the discriminative power necessary for precise recommendations. To address these limitations, we propose a novel framework for multimodal recommendation, the Dual Calibration and Sharpening Network (DCSN). This framework operates on a two-stage calibration-sharpening paradigm to optimize feature fusion and representation quality. For calibration, DCSN employs a collaborative interaction gating module where an item’s physical attributes serve as dynamic contexts to reshape its core behavioral representation, thus overcoming static fusion limitations. To counteract representation over-smoothing, the sharpening stage introduces a Decoupled Modal Contrastive Control (DMCC) module. This module utilizes a combined contrastive and reconstruction loss to refine the modal representations, maximizing their discriminative power while enhancing robustness. Extensive experiments conducted on two public datasets validate the effectiveness of our method."},"title":{"value":"DCSN: Dynamic Calibration and Contrastive Sharpening for MultiModal Recommendation"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jicheng Sun","username":""},{"fullname":"Shuai Xu","username":"~Shuai_Xu3"},{"fullname":"Yicong Li","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784603885122,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/besc/SunXL25"],"tcdate":1784603879961,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Shuai_Xu3"],"forum":"SBwWswsZYF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":78750,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784603885122,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"SBwWswsZYF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SEA @ NeurIPS 2025 Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d91aa9a8e3eb5236b22b1e010d2fcf734989adec.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Uncertainty Estimation","Calibration","Large Language Models"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SEA"},"paperhash":{"value":"tao|revisiting_uncertainty_estimation_and_calibration_of_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Linwei_Tao2","~Yi-Fan_Yeh1","~Minjing_Dong1","~Tao_Huang5","~Jialin_Yu2","~Philip_Torr1","~Chang_Xu4"]},"archival_option":{"value":"The authors of this submission do *not* want it to appear in the archival proceedings."},"abstract":{"value":"As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in high-stakes applications, robust uncertainty estimation is essential for ensuring the safe and trustworthy deployment of LLMs. We present the most comprehensive study to date of uncertainty estimation in LLMs, evaluating 80 models spanning open- and closed-source families, dense and Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures, reasoning and non-reasoning modes, quantization variants and parameter scales from 0.6B to 671B. Focusing on three representative black-box single-pass methods, including token probability-based uncertainty (TPU), numerical verbal uncertainty (NVU), and linguistic verbal uncertainty (LVU), we systematically evaluate uncertainty calibration and selective classification using the challenging MMLU-Pro benchmark, which covers both reasoning-intensive and knowledge-based tasks. Our results show that LVU consistently outperforms TPU and NVU, offering stronger calibration and discrimination while being more interpretable. We also find that high accuracy does not imply reliable uncertainty, and that model scale, post-training, reasoning ability and quantization all influence estimation performance. Notably, LLMs exhibit better uncertainty estimates on reasoning tasks than on knowledge-heavy ones, and good calibration does not necessarily translate to effective error ranking. These findings highlight the need for multi-perspective evaluation and position LVU as a practical tool for improving the reliability of LLMs in real-world settings."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\ntao2025revisiting,\ntitle={Revisiting Uncertainty Estimation and Calibration of Large Language Models},\nauthor={Linwei Tao and Yi-Fan Yeh and Minjing Dong and Tao Huang and Jialin Yu and Philip Torr and Chang Xu},\nbooktitle={Workshop on Scaling Environments for Agents},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Q9CreVjHH7}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Revisiting Uncertainty Estimation and Calibration of Large Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Linwei Tao","Yi-Fan Yeh","Minjing Dong","Tao Huang","Jialin Yu","Philip Torr","Chang Xu"]}},"tmdate":1761648790895,"pdate":1759041170246,"tcdate":1756636917733,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SEA","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SEA/Submission68/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SEA/Submission68/Authors"],"forum":"Q9CreVjHH7","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":68,"cdate":1756636917733,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SEA/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SEA/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SEA/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SEA/Submission68/-/Revision"],"mdate":1761648790895,"odate":1759977491097,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SEA","id":"Q9CreVjHH7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2021/papers/Xu_Wide-Baseline_Multi-Camera_Calibration_Using_Person_Re-Identification_CVPR_2021_paper.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|widebaseline_multicamera_calibration_using_person_reidentification"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yan_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu-Jhe_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinshuo_Weng:","~Kris_Kitani2"]},"html":{"value":"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2021/html/Xu_Wide-Baseline_Multi-Camera_Calibration_Using_Person_Re-Identification_CVPR_2021_paper.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/XuLWK21,\n  author={Yan Xu and Yu-Jhe Li and Xinshuo Weng and Kris Kitani},\n  title={Wide-Baseline Multi-Camera Calibration Using Person Re-Identification},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={13134-13143},\n  url={https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2021/html/Xu_Wide-Baseline_Multi-Camera_Calibration_Using_Person_Re-Identification_CVPR_2021_paper.html},\n  booktitle={CVPR},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We address the problem of estimating the 3D pose of a network of cameras for large-environment wide-baseline scenarios, e.g., cameras for construction sites, sports stadiums, and public spaces. This task is challenging since detecting and matching the same 3D keypoint observed from two very different camera views is difficult, making standard structure-from-motion (SfM) pipelines inapplicable. In such circumstances, treating people in the scene as \"keypoints\" and associating them across different camera views can be an alternative method for obtaining correspondences. Based on this intuition, we propose a method that uses ideas from person re-identification (re-ID) for wide-baseline camera calibration. Our method first employs a re-ID method to associate human bounding boxes across cameras, then converts bounding box correspondences to point correspondences, and finally solves for camera pose using multi-view geometry and bundle adjustment. Since our method does not require specialized calibration targets except for visible people, it applies to situations where frequent calibration updates are required. We perform extensive experiments on datasets captured from scenes of different sizes, camera settings (indoor and outdoor), and human activities (walking, playing basketball, construction). Experiment results show that our method achieves similar performance to standard SfM methods relying on manually labeled point correspondences."},"title":{"value":"Wide-Baseline Multi-Camera Calibration Using Person Re-Identification"},"authors":{"value":["Yan Xu","Yu-Jhe Li","Xinshuo Weng","Kris Kitani"]}},"tmdate":1729789409754,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1729789348684,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kris_M._Kitani1"],"forum":"0l4P1bioyK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":159693,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1729789409754,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0l4P1bioyK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.08568v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|widebaseline_multicamera_calibration_using_person_reidentification"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yan_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu-Jhe_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinshuo_Weng:","~Kris_Kitani2"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08568"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2104-08568,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yan Xu and Yu-Jhe Li and Xinshuo Weng and Kris Kitani},\n  title={Wide-Baseline Multi-Camera Calibration using Person Re-Identification},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2104.08568},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08568}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We address the problem of estimating the 3D pose of a network of cameras for large-environment wide-baseline scenarios, e.g., cameras for construction sites, sports stadiums, and public spaces. This task is challenging since detecting and matching the same 3D keypoint observed from two very different camera views is difficult, making standard structure-from-motion (SfM) pipelines inapplicable. In such circumstances, treating people in the scene as \"keypoints\" and associating them across different camera views can be an alternative method for obtaining correspondences. Based on this intuition, we propose a method that uses ideas from person re-identification (re-ID) for wide-baseline camera calibration. Our method first employs a re-ID method to associate human bounding boxes across cameras, then converts bounding box correspondences to point correspondences, and finally solves for camera pose using multi-view geometry and bundle adjustment. Since our method does not require specialized calibration targets except for visible people, it applies to situations where frequent calibration updates are required. We perform extensive experiments on datasets captured from scenes of different sizes, camera settings (indoor and outdoor), and human activities (walking, playing basketball, construction). Experiment results show that our method achieves similar performance to standard SfM methods relying on manually labeled point correspondences."},"title":{"value":"Wide-Baseline Multi-Camera Calibration using Person Re-Identification"},"authors":{"value":["Yan Xu","Yu-Jhe Li","Xinshuo Weng","Kris Kitani"]}},"tmdate":1729789406062,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1729789348632,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kris_M._Kitani1"],"forum":"drxLdlWKcE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":159687,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1729789406062,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"drxLdlWKcE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Empir. Softw. Eng. 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10664-015-9421-5.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ESE/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"niu|learning_to_rank_code_examples_for_code_search_engines"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haoran_Niu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Iman_Keivanloo:","~Ying_Zou4"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-015-9421-5"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ese/NiuKZ17,\n  author={Haoran Niu and Iman Keivanloo and Ying Zou},\n  title={Learning to rank code examples for code search engines},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  journal={Empir. Softw. Eng.},\n  volume={22},\n  number={1},\n  pages={259-291},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-015-9421-5}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Source code examples are used by developers to implement unfamiliar tasks by learning from existing solutions. To better support developers in finding existing solutions, code search engines are designed to locate and rank code examples relevant to user’s queries. Essentially, a code search engine provides a ranking schema, which combines a set of ranking features to calculate the relevance between a query and candidate code examples. Consequently, the ranking schema places relevant code examples at the top of the result list. However, it is difficult to determine the configurations of the ranking schemas subjectively. In this paper, we propose a code example search approach that applies a machine learning technique to automatically train a ranking schema. We use the trained ranking schema to rank candidate code examples for new queries at run-time. We evaluate the ranking performance of our approach using a corpus of over 360,000 code snippets crawled from 586 open-source Android projects. The performance evaluation study shows that the learning-to-rank approach can effectively rank code examples, and outperform the existing ranking schemas by about 35.65 % and 48.42 % in terms of normalized discounted cumulative gain (NDCG) and expected reciprocal rank (ERR) measures respectively."},"title":{"value":"Learning to rank code examples for code search engines"},"authors":{"value":["Haoran Niu","Iman Keivanloo","Ying Zou"]}},"tmdate":1753060427735,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1753060372019,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ying_Zou4"],"forum":"QJIaJ0Z3iU","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":578186,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753060427735,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"QJIaJ0Z3iU","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We reconfigure a transformer as a probability path, modeling each block probabilistically, with a unified model propagating representation uncertainty throughout the network."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Uncertainty calibration","Transformer"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/ea2c41cb73f594ea3fff64d7cb86f933e8b8fed3.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Uncertainty calibration in pre-trained transformers is critical for their reliable deployment in risk-sensitive applications. Yet, most existing pre-trained transformers do not have a principled mechanism for uncertainty propagation through their feature transformation stack. In this work, we propose a diffusion-inspired reconfiguration of transformers in which each feature transformation block is modeled as a probabilistic mapping. Composing these probabilistic mappings reveals a probability path that mimics the structure of a diffusion process, transporting data mass from the input distribution to the pre-trained feature distribution. This probability path can then be recompiled on a diffusion process with a unified transition model to enable principled propagation of representation uncertainty throughout the pre-trained model’s architecture while maintaining its original predictive performance. Empirical results across a variety of vision and language benchmarks demonstrate that our method achieves superior calibration and predictive accuracy compared to existing uncertainty-aware transformers."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026diffusioninspired,\ntitle={Diffusion-Inspired Reconfiguration of Transformers for Uncertainty Calibration},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=xv9YB72Qqh},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Diffusion-Inspired Reconfiguration of Transformers for Uncertainty Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8bccf7d85fd22e130b82ac76e45ad74620196f42.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791234852111,"tcdate":1789791660771,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission53927/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission53927/Authors"],"forum":"xv9YB72Qqh","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":53927,"cdate":1789791660771,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission53927/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791234852111,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"xv9YB72Qqh","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.08920v2"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"dao|diffusioninspired_reconfiguration_of_transformers_for_uncertainty_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.08920"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2602-08920,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Manh Cuong Dao and Quang Hung Pham and Phi Le Nguyen and Truong Thao Nguyen and Bryan Kian Hsiang Low and Trong Nghia Hoang},\n  title={Diffusion-Inspired Reconfiguration of Transformers for Uncertainty Calibration},\n  year={2026},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1769904000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2602.08920},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.08920}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Uncertainty calibration in pre-trained transformers is critical for their reliable deployment in risk-sensitive applications. Yet, most existing pre-trained transformers do not have a principled mechanism for uncertainty propagation through their feature transformation stack. In this work, we propose a diffusion-inspired reconfiguration of transformers in which each feature transformation block is modeled as a probabilistic mapping. Composing these probabilistic mappings reveals a probability path that mimics the structure of a diffusion process, transporting data mass from the input distribution to the pre-trained feature distribution. This probability path can then be recompiled on a diffusion process with a unified transition model to enable principled propagation of representation uncertainty throughout the pre-trained model's architecture while maintaining its original predictive performance. Empirical results across a variety of vision and language benchmarks demonstrate that our method achieves superior calibration and predictive accuracy compared to existing uncertainty-aware transformers."},"title":{"value":"Diffusion-Inspired Reconfiguration of Transformers for Uncertainty Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Manh Cuong Dao","username":"~Manh_Cuong_Dao1"},{"fullname":"Quang Hung Pham","username":""},{"fullname":"Phi Le Nguyen","username":""},{"fullname":"Truong Thao Nguyen","username":""},{"fullname":"Bryan Kian Hsiang Low","username":""},{"fullname":"Trong Nghia Hoang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784652441627,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2602-08920"],"tcdate":1784652436077,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Manh_Cuong_Dao1"],"forum":"abbSF8h9qK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":85598,"cdate":1769904000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784652441627,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"abbSF8h9qK","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"large language model reasoning, conformal prediction, calibration"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2025 May Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025thought,\ntitle={Thought calibration: Efficient and confident test-time scaling},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=qRpfyod3Nt},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Thought calibration: Efficient and confident test-time scaling"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Approaches low compute settings-efficiency","Theory"]},"abstract":{"value":"Reasoning large language models achieve impressive test-time scaling by thinking for longer, but this performance gain comes at significant compute cost. Directly limiting test-time budget hurts overall performance, but not all problems are equally difficult. We propose thought calibration to decide dynamically when thinking can be terminated. To calibrate our decision rule, we view a language model's growing body of thoughts as a nested sequence of reasoning trees, where the goal is to identify the point at which novel reasoning plateaus. We realize this framework through lightweight probes that operate on top of the language model's hidden representations, which are informative of both the reasoning structure and overall consistency of response. Based on three reasoning language models and four datasets, thought calibration preserves model performance with up to a 60% reduction in thinking tokens on in-distribution data, and up to 20% in out-of-distribution data."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ef0119d888ae7b1767ca93a744fcbd2beea64367.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Machine Learning for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782962174875,"tcdate":1747702109358,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission4505/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission4505/Authors"],"forum":"qRpfyod3Nt","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4505,"cdate":1747702109358,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Desk_Reject_Verification_Flag","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782962174875,"odate":1753766020949,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","id":"qRpfyod3Nt","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose thought calibration to decide confidently and dynamically when thinking can be terminated for efficient reasoning."},"venue":{"value":"ICML 2025 R2-FM Workshop Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/4f91713fea06dc6a6f32c1df90329a7eb96d1273.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["large language model reasoning","conformal prediction","calibration"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM"},"paperhash":{"value":"wu|thought_calibration_efficient_and_confident_testtime_scaling"},"authorids":{"value":["~Menghua_Wu1","~Cai_Zhou2","~Stephen_Bates1","~Tommi_Jaakkola1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Reasoning large language models achieve impressive test-time scaling by thinking for longer, but this performance gain comes at significant compute cost. Directly limiting test-time budget hurts overall performance, but not all problems are equally difficult. We propose thought calibration to decide dynamically when thinking can be terminated. To calibrate our decision rule, we view a language model's growing body of thoughts as a nested sequence of reasoning trees, where the goal is to identify the point at which novel reasoning plateaus. We realize this framework through lightweight probes that operate on top of the language model's hidden representations, which are informative of both the reasoning structure and overall consistency of response. Based on three reasoning language models and four datasets, thought calibration preserves model performance with up to a 60% reduction in thinking tokens on in-distribution data, and up to 20% in out-of-distribution data."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nwu2025thought,\ntitle={Thought calibration: Efficient and confident test-time scaling},\nauthor={Menghua Wu and Cai Zhou and Stephen Bates and Tommi Jaakkola},\nbooktitle={ICML 2025 Workshop on Reliable and Responsible Foundation Models},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=pgF6VBGkN8}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Thought calibration: Efficient and confident test-time scaling"},"authors":{"value":["Menghua Wu","Cai Zhou","Stephen Bates","Tommi Jaakkola"]}},"tmdate":1751335846209,"pdate":1751335657003,"tcdate":1747706934621,"writers":["ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/Submission16/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/Submission16/Authors"],"forum":"pgF6VBGkN8","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":16,"cdate":1747706934621,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/-/Edit"],"mdate":1751335846209,"odate":1751335846191,"domain":"ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM","id":"pgF6VBGkN8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|selfcalibrating_vicinal_risk_minimisation_for_model_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jiawei_Liu10"]},"html":{"value":"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2024/papers/Liu_Self-Calibrating_Vicinal_Risk_Minimisation_for_Model_Calibration_CVPR_2024_paper.pdf"},"abstract":{"value":"Model calibration measuring the alignment between the prediction accuracy and model confidence is an important metric reflecting model trustworthiness. Existing dense binary classification methods without proper regularisation of model confidence are prone to being over-confident. To calibrate Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) we propose a Self-Calibrating Vicinal Risk Minimisation (SCVRM) that explores the vicinity space of labeled data where vicinal images that are farther away from labeled images adopt the groundtruth label with decreasing label confidence. We prove that in the logistic regression problem SCVRM can be seen as a Vicinal Risk Minimisation plus a regularisation term that penalises the over-confident predictions. In practical implementation SCVRM is approximated using Monte Carlo sampling that samples additional augmented training images and labels from the vicinal distributions. Experimental results demonstrate that SCVRM can significantly enhance model calibration for different dense classification tasks on both in-distribution and out-of-distribution data."},"title":{"value":"Self-Calibrating Vicinal Risk Minimisation for Model Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Jiawei Liu"]}},"tmdate":1721694001964,"pdate":1718583508863,"tcdate":1721694001964,"writers":["~Jiawei_Liu10"],"signatures":["~Jiawei_Liu10"],"forum":"x0ssGO0wnS","license":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","number":27334,"cdate":1721694001964,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1721694001964,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"x0ssGO0wnS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/41117/45078"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"pandey|refine_and_align_confidence_calibration_through_multiagent_interaction_in_vqa"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v40i44.41117"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aaai/PandeyBJHDV26,\n  author={Ayush Pandey and Jai Bardhan and Ishita Jain and Ramya S. Hebbalaguppe and Rohan Raju Dhanakshirur and Lovekesh Vig},\n  title={Refine and Align: Confidence Calibration Through Multi-Agent Interaction in VQA},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={37810-37819},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v40i44.41117},\n  booktitle={AAAI},\n  crossref={conf/aaai/2026}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In the context of Visual Question Answering (VQA) and Agentic AI, calibration refers to how closely an AI system's confidence in its answers reflects their actual correctness. This aspect becomes especially important when such systems operate autonomously and must make decisions under visual uncertainty. While modern VQA systems, powered by advanced vision-language models (VLMs), are increasingly used in high-stakes domains like medical diagnostics and autonomous navigation due to their improved accuracy, the reliability of their confidence estimates remains under-examined. Particularly, these systems often produce overconfident responses. To address this, we introduce AlignVQA, a debate-based multi-agent framework, in which diverse specialized VLM -- each following distinct prompting strategies -- generate candidate answers and then engage in two-stage interaction: generalist agents critique, refine and aggregate these proposals. This debate process yields confidence estimates that more accurately reflect the model’s true predictive performance. We find that more calibrated specialized agents produce better aligned confidences. Furthermore, we introduce a novel differentiable calibration-aware loss function called AlignCal designed to fine-tune the specialized agents by minimizing an upper bound on the calibration error. This objective explicitly improves the fidelity of each agent’s confidence estimates. Empirical results across multiple benchmark VQA datasets substantiate the efficacy of our approach, demonstrating substantial reductions in calibration discrepancies."},"title":{"value":"Refine and Align: Confidence Calibration Through Multi-Agent Interaction in VQA"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Ayush Pandey","username":""},{"fullname":"Jai Bardhan","username":""},{"fullname":"Ishita Jain","username":""},{"fullname":"Ramya S. Hebbalaguppe","username":""},{"fullname":"Rohan Raju Dhanakshirur","username":"~Rohan_Raju_Dhanakshirur1"},{"fullname":"Lovekesh Vig","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1790344142706,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/aaai/PandeyBJHDV26"],"tcdate":1790344130608,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Rohan_Raju_Dhanakshirur1"],"forum":"MqOzYaknDx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":156498,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1790344142706,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"MqOzYaknDx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2026"},"abstract":{"value":"The conventional checkerboard-based calibration for standard cameras faces fundamental limitations when applied to bio-inspired event cameras. Specifically, this stems from two challenges: (i) Events are triggered asynchronously at different timestamps along motion trajectories. If we accumulate them directly on the image plane, it causes temporal misalignment and produces blurred edges. (ii) Checkerboard corners on event cameras show near-zero event occurrence at the corner itself. This hinders reliable corner localization and makes calibration difficult. To address these issues, we present a novel calibration framework that directly detects checkerboard corners from event data without learning-based grayscale image reconstruction. We first mathematically analyze the absence of events at corner points. Based on this fact, we then leverage edge-driven event cues to initialize corner positions. Using the near-zero event occurrence at checkerboard corners, we gradually refine the estimated corner toward low event-density regions, achieving sub-pixel accuracy. Furthermore, we extend the corner detection to fiducial markers such as AprilTag, resulting in reliable detection even under partial visibility or occlusion. Evaluations on self-collected and public data demonstrate reliable checkerboard corner detection and stable camera calibration. Additional information is available at the following link: https://vision3d-lab.github.io/corner2tag/."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nryu2026from,\ntitle={From Corners to Fiducial Tags: Revisiting Checkerboard Calibration for Event Cameras},\nauthor={Taehun Ryu and Changwoo Kang and Kyungdon Joo},\nbooktitle={Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=cDSyQvOtmP}\n}"},"title":{"value":"From Corners to Fiducial Tags: Revisiting Checkerboard Calibration for Event Cameras"},"pdf":{"value":"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2026/papers/Ryu_From_Corners_to_Fiducial_Tags_Revisiting_Checkerboard_Calibration_for_Event_CVPR_2026_paper.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"ryu|from_corners_to_fiducial_tags_revisiting_checkerboard_calibration_for_event_cameras"},"authorids":{"value":["~Taehun_Ryu1","~Changwoo_Kang1","~Kyungdon_Joo3"]},"authors":{"value":["Taehun Ryu","Changwoo Kang","Kyungdon Joo"]}},"tmdate":1789656651611,"pdate":1789656438333,"tcdate":1765222192502,"writers":["thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference","thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference/Submission39205/Authors"],"signatures":["thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference/Submission39205/Authors"],"forum":"cDSyQvOtmP","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":39205,"cdate":1765222192502,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference/-/Submission","thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference/Submission39205/-/Full_Submission","thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference/Submission39205/-/Supplementary_Material","thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference/-/Edit","thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference/-/Compute_Flag"],"mdate":1789656651611,"odate":1789656438333,"domain":"thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Conference","id":"cDSyQvOtmP","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose Online Reasoning Calibration (ORCA), which adopts the TTT framework in calibration for generalizable and controllable LLM reasoning"},"venue":{"value":"COLM 2026"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the COLM Code of Ethics on https://colmweb.org/CoE.html"},"LLM_usage":{"value":"In accordance with the Policy on the use of Large Language Models in the Call for Papers https://colmweb.org/cfp.html, I certify that my submission discloses any substantive use of LLMs in the research and paper-writing process."},"keywords":{"value":["Uncertainty Quantification","Conformal Prediction","LLM Reasoning","Test-Time Training","Generalizable Calibration"]},"abstract":{"value":"While test-time scaling has enabled large language models to solve highly difficult tasks, state-of-the-art results come at exorbitant compute costs.\nThese inefficiencies can be attributed to the miscalibration of post-trained language models, and the lack of calibration in popular sampling techniques.\nHere, we present Online Reasoning Calibration (ORCA), a framework for calibrating the sampling process that draws upon conformal prediction and test-time training.\nSpecifically, we introduce a meta-learning procedure that updates the calibration module for each input.\nThis allows us to provide valid confidence estimates under distributional shift, e.g. in thought patterns that occur across different stages of reasoning, or in prompt distributions between model development and deployment.\nORCA not only provides theoretical guarantees on conformal risks, but also empirically shows higher efficiency and generalization across different reasoning tasks. At risk level $\\delta{=}0.1$, ORCA improves Qwen2.5-32B efficiency on in-distribution tasks with savings up to 47.5\\% with supervised labels and 40.7\\% with self-consistency labels. Under zero-shot out-of-domain settings, it improves MATH-500 savings from 24.8\\% of the static calibration baseline to 67.0\\% while maintaining a low empirical error rate, and the same trend holds across model families and downstream benchmarks."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhou2026online,\ntitle={Online Reasoning Calibration: Test-Time Training Enables Generalizable Conformal {LLM} Reasoning},\nauthor={Cai Zhou and Zekai Wang and Menghua Wu and Qianyu Julie Zhu and Flora C. Shi and Chenyu Wang and Ashia C. Wilson and Tommi Jaakkola and Stephen Bates},\nbooktitle={Third Conference on Language Modeling},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=QG88bWNHT0}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Online Reasoning Calibration: Test-Time Training Enables Generalizable Conformal LLM Reasoning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c78a1b58b284785bb448a16a827d4449784956f6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|online_reasoning_calibration_testtime_training_enables_generalizable_conformal_llm_reasoning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Cai_Zhou2","~Zekai_Wang1","~Menghua_Wu1","~Qianyu_Julie_Zhu1","~Flora_C._Shi1","~Chenyu_Wang7","~Ashia_C._Wilson1","~Tommi_Jaakkola1","~Stephen_Bates1"]},"author_guide":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://colmweb.org/AuthorGuide.html"},"authors":{"value":["Cai Zhou","Zekai Wang","Menghua Wu","Qianyu Julie Zhu","Flora C. Shi","Chenyu Wang","Ashia C. Wilson","Tommi Jaakkola","Stephen Bates"]}},"tmdate":1787537181678,"pdate":1783543405211,"tcdate":1774589506049,"writers":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission3162/Authors"],"signatures":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission3162/Authors"],"forum":"QG88bWNHT0","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":3162,"cdate":1774589506049,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission3162/-/Full_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Edit","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission3162/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1787537181678,"odate":1787537181667,"domain":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","id":"QG88bWNHT0","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We present that off-the-shelf PRMs are often poorly calibrated. To this end, we introduce a quantile-regression calibration that aligns their outputs with success probabilities. We show calibration unlocks instance-adaptive inference-time scaling."},"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2025 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["process reward model","calibration","inference-time-scaling","instance-adaptive scaling"]},"primary_area":{"value":"deep_learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Process reward models (PRMs) play a central role in guiding inference-time scaling algorithms for large language models (LLMs).\nHowever, we observe that even state-of-the-art PRMs can be poorly calibrated. \nSpecifically, they tend to overestimate the success probability that a partial reasoning step will lead to a correct final answer, particularly when smaller LLMs are used to complete the reasoning trajectory.\nTo address this, we present a calibration approach—performed via quantile regression—that adjusts PRM outputs to better align with true success probabilities. \nLeveraging these calibrated success estimates and their associated confidence bounds, we introduce an instance-adaptive scaling (IAS) framework that dynamically adjusts the compute budget based on the estimated likelihood that a partial reasoning trajectory will yield a correct final answer.\nUnlike conventional methods that allocate a fixed number of reasoning trajectories per query, this approach adapts to each instance and reasoning step when using our calibrated PRMs. Experiments on mathematical reasoning benchmarks show that (i) our PRM calibration method achieves small calibration error, outperforming the baseline methods, (ii) calibration is crucial for enabling effective IAS, and (iii) the proposed IAS strategy reduces inference costs while maintaining final answer accuracy, utilizing less compute on more confident problems as desired."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\npark2025know,\ntitle={Know What You Don't Know: Uncertainty Calibration of Process Reward Models},\nauthor={Young-Jin Park and Kristjan Greenewald and Kaveh Alim and Hao Wang and Navid Azizan},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=hzMkfIrdDT}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Know What You Don't Know: Uncertainty Calibration of Process Reward Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/606f3ae7036f51301cf4b6ea9a67d121af6af571.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"park|know_what_you_dont_know_uncertainty_calibration_of_process_reward_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Young-Jin_Park1","~Kristjan_Greenewald1","~Kaveh_Alim1","~Hao_Wang22","~Navid_Azizan1"]},"authors":{"value":["Young-Jin Park","Kristjan Greenewald","Kaveh Alim","Hao Wang","Navid Azizan"]}},"tmdate":1783627333121,"pdate":1758217207225,"tcdate":1746934286223,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission18992/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission18992/Authors"],"forum":"hzMkfIrdDT","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":18992,"cdate":1746934286223,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission18992/-/Full_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission18992/-/Supplementary_Material","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission18992/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1783627333121,"odate":1761704973693,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference","id":"hzMkfIrdDT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["Federated Continual Learning","Asynchronous Learning","Cross-task Calibration"]},"primary_area":{"value":"other topics in machine learning (i.e., none of the above)"},"abstract":{"value":"Federated Continual Learning (FCL) aims to empower distributed devices to learn a sequence of tasks over time. However, existing FCL research largely relies on the impractical assumption of synchronous new task arrival. This overlooks the reality of asynchronous user behavior and system latencies, forcing more efficient clients to endure costly inactivity. The practical necessity of an asynchronous method gives rise to Asynchronous Federated Continual Learning (AFCL). The server constantly receives a mixture of updates from clients at different time steps, leading to a catastrophic task drift that corrupts the global model and prevents effective learning. In this paper, we introduce a novel Cross-task Calibration framework called **C$^2$-AFCL** that is the first to tackle task drift at a semantic level within an Asynchronous FCL setting. Its core is a two-stage orthogonal calibration mechanism. First, intra-client calibration uses task-aware caches to mitigate variance from local client drift. Second, and more critically, inter-task interference calibration dynamically estimates an interference subspace from historical task knowledge. New updates are orthogonally projected to isolate and remove components that conflict with this subspace, preserving previous knowledge while learning new tasks. \nExtensive experiments show that C$^2$-AFCL significantly outperforms existing methods, demonstrating robust and efficient learning in dynamic federated environments."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nli2026cafcl,\ntitle={C\\${\\textasciicircum}2\\$-{AFCL}: Cross-task Calibration for Asynchronous Federated Continual Learning},\nauthor={Yichen Li and Haozhao Wang and Hang Su and Yulong Li and Xiaoquan Yi and Yankai Jiang and Abdalla Swikir and Imran Razzak and Ruixuan Li},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=CDwLWmVl9O}\n}"},"title":{"value":"C$^2$-AFCL: Cross-task Calibration for Asynchronous Federated Continual Learning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/150cfa71624a333c224e8a4ce7a442011951ed4e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|c^2afcl_crosstask_calibration_for_asynchronous_federated_continual_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yichen_Li5","~Haozhao_Wang1","~Hang_Su15","~Yulong_Li7","~Xiaoquan_Yi1","~Yankai_Jiang1","~Abdalla_Swikir2","~Imran_Razzak2","~Ruixuan_Li1"]},"authors":{"value":["Yichen Li","Haozhao Wang","Hang Su","Yulong Li","Xiaoquan Yi","Yankai Jiang","Abdalla Swikir","Imran Razzak","Ruixuan Li"]}},"tmdate":1770805101593,"tcdate":1758294743786,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19242/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19242/Authors"],"forum":"CDwLWmVl9O","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":19242,"cdate":1758294743786,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission19242/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770805101593,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"CDwLWmVl9O","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10801246/10801290/10802098.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"qu|v2icalib_a_novel_calibration_approach_for_collaborative_vehicle_and_infrastructure_lidar_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["~Qianxin_Qu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yijin_Xiong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guipeng_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Wu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaohan_Gao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Gao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hanyu_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shichun_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guoying_Zhang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS58592.2024.10802098"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/QuXZWGGLGZ24,\n  author={Qianxin Qu and Yijin Xiong and Guipeng Zhang and Xin Wu and Xiaohan Gao and Xin Gao and Hanyu Li and Shichun Guo and Guoying Zhang},\n  title={V2I-Calib: A Novel Calibration Approach for Collaborative Vehicle and Infrastructure LiDAR Systems},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={892-897},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS58592.2024.10802098},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Cooperative LiDAR systems integrating vehicles and road infrastructure, termed V2I calibration, exhibit substantial potential, yet their deployment encounters numerous challenges. A pivotal aspect of ensuring data accuracy and consistency across such systems involves the calibration of LiDAR units across heterogeneous vehicular and infrastructural endpoints. This necessitates the development of calibration methods that are both real-time and robust, particularly those that can ensure robust performance in urban canyon scenarios without relying on initial positioning values. Accordingly, this paper introduces a novel approach to V2I calibration, leveraging spatial association information among perceived objects. Central to this method is the innovative Overall Intersection over Union (oIoU) metric, which quantifies the correlation between targets identified by vehicle and infrastructure systems, thereby facilitating the real-time monitoring of calibration results. Our approach involves identifying common targets within the perception results of vehicle and infrastructure LiDAR systems through the construction of an affinity matrix. These common targets then form the basis for the calculation and optimization of extrinsic parameters. Comparative and ablation studies conducted using the DAIR-V2X dataset substantiate the superiority of our approach. For further insights and resources, our project repository is accessible at https://github.com/MassimoQu/v2i-calib."},"title":{"value":"V2I-Calib: A Novel Calibration Approach for Collaborative Vehicle and Infrastructure LiDAR Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Qianxin Qu","Yijin Xiong","Guipeng Zhang","Xin Wu","Xiaohan Gao","Xin Gao","Hanyu Li","Shichun Guo","Guoying Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1743837792387,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1743837788392,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Qianxin_Qu1"],"forum":"bThi0gDCVf","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":376201,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1743837792387,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bThi0gDCVf","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"3DV 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9665713/9665818/09665837.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/3DIM/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"wodtko|globally_optimal_multiscale_monocular_handeye_calibration_using_dual_quaternions"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Thomas_Wodtko:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Markus_Horn:","~Michael_Buchholz1","~Klaus_Dietmayer1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/3DV53792.2021.00035"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/3dim/WodtkoHBD21,\n  author={Thomas Wodtko and Markus Horn and Michael Buchholz and Klaus Dietmayer},\n  title={Globally Optimal Multi-Scale Monocular Hand-Eye Calibration Using Dual Quaternions},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={249-257},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/3DV53792.2021.00035},\n  booktitle={3DV},\n  crossref={conf/3dim/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this work, we present an approach for monocular hand-eye calibration from per-sensor ego-motion based on dual quaternions. Due to non-metrically scaled translations of monocular odometry, a scaling factor has to be estimated in addition to the rotation and translation calibration. For this, we derive a quadratically constrained quadratic program that allows a combined estimation of all extrinsic calibration parameters. Using dual quaternions leads to low run-times due to their compact representation. Our problem formulation further allows to estimate multiple scalings simultaneously for different sequences of the same sensor setup. Based on our problem formulation, we derive both, a fast local and a globally optimal solving approach. Finally, our algorithms are evaluated and compared to state-of-the-art approaches on simulated and real-world data, e.g., the EuRoC MAV dataset."},"title":{"value":"Globally Optimal Multi-Scale Monocular Hand-Eye Calibration Using Dual Quaternions"},"authors":{"value":["Thomas Wodtko","Markus Horn","Michael Buchholz","Klaus Dietmayer"]}},"tmdate":1794048259692,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1746706690715,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Michael_Buchholz1"],"forum":"Zxwl6W1fXq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":413170,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1794048259692,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Zxwl6W1fXq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.04473v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"wodtko|globally_optimal_multiscale_monocular_handeye_calibration_using_dual_quaternions"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Thomas_Wodtko:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Markus_Horn:","~Michael_Buchholz1","~Klaus_Dietmayer1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.04473"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2201-04473,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Thomas Wodtko and Markus Horn and Michael Buchholz and Klaus Dietmayer},\n  title={Globally Optimal Multi-Scale Monocular Hand-Eye Calibration Using Dual Quaternions},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2201.04473},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.04473}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this work, we present an approach for monocular hand-eye calibration from per-sensor ego-motion based on dual quaternions. Due to non-metrically scaled translations of monocular odometry, a scaling factor has to be estimated in addition to the rotation and translation calibration. For this, we derive a quadratically constrained quadratic program that allows a combined estimation of all extrinsic calibration parameters. Using dual quaternions leads to low run-times due to their compact representation. Our problem formulation further allows to estimate multiple scalings simultaneously for different sequences of the same sensor setup. Based on our problem formulation, we derive both, a fast local and a globally optimal solving approach. Finally, our algorithms are evaluated and compared to state-of-the-art approaches on simulated and real-world data, e.g., the EuRoC MAV dataset."},"title":{"value":"Globally Optimal Multi-Scale Monocular Hand-Eye Calibration Using Dual Quaternions"},"authors":{"value":["Thomas Wodtko","Markus Horn","Michael Buchholz","Klaus Dietmayer"]}},"tmdate":1794048259209,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1746706691273,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Michael_Buchholz1"],"forum":"FNbCyCZzSc","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":413185,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1794048259209,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"FNbCyCZzSc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AsiaSim 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-981-97-7225-4_12.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"laili|model_calibration_for_agentbased_simulation_using_a_pattern_clustering_network"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-97-7225-4_12"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/asiasim/LailiGHZW24,\n  author={Yuanjun Laili and Jiabei Gong and Ke Hu and Lin Zhang and Fei Wang},\n  title={Model Calibration for Agent-Based Simulation Using a Pattern Clustering Network},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={152-164},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-97-7225-4_12},\n  booktitle={AsiaSim},\n  crossref={conf/asiasim/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Agent-based simulation has become essential for simulating complex evolving systems, such as social systems and complex manufacturing systems. However, due to the uncertainty in agent behaviors, model calibration poses a significant challenge. Traditional methods like try-and-error and regression are inefficient, requiring numerous simulation runs under different parameter settings. To address this, we propose an online pattern clustering network-based calibration method, termed PCN-Calibration. This method establishes a pattern clustering network using reference data from the target system and compares simulation results under random parameter settings with this network. Ten weighting rules are introduced to estimate the best parameters based on the distance between the simulation results and the clustering network. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method can find feasible parameter settings within seconds. Furthermore, the experimental discussions provide guidelines for selecting suitable weighting rules for calibrating different models."},"title":{"value":"Model Calibration for Agent-Based Simulation Using a Pattern Clustering Network"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yuanjun Laili","username":""},{"fullname":"Jiabei Gong","username":""},{"fullname":"Ke Hu","username":""},{"fullname":"Lin Zhang","username":"~Lin_Zhang47"},{"fullname":"Fei Wang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784650115011,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/asiasim/LailiGHZW24"],"tcdate":1784650102197,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Lin_Zhang47"],"forum":"Nos97tMQxO","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":85386,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784650115011,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Nos97tMQxO","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We improve medical image segmentation model calibration by addressing spectral bias and confidence saturation through a frequency-domain framework that balances low and high-frequency components in uncertainty estimation."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["medical image segmentation","model calibration","spectral entropy"]},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep neural networks for medical image segmentation often produce overconfident predictions, posing clinical risks due to miscalibrated uncertainty estimates. In this work, we rethink model calibration from a frequency-domain perspective and identify two critical factors causing miscalibration: spectral bias, where models overemphasize low-frequency components, and confidence saturation, which suppresses overall power spectral density in confidence maps. To address these challenges, we propose a novel frequency-aware calibration framework integrating spectral entropy regularization and power spectral smoothing. The spectral entropy term promotes a balanced frequency spectrum and enhances overall spectral power,  enabling better modeling of high-frequency boundary and low-frequency structural uncertainty. The smoothing module stabilizes frequency-wise statistics across training batches, reducing sample-specific fluctuations. Extensive experiments on six public medical imaging datasets and multiple segmentation architectures demonstrate that our approach consistently improves calibration metrics without sacrificing segmentation accuracy."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\ncheng2026rethinking,\ntitle={Rethinking Model Calibration through Spectral Entropy Regularization in Medical Image Segmentation},\nauthor={Kun Cheng and Yukun Zhang and William Henry Nailon and Tonggang Zhao},\nbooktitle={The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=SOFSVaZXSj}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Rethinking Model Calibration through Spectral Entropy Regularization in Medical Image Segmentation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/79d2a2920dec232ead16ba1bb9579c4a5d23e720.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"cheng|rethinking_model_calibration_through_spectral_entropy_regularization_in_medical_image_segmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kun_Cheng4","~Yukun_Zhang7","~William_Henry_Nailon1","~Tonggang_Zhao1"]},"authors":{"value":["Kun Cheng","Yukun Zhang","William Henry Nailon","Tonggang Zhao"]}},"tmdate":1775876881388,"pdate":1769435649122,"tcdate":1756864284934,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission1204/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission1204/Authors"],"forum":"SOFSVaZXSj","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1204,"cdate":1756864284934,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission1204/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission1204/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1775876881388,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"SOFSVaZXSj","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISSNIP 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6820824/6827478/06827632.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISSNIP/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"qiu|microwave_power_sensor_calibration_using_direct_comparison_technique_with_different_adaptors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tie_Qiu_0002:","~Zhiping_Lin1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yueyan_Shan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu_Song_Meng:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ISSNIP.2014.6827632"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/issnip/QiuLSM14,\n  author={Tie Qiu and Zhiping Lin and Yueyan Shan and Yu Song Meng},\n  title={Microwave power sensor calibration using direct comparison technique with different adaptors},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={1-6},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ISSNIP.2014.6827632},\n  booktitle={ISSNIP},\n  crossref={conf/issnip/2014}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The effects of different adaptors on microwave power sensor calibration using direct comparison transfer technique have been investigated in this paper. The calibrations are implemented with a mathematical model developed recently. To verify its accuracy in calculating the calibration factor, a comparison is carried out between the mathematical model developed and those reported by other researchers. The comparison indicates that the model is suitable for microwave power sensor calibrations. For its implementations, a 0 dB adaptor, 10 dB, 20 dB and 30 dB attenuators are used to evaluate the effects from different adaptors. Preliminary results show that when the attenuation of an adaptor increases, the expanded uncertainty of the calibration factor increases correspondingly, following the Guide to the Expression of Uncertainty in Measurement (GUM). Moreover, the results of GUM are also verified with the Monte Carlo Method."},"title":{"value":"Microwave power sensor calibration using direct comparison technique with different adaptors"},"authors":{"value":["Tie Qiu","Zhiping Lin","Yueyan Shan","Yu Song Meng"]}},"tmdate":1762415899202,"pdate":1388534400000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/issnip/QiuLSM14"],"tcdate":1762415732361,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhiping_Lin1"],"forum":"W3HjUyjWno","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":664620,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762415899202,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"W3HjUyjWno","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7083369/10440130/10452804.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"yan|sensorx2vehicle_online_sensorstovehicle_rotation_calibration_methods_in_road_scenarios"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guohang_Yan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhaotong_Luo:","~Zhuochun_Liu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yikang_Li_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Botian_Shi:","~Kai_Zhang16"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/lra.2024.3371261"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/YanLLLSZ24,\n  author={Guohang Yan and Zhaotong Luo and Zhuochun Liu and Yikang Li and Botian Shi and Kai Zhang},\n  title={SensorX2Vehicle: Online Sensors-to-Vehicle Rotation Calibration Methods in Road Scenarios},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={9},\n  number={4},\n  pages={3775-3782},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/lra.2024.3371261}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Properly-calibrated sensors are the prerequisite for a dependable autonomous driving system. Besides the extrinsic calibration between the sensors, the extrinsic between the sensor and the vehicle is also important, especially the rotation. Most of the existing sensor-to-vehicle calibration approaches have requirements on facilities, manual work, road features or vehicle trajectory. In this work, we propose more general and flexible methods for four commonly used sensors: Camera, LiDAR, GNSS/INS, and millimeter-wave Radar, composing a toolbox named SensorX2car. In each method, the rotation between a sensor and the vehicle is calibrated individually in road scenarios. Experiments on large-scale real-world datasets demonstrate the practicality of our proposed methods. Meanwhile, the related codes have been open-sourced to benefit the community. To our knowledge, SensorX2car is the first open-source sensor to vehicle calibration toolbox."},"title":{"value":"SensorX2Vehicle: Online Sensors-to-Vehicle Rotation Calibration Methods in Road Scenarios"},"authors":{"value":["Guohang Yan","Zhaotong Luo","Zhuochun Liu","Yikang Li","Botian Shi","Kai Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1744159901990,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731484523673,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kai_Zhang16"],"forum":"OOVwsZulAW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":214595,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1744159901990,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"OOVwsZulAW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICINCO (2) 2013"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICINCO/2013"},"paperhash":{"value":"cucci|a_flexible_framework_for_mobile_robot_pose_estimation_and_multisensor_selfcalibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Davide_Antonio_Cucci:","~Matteo_Matteucci1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.5220/0004484703610368"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icinco/CucciM13,\n  author={Davide Antonio Cucci and Matteo Matteucci},\n  title={A Flexible Framework for Mobile Robot Pose Estimation and Multi-Sensor Self-Calibration},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  pages={361-368},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.5220/0004484703610368},\n  booktitle={ICINCO (2)},\n  crossref={conf/icinco/2013-2}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The design and the development of the position and orientation tracking system of a mobile robot, and the calibration of its sensor parameters (e.g., displacement, misalignment and iron distortions), are challenging and time consuming tasks in every autonomous robotics project. The ROAMFREE framework delivers turn-on-and-go multi-sensors pose tracking and self-calibration modules and it is designed to be flexible and to adapt to every kind of mobile robotic platform. In ROAMFREE, the sensor data fusion problem is formulated as a hyper-graph optimization where nodes represent poses and calibration parameters and edges the non-linear measurement constraints. This formulation allows us to solve both the on-line pose tracking and the off-line sensor self-calibration problems. In this paper, we introduce the approach and we discuss a real platform case study, along with experimental results."},"title":{"value":"A Flexible Framework for Mobile Robot Pose Estimation and Multi-Sensor Self-Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Davide Antonio Cucci","Matteo Matteucci"]}},"tmdate":1731500115187,"pdate":1356998400000,"tcdate":1731499833407,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Matteo_Matteucci1"],"forum":"eoPXGglZyX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":226673,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731500115187,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"eoPXGglZyX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SCAM 2013"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6636284/6648171/06648181.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/SCAM/2013"},"paperhash":{"value":"annervaz|code_clustering_workbench"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:K._M._Annervaz:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Vikrant_S._Kaulgud:","~Janardan_Misra1","~Shubhashis_Sengupta1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gary_Titus:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Azmat_Munshi:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/SCAM.2013.6648181"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/scam/AnnervazKMSTM13,\n  author={K. M. Annervaz and Vikrant S. Kaulgud and Janardan Misra and Shubhashis Sengupta and Gary Titus and Azmat Munshi},\n  title={Code clustering workbench},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  pages={31-36},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/SCAM.2013.6648181},\n  booktitle={SCAM},\n  crossref={conf/scam/2013}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Source code clustering is an important technique used in software development and maintenance to understand the modular structure of code. An array of algorithms are available for clustering like simulated annealing based search. Source code have different kinds of features such as structural or textual features. The collection of these different types of source code features and computation of relevant feature metrics is a difficult task. Further, the clustering algorithms can run on metrics based on different types of source code features or their combinations. This flexibility makes it non-trivial to test effectiveness of clustering algorithms on a source code. In this paper, we present a highly configurable clustering workbench that allows the user to collect the various source code features and then to select the code features used for clustering, the clustering algorithm and its various parameters. Clustering quality metrics are computed. They allow comparison of algorithm output based on different combinations of code-features and algorithms. We also present the specific contribution made in multi-dimensional feature analysis and clustering. The tool hides the algorithm complexity from the user, thus allowing complete focus on understanding the 'effect' of the configuration choices. We have also applied this tool in real-life maintenance projects, where the users found it useful to tweak the clustering techniques for the source-code peculiarities."},"title":{"value":"Code clustering workbench"},"authors":{"value":["K. M. Annervaz","Vikrant S. Kaulgud","Janardan Misra","Shubhashis Sengupta","Gary Titus","Azmat Munshi"]}},"tmdate":1747788359736,"pdate":1356998400000,"tcdate":1713530018991,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shubhashis_Sengupta1"],"forum":"z8bWALXSdA","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":2297,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747788359736,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"z8bWALXSdA","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/6940066/06853370.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|robust_camera_calibration_by_optimal_localization_of_spatial_control_points"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianyang_Liu:","~Youfu_Li1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shengyong_Chen:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2014.2324792"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/LiuLC14,\n  author={Jianyang Liu and Youfu Li and Shengyong Chen},\n  title={Robust Camera Calibration by Optimal Localization of Spatial Control Points},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={63},\n  number={12},\n  pages={3076-3087},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2014.2324792}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes a novel method for localization optimization of control points for robust calibration of a pinhole model camera. Instead of performing accurate subpixel control point detection by specialized operators, which are normally adopted in conventional work, our proposed method concentrates on estimating the optimal control points in regions of plausibility determined by distortion bias from perspective distortion, lens distortion, and localization bias from out-of-focus blurring. With this method, the two main strict preconditions for camera calibration in conventional work are relieved. The first one is that the input images for calibration are assumed to be well focused and the second one is that the individual control point needs to be detected with high accuracy. In our work, we formulate the accurate determination of control points' localization as an optimization process. This optimization process takes determined control points' uncertainty area as input. A global searching algorithm combined with Levenberg-Marquardt optimization algorithm is introduced for searching the optimal control points and refining camera parameters. Experimental results show that the proposed method achieves higher accuracy than the conventional methods."},"title":{"value":"Robust Camera Calibration by Optimal Localization of Spatial Control Points"},"authors":{"value":["Jianyang Liu","Youfu Li","Shengyong Chen"]}},"tmdate":1747450067694,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1747450048748,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Youfu_Li1"],"forum":"BAWEXjLvXP","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":518541,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747450067694,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"BAWEXjLvXP","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.06671v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"schiffers|disassemblable_fieldwork_ct_scanner_using_a_3dprinted_calibration_phantom"},"authorids":{"value":["~Florian_Schiffers2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Thomas_Bochynek:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:André_Aichert:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tobias_Würfl:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Michael_Rubenstein:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Oliver_Cossairt:"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.06671"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2011-06671,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Florian Schiffers and Thomas Bochynek and André Aichert and Tobias Würfl and Michael Rubenstein and Oliver Cossairt},\n  title={Disassemblable Fieldwork CT Scanner Using a 3D-printed Calibration Phantom},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2011.06671},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.06671}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The use of computed tomography (CT) imaging has become of increasing interest to academic areas outside of the field of medical imaging and industrial inspection, e.g., to biology and cultural heritage research. The pecularities of these fields, however, sometimes require that objects need to be imaged on-site, e.g., in field-work conditions or in museum collections. Under these circumstances, it is often not possible to use a commercial device and a custom solution is the only viable option. In order to achieve high image quality under adverse conditions, reliable calibration and trajectory reproduction are usually key requirements for any custom CT scanning system. Here, we introduce the construction of a low-cost disassemblable CT scanner that allows calibration even when trajectory reproduction is not possible due to the limitations imposed by the project conditions. Using 3D-printed in-image calibration phantoms, we compute a projection matrix directly from each captured X-ray projection. We describe our method in detail and show successful tomographic reconstructions of several specimen as proof of concept."},"title":{"value":"Disassemblable Fieldwork CT Scanner Using a 3D-printed Calibration Phantom"},"authors":{"value":["Florian Schiffers","Thomas Bochynek","André Aichert","Tobias Würfl","Michael Rubenstein","Oliver Cossairt"]}},"tmdate":1744005473891,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1744005457499,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Florian_Schiffers2"],"forum":"iMBucLHuFa","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":376870,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744005473891,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"iMBucLHuFa","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.14848v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhao|objectlevel_verbalized_confidence_calibration_in_visionlanguage_models_via_semantic_perturbation"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.14848"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-14848,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yunpu Zhao and Rui Zhang and Junbin Xiao and Ruibo Hou and Jiaming Guo and Zihao Zhang and Yifan Hao and Yunji Chen},\n  title={Object-Level Verbalized Confidence Calibration in Vision-Language Models via Semantic Perturbation},\n  year={2025},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1743465600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2504.14848},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.14848}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Vision-language models (VLMs) excel in various multimodal tasks but frequently suffer from poor calibration, resulting in misalignment between their verbalized confidence and response correctness. This miscalibration undermines user trust, especially when models confidently provide incorrect or fabricated information. In this work, we propose a novel Confidence Calibration through Semantic Perturbation (CSP) framework to improve the calibration of verbalized confidence for VLMs in response to object-centric queries. We first introduce a perturbed dataset where Gaussian noise is applied to the key object regions to simulate visual uncertainty at different confidence levels, establishing an explicit mapping between visual ambiguity and confidence levels. We further enhance calibration through a two-stage training process combining supervised fine-tuning on the perturbed dataset with subsequent preference optimization. Extensive experiments on popular benchmarks demonstrate that our method significantly improves the alignment between verbalized confidence and response correctness while maintaining or enhancing overall task performance. These results highlight the potential of semantic perturbation as a practical tool for improving the reliability and interpretability of VLMs."},"title":{"value":"Object-Level Verbalized Confidence Calibration in Vision-Language Models via Semantic Perturbation"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yunpu Zhao","username":""},{"fullname":"Rui Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Junbin Xiao","username":"~Junbin_Xiao1"},{"fullname":"Ruibo Hou","username":""},{"fullname":"Jiaming Guo","username":""},{"fullname":"Zihao Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Yifan Hao","username":""},{"fullname":"Yunji Chen","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1787736531454,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2504-14848"],"tcdate":1787736517621,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Junbin_Xiao1"],"forum":"xLAXHinuPk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":142879,"cdate":1743465600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1787736531454,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"xLAXHinuPk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.10132v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|cooperative_visuallidar_extrinsic_calibration_technology_for_intersection_vehicleinfrastructure_a_review"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinyu_Zhang_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yijin_Xiong:","~Qianxin_Qu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Renjie_Wang:","","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jing_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shichun_Guo:","~Jun_Li25"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.10132"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2405-10132,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Xinyu Zhang and Yijin Xiong and Qianxin Qu and Renjie Wang and Xin Gao and Jing Liu and Shichun Guo and Jun Li},\n  title={Cooperative Visual-LiDAR Extrinsic Calibration Technology for Intersection Vehicle-Infrastructure: A review},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2405.10132},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.10132}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In the typical urban intersection scenario, both vehicles and infrastructures are equipped with visual and LiDAR sensors. By successfully integrating the data from vehicle-side and road monitoring devices, a more comprehensive and accurate environmental perception and information acquisition can be achieved. The Calibration of sensors, as an essential component of autonomous driving technology, has consistently drawn significant attention. Particularly in scenarios involving multiple sensors collaboratively perceiving and addressing localization challenges, the requirement for inter-sensor calibration becomes crucial. Recent years have witnessed the emergence of the concept of multi-end cooperation, where infrastructure captures and transmits surrounding environment information to vehicles, bolstering their perception capabilities while mitigating costs. However, this also poses technical complexities, underscoring the pressing need for diverse end calibration. Camera and LiDAR, the bedrock sensors in autonomous driving, exhibit expansive applicability. This paper comprehensively examines and analyzes the calibration of multi-end camera-LiDAR setups from vehicle, roadside, and vehicle-road cooperation perspectives, outlining their relevant applications and profound significance. Concluding with a summary, we present our future-oriented ideas and hypotheses."},"title":{"value":"Cooperative Visual-LiDAR Extrinsic Calibration Technology for Intersection Vehicle-Infrastructure: A review"},"authors":{"value":["Xinyu Zhang","Yijin Xiong","Qianxin Qu","Renjie Wang","Xin Gao","Jing Liu","Shichun Guo","Jun Li"]}},"tmdate":1762953549125,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731561297600,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jun_Li25"],"forum":"Ka3UuBkTza","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":237704,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762953549125,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Ka3UuBkTza","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IECON 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10311571/10311610/10311803.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IECON/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"fu|slambased_joint_calibration_of_differential_rss_sensor_array_and_source_localization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Linya_Fu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xu_Qiao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shoudong_Huang:","~Guoqiang_Mao1","~Zhiyun_Lin1","~Youfu_Li1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:He_Kong:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IECON51785.2023.10311803"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iecon/FuQHMLLK23,\n  author={Linya Fu and Xu Qiao and Shoudong Huang and Guoqiang Mao and Zhiyun Lin and Youfu Li and He Kong},\n  title={SLAM-Based Joint Calibration of Differential RSS Sensor Array and Source Localization},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1-8},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IECON51785.2023.10311803},\n  booktitle={IECON},\n  crossref={conf/iecon/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Sensor arrays generating differential received signal strength (DRSS) measurements have found many applications in robotics. However, accurate calibration of these sensor arrays remains a challenge. Most existing methods are impractical in that they assume to know signal source positions or certain parameters (i.e., path loss exponent), and try to estimate the others. In this paper, we adopt graph simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) as a general framework for jointly estimating the source positions and parameters of the DRSS sensor array. Our contributions are twofold. On the one hand, by using a Fisher information matrix approach, we conduct a systematic observability analysis of the corresponding SLAM setup for the calibration problem. On the other hand, we propose an effective procedure to select the initial value which is fed to Levenberg-Marquardt iterations for further improving optimization accuracy and convergence. Extensive simulation and hardware experiments show that the proposed method renders high-quality calibration results. All the codes and data are publicly available at https://github.com/SUSTech2022/DRSS-sensor-array-calibration."},"title":{"value":"SLAM-Based Joint Calibration of Differential RSS Sensor Array and Source Localization"},"authors":{"value":["Linya Fu","Xu Qiao","Shoudong Huang","Guoqiang Mao","Zhiyun Lin","Youfu Li","He Kong"]}},"tmdate":1747450048951,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1738811761753,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Guoqiang_Mao1"],"forum":"tNs0oHW8sm","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":299523,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747450048951,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"tNs0oHW8sm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AISTATS Submission"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/109b823d288838f6ca07e7762399f9ce4a553e77.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"young|beyond_calibration_assessing_the_probabilistic_fit_of_neural_regressors_via_conditional_congruence"},"authorids":{"value":["~Spencer_Young1","~Cole_Edgren1","~Riley_Sinema1","~Andrew_Hall1","~Nathan_Dong1","~Porter_Jenkins1"]},"abstract":{"value":"While significant progress has been made in specifying neural networks capable of representing uncertainty, deep networks still often suffer from overconfidence and misaligned predictive distributions. Existing approaches for addressing this misalignment are primarily developed under the framework of calibration, with common metrics such as Expected Calibration Error (ECE). However, calibration can only provide a strictly marginal assessment of probabilistic alignment. Con- sequently, calibration metrics such as ECE are distribution-wise measures and cannot diagnose the point-wise reliability of individual inputs, which is impor- tant for real-world decision-making. We propose a stronger condition, which we term conditional congruence, for assessing probabilistic fit. We also introduce a metric, Conditional Congruence Error (CCE), that uses conditional kernel mean embeddings to estimate the distance, at any point, between the learned predictive distribution and the empirical, conditional distribution in a dataset. We show that using CCE to measure congruence 1) accurately quantifies misalignment between distributions when the data generating process is known, 2) effectively scales to real-world, high dimensional image regression tasks, and 3) can be used to gauge model reliability on unseen instances."},"title":{"value":"Beyond Calibration: Assessing the Probabilistic Fit of Neural Regressors via Conditional Congruence"},"authors":{"value":["Spencer Young","Cole Edgren","Riley Sinema","Andrew Hall","Nathan Dong","Porter Jenkins"]}},"tmdate":1736640600745,"pdate":1728889200000,"tcdate":1736640600745,"writers":["~Spencer_Young1","~Cole_Edgren1","~Riley_Sinema1","~Andrew_Hall1","~Nathan_Dong1","~Porter_Jenkins1"],"signatures":["~Porter_Jenkins1"],"forum":"qBF4fX8Bre","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":31991,"cdate":1736640600745,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1736640600745,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"qBF4fX8Bre","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.09078v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"salah|ecalib_a_fast_robust_and_accurate_calibration_toolbox_for_event_cameras"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohammed_Salah:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abdulla_Ayyad:","~Muhammad_Humais1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daniel_Gehrig:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abdelqader_Abusafieh:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lakmal_D._Seneviratne:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Davide_Scaramuzza_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yahya_H._Zweiri:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2306.09078"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2306-09078,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Mohammed Salah and Abdulla Ayyad and Muhammad Humais and Daniel Gehrig and Abdelqader Abusafieh and Lakmal D. Seneviratne and Davide Scaramuzza and Yahya H. Zweiri},\n  title={E-Calib: A Fast, Robust and Accurate Calibration Toolbox for Event Cameras},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2306.09078},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2306.09078}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Event cameras triggered a paradigm shift in the computer vision community delineated by their asynchronous nature, low latency, and high dynamic range. Calibration of event cameras is always essential to account for the sensor intrinsic parameters and for 3D perception. However, conventional image-based calibration techniques are not applicable due to the asynchronous, binary output of the sensor. The current standard for calibrating event cameras relies on either blinking patterns or event-based image reconstruction algorithms. These approaches are difficult to deploy in factory settings and are affected by noise and artifacts degrading the calibration performance. To bridge these limitations, we present E-Calib, a novel, fast, robust, and accurate calibration toolbox for event cameras utilizing the asymmetric circle grid, for its robustness to out-of-focus scenes. The proposed method is tested in a variety of rigorous experiments for different event camera models, on circle grids with different geometric properties, and under challenging illumination conditions. The results show that our approach outperforms the state-of-the-art in detection success rate, reprojection error, and estimation accuracy of extrinsic parameters."},"title":{"value":"E-Calib: A Fast, Robust and Accurate Calibration Toolbox for Event Cameras"},"authors":{"value":["Mohammed Salah","Abdulla Ayyad","Muhammad Humais","Daniel Gehrig","Abdelqader Abusafieh","Lakmal D. Seneviratne","Davide Scaramuzza","Yahya H. Zweiri"]}},"tmdate":1730739496182,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1730739481436,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Muhammad_Ahmed_Humais1"],"forum":"j0c7w3flye","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":170052,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730739496182,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"j0c7w3flye","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"BMEI 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6093307/6098366/06098408.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/BMEI/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|a_fully_automatic_2d_ultrasound_probe_calibration_method_using_a_simple_phantom"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junchen_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianmiao_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Hu:","~Hongen_Liao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kuan_Luan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ichiro_Sakuma:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/BMEI.2011.6098408"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/bmei/WangWHLLS11,\n  author={Junchen Wang and Tianmiao Wang and Lei Hu and Hongen Liao and Kuan Luan and Ichiro Sakuma},\n  title={A fully automatic 2D ultrasound probe calibration method using a simple phantom},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={588-592},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/BMEI.2011.6098408},\n  booktitle={BMEI},\n  crossref={conf/bmei/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Probe calibration should be performed for free-hand tracking and ultrasound (US) fusion with a position sensor-attached US probe in US guided interventions. Difficulties in locating the probe imaging plane and adjusting the alignment of the probe with the calibration phantom call for more complicated and precise phantoms. However, the manufacture of such phantoms is time-consuming and expensive. Furthermore, the segmentation of the phantom features in US images cannot be reliably automated, which requires manual intervention. This paper presents a fully automatic 2D probe calibration method using a rather simple phantom. By detecting and analyzing the temporal signal arising from the ultrasound artifacts when sweeping the 2D probe over the phantom, the imaging plane of the probe and the image features can be reliably identified. Then the features are fed to a nonlinear optimizer to estimate the calibration parameters. The whole procedure including the setup can be done within 2 minutes with the fiducial registration error (FRE) of 0.16 mm. A US-guided biopsy experiment has also been conducted which shows the target registration error (TRE) of less than 1 mm."},"title":{"value":"A fully automatic 2D ultrasound probe calibration method using a simple phantom"},"authors":{"value":["Junchen Wang","Tianmiao Wang","Lei Hu","Hongen Liao","Kuan Luan","Ichiro Sakuma"]}},"tmdate":1730287204993,"pdate":1293840000000,"tcdate":1730287088277,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hongen_Liao1"],"forum":"e5gjuSJH16","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":164392,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730287204993,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"e5gjuSJH16","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.emnlp-main.178.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|calibration_meets_explanation_a_simple_and_effective_approach_for_model_confidence_estimates"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongfang_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Baotian_Hu:","~Qingcai_Chen2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2022.emnlp-main.178"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/LiHC22,\n  author={Dongfang Li and Baotian Hu and Qingcai Chen},\n  title={Calibration Meets Explanation: A Simple and Effective Approach for Model Confidence Estimates},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={2775-2784},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2022.emnlp-main.178},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration strengthens the trustworthiness of black-box models by producing better accurate confidence estimates on given examples. However, little is known about if model explanations can help confidence calibration. Intuitively, humans look at important features attributions and decide whether the model is trustworthy. Similarly, the explanations may tell us when the model might know and when it does not. Inspired by this, we propose a method named CME that leverages model explanations to make the model less confident with non-inductive attributions. The idea is that when the model is not highly confident, it is difficult to identify strong indications of any class, and the tokens accordingly do not have high attribution scores for any class and vice versa. We conduct extensive experiments on six datasets with two popular pre-trained language models in the in-domain and out-of-domain settings. The results show that CME improves calibration performance in all settings. The expected calibration errors are further reduced when combined with temperature scaling. Our findings highlight that model explanations can help calibrate posterior estimates."},"title":{"value":"Calibration Meets Explanation: A Simple and Effective Approach for Model Confidence Estimates"},"authors":{"value":["Dongfang Li","Baotian Hu","Qingcai Chen"]}},"tmdate":1723129546047,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1723129513144,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Qingcai_Chen2"],"forum":"siBwvwCkia","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":62796,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1723129546047,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"siBwvwCkia","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2211.03041v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|calibration_meets_explanation_a_simple_and_effective_approach_for_model_confidence_estimates"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongfang_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Baotian_Hu:","~Qingcai_Chen2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2211.03041"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2211-03041,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Dongfang Li and Baotian Hu and Qingcai Chen},\n  title={Calibration Meets Explanation: A Simple and Effective Approach for Model Confidence Estimates},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2211.03041},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2211.03041}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration strengthens the trustworthiness of black-box models by producing better accurate confidence estimates on given examples. However, little is known about if model explanations can help confidence calibration. Intuitively, humans look at important features attributions and decide whether the model is trustworthy. Similarly, the explanations can tell us when the model may or may not know. Inspired by this, we propose a method named CME that leverages model explanations to make the model less confident with non-inductive attributions. The idea is that when the model is not highly confident, it is difficult to identify strong indications of any class, and the tokens accordingly do not have high attribution scores for any class and vice versa. We conduct extensive experiments on six datasets with two popular pre-trained language models in the in-domain and out-of-domain settings. The results show that CME improves calibration performance in all settings. The expected calibration errors are further reduced when combined with temperature scaling. Our findings highlight that model explanations can help calibrate posterior estimates."},"title":{"value":"Calibration Meets Explanation: A Simple and Effective Approach for Model Confidence Estimates"},"authors":{"value":["Dongfang Li","Baotian Hu","Qingcai Chen"]}},"tmdate":1723129523401,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1723129511949,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Qingcai_Chen2"],"forum":"bi9ReBaptk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":62758,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1723129523401,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bi9ReBaptk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"COLT 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlresearch/v336/main/assets/frongillo26a/frongillo26a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"frongillo|toward_simultaneously_optimal_regret_in_ucalibration"},"html":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v336/frongillo26a.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/colt/FrongilloLMS26,\n  author={Rafael M. Frongillo and Haipeng Luo and Nishant A. Mehta and Jon Schneider},\n  title={Toward Simultaneously Optimal Regret in U-Calibration},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={2503-2534},\n  url={https://proceedings.mlr.press/v336/frongillo26a.html},\n  booktitle={COLT},\n  crossref={conf/colt/2026}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"U-calibration studies online forecasting algorithms whose predictions can be consumed by any unknown downstream agent, guaranteeing sublinear regret simultaneously for all proper loss functions. Existing U-calibration algorithms achieve worst-case optimal $O(\\sqrt{T})$ regret for every bounded proper loss, but they fail to adapt to easier losses: as we show, even for smooth losses such as squared loss, they incur $\\Omega(\\sqrt{T})$ regret instead of the optimal $O(\\log T)$ regret. In this work, we show that this limitation is not inherent. Specifically, we design a single forecast algorithm that simultaneously achieves $\\tilde O(\\sqrt{T})$ regret for every bounded proper loss and $O(\\log T)$ regret for every bounded smooth proper loss. More generally, our algorithm also attains logarithmic regret for losses that are smooth relative to the log-barrier, which include several non-Lipschitz examples. Our approach is based on a novel variant of Follow-the-Perturbed-Leader (FTPL) in which perturbations are applied directly in the prediction space using \\emph{self-concordant noise}. The resulting analysis also departs substantially from prior FTPL analyses due to the complex nature of this noise and may be of independent interest."},"title":{"value":"Toward Simultaneously Optimal Regret in U-Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Rafael M. Frongillo","username":""},{"fullname":"Haipeng Luo","username":""},{"fullname":"Nishant A. Mehta","username":"~Nishant_A._Mehta1"},{"fullname":"Jon Schneider","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1791414900862,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/colt/FrongilloLMS26"],"tcdate":1791414898898,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Nishant_A_Mehta1"],"forum":"sPDPbgUzn7","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":165742,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791414900862,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"sPDPbgUzn7","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"Calibration masks incoherent LLM confidence; we benchmark probabilistic coherence and restore it by training against exploitability rather than calibration alone."},"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 Workshop AgenticUQ Poster"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["Machine Learning","Confidence Estimation","Calibration","Large Language Models","Uncertainty Quantification","Epistemology","confidence calibration","self-consistency","trustworthy ML"]},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration is the primary criterion for evaluating LLM confidence, but it is insufficient: it admits trivially incoherent estimators, depends on the evaluation distribution, and does not test the extent to which the estimation can be interpreted as a consistent, underlying probability function. To use in real scenarios, we care more how well LLM confidence estimates satisfy the conditions required of coherent probabilistic beliefs. We formalize these conditions along three axes (structural coherence, faithfulness, and usefulness) and operationalize them in $\\textbf{CoherenceBench}.$ Widely used estimators systematically violate these conditions despite appearing well-calibrated: models assign lower confidence to logically easier questions 31\\% of the time, and common interventions reducing RMSCE leave structural violations unchanged, suggesting calibration is orthogonal to probabilistic validity. RLHF and chain-of-thought improve usefulness metrics without restoring coherence. To close this gap, we introduce $\\textbf{Reinforcement Learning from Exploitation (RLE)}$, which post-trains a model by directly penalizing Dutch-book exploitability across four coherence templates. RLE outperforms Brier-score fine-tuning on structural coherence in- and out-of-distribution, demonstrating that training against axiom violations is more effective than fitting labelled correctness data alone."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nmatta2026rethinking,\ntitle={Rethinking {LLM} Confidence: From Calibration to Coherence},\nauthor={Krish Matta and Atharv Naphade and Andy Zou},\nbooktitle={ICML 2026 Statistical Frameworks for Uncertainty in Agentic Systems},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=TAhXHpa2nW}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Rethinking LLM Confidence: From Calibration to Coherence"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/cd36f2a2b33aa77e6cd26b997900b625f5cce122.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ"},"paperhash":{"value":"matta|rethinking_llm_confidence_from_calibration_to_coherence"},"authorids":{"value":["~Krish_Matta1","~Atharv_Naphade2","~Andy_Zou1"]},"authors":{"value":["Krish Matta","Atharv Naphade","Andy Zou"]}},"tmdate":1782482405388,"pdate":1780246182349,"tcdate":1777705764207,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/Submission65/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/Submission65/Authors"],"forum":"TAhXHpa2nW","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":65,"cdate":1777705764207,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1782482405388,"odate":1782482401292,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ","id":"TAhXHpa2nW","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce CoherenceBench to evaluate LLM confidence beyond standard calibration, exposing key trade-offs between structural coherence and empirical faithfulness."},"venue":{"value":"CTB@ICML 2026"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["Machine Learning","Confidence Estimation","Calibration","Large Language Models","Uncertainty Quantification","Epistemology","confidence calibration","self-consistency","trustworthy ML"]},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration is the primary criterion for evaluating LLM confidence, but it is insufficient: it admits trivially incoherent estimators, depends on the evaluation distribution, and does not test the extent to which the estimation can be interpreted as a consistent, underlying probability function. To use in real scenarios, we care more how well LLM confidence estimates satisfy the conditions required of coherent probabilistic beliefs. We formalize these conditions along three axes (structural coherence, faithfulness, and usefulness) and operationalize them in $\\textbf{CoherenceBench}.$ Widely used estimators systematically violate these conditions despite appearing well-calibrated: models assign lower confidence to logically easier questions 31% of the time, and common interventions reducing RMSCE leave structural violations unchanged, suggesting calibration is orthogonal to probabilistic validity. RLHF and chain-of-thought improve usefulness metrics without restoring coherence. To close this gap, we introduce Reinforcement Learning from Exploitation (RLE), which post-trains a model by directly penalizing Dutch-book exploitability across four coherence templates. RLE outperforms Brier-score fine-tuning on structural coherence in- and out-of-distribution, demonstrating that training against axiom violations is more effective than fitting labelled correctness data alone."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nmatta2026rethinking,\ntitle={Rethinking {LLM} Confidence: From Calibration to Coherence},\nauthor={Krish Matta and Atharv Naphade and Andy Zou},\nbooktitle={ICML 2026 Workshop on Combining Theory and Benchmarks: Towards A Virtuous Cycle to Understand and Guarantee Foundation Model Performance},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=SmwCItfHoC}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Rethinking LLM Confidence: From Calibration to Coherence"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ad16df3fc9e109ba45ff00a9ba70a62782fe3a6b.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB"},"paperhash":{"value":"matta|rethinking_llm_confidence_from_calibration_to_coherence"},"authorids":{"value":["~Krish_Matta1","~Atharv_Naphade2","~Andy_Zou1"]},"authors":{"value":["Krish Matta","Atharv Naphade","Andy Zou"]}},"tmdate":1779714431615,"pdate":1779714420438,"tcdate":1778230056173,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/Submission111/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/Submission111/Authors"],"forum":"SmwCItfHoC","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":111,"cdate":1778230056173,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1779714431615,"odate":1779714420438,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB","id":"SmwCItfHoC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Journal of Microwaves"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/9171629/11074449/11023546.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"braunwarth|an_advanced_method_for_precise_ula_simo_radar_calibration_utilizing_synthetic_aperture_radar_imaging_artifacts"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/JMW.2025.3569103"},"abstract":{"value":"Radar calibration has always been essential for compensating unavoidable manufacturing inaccuracies, component variations, or aging effects in multichannel radar systems. The demand for high-resolution radars, particularly in automotive applications, necessitates increasing carrier frequencies, bandwidths, synthetic apertures, and channel counts, imposing exceptionally high calibration requirements. Conventional calibration methods often rely on expensive positioning systems and large-scale anechoic chambers, yet offer only limited calibration accuracy. This publication presents a novel calibration method that achieves exceptionally high accuracy for single-input multiple-output (SIMO) radars with uniform linear arrays (ULAs) by estimating amplitude and phase deviations as well as mutual coupling between the channels. The proposed method leverages the established theory of accumulating channel imbalances in synthetic aperture radar (SAR) processing, enabling the isolation of error power from the desired signal and noise within the image. The applied optimization minimizes only at deterministic artifact locations, which enhances the optimization sensitivity and improves calibration precision, while reducing the computational complexity. The proposed approach demonstrates high performance by calibrating an antenna array with eight elements in a 77 GHz frequency-modulated continuous wave SIMO radar. The resulting calibration quality is validated in a test scene, demonstrating significantly reduced artifacts within the generated image compared to the uncalibrated array."},"title":{"value":"An Advanced Method for Precise ULA SIMO Radar Calibration Utilizing Synthetic Aperture Radar Imaging Artifacts"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Michael Braunwarth"},{"fullname":"Johanna Geiss"},{"fullname":"Erik Sippel"},{"fullname":"Martin Vossiek","username":"~Martin_Vossiek1"}]}},"tmdate":1789092832427,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/jmw.2025.3569103"],"tcdate":1772362361042,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Martin_Vossiek1"],"forum":"8AjnRYpWMG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":50656,"cdate":1749062969604,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789092832427,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"8AjnRYpWMG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.17761v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wibbeke|evaluating_the_quality_of_the_quantified_uncertainty_for_recalibration_of_datadriven_regression_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jelke_Wibbeke1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nico_Schönfisch:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sebastian_Rohjans:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andreas_Rauh:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.17761"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2508-17761,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jelke Wibbeke and Nico Schönfisch and Sebastian Rohjans and Andreas Rauh},\n  title={Evaluating the Quality of the Quantified Uncertainty for (Re)Calibration of Data-Driven Regression Models},\n  year={2025},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1754006400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2508.17761},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.17761}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In safety-critical applications data-driven models must not only be accurate but also provide reliable uncertainty estimates. This property, commonly referred to as calibration, is essential for risk-aware decision-making. In regression a wide variety of calibration metrics and recalibration methods have emerged. However, these metrics differ significantly in their definitions, assumptions and scales, making it difficult to interpret and compare results across studies. Moreover, most recalibration methods have been evaluated using only a small subset of metrics, leaving it unclear whether improvements generalize across different notions of calibration. In this work, we systematically extract and categorize regression calibration metrics from the literature and benchmark these metrics independently of specific modelling methods or recalibration approaches. Through controlled experiments with real-world, synthetic and artificially miscalibrated data, we demonstrate that calibration metrics frequently produce conflicting results. Our analysis reveals substantial inconsistencies: many metrics disagree in their evaluation of the same recalibration result, and some even indicate contradictory conclusions. This inconsistency is particularly concerning as it potentially allows cherry-picking of metrics to create misleading impressions of success. We identify the Expected Normalized Calibration Error (ENCE) and the Coverage Width-based Criterion (CWC) as the most dependable metrics in our tests. Our findings highlight the critical role of metric selection in calibration research."},"title":{"value":"Evaluating the Quality of the Quantified Uncertainty for (Re)Calibration of Data-Driven Regression Models"},"authors":{"value":["Jelke Wibbeke","Nico Schönfisch","Sebastian Rohjans","Andreas Rauh"]}},"tmdate":1769421087590,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2508-17761"],"tcdate":1769421084727,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jelke_Wibbeke1"],"forum":"UFTEeTDhlL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":801986,"cdate":1754006400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769421087590,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UFTEeTDhlL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Reinforcement Learning","On-Policy Distillation","Multi-turn Agent"]},"primary_area":{"value":"reinforcement learning"},"abstract":{"value":"On-policy distillation trains a language model by matching a teacher on the student's own rollouts, and the dense token-level signal complements the sparse terminal reward of reinforcement learning.\nRollouts of multi-turn agents divide into turns at tool-call boundaries. Recent methods reweight the teacher-matching loss turn by turn from a gap between teacher and student probabilities, from the terminal reward, or from a depth schedule, without estimating how the teacher supervision at a turn contributes to task success.\nWe view on-policy distillation as a policy-gradient step whose advantage is a proxy, the weighted distillation signal, so a turn-level weight scales that proxy toward the true advantage. The weight that fits the proxy best under the student's action distribution is the covariance of the signal with the return divided by the variance of the signal, which is the projection coefficient of the reward gradient onto the distillation direction in the Fisher metric. The projection splits the policy gradient into a calibrated distillation direction and a residual identified by the return alone, so calibrated distillation is the part of reinforcement learning that the teacher supplies. The nonnegative parts of the per-turn projections are also the assignment that gains the most reward in the second-order KL geometry of one update.\nWe instantiate the calibration as *Turn-Level Advantage Calibration in the Trust Region* (**TACT**). The loss already computes the divergence at each position, and adding it to the token log-ratio makes one rollout an unbiased sample of both the covariance and the variance of every turn it visits. TACT therefore estimates the weights from the training batch, pooled over classes of turns, and adds a reinforcement step on the residual in the classes where the signal explains little of the return.\nExperiments on ALFWorld, ScienceWorld, WebShop, Search-QA, and $\\tau$-bench, on the same rollouts for every method, show that TACT exceeds the strongest existing rule on every environment, by 4.9 points on average over the rule that uses the return, at the same cost as on-policy distillation. The code is available at [TACT Codebase](https://anonymous.4open.science/r/TACT_ICLR-BD0D/README.md)."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026naturalgradient,\ntitle={Natural-Gradient Calibration of Multi-Turn On-Policy Distillation},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Pd6IfvSUrd},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Natural-Gradient Calibration of Multi-Turn On-Policy Distillation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f15db841083f0f892425fab338d846d98ec908cb.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794288498852,"tcdate":1787602259912,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission4223/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission4223/Authors"],"forum":"Pd6IfvSUrd","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4223,"cdate":1787602259912,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission4223/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission4223/-/Rebuttal_Revision"],"mdate":1794288498852,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"Pd6IfvSUrd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (Findings) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.979.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|from_observation_to_understanding_frontdoor_adjustments_with_uncertainty_calibration_for_enhancing_egocentric_reasoning_in_lvlms"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shenshen_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenxin_Meng:","~Lei_Wang28","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hao_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chong_Peng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peng_Yan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fumin_Shen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jingkuan_Song:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Heng_Tao_Shen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xing_Xu_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.979/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/LiMWYPYSSS025,\n  author={Shenshen Li and Wenxin Meng and Lei Wang and Hao Yang and Chong Peng and Peng Yan and Fumin Shen and Jingkuan Song and Heng Tao Shen and Xing Xu},\n  title={From Observation to Understanding: Front-Door Adjustments with Uncertainty Calibration for Enhancing Egocentric Reasoning in LVLMs},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={19152-19169},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.979/},\n  booktitle={ACL (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2025f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent progress in large vision-language models (LVLMs) has shown substantial potential across a broad spectrum of third-person tasks. However, adapting these LVLMs to egocentric scenarios remains challenging due to their third-person training bias. Existing methods that adapt LVLMs for first-person tasks often overlook critical agent-environment interactions, limiting their ability to perform egocentric reasoning. To address these challenges, we propose a novel zero-shot paradigm termed Front-Door Adjustments with Uncertainty Calibration (FRUIT) to enhance the egocentric reasoning abilities of LVLMs by simulating human causal reasoning. Specifically, the FRUIT operates in two stages: observation and understanding. Unlike conventional prompting techniques, we formalize egocentric reasoning using a structural causal model. Then, we ground interaction regions and expand them into hierarchical visual cues, augmented with corresponding captions, to form the initial observations. To reduce noise in these observations, we employ uncertainty calibration to filter out unreliable information. These refined observations as mediators are then incorporated into the prompt template, guiding the model to understand semantics from a first-person perspective. Extensive experiments conducted on the EgoThink benchmark demonstrate that our FRUIT method consistently enhances the performance of existing LVLMs on six distinct tasks. Our code is available at https://github.com/Mrshenshen/FRUIT."},"title":{"value":"From Observation to Understanding: Front-Door Adjustments with Uncertainty Calibration for Enhancing Egocentric Reasoning in LVLMs"},"authors":{"value":["Shenshen Li","Wenxin Meng","Lei Wang","Hao Yang","Chong Peng","Peng Yan","Fumin Shen","Jingkuan Song","Heng Tao Shen","Xing Xu"]}},"tmdate":1767679894358,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/LiMWYPYSSS025"],"tcdate":1767679887352,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lei_Wang28"],"forum":"2KwA5yZ2gg","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":718149,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1767679894358,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2KwA5yZ2gg","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"An extensive evaluation of BERTScore for NL->code evaluation"},"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2023 Main"},"Submission_Track_2":{"value":"Resources and Evaluation"},"keywords":{"value":["nl2code","code generation","code","evaluation","codebert","bertscore"]},"abstract":{"value":"Since the rise of neural natural-language-to-code models (NL$\\rightarrow$Code) that can generate long expressions and statements rather than a single next-token, one of the major problems has been reliably evaluating their generated output. \nIn this paper, we propose CodeBERTScore: an evaluation metric for code generation, which builds on BERTScore (Zhang et al., 2020).\nInstead of encoding only the generated tokens as in BERTScore, CodeBERTScore also encodes the natural language input preceding the generated code, thus modeling the consistency between the generated code and its given natural language context as well.    We perform an extensive evaluation of CodeBERTScore across four programming languages. We find that CodeBERTScore achieves a higher correlation with human preference and with functional correctness than all existing metrics. That is, generated code that receives a higher score by CodeBERTScore is more likely to be preferred by humans, as well as to function correctly when executed.\nWe release five language-specific pretrained models to use with our publicly available code.\nOur language-specific models have been downloaded more than **1,000,000** times from the Huggingface Hub. Our code and data are available at https://github.com/neulab/code-bert-score"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhou2023codebertscore,\ntitle={Code{BERTS}core: Evaluating Code Generation with Pretrained Models of Code},\nauthor={Shuyan Zhou and Uri Alon and Sumit Agarwal and Graham Neubig},\nbooktitle={The 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=7cXoueVCoL}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CodeBERTScore: Evaluating Code Generation with Pretrained Models of Code"},"Submission_Type":{"value":"Regular Long Paper"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8729476011fb7acda5c52e3eb8ccedf5fdab2d7d.pdf"},"Submission_Track":{"value":"NLP Applications"},"venueid":{"value":"EMNLP/2023/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|codebertscore_evaluating_code_generation_with_pretrained_models_of_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shuyan_Zhou1","~Uri_Alon1","~Sumit_Agarwal2","~Graham_Neubig1"]},"authors":{"value":["Shuyan Zhou","Uri Alon","Sumit Agarwal","Graham Neubig"]}},"tmdate":1701459772889,"pdate":1696709290569,"tcdate":1686969078389,"writers":["EMNLP/2023/Conference","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission4907/Authors"],"signatures":["EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission4907/Authors"],"forum":"7cXoueVCoL","number":4907,"cdate":1686969078389,"mdate":1701459772889,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Submission","EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Post_Submission","EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Edit","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission4907/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"odate":1701459772873,"domain":"EMNLP/2023/Conference","id":"7cXoueVCoL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Journal of Microwaves"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9171629/10091703/10077200.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"geiss|the_impact_of_antenna_array_calibration_errors_on_mimo_and_multichannel_synthetic_aperture_radar_imaging"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/JMW.2023.3254135"},"abstract":{"value":"In radar imaging, any lateral resolution is either attained using antenna arrays or by synthetic aperture radar (SAR) principles. Recently, the combination of both approaches to a multi-channel SAR (MCSAR) has gained increased attention. However, antenna arrays are impaired by unknown calibration errors, particularly phase errors, amplification deviations, mutual coupling, and phase center position deviation of the antenna element. Since, first, established calibration procedures are often non-ideal and, second, the calibration parameters usually vary over time due to aging and changes in temperature, calibration errors distort the measurements during the radar operation. Therefore, current research focuses on finding new practical calibration procedures. However, hardly any research has been conducted to analyze the basic effects which erroneous calibration parameters have on the reconstructed image in MCSAR or MIMO radar. In this work, we analyze the effects of multiple calibration error types on MCSAR and MIMO radar imaging. Both measurement principles lead to periodic error patterns, resulting in deterministic distortions. The derived relations can be used to precisely predict the position and number of ghost targets caused by calibration errors for known measurement setups and targets. For this, no information about the actual calibration errors is required. Furthermore, worst-case distortion levels are provided for different bounded errors allowing rough estimates of the impact of different error types on the image's distortion level. The findings are validated via measurements using a 76$\\,$GHz FMCW MIMO radar."},"title":{"value":"The Impact of Antenna Array Calibration Errors on MIMO and Multi-Channel Synthetic Aperture Radar Imaging"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Johanna Geiss"},{"fullname":"Erik Sippel"},{"fullname":"Michael Braunwarth"},{"fullname":"Martin Vossiek","username":"~Martin_Vossiek1"}]}},"tmdate":1789091084529,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/jmw.2023.3254135"],"tcdate":1772362359376,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Martin_Vossiek1"],"forum":"bPpSrBnPgD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":50616,"cdate":1679336217655,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091084529,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"bPpSrBnPgD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Instrumentation and Measurement"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/19/10764799/10979544.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhu|targetless_automatic_edgetoedge_extrinsic_calibration_of_lidarcamera_system_based_on_pure_laser_reflectivity"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2025.3560719"},"abstract":{"value":"High-precision calibration of LiDAR and cameras is an essential precondition for multisensor fusion systems. Since controlled targets are not always available in field, targetless calibration methods have emerged as a practical alternative to traditional target-based approaches. However, the complexity and variability of natural scenes pose significant challenges to feature extraction and matching in the targetless calibration process. To address these issues, this article proposes a targetless, automatic edge-to-edge calibration method based solely on laser reflectivity, aimed at enhancing calibration accuracy and feature consistency in the LiDAR-camera calibration (LCC) task. Traditional methods primarily focus on the 3-D information of LiDAR point clouds for feature detection. In contrast, the proposed method leverages the superior capabilities of laser reflectivity to perceive color, depth, and material properties. This strategy enables robust edge feature consistency in 2-D, thereby enhancing overall calibration accuracy. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method reduces translation error by an average 61.9% compared to existing state-of-the-art targetless approaches, offering enhanced feature extraction accuracy and robustness, particularly in complex and unstructured environments."},"title":{"value":"Targetless Automatic Edge-to-Edge Extrinsic Calibration of LiDAR-Camera System Based on Pure Laser Reflectivity"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Weijie Zhu"},{"fullname":"Shuo Shan"},{"fullname":"Chaoliu Tong"},{"fullname":"Kanjian Zhang"},{"fullname":"Haikun Wei","username":"~Haikun_Wei1"}]}},"tmdate":1789091828708,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tim.2025.3560719"],"tcdate":1767450097625,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Haikun_Wei1"],"forum":"OfU6Chsrjh","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":21388,"cdate":1745868799002,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091828708,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"OfU6Chsrjh","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/19/10764799/10979544.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhu|targetless_automatic_edgetoedge_extrinsic_calibration_of_lidarcamera_system_based_on_pure_laser_reflectivity"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weijie_Zhu:","~Shuo_Shan2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chaoliu_Tong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kanjian_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haikun_Wei:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2025.3560719"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/ZhuSTZW25,\n  author={Weijie Zhu and Shuo Shan and Chaoliu Tong and Kanjian Zhang and Haikun Wei},\n  title={Targetless Automatic Edge-to-Edge Extrinsic Calibration of LiDAR-Camera System Based on Pure Laser Reflectivity},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={74},\n  pages={1-14},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2025.3560719}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"High-precision calibration of LiDAR and cameras is an essential precondition for multisensor fusion systems. Since controlled targets are not always available in field, targetless calibration methods have emerged as a practical alternative to traditional target-based approaches. However, the complexity and variability of natural scenes pose significant challenges to feature extraction and matching in the targetless calibration process. To address these issues, this article proposes a targetless, automatic edge-to-edge calibration method based solely on laser reflectivity, aimed at enhancing calibration accuracy and feature consistency in the LiDAR-camera calibration (LCC) task. Traditional methods primarily focus on the 3-D information of LiDAR point clouds for feature detection. In contrast, the proposed method leverages the superior capabilities of laser reflectivity to perceive color, depth, and material properties. This strategy enables robust edge feature consistency in 2-D, thereby enhancing overall calibration accuracy. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method reduces translation error by an average 61.9% compared to existing state-of-the-art targetless approaches, offering enhanced feature extraction accuracy and robustness, particularly in complex and unstructured environments."},"title":{"value":"Targetless Automatic Edge-to-Edge Extrinsic Calibration of LiDAR-Camera System Based on Pure Laser Reflectivity"},"authors":{"value":["Weijie Zhu","Shuo Shan","Chaoliu Tong","Kanjian Zhang","Haikun Wei"]}},"tmdate":1767887112730,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tim/ZhuSTZW25"],"tcdate":1767887110507,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~shuoshan1"],"forum":"OsQSko0jNq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":736979,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1767887112730,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"OsQSko0jNq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/24/4/1127/pdf?version=1707408291"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|adaptive_pointline_fusion_a_targetless_lidarcamera_calibration_method_with_scheme_selection_for_autonomous_driving"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yingtong_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tiansi_Han:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qiong_Nie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuxuan_Zhu_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Minghu_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ning_Bian:","~Zhiheng_Li3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s24041127"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/ZhouHNZLBL24,\n  author={Yingtong Zhou and Tiansi Han and Qiong Nie and Yuxuan Zhu and Minghu Li and Ning Bian and Zhiheng Li},\n  title={Adaptive Point-Line Fusion: A Targetless LiDAR-Camera Calibration Method with Scheme Selection for Autonomous Driving},\n  year={2024},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1706745600000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={24},\n  number={4},\n  pages={1127},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s24041127}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate calibration between LiDAR and camera sensors is crucial for autonomous driving systems to perceive and understand the environment effectively. Typically, LiDAR–camera extrinsic calibration requires feature alignment and overlapping fields of view. Aligning features from different modalities can be challenging due to noise influence. Therefore, this paper proposes a targetless extrinsic calibration method for monocular cameras and LiDAR sensors that have a non-overlapping field of view. The proposed solution uses pose transformation to establish data association across different modalities. This conversion turns the calibration problem into an optimization problem within a visual SLAM system without requiring overlapping views. To improve performance, line features serve as constraints in visual SLAM. Accurate positions of line segments are obtained by utilizing an extended photometric error optimization method. Moreover, a strategy is proposed for selecting appropriate calibration methods from among several alternative optimization schemes. This adaptive calibration method selection strategy ensures robust calibration performance in urban autonomous driving scenarios with varying lighting and environmental textures while avoiding failures and excessive bias that may result from relying on a single approach."},"title":{"value":"Adaptive Point-Line Fusion: A Targetless LiDAR-Camera Calibration Method with Scheme Selection for Autonomous Driving"},"authors":{"value":["Yingtong Zhou","Tiansi Han","Qiong Nie","Yuxuan Zhu","Minghu Li","Ning Bian","Zhiheng Li"]}},"tmdate":1747398730117,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747398718446,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhiheng_Li3"],"forum":"5MJNXql7ec","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":514118,"cdate":1706745600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747398730117,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5MJNXql7ec","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.12074v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"singh|online_refractive_camera_model_calibration_in_visual_inertial_odometry"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohit_Singh:","~Kostas_Alexis1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.12074"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2409-12074,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Mohit Singh and Kostas Alexis},\n  title={Online Refractive Camera Model Calibration in Visual Inertial Odometry},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2409.12074},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.12074}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents a general refractive camera model and online co-estimation of odometry and the refractive index of unknown media. This enables operation in diverse and varying refractive fluids, given only the camera calibration in air. The refractive index is estimated online as a state variable of a monocular visual-inertial odometry framework in an iterative formulation using the proposed camera model. The method was verified on data collected using an underwater robot traversing inside a pool. The evaluations demonstrate convergence to the ideal refractive index for water despite significant perturbations in the initialization. Simultaneously, the approach enables on-par visual-inertial odometry performance in refractive media without prior knowledge of the refractive index or requirement of medium-specific camera calibration."},"title":{"value":"Online Refractive Camera Model Calibration in Visual Inertial Odometry"},"authors":{"value":["Mohit Singh","Kostas Alexis"]}},"tmdate":1737728694114,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1737728691877,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kostas_Alexis1"],"forum":"PsPhT2qELk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":271125,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1737728694114,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"PsPhT2qELk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10801246/10801290/10802302.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"singh|online_refractive_camera_model_calibration_in_visual_inertial_odometry"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohit_Singh:","~Kostas_Alexis1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS58592.2024.10802302"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/SinghA24,\n  author={Mohit Singh and Kostas Alexis},\n  title={Online Refractive Camera Model Calibration in Visual Inertial Odometry},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={12609-12616},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS58592.2024.10802302},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents a general refractive camera model and online co-estimation of odometry and the refractive index of an unknown media. This enables operation in diverse and varying refractive fluids, given only the camera calibration in air. The refractive index is estimated online as a state variable of a monocular visual-inertial odometry framework in an iterative formulation using the proposed camera model. The method was verified on data collected using an underwater robot traversing inside a pool. The evaluations demonstrate convergence to the ideal refractive index for water despite significant perturbations in the initialization. Simultaneously, the approach enables on-par visual-inertial odometry performance in refractive media without prior knowledge of the refractive index or requirement of medium-specific camera calibration."},"title":{"value":"Online Refractive Camera Model Calibration in Visual Inertial Odometry"},"authors":{"value":["Mohit Singh","Kostas Alexis"]}},"tmdate":1737728692760,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1737728690307,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kostas_Alexis1"],"forum":"HEd94GExHx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":271120,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1737728692760,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"HEd94GExHx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10801246/10801290/10802302.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"singh|online_refractive_camera_model_calibration_in_visual_inertial_odometry"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohit_Singh:","~Kostas_Alexis1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10