bration_in_visual_inertial_odometry"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohit_Singh:","~Kostas_Alexis1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS58592.2024.10802302"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/SinghA24,\n  author={Mohit Singh and Kostas Alexis},\n  title={Online Refractive Camera Model Calibration in Visual Inertial Odometry},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={12609-12616},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS58592.2024.10802302},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents a general refractive camera model and online co-estimation of odometry and the refractive index of an unknown media. This enables operation in diverse and varying refractive fluids, given only the camera calibration in air. The refractive index is estimated online as a state variable of a monocular visual-inertial odometry framework in an iterative formulation using the proposed camera model. The method was verified on data collected using an underwater robot traversing inside a pool. The evaluations demonstrate convergence to the ideal refractive index for water despite significant perturbations in the initialization. Simultaneously, the approach enables on-par visual-inertial odometry performance in refractive media without prior knowledge of the refractive index or requirement of medium-specific camera calibration."},"title":{"value":"Online Refractive Camera Model Calibration in Visual Inertial Odometry"},"authors":{"value":["Mohit Singh","Kostas Alexis"]}},"tmdate":1737728652805,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1737728642168,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kostas_Alexis1"],"forum":"jsvFSlBz0L","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":271016,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1737728652805,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"jsvFSlBz0L","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/10012124/10209071.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"aljlailaty|imu_hand_calibration_for_lowcost_mems_inertial_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hussein_Al-Jlailaty:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abdulkadir_Celik:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohammad_M._Mansour:","~Ahmed_M._Eltawil1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2023.3301860"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/AlJlailatyCME23,\n  author={Hussein Al-Jlailaty and Abdulkadir Celik and Mohammad M. Mansour and Ahmed M. Eltawil},\n  title={IMU Hand Calibration for Low-Cost MEMS Inertial Sensors},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={72},\n  pages={1-16},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2023.3301860}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This article proposes a technique to calibrate a low-cost inertial measurement unit (IMU) in the field without relying on expensive laboratory-based equipment. The main parameters to be calibrated in any IMU are misalignment factors, sensitivity scale factors, and gyro and accelerometer biases, all of which are temperature-dependent. Usually, IMU manufacturers have highly accurate and expensive rate tables and centrifuges that can provide high-resolution angular rates to the equipment under test but require costly regular maintenance and highly trained technicians. We propose an alternative method of performing this calibration by hand in the field, using a special Kalman filter that benefits from zero-velocity measurement updates. The performance of the proposed calibration technique is evaluated and validated via extensive simulations and real experiments."},"title":{"value":"IMU Hand Calibration for Low-Cost MEMS Inertial Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Hussein Al-Jlailaty","Abdulkadir Celik","Mohammad M. Mansour","Ahmed M. Eltawil"]}},"tmdate":1731525733320,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731525714799,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ahmed_M._Eltawil1"],"forum":"U6XHZ0BSps","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":234700,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731525733320,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"U6XHZ0BSps","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10203037/10203050/10204180.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|transfer_knowledge_from_head_to_tail_uncertainty_calibration_under_longtailed_distribution"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiahao_Chen:","~Bing_Su1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52729.2023.01913"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/ChenS23a,\n  author={Jiahao Chen and Bing Su},\n  title={Transfer Knowledge from Head to Tail: Uncertainty Calibration under Long-tailed Distribution},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={19978-19987},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52729.2023.01913},\n  booktitle={CVPR},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"How to estimate the uncertainty of a given model is a crucial problem. Current calibration techniques treat different classes equally and thus implicitly assume that the distribution of training data is balanced, but ignore the fact that real-world data often follows a long-tailed distribution. In this paper, we explore the problem of calibrating the model trained from a long-tailed distribution. Due to the difference between the imbalanced training distribution and balanced test distribution, existing calibration methods such as temperature scaling can not generalize well to this problem. Specific calibration methods for domain adaptation are also not applicable because they rely on unlabeled target domain instances which are not available. Models trained from a long-tailed distribution tend to be more over-confident to head classes. To this end, we propose a novel knowledge-transferring-based calibration method by estimating the importance weights for samples of tail classes to realize long-tailed calibration. Our method models the distribution of each class as a Gaussian distribution and views the source statistics of head classes as a prior to calibrate the target distributions of tail classes. We adaptively transfer knowledge from head classes to get the target probability density of tail classes. The importance weight is estimated by the ratio of the target probability density over the source probability density. Extensive experiments on CIFAR-10-LT, MNIST-LT, CIFAR-100-LT, and ImageNet-LT datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of our method."},"title":{"value":"Transfer Knowledge from Head to Tail: Uncertainty Calibration under Long-tailed Distribution"},"authors":{"value":["Jiahao Chen","Bing Su"]}},"tmdate":1744716524165,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1744716519023,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bing_Su1"],"forum":"KjMNUROJtu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":397459,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744716524165,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KjMNUROJtu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10203037/10203050/10204180.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|transfer_knowledge_from_head_to_tail_uncertainty_calibration_under_longtailed_distribution"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiahao_Chen:","~Bing_Su1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52729.2023.01913"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/ChenS23a,\n  author={Jiahao Chen and Bing Su},\n  title={Transfer Knowledge from Head to Tail: Uncertainty Calibration under Long-tailed Distribution},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={19978-19987},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52729.2023.01913},\n  booktitle={CVPR},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"How to estimate the uncertainty of a given model is a crucial problem. Current calibration techniques treat different classes equally and thus implicitly assume that the distribution of training data is balanced, but ignore the fact that real-world data often follows a long-tailed distribution. In this paper, we explore the problem of calibrating the model trained from a long-tailed distribution. Due to the difference between the imbalanced training distribution and balanced test distribution, existing calibration methods such as temperature scaling can not generalize well to this problem. Specific calibration methods for domain adaptation are also not applicable because they rely on unlabeled target domain instances which are not available. Models trained from a long-tailed distribution tend to be more over-confident to head classes. To this end, we propose a novel knowledge-transferring-based calibration method by estimating the importance weights for samples of tail classes to realize long-tailed calibration. Our method models the distribution of each class as a Gaussian distribution and views the source statistics of head classes as a prior to calibrate the target distributions of tail classes. We adaptively transfer knowledge from head classes to get the target probability density of tail classes. The importance weight is estimated by the ratio of the target probability density over the source probability density. Extensive experiments on CIFAR-10-LT, MNIST-LT, CIFAR-100-LT, and ImageNet-LT datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of our method."},"title":{"value":"Transfer Knowledge from Head to Tail: Uncertainty Calibration under Long-tailed Distribution"},"authors":{"value":["Jiahao Chen","Bing Su"]}},"tmdate":1715045563574,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1715045554885,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bing_Su1"],"forum":"x9YPGciSQP","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":6583,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1715045563574,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"x9YPGciSQP","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/20/16/4577/pdf?version=1597888216"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"delaine|framework_for_the_simulation_of_sensor_networks_aimed_at_evaluating_in_situ_calibration_algorithms"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Florentin_Delaine:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bérengère_Lebental:","~Herve_Rivano1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s20164577"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/DelaineLR20,\n  author={Florentin Delaine and Bérengère Lebental and Hervé Rivano},\n  title={Framework for the Simulation of Sensor Networks Aimed at Evaluating In Situ Calibration Algorithms},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={20},\n  number={16},\n  pages={4577},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s20164577}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The drastically increasing availability of low-cost sensors for environmental monitoring has fostered a large interest in the literature. One particular challenge for such devices is the fast degradation over time of the quality of their data. Therefore, the instruments require frequent calibrations. Traditionally, this operation is carried out on each sensor in dedicated laboratories. This is not economically sustainable for dense networks of low-cost sensors. An alternative that has been investigated is in situ calibration: exploiting the properties of the sensor network, the instruments are calibrated while staying in the field and preferably without any physical intervention. The literature indicates there is wide variety of in situ calibration strategies depending on the type of sensor network deployed. However, there is a lack for a systematic benchmark of calibration algorithms. In this paper, we propose the first framework for the simulation of sensor networks enabling a systematic comparison of in situ calibration strategies with reproducibility, and scalability. We showcase it on a primary test case applied to several calibration strategies for blind and static sensor networks. The performances of calibration are shown to be tightly related to the deployment of the network itself, the parameters of the algorithm and the metrics used to evaluate the results. We study the impact of the main modelling choices and adjustments of parameters in our framework and highlight their influence on the results of the calibration algorithms. We also show how our framework can be used as a tool for the design of a network of low-cost sensors."},"title":{"value":"Framework for the Simulation of Sensor Networks Aimed at Evaluating In Situ Calibration Algorithms"},"authors":{"value":["Florentin Delaine","Bérengère Lebental","Hervé Rivano"]}},"tmdate":1753523446071,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1753523419954,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Herve_Rivano1"],"forum":"zrNjxtRAsv","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":592748,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753523446071,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zrNjxtRAsv","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/20/3/639/pdf?version=1580783446"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|a_rangeindependent_disparitybased_calibration_model_for_structured_light_patternbased_rgbd_sensor"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenbin_Li_0010:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yaxin_Li_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Walid_Darwish:","~Shengjun_Tang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuling_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wu_Chen_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s20030639"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/LiLDTHC20,\n  author={Wenbin Li and Yaxin Li and Walid Darwish and Shengjun Tang and Yuling Hu and Wu Chen},\n  title={A Range-Independent Disparity-Based Calibration Model for Structured Light Pattern-Based RGBD Sensor},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={20},\n  number={3},\n  pages={639},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s20030639}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Consumer-grade RGBD sensors that provide both colour and depth information have many potential applications, such as robotics control, localization, and mapping, due to their low cost and simple operation. However, the depth measurement provided by consumer-grade RGBD sensors is still inadequate for many high-precision applications, such as rich 3D reconstruction, accurate object recognition and precise localization, due to the fact that the systematic errors of RGB sensors increase exponentially with the ranging distance. Most existing calibration models for depth measurement must be carried out with different distances. In this paper, we reveal the mechanism of how an infrared (IR) camera and IR projector contribute to the overall non-centrosymmetric distortion of a structured light pattern-based RGBD sensor. Then, a new two-step calibration method for RGBD sensors based on the disparity measurement is proposed, which is range-independent and has full frame coverage. Three independent calibration models are used for the calibration for the three main components of the RGBD sensor errors: the infrared camera distortion, the infrared projection distortion, and the infrared cone-caused bias. Experiments show the proposed calibration method can provide precise calibration results in full-range and full-frame coverage of depth measurement. The offset in the edge area of long-range depth (8 m) is reduced from 86 cm to 30 cm, and the relative error is reduced from 11% to 3% of the range distance. Overall, at far range the proposed calibration method can improve the depth accuracy by 70% in the central region of depth frame and 65% in the edge region."},"title":{"value":"A Range-Independent Disparity-Based Calibration Model for Structured Light Pattern-Based RGBD Sensor"},"authors":{"value":["Wenbin Li","Yaxin Li","Walid Darwish","Shengjun Tang","Yuling Hu","Wu Chen"]}},"tmdate":1744100455270,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1744100443876,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shengjun_Tang1"],"forum":"jovKfiFrs3","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":381541,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744100455270,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"jovKfiFrs3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Medical Image Anal. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1361841523001214/pdfft?md5=534012105ff1462024f2df60098912b4&pid=1-s2.0-S1361841523001214-main.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/MIA/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"dawood|uncertainty_aware_training_to_improve_deep_learning_model_calibration_for_classification_of_cardiac_mr_images"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tareen_Dawood:","~Chen_Chen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Baldeep_S._Sidhu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bram_Ruijsink:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Justin_Gould:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bradley_Porter:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mark_K._Elliott:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Vishal_Mehta:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Christopher_A._Rinaldi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Esther_Puyol-Antón:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Reza_Razavi:","~Andrew_P._King1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.media.2023.102861"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/mia/DawoodCSRGPEMRPRK23,\n  author={Tareen Dawood and Chen Chen and Baldeep S. Sidhu and Bram Ruijsink and Justin Gould and Bradley Porter and Mark K. Elliott and Vishal Mehta and Christopher A. Rinaldi and Esther Puyol-Antón and Reza Razavi and Andrew P. King},\n  title={Uncertainty aware training to improve deep learning model calibration for classification of cardiac MR images},\n  year={2023},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1690848000000},\n  journal={Medical Image Anal.},\n  volume={88},\n  pages={102861},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.media.2023.102861}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•Propose three novel uncertainty-aware training strategies.•Compare with state-of-the-art methods on two medical image classification tasks.•Report a wide range of accuracy and calibration performance measures.•Quantify performance of all models in terms of aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty.•Evaluate use of calibration-based model selection on accuracy and calibration."},"title":{"value":"Uncertainty aware training to improve deep learning model calibration for classification of cardiac MR images"},"authors":{"value":["Tareen Dawood","Chen Chen","Baldeep S. Sidhu","Bram Ruijsink","Justin Gould","Bradley Porter","Mark K. Elliott","Vishal Mehta","Christopher A. Rinaldi","Esther Puyol-Antón","Reza Razavi","Andrew P. King"]}},"tmdate":1772172820223,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731534087903,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chen_Chen23"],"forum":"KwqMF854EF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":235800,"cdate":1690848000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772172820223,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KwqMF854EF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/4420/4298"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AAAI/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"shi|automatic_code_review_by_learning_the_revision_of_source_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shu-Ting_Shi:","~Ming_Li1","~David_Lo1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ferdian_Thung:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuan_Huo:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v33i01.33014910"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aaai/ShiL0TH19,\n  author={Shu-Ting Shi and Ming Li and David Lo and Ferdian Thung and Xuan Huo},\n  title={Automatic Code Review by Learning the Revision of Source Code},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  pages={4910-4917},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v33i01.33014910},\n  booktitle={AAAI},\n  crossref={conf/aaai/2019}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code review is the process of manual inspection on the revision of the source code in order to find out whether the revised source code eventually meets the revision requirements. However, manual code review is time-consuming, and automating such the code review process will alleviate the burden of code reviewers and speed up the software maintenance process. To construct the model for automatic code review, the characteristics of the revisions of source code (i.e., the difference between the two pieces of source code) should be properly captured and modeled. Unfortunately, most of the existing techniques can easily model the overall correlation between two pieces of source code, but not for the “difference” between two pieces of source code. In this paper, we propose a novel deep model named DACE for automatic code review. Such a model is able to learn revision features by contrasting the revised hunks from the original and revised source code with respect to the code context containing the hunks. Experimental results on six open source software projects indicate by learning the revision features, DACE can outperform the competing approaches in automatic code review."},"title":{"value":"Automatic Code Review by Learning the Revision of Source Code"},"authors":{"value":["Shu-Ting Shi","Ming Li","David Lo","Ferdian Thung","Xuan Huo"]}},"tmdate":1744863859184,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1727720453748,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ming_Li1"],"forum":"9smMm178vK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":122714,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1744863859184,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9smMm178vK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"PerCom Workshops 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7452853/7457013/07457062.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/PERCOM/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"simonjan|autonomous_lightweight_calibration_of_visual_sensor_networks_with_dense_coverage"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jennifer_Simonjan:","~Bernhard_Rinner2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/PERCOMW.2016.7457062"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/percom/SimonjanR16,\n  author={Jennifer Simonjan and Bernhard Rinner},\n  title={Autonomous, lightweight calibration of visual sensor networks with dense coverage},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  pages={1-4},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/PERCOMW.2016.7457062},\n  booktitle={PerCom Workshops},\n  crossref={conf/percom/2016w}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present an algorithm for autonomous network calibration of visual sensor networks, which become more and more pervasive since they can be found in various everyday life environments. The proposed algorithm works in a fully decentralized way and minimizes usage of cost-intensive vision algorithms. To achieve network calibration, our approach relies on jointly detected objects and geometric relations between camera nodes. Distances and angles are the only information required to be exchanged between nodes. The process works iteratively until cameras have determined the relative position and orientation of their neighbors. Preliminary results are demonstrated using our visual sensor network simulator."},"title":{"value":"Autonomous, lightweight calibration of visual sensor networks with dense coverage"},"authors":{"value":["Jennifer Simonjan","Bernhard Rinner"]}},"tmdate":1731564490317,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1731564459395,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bernhard_Rinner2"],"forum":"9hpDYN170o","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":238468,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731564490317,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9hpDYN170o","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACM Multimedia 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MM/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"liao|calibrbev_multicamera_calibration_via_reversed_birdseyeview_representations_for_autonomous_driving"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenlong_Liao:","","~Xianfei_Li1","~Xiaolei_Chen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haoyu_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yanyan_Liang_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junchi_Yan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_He:","~Pai_Peng2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3664647.3680572"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/mm/LiaoQLCWLYHP24,\n  author={Wenlong Liao and Sunyuan Qiang and Xianfei Li and Xiaolei Chen and Haoyu Wang and Yanyan Liang and Junchi Yan and Tao He and Pai Peng},\n  title={CalibRBEV: Multi-Camera Calibration via Reversed Bird's-eye-view Representations for Autonomous Driving},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={9145-9154},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3664647.3680572},\n  booktitle={ACM Multimedia},\n  crossref={conf/mm/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration is crucial in computer vision tasks and applications, e.g., autonomous driving (AD). However, prevailing camera calibration models pose a time-consuming and labor-intensive off-board process in mass production settings, while simultaneously lacking exploration of real-world AD scenarios. To this end, inspired by recent advancements in bird's-eye-view (BEV) perception models, this paper proposes a novel multi-camera Calibration method via Reversed BEV representations for AD, termed CalibRBEV. Specifically, the proposed CalibRBEV model primarily comprises two stages. Initially, we innovatively reverse the BEV perception pipeline, reconstructing bounding boxes through an attention auto-encoder module to fully extract the latent reversed BEV representations. Subsequently, the obtained representations from encoder are interacted with the surrounding multi-view image features for further refinement and calibration parameters prediction. Extensive experimental results on nuScenes and Waymo datasets validate the effectiveness of our proposed model."},"title":{"value":"CalibRBEV: Multi-Camera Calibration via Reversed Bird's-eye-view Representations for Autonomous Driving"},"authors":{"value":["Wenlong Liao","Sunyuan Qiang","Xianfei Li","Xiaolei Chen","Haoyu Wang","Yanyan Liang","Junchi Yan","Tao He","Pai Peng"]}},"tmdate":1762875110262,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1739631074895,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiaolei_Chen1"],"forum":"ipOhn7QiAY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":322448,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762875110262,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ipOhn7QiAY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SBQS 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://sol.sbc.org.br/index.php/sbqs/article/download/39053/38825"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"uchoa|exploring_code_clone_behavior_in_modern_code_review"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqs.2025.15740"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/sbqs/UchoaSPMUR25,\n  author={Italo Uchoa and Denis Sousa and Matheus Paixão and Pedro Maia and Anderson G. Uchôa and Chaiyong Ragkhitwetsagul},\n  title={Exploring Code Clone Behavior in Modern Code Review},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={606-608},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.5753/sbqs.2025.15740},\n  booktitle={SBQS},\n  crossref={conf/sbqs/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Resumo The Modern Code Review (MCR) process is iterative and asynchronous, enabling the early identification of issues during development. One of the main challenges in this context is the presence of code clones, fragments of code copied with small modifications that hinder maintainability. In this study, we analyzed 80k revisions from the CROP dataset using the Siamese detector, identifying 27,656 relevant clones across six systems. A manual validation indicated a predominance of Type-I (46.7%) and Type-III (45.3%) clones. We also identified 224 reviews in which clones appeared in a single revision (Single), 1,258 reviews in which clones appeared across multiple revisions (Recurring), and 236 reviews at the intersection of both categories. To deepen the analysis, we introduced two metrics, Duration and Distance, to assess how clones are introduced or removed during the review. This paper presents an expanded abstract of the study “An Exploratory Study on the lifecycle of Code Clones During Code Review”, published at SBES 2025."},"title":{"value":"Exploring Code Clone Behavior in Modern Code Review"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Italo Uchoa","username":""},{"fullname":"Denis Sousa","username":""},{"fullname":"Matheus Paixão","username":""},{"fullname":"Pedro Maia","username":""},{"fullname":"Anderson G. Uchôa","username":""},{"fullname":"Chaiyong Ragkhitwetsagul","username":"~Chaiyong_Ragkhitwetsagul1"}]}},"tmdate":1785897588929,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/sbqs/UchoaSPMUR25"],"tcdate":1785897548384,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Chaiyong_Ragkhitwetsagul1"],"forum":"KmzrisPzOa","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":120983,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785897588929,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"KmzrisPzOa","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"DualQuant finds optimal dual scales for weight quantization via closed-form MSE minimization; column scales serve as activation rescalings, matching SmoothQuant across INT4, MX, NVFP4 without calibration; validated on real packed W4A4 hardware."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["post-training quantization","dual-scale quantization","calibration-free","low-bit inference","efficient inference","block coordinate descent","weight-reconstruction error","block data format"]},"primary_area":{"value":"optimization"},"abstract":{"value":"Low-bit inference on accelerators with native 4-bit support requires quantizing both weights and activations to realize the full throughput gains these formats provide. While post-training weight quantization has been extensively studied, activation quantization remains considerably more challenging and typically requires calibration data. In this work, we introduce DUALQUANT, a dual-scale,\ncalibration-free post-training quantization framework that directly minimizes the weight-reconstruction Mean Squared Error (MSE) and, as an immediate by-product, yields per-channel activation rescalings prior to activation quantization. Our key observation is that the scaling factors obtained by minimizing weight-reconstruction error also provide effective activation rescalings, enabling competitive activation quantization without calibration data. We formulate DUALQUANT as a non-differentiable weight-reconstruction problem and develop an efficient solver based on variable splitting and block coordinate descent, where each subproblem admits a closed-form update. By treating the quantizer as a black box and projecting scales onto their format-specific admissible set after each update, DUALQUANT provides a unified algorithm supporting a wide range of formats, including INT4, INT8, the full MX family, and NVFP4. To our knowledge, DUALQUANT is the first dual-scaling method to formally support MX and NV block formats. Across Llama and Qwen models evaluated on WikiText-2, C4, and zero-shot downstream tasks, DUALQUANT matches or exceeds the accuracy of calibration-based SmoothQuant across most models and formats, and substantially outperforms calibration-free baselines, with particularly large gains for MX and NV block formats. We further validate these gains with a real packed W4A4 inference path for Llama-3.1-8B on an RTX 4090. The resulting implementation closely matches the accuracy of fake quantization while delivering up to 2.61× memory reduction and up to 2.46× throughput speedup over BF16. Our anonymized codebase is available https://anonymous.4open.science/r/dualquant-69B3/README.md."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026dualquant,\ntitle={{DUALQUANT}: Dual-Scale Calibration-Free Weight-and-Activation Quantization for {LLM}s},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=DuDwRhNU6j},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"DUALQUANT: Dual-Scale Calibration-Free Weight-and-Activation Quantization for LLMs"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/9b1d9dc7856e733bb721dc3a59137530bbbd29ca.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791234307288,"tcdate":1789771180028,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission49818/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission49818/Authors"],"forum":"DuDwRhNU6j","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":49818,"cdate":1789771180028,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission49818/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing"],"mdate":1791234307288,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"DuDwRhNU6j","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2005.08420v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"peng|a_visual_kinematics_calibration_method_for_manipulator_based_on_nonlinear_optimization"},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08420"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2005-08420,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Gang Peng and Zhihao Wang and Jin Yang and Xinde Li},\n  title={A Visual Kinematics Calibration Method for Manipulator Based on Nonlinear Optimization},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2005.08420},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.08420}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The traditional kinematic calibration method for manipulators requires precise three-dimensional measuring instruments to measure the end pose, which is not only expensive due to the high cost of the measuring instruments but also not applicable to all manipulators. Another calibration method uses a camera, but the system error caused by the camera's parameters affects the calibration accuracy of the kinematics of the robot arm. Therefore, this paper proposes a method for calibrating the geometric parameters of a kinematic model of a manipulator based on monocular vision. Firstly, the classic Denavit-Hartenberg(D-H) modeling method is used to establish the kinematic parameters of the manipulator. Secondly, nonlinear optimization and parameter compensation are performed. The three-dimensional positions of the feature points of the calibration plate under each manipulator attitude corresponding to the actual kinematic model and the classic D-H kinematic model are mapped into the pixel coordinate system, and the sum of Euclidean distance errors of the pixel coordinates of the two is used as the objective function to be optimized. The experimental results show that the pixel deviation of the end pose corresponding to the optimized D-H kinematic model proposed in this paper and the end pose corresponding to the actual kinematic model in the pixel coordinate system is 0.99 pixels. Compared with the 7.9 deviation pixels between the pixel coordinates calculated by the classic D-H kinematic model and the actual pixel coordinates, the deviation is reduced by nearly 7 pixels for an 87% reduction in error. Therefore, the proposed method can effectively avoid system errors caused by camera parameters in visual calibration, can improve the absolute positioning accuracy of the end of the robotic arm, and has good economy and universality."},"title":{"value":"A Visual Kinematics Calibration Method for Manipulator Based on Nonlinear Optimization"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Gang Peng","username":""},{"fullname":"Zhihao Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Jin Yang","username":""},{"fullname":"Xinde Li","username":"~Xinde_Li1"}]}},"tmdate":1784821565272,"pdate":1609372800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2005-08420"],"tcdate":1784821541219,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Xinde_Li1"],"forum":"drhgoS0jgr","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":97177,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784821565272,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"drhgoS0jgr","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.09534v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"grizou|ifttpin_a_pinentry_method_leveraging_the_selfcalibration_paradigm"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jonathan_Grizou1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2205.09534"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2205-09534,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jonathan Grizou},\n  title={IFTT-PIN: A PIN-Entry Method Leveraging the Self-Calibration Paradigm},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2205.09534},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2205.09534}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"IFTT-PIN is a self-calibrating version of the PIN-entry method introduced in Roth et al. (2004) [1]. In [1], digits are split into two sets and assigned a color respectively. To communicate their digit, users press the button with the same color that is assigned to their digit, which can thus be identified by elimination after a few iterations. IFTT-PIN uses the same principle but does not pre-assign colors to each button. Instead, users are free to choose which button to use for each color. The button-to-color mapping only exists in the user's mind and is never directly communicated to the interface. In other words, IFTT-PIN infers both the user's PIN and their preferred button-to-color mapping at the same time, a process called self-calibration. In this paper, we present online interactive demonstrations of IFTT-PIN with and without self-calibration and introduce the key concepts and assumptions making self-calibration possible. IFTT-PIN can be tested at https://jgrizou.github.io/IFTT-PIN/ with a video introduction available at https://youtu.be/5I1ibPJdLHM. We review related work in the field of brain-computer interface and further propose self-calibration as a novel approach to protect users against shoulder surfing attacks. Finally, we introduce a vault cracking challenge as a test of usability and security that was informally tested at our institute. With IFTT-PIN, we wish to demonstrate a new interactive experience where users can decide actively and on-the-fly how to use an interface. The self-calibration paradigm might lead to novel opportunities for interaction in other applications or domains. We hope this work will inspire the community to invent them."},"title":{"value":"IFTT-PIN: A PIN-Entry Method Leveraging the Self-Calibration Paradigm"},"authors":{"value":["Jonathan Grizou"]}},"tmdate":1747346902868,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1747346897529,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jonathan_Grizou1"],"forum":"yE7e0qKUlu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":499359,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747346902868,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"yE7e0qKUlu","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"calibration/uncertainty"},"languages_studied":{"value":"Not specific to any particular language"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2024 April Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2024uncertainty,\ntitle={Uncertainty in Language Models: Assessment through Rank-Calibration},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - April 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=i4Gm9K7mm1},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Uncertainty in Language Models: Assessment through Rank-Calibration"},"section_2_permission_to_publish_peer_reviewers_content_agreement":{"value":"Authors decline to grant permission for ACL to publish peer reviewers' content"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability"]},"abstract":{"value":"Language Models (LMs) have shown promising performance in natural language generation.\nHowever, as LMs often generate incorrect or hallucinated\nresponses, it is crucial to correctly quantify their uncertainty in responding to given inputs. \nIn addition to verbalized confidence elicited via prompting, \nmany uncertainty measures (e.g., semantic entropy and affinity-graph-based measures) have been proposed. \nHowever, these measures can differ greatly, and it is unclear how to compare them, partly because they take values over different ranges (e.g., $[0,\\infty)$ or $[0,1]$). \nIn this work, we address this issue by developing a novel and practical framework, termed *Rank-Calibration*, to assess uncertainty and confidence measures for LMs.\nOur key tenet is that higher\nuncertainty (or lower confidence) should imply lower generation quality, on average.\nRank-calibration quantifies deviations from this ideal relationship in a principled manner, without requiring ad hoc binary thresholding of the correctness score (e.g., ROUGE or METEOR).\nThe broad applicability and the granular interpretability of our methods are demonstrated empirically."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/916f02476b188f7ed94de664ae1142d9d2dfe7ed.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Interpretability and Analysis of Models for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794063131010,"tcdate":1712690195020,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission31/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission31/Authors"],"forum":"i4Gm9K7mm1","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":31,"cdate":1712690195020,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794063131010,"odate":1713279540366,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April","id":"i4Gm9K7mm1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.03163v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|uncertainty_in_language_models_assessment_through_rankcalibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xinmeng_Huang1","~Shuo_Li7","","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Matteo_Sesia:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hamed_Hassani:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Insup_Lee_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Osbert_Bastani:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Edgar_Dobriban:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.03163"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2404-03163,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Xinmeng Huang and Shuo Li and Mengxin Yu and Matteo Sesia and Hamed Hassani and Insup Lee and Osbert Bastani and Edgar Dobriban},\n  title={Uncertainty in Language Models: Assessment through Rank-Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2404.03163},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.03163}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Language Models (LMs) have shown promising performance in natural language generation. However, as LMs often generate incorrect or hallucinated responses, it is crucial to correctly quantify their uncertainty in responding to given inputs. In addition to verbalized confidence elicited via prompting, many uncertainty measures ($e.g.$, semantic entropy and affinity-graph-based measures) have been proposed. However, these measures can differ greatly, and it is unclear how to compare them, partly because they take values over different ranges ($e.g.$, $[0,\\infty)$ or $[0,1]$). In this work, we address this issue by developing a novel and practical framework, termed $Rank$-$Calibration$, to assess uncertainty and confidence measures for LMs. Our key tenet is that higher uncertainty (or lower confidence) should imply lower generation quality, on average. Rank-calibration quantifies deviations from this ideal relationship in a principled manner, without requiring ad hoc binary thresholding of the correctness score ($e.g.$, ROUGE or METEOR). The broad applicability and the granular interpretability of our methods are demonstrated empirically."},"title":{"value":"Uncertainty in Language Models: Assessment through Rank-Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Xinmeng Huang","Shuo Li","Mengxin Yu","Matteo Sesia","Hamed Hassani","Insup Lee","Osbert Bastani","Edgar Dobriban"]}},"tmdate":1766551428377,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727710972910,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shuo_Li7"],"forum":"h6rncelQp1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":120369,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1766551428377,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"h6rncelQp1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9187508/9196508/09197581.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"fan|stereo_visual_inertial_odometry_with_online_baseline_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ruofu_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yinian_Mao:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA40945.2020.9197581"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/FanWM20,\n  author={Yunfei Fan and Ruofu Wang and Yinian Mao},\n  title={Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry with Online Baseline Calibration},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={1084-1090},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA40945.2020.9197581},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Stereo-vision devices have rigorous requirements for extrinsic parameter calibration. In Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry (VIO), inaccuracy in or changes to camera extrinsic parameters may lead to serious degradation in estimation performance. In this manuscript, we propose an online calibration method for stereo VIO extrinsic parameters correction. In particular, we focus on Multi-State Constraint Kalman Filter (MSCKF [1]) framework to implement our method. The key component is to formulate stereo extrinsic parameters as part of the state variables and model the Jacobian of feature reprojection error with respect to stereo extrinsic parameters as sub-block of update Jacobian. Therefore we can estimate stereo extrinsic parameters simultaneously with inertial measurement unit (IMU) states and camera poses. Experiments on EuRoC dataset and real-world outdoor dataset demonstrate that the proposed algorithm produce higher positioning accuracy than the original S-MSCKF [2], and the noise of camera extrinsic parameters are self-corrected within the system."},"title":{"value":"Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry with Online Baseline Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Yunfei Fan","Ruofu Wang","Yinian Mao"]}},"tmdate":1762774152870,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1747353522422,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunfei_Fan1"],"forum":"eZszf85wlG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":500548,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762774152870,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"eZszf85wlG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9187508/9196508/09197581.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"fan|stereo_visual_inertial_odometry_with_online_baseline_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ruofu_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yinian_Mao:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA40945.2020.9197581"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/FanWM20,\n  author={Yunfei Fan and Ruofu Wang and Yinian Mao},\n  title={Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry with Online Baseline Calibration},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={1084-1090},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA40945.2020.9197581},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Stereo-vision devices have rigorous requirements for extrinsic parameter calibration. In Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry (VIO), inaccuracy in or changes to camera extrinsic parameters may lead to serious degradation in estimation performance. In this manuscript, we propose an online calibration method for stereo VIO extrinsic parameters correction. In particular, we focus on Multi-State Constraint Kalman Filter (MSCKF [1]) framework to implement our method. The key component is to formulate stereo extrinsic parameters as part of the state variables and model the Jacobian of feature reprojection error with respect to stereo extrinsic parameters as sub-block of update Jacobian. Therefore we can estimate stereo extrinsic parameters simultaneously with inertial measurement unit (IMU) states and camera poses. Experiments on EuRoC dataset and real-world outdoor dataset demonstrate that the proposed algorithm produce higher positioning accuracy than the original S-MSCKF [2], and the noise of camera extrinsic parameters are self-corrected within the system."},"title":{"value":"Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry with Online Baseline Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Yunfei Fan","Ruofu Wang","Yinian Mao"]}},"tmdate":1762774152769,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1751595690453,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunfei_Fan1"],"forum":"Ju177KDKtY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":565887,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762774152769,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Ju177KDKtY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9187508/9196508/09197581.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"fan|stereo_visual_inertial_odometry_with_online_baseline_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ruofu_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yinian_Mao:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA40945.2020.9197581"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/FanWM20,\n  author={Yunfei Fan and Ruofu Wang and Yinian Mao},\n  title={Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry with Online Baseline Calibration},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={1084-1090},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA40945.2020.9197581},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Stereo-vision devices have rigorous requirements for extrinsic parameter calibration. In Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry (VIO), inaccuracy in or changes to camera extrinsic parameters may lead to serious degradation in estimation performance. In this manuscript, we propose an online calibration method for stereo VIO extrinsic parameters correction. In particular, we focus on Multi-State Constraint Kalman Filter (MSCKF [1]) framework to implement our method. The key component is to formulate stereo extrinsic parameters as part of the state variables and model the Jacobian of feature reprojection error with respect to stereo extrinsic parameters as sub-block of update Jacobian. Therefore we can estimate stereo extrinsic parameters simultaneously with inertial measurement unit (IMU) states and camera poses. Experiments on EuRoC dataset and real-world outdoor dataset demonstrate that the proposed algorithm produce higher positioning accuracy than the original S-MSCKF [2], and the noise of camera extrinsic parameters are self-corrected within the system."},"title":{"value":"Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry with Online Baseline Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Yunfei Fan","Ruofu Wang","Yinian Mao"]}},"tmdate":1762774152744,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1747353127132,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunfei_Fan1"],"forum":"iQ4YachYGw","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":500470,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762774152744,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"iQ4YachYGw","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.14398v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"kurz|languagespecific_calibration_for_pruning_multilingual_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Simon_Kurz1","~Jian-Jia_Chen1","~Lucie_Flek1","~Zhixue_Zhao2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.14398"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2408-14398,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Simon Kurz and Jian-Jia Chen and Lucie Flek and Zhixue Zhao},\n  title={Language-specific Calibration for Pruning Multilingual Language Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2408.14398},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.14398}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent advances in large language model (LLM) pruning have shown state-of-the-art compression results in post-training and retraining-free settings while maintaining high predictive performance. However, such research mainly considers calibrating pruning using English text, despite the multilingual nature of modern LLMs and their frequent uses in non-English languages. In this paper, we set out to explore effective strategies for calibrating the pruning of multilingual language models. We present the first comprehensive empirical study, comparing different calibration languages for pruning multilingual models across diverse tasks, models, and state-of-the-art pruning techniques. Our results present practical suggestions, for example, calibrating in the target language can efficiently yield lower perplexity, but does not necessarily benefit downstream tasks. Our further analysis experiments unveil that calibration in the target language mainly contributes to preserving language-specific features related to fluency and coherence, but might not contribute to capturing language-agnostic features such as language understanding and reasoning. Last, we provide practical recommendations for future practitioners."},"title":{"value":"Language-specific Calibration for Pruning Multilingual Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Simon Kurz","Jian-Jia Chen","Lucie Flek","Zhixue Zhao"]}},"tmdate":1747831457648,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727703389629,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~ZHIXUE_ZHAO1"],"forum":"0rt2Hqp1BX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":115824,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747831457648,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0rt2Hqp1BX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICANN (4) 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-030-86380-7_16.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICANN/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|classsimilarity_based_label_smoothing_for_confidence_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chihuang_Liu1","~Joseph_F._JáJá1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-86380-7_16"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icann/LiuJ21,\n  author={Chihuang Liu and Joseph F. JáJá},\n  title={Class-Similarity Based Label Smoothing for Confidence Calibration},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={190-201},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-86380-7_16},\n  booktitle={ICANN (4)},\n  crossref={conf/icann/2021-4}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Generating confidence calibrated outputs is of utmost importance for the applications of deep neural networks in safety-critical decision-making systems. The output of a neural network is a probability distribution where the scores are estimated confidences of the input belonging to the corresponding classes, and hence they represent a complete estimate of the output likelihood relative to all classes. In this paper, we propose a novel form of label smoothing to improve confidence calibration. Since different classes are of different intrinsic similarities, more similar classes should result in closer probability values in the final output. This motivates the development of a new smooth label where the label values are based on similarities with the reference class. We adopt different similarity measurements, including those that capture feature-based similarities or semantic similarity. We demonstrate through extensive experiments, on various datasets and network architectures, that our approach consistently outperforms state-of-the-art calibration techniques including uniform label smoothing."},"title":{"value":"Class-Similarity Based Label Smoothing for Confidence Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Chihuang Liu","Joseph F. JáJá"]}},"tmdate":1747338462765,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1738817791722,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chihuang_Liu1"],"forum":"P7XOJvgyrI","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":300787,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747338462765,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"P7XOJvgyrI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICPR 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9411940/9411911/09413010.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICPR/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"patel|onmanifold_adversarial_data_augmentation_improves_uncertainty_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kanil_Patel:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:William_Beluch:","~Dan_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Michael_Pfeiffer_0001:","~Bin_Yang5"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPR48806.2021.9413010"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icpr/PatelBZ0Y20,\n  author={Kanil Patel and William Beluch and Dan Zhang and Michael Pfeiffer and Bin Yang},\n  title={On-manifold Adversarial Data Augmentation Improves Uncertainty Calibration},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={8029-8036},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPR48806.2021.9413010},\n  booktitle={ICPR},\n  crossref={conf/icpr/2020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Uncertainty estimates help to identify ambiguous, novel, or anomalous inputs, but the reliable quantification of uncertainty has proven to be challenging for modern deep networks. To improve uncertainty estimation, we propose On-Manifold Adversarial Data Augmentation or OMADA, which specifically attempts to generate challenging examples by following an on-manifold adversarial attack path in the latent space of an autoencoder that closely approximates the decision boundaries between classes. On a variety of datasets and for multiple network architectures, OMADA consistently yields more accurate and better calibrated classifiers than baseline models, and outperforms competing approaches such as Mixup, as well as achieving similar performance to (at times better than) postprocessing calibration methods such as temperature scaling. Variants of OMADA can employ different sampling schemes for ambiguous on-manifold examples based on the entropy of their estimated soft labels, which exhibit specific strengths for generalization, calibration of predicted uncertainty, or detection of out-of-distribution inputs."},"title":{"value":"On-manifold Adversarial Data Augmentation Improves Uncertainty Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Kanil Patel","William Beluch","Dan Zhang","Michael Pfeiffer","Bin Yang"]}},"tmdate":1747136572642,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1727457637245,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dan_Zhang1"],"forum":"fT0qWX7NaC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":91231,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747136572642,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"fT0qWX7NaC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.07458v5"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"patel|onmanifold_adversarial_data_augmentation_improves_uncertainty_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kanil_Patel:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:William_Beluch:","~Dan_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Michael_Pfeiffer_0001:","~Bin_Yang5"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1912.07458"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1912-07458,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Kanil Patel and William Beluch and Dan Zhang and Michael Pfeiffer and Bin Yang},\n  title={On-manifold Adversarial Data Augmentation Improves Uncertainty Calibration},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1912.07458},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1912.07458}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Uncertainty estimates help to identify ambiguous, novel, or anomalous inputs, but the reliable quantification of uncertainty has proven to be challenging for modern deep networks. In order to improve uncertainty estimation, we propose On-Manifold Adversarial Data Augmentation or OMADA, which specifically attempts to generate the most challenging examples by following an on-manifold adversarial attack path in the latent space of an autoencoder-based generative model that closely approximates decision boundaries between two or more classes. On a variety of datasets as well as on multiple diverse network architectures, OMADA consistently yields more accurate and better calibrated classifiers than baseline models, and outperforms competing approaches such as Mixup, as well as achieving similar performance to (at times better than) post-processing calibration methods such as temperature scaling. Variants of OMADA can employ different sampling schemes for ambiguous on-manifold examples based on the entropy of their estimated soft labels, which exhibit specific strengths for generalization, calibration of predicted uncertainty, or detection of out-of-distribution inputs."},"title":{"value":"On-manifold Adversarial Data Augmentation Improves Uncertainty Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Kanil Patel","William Beluch","Dan Zhang","Michael Pfeiffer","Bin Yang"]}},"tmdate":1747136568490,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1727457637269,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dan_Zhang1"],"forum":"raRIqJwrPW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":91232,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747136568490,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"raRIqJwrPW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"2020 IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA)"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/de784adf67b85dac42eb8994f31922f80139356f.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"fan|stereo_visual_inertial_odometry_with_online_baseline_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yunfei_Fan1"]},"html":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/9197581"},"abstract":{"value":"Stereo-vision devices have rigorous requirements for extrinsic parameter calibration. In Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry (VIO), inaccuracy in or changes to camera extrinsic parameters may lead to serious degradation in estimation performance. In this manuscript, we propose an online calibration method for stereo VIO extrinsic parameters correction. In particular, we focus on Multi-State Constraint Kalman Filter (MSCKF [1]) framework to implement our method. The key component is to formulate stereo extrinsic parameters as part of the state variables and model the Jacobian of feature reprojection error with respect to stereo extrinsic parameters as sub-block of update Jacobian. Therefore we can estimate stereo extrinsic parameters simultaneously with inertial measurement unit (IMU) states and camera poses. Experiments on EuRoC dataset and real-world outdoor dataset demonstrate that the proposed algorithm produce higher positioning accuracy than the original S-MSCKF [2], and the noise of camera extrinsic parameters are self-corrected within the system."},"title":{"value":"Stereo Visual Inertial Odometry with Online Baseline Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Yunfei Fan"]}},"tmdate":1746488576363,"pdate":1590854400000,"tcdate":1746488576363,"writers":["~Yunfei_Fan1"],"signatures":["~Yunfei_Fan1"],"forum":"3y1b0h33pr","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","number":35837,"cdate":1746488576363,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1746488576363,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"3y1b0h33pr","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a new and more accurate calibration error estimator that leads to more reliable uncertainty quantification."},"venue":{"value":"IMLH 2023 Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2d5db8bac72d20afcb9ff824ca235764f9b28b22.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["uncertainty quantification","calibration error estimation"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/IMLH"},"paperhash":{"value":"peng|better_calibration_error_estimation_for_reliable_uncertainty_quantification"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shuman_Peng1","~Parsa_Alamzadeh1","~Martin_Ester1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Reliable uncertainty quantification is crucial in high-stakes applications, such as healthcare. The $\\text{ECE}_{EW}$ has been the most commonly used estimator to quantify the calibration error (CE), but it is heavily biased and can significantly underestimate the true calibration error. While alternative estimators, such as $\\text{ECE}_\\text{DEBIASED}$ and $\\text{ECE}_\\text{SWEEP}$, achieve smaller estimation bias in comparison, they exhibit a trade-off between overestimation of the CE on uncalibrated models and underestimation on recalibrated models. To address this trade-off, we propose a new estimator based on K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), called $\\text{ECE}_\\text{KNN}$, which constructs representative overlapping local neighbourhoods for improved CE estimation. Empirical evaluation results demonstrate that $\\text{ECE}_\\text{KNN}$ simultaneously achieves near-zero underestimation of the CE on uncalibrated models while also achieving lower degrees of overestimation on recalibrated models. The implementation of our proposed  $\\text{ECE}_\\text{KNN}$ is available at https://github.com/esterlab/KNN-ECE/."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\npeng2023better,\ntitle={Better Calibration Error Estimation for Reliable Uncertainty Quantification},\nauthor={Shuman Peng and Parsa Alamzadeh and Martin Ester},\nbooktitle={ICML 3rd Workshop on Interpretable Machine Learning in Healthcare (IMLH) },\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=jghR4uNTlA}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Better Calibration Error Estimation for Reliable Uncertainty Quantification"},"authors":{"value":["Shuman Peng","Parsa Alamzadeh","Martin Ester"]}},"tmdate":1689790795238,"pdate":1687278246967,"tcdate":1685437885558,"writers":["ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/IMLH","ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/IMLH/Submission44/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/IMLH/Submission44/Authors"],"forum":"jghR4uNTlA","number":44,"cdate":1685437885558,"mdate":1689790795238,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/IMLH/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/IMLH/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/IMLH/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/IMLH/Submission44/-/Revision"],"odate":1689787158019,"domain":"ICML.cc/2023/Workshop/IMLH","id":"jghR4uNTlA","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICICIC (3) 2006"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/11141/35658/01692239.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICICIC/2006"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|rectification_and_calibration_for_biomedical_image_processing"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Houjin_Chen1","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICICIC.2006.502"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icicic/JupengHYW06,\n  author={Jupeng Li and Houjin Chen and Ying Wang and Wei Wang},\n  title={Rectification and Calibration for Biomedical Image Processing},\n  year={2006},\n  cdate={1136073600000},\n  pages={564-567},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICICIC.2006.502},\n  booktitle={ICICIC (3)},\n  crossref={conf/icicic/2006}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Biomedical image analysis is the cross-disciplinary science of information and biology, which is significant in theory and clinic diagnosis. Taking the microcirculation image as object, this paper studies some key problems in the biomedical image processing, such as image rectification, image calibration and parameters measurement. And these key techniques have been applied to \"microcirculation expert system\" (MES) to improve the level of clinic diagnosis"},"title":{"value":"Rectification and Calibration for Biomedical Image Processing"},"authors":{"value":["Jupeng Li","Houjin Chen","Ying Wang","Wei Wang"]}},"tmdate":1775116269084,"pdate":1167523200000,"tcdate":1775116247523,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Houjin_Chen1"],"forum":"ddSACvjweP","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":863340,"cdate":1136073600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1775116269084,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ddSACvjweP","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"stance detection, applications"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2024 June Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2024mitigating,\ntitle={Mitigating Biases of Large Language Models in Stance Detection with Calibration},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - June 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=nvVv9udFyo},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Mitigating Biases of Large Language Models in Stance Detection with Calibration"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","NLP engineering experiment","Data analysis"]},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in many natural language processing tasks. However, our experiment reveals that, in stance detection tasks, LLMs may generate biased stances due to sentiment-stance spurious correlations and preference towards certain individuals and topics, thus harming their performance. Therefore, in this paper, we propose to Mitigate Biases of LLMs in stance detection with Calibration (MB-Cal). To be specific, a novel calibration network is devised to calibrate potential bias in the stance prediction of LLMs. Further, to address the challenge of effectively learning bias representations and the difficulty in the generalizability of debiasing, we construct counterfactual augmented data. This approach enhances the calibration network, facilitating the debiasing and out-of-domain generalization. Experimental results on in-target and zero-shot stance detection tasks show that the proposed MB-Cal can effectively mitigate biases of LLMs, achieving state-of-the-art results."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/db06e6aaf0c9ccce6f6063af9b23f701f77901cd.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Sentiment Analysis, Stylistic Analysis, and Argument Mining"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794065371158,"tcdate":1718534128179,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission4987/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission4987/Authors"],"forum":"nvVv9udFyo","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4987,"cdate":1718534128179,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Desk_Reject_Verification_Flag","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794065371158,"odate":1719930600391,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June","id":"nvVv9udFyo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.14296v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|mitigating_biases_of_large_language_models_in_stance_detection_with_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ang_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jingqian_Zhao:","~Bin_Liang6","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lin_Gui_0003:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hui_Wang:","~Xi_Zeng1","~Kam-Fai_Wong2","~Ruifeng_Xu1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.14296"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2402-14296,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ang Li and Jingqian Zhao and Bin Liang and Lin Gui and Hui Wang and Xi Zeng and Kam-Fai Wong and Ruifeng Xu},\n  title={Mitigating Biases of Large Language Models in Stance Detection with Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2402.14296},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.14296}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in many natural language processing tasks. However, our experiment reveals that, in stance detection tasks, LLMs may generate biased stances due to sentiment-stance spurious correlations and preference towards certain individuals and topics, thus harming their performance. Therefore, in this paper, we propose to Mitigate Biases of LLMs in stance detection with Calibration (MB-Cal). To be specific, a novel calibration network is devised to calibrate potential bias in the stance prediction of LLMs. Further, to address the challenge of effectively learning bias representations and the difficulty in the generalizability of debiasing, we construct counterfactual augmented data. This approach enhances the calibration network, facilitating the debiasing and out-of-domain generalization. Experimental results on in-target and zero-shot stance detection tasks show that the proposed MB-Cal can effectively mitigate biases of LLMs, achieving state-of-the-art results."},"title":{"value":"Mitigating Biases of Large Language Models in Stance Detection with Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Ang Li","Jingqian Zhao","Bin Liang","Lin Gui","Hui Wang","Xi Zeng","Kam-Fai Wong","Ruifeng Xu"]}},"tmdate":1768983082204,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1718871209993,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ruifeng_Xu1"],"forum":"ygX679Re4l","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":38061,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768983082204,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ygX679Re4l","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.15141v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"chouchen|predicting_code_review_completion_time_in_modern_code_review"},"authorids":{"value":["~Moataz_Chouchen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jefferson_Olongo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ali_Ouni_0001:","~Mohamed_Wiem_Mkaouer1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.15141"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2109-15141,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Moataz Chouchen and Jefferson Olongo and Ali Ouni and Mohamed Wiem Mkaouer},\n  title={Predicting Code Review Completion Time in Modern Code Review},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2109.15141},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.15141}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Context. Modern Code Review (MCR) is being adopted in both open source and commercial projects as a common practice. MCR is a widely acknowledged quality assurance practice that allows early detection of defects as well as poor coding practices. It also brings several other benefits such as knowledge sharing, team awareness, and collaboration. Problem. In practice, code reviews can experience significant delays to be completed due to various socio-technical factors which can affect the project quality and cost. For a successful review process, peer reviewers should perform their review tasks in a timely manner while providing relevant feedback about the code change being reviewed. However, there is a lack of tool support to help developers estimating the time required to complete a code review prior to accepting or declining a review request. Objective. Our objective is to build and validate an effective approach to predict the code review completion time in the context of MCR and help developers better manage and prioritize their code review tasks. Method. We formulate the prediction of the code review completion time as a learning problem. In particular, we propose a framework based on regression models to (i) effectively estimate the code review completion time, and (ii) understand the main factors influencing code review completion time."},"title":{"value":"Predicting Code Review Completion Time in Modern Code Review"},"authors":{"value":["Moataz Chouchen","Jefferson Olongo","Ali Ouni","Mohamed Wiem Mkaouer"]}},"tmdate":1757195178699,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1748006597057,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mohamed_Mkaouer1"],"forum":"BF8qjhEEHd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":549780,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1757195178699,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"BF8qjhEEHd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6895053/6906581/06907016.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"cucci|position_tracking_and_sensors_selfcalibration_in_autonomous_mobile_robots_by_gaussnewton_optimization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Davide_Antonio_Cucci:","~Matteo_Matteucci1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA.2014.6907016"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/CucciM14,\n  author={Davide Antonio Cucci and Matteo Matteucci},\n  title={Position tracking and sensors self-calibration in autonomous mobile robots by Gauss-Newton optimization},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={1269-1275},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA.2014.6907016},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2014}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The design and development of the pose tracking system for an autonomous mobile robot and the time consuming calibration of its intrinsic sensor parameters (e.g., displacement, misalignment and iron distortions of an inertial measurement unit) are one of the preliminary requirements of any project involving a mobile robot platform. This paper introduces ROAMFREE, a turn-on-and-go multiple sensors pose tracking and self-calibration framework adaptable to different mobile robot platforms (e.g., Ackerman steering vehicles, quadrotor aerial vehicles, omnidirectional mobile robots). We formulate the sensor fusion problem as a Gauss-Newton optimization on an hyper-graph where nodes represent poses and calibration parameters while edges represent nonlinear measurement constraints. This formulation allows us to solve both online pose tracking and offline sensor self-calibration problems."},"title":{"value":"Position tracking and sensors self-calibration in autonomous mobile robots by Gauss-Newton optimization"},"authors":{"value":["Davide Antonio Cucci","Matteo Matteucci"]}},"tmdate":1731500127113,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1731499834305,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Matteo_Matteucci1"],"forum":"V97SUX97pn","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":226695,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731500127113,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"V97SUX97pn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/9717300/09618952.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"boi|semidatadriven_calibration_of_galvanometric_setups_using_gaussian_processes"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ivan_De_Boi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Seppe_Sels:","~Rudi_Penne1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2021.3128956"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/BoiSP22,\n  author={Ivan De Boi and Seppe Sels and Rudi Penne},\n  title={Semidata-Driven Calibration of Galvanometric Setups Using Gaussian Processes},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={71},\n  pages={1-8},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2021.3128956}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Traditionally, calibration of a galvanometric setup is based upon a mathematical model of an underlying physical reality. These models make a considerable number of assumptions and simplifications. Moreover, they tend to be nongeneralizable and lead to nonconvex optimization problems encompassing many parameters. Alternatively, several data-driven statistical approaches have been proposed, in which any model of the underlying reality is completely bypassed. The often black-box model is trained purely on the data itself. Although some precautions for overfitting need to be kept in mind, it has been shown that this radically different approach can outperform the traditional mathematical models. On the other hand, some assumptions about the underlying physical truth are both reasonable and simple to implement. We propose to keep the best of both worlds and construct a semidata-driven calibration model with a single built-in assumption: rays exiting the galvanometric setup are straight lines. The data-driven approaches do not exploit this obvious fact. In this work, we focus on intrinsic calibration, i.e., finding the relationship between the input parameters that control the galvanometers and the rays exiting the device. We investigate four different models to predict lines and evaluate them in cross-validation, predicting intersection points on a validation plane and aiming the laser at a specific point in 3-D space. We show that our approach outperforms a purely data-driven approach."},"title":{"value":"Semidata-Driven Calibration of Galvanometric Setups Using Gaussian Processes"},"authors":{"value":["Ivan De Boi","Seppe Sels","Rudi Penne"]}},"tmdate":1731503540842,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731503520942,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rudi_Penne1"],"forum":"OnD5tF7G1v","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":227367,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731503540842,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"OnD5tF7G1v","version":2},{"content":{"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"modenesi|generalized_machine_learning_for_fast_calibration_of_agentbased_epidemic_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Bernardo_Modenesi1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.07013"},"abstract":{"value":"Agent-based models (ABMs) are widely used to study infectious disease dynamics, but their calibration is often computationally intensive, limiting their applicability in time-sensitive public health settings. We propose DeepIMC (Deep Inverse Mapping Calibration), a machine learning-based calibration framework that directly learns the inverse mapping from epidemic time series to epidemiological parameters. DeepIMC trains a bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM) neural network on synthetic epidemic trajectories generated from agent-based models such as the Susceptible-Infected-Recovered (SIR) model, enabling rapid parameter estimation without repeated simulation at inference time. We evaluate DeepIMC through an extensive simulation study comprising 5,000 heterogeneous epidemic scenarios and benchmark its performance against Approximate Bayesian Computation (ABC) using likelihood-free Markov Chain Monte Carlo. The results show that DeepIMC substantially improves parameter recovery accuracy, produces sharp and well-calibrated predictive intervals, and reduces computational time by more than an order of magnitude relative to ABC. Although structural parameter identifiability constraints limit the precise recovery of all model parameters simultaneously, the calibrated models reliably reproduce epidemic trajectories and support accurate forward prediction with their estimated parameters. DeepIMC is implemented in the open-source R package epiworldRCalibrate, facilitating practical adoption for real-time epidemic modeling and policy analysis. Overall, our findings demonstrate that DeepIMC provides a scalable, operationally effective alternative to traditional simulation-based calibration methods for agent-based epidemic models."},"title":{"value":"Generalized Machine Learning for Fast Calibration of Agent-Based Epidemic Models"},"authors":{"value":["Bernardo Modenesi"]}},"tmdate":1778397059621,"pdate":1775109600000,"tcdate":1778397059621,"writers":["~Bernardo_Modenesi1"],"signatures":["~Bernardo_Modenesi1"],"forum":"aHz5sjBUnt","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":50482,"cdate":1778397059621,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1778397059621,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"aHz5sjBUnt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Remote Sensing"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/12/3/555/pdf?version=1581081656"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"heinz|design_and_evaluation_of_a_permanently_installed_planebased_calibration_field_for_mobile_laser_scanning_systems"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/rs12030555"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{Heinz_2020,\n\tdoi = {10.3390/rs12030555},\n\turl = {https://doi.org/10.3390%2Frs12030555},\n\tyear = 2020,\n\tmonth = {feb},\n\tpublisher = {{MDPI} {AG}},\n\tvolume = {12},\n\tnumber = {3},\n\tpages = {555},\n\tauthor = {Erik Heinz and Christoph Holst and Heiner Kuhlmann and Lasse Klingbeil},\n\ttitle = {Design and Evaluation of a Permanently Installed Plane-Based Calibration Field for Mobile Laser Scanning Systems},\n\tjournal = {Remote Sensing}\n}"},"abstract":{"value":"Mobile laser scanning has become an established measuring technique that is used for many applications in the fields of mapping, inventory, and monitoring. Due to the increasing operationality of such systems, quality control w.r.t. calibration and evaluation of the systems becomes more and more important and is subject to on-going research. This paper contributes to this topic by using tools from geodetic configuration analysis in order to design and evaluate a plane-based calibration field for determining the lever arm and boresight angles of a 2D laser scanner w.r.t. a GNSS/IMU unit (Global Navigation Satellite System, Inertial Measurement Unit). In this regard, the impact of random, systematic, and gross observation errors on the calibration is analyzed leading to a plane setup that provides accurate and controlled calibration parameters. The designed plane setup is realized in the form of a permanently installed calibration field. The applicability of the calibration field is tested with a real mobile laser scanning system by frequently repeating the calibration. Empirical standard deviations of <1 ... 1.5 mm for the lever arm and <0.005 ∘ for the boresight angles are obtained, which was priorly defined to be the goal of the calibration. In order to independently evaluate the mobile laser scanning system after calibration, an evaluation environment is realized consisting of a network of control points as well as TLS (Terrestrial Laser Scanning) reference point clouds. Based on the control points, both the horizontal and vertical accuracy of the system is found to be < 10 mm (root mean square error). This is confirmed by comparisons to the TLS reference point clouds indicating a well calibrated system. Both the calibration field and the evaluation environment are permanently installed and can be used for arbitrary mobile laser scanning systems."},"title":{"value":"Design and Evaluation of a Permanently Installed Plane-Based Calibration Field for Mobile Laser Scanning Systems"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Erik Heinz"},{"fullname":"Christoph Holst","username":"~Christoph_Holst1"},{"fullname":"Heiner Kuhlmann"},{"fullname":"Lasse Klingbeil"}]}},"tmdate":1789093254796,"pdate":1581033600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.3390/rs12030555"],"tcdate":1772285246217,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Christoph_Holst1"],"forum":"1AYhhOzdGN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":50021,"cdate":1581099966016,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789093254796,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"1AYhhOzdGN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Remote. Sens. 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/12/3/555/pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/REMOTESENSING/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"heinz|design_and_evaluation_of_a_permanently_installed_planebased_calibration_field_for_mobile_laser_scanning_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Christoph_Holst1","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/rs12030555"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/remotesensing/HeinzHKK20,\n  author={Erik Heinz and Christoph Holst and Heiner Kuhlmann and Lasse Klingbeil},\n  title={Design and Evaluation of a Permanently Installed Plane-Based Calibration Field for Mobile Laser Scanning Systems},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={12},\n  number={3},\n  pages={555},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/rs12030555}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Mobile laser scanning has become an established measuring technique that is used for many applications in the fields of mapping, inventory, and monitoring. Due to the increasing operationality of such systems, quality control w.r.t. calibration and evaluation of the systems becomes more and more important and is subject to on-going research. This paper contributes to this topic by using tools from geodetic configuration analysis in order to design and evaluate a plane-based calibration field for determining the lever arm and boresight angles of a 2D laser scanner w.r.t. a GNSS/IMU unit (Global Navigation Satellite System, Inertial Measurement Unit). In this regard, the impact of random, systematic, and gross observation errors on the calibration is analyzed leading to a plane setup that provides accurate and controlled calibration parameters. The designed plane setup is realized in the form of a permanently installed calibration field. The applicability of the calibration field is tested with a real mobile laser scanning system by frequently repeating the calibration. Empirical standard deviations of <1 ... 1.5 mm for the lever arm and <0.005 ∘ for the boresight angles are obtained, which was priorly defined to be the goal of the calibration. In order to independently evaluate the mobile laser scanning system after calibration, an evaluation environment is realized consisting of a network of control points as well as TLS (Terrestrial Laser Scanning) reference point clouds. Based on the control points, both the horizontal and vertical accuracy of the system is found to be < 10 mm (root mean square error). This is confirmed by comparisons to the TLS reference point clouds indicating a well calibrated system. Both the calibration field and the evaluation environment are permanently installed and can be used for arbitrary mobile laser scanning systems."},"title":{"value":"Design and Evaluation of a Permanently Installed Plane-Based Calibration Field for Mobile Laser Scanning Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Erik Heinz","Christoph Holst","Heiner Kuhlmann","Lasse Klingbeil"]}},"tmdate":1772285240082,"pdate":1609372800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/remotesensing/HeinzHKK20"],"tcdate":1772285232420,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Christoph_Holst1"],"forum":"g50ZwnBGtH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":839173,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772285240082,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"g50ZwnBGtH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.01969v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"heander|trustcalibrated_code_review_a_participatory_design_study_of_review_workflows_for_llmgenerated_multifile_changes"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.01969"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2606-01969,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Lo Gullstrand Heander and Agnia Sergeyuk and Ilya Zakharov and Emma Söderberg and Nikita Mukhortov},\n  title={Trust-Calibrated Code Review: A Participatory Design Study of Review Workflows for LLM-Generated Multi-File Changes},\n  year={2026},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1780272000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2606.01969},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.01969}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Background: Developers increasingly review multi-file code changes generated by LLM-based agents, yet no validated end-to-end workflow or IDE tooling design exists for this scenario. Aims: We investigate (RQ1) the challenges developers face when reviewing LLM-generated multi-file changes and (RQ2) how developers envision effective workflows for this task. Method: In collaboration with JetBrains, we conducted a participatory design study structured using the double-diamond design process with Discover, Define, Develop, and Deliver phases. Industry practitioners participated in the Discover phase (N=17); seven of these returned for the Develop phase. The Define phase was an author-led synthesis. The Deliver phase produced a conceptual design and a high-fidelity semi-interactive prototype evaluated through a follow-up survey with N=43 practitioners. Results: Participants identified trust-calibration as the central challenge. The study yielded a three-level review workflow (overview, file-analysis, code snippet review) supported by seven design constructs (chunk, risk-per-line, risk-per-file, judge, walk-through, zooming in/out, and security cage). In the validation survey, all three workflow levels scored above the neutral midpoint (means 3.50--3.91 on a five-point scale). Of the respondents, 63% expected reduced overall review effort, and 52% reduced trust-assessment effort, relative to their current tools. These findings suggest that the design constructs indicate a positive direction for future tool development. Conclusions: Reviewing LLM-generated multi-file changes is a trust-calibration problem rather than a diffing problem. The three-level workflow and the seven constructs we report give tool designers a conceptual framework for building AI-ready code review tools that surface risk and confidence signals at the granularity at which developers allocate attention."},"title":{"value":"Trust-Calibrated Code Review: A Participatory Design Study of Review Workflows for LLM-Generated Multi-File Changes"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Lo Gullstrand Heander","username":""},{"fullname":"Agnia Sergeyuk","username":"~Agnia_Sergeyuk1"},{"fullname":"Ilya Zakharov","username":""},{"fullname":"Emma Söderberg","username":""},{"fullname":"Nikita Mukhortov","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1791188000724,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2606-01969"],"tcdate":1791187994164,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Agnia_Sergeyuk1"],"forum":"Cm3y4BbpHK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":163303,"cdate":1780272000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791188000724,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Cm3y4BbpHK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"VR Workshops 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9757254/9757255/09757408.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"gasques|the_virtualaugmented_reality_simulator_evaluating_osthmd_ar_calibration_algorithms_in_vr"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/VRW55335.2022.00202"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/vr/GasquesLW22,\n  author={Danilo Gasques and Weichen Liu and Nadir Weibel},\n  title={The Virtual-Augmented Reality Simulator: Evaluating OST-HMD AR calibration algorithms in VR},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={702-703},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/VRW55335.2022.00202},\n  booktitle={VR Workshops},\n  crossref={conf/vr/2022w}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"When developing AR applications for high-precision domains such as surgery, we face a common problem: how can the system guarantee that the end-user will see a virtual object aligned with its real-world counterpart? Alignment, or registration, is a crucial feature of AR displays, but achieving accurate alignment between real and virtual objects is not trivial. With hundreds of calibration approaches available, we need better tools to understand how and when calibration algorithms fail as well as understand what can be done to improve alignment. This poster introduces a novel AR simulator in VR that facilitates experimentation with different calibration algorithms."},"title":{"value":"The Virtual-Augmented Reality Simulator: Evaluating OST-HMD AR calibration algorithms in VR"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Danilo Gasques","username":""},{"fullname":"Weichen Liu","username":"~Weichen_Liu4"},{"fullname":"Nadir Weibel","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1779870563008,"pdate":1672444800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/vr/GasquesLW22"],"tcdate":1779870557883,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Weichen_Liu4"],"forum":"S9VYT8pGie","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":33460,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1779870563008,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"S9VYT8pGie","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/7083369/11215960/11217188.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"shin|squeezing_the_last_drop_of_accuracy_handeye_calibration_via_deep_reinforcement_learningguided_pose_tuning"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2025.3625510"},"abstract":{"value":"Hand-eye calibration is a fundamental task in robotics, requiring high precision to ensure accurate manipulation. This is especially crucial for recent markerless methods, which depend on precise pose estimation for effective end-effector calibration. In this letter, we propose a novel approach that improves calibration performance by adjusting the end-effector’s pose to reduce prediction error. Our method utilizes a reward structure derived from trained pose estimation networks, enabling a Soft Actor-Critic-Discrete agent to learn in a simulated environment how to enhance calibration performance through action selection. Our experiments show that calibration results achieved with our method outperform those from initial poses alone in both markerless and marker-based methods. Real-world experiments further validate the efficacy of our approach in actual robotic systems. These results demonstrate that our proposed method effectively enhances the performance of pose estimation-based hand-eye calibration."},"title":{"value":"Squeezing the Last Drop of Accuracy: Hand-Eye Calibration via Deep Reinforcement Learning-Guided Pose Tuning"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Seunghui Shin"},{"fullname":"Daeho Kim"},{"fullname":"Hyoseok Hwang","username":"~Hyoseok_Hwang1"}]}},"tmdate":1789091267655,"pdate":1764547200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/lra.2025.3625510"],"tcdate":1769330912061,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Hyoseok_Hwang1"],"forum":"Y9Z1yzPYlX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":37791,"cdate":1762199265185,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091267655,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Y9Z1yzPYlX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.14510v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"chaudhry|deepbrownconrady_prediction_of_camera_calibration_and_distortion_parameters_using_deep_learning_and_synthetic_data"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Faiz_Muhammad_Chaudhry:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jarno_Ralli:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jérôme_Leudet:","~Fahad_Sohrab1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Farhad_Pakdaman:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pierre_Corbani:","~Moncef_Gabbouj1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.14510"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-14510,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Faiz Muhammad Chaudhry and Jarno Ralli and Jérôme Leudet and Fahad Sohrab and Farhad Pakdaman and Pierre Corbani and Moncef Gabbouj},\n  title={Deep-BrownConrady: Prediction of Camera Calibration and Distortion Parameters Using Deep Learning and Synthetic Data},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2501.14510},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.14510}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This research addresses the challenge of camera calibration and distortion parameter prediction from a single image using deep learning models. The main contributions of this work are: (1) demonstrating that a deep learning model, trained on a mix of real and synthetic images, can accurately predict camera and lens parameters from a single image, and (2) developing a comprehensive synthetic dataset using the AILiveSim simulation platform. This dataset includes variations in focal length and lens distortion parameters, providing a robust foundation for model training and testing. The training process predominantly relied on these synthetic images, complemented by a small subset of real images, to explore how well models trained on synthetic data can perform calibration tasks on real-world images. Traditional calibration methods require multiple images of a calibration object from various orientations, which is often not feasible due to the lack of such images in publicly available datasets. A deep learning network based on the ResNet architecture was trained on this synthetic dataset to predict camera calibration parameters following the Brown-Conrady lens model. The ResNet architecture, adapted for regression tasks, is capable of predicting continuous values essential for accurate camera calibration in applications such as autonomous driving, robotics, and augmented reality. Keywords: Camera calibration, distortion, synthetic data, deep learning, residual networks (ResNet), AILiveSim, horizontal field-of-view, principal point, Brown-Conrady Model."},"title":{"value":"Deep-BrownConrady: Prediction of Camera Calibration and Distortion Parameters Using Deep Learning and Synthetic Data"},"authors":{"value":["Faiz Muhammad Chaudhry","Jarno Ralli","Jérôme Leudet","Fahad Sohrab","Farhad Pakdaman","Pierre Corbani","Moncef Gabbouj"]}},"tmdate":1752875333242,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1741251712816,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Moncef_Gabbouj1"],"forum":"0gsqHcpurU","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":359985,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1752875333242,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0gsqHcpurU","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10609961/10609862/10610756.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"lee|lccraft_lidar_and_camera_calibration_using_recurrent_allpairs_field_transforms_without_precise_initial_guess"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu-Chen_Lee:","~Kuan-Wen_Chen2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA57147.2024.10610756"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/LeeC24,\n  author={Yu-Chen Lee and Kuan-Wen Chen},\n  title={LCCRAFT: LiDAR and Camera Calibration Using Recurrent All-Pairs Field Transforms Without Precise Initial Guess},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={16669-16675},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA57147.2024.10610756},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"LiDAR-camera fusion plays a pivotal role in 3D reconstruction for self-driving applications. A fundamental prerequisite for effective fusion is the precise calibration between LiDAR and camera systems. Many existing calibration methods are constrained by predefined mis-calibration ranges in the training data, essentially tying the network to a specific data distribution. However, if the range of evaluation data differs from what the network has been trained on, the resulting estimates may not meet expectations. Moreover, most methods require a precise initial guess for calibration to succeed. In this paper, we introduce LCCRAFT, an online calibration network designed for LiDAR and camera systems. Leveraging the 4D correlation volume and correlation lookup techniques inherited from RAFT, we apply them to correlate RGB images and depth maps derived from the projection of point clouds. Through weight sharing between update iterations and by enabling the update operator to learn from data with varying degrees of error, LCCRAFT demonstrates adaptability to diverse miscalibration scenarios. This includes cases where the initial mis- calibration is even more severe than what the system encountered during training, demonstrating the robustness of the model. The calibration process executes in 93ms on a single GPU, meeting real-time requirements. Despite the modest 9M model parameters, LCCRAFT achieves competitive performance as compared to the state-of-the-art method, which entails 69M parameters."},"title":{"value":"LCCRAFT: LiDAR and Camera Calibration Using Recurrent All-Pairs Field Transforms Without Precise Initial Guess"},"authors":{"value":["Yu-Chen Lee","Kuan-Wen Chen"]}},"tmdate":1740745736430,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1740745731924,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kuan-Wen_Chen2"],"forum":"ir11qvm3aW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":342318,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740745736430,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ir11qvm3aW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (Findings) 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.1157.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"huo|ditreducio_a_trainingfree_acceleration_for_ditbased_tts_via_progressive_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.1157"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/HuoJTHZ26,\n  author={Yanru Huo and Ziyue Jiang and Zuoli Tang and Qingyang Hong and Zhou Zhao},\n  title={DiTReducio: A Training-Free Acceleration for DiT-Based TTS via Progressive Calibration},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={23101-23116},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.1157},\n  booktitle={ACL (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2026f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"While Diffusion Transformers (DiT) have advanced non-autoregressive (NAR) speech synthesis, their high computational demands remain an obvious limitation. Existing DiT-based text-to-speech (TTS) model acceleration approaches predominantly focus on reducing sampling steps through distillation techniques, yet they remain constrained by training costs. We introduce DiTReducio, a training-free acceleration framework that compresses computations in DiT-based TTS models through a progressive calibration process. We propose two compression methods, Temporal Skipping and Branch Skipping, to eliminate redundant computations during inference. Moreover, based on two characteristic attention patterns identified within DiT layers, we devise a pattern-guided strategy to selectively apply the compression methods. Our method allows flexible modulation between generation quality and computational efficiency through adjustable compression thresholds. Experimental evaluations conducted on F5-TTS and MegaTTS 3 demonstrate that DiTReducio achieves a 75.4% reduction in FLOPs and improves the Real-Time Factor (RTF) by 37.1%, while preserving generation quality. The code is available at https://github.com/MM-Speech/DiTReducio."},"title":{"value":"DiTReducio: A Training-Free Acceleration for DiT-Based TTS via Progressive Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yanru Huo","username":"~Yanru_Huo1"},{"fullname":"Ziyue Jiang","username":""},{"fullname":"Zuoli Tang","username":""},{"fullname":"Qingyang Hong","username":""},{"fullname":"Zhou Zhao","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1789871136673,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/HuoJTHZ26"],"tcdate":1789871134301,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yanru_Huo1"],"forum":"e7V9mNY28m","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":151724,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789871136673,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"e7V9mNY28m","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Image Process. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/83/10346232/10614124.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"pei|calibnet_dualbranch_crossmodal_calibration_for_rgbd_salient_instance_segmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jialun_Pei:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_Jiang:","~He_Tang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nian_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yueming_Jin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Deng-Ping_Fan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pheng-Ann_Heng:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2024.3432328"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tip/PeiJTLJFH24,\n  author={Jialun Pei and Tao Jiang and He Tang and Nian Liu and Yueming Jin and Deng-Ping Fan and Pheng-Ann Heng},\n  title={CalibNet: Dual-Branch Cross-Modal Calibration for RGB-D Salient Instance Segmentation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Image Process.},\n  volume={33},\n  pages={4348-4362},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2024.3432328}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this study, we propose a novel approach for RGB-D salient instance segmentation using a dual-branch cross-modal feature calibration architecture called CalibNet. Our method simultaneously calibrates depth and RGB features in the kernel and mask branches to generate instance-aware kernels and mask features. CalibNet consists of three simple modules, a dynamic interactive kernel (DIK) and a weight-sharing fusion (WSF), which work together to generate effective instance-aware kernels and integrate cross-modal features. To improve the quality of depth features, we incorporate a depth similarity assessment (DSA) module prior to DIK and WSF. In addition, we further contribute a new DSIS dataset, which contains 1,940 images with elaborate instance-level annotations. Extensive experiments on three challenging benchmarks show that CalibNet yields a promising result, i.e., 58.0% AP with $320\\times 480$ input size on the COME15K-E test set, which significantly surpasses the alternative frameworks. Our code and dataset will be publicly available at: https://github.com/PJLallen/CalibNet ."},"title":{"value":"CalibNet: Dual-Branch Cross-Modal Calibration for RGB-D Salient Instance Segmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Jialun Pei","Tao Jiang","He Tang","Nian Liu","Yueming Jin","Deng-Ping Fan","Pheng-Ann Heng"]}},"tmdate":1731481773607,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731478942772,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~He_Tang1"],"forum":"s9qNLaxXkM","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":206830,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731481773607,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"s9qNLaxXkM","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2208.00702v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|a_new_calibration_method_for_industrial_robot_based_on_stepsize_levenbergmarquardt_algorithm"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2208.00702"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2208-00702,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhibin Li and Shuai Li and Xin Luo},\n  title={A New Calibration Method for Industrial Robot Based on Step-Size Levenberg-Marquardt Algorithm},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2208.00702},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2208.00702}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Industrial robots play a vital role in automatic production, which have been widely utilized in industrial production activities, like handling and welding. However, due to an uncalibrated robot with machining tolerance and assembly tolerance, it suffers from low absolute positioning accuracy, which cannot satisfy the requirements of high-precision manufacture. To address this hot issue, we propose a novel calibration method based on an unscented Kalman filter and variable step-size Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm. This work has three ideas: a) proposing a novel variable step-size Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm to addresses the issue of local optimum in a Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm; b) employing an unscented Kalman filter to reduce the influence of the measurement noises; and c) developing a novel calibration method incorporating an unscented Kalman filter with a variable step-size Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm. Furthermore, we conduct enough experiments on an ABB IRB 120 industrial robot. From the experimental results, the proposed method achieves much higher calibration accuracy than some state-of-the-art calibration methods. Hence, this work is an important milestone in the field of robot calibration."},"title":{"value":"A New Calibration Method for Industrial Robot Based on Step-Size Levenberg-Marquardt Algorithm"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhibin Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Shuai Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Xin Luo","username":"~Xin_Luo10"}]}},"tmdate":1784540521760,"pdate":1672444800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2208-00702"],"tcdate":1784540500610,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Xin_Luo10"],"forum":"dxUFKbONyp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":69977,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784540521760,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"dxUFKbONyp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ITSC 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10919469/10919190/10920170.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ITSC/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|a_novel_featuresharing_autoregressive_neural_network_for_enhanced_carfollowing_model_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chengming_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongyao_Jia:","~Zuduo_Zheng1","~Wei_Wang80","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shangbo_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC58415.2024.10920170"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/itsc/WangJZ0W24,\n  author={Chengming Wang and Dongyao Jia and Zuduo Zheng and Wei Wang and Shangbo Wang},\n  title={A Novel Feature-Sharing Auto-Regressive Neural Network for Enhanced Car-Following Model Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={2474-2481},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC58415.2024.10920170},\n  booktitle={ITSC},\n  crossref={conf/itsc/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate calibration and validation are crucial for physics-based car-following models (CFMs) to effectively capture longitudinal human driving behaviors. It may also facilitate the development of hybrid CFMs that integrate physics-based and data-driven models. However, existing calibration methods often produce inaccurate parameters, especially with incomplete trajectories, where certain driving regimes are missing. Additionally, most research only uses individual trajectory data for calibration, neglecting common patterns across trajectories. This research proposes to address the above issues by introducing a feature-sharing approach using an auto-regressive neural network for parameter calibration. This approach allows parameters to generalize to missing driving regimes by leveraging shared information through common features, such as lane information, among different trajectories. We validated the effectiveness of our approach through parameter estimation accuracy with simulated data and trajectory simulation accuracy with real-world traffic data, showing it outperforms existing calibration methods. Furthermore, we evaluated our approach in the recent promising paradigm of physics-informed deep learning (PIDL). Experiments show significant performance improvements of PIDL upon integration with a more accurate CFM acting as a physics informer."},"title":{"value":"A Novel Feature-Sharing Auto-Regressive Neural Network for Enhanced Car-Following Model Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Chengming Wang","Dongyao Jia","Zuduo Zheng","Wei Wang","Shangbo Wang"]}},"tmdate":1770182788873,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/itsc/WangJZ0W24"],"tcdate":1768457464347,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wei_Wang80"],"forum":"ol0qwCmkDu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":759012,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1770182788873,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ol0qwCmkDu","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a new learning method for biased positive-unlabeled learning based on calibrating the posterior probability estimated by a non-traditional classifier."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Positive-Unlabeled Learning","PU learning","biased PU learning","propensity score","calibration"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/2c22f98775146a62a707ebbb986fca4cd20e9a1a.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"unsupervised, self-supervised, semi-supervised, and supervised representation learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Positive-Unlabeled (PU) learning addresses the problem of training a binary classifier using an incomplete training dataset consisting of a subset of labeled positive instances and a set of unlabeled instances, which  contains both positive and negative examples. A practically relevant and recently intensively studied scenario is biased PU learning, where the labeling mechanism determining which positive examples receive labels may depend on the feature values of the instances.\nA  naive method in PU learning, known as the non-traditional classifier (NTC), treats all unlabeled instances as negative. However, this leads to a biased estimation of the true class posterior probability. More effective learning from biased PU data is possible but challenging, as it typically requires estimating the propensity score, defined as the instance-specific probability that a positive example is labeled. Existing propensity score estimation methods are often computationally expensive, since they rely on iterative procedures that alternately update the classifier and the propensity score model. In this work, we show that under relatively weak functional assumption on the propensity score, it is possible to accurately estimate the class posterior without explicitly modeling the former, provided that a small calibration set is available. The proposed approach (called PUCal) is based on an appropriate calibration of probabilities produced by the NTC on a small set of fully observable observations.\nWe show that such a procedure leads to a more accurate classifier compared to existing methods for biased PU learning even when only a  very limited number of observations are available in the calibration set. We also provide estimation error bounds for the proposed method."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026calibrationenhanced,\ntitle={Calibration-Enhanced Approach to Biased Positive-Unlabeled Learning},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=a89oMQg1vN},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration-Enhanced Approach to Biased Positive-Unlabeled Learning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d5da04f8741c06aefbe4e02ed88afa0d7f35f055.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791231487771,"tcdate":1789654587650,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission32100/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission32100/Authors"],"forum":"a89oMQg1vN","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":32100,"cdate":1789654587650,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission32100/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791231487771,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"a89oMQg1vN","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Data quality should be reflected in model uncertainty: QAC generalizes calibration to consume a per-sample quality signal Q, jointly with the model input or output, so lower-quality inputs produce less confident predictions."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["uncertainty calibration","post-hoc calibration","data quality","aleatoric uncertainty","selective prediction","error detection","proper scoring rules","trustworthy machine learning"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/287bf6624d0a309fcfb4bbfcbe11a642173fda86.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"A classifier's predicted probability is its statement of uncertainty about the label, and calibration makes this statement match observed frequencies. Post-hoc calibrators use only the model's output, so two samples with the same output receive the same probability. Consider two patients whose laboratory values are almost the same, but one patient's value is wrong because the blood sample was stored too long before analysis: the model gives both the same prediction and the same confidence, although the second prediction is more likely to be wrong. Laboratories and other data pipelines often record such problems. We call a per-sample value that describes how reliable a data point's input or label is its data quality. When it is not available at training time, it cannot be added to the model's input, but it can be given to the calibrator. A standard result implies that, for the best possible predictor, a data-quality value can improve the prediction only if it carries information about the label that the input does not. We propose Quality-Aware Uncertainty Calibration (QAC), a second calibration stage that can follow any existing calibrator. For each class, it estimates on the calibration set how often the class is correct given the first-stage probability and the quality score; the direction of the effect of quality is learned, not assumed. On seven datasets, nine base calibrators and ten seeds, and compared with the same stage given a constant quality score, QAC lowers the Brier score by $0.001$-$0.016$ on the four datasets where quality is informative (a simulation and three datasets where quality is annotator agreement). It does not help on the two datasets whose quality score is computed from the input, and gives no significant gain on a clinical dataset. The gain is mainly in separating correct from wrong predictions (error-detection AUROC $+0.021$ to $+0.059$), while the separation between classes improves about a quarter as much or less."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026qac,\ntitle={{QAC}: Data Quality-Aware Uncertainty Calibration for Trustworthy {AI}},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=RBljxB74dg},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"QAC: Data Quality-Aware Uncertainty Calibration for Trustworthy AI"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/bca6c2e0d34b5c511591b39cc082bdf0f9c54938.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791234537147,"tcdate":1789780763828,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission51421/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission51421/Authors"],"forum":"RBljxB74dg","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":51421,"cdate":1789780763828,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission51421/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791234537147,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"RBljxB74dg","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.14176v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|copilot_refinement_addressing_code_smells_in_copilotgenerated_python_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Beiqi_Zhang:","~Peng_Liang3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qiong_Feng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yujia_Fu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zengyang_Li:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2401.14176"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2401-14176,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Beiqi Zhang and Peng Liang and Qiong Feng and Yujia Fu and Zengyang Li},\n  title={Copilot Refinement: Addressing Code Smells in Copilot-Generated Python Code},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2401.14176},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2401.14176}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As one of the most popular dynamic languages, Python experiences a decrease in readability and maintainability when code smells are present. Recent advancements in Large Language Models have sparked growing interest in AI-enabled tools for both code generation and refactoring. GitHub Copilot is one such tool that has gained widespread usage. Copilot Chat, released in September 2023, functions as an interactive tool aimed at facilitating natural language-powered coding. However, limited attention has been given to understanding code smells in Copilot-generated Python code and Copilot Chat's ability to fix the code smells. To this end, we built a dataset comprising 102 code smells in Copilot-generated Python code. Our aim is to first explore the occurrence of code smells in Copilot-generated Python code and then evaluate the effectiveness of Copilot Chat in fixing these code smells employing different prompts. The results show that 8 out of 10 types of code smells can be detected in Copilot-generated Python code, among which Multiply-Nested Container is the most common one. For these code smells, Copilot Chat achieves a highest fixing rate of 87.1%, showing promise in fixing Python code smells generated by Copilot itself. In addition, the effectiveness of Copilot Chat in fixing these smells can be improved by providing more detailed prompts."},"title":{"value":"Copilot Refinement: Addressing Code Smells in Copilot-Generated Python Code"},"authors":{"value":["Beiqi Zhang","Peng Liang","Qiong Feng","Yujia Fu","Zengyang Li"]}},"tmdate":1753172054722,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1753171987657,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Peng_Liang3"],"forum":"rurVGS5WgF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":581041,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753172054722,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rurVGS5WgF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.11031v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"linder|acquisition_of_highquality_images_for_camera_calibration_in_robotics_applications_via_speech_prompts"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.11031"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-11031,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Timm Linder and Kadir Yilmaz and David B. Adrian and Bastian Leibe},\n  title={Acquisition of high-quality images for camera calibration in robotics applications via speech prompts},\n  year={2025},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1743465600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2504.11031},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.11031}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate intrinsic and extrinsic camera calibration can be an important prerequisite for robotic applications that rely on vision as input. While there is ongoing research on enabling camera calibration using natural images, many systems in practice still rely on using designated calibration targets with e.g. checkerboard patterns or April tag grids. Once calibration images from different perspectives have been acquired and feature descriptors detected, those are typically used in an optimization process to minimize the geometric reprojection error. For this optimization to converge, input images need to be of sufficient quality and particularly sharpness; they should neither contain motion blur nor rolling-shutter artifacts that can arise when the calibration board was not static during image capture. In this work, we present a novel calibration image acquisition technique controlled via voice commands recorded with a clip-on microphone, that can be more robust and user-friendly than e.g. triggering capture with a remote control, or filtering out blurry frames from a video sequence in postprocessing. To achieve this, we use a state-of-the-art speech-to-text transcription model with accurate per-word timestamping to capture trigger words with precise temporal alignment. Our experiments show that the proposed method improves user experience by being fast and efficient, allowing us to successfully calibrate complex multi-camera setups."},"title":{"value":"Acquisition of high-quality images for camera calibration in robotics applications via speech prompts"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Timm Linder","username":"~Timm_Linder1"},{"fullname":"Kadir Yilmaz","username":""},{"fullname":"David B. Adrian","username":""},{"fullname":"Bastian Leibe","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784752634311,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2504-11031"],"tcdate":1784752629657,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Timm_Linder1"],"forum":"G6N24Kaowv","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":92376,"cdate":1743465600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784752634311,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"G6N24Kaowv","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/22/5/1832/pdf?version=1645786922"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"si|research_on_a_partial_aperture_factor_measurement_method_for_the_agri_onboard_calibration_assembly"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaolong_Si:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiuju_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongyao_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shiwei_Bao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Heyu_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liming_Zhang:","~Wenxin_Huang1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s22051832"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/SiLCBXZH22,\n  author={Xiaolong Si and Xiuju Li and Hongyao Chen and Shiwei Bao and Heyu Xu and Liming Zhang and Wenxin Huang},\n  title={Research on a Partial Aperture Factor Measurement Method for the AGRI Onboard Calibration Assembly},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={22},\n  number={5},\n  pages={1832},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s22051832}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"A partial aperture onboard calibration method can solve the onboard calibration problems of some large aperture remote sensors, which is of great significance for the development trend of increasingly large apertures in optical remote sensors. In this paper, the solar diffuser reflectance degradation monitor (SDRDM) in the onboard calibration assembly (CA) of the FengYun-4 (FY-4) advanced geostationary radiance imager (AGRI) was used as the reference radiometer. It was designed for measuring the partial aperture factor (PAF) for the AGRI onboard calibration. First, the linear response count variation relationship between the two was established under the same radiance source input. Then, according to the known bidirectional reflection distribution function (BRDF) of the solar diffuser (SD) in the CA, the relative reflectance ratio coefficient between the AGRI observation direction and the SDRDM observation direction was calculated. On this basis, the response count value of the AGRI and the SDRDM was used to realize the high-precision measurement of the PAF of the AGRI B1~B3 bands by simulating the AGRI onboard calibration measurement under the illumination of a solar simulator in the laboratory. According to the determination process of the relevant parameters of the PAF, the measurement uncertainty of the PAF was analyzed; this uncertainty was greater than 2.04% and provided an important reference for the evaluation of the onboard absolute radiometric calibration uncertainty after launch."},"title":{"value":"Research on a Partial Aperture Factor Measurement Method for the AGRI Onboard Calibration Assembly"},"authors":{"value":["Xiaolong Si","Xiuju Li","Hongyao Chen","Shiwei Bao","Heyu Xu","Liming Zhang","Wenxin Huang"]}},"tmdate":1736523209026,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1736523205261,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wenxin_Huang1"],"forum":"rBXuWEppE5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":263264,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1736523209026,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rBXuWEppE5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.27301v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"rossellini|an_analysis_of_binary_isotonic_regression_degrees_of_freedom_and_implications_for_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2607.27301"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2607-27301,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Raphael Rossellini and Rina Foygel Barber and Zhimei Ren and Jake A. Soloff},\n  title={An analysis of binary isotonic regression: degrees of freedom and implications for calibration},\n  year={2026},\n  month={July},\n  cdate={1782864000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2607.27301},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2607.27301}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Isotonic regression is a canonical tool for estimating monotone functions and calibrating probabilistic predictors. We provide a fully sharp finite-sample characterization of its worst-case degrees of freedom on binary samples. Specifically, we identify the binary sequences that maximize the number of distinct fitted values produced by isotonic regression. We develop a sharp bound on the degrees of freedom with a leading term of $\\frac{3}{(4π^2)^{1/3}} n^{2/3}$ using analytic number theory, improving on previous bounds. We then apply this result to calibration. Calibration is a central requirement for probabilistic prediction, and isotonic regression is a widely used post-processing method for improving calibration. Building on deterministic degrees-of-freedom bounds, we derive, to our knowledge, the first nontrivial distribution-free guarantee on the Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of isotonic regression. This ECE bound is fully model-free and distribution-free, only assuming $Y \\in \\{0,1\\}$."},"title":{"value":"An analysis of binary isotonic regression: degrees of freedom and implications for calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Raphael Rossellini","username":"~Raphael_Rossellini1"},{"fullname":"Rina Foygel Barber","username":""},{"fullname":"Zhimei Ren","username":""},{"fullname":"Jake A. Soloff","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1791406636843,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2607-27301"],"tcdate":1791406634405,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Raphael_Rossellini1"],"forum":"oIgk1CjtNC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":165180,"cdate":1782864000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791406636843,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"oIgk1CjtNC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/10012124/10012371.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"fan|development_calibration_and_image_processing_of_underwater_structured_light_vision_system_a_survey"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junfeng_Fan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuan_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chao_Zhou_0002:","~Yaming_Ou1","~Fengshui_Jing1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zengguang_Hou:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2023.3235420"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/FanWZOJH23,\n  author={Junfeng Fan and Xuan Wang and Chao Zhou and Yaming Ou and Fengshui Jing and Zengguang Hou},\n  title={Development, Calibration, and Image Processing of Underwater Structured Light Vision System: A Survey},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={72},\n  pages={1-18},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2023.3235420}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The underwater structured light vision system has the characteristics of a large amount of information, high measurement accuracy, and good robustness, which has attracted the attention of researchers and is widely used in the underwater 3-D measurement. In this article, the state-of-the-art techniques of the underwater structured light vision system are comprehensively summarized, which provides valuable guidance for relevant researchers. First, underwater structured light vision systems are classified and introduced, and their advantages and disadvantages are analyzed. Then, the calibration methods of the underwater structured light vision system are introduced, including underwater camera calibration, underwater laser calibration, and underwater projector calibration. Besides, the image processing methods of the underwater structured light vision system are analyzed, including laser structured light image processing and coded structured light image processing. Moreover, the applications of the underwater structured light vision system in underwater archeology, seabed mapping, and underwater structure inspection are introduced. On this basis, the research directions of the underwater structured light vision system are prospected."},"title":{"value":"Development, Calibration, and Image Processing of Underwater Structured Light Vision System: A Survey"},"authors":{"value":["Junfeng Fan","Xuan Wang","Chao Zhou","Yaming Ou","Fengshui Jing","Zengguang Hou"]}},"tmdate":1784515036334,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1740981239832,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fengshui_Jing1"],"forum":"UCw2YRV5qi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":344540,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784515036334,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UCw2YRV5qi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICCV 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10376473/10376477/10378257.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICCV/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"jin|lighting_every_darkness_in_two_pairs_a_calibrationfree_pipeline_for_raw_denoising"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Jin_0005:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jia-Wen_Xiao:","~Linghao_Han1","~Chunle_Guo1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ruixun_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xialei_Liu:","~Chongyi_Li1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCV51070.2023.01221"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iccv/JinXHGZLL23,\n  author={Xin Jin and Jia-Wen Xiao and Linghao Han and Chunle Guo and Ruixun Zhang and Xialei Liu and Chongyi Li},\n  title={Lighting Every Darkness in Two Pairs : A Calibration-Free Pipeline for RAW Denoising},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={13229-13238},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCV51070.2023.01221},\n  booktitle={ICCV},\n  crossref={conf/iccv/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration-based methods have dominated RAW image denoising under extremely low-light environments. However, these methods suffer from several main deficiencies: 1) the calibration procedure is laborious and time-consuming, 2) denoisers for different cameras are difficult to transfer, and 3) the discrepancy between synthetic noise and real noise is enlarged by high digital gain. To overcome the above shortcomings, we propose a calibration-free pipeline for Lighting Every Drakness (LED), regardless of the digital gain or camera sensor. Instead of calibrating the noise parameters and training repeatedly, our method could adapt to a target camera only with few-shot paired data and fine-tuning. In addition, well-designed structural modification during both stages alleviates the domain gap between synthetic and real noise without any extra computational cost. With 2 pairs for each additional digital gain (in total 6 pairs) and 0.5% iterations, our method achieves superior performance over other calibration-based methods."},"title":{"value":"Lighting Every Darkness in Two Pairs : A Calibration-Free Pipeline for RAW Denoising"},"authors":{"value":["Xin Jin","Jia-Wen Xiao","Linghao Han","Chunle Guo","Ruixun Zhang","Xialei Liu","Chongyi Li"]}},"tmdate":1762422107514,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731465546889,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chongyi_Li1"],"forum":"oyWVvAJa1i","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":190651,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762422107514,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"oyWVvAJa1i","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/8836328/08588371.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"tahir|a_stochastic_optimization_approach_to_magnetometer_calibration_with_gradient_estimates_using_simultaneous_perturbations"},"authorids":{"value":["~Muhammad_Tahir2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abdullah_Moazzam:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Khurram_Ali:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2018.2885624"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/TahirMA19,\n  author={Muhammad Tahir and Abdullah Moazzam and Khurram Ali},\n  title={A Stochastic Optimization Approach to Magnetometer Calibration With Gradient Estimates Using Simultaneous Perturbations},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={68},\n  number={10},\n  pages={4152-4161},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2018.2885624}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Triaxial magnetometers are indispensable part of every attitude sensing equipment for providing heading information. However, magnetometer measurements are affected by a number of error sources such as soft iron, hard iron, sensor nonorthogonality, and bias. These erroneous measurements, if not properly calibrated, deteriorate the quality of attitude estimates. In this paper, we present a new approach to calibrate triaxial magnetometer measurements by posing the calibration problem as a stochastic optimization problem. A scalar objective function is derived which involves magnetometer calibration parameters corresponding to all distortions. In order to recursively solve for optimized calibration parameters, a modified version of simultaneous perturbation stochastic approximation for the gradient is proposed for faster convergence. The proposed approach places no restrictions on the number of parameters involved and offers a complete solution by solving for all calibration parameters. The proposed approach is tested and validated using experimental data and the performance is compared against a reference approach."},"title":{"value":"A Stochastic Optimization Approach to Magnetometer Calibration With Gradient Estimates Using Simultaneous Perturbations"},"authors":{"value":["Muhammad Tahir","Abdullah Moazzam","Khurram Ali"]}},"tmdate":1747374703399,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1747374638753,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Muhammad_Tahir2"],"forum":"iv1dBLFrue","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":505498,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747374703399,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"iv1dBLFrue","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICDIP 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.spiedigitallibrary.org/conference-proceedings-of-spie/8009/80092F/Calibration-of-digital-camera-IOP-using-radial-alignment-constrain-and/10.1117/12.896196.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICDIP/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"wu|calibration_of_digital_camera_iop_using_radial_alignment_constrain_and_vanish_point_geometry"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Wu:","~Gang_Xu9","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zenglai_Dong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiejun_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1117/12.896196"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icdip/WuXDW11,\n  author={Jun Wu and Gang Xu and Zenglai Dong and Jiejun Wang},\n  title={Calibration of digital camera IOP using radial alignment constrain and vanish point geometry},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={80092F},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1117/12.896196},\n  booktitle={ICDIP},\n  crossref={conf/icdip/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents our research on calibrating IOP of digital camera with mono-view. First, the Radial Alignment Constrain (RAC) from Tsai's two-stage calibration is outlined and extended to handle with camera principle point parameters. Second, we relate principle point to other camera parameters by means of two vanishing points from the perspective projection of group lines, parallel to World Coordinate System X, Y axis, respectively, and with the relation, the function-dependence among parameters within the equation derived from extended RAC are eliminated. Third, based on the extended RAC, a new nonlinear equation for IOP calibration purpose is formulated and optimally solved with Least Square technique as well as initial value for parameters are determined. Finally, a set of stimulated images generated with virtual plane grid lines and known camera parameters are used for calibration experiment. The comparison of proposed approach with Tsai's two-stage calibration is also given in this paper and valuable conclusions are conducted as well."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of digital camera IOP using radial alignment constrain and vanish point geometry"},"authors":{"value":["Jun Wu","Gang Xu","Zenglai Dong","Jiejun Wang"]}},"tmdate":1731490863813,"pdate":1293840000000,"tcdate":1731487527272,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~gang_xu8"],"forum":"Nck4CTMit1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":218157,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731490863813,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Nck4CTMit1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Vis. Comput. 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s00371-021-02172-9.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/VC/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"pintus|a_practical_and_efficient_model_for_intensity_calibration_of_multilight_image_collections"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ruggero_Pintus:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alberto_Jaspe-Villanueva:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Antonio_Zorcolo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Markus_Hadwiger:","~Enrico_Gobbetti1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00371-021-02172-9"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/vc/PintusVZHG21,\n  author={Ruggero Pintus and Alberto Jaspe-Villanueva and Antonio Zorcolo and Markus Hadwiger and Enrico Gobbetti},\n  title={A practical and efficient model for intensity calibration of multi-light image collections},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={Vis. Comput.},\n  volume={37},\n  number={9-11},\n  pages={2755-2767},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s00371-021-02172-9}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present a novel practical and efficient mathematical formulation for light intensity calibration of multi-light image collections (MLICs). Inspired by existing and orthogonal calibration methods, we design a hybrid solution that leverages their strengths while overcoming most of their weaknesses. We combine the rationale of approaches based on fixed analytical models with the interpolation scheme of image domain methods. This allows us to minimize the final residual error in light intensity estimation, without imposing an overly constraining illuminant type. Unlike previous approaches, the proposed calibration strategy proved to be simpler, more efficient and versatile, and extremely adaptable in different setup scenarios. We conduct an extensive analysis and validation of our new light model compared to several state-of-the-art techniques, and we show how the proposed solution provides a more reliable outcomes in terms of accuracy and precision, and a more stable calibration across different light positions/orientations, and with a more general light form factor."},"title":{"value":"A practical and efficient model for intensity calibration of multi-light image collections"},"authors":{"value":["Ruggero Pintus","Alberto Jaspe-Villanueva","Antonio Zorcolo","Markus Hadwiger","Enrico Gobbetti"]}},"tmdate":1731464072167,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1731462528646,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Enrico_Gobbetti1"],"forum":"sqIdtZ6AO2","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":188158,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731464072167,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"sqIdtZ6AO2","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Robotics 2009"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/8860/4773132/04738394.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TROB/2009"},"paperhash":{"value":"ammi|automatic_camerabased_microscope_calibration_for_a_telemicromanipulation_system_using_a_virtual_pattern"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mehdi_Ammi:","~Vincent_Frémont1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Antoine_Ferreira:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TRO.2008.2006866"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/trob/AmmiFF09,\n  author={Mehdi Ammi and Vincent Frémont and Antoine Ferreira},\n  title={Automatic Camera-Based Microscope Calibration for a Telemicromanipulation System Using a Virtual Pattern},\n  year={2009},\n  cdate={1230768000000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Robotics},\n  volume={25},\n  number={1},\n  pages={184-191},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TRO.2008.2006866}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In the context of virtualized-reality-based telemicromanipulation, this paper presents a visual calibration technique for an optical microscope coupled to a charge-coupled device (CCD) camera. The accuracy and flexibility of the proposed automatic virtual calibration method, based on parallel single-plane properties, are outlined. In contrast to standard approaches, a 3-D virtual calibration pattern is constructed using the micromanipulator tip with subpixel-order localization in the image frame. The proposed procedure leads to a linear system whose solution provides directly both the intrinsic and extrinsic parameters of the geometrical model. Computer simulations and real data have been used to test the proposed technique, and promising results have been obtained. Based on the proposed calibration techniques, a 3-D virtual microenvironment of the workspace is reconstructed through the real-time imaging of two perpendicular optical microscopes. Our method provides a flexible, easy-to-use technical alternative to the classical techniques used in micromanipulation systems."},"title":{"value":"Automatic Camera-Based Microscope Calibration for a Telemicromanipulation System Using a Virtual Pattern"},"authors":{"value":["Mehdi Ammi","Vincent Frémont","Antoine Ferreira"]}},"tmdate":1730366106774,"pdate":1230768000000,"tcdate":1730366084410,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~FREMONT_Vincent1"],"forum":"Tl2lECOQzm","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":165381,"cdate":1230768000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730366106774,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Tl2lECOQzm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7083369/10254630/10251600.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|easyhec_accurate_and_automatic_handeye_calibration_via_differentiable_rendering_and_space_exploration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Linghao_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuzhe_Qin:","~Xiaowei_Zhou1","~Hao_Su1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2023.3315551"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/ChenQZS23,\n  author={Linghao Chen and Yuzhe Qin and Xiaowei Zhou and Hao Su},\n  title={EasyHeC: Accurate and Automatic Hand-Eye Calibration Via Differentiable Rendering and Space Exploration},\n  year={2023},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1698796800000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={8},\n  number={11},\n  pages={7234-7241},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2023.3315551}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Hand-eye calibration is a critical task in robotics, as it directly affects the efficacy of critical operations such as manipulation and grasping. Traditional methods for achieving this objective necessitate the careful design of joint poses and the use of specialized calibration markers, while most recent learning-based approaches using solely pose regression are limited in their abilities to diagnose inaccuracies. In this work, we introduce a new approach to hand-eye calibration called EasyHeC, which is markerless, white-box, and delivers superior accuracy and robustness. We propose to use two key technologies: differentiable rendering-based camera pose optimization and consistency-based joint space exploration, which enables accurate end-to-end optimization of the calibration process and eliminates the need for the laborious manual design of robot joint poses. Our evaluation demonstrates superior performance in synthetic and real-world datasets, enhancing downstream manipulation tasks by providing precise camera poses for locating and interacting with objects."},"title":{"value":"EasyHeC: Accurate and Automatic Hand-Eye Calibration Via Differentiable Rendering and Space Exploration"},"authors":{"value":["Linghao Chen","Yuzhe Qin","Xiaowei Zhou","Hao Su"]}},"tmdate":1730256413990,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1728413692916,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hao_Su1"],"forum":"B3wvrodg8F","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":145714,"cdate":1698796800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1730256413990,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"B3wvrodg8F","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6287639/8600701/08600308.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ACCESS/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"darwish|a_robust_calibration_method_for_consumer_grade_rgbd_sensors_for_precise_indoor_reconstruction"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Walid_Darwish:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenbin_Li_0010:","~Shengjun_Tang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bo_Wu_0004:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wu_Chen_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2018.2890713"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/DarwishLTWC19,\n  author={Walid Darwish and Wenbin Li and Shengjun Tang and Bo Wu and Wu Chen},\n  title={A Robust Calibration Method for Consumer Grade RGB-D Sensors for Precise Indoor Reconstruction},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={7},\n  pages={8824-8833},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2018.2890713}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"RGB-D cameras, which can be attached to any mobile device and work under different operation platforms (e.g., iOS, Android, and Windows), have great potential for indoor 3D modeling and navigation due to their low cost and small size. The main problems of RGB-D cameras for such applications are their range limitations and deteriorated depth accuracy. For example, for a 7-m range, the distance error of structure sensor (one type of RGB-D camera) reaches nearly 0.5 m. We propose a new calibration procedure for RGB-D sensors to improve the depth accuracy. First, the baseline between RGB and IR cameras is calibrated using the direct linear transform method. The distortions of the RGB and IR cameras and the IR projector are then calibrated using the newly proposed two-lens distortion model. Finally, the remaining depth systematic errors are calibrated using an empirical model. Compared to existing calibration methods, the new calibration method considers distortions from both the IR camera and projector and significantly improves the accuracy of far-range depth measurements. The experimental results show that the proposed calibration method can precisely calibrate the full range of the RGB-D sensor, up to 7 m, with an overall depth accuracy of 1.9%, compared to the 5.5% accuracy of the manufacturer’s depth estimation. To demonstrate the significance of calibration in indoor mapping, the 3D point cloud of a room (4.5 m x 3.5 m) is generated using the RGB-D SLAM system. The accuracy of the 3D model with the proposed calibration method is approximately 1.5 cm, compared to 7.0 cm using the manufacturer’s calibration parameters."},"title":{"value":"A Robust Calibration Method for Consumer Grade RGB-D Sensors for Precise Indoor Reconstruction"},"authors":{"value":["Walid Darwish","Wenbin Li","Shengjun Tang","Bo Wu","Wu Chen"]}},"tmdate":1744100446105,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1744100443177,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shengjun_Tang1"],"forum":"rmUEIi9Pet","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":381534,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744100446105,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rmUEIi9Pet","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.16041v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"han|on_flangebased_3d_handeye_calibration_for_soft_robotic_tactile_welding"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xudong_Han:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ning_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu_Jie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:He_Wang:","~Fang_Wan2","~Chaoyang_Song1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.16041"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2407-16041,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Xudong Han and Ning Guo and Yu Jie and He Wang and Fang Wan and Chaoyang Song},\n  title={On Flange-based 3D Hand-Eye Calibration for Soft Robotic Tactile Welding},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2407.16041},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.16041}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper investigates the direct application of standardized designs on the robot for conducting robot hand-eye calibration by employing 3D scanners with collaborative robots. The well-established geometric features of the robot flange are exploited by directly capturing its point cloud data. In particular, an iterative method is proposed to facilitate point cloud processing toward a refined calibration outcome. Several extensive experiments are conducted over a range of collaborative robots, including Universal Robots UR5 & UR10 e-series, Franka Emika, and AUBO i5 using an industrial-grade 3D scanner Photoneo Phoxi S & M and a commercial-grade 3D scanner Microsoft Azure Kinect DK. Experimental results show that translational and rotational errors converge efficiently to less than 0.28 mm and 0.25 degrees, respectively, achieving a hand-eye calibration accuracy as high as the camera's resolution, probing the hardware limit. A welding seam tracking system is presented, combining the flange-based calibration method with soft tactile sensing. The experiment results show that the system enables the robot to adjust its motion in real-time, ensuring consistent weld quality and paving the way for more efficient and adaptable manufacturing processes."},"title":{"value":"On Flange-based 3D Hand-Eye Calibration for Soft Robotic Tactile Welding"},"authors":{"value":["Xudong Han","Ning Guo","Yu Jie","He Wang","Fang Wan","Chaoyang Song"]}},"tmdate":1747233110765,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747232533992,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chaoyang_Song1"],"forum":"9g1IAAfzFq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":459318,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747233110765,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9g1IAAfzFq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6287639/10005208/10076445.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ACCESS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|novel_geometric_calibration_method_for_pantilt_camera_with_single_control_point"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Zhang_0062:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiefu_Wei:","~Pei_An2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaomao_Liu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2023.3259219"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/ZhangWAL23,\n  author={Jun Zhang and Jiefu Wei and Pei An and Xiaomao Liu},\n  title={Novel Geometric Calibration Method for Pan-Tilt Camera With Single Control Point},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={11},\n  pages={34175-34185},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2023.3259219}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Pan-tilt (PT) camera is an indispensable part of the video surveillance systems due to its rotatable property and low cost. As the primitive output of the PT camera limits its practical applications, an accurate calibration method is required. Previous single point calibration method (SPCM) was presented to estimate angles Pan and Tilt via single control point. For the more intuitive geometric interpretation and more robust performance, we propose a novel single point calibration method (novel SPCM). In this scheme, a nonlinear PT camera function (PT function) is established via a normalization approach. With PT function, calibration problem is converted as the intersection situation of two circles formed by Pan and Tilt. Solutions can be regarded as the intersection points of two circles in 3D space. Theoretical analysis shows that novel SPCM is stable to measurement noise, for it still finds the least-square solutions even if two circles have no intersection. In the simulation experiments, reprojection error of novel SPCM is 32.4% smaller than SPCM for the large noise situation. It is 25.1% faster than SPCM. With the angle smooth strategy, novel SPCM achieves accurate and stable performance in the real data experiment."},"title":{"value":"Novel Geometric Calibration Method for Pan-Tilt Camera With Single Control Point"},"authors":{"value":["Jun Zhang","Jiefu Wei","Pei An","Xiaomao Liu"]}},"tmdate":1727396740695,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727396733886,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~anpei1"],"forum":"RWKesAiCDl","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":84327,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727396740695,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"RWKesAiCDl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2024"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Uncertainty Quantification","Large Language Models"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/da72b14dcbc5886b13d4e6055c036735a6205ef5.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Despite the significant progress made in practical applications of aligned language models (LMs), they tend to be overconfident in output answers compared to the corresponding pre-trained LMs. In this work, we systematically evaluate the impact of the alignment process on logit-based uncertainty calibration of LMs under the multiple-choice setting. We first conduct a thoughtful empirical study on how aligned LMs differ in calibration from their pre-trained counterparts. Experimental results reveal that there are two distinct uncertainties in LMs under the multiple-choice setting, which are responsible for the answer decision and the format preference of the LMs, respectively. Then, we investigate the role of these two uncertainties on aligned LM's calibration through fine-tuning in simple synthetic alignment schemes and conclude that one reason for aligned LMs' overconfidence is the conflation of these two types of uncertainty. Furthermore, we examine the utility of common post-hoc calibration methods for aligned LMs and propose an easy-to-implement and sample-efficient method to calibrate aligned LMs. We hope our findings could provide insights into the design of more reliable alignment processes for LMs."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nhe2024investigating,\ntitle={Investigating Uncertainty Calibration of Aligned Language Models under the Multiple-Choice Setting},\nauthor={Guande He and Peng Cui and Jianfei Chen and Wenbo Hu and Jun Zhu},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=pVKEFtGkM6}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Investigating Uncertainty Calibration of Aligned Language Models under the Multiple-Choice Setting"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d2a48d8e4e8994c5166a70f7eeab8e2e85f90d86.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"he|investigating_uncertainty_calibration_of_aligned_language_models_under_the_multiplechoice_setting"},"authorids":{"value":["~Guande_He1","~Peng_Cui6","~Jianfei_Chen1","~Wenbo_Hu1","~Jun_Zhu2"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Guande He","Peng Cui","Jianfei Chen","Wenbo Hu","Jun Zhu"]}},"tmdate":1707625710514,"tcdate":1695351191714,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission4513/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission4513/Authors"],"forum":"pVKEFtGkM6","number":4513,"cdate":1695351191714,"mdate":1707625710514,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission4513/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission4513/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"pVKEFtGkM6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.1522.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|kge_calibrator_an_efficient_probability_calibration_method_of_knowledge_graph_embedding_models_for_trustworthy_link_prediction"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.1522"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/YangTC25,\n  author={Yang Yang and Mohan Timilsina and Edward Curry},\n  title={KGE Calibrator: An Efficient Probability Calibration Method of Knowledge Graph Embedding Models for Trustworthy Link Prediction},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={29964-29987},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.1522},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Knowledge graph embedding (KGE) models are designed for the task of link prediction, which aims to infer missing triples by learning representations for entities and relations. While KGE models excel at ranking-based link prediction, the critical issue of probability calibration has been largely overlooked, resulting in uncalibrated estimates that limit their adoption in high-stakes domains where trustworthy predictions are essential. Addressing this is challenging, as we demonstrate that existing calibration methods are ill-suited to KGEs, often significantly degrading the essential ranking performance they are meant to support. To overcome this, we introduce the KGE Calibrator (KGEC), the first probability calibration method tailored for KGE models to enhance the trustworthiness of their predictions. KGEC integrates three key techniques: a Jump Selection Strategy that improves efficiency by selecting the most informative instances while filtering out less significant ones; Multi-Binning Scaling, which models different confidence levels separately to increase capacity and flexibility; and a Wasserstein distance-based calibration loss that further boosts calibration performance. Extensive experiments across multiple datasets demonstrate that KGEC consistently outperforms existing calibration methods in terms of both effectiveness and efficiency, making it a promising solution for calibration in KGE models."},"title":{"value":"KGE Calibrator: An Efficient Probability Calibration Method of Knowledge Graph Embedding Models for Trustworthy Link Prediction"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yang Yang","username":""},{"fullname":"Mohan Timilsina","username":""},{"fullname":"Edward Curry","username":"~Edward_Curry1"}]}},"tmdate":1791884435820,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/YangTC25"],"tcdate":1791884425917,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Edward_Curry1"],"forum":"DA339upTrA","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":176646,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791884435820,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"DA339upTrA","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Probability Calibration; Knowledge Graph Embedding Model"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2025 May Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025kge,\ntitle={{KGE} Calibrator: An Efficient Probability Calibration Method of Knowledge Graph Embedding Models for Trustworthy Link Prediction},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=6BZ47mcGEw},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"KGE Calibrator: An Efficient Probability Calibration Method of Knowledge Graph Embedding Models for Trustworthy Link Prediction"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Approaches low compute settings-efficiency"]},"abstract":{"value":"Knowledge graph embedding (KGE) models are designed for the task of link prediction, which aims to infer missing triples by learning accurate representations for entities and relations within a knowledge graph. However, existing KGE research largely overlooks the issue of probability calibration, leading to uncalibrated probability estimates that fail to reflect the true correctness of predicted triples, potentially resulting in erroneous decisions. Moreover, current calibration methods are not well-suited for KGE models, and no dedicated probability calibration method has been specifically designed for them. In this paper, we propose KGE Calibrator (KGEC), the first probability calibration method tailored for KGE models to enhance the trustworthiness of their predictions. To achieve this, we introduce a Jump Selection Strategy that improves efficiency by selecting the most informative instances while filtering out less significant ones. We also propose Multi-Binning Scaling, which models different probability levels separately to increase the model’s capacity and flexibility. Additionally, we propose a Wasserstein distance-based loss function to further boost calibration performance. Extensive experiments across multiple datasets demonstrate that KGEC consistently outperforms existing calibration methods in terms of both effectiveness and efficiency, making it a promising solution for probability calibration in KGE models."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a2e044bf3f2446b38db7697275ca8eb7287397d6.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Efficient/Low-Resource Methods for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782962245590,"tcdate":1747741152247,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission7556/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission7556/Authors"],"forum":"6BZ47mcGEw","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":7556,"cdate":1747741152247,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782962245590,"odate":1753766020949,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","id":"6BZ47mcGEw","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Probability Calibration; Knowledge Graph Embedding Model"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2025 February Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025kge,\ntitle={{KGE} Calibrator+: An Efficient Probability Calibration Method of Knowledge Graph Embedding Models for Trustworthy Link Prediction},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - February 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=7wjw1hc4Qh},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"KGE Calibrator+: An Efficient Probability Calibration Method of Knowledge Graph Embedding Models for Trustworthy Link Prediction"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Approaches low compute settings-efficiency"]},"abstract":{"value":"Knowledge graph embedding (KGE) models are designed for the task of link prediction, which aims to infer missing triples by learning accurate representations for entities and relations within a knowledge graph. However, existing KGE research largely overlooks the issue of probability calibration, leading to uncalibrated probability estimates that fail to reflect the true correctness of predicted triples, potentially resulting in erroneous decisions. Moreover, existing calibration methods are not well-suited for KGE models, and no dedicated probability calibration method has been specifically designed for them. In this paper, we propose KGE Calibrator+, the first probability calibration method tailored for KGE models to enhance the trustworthiness of their predictions. To achieve this, we introduce Jump Selection Strategy, which selects the most informative data while filtering out less significant data, and Multi-Binning Scaling, which models different probability levels separately to enhance model capacity and flexibility. Furthermore, we propose a Wasserstein distance-based loss function, improving both calibration performance and optimization stability. Extensive experiments across multiple data sets demonstrate that KGE Calibrator+ consistently outperforms existing calibration methods in terms of both effectiveness and efficiency, making it a promising solution for probability calibration in KGE models."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/57e7d627bd2720ac5aaad13db57bede49bc0ec00.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Efficient/Low-Resource Methods for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782962133235,"tcdate":1739707023982,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission8309/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission8309/Authors"],"forum":"7wjw1hc4Qh","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":8309,"cdate":1739707023982,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782962133235,"odate":1746821355472,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February","id":"7wjw1hc4Qh","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"LREC/COLING 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.lrec-main.205.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/COLING/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"cao|beyond_code_evaluate_thought_steps_for_complex_code_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liuwen_Cao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Cai_0001:","~Jiexin_Wang1","~Hongkui_He1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hailin_Huang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.lrec-main.205"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/coling/Cao0WHH24,\n  author={Liuwen Cao and Yi Cai and Jiexin Wang and Hongkui He and Hailin Huang},\n  title={Beyond Code: Evaluate Thought Steps for Complex Code Generation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={2296-2306},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2024.lrec-main.205},\n  booktitle={LREC/COLING},\n  crossref={conf/coling/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code generation aims to generate code in a general-purpose programming language, such as C++, based on natural language intents. Existing efforts primarily focus on relatively simple programming problems and fail to evaluate the thought process involved in complex programming scenarios. In this paper, we introduce “steps-guided code generation,” a task that assesses the quality of both thought steps and code implementation to evaluate the overall management of handling a complex programming problem. To support this task, we construct CodeStepsEval, a real-world scenario dataset of complex programming problems in the C++ programming language with varying levels of difficulty. Comprehensive experiments on this dataset demonstrate the importance of high-quality steps in enhancing code generation performance and the challenges faced by the code LLMs in this task."},"title":{"value":"Beyond Code: Evaluate Thought Steps for Complex Code Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Liuwen Cao","Yi Cai","Jiexin Wang","Hongkui He","Hailin Huang"]}},"tmdate":1767748163052,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/coling/Cao0WHH24"],"tcdate":1767664984605,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jiexin_Wang1"],"forum":"GCcAxSCsR8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":717592,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1767748163052,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"GCcAxSCsR8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Circuits and Systems for Video Technology"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"xue|selfcalibration_flow_guided_denoising_diffusion_model_for_human_pose_transfer"},"html":{"value":"https://scholars.cityu.edu.hk/en/publications/d39cf686-8566-4d6d-8abc-cde389c9987f"},"abstract":{"value":"The human pose transfer task aims to generate synthetic person images that preserve the style of reference images while accurately aligning them with the desired target pose. However, existing methods based on generative adversarial networks (GANs) struggle to produce realistic details and often face spatial misalignment issues. On the other hand, methods relying on denoising diffusion models require a large number of model parameters, resulting in slower convergence rates. To address these challenges, we propose a self-calibration flow-guided module (SCFM) to establish precise spatial correspondence between reference images and target poses. This module facilitates the denoising diffusion model in predicting the noise at each denoising step more effectively. Additionally, we introduce a multi-scale feature fusing module (MSFF) that enhances the denoising U-Net architecture through a cross-attention mechanism, achieving better performance with a reduced parameter count. Our proposed model outperforms state-of-the-art methods on the DeepFashion and Market-1501 datasets in terms of both the quantity and quality of the synthesized images. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/zylwithxy/SCFM-guided-DDPM. © 2024 IEEE."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{d39cf68685664d6d8abccde389c9987f,\n  title     = \"Self-Calibration Flow Guided Denoising Diffusion Model for Human Pose Transfer\",\n  abstract  = \"The human pose transfer task aims to generate synthetic person images that preserve the style of reference images while accurately aligning them with the desired target pose. However, existing methods based on generative adversarial networks (GANs) struggle to produce realistic details and often face spatial misalignment issues. On the other hand, methods relying on denoising diffusion models require a large number of model parameters, resulting in slower convergence rates. To address these challenges, we propose a self-calibration flow-guided module (SCFM) to establish precise spatial correspondence between reference images and target poses. This module facilitates the denoising diffusion model in predicting the noise at each denoising step more effectively. Additionally, we introduce a multi-scale feature fusing module (MSFF) that enhances the denoising U-Net architecture through a cross-attention mechanism, achieving better performance with a reduced parameter count. Our proposed model outperforms state-of-the-art methods on the DeepFashion and Market-1501 datasets in terms of both the quantity and quality of the synthesized images. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/zylwithxy/SCFM-guided-DDPM. {\\textcopyright} 2024 IEEE.\",\n  keywords  = \"Circuits and systems, Correlation, Diffusion models, Human pose transfer, Image synthesis, Multi-scale feature fusing module, Noise reduction, Optical flow, Self-Calibration flow module, Task analysis, Training\",\n  author    = \"Yu Xue and Lai-Man Po and Wing-Yin Yu and Haoxuan Wu and Xuyuan Xu and Kun Li and Yuyang Liu\",\n  year      = \"2024\",\n  month     = sep,\n  doi       = \"10.1109/TCSVT.2024.3382948\",\n  language  = \"English\",\n  volume    = \"34\",\n  pages     = \"7896--7911\",\n  journal   = \"IEEE Transactions on Circuits and Systems for Video Technology\",\n  issn      = \"1051-8215\",\n  publisher = \"IEEE\",\n  number    = \"9\",\n}"},"title":{"value":"Self-Calibration Flow Guided Denoising Diffusion Model for Human Pose Transfer"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yu Xue","username":"~Yu_Xue6"},{"fullname":"Lai-Man Po"},{"fullname":"Wing-Yin Yu"},{"fullname":"Haoxuan Wu"},{"fullname":"Xuyuan Xu"},{"fullname":"Kun Li"},{"fullname":"Yuyang Liu"}]}},"tmdate":1789089556064,"pdate":1725148800000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tcsvt.2024.3382948"],"tcdate":1764342067274,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yu_XUE2"],"forum":"wyb7nU4hW7","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":18931,"cdate":1749326314328,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789089556064,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"wyb7nU4hW7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.1384.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|probing_relative_interaction_and_dynamic_calibration_in_multimodal_entity_alignment"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chenxiao_Li:","~Jingwei_Cheng1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qiang_Tong_0003:","~Fu_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cairui_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.1384/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/LiC00W25,\n  author={Chenxiao Li and Jingwei Cheng and Qiang Tong and Fu Zhang and Cairui Wang},\n  title={Probing Relative Interaction and Dynamic Calibration in Multi-modal Entity Alignment},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={28504-28516},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.1384/},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2025-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-modal entity alignment aims to identify equivalent entities between two different multi-modal knowledge graphs. Current methods have made significant progress by improving embedding and cross-modal fusion. However, most of them depend on using loss functions to capture the relationship between modalities or adopt a one-time strategy to directly compute modality weights using attention mechanisms, which overlooks the relative interactions between modalities at the entity level and the accuracy of modality weights, thereby hindering the generalization to diverse entities. To address this challenge, we propose RICEA, a relative interaction and calibration framework for multi-modal entity alignment, which dynamically computes weights based on the relative interaction and recalibrates the weights according to their uncertainties. Among these, we propose a novel method called ADC that utilizes attention mechanisms to perceive the uncertainty of the weight for each modality, rather than directly calculating the weight of each modality as in previous works. Across 5 datasets and 23 settings, our proposed framework significantly outperforms other baselines. Our code and data are available at https://github.com/ChenxiaoLi-Joe/RICEA."},"title":{"value":"Probing Relative Interaction and Dynamic Calibration in Multi-modal Entity Alignment"},"authors":{"value":["Chenxiao Li","Jingwei Cheng","Qiang Tong","Fu Zhang","Cairui Wang"]}},"tmdate":1768878956703,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/LiC00W25"],"tcdate":1768476979434,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jingwei_Cheng1"],"forum":"DgLHg1uQgb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":762227,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768878956703,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"DgLHg1uQgb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/22/21/8136/pdf?version=1666923100"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|an_improved_online_selfcalibration_method_utilizing_angular_velocity_observation_for_ultra_high_accuracy_pigabased_imu"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yongfeng_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuling_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gongliu_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaojun_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongwu_Liu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s22218136"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/ZhangHYZL22,\n  author={Yongfeng Zhang and Shuling Hu and Gongliu Yang and Xiaojun Zhou and Hongwu Liu},\n  title={An Improved Online Self-Calibration Method Utilizing Angular Velocity Observation for Ultra High Accuracy PIGA-Based IMU},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={22},\n  number={21},\n  pages={8136},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s22218136}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In the field of ultra high accuracy inertial measurement unit (IMU), pendulous integrating gyroscopic accelerometer (PIGA) has become a research hot spot due to its high-end performance. However, PIGA is sensitive to angular velocity, and the calibration process of PIGA-based IMU will be very complicated, which makes online self-calibration difficult to implement. To solve the above problems, we proposed an online self-calibration method utilizing angular velocity observation. The main contributions of this study are twofold: (1) An error analysis of PIGA is conducted in this paper, and the error model has also been simplified to suit the self-calibration model. (2) An improved online self-calibration method utilizing angular observation based on a simplified PIGA error model is proposed in this study. Experimental results show that the self-calibration method proposed in this study can improve the PIGA online calibration accuracy effectively (with the accuracy within 0.02 m/s/pulse), which can improve the dynamic accuracy of the PIGA."},"title":{"value":"An Improved Online Self-Calibration Method Utilizing Angular Velocity Observation for Ultra High Accuracy PIGA-Based IMU"},"authors":{"value":["Yongfeng Zhang","Shuling Hu","Gongliu Yang","Xiaojun Zhou","Hongwu Liu"]}},"tmdate":1730423640195,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1730423606076,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yongfeng_Zhang1"],"forum":"ZS3Bhrcm83","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":166618,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730423640195,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ZS3Bhrcm83","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Medical Imaging"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/42/9969446/09825706.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"gungor|transms_transformers_for_superresolution_calibration_in_magnetic_particle_imaging"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TMI.2022.3189693"},"abstract":{"value":"Magnetic particle imaging (MPI) offers exceptional contrast for magnetic nanoparticles (MNP) at high spatio-temporal resolution. A common procedure in MPI starts with a calibration scan to measure the system matrix (SM), which is then used to set up an inverse problem to reconstruct images of the MNP distribution during subsequent scans. This calibration enables the reconstruction to sensitively account for various system imperfections. Yet time-consuming SM measurements have to be repeated under notable changes in system properties. Here, we introduce a novel deep learning approach for accelerated MPI calibration based on Transformers for SM super-resolution (TranSMS). Low-resolution SM measurements are performed using large MNP samples for improved signal-to-noise ratio efficiency, and the high-resolution SM is super-resolved via model-based deep learning. TranSMS leverages a vision transformer module to capture contextual relationships in low-resolution input images, a dense convolutional module for localizing high-resolution image features, and a data-consistency module to ensure measurement fidelity. Demonstrations on simulated and experimental data indicate that TranSMS significantly improves SM recovery and MPI reconstruction for up to 64-fold acceleration in two-dimensional imaging."},"title":{"value":"TranSMS: Transformers for Super-Resolution Calibration in Magnetic Particle Imaging"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Alper Gungor"},{"fullname":"Baris Askin"},{"fullname":"Damla Alptekin Soydan"},{"fullname":"Emine Ulku Saritas"},{"fullname":"Can Baris Top"},{"fullname":"Tolga Cukur","username":"~Tolga_Cukur1"}]}},"tmdate":1789091404450,"pdate":1669852800000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tmi.2022.3189693"],"tcdate":1762172023466,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Tolga_Cukur1"],"forum":"Vn8lbfDCQ4","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":2569,"cdate":1657569130589,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091404450,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Vn8lbfDCQ4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.16299v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"jürgens|a_calibration_test_for_evaluating_setbased_epistemic_uncertainty_representations"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mira_Jürgens:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Thomas_Mortier:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Eyke_Hüllermeier:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Viktor_Bengs:","~Willem_Waegeman1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.16299"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2502-16299,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Mira Jürgens and Thomas Mortier and Eyke Hüllermeier and Viktor Bengs and Willem Waegeman},\n  title={A calibration test for evaluating set-based epistemic uncertainty representations},\n  year={2025},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1738368000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2502.16299},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.16299}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The accurate representation of epistemic uncertainty is a challenging yet essential task in machine learning. A widely used representation corresponds to convex sets of probabilistic predictors, also known as credal sets. One popular way of constructing these credal sets is via ensembling or specialized supervised learning methods, where the epistemic uncertainty can be quantified through measures such as the set size or the disagreement among members. In principle, these sets should contain the true data-generating distribution. As a necessary condition for this validity, we adopt the strongest notion of calibration as a proxy. Concretely, we propose a novel statistical test to determine whether there is a convex combination of the set's predictions that is calibrated in distribution. In contrast to previous methods, our framework allows the convex combination to be instance dependent, recognizing that different ensemble members may be better calibrated in different regions of the input space. Moreover, we learn this combination via proper scoring rules, which inherently optimize for calibration. Building on differentiable, kernel-based estimators of calibration errors, we introduce a nonparametric testing procedure and demonstrate the benefits of capturing instance-level variability on of synthetic and real-world experiments."},"title":{"value":"A calibration test for evaluating set-based epistemic uncertainty representations"},"authors":{"value":["Mira Jürgens","Thomas Mortier","Eyke Hüllermeier","Viktor Bengs","Willem Waegeman"]}},"tmdate":1747378600777,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1747378592754,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Willem_Waegeman1"],"forum":"msI4EQfJIS","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":506599,"cdate":1738368000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747378600777,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"msI4EQfJIS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Signal Image Video Process. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11760-025-04821-1.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|spatiotemporalmodal_collaborative_calibration_for_dynamic_depth_video_enhancement"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11760-025-04821-1"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sivp/XuZCWS25,\n  author={Xiaoling Xu and Sheng Zhao and Jian Cao and Xiaoqun Wu and Huilin Si},\n  title={Spatiotemporal-Modal Collaborative Calibration for Dynamic Depth Video Enhancement},\n  year={2025},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1761955200000},\n  journal={Signal Image Video Process.},\n  volume={19},\n  number={14},\n  pages={1216},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s11760-025-04821-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Modern depth sensors face limitations including low resolution, narrow FOV, and dynamic interference, leading to missing data and distortions. Existing methods mainly process single-frame depth features, underutilizing temporal cues like motion trajectories and occlusions in sequential frames. To address these limitations, we propose a three-stage Guidance-Calibration-Optimization architecture. Spatio-temporal guidance network employs memory units to process sequential frames and generate initial guided depth maps; depth-dominant network performs multi-modal calibration on the guided depth map, RGB semantics, and raw depth map to suppress modal interference and reinforce reliable features; geometric optimization smooths noise in homogeneous regions while preserving boundaries, improving visual consistency. This design reduces optical flow dependency and facilitates depth-focused temporal modeling. Experiments indicate enhanced depth video quality with improved completeness and accuracy, supporting dynamic scene understanding."},"title":{"value":"Spatiotemporal-Modal Collaborative Calibration for Dynamic Depth Video Enhancement"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Xiaoling Xu","username":""},{"fullname":"Sheng Zhao","username":""},{"fullname":"Jian Cao","username":""},{"fullname":"Xiaoqun Wu","username":"~Xiaoqun_Wu2"},{"fullname":"Huilin Si","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784640349268,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/sivp/XuZCWS25"],"tcdate":1784640345736,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Xiaoqun_Wu2"],"forum":"aflrcmNFiQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":83604,"cdate":1761955200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784640349268,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"aflrcmNFiQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/32977/35132"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AAAI/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"xue|freecap_hybrid_calibrationfree_motion_capture_in_open_environments"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Yiming_Ren1","","","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i9.32977"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aaai/XueRSYZM25,\n  author={Aoru Xue and Yiming Ren and Zining Song and Mao Ye and Xinge Zhu and Yuexin Ma},\n  title={FreeCap: Hybrid Calibration-Free Motion Capture in Open Environments},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={9032-9040},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i9.32977},\n  booktitle={AAAI},\n  crossref={conf/aaai/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We propose a novel hybrid calibration-free method FreeCap to accurately capture global multi-person motions in open environments. Our system combines a single LiDAR with expandable moving cameras, allowing for flexible and precise motion estimation in a unified world coordinate. In particular, We introduce a local-to-global pose-aware cross-sensor human-matching module that predicts the alignment among each sensor, even in the absence of calibration. Additionally, our coarse-to-fine sensor-expandable pose optimizer further optimizes the 3D human key points and the alignments, it is also capable of incorporating additional cameras to enhance accuracy. Extensive experiments on Human-M3 and FreeMotion datasets demonstrate that our method significantly outperforms state-of-the-art single-modal methods, offering an expandable and efficient solution for multi-person motion capture across various applications."},"title":{"value":"FreeCap: Hybrid Calibration-Free Motion Capture in Open Environments"},"authors":{"value":["Aoru Xue","Yiming Ren","Zining Song","Mao Ye","Xinge Zhu","Yuexin Ma"]}},"tmdate":1772263354249,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/aaai/XueRSYZM25"],"tcdate":1772263349017,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yiming_Ren1"],"forum":"T6yRXuyFFY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":838233,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772263354249,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"T6yRXuyFFY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICCP 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8103830/8116963/08117016.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICCP2/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"itu|automatic_extrinsic_camera_parameters_calibration_using_convolutional_neural_networks"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Razvan_Itu:","~Diana_Borza1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Radu_Danescu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCP.2017.8117016"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iccp2/ItuBD17,\n  author={Razvan Itu and Diana Borza and Radu Danescu},\n  title={Automatic extrinsic camera parameters calibration using convolutional neural networks},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  pages={273-278},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCP.2017.8117016},\n  booktitle={ICCP},\n  crossref={conf/iccp2/2017}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration is essential for accurate computer vision, and automatic calibration of some extrinsic parameters is needed in case the camera is placed on a mobile platform. The pitch and yaw angles, which are the most likely ones to change as the vehicle moves, can be inferred from the image coordinates of the vanishing point (VP). In this paper we present an artificial neural network approach for detecting the vanishing point position in road traffic scenarios. The network is trained using 2500 images which are first automatically annotated using a classical vanishing point detection algorithm, and then manually validated. The training and test datasets are made publicly available. The trained network was tested on more than 250 images not previously seen by the network, locating the VP accurately in more than 90% of the cases."},"title":{"value":"Automatic extrinsic camera parameters calibration using convolutional neural networks"},"authors":{"value":["Razvan Itu","Diana Borza","Radu Danescu"]}},"tmdate":1762519249095,"pdate":1483228800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iccp2/ItuBD17"],"tcdate":1762517652052,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~DIANA-LAURA_BORZA1"],"forum":"rNo3pWM1AJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":674932,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762519249095,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rNo3pWM1AJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MICCAI (2) 2005"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/11566489_15.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MICCAI/2005"},"paperhash":{"value":"stoyanov|laparoscope_selfcalibration_for_robotic_assisted_minimally_invasive_surgery"},"authorids":{"value":["~Danail_Stoyanov1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ara_Darzi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guang-Zhong_Yang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/11566489_15"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/miccai/StoyanovDY05,\n  author={Danail Stoyanov and Ara Darzi and Guang-Zhong Yang},\n  title={Laparoscope Self-calibration for Robotic Assisted Minimally Invasive Surgery},\n  year={2005},\n  cdate={1104537600000},\n  pages={114-121},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/11566489_15},\n  booktitle={MICCAI (2)},\n  crossref={conf/miccai/2005-2}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"For robotic assisted minimal access surgery, recovering 3D soft tissue deformation is important for intra-operative surgical guidance, motion compensation, and prescribing active constraints. We propose in this paper a method for determining varying focal lengths of stereo laparoscope cameras during robotic surgery. Laparoscopic images typically feature dynamic scenes of soft-tissue deformation and self-calibration is difficult with existing approaches due to the lack of rigid temporal constraints. The proposed method is based on the direct derivation of the focal lengths from the fundamental matrix of the stereo cameras with known extrinsic parameters. This solves a restricted self-calibration problem, and the introduction of the additional constraints improves the inherent accuracy of the algorithm. The practical value of the method is demonstrated with analysis of results from both synthetic and in vivo data sets."},"title":{"value":"Laparoscope Self-calibration for Robotic Assisted Minimally Invasive Surgery"},"authors":{"value":["Danail Stoyanov","Ara Darzi","Guang-Zhong Yang"]}},"tmdate":1731497502840,"pdate":1104537600000,"tcdate":1731497402269,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Danail_Stoyanov1"],"forum":"6GBhAocx4X","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":225503,"cdate":1104537600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731497502840,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"6GBhAocx4X","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6287639/9312710/09378540.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ACCESS/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|a_novel_airborne_dualantenna_insar_calibration_method_for_backprojection_imaging_model"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoning_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bingnan_Wang:","~Maosheng_Xiang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhongbin_Wang_0003:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2021.3066098"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/HuWXW21,\n  author={Xiaoning Hu and Bingnan Wang and Maosheng Xiang and Zhongbin Wang},\n  title={A Novel Airborne Dual-Antenna InSAR Calibration Method for Backprojection Imaging Model},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={9},\n  pages={43001-43012},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2021.3066098}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The interferometric synthetic aperture radar (InSAR) elevation inversion method based on the backprojection (BP) imaging model simplifies the interferometric processing chain where image registration and phase unwrapping are not required. Meanwhile, BP algorithm is suitable for radar with different imaging modes and geometry. However, the existing calibration methods are all aimed at traditional InSAR which adopts frequency domain imaging algorithm, but the calibration method for backprojection InSAR elevation inversion model has not been published. In this paper, we propose a novel InSAR calibration method which combines Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) estimation method with sensitivity equation method based on backprojection imaging model. FFT is used to process the interferometric phase which has removed the terrain phase of external digital elevation model (DEM) under BP imaging, and the estimation of interferometric parameters is realized through the phase fringe frequency. In the calibration method based on sensitivity equations, the deviations of interferometric parameters are calculated by solving sensitivity equations according to the three-dimensional information of ground control points. FFT estimation method not only generates the initial baseline parameters for sensitivity equation method, which is conducive to obtaining the global optimal solution, but also separates the calibration of phase offset and baseline parameters, which makes it possible to minimize the condition number of sensitivity matrix in sensitivity equation method. Finally, we obtain the calibrated interferometric parameters through iteration of sensitivity equation method and realize high accuracy DEM inversion. Both simulation experiment and airborne dual-antenna InSAR data processing are performed to verify the effectiveness of this method."},"title":{"value":"A Novel Airborne Dual-Antenna InSAR Calibration Method for Backprojection Imaging Model"},"authors":{"value":["Xiaoning Hu","Bingnan Wang","Maosheng Xiang","Zhongbin Wang"]}},"tmdate":1730697919437,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1730697913251,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Maosheng_Xiang1"],"forum":"9ZSDH6grG8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":169182,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730697919437,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9ZSDH6grG8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"HCI (23) 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-22095-1_55.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/HCI/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"ahn|calibration_time_reduction_through_source_imaging_in_brain_computer_interface_bci"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Minkyu_Ahn:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hohyun_Cho:","~Sung_Chan_Jun2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-22095-1_55"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/hci/AhnCJ11,\n  author={Minkyu Ahn and Hohyun Cho and Sung Chan Jun},\n  title={Calibration Time Reduction through Source Imaging in Brain Computer Interface (BCI)},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={269-273},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-22095-1_55},\n  booktitle={HCI (23)},\n  crossref={conf/hci/2011-23}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Brain Computer Interface (BCI) is mainly divided into two phases; calibration phase for training and feedback phase. A calibration phase is usually time-consuming, thereby, being likely to raise subjects’ fatigue at the early stage. For more convenient and applicable BCI system it should be investigated to reduce such preparation (calibration) time before feedback phase. Beamformer is a source imaging technique widely used in MEG/EEG source localization problem. It passes only signals produced at the designated source point and filters out other signals such as noise. We conjecture information in source space may be consistent over well trained and good subjects. This idea facilitates to reuse existing datasets from the same or different subjects. Using IVa data in BCI competition III, we constructed a classifier from other 4 subject’s training data and performance was evaluated in source domain. In this work, we observed the proposed approach worked well, resulting in relatively good accuracies (73.21%, 74.21%) for two subjects."},"title":{"value":"Calibration Time Reduction through Source Imaging in Brain Computer Interface (BCI)"},"authors":{"value":["Minkyu Ahn","Hohyun Cho","Sung Chan Jun"]}},"tmdate":1769653576721,"pdate":1325289600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/hci/AhnCJ11"],"tcdate":1769653555789,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sung_Chan_Jun2"],"forum":"0sKclJs77i","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":814201,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769653576721,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0sKclJs77i","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2024 Workshop on 3D Visual Representations for Robot Manipulation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/001cc81a9706cf9d4c82cc0b1e180817a57bbe2a.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Scene Representation","Hand-eye Calibration","Foundation Models"]},"venueid":{"value":"IEEE.org/2024/ICRA/Workshop/3D_Manipulation"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhi|unifying_scene_representation_and_handeye_calibration_with_3d_foundation_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Weiming_Zhi1","haozhant@andrew.cmu.edu","~Tianyi_Zhang14","~Matthew_Johnson-Roberson3"]},"abstract":{"value":"Representing the environment is a central problem for robots and is a prerequisite for downstream decision-making and motion planning. In the past, constructing a representation from a manipulator-mounted camera requires carefully calibrating the camera with a specific external calibration marker, such as a checkerboard, in advance. Recent advances in computer vision have given rise to \\emph{3D foundation models}: large pre-trained neural network models, capable of fast and accurate multi-view correspondence with very few images, and in the absence of rich visual features. This paper advocates for integrating 3D foundation models into scene representation approaches for robot systems with a manipulator-mounted RGB camera. In particular, we propose the Joint Calibration and Representation (JCR) method. JCR leverages RGB images captured by a manipulator-mounted camera, employing them to simultaneously construct a representation of the environment and calibrate the camera. We demonstrate the ability of JCR to build accurate scene representations with a low-cost RGB camera attached onto a manipulator without additional calibration."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhi2024unifying,\ntitle={Unifying Scene Representation and Hand-Eye Calibration with 3D Foundation Models},\nauthor={Weiming Zhi and haozhan tang and Tianyi Zhang and Matthew Johnson-Roberson},\nbooktitle={ICRA 2024 Workshop on 3D Visual Representations for Robot Manipulation},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=3DeSGbmgZ1}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Unifying Scene Representation and Hand-Eye Calibration with 3D Foundation Models"},"authors":{"value":["Weiming Zhi","haozhan tang","Tianyi Zhang","Matthew Johnson-Roberson"]}},"tmdate":1759166342311,"pdate":1713924004260,"tcdate":1712312558568,"writers":["IEEE.org/2024/ICRA/Workshop/3D_Manipulation","IEEE.org/2024/ICRA/Workshop/3D_Manipulation/Submission11/Authors"],"signatures":["IEEE.org/2024/ICRA/Workshop/3D_Manipulation/Submission11/Authors"],"forum":"3DeSGbmgZ1","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":11,"cdate":1712312558568,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["IEEE.org/2024/ICRA/Workshop/3D_Manipulation/-/Submission","IEEE.org/2024/ICRA/Workshop/3D_Manipulation/-/Post_Submission","IEEE.org/2024/ICRA/Workshop/3D_Manipulation/-/Edit"],"mdate":1759166342311,"odate":1713924004260,"domain":"IEEE.org/2024/ICRA/Workshop/3D_Manipulation","id":"3DeSGbmgZ1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.14081v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"jung|modular_meshed_ultrawideband_aided_inertial_navigation_with_robust_anchor_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Roland_Jung:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luca_Santoro:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Davide_Brunelli:","~Daniele_Fontanelli1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Stephan_Weiss_0002:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.14081"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2408-14081,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Roland Jung and Luca Santoro and Davide Brunelli and Daniele Fontanelli and Stephan Weiss},\n  title={Modular Meshed Ultra-Wideband Aided Inertial Navigation with Robust Anchor Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2408.14081},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.14081}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper introduces a generic filter-based state estimation framework that supports two state-decoupling strategies based on cross-covariance factorization. These strategies reduce the computational complexity and inherently support true modularity -- a perquisite for handling and processing meshed range measurements among a time-varying set of devices. In order to utilize these measurements in the estimation framework, positions of newly detected stationary devices (anchors) and the pairwise biases between the ranging devices are required. In this work an autonomous calibration procedure for new anchors is presented, that utilizes range measurements from multiple tags as well as already known anchors. To improve the robustness, an outlier rejection method is introduced. After the calibration is performed, the sensor fusion framework obtains initial beliefs of the anchor positions and dictionaries of pairwise biases, in order to fuse range measurements obtained from new anchors tightly-coupled. The effectiveness of the filter and calibration framework has been validated through evaluations on a recorded dataset and real-world experiments."},"title":{"value":"Modular Meshed Ultra-Wideband Aided Inertial Navigation with Robust Anchor Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Roland Jung","Luca Santoro","Davide Brunelli","Daniele Fontanelli","Stephan Weiss"]}},"tmdate":1747311842578,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747311756329,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Daniele_Fontanelli1"],"forum":"xp4x0FGwmN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":485666,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747311842578,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xp4x0FGwmN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10801246/10801290/10802297.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"jung|modular_meshed_ultrawideband_aided_inertial_navigation_with_robust_anchor_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Roland_Jung:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luca_Santoro:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Davide_Brunelli:","~Daniele_Fontanelli1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Stephan_Weiss_0002:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS58592.2024.10802297"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/JungSBF024,\n  author={Roland Jung and Luca Santoro and Davide Brunelli and Daniele Fontanelli and Stephan Weiss},\n  title={Modular Meshed Ultra-Wideband Aided Inertial Navigation with Robust Anchor Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={5627-5634},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS58592.2024.10802297},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper introduces a generic filter-based state estimation framework that supports two state-decoupling strategies based on cross-covariance factorization. These strategies reduce the computational complexity and inherently support true modularity – a perquisite for handling and processing meshed range measurements among a time-varying set of devices. In order to utilize these measurements in the estimation framework, positions of newly detected stationary devices (anchors) and the pairwise biases between the ranging devices are required. In this work an autonomous calibration procedure for new anchors is presented, that utilizes range measurements from multiple tags as well as already known anchors. To improve the robustness, an outlier rejection method is introduced. After the calibration is performed, the sensor fusion framework obtains initial beliefs of the anchor positions and dictionaries of pairwise biases, in order to fuse range measurements obtained from new anchors tightly-coupled. The effectiveness of the filter and calibration framework has been validated through evaluations on a recorded dataset and real-world experiments."},"title":{"value":"Modular Meshed Ultra-Wideband Aided Inertial Navigation with Robust Anchor Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Roland Jung","Luca Santoro","Davide Brunelli","Daniele Fontanelli","Stephan Weiss"]}},"tmdate":1747311759604,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747311757020,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Daniele_Fontanelli1"],"forum":"uCpC1mmJ89","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":485693,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747311759604,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"uCpC1mmJ89","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.07505v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"wise|a_continuoustime_approach_for_3d_radartocamera_extrinsic_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Emmett_Wise:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Juraj_Persic:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Christopher_Grebe:","~Ivan_Petrovic1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jonathan_Kelly:"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.07505"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2103-07505,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Emmett Wise and Juraj Persic and Christopher Grebe and Ivan Petrovic and Jonathan Kelly},\n  title={A Continuous-Time Approach for 3D Radar-to-Camera Extrinsic Calibration},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2103.07505},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.07505}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Reliable operation in inclement weather is essential to the deployment of safe autonomous vehicles (AVs). Robustness and reliability can be achieved by fusing data from the standard AV sensor suite (i.e., lidars, cameras) with weather robust sensors, such as millimetre-wavelength radar. Critically, accurate sensor data fusion requires knowledge of the rigid-body transform between sensor pairs, which can be determined through the process of extrinsic calibration. A number of extrinsic calibration algorithms have been designed for 2D (planar) radar sensors - however, recently-developed, low-cost 3D millimetre-wavelength radars are set to displace their 2D counterparts in many applications. In this paper, we present a continuous-time 3D radar-to-camera extrinsic calibration algorithm that utilizes radar velocity measurements and, unlike the majority of existing techniques, does not require specialized radar retroreflectors to be present in the environment. We derive the observability properties of our formulation and demonstrate the efficacy of our algorithm through synthetic and real-world experiments."},"title":{"value":"A Continuous-Time Approach for 3D Radar-to-Camera Extrinsic Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Emmett Wise","Juraj Persic","Christopher Grebe","Ivan Petrovic","Jonathan Kelly"]}},"tmdate":1731491489524,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1731488287018,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ivan_Petrovic1"],"forum":"5iohC92hsY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":218915,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731491489524,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5iohC92hsY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9560720/9560666/09561938.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"wise|a_continuoustime_approach_for_3d_radartocamera_extrinsic_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Emmett_Wise:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Juraj_Persic:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Christopher_Grebe:","~Ivan_Petrovic1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jonathan_Kelly:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA48506.2021.9561938"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/WisePGPK21,\n  author={Emmett Wise and Juraj Persic and Christopher Grebe and Ivan Petrovic and Jonathan Kelly},\n  title={A Continuous-Time Approach for 3D Radar-to-Camera Extrinsic Calibration},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={13164-13170},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA48506.2021.9561938},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Reliable operation in inclement weather is essential to the deployment of safe autonomous vehicles (AVs). Robustness and reliability can be achieved by fusing data from the standard AV sensor suite (i.e., lidars, cameras) with weather robust sensors, such as millimetre-wavelength radar. Critically, accurate sensor data fusion requires knowledge of the rigidbody transform between sensor pairs, which can be determined through the process of extrinsic calibration. A number of extrinsic calibration algorithms have been designed for 2D (planar) radar sensors—however, recently-developed, low-cost 3D millimetre-wavelength radars are set to displace their 2D counterparts in many applications. In this paper, we present a continuous-time 3D radar-to-camera extrinsic calibration algorithm that utilizes radar velocity measurements and, unlike the majority of existing techniques, does not require specialized radar retroreflectors to be present in the environment. We derive the observability properties of our formulation and demonstrate the efficacy of our algorithm through synthetic and real-world experiments."},"title":{"value":"A Continuous-Time Approach for 3D Radar-to-Camera Extrinsic Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Emmett Wise","Juraj Persic","Christopher Grebe","Ivan Petrovic","Jonathan Kelly"]}},"tmdate":1731491423865,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1731488285241,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ivan_Petrovic1"],"forum":"KynpuPSm0F","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":218838,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731491423865,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KynpuPSm0F","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2002"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/7916/21827/01013612.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2002"},"paperhash":{"value":"nakano|camera_calibration_with_precise_extraction_of_feature_points_using_projective_transformation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Katsuyuki_Nakano:","~Masatoshi_Okutomi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuichi_Hasegawa:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2002.1013612"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/NakanoOH02,\n  author={Katsuyuki Nakano and Masatoshi Okutomi and Yuichi Hasegawa},\n  title={Camera Calibration with Precise Extraction of Feature Points using Projective Transformation},\n  year={2002},\n  cdate={1009843200000},\n  pages={2532-2538},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2002.1013612},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2002}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we propose a method for precise camera calibration which conducts feature point extraction and camera parameter estimation iteratively. Many of conventional researches on camera calibration have focused on how to calculate camera parameters using data obtained from input images, that is, location of feature points. However, these input images suffer from distortions caused by perspective and lens imperfections. In our proposed method, at the beginning of the procedure, projective transformation matrices between image planes and a calibration target and lens distortion parameters are approximately estimated. These parameters are used in order to reduce the influence of distortions in input images. After the removal of distortions, feature points in processed images are localized precisely and used to update/calculate the projective transformation matrices, the lens distortion parameters and intrinsic camera parameters. These procedures are iterated until they converge and this iteration results in precise estimation of the parameters. The effectiveness of the proposed method has been recognized through experiments using synthesized data and real images."},"title":{"value":"Camera Calibration with Precise Extraction of Feature Points using Projective Transformation"},"authors":{"value":["Katsuyuki Nakano","Masatoshi Okutomi","Yuichi Hasegawa"]}},"tmdate":1731477502461,"pdate":1009843200000,"tcdate":1731476230874,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Masatoshi_Okutomi1"],"forum":"KQwC10MMtD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":201761,"cdate":1009843200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731477502461,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KQwC10MMtD","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce a post-hoc calibration method for LLMs that uses mixtures of experts and explicitly accounts for distribution shift at test time."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Trustworthy AI"},"keywords":{"value":["medical QA","calibration","distribution shift"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/Trustworthy_AI"},"paperhash":{"value":"mandyam|buddy_blending_training_and_deployment_data_with_weighted_expert_ensembles_for_posthoc_llm_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Aishwarya_Mandyam1","~Wenhui_Sophia_Lu1","~Wing_Hung_Wong1","~John_Duchi1","~Barbara_Engelhardt1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in high-stakes domains such as healthcare, but poor calibration and an inability to robustly handle distribution shift during deployment limit their utility. While existing approaches, such as fine-tuning and conformal prediction, can improve calibration, they are often computationally expensive or unreliable in small sample regimes. We propose a post-hoc calibration framework for a setting in which we have access to a large corpus dataset and a small dataset from a target deployment distribution. We assume these datasets correspond to the same task and have some overlap, but that the target distribution is shifted from that of the corpus dataset. Our method learns an ensemble calibrator on the corpus distribution, applies group-conditional temperature scaling to correct structured miscalibration, and then performs lightweight target adaptation by optimizing a discriminator-weighted, tilted empirical risk that emphasizes target-like examples. We provide finite-sample guarantees for groupwise temperature estimation and the resulting groupwise calibration error. Empirically, we demonstrate calibration under distribution shift in settings with few target samples. Across multiple-choice and open-ended question answering (QA) datasets, our method consistently improves calibration over baselines."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nmandyam2026buddy,\ntitle={{BUDDY}: Blending Training and Deployment Data with Weighted Expert Ensembles for Post-hoc {LLM} Calibration},\nauthor={Aishwarya Mandyam and Wenhui Sophia Lu and Wing Hung Wong and John Duchi and Barbara E Engelhardt},\nbooktitle={ICLR 2026 Workshop on Principled Design for Trustworthy AI - Interpretability, Robustness, and Safety across Modalities},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=zY5rw5SUGE}\n}"},"title":{"value":"BUDDY: Blending Training and Deployment Data with Weighted Expert Ensembles for Post-hoc LLM Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Aishwarya Mandyam","Wenhui Sophia Lu","Wing Hung Wong","John Duchi","Barbara E Engelhardt"]}},"tmdate":1772879485990,"pdate":1772431446185,"tcdate":1770099972957,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/Trustworthy_AI","ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/Trustworthy_AI/Submission257/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/Trustworthy_AI/Submission257/Authors"],"forum":"zY5rw5SUGE","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":257,"cdate":1770099972957,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/Trustworthy_AI/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/Trustworthy_AI/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/Trustworthy_AI/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/Trustworthy_AI/Submission257/-/Revision"],"mdate":1772879485990,"odate":1772431446185,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Workshop/Trustworthy_AI","id":"zY5rw5SUGE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/file/1df1df43b58845650b8dada00fca9772-Paper-Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/NIPS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"du|mercury_a_code_efficiency_benchmark_for_code_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Mingzhe_Du1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Anh_Tuan_Luu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bin_Ji:","~Qian_Liu20","~See-Kiong_Ng1"]},"html":{"value":"http://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/1df1df43b58845650b8dada00fca9772-Abstract-Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/nips/DuLJLN24,\n  author={Mingzhe Du and Anh Tuan Luu and Bin Ji and Qian Liu and See-Kiong Ng},\n  title={Mercury: A Code Efficiency Benchmark for Code Large Language Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  url={http://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/1df1df43b58845650b8dada00fca9772-Abstract-Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track.html},\n  booktitle={NeurIPS},\n  crossref={conf/nips/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Amidst the recent strides in evaluating Large Language Models for Code (Code LLMs), existing benchmarks have mainly focused on the functional correctness of generated code, neglecting the importance of their computational efficiency. To fill the gap, we present Mercury, the first code efficiency benchmark for Code LLMs. It comprises 1,889 Python tasks, each accompanied by adequate solutions that serve as real-world efficiency baselines, enabling a comprehensive analysis of the runtime distribution. Based on the distribution, we introduce a new metric Beyond, which computes a runtime-percentile-weighted Pass score to reflect functional correctness and code efficiency simultaneously. On Mercury, leading Code LLMs can achieve 65% on Pass, while less than 50% on Beyond. Given that an ideal Beyond score would be aligned with the Pass score, it indicates that while Code LLMs exhibit impressive capabilities in generating functionally correct code, there remains a notable gap in their efficiency. Finally, our empirical experiments reveal that Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) serves as a robust baseline for enhancing code efficiency compared with Supervised Fine Tuning (SFT), which paves a promising avenue for future exploration of efficient code generation. Our code and data are available on GitHub: https://github.com/Elfsong/Mercury."},"title":{"value":"Mercury: A Code Efficiency Benchmark for Code Large Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Mingzhe Du","Anh Tuan Luu","Bin Ji","Qian Liu","See-Kiong Ng"]}},"tmdate":1768455380801,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1741950099412,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mingzhe_Du1"],"forum":"TwOyXq3cNp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":368964,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768455380801,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"TwOyXq3cNp","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce a code efficiency benchmark to explore whether LLMs can generate efficient code."},"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2024 Track Datasets and Benchmarks Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["code generation","language model","computational efficiency","benchmark"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/a855b892e7a31aa47d0cb503fc579de642a679f5.pdf"},"abstract":{"value":"Amidst the recent strides in evaluating Large Language Models for Code (Code LLMs), existing benchmarks have mainly focused on the functional correctness of generated code, neglecting the importance of their computational efficiency. To fill the gap, we present Mercury, the first code efficiency benchmark for Code LLMs. It comprises 1,889 Python tasks, each accompanied by adequate solutions that serve as real-world efficiency baselines, enabling a comprehensive analysis of the runtime distribution. Based on the distribution, we introduce a new metric Beyond, which computes a runtime-percentile-weighted Pass score to reflect functional correctness and code efficiency simultaneously. On Mercury, leading Code LLMs can achieve 65% on Pass, while less than 50% on Beyond. Given that an ideal Beyond score would be aligned with the Pass score, it indicates that while Code LLMs exhibit impressive capabilities in generating functionally correct code, there remains a notable gap in their efficiency. Finally, our empirical experiments reveal that Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) serves as a robust baseline for enhancing code efficiency compared with Supervised Fine Tuning (SFT), which paves a promising avenue for future exploration of efficient code generation. Our code and data are available on GitHub: https://github.com/Elfsong/Mercury."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\ndu2024mercury,\ntitle={Mercury: A Code Efficiency Benchmark for Code Large Language Models},\nauthor={Mingzhe Du and Anh Tuan Luu and Bin Ji and Qian Liu and See-Kiong Ng},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-eight Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems Datasets and Benchmarks Track},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=vyraA7xt4c}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Mercury: A Code Efficiency Benchmark for Code Large Language Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/0bbc002b1388393dd711702e93f601e59e8ae470.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track"},"paperhash":{"value":"du|mercury_a_code_efficiency_benchmark_for_code_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Mingzhe_Du1","~Anh_Tuan_Luu2","~Bin_Ji3","~Qian_Liu2","~See-Kiong_Ng1"]},"authors":{"value":["Mingzhe Du","Anh Tuan Luu","Bin Ji","Qian Liu","See-Kiong Ng"]}},"tmdate":1731500226662,"pdate":1727347089238,"tcdate":1714885513165,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission148/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission148/Authors"],"forum":"vyraA7xt4c","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":148,"cdate":1714885513165,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission148/-/Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission148/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1731500226662,"odate":1731500226647,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track","id":"vyraA7xt4c","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"3DTV-Conference 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7547443/7548877/07548887.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/3DTV/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"dima|assessment_of_multicamera_calibration_algorithms_for_twodimensional_camera_arrays_relative_to_ground_truth_position_and_direction"},"authorids":{"value":["~Elijs_Dima1","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/3DTV.2016.7548887"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/3dtv/DimaSO16,\n  author={Elijs Dima and Mårten Sjöström and Roger Olsson},\n  title={Assessment of multi-camera calibration algorithms for two-dimensional camera arrays relative to ground truth position and direction},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  pages={1-4},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/3DTV.2016.7548887},\n  booktitle={3DTV-Conference},\n  crossref={conf/3dtv/2016}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration methods are commonly evaluated on cumulative reprojection error metrics, on disparate one-dimensional datasets. To evaluate calibration of cameras in two-dimensional ar-rays, assessments need to be made on two-dimensional datasets with constraints on camera parameters. In this study, accuracy of several multi-camera calibration methods has been evaluated on camera parameters that are affecting view projection the most. As input data, we used a 15-viewpoint two-dimensional dataset with intrinsic and extrinsic parameter constraints and extrinsic ground truth. The assessment showed that self-calibration methods using structure-from-motion reach equal intrinsic and extrinsic parameter estimation accuracy with standard checkerboard calibration algorithm, and surpass a well-known self-calibration toolbox, BlueCCal. These results show that self-calibration is a viable approach to calibrating two-dimensional camera arrays, but improvements to state-of-art multi-camera feature matching are necessary to make BlueCCal as accurate as other self-calibration methods for two-dimensional camera arrays."},"title":{"value":"Assessment of multi-camera calibration algorithms for two-dimensional camera arrays relative to ground truth position and direction"},"authors":{"value":["Elijs Dima","Mårten Sjöström","Roger Olsson"]}},"tmdate":1772170846421,"pdate":1483142400000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/3dtv/DimaSO16"],"tcdate":1772170832273,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Elijs_Dima1"],"forum":"xlfrOj5VOF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":831426,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772170846421,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xlfrOj5VOF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"2022 International Symposium on Medical Robotics (ISMR)"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/69501a7b70dd4e59aa30ee4cbfb324686d104797.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"xiao|delta_robot_kinematic_calibration_for_precise_robotassisted_retinal_surgery"},"authorids":{"value":["bxiao4@jhu.edu","aalamda3@jhu.edu","~Kefan_Song2","aebrahi5@jhu.edu","~PETER_L_GEHLBACH1","~Russell_Taylor1","~Iulian_Iordachita1"]},"abstract":{"value":"High precision is required for ophthalmic robotic systems. This paper presents the kinematic calibration for the delta robot which is part of the next generation of Steady-Hand Eye Robot (SHER). A linear error model is derived based on geometric error parameters. Two experiments with different ranges of workspace are conducted with laser sensors measuring displacement. The error parameters are identified and applied in the kinematics to compensate for modeling error. To achieve better accuracy, Bernstein polynomials are adopted to fit the error residuals after compensation. After the kinematic calibration process, the error residuals of the delta robot are reduced to satisfy the clinical requirements."},"title":{"value":"Delta Robot Kinematic Calibration for Precise Robot-Assisted Retinal Surgery"},"authors":{"value":["Boyang Xiao","Alireza Alamdar","Kefan Song","Ali Ebrahimi","PETER L GEHLBACH","Russ Taylor","Iulian Iordachita"]}},"tmdate":1749481340810,"pdate":1656388800000,"tcdate":1749481340810,"writers":["bxiao4@jhu.edu","aalamda3@jhu.edu","~Kefan_Song2","aebrahi5@jhu.edu","~PETER_L_GEHLBACH1","~Russell_Taylor1","~Iulian_Iordachita1"],"signatures":["~Kefan_Song2"],"forum":"0aOY9a1fqY","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":37366,"cdate":1749481340810,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1749481340810,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"0aOY9a1fqY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"TAROS (1) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-72059-8_21.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/TAROS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|3d_printer_based_open_source_calibration_platform_for_whisker_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liyou_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Omar_Ali:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Soumo_Emmanuel_Arnaud:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Eden_Attenborough:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jacob_Swindell:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:George_Davies:","~Charles_Fox1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-72059-8_21"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/taros/ZhouAAASDF24,\n  author={Liyou Zhou and Omar Ali and Soumo Emmanuel Arnaud and Eden Attenborough and Jacob Swindell and George Davies and Charles Fox},\n  title={3D Printer Based Open Source Calibration Platform for Whisker Sensors},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={249-255},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-72059-8_21},\n  booktitle={TAROS (1)},\n  crossref={conf/taros/2024-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Whisker sensors have been an area of active research in recent years for their interesting use cases in tactile robotics and mammal physiology research. Several attempts have been made to develop open-source versions of the sensor to promote wider adoption. However, the existing calibration solutions for these sensors are highly proprietary, cost-prohibitive and error-prone. In this paper, we present a low-cost open-source calibration and testing platform for whisker sensors based on an off-the-shelf 3D printer. We demonstrate its effectiveness by calibrating a whisker sensor for radial contact distance inference. All artefacts of the design are open-sourced and are fully reproducible."},"title":{"value":"3D Printer Based Open Source Calibration Platform for Whisker Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Liyou Zhou","Omar Ali","Soumo Emmanuel Arnaud","Eden Attenborough","Jacob Swindell","George Davies","Charles Fox"]}},"tmdate":1747168729200,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747168698014,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Charles_Fox1"],"forum":"2LiY5iHl8B","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":451080,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747168729200,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2LiY5iHl8B","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"TAROS (1) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-72059-8_21.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/TAROS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|3d_printer_based_open_source_calibration_platform_for_whisker_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liyou_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Omar_Ali:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Soumo_Emmanuel_Arnaud:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Eden_Attenborough:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jacob_Swindell:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:George_Davies:","~Charles_Fox1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-72059-8_21"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/taros/ZhouAAASDF24,\n  author={Liyou Zhou and Omar Ali and Soumo Emmanuel Arnaud and Eden Attenborough and Jacob Swindell and George Davies and Charles Fox},\n  title={3D Printer Based Open Source Calibration Platform for Whisker Sensors},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={249-255},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-72059-8_21},\n  booktitle={TAROS (1)},\n  crossref={conf/taros/2024-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Whisker sensors have been an area of active research in recent years for their interesting use cases in tactile robotics and mammal physiology research. Several attempts have been made to develop open-source versions of the sensor to promote wider adoption. However, the existing calibration solutions for these sensors are highly proprietary, cost-prohibitive and error-prone. In this paper, we present a low-cost open-source calibration and testing platform for whisker sensors based on an off-the-shelf 3D printer. We demonstrate its effectiveness by calibrating a whisker sensor for radial contact distance inference. All artefacts of the design are open-sourced and are fully reproducible."},"title":{"value":"3D Printer Based Open Source Calibration Platform for Whisker Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Liyou Zhou","Omar Ali","Soumo Emmanuel Arnaud","Eden Attenborough","Jacob Swindell","George Davies","Charles Fox"]}},"tmdate":1747168700392,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747168687003,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Charles_Fox1"],"forum":"drJ1k73Cny","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":451054,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747168700392,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"drJ1k73Cny","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IP&C 2015"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-319-23814-2_25.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IP-C/2015"},"paperhash":{"value":"schmidt|the_calibration_of_the_pantilt_units_for_the_active_stereo_head"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Adam_Schmidt:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Li_Sun_0005:","~Gerardo_Aragon-Camarasa1","~Jan_Paul_Siebert1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-23814-2_25"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ip-c/SchmidtSAS15,\n  author={Adam Schmidt and Li Sun and Gerardo Aragon-Camarasa and Jan Paul Siebert},\n  title={The Calibration of the Pan-Tilt Units for the Active Stereo Head},\n  year={2015},\n  cdate={1420070400000},\n  pages={213-221},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-23814-2_25},\n  booktitle={IP&C},\n  crossref={conf/ip-c/2015}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present a method for precise calibration of the pan-tilt unit based on the Levenberg-Marquardt optimization. Two different error functions are proposed—the first based on the position of the calibration marker and the second one indirectly incorporating the orientation of the marker. The proposed method was experimentally evaluated and the obtained results confirm its usefulness for the precise stereo-head operation."},"title":{"value":"The Calibration of the Pan-Tilt Units for the Active Stereo Head"},"authors":{"value":["Adam Schmidt","Li Sun","Gerardo Aragon-Camarasa","Jan Paul Siebert"]}},"tmdate":1731529875494,"pdate":1420070400000,"tcdate":1731492529687,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Gerardo_Aragon-Camarasa1"],"forum":"FVDtZs9E4l","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":222772,"cdate":1420070400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1731529875494,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"FVDtZs9E4l","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ASE 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/KBSE/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|how_effectively_do_code_language_models_understand_poorreadability_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chao_Hu3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yitian_Chai:","~Hao_Zhou47","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fandong_Meng:","~Jie_Zhou6","~Xiaodong_Gu1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3691620.3695072"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/kbse/HuCZM0024,\n  author={Chao Hu and Yitian Chai and Hao Zhou and Fandong Meng and Jie Zhou and Xiaodong Gu},\n  title={How Effectively Do Code Language Models Understand Poor-Readability Code?},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={795-806},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3691620.3695072},\n  booktitle={ASE},\n  crossref={conf/kbse/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code language models such as CodeT5 and CodeLlama have demonstrated substantial achievement in code comprehension. While the majority of research efforts have focused on improving model architectures and training processes, we find that the current benchmarks used for evaluating code comprehension models are confined to high-readability code, regardless of the popularity of low-readability code in reality. As such, they are inadequate to demonstrate the full spectrum of the model's ability, particularly the robustness to varying readability degrees. In this paper, we analyze the robustness of code summarization models to code with varying readability, including seven obfuscated datasets derived from existing benchmarks. Our findings indicate that current code summarization models are vulnerable to code with poor readability. In particular, their performance predominantly depends on semantic cues within the code, often neglecting the syntactic aspects. Existing benchmarks are biased toward evaluating semantic features, thereby overlooking the models' ability to understand nonsensitive syntactic features. Based on the findings, we present Poor-CodeSumEval, a new evaluation benchmark on code summarization tasks. PoorCodeSumEval innovatively introduces readability into the testing process, considering semantic, syntactic, and their cross-obfuscation, thereby providing a more comprehensive and rigorous evaluation of code summarization models. Our studies also provide more insightful suggestions for future research, such as constructing multi-readability benchmarks to evaluate the robustness of models on poor-readability code, proposing readability-awareness metrics, and automatic methods for code data cleaning and normalization."},"title":{"value":"How Effectively Do Code Language Models Understand Poor-Readability Code?"},"authors":{"value":["Chao Hu","Yitian Chai","Hao Zhou","Fandong Meng","Jie Zhou","Xiaodong Gu"]}},"tmdate":1792984179677,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1739940330677,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiaodong_Gu1"],"forum":"AyKGvP0tAE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":326379,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1792984179677,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"AyKGvP0tAE","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We compare score adaptation and localized calibration under a common label budget, deriving finite-sample coverage and length bounds that reveal trade-offs between global error propagation, quantile structure, and local sample size."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Conformal prediction","conditional coverage","score adaptation","localized calibration","quantile regression","finite-sample guarantees."]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/bacb1a176f97f22c45befce4c34ce8844e8f2fae.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Given a fixed nonconformity score $S$ and target coverage $1-\\alpha$, should extra labels transform the score before global calibration, or calibrate it locally?\nUnder score regularity, we bound both routes' errors in conditional coverage and oracle-relative length, with high probability over calibration, given the training data.\nSubtracting an estimated conditional score quantile $q_{S}^{1-\\alpha}(\\cdot)$ before global calibration gives a three-term error bound, valid simultaneously wherever the score assumptions hold: the pointwise quantile-estimation error, its average over covariates, and a calibration fluctuation.\nWe prove that this average term is needed: an error confined to one region causes over-coverage elsewhere, even with population calibration.\nFor randomly localized conformal prediction (RLCP), we bound localization bias by the target quantile's variation alone; the conditional score distribution need not be continuous in the covariate.\nThese bounds hold over the realized centre's neighbourhood and account for the smaller effective calibration sample.\nWe apply the global bounds to local-polynomial and deep-network quantile estimators.\nNeural estimators can exploit additive or compositional structure, whereas our RLCP bound depends on neighbourhood data mass in the full covariate space.\nThe bounds favour quantile learning when the fitting data can capture this structure; local calibration remains useful when the fit is poor but nearby data are plentiful.\nSimulations and eight real datasets illustrate the global route's gains; low-dimensional simulations show when localization is preferable."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026adapt,\ntitle={Adapt the Score or Localize the Calibration? A Finite-Sample Comparison for Conditional Conformal Prediction},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=eObZON9uO0},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Adapt the Score or Localize the Calibration? A Finite-Sample Comparison for Conditional Conformal Prediction"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7e55046675ff548085cac3ad7f8db616590af80a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791233287716,"tcdate":1789737104237,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission43062/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission43062/Authors"],"forum":"eObZON9uO0","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":43062,"cdate":1789737104237,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission43062/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791233287716,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"eObZON9uO0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICASSP 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10887540/10887541/10888381.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICASSP/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|improving_robustness_of_posthoc_calibration_against_common_corruptions_by_learnable_augmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Zhang:","~Minghao_Hu2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhunchen_Luo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wei_Luo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guotong_Geng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoyin_Bai:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP49660.2025.10888381"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icassp/ZhangHLLGB25,\n  author={Jun Zhang and Minghao Hu and Zhunchen Luo and Wei Luo and Guotong Geng and Xiaoyin Bai},\n  title={Improving Robustness of Post-hoc Calibration Against Common Corruptions By Learnable Augmentation},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={1-5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP49660.2025.10888381},\n  booktitle={ICASSP},\n  crossref={conf/icassp/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Various research has addressed the overconfidence problem, and we focus on improving the robustness of post-hoc calibration (e.g., temperature scaling, TS) when the test set shifts from the training set by image corruption. TS is greatly affected by the validation set, which previous work has proposed to perturb by Gaussian noise to improve calibration under domain drift. Inspired by this, we discovered that the same or similar augmentation on the validation set substantially improved TS under corrupted shift. We proposed a learnable and dynamic augmentation-based TS method, AugTS, which minimizes the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) between the augmented validation and corrupted test set. Experiments on corrupted versions of CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and TinyImageNet show that AugTS can significantly improve calibration under corrupted shifts compared with competitive baselines."},"title":{"value":"Improving Robustness of Post-hoc Calibration Against Common Corruptions By Learnable Augmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Jun Zhang","Minghao Hu","Zhunchen Luo","Wei Luo","Guotong Geng","Xiaoyin Bai"]}},"tmdate":1754099096032,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icassp/ZhangHLLGB25"],"tcdate":1754099085774,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Minghao_Hu2"],"forum":"aufNyRSzOt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":610323,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1754099096032,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"aufNyRSzOt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"J. Math. Imaging Vis. 2012"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10851-012-0332-4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/JMIV/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"chung|optimal_filters_from_calibration_data_for_image_deconvolution_with_data_acquisition_error"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Julianne_Chung:","~Matthias_Chung2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dianne_P._O'Leary:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10851-012-0332-4"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/jmiv/ChungCO12,\n  author={Julianne Chung and Matthias Chung and Dianne P. O'Leary},\n  title={Optimal Filters from Calibration Data for Image Deconvolution with Data Acquisition Error},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  journal={J. Math. Imaging Vis.},\n  volume={44},\n  number={3},\n  pages={366-374},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s10851-012-0332-4}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Data acquisition errors due to dead pixels or other hardware defects can cause undesirable artifacts in imaging applications. Compensating for these defects typically requires knowledge such as a defective pixel map, which can be difficult or costly to obtain and which is not necessarily static. However, recent calibration data is readily available in many applications. In this paper, we compute optimal filters for image deconvolution with denoising using only this calibration data, by minimizing the empirical Bayes risk. We derive a bound on how the reconstruction changes as the number of dead pixels grows. We show that our approach is able to reconstruct missing information better than standard filtering approaches and is robust even in the presence of a large number of defects and to defects that arise after calibration."},"title":{"value":"Optimal Filters from Calibration Data for Image Deconvolution with Data Acquisition Error"},"authors":{"value":["Julianne Chung","Matthias Chung","Dianne P. O'Leary"]}},"tmdate":1715186810797,"pdate":1325376000000,"tcdate":1715186786620,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Matthias_Chung2"],"forum":"NXzCeu3BTZ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":7935,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1715186810797,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"NXzCeu3BTZ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"VMV 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"http://localhost:4000/bitstreams/99ed5ae9-3753-4d96-a5c6-23a5838a24db/download"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/VMV/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"manakov|a_mathematical_model_and_calibration_procedure_for_galvanometric_laser_scanning_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alkhazur_Manakov:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hans-Peter_Seidel:","~Ivo_Ihrke1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.2312/PE/VMV/VMV11/207-214"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/vmv/ManakovSI11,\n  author={Alkhazur Manakov and Hans-Peter Seidel and Ivo Ihrke},\n  title={A Mathematical Model and Calibration Procedure for Galvanometric Laser Scanning Systems},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={207-214},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.2312/PE/VMV/VMV11/207-214},\n  booktitle={VMV},\n  crossref={conf/vmv/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Laser galvanometric scanning systems are commonly used in various fields such as three dimensional scanning, medical imaging, material processing, measurement devices and laser display systems. The systems of such kind suffer from distortions. On top of that they do not have a center of projection, which makes it impossible to use common projector calibration procedures. The paper presents a novel mathematical model to predict the image distortions caused by galvanometric mirror scanning systems. In addition, we describe a calibration procedure for recovering its intrinsic and extrinsic parameters."},"title":{"value":"A Mathematical Model and Calibration Procedure for Galvanometric Laser Scanning Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Alkhazur Manakov","Hans-Peter Seidel","Ivo Ihrke"]}},"tmdate":1741193405562,"pdate":1293840000000,"tcdate":1741193379695,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ivo_Ihrke1"],"forum":"iMhr1AlL45","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":354817,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1741193405562,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"iMhr1AlL45","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Image Processing"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/83/11355710/11321075.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"fu|token_calibration_for_transformerbased_domain_adaptation"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2025.3647367"},"abstract":{"value":"Unsupervised Domain Adaptation (UDA) aims to transfer knowledge from a labeled source domain to an unlabeled target domain by learning domain-invariant representations. Motivated by the recent success of Vision Transformers (ViTs), several UDA approaches have adopted ViT architectures to exploit fine-grained patch-level representations, which are unified as Transformer-based  $D$ omain  $A$ daptation (TransDA) independent of CNN-based. However, we have a key observation in TransDA: due to inherent domain shifts, patches (tokens) from different semantic categories across domains may exhibit abnormally high similarities, which can mislead the self-attention mechanism and degrade adaptation performance. To solve that, we propose a novel  $P$ atch- $A$ daptation Transformer (PATrans), which first identifies similarity-anomalous patches and then adaptively suppresses their negative impact to domain alignment, i.e. token calibration. Specifically, we introduce a  $P$ atch- $A$ daptation  $A$ ttention (PAA) mechanism to replace the standard self-attention mechanism, which consists of a weight-shared triple-branch mixed attention mechanism and a patch-level domain discriminator. The mixed attention integrates self-attention and cross-attention to enhance intra-domain feature modeling and inter-domain similarity estimation. Meanwhile, the patch-level domain discriminator quantifies the anomaly probability of each patch, enabling dynamic reweighting to mitigate the impact of unreliable patch correspondences. Furthermore, we introduce a contrastive attention regularization strategy, which leverages category-level information in a contrastive learning framework to promote class-consistent attention distributions. Extensive experiments on four benchmark datasets demonstrate that PATrans attains significant improvements over existing state-of-the-art UDA methods (e.g., 89.2% on the VisDA-2017). Code is available at: https://github.com/YSY145/PATrans"},"title":{"value":"Token Calibration for Transformer-Based Domain Adaptation"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Xiaowei Fu"},{"fullname":"Shiyu Ye"},{"fullname":"Chenxu Zhang"},{"fullname":"Fuxiang Huang","username":"~Fuxiang_Huang2"},{"fullname":"Xin Xu"},{"fullname":"Lei Zhang"}]}},"tmdate":1789091385680,"pdate":1767225600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tip.2025.3647367"],"tcdate":1769613924507,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Fuxiang_Huang2"],"forum":"W88zqBhS78","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":42648,"cdate":1767295061079,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091385680,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"W88zqBhS78","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2308.09419v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|attention_calibration_for_transformerbased_sequential_recommendation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Peilin_Zhou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qichen_Ye:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yueqi_Xie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jingqi_Gao:","~Shoujin_Wang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jae_Boum_Kim:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chenyu_You:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sunghun_Kim_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2308.09419"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2308-09419,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Peilin Zhou and Qichen Ye and Yueqi Xie and Jingqi Gao and Shoujin Wang and Jae Boum Kim and Chenyu You and Sunghun Kim},\n  title={Attention Calibration for Transformer-based Sequential Recommendation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2308.09419},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2308.09419}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Transformer-based sequential recommendation (SR) has been booming in recent years, with the self-attention mechanism as its key component. Self-attention has been widely believed to be able to effectively select those informative and relevant items from a sequence of interacted items for next-item prediction via learning larger attention weights for these items. However, this may not always be true in reality. Our empirical analysis of some representative Transformer-based SR models reveals that it is not uncommon for large attention weights to be assigned to less relevant items, which can result in inaccurate recommendations. Through further in-depth analysis, we find two factors that may contribute to such inaccurate assignment of attention weights: sub-optimal position encoding and noisy input. To this end, in this paper, we aim to address this significant yet challenging gap in existing works. To be specific, we propose a simple yet effective framework called Attention Calibration for Transformer-based Sequential Recommendation (AC-TSR). In AC-TSR, a novel spatial calibrator and adversarial calibrator are designed respectively to directly calibrates those incorrectly assigned attention weights. The former is devised to explicitly capture the spatial relationships (i.e., order and distance) among items for more precise calculation of attention weights. The latter aims to redistribute the attention weights based on each item's contribution to the next-item prediction. AC-TSR is readily adaptable and can be seamlessly integrated into various existing transformer-based SR models. Extensive experimental results on four benchmark real-world datasets demonstrate the superiority of our proposed ACTSR via significant recommendation performance enhancements. The source code is available at https://github.com/AIM-SE/AC-TSR."},"title":{"value":"Attention Calibration for Transformer-based Sequential Recommendation"},"authors":{"value":["Peilin Zhou","Qichen Ye","Yueqi Xie","Jingqi Gao","Shoujin Wang","Jae Boum Kim","Chenyu You","Sunghun Kim"]}},"tmdate":1768791981893,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2308-09419"],"tcdate":1768448048461,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shoujin_Wang1"],"forum":"BjBrB3hv1Y","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":754722,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768791981893,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"BjBrB3hv1Y","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CIKM 2023"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CIKM/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|attention_calibration_for_transformerbased_sequential_recommendation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Peilin_Zhou1","~Qichen_Ye1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yueqi_Xie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jingqi_Gao:","~Shoujin_Wang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jae_Boum_Kim:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chenyu_You:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sunghun_Kim_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3583780.3614785"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cikm/ZhouYXGWKYK23,\n  author={Peilin Zhou and Qichen Ye and Yueqi Xie and Jingqi Gao and Shoujin Wang and Jae Boum Kim and Chenyu You and Sunghun Kim},\n  title={Attention Calibration for Transformer-based Sequential Recommendation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={3595-3605},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3583780.3614785},\n  booktitle={CIKM},\n  crossref={conf/cikm/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Transformer-based sequential recommendation (SR) has been booming in recent years, with the self-attention mechanism as its key component. Self-attention has been widely believed to be able to effectively select those informative and relevant items from a sequence of interacted items for next-item prediction via learning larger attention weights for these items. However, this may not always be true in reality. Our empirical analysis of some representative Transformer-based SR models reveals that it is not uncommon for large attention weights to be assigned to less relevant items, which can result in inaccurate recommendations. Through further in-depth analysis, we find two factors that may contribute to such inaccurate assignment of attention weights:sub-optimal position encoding andnoisy input. To this end, in this paper, we aim to address this significant yet challenging gap in existing works. To be specific, we propose a simple yet effective framework called Attention Calibration for Transformer-based Sequential Recommendation (AC-TSR). In AC-TSR, a novel spatial calibrator and adversarial calibrator are designed respectively to directly calibrates those incorrectly assigned attention weights. The former is devised to explicitly capture the spatial relationships (i.e., order and distance) among items for more precise calculation of attention weights. The latter aims to redistribute the attention weights based on each item's contribution to the next-item prediction. AC-TSR is readily adaptable and can be seamlessly integrated into various existing transformer-based SR models. Extensive experimental results on four benchmark real-world datasets demonstrate the superiority of our proposed AC-TSR via significant recommendation performance enhancements. The source code is available at https://github.com/AIM-SE/AC-TSR."},"title":{"value":"Attention Calibration for Transformer-based Sequential Recommendation"},"authors":{"value":["Peilin Zhou","Qichen Ye","Yueqi Xie","Jingqi Gao","Shoujin Wang","Jae Boum Kim","Chenyu You","Sunghun Kim"]}},"tmdate":1768448047865,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731477405639,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Peilin_Zhou1"],"forum":"MRc9VfbkPC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":204186,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768448047865,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"MRc9VfbkPC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.01646v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"fang|esale_enhancing_codesummary_alignment_learning_for_source_code_summarization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chunrong_Fang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weisong_Sun:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuchen_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiao_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhao_Wei:","~Quanjun_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yudu_You:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bin_Luo_0003:","~Yang_Liu36","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhenyu_Chen_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.01646"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2407-01646,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Chunrong Fang and Weisong Sun and Yuchen Chen and Xiao Chen and Zhao Wei and Quanjun Zhang and Yudu You and Bin Luo and Yang Liu and Zhenyu Chen},\n  title={ESALE: Enhancing Code-Summary Alignment Learning for Source Code Summarization},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2407.01646},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2407.01646}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Source) code summarization aims to automatically generate succinct natural language summaries for given code snippets. Such summaries play a significant role in promoting developers to understand and maintain code. Inspired by neural machine translation, deep learning-based code summarization techniques widely adopt an encoder-decoder framework, where the encoder transforms given code snippets into context vectors, and the decoder decodes context vectors into summaries. Recently, large-scale pre-trained models for source code are equipped with encoders capable of producing general context vectors and have achieved substantial improvements on code summarization. However, although they are usually trained mainly on code-focused tasks and can capture general code features, they still fall short in capturing specific features that need to be summarized. This paper proposes a novel approach to improve code summarization based on summary-focused tasks. Specifically, we exploit a multi-task learning paradigm to train the encoder on three summary-focused tasks to enhance its ability to learn code-summary alignment, including unidirectional language modeling (ULM), masked language modeling (MLM), and action word prediction (AWP). Unlike pre-trained models that mainly predict masked tokens in code snippets, we design ULM and MLM to predict masked words in summaries. Intuitively, predicting words based on given code snippets would help learn the code-summary alignment. Additionally, we introduce the domain-specific task AWP to enhance the ability of the encoder to learn the alignment between action words and code snippets. The extensive experiments on four datasets demonstrate that our approach, called ESALE significantly outperforms baselines in all three widely used metrics, including BLEU, METEOR, and ROUGE-L."},"title":{"value":"ESALE: Enhancing Code-Summary Alignment Learning for Source Code Summarization"},"authors":{"value":["Chunrong Fang","Weisong Sun","Yuchen Chen","Xiao Chen","Zhao Wei","Quanjun Zhang","Yudu You","Bin Luo","Yang Liu","Zhenyu Chen"]}},"tmdate":1779712056170,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731474912391,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chunrong_Fang1"],"forum":"QRFLRREwvI","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":198672,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1779712056170,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"QRFLRREwvI","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"SupportCal calibrates post-trained LLMs without labels by preserving agreement examples and assigning continuous weights to disagreement examples using own-base support and cross-reference corroboration."},"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2026 Workshop STODY Poster"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f76c37a70f7697f8d28957bd96eefd875154bccd.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["large language models","confidence calibration","uncertainty quantification","label-free calibration","post-training"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/STODY"},"paperhash":{"value":"luo|supportcal_labelfree_calibration_of_posttrained_llms_via_reference_support_and_corroboration"},"abstract":{"value":"Post-training often improves task performance but can degrade confidence calibration, leaving post-trained language models (PoLMs) more overconfident than their corresponding pretrained language models (PLMs). Because task-specific labeled calibration data can be costly or unavailable, the corresponding PLM provides a natural label-free reference for post-hoc calibration. Prior agreement-gated PLM-referenced calibration fits a scalar temperature using only examples on which the PoLM and its PLM reference agree, excluding disagreement examples because direct alignment can drive the fitted temperature excessively high and induce under-confidence. We revisit this binary treatment. A controlled reintroduction diagnostic reveals a non-monotonic aggregate effect: admitting a moderate fraction of disagreement examples can improve calibration, whereas the benefit diminishes as unit-weight inclusion approaches the full disagreement set. We introduce SupportCal, a label-free post-hoc method that retains agreement examples at unit weight and assigns disagreement examples continuous weights based on the own-base PLM's relative support and corroboration from pretrained references selected from a size-compatible candidate pool. We further characterize when the resulting weighted objective admits a finite optimal temperature. Across MedMCQA and MathQA, SupportCal yields lower mean ECE than the agreement-only baseline for nearly all evaluated target-model configurations; supplementary TweetEval Sentiment results show the same pattern on a fixed-label classification task."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nluo2026supportcal,\ntitle={SupportCal: Label-Free Calibration of Post-Trained {LLM}s via Reference Support and Corroboration},\nauthor={Linhan Luo and Lequan Lin and Dai Shi and Feng Chen and Jos{\\'e} Miguel Hern{\\'a}ndez-Lobato and Junbin Gao},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2026 Workshop on AI for Stochastic Dynamics: From Theoretical Foundations to Scientific Applications},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=zjBjHulxD2}\n}"},"title":{"value":"SupportCal: Label-Free Calibration of Post-Trained LLMs via Reference Support and Corroboration"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Linhan_Luo1","fullname":"Linhan Luo","institutions":[{"name":"University of Sydney, University of Sydney","domain":"usyd.edu.au","country":"AU"}]},{"username":"~Lequan_Lin1","fullname":"Lequan Lin","institutions":[{"name":"University of Sydney","domain":"usyd.edu.au","country":"AU"}]},{"username":"~Dai_Shi1","fullname":"Dai Shi","institutions":[{"name":"University of Cambridge","domain":"cam.ac.uk","country":"GB"}]},{"username":"~Feng_Chen10","fullname":"Feng Chen","institutions":[{"name":"University of Adelaide","domain":"adelaide.edu.au","country":"AU"}]},{"username":"~José_Miguel_Hernández-Lobato1","fullname":"José Miguel Hernández-Lobato","institutions":[{"name":"University of Cambridge","domain":"cam.ac.uk","country":"GB"}]},{"username":"~Junbin_Gao1","fullname":"Junbin Gao","institutions":[{"name":"University of Sydney","domain":"sydney.edu.au","country":"AU"}]}]}},"tmdate":1791117319220,"pdate":1791117315179,"tcdate":1788662050583,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/STODY","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/STODY/Submission121/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/STODY/Submission121/Authors"],"forum":"zjBjHulxD2","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":121,"cdate":1788662050583,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/STODY/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/STODY/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/STODY/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/STODY/Submission121/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/STODY/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1791117319220,"odate":1791117315179,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/STODY","id":"zjBjHulxD2","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISBI 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10230311/10230322/10230515.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISBI/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"dawood|addressing_deep_learning_model_calibration_using_evidential_neural_networks_and_uncertaintyaware_training"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","~Andrew_P._King1",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ISBI53787.2023.10230515"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/isbi/DawoodCRKP23,\n  author={Tareen Dawood and Emily Chan and Reza Razavi and Andrew P. King and Esther Puyol-Antón},\n  title={Addressing Deep Learning Model Calibration Using Evidential Neural Networks and Uncertainty-Aware Training},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1-5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ISBI53787.2023.10230515},\n  booktitle={ISBI},\n  crossref={conf/isbi/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In terms of accuracy, deep learning (DL) models have had considerable success in classification problems for medical imaging applications. However, it is well-known that the outputs of such models, which typically utilise the SoftMax function in the final classification layer can be over-confident, i.e. they are poorly calibrated. Two competing solutions to this problem have been proposed: uncertainty-aware training and evidential neural networks (ENNs). In this paper we perform an investigation into the improvements to model calibration that can be achieved by each of these approaches individually, and their combination. We perform experiments on two classification tasks: a simpler MNIST digit classification task and a more complex and realistic medical imaging artefact detection task using Phase Contrast Cardiac Magnetic Resonance images. The experimental results demonstrate that model calibration can suffer when the task becomes challenging enough to require a higher capacity model. However, in our complex artefact detection task we saw an improvement in calibration for both a low and higher capacity model when implementing both the ENN and uncertainty-aware training together, indicating that this approach can offer a promising way to improve calibration in such settings. The findings highlight the potential use of these approaches to improve model calibration in a complex application, which would in turn improve clinician trust in DL models."},"title":{"value":"Addressing Deep Learning Model Calibration Using Evidential Neural Networks and Uncertainty-Aware Training"},"authors":{"value":["Tareen Dawood","Emily Chan","Reza Razavi","Andrew P. King","Esther Puyol-Antón"]}},"tmdate":1772172841040,"pdate":1703980800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/isbi/DawoodCRKP23"],"tcdate":1772172819451,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Andrew_P._King1"],"forum":"YXhwBUcPiR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":831684,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772172841040,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"YXhwBUcPiR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.08685v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"rieramarin|calibration_and_uncertainty_for_multirater_volume_assessment_in_multiorgan_segmentation_curvas_challenge_results"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Meritxell_Riera-Marin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:O._K._Sikha_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Júlia_Rodríguez-Comas:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Matthias_Stefan_May:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhaohong_Pan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiang_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaokun_Liang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Franciskus_Xaverius_Erick:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrea_Prenner:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cédric_Hémon:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Valentin_Boussot:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jean-Louis_Dillenseger:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jean-Claude_Nunes:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Abdul_Qayyum_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Moona_Mazher:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Steven_A._Niederer:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaisar_Kushibar:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Carlos_Martín-Isla:","~Petia_Radeva1","~Karim_Lekadir2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Theodore_Barfoot:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luis_C._García-Peraza-Herrera:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ben_Glocker:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tom_Vercauteren:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lucas_Gago:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Justin_Englemann:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Joy-Marie_Kleiss:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Anton_Aubanell:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andreu_Antolin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Javier_García_López:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Miguel_Ángel_González_Ballester:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Adrian_Galdran:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.08685"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-08685,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Meritxell Riera-Marin and O. K. Sikha and Júlia Rodríguez-Comas and Matthias Stefan May and Zhaohong Pan and Xiang Zhou and Xiaokun Liang and Franciskus Xaverius Erick and Andrea Prenner and Cédric Hémon and Valentin Boussot and Jean-Louis Dillenseger and Jean-Claude Nunes and Abdul Qayyum and Moona Mazher and Steven A. Niederer and Kaisar Kushibar and Carlos Martín-Isla and Petia Radeva and Karim Lekadir and Theodore Barfoot and Luis C. García-Peraza-Herrera and Ben Glocker and Tom Vercauteren and Lucas Gago and Justin Englemann and Joy-Marie Kleiss and Anton Aubanell and Andreu Antolin and Javier García López and Miguel Ángel González Ballester and Adrian Galdran},\n  title={Calibration and Uncertainty for multiRater Volume Assessment in multiorgan Segmentation (CURVAS) challenge results},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2505.08685},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.08685}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep learning (DL) has become the dominant approach for medical image segmentation, yet ensuring the reliability and clinical applicability of these models requires addressing key challenges such as annotation variability, calibration, and uncertainty estimation. This is why we created the Calibration and Uncertainty for multiRater Volume Assessment in multiorgan Segmentation (CURVAS), which highlights the critical role of multiple annotators in establishing a more comprehensive ground truth, emphasizing that segmentation is inherently subjective and that leveraging inter-annotator variability is essential for robust model evaluation. Seven teams participated in the challenge, submitting a variety of DL models evaluated using metrics such as Dice Similarity Coefficient (DSC), Expected Calibration Error (ECE), and Continuous Ranked Probability Score (CRPS). By incorporating consensus and dissensus ground truth, we assess how DL models handle uncertainty and whether their confidence estimates align with true segmentation performance. Our findings reinforce the importance of well-calibrated models, as better calibration is strongly correlated with the quality of the results. Furthermore, we demonstrate that segmentation models trained on diverse datasets and enriched with pre-trained knowledge exhibit greater robustness, particularly in cases deviating from standard anatomical structures. Notably, the best-performing models achieved high DSC and well-calibrated uncertainty estimates. This work underscores the need for multi-annotator ground truth, thorough calibration assessments, and uncertainty-aware evaluations to develop trustworthy and clinically reliable DL-based medical image segmentation models."},"title":{"value":"Calibration and Uncertainty for multiRater Volume Assessment in multiorgan Segmentation (CURVAS) challenge results"},"authors":{"value":["Meritxell Riera-Marin","O. K. Sikha","Júlia Rodríguez-Comas","Matthias Stefan May","Zhaohong Pan","Xiang Zhou","Xiaokun Liang","Franciskus Xaverius Erick","Andrea Prenner","Cédric Hémon","Valentin Boussot","Jean-Louis Dillenseger","Jean-Claude Nunes","Abdul Qayyum","Moona Mazher","Steven A. Niederer","Kaisar Kushibar","Carlos Martín-Isla","Petia Radeva","Karim Lekadir","Theodore Barfoot","Luis C. García-Peraza-Herrera","Ben Glocker","Tom Vercauteren","Lucas Gago","Justin Englemann","Joy-Marie Kleiss","Anton Aubanell","Andreu Antolin","Javier García López","Miguel Ángel González Ballester","Adrian Galdran"]}},"tmdate":1762725396770,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2505-08685"],"tcdate":1762537564335,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Karim_Lekadir2"],"forum":"MhAoWZN0Hx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":677706,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762725396770,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"MhAoWZN0Hx","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Our work enables parameter-efficient calibration of  simulatator trained inference model under simulator misspecification while preserving amortized inference."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Simulation-Based Inference","Simulator Misspecification","Robust Neural Posterior Estimation"]},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Simulator based inference provides a framework for inferring parameters of complex stochastic simulators in the absence of explicit likelihood. However, misspecification in the simulator often creates systemic discrepancies, which leads to poor posterior estimates for real data samples. Recent works have attempted to address this by explicitly handling the discrepancy using methods such as transport-based correction, or robust summary learning. However, this involves additional objectives or multi-stage procedures. We show that, surprisingly, such elaborate adaptation is often not required in many cases of practical interest.  We introduce PACE, a simple and deliberately minimal calibration strategy for simulator-pretrained neural posterior inference.Rather than defining explicit correction mechanisms, PACE leverages the representation and posterior structure learnt using abundant simulated data and calibrates the posterior using light weight feature wise transformations learnt using a small set of labeled real observations. These light-weight transformations are parameterized using a small set of calibration parameters and optimized directly for posterior inference. Despite its compact calibration space and simple strategy, PACE demonstrates effective outcome across diverse data modalities ranging from time-series to images.\nOn multiple evaluation metrics, PACE yields comparable or better results than SOTA approaches while requiring adaptation of a very small number of parameters. Our results suggest that when simulation-trained representations retain the information required for posterior inference, expensive re-learning or explicit alignment to the target distribution is unnecessary and a lightweight calibration of existing inference pathway suffices."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026pace,\ntitle={{PACE}: Parameter-Efficient Calibration for Robust Posterior Inference under Simulator Misspecification},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=4phoKXWSPV},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"PACE: Parameter-Efficient Calibration for Robust Posterior Inference under Simulator Misspecification"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/71e32922fc325e7715e8b6b92726cab7b505dd9c.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791235314131,"tcdate":1789807975538,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission58065/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission58065/Authors"],"forum":"4phoKXWSPV","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":58065,"cdate":1789807975538,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission58065/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing"],"mdate":1791235314131,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"4phoKXWSPV","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Crossref"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"qi|crosssilo_prototypical_calibration_for_federated_learning_with_noniid_data"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3581783.3612481"},"abstract":{"value":"Federated Learning aims to learn a global model on the server side that generalizes to all clients in a privacy-preserving manner, by leveraging the local models from different clients. Existing solutions focus on either regularizing the objective functions among clients or improving the aggregation mechanism for the improved model generalization capability. However, their performance is typically limited by the dataset biases, such as the heterogeneous data distributions and the missing classes. To address this issue, this paper presents a cross-silo prototypical calibration method (FedCSPC), which takes additional prototype information from the clients to learn a unified feature space on the server side. Specifically, FedCSPC first employs the Data Prototypical Modeling (DPM) module to learn data patterns via clustering to aid calibration. Subsequently, the cross-silo prototypical calibration (CSPC) module develops an augmented contrastive learning method to improve the robustness of the calibration, which can effectively project cross-source features into a consistent space while maintaining clear decision boundaries. Moreover, the CSPC module's ease of implementation and plug-and-play characteristics make it even more remarkable. Experiments were conducted on four datasets in terms of performance comparison, ablation study, in-depth analysis and case study, and the results verified that FedCSPC is capable of learning the consistent features across different data sources of the same class under the guidance of calibrated model, which leads to better performance than the state-of-the-art methods. The source codes have been released at https://github.com/qizhuang-qz/FedCSPC."},"title":{"value":"Cross-Silo Prototypical Calibration for Federated Learning with Non-IID Data"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhuang Qi"},{"fullname":"Lei Meng"},{"fullname":"Zitan Chen"},{"fullname":"Han Hu","username":"~Han_Hu6"},{"fullname":"Hui Lin"},{"fullname":"Xiangxu Meng"}]}},"tmdate":1789092440647,"pdate":1698278400000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1145/3581783.3612481"],"tcdate":1762525231966,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Han_Hu6"],"forum":"EeR29VX22d","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":10856,"cdate":1698411964773,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789092440647,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"EeR29VX22d","version":2},{"content":{"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/db2cd94239872ad037b11408655b65d398377117.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"qi|crosssilo_prototypical_calibration_for_federated_learning_with_noniid_data"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhuang_Qi2","~Lei_Meng1","~Zitan_Chen1","~Han_Hu6","~Hui_Lin7","~Xiangxu_Meng2"]},"html":{"value":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/3581783.3612481"},"abstract":{"value":"Federated Learning aims to learn a global model on the server side that generalizes to all clients in a privacy-preserving manner, by leveraging the local models from different clients. Existing solutions focus on either regularizing the objective functions among clients or improving the aggregation mechanism for the improved model generalization capability. However, their performance is typically limited by the dataset biases, such as the heterogeneous data distributions and the missing classes. To address this issue, this paper presents a cross-silo prototypical calibration method (FedCSPC), which takes additional prototype information from the clients to learn a unified feature space on the server side. Specifically, FedCSPC first employs the Data Prototypical Modeling (DPM) module to learn data patterns via clustering to aid calibration. Subsequently, the cross-silo prototypical calibration (CSPC) module develops an augmented contrastive learning method to improve the robustness of the calibration, which can effectively project cross-source features into a consistent space while maintaining clear decision boundaries. Moreover, the CSPC module's ease of implementation and plug-and-play characteristics make it even more remarkable. Experiments were conducted on four datasets in terms of performance comparison, ablation study, in-depth analysis and case study, and the results verified that FedCSPC is capable of learning the consistent features across different data sources of the same class under the guidance of calibrated model, which leads to better performance than the state-of-the-art methods. The source codes have been released at https://github.com/qizhuang-qz/FedCSPC."},"title":{"value":"Cross-Silo Prototypical Calibration for Federated Learning with Non-IID Data"},"authors":{"value":["Zhuang Qi","Lei Meng","Zitan Chen","Han Hu","Hui Lin","Xiangxu Meng"]}},"tmdate":1736502385643,"pdate":1698370691861,"tcdate":1723513096848,"writers":["~Zhuang_Qi2","~Lei_Meng1","~Zitan_Chen1","~Han_Hu6","~Hui_Lin7","~Xiangxu_Meng2"],"signatures":["~Zhuang_Qi2"],"forum":"0v7YjBNeHX","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","number":27554,"cdate":1723513096848,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload","OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Edit"],"mdate":1736502385643,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"0v7YjBNeHX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"COMPSTAT 2010"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-7908-2604-3_61.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/COMPSTAT/2010"},"paperhash":{"value":"verdonck|detmcd_in_a_calibration_framework"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tim_Verdonck1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mia_Hubert:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peter_J._Rousseeuw:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-7908-2604-3_61"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/compstat/VerdonckHR10,\n  author={Tim Verdonck and Mia Hubert and Peter J. Rousseeuw},\n  title={DetMCD in a Calibration Framework},\n  year={2010},\n  cdate={1262304000000},\n  pages={589-596},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-7908-2604-3_61},\n  booktitle={COMPSTAT},\n  crossref={conf/compstat/2010}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The minimum covariance determinant (MCD) method is a robust estimator of multivariate location and scatter (Rousseeuw (1984)). Computing the exact MCD is very hard, so in practice one resorts to approximate algorithms. Most often the FASTMCD algorithm of Rousseeuw and Van Driessen (1999) is used. The FASTMCD algorithm is affine equivariant but not permutation invariant. Recently a deterministic algorithm, denoted as DetMCD, is developed which does not use random subsets and which is much faster (Hubert et al. (2010)). In this paper DetMCD is illustrated in a calibration framework. We focus on robust principal component regression and partial least squares regression, two very popular regression techniques for collinear data. We also apply DetMCD on data with missing elements after plugging it into the M-RPCR technique of Serneels and Verdonck (2009)."},"title":{"value":"DetMCD in a Calibration Framework"},"authors":{"value":["Tim Verdonck","Mia Hubert","Peter J. Rousseeuw"]}},"tmdate":1747385848864,"pdate":1262304000000,"tcdate":1747385809212,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Tim_Verdonck1"],"forum":"DoViaQekTJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":509535,"cdate":1262304000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747385848864,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"DoViaQekTJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6034548/6094399/06094817.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"kümmerle|simultaneous_calibration_localization_and_mapping"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rainer_Kümmerle:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Giorgio_Grisetti:","~Wolfram_Burgard3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.2011.6094817"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/KummerleGB11,\n  author={Rainer Kümmerle and Giorgio Grisetti and Wolfram Burgard},\n  title={Simultaneous calibration, localization, and mapping},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={3716-3721},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.2011.6094817},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The calibration parameters of a mobile robot play a substantial role in navigation tasks. Often these parameters are subject to variations that depend either on environmental changes or on the wear of the devices. In this paper, we propose an approach to simultaneously estimate a map of the environment, the position of the on-board sensors of the robot, and its kinematic parameters. Our method requires no prior knowledge about the environment and relies only on a rough initial guess of the platform parameters. The proposed approach performs on-line estimation of the parameters and it is able to adapt to non-stationary changes of the configuration. We tested our approach in simulated environments and on a wide range of real world data using different types of robotic platforms."},"title":{"value":"Simultaneous calibration, localization, and mapping"},"authors":{"value":["Rainer Kümmerle","Giorgio Grisetti","Wolfram Burgard"]}},"tmdate":1746122181667,"pdate":1293840000000,"tcdate":1746122013688,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wolfram_Burgard3"],"forum":"wjUbrp1Q58","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":409122,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1746122181667,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"wjUbrp1Q58","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/10367905/10444561.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zheng|a_simultaneous_ultrasoundrobot_calibration_approach_for_dualrobot_intervention_by_solving_the_axp_ycq_problem"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guo_Zheng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xingwei_Zhao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Teru_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qing_Ling_0004:","~Bo_Tao3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Han_Ding_0002:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2024.3369147"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/ZhengZCLTD24,\n  author={Guo Zheng and Xingwei Zhao and Teru Chen and Qing Ling and Bo Tao and Han Ding},\n  title={A Simultaneous Ultrasound-Robot Calibration Approach for Dual-Robot Intervention by Solving the AXP = YCQ Problem},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={73},\n  pages={1-10},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2024.3369147}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Robotic ultrasound-guided intervention is an effective approach to ameliorating the quality of the intervention surgery. Ultrasound-robot calibration, conducted to obtain spatial relationships among different frames, is an essential step for the ultrasound-guided robotic system. However, existing step-by-step calibration methods mainly rely on phantoms or external trackers and are prone to error accumulation and workflow complexity. In this article, a novel simultaneous ultrasound-robot calibration approach is proposed. It calibrates the ultrasound-guided dual-robot intervention system in a one-step process without resorting to any phantoms or external trackers, thereby enhancing the calibration accuracy and simplifying the workflow. Specifically, an  ${\\text {AXP} = \\text {YCQ}}$  calibration equation is formulated to transform the calibration problem into the equation solving. For simultaneously deciding the equation’s unknowns, the Kronecker-product-based closed-form method and the Gauss–Newton iterative method based on the Lie algebra are proposed. The closed-form method offers a coarse estimation to the iterative method. Evaluation experiments are implemented to illustrate the favorable performance of the proposed approach. Furthermore, an in vitro ultrasound-guided dual-robot intervention experiment was also realized with the proposed approach and the intervention errors were 0.75 ± 0.27 mm when the calibration sample data size is 50."},"title":{"value":"A Simultaneous Ultrasound-Robot Calibration Approach for Dual-Robot Intervention by Solving the AXP = YCQ Problem"},"authors":{"value":["Guo Zheng","Xingwei Zhao","Teru Chen","Qing Ling","Bo Tao","Han Ding"]}},"tmdate":1747106838544,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747106811461,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bo_Tao3"],"forum":"y2JUI3tVz5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":433695,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747106838544,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"y2JUI3tVz5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Medical Imaging"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/42/10547147/10399826.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"penso|confidence_calibration_of_a_medical_imaging_classification_system_that_is_robust_to_label_noise"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TMI.2024.3353762"},"abstract":{"value":"A classification model is calibrated if its predicted probabilities of outcomes reflect their accuracy. Calibrating neural networks is critical in medical analysis applications where clinical decisions rely upon the predicted probabilities. Most calibration procedures, such as temperature scaling, operate as a post processing step by using holdout validation data. In practice, it is difficult to collect medical image data with correct labels due to the complexity of the medical data and the considerable variability across experts. This study presents a network calibration procedure that is robust to label noise. We draw on the fact that the confusion matrix of the noisy labels can be expressed as the matrix product between the confusion matrix of the clean labels and the label noises. The method is based on estimating the noise level as part of a noise-robust training method. The noise level is then used to estimate the network accuracy required by the calibration procedure. We show that despite the unreliable labels, we can still achieve calibration results that are on a par with the results of a calibration procedure using data with reliable labels."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration of a Medical Imaging Classification System That is Robust to Label Noise"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Coby Penso","username":"~Coby_Penso1"},{"fullname":"Lior Frenkel"},{"fullname":"Jacob Goldberger"}]}},"tmdate":1789093156590,"pdate":1717200000000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tmi.2024.3353762"],"tcdate":1772482501944,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Coby_Penso1"],"forum":"2pTdawoHm6","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":52408,"cdate":1722694135413,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789093156590,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"2pTdawoHm6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Medical Imaging 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/42/10547147/10399826.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TMI/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"penso|confidence_calibration_of_a_medical_imaging_classification_system_that_is_robust_to_label_noise"},"authorids":{"value":["~Coby_Penso1","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TMI.2024.3353762"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tmi/PensoFG24,\n  author={Coby Penso and Lior Frenkel and Jacob Goldberger},\n  title={Confidence Calibration of a Medical Imaging Classification System That is Robust to Label Noise},\n  year={2024},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1717200000000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Medical Imaging},\n  volume={43},\n  number={6},\n  pages={2050-2060},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TMI.2024.3353762}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"A classification model is calibrated if its predicted probabilities of outcomes reflect their accuracy. Calibrating neural networks is critical in medical analysis applications where clinical decisions rely upon the predicted probabilities. Most calibration procedures, such as temperature scaling, operate as a post processing step by using holdout validation data. In practice, it is difficult to collect medical image data with correct labels due to the complexity of the medical data and the considerable variability across experts. This study presents a network calibration procedure that is robust to label noise. We draw on the fact that the confusion matrix of the noisy labels can be expressed as the matrix product between the confusion matrix of the clean labels and the label noises. The method is based on estimating the noise level as part of a noise-robust training method. The noise level is then used to estimate the network accuracy required by the calibration procedure. We show that despite the unreliable labels, we can still achieve calibration results that are on a par with the results of a calibration procedure using data with reliable labels."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration of a Medical Imaging Classification System That is Robust to Label Noise"},"authors":{"value":["Coby Penso","Lior Frenkel","Jacob Goldberger"]}},"tmdate":1772483168075,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tmi/PensoFG24"],"tcdate":1772483165036,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Coby_Penso1"],"forum":"qHGZkoFoWg","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":846085,"cdate":1717200000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772483168075,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"qHGZkoFoWg","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISCAS 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10181241/10181318/10195888.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISCAS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"luo|alldigital_background_calibration_of_a_pipelinedsar_adc_using_the_split_adc_architecture"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhiqiang_Luo:","~Peng_Wang11","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jingpeng_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fule_Li:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ISCAS46773.2023.10195888"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iscas/LuoWZL23,\n  author={Zhiqiang Luo and Peng Wang and Jingpeng Zhou and Fule Li},\n  title={All-Digital Background Calibration of a Pipelined-SAR ADC Using the \"Split ADC\" Architecture},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1-5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ISCAS46773.2023.10195888},\n  booktitle={ISCAS},\n  crossref={conf/iscas/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"A split based all-digital background calibration for an 18-bit pipelined-successive approximation register (SAR) analog-to-digital converter (ADC) is proposed in this paper. The \"split ADC\" architecture is exploited to eliminate the signal interference and accelerate the convergence speed of the calibration. The output difference of two channels continuously drives the background calibration algorithm to estimate and correct the error caused by nonlinearities. To guarantee the validity of calibration with different inputs, shuffling scheme is adopted in capacitor array. Different dither signals are injected into two channels to achieve faster convergence, especially under the DC input. In the meanwhile, enough redundancy is introduced to capacitor array to avoid the decrease of input range caused by dither signal. A 4-bit coarse ADC is exploited to alleviate conversion time and provide dither injection. A behavior level model with various nonideal factors is established in MATLAB to prove the availability of calibration for an 18-bit pipelined-SAR ADC. The simulation shows that the ENOB achieves 16.5-bit, the SNDR is increased from 52.4 dB to 100.9 dB and the SFDR is improved from 72.3 dB to 119.6 dB."},"title":{"value":"All-Digital Background Calibration of a Pipelined-SAR ADC Using the \"Split ADC\" Architecture"},"authors":{"value":["Zhiqiang Luo","Peng Wang","Jingpeng Zhou","Fule Li"]}},"tmdate":1759733441939,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iscas/LuoWZL23"],"tcdate":1759733421565,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Peng_Wang11"],"forum":"ZDHOMv9pB3","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":636751,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1759733441939,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ZDHOMv9pB3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/7083369/10683798/10705060.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|efcalib_spatiotemporal_calibration_of_event_and_framebased_cameras_using_continuoustime_trajectories"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shaoan_Wang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhanhua_Xin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yaoqing_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongyue_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mingzhu_Zhu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junzhi_Yu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3474475"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/WangXHLZY24,\n  author={Shaoan Wang and Zhanhua Xin and Yaoqing Hu and Dongyue Li and Mingzhu Zhu and Junzhi Yu},\n  title={EF-Calib: Spatiotemporal Calibration of Event- and Frame-Based Cameras Using Continuous-Time Trajectories},\n  year={2024},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1730419200000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={9},\n  number={11},\n  pages={10280-10287},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3474475}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The event camera, a bio-inspired asynchronous triggered camera, offers promising prospects for fusion with frame-based cameras owing to its low latency and high dynamic range. However, calibrating stereo vision systems that incorporate both event- and frame-based cameras remains a significant challenge. In this letter, we present EF-Calib, a spatiotemporal calibration framework for event- and frame-based cameras using continuous-time trajectories. A novel calibration pattern applicable to both camera types and the corresponding event recognition algorithm are proposed. Leveraging the asynchronous nature of events, a derivable piece-wise B-spline to represent camera pose continuously is introduced, enabling calibration for intrinsic parameters, extrinsic parameters, and time offset, with analytical Jacobians provided. Various experiments are carried out to evaluate the calibration performance of EF-Calib, including calibration experiments for intrinsic parameters, extrinsic parameters, and time offset. Experimental results demonstrate that EF-Calib outperforms current methods by achieving the most accurate intrinsic parameters, comparable accuracy in extrinsic parameters to frame-based method, and precise time offset estimation. EF-Calib provides a convenient and accurate toolbox for calibrating the system that fuses events and frames."},"title":{"value":"EF-Calib: Spatiotemporal Calibration of Event- and Frame-Based Cameras Using Continuous-Time Trajectories"},"authors":{"value":["Shaoan Wang","Zhanhua Xin","Yaoqing Hu","Dongyue Li","Mingzhu Zhu","Junzhi Yu"]}},"tmdate":1731032657244,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731032651714,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shaoan_Wang1"],"forum":"wfpVoYtpzl","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":174491,"cdate":1730419200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731032657244,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"wfpVoYtpzl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AIM 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7999201/8013982/08014014.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AIMECH/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"yamao|calibration_of_a_rotating_2d_lrf_in_unprepared_environments_by_minimizing_redundant_measurement_errors"},"authorids":{"value":["~Sosuke_Yamao2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hiroshi_Hidaka:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shigeyuki_Odashima:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shan_Jiang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuichi_Murase:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/AIM.2017.8014014"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aimech/YamaoHOJM17,\n  author={Sosuke Yamao and Hiroshi Hidaka and Shigeyuki Odashima and Shan Jiang and Yuichi Murase},\n  title={Calibration of a rotating 2D LRF in unprepared environments by minimizing redundant measurement errors},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  pages={172-177},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/AIM.2017.8014014},\n  booktitle={AIM},\n  crossref={conf/aimech/2017}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes a calibration method for a 3D laser scanning system built with a rotating 2D laser rangefinder (LRF) in unprepared environments. Although the combination of a 2D LRF with a rotating unit yields a low-cost and compact 3D scanner, construction misalignments must be calibrated to reduce measurement error. Existing calibration techniques are generally used either with a special target object or in prepared environments with flat surfaces, and this imposes a limitation on the applicable domain of the system. To solve this problem, we propose a novel calibration method using redundant measurements of 3D point clouds. The main idea of the proposed method is to focus on the fact that rotating LRF systems observe the same object twice during a single rotation at different times. Therefore, by minimizing the extent of misalignment of these redundant measurements, we can estimate the calibration parameters without using any special target object. We formulate this calibration by redundant measurements as a non-linear cost minimization problem. The results of the simulation and real-world experiments in challenging, real environments show that the proposed method works well in unprepared and even cluttered environments."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of a rotating 2D LRF in unprepared environments by minimizing redundant measurement errors"},"authors":{"value":["Sosuke Yamao","Hiroshi Hidaka","Shigeyuki Odashima","Shan Jiang","Yuichi Murase"]}},"tmdate":1730812144369,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1730812136065,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sosuke_Yamao2"],"forum":"fhbS0alEh1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":170824,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730812144369,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"fhbS0alEh1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.07781v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"guo|redcode_risky_code_execution_and_generation_benchmark_for_code_agents"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chengquan_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xun_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chulin_Xie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andy_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Zeng_0005:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zinan_Lin_0001:","~Dawn_Song1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bo_Li_0026:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2411.07781"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2411-07781,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Chengquan Guo and Xun Liu and Chulin Xie and Andy Zhou and Yi Zeng and Zinan Lin and Dawn Song and Bo Li},\n  title={RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2411.07781},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2411.07781}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With the rapidly increasing capabilities and adoption of code agents for AI-assisted coding, safety concerns, such as generating or executing risky code, have become significant barriers to the real-world deployment of these agents. To provide comprehensive and practical evaluations on the safety of code agents, we propose RedCode, a benchmark for risky code execution and generation: (1) RedCode-Exec provides challenging prompts that could lead to risky code execution, aiming to evaluate code agents' ability to recognize and handle unsafe code. We provide a total of 4,050 risky test cases in Python and Bash tasks with diverse input formats including code snippets and natural text. They covers 25 types of critical vulnerabilities spanning 8 domains (e.g., websites, file systems). We provide Docker environments and design corresponding evaluation metrics to assess their execution results. (2) RedCode-Gen provides 160 prompts with function signatures and docstrings as input to assess whether code agents will follow instructions to generate harmful code or software. Our empirical findings, derived from evaluating three agent frameworks based on 19 LLMs, provide insights into code agents' vulnerabilities. For instance, evaluations on RedCode-Exec show that agents are more likely to reject executing risky operations on the operating system, but are less likely to reject executing technically buggy code, indicating high risks. Risky operations described in natural text lead to a lower rejection rate than those in code format. Additionally, evaluations on RedCode-Gen show that more capable base models and agents with stronger overall coding abilities, such as GPT4, tend to produce more sophisticated and effective harmful software. Our findings highlight the need for stringent safety evaluations for diverse code agents. Our dataset and code are available at https://github.com/AI-secure/RedCode."},"title":{"value":"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents"},"authors":{"value":["Chengquan Guo","Xun Liu","Chulin Xie","Andy Zhou","Yi Zeng","Zinan Lin","Dawn Song","Bo Li"]}},"tmdate":1738280971453,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1738280926584,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dawn_Song1"],"forum":"biYdEj1y6p","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":288131,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738280971453,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"biYdEj1y6p","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IJCAI 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2021/0512.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IJCAI/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"yan|towards_generating_summaries_for_lexically_confusing_code_through_code_erosion"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fan_Yan:","~Ming_Li1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.24963/ijcai.2021/512"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ijcai/YanL21,\n  author={Fan Yan and Ming Li},\n  title={Towards Generating Summaries for Lexically Confusing Code through Code Erosion},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={3721-3727},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.24963/ijcai.2021/512},\n  booktitle={IJCAI},\n  crossref={conf/ijcai/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code summarization aims to summarize code functionality as high-level nature language descriptions to assist in code comprehension. Recent approaches in this field mainly focus on generating summaries for code with precise identifier names, in which meaningful words can be found indicating code functionality. When faced with lexically confusing code, current approaches are likely to fail since the correlation between code lexical tokens and summaries is scarce. To tackle this problem, we propose a novel summarization framework named VECOS. VECOS introduces an erosion mechanism to conquer the model's reliance on precisely defined lexical information. To facilitate learning the eroded code's functionality, we force the representation of the eroded code to align with the representation of its original counterpart via variational inference. Experimental results show that our approach outperforms the state-of-the-art approaches to generate coherent and reliable summaries for various lexically confusing code."},"title":{"value":"Towards Generating Summaries for Lexically Confusing Code through Code Erosion"},"authors":{"value":["Fan Yan","Ming Li"]}},"tmdate":1727720456022,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1727720453675,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ming_Li1"],"forum":"1wCxNnhRYW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":122710,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727720456022,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"1wCxNnhRYW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2110.00272v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"ma|learn_to_communicate_with_neural_calibration_scalability_and_generalization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yifan_Ma:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yifei_Shen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xianghao_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Zhang_0004:","~Shenghui_Song1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Khaled_B._Letaief:"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.00272"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2110-00272,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yifan Ma and Yifei Shen and Xianghao Yu and Jun Zhang and Shenghui Song and Khaled B. Letaief},\n  title={Learn to Communicate with Neural Calibration: Scalability and Generalization},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2110.00272},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.00272}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The conventional design of wireless communication systems typically relies on established mathematical models that capture the characteristics of different communication modules. Unfortunately, such design cannot be easily and directly applied to future wireless networks, which will be characterized by large-scale ultra-dense networks whose design complexity scales exponentially with the network size. Furthermore, such networks will vary dynamically in a significant way, which makes it intractable to develop comprehensive analytical models. Recently, deep learning-based approaches have emerged as potential alternatives for designing complex and dynamic wireless systems. However, existing learning-based methods have limited capabilities to scale with the problem size and to generalize with varying network settings. In this paper, we propose a scalable and generalizable neural calibration framework for future wireless system design, where a neural network is adopted to calibrate the input of conventional model-based algorithms. Specifically, the backbone of a traditional time-efficient algorithm is integrated with deep neural networks to achieve a high computational efficiency, while enjoying enhanced performance. The permutation equivariance property, carried out by the topological structure of wireless systems, is furthermore utilized to develop a generalizable neural network architecture. The proposed neural calibration framework is applied to solve challenging resource management problems in massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) systems. Simulation results will show that the proposed neural calibration approach enjoys significantly improved scalability and generalization compared with the existing learning-based methods."},"title":{"value":"Learn to Communicate with Neural Calibration: Scalability and Generalization"},"authors":{"value":["Yifan Ma","Yifei Shen","Xianghao Yu","Jun Zhang","Shenghui Song","Khaled B. Letaief"]}},"tmdate":1747285433774,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1747285336760,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shenghui_Song1"],"forum":"qKh1x2AJUR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":472739,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747285433774,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"qKh1x2AJUR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Wirel. Commun. 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7693/9944944/09796036.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TWC/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"ma|learn_to_communicate_with_neural_calibration_scalability_and_generalization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yifan_Ma:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yifei_Shen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xianghao_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Zhang_0004:","~Shenghui_Song1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Khaled_B._Letaief:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TWC.2022.3181219"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/twc/MaSYZSL22,\n  author={Yifan Ma and Yifei Shen and Xianghao Yu and Jun Zhang and Shenghui Song and Khaled B. Letaief},\n  title={Learn to Communicate With Neural Calibration: Scalability and Generalization},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Wirel. Commun.},\n  volume={21},\n  number={11},\n  pages={9947-9961},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TWC.2022.3181219}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The conventional design of wireless communication systems typically relies on established mathematical models that capture the characteristics of different communication modules. Unfortunately, such design cannot be easily and directly applied to future wireless networks, which will be characterized by large-scale ultra-dense networks whose design complexity scales exponentially with the network size. Furthermore, such networks will vary dynamically in a significant way, which makes it intractable to develop comprehensive analytical models. Recently, deep learning-based approaches have emerged as potential alternatives for designing complex and dynamic wireless systems. However, existing learning-based methods have limited capabilities to scale with the problem size and to generalize with varying network settings. In this paper, we propose a scalable and generalizable neural calibration framework for future wireless system design, where a neural network is adopted to calibrate the input of conventional model-based algorithms. Specifically, the backbone of a traditional time-efficient algorithm is integrated with deep neural networks to achieve a high computational efficiency, while enjoying enhanced performance. The permutation equivariance property, carried out by the topological structure of wireless systems, is furthermore utilized to develop a generalizable neural network architecture. The proposed neural calibration framework is applied to solve challenging resource management problems in massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) systems. Simulation results will show that the proposed neural calibration approach enjoys significantly improved scalability and generalization compared with the existing learning-based methods."},"title":{"value":"Learn to Communicate With Neural Calibration: Scalability and Generalization"},"authors":{"value":["Yifan Ma","Yifei Shen","Xianghao Yu","Jun Zhang","Shenghui Song","Khaled B. Letaief"]}},"tmdate":1747285402892,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1747285336181,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shenghui_Song1"],"forum":"PhGTOM27oE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":472716,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747285402892,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"PhGTOM27oE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Eng. Appl. Artif. Intell. 2014"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/EAAI/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"parkinson|automated_planning_to_minimise_uncertainty_of_machine_tool_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Simon_Parkinson1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrew_Longstaff:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Simon_Fletcher:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.engappai.2014.02.002"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/eaai/ParkinsonLF14,\n  author={Simon Parkinson and Andrew Longstaff and Simon Fletcher},\n  title={Automated planning to minimise uncertainty of machine tool calibration},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  journal={Eng. Appl. Artif. Intell.},\n  volume={30},\n  pages={63-72},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.engappai.2014.02.002}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"When calibrating a machine tool, multiple measurement tasks will be performed, each of which has an associated uncertainty of measurement. International Standards and best-practice guides are available to aid with estimating uncertainty of measurement for individual tasks, but there is little consideration for the temporal influence on the uncertainty when considering interrelated measurements. Additionally, there is an absence of any intelligent method capable of optimising (reducing) the estimated uncertainty of the calibration plan as a whole. In this work, the uncertainty of measurement reduction problem is described and modelled in a suitable language to allow state-of-the-art artificial intelligence planning tools to produce optimal calibration plans. The paper describes how the continuous, non-linear temperature aspects are discretized and modelled to make them easier for the planner to solve. In addition, detail is provided as how the complex uncertainty equations are modelled in a restrictive language where its syntax heavily influences the encoding. An example is shown for a three-axis machine, where the produced plan exhibits intelligent behaviour in terms of scheduling measurements against temperature deviation and the propagation of error uncertainties. In this example, a reduction of 58% in the estimated uncertainty of measurement due to intelligently scheduling a calibration plan is observed. This reduction in the estimated uncertainty of measurement will result in an increased conformance zone, thus reducing false acceptance and rejection of work-pieces."},"title":{"value":"Automated planning to minimise uncertainty of machine tool calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Simon Parkinson","Andrew Longstaff","Simon Fletcher"]}},"tmdate":1730454363729,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1730454324897,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Simon_Parkinson1"],"forum":"uinSlqrFyz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":167096,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730454363729,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"uinSlqrFyz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Remote. Sens. 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/11/13/1519/pdf?version=1561627788"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/REMOTESENSING/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"medic|sensitivity_analysis_and_minimal_measurement_geometry_for_the_targetbased_calibration_of_highend_panoramic_terrestrial_laser_scanners"},"authorids":{"value":["","","~Christoph_Holst1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/rs11131519"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/remotesensing/MedicKH19,\n  author={Tomislav Medic and Heiner Kuhlmann and Christoph Holst},\n  title={Sensitivity Analysis and Minimal Measurement Geometry for the Target-Based Calibration of High-End Panoramic Terrestrial Laser Scanners},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={11},\n  number={13},\n  pages={1519},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/rs11131519}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Target-based calibration is the most widely used calibration procedure for terrestrial laser scanners. Nevertheless, the current implementations of this procedure are suboptimal due to the high complexity of their calibration fields. Therefore, designing calibration fields, by selecting optimal target locations with respect to the instrument, is a pending research question. Answering this question would lead to a more efficient and user-friendly calibration, as well as improved calibration results. Herein, we conduct a detailed analysis of the most sensitive target-to-instrument measurement configuration to estimate individual calibration parameters. Based on this analysis, we derive a minimal measurement geometry, sensitive to all mechanical misalignments, relevant for high-end panoramic terrestrial laser scanners. We support our sensitivity analysis by a simulation and empirical experiments followed by a discussion about the advantages and shortcomings of the proposed minimal measurement geometry."},"title":{"value":"Sensitivity Analysis and Minimal Measurement Geometry for the Target-Based Calibration of High-End Panoramic Terrestrial Laser Scanners"},"authors":{"value":["Tomislav Medic","Heiner Kuhlmann","Christoph Holst"]}},"tmdate":1772285243325,"pdate":1577750400000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/remotesensing/MedicKH19"],"tcdate":1772285232441,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Christoph_Holst1"],"forum":"nNPPWsr2q4","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":839169,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772285243325,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"nNPPWsr2q4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote. Sens. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/36/10006360/10034812.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TGRS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|bridging_the_gap_between_cumbersome_and_light_detectors_via_layercalibration_and_taskdisentangle_distillation_in_remote_sensing_imagery"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yidan_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhiyuan_Yan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xian_Sun:","~Xiaonan_Lu2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junxi_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yongqiang_Mao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Wang_0077:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2023.3241342"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tgrs/ZhangYSLLMW23,\n  author={Yidan Zhang and Zhiyuan Yan and Xian Sun and Xiaonan Lu and Junxi Li and Yongqiang Mao and Lei Wang},\n  title={Bridging the Gap Between Cumbersome and Light Detectors via Layer-Calibration and Task-Disentangle Distillation in Remote Sensing Imagery},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={61},\n  pages={1-18},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2023.3241342}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With urgent application requirements, such as satellite in-orbit processing and unmanned aerial vehicle tracking, knowledge distillation (KD) following the teacher–student teaching mechanism has shown great potential to obtain lightweight detectors. However, compact students have limited accuracy due to the interference of large-scale variations and blurred boundaries in remote sensing objects. Specifically, previous methods mostly force teacher–student responses from the layer of the same depth and scale to align. Stereotyped manual interlayer associations may cause discriminative features of multiscale objects to be incorrectly bundled. Furthermore, the regression branch follows the identical distillation paradigm as the classification branch, resulting in ambiguous object bounding box deviations. To solve the above two issues, we propose an effective KD framework called layer-calibration and task-disentangle distillation (LTD). First, the cross-layer calibration distillation (CCD) structure is innovatively proposed. It adaptively binds a student layer with several related target layers, rather than a fixed layer in the teacher model. Appropriate and clear knowledge of large and small objects is transmitted. Since the CCD structure requires explicit global inner product computation between multiple layers, the local implicit calibration (LIC) module is further proposed to reduce distilled convergence difficulty. Second, the task-aware spatial disentangle distillation (TASD) structure is devised to transfer task-decoupled semantics and localization knowledge in a divide-and-conquer manner, alleviating objects’ localization imprecision. Experiments demonstrate that our LTD achieves state-of-the-art performance on several datasets and is a plug-and-play approach to most detectors. The code will be available soon."},"title":{"value":"Bridging the Gap Between Cumbersome and Light Detectors via Layer-Calibration and Task-Disentangle Distillation in Remote Sensing Imagery"},"authors":{"value":["Yidan Zhang","Zhiyuan Yan","Xian Sun","Xiaonan Lu","Junxi Li","Yongqiang Mao","Lei Wang"]}},"tmdate":1754013691399,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731639563219,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiaonan_Lu2"],"forum":"A3vqXIdgkp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":246149,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1754013691399,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"A3vqXIdgkp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/6287639/10820123/10971343.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ACCESS/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"batalo|model_selection_methods_for_modelbridge_simulation_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Bojan_Batalo1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lincon_S._Souza:","~Keisuke_Yamazaki1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3562627"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/BataloSY25,\n  author={Bojan Batalo and Lincon S. Souza and Keisuke Yamazaki},\n  title={Model Selection Methods for Model-Bridge Simulation Calibration},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={13},\n  pages={70298-70312},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3562627}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Computer simulations are ubiquitous in many scientific communities analyzing complex phenomena, such as physics, material science, medicine, and others. For simulations to yield credible insight, they must accurately represent key aspects of their real-world counterparts, making the calibration of simulation parameters crucial. Often, manual calibration is time-consuming, error-prone, and dependent on expert knowledge. Therefore, many algorithmic approaches have been explored, from heuristic-based and Bayesian methods to search and genetic algorithms. Such attempts often obtain good parameters, though at a high computational cost, as they require running the simulation many times to explore the parameter space. In contrast, the recently proposed model-bridge framework significantly speeds up this process by machine learning on a set of previous observations. In model-bridge framework, a complex simulation is represented by a simpler, uninterpretable surrogate; then, using past simulation data, a bridge model is trained to map predictions of the uninterpretable surrogate model to calibrated, interpretable simulation parameters. However, this approach introduces another problem: designing surrogate models and choosing their parameters. In this paper, we evaluate cross-validation and information theory-based model selection strategies for choosing the optimal surrogate models. Through experiments on synthetic signal and fluid dynamics simulations based on the finite element method, we show that model selection and the choice of the surrogate are essential to enabling high model-bridge performance, comparable to and surpassing established calibration approaches, at a fraction of the computational cost and time. Further, our experiments show that information theory-based methods such as Akaike information criterion (AIC) can obtain close to optimal models several orders of magnitude faster than cross-validation strategies. Finally, we discuss theoretical requirements which a surrogate model should satisfy to allow the use of information theory-based methods."},"title":{"value":"Model Selection Methods for Model-Bridge Simulation Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Bojan Batalo","Lincon S. Souza","Keisuke Yamazaki"]}},"tmdate":1751509651673,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1749686242919,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Keisuke_Yamazaki1"],"forum":"aUYI3ZGmVZ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":559514,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1751509651673,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"aUYI3ZGmVZ","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Post-training quantization stores statistics of its calibration data in the released checkpoint, so attackers can tell which records were used; stock exporters expose about 68% of calibration records at very low false-positive rates."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["membership inference","post-training quantization","calibration data","privacy","SmoothQuant","model compression","extreme value theory","probability calibration"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Before deploying a model at lower numerical precision, developers often run a small set of example inputs through it to choose the numerical scales used for quantization. These inputs, or records, form the calibration set. We show that someone with access to the saved model can infer whether a particular input was used for calibration. In methods based on SmoothQuant, comparison with the original public model recovers statistics of the model's internal responses, called activations. When calibration uses a maximum, the checkpoint can preserve an individual input's activation exactly. A match can reveal whether that input was used for calibration. We characterize when exact identification is possible and how many stored maxima each record sets. Using only an OPT-125M checkpoint produced by unmodified NVIDIA Model Optimizer, we identify 40.4% of calibration records at a measured false-positive rate of 0.0038%. In a controlled comparison on OPT-125M, averaging the two largest activations in place of the maximum reduces detection from 59% to 15% at zero observed false positives, while attack AUC changes from 0.97 to 0.95. The same mechanism appears in isotonic probability calibration, where retained classifier scores identify 34% of calibration records on 20 Newsgroups with zero observed false positives. Calibration statistics retained in released models are a distinct source of membership leakage."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026the,\ntitle={The Calibration Set Is in the Checkpoint: How Release Geometry Governs Membership Leakage in Post-Training Quantization},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=lL0RG6hq8D},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"The Calibration Set Is in the Checkpoint: How Release Geometry Governs Membership Leakage in Post-Training Quantization"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/5b85c3e1b24a7d6ef01114b12024b944b33826bc.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794284917503,"tcdate":1789776668283,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission50697/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission50697/Authors"],"forum":"lL0RG6hq8D","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":50697,"cdate":1789776668283,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission50697/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission50697/-/Rebuttal_Revision"],"mdate":1794284917503,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"lL0RG6hq8D","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SANER 2015"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7066219/7081802/07081824.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/WCRE/2015"},"paperhash":{"value":"thongtanunam|who_should_review_my_code_a_file_locationbased_codereviewer_recommendation_approach_for_modern_code_review"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Patanamon_Thongtanunam:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chakkrit_Tantithamthavorn:","~Raula_Gaikovina_Kula1","~Norihiro_Yoshida1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hajimu_Iida:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ken-ichi_Matsumoto:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/SANER.2015.7081824"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/wcre/ThongtanunamTKY15,\n  author={Patanamon Thongtanunam and Chakkrit Tantithamthavorn and Raula Gaikovina Kula and Norihiro Yoshida and Hajimu Iida and Ken-ichi Matsumoto},\n  title={Who should review my code? A file location-based code-reviewer recommendation approach for Modern Code Review},\n  year={2015},\n  cdate={1420070400000},\n  pages={141-150},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/SANER.2015.7081824},\n  booktitle={SANER},\n  crossref={conf/wcre/2015}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Software code review is an inspection of a code change by an independent third-party developer in order to identify and fix defects before an integration. Effectively performing code review can improve the overall software quality. In recent years, Modern Code Review (MCR), a lightweight and tool-based code inspection, has been widely adopted in both proprietary and open-source software systems. Finding appropriate code-reviewers in MCR is a necessary step of reviewing a code change. However, little research is known the difficulty of finding code-reviewers in a distributed software development and its impact on reviewing time. In this paper, we investigate the impact of reviews with code-reviewer assignment problem has on reviewing time. We find that reviews with code-reviewer assignment problem take 12 days longer to approve a code change. To help developers find appropriate code-reviewers, we propose RevFinder, a file location-based code-reviewer recommendation approach. We leverage a similarity of previously reviewed file path to recommend an appropriate code-reviewer. The intuition is that files that are located in similar file paths would be managed and reviewed by similar experienced code-reviewers. Through an empirical evaluation on a case study of 42,045 reviews of Android Open Source Project (AOSP), OpenStack, Qt and LibreOffice projects, we find that RevFinder accurately recommended 79% of reviews with a top 10 recommendation. RevFinder also correctly recommended the code-reviewers with a median rank of 4. The overall ranking of RevFinder is 3 times better than that of a baseline approach. We believe that RevFinder could be applied to MCR in order to help developers find appropriate code-reviewers and speed up the overall code review process."},"title":{"value":"Who should review my code? A file location-based code-reviewer recommendation approach for Modern Code Review"},"authors":{"value":["Patanamon Thongtanunam","Chakkrit Tantithamthavorn","Raula Gaikovina Kula","Norihiro Yoshida","Hajimu Iida","Ken-ichi Matsumoto"]}},"tmdate":1754372823979,"pdate":1420070400000,"tcdate":1753024462609,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Norihiro_Yoshida1"],"forum":"ZubWRE5XNM","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":577462,"cdate":1420070400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1754372823979,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ZubWRE5XNM","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Software Eng. 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/32/11346545/11250608.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|synthetic_malware_at_scale_malicious_code_generation_with_code_transplanting"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2025.3633280"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tse/WangCGCS26,\n  author={Guangzhan Wang and Diwei Chen and Xiaodong Gu and Yuting Chen and Beijun Shen},\n  title={Synthetic Malware at Scale: Malicious Code Generation With Code Transplanting},\n  year={2026},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Software Eng.},\n  volume={52},\n  number={1},\n  pages={171-186},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2025.3633280}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Malicious code detection is one of the most essential tasks in safeguarding against security breaches, data compromise, and related threats. While machine learning has emerged as a predominant method for pattern detection, the training process is intricate due to the severe scarcity of malicious code samples. Consequently, machine learning detectors often encounter malicious patterns in limited and isolated scenarios, hindering their ability to generalize effectively across diverse threat landscapes. In this paper, we introduce MalCoder, a novel method for synthesizing malicious code samples. MalCoder enlarges the quantity and diversity of malicious instances by transplanting a set of malicious prototypes into a vast pool of benign code, thereby crafting a diverse array of malicious instances tailored to various application scenarios. For each malware prototype, MalCoder treats it as an incomplete code fragment and crafts its preceding and subsequent contexts through right-to-left and left-to-right code completion respectively. By leveraging GPTs with various sampling strategies, we can instantiate a large number of code samples bearing the malware prototype. Subsequently, MalCoder masks the original prototypes within the transplanted samples and fine-tunes an LLM code generator to reconstruct the original prototype. This process enables the model to seamlessly transplant malicious code fragments into benign code. During inference, MalCoder can automatically insert malicious fragments into benign samples at random positions, transforming benign code into malicious code. We apply MalCoder to a large pool of benign code in CodeSearchNet and craft over 50,000 malicious samples stemming from 39 malicious prototypes. Both qualitative and quantitative analyses show that the generated samples maintain key characteristics of malicious code while blending seamlessly with benign code, which helps in creating realistic and varied training data. Additionally, by using the generated samples as augmented training data, we witness a remarkable surge in malicious code detection capabilities. Specifically, the F1-score experiences a significant increase compared to utilizing only the original prototype samples."},"title":{"value":"Synthetic Malware at Scale: Malicious Code Generation With Code Transplanting"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Guangzhan Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Diwei Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Xiaodong Gu","username":""},{"fullname":"Yuting Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Beijun Shen","username":"~Beijun_Shen2"}]}},"tmdate":1780122307948,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tse/WangCGCS26"],"tcdate":1780122300611,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Beijun_Shen2"],"forum":"EKCnBTR6Fh","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":38961,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1780122307948,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"EKCnBTR6Fh","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"A semi-supervised code translation method that leverages parallel code generation with enhanced cross-lingual alignment."},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2024"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Code Translation; Machine Translation; Code Generation; Semi-Supervised Learning"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/a2e16e87e879ebd649cb354405636ac58598081e.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"unsupervised, self-supervised, semi-supervised, and supervised representation learning"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Code translation is the task of converting source code from one programming language to another. Sufficient parallel code data is essential for neural code translation models to learn the correct alignment across different languages. However, existing parallel code data is limited in quantity and supported languages. In this paper, we propose a semi-supervised code translation method, SPACoder, that leverages snippet training, static analysis, and compilation to generate synthetic parallel code with enhanced alignment in a scalable way, and improves code translation by curriculum learning based on the alignment level of training instances. SPACoder can be generalized to multiple languages and various models with little overhead. Extensive experiments show that SPACoder significantly improves code translation performance on C++, Java, Python, and C, outperforming state-of-the-art baselines by wide margins in execution-based evaluation (CA@1). Notably, we improve C translation by up to 43% with less than 150 annotated training instances."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nzhu2024alignmentenhancing,\ntitle={Alignment-Enhancing Parallel Code Generation for Semi-Supervised Code Translation},\nauthor={Ming Zhu and Ismini Lourentzou and Danfeng Yao},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=XK7kyCVjqr}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Alignment-Enhancing Parallel Code Generation for Semi-Supervised Code Translation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/b651987a56f4ae5429e456aa81da92952081bb63.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhu|alignmentenhancing_parallel_code_generation_for_semisupervised_code_translation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ming_Zhu1","~Ismini_Lourentzou1","~Danfeng_Yao2"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Ming Zhu","Ismini Lourentzou","Danfeng Yao"]}},"tmdate":1707625734988,"tcdate":1695415577700,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission6285/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission6285/Authors"],"forum":"XK7kyCVjqr","number":6285,"cdate":1695415577700,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission6285/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission6285/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1707625734988,"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"XK7kyCVjqr","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.05125v2"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"plaud|singlequery_blackbox_calibration_auditing_via_logit_bias"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2609.05125"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2609-05125,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Roman Plaud and Antoine Saillenfest and Matthieu Labeau and Thomas Bonald and Willem Waegeman},\n  title={Single-Query Black-Box Calibration Auditing via Logit Bias},\n  year={2026},\n  month={September},\n  cdate={1788220800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2609.05125},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2609.05125}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Standard calibration evaluation and recalibration methods assume access to predicted probabilities. We study one-bit threshold access, a setting in which probabilities are hidden and the evaluator observes only whether the predicted probability exceeds a single chosen threshold per sample. Under this restriction, we show that classifiers can nevertheless be evaluated and recalibrated without reconstructing their hidden scores. We derive a consistent estimator of True Calibration Error, unbiased estimators of strictly proper scoring rules, and a one-bit objective for Temperature Scaling whose expectation matches the standard log-loss objective. Across these settings, we quantify the statistical cost incurred by replacing standard probability-access procedures by our one-bit estimators. Finally, we instantiate the framework for black-box LLMs, where the logit_bias API parameter allows us to realize the required threshold queries in zero-shot binary question answering."},"title":{"value":"Single-Query Black-Box Calibration Auditing via Logit Bias"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Roman Plaud","username":""},{"fullname":"Antoine Saillenfest","username":""},{"fullname":"Matthieu Labeau","username":"~Matthieu_Labeau2"},{"fullname":"Thomas Bonald","username":""},{"fullname":"Willem Waegeman","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1793090089208,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2609-05125"],"tcdate":1793090083513,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Matthieu_Labeau2"],"forum":"rp7xGDDfs9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":215933,"cdate":1788220800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1793090089208,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"rp7xGDDfs9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1906.09551v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|confidence_calibration_for_convolutional_neural_networks_using_structured_dropout"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhilu_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Adrian_V._Dalca:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mert_R._Sabuncu:"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1906.09551"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1906-09551,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhilu Zhang and Adrian V. Dalca and Mert R. Sabuncu},\n  title={Confidence Calibration for Convolutional Neural Networks Using Structured Dropout},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1906.09551},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1906.09551}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In classification applications, we often want probabilistic predictions to reflect confidence or uncertainty. Dropout, a commonly used training technique, has recently been linked to Bayesian inference, yielding an efficient way to quantify uncertainty in neural network models. However, as previously demonstrated, confidence estimates computed with a naive implementation of dropout can be poorly calibrated, particularly when using convolutional networks. In this paper, through the lens of ensemble learning, we associate calibration error with the correlation between the models sampled with dropout. Motivated by this, we explore the use of structured dropout to promote model diversity and improve confidence calibration. We use the SVHN, CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 datasets to empirically compare model diversity and confidence errors obtained using various dropout techniques. We also show the merit of structured dropout in a Bayesian active learning application."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration for Convolutional Neural Networks Using Structured Dropout"},"authors":{"value":["Zhilu Zhang","Adrian V. Dalca","Mert R. Sabuncu"]}},"tmdate":1739211624838,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1739211621451,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhilu_Zhang1"],"forum":"xWOSV8rlxe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":316984,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1739211624838,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xWOSV8rlxe","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.11682v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"fleith|lifcal_online_light_field_camera_calibration_via_bundle_adjustment"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Aymeric_Fleith:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Doaa_Ahmed:","~Daniel_Cremers1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Niclas_Zeller:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.11682"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2408-11682,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Aymeric Fleith and Doaa Ahmed and Daniel Cremers and Niclas Zeller},\n  title={LiFCal: Online Light Field Camera Calibration via Bundle Adjustment},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2408.11682},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.11682}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We propose LiFCal, a novel geometric online calibration pipeline for MLA-based light field cameras. LiFCal accurately determines model parameters from a moving camera sequence without precise calibration targets, integrating arbitrary metric scaling constraints. It optimizes intrinsic parameters of the light field camera model, the 3D coordinates of a sparse set of scene points and camera poses in a single bundle adjustment defined directly on micro image points. We show that LiFCal can reliably and repeatably calibrate a focused plenoptic camera using different input sequences, providing intrinsic camera parameters extremely close to state-of-the-art methods, while offering two main advantages: it can be applied in a target-free scene, and it is implemented online in a complete and continuous pipeline. Furthermore, we demonstrate the quality of the obtained camera parameters in downstream tasks like depth estimation and SLAM. Webpage: https://lifcal.github.io/"},"title":{"value":"LiFCal: Online Light Field Camera Calibration via Bundle Adjustment"},"authors":{"value":["Aymeric Fleith","Doaa Ahmed","Daniel Cremers","Niclas Zeller"]}},"tmdate":1731496896577,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731496887150,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Daniel_Cremers1"],"forum":"NSFiP9uwwA","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":225151,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731496896577,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"NSFiP9uwwA","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Remote. Sens. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/15/17/4256/pdf?version=1693387715"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/REMOTESENSING/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|onorbit_geometric_calibration_and_performance_validation_of_the_gaofen14_stereo_mapping_satellite"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yan_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_Wang_0075:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tuanjie_Zheng:","~Yongsheng_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ying_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Li_Li_0100:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/rs15174256"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/remotesensing/ZhangWZZLYL23,\n  author={Yan Zhang and Tao Wang and Tuanjie Zheng and Yongsheng Zhang and Lei Li and Ying Yu and Li Li},\n  title={On-Orbit Geometric Calibration and Performance Validation of the GaoFen-14 Stereo Mapping Satellite},\n  year={2023},\n  month={September},\n  cdate={1693526400000},\n  journal={Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={15},\n  number={17},\n  pages={4256},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/rs15174256}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The GaoFen-14 satellite is primarily utilized for global high-precision positioning and generating 1:10,000 scale geographic information products, making it one of the most accurate stereo mapping satellites in China. With a long-stitched CCD (charge couple device) consisting of nine chips for the forward view camera and six chips for the backward view camera, it is crucial to validate the satellite’s geometric performance and achieve high uncontrolled global positioning precision. In this study, two calibration models were proposed for the on-orbit calibration of the GaoFen-14 satellite: one accounting for the inter-chip geometry constraint and another without the constraint. Correspondingly, five calibration schemes (A, B, C, D, and E) were designed, with varying settings of the external calibration parameters, interior parameters, and utilization of the optical axis measurement data. Schemes A and B utilized the same set of alignment angles for the forward and backward images and differed in the set of interior calibration parameters. Scheme A took a single set of interior calibration parameters for all chips within the same view, while Scheme B set independent interior calibration parameters for each chip in both the forward and backward views considering the inter-chip geometry constraint. Schemes C, D, and E set two groups of alignment angles for the forward and backward views, respectively. In addition, Schemes C, D, and E utilized GaoFen-14′s specific real-time recording data of the optical axis, incorporating a slight revision of the alignment angle calibration value using dichotomous search. The interior models of Schemes A and C were identical, and those of Schemes B and D were the same. In comparison with scheme D, scheme E only lacks the inter-chip geometry constraint. These five schemes were taken to validate the most suitable exterior model and interior model for the GaoFen-14 satellite. Calibration experiments were conducted using original multichip images and the stitched images from the Zhongwei, Ningxia, and Songshan (Henan) test fields. Additionally, four sets of data from around the world are used to verify the calibration effect. The results demonstrated significant improvements in uncontrolled positioning accuracy for all five calibration schemes, transitioning from the 100 m level to the meter or even the submeter level. However, Schemes A and B exhibited unstable controlled positioning accuracy, with fluctuations ranging from 1 m to 5 m. In contrast, Schemes C, D, and E achieved substantial enhancements in uncontrolled positioning accuracy, maintaining stability with variations ranging from 0.94 to 1.14 m in the X direction, 0.41 to 0.48 m in the Y direction, and 0.77 to 0.81 m in the Z direction. The positions of the CCD probes obtained from Schemes C and D demonstrated consistency, with over 80% of the probes falling within 0.1 pixels, and all of the sampled probes within 0.3 pixels. However, Scheme D behaves better in describing the geometric deformation of each CCD chip and keeping the integrity of the long-stitched CCD simultaneously. These experimental results validate the GaoFen-14 satellite’s ability to achieve stable uncontrolled positioning accuracy worldwide and a stable geometric structure between multichips. For the GaoFen-14 satellite, it is more appropriate to adopt two sets of alignment angles for the forward and backward views, respectively. As the satellite’s on-orbit operation time increases, it is recommended to employ Scheme D for monitoring inconsistent local geometric deformation in future calibration work."},"title":{"value":"On-Orbit Geometric Calibration and Performance Validation of the GaoFen-14 Stereo Mapping Satellite"},"authors":{"value":["Yan Zhang","Tao Wang","Tuanjie Zheng","Yongsheng Zhang","Lei Li","Ying Yu","Li Li"]}},"tmdate":1744851722347,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1744851718928,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yongsheng_Zhang1"],"forum":"61LK4D9udH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":400104,"cdate":1693526400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744851722347,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"61LK4D9udH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Intell. Transp. Syst. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/6979/10742224/10591453.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TITS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"an|survey_of_extrinsic_calibration_on_lidarcamera_system_for_intelligent_vehicle_challenges_approaches_and_trends"},"authorids":{"value":["~Pei_An2","~Junfeng_Ding1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Siwen_Quan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiaqi_Yang_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:You_Yang:","~Qiong_Liu4","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Ma_0003:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TITS.2024.3419758"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tits/AnDQYYLM24,\n  author={Pei An and Junfeng Ding and Siwen Quan and Jiaqi Yang and You Yang and Qiong Liu and Jie Ma},\n  title={Survey of Extrinsic Calibration on LiDAR-Camera System for Intelligent Vehicle: Challenges, Approaches, and Trends},\n  year={2024},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1730419200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Intell. Transp. Syst.},\n  volume={25},\n  number={11},\n  pages={15342-15366},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TITS.2024.3419758}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"A system with light detection and ranging (LiDAR) and camera (named as LiDAR-camera system) plays the essential role in intelligent vehicle (IV), for it provides 3D spatial and 2D texture features for 3D scene understanding. To leverage LiDAR point cloud and image, extrinsic calibration is a crucial technique, for it can align 2D pixel and 3D point in the pixel-level accuracy. With the rapid development of IV, calibration demand is shifted from offline to online, from the specific scenes to the open scenes. It brings new challenge to the calibration task. Although numbers of approaches have been proposed in the last decade, there lacks an in-depth summary about this topic. Thus, we conduct a survey of extrinsic calibration. Theoretically, the key of calibration is to build correspondence from LiDAR point cloud and optical image. From the viewpoint of correspondence, we attempt to divide the mainstream approaches into explicit and implicit correspondence based methods. After that, we summarize both the strength and weakness of the current works, provide the methods comparison, and list the open-source implementations. Finally, we analyze the tendency of calibration approach, discuss the remained problems in this field. We believe that this survey benefits to the community of autonomous driving."},"title":{"value":"Survey of Extrinsic Calibration on LiDAR-Camera System for Intelligent Vehicle: Challenges, Approaches, and Trends"},"authors":{"value":["Pei An","Junfeng Ding","Siwen Quan","Jiaqi Yang","You Yang","Qiong Liu","Jie Ma"]}},"tmdate":1786434171863,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1744825890536,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Junfeng_Ding1"],"forum":"HAhJkhLUAl","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":399464,"cdate":1730419200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1786434171863,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"HAhJkhLUAl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.27266v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|hyperclick_advancing_reliable_gui_grounding_via_uncertainty_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shaojie_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pei_Fu:","~Ruoceng_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiahui_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Anan_Du:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiuwen_Xi:","~Shaokang_Wang2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ying_Huang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bin_Qin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhenbo_Luo:","~Jian_Luan1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.27266"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2510-27266,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shaojie Zhang and Pei Fu and Ruoceng Zhang and Jiahui Yang and Anan Du and Xiuwen Xi and Shaokang Wang and Ying Huang and Bin Qin and Zhenbo Luo and Jian Luan},\n  title={HyperClick: Advancing Reliable GUI Grounding via Uncertainty Calibration},\n  year={2025},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1759276800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2510.27266},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.27266}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Autonomous Graphical User Interface (GUI) agents rely on accurate GUI grounding, which maps language instructions to on-screen coordinates, to execute user commands. However, current models, whether trained via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT), lack self-awareness of their capability boundaries, leading to overconfidence and unreliable predictions. We first systematically evaluate probabilistic and verbalized confidence in general and GUI-specific models, revealing a misalignment between confidence and actual accuracy, which is particularly critical in dynamic GUI automation tasks, where single errors can cause task failure. To address this, we propose HyperClick, a novel framework that enhances reliable GUI grounding through uncertainty calibration. HyperClick introduces a dual reward mechanism, combining a binary reward for correct actions with a truncated Gaussian-based spatial confidence modeling, calibrated using the Brier score. This approach jointly optimizes grounding accuracy and confidence reliability, fostering introspective self-criticism. Extensive experiments on seven challenge benchmarks show that HyperClick achieves state-of-the-art performance while providing well-calibrated confidence. By enabling explicit confidence calibration and introspective self-criticism, HyperClick reduces overconfidence and supports more reliable GUI automation."},"title":{"value":"HyperClick: Advancing Reliable GUI Grounding via Uncertainty Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Shaojie Zhang","Pei Fu","Ruoceng Zhang","Jiahui Yang","Anan Du","Xiuwen Xi","Shaokang Wang","Ying Huang","Bin Qin","Zhenbo Luo","Jian Luan"]}},"tmdate":1784519281752,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2510-27266"],"tcdate":1768809651498,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jian_Luan1"],"forum":"F18MXpFkYr","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":771015,"cdate":1759276800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784519281752,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"F18MXpFkYr","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2025 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Models","Quantization","Rotation","Efficient inference"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/21eb70669ad871d4650fe30c6169d33184e3d64a.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"deep_learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Quantization plays a crucial role in accelerating the inference of large-scale models, and rotational matrices have been shown to effectively improve quantization performance by smoothing outliers. However, end-to-end fine-tuning of rotational optimization algorithms incurs high computational costs and is prone to overfitting. To address this challenge, we propose an efficient distribution-aware rotational calibration method, DartQuant, which reduces the complexity of rotational optimization by constraining the distribution of the activations after rotation. This approach also effectively reduces reliance on task-specific losses, thereby mitigating the risk of overfitting. Additionally, we introduce the QR-Orth optimization scheme, which replaces expensive alternating optimization with a more efficient solution. In a variety of model quantization experiments, DartQuant demonstrates superior performance. Compared to existing methods, it achieves 47$\\times$ acceleration and 10$\\times$ memory savings for rotational optimization on a 70B model. Furthermore, it is the first to successfully complete rotational calibration for a 70B model on a single 3090 GPU, making quantization of large language models feasible in resource-constrained environments."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nshao2025dartquant,\ntitle={DartQuant: Efficient Rotational Distribution Calibration for {LLM} Quantization},\nauthor={Yuantian Shao and Yuanteng Chen and Peisong Wang and Jianlin Yu and Jing Lin and Yiwu Yao and Zhihui Wei and Jian Cheng},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=LfcfwlLCHM}\n}"},"title":{"value":"DartQuant: Efficient Rotational Distribution Calibration for LLM Quantization"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/558f2c2f758af90ca5cc88dc8c7a55449762ca20.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"shao|dartquant_efficient_rotational_distribution_calibration_for_llm_quantization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yuantian_Shao1","~Yuanteng_Chen1","~Peisong_Wang1","~Jianlin_Yu1","~Jing_Lin6","~Yiwu_Yao1","~Zhihui_Wei1","~Jian_Cheng7"]},"authors":{"value":["Yuantian Shao","Yuanteng Chen","Peisong Wang","Jianlin Yu","Jing Lin","Yiwu Yao","Zhihui Wei","Jian Cheng"]}},"tmdate":1783627456978,"pdate":1758216543602,"tcdate":1744600449665,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2223/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2223/Authors"],"forum":"LfcfwlLCHM","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2223,"cdate":1744600449665,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2223/-/Full_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2223/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1783627456978,"odate":1761704739052,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Conference","id":"LfcfwlLCHM","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"HCI (13) 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-319-39955-3_6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/HCI/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"mao|predicting_eeg_sample_size_required_for_classification_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","~Yufei_Huang5"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-39955-3_6"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/hci/MaoJLH16,\n  author={Zijing Mao and Tzyy-Ping Jung and Chin-Teng Lin and Yufei Huang},\n  title={Predicting EEG Sample Size Required for Classification Calibration},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  pages={57-68},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-39955-3_6},\n  booktitle={HCI (13)},\n  crossref={conf/hci/2016-13}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This study considers an important problem of predicting required calibration sample size for electroencephalogram (EEG)-based classification in brain computer interaction (BCI). We propose an adaptive algorithm based on learning curve fitting to learn the relationship between sample size and classification performance for each individual subject. The algorithm can always provide the predicted result in advance of reaching the baseline performance with an average error of 17.4 %. By comparing the learning curve of different classifiers, the algorithm can also recommend the best classifier for a BCI application. The algorithm also learns a sample size upper bound from the prior datasets and uses it to detect subject outliers that potentially need excessive amount of calibration data. The algorithm is applied to three EEG-based BCI datasets to demonstrate its utility and efficacy. A Matlab package with GUI is also developed and available for downloading at https://github.com/ZijingMao/LearningCurveFittingForSampleSizePrediction. Since few algorithms are yet available to predict performance for BCIs, our algorithm will be an important tool for real-life BCI applications."},"title":{"value":"Predicting EEG Sample Size Required for Classification Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Zijing Mao","Tzyy-Ping Jung","Chin-Teng Lin","Yufei Huang"]}},"tmdate":1773927818931,"pdate":1483142400000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/hci/MaoJLH16"],"tcdate":1773927793438,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yufei_Huang5"],"forum":"Y1uU5qqpVN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":856284,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1773927818931,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Y1uU5qqpVN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM","KV cache compression","low-resource","token eviction"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) require substantial computational resources during generation. While the Key-Value (KV) cache significantly accelerates this process by storing attention intermediates, its memory footprint grows linearly with sequence length, batch size, and model size, creating a bottleneck in long-context scenarios. Various KV cache compression techniques, including token eviction, quantization, and low-rank projection, have been proposed to mitigate this bottleneck, often complementing each other.\nThis paper focuses on enhancing token eviction strategies.\nToken eviction leverages the observation that the attention patterns are often sparse, allowing for the removal of less critical KV entries to save memory. However, this reduction usually comes at the cost of notable accuracy degradation, particularly under high compression ratios. \nTo address this issue, we propose CaliDrop, a novel strategy that enhances token eviction through calibration. Our preliminary experiments show that queries at nearby positions exhibit high similarity. Building on this observation, CaliDrop performs speculative calibration on the discarded tokens to mitigate the accuracy loss caused by token eviction.\nExtensive experiments demonstrate that CaliDrop significantly improves the accuracy of existing token eviction methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nsu2026calidrop,\ntitle={CaliDrop: {KV} Cache Compression with Query-based Calibration},\nauthor={Yi Su and Quantong Qiu and Yuechi Zhou and Qingrong Xia and Ping Li and Xinyu Duan and Zhefeng Wang and Juntao Li and Min Zhang},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=2WzCzpkeTc}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CaliDrop: KV Cache Compression with Query-based Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2d2ca0c88ea8ae9820d1913c0766766ed3a42c18.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"su|calidrop_kv_cache_compression_with_querybased_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yi_Su3","~Quantong_Qiu1","~Yuechi_Zhou1","~Qingrong_Xia1","~Ping_Li16","~Xinyu_Duan1","~Zhefeng_Wang1","~Juntao_Li2","~Min_Zhang9"]},"authors":{"value":["Yi Su","Quantong Qiu","Yuechi Zhou","Qingrong Xia","Ping Li","Xinyu Duan","Zhefeng Wang","Juntao Li","Min Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1770804960521,"tcdate":1758198498568,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission11414/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission11414/Authors"],"forum":"2WzCzpkeTc","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":11414,"cdate":1758198498568,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission11414/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770804960521,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"2WzCzpkeTc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"TLDR":{"value":"Introduce uncertainty calibration to reliable GUI grounding"},"keywords":{"value":["GUI Agent; Uncertainty Calibration; Self-Evolution;"]},"primary_area":{"value":"applications to computer vision, audio, language, and other modalities"},"abstract":{"value":"Autonomous Graphical User Interface (GUI) agents rely on accurate GUI grounding, which maps language instructions to on-screen coordinates, to execute user commands. However, current models, whether trained via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT), lack self-awareness of their capability boundaries, leading to overconfidence and unreliable predictions. We first systematically evaluate probabilistic and verbalized confidence in general and GUI-specific models, revealing a misalignment between confidence and actual accuracy, which is particularly critical in dynamic GUI automation tasks, where single errors can cause task failure. To address this, we propose HyperClick, a novel framework that enhances reliable GUI grounding through uncertainty calibration. HyperClick introduces a dual reward mechanism, combining a binary reward for correct actions with a truncated Gaussian–based spatial confidence modeling, calibrated using the Brier score. This approach jointly optimizes grounding accuracy and confidence reliability, fostering introspective self-criticism. Extensive experiments on seven challenge benchmarks show that HyperClick achieves state-of-the-art performance while providing well-calibrated confidence. By enabling explicit confidence calibration and introspective self-criticism, HyperClick reduces overconfidence and supports more reliable GUI automation."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nzhang2025hyperclick,\ntitle={HyperClick: Advancing Reliable {GUI} Grounding via Uncertainty Calibration},\nauthor={Shaojie Zhang and Pei Fu and Ruoceng Zhang and Jiahui Yang and Anan Du and Xiuwen Xi and Shaokang Wang and Ying Huang and Bin Qin and Zhenbo Luo and Jian Luan},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=pXYwksqDyE}\n}"},"title":{"value":"HyperClick: Advancing Reliable GUI Grounding via Uncertainty Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e3a735f3272691316d28392b9f3f8e7f42e28ba1.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|hyperclick_advancing_reliable_gui_grounding_via_uncertainty_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shaojie_Zhang5","~Pei_Fu1","~Ruoceng_Zhang1","~Jiahui_Yang5","~Anan_Du2","~Xiuwen_Xi1","~Shaokang_Wang2","~Ying_Huang10","~Bin_Qin2","~Zhenbo_Luo2","~Jian_Luan1"]},"authors":{"value":["Shaojie Zhang","Pei Fu","Ruoceng Zhang","Jiahui Yang","Anan Du","Xiuwen Xi","Shaokang Wang","Ying Huang","Bin Qin","Zhenbo Luo","Jian Luan"]}},"tmdate":1763525045472,"tcdate":1756732289534,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission240/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission240/Authors"],"forum":"pXYwksqDyE","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":240,"cdate":1756732289534,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission240/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1763525045472,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"pXYwksqDyE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.02454v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|graphbased_confidence_calibration_for_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yukun_Li2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sijia_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lifu_Huang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Li-Ping_Liu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2411.02454"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2411-02454,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yukun Li and Sijia Wang and Lifu Huang and Li-Ping Liu},\n  title={Graph-based Confidence Calibration for Large Language Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2411.02454},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2411.02454}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"One important approach to improving the reliability of large language models (LLMs) is to provide accurate confidence estimations regarding the correctness of their answers. However, developing a well-calibrated confidence estimation model is challenging, as mistakes made by LLMs can be difficult to detect. We propose a novel method combining the LLM's self-consistency with labeled data and training an auxiliary model to estimate the correctness of its responses to questions. This auxiliary model predicts the correctness of responses based solely on their consistent information. To set up the learning problem, we use a weighted graph to represent the consistency among the LLM's multiple responses to a question. Correctness labels are assigned to these responses based on their similarity to the correct answer. We then train a graph neural network to estimate the probability of correct responses. Experiments demonstrate that the proposed approach substantially outperforms several of the most recent methods in confidence calibration across multiple widely adopted benchmark datasets. Furthermore, the proposed approach significantly improves the generalization capability of confidence calibration on out-of-domain (OOD) data."},"title":{"value":"Graph-based Confidence Calibration for Large Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Yukun Li","Sijia Wang","Lifu Huang","Li-Ping Liu"]}},"tmdate":1747286140602,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747286131928,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yukun_Li2"],"forum":"JnvvIn42OZ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":473030,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747286140602,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"JnvvIn42OZ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2025 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Language Models; Uncertainty Calibration"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/99f88b98cfd0e4d8d5a58d66d335bbcc12b4c324.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"reciprocal_reviewing":{"value":"I understand the reciprocal reviewing requirement as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/CallForPapers. If none of the authors are registered as a reviewer, it may result in a desk rejection at the discretion of the program chairs. To request an exception, please complete this form at https://forms.gle/Huojr6VjkFxiQsUp6."},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"One important approach to improving the reliability of large language models (LLMs) is to provide accurate confidence estimations regarding the correctness of their answers. However, developing a well-calibrated confidence estimation model is challenging, as mistakes made by LLMs can be difficult to detect. We propose a novel method combining the LLM's self-consistency with labeled data and training an auxiliary model to estimate the correctness of its responses to questions. This auxiliary model predicts the correctness of responses based solely on their consistent information. To set up the learning problem, we use a weighted graph to represent the consistency among the LLM's multiple responses to a question. Correctness labels are assigned to these responses based on their similarity to the correct answer. We then train a graph neural network to estimate the probability of correct responses. Experiments demonstrate that the proposed approach substantially outperforms several of the most recent methods in confidence calibration across multiple widely adopted benchmark datasets. Furthermore, the proposed approach significantly improves the generalization capability of confidence calibration on out-of-domain (OOD) data."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nli2025graphbased,\ntitle={Graph-based Confidence Calibration for Large Language Models},\nauthor={Yukun Li and Sijia Wang and Lifu Huang and Liping Liu},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=uuXPWRtwvK}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Graph-based Confidence Calibration for Large Language Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/699b83577eae421b65773003a2c532897d8836d6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|graphbased_confidence_calibration_for_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yukun_Li2","~Sijia_Wang1","~Lifu_Huang1","~Liping_Liu1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Yukun Li","Sijia Wang","Lifu Huang","Liping Liu"]}},"tmdate":1737564982450,"tcdate":1727486586151,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission12803/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission12803/Authors"],"forum":"uuXPWRtwvK","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":12803,"cdate":1727486586151,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission12803/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission12803/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1737564982450,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"uuXPWRtwvK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.01730v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"xing|distancebased_learning_from_errors_for_confidence_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chen_Xing:","~Sercan_Ömer_Arik2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zizhao_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tomas_Pfister:"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1912.01730"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1912-01730,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Chen Xing and Sercan Ömer Arik and Zizhao Zhang and Tomas Pfister},\n  title={Distance-Based Learning from Errors for Confidence Calibration},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1912.01730},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1912.01730}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep neural networks (DNNs) are poorly calibrated when trained in conventional ways. To improve confidence calibration of DNNs, we propose a novel training method, distance-based learning from errors (DBLE). DBLE bases its confidence estimation on distances in the representation space. In DBLE, we first adapt prototypical learning to train classification models. It yields a representation space where the distance between a test sample and its ground truth class center can calibrate the model's classification performance. At inference, however, these distances are not available due to the lack of ground truth labels. To circumvent this by inferring the distance for every test sample, we propose to train a confidence model jointly with the classification model. We integrate this into training by merely learning from mis-classified training samples, which we show to be highly beneficial for effective learning. On multiple datasets and DNN architectures, we demonstrate that DBLE outperforms alternative single-model confidence calibration approaches. DBLE also achieves comparable performance with computationally-expensive ensemble approaches with lower computational cost and lower number of parameters."},"title":{"value":"Distance-Based Learning from Errors for Confidence Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Chen Xing","Sercan Ömer Arik","Zizhao Zhang","Tomas Pfister"]}},"tmdate":1723037627937,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1723037608800,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sercan_O_Arik1"],"forum":"ZpwBvuBvic","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":60148,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1723037627937,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ZpwBvuBvic","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/32/10636961/10584357.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"fang|esale_enhancing_codesummary_alignment_learning_for_source_code_summarization"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2024.3422274"},"abstract":{"value":"Source) code summarization aims to automatically generate succinct natural language summaries for given code snippets. Such summaries play a significant role in promoting developers to understand and maintain code. Inspired by neural machine translation, deep learning-based code summarization techniques widely adopt an encoder-decoder framework, where the encoder transforms given code snippets into context vectors, and the decoder decodes context vectors into summaries. Recently, large-scale pre-trained models for source code (e.g., CodeBERT and UniXcoder) are equipped with encoders capable of producing general context vectors and have achieved substantial improvements on the code summarization task. However, although they are usually trained mainly on code-focused tasks and can capture general code features, they still fall short in capturing specific features that need to be summarized. In a nutshell, they fail to learn the alignment between code snippets and summaries (code-summary alignment for short). In this paper, we propose a novel approach to improve code summarization based on summary-focused tasks. Specifically, we exploit a multi-task learning paradigm to train the encoder on three summary-focused tasks to enhance its ability to learn code-summary alignment, including unidirectional language modeling (ULM), masked language modeling (MLM), and action word prediction (AWP). Unlike pre-trained models that mainly predict masked tokens in code snippets, we design ULM and MLM to predict masked words in summaries. Intuitively, predicting words based on given code snippets would help learn the code-summary alignment. In addition, existing work shows that AWP affects the prediction of the entire summary. Therefore, we further introduce the domain-specific task AWP to enhance the ability of the encoder to learn the alignment between action words and code snippets. We evaluate the effectiveness of our approach, called Esale, by conducting extensive experiments on four datasets, including two widely used datasets JCSD and PCSD, a cross-project Java dataset CPJD, and a multilingual language dataset CodeSearchNet. Experimental results show that Esale significantly outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in all three widely used metrics, including BLEU, METEOR, and ROUGE-L. Moreover, the human evaluation proves that the summaries generated by Esale are more informative and closer to the ground-truth summaries."},"title":{"value":"Esale: Enhancing Code-Summary Alignment Learning for Source Code Summarization"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Chunrong Fang"},{"fullname":"Weisong Sun"},{"fullname":"Yuchen Chen","username":"~Yuchen_Chen4"},{"fullname":"Xiao Chen"},{"fullname":"Zhao Wei"},{"fullname":"Quanjun Zhang","username":"~Quanjun_Zhang1"},{"fullname":"Yudu You"},{"fullname":"Bin Luo"},{"fullname":"Yang Liu"},{"fullname":"Zhenyu Chen","username":"~Zhenyu_Chen6"}]}},"tmdate":1791890371148,"pdate":1722470400000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tse.2024.3422274"],"tcdate":1773394586406,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yuchen_Chen4"],"forum":"KJKFbcmh8o","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":56285,"cdate":1723670405921,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791890371148,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"KJKFbcmh8o","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Software Eng. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/32/10636961/10584357.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TSE/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"fang|esale_enhancing_codesummary_alignment_learning_for_source_code_summarization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chunrong_Fang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weisong_Sun:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuchen_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiao_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhao_Wei:","~Quanjun_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yudu_You:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bin_Luo_0003:","~Yang_Liu36","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhenyu_Chen_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2024.3422274"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tse/FangSCCWZYLLC24,\n  author={Chunrong Fang and Weisong Sun and Yuchen Chen and Xiao Chen and Zhao Wei and Quanjun Zhang and Yudu You and Bin Luo and Yang Liu and Zhenyu Chen},\n  title={Esale: Enhancing Code-Summary Alignment Learning for Source Code Summarization},\n  year={2024},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1722470400000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Software Eng.},\n  volume={50},\n  number={8},\n  pages={2077-2095},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2024.3422274}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Source) code summarization aims to automatically generate succinct natural language summaries for given code snippets. Such summaries play a significant role in promoting developers to understand and maintain code. Inspired by neural machine translation, deep learning-based code summarization techniques widely adopt an encoder-decoder framework, where the encoder transforms given code snippets into context vectors, and the decoder decodes context vectors into summaries. Recently, large-scale pre-trained models for source code (e.g., CodeBERT and UniXcoder) are equipped with encoders capable of producing general context vectors and have achieved substantial improvements on the code summarization task. However, although they are usually trained mainly on code-focused tasks and can capture general code features, they still fall short in capturing specific features that need to be summarized. In a nutshell, they fail to learn the alignment between code snippets and summaries (code-summary alignment for short). In this paper, we propose a novel approach to improve code summarization based on summary-focused tasks. Specifically, we exploit a multi-task learning paradigm to train the encoder on three summary-focused tasks to enhance its ability to learn code-summary alignment, including unidirectional language modeling (ULM), masked language modeling (MLM), and action word prediction (AWP). Unlike pre-trained models that mainly predict masked tokens in code snippets, we design ULM and MLM to predict masked words in summaries. Intuitively, predicting words based on given code snippets would help learn the code-summary alignment. In addition, existing work shows that AWP affects the prediction of the entire summary. Therefore, we further introduce the domain-specific task AWP to enhance the ability of the encoder to learn the alignment between action words and code snippets. We evaluate the effectiveness of our approach, called Esale , by conducting extensive experiments on four datasets, including two widely used datasets JCSD and PCSD, a cross-project Java dataset CPJD, and a multilingual language dataset CodeSearchNet. Experimental results show that Esale significantly outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in all three widely used metrics, including BLEU, METEOR, and ROUGE-L. Moreover, the human evaluation proves that the summaries generated by Esale are more informative and closer to the ground-truth summaries."},"title":{"value":"Esale: Enhancing Code-Summary Alignment Learning for Source Code Summarization"},"authors":{"value":["Chunrong Fang","Weisong Sun","Yuchen Chen","Xiao Chen","Zhao Wei","Quanjun Zhang","Yudu You","Bin Luo","Yang Liu","Zhenyu Chen"]}},"tmdate":1779712055541,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731474912198,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chunrong_Fang1"],"forum":"7W0lNhiel8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":198665,"cdate":1722470400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1779712055541,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"7W0lNhiel8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2024 Track Datasets and Benchmarks Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/1bbbfe1c0e04ebf634e5a978199228e00fe6c33c.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Code Agents","LLM","Safety benchmark","Trustworthy machine learning"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track"},"paperhash":{"value":"guo|redcode_risky_code_execution_and_generation_benchmark_for_code_agents"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chengquan_Guo1","~Xun_Liu6","~Chulin_Xie1","~Andy_Zhou2","~Yi_Zeng3","~Zinan_Lin1","~Dawn_Song1","~Bo_Li19"]},"abstract":{"value":"With the rapidly increasing capabilities and adoption of code agents for AI-assisted coding and software development, safety and security concerns, such as generating or executing malicious code, have become significant barriers to the real-world deployment of these agents. To provide comprehensive and practical evaluations on the safety of code agents, we propose RedCode, an evaluation platform with benchmarks grounded in four key principles: real interaction with systems, holistic evaluation of unsafe code generation and execution, diverse input formats, and high-quality safety scenarios and tests. RedCode consists of two parts to evaluate agents’ safety in unsafe code execution and generation: (1) RedCode-Exec provides challenging code prompts in Python as inputs, aiming to evaluate code agents’ ability to recognize and handle unsafe code. We then map the Python code to other programming languages (e.g., Bash) and natural text summaries or descriptions for evaluation, leading to a total of over 4,000 testing instances. We provide 25 types of critical vulnerabilities spanning various domains, such as websites, file systems, and operating systems. We provide a Docker sandbox environment to evaluate the execution capabilities of code agents and design corresponding evaluation metrics to assess their execution results. (2) RedCode-Gen provides 160 prompts with function signatures and docstrings as input to assess whether code agents will follow instructions to generate harmful code or software. Our empirical findings, derived from evaluating three agent frameworks based on 19 LLMs, provide insights into code agents’ vulnerabilities. For instance, evaluations on RedCode-Exec show that agents are more likely to reject executing unsafe operations on the operating system, but are less likely to reject executing technically buggy code, indicating high risks. Unsafe operations described in natural text lead to a lower rejection rate than those in code format. Additionally, evaluations on RedCode-Gen reveal that more capable base models and agents with stronger overall coding abilities, such as GPT4, tend to produce more sophisticated and effective harmful software. Our findings highlight the need for stringent safety evaluations for diverse code agents. Our dataset and code are publicly available at https://github.com/AI-secure/RedCode."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nguo2024redcode,\ntitle={RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents},\nauthor={Chengquan Guo and Xun Liu and Chulin Xie and Andy Zhou and Yi Zeng and Zinan Lin and Dawn Song and Bo Li},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-eight Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems Datasets and Benchmarks Track},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=mAG68wdggA}\n}"},"title":{"value":"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents"},"authors":{"value":["Chengquan Guo","Xun Liu","Chulin Xie","Andy Zhou","Yi Zeng","Zinan Lin","Dawn Song","Bo Li"]}},"tmdate":1731500242937,"pdate":1727347153335,"tcdate":1716997380113,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission1837/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission1837/Authors"],"forum":"mAG68wdggA","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1837,"cdate":1716997380113,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission1837/-/Supplementary_Material","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission1837/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1731500242937,"odate":1731500242926,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2024/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track","id":"mAG68wdggA","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["continual learning","conformal prediction","uncertainty quantification","calibration","large language models"]},"primary_area":{"value":"transfer learning, meta learning, and lifelong learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Continual fine-tuning changes not only a language model's predictions but also the score distribution used by post-hoc uncertainty procedures, and this creates a specific failure mode for conformal prediction: thresholds calibrated before later fine-tuning can lose task-level coverage even when top-1 accuracy changes only modestly. We measure this failure mode on sequentially fine-tuned LLMs across five model configurations and eight task sequences, seven of which are classification-style or multiple-choice settings covered by our formal framework. In the classification-style grid, stale-threshold coverage loss is substantially larger than accuracy loss; by aggregate loss, coverage degrades by roughly $3.7\\times$ as much as accuracy, and the effect is diagnosed by drift in the nonconformity-score CDF rather than by argmax-accuracy drift. We propose task-specific calibration replay, which maintains a small held-out calibration buffer per task and recomputes that task's split-conformal threshold under the current model after each update. Under standard split-conformal exchangeability assumptions, and assuming the calibration buffer is not used for training or model selection, this restores finite-sample marginal coverage for the corresponding task without adding gradient updates. Empirically, using a default calibration-buffer cap of $m_0=200$ examples per task, with $m_j=\\min\\{m_0,n_j^{\\mathrm{cal}}\\}$ for low-resource tasks, task-specific recalibration  (which acts as a continual Mondrian conformal predictor) reduces coverage loss to near nominal while preserving utility, with average set sizes and singleton rates close to a large fresh-calibration reference. The results hold against competitive baselines including online adaptive conformal inference (ACI) and weighted conformal prediction, both of which recover only part of the coverage gap, and a simple zero-shot router retains most of the benefit when task identity is unknown. Our formal guarantees apply to classification-style tasks with known task identity and task-specific buffers; open-ended generation results are visually separated and reported only as exploratory."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nakter2026continual,\ntitle={Continual Calibration: Stale Conformal Thresholds Fail Under Lifelong {LLM} Fine-Tuning},\nauthor={Sanjeda Akter and Ibne Farabi Shihab},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=vTEaDFYVQC}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Continual Calibration: Stale Conformal Thresholds Fail Under Lifelong LLM Fine-Tuning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8275b65def8f42a59c637eb24d5a1998bd4cd94c.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"akter|continual_calibration_stale_conformal_thresholds_fail_under_lifelong_llm_finetuning"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Sanjeda_Akter1","fullname":"Sanjeda Akter","institutions":[{"name":"Iowa State University","domain":"iastate.edu","country":"US"}]},{"username":"~Ibne_Farabi_Shihab1","fullname":"Ibne Farabi Shihab","institutions":[{"name":"Amazon","domain":"amazon.com","country":"US"}]}]}},"tmdate":1794039027658,"tcdate":1789614906999,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission27931/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission27931/Authors"],"forum":"vTEaDFYVQC","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":27931,"cdate":1789614906999,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission27931/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1794039027658,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"vTEaDFYVQC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.21019v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|targetaware_calibration_data_selection_for_preserving_uncertainty_in_quantized_language_models"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.21019"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2608-21019,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhen Yang and Sizai Hou and Kaiwen Zheng and Yaofang Liu and Liang He and Yixuan Chen and Kangning Cui},\n  title={Target-Aware Calibration Data Selection for Preserving Uncertainty in Quantized Language Models},\n  year={2026},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1785542400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2608.21019},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.21019}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Quantization is widely used to deploy large language models, but its effect on uncertainty behavior, such as confidence, margins, and abstention, is rarely treated as a primary objective. We frame calibration-data selection for quantization as a target-dependent uncertainty-preservation problem. Different deployments emphasize different regions of the input distribution, yet prior work mainly optimizes accuracy-oriented compression metrics or adjusts scores after quantization. We formalize this goal with distributional and boundary preservation risks, and provide a simple mixture-mismatch argument explaining why no single calibration recipe should be expected to fit all targets. We introduce Doubt-Preserving Quantization (DPQ), a lightweight pre-quantization recipe family that uses full-precision predictions to construct target-aligned calibration mixtures of high-doubt examples and generic anchors. Across 8 language models, 9 NLP benchmarks, and 22 comparison methods, the leading fixed recipe changes with the preservation target: DPQ-r75 leads on SQuAD2 answerability-boundary preservation, while milder or single-signal variants, including DPQ-r50, confidence-only, and entropy-only, better preserve broad multiple-choice QA behavior. These results show that calibration data should be selected for the specific full-precision score behavior a deployment needs to preserve, rather than treated as a fixed quantization detail."},"title":{"value":"Target-Aware Calibration Data Selection for Preserving Uncertainty in Quantized Language Models"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhen Yang","username":""},{"fullname":"Sizai Hou","username":""},{"fullname":"Kaiwen Zheng","username":"~Kaiwen_Zheng6"},{"fullname":"Yaofang Liu","username":""},{"fullname":"Liang He","username":""},{"fullname":"Yixuan Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Kangning Cui","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1794228889062,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2608-21019"],"tcdate":1794228885018,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Kaiwen_Zheng6"],"forum":"spDaFJpB79","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":228669,"cdate":1785542400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1794228889062,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"spDaFJpB79","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Neural Systems and Rehabilitation Engineering"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7333/9250399/09195541.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wei|reducing_calibration_efforts_in_rsvp_tasks_with_multisource_adversarial_domain_adaptation"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TNSRE.2020.3023761"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{Wei_2020,\n\tdoi = {10.1109/tnsre.2020.3023761},\n\turl = {https://doi.org/10.1109%2Ftnsre.2020.3023761},\n\tyear = 2020,\n\tmonth = {nov},\n\tpublisher = {Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers ({IEEE})},\n\tvolume = {28},\n\tnumber = {11},\n\tpages = {2344--2355},\n\tauthor = {Wei Wei and Shuang Qiu and Xuelin Ma and Dan Li and Bo Wang and Huiguang He},\n\ttitle = {Reducing Calibration Efforts in {RSVP} Tasks With Multi-Source Adversarial Domain Adaptation},\n\tjournal = {{IEEE} Transactions on Neural Systems and Rehabilitation Engineering}\n}"},"abstract":{"value":"Rapid Serial Visual Presentation (RSVP)-based Brain-Computer Interface (BCI) is an efficient information detection technology by detecting event-related brain responses evoked by target visual stimuli. However, a time-consuming calibration procedure is needed before a new user can use this system. Thus, it is important to reduce calibration efforts for BCI applications. In this article, we propose a multi-source conditional adversarial domain adaptation with the correlation metric learning (mCADA-C) framework that utilizes data from other subjects to reduce the data requirement from the new subject for training the model. This model utilizes adversarial training to enable a CNN-based feature extraction network to extract common features from different domains. A correlation metric learning (CML) loss is proposed to constrain the correlation of features based on class and domain to maximize the intra-class similarity and minimize inter-class similarity. Also, a multi-source framework with a source selection strategy is adopted to integrate the results of multiple domain adaptation. We constructed an RSVP-based dataset that includes 11 subjects each performing three RSVP experiments on three different days. The experimental results demonstrate that our proposed method can achieve 87.72% cross-subject balanced-accuracy under one block calibration. The results indicate our method can realize a higher performance with less calibration efforts."},"title":{"value":"Reducing Calibration Efforts in RSVP Tasks With Multi-Source Adversarial Domain Adaptation"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Wei Wei"},{"fullname":"Shuang Qiu"},{"fullname":"Xuelin Ma"},{"fullname":"Dan Li"},{"fullname":"Bo Wang"},{"fullname":"Huiguang He","username":"~Huiguang_He1"}]}},"tmdate":1789091553793,"pdate":1604188800000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tnsre.2020.3023761"],"tcdate":1764080391793,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Huiguang_He1"],"forum":"TEVaf4IVyT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":18460,"cdate":1604699037315,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091553793,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"TEVaf4IVyT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.06262v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhao|from_exposure_to_internalization_dualstream_calibration_for_incontext_clinical_reasoning"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.06262"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2604-06262,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Chuang Zhao and Hongke Zhao and Xiaofang Zhou and Xiaomeng Li},\n  title={From Exposure to Internalization: Dual-Stream Calibration for In-context Clinical Reasoning},\n  year={2026},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1775001600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2604.06262},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.06262}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Contextual clinical reasoning demands robust inference grounded in complex, heterogeneous clinical records. While state-of-the-art fine-tuning, in-context learning (ICL), and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) enable knowledge exposure, they often fall short of genuine contextual internalization: dynamically adjusting a model's internal representations to the subtle nuances of individual cases at inference time. To address this, we propose Dual-Stream Calibration (DSC), a test-time training framework that transcends superficial knowledge exposure to achieve deep internalization during inference. DSC facilitates input internalization by synergistically aligning two calibration streams. Unlike passive context exposure, the Semantic Calibration Stream enforces a deliberative reflection on core evidence, internalizing semantic anchors by minimizing entropy to stabilize generative trajectories. Simultaneously, the Structural Calibration Stream assimilates latent inferential dependencies through an iterative meta-learning objective. By training on specialized support sets at test-time, this stream enables the model to bridge the gap between external evidence and internal logic, synthesizing fragmented data into a coherent response. Our approach shifts the reasoning paradigm from passive attention-based matching to an active refinement of the latent inferential space. Validated against thirteen clinical datasets, DSC demonstrates superiority across three distinct task paradigms, consistently outstripping state-of-the-art baselines ranging from training-dependent models to test-time learning frameworks."},"title":{"value":"From Exposure to Internalization: Dual-Stream Calibration for In-context Clinical Reasoning"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Chuang Zhao","username":""},{"fullname":"Hongke Zhao","username":"~Hongke_Zhao1"},{"fullname":"Xiaofang Zhou","username":""},{"fullname":"Xiaomeng Li","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1785845607910,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2604-06262"],"tcdate":1785845603007,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Hongke_Zhao1"],"forum":"zr6O1RZRqJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":114921,"cdate":1775001600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785845607910,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"zr6O1RZRqJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Knowl. Based Syst. 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/KBS/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|ddc_dynamic_distribution_calibration_for_fewshot_learning_under_multiscale_representation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lingxing_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Gu_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fan_Dong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongmei_Jiang:","~Yiqiang_Chen1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2025.113030"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/kbs/ChenGGDJC25,\n  author={Lingxing Chen and Yang Gu and Yi Guo and Fan Dong and Dongmei Jiang and Yiqiang Chen},\n  title={DDC: Dynamic distribution calibration for few-shot learning under multi-scale representation},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={Knowl. Based Syst.},\n  volume={311},\n  pages={113030},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2025.113030}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent advances in few-shot learning (FSL) have attracted significant attention. However, many existing methods focus primarily on optimizing training processes and sample generation, often overlooking the crucial task of accurately modeling the true feature distribution of few-shot classes. In this paper, we propose a novel dynamic distribution calibration method for few-shot learning under multi-scale representation. This method captures multi-dimensional and directional information in the feature space and dynamically learns a similarity matrix between samples, enabling the few-shot class distributions to better approximate the true distribution. The method involves two key steps. First, a multi-scale representation strategy optimizes feature representation across multiple dimensions and directions using a feature description matrix and composite metric, leading to more accurate similarity calculations between base and novel class samples. Second, a dynamic calibration strategy constructs a dynamic weight matrix based on sample similarity, automatically selecting the appropriate number and weight of base class samples for distribution calibration. This ensures that the calibrated distribution closely aligns with the true unbiased distribution. On benchmarks miniImageNet, CUB and CIFAR-FS, our method outperformed state-of-the-art methods in both the 5-way-1-shot and 5-way-5-shot settings, demonstrating its effectiveness in few-shot distribution calibration."},"title":{"value":"DDC: Dynamic distribution calibration for few-shot learning under multi-scale representation"},"authors":{"value":["Lingxing Chen","Yang Gu","Yi Guo","Fan Dong","Dongmei Jiang","Yiqiang Chen"]}},"tmdate":1753157567137,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1753157512325,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yiqiang_Chen1"],"forum":"vpzcDQEy0E","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":580615,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753157567137,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"vpzcDQEy0E","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP (Findings) 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.findings-emnlp.180.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"dan|on_the_effects_of_transformer_size_on_in_and_outofdomain_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Soham_Dan1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dan_Roth:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2021.findings-emnlp.180"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/DanR21,\n  author={Soham Dan and Dan Roth},\n  title={On the Effects of Transformer Size on In- and Out-of-Domain Calibration},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={2096-2101},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2021.findings-emnlp.180},\n  booktitle={EMNLP (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2021f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large, pre-trained transformer language models, which are pervasive in natural language processing tasks, are notoriously expensive to train. To reduce the cost of training such large models, prior work has developed smaller, more compact models which achieves a significant speedup in training time while maintaining competitive accuracy to the original model on downstream tasks. Though these smaller pre-trained models have been widely adopted by the community, it is not known how well are they calibrated compared to their larger counterparts. In this paper, focusing on a wide range of tasks, we thoroughly investigate the calibration properties of pre-trained transformers, as a function of their size. We demonstrate that when evaluated in-domain, smaller models are able to achieve competitive, and often better, calibration compared to larger models, while achieving significant speedup in training time. Post-hoc calibration techniques further reduce calibration error for all models in-domain. However, when evaluated out-of-domain, larger models tend to be better calibrated, and label-smoothing instead is an effective strategy to calibrate models in this setting."},"title":{"value":"On the Effects of Transformer Size on In- and Out-of-Domain Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Soham Dan","Dan Roth"]}},"tmdate":1743122802202,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1743122792337,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Soham_Dan1"],"forum":"gWo6ssJV87","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":373724,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1743122802202,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"gWo6ssJV87","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MM2024 Poster"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera calibration consists of determining the intrinsic and extrinsic parameters of an imaging system, which forms the fundamental basis for various computer vision tasks and applications, e.g., robotics and autonomous driving (AD). However, prevailing camera calibration models pose a time-consuming and labor-intensive off-board process particularly in mass production settings, while simultaneously lacking exploration of real-world autonomous driving scenarios. To this end, in this paper, inspired by recent advancements in bird's-eye-view (BEV) perception models, we proposes a novel automatic multi-camera Calibration method via Reversed BEV representations for autonomous driving, termed CalibRBEV. Specifically, the proposed CalibRBEV model primarily comprises two stages. Initially, we innovatively reverse the BEV perception pipeline, reconstructing bounding boxes through an attention auto-encoder module to fully extract the latent reversed BEV representations. Subsequently, the obtained representations from encoder are interacted with the surrounding multi-view image features for further refinement and calibration parameters prediction. Extensive experimental results on nuScenes and Waymo datasets validate the effectiveness of our proposed model."},"relevance_to_conference":{"value":"Autonomous driving applications represent a crucial domain for multimedia information processing. Within autonomous driving systems, various sensors such as cameras, LiDAR, and radar are employed to perceive the surrounding environment of the vehicle, converting the perceived information into digital signals for processing. Among these, precise environmental perception (e.g. BEV perception) is of paramount importance for the safety and performance of autonomous driving systems. Camera calibration, therefore, stands as a pivotal task to ensure that the multi-view multimedia images captured by the cameras can be accurately interpreted and understood, thereby providing a reliable foundation for the system to make correct decisions and actions. (i) This work introduces the concept of Bird's Eye View (BEV) perception model into the calibration, achieving camera calibration tasks through a reversed BEV pipeline in autonomous driving, which presents an innovative and inspiring approach. (ii) This work proposes an autoencoder-based reconstruction method for bounding box data to achieve high-quality reversed BEV feature extraction, addressing the limitations of current forward BEV perception models in autonomous driving."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nliao2024calibrbev,\ntitle={Calib{RBEV}: Multi-Camera Calibration via Reversed Bird's-eye-view Representations for Autonomous Driving},\nauthor={Wenlong Liao and Sunyuan Qiang and Xianfei Li and Xiaolei Chen and Haoyu Wang and Yanyan Liang and Junchi Yan and Tao He and Pai Peng},\nbooktitle={ACM Multimedia 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=4AtVBoEUgi}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CalibRBEV: Multi-Camera Calibration via Reversed Bird's-eye-view Representations for Autonomous Driving"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a6452b5bcd03667a7fa23e72676956cfb30a5038.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"acmmm.org/ACMMM/2024/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"liao|calibrbev_multicamera_calibration_via_reversed_birdseyeview_representations_for_autonomous_driving"},"primary_subject_area":{"value":"[Content] Media Interpretation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Wenlong_Liao1","~Sunyuan_Qiang1","~Xianfei_Li1","~Xiaolei_Chen1","~Haoyu_Wang25","~Yanyan_Liang1","~Junchi_Yan2","~Tao_He6","~Pai_Peng2"]},"authors":{"value":["Wenlong Liao","Sunyuan Qiang","Xianfei Li","Xiaolei Chen","Haoyu Wang","Yanyan Liang","Junchi Yan","Tao He","Pai Peng"]}},"tmdate":1721528141408,"pdate":1721513483555,"tcdate":1711505417594,"writers":["acmmm.org/ACMMM/2024/Conference","acmmm.org/ACMMM/2024/Conference/Submission1076/Authors"],"signatures":["acmmm.org/ACMMM/2024/Conference/Submission1076/Authors"],"forum":"4AtVBoEUgi","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1076,"cdate":1711505417594,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["acmmm.org/ACMMM/2024/Conference/-/Submission","acmmm.org/ACMMM/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","acmmm.org/ACMMM/2024/Conference/Submission1076/-/Revision","acmmm.org/ACMMM/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1721528141408,"odate":1721513483555,"domain":"acmmm.org/ACMMM/2024/Conference","id":"4AtVBoEUgi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6287639/8948470/09086627.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|efficient_qr_code_secret_embedding_mechanism_based_on_hamming_code"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2020.2992694"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{Huang_2020,\n\tdoi = {10.1109/access.2020.2992694},\n\turl = {https://doi.org/10.1109%2Faccess.2020.2992694},\n\tyear = 2020,\n\tpublisher = {Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers ({IEEE})},\n\tvolume = {8},\n\tpages = {86706--86714},\n\tauthor = {Peng-Cheng Huang and Chin-Chen Chang and Yung-Hui Li and Yanjun Liu},\n\ttitle = {Efficient {QR} Code Secret Embedding Mechanism Based on Hamming Code},\n\tjournal = {{IEEE} Access}\n}"},"abstract":{"value":"QR code is designed as machine readable symbol, which is widely used in various fields of life due to its large message capacity and fast decoding speed. However, as a public standard, it will give rise to the security issue when delivering sensitive information with QR code. To overcome this weakness, this paper explores the characteristic of QR code to propose an efficient secret hiding mechanism to protect the sensitive information within QR code. The secret message would be embedded into cover QR code based on (8, 4) Hamming code. The error correction capacity (ECC) of QR code would correct the errors produced in the secret embedding procedure, and the valid marked QR code would reduce people’s curious. Compared to the state-of-art works, the proposed scheme achieves a better performance on the aspects of secret payload and embedding efficiency."},"title":{"value":"Efficient QR Code Secret Embedding Mechanism Based on Hamming Code"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Peng-Cheng Huang"},{"fullname":"Chin-Chen Chang"},{"fullname":"Yung-Hui Li","username":"~Yung-Hui_Li3"},{"fullname":"Yanjun Liu"}]}},"tmdate":1789091684655,"pdate":1577836800000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/access.2020.2992694"],"tcdate":1762438359054,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yung-hui_Li1"],"forum":"R0wkFegLXu","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":8623,"cdate":1589839536846,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091684655,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"R0wkFegLXu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Graz Brain-Computer Interface Conference"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"jong|transferring_bci_models_from_calibration_to_control_observing_shifts_in_eeg_features"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ivo_Pascal_de_Jong1","luke@lukeluna.nl","~Matias_Valdenegro-Toro1","~Andreea_Ioana_Sburlea1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.15431"},"abstract":{"value":"Public Motor Imagery-based brain-computer interface (BCI) datasets are being used to develop increasingly good classifiers. However, they usually follow discrete paradigms where participants perform Motor Imagery at regularly timed intervals. It is often unclear what changes may happen in the EEG patterns when users attempt to perform a control task with such a BCI. This may lead to generalisation errors. We demonstrate a new paradigm containing a standard calibration session and a novel BCI control session based on EMG. This allows us to observe similarities in sensorimotor rhythms, and observe the additional preparation effects introduced by the control paradigm. In the Movement Related Cortical Potentials we found large differences between the calibration and control sessions. We demonstrate a CSP-based Machine Learning model trained on the calibration data that can make surprisingly good predictions on the BCI-controlled driving data."},"title":{"value":"Transferring BCI models from calibration to control: Observing shifts in EEG features"},"authors":{"value":["Ivo Pascal de Jong","Lüke Luna van den Wittenboer","Matias Valdenegro-Toro","Andreea Ioana Sburlea"]}},"tmdate":1769420262620,"pdate":1725141600000,"tcdate":1769420192371,"writers":["~Ivo_Pascal_de_Jong1","luke@lukeluna.nl","~Matias_Valdenegro-Toro1","~Andreea_Ioana_Sburlea1"],"signatures":["~Ivo_Pascal_de_Jong1"],"forum":"pqAvZ4o8le","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":43092,"cdate":1769420192371,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1769420262620,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"pqAvZ4o8le","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.15431v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"jong|transferring_bci_models_from_calibration_to_control_observing_shifts_in_eeg_features"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ivo_Pascal_de_Jong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lüke_Luna_van_den_Wittenboer:","~Matias_Valdenegro-Toro1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andreea_Ioana_Sburlea:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.15431"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2403-15431,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ivo Pascal de Jong and Lüke Luna van den Wittenboer and Matias Valdenegro-Toro and Andreea Ioana Sburlea},\n  title={Transferring BCI models from calibration to control: Observing shifts in EEG features},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2403.15431},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.15431}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Public Motor Imagery-based brain-computer interface (BCI) datasets are being used to develop increasingly good classifiers. However, they usually follow discrete paradigms where participants perform Motor Imagery at regularly timed intervals. It is often unclear what changes may happen in the EEG patterns when users attempt to perform a control task with such a BCI. This may lead to generalisation errors. We demonstrate a new paradigm containing a standard calibration session and a novel BCI control session based on EMG. This allows us to observe similarities in sensorimotor rhythms, and observe the additional preparation effects introduced by the control paradigm. In the Movement Related Cortical Potentials we found large differences between the calibration and control sessions. We demonstrate a CSP-based Machine Learning model trained on the calibration data that can make surprisingly good predictions on the BCI-controlled driving data."},"title":{"value":"Transferring BCI models from calibration to control: Observing shifts in EEG features"},"authors":{"value":["Ivo Pascal de Jong","Lüke Luna van den Wittenboer","Matias Valdenegro-Toro","Andreea Ioana Sburlea"]}},"tmdate":1716500436398,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1716500426311,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Matias_Alejandro_Valdenegro-Toro1"],"forum":"aDr5xtVUL8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":20900,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1716500436398,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"aDr5xtVUL8","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce several calibration methods to prompt learning of multilingual encoders and improve the zero-/few-shot performance."},"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2023 Findings"},"Submission_Track_2":{"value":"Efficient Methods for NLP"},"keywords":{"value":["prompt learning","multilingual encoders","calibration"]},"abstract":{"value":"Pretrained multilingual encoder models can directly perform zero-shot multilingual tasks or linguistic probing by reformulating the input examples into cloze-style prompts.\n This is accomplished by predicting the probabilities of the label words at the masked token position, without requiring any updates to the model parameters.\n However, the performance of this method is limited by the model's bias toward predicting label words which frequently occurred during the pretraining. \n These words typically receive high probabilities. \n To address this issue, we combine the models with calibration techniques which modify the probabilities of label words predicted by the models.\nWe first validate the effectiveness of a proposed simple calibration method together with other existing techniques on monolingual encoders in both zero- and few-shot scenarios.\nWe subsequently employ these calibration techniques on multilingual encoders, resulting in substantial performance improvements across a wide range of tasks."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nnie2023unleashing,\ntitle={Unleashing the Multilingual Encoder Potential: Boosting Zero-Shot Performance via Probability Calibration},\nauthor={Ercong Nie and Helmut Schmid and Hinrich Schuetze},\nbooktitle={The 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=0GO8Dtl8lJ}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Unleashing the Multilingual Encoder Potential: Boosting Zero-Shot Performance via Probability Calibration"},"Submission_Type":{"value":"Regular Short Paper"},"pdf":{"value":"/attachment/381c306feeb97dcdb52085e1dbbc406038be2b9f.pdf"},"Submission_Track":{"value":"Multilinguality and Linguistic Diversity"},"venueid":{"value":"EMNLP/2023/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"nie|unleashing_the_multilingual_encoder_potential_boosting_zeroshot_performance_via_probability_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ercong_Nie1","~Helmut_Schmid1","~Hinrich_Schuetze3"]},"authors":{"value":["Ercong Nie","Helmut Schmid","Hinrich Schuetze"]}},"tmdate":1701459763836,"pdate":1696709282029,"tcdate":1686940383702,"writers":["EMNLP/2023/Conference","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission4118/Authors"],"signatures":["EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission4118/Authors"],"forum":"0GO8Dtl8lJ","number":4118,"cdate":1686940383702,"mdate":1701459763836,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Submission","EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Post_Submission","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission4118/-/Revision","EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Edit","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission4118/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"odate":1701459763822,"domain":"EMNLP/2023/Conference","id":"0GO8Dtl8lJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Inf. Media Technol. 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/imt/6/1/6_1_91/_pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IMT/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"nobuhara|objectoriented_color_calibration_of_multiviewpoint_cameras_in_sparse_and_convergent_arrangement"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shohei_Nobuhara1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuta_Kimura:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Takashi_Matsuyama:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.11185/imt.6.91"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/imt/NobuharaKM11,\n  author={Shohei Nobuhara and Yuta Kimura and Takashi Matsuyama},\n  title={Object-Oriented Color Calibration of Multi-viewpoint Cameras in Sparse and Convergent Arrangement},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  journal={Inf. Media Technol.},\n  volume={6},\n  number={1},\n  pages={91-103},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.11185/imt.6.91}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes a new color calibration method for multi-viewpoint images captured by sparsely and convergently arranged cameras. The main contribution of our method is its practical and efficient procedure while traditional methods are known to be labor-intensive. Because our method automatically extracts 3D points in the scene for color calibration, we do not need to capture color calibration objects like Macbeth chart. This enables us to calibrate a set of multi-viewpoint images whose capture environment is not available. Experiments with real images show that our method can minimize the difference of pixel values (1) quantitatively by leave-one-out evaluation, and (2) qualitatively by rendering a 3D video."},"title":{"value":"Object-Oriented Color Calibration of Multi-viewpoint Cameras in Sparse and Convergent Arrangement"},"authors":{"value":["Shohei Nobuhara","Yuta Kimura","Takashi Matsuyama"]}},"tmdate":1766185681192,"pdate":1293840000000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/imt/NobuharaKM11"],"tcdate":1766185664820,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shohei_Nobuhara2"],"forum":"FZsSNVkgbv","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":710739,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1766185681192,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"FZsSNVkgbv","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph. 2009"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/2945/4675189/04492774.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TVCG/2009"},"paperhash":{"value":"harders|calibration_registration_and_synchronization_for_high_precision_augmented_reality_haptics"},"authorids":{"value":["~Matthias_Harders1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gérald_Bianchi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Benjamin_Knoerlein:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gábor_Székely:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2008.63"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tvcg/HardersBKS09,\n  author={Matthias Harders and Gérald Bianchi and Benjamin Knoerlein and Gábor Székely},\n  title={Calibration, Registration, and Synchronization for High Precision Augmented Reality Haptics},\n  year={2009},\n  cdate={1230768000000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph.},\n  volume={15},\n  number={1},\n  pages={138-149},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2008.63}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In our current research we examine the application of visuo-haptic augmented reality setups in medical training. To this end, highly accurate calibration, system stability, and low latency are indispensable prerequisites. These are necessary to maintain user immersion and avoid breaks in presence which potentially diminish the training outcome. In this paper we describe the developed calibration methods for visuo-haptic integration, the hybrid tracking technique for stable alignment of the augmentation, and the distributed framework ensuring low latency and component synchronization. Finally, we outline an early prototype system based on the multimodal augmented reality framework. The latter allows colocated visuo-haptic interaction with real and virtual scene components in a simplified open surgery setting."},"title":{"value":"Calibration, Registration, and Synchronization for High Precision Augmented Reality Haptics"},"authors":{"value":["Matthias Harders","Gérald Bianchi","Benjamin Knoerlein","Gábor Székely"]}},"tmdate":1762417711436,"pdate":1230768000000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tvcg/HardersBKS09"],"tcdate":1762417668698,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Matthias_Harders1"],"forum":"1zv0CLH9e4","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":664873,"cdate":1230768000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762417711436,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"1zv0CLH9e4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"LoResLM"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/960df4458e2e1caebc1b1cf795a1b11c5d444886.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"yadav|competence_collapse_in_codemixed_generation_spectral_evidence_and_mechanistic_recovery_via_crosslingual_activation_steering"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tanushree_Ravindra_Pratap_Yadav1"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.loreslm-1.3/"},"abstract":{"value":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) approach\nhuman-level reasoning in English, their performance in low-resource, code-mixed languages\nremains surprisingly brittle. We identify Competence Collapse, a distinct pathology where\nmodels capable of complex reasoning in English exhibit severe utility degradation when\nprompted in Hinglish (Hindi-English). We\nquantify this as a Service Gap, observing a\nstatistically significant decline in instructional\nquality (∆D ≈ −11.3%, p < 0.001) across\n9 diverse architectures. Spectral analysis suggests that this stems from a representational\ndivergence between the model’s High-Utility\nDirection and its Generation Subspace. To\nbridge this gap, we propose Cross-Lingual\nActivation Steering (CLAS), an inferencetime intervention that injects a \"Competence\nGap Vector\" into the residual stream. Evaluated across 6 open-weight models (using a\nlightweight calibration set, N = 50), CLAS\nrecovered utility by ∆D = +2.22 (d = 0.60)\nwhile preserving code-mixed fidelity (CMI ≈\n0.4) and reinforcing safety protocols"},"title":{"value":"Competence Collapse in Code-Mixed Generation: Spectral Evidence and Mechanistic Recovery via Cross-Lingual Activation Steering"},"authors":{"value":["Tanushree Ravindra Pratap Yadav"]}},"tmdate":1779983668037,"pdate":1774722600000,"tcdate":1779983668037,"writers":["~Tanushree_Ravindra_Pratap_Yadav1"],"signatures":["~Tanushree_Ravindra_Pratap_Yadav1"],"forum":"M9oGVSIhNd","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":50851,"cdate":1779983668037,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1779983668037,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"M9oGVSIhNd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.acl-long.45.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|selfedit_faultaware_code_editor_for_code_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kechi_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhuo_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jia_Li_0011:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ge_Li_0001:","~Zhi_Jin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.acl-long.45"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/ZhangLLLJ23,\n  author={Kechi Zhang and Zhuo Li and Jia Li and Ge Li and Zhi Jin},\n  title={Self-Edit: Fault-Aware Code Editor for Code Generation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={769-787},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.acl-long.45},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2023-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated an impressive ability to generate codes on competitive programming tasks. However, with limited sample numbers, LLMs still suffer from poor accuracy. Inspired by the process of human programming, we propose a generate-and-edit approach named Self-Edit that utilizes execution results of the generated code from LLMs to improve the code quality on the competitive programming task. We execute the generated code on the example test case provided in the question and wrap execution results into a supplementary comment. Utilizing this comment as guidance, our fault-aware code editor is employed to correct errors in the generated code. We perform extensive evaluations across two competitive programming datasets with nine different LLMs. Compared to directly generating from LLMs, our approach can improve the average of pass@1 by 89% on APPS-dev, 31% on APPS-test, and 48% on HumanEval over nine popular code generation LLMs with parameter sizes ranging from 110M to 175B. Compared to other post-processing methods, our method demonstrates superior accuracy and efficiency."},"title":{"value":"Self-Edit: Fault-Aware Code Editor for Code Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Kechi Zhang","Zhuo Li","Jia Li","Ge Li","Zhi Jin"]}},"tmdate":1747722870447,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1738825549425,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhi_Jin1"],"forum":"zhfXqCKeOi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":303575,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747722870447,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zhfXqCKeOi","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a null-space calibration module that separately adjusts target-associated and residual components of auxiliary behavior representations for multi-behavior recommendation."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Multi-behavior Recommendation","Null-space Projection","Representation Calibration","Cross-behavior Knowledge Transfer"]},"primary_area":{"value":"unsupervised, self-supervised, semi-supervised, and supervised representation learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-behavior recommendation learns user and item representations from target and auxiliary interactions to improve target prediction. Auxiliary representations may encode preferences not fully reflected in target interactions, but association strength alone does not determine the ranking value of this evidence. A key challenge is to retain target-associated information while learning to use potentially complementary evidence. To this end, we propose Target-referenced Null-space Calibration (TNC), a plug-in module that calibrates auxiliary representations through null-space constraints. Using cross-covariance between paired auxiliary and target representations, TNC constructs a target-associated subspace through truncated singular value decomposition. It retains both the target-associated and null-space components and learns separate calibration gains under target ranking supervision. For a fixed representation, reference, and target-associated gain, adjusting the null-space gain calibrates potentially complementary evidence without changing the prescribed target-associated projection. The module operates between behavior encoding and fusion and is jointly trained with compatible encoders, allowing target supervision to guide both representation learning and calibration. Experiments on three real-world datasets show that integrating TNC improves ranking performance across multiple multi-behavior recommendation backbones. To facilitate reproducibility, our implementation is available in an anonymous repository at this url."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026nullspace,\ntitle={Null-Space Calibration of Auxiliary Evidence for Multi-Behavior Recommendation},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=UvVcH7gP7m},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Null-Space Calibration of Auxiliary Evidence for Multi-Behavior Recommendation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fb2377aec7a1e33bf3f97cb1a86337f190dffcf3.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791235084065,"tcdate":1789800219555,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55896/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55896/Authors"],"forum":"UvVcH7gP7m","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":55896,"cdate":1789800219555,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55896/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791235084065,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"UvVcH7gP7m","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"A paper that propose adaptive sample weight to address gradient conflict problem of diffusion quantization"},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Quantization","Diffusion"]},"primary_area":{"value":"generative models"},"abstract":{"value":"Diffusion models have shown remarkable performance in image synthesis by progressively estimating a smooth transition from a Gaussian distribution of noise to a real image. Unfortunately, their practical deployment is limited by slow inference speed, high memory usage, and the computational demands of the noise estimation process. Post-training quantization (PTQ) emerges as a promising solution to accelerate sampling and reduce the memory overhead of diffusion models.  Existing PTQ methods for diffusion models typically apply uniform weights to calibration samples across timesteps, which is sub-optimal since data at different timesteps may contribute differently to the diffusion process. Additionally, due to varying activation distributions and gradients across timesteps, a uniform quantization approach is sub-optimal. Each timestep requires a different gradient direction for optimal quantization, and treating them equally can lead to conflicting gradients that degrade performance. In this paper, we propose a novel PTQ method that addresses these challenges by assigning appropriate weights to calibration samples. \n    Specifically, our approach learns to assign optimal weights to calibration samples to align the quantized model’s gradients across timesteps, facilitating the quantization process.  Extensive experiments on CIFAR-10, LSUN-Bedrooms, and ImageNet datasets demonstrate the superiority of our method compared to other PTQ methods for diffusion models."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhoang2026gradientaligned,\ntitle={Gradient-Aligned Calibration for Post-Training Quantization of Diffusion Models},\nauthor={Dung Anh Hoang and Cuong Pham and Trung Le and Jianfei Cai and Thanh-Toan Do},\nbooktitle={The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=CtFSOlrjth}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Gradient-Aligned Calibration for Post-Training Quantization of Diffusion Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8762b63901faaf00fb4b47dd1e104749091a9559.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"hoang|gradientaligned_calibration_for_posttraining_quantization_of_diffusion_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Dung_Anh_Hoang1","~Cuong_Pham3","~Trung_Le2","~Jianfei_Cai1","~Thanh-Toan_Do4"]},"authors":{"value":["Dung Anh Hoang","Cuong Pham","Trung Le","Jianfei Cai","Thanh-Toan Do"]}},"tmdate":1775877256370,"pdate":1769436359715,"tcdate":1758279321992,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission17689/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission17689/Authors"],"forum":"CtFSOlrjth","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":17689,"cdate":1758279321992,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission17689/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission17689/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission17689/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1775877256370,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"CtFSOlrjth","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Medical Imaging 2018"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/42/8241789/08017620.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TMI/2018"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|learning_jointsparse_codes_for_calibrationfree_parallel_mr_imaging"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","~Qiegen_Liu3","","","","","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TMI.2017.2746086"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tmi/WangTGLYXLLZL18,\n  author={Shanshan Wang and Sha Tan and Yuan Gao and Qiegen Liu and Leslie Ying and Taohui Xiao and Yuanyuan Liu and Xin Liu and Hairong Zheng and Dong Liang},\n  title={Learning Joint-Sparse Codes for Calibration-Free Parallel MR Imaging},\n  year={2018},\n  cdate={1514764800000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Medical Imaging},\n  volume={37},\n  number={1},\n  pages={251-261},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TMI.2017.2746086}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The integration of compressed sensing and parallel imaging (CS-PI) has shown an increased popularity in recent years to accelerate magnetic resonance (MR) imaging. Among them, calibration-free techniques have presented encouraging performances due to its capability in robustly handling the sensitivity information. Unfortunately, existing calibration-free methods have only explored joint-sparsity with direct analysis transform projections. To further exploit joint-sparsity and improve reconstruction accuracy, this paper proposes to Learn joINt-sparse coDes for caliBration-free parallEl mR imaGing (LINDBERG) by modeling the parallel MR imaging problem as an ℓ2-ℓF-ℓ2,1 minimization objective with an ℓ2 norm constraining data fidelity, Frobenius norm enforcing sparse representation error and the ℓ2,1 mixed norm triggering joint sparsity across multichannels. A corresponding algorithm has been developed to alternatively update the sparse representation, sensitivity encoded images and K-space data. Then, the final image is produced as the square root of sum of squares of all channel images. Experimental results on both physical phantom and in vivo data sets show that the proposed method is comparable and even superior to stateof-the-art CS-PI reconstruction approaches. Specifically, LINDBERG has presented strong capability in suppressing noise and artifacts while reconstructing MR images from highly undersampled multichannel measurements."},"title":{"value":"Learning Joint-Sparse Codes for Calibration-Free Parallel MR Imaging"},"authors":{"value":["Shanshan Wang","Sha Tan","Yuan Gao","Qiegen Liu","Leslie Ying","Taohui Xiao","Yuanyuan Liu","Xin Liu","Hairong Zheng","Dong Liang"]}},"tmdate":1772386741885,"pdate":1546214400000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tmi/WangTGLYXLLZL18"],"tcdate":1772386713503,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Qiegen_Liu3"],"forum":"SLHRM4scC0","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":841634,"cdate":1514764800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772386741885,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SLHRM4scC0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"3DPVT 2006"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/4155696/4155697/04155728.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/3DPVT/2006"},"paperhash":{"value":"furukawa|selfcalibration_of_multiple_laser_planes_for_3d_scene_reconstruction"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ryo_Furukawa1","~Hiroshi_Kawasaki1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/3DPVT.2006.125"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/3dpvt/FurukawaK06,\n  author={Ryo Furukawa and Hiroshi Kawasaki},\n  title={Self-Calibration of Multiple Laser Planes for 3D Scene Reconstruction},\n  year={2006},\n  cdate={1136073600000},\n  pages={200-207},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/3DPVT.2006.125},\n  booktitle={3DPVT},\n  crossref={conf/3dpvt/2006}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Self-calibration is one of the most active issues concerning vision-based 3D measurements. However, in the case of the light sectioning method, there has been little research conducted on self-calibration techniques. In this paper, we study the problem of self-calibration for an active vision system which uses line lasers and a single camera. The problem can be defined as the estimation of multiple laser planes from the curves of laser reflections observed from a sequence of images captured by a single camera. The constraints of the problem can be obtained from observed intersection points between the curves. In this condition, the problem is formulated as simultaneous polynomial equations, in which the number of equations is larger than the number of variables. Approximated solutions of the equations can be computed by using Grobner bases. By refining them using nonlinear optimization, the final result can be obtained. We developed an actual 3D measurement system using the proposed method, which consists of only a laser projector with two line lasers and a single camera. Users are just required to move the projector freely so that the projected lines sweep across the surface of the scene to get the 3D shape."},"title":{"value":"Self-Calibration of Multiple Laser Planes for 3D Scene Reconstruction"},"authors":{"value":["Ryo Furukawa","Hiroshi Kawasaki"]}},"tmdate":1730110384662,"pdate":1136073600000,"tcdate":1727683013615,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ryo_Furukawa1"],"forum":"2FvhvAbDhm","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":109371,"cdate":1136073600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1730110384662,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2FvhvAbDhm","version":2},{"content":{"submission_guidelines_blind":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"TheWebConf24 Oral"},"student_author":{"value":"No"},"keywords":{"value":["Similarity Search","Incomplete Query","Positive Semi-definiteness","Similarity Matrix","Matrix Calibration"]},"abstract":{"value":"The similarity matrix is at the core of similarity search problems. However, incomplete observations are ubiquitous in real scenarios making the similarity matrix less accurate. To estimate a high-quality similarity matrix, one popular trend is to impute the missing values into the vectors directly, which provides a simple and highly efficient way to recover the similarity matrix. However, these methods lack of theoretical guarantee due to ignoring the entire similarity matrix property directly. In this paper, based on the key insight that the similarity matrix is symmetric and enjoys the positive semi-definiteness (PSD) property, we proposed a novel similarity matrix calibration method, which is scalable, adaptive, and sound. Specifically, we first show the similarity matrix provably holds the PSD property as the constraint. Then, we proposed a parallel matrix calibration method to estimate the similarity matrix to approximate the unknown fully observed ground-truth similarity matrix. Further, we discover its factored form which bypasses the computation of singular values and allows fast optimization by general optimization algorithm. Stable recovery and convergence are guaranteed. Extensive similarity matrix calibration experiments on the real-world dataset demonstrated that the proposed method obtains superior performance while being the fastest in comparison to baseline methods."},"submission_guidelines_limit":{"value":"Yes"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nma2024a,\ntitle={A Matrix Calibration Method for Similarity Matrix with Incomplete Query},\nauthor={Changyi Ma and Runsheng Yu and Youzhi Zhang},\nbooktitle={The Web Conference 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=5ezQQKTjUN}\n}"},"title":{"value":"A Matrix Calibration Method for Similarity Matrix with Incomplete Query"},"submission_guidelines_format":{"value":"Yes"},"submission_guidelines_scope":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7c9c402c4cdfd2f6fb2c9e18d6a8c43e1c1c698a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ACM.org/TheWebConf/2024/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"ma|a_matrix_calibration_method_for_similarity_matrix_with_incomplete_query"},"authorids":{"value":["~Changyi_Ma1","~Runsheng_Yu2","~Youzhi_Zhang2"]},"submission_guidelines_authorship":{"value":"Yes"},"track":{"value":"Search"},"authors":{"value":["Changyi Ma","Runsheng Yu","Youzhi Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1716428655041,"pdate":1705992205370,"tcdate":1696396687728,"writers":["ACM.org/TheWebConf/2024/Conference","ACM.org/TheWebConf/2024/Conference/Submission791/Authors"],"signatures":["ACM.org/TheWebConf/2024/Conference/Submission791/Authors"],"forum":"5ezQQKTjUN","number":791,"cdate":1696396687728,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ACM.org/TheWebConf/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ACM.org/TheWebConf/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ACM.org/TheWebConf/2024/Conference/Submission791/-/Revision","ACM.org/TheWebConf/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1716428655041,"odate":1716428655030,"domain":"ACM.org/TheWebConf/2024/Conference","id":"5ezQQKTjUN","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We show that using matching based on qunatile distribution provides a faster and more robust way of calibrating LLMaJ scores than using point-to-point matching."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["llm as a judge","evaluation","post-policy calibration"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/3a12837988629b4e52ba7d9ec961bd84ba450dbc.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"LLM-as-a-judge scores are often distributionally shifted from human ratings on any given evaluation task, and post-hoc calibration (mapping judge scores to human scores in a small labeled anchor set) has become a popular method to correct for this shift. We present streaming quantile matching (SQM), a method previously unexplored for LLM-as-a-judge calibration that wins outright over every other method by directly computing the optimal-transport map between the judge and human score distributions. We develop a novel framework for evaluating post-hoc calibration performance and within it we show that unlike point-wise calibrations, SQM respects the ordinality of the human anchor set and offers superior performance regardless of model or data budget. We also show that in general, post-hoc calibration narrows the raw error gap between smaller and larger judge models, which opens the possibility of more efficient LLM judges."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026quantiles,\ntitle={Quantiles, Not Points: Streaming Quantile Matching for Ordinal-Aware {LLM}-as-a-Judge Calibration},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ksA5SnB8s9},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Quantiles, Not Points: Streaming Quantile Matching for Ordinal-Aware LLM-as-a-Judge Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/58589b1698fb6d7950ba5548d76c460fbbebcf2e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791231776350,"tcdate":1789673283693,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission34110/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission34110/Authors"],"forum":"ksA5SnB8s9","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":34110,"cdate":1789673283693,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission34110/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791231776350,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"ksA5SnB8s9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.05519v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"ji|physical_color_calibration_of_digital_pathology_scanners_for_robust_artificial_intelligence_assisted_cancer_diagnosis"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoyi_Ji:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Richard_Salmon:","~Nita_Mulliqi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Umair_Khan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yinxi_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Anders_Blilie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Henrik_Olsson:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bodil_Ginnerup_Pedersen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Karina_Dalsgaard_Sørensen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Benedicte_Parm_Ulhøi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Svein_Reidar_Kjosavik:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Emilius_A._M._Janssen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mattias_Rantalainen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lars_Egevad:","~Pekka_Ruusuvuori1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Martin_Eklund:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kimmo_Kartasalo:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.05519"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2307-05519,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Xiaoyi Ji and Richard Salmon and Nita Mulliqi and Umair Khan and Yinxi Wang and Anders Blilie and Henrik Olsson and Bodil Ginnerup Pedersen and Karina Dalsgaard Sørensen and Benedicte Parm Ulhøi and Svein Reidar Kjosavik and Emilius A. M. Janssen and Mattias Rantalainen and Lars Egevad and Pekka Ruusuvuori and Martin Eklund and Kimmo Kartasalo},\n  title={Physical Color Calibration of Digital Pathology Scanners for Robust Artificial Intelligence Assisted Cancer Diagnosis},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2307.05519},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.05519}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The potential of artificial intelligence (AI) in digital pathology is limited by technical inconsistencies in the production of whole slide images (WSIs), leading to degraded AI performance and posing a challenge for widespread clinical application as fine-tuning algorithms for each new site is impractical. Changes in the imaging workflow can also lead to compromised diagnoses and patient safety risks. We evaluated whether physical color calibration of scanners can standardize WSI appearance and enable robust AI performance. We employed a color calibration slide in four different laboratories and evaluated its impact on the performance of an AI system for prostate cancer diagnosis on 1,161 WSIs. Color standardization resulted in consistently improved AI model calibration and significant improvements in Gleason grading performance. The study demonstrates that physical color calibration provides a potential solution to the variation introduced by different scanners, making AI-based cancer diagnostics more reliable and applicable in clinical settings."},"title":{"value":"Physical Color Calibration of Digital Pathology Scanners for Robust Artificial Intelligence Assisted Cancer Diagnosis"},"authors":{"value":["Xiaoyi Ji","Richard Salmon","Nita Mulliqi","Umair Khan","Yinxi Wang","Anders Blilie","Henrik Olsson","Bodil Ginnerup Pedersen","Karina Dalsgaard Sørensen","Benedicte Parm Ulhøi","Svein Reidar Kjosavik","Emilius A. M. Janssen","Mattias Rantalainen","Lars Egevad","Pekka Ruusuvuori","Martin Eklund","Kimmo Kartasalo"]}},"tmdate":1789558272035,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1748871539374,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Pekka_Ruusuvuori1"],"forum":"HxFdvW1Q2d","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":554667,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1789558272035,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"HxFdvW1Q2d","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IGARSS 2005"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/10226/32595/01526255.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IGARSS/2005"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|crosscalibration_of_eo1_modis_to_sz3_cmodis_using_dunhuang_test_site"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuan_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhifeng_Guo:","~Lianru_Gao1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS.2005.1526255"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/igarss/LiGG05,\n  author={Xuan Li and Zhifeng Guo and Lianru Gao},\n  title={Cross-calibration of EO-1 MODIS to SZ-3 CMODIS using Dunhuang test site},\n  year={2005},\n  cdate={1104537600000},\n  pages={3},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS.2005.1526255},\n  booktitle={IGARSS},\n  crossref={conf/igarss/2005}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents a methodology for radiometric cross-calibration of the solar reflective spectral bands of Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) and Chinese Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (CMODIS) sensors based on analysis of two difference time image pair for Dunhuang test site on July 27, 2002. With the well-calibrated MODIS as a reference, we derive top-of-atmosphere(TOA) reflectance using MODIS data and then use these TOA reflectance to compute TOA radiance for CMODIS taking into account the effect of spectral band different and the changes in solar zenith angle due to any temporal differences in the overpass times as well as differences in the view angles between the sensors. This TOA radiance, which is correlated with the sensor digital number (DN) output, determines the in-flight calibration coefficients of CMODIS. The relatively error between the cross-calibration and the reflectance-based method calibration results is within 9%."},"title":{"value":"Cross-calibration of EO-1 MODIS to SZ-3 CMODIS using Dunhuang test site"},"authors":{"value":["Xuan Li","Zhifeng Guo","Lianru Gao"]}},"tmdate":1730955430351,"pdate":1104537600000,"tcdate":1730955401577,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lianru_Gao1"],"forum":"UPGYpa7OVo","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":173245,"cdate":1104537600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730955430351,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UPGYpa7OVo","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce a training-aware, calibration-free framework for joint reduction of the state order and step-size scheduling rank of Mamba selective state-space models."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["selective state-space models","Mamba","model compression","model order reduction","low-rank approximation"]},"primary_area":{"value":"learning on time series and dynamical systems"},"abstract":{"value":"Modeling long sequences, such as text and audio, requires computationally efficient architectures; Mamba addresses this need using selective state-space models (SSMs). However, two structural dimensions intrinsic to these selective SSMs—the state order and the step-size scheduling dimension—directly affect per-step cost and parameter count. We introduce a training-aware framework that uses structural regularization during full-order training to enable joint reduction of both dimensions. A per-layer finite-horizon output-error bound motivates a scale-invariant, decay-aware state score and a factorized low-rank surrogate for the step-size map. After training, we perform one-shot reduction using state selection and truncated SVD to construct a model with the requested dimensions from the learned parameters alone, without calibration data or post-compression fine-tuning. On Selective Copying, SC09, and The Pile, the jointly reduced models outperform vanilla post-hoc reduction in reported point estimates at matched reduction levels. In isolated state reduction, our calibration-free method outperforms the adapted calibration-based baselines on Selective Copying and SC09 and remains competitive on The Pile."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026trainingaware,\ntitle={Training-Aware, Calibration-Free Model Reduction for Selective State Space Models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=rLRq4RvB7I},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Training-Aware, Calibration-Free Model Reduction for Selective State Space Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7981993a584250a21e4e213596c3a48b15b4ea38.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791233494554,"tcdate":1789742753112,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission44307/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission44307/Authors"],"forum":"rLRq4RvB7I","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":44307,"cdate":1789742753112,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission44307/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791233494554,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"rLRq4RvB7I","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Learning-to-Optimize; Geometric Optimization; Robot–Camera Calibration"]},"primary_area":{"value":"optimization"},"abstract":{"value":"Camera-robot extrinsic calibration is essential for aligning surgical robot kinematics with endoscopic observations, yet remains challenging under specular appearance, articulated motion, occlusion, and partial visibility. We introduce SurgURDF-Calib, a geometry-structured learned optimization framework that preserves an analytical SE(3) solver rather than directly regressing camera extrinsics. Given a surgical video, robot joint states, URDF geometry, and an initial extrinsic estimate, we first construct a compact optimization candidate pool using motion diversity and instrument visibility. A learned coarse calibration module then predicts a sequence-level correction to move the estimate into a favorable convergence basin. Starting from this initialization, the second stage performs geometry-guided refinement through observability-aware D-optimal frame scheduling and a learned robust sparse Levenberg-Marquardt (LM) solver, which adaptively estimates geometric factor weights and anisotropic damping while retaining analytical pose updates. We further introduce SurgURDF-100K, a geometry-consistent benchmark containing 1,000 surgical sequences and 100K annotated observations. Experiments on synthetic and real surgical videos demonstrate improved calibration accuracy and robustness under challenging initialization and unreliable observations, together with generalization to unseen instrument geometries."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026surgurdfcalib,\ntitle={Surg{URDF}-Calib: Learning Robust Geometric Optimization for Surgical Robot Camera Calibration},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=J2AocPT9RY},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"SurgURDF-Calib: Learning Robust Geometric Optimization for Surgical Robot Camera Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7c2b01d6f10ffa6d5272a41d1f63edf36d85a963.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791231384593,"tcdate":1789649734446,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission31455/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission31455/Authors"],"forum":"J2AocPT9RY","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":31455,"cdate":1789649734446,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission31455/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791231384593,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"J2AocPT9RY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["deep learning， long-tailed learning，distribution reconstruction"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/190914f29cc677cfc0c4af39f213bbdc6ee4ff3a.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nliu2026riskbounded,\ntitle={Risk-Bounded Distribution Reconstruction: Stable Statistic Calibration for Long-Tailed Recognition},\nauthor={Guanliang Liu and Wenchao Chen and Long Tian and Xuefei Cao and Hongwei Liu and Bo Chen},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=XIvx32C4IM}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Risk-Bounded Distribution Reconstruction: Stable Statistic Calibration for Long-Tailed Recognition"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|riskbounded_distribution_reconstruction_stable_statistic_calibration_for_longtailed_recognition"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/1d4e062390faca244d12777d967572cf7a3d4d5b.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"general_machine_learning->supervised_learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Long-tailed recognition suffers from extreme class imbalance, where scarce tail data leads to biased and fragile feature distributions that exacerbate confusion with semantically or visually similar classes. Prior feature-space reconstruction methods transfer head-class structure or train conditional generators to synthesize tail features, yet the resulting \\emph{statistical} updates are often heuristic and can degrade multi-class separability when tail estimates are unreliable. Given this issue, we propose \\emph{Risk-Bounded Distribution Reconstruction} (RBDR), an offline statistic calibration framework for the two-stage long-tailed pipeline, grounded in an analysis of rival-induced discriminative directions. RBDR performs (i) risk-aware mean calibration by softly projecting any candidate update onto a supportive set such that a surrogate discriminative-risk upper bound does not increase, and (ii) covariance control by shrinking dispersion in a rival subspace while preserving diversity in orthogonal components. \nIn the frozen-feature reconstruction setting, these calibration components convert heuristic reconstruction cues into controllable distributional updates, improving both performance and stability across multiple long-tailed benchmarks."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7b2d1e2c85f55b33a9bdbe6b7c6fc80f076feff8.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Real-world image datasets are often uneven: some categories have many examples, while rare categories have only a few. This makes recognition systems less reliable for rare categories, which can matter when uncommon cases are important. Our paper studies how to improve recognition under this kind of imbalance. After training a model once, we examine the patterns it has learned for each category and carefully refine the estimates for rare categories. The key idea is to avoid corrections that make a rare category look more like categories it is often confused with. We identify these likely “rival” categories and use them as safety checks when generating extra training examples for rare categories. Across several standard image recognition test datasets, this approach improves accuracy and stability, especially for rare categories. Because the refinement is done after the main model has been trained, the process is easier to inspect and control before the final decision layer is updated."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Guanliang_Liu1","~Wenchao_Chen1","~Long_Tian1","~Xuefei_Cao3","~Hongwei_Liu6","~Bo_Chen1"]},"authors":{"value":["Guanliang Liu","Wenchao Chen","Long Tian","Xuefei Cao","Hongwei Liu","Bo Chen"]}},"tmdate":1790067405009,"pdate":1777576686565,"tcdate":1769169317433,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission24060/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission24060/Authors"],"forum":"XIvx32C4IM","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":24060,"cdate":1769169317433,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission24060/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission24060/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067405009,"odate":1782341991602,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"XIvx32C4IM","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Intelligent Transportation Systems"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/6979/10403543/10247105.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"feng|an_interpretable_nonlinear_decoupling_and_calibration_approach_to_wheel_force_transducers"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TITS.2023.3309822"},"abstract":{"value":"The multi-dimensional force/torque decoupling and calibration is extremely crucial to increase the accuracy of the Wheel Force Transducer/Sensor (WFT). A novel interpretable nonlinear decoupling and calibration approach to WFT is presented. A physical interpretable prime-error framework is developed such that the linear prime part accounts for most force-voltage responses while the nonlinear error part accounts for the gross error deviation. The conventional least-square decoupling is improved with the delicate nonlinear error modeling using a polynomial base module and a hyperbolic activation function. The developed framework is proved to be mathematically solvable and physically feasible by a two-step calibration scheme. A two-axis WFT is tested and compared with the proposed interpretable nonlinear decoupling model (IND), the least-square-based method (LSM), and the error-based neural network model (eNN). Results demonstrate that the proposed IND provides an accurate, practical, and effective scheme for modeling and calibrating WFTs and maintains a good balance among accuracy, generalization ability, and computational efficiency for real applications."},"title":{"value":"An Interpretable Nonlinear Decoupling and Calibration Approach to Wheel Force Transducers"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Lihang Feng"},{"fullname":"Sui Wang","username":"~Sui_Wang1"},{"fullname":"Jiantao Shi"},{"fullname":"Pengwen Xiong"},{"fullname":"Chuang Chen"},{"fullname":"Di Xiao"},{"fullname":"Aiguo Song"},{"fullname":"Peter Xiaoping Liu"}]}},"tmdate":1789091092752,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tits.2023.3309822"],"tcdate":1762499189378,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Sui_Wang1"],"forum":"bFnthcMK2M","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":9564,"cdate":1699688710762,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789091092752,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"bFnthcMK2M","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Technometrics 2026"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"chakraborty|orthogonal_calibration_via_posterior_projections_with_applications_to_the_schwarzschild_model"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.2025.2526428"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/technometrics/ChakrabortyWSMB26,\n  author={Antik Chakraborty and Jonelle L. Walsh and Louis Strigari and Bani K. Mallick and Anirban Bhattacharya},\n  title={Orthogonal Calibration via Posterior Projections with Applications to the Schwarzschild Model},\n  year={2026},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  journal={Technometrics},\n  volume={68},\n  number={1},\n  pages={14-23},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.2025.2526428}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The orbital superposition method originally developed by Schwarzschild is used to study the dynamics of growth of a black hole and its host galaxy, and has uncovered new relationships between the galaxy’s global characteristics. Scientists are specifically interested in finding optimal parameter choices for this model that best match physical measurements along with quantifying the uncertainty of such procedures. This renders a statistical calibration problem with multivariate outcomes. In this article, we develop a Bayesian method for calibration with multivariate outcomes using orthogonal bias functions thus ensuring parameter identifiability. Our approach is based on projecting the posterior to an appropriate space which allows the user to choose any nonparametric prior on the bias function(s) instead of having to model it (them) with Gaussian processes. We develop a functional projection approach using the theory of Hilbert spaces. A finite-dimensional analogue of the projection problem is also considered. We illustrate the proposed approach using a BART prior and apply it to calibrate the Schwarzschild model illustrating how a multivariate approach may resolve discrepancies resulting from a univariate calibration."},"title":{"value":"Orthogonal Calibration via Posterior Projections with Applications to the Schwarzschild Model"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Antik Chakraborty","username":""},{"fullname":"Jonelle L. Walsh","username":""},{"fullname":"Louis Strigari","username":""},{"fullname":"Bani K. Mallick","username":""},{"fullname":"Anirban Bhattacharya","username":"~Anirban_Bhattacharya1"}]}},"tmdate":1778005467767,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/technometrics/ChakrabortyWSMB26"],"tcdate":1778005452125,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Anirban_Bhattacharya1"],"forum":"1ce7Yqqzgf","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":6293,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1778005467767,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"1ce7Yqqzgf","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SII 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10038976/10039084/10039085.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/SII/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|a_smartphone_pose_autocalibration_method_using_hashbased_doa_estimation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chaoran_Liu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Carlos_Toshinori_Ishi:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/SII55687.2023.10039085"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/sii/LiuI23,\n  author={Chaoran Liu and Carlos Toshinori Ishi},\n  title={A Smartphone Pose Auto-calibration Method using Hash-based DOA Estimation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1-6},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/SII55687.2023.10039085},\n  booktitle={SII},\n  crossref={conf/sii/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents a method to utilize multiple off-the-shelf smartphones to localize speakers. For DOA (direction of arrival) estimation on every single smartphone, we proposed an O(1) complexity hash table-based modified phase transform (PHAT) estimation method without scanning all possible directions to achieve lower CPU usage and longer battery life. Additionally, to increase DOA estimation accuracy, we measured two types of smartphone impulse responses and made them publicly available. In the auto-calibration process, each smartphone detects a pure tone emitted from another smartphone's speaker. Assuming that all smartphones are on the same desktop surface, each smartphone's 2D position and rotation are estimated using these detected DOAs and the speaker position relative to their central point. A bundle adjustment-like optimization method is employed to reduce the re-projection error in this process. After auto-calibration, we can easily integrate the DOAs found by each smartphone and estimate the speaker's position using simple triangulation. The experimental results show that the proposed hash table-based DOA estimation method and 2D version bundle adjustment can perform auto-calibration precisely."},"title":{"value":"A Smartphone Pose Auto-calibration Method using Hash-based DOA Estimation"},"authors":{"value":["Chaoran Liu","Carlos Toshinori Ishi"]}},"tmdate":1767920544270,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/sii/LiuI23"],"tcdate":1767920529168,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chaoran_Liu1"],"forum":"oeQENcbzms","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":739736,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1767920544270,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"oeQENcbzms","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.01788v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"fan|universal_online_temporal_calibration_for_optimizationbased_visualinertial_navigation_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianyu_Zhao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Linan_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chen_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fengyi_Zhou:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.01788"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-01788,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yunfei Fan and Tianyu Zhao and Linan Guo and Chen Chen and Xin Wang and Fengyi Zhou},\n  title={Universal Online Temporal Calibration for Optimization-based Visual-Inertial Navigation Systems},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2501.01788},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.01788}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"6-Degree of Freedom (6DoF) motion estimation with a combination of visual and inertial sensors is a growing area with numerous real-world applications. However, precise calibration of the time offset between these two sensor types is a prerequisite for accurate and robust tracking. To address this, we propose a universal online temporal calibration strategy for optimization-based visual-inertial navigation systems. Technically, we incorporate the time offset td as a state parameter in the optimization residual model to align the IMU state to the corresponding image timestamp using td, angular velocity and translational velocity. This allows the temporal misalignment td to be optimized alongside other tracking states during the process. As our method only modifies the structure of the residual model, it can be applied to various optimization-based frameworks with different tracking frontends. We evaluate our calibration method with both EuRoC and simulation data and extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach provides more accurate time offset estimation and faster convergence, particularly in the presence of noisy sensor data."},"title":{"value":"Universal Online Temporal Calibration for Optimization-based Visual-Inertial Navigation Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Yunfei Fan","Tianyu Zhao","Linan Guo","Chen Chen","Xin Wang","Fengyi Zhou"]}},"tmdate":1762774153025,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1747353126966,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunfei_Fan1"],"forum":"QtcxcPp2vo","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":500469,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762774153025,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"QtcxcPp2vo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.01788v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"fan|universal_online_temporal_calibration_for_optimizationbased_visualinertial_navigation_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianyu_Zhao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Linan_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chen_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fengyi_Zhou:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.01788"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-01788,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yunfei Fan and Tianyu Zhao and Linan Guo and Chen Chen and Xin Wang and Fengyi Zhou},\n  title={Universal Online Temporal Calibration for Optimization-based Visual-Inertial Navigation Systems},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2501.01788},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.01788}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"6-Degree of Freedom (6DoF) motion estimation with a combination of visual and inertial sensors is a growing area with numerous real-world applications. However, precise calibration of the time offset between these two sensor types is a prerequisite for accurate and robust tracking. To address this, we propose a universal online temporal calibration strategy for optimization-based visual-inertial navigation systems. Technically, we incorporate the time offset td as a state parameter in the optimization residual model to align the IMU state to the corresponding image timestamp using td, angular velocity and translational velocity. This allows the temporal misalignment td to be optimized alongside other tracking states during the process. As our method only modifies the structure of the residual model, it can be applied to various optimization-based frameworks with different tracking frontends. We evaluate our calibration method with both EuRoC and simulation data and extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach provides more accurate time offset estimation and faster convergence, particularly in the presence of noisy sensor data."},"title":{"value":"Universal Online Temporal Calibration for Optimization-based Visual-Inertial Navigation Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Yunfei Fan","Tianyu Zhao","Linan Guo","Chen Chen","Xin Wang","Fengyi Zhou"]}},"tmdate":1762774152909,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1751595690015,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunfei_Fan1"],"forum":"YkRwvxV6rk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":565885,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762774152909,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"YkRwvxV6rk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.01788v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"fan|universal_online_temporal_calibration_for_optimizationbased_visualinertial_navigation_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianyu_Zhao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Linan_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chen_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fengyi_Zhou:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.01788"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-01788,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yunfei Fan and Tianyu Zhao and Linan Guo and Chen Chen and Xin Wang and Fengyi Zhou},\n  title={Universal Online Temporal Calibration for Optimization-based Visual-Inertial Navigation Systems},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2501.01788},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.01788}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"6-Degree of Freedom (6DoF) motion estimation with a combination of visual and inertial sensors is a growing area with numerous real-world applications. However, precise calibration of the time offset between these two sensor types is a prerequisite for accurate and robust tracking. To address this, we propose a universal online temporal calibration strategy for optimization-based visual-inertial navigation systems. Technically, we incorporate the time offset td as a state parameter in the optimization residual model to align the IMU state to the corresponding image timestamp using td, angular velocity and translational velocity. This allows the temporal misalignment td to be optimized alongside other tracking states during the process. As our method only modifies the structure of the residual model, it can be applied to various optimization-based frameworks with different tracking frontends. We evaluate our calibration method with both EuRoC and simulation data and extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach provides more accurate time offset estimation and faster convergence, particularly in the presence of noisy sensor data."},"title":{"value":"Universal Online Temporal Calibration for Optimization-based Visual-Inertial Navigation Systems"},"authors":{"value":["Yunfei Fan","Tianyu Zhao","Linan Guo","Chen Chen","Xin Wang","Fengyi Zhou"]}},"tmdate":1762774152873,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1747353522193,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunfei_Fan1"],"forum":"91nwb9gVG3","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":500547,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762774152873,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"91nwb9gVG3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICIGP 2021"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICIGP/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"sha|fisheye_image_calibration_and_superresolution_method_based_on_deep_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shihao_Sha:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhiwei_Wen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaohui_Lin_0001:","~Qiuming_Luo1","~Chang_Kong1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3447587.3447602"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icigp/ShaWLLK21,\n  author={Shihao Sha and Zhiwei Wen and Xiaohui Lin and Qiuming Luo and Chang Kong},\n  title={Fisheye Image Calibration and Super-Resolution Method Based On Deep Learning},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={102-108},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3447587.3447602},\n  booktitle={ICIGP},\n  crossref={conf/icigp/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Fisheye lens is a kind of ultra-wide-angle lens, which can get more field of view information than ordinary perspective lens. However, there is radial lens distortion in the photos taken by fisheye lens. In order to improve the visual quality of fisheye images and to perform subsequent computer vision tasks, fisheye images must be calibrated first. Traditional calibration methods are often based on a specific type of fisheye lens, which is not universal, and often need to take multiple images of a calibration pattern (typically a checkerboard) for calibration. And limited by hardware, many fisheye cameras take photos with low resolution. The method proposed in this paper not only overcomes the above limitations, but also jointly solves the problem of image super-resolution, which makes the final result more appreciable. This method uses a depth convolution neural network model to predict the fisheye image correction parameters, and then completes the correction process, but also completes the image super-resolution processing. Our quantitative experiments have proved the feasibility and superiority of our method."},"title":{"value":"Fisheye Image Calibration and Super-Resolution Method Based On Deep Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Shihao Sha","Zhiwei Wen","Xiaohui Lin","Qiuming Luo","Chang Kong"]}},"tmdate":1749695506577,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1748766431478,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chang_Kong1"],"forum":"9qWxUqU7pQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":554370,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1749695506577,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9qWxUqU7pQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"UR 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9139891/9144696/09144877.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/URAI/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"ince|accurate_online_extrinsic_calibration_for_a_multicamera_slam_system"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Omer_Faruk_Ince:","~Jun-Sik_Kim2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/UR49135.2020.9144877"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/urai/InceK20,\n  author={Omer Faruk Ince and Jun-Sik Kim},\n  title={Accurate On-line Extrinsic Calibration for a Multi-camera SLAM System},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={540-545},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/UR49135.2020.9144877},\n  booktitle={UR},\n  crossref={conf/urai/2020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) system has an important role in providing an accurate and comprehensive solution for situational awareness in unknown environments. In order to maximize the situational awareness, the wider field of view is required. It is possible to achieve a wide field of view with an omnidirectional lense or multiple perspective cameras. However, calibration of such systems is sensitive and difficult. For this reason, we present a practical solution to a multi-camera SLAM system. The goal of this study is to obtain robust localization and mapping for multi-camera setup without requiring pre-calibration of the camera system calibration. With this goal, we associate measurements from cameras with their relative poses and propose an iterative optimization method to refine the map, keyframe poses and relative poses between cameras simultaneously. We evaluated our method on a dataset which consists of three cameras with small overlapping regions, and on the KITTI odometry dataset which is set in stereo configuration. The experiments demonstrated that the proposed method provides not only a practical but also robust SLAM solution for multi-camera systems."},"title":{"value":"Accurate On-line Extrinsic Calibration for a Multi-camera SLAM System"},"authors":{"value":["Omer Faruk Ince","Jun-Sik Kim"]}},"tmdate":1731563696979,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1731563694593,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jun-Sik_Kim2"],"forum":"4Miq5QhUbL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":238214,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731563696979,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4Miq5QhUbL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Comput. Methods Programs Biomed. 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CMPB/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|pfedbcc_personalizing_federated_multitarget_domain_adaptive_segmentation_via_bipole_collaborative_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Huaqi_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pengyu_Wang_0005:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Liu_0044:","~Jing_Qin3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cmpb.2025.108635"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/cmpb/ZhangWLQ25,\n  author={Huaqi Zhang and Pengyu Wang and Jie Liu and Jing Qin},\n  title={pFedBCC: Personalizing Federated multi-target domain adaptive segmentation via Bi-pole Collaborative Calibration},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={Comput. Methods Programs Biomed.},\n  volume={263},\n  pages={108635},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cmpb.2025.108635}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•This study cooperates multiple medical institutions for multi-type IHC image segmentation through federated multi-target domain adaptation.•Semantic-affinity-driven personalized label calibration reduces client prediction drift via cross-domain alignment.•Source-knowledge-oriented consistent gradient calibration mitigates server aggregation drift with gradient unification.•Multi-type IHC dataset includes 10 thymoma types stained with 10 different proteins."},"title":{"value":"pFedBCC: Personalizing Federated multi-target domain adaptive segmentation via Bi-pole Collaborative Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Huaqi Zhang","Pengyu Wang","Jie Liu","Jing Qin"]}},"tmdate":1747225109323,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1747224995636,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jing_Qin3"],"forum":"kbIcS9dEe2","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":456713,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747225109323,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"kbIcS9dEe2","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICIRA (1) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-981-96-0771-6_8.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICIRA/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"sheng|kinematic_calibration_method_based_on_point_cloud_measurement_for_3rps_parallel_robot"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lingyun_Sheng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xingwei_Zhao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yajun_Fan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Xue:","~Bo_Tao3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-96-0771-6_8"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icira/ShengZFXT24,\n  author={Lingyun Sheng and Xingwei Zhao and Yajun Fan and Lei Xue and Bo Tao},\n  title={Kinematic Calibration Method Based on Point Cloud Measurement for 3-RPS Parallel Robot},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={95-107},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-96-0771-6_8},\n  booktitle={ICIRA (1)},\n  crossref={conf/icira/2024-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In order to control the end-effector to achieve accurate and suitable posture for high-precision processes in complex industrial environments, a mechanism is often added to make secondary adjustment of the tool end posture. The 3-RPS parallel connection is widely used for target posture control in small workspaces due to its features of high control accuracy, large load, and small size and mass. In order to avoid the influence of process error and assembly error on the control accuracy of the parallel system, it is necessary to identify its parameters with high accuracy before use. However, the traditional non-contact calibration methods, such as position sensors and cameras, have the disadvantages of complex system, difficult to build, lower accuracy and cumbersome operation. To address the above problems, this paper proposes a non-contact measurement calibration method based on facial laser scanner, and establishes a calibration system based on a self-designed and fabricated 3-RPS parallel robot, realizes the high-precision measurement of the overall position and attitude information of the robot through the splicing of multi-segmented point clouds and feature extraction algorithms, establishes an inverse kinematic model of the robot, combines the 3D model with the parameter identification equations, realizes the parallel robot kinematic calibration, and designs the accuracy verification method. The effectiveness of the parameter calibration and the control algorithm of the parallel robot are verified by experiments."},"title":{"value":"Kinematic Calibration Method Based on Point Cloud Measurement for 3-RPS Parallel Robot"},"authors":{"value":["Lingyun Sheng","Xingwei Zhao","Yajun Fan","Lei Xue","Bo Tao"]}},"tmdate":1747106855159,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747106811728,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bo_Tao3"],"forum":"4Rnt4X8ieM","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":433715,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747106855159,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4Rnt4X8ieM","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"This paper introduce a synthetic data generation pipeline and a robust model adaptation algorithm to train models for diverse code-editing tasks without losing their original code generation abilities."},"venue":{"value":"DL4C @ ICLR 2025"},"keywords":{"value":["Code-LMs","code-editing","code-generation","software engineering"]},"abstract":{"value":"Software engineering activities frequently involve edits to existing code. However, contemporary code language models (LMs) lack the ability to handle diverse types of code-edit requirements. In this work, we attempt to overcome this shortcoming through (1) a novel synthetic data generation pipeline and (2) a robust model adaptation algorithm. Starting with seed code examples and diverse editing criteria, our pipeline generates high-quality samples comprising original and modified code, along with natural language instructions in different styles and verbosity. Today's code LMs come bundled with strong abilities, such as code generation and instruction following, which should not be lost due to fine-tuning. To ensure this, we propose a novel adaptation algorithm, SeleKT, that (a) leverages a dense gradient-based step to identify the weights that are most important for code editing, and (b) does a sparse projection onto the base model to avoid overfitting. Using our approach, we obtain a new model NextCoder (adapted from Qwen2.5-Coder-7B) that achieves strong results on four code-editing benchmarks, outperforming comparable size models and even several larger ones. We show the generality of our approach by improving DeepSeekCoder-6.7B and Qwen2.5-Coder-7B, compare against other fine-tuning approaches, and demonstrate robustness by showing retention of code generation abilities post adaptation."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\naggarwal2025nextcoder,\ntitle={NextCoder: Robust Adaptation of Code {LM}s to Diverse Code Edits},\nauthor={Tushar Aggarwal and Swayam Singh and Abhijeet Awasthi and Aditya Kanade and Nagarajan Natarajan},\nbooktitle={ICLR 2025 Third Workshop on Deep Learning for Code},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=r1kOC0MiyG}\n}"},"title":{"value":"NextCoder: Robust Adaptation of Code LMs to Diverse Code Edits"},"Anonymization":{"value":"This submission has been anonymized for double-blind review via the removal of identifying information such as names, affiliations, and identifying URLs."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c83d3e17153fa2b06194b20c68c31d6a512bfdd5.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C"},"paperhash":{"value":"aggarwal|nextcoder_robust_adaptation_of_code_lms_to_diverse_code_edits"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tushar_Aggarwal1","~Swayam_Singh1","~Abhijeet_Awasthi1","~Aditya_Kanade2","~Nagarajan_Natarajan2"]},"Track":{"value":"long paper (up to 9 pages)"},"authors":{"value":["Tushar Aggarwal","Swayam Singh","Abhijeet Awasthi","Aditya Kanade","Nagarajan Natarajan"]}},"tmdate":1745072496068,"pdate":1741234619646,"tcdate":1739102721954,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission24/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission24/Authors"],"forum":"r1kOC0MiyG","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":24,"cdate":1739102721954,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission24/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1745072496068,"odate":1741234619646,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C","id":"r1kOC0MiyG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10203037/10203050/10203195.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"peng|perception_and_semantic_aware_regularization_for_sequential_confidence_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhenghua_Peng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu_Luo:","~Tianshui_Chen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Keke_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuangping_Huang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52729.2023.01027"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/PengLCXH23,\n  author={Zhenghua Peng and Yu Luo and Tianshui Chen and Keke Xu and Shuangping Huang},\n  title={Perception and Semantic Aware Regularization for Sequential Confidence Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={10658-10668},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52729.2023.01027},\n  booktitle={CVPR},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep sequence recognition (DSR) models receive increasing attention due to their superior application to various applications. Most DSR models use merely the target sequences as supervision without considering other related sequences, leading to over-confidence in their predictions. The DSR models trained with label smoothing regularize labels by equally and independently smoothing each token, reallocating a small value to other tokens for mitigating overconfidence. However, they do not consider tokens/sequences correlations that may provide more effective information to regularize training and thus lead to sub-optimal performance. In this work, we find tokens/sequences with high perception and semantic correlations with the target ones contain more correlated and effective information and thus facilitate more effective regularization. To this end, we propose a Perception and Semantic aware Sequence Regularization framework, which explore perceptively and semantically correlated tokens/sequences as regularization. Specifically, we introduce a semantic context-free recognition and a language model to acquire similar sequences with high perceptive similarities and semantic correlation, respectively. Moreover, over-confidence degree varies across samples according to their difficulties. Thus, we further design an adaptive calibration intensity module to compute a difficulty score for each samples to obtain finer-grained regularization. Extensive experiments on canonical sequence recognition tasks, including scene text and speech recognition, demonstrate that our method sets novel state-of-the-art results. Code is available at https://github.com/husterpzh/PSSR."},"title":{"value":"Perception and Semantic Aware Regularization for Sequential Confidence Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Zhenghua Peng","Yu Luo","Tianshui Chen","Keke Xu","Shuangping Huang"]}},"tmdate":1727508604775,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727508595219,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Tianshui_Chen1"],"forum":"hcsQ2Ag3og","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":100218,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727508604775,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"hcsQ2Ag3og","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"TLDR":{"value":"ReCAP enables robust incomplete and noisy multi-view clustering by learning reciprocal cross-view prototype correspondences through cyclic calibration."},"keywords":{"value":["Incomplete Multi-view Clustering; Prototype Alignment; Cyclic Calibration; Representation Learning."]},"primary_area":{"value":"unsupervised, self-supervised, semi-supervised, and supervised representation learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-view clustering aims to uncover a consensus cluster structure from heterogeneous views of the same objects. Prototype-based methods summarize the clusters of each view with semantic prototypes and align them across views. However, missing views and feature noise distort local neighborhood structures and shift view-specific prototypes, rendering cross-view correspondences ambiguous and prone to many-to-one matches. Establishing reliable and reciprocal prototype correspondences under such degradation therefore remains challenging. We propose ReCAP, an imputation-free framework for Reciprocal Cyclic cAlibration of cross-view Prototypes under incomplete and noisy settings. ReCAP first applies mutual k-nearest-neighbor graph contrastive learning to preserve reliable local neighborhoods and stabilize prototype estimation. It then constructs sample-mediated cyclic transitions that propagate probability mass from each source prototype through the samples and prototypes of another view and back to the source view. Maximizing the probability of returning to the source prototype couples forward and backward transitions; we prove that, in the zero-loss limit, the two directional transitions reduce to a permutation and its inverse, i.e., a reciprocal one-to-one correspondence. A visitation regularizer further prevents the returned mass from collapsing onto a few prototypes. Experiments on six benchmark datasets demonstrate competitive or superior clustering performance under incomplete, noisy, and compound degradation settings, while further analyses support the effectiveness of cyclic prototype calibration. Our code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/ReCAP-0301."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026crossview,\ntitle={Cross-View Prototype Calibration via Reciprocal Cyclic Transitions for Incomplete and Noisy Multi-View Clustering},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Vya5rcQHsQ},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Cross-View Prototype Calibration via Reciprocal Cyclic Transitions for Incomplete and Noisy Multi-View Clustering"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c7a4489e7d4e14a3d90c4b270fb09e1ed5422679.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791219704410,"tcdate":1787303851558,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission2621/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission2621/Authors"],"forum":"Vya5rcQHsQ","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2621,"cdate":1787303851558,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission2621/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing"],"mdate":1791219704410,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"Vya5rcQHsQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.00521v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"fan|a_new_approach_for_encoding_code_and_assisting_code_understanding"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mengdan_Fan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wei_Zhang_0004:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haiyan_Zhao_0001:","~Zhi_Jin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.00521"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2408-00521,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Mengdan Fan and Wei Zhang and Haiyan Zhao and Zhi Jin},\n  title={A new approach for encoding code and assisting code understanding},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2408.00521},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.00521}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Some companies (e.g., Microsoft Research and Google DeepMind) have discovered some of the limitations of GPTs' autoregressive paradigm next-word prediction, manifested in the model's lack of planning, working memory, backtracking, and reasoning skills. GPTs rely on a local and greedy process of generating the next word, without a global understanding of the task or the output. We have confirmed the above limitations through specialized empirical studies of code comprehension. Although GPT-4 is good at producing fluent and coherent text, it cannot handle complex logic and generate new code that hasn't been seen, and it relies too much on the formatting of the prompt to generate the correct code. We propose a new paradigm for code understanding that goes beyond the next-word prediction paradigm, inspired by the successful application of diffusion techniques to image generation (Dalle-2, Sora) and protein structure generation (AlphaFold-3), which have no autoregressive constraints. Instead of encoding the code in a form that mimics natural language, we encode the code as a heterogeneous image paradigm with a memory of global information that mimics both images and protein structures. We then refer to Sora's CLIP upstream text-to-image encoder model to design a text-to-code encoder model that can be applied to various downstream code understanding tasks. The model learns the global understanding of code under the new paradigm heterogeneous image, connects the encoding space of text and code, and encodes the input of text into the vector of code most similar to it. Using self-supervised comparative learning on 456,360 text-code pairs, the model achieved a zero-shot prediction of new data. This work is the basis for future work on code generation using diffusion techniques under a new paradigm to avoid autoregressive limitations."},"title":{"value":"A new approach for encoding code and assisting code understanding"},"authors":{"value":["Mengdan Fan","Wei Zhang","Haiyan Zhao","Zhi Jin"]}},"tmdate":1747645565915,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747645540242,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhi_Jin1"],"forum":"ASlSjBVonW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":525782,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747645565915,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ASlSjBVonW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.03296v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|plscalib_a_partial_least_squares_framework_for_event_camera_and_odometry_calibration_under_ground_motion_constraints"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.03296"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2608-03296,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Guangyu Li and Xiao Li and Yujie Wu and Changshuo Wang and Prayag Tiwari and Jiang Cai and Fangwen Yu and Mingkun Xu},\n  title={PLS-Calib: A Partial Least Squares Framework for Event Camera and Odometry Calibration under Ground Motion Constraints},\n  year={2026},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1785542400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2608.03296},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.03296}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate extrinsic rotation calibration between sensors is fundamental to the performance of robotic perception systems. However, most existing calibration techniques rely on full 6-DoF motion to excite all degrees of freedom, which is often infeasible for ground-constrained robots with limited motion capabilities. Recent approaches designed for such restricted settings, such as Canonical Correlation Analysis (CCA)-based methods, suffer from ill-conditioned covariance matrices that lead to numerical instability and suboptimal calibration accuracy. To overcome these limitations, we present a novel rotation calibration framework named PLS-Calib that, for the first time, leverages Partial Least Squares (PLS) regression to model the latent kinematic correlations between asynchronous, heterogeneous sensor streams. Specifically, we apply our method to the calibration of an event camera and an odometry onboard a ground robot. To improve event-based pattern detection, we introduce a polarity-aware event representation, which enhances spatiotemporal contrast in circular calibration targets. Our PLS-based formulation yields a closed-form, stable solution that avoids matrix singularities inherent in CCA-based approaches. Extensive experiments on both synthetic and real-world datasets validate the effectiveness of our approach, demonstrating significant improvements in calibration robustness and accuracy over state-of-the-art methods. This work offers a practical and theoretically grounded solution for rotation calibration in constrained robotic systems and opens up new directions for applying statistical learning techniques in neuromorphic vision."},"title":{"value":"PLS-Calib: A Partial Least Squares Framework for Event Camera and Odometry Calibration under Ground Motion Constraints"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Guangyu Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Xiao Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Yujie Wu","username":""},{"fullname":"Changshuo Wang","username":"~Changshuo_Wang1"},{"fullname":"Prayag Tiwari","username":""},{"fullname":"Jiang Cai","username":""},{"fullname":"Fangwen Yu","username":""},{"fullname":"Mingkun Xu","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1791143144791,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2608-03296"],"tcdate":1791143137401,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Changshuo_Wang1"],"forum":"buumH6o6Vp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":163223,"cdate":1785542400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791143144791,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"buumH6o6Vp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (Findings) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.809.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"song|code_needs_comments_enhancing_code_llms_with_comment_augmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Demin_Song1","~Honglin_Guo1","~Yunhua_Zhou1","~Shuhao_Xing1","~Yudong_Wang3","","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenwei_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qipeng_Guo:","~Hang_Yan2","~Xipeng_Qiu1","~Dahua_Lin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.findings-acl.809"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/SongGZXWSZG0QL24,\n  author={Demin Song and Honglin Guo and Yunhua Zhou and Shuhao Xing and Yudong Wang and Zifan Song and Wenwei Zhang and Qipeng Guo and Hang Yan and Xipeng Qiu and Dahua Lin},\n  title={Code Needs Comments: Enhancing Code LLMs with Comment Augmentation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={13640-13656},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.findings-acl.809},\n  booktitle={ACL (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2024f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The programming skill is one crucial ability for Large Language Models (LLMs), necessitating a deep understanding of programming languages (PLs) and their correlation with natural languages (NLs). We examine the impact of pre-training data on code-focused LLMs’ performance by assessing the comment density as a measure of PL-NL alignment. Given the scarcity of code-comment aligned data in pre-training corpora, we introduce a novel data augmentation method that generates comments for existing code, coupled with a data filtering strategy that filters out code data poorly correlated with natural language. We conducted experiments on three code-focused LLMs and observed consistent improvements in performance on two widely-used programming skill benchmarks. Notably, the model trained on the augmented data outperformed both the model used for generating comments and the model further trained on the data without augmentation."},"title":{"value":"Code Needs Comments: Enhancing Code LLMs with Comment Augmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Demin Song","Honglin Guo","Yunhua Zhou","Shuhao Xing","Yudong Wang","Zifan Song","Wenwei Zhang","Qipeng Guo","Hang Yan","Xipeng Qiu","Dahua Lin"]}},"tmdate":1784699828900,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727715234148,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunhua_Zhou1"],"forum":"Cp5LdkjnhD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":121284,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784699828900,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Cp5LdkjnhD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.13013v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"song|code_needs_comments_enhancing_code_llms_with_comment_augmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Demin_Song1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Honglin_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yunhua_Zhou:","~Shuhao_Xing1","~Yudong_Wang3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zifan_Song:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenwei_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qipeng_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hang_Yan_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xipeng_Qiu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dahua_Lin:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.13013"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2402-13013,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Demin Song and Honglin Guo and Yunhua Zhou and Shuhao Xing and Yudong Wang and Zifan Song and Wenwei Zhang and Qipeng Guo and Hang Yan and Xipeng Qiu and Dahua Lin},\n  title={Code Needs Comments: Enhancing Code LLMs with Comment Augmentation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2402.13013},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.13013}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The programming skill is one crucial ability for Large Language Models (LLMs), necessitating a deep understanding of programming languages (PLs) and their correlation with natural languages (NLs). We examine the impact of pre-training data on code-focused LLMs' performance by assessing the comment density as a measure of PL-NL alignment. Given the scarcity of code-comment aligned data in pre-training corpora, we introduce a novel data augmentation method that generates comments for existing code, coupled with a data filtering strategy that filters out code data poorly correlated with natural language. We conducted experiments on three code-focused LLMs and observed consistent improvements in performance on two widely-used programming skill benchmarks. Notably, the model trained on the augmented data outperformed both the model used for generating comments and the model further trained on the data without augmentation."},"title":{"value":"Code Needs Comments: Enhancing Code LLMs with Comment Augmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Demin Song","Honglin Guo","Yunhua Zhou","Shuhao Xing","Yudong Wang","Zifan Song","Wenwei Zhang","Qipeng Guo","Hang Yan","Xipeng Qiu","Dahua Lin"]}},"tmdate":1784699828659,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747806263188,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Demin_Song1"],"forum":"5hDtR8ESUk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":546435,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784699828659,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5hDtR8ESUk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.13013v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"song|code_needs_comments_enhancing_code_llms_with_comment_augmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Demin_Song1","~Honglin_Guo1","~Yunhua_Zhou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuhao_Xing:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yudong_Wang:","","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenwei_Zhang:","~Qipeng_Guo1","~Hang_Yan2","~Xipeng_Qiu1","~Dahua_Lin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.13013"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2402-13013,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Demin Song and Honglin Guo and Yunhua Zhou and Shuhao Xing and Yudong Wang and Zifan Song and Wenwei Zhang and Qipeng Guo and Hang Yan and Xipeng Qiu and Dahua Lin},\n  title={Code Needs Comments: Enhancing Code LLMs with Comment Augmentation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2402.13013},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.13013}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The programming skill is one crucial ability for Large Language Models (LLMs), necessitating a deep understanding of programming languages (PLs) and their correlation with natural languages (NLs). We examine the impact of pre-training data on code-focused LLMs' performance by assessing the comment density as a measure of PL-NL alignment. Given the scarcity of code-comment aligned data in pre-training corpora, we introduce a novel data augmentation method that generates comments for existing code, coupled with a data filtering strategy that filters out code data poorly correlated with natural language. We conducted experiments on three code-focused LLMs and observed consistent improvements in performance on two widely-used programming skill benchmarks. Notably, the model trained on the augmented data outperformed both the model used for generating comments and the model further trained on the data without augmentation."},"title":{"value":"Code Needs Comments: Enhancing Code LLMs with Comment Augmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Demin Song","Honglin Guo","Yunhua Zhou","Shuhao Xing","Yudong Wang","Zifan Song","Wenwei Zhang","Qipeng Guo","Hang Yan","Xipeng Qiu","Dahua Lin"]}},"tmdate":1747744495678,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1713422732998,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Qipeng_Guo1"],"forum":"EDPlyLdBqb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":273,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747744495678,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"EDPlyLdBqb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (Findings) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.809.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"song|code_needs_comments_enhancing_code_llms_with_comment_augmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Demin_Song:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Honglin_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yunhua_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuhao_Xing:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yudong_Wang_0002:","~Zifan_Song1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenwei_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qipeng_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hang_Yan_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xipeng_Qiu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dahua_Lin:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.findings-acl.809"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/SongGZXWSZG0QL24,\n  author={Demin Song and Honglin Guo and Yunhua Zhou and Shuhao Xing and Yudong Wang and Zifan Song and Wenwei Zhang and Qipeng Guo and Hang Yan and Xipeng Qiu and Dahua Lin},\n  title={Code Needs Comments: Enhancing Code LLMs with Comment Augmentation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={13640-13656},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.findings-acl.809},\n  booktitle={ACL (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2024f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The programming skill is one crucial ability for Large Language Models (LLMs), necessitating a deep understanding of programming languages (PLs) and their correlation with natural languages (NLs). We examine the impact of pre-training data on code-focused LLMs’ performance by assessing the comment density as a measure of PL-NL alignment. Given the scarcity of code-comment aligned data in pre-training corpora, we introduce a novel data augmentation method that generates comments for existing code, coupled with a data filtering strategy that filters out code data poorly correlated with natural language. We conducted experiments on three code-focused LLMs and observed consistent improvements in performance on two widely-used programming skill benchmarks. Notably, the model trained on the augmented data outperformed both the model used for generating comments and the model further trained on the data without augmentation."},"title":{"value":"Code Needs Comments: Enhancing Code LLMs with Comment Augmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Demin Song","Honglin Guo","Yunhua Zhou","Shuhao Xing","Yudong Wang","Zifan Song","Wenwei Zhang","Qipeng Guo","Hang Yan","Xipeng Qiu","Dahua Lin"]}},"tmdate":1747102462353,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747102458774,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zifan_Song1"],"forum":"cjzgZcMJnH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":432049,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747102462353,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"cjzgZcMJnH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/32/10601498/10535138.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|fusing_code_searchers"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2024.3403042"},"abstract":{"value":"Code search, which consists in retrieving relevant code snippets from a codebase based on a given query, provides developers with useful references during software development. Over the years, techniques alternatively adopting different mechanisms to compute the relevance score between a query and a code snippet have been proposed to advance the state of the art in this domain, including those relying on information retrieval, supervised learning, and pre-training. Despite that, the usefulness of existing techniques is still compromised since they cannot effectively handle all the diversified queries and code in practice. To tackle this challenge, we present Dancer, a data fusion based code searcher. Our intuition (also the basic hypothesis of this study) is that existing techniques may complement each other because of the intrinsic differences in their working mechanisms. We have validated this hypothesis via an exploratory study. Based on that, we propose to fuse the results generated by different code search techniques so that the advantage of each standalone technique can be fully leveraged. Specifically, we treat each technique as a retrieval system and leverage well-known data fusion approaches to aggregate the results from different systems. We evaluate six existing code search techniques on two large-scale datasets, and exploit eight classic data fusion approaches to incorporate their results. Our experiments show that the best fusion approach is able to outperform the standalone techniques by 35% - 550% and 65% - 825% in terms of MRR (mean reciprocal rank) on the two datasets, respectively."},"title":{"value":"Fusing Code Searchers"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Shangwen Wang"},{"fullname":"Mingyang Geng"},{"fullname":"Bo Lin"},{"fullname":"Zhensu Sun","username":"~Zhensu_Sun1"},{"fullname":"Ming Wen"},{"fullname":"Yepang Liu","username":"~Yepang_Liu1"},{"fullname":"Li Li"},{"fullname":"Tegawendé F. Bissyandé"},{"fullname":"Xiaoguang Mao"}]}},"tmdate":1791790910663,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tse.2024.3403042"],"tcdate":1773233119040,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Zhensu_Sun1"],"forum":"P6hnDegGZd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":54244,"cdate":1716314678239,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1791790910663,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"P6hnDegGZd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Software Eng. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/32/10601498/10535138.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TSE/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|fusing_code_searchers"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shangwen_Wang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mingyang_Geng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bo_Lin:","~Zhensu_Sun1","~Ming_Wen1","~Yepang_Liu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Li_Li_0029:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tegawendé_F._Bissyandé:","~Xiaoguang_Mao1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2024.3403042"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tse/WangGLSWLLBM24,\n  author={Shangwen Wang and Mingyang Geng and Bo Lin and Zhensu Sun and Ming Wen and Yepang Liu and Li Li and Tegawendé F. Bissyandé and Xiaoguang Mao},\n  title={Fusing Code Searchers},\n  year={2024},\n  month={July},\n  cdate={1719792000000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Software Eng.},\n  volume={50},\n  number={7},\n  pages={1852-1866},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2024.3403042}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code search, which consists in retrieving relevant code snippets from a codebase based on a given query, provides developers with useful references during software development. Over the years, techniques alternatively adopting different mechanisms to compute the relevance score between a query and a code snippet have been proposed to advance the state of the art in this domain, including those relying on information retrieval, supervised learning, and pre-training. Despite that, the usefulness of existing techniques is still compromised since they cannot effectively handle all the diversified queries and code in practice. To tackle this challenge, we present Dancer , a data fusion based code searcher. Our intuition (also the basic hypothesis of this study) is that existing techniques may complement each other because of the intrinsic differences in their working mechanisms. We have validated this hypothesis via an exploratory study. Based on that, we propose to fuse the results generated by different code search techniques so that the advantage of each standalone technique can be fully leveraged. Specifically, we treat each technique as a retrieval system and leverage well-known data fusion approaches to aggregate the results from different systems. We evaluate six existing code search techniques on two large-scale datasets, and exploit eight classic data fusion approaches to incorporate their results. Our experiments show that the best fusion approach is able to outperform the standalone techniques by 35% - 550% and 65% - 825% in terms of MRR (mean reciprocal rank) on the two datasets, respectively."},"title":{"value":"Fusing Code Searchers"},"authors":{"value":["Shangwen Wang","Mingyang Geng","Bo Lin","Zhensu Sun","Ming Wen","Yepang Liu","Li Li","Tegawendé F. Bissyandé","Xiaoguang Mao"]}},"tmdate":1769610417428,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727774506450,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shangwen_Wang1"],"forum":"qjjJhb1LqN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":129669,"cdate":1719792000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1769610417428,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"qjjJhb1LqN","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"VR-SAG is a lightweight post-hoc calibrator that learns semantic groups over frozen embeddings with per-group temperature+bias and intra-group variance regularization; LCCE is an unsupervised logit-space calibration metric."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["calibration","click-through rate prediction","post-hoc calibration","uncertainty quantification","semantic grouping","clustering","proper scoring rules","advertising systems"]},"primary_area":{"value":"probabilistic methods (Bayesian methods, variational inference, sampling, UQ, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate click-through and conversion-rate estimates are pivotal for bid optimization in large-scale advertising, yet modern deep CTR/CVR models are often miscalibrated. Classical global calibrators (Platt scaling, isotonic regression) and feature-based binning struggle to capture latent user-item heterogeneity. We approach calibration through the lens of \\emph{latent, calibration-aware groupings} and propose Variance-Reduced Semantic-Aware Grouping (VR-SAG), a lightweight post-hoc layer over a frozen backbone that (i) learns calibration-aware groups in embedding space, (ii) fits per-group temperature+bias calibrators, and (iii) explicitly penalizes intra-group variance to tighten probability spreads.\nA group-wise decomposition of the Brier score guides our design. It separates within-group variance from score--label covariance and motivates a grouping regularizer. To decouple evaluation from training, we introduce Logit-Cluster Calibration Error (LCCE), an unsupervised fixed-partition metric obtained via $K$-means in logit space; LCCE aligns with the reliability term of proper scores while avoiding pitfalls of trainable grouping heads used as metrics.\nAcross offline CTR logs and AdAuction (an ad-auction dataset with simulator-defined oracle CTRs), VR-SAG improves the reported calibration metrics over the corresponding SAG variants. Its additional computation scales linearly with the number of grouping heads and groups."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026calibration,\ntitle={Calibration Is Grouping: {VR}-{SAG} with Intra-Group Variance Control and Logit-Cluster Evaluation},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=zobgEn3cul},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration Is Grouping: VR-SAG with Intra-Group Variance Control and Logit-Cluster Evaluation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d51fd5c771f08f3db50382f777f549389101f2cb.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791231477301,"tcdate":1789654218907,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission32035/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission32035/Authors"],"forum":"zobgEn3cul","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":32035,"cdate":1789654218907,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission32035/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing"],"mdate":1791231477301,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"zobgEn3cul","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 October Submission"},"research_area_keywords":{"value":"language-to-code generation"},"keywords":{"value":["Code Models","Language Modeling"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026code,\ntitle={Code Is More Than Text: Uncertainty Estimation for Code Generation},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - October 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ZWKtivCS8O},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Code Is More Than Text: Uncertainty Estimation for Code Generation"},"contribution_types":{"value":"NLP engineering experiment"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as code generators, where silently wrong programs pose real safety and reliability risks. Reliable uncertainty estimation (UE) is essential for selective prediction, human-in-the-loop review, and downstream agentic decisions. Yet most existing code UE methods are inherited from natural language (NL) generation and ignore properties that make code distinct. We argue that code differs from NL in three ways: a single wrong token can break an entire program (token fragility); algorithmic intent and concrete implementation can disagree independently (intent–code gap); and programs can be executed (executability). We instantiate these properties as three orthogonal uncertainty axes: lexical (Top-$K$ token entropy), algorithmic (pseudo-code consistency), and functional (behavioral consistency). Across five code LLMs, our three-axis ensemble improves average AUROC from 0.696 for the strongest NL-derived baseline to 0.776 (+8.1 points). Notably, on Qwen3-14B, our single-pass Top-$K$ token entropy matches the strongest multi-pass baseline while being over $3\\times$ cheaper; across models, it remains a competitive low-cost signal. These results suggest that code UE deserves code-specific design rather than direct NL ports."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/0a36707828a0e685da1898278b1191541a3c1194.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"NLP and Code Models"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission"}},"tmdate":1793778850461,"tcdate":1791756583692,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission4590/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission4590/Authors"],"forum":"ZWKtivCS8O","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4590,"cdate":1791756583692,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Responsible_NLP_Checklist_Status","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1793778850461,"odate":1791894541772,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","id":"ZWKtivCS8O","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Crossref"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|aacegen_attention_guided_adversarial_code_example_generation_for_deep_code_models"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3691620.3695500"},"abstract":{"value":"Adversarial code examples are important to investigate the robustness of deep code models. Existing work on adversarial code example generation has shown promising results yet still falls short in practical applications due to either the high number of model invocations or the limited naturalness of generated examples. In this paper, we propose AaceGEN, an attention-guided adversarial code example generation method for deep code models. The key idea of AaceGEN is to utilize the attention distributions behind deep code models to guide the generation of adversarial code examples. As such, the code elements critical for model predictions could be prioritized for exploration, enhancing the effectiveness and efficiency of adversarial code example generation. In addition, AaceGEN implements a code transformation library providing diverse semantic-preserving code transformations for various code elements, and further conducts a search under the constraint of a maximum number of allowable code transformations to generate adversarial code examples with subtlety and stealth. Our extensive experiments on 9 diverse subjects, taking into account different software engineering tasks and varied deep code models, demonstrate that AaceGEN outperforms 3 baseline approaches under comprehensive evaluation."},"title":{"value":"AACEGEN: Attention Guided Adversarial Code Example Generation for Deep Code Models"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhong Li","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Zhong%20Li"},{"fullname":"Chong Zhang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Chong%20Zhang"},{"fullname":"Minxue Pan","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Minxue%20Pan"},{"fullname":"Tian Zhang","username":"~Tian_Zhang8"},{"fullname":"Xuandong Li","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Xuandong%20Li"}]}},"tmdate":1778200754391,"pdate":1729987200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1145/3691620.3695500"],"tcdate":1778200733663,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Tian_Zhang8"],"forum":"VW4TzMnoc0","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":69064,"cdate":1729270572245,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1778200754391,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"VW4TzMnoc0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Neural Networks Learn. Syst. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/5962385/10982361/10580993.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TNN/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"fu|double_confidence_calibration_focused_distillation_for_taskincremental_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhiling_Fu:","~Zhe_Wang15","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chengwei_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinlei_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongdong_Li:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TNNLS.2024.3418811"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tnn/FuWYXL25,\n  author={Zhiling Fu and Zhe Wang and Chengwei Yu and Xinlei Xu and Dongdong Li},\n  title={Double Confidence Calibration Focused Distillation for Task-Incremental Learning},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Neural Networks Learn. Syst.},\n  volume={36},\n  number={5},\n  pages={9070-9083},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TNNLS.2024.3418811}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Task-incremental learning methods that adopt knowledge distillation face two significant challenges: confidence bias and knowledge loss. These challenges make it difficult to effectively balance the stability and plasticity of the network in the incremental learning process. In this article, we propose double confidence calibration focused distillation (DCCFD) to address these challenges. We introduce intratask and intertask confidence calibration (ECC) modules that can mitigate network overconfidence during incremental learning and reduce the degree of feature representation bias. We also propose a focused distillation (FD) module that can alleviate the problem of knowledge loss during the task increment process, improving model stability without reducing plasticity. Experimental results on the CIFAR-100, TinyImageNet, and CORE-50 datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of our method, with performance that matches or exceeds the state of the art. Furthermore, our method can be used as a plug-and-play module to consistently improve class-incremental learning methods."},"title":{"value":"Double Confidence Calibration Focused Distillation for Task-Incremental Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Zhiling Fu","Zhe Wang","Chengwei Yu","Xinlei Xu","Dongdong Li"]}},"tmdate":1753015571995,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1753015535089,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhe_Wang15"],"forum":"FGgqB60YeT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":577180,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753015571995,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"FGgqB60YeT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICBINB 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a1e262d008ea07083b5ae71db9ff17068a0b987d.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Calibraiton","Language Models","In-context Learning"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/ICBINB"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|a_study_on_the_calibration_of_incontext_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Hanlin_Zhang1","~YiFan_Zhang8","~Yaodong_Yu4","~Dhruv_Madeka1","~Dean_Foster1","~Eric_Xing1","~Himabindu_Lakkaraju1","~Sham_M._Kakade1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Modern auto-regressive models are trained to minimize log loss by predicting the next token. As a result, they are expected to get calibrated answers when framing problems as next-token prediction tasks. We study this for in-context learning (ICL), a widely used way to adapt frozen large language models (LLMs) via crafting prompts and investigate the trade-offs between performance and calibration on a wide range of natural language understanding and reasoning tasks. We conduct extensive experiments to show that such trade-offs may get worse as we increase model size, incorporate more ICL examples, and fine-tune models using instruction or dialog tuning on carefully curated datasets. Furthermore, we find that common recalibration techniques that are widely effective such as temperature scaling may provide limited gains for calibration errors, suggesting that new methods may be required for settings where models are expected to be reliable."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhang2024a,\ntitle={A Study on the Calibration of In-context Learning},\nauthor={Hanlin Zhang and YiFan Zhang and Yaodong Yu and Dhruv Madeka and Dean Foster and Eric P. Xing and Himabindu Lakkaraju and Sham M. Kakade},\nbooktitle={I Can't Believe It's Not Better Workshop: Failure Modes in the Age of Foundation Models},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=FWTqwlHBC5}\n}"},"title":{"value":"A Study on the Calibration of In-context Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Hanlin Zhang","YiFan Zhang","Yaodong Yu","Dhruv Madeka","Dean Foster","Eric P. Xing","Himabindu Lakkaraju","Sham M. Kakade"]}},"tmdate":1713923315135,"pdate":1698444214790,"tcdate":1695797617305,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/ICBINB","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/ICBINB/Submission8/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/ICBINB/Submission8/Authors"],"forum":"FWTqwlHBC5","number":8,"cdate":1695797617305,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/ICBINB/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/ICBINB/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/ICBINB/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/ICBINB/Submission8/-/Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/ICBINB/-/PC_Revision"],"mdate":1713923315135,"odate":1696512540468,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/ICBINB","id":"FWTqwlHBC5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10609961/10609862/10610116.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"gossard|ewand_an_extrinsic_calibration_framework_for_wide_baseline_framebased_and_eventbased_camera_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["~Thomas_Gossard1","~Andreas_Ziegler1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Levin_Kolmar:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jonas_Tebbe:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andreas_Zell:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA57147.2024.10610116"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/Gossard0KTZ24,\n  author={Thomas Gossard and Andreas Ziegler and Levin Kolmar and Jonas Tebbe and Andreas Zell},\n  title={eWand: An extrinsic calibration framework for wide baseline frame-based and event-based camera systems},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={14534-14540},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA57147.2024.10610116},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate calibration is crucial for using multiple cameras to triangulate the position of objects precisely. However, it is also a time-consuming process that needs to be repeated for every displacement of the cameras. The standard approach is to use a printed pattern with known geometry to estimate the intrinsic and extrinsic parameters of the cameras. The same idea can be applied to event-based cameras, though it requires extra work. By using frame reconstruction from events, a printed pattern can be detected. A blinking pattern can also be displayed on a screen. Then, the pattern can be directly detected from the events. Such calibration methods can provide accurate intrinsic calibration for both frame- and event-based cameras. However, using 2D patterns has several limitations for multi-camera extrinsic calibration, with cameras possessing highly different points of view and a wide baseline. The 2D pattern can only be detected from one direction and needs to be of significant size to compensate for its distance to the camera. This makes the extrinsic calibration time-consuming and cumbersome. To overcome these limitations, we propose eWand, a new method that uses blinking LEDs inside opaque spheres instead of a printed or displayed pattern. Our method provides a faster, easier-to-use extrinsic calibration approach that maintains high accuracy for both event- and frame-based cameras."},"title":{"value":"eWand: An extrinsic calibration framework for wide baseline frame-based and event-based camera systems"},"authors":{"value":["Thomas Gossard","Andreas Ziegler","Levin Kolmar","Jonas Tebbe","Andreas Zell"]}},"tmdate":1771134642125,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icra/Gossard0KTZ24"],"tcdate":1757439330961,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Andreas_Ziegler1"],"forum":"bThZ0mavIO","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":621522,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1771134642125,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bThZ0mavIO","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2025 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Code-LMs","code-editing","code-generation","software engineering"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\naggarwal2025nextcoder,\ntitle={NextCoder: Robust Adaptation of Code {LM}s to Diverse Code Edits},\nauthor={Tushar Aggarwal and Swayam Singh and Abhijeet Awasthi and Aditya Kanade and Nagarajan Natarajan},\nbooktitle={Forty-second International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=3B6fF1PxYD}\n}"},"title":{"value":"NextCoder: Robust Adaptation of Code LMs to Diverse Code Edits"},"paperhash":{"value":"aggarwal|nextcoder_robust_adaptation_of_code_lms_to_diverse_code_edits"},"TLDR":{"value":"This paper introduce a synthetic data generation pipeline and a robust model adaptation algorithm to train models for diverse code-editing tasks without losing their original code generation abilities."},"primary_area":{"value":"deep_learning->large_language_models"},"abstract":{"value":"Software engineering activities frequently involve edits to existing code. However, contemporary code language models (LMs) lack the ability to handle diverse types of code-edit requirements. In this work, we attempt to overcome this shortcoming through (1) a novel synthetic data generation pipeline and (2) a robust model adaptation algorithm. Starting with seed code examples and diverse editing criteria, our pipeline generates high-quality samples comprising original and modified code, along with natural language instructions in different styles and verbosity. Today's code LMs come bundled with strong abilities, such as code generation and instruction following, which should not be lost due to fine-tuning. To ensure this, we propose a novel adaptation algorithm, SeleKT, that (a) leverages a dense gradient-based step to identify the weights that are most important for code editing, and (b) does a sparse projection onto the base model to avoid overfitting. Using our approach, we obtain a new series of models NextCoder (adapted from QwenCoder-2.5) that achieves strong results on five code-editing benchmarks, outperforming comparable size models and even several larger ones. We show the generality of our approach on two model families DeepSeekCoder and QwenCoder), compare against other fine-tuning approaches, and demonstrate robustness by showing retention of code generation and general problem-solving abilities post adaptation. We opensource the models, synthetic dataset, and implementation at http://aka.ms/nextcoder."},"link_to_code":{"value":"http://aka.ms/nextcoder"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e4c45e8d4642143f7bff681474b7ce9634b0db2d.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"We often need to update or change computer programs by editing their code, but today’s AI code assistants struggle to handle the wide variety of edits people want. In this work, we created two key innovations to help: a method for generating lots of realistic code-edit examples and a smart way to train models on them without losing their existing abilities.\n\nFirst, we built a pipeline that takes sample code (written in different styles) to generate many high-quality code-edit examples. Then, we designed an approach called SeleKT that carefully adjusts the model’s weights, focusing only on the parts most useful for editing while keeping its general programming skills intact.\n\nOur adapted models, called NextCoder, performed impressively on multiple code-editing tests, even beating larger models. We’re sharing the models, dataset, and tools at http://aka.ms/nextcoder so others can explore and use them for their tasks."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2025/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tushar_Aggarwal1","~Swayam_Singh1","~Abhijeet_Awasthi1","~Aditya_Kanade2","~Nagarajan_Natarajan2"]},"authors":{"value":["Tushar Aggarwal","Swayam Singh","Abhijeet Awasthi","Aditya Kanade","Nagarajan Natarajan"]}},"tmdate":1753293130589,"pdate":1746105698919,"tcdate":1737699372542,"writers":["ICML.cc/2025/Conference","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission15174/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission15174/Authors"],"forum":"3B6fF1PxYD","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":15174,"cdate":1737699372542,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission15174/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2025/Conference/Submission15174/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1753293130589,"odate":1750231525056,"domain":"ICML.cc/2025/Conference","id":"3B6fF1PxYD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Virtual Real. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10055-022-00686-8.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/VR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"jackin|design_and_calibration_of_curved_and_seethrough_integral_imaging_3d_display"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Boaz_Jessie_Jackin:","~Lode_Jorissen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ryutaro_Oi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Koki_Wakunami:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kenji_Yamamoto:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yasuyuki_Ichihashi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Philippe_Bekaert:","~Gauthier_Lafruit3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10055-022-00686-8"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/vr/JackinJOWYIBL23,\n  author={Boaz Jessie Jackin and Lode Jorissen and Ryutaro Oi and Koki Wakunami and Kenji Yamamoto and Yasuyuki Ichihashi and Philippe Bekaert and Gauthier Lafruit},\n  title={Design and calibration of curved and see-through integral imaging 3D display},\n  year={2023},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1685577600000},\n  journal={Virtual Real.},\n  volume={27},\n  number={2},\n  pages={761-775},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s10055-022-00686-8}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Heads-up displays that are ‘see-through’ and ‘curved’ and capable of displaying 3D contents are considered crucial for augmented reality-based navigation in automobiles. Here we report the development, calibration and experimental evaluation of a 3D display system that satisfies the above requirements. Integral imaging is used as the 3D display technique, which is realized using a flexible ‘concave-micro-mirror array’ screen (equivalent of a ‘micro-lens array’, but working in reflection mode). The screen itself is fabricated as a holographic optical element. The holographic nature of the screen enables a ‘see-through’ effect. The 3D content to be displayed is served by a 2D projector as integral images. A novel calibration method is developed which employs diffusive markers, that are invisible to the naked eye, being placed at one corner of each elemental micro-mirror. The calibration enables proper treatment of the effects and artifacts caused by screen ‘curvature’, but the presence of markers itself does not degrade the display characteristics. A curved micro-mirror array screen of size 10 cm \\(\\times \\) 10 cm consisting of 100 \\(\\times \\) 200 elemental concave mirrors is fabricated as a flexible holographic optical element with diffusive markers of size 300 \\(\\upmu \\)m \\(\\times \\) 300 \\(\\upmu \\)m. The screen, when illuminated with a projector (that serves integral images), was able to reconstruct a 3D scene of size 10 cm \\(\\times \\) 10 cm with a depth of 5 cm. The novel calibration method employing diffusive markers demonstrates significant improvement in calibration accuracy. The curved and see-through nature of the display screen makes it a good choice for windshield displays. The reported system requires further improvements in enlarging the screen size and increasing depth of the 3D scene in order to meet real-world requirements, which can be achieved by scaling-up the system."},"title":{"value":"Design and calibration of curved and see-through integral imaging 3D display"},"authors":{"value":["Boaz Jessie Jackin","Lode Jorissen","Ryutaro Oi","Koki Wakunami","Kenji Yamamoto","Yasuyuki Ichihashi","Philippe Bekaert","Gauthier Lafruit"]}},"tmdate":1772393900165,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/vr/JackinJOWYIBL23"],"tcdate":1762936295258,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lode_Jorissen1"],"forum":"gfpaBfc2Di","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":691562,"cdate":1685577600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772393900165,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"gfpaBfc2Di","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICIC (2) 2009"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-04020-7_86.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"gao|an_improved_twostage_camera_calibration_method_based_on_particle_swarm_optimization"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-04020-7_86"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icic/GaoNYC09,\n  author={Hongwei Gao and Ben Niu and Yang Yu and Liang Chen},\n  title={An Improved Two-Stage Camera Calibration Method Based on Particle Swarm Optimization},\n  year={2009},\n  cdate={1230768000000},\n  pages={804-813},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-04020-7_86},\n  booktitle={ICIC (2)},\n  crossref={conf/icic/2009-2}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"According to the calibration of binocular vision, an improved two-stage camera calibration method involved with multi-distortion coefficients is introduced in this paper. At the first stage, the 3D points’ coordinate are calculated by the imitated direct linear transformation (DLT) triangulation based on distortion compensation. And at the second stage, particle swarm optimization (PSO) is selected to determine two cameras’ parameters. In this way the parameters of the two cameras can be tuned simultaneously. In order to assist estimating the performance of the proposed method, a new cost function is designed. Simulation and experiment are made under the same calibration data sets. The performance of PSO used to tune the parameters is also compared to that of GA. The experiment results show that the strategy of taking the 3D reconstruction errors as object function is feasible and PSO is the best choice for camera parameters’ optimization."},"title":{"value":"An Improved Two-Stage Camera Calibration Method Based on Particle Swarm Optimization"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Hongwei Gao","username":"~Hongwei_Gao1"},{"fullname":"Ben Niu","username":""},{"fullname":"Yang Yu","username":""},{"fullname":"Liang Chen","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1793865784358,"pdate":1262217600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icic/GaoNYC09"],"tcdate":1793865771351,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Hongwei_Gao1"],"forum":"dybEKJ2DMY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":223710,"cdate":1230768000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1793865784358,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"dybEKJ2DMY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 1998"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel4/5562/14922/00680948.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/1998"},"paperhash":{"value":"calafiore|experiment_design_for_robot_dynamic_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Giuseppe_Carlo_Calafiore1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marina_Indri:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.1998.680948"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/CalafioreI98,\n  author={Giuseppe Carlo Calafiore and Marina Indri},\n  title={Experiment Design for Robot Dynamic Calibration},\n  year={1998},\n  cdate={883612800000},\n  pages={3303-3309},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.1998.680948},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/1998}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Common robot calibration procedures use least-squares (LS) techniques to obtain estimates of the identifiable parameters. The \"quality\" of the resulting estimates depends significantly upon the used excitation input. The search for the best excitation trajectory is usually posed as a nonlinear path optimization problem aimed at optimizing suitable measures of the LS normal equations matrix. In this paper a parametrization of the class of reference joints trajectories is introduced and a solution framework based on genetic evolution is proposed. The efficiency of the method is illustrated by experimental tests on a SCARA two-link manipulator. Issues related to data acquisition and signals reconstruction and filtering are also discussed."},"title":{"value":"Experiment Design for Robot Dynamic Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Giuseppe Carlo Calafiore","Marina Indri"]}},"tmdate":1714976059004,"pdate":883612800000,"tcdate":1714976047756,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Giuseppe_Calafiore1"],"forum":"p7lNiW0o1z","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":6168,"cdate":883612800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1714976059004,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"p7lNiW0o1z","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Remote Sensing Image-Text Retrieval"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Remote sensing image-text retrieval (RSITR) aims to match remote sensing images with their corresponding natural language descriptions. Existing methods primarily focus on learning a discriminative cross-modal space, while paying insufficient attention to the collaborative calibration of image-text correspondences at the levels of prior supervision and posterior scores. Such calibration can facilitate both the learning of this space and the subsequent refinement of retrieval scores. To address this limitation, we propose P$^{2}$Cal, a prior-posterior collaborative calibration framework for robust RSITR. Specifically, we first propose a Prior Semantic Calibration Module (PSCM), which purifies supervision through prior semantic calibration to obtain highly reliable supervisory signals. These signals are then used to train our proposed Manifold Residual Adapter (MRA) and Posterior Score Calibration Flow (PSCF). MRA incorporates lightweight manifold residual adapters into the representation pathways to construct a stable discriminative score space. PSCF takes the resulting matching scores as its initial state and learns a continuous residual velocity field to progressively optimize retrieval scores. Extensive experiments on multiple RSITR datasets demonstrate that P$^{2}$Cal consistently outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026pcal,\ntitle={P\\${\\textasciicircum}\\{2\\}\\$Cal: A Prior-Posterior Collaborative Calibration Framework for Robust Remote Sensing Image-Text Retrieval},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=4XWcFayyx0},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"P$^{2}$Cal: A Prior-Posterior Collaborative Calibration Framework for Robust Remote Sensing Image-Text Retrieval"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/82b5f5ab706a6d52f192e543119af84b34bb0c61.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791222737859,"tcdate":1788486484786,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission8810/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission8810/Authors"],"forum":"4XWcFayyx0","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":8810,"cdate":1788486484786,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission8810/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791222737859,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"4XWcFayyx0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.19851v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|planehec_efficient_handeye_calibration_for_multiview_robotic_arm_via_any_point_cloud_plane_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ye_Wang:","~Haodong_Jing1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Liao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yongqiang_Ma:","~Nanning_Zheng1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.19851"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2507-19851,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ye Wang and Haodong Jing and Yang Liao and Yongqiang Ma and Nanning Zheng},\n  title={PlaneHEC: Efficient Hand-Eye Calibration for Multi-view Robotic Arm via Any Point Cloud Plane Detection},\n  year={2025},\n  month={July},\n  cdate={1751328000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2507.19851},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.19851}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Hand-eye calibration is an important task in vision-guided robotic systems and is crucial for determining the transformation matrix between the camera coordinate system and the robot end-effector. Existing methods, for multi-view robotic systems, usually rely on accurate geometric models or manual assistance, generalize poorly, and can be very complicated and inefficient. Therefore, in this study, we propose PlaneHEC, a generalized hand-eye calibration method that does not require complex models and can be accomplished using only depth cameras, which achieves the optimal and fastest calibration results using arbitrary planar surfaces like walls and tables. PlaneHEC introduces hand-eye calibration equations based on planar constraints, which makes it strongly interpretable and generalizable. PlaneHEC also uses a comprehensive solution that starts with a closed-form solution and improves it withiterative optimization, which greatly improves accuracy. We comprehensively evaluated the performance of PlaneHEC in both simulated and real-world environments and compared the results with other point-cloud-based calibration methods, proving its superiority. Our approach achieves universal and fast calibration with an innovative design of computational models, providing a strong contribution to the development of multi-agent systems and embodied intelligence."},"title":{"value":"PlaneHEC: Efficient Hand-Eye Calibration for Multi-view Robotic Arm via Any Point Cloud Plane Detection"},"authors":{"value":["Ye Wang","Haodong Jing","Yang Liao","Yongqiang Ma","Nanning Zheng"]}},"tmdate":1769158285762,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2507-19851"],"tcdate":1768456956228,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~HaodongJing1"],"forum":"MRHJaCO5v0","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":758783,"cdate":1751328000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1769158285762,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"MRHJaCO5v0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11127273/11127223/11127326.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|planehec_efficient_handeye_calibration_for_multiview_robotic_arm_via_any_point_cloud_plane_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ye_Wang:","~Haodong_Jing1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Liao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yongqiang_Ma:","~Nanning_Zheng1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA55743.2025.11127326"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/WangJLMZ25,\n  author={Ye Wang and Haodong Jing and Yang Liao and Yongqiang Ma and Nanning Zheng},\n  title={PlaneHEC: Efficient Hand-Eye Calibration for Multi-View Robotic Arm via Any Point Cloud Plane Detection},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={648-654},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICRA55743.2025.11127326},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Hand-eye calibration is an important task in vision-guided robotic systems and is crucial for determining the transformation matrix between the camera coordinate system and the robot end-effector. Existing methods, for multi-view robotic systems, usually rely on accurate geometric models or manual assistance, generalize poorly, and can be very complicated and inefficient. Therefore, in this study, we propose PlaneHEC, a generalized hand-eye calibration method that does not require complex models and can be accomplished using only depth cameras, which achieves the optimal and fastest calibration results using arbitrary planar surfaces like walls and tables. PlaneHEC introduces hand-eye calibration equations based on planar constraints, which makes it strongly interpretable and generalizable. PlaneHEC also uses a comprehensive solution that starts with a closed-form solution and improves it with iterative optimization, which greatly improves accuracy. We comprehensively evaluated the performance of PlaneHEC in both simulated and real-world environments and compared the results with other point-cloud-based calibration methods, proving its superiority. Our approach achieves universal and fast calibration with an innovative design of computational models, providing a strong contribution to the development of multi-agent systems and embodied intelligence."},"title":{"value":"PlaneHEC: Efficient Hand-Eye Calibration for Multi-View Robotic Arm via Any Point Cloud Plane Detection"},"authors":{"value":["Ye Wang","Haodong Jing","Yang Liao","Yongqiang Ma","Nanning Zheng"]}},"tmdate":1769158285687,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icra/WangJLMZ25"],"tcdate":1768456956232,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~HaodongJing1"],"forum":"pdHzyYpwuT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":758784,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1769158285687,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"pdHzyYpwuT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.09115v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"xia|gcc_generative_calibration_clustering"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haifeng_Xia:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hai_Huang:","~Zhengming_Ding5"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.09115"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2404-09115,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Haifeng Xia and Hai Huang and Zhengming Ding},\n  title={GCC: Generative Calibration Clustering},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2404.09115},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.09115}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep clustering as an important branch of unsupervised representation learning focuses on embedding semantically similar samples into the identical feature space. This core demand inspires the exploration of contrastive learning and subspace clustering. However, these solutions always rely on the basic assumption that there are sufficient and category-balanced samples for generating valid high-level representation. This hypothesis actually is too strict to be satisfied for real-world applications. To overcome such a challenge, the natural strategy is utilizing generative models to augment considerable instances. How to use these novel samples to effectively fulfill clustering performance improvement is still difficult and under-explored. In this paper, we propose a novel Generative Calibration Clustering (GCC) method to delicately incorporate feature learning and augmentation into clustering procedure. First, we develop a discriminative feature alignment mechanism to discover intrinsic relationship across real and generated samples. Second, we design a self-supervised metric learning to generate more reliable cluster assignment to boost the conditional diffusion generation. Extensive experimental results on three benchmarks validate the effectiveness and advantage of our proposed method over the state-of-the-art methods."},"title":{"value":"GCC: Generative Calibration Clustering"},"authors":{"value":["Haifeng Xia","Hai Huang","Zhengming Ding"]}},"tmdate":1727484880540,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727484858783,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhengming_Ding5"],"forum":"MaZpPRzEpX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":96198,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727484880540,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"MaZpPRzEpX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IGARSS 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10640349/10640352/10642132.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IGARSS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|assessment_and_correction_of_fy3dhiras_spectral_calibration_error_caused_by_silica_gel_gas_contamination"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junwei_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chengli_Qi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiuqing_Hu:","~Zefeng_Zhang2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lu_Lee:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Panxiang_Zhang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS53475.2024.10642132"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/igarss/WangQHZLZ24,\n  author={Junwei Wang and Chengli Qi and Xiuqing Hu and Zefeng Zhang and Lu Lee and Panxiang Zhang},\n  title={Assessment and Correction of FY-3D/HIRAS Spectral Calibration Error Caused by Silica Gel Gas Contamination},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={6412-6415},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS53475.2024.10642132},\n  booktitle={IGARSS},\n  crossref={conf/igarss/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The Hyperspectral Infrared Atmospheric Sounder (HIRAS) carried on the FengYun(FY)-3D satellite is a Fourier transform infrared spectrometer, which can improve the vertical detection capability of the atmosphere. The radiometric calibration accuracy of HIRAS depends on accurate spectral calibration. During the operation of the satellite, the spectral calibration accuracy of HIRAS will change continuously due to the mirror misalignment and the volatile silica gel gas appearing in the path of the interferometer optical system. Here the long-term absolute accuracy of the HIRAS spectral calibration is assessed by a forward radiative transfer model through a cross-correlation method. Subsequently, A two-step method is developed to determine the final configurations of the four detectors on each of the three HIRAS focal planes. The off-axis apodization correction is then performed using the dynamically updated off-axis parameters for all the detectors, achieving a spectral calibration accuracy superior to 2 ppm."},"title":{"value":"Assessment and Correction of FY-3D/HIRAS Spectral Calibration Error Caused by Silica Gel Gas Contamination"},"authors":{"value":["Junwei Wang","Chengli Qi","Xiuqing Hu","Zefeng Zhang","Lu Lee","Panxiang Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1762435737612,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/igarss/WangQHZLZ24"],"tcdate":1762435735664,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zefeng_Zhang2"],"forum":"IT41JfSzdz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":666630,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762435737612,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"IT41JfSzdz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Time Series","Time Series Forecasting","Test-Time Adaptation"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/06b789e15ffafe239dc3a3f4a87cc5c13918c2df.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"learning on time series and dynamical systems"},"abstract":{"value":"Non-stationarity induces test-time distribution shifts in time series forecasting (TSF), resulting in forecast residuals between predictions and the corresponding ground truth.\nRecent works on test-time adaptation for TSF address this by introducing lightweight calibration modules around frozen forecasters, updated using observations that become incrementally available at test time.\nExisting approaches typically employ channel-wise adapters, implicitly assuming that forecast residuals are inherently channel-specific.\nHowever, we observe that forecast residuals often exhibit substantial shared structure across channels, suggesting that fully independent channel-wise parameterization may be redundant.\nMotivated by this observation, we propose the \\textbf{D}ual-\\textbf{B}ranch \\textbf{C}alibration \\textbf{M}odule (DBCM), which shares adaptation capacity across channels through a shared adapter while retaining limited channel-specific flexibility through a low-rank channel-wise adapter, further enhanced by channel mixing and nonlinear gated modulation.\nExtensive experiments show that DBCM consistently outperforms prior methods across diverse datasets, horizons, and backbones. Code will be released."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026dbcm,\ntitle={{DBCM}: Dual-Branch Calibration Module for Test-Time Adaptation in Time Series Forecasting},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=QsxRSVWNAe},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"DBCM: Dual-Branch Calibration Module for Test-Time Adaptation in Time Series Forecasting"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/316660a3d2c8199f7a63f150e5fd96696c906074.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791226123305,"tcdate":1789091221416,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission13610/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission13610/Authors"],"forum":"QsxRSVWNAe","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":13610,"cdate":1789091221416,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission13610/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791226123305,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"QsxRSVWNAe","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.17844v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|thcmcal_temporalhierarchical_causal_modelling_with_conformal_calibration_for_clinical_risk_prediction"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xin_Zhang39","~Qiyu_Wei1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yingjie_Zhu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fanyi_Wu:","~Sophia_Ananiadou1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2506.17844"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2506-17844,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Xin Zhang and Qiyu Wei and Yingjie Zhu and Fanyi Wu and Sophia Ananiadou},\n  title={THCM-CAL: Temporal-Hierarchical Causal Modelling with Conformal Calibration for Clinical Risk Prediction},\n  year={2025},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1748736000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2506.17844},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2506.17844}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Automated clinical risk prediction from electronic health records (EHRs) demands modeling both structured diagnostic codes and unstructured narrative notes. However, most prior approaches either handle these modalities separately or rely on simplistic fusion strategies that ignore the directional, hierarchical causal interactions by which narrative observations precipitate diagnoses and propagate risk across admissions. In this paper, we propose THCM-CAL, a Temporal-Hierarchical Causal Model with Conformal Calibration. Our framework constructs a multimodal causal graph where nodes represent clinical entities from two modalities: Textual propositions extracted from notes and ICD codes mapped to textual descriptions. Through hierarchical causal discovery, THCM-CAL infers three clinically grounded interactions: intra-slice same-modality sequencing, intra-slice cross-modality triggers, and inter-slice risk propagation. To enhance prediction reliability, we extend conformal prediction to multi-label ICD coding, calibrating per-code confidence intervals under complex co-occurrences. Experimental results on MIMIC-III and MIMIC-IV demonstrate the superiority of THCM-CAL."},"title":{"value":"THCM-CAL: Temporal-Hierarchical Causal Modelling with Conformal Calibration for Clinical Risk Prediction"},"authors":{"value":["Xin Zhang","Qiyu Wei","Yingjie Zhu","Fanyi Wu","Sophia Ananiadou"]}},"tmdate":1770541964669,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2506-17844"],"tcdate":1758828212833,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wei_Qiyu1"],"forum":"MpgrHNei9I","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":629706,"cdate":1748736000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1770541964669,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"MpgrHNei9I","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Access 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/6287639/10380310/10550442.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ACCESS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"han|unical_a_universal_aidriven_model_for_air_pollutant_sensor_calibration_with_domainspecific_knowledge_inputs"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yang_Han8","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shiguang_Song:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yangwen_Yu:","~Jacqueline_C._K._Lam1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Victor_O._K._Li:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2024.3410171"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/access/HanSYLL24,\n  author={Yang Han and Shiguang Song and Yangwen Yu and Jacqueline C. K. Lam and Victor O. K. Li},\n  title={UNI-CAL: A Universal AI-Driven Model for Air Pollutant Sensor Calibration With Domain-Specific Knowledge Inputs},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Access},\n  volume={12},\n  pages={126531-126544},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2024.3410171}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Portable Sensor Nodes (PSNs) can supplement geographically sparse government-run static air quality monitoring stations (AQMSs). A PSN typically consists of several low-cost pollution sensors for different air pollutants, which must be calibrated to improve the accuracy of measurements. These sensors can be co-located with the high accuracy monitoring equipment (HAME) at AQMSs for calibration. Existing studies have suggested that different pollution sensors may favor different calibration models; even the same pollution sensors in different PSNs may favor different models. However, it is impractical to co-locate each PSN with HAME due to limited access to AQMSs, making large-scale sensor calibration difficult. This study proposes UNI-CAL for calibrating different pollutants, including nitrogen dioxide (NO2), ozone (O3), and particulate matter (PM2.5 and PM10), based on a novel AI-driven model with residual blocks capturing the complex non-linear interactions of raw measurements plus citywide domain-specific information, including meteorology, background pollution, and temporal characteristics. UNI-CAL further allows transfer calibration, i.e., the calibration of sensors from calibrated ones. UNI-CAL has improved the performance of direct calibration by 3.143% on average compared to the best baseline across all pollutants and PSNs on all evaluation metrics. Moreover, domain-specific information has significantly improved the direct calibration performance of UNI-CAL by 4.852% on average. Furthermore, UNI-CAL has demonstrated a strong capability in transfer calibration and achieved the best performance in most scenarios after incorporating domain-specific information. In the future, one can collect more data covering different environmental conditions and explore advanced semi-supervised learning techniques to improve the consistency, robustness, generalizability, and transferability of the proposed calibration framework."},"title":{"value":"UNI-CAL: A Universal AI-Driven Model for Air Pollutant Sensor Calibration With Domain-Specific Knowledge Inputs"},"authors":{"value":["Yang Han","Shiguang Song","Yangwen Yu","Jacqueline C. K. Lam","Victor O. K. Li"]}},"tmdate":1747806385763,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747096185958,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yang_Han8"],"forum":"mR1mVprA2F","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":429235,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747806385763,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"mR1mVprA2F","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"re-ranking, free-text/natural language explanations, calibration/uncertainty"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2024 June Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2024explain,\ntitle={Explain then Rank: Scale Calibration of Neural Rankers Using Natural Language Explanations from {LLM}s},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - June 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ct8iMUwc9m},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Explain then Rank: Scale Calibration of Neural Rankers Using Natural Language Explanations from LLMs"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment"]},"abstract":{"value":"In search settings, calibrating the scores during the ranking process to quantities such as click-through rates or relevance levels enhances a system's usefulness and trustworthiness for downstream users. While previous research has improved this notion of calibration for low complexity learning-to-rank models, the larger data demands and parameter count specific to modern neural text rankers produce unique obstacles that hamper the efficacy of methods intended for the learning-to-rank setting.\n\nThis paper proposes exploiting large language models (LLMs) to provide relevance and uncertainty signals for these neural text rankers to produce scale-calibrated scores  through Monte Carlo sampling of natural language explanations (NLEs). Our approach transforms the neural ranking task from ranking textual query-document pairs to ranking corresponding synthesized NLEs. Comprehensive experiments on two popular document ranking datasets show that the NLE-based calibration approach consistently outperforms past calibration methods and LLM-based methods for ranking, calibration, and query performance prediction tasks."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/88f3ae56cf0f6e54974d39509cccd6c61eccb034.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Information Retrieval and Text Mining"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794065352949,"tcdate":1718475428398,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission2849/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/Submission2849/Authors"],"forum":"ct8iMUwc9m","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2849,"cdate":1718475428398,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794065352949,"odate":1719930600391,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/June","id":"ct8iMUwc9m","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (Findings) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.1167.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"yu|explain_then_rank_scale_calibration_of_neural_rankers_using_natural_language_explanations_from_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["~Puxuan_Yu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daniel_Cohen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hemank_Lamba:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Joel_R._Tetreault:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alejandro_Jaimes:"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.1167/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/YuCLTJ25,\n  author={Puxuan Yu and Daniel Cohen and Hemank Lamba and Joel R. Tetreault and Alejandro Jaimes},\n  title={Explain then Rank: Scale Calibration of Neural Rankers Using Natural Language Explanations from LLMs},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={22716-22730},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.1167/},\n  booktitle={ACL (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2025f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In search settings, calibrating the scores during the ranking process to quantities such as click-through rates or relevance levels enhances a system’s usefulness and trustworthiness for downstream users. While previous research has improved this notion of calibration for low complexity learning-to-rank models, the larger data demands and parameter count specific to modern neural text rankers produce unique obstacles that hamper the efficacy of methods intended for the learning-to-rank setting.This paper proposes exploiting large language models (LLMs) to provide relevance and uncertainty signals for these neural text rankers to produce scale-calibrated scores through Monte Carlo sampling of natural language explanations (NLEs). Our approach transforms the neural ranking task from ranking textual query-document pairs to ranking corresponding synthesized NLEs. Comprehensive experiments on two popular document ranking datasets show that the NLE-based calibration approach consistently outperforms past calibration methods and LLM-based methods for ranking, calibration, and query performance prediction tasks."},"title":{"value":"Explain then Rank: Scale Calibration of Neural Rankers Using Natural Language Explanations from LLMs"},"authors":{"value":["Puxuan Yu","Daniel Cohen","Hemank Lamba","Joel R. Tetreault","Alejandro Jaimes"]}},"tmdate":1768375010706,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/YuCLTJ25"],"tcdate":1768375009071,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Puxuan_Yu1"],"forum":"28VCTfS005","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":749680,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768375010706,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"28VCTfS005","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Pattern Anal. Mach. Intell. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/34/10345401/10293257.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/PAMI/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"park|fewshot_calibration_of_set_predictors_via_metalearned_crossvalidationbased_conformal_prediction"},"authorids":{"value":["~Sangwoo_Park1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kfir_M._Cohen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Osvaldo_Simeone:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2023.3327300"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/pami/ParkCS24,\n  author={Sangwoo Park and Kfir M. Cohen and Osvaldo Simeone},\n  title={Few-Shot Calibration of Set Predictors via Meta-Learned Cross-Validation-Based Conformal Prediction},\n  year={2024},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Pattern Anal. Mach. Intell.},\n  volume={46},\n  number={1},\n  pages={280-291},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2023.3327300}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Conventional frequentist learning is known to yield poorly calibrated models that fail to reliably quantify the uncertainty of their decisions. Bayesian learning can improve calibration, but formal guarantees apply only under restrictive assumptions about correct model specification. Conformal prediction (CP) offers a general framework for the design of set predictors with calibration guarantees that hold regardless of the underlying data generation mechanism. However, when training data are limited, CP tends to produce large, and hence uninformative, predicted sets. This paper introduces a novel meta-learning solution that aims at reducing the set prediction size. Unlike prior work, the proposed meta-learning scheme, referred to as meta-XB, i) builds on cross-validation-based CP, rather than the less efficient validation-based CP; and ii) preserves formal per-task calibration guarantees, rather than less stringent task-marginal guarantees. Finally, meta-XB is extended to adaptive non-conformal scores, which are shown empirically to further enhance marginal per-input calibration."},"title":{"value":"Few-Shot Calibration of Set Predictors via Meta-Learned Cross-Validation-Based Conformal Prediction"},"authors":{"value":["Sangwoo Park","Kfir M. Cohen","Osvaldo Simeone"]}},"tmdate":1727781867509,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727781858330,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sangwoo_Park1"],"forum":"fTNdfAz5pM","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":131103,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727781867509,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"fTNdfAz5pM","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote. Sens. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/36/10807682/11071868.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TGRS/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"sun|examination_of_longterm_fengyun4_agri_reflective_solar_bands_calibration_using_cloud_targets"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chengjie_Sun1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chao_Liu_0013:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Feng_Lu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fukun_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ran_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhiwei_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shihao_Tang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Byung-Ju_Sohn:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2025.3585943"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tgrs/SunLLWXWTS25,\n  author={Chengjie Sun and Chao Liu and Feng Lu and Fukun Wang and Ran Xu and Zhiwei Wang and Shihao Tang and Byung-Ju Sohn},\n  title={Examination of Long-Term Fengyun-4 AGRI Reflective Solar Bands Calibration Using Cloud Targets},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={63},\n  pages={1-13},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2025.3585943}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Fengyun-4 (FY-4) is a series of Chinese operational geostationary meteorological satellites, providing crucial data for weather forecasting, climate prediction, and environmental monitoring. Advanced geostationary radiation imagers (AGRIs) onboard the FY-4A and FY-4B satellites play a key role in observing the Earth’s surface, oceans, and atmosphere. However, their calibration stability is still uncertain, which clearly limits corresponding downstream applications. This study evaluates the long-term radiometric stability of AGRI reflective solar bands (RSBs) using a general cloud target (CT) calibration method, covering the periods from March 2018 to December 2024 for FY-4A/AGRI and from June 2022 to December 2024 for FY-4B/AGRI. By utilizing MODIS cloud products as references for cloud properties, we simulate the top-of-atmosphere (TOA) reflectances of CTs through the Discrete Ordinates Radiative Transfer (DISORT) model and compare results with observed reflectances to infer the instrumental calibration stability. Our results indicate that the radiometric responses of AGRI exhibit significant degradation in visible (VIS) bands, while showing relatively smaller degradation rates in near- and shortwave-infrared bands. Specifically, the annual degradation rates for band 1 ( $0.47~\\mu $ m) of FY-4A/AGRI and FY-4B/AGRI are 4.3% and 8.8%, respectively. Both AGRIs demonstrate comparable degradation rates of approximately 3.5% for band 2 ( $0.65~\\mu $ m). In contrast, bands 3 ( $0.83~\\mu $ m), 5 ( $1.61~\\mu $ m), and 6 ( $2.25~\\mu $ m) show annual degradation rates around −1.0%, despite they exhibit notable fluctuations. The operational calibration of FY-4B/AGRI is more accurate than that of FY-4A/AGRI and with smaller fluctuations. By fitting the time series of relative errors (REs) between simulated and current calibrated reflectances, we calculate daily recalibration coefficients and effectively recalibrate the long-term data, with a calibration accuracy within ±3%. This study demonstrates that the CT-based calibration method can successfully track the radiometric stability of AGRI and provide a robust calibration solution to ensure data stability and accuracy."},"title":{"value":"Examination of Long-Term Fengyun-4 AGRI Reflective Solar Bands Calibration Using Cloud Targets"},"authors":{"value":["Chengjie Sun","Chao Liu","Feng Lu","Fukun Wang","Ran Xu","Zhiwei Wang","Shihao Tang","Byung-Ju Sohn"]}},"tmdate":1768830881683,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tgrs/SunLLWXWTS25"],"tcdate":1768830867786,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chengjie_Sun1"],"forum":"usxxpfrxKo","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":771284,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768830881683,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"usxxpfrxKo","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"logical reasoning, reasoning"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2024 April Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2024code,\ntitle={Code Prompting Elicits Conditional Reasoning Abilities in Text+Code {LLM}s},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - April 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=6OZm8mkwrp},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Code Prompting Elicits Conditional Reasoning Abilities in Text+Code LLMs"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","NLP engineering experiment"]},"abstract":{"value":"Reasoning is a fundamental component of language understanding.  Recent prompting techniques, such as chain of thought, have consistently improved LLMs' performance on various reasoning tasks. Nevertheless, there is still little understanding of what triggers reasoning abilities in LLMs in the inference stage. In this paper, we investigate the effect of the input representation on the reasoning abilities of LLMs. We hypothesize that representing natural language tasks as code can enhance specific reasoning abilities such as entity tracking or logical reasoning. To study this, we propose code prompting, a methodology we operationalize as a chain of prompts that transforms a natural language problem into code and directly prompts the LLM using the generated code without resorting to external code execution. We find that code prompting exhibits a high-performance boost for multiple LLMs (up to 22.52 percentage points on GPT 3.5, 7.75 on Mixtral, and 16.78 on Mistral) across multiple conditional reasoning datasets. We then conduct comprehensive experiments to understand how the code representation triggers reasoning abilities and which capabilities are elicited in the underlying models. Our analysis on GPT 3.5 reveals that the code formatting of the input problem is essential for performance improvement. Furthermore, the code representation improves sample efficiency of in-context learning and facilitates state tracking of entities."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/1682b97ff22e96efad345b2df76152551f0a0720.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Question Answering"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794063135116,"tcdate":1713216033601,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission385/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission385/Authors"],"forum":"6OZm8mkwrp","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":385,"cdate":1713216033601,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794063135116,"odate":1713279540366,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April","id":"6OZm8mkwrp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.629.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"puerto|code_prompting_elicits_conditional_reasoning_abilities_in_textcode_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["~Haritz_Puerto1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Martin_Tutek:","~Somak_Aditya1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaodan_Zhu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Iryna_Gurevych:"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.629"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/PuertoTAZG24,\n  author={Haritz Puerto and Martin Tutek and Somak Aditya and Xiaodan Zhu and Iryna Gurevych},\n  title={Code Prompting Elicits Conditional Reasoning Abilities in Text+Code LLMs},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={11234-11258},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.629},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Reasoning is a fundamental component of language understanding. Recent prompting techniques, such as chain of thought, have consistently improved LLMs’ performance on various reasoning tasks. Nevertheless, there is still little understanding of what triggers reasoning abilities in LLMs in the inference stage. In this paper, we investigate the effect of the input representation on the reasoning abilities of LLMs. We hypothesize that representing natural language tasks as code can enhance specific reasoning abilities such as entity tracking or logical reasoning. To study this, we propose code prompting, a methodology we operationalize as a chain of prompts that transforms a natural language problem into code and directly prompts the LLM using the generated code without resorting to external code execution. We find that code prompting exhibits a high-performance boost for multiple LLMs (up to 22.52 percentage points on GPT 3.5, 7.75 on Mixtral, and 16.78 on Mistral) across multiple conditional reasoning datasets. We then conduct comprehensive experiments to understand how the code representation triggers reasoning abilities and which capabilities are elicited in the underlying models. Our analysis on GPT 3.5 reveals that the code formatting of the input problem is essential for performance improvement. Furthermore, the code representation improves sample efficiency of in-context learning and facilitates state tracking of entities."},"title":{"value":"Code Prompting Elicits Conditional Reasoning Abilities in Text+Code LLMs"},"authors":{"value":["Haritz Puerto","Martin Tutek","Somak Aditya","Xiaodan Zhu","Iryna Gurevych"]}},"tmdate":1773051481542,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747057037722,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Somak_Aditya1"],"forum":"5FyfIefGpC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":418114,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1773051481542,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5FyfIefGpC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"FSE 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/381ef00be9006675f57578f5c73c2a6c2855328e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"wu|an_empirical_study_of_code_clones_from_commercial_ai_code_generators"},"authorids":{"value":["~Weibin_Wu1","~Haoxuan_Hu1","~Zhaoji_Fan1","~Yitong_Qiao1","~Yizhan_Huang1","~Yichen_LI6","~Zibin_Zheng1","~Michael_R._Lyu1"]},"html":{"value":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3729397"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep learning (DL) has revolutionized various software engineering tasks. Particularly, the emergence of AI code generators has pushed the boundaries of automatic programming to synthesize entire programs based on user-defined specifications in natural language. However, it remains a mystery if these AI code generators rely on the copy-and-paste programming practice, resulting in code clone concerns. In this work, to comprehensively study the code cloning behavior of AI code generators, we conduct an empirical study on three state-of-the-art commercial AI code generators to investigate the existence of all types of clones, which remains underexplored. Our experimental results show that the total Type-1 and Type-2 clone rates of the state-of-the-art commercial AI code generators can reach up to 7.50%, indicating marked code clone issues. Furthermore, it is observed that AI code generators risk infringing copyrights and propagating buggy and vulnerable code resulting from cloning code and show a certain degree of stability in generating code clones."},"title":{"value":"An Empirical Study of Code Clones from Commercial AI Code Generators"},"authors":{"value":["Weibin Wu","Haoxuan Hu","Zhaoji Fan","Yitong Qiao","Yizhan Huang","Yichen LI","Zibin Zheng","Michael R. Lyu"]}},"tmdate":1793898310843,"pdate":1750262400000,"tcdate":1791950717409,"writers":["~Weibin_Wu1","~Haoxuan_Hu1","~Zhaoji_Fan1","~Yitong_Qiao1","~Yizhan_Huang1","~Yichen_LI6","~Zibin_Zheng1","~Michael_R._Lyu1"],"signatures":["~Yitong_Qiao1"],"forum":"taT9QWcipD","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":55907,"cdate":1791950717409,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1793898310843,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"taT9QWcipD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Proc. ACM Softw. Eng. 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/PACMSE/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wu|an_empirical_study_of_code_clones_from_commercial_ai_code_generators"},"authorids":{"value":["~Weibin_Wu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haoxuan_Hu:","~Zhaoji_Fan1","~Yitong_Qiao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yizhan_Huang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yichen_Li_0003:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zibin_Zheng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Michael_R._Lyu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3729397"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/pacmse/WuHFQHLZL25,\n  author={Weibin Wu and Haoxuan Hu and Zhaoji Fan and Yitong Qiao and Yizhan Huang and Yichen Li and Zibin Zheng and Michael R. Lyu},\n  title={An Empirical Study of Code Clones from Commercial AI Code Generators},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={Proc. ACM Softw. Eng.},\n  volume={2},\n  number={FSE},\n  pages={2874-2896},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3729397}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep learning (DL) has revolutionized various software engineering tasks. Particularly, the emergence of AI code generators has pushed the boundaries of automatic programming to synthesize entire programs based on user-defined specifications in natural language. However, it remains a mystery if these AI code generators rely on the copy-and-paste programming practice, resulting in code clone concerns. In this work, to comprehensively study the code cloning behavior of AI code generators, we conduct an empirical study on three state-of-the-art commercial AI code generators to investigate the existence of all types of clones, which remains underexplored. Our experimental results show that the total Type-1 and Type-2 clone rates of the state-of-the-art commercial AI code generators can reach up to 7.50%, indicating marked code clone issues. Furthermore, it is observed that AI code generators risk infringing copyrights and propagating buggy and vulnerable code resulting from cloning code and show a certain degree of stability in generating code clones."},"title":{"value":"An Empirical Study of Code Clones from Commercial AI Code Generators"},"authors":{"value":["Weibin Wu","Haoxuan Hu","Zhaoji Fan","Yitong Qiao","Yizhan Huang","Yichen Li","Zibin Zheng","Michael R. Lyu"]}},"tmdate":1767755351049,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/pacmse/WuHFQHLZL25"],"tcdate":1756869061740,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Weibin_Wu1"],"forum":"4vkHyVPPXk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":619910,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1767755351049,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4vkHyVPPXk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.01407v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"horn|extrinsic_infrastructure_calibration_using_the_handeye_robotworld_formulation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Markus_Horn:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Thomas_Wodtko:","~Michael_Buchholz1","~Klaus_Dietmayer1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2305.01407"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2305-01407,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Markus Horn and Thomas Wodtko and Michael Buchholz and Klaus Dietmayer},\n  title={Extrinsic Infrastructure Calibration Using the Hand-Eye Robot-World Formulation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2305.01407},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2305.01407}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We propose a certifiably globally optimal approach for solving the hand-eye robot-world problem supporting multiple sensors and targets at once. Further, we leverage this formulation for estimating a geo-referenced calibration of infrastructure sensors. Since vehicle motion recorded by infrastructure sensors is mostly planar, obtaining a unique solution for the respective hand-eye robot-world problem is unfeasible without incorporating additional knowledge. Hence, we extend our proposed method to include a-priori knowledge, i.e., the translation norm of calibration targets, to yield a unique solution. Our approach achieves state-of-the-art results on simulated and real-world data. Especially on real-world intersection data, our approach utilizing the translation norm is the only method providing accurate results."},"title":{"value":"Extrinsic Infrastructure Calibration Using the Hand-Eye Robot-World Formulation"},"authors":{"value":["Markus Horn","Thomas Wodtko","Michael Buchholz","Klaus Dietmayer"]}},"tmdate":1794048259472,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1746706690546,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Michael_Buchholz1"],"forum":"yGj0MHIORt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":413181,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1794048259472,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"yGj0MHIORt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IV 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10186382/10186383/10186703.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IVS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"horn|extrinsic_infrastructure_calibration_using_the_handeye_robotworld_formulation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Markus_Horn:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Thomas_Wodtko:","~Michael_Buchholz1","~Klaus_Dietmayer1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IV55152.2023.10186703"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ivs/HornWBD23,\n  author={Markus Horn and Thomas Wodtko and Michael Buchholz and Klaus Dietmayer},\n  title={Extrinsic Infrastructure Calibration Using the Hand-Eye Robot-World Formulation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1-8},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IV55152.2023.10186703},\n  booktitle={IV},\n  crossref={conf/ivs/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We propose a certifiably globally optimal approach for solving the hand-eye robot-world problem supporting multiple sensors and targets at once. Further, we leverage this formulation for estimating a geo-referenced calibration of infrastructure sensors. Since vehicle motion recorded by infrastructure sensors is mostly planar, obtaining a unique solution for the respective hand-eye robot-world problem is unfeasible without incorporating additional knowledge. Hence, we extend our proposed method to include a-priori knowledge, i.e., the translation norm of calibration targets, to yield a unique solution. Our approach achieves state-of-the-art results on simulated and real-world data. Especially on real-world intersection data, our approach utilizing the translation norm is the only method providing accurate results."},"title":{"value":"Extrinsic Infrastructure Calibration Using the Hand-Eye Robot-World Formulation"},"authors":{"value":["Markus Horn","Thomas Wodtko","Michael Buchholz","Klaus Dietmayer"]}},"tmdate":1794048259280,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1746706690630,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Michael_Buchholz1"],"forum":"bPVdCTm7cO","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":413180,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1794048259280,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bPVdCTm7cO","version":2},{"content":{"submission_length":{"value":"Regular submission (no more than 12 pages of main content)"},"venue":{"value":"Rejected by TMLR"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrating a multiclass predictor, that outputs a distribution over labels, is particularly challenging due to the exponential number\nof possible prediction values. In this work, we propose a new definition of calibration error that interpolates between two established calibration error notions, one with known exponential sample complexity and one with polynomial sample complexity for calibrating a given predictor. Our algorithm can calibrate any given predictor for the entire range of interpolation, except for one endpoint, using only a polynomial number of samples. At the other endpoint, we achieve nearly optimal  dependence on the error parameter, improving upon previous work. A key technical contribution is a novel application of adaptive data analysis with high adaptivity but only logarithmic overhead in the sample complexity."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{\nanonymous2026sampleefficient,\ntitle={Sample-efficient Multiclass Calibration under Probability-weighted \\${\\textbackslash}ell\\_\\{p\\}\\$ Error},\nauthor={Anonymous},\njournal={Submitted to Transactions on Machine Learning Research},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=6gNBKKY1NX},\nnote={Rejected}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Sample-efficient Multiclass Calibration under Probability-weighted $\\ell_{p}$ Error"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f793dfae26808ffb89801d22c6928738684672b9.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"TMLR/Rejected"},"assigned_action_editor":{"value":"~Sivan_Sabato1"}},"tmdate":1792786477604,"tcdate":1778654559599,"writers":["TMLR","TMLR/Paper8910/Authors"],"signatures":["TMLR/Paper8910/Authors"],"forum":"6gNBKKY1NX","number":8910,"license":"CC BY 4.0","cdate":1778654559599,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["TMLR/-/Submission","TMLR/-/Edit","TMLR/-/Under_Review","TMLR/-/Rejected"],"mdate":1792786477604,"odate":1779467394289,"domain":"TMLR","id":"6gNBKKY1NX","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 Workshop AgenticUQ Poster"},"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a new method for calibrating confidence estimates for reasoning LLMs when no labels are available and multiple sampling is not possible in deployment."},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["uncertainty","calibration","LLMs","reasoning","reliability"]},"abstract":{"value":"Reasoning language models can solve increasingly complex tasks, but struggle to produce the calibrated confidence estimates necessary for reliable deployment. Existing calibration methods usually depend on labels or repeated sampling at inference time, making them impractical in many settings. We introduce a method for unsupervised confidence calibration of reasoning LLMs when only a single generation is available at inference time. Our approach uses offline sampling on unlabeled data to derive a self-consistency-based proxy target, then distills this signal into a lightweight deployment-time confidence predictor. In a broad evaluation across 5 math and question-answering tasks using 9 reasoning models, our method substantially outperforms baselines, including under distribution shift, and improves downstream performance in selective prediction and simulated downstream decision-making."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzollo2026unsupervised,\ntitle={Unsupervised Confidence Calibration for Reasoning {LLM}s from a Single Generation},\nauthor={Thomas P Zollo and Jimmy Wang and Richard Zemel},\nbooktitle={ICML 2026 Statistical Frameworks for Uncertainty in Agentic Systems},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=4oBTV3kFFH}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Unsupervised Confidence Calibration for Reasoning LLMs from a Single Generation"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/befb2af5f47e23a0531e6fbc5acc2ac13ef50902.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ"},"paperhash":{"value":"zollo|unsupervised_confidence_calibration_for_reasoning_llms_from_a_single_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Thomas_P_Zollo1","~Jimmy_Wang1","~Richard_Zemel1"]},"authors":{"value":["Thomas P Zollo","Jimmy Wang","Richard Zemel"]}},"tmdate":1782482401637,"pdate":1780246182349,"tcdate":1776186885493,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/Submission1/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/Submission1/Authors"],"forum":"4oBTV3kFFH","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1,"cdate":1776186885493,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/Submission1/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1782482401637,"odate":1782482401292,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticUQ","id":"4oBTV3kFFH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Comput. Vis. Image Underst. 2012"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CVIU/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"puig|calibration_of_omnidirectional_cameras_in_practice_a_comparison_of_methods"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luis_Puig:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jesús_Bermúdez:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peter_F._Sturm:","~José_Jesús_Guerrero1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cviu.2011.08.003"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/cviu/PuigBSG12,\n  author={Luis Puig and Jesús Bermúdez and Peter F. Sturm and José Jesús Guerrero},\n  title={Calibration of omnidirectional cameras in practice: A comparison of methods},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  journal={Comput. Vis. Image Underst.},\n  volume={116},\n  number={1},\n  pages={120-137},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cviu.2011.08.003}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Omnidirectional cameras are becoming increasingly popular in computer vision and robotics. Camera calibration is a step before performing any task involving metric scene measurement, required in nearly all robotics tasks. In recent years many different methods to calibrate central omnidirectional cameras have been developed, based on different camera models and often limited to a specific mirror shape. In this paper we review the existing methods designed to calibrate any central omnivision system and analyze their advantages and drawbacks doing a deep comparison using simulated and real data. We choose methods available as OpenSource and which do not require a complex pattern or scene. The evaluation protocol of calibration accuracy also considers 3D metric reconstruction combining omnidirectional images. Comparative results are shown and discussed in detail."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of omnidirectional cameras in practice: A comparison of methods"},"authors":{"value":["Luis Puig","Jesús Bermúdez","Peter F. Sturm","José Jesús Guerrero"]}},"tmdate":1731491060676,"pdate":1325376000000,"tcdate":1731488032203,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jose_J_Guerrero1"],"forum":"J98QJJ3ttz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":218381,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731491060676,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"J98QJJ3ttz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE ICIST 2024 Conference Submission"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/367384599d785355e23cd768568e285811044390.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"IEEE.org/ICIST/2024/Conference/Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhao|source_code_changes_justintime_update_via_code_semantics"},"authorids":{"value":["zlxdeyou@dlmu.edu.cn","lawtysuel@gmail.com","~Hui_Li9","~Shikai_Guo1","haoliying@dlmu.edu.cn"]},"abstract":{"value":"To tackle the trouble of incomplete, insufficient, or misaligned code comments during software development and maintenance, various techniques are emerged to modify the comments of plain language in accordance with code alterations. However, these methods have two significant limitations: addressing the source code involving non-temporal and  long-rang dependencies poses challenges. With the aim of surpassing these restrictions, we present a novel approach named Code Comment Update  (CCU) model, which incorporates self-attention, positional encoding, and relative positional representation to effectively capture the relationships between different source code tags. This allows it to effectively grasp extended and non-temporal interdependencies within the source code. The comment-update module of CCU produces fresh comments by harnessing the power of existing code alterations and comments. The results of Experiment demonstrate that CCU outperforms the three baseline methods in terms of metrics such as exact match, METEOR, BLEU, and SARI."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhao2024source,\ntitle={Source Code Changes Just-In-Time Update Via Code Semantics},\nauthor={Lixiao Zhao and Wen Zhao and Hui Li and Shikai Guo and Li-Ying Hao},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The 14th International Conference on Information Science and Technology},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=deZog6GVIF},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Source Code Changes Just-In-Time Update Via Code Semantics"},"authors":{"value":["Lixiao Zhao","Wen Zhao","Hui Li","Shikai Guo","Li-Ying Hao"]}},"tmdate":1724201885803,"tcdate":1723616240954,"writers":["IEEE.org/ICIST/2024/Conference","IEEE.org/ICIST/2024/Conference/Submission120/Authors"],"signatures":["IEEE.org/ICIST/2024/Conference/Submission120/Authors"],"forum":"deZog6GVIF","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":120,"cdate":1723616240954,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["IEEE.org/ICIST/2024/Conference/-/Submission","IEEE.org/ICIST/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission"],"mdate":1724201885803,"odate":1724201077660,"domain":"IEEE.org/ICIST/2024/Conference","id":"deZog6GVIF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Int. J. Comput. Vis. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11263-025-02382-x.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJCV/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"jiang|imbuing_enrichment_and_calibration_leveraging_language_for_unseen_domain_extension"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chenyi_Jiang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianqin_Zhao:","~Jingjing_Deng2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zechao_Li:","~Haofeng_Zhang2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11263-025-02382-x"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijcv/JiangZDLZ25,\n  author={Chenyi Jiang and Jianqin Zhao and Jingjing Deng and Zechao Li and Haofeng Zhang},\n  title={Imbuing, Enrichment and Calibration: Leveraging Language for Unseen Domain Extension},\n  year={2025},\n  month={July},\n  cdate={1751328000000},\n  journal={Int. J. Comput. Vis.},\n  volume={133},\n  number={7},\n  pages={4064-4090},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s11263-025-02382-x}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The incorporation of language to enable model extension into unseen domains has gained significant interest in recent years. Previous methods commonly utilize semantically guided distributional shifts in training features to achieve this. Nevertheless, the intrinsic modal disparities between language and pixel-level images frequently result in a divergence within the feature manifold when employing semantic guidelines to augment features. This paper presents the IMbuing, Enrichment, and Calibration (IMEC) strategy as a concise solution for these issues. Unlike previous approaches, IMEC reverses the target domain style mining process to ensure the retention of semantic content within a more structured framework. Guided by global semantics, we conditionally generate style vectors for imbuing into visual features. After which IMEC introduces minor perturbations to disperse these vectors using local semantics and selectively calibrates semantic content in features through a dimensional activation strategy. IMEC integrates semantic abstract knowledge with detail image content, bridging the gap between synthetic and real samples in the target domain and mitigating content collapse resulting from semantic-visual disparities. Our model is evaluated on semantic segmentation, object detection, and image classification tasks across challenging datasets, demonstrating superior performance over existing methods in both the target and source domains. The code for IMEC is available at https://github.com/LanchJL/IMEC-ZSDE."},"title":{"value":"Imbuing, Enrichment and Calibration: Leveraging Language for Unseen Domain Extension"},"authors":{"value":["Chenyi Jiang","Jianqin Zhao","Jingjing Deng","Zechao Li","Haofeng Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1777120931938,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/ijcv/JiangZDLZ25"],"tcdate":1769683270409,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chenyi_Jiang1"],"forum":"ZFmKVeJite","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":814852,"cdate":1751328000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1777120931938,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ZFmKVeJite","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We present OrthoLoC, a large-scale UAV localization dataset pairing aerial imagery with orthographic geodata, enabling a new localization paradigm. Our AdHoP technique enhances matching-based localization pipelines."},"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2025 Datasets and Benchmarks Track oral"},"keywords":{"value":["UAV localization","orthographic geodata","aerial imagery","cross-domain matching"]},"primary_area":{"value":"datasets_&_benchmarks_for_computer_vision"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate visual localization from aerial views is a fundamental problem with applications in mapping, large-area inspection, and search-and-rescue operations. In many scenarios, these systems require high-precision localization while operating with limited resources (e.g., no internet connection or GNSS/GPS support), making large image databases or heavy 3D models impractical. Surprisingly, little attention has been given to leveraging orthographic geodata as an alternative paradigm, which is lightweight and increasingly available through free releases by governmental authorities (e.g., the European Union). To fill this gap, we propose OrthoLoC, the first large-scale dataset comprising 16,425 UAV images from Germany and the United States with multiple modalities. The dataset addresses domain shifts between UAV imagery and geospatial data. Its paired structure enables fair benchmarking of existing solutions by decoupling image retrieval from feature matching, allowing isolated evaluation of localization and calibration performance. Through comprehensive evaluation, we examine the impact of domain shifts, data resolutions, and covisibility on localization accuracy. Finally, we introduce a refinement technique called AdHoP, which can be integrated with any feature matcher, improving matching by up to 95% and reducing translation error by up to 63%. The dataset and code are available at: https://deepscenario.github.io/OrthoLoC ."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\ndhaouadi2026ortholoc,\ntitle={OrthoLoC: {UAV} 6-DoF Localization and Calibration Using Orthographic Geodata},\nauthor={Oussema Dhaouadi and Riccardo Marin and Johannes Michael Meier and Jacques Kaiser and Daniel Cremers},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems Datasets and Benchmarks Track},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=VcUmuGXhaI}\n}"},"title":{"value":"OrthoLoC: UAV 6-DoF Localization and Calibration Using Orthographic Geodata"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/b30310ec6f62666229bf28b11fc65ee847e258d1.pdf"},"croissant_file":{"value":"/attachment/4d3aaa6ec068a9b8f335e7786a2f5503fe6ff9ad.json"},"code_URL":{"value":"https://github.com/deepscenario/OrthoLoC"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track"},"paperhash":{"value":"dhaouadi|ortholoc_uav_6dof_localization_and_calibration_using_orthographic_geodata"},"authorids":{"value":["~Oussema_Dhaouadi1","~Riccardo_Marin1","~Johannes_Michael_Meier1","~Jacques_Kaiser1","~Daniel_Cremers1"]},"dataset_URL":{"value":"https://cvg.cit.tum.de/webshare/g/papers/Dhaouadi/OrthoLoC/"},"authors":{"value":["Oussema Dhaouadi","Riccardo Marin","Johannes Michael Meier","Jacques Kaiser","Daniel Cremers"]}},"tmdate":1776955803542,"pdate":1758215817697,"tcdate":1746960264195,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission1689/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission1689/Authors"],"forum":"VcUmuGXhaI","license":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","number":1689,"cdate":1746960264195,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission1689/-/Full_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission1689/-/Supplementary_Material","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission1689/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1776955803542,"odate":1761793880235,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track","id":"VcUmuGXhaI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.05229v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|fewshot_classincremental_learning_via_trainingfree_prototype_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qi-Wei_Wang:","~Da-Wei_Zhou1","~Yi-Kai_Zhang2","~De-Chuan_Zhan1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Han-Jia_Ye:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2312.05229"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2312-05229,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Qi-Wei Wang and Da-Wei Zhou and Yi-Kai Zhang and De-Chuan Zhan and Han-Jia Ye},\n  title={Few-Shot Class-Incremental Learning via Training-Free Prototype Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2312.05229},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2312.05229}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Real-world scenarios are usually accompanied by continuously appearing classes with scare labeled samples, which require the machine learning model to incrementally learn new classes and maintain the knowledge of base classes. In this Few-Shot Class-Incremental Learning (FSCIL) scenario, existing methods either introduce extra learnable components or rely on a frozen feature extractor to mitigate catastrophic forgetting and overfitting problems. However, we find a tendency for existing methods to misclassify the samples of new classes into base classes, which leads to the poor performance of new classes. In other words, the strong discriminability of base classes distracts the classification of new classes. To figure out this intriguing phenomenon, we observe that although the feature extractor is only trained on base classes, it can surprisingly represent the semantic similarity between the base and unseen new classes. Building upon these analyses, we propose a simple yet effective Training-frEE calibratioN (TEEN) strategy to enhance the discriminability of new classes by fusing the new prototypes (i.e., mean features of a class) with weighted base prototypes. In addition to standard benchmarks in FSCIL, TEEN demonstrates remarkable performance and consistent improvements over baseline methods in the few-shot learning scenario. Code is available at: https://github.com/wangkiw/TEEN"},"title":{"value":"Few-Shot Class-Incremental Learning via Training-Free Prototype Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Qi-Wei Wang","Da-Wei Zhou","Yi-Kai Zhang","De-Chuan Zhan","Han-Jia Ye"]}},"tmdate":1768981325102,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1728274654959,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yi-Kai_Zhang2"],"forum":"Riy8b8LUOV","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":141097,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768981325102,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Riy8b8LUOV","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/file/30dfe47a3ccbee68cffa0c19ccb1bc00-Paper-Conference.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/NIPS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|fewshot_classincremental_learning_via_trainingfree_prototype_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qi-Wei_Wang:","~Da-Wei_Zhou1","~Yi-Kai_Zhang2","~De-Chuan_Zhan1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Han-Jia_Ye:"]},"html":{"value":"http://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/30dfe47a3ccbee68cffa0c19ccb1bc00-Abstract-Conference.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/nips/Wang0ZZY23,\n  author={Qi-Wei Wang and Da-Wei Zhou and Yi-Kai Zhang and De-Chuan Zhan and Han-Jia Ye},\n  title={Few-Shot Class-Incremental Learning via Training-Free Prototype Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  url={http://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/30dfe47a3ccbee68cffa0c19ccb1bc00-Abstract-Conference.html},\n  booktitle={NeurIPS},\n  crossref={conf/nips/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Real-world scenarios are usually accompanied by continuously appearing classes with scare labeled samples, which require the machine learning model to incrementally learn new classes and maintain the knowledge of base classes. In this Few-Shot Class-Incremental Learning (FSCIL) scenario, existing methods either introduce extra learnable components or rely on a frozen feature extractor to mitigate catastrophic forgetting and overfitting problems. However, we find a tendency for existing methods to misclassify the samples of new classes into base classes, which leads to the poor performance of new classes. In other words, the strong discriminability of base classes distracts the classification of new classes. To figure out this intriguing phenomenon, we observe that although the feature extractor is only trained on base classes, it can surprisingly represent the semantic similarity between the base and unseen new classes. Building upon these analyses, we propose a simple yet effective Training-frEE calibratioN (TEEN) strategy to enhance the discriminability of new classes by fusing the new prototypes (i.e., mean features of a class) with weighted base prototypes. In addition to standard benchmarks in FSCIL, TEEN demonstrates remarkable performance and consistent improvements over baseline methods in the few-shot learning scenario. Code is available at: https://github.com/wangkiw/TEEN"},"title":{"value":"Few-Shot Class-Incremental Learning via Training-Free Prototype Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Qi-Wei Wang","Da-Wei Zhou","Yi-Kai Zhang","De-Chuan Zhan","Han-Jia Ye"]}},"tmdate":1768981324611,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1728274654981,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yi-Kai_Zhang2"],"forum":"0LMT13diJN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":141099,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768981324611,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0LMT13diJN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10654794/10654797/10657505.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"kocur|robust_selfcalibration_of_focal_lengths_from_the_fundamental_matrix"},"authorids":{"value":["~Viktor_Kocur1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daniel_Kyselica:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zuzana_Kukelova:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52733.2024.00499"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/KocurKK24,\n  author={Viktor Kocur and Daniel Kyselica and Zuzana Kukelova},\n  title={Robust Self-Calibration of Focal Lengths from the Fundamental Matrix},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={5220-5229},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52733.2024.00499},\n  booktitle={CVPR},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The problem of self-calibration of two cameras from a given fundamental matrix is one of the basic problems in geometric computer vision. Under the assumption of known principal points and square pixels, the Bougnoux formula offers a means to compute the two unknown focal lengths. However, in many practical situations, the formula yields inaccurate results due to commonly occurring singularities. Moreover, the estimates are sensitive to noise in the com-puted fundamental matrix and to the assumed positions of the principal points. In this paper, we therefore propose an efficient and robust iterative method to estimate the focal lengths along with the principal points of the cameras given a fundamental matrix and priors for the estimated camera intrinsics. In addition, we study a computationally efficient check of models generated within RANSAC that improves the accuracy of the estimated models while reducing the to-tal computational time. Extensive experiments on real and synthetic data show that our iterative method brings signifi-cant improvements in terms of the accuracy of the estimated focal lengths over the Bougnoux formula and other state-of-the-art methods, even when relying on inaccurate priors. The code for the methods and experiments is available at https://github.com/kocurvik/robust.self.calibration"},"title":{"value":"Robust Self-Calibration of Focal Lengths from the Fundamental Matrix"},"authors":{"value":["Viktor Kocur","Daniel Kyselica","Zuzana Kukelova"]}},"tmdate":1762766422312,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/cvpr/KocurKK24"],"tcdate":1762766413277,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Viktor_Kocur1"],"forum":"2mBil34MSj","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":686428,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762766422312,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2mBil34MSj","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2023 poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Few-Shot Class-Incremental Learning","Continual Learning","Class-Incremental Learning"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nwang2023fewshot,\ntitle={Few-Shot Class-Incremental Learning via Training-Free Prototype Calibration},\nauthor={Qi-Wei Wang and Da-Wei Zhou and Yi-Kai Zhang and De-Chuan Zhan and Han-Jia Ye},\nbooktitle={Thirty-seventh Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=8NAxGDdf7H}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Few-Shot Class-Incremental Learning via Training-Free Prototype Calibration"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|fewshot_classincremental_learning_via_trainingfree_prototype_calibration"},"abstract":{"value":"Real-world scenarios are usually accompanied by continuously appearing classes with scare labeled samples, which require the machine learning model to incrementally learn new classes and maintain the knowledge of base classes. In this Few-Shot Class-Incremental Learning (FSCIL) scenario, existing methods either introduce extra learnable components or rely on a frozen feature extractor to mitigate catastrophic forgetting and overfitting problems.  However, we find a tendency for existing methods to misclassify the samples of new classes into base classes, which leads to the poor performance of new classes. In other words, the strong discriminability of base classes distracts the classification of new classes. To figure out this intriguing phenomenon, we observe that although the feature extractor is only trained on base classes, it can surprisingly represent the *semantic similarity* between the base and *unseen* new classes. Building upon these analyses, we propose a *simple yet effective* Training-frEE calibratioN (TEEN) strategy to enhance the discriminability of new classes by fusing the new prototypes (i.e., mean features of a class) with weighted base prototypes. In addition to standard benchmarks in FSCIL, TEEN demonstrates remarkable performance and consistent improvements over baseline methods in the few-shot learning scenario. Code is available at: https://github.com/wangkiw/TEEN"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/74ad2d757ed402662e571148d65f47975e0d9e1d.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Qi-Wei_Wang1","~Da-Wei_Zhou1","~Yi-Kai_Zhang2","~De-Chuan_Zhan1","~Han-Jia_Ye1"]},"authors":{"value":["Qi-Wei Wang","Da-Wei Zhou","Yi-Kai Zhang","De-Chuan Zhan","Han-Jia Ye"]}},"tmdate":1705236656928,"pdate":1695325936282,"tcdate":1683718176436,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission6802/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission6802/Authors"],"forum":"8NAxGDdf7H","number":6802,"cdate":1683718176436,"mdate":1705236656928,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission6802/-/Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission6802/-/Supplementary_Material_Revision","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference/Submission6802/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"odate":1698949737374,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Conference","id":"8NAxGDdf7H","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10341341/10341342/10341453.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"qiao|viwfusion_extrinsic_calibration_and_pose_estimation_for_visualimuwheel_encoder_system"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chunxiao_Qiao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuying_Zhao:","~Yunzhou_Zhang2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yahui_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dan_Zhang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS55552.2023.10341453"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/QiaoZZWZ23,\n  author={Chunxiao Qiao and Shuying Zhao and Yunzhou Zhang and Yahui Wang and Dan Zhang},\n  title={VIW-Fusion: Extrinsic Calibration and Pose Estimation for Visual-IMU-Wheel Encoder System},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1489-1496},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS55552.2023.10341453},\n  booktitle={IROS}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The data fusion of camera, IMU, and wheel encoder measurements has proved its effectiveness in localizing ground robots, and obtaining accurate sensor extrinsic parameters is its premise. We propose an extrinsic parameter calibration algorithm and a multi-sensor-based pose estimation algorithm for the camera-IMU-wheel encoder system. First, we propose a joint calibration algorithm for the extrinsic parameters of the camera-IMU-wheel encoder system, which improves the accuracy and robustness of the camera-wheel encoder calibration. We then extend the visual-inertial odometry (VIO) to incorporate the measurements from the wheel encoder and weight the wheel encoder measurements according to angular velocity in global optimization to improve the performance. We further propose a novel method for VIO initialization by integrating wheel encoder information, which significantly reduces the scale error in initialization. We conduct extrinsic parameter calibration experiments on a real self-driving car and validate the performance of our multi-sensor-based localization system on the KAIST dataset and a dataset collected by our self-driving vehicles by performing an exhaust comparison with the state-of-the-art algorithms. Our implementations are open source11https://github.com/chunxiaoqiao/VIW-Fusion.git."},"title":{"value":"VIW-Fusion: Extrinsic Calibration and Pose Estimation for Visual-IMU-Wheel Encoder System"},"authors":{"value":["Chunxiao Qiao","Shuying Zhao","Yunzhou Zhang","Yahui Wang","Dan Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1740928145290,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1740928135786,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yunzhou_Zhang2"],"forum":"DamrPEAdf8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":344234,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740928145290,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"DamrPEAdf8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Color Imaging: Displaying, Processing, Hardcopy, and Applications 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://library.imaging.org/admin/apis/public/api/ist/website/downloadArticle/ei/28/20/art00002"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CLRIMG/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"rodríguezpardo|multiprimary_display_color_calibration_a_variational_framework_for_robustness_to_device_variation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Carlos_Eduardo_Rodríguez-Pardo:","~Gaurav_Sharma3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.2352/ISSN.2470-1173.2016.20.COLOR-304"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/clrimg/Rodriguez-Pardo16,\n  author={Carlos Eduardo Rodríguez-Pardo and Gaurav Sharma},\n  title={Multiprimary Display Color Calibration: A Variational Frame-work for Robustness to Device Variation},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  pages={1-7},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.2352/ISSN.2470-1173.2016.20.COLOR-304},\n  booktitle={Color Imaging: Displaying, Processing, Hardcopy, and Applications},\n  crossref={conf/clrimg/2016}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"For multiprimary displays, color within the interior of the gamut can be reproduced with several different control values, a situation that is in contrast with the three primary scenario, where the control values are unique. For a given color, the selection of the control values, or color calibration, becomes a fundamental step for color rendition on multiprimary devices. Because spatially smooth variations in color are common in imagery and it is critical that despite device variations these be maintained as smooth variations in renditions, it is also desirable that the calibration strategy preserve smoothness of the device control values over color space. Based on this motivation, we propose a variational framework for color calibration of multiprimary displays that emphasizes smoothness by minimizing the squared norm of the gradient of the calibration function over the display gamut. We test our proposed methodology on a four primary system, and compare its performance with calibrations obtained from other standard methodologies. Results indicate that, compared with the alternatives, the proposed variational approach offers the smoothest variation in the control values over the entire color space and as a result also exhibits enhanced robustness in the presence of device variations."},"title":{"value":"Multiprimary Display Color Calibration: A Variational Frame-work for Robustness to Device Variation"},"authors":{"value":["Carlos Eduardo Rodríguez-Pardo","Gaurav Sharma"]}},"tmdate":1740106905539,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1740106884627,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Gaurav_Sharma3"],"forum":"7XE2GVHXDt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":337224,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740106905539,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"7XE2GVHXDt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Comput. Vis. Image Underst. 2021"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CVIU/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"melanitis|a_linear_method_for_camera_pair_selfcalibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nikos_Melanitis:","~Petros_Maragos1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cviu.2021.103223"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/cviu/MelanitisM21,\n  author={Nikos Melanitis and Petros Maragos},\n  title={A linear method for camera pair self-calibration},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={Comput. Vis. Image Underst.},\n  volume={210},\n  pages={103223},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cviu.2021.103223}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•We introduce a linear camera pair self-calibration and metric reconstruction method.•Our method upgrades a projective reconstruction assuming varying focal lengths.•We describe in two theorems the relative orientation of the camera-solutions recovered.•We introduce a focal length averaging method to improve the accuracy of our estimates.•We get multi-view reconstructions averaging multiple rotation and f. length estimates."},"title":{"value":"A linear method for camera pair self-calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Nikos Melanitis","Petros Maragos"]}},"tmdate":1746905691143,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1746905625369,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Petros_Maragos1"],"forum":"MDUUFu9sQc","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":416096,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1746905691143,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"MDUUFu9sQc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Neurocomputing 2018"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJON/2018"},"paperhash":{"value":"wong|online_extreme_learning_machine_based_modeling_and_optimization_for_pointbypoint_engine_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pak-Kin_Wong_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiang_Hui_Gao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ka_In_Wong:","~Chi-Man_Vong2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2017.02.104"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijon/WongGWV18,\n  author={Pak-Kin Wong and Xiang Hui Gao and Ka In Wong and Chi-Man Vong},\n  title={Online extreme learning machine based modeling and optimization for point-by-point engine calibration},\n  year={2018},\n  cdate={1514764800000},\n  journal={Neurocomputing},\n  volume={277},\n  pages={187-197},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2017.02.104}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"An online extreme learning machine (ELM) based modeling and optimization approach for point-by-point engine calibration is proposed to improve the efficiency of conventional model-based calibration approach. Instead of building hundreds of local engine models for every engine operating point, only one ELM model is necessary for the whole process. This ELM model is firstly constructed for a starting operating point, and calibration of this starting point is conducted by determining the optimal parameters of the model. This ELM model is then re-used as a base model for a nearby target operating point, and optimization is performed on the model to search for its best parameters. With a design of experiment strategy on the best parameters obtained, new measurements from the target operating point can be collected and used to update the model. By repeating the optimization and model update procedures, the optimal parameters for the target point can be found after several iterations. By using the model of this target point as the base model for another nearby operating point and repeating the same process again, calibration for all the operating points can be done online efficiently. The contribution of the proposed method is to save the number of experiments in the calibration process. To verify the effectiveness of the proposed approach, experiments on a commercial engine simulation software have been conducted. Three variants of online ELM are utilized in the model update process for comparison. The results show that engine calibration can be carried out with much fewer measurements and time using the proposed approach, and the initial training free online ELM is the most efficient online modeling method for this application."},"title":{"value":"Online extreme learning machine based modeling and optimization for point-by-point engine calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Pak-Kin Wong","Xiang Hui Gao","Ka In Wong","Chi-Man Vong"]}},"tmdate":1747108287516,"pdate":1514764800000,"tcdate":1747108219833,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chi_Man_VONG1"],"forum":"dGhJ5lJDzd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":434207,"cdate":1514764800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747108287516,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"dGhJ5lJDzd","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"A Method for Mitigating Code Leakage by LLM Code Assistants"},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2025"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["privacy","DRL","LLM","code assistant","generative models"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"LLM-based code assistants are becoming increasingly popular among developers.\nThese tools help developers improve their coding efficiency and reduce errors by providing real-time suggestions based on the developer’s codebase. \nWhile beneficial, the use of these tools can inadvertently expose the developer’s proprietary code to the code assistant service provider during the development process. \nIn this work, we propose a method to mitigate the risk of code leakage when using LLM-based code assistants. CodeCloak is a novel deep reinforcement learning agent that manipulates the prompts before sending them to the code assistant service.\nCodeCloak aims to achieve the following two contradictory goals: (i) minimizing code leakage, while (ii) preserving relevant and useful suggestions for the developer. \nOur evaluation, employing StarCoder and Code Llama, LLM-based code assistants models, demonstrates CodeCloak’s effectiveness on a diverse set of code repositories of varying sizes, as well as its transferability across different models.\nWe also designed a method for reconstructing the developer’s original codebase from code segments sent to the code assistant service (i.e., prompts) during the development process, to thoroughly analyze code leakage risks and evaluate the effectiveness of CodeCloak under practical development scenarios."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nnoah2025codecloak,\ntitle={CodeCloak: A Method for Mitigating Code Leakage by {LLM} Code Assistants},\nauthor={Amit Finkman Noah and Avishag Shapira and Eden Bar Kochva and Inbar Maimon and Dudu Mimran and Yuval Elovici and Asaf Shabtai},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=z3KmG5JIN4}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CodeCloak: A Method for Mitigating Code Leakage by LLM Code Assistants"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/62e64bb8c116285abcc5bf46fc0a4ba0f58231a8.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"noah|codecloak_a_method_for_mitigating_code_leakage_by_llm_code_assistants"},"authorids":{"value":["~Amit_Finkman_Noah1","~Avishag_Shapira2","~Eden_Bar_Kochva1","~Inbar_Maimon1","~Dudu_Mimran1","~Yuval_Elovici1","~Asaf_Shabtai1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Amit Finkman Noah","Avishag Shapira","Eden Bar Kochva","Inbar Maimon","Dudu Mimran","Yuval Elovici","Asaf Shabtai"]}},"tmdate":1738735651656,"tcdate":1727003147514,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2580/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2580/Authors"],"forum":"z3KmG5JIN4","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2580,"cdate":1727003147514,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2580/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission2580/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738735651656,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"z3KmG5JIN4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.2128.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"fonseca|when_high_accuracy_hides_poor_calibration_rethinking_confidence_evaluation_in_transformerbased_text_classification_with_balanced_brier_score"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.acl-long.2128"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/FonsecaPCRG26,\n  author={Guilherme Fonseca and Gabriel Prenassi and Washington Cunha and Leonardo Chaves Dutra da Rocha and Marcos André Gonçalves},\n  title={When High Accuracy Hides Poor Calibration: Rethinking Confidence Evaluation in Transformer-Based Text Classification with Balanced Brier Score},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={45888-45900},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.acl-long.2128},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2026-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Transformer-based Small (SLMs) and Large Language Models (LLMs) achieve strong effectiveness in text classification (TC), yet deployment requires reliable confidence estimates. Although miscalibration in Transformers has been reported, evidence for TC under fine-tuning remains limited. We evaluate the calibration of fine-tuned SLMs and LLMs against Logistic Regression, a classical, well-calibrated baseline, and find that, despite superior effectiveness, Transformers remain markedly overconfident. Crucially, we show that widely used calibration metrics, such as Expected Calibration Error and Brier Score, become biased in high-effectiveness regimes, where the dominance of correct predictions masks severe miscalibration on errors, sometimes even suggesting better calibration than Logistic Regression, a well-known calibrated method. To address this limitation, we propose the Balanced Brier Score (BBS), which balances the contribution of correct and incorrect predictions within confidence bins. BBS reveals substantially poorer calibration in both SLMs and LLMs, consistent with qualitative evidence from calibration curves. These findings challenge current calibration assessment practices and provide a more reliable alternative for evaluating confidence quality in Transformer-based TC."},"title":{"value":"When High Accuracy Hides Poor Calibration: Rethinking Confidence Evaluation in Transformer-Based Text Classification with Balanced Brier Score"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Guilherme Fonseca","username":""},{"fullname":"Gabriel Prenassi","username":"~Gabriel_Prenassi1"},{"fullname":"Washington Cunha","username":""},{"fullname":"Leonardo Chaves Dutra da Rocha","username":""},{"fullname":"Marcos André Gonçalves","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1787661801638,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/FonsecaPCRG26"],"tcdate":1787661798922,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Gabriel_Prenassi1"],"forum":"Lhd5AO690L","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":141868,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1787661801638,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Lhd5AO690L","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph. 2015"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/2945/7283688/07164353.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TVCG/2015"},"paperhash":{"value":"resch|onsite_semiautomatic_calibration_and_registration_of_a_projectorcamera_system_using_arbitrary_objects_with_known_geometry"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Christoph_Resch:","~Hemal_Naik1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peter_Keitler:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Steven_Benkhardt:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gudrun_Klinker:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2015.2459898"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tvcg/ReschNKBK15,\n  author={Christoph Resch and Hemal Naik and Peter Keitler and Steven Benkhardt and Gudrun Klinker},\n  title={On-Site Semi-Automatic Calibration and Registration of a Projector-Camera System Using Arbitrary Objects with Known Geometry},\n  year={2015},\n  cdate={1420070400000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Vis. Comput. Graph.},\n  volume={21},\n  number={11},\n  pages={1211-1220},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2015.2459898}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In the Shader Lamps concept, a projector-camera system augments physical objects with projected virtual textures, provided that a precise intrinsic and extrinsic calibration of the system is available. Calibrating such systems has been an elaborate and lengthy task in the past and required a special calibration apparatus. Self-calibration methods in turn are able to estimate calibration parameters automatically with no effort. However they inherently lack global scale and are fairly sensitive to input data. We propose a new semi-automatic calibration approach for projector-camera systems that - unlike existing auto-calibration approaches - additionally recovers the necessary global scale by projecting on an arbitrary object of known geometry. To this end our method combines surface registration with bundle adjustment optimization on points reconstructed from structured light projections to refine a solution that is computed from the decomposition of the fundamental matrix. In simulations on virtual data and experiments with real data we demonstrate that our approach estimates the global scale robustly and is furthermore able to improve incorrectly guessed intrinsic and extrinsic calibration parameters thus outperforming comparable metric rectification algorithms."},"title":{"value":"On-Site Semi-Automatic Calibration and Registration of a Projector-Camera System Using Arbitrary Objects with Known Geometry"},"authors":{"value":["Christoph Resch","Hemal Naik","Peter Keitler","Steven Benkhardt","Gudrun Klinker"]}},"tmdate":1717420040478,"pdate":1420070400000,"tcdate":1717420035675,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hemal_Naik1"],"forum":"yjGPs8QvEz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":23830,"cdate":1420070400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1717420040478,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"yjGPs8QvEz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Crossref"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-72949-2_18.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"aimar|flexible_distribution_alignment_towards_longtailed_semisupervised_learning_with_proper_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-72949-2_18"},"abstract":{"value":"Long-tailed semi-supervised learning (LTSSL) represents a practical scenario for semi-supervised applications, challenged by skewed labeled distributions that bias classifiers. This problem is often aggravated by discrepancies between labeled and unlabeled class distributions, leading to biased pseudo-labels, neglect of rare classes, and poorly calibrated probabilities. To address these issues, we introduce Flexible Distribution Alignment (FlexDA), a novel adaptive logit-adjusted loss framework designed to dynamically estimate and align predictions with the actual distribution of unlabeled data and achieve a balanced classifier by the end of training. FlexDA is further enhanced by a distillation-based consistency loss, promoting fair data usage across classes and effectively leveraging underconfident samples. This method, encapsulated in ADELLO (Align and Distill Everything All at Once), proves robust against label shift, significantly improves model calibration in LTSSL contexts, and surpasses previous state-of-of-art approaches across multiple benchmarks, including CIFAR100-LT, STL10-LT, and ImageNet127, addressing class imbalance challenges in semi-supervised learning. Our code is available at https://github.com/emasa/ADELLO-LTSSL."},"title":{"value":"Flexible Distribution Alignment: Towards Long-Tailed Semi-supervised Learning with Proper Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Emanuel Sanchez Aimar"},{"fullname":"Nathaniel Helgesen"},{"fullname":"Yonghao Xu"},{"fullname":"Marco Kuhlmann"},{"fullname":"Michael Felsberg","username":"~Michael_Felsberg1"}]}},"tmdate":1789093313615,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1007/978-3-031-72949-2_18"],"tcdate":1772009604341,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Michael_Felsberg2"],"forum":"0DxKsABSFw","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":47131,"cdate":1730309249281,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789093313615,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"0DxKsABSFw","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ECCV (54) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-72949-2_18.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ECCV/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"aimar|flexible_distribution_alignment_towards_longtailed_semisupervised_learning_with_proper_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Emanuel_Sanchez_Aimar1","~Nathaniel_Helgesen1","~Yonghao_Xu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marco_Kuhlmann:","~Michael_Felsberg1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-72949-2_18"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/eccv/AimarHXKF24,\n  author={Emanuel Sanchez Aimar and Nathaniel Helgesen and Yonghao Xu and Marco Kuhlmann and Michael Felsberg},\n  title={Flexible Distribution Alignment: Towards Long-Tailed Semi-supervised Learning with Proper Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={307-327},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-72949-2_18},\n  booktitle={ECCV (54)},\n  crossref={conf/eccv/2024-54}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Long-tailed semi-supervised learning (LTSSL) represents a practical scenario for semi-supervised applications, challenged by skewed labeled distributions that bias classifiers. This problem is often aggravated by discrepancies between labeled and unlabeled class distributions, leading to biased pseudo-labels, neglect of rare classes, and poorly calibrated probabilities. To address these issues, we introduce Flexible Distribution Alignment (FlexDA), a novel adaptive logit-adjusted loss framework designed to dynamically estimate and align predictions with the actual distribution of unlabeled data and achieve a balanced classifier by the end of training. FlexDA is further enhanced by a distillation-based consistency loss, promoting fair data usage across classes and effectively leveraging underconfident samples. This method, encapsulated in ADELLO (Align and Distill Everything All at Once), proves robust against label shift, significantly improves model calibration in LTSSL contexts, and surpasses previous state-of-of-art approaches across multiple benchmarks, including CIFAR100-LT, STL10-LT, and ImageNet127, addressing class imbalance challenges in semi-supervised learning. Our code is available at https://github.com/emasa/ADELLO-LTSSL."},"title":{"value":"Flexible Distribution Alignment: Towards Long-Tailed Semi-supervised Learning with Proper Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Emanuel Sanchez Aimar","Nathaniel Helgesen","Yonghao Xu","Marco Kuhlmann","Michael Felsberg"]}},"tmdate":1772464420584,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1736507265965,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Michael_Felsberg2"],"forum":"6BK6NJ7wLR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":262000,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772464420584,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"6BK6NJ7wLR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.00934v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"oksuz|towards_building_selfaware_object_detectors_via_reliable_uncertainty_quantification_and_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kemal_Oksuz:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tom_Joy:","~Puneet_K._Dokania1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.00934"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2307-00934,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Kemal Oksuz and Tom Joy and Puneet K. Dokania},\n  title={Towards Building Self-Aware Object Detectors via Reliable Uncertainty Quantification and Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2307.00934},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.00934}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The current approach for testing the robustness of object detectors suffers from serious deficiencies such as improper methods of performing out-of-distribution detection and using calibration metrics which do not consider both localisation and classification quality. In this work, we address these issues, and introduce the Self-Aware Object Detection (SAOD) task, a unified testing framework which respects and adheres to the challenges that object detectors face in safety-critical environments such as autonomous driving. Specifically, the SAOD task requires an object detector to be: robust to domain shift; obtain reliable uncertainty estimates for the entire scene; and provide calibrated confidence scores for the detections. We extensively use our framework, which introduces novel metrics and large scale test datasets, to test numerous object detectors in two different use-cases, allowing us to highlight critical insights into their robustness performance. Finally, we introduce a simple baseline for the SAOD task, enabling researchers to benchmark future proposed methods and move towards robust object detectors which are fit for purpose. Code is available at https://github.com/fiveai/saod"},"title":{"value":"Towards Building Self-Aware Object Detectors via Reliable Uncertainty Quantification and Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Kemal Oksuz","Tom Joy","Puneet K. Dokania"]}},"tmdate":1727607798353,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727607764877,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Puneet_Dokania1"],"forum":"Keta764rV2","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":105943,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727607798353,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Keta764rV2","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10203037/10203050/10203797.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"oksuz|towards_building_selfaware_object_detectors_via_reliable_uncertainty_quantification_and_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kemal_Oksuz:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tom_Joy:","~Puneet_K._Dokania1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52729.2023.00894"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/OksuzJD23,\n  author={Kemal Oksuz and Tom Joy and Puneet K. Dokania},\n  title={Towards Building Self-Aware Object Detectors via Reliable Uncertainty Quantification and Calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={9263-9274},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52729.2023.00894},\n  booktitle={CVPR},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The current approach for testing the robustness of object detectors suffers from serious deficiencies such as improper methods of performing out-of-distribution detection and using calibration metrics which do not consider both localisation and classification quality. In this work, we address these issues, and introduce the Self Aware Object Detection (SAOD) task, a unified testing framework which respects and adheres to the challenges that object detectors face in safety-critical environments such as autonomous driving. Specifically, the SAOD task requires an object detector to be: robust to domain shift; obtain reliable uncertainty estimates for the entire scene; and provide calibrated confidence scores for the detections. We extensively use our framework, which introduces novel metrics and large scale test datasets, to test numerous object detectors in two different use-cases, allowing us to highlight critical insights into their robustness performance. Finally, we introduce a simple baseline for the SAOD task, enabling researchers to benchmark future proposed methods and move towards robust object detectors which are fit for purpose. Code is available at: https://github.com/fiveai/saod."},"title":{"value":"Towards Building Self-Aware Object Detectors via Reliable Uncertainty Quantification and Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Kemal Oksuz","Tom Joy","Puneet K. Dokania"]}},"tmdate":1727607755148,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727607745339,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Puneet_Dokania1"],"forum":"2sC9iQPOXF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":105940,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727607755148,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2sC9iQPOXF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.19495v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"dupuis|qiskit_code_assistant_training_llms_for_generating_quantum_computing_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nicolas_Dupuis:","~Luca_Buratti1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sanjay_Vishwakarma:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Aitana_Viudes_Forrat:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:David_Kremer:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ismael_Faro:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ruchir_Puri:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Juan_Cruz-Benito:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.19495"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2405-19495,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Nicolas Dupuis and Luca Buratti and Sanjay Vishwakarma and Aitana Viudes Forrat and David Kremer and Ismael Faro and Ruchir Puri and Juan Cruz-Benito},\n  title={Qiskit Code Assistant: Training LLMs for generating Quantum Computing Code},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2405.19495},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.19495}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code Large Language Models (Code LLMs) have emerged as powerful tools, revolutionizing the software development landscape by automating the coding process and reducing time and effort required to build applications. This paper focuses on training Code LLMs to specialize in the field of quantum computing. We begin by discussing the unique needs of quantum computing programming, which differ significantly from classical programming approaches or languages. A Code LLM specializing in quantum computing requires a foundational understanding of quantum computing and quantum information theory. However, the scarcity of available quantum code examples and the rapidly evolving field, which necessitates continuous dataset updates, present significant challenges. Moreover, we discuss our work on training Code LLMs to produce high-quality quantum code using the Qiskit library. This work includes an examination of the various aspects of the LLMs used for training and the specific training conditions, as well as the results obtained with our current models. To evaluate our models, we have developed a custom benchmark, similar to HumanEval, which includes a set of tests specifically designed for the field of quantum computing programming using Qiskit. Our findings indicate that our model outperforms existing state-of-the-art models in quantum computing tasks. We also provide examples of code suggestions, comparing our model to other relevant code LLMs. Finally, we introduce a discussion on the potential benefits of Code LLMs for quantum computing computational scientists, researchers, and practitioners. We also explore various features and future work that could be relevant in this context."},"title":{"value":"Qiskit Code Assistant: Training LLMs for generating Quantum Computing Code"},"authors":{"value":["Nicolas Dupuis","Luca Buratti","Sanjay Vishwakarma","Aitana Viudes Forrat","David Kremer","Ismael Faro","Ruchir Puri","Juan Cruz-Benito"]}},"tmdate":1758894992486,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2405-19495"],"tcdate":1758894983736,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Luca_Buratti2"],"forum":"0Qt65KqcV0","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":630547,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1758894992486,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0Qt65KqcV0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 October Submission"},"research_area_keywords":{"value":"quantization, LLM efficiency, data augmentation"},"keywords":{"value":["Compute efficient LMs","Post-Training Quantization","Calibration Data","Prompt Evolution","Quantization Error","Model Compression","RSI"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026qrpe,\ntitle={{QRPE}: Self-Improving Calibration Prompts with Quantization Feedback},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - October 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Wa7bd0lmVN},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"QRPE: Self-Improving Calibration Prompts with Quantization Feedback"},"languages_studied":{"value":["eng"]},"contribution_types":{"value":"Approaches to low-compute settings (efficiency)"},"abstract":{"value":"Post-training quantization (PTQ) is crucial for efficient LLM deployment but often suffers from activation outliers, leading to significant accuracy degradation. Traditional calibration methods rely on static datasets, which may fail to capture the model's most quantization-sensitive features.\n\nIn this work, we propose Quantization-guided Relative Prompt Evolution (QRPE), a training-free framework that synthesizes calibration data by optimizing prompts based on quantization feedback. Instead of relying on ground-truth labels, QRPE leverages a relative comparison mechanism: it generates a group of candidate outputs, evaluates their quantization robustness by using quantization loss as a reward signal, and evolves the generation prompt through natural language feedback to favor \"quantization-friendly\" patterns.\nExperiments across four model families demonstrate the effectiveness of QRPE. On Qwen3-8B, QRPE improves average accuracy by 1.19 points over GPTQ and 0.62 points over FAQ under INT4 quantization across five commonsense reasoning and scientific knowledge benchmarks.\n\nWe further show that QRPE generalizes across quantizers with different internal objectives, and that replacing the quantization loss with a random reward underperforms QRPE in every setting we evaluate, supporting the contribution of the reward signal."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/b4f76ca0d08a9d872055148db730a30b1f3d5cf1.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Efficient Methods for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794201844115,"tcdate":1791889548497,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission12231/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission12231/Authors"],"forum":"Wa7bd0lmVN","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":12231,"cdate":1791889548497,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Responsible_NLP_Checklist_Status","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794201844115,"odate":1791894541772,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","id":"Wa7bd0lmVN","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"uncertainty, calibration, verbalized confidence"},"keywords":{"value":["Confidence","Uncertainty","Reliability"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"N/A"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"N/A"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"We provide details in the appendix, section A3"},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"N/A"},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"No"},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"All results in section 5"},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4 in the main paper and Section A in the appendix"},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"Yes"},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"Sections A1-A3 in the appendix"},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"We cite creators of datasets and models in the main paper and the appendix."},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"At the very end, after limitation"},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"N/A"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026consistency,\ntitle={Consistency of Verbalized Confidence Calibration in Language Models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=wbBvQWQpmu},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Consistency of Verbalized Confidence Calibration in Language Models"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"Yes"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"N/A"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"N/A"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"N/A"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"Verbalized Confidence has emerged as an efficient, straightforward, and widely applicable means to assess LLM uncertainty and to mitigate its reliability issues. Despite a growing body of literature that studies the impact and effectiveness of verbalized confidence, many questions remain about its consistency and the reliability of its calibration. For example, the effectiveness trends on narrowly studied domains are sometimes inconsistent, and it is not clear if they calibrate well to critical domains, and whether the sampling uncertainty of the confidence itself has an impact on its reliability. \n\nTo address these, we first present a systematic study that assesses the variability of verbalized confidence across different contexts (task domains, complexity) and in sensitive application settings (e.g., medicine). We then quantify the variance resulting from the sampled confidences and present a simple mitigation strategy. Our study shows that trends in verbalized confidence calibration are highly inconsistent across different contexts and are not always correlated with task performance. Also, we observe that sampled verbalized confidences have large variance, especially for low-confidence answers, and can be improved substantially (e.g., +0.15 points in AUROC for GPQA) through simple aggregates of confidence samples with almost no extra overhead."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"N/A"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/9bb7fdcc860d2eb2999326d4f2c6e96da0ecbfdd.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Interpretability and Analysis of Models for NLP"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"N/A"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"yes"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"N/A"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794005686800,"tcdate":1779779654813,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission12612/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission12612/Authors"],"forum":"wbBvQWQpmu","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":12612,"cdate":1779779654813,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794005686800,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"wbBvQWQpmu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICPR (6) 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-032-31673-8_8.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"fall|learning_rate_informed_priors_for_neural_network_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-31673-8_8"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icpr/FallALT26,\n  author={Mouhamadou Makhtar Fall and Samia Ainouz and Pierre-Jean Lapray and Jean-Philippe Tarel},\n  title={Learning Rate Informed Priors for Neural Network Calibration},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={112-126},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-31673-8_8},\n  booktitle={ICPR (6)},\n  crossref={conf/icpr/2026-6}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Larger learning rates are frequently associated with better generalization, as they encourage exploration of flatter minima in the loss landscape. In this work, we show that the large learning rate phase produces predictive distributions that naturally capture dataset-dependent uncertainty and structured relationships between classes. Across several datasets, we observe that models trained with a large learning rate exhibit significantly lower calibration error early in training, indicating that large learning rate dynamics generate probability distributions that are more faithful to the intrinsic uncertainty of the data labels. Building on this observation, we introduce Learning Rate Informed Priors (LRIP), a simple training strategy that reuses the predictive distributions obtained at the end of the large learning rate phase as improved supervision targets. LRIP replaces uniform or heuristic smoothing with sample-dependent priors extracted directly from the model when its predictions are the most calibrated. Extensive experiments across different modalities and learning-rate schedules demonstrate that LRIP consistently improves calibration while maintaining competitive accuracy. These findings highlight a new perspective on the role of large learning rates: beyond optimization benefits, they induce informative predictive uncertainty that can be directly leveraged to build more reliable and better-calibrated models."},"title":{"value":"Learning Rate Informed Priors for Neural Network Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Mouhamadou Makhtar Fall","username":""},{"fullname":"Samia Ainouz","username":""},{"fullname":"Pierre-Jean Lapray","username":""},{"fullname":"Jean-Philippe Tarel","username":"~Jean-Philippe_Tarel1"}]}},"tmdate":1791457911758,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icpr/FallALT26"],"tcdate":1791457897605,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Jean-Philippe_Tarel1"],"forum":"f8WUQXgoGk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":167003,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791457911758,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"f8WUQXgoGk","version":2},{"content":{"workshop_reviewers":{"value":"~Jiaming_Hu5"},"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"venue":{"value":"Mathematical Foundations and Operational Integration of ML for Uncertainty-Aware Decision-Making Poster"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["Robustness","Data augmentation，Image Classification"]},"abstract":{"value":"In many real-world applications, ensuring the robustness and stability of deep neural networks (DNNs) is crucial, particularly for image classification tasks that encounter various input perturbations. While Mixup-based data augmentation techniques have been widely adopted to enhance the resilience of trained models against such perturbations, our experiments reveal an important corruption robustness–calibration trade-off: stronger Mixup-based augmentation can improve robustness against corrupted data while substantially increasing expected calibration error (ECE). To address this challenge, we introduce DRO-Augment, a framework that integrates Wasserstein Distributionally Robust Optimization (W-DRO) with various Mixup-based data augmentation strategies to mitigate this trade-off. Our method substantially reduces ECE under strong Mixup-based augmentation while largely preserving corruption accuracy across CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, CIFAR-10-C, and CIFAR-100-C. On the theoretical side, we establish novel generalization error bounds for neural networks trained using a variation-regularized loss function with augmented data, closely related to the W-DRO problem. Furthermore, we introduce a refined CIFAR-C benchmark that corrects inconsistencies in corruption intensities, providing a more reliable evaluation for future robustness research."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhu2026improving,\ntitle={Improving Mixup Calibration with Wasserstein Distributionally Robust Optimization},\nauthor={Jiaming Hu and Yeping Jin and Debarghya Mukherjee and Ioannis Ch. Paschalidis},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2026 Second Workshop on MLxOR},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=LWbMmsQipW}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Improving Mixup Calibration with Wasserstein Distributionally Robust Optimization"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2be57357ff4fbdb3d55bc835aacc550dfab6e726.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/MLxOR"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|improving_mixup_calibration_with_wasserstein_distributionally_robust_optimization"},"workshop_to_journal_pathway":{"value":"Mathematics of Operations Research"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Jiaming_Hu5","fullname":"Jiaming Hu","institutions":[{"name":"Boston University","domain":"bu.edu","country":"US"}]},{"username":"~Yeping_Jin1","fullname":"Yeping Jin","institutions":[{"name":"Boston University, Boston University","domain":"bu.edu","country":"US"}]},{"username":"~Debarghya_Mukherjee1","fullname":"Debarghya Mukherjee","institutions":[{"name":"Boston University, Boston University","domain":"bu.edu","country":"US"}]},{"username":"~Ioannis_Ch._Paschalidis1","fullname":"Ioannis Ch. Paschalidis","institutions":[{"name":"Boston University","domain":"bu.edu","country":"US"}]}]}},"tmdate":1791148576476,"pdate":1791148569744,"tcdate":1788261395978,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/MLxOR","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/MLxOR/Submission266/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/MLxOR/Submission266/Authors"],"forum":"LWbMmsQipW","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":266,"cdate":1788261395978,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/MLxOR/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/MLxOR/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/MLxOR/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/MLxOR/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1791148576476,"odate":1791148569744,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/MLxOR","id":"LWbMmsQipW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AISTATS 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v238/abbas24a/abbas24a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AISTATS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"abbas|enhancing_incontext_learning_via_linear_probe_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Momin_Abbas1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Zhou_0015:","~Parikshit_Ram1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nathalie_Baracaldo:","~Horst_Samulowitz1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Theodoros_Salonidis:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianyi_Chen:"]},"html":{"value":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v238/abbas24a.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aistats/AbbasZRBSSC24,\n  author={Momin Abbas and Yi Zhou and Parikshit Ram and Nathalie Baracaldo and Horst Samulowitz and Theodoros Salonidis and Tianyi Chen},\n  title={Enhancing In-context Learning via Linear Probe Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={307-315},\n  url={https://proceedings.mlr.press/v238/abbas24a.html},\n  booktitle={AISTATS},\n  crossref={conf/aistats/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In-context learning (ICL) is a new paradigm for natural language processing that utilizes Generative Pre-trained Transformer (GPT)-like models. This approach uses prompts that include in-context demonstrations to generate the corresponding output for a new query input. However, applying ICL in real cases does not scale with the number of samples, and lacks robustness to different prompt templates and demonstration permutations. In this paper, we first show that GPT-like models using ICL result in unreliable predictions based on a new metric based on Shannon entropy. Then, to solve this problem, we propose a new technique called the Linear Probe Calibration (LinC), a method that calibrates the model’s output probabilities, resulting in reliable predictions and improved performance, while requiring only minimal additional samples (as few as five labeled data samples). LinC significantly enhances the ICL test performance of GPT models on various benchmark datasets, with an average improvement of up to 21%, and up to a 50% improvement in some cases, and significantly boosts the performance of PEFT methods, especially in the low resource regime. Moreover, LinC achieves lower expected calibration error, and is highly robust to varying label proportions, prompt templates, and demonstration permutations."},"title":{"value":"Enhancing In-context Learning via Linear Probe Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Momin Abbas","Yi Zhou","Parikshit Ram","Nathalie Baracaldo","Horst Samulowitz","Theodoros Salonidis","Tianyi Chen"]}},"tmdate":1769438251913,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1725571274671,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Momin_Abbas1"],"forum":"h8q3qIO8Zv","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":79327,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1769438251913,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"h8q3qIO8Zv","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.12546v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"lee|spatiotemporal_multicamera_calibration_using_freely_moving_people"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sang-Eun_Lee:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ko_Nishino:","~Shohei_Nobuhara1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.12546"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2502-12546,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Sang-Eun Lee and Ko Nishino and Shohei Nobuhara},\n  title={Spatiotemporal Multi-Camera Calibration using Freely Moving People},\n  year={2025},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1738368000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2502.12546},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.12546}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We propose a novel method for spatiotemporal multi-camera calibration using freely moving people in multiview videos. Since calibrating multiple cameras and finding matches across their views are inherently interdependent, performing both in a unified framework poses a significant challenge. We address these issues as a single registration problem of matching two sets of 3D points, leveraging human motion in dynamic multi-person scenes. To this end, we utilize 3D human poses obtained from an off-the-shelf monocular 3D human pose estimator and transform them into 3D points on a unit sphere, to solve the rotation, time offset, and the association alternatingly. We employ a probabilistic approach that can jointly solve both problems of aligning spatiotemporal data and establishing correspondences through soft assignment between two views. The translation is determined by applying coplanarity constraints. The pairwise registration results are integrated into a multiview setup, and then a nonlinear optimization method is used to improve the accuracy of the camera poses, temporal offsets, and multi-person associations. Extensive experiments on synthetic and real data demonstrate the effectiveness and flexibility of the proposed method as a practical marker-free calibration tool."},"title":{"value":"Spatiotemporal Multi-Camera Calibration using Freely Moving People"},"authors":{"value":["Sang-Eun Lee","Ko Nishino","Shohei Nobuhara"]}},"tmdate":1766185675100,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2502-12546"],"tcdate":1766185664130,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shohei_Nobuhara2"],"forum":"vNhRN5tZIY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":710728,"cdate":1738368000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1766185675100,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"vNhRN5tZIY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/7083369/10935293/10937736.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"lee|spatiotemporal_multicamera_calibration_using_freely_moving_people"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sangeun_Lee:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ko_Nishino:","~Shohei_Nobuhara1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2025.3554102"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/LeeNN25,\n  author={Sangeun Lee and Ko Nishino and Shohei Nobuhara},\n  title={Spatiotemporal Multi-Camera Calibration Using Freely Moving People},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={10},\n  number={5},\n  pages={4818-4825},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2025.3554102}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We propose a novel method for spatiotemporal multi-camera calibration using freely moving people in multi-view videos. Since calibrating multiple cameras and finding matches across their views are inherently interdependent, performing both in a unified framework poses a significant challenge. We address these issues as a single registration problem of matching two sets of 3D points, leveraging human motion in dynamic multi-person scenes. To this end, we utilize 3D human poses obtained from an off-the-shelf monocular 3D human pose estimator and transform them into 3D points on a unit sphere, to solve the rotation, time offset, and the association alternatingly. We employ a probabilistic approach that can jointly solve both problems of aligning spatiotemporal data and establishing correspondences through soft assignment between two views. The translation is determined by applying coplanarity constraints. The pairwise registration results are integrated into a multiview setup, and then a nonlinear optimization method is used to improve the accuracy of the camera poses, temporal offsets, and multi-person associations. Extensive experiments on synthetic and real data demonstrate the effectiveness and flexibility of the proposed method as a practical marker-free calibration tool."},"title":{"value":"Spatiotemporal Multi-Camera Calibration Using Freely Moving People"},"authors":{"value":["Sangeun Lee","Ko Nishino","Shohei Nobuhara"]}},"tmdate":1766185670972,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/ral/LeeNN25"],"tcdate":1766185663995,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shohei_Nobuhara2"],"forum":"RsG8fMBZKs","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":710727,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1766185670972,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"RsG8fMBZKs","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"WACV 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10483279/10483282/10483620.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/WACV/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"damicantonio|automated_camera_calibration_via_homography_estimation_with_gnns"},"authorids":{"value":["~Giacomo_D'Amicantonio1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Egor_Bondarev:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peter_H._N._de_With:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV57701.2024.00577"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/wacv/DAmicantonioBW24,\n  author={Giacomo D'Amicantonio and Egor Bondarev and Peter H. N. de With},\n  title={Automated Camera Calibration via Homography Estimation with GNNs},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={5864-5471},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV57701.2024.00577},\n  booktitle={WACV},\n  crossref={conf/wacv/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Over the past few decades, a significant rise of camera-based applications for traffic monitoring has occurred. Governments and local administrations are increasingly relying on the data collected from these cameras to enhance road safety and optimize traffic conditions. However, for effective data utilization, it is imperative to ensure accurate and automated calibration of the involved cameras. This paper proposes a novel approach to address this challenge by leveraging the topological structure of intersections.We propose a framework involving the generation of a set of synthetic intersection viewpoint images from a bird’seye-view image, framed as a graph of virtual cameras to model these images. Using the capabilities of Graph Neural Networks, we effectively learn the relationships within this graph, thereby facilitating the estimation of a homography matrix. This estimation leverages the neighbourhood representation for any real-world camera and is enhanced by exploiting multiple images instead of a single match. In turn, the homography matrix allows the retrieval of extrinsic calibration parameters. As a result, the proposed framework demonstrates superior performance on both synthetic datasets and real-world cameras, setting a new state-of-the-art benchmark."},"title":{"value":"Automated Camera Calibration via Homography Estimation with GNNs"},"authors":{"value":["Giacomo D'Amicantonio","Egor Bondarev","Peter H. N. de With"]}},"tmdate":1727789186205,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727789184395,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Giacomo_D'Amicantonio1"],"forum":"3H56c6iafN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":132083,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727789186205,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"3H56c6iafN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE/ASME Transactions on Mechatronics"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/3516/4785241/10926505.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"luo|a_defocused_calibration_method_using_roipatched_mamunet_and_progressive_training_for_froth_flotation_process"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TMECH.2025.3545743"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate and robust calibration of industrial cameras is fundamental to the three-dimensional reconstruction of flotation froth. Nonetheless, during the froth flotation process, the nonuniformity of the planar pattern and froth layer can cause significant local image blurring, thus compromising the calibration accuracy. To address this issue, we propose a camera calibration method for defocused froth images based on ROI-Patched MAM-UNet and progressive training. Initially, we propose a novel circular map deblurring framework, named MAM-UNet, which augments deblurring efficacy through the integration of specific circular masks. Concurrently, we introduce an ROI-Patched-based parallel computing method, enhancing the model's adaptability to nonuniform image blurring, and effectively balancing calibration accuracy and computational efficiency. Furthermore, we implement a progressive training strategy for ROI-Patched MAM-UNet, and devise pretraining and fine-tuning datasets tailored for circular planar patterns. Finally, extensive experiments on both synthetic datasets and real-world froth flotation processes demonstrate the efficacy and robustness of the proposed defocused calibration method."},"title":{"value":"A Defocused Calibration Method Using ROI-Patched MAM-UNet and Progressive Training for Froth Flotation Process"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jin Luo","username":"~Jin_Luo5"},{"fullname":"Zhaohui Tang"},{"fullname":"Hu Zhang"},{"fullname":"Ying Fan"},{"fullname":"Yongfang Xie"},{"fullname":"Zibang Nie"},{"fullname":"Weihua Gui"}]}},"tmdate":1789089579294,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tmech.2025.3545743"],"tcdate":1762246945651,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Jin_Luo5"],"forum":"wZpZvqHZh8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":3427,"cdate":1741982532655,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789089579294,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"wZpZvqHZh8","version":2},{"content":{"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"cioppa|camera_calibration_and_player_localization_in_soccernetv2_and_investigation_of_their_representations_for_action_spotting"},"authorids":{"value":["~Anthony_Cioppa1","~Adrien_Deliege1","~Floriane_Magera1","~Silvio_Giancola1","~Olivier_Barnich1","~Bernard_Ghanem1","~Marc_Van_Droogenbroeck1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.09333"},"abstract":{"value":"Soccer broadcast video understanding has been drawing a lot of attention in recent years within data scientists and industrial companies. This is mainly due to the lucrative potential unlocked by effective deep learning techniques developed in the field of computer vision. In this work, we focus on the topic of camera calibration and on its current limitations for the scientific community. More precisely, we tackle the absence of a large-scale calibration dataset and of a public calibration network trained on such a dataset. Specifically, we distill a powerful commercial calibration tool in a recent neural network architecture on the large-scale SoccerNet dataset, composed of untrimmed broadcast videos of 500 soccer games. We further release our distilled network, and leverage it to provide 3 ways of representing the calibration results along with player localization. Finally, we exploit those representations within the current best architecture for the action spotting task of SoccerNet-v2, and achieve new state-of-the-art performances."},"title":{"value":"Camera calibration and player localization in SoccerNet-v2 and investigation of their representations for action spotting"},"authors":{"value":["Anthony Cioppa","Adrien Deliege","Floriane Magera","Silvio Giancola","Olivier Barnich","Bernard Ghanem","Marc Van Droogenbroeck"]}},"tmdate":1788945502524,"pdate":1618783200000,"tcdate":1788945502524,"writers":["~Anthony_Cioppa1","~Adrien_Deliege1","~Floriane_Magera1","~Silvio_Giancola1","~Olivier_Barnich1","~Bernard_Ghanem1","~Marc_Van_Droogenbroeck1"],"signatures":["~Floriane_Magera1"],"forum":"7tBsetH4sJ","license":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license","number":53863,"cdate":1788945502524,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1788945502524,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"7tBsetH4sJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.09333v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"cioppa|camera_calibration_and_player_localization_in_soccernetv2_and_investigation_of_their_representations_for_action_spotting"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Anthony_Cioppa:","~Adrien_Deliege1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Floriane_Magera:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Silvio_Giancola:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Olivier_Barnich:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bernard_Ghanem:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marc_Van_Droogenbroeck:"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.09333"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2104-09333,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Anthony Cioppa and Adrien Deliège and Floriane Magera and Silvio Giancola and Olivier Barnich and Bernard Ghanem and Marc Van Droogenbroeck},\n  title={Camera Calibration and Player Localization in SoccerNet-v2 and Investigation of their Representations for Action Spotting},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2104.09333},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.09333}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Soccer broadcast video understanding has been drawing a lot of attention in recent years within data scientists and industrial companies. This is mainly due to the lucrative potential unlocked by effective deep learning techniques developed in the field of computer vision. In this work, we focus on the topic of camera calibration and on its current limitations for the scientific community. More precisely, we tackle the absence of a large-scale calibration dataset and of a public calibration network trained on such a dataset. Specifically, we distill a powerful commercial calibration tool in a recent neural network architecture on the large-scale SoccerNet dataset, composed of untrimmed broadcast videos of 500 soccer games. We further release our distilled network, and leverage it to provide 3 ways of representing the calibration results along with player localization. Finally, we exploit those representations within the current best architecture for the action spotting task of SoccerNet-v2, and achieve new state-of-the-art performances."},"title":{"value":"Camera Calibration and Player Localization in SoccerNet-v2 and Investigation of their Representations for Action Spotting"},"authors":{"value":["Anthony Cioppa","Adrien Deliège","Floriane Magera","Silvio Giancola","Olivier Barnich","Bernard Ghanem","Marc Van Droogenbroeck"]}},"tmdate":1747228404636,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1747228389102,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Adrien_Deliege1"],"forum":"JOK5XBWhK7","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":458362,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747228404636,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"JOK5XBWhK7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR Workshops 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2021W/CVSports/papers/Cioppa_Camera_Calibration_and_Player_Localization_in_SoccerNet-v2_and_Investigation_of_CVPRW_2021_paper.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"cioppa|camera_calibration_and_player_localization_in_soccernetv2_and_investigation_of_their_representations_for_action_spotting"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Anthony_Cioppa:","~Adrien_Deliege1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Floriane_Magera:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Silvio_Giancola:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Olivier_Barnich:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bernard_Ghanem:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marc_Van_Droogenbroeck:"]},"html":{"value":"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2021W/CVSports/html/Cioppa_Camera_Calibration_and_Player_Localization_in_SoccerNet-v2_and_Investigation_of_CVPRW_2021_paper.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/CioppaDMGBGD21,\n  author={Anthony Cioppa and Adrien Deliège and Floriane Magera and Silvio Giancola and Olivier Barnich and Bernard Ghanem and Marc Van Droogenbroeck},\n  title={Camera Calibration and Player Localization in SoccerNet-v2 and Investigation of Their Representations for Action Spotting},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={4537-4546},\n  url={https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2021W/CVSports/html/Cioppa_Camera_Calibration_and_Player_Localization_in_SoccerNet-v2_and_Investigation_of_CVPRW_2021_paper.html},\n  booktitle={CVPR Workshops},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2021w}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Soccer broadcast video understanding has been drawing a lot of attention in recent years within data scientists and industrial companies. This is mainly due to the lucrative potential unlocked by effective deep learning techniques developed in the field of computer vision. In this work, we focus on the topic of camera calibration and on its current limitations for the scientific community. More precisely, we tackle the absence of a large-scale calibration dataset and of a public calibration network trained on such a dataset. Specifically, we distill a powerful commercial calibration tool in a recent neural network architecture on the large-scale SoccerNet dataset, composed of untrimmed broadcast videos of 500 soccer games. We further release our distilled network, and leverage it to provide 3 ways of representing the calibration results along with player localization. Finally, we exploit those representations within the current best architecture for the action spotting task of SoccerNet-v2, and achieve new state-of-the-art performances."},"title":{"value":"Camera Calibration and Player Localization in SoccerNet-v2 and Investigation of Their Representations for Action Spotting"},"authors":{"value":["Anthony Cioppa","Adrien Deliège","Floriane Magera","Silvio Giancola","Olivier Barnich","Bernard Ghanem","Marc Van Droogenbroeck"]}},"tmdate":1747228395290,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1747228388875,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Adrien_Deliege1"],"forum":"ph8cDMDIZQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":458354,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747228395290,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ph8cDMDIZQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACM Trans. Archit. Code Optim. 2017"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TACO/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"weber|matog_array_layout_autotuning_for_cuda"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nicolas_Weber:","~Michael_Goesele3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3106341"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/taco/WeberG17,\n  author={Nicolas Weber and Michael Goesele},\n  title={MATOG: Array Layout Auto-Tuning for CUDA},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  journal={ACM Trans. Archit. Code Optim.},\n  volume={14},\n  number={3},\n  pages={28:1-28:26},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3106341}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Optimal code performance is (besides correctness and accuracy) the most important objective in compute intensive applications. In many of these applications, Graphic Processing Units (GPUs) are used because of their high amount of compute power. However, caused by their massively parallel architecture, the code has to be specifically adjusted to the underlying hardware to achieve optimal performance and therefore has to be reoptimized for each new generation. In reality, this is usually not the case as productive code is normally at least several years old and nobody has the time to continuously adjust existing code to new hardware. In recent years more and more approaches have emerged that automatically tune the performance of applications toward the underlying hardware. In this article, we present the MATOG auto-tuner and its concepts. It abstracts the array memory access in CUDA applications and automatically optimizes the code according to the used GPUs. MATOG only requires few profiling runs to analyze even complex applications, while achieving significant speedups over non-optimized code, independent of the used GPU generation and without the need to manually tune the code."},"title":{"value":"MATOG: Array Layout Auto-Tuning for CUDA"},"authors":{"value":["Nicolas Weber","Michael Goesele"]}},"tmdate":1730845379218,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1730845337827,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Michael_Goesele3"],"forum":"YtLXydp1Lk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":171383,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1730845379218,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"YtLXydp1Lk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACM Trans. Softw. Eng. Methodol. 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TOSEM/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|refining_chatgptgenerated_code_characterizing_and_mitigating_code_quality_issues"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yue_Liu17","~Thanh_Le-Cong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ratnadira_Widyasari:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chakkrit_Tantithamthavorn:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Li_Li_0029:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuan-Bach_Dinh_Le_0001:","~David_Lo1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3643674"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tosem/LiuLWTLLL24,\n  author={Yue Liu and Thanh Le-Cong and Ratnadira Widyasari and Chakkrit Tantithamthavorn and Li Li and Xuan-Bach Dinh Le and David Lo},\n  title={Refining ChatGPT-Generated Code: Characterizing and Mitigating Code Quality Issues},\n  year={2024},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1717200000000},\n  journal={ACM Trans. Softw. Eng. Methodol.},\n  volume={33},\n  number={5},\n  pages={116:1-116:26},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3643674}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Since its introduction in November 2022, ChatGPT has rapidly gained popularity due to its remarkable ability in language understanding and human-like responses. ChatGPT, based on GPT-3.5 architecture, has shown great promise for revolutionizing various research fields, including code generation. However, the reliability and quality of code generated by ChatGPT remain unexplored, raising concerns about potential risks associated with the widespread use of ChatGPT-driven code generation.In this article, we systematically study the quality of 4,066 ChatGPT-generated programs of code implemented in two popular programming languages, i.e., Java and Python, for 2,033 programming tasks. The goal of this work is threefold. First, we analyze the correctness of ChatGPT on code generation tasks and uncover the factors that influence its effectiveness, including task difficulty, programming language, time that tasks are introduced, and program size. Second, we identify and characterize potential issues with the quality of ChatGPT-generated code. Last, we provide insights into how these issues can be mitigated. Experiments highlight that out of 4,066 programs generated by ChatGPT, 2,756 programs are deemed correct, 1,082 programs provide wrong outputs, and 177 programs contain compilation or runtime errors. Additionally, we further analyze other characteristics of the generated code through static analysis tools, such as code style and maintainability, and find that 1,930 ChatGPT-generated code snippets suffer from maintainability issues. Subsequently, we investigate ChatGPT’s self-repairing ability and its interaction with static analysis tools to fix the errors uncovered in the previous step. Experiments suggest that ChatGPT can partially address these challenges, improving code quality by more than 20%, but there are still limitations and opportunities for improvement. Overall, our study provides valuable insights into the current limitations of ChatGPT and offers a roadmap for future research and development efforts to enhance the code generation capabilities of artificial intelligence models such as ChatGPT."},"title":{"value":"Refining ChatGPT-Generated Code: Characterizing and Mitigating Code Quality Issues"},"authors":{"value":["Yue Liu","Thanh Le-Cong","Ratnadira Widyasari","Chakkrit Tantithamthavorn","Li Li","Xuan-Bach Dinh Le","David Lo"]}},"tmdate":1744863848941,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1734956621303,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yue_Liu17"],"forum":"lG9zyrrW0S","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":259207,"cdate":1717200000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1744863848941,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"lG9zyrrW0S","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Knowledge Graphs, Entity Alignment, Multi-Modal"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2025 February Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025probing,\ntitle={Probing Relative Interaction and Dynamic Calibration in Multi-modal Entity Alignment},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - February 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=kau10srpp3},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Probing Relative Interaction and Dynamic Calibration in Multi-modal Entity Alignment"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","NLP engineering experiment","Approaches to low-resource settings","Theory"]},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-modal entity alignment aims to identify equivalent entities between two different multi-modal knowledge graphs. Current methods have made significant progress by improving embedding and cross-modal fusion. However, most of them depend on using loss functions to capture the relationship between modalities or adopt a one-time strategy to directly compute modality weights using attention mechanisms, which overlooks the relative interactions between modalities at the entity level and the accuracy of modality weights, thereby hindering the generalization to diverse entities. To address this challenge, we propose RICEA, a relative interaction and calibration framework for multi-modal entity alignment, which dynamically computes weights based on the relative interaction and recalibrates the weights according to their uncertainties. Among these, we propose a novel method called $ADC$ that utilizes attention mechanisms to perceive the uncertainty of the weight for each modality, rather than directly calculating the weight of each modality as in previous works. Across 5 datasets and 22 settings, our proposed framework significantly outperforms other baselines. Our code and data are available at \\href{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/RICEA-12D7/}{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/RICEA-12D7/}."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/59bbb76359ab5f32f5095eb6f7898530567faf1f.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"NLP Applications"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782962071495,"tcdate":1739692698203,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission6107/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission6107/Authors"],"forum":"kau10srpp3","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6107,"cdate":1739692698203,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782962071495,"odate":1746821355472,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February","id":"kau10srpp3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.22769v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"lei|macgaze_motionaware_continual_calibration_for_mobile_gaze_tracking"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yaxiong_Lei1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mingyue_Zhao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuheng_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shijing_He:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yusuke_Sugano:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yafei_Wang:","~Kaixing_Zhao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohamed_Khamis:","~Juan_Ye1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.22769"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-22769,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yaxiong Lei and Mingyue Zhao and Yuheng Wang and Shijing He and Yusuke Sugano and Yafei Wang and Kaixing Zhao and Mohamed Khamis and Juan Ye},\n  title={MAC-Gaze: Motion-Aware Continual Calibration for Mobile Gaze Tracking},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2505.22769},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.22769}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Mobile gaze tracking faces a fundamental challenge: maintaining accuracy as users naturally change their postures and device orientations. Traditional calibration approaches, like one-off, fail to adapt to these dynamic conditions, leading to degraded performance over time. We present MAC-Gaze, a Motion-Aware continual Calibration approach that leverages smartphone Inertial measurement unit (IMU) sensors and continual learning techniques to automatically detect changes in user motion states and update the gaze tracking model accordingly. Our system integrates a pre-trained visual gaze estimator and an IMU-based activity recognition model with a clustering-based hybrid decision-making mechanism that triggers recalibration when motion patterns deviate significantly from previously encountered states. To enable accumulative learning of new motion conditions while mitigating catastrophic forgetting, we employ replay-based continual learning, allowing the model to maintain performance across previously encountered motion conditions. We evaluate our system through extensive experiments on the publicly available RGBDGaze dataset and our own 10-hour multimodal MotionGaze dataset (481K+ images, 800K+ IMU readings), encompassing a wide range of postures under various motion conditions including sitting, standing, lying, and walking. Results demonstrate that our method reduces gaze estimation error by 19.9% on RGBDGaze (from 1.73 cm to 1.41 cm) and by 31.7% on MotionGaze (from 2.81 cm to 1.92 cm) compared to traditional calibration approaches. Our framework provides a robust solution for maintaining gaze estimation accuracy in mobile scenarios."},"title":{"value":"MAC-Gaze: Motion-Aware Continual Calibration for Mobile Gaze Tracking"},"authors":{"value":["Yaxiong Lei","Mingyue Zhao","Yuheng Wang","Shijing He","Yusuke Sugano","Yafei Wang","Kaixing Zhao","Mohamed Khamis","Juan Ye"]}},"tmdate":1790259110935,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2505-22769"],"tcdate":1769364762584,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Juan_Ye1"],"forum":"qVpmu8hszb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":800408,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1790259110935,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"qVpmu8hszb","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"venue":{"value":"DeCaF 2026 Oral"},"TLDR":{"value":"Federated conformal calibration can hide failures at small hospitals; we show this on brain tumor segmentation and propose risk-curve shrinkage to recover site-level reliability with practical prediction-set sizes."},"note":{"value":1},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["Federated Learning","Conformal Risk Control","Segmentation","Uncertainty Quantification"]},"abstract":{"value":"Conformal risk control (CRC) provides distribution-free segmentation guarantees by calibrating a prediction-set threshold on held-out data. In federated deployments, the standard approach pools calibration scores into a single threshold. We quantify, on real multi-institutional brain tumor data (FeTS-2022, 1,251 subjects, 20 institutions), a critical failure: naive pooled CRC protects the average hospital but violates coverage at 40% of individual institutions, with the worst site exceeding the target false-negative rate by 7.8 percentage points. We trace this failure to a hidden design choice: the aggregation weights implicitly determine whose coverage is protected. Sample-size weighting optimizes patient-level validity but can sacrifice institution-level reliability; equal-site weighting improves institution-level reliability on this benchmark at comparable efficiency, using only a single scalar per site. We propose risk-curve shrinkage as a principled mechanism: each site transmits its empirical risk curve (G scalars) and a single hyperparameter n0 smoothly interpolates between site-specific local calibration and sample-size-weighted pooled calibration. Leave-one-site-out sensitivity analysis identifies n0=19, achieving 2.7/20 violations at 2.0x stretch. Direct Lagrangian budget optimization fails by concentrating risk on vulnerable hospitals; the finite-sample correction term is essential: removing it triples violations. No patient-level images, masks, or per-volume scores leave any site."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nshahid2026when,\ntitle={When Average Calibration Fails: Site-Conditional Federated Conformal Risk Control},\nauthor={Nafis Fuad Shahid},\nbooktitle={7th MICCAI Workshop on {\\textquotedblleft}Distributed, Collaborative and Federated Learning{\\textquotedblright}},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=pe2Lr0Zdlq}\n}"},"title":{"value":"When Average Calibration Fails: Site-Conditional Federated Conformal Risk Control"},"original_pdf":{"value":"/pdf/01809b726f721bcd98cf1e15af74698fcdde634f.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF"},"paperhash":{"value":"shahid|when_average_calibration_fails_siteconditional_federated_conformal_risk_control"},"authorids":{"value":["~Nafis_Fuad_Shahid1"]},"authors":{"value":["Nafis Fuad Shahid"]}},"tmdate":1787933529188,"pdate":1787813190775,"tcdate":1781702022588,"writers":["MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF","MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF/Submission4/Authors"],"signatures":["MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF/Submission4/Authors"],"forum":"pe2Lr0Zdlq","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4,"cdate":1781702022588,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF/-/Submission","MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF/Submission4/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF/-/Submission_Release","MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF/-/Edit"],"mdate":1787933529188,"odate":1787813190775,"domain":"MICCAI.org/2026/Workshop/DeCaF","id":"pe2Lr0Zdlq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Wirel. Commun. 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7693/6836134/06836139.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TWC/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|temperatureassisted_clock_synchronization_and_selfcalibration_for_sensor_networks"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhe_Yang_0008:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liang_He_0002:","~Lin_Cai2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianping_Pan_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TWC.2014.051414.130270"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/twc/YangHCP14,\n  author={Zhe Yang and Liang He and Lin Cai and Jianping Pan},\n  title={Temperature-Assisted Clock Synchronization and Self-Calibration for Sensor Networks},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Wirel. Commun.},\n  volume={13},\n  number={6},\n  pages={3419-3429},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TWC.2014.051414.130270}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Synchronization is a pre-requisite for many sensor network applications. However, it remains challenging in sensor networks due to both the limited resources and the dynamic environments. In this paper, we propose a new two-phase clock synchronization scheme. The first one is the external clock synchronization phase, during which nodes update their clock by exchanging timestamp messages with the reference clock. Different from the conventional solutions, we propose to directly remove the clock skew during the external synchronization to achieve a higher synchronization accuracy and lower computational complexity. The second one is the clock self-calibration phase, as the accumulated clock skew will make the synchronized clock drift away again, we need to compensate the clock skew to maintain the clock synchronization accuracy. However, the compensation is non-trivial as the clock skew may not be constant due to the changing environment. Thus we propose the temperature-assisted clock self-calibration (TACSC) to dynamically compensate the clock skew according to the working temperature. Extensive simulation demonstrates that the proposed synchronization scheme can achieve a much lower root mean square error in the external synchronization phase. Furthermore, during the clock self-calibration phase, the TACSC scheme can improve the synchronization accuracy by more than one order of magnitude, which is verified by both simulation and testbed experimentation."},"title":{"value":"Temperature-Assisted Clock Synchronization and Self-Calibration for Sensor Networks"},"authors":{"value":["Zhe Yang","Liang He","Lin Cai","Jianping Pan"]}},"tmdate":1762963584017,"pdate":1388534400000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/twc/YangHCP14"],"tcdate":1762963540797,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lin_Cai2"],"forum":"eFNEkaimHx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":697611,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762963584017,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"eFNEkaimHx","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"multimodality,cross-modal application,cross-modal content generation,image text matching,multimodality"},"keywords":{"value":["Multimodal Jailbreak Detection","Hidden-State Monitoring","Depth Misalignment","Modality-Aware Calibration"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"No"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"Yes"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"Section 5, Appendix J"},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_elaboration":{"value":"No user-specific or personally identifiable information (PII) is involved. All datasets (XSTest, MM-SafetyBench, audio benchmarks) are public, standardized safety benchmarks with anonymized, non-identifiable content, and no offensive material was introduced or used."},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"No"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"No"},"B5_elaboration":{"value":"The paper focuses on methodology and experimental validation of jailbreak detection. It does not provide dedicated documentation detailing artifact domains, languages, linguistic phenomena, or demographic groups. All artifacts are standard public benchmarks widely adopted in the research community."},"A2_elaboration":{"value":"A2 Elaboration Section 6、Section 8、Section 9"},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"Section 6, Appendix D, Appendix F, Appendix G"},"B2_elaboration":{"value":"All artifacts adopted in this work, including referenced datasets and research resources from prior studies, are sourced from officially released academic resources that allow legitimate secondary research usage. The original publications and affiliated repositories have specified standard academic usage terms for non-commercial research scenarios. This study strictly abides by the inherent usage regulations of the original artifacts, and no additional independent license declaration and discussion is required for the current research application."},"B3_elaboration":{"value":"Section 9"},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"Section 5, Appendix I, Appendix J, Appendix R"},"C4_elaboration":{"value":"Section 5"},"D3_elaboration":{"value":"All data sourced and referenced from existing published studies is publicly available and officially downloadable from open academic databases and journal repositories. The original published papers have completed formal data release and ethical compliance procedures, and the publicly accessible data does not involve individual private, sensitive or unauthorised personal information. Therefore, no additional separate data consent acquisition procedures are required for the secondary use of these standard open research datasets in this work."},"D4_elaboration":{"value":"This research does not involve new human data collection or human subjects. All experiments are conducted on existing public multimodal safety benchmarks. Therefore, ethics review board approval is not applicable."},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"No"},"D1_elaboration":{"value":"No human participants or annotators were involved in this research, so no instructions, screenshots, or risk disclaimers are applicable."},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"Section 5, Appendix U"},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3, Section 5, Section M, Section References"},"D2_elaboration":{"value":"No human participants or annotators were recruited or paid in this work, so this information is not applicable."},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"No AI assistants were used in the research, writing, or experimental process of this work."},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"No"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026where,\ntitle={Where is the Safety Signal? Modality-Aware Depth Calibration for Jailbreak Detection},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=keDLRc6wnv},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Where is the Safety Signal? Modality-Aware Depth Calibration for Jailbreak Detection"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"Yes"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","Publicly available software and/or pre-trained models","Theory"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"No"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"Yes"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"No"},"abstract":{"value":"Current hidden-state jailbreak monitors typically rely on pooled or fixed-depth representations. In this paper, we reveal a critical structural vulnerability in this paradigm: Depth misalignment. Because multimodal models process native text and external modalities with different semantic delays, the layers that best separate harmful from benign inputs systematically diverge depending on the input modality, creating substantial gaps in optimal detection depths. To address this, we introduce Modality-Aware Layer Search (MALS), a lightweight calibration procedure that dynamically selects layer subsets conditioned on input modality. On severely misaligned text-only settings, MALS improves AUPRC by 12.7-20.2 percentage points, while maintaining parity on already-aligned conditions. Crucially, we demonstrate that the fundamental principle of modality-conditioned depth calibration itself drives these massive gains, with MALS serving as an optimized, practical recipe. Ultimately, our findings establish that modality-aware depth calibration is not merely a performance tweak, but a structural necessity for robust multimodal hidden-state monitoring."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"No"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/252add50e658b97fdef3eeb3b2c8d080719104a0.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Multimodality and Language Grounding to Vision, Robotics and Beyond"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"yes"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"No"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"No"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794005673701,"tcdate":1778845956370,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission969/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission969/Authors"],"forum":"keDLRc6wnv","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":969,"cdate":1778845956370,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794005673701,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"keDLRc6wnv","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["Prediction-Powered Inference","Semi-Supervised Inference","Generalized Entropy Calibration","Weight Calibration","Semiparametric Efficiency"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nlee2026mec,\ntitle={{MEC}: Machine-Learning-Assisted Generalized Entropy Calibration for Semi-Supervised Mean Estimation},\nauthor={Se Yoon Lee and Jae Kwang Kim},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=kWxN36TDN8}\n}"},"title":{"value":"MEC: Machine-Learning-Assisted Generalized Entropy Calibration for Semi-Supervised Mean Estimation"},"paperhash":{"value":"lee|mec_machinelearningassisted_generalized_entropy_calibration_for_semisupervised_mean_estimation"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/8c763daf6c454dfb1b1e1f26ec56f214e1711e47.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a new variant of prediction-powered inference for semi-supervised mean estimation: machine-learning-assisted generalized entropy calibration."},"primary_area":{"value":"general_machine_learning->unsupervised_and_semisupervised_learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Obtaining high-quality labels is costly, whereas unlabeled covariates are often abundant, motivating semi-supervised inference methods with reliable uncertainty quantification. Prediction-powered inference (PPI) leverages a machine-learning predictor trained on a small labeled sample to improve efficiency, but it can lose efficiency under model misspecification and suffer from coverage distortions due to label reuse. We introduce Machine‑Learning‑Assisted Generalized Entropy Calibration (MEC), a cross‑fitted, calibration‑weighted variant of PPI. MEC improves efficiency by reweighting labeled samples to better align with the target population, using a principled calibration framework based on Bregman projections. This yields robustness to affine transformations of the predictor and relaxes requirements for validity by replacing conditions on raw prediction error with weaker projection‑error conditions. As a result, MEC attains the semiparametric efficiency bound under weaker assumptions than existing PPI variants. Across simulations and a real‑data application, MEC achieves near‑nominal coverage and tighter confidence intervals than CF‑PPI and vanilla PPI."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/1db0babd0d09d934af9db64b6d7adc069cab84b0.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Many scientific studies have a small amount of high-quality labeled data, such as carefully measured outcomes, but a much larger amount of unlabeled data, such as patient characteristics, images, or other features. Machine learning can help make use of these unlabeled data, but naive use of predictions can lead to unreliable uncertainty estimates.\n\nThis paper proposes a new method called Machine-Learning-Assisted Generalized Entropy Calibration, or MEC. The method uses machine-learning predictions to guide how the labeled data are reweighted, so that the labeled sample better represents the larger target population. This improves statistical efficiency while maintaining valid confidence intervals.\n\nCompared with existing prediction-powered inference methods, MEC is more robust when the machine-learning predictor is imperfect. It also avoids the double use of the same labeled data for both training and evaluation by using cross-fitting. In simulations and a real-data example, MEC provides confidence intervals with close-to-nominal coverage and often shorter lengths than existing methods.\n\nOverall, MEC provides a principled way to combine machine learning with statistical inference when labels are expensive but unlabeled covariates are plentiful."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Se_Yoon_Lee1","~Jae_Kwang_Kim1"]},"authors":{"value":["Se Yoon Lee","Jae Kwang Kim"]}},"tmdate":1790067425600,"pdate":1777576825124,"tcdate":1769203198106,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission28376/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission28376/Authors"],"forum":"kWxN36TDN8","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":28376,"cdate":1769203198106,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission28376/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission28376/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067425600,"odate":1782342018850,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"kWxN36TDN8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 spotlight"},"keywords":{"value":["Monocular SLAM","Calibration-Free","Vision Foundation Models","Long-Range Consistency","Autonomous Driving"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nxiong2026vggtmotion,\ntitle={{VGGT}-Motion: Motion-Aware Calibration-Free Monocular {SLAM} for Long-Range Consistency},\nauthor={Zhuang Xiong and Chen Zhang and Qingshan Xu and Wenbing Tao},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=GyRMbsYFiG}\n}"},"title":{"value":"VGGT-Motion: Motion-Aware Calibration-Free Monocular SLAM for Long-Range Consistency"},"paperhash":{"value":"xiong|vggtmotion_motionaware_calibrationfree_monocular_slam_for_longrange_consistency"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/5f8f991c4f1da80154acd1bd4be92d6bfd9ac838.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"Efficient and Robust Calibration-Free Monocular SLAM"},"primary_area":{"value":"applications->computer_vision"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite recent progress in calibration-free monocular SLAM via 3D vision foundation models, scale drift remains severe on long sequences. Motion-agnostic partitioning breaks contextual coherence and causes zero-motion drift, while conventional geometric alignment is computationally expensive. To address these issues, we propose VGGT-Motion, a calibration-free SLAM system for efficient and robust global consistency over kilometer-scale trajectories. Specifically, we first propose a motion-aware submap construction mechanism that uses optical flow to guide adaptive partitioning, prune static redundancy, and encapsulate turns for stable local geometry. We then design an anchor-driven direct Sim(3) registration strategy. By exploiting context-balanced anchors, it achieves search-free, pixel-wise dense alignment and efficient loop closure without costly feature matching. Finally, a lightweight submap-level pose graph optimization enforces global consistency with linear complexity, enabling scalable long-range operation. Experiments show that VGGT-Motion markedly improves trajectory accuracy and efficiency, achieving state-of-the-art performance in zero-shot, long-range calibration-free monocular SLAM."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/dd631f65ff2ca6199a6897ee3816879152720eef.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"Monocular SLAM allows a computer to estimate a camera's motion and build a map of the surrounding scene using only a single camera. This is useful for robots, autonomous vehicles, and augmented reality systems, but it becomes difficult over long distances because small errors can accumulate and cause the estimated trajectory to drift. Our work introduces VGGT-Motion, a system that uses motion information to divide long videos into better local segments and then aligns them efficiently. By reducing unnecessary frames, preserving important turning motions, and improving the alignment between segments, VGGT-Motion produces more consistent trajectories over long sequences without requiring camera calibration. Experiments on multiple driving datasets show that our method is more accurate and scalable than previous foundation-model-based monocular SLAM systems."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhuang_Xiong1","~Chen_Zhang20","~Qingshan_Xu1","~Wenbing_Tao1"]},"authors":{"value":["Zhuang Xiong","Chen Zhang","Qingshan Xu","Wenbing Tao"]}},"tmdate":1790067357466,"pdate":1777576453183,"tcdate":1769068445751,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14508/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14508/Authors"],"forum":"GyRMbsYFiG","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":14508,"cdate":1769068445751,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14508/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission14508/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067357466,"odate":1782341954160,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"GyRMbsYFiG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/19/10012124/10256229.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIM/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"lin|a_threestep_calibration_method_of_sensors_pose_for_magnetic_localization_system"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weijun_Lin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shijie_Ding:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zheng_Li:","~Ning_Yang1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2023.3317478"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tim/LinDLY23,\n  author={Weijun Lin and Shijie Ding and Zheng Li and Ning Yang},\n  title={A Three-Step Calibration Method of Sensors' Pose for Magnetic Localization System},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Instrum. Meas.},\n  volume={72},\n  pages={1-10},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2023.3317478}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrating the pose of all magnetic sensors is a necessary prepossessing step in tracking intrabody objects such as wireless capsule endoscopy (WCE) by magnetic fields. The existing calibration methods are optimization-based and need good initial values for the pose of the sensors. Manual measurement of the initial values can be a cumbersome and sometimes challenging process. Therefore, we propose a three-step calibration method to automatically obtain the rough initial value of the sensor pose. First, the initial sensor orientation can be estimated by rotating the system. Next, the initial position is obtained using the orientation estimated earlier and restricting a strong magnet to face two specific orientations. We provide a detailed deduction process for obtaining the analytical solution for the position. Finally, bundle adjustment is used to further optimize the sensor orientation and position. Experimental results have shown that the magnetic localization system achieves higher accuracy after applying the proposed calibration method."},"title":{"value":"A Three-Step Calibration Method of Sensors' Pose for Magnetic Localization System"},"authors":{"value":["Weijun Lin","Shijie Ding","Zheng Li","Ning Yang"]}},"tmdate":1768451238571,"pdate":1672531200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tim/LinDLY23"],"tcdate":1768451198955,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ning_Yang1"],"forum":"RudRWY1c8L","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":757146,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768451238571,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"RudRWY1c8L","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Robotics 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/8860/10778592/10938346.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TROB/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|aquaslam_tightly_coupled_underwater_acousticvisualinertial_slam_with_sensor_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shida_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaicheng_Zhang:","~Sen_Wang7"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TRO.2025.3554396"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/trob/XuZW25,\n  author={Shida Xu and Kaicheng Zhang and Sen Wang},\n  title={AQUA-SLAM: Tightly Coupled Underwater Acoustic-Visual-Inertial SLAM With Sensor Calibration},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Robotics},\n  volume={41},\n  pages={2785-2803},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TRO.2025.3554396}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Underwater environments pose significant challenges for visual simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) systems due to limited visibility, inadequate illumination, and sporadic loss of structural features in images. Addressing these challenges, this article introduces a novel, tightly coupled acoustic-visual-inertial SLAM approach, termed AQUA-SLAM, to fuse a Doppler velocity log (DVL), a stereo camera, and an inertial measurement unit (IMU) within a graph optimization framework. Moreover, we propose an efficient sensor calibration technique, encompassing the multisensor extrinsic calibration (among the DVL, camera, and IMU) and the DVL transducer misalignment calibration, with a fast linear approximation procedure for real-time online execution. The proposed methods are extensively evaluated in a tank environment with ground truth, and validated for offshore applications in the North Sea. The results demonstrate that our method surpasses current state-of-the-art underwater and visual-inertial SLAM systems in terms of localization accuracy and robustness. The proposed system will be made open-source for the community."},"title":{"value":"AQUA-SLAM: Tightly Coupled Underwater Acoustic-Visual-Inertial SLAM With Sensor Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Shida Xu","Kaicheng Zhang","Sen Wang"]}},"tmdate":1765901638421,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/trob/XuZW25"],"tcdate":1765901620605,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sen_Wang7"],"forum":"escSc53cT6","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":709580,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1765901638421,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"escSc53cT6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.11420v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|aquaslam_tightlycoupled_underwater_acousticvisualinertial_slam_with_sensor_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shida_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaicheng_Zhang:","~Sen_Wang7"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.11420"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2503-11420,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shida Xu and Kaicheng Zhang and Sen Wang},\n  title={AQUA-SLAM: Tightly-Coupled Underwater Acoustic-Visual-Inertial SLAM with Sensor Calibration},\n  year={2025},\n  month={March},\n  cdate={1740787200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2503.11420},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.11420}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Underwater environments pose significant challenges for visual Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) systems due to limited visibility, inadequate illumination, and sporadic loss of structural features in images. Addressing these challenges, this paper introduces a novel, tightly-coupled Acoustic-Visual-Inertial SLAM approach, termed AQUA-SLAM, to fuse a Doppler Velocity Log (DVL), a stereo camera, and an Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU) within a graph optimization framework. Moreover, we propose an efficient sensor calibration technique, encompassing multi-sensor extrinsic calibration (among the DVL, camera and IMU) and DVL transducer misalignment calibration, with a fast linear approximation procedure for real-time online execution. The proposed methods are extensively evaluated in a tank environment with ground truth, and validated for offshore applications in the North Sea. The results demonstrate that our method surpasses current state-of-the-art underwater and visual-inertial SLAM systems in terms of localization accuracy and robustness. The proposed system will be made open-source for the community."},"title":{"value":"AQUA-SLAM: Tightly-Coupled Underwater Acoustic-Visual-Inertial SLAM with Sensor Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Shida Xu","Kaicheng Zhang","Sen Wang"]}},"tmdate":1765901634190,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2503-11420"],"tcdate":1765901620743,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sen_Wang7"],"forum":"MuRkonThfa","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":709571,"cdate":1740787200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1765901634190,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"MuRkonThfa","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ROBOT (1) 2015"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-319-27146-0_37.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ROBOT/2015"},"paperhash":{"value":"pereira|automatic_calibration_of_multiple_lidar_sensors_using_a_moving_sphere_as_target"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marcelo_Pereira:","~Vítor_M._F._Santos2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Paulo_Dias:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-27146-0_37"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/robot/PereiraSD15,\n  author={Marcelo Pereira and Vítor M. F. Santos and Paulo Dias},\n  title={Automatic Calibration of Multiple LIDAR Sensors Using a Moving Sphere as Target},\n  year={2015},\n  cdate={1420070400000},\n  pages={477-489},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-27146-0_37},\n  booktitle={ROBOT (1)},\n  crossref={conf/robot/2015-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The number of LIDAR sensors installed in robotic vehicles has been increasing, which is a situation that reinforces the concern of sensor calibration. Most calibration systems rely on manual or semi-automatic interactive procedures, but fully automatic methods are still missing due to the variability of the nearby objects with the point of view. However, if some simple objects could be detected and identified automatically by all the sensors from several points of view, then automatic calibration would be possible on the fly. This is indeed feasible if a ball is placed in motion in front of the set of uncalibrated sensors allowing them to detect its center along the successive positions. This set of centers generates a point cloud per sensor, which, by using segmentation and fitting techniques, allows the calculation of the rigid body transformation between all pairs of sensors. This paper proposes and describes such a method with encouraging preliminary results."},"title":{"value":"Automatic Calibration of Multiple LIDAR Sensors Using a Moving Sphere as Target"},"authors":{"value":["Marcelo Pereira","Vítor M. F. Santos","Paulo Dias"]}},"tmdate":1753454307267,"pdate":1420070400000,"tcdate":1753454280319,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Vitor_Santos1"],"forum":"TCsV1KNnpC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":589039,"cdate":1420070400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753454307267,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"TCsV1KNnpC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10654794/10654797/10655202.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"herau|soac_spatiotemporal_overlapaware_multisensor_calibration_using_neural_radiance_fields"},"authorids":{"value":["~Quentin_Herau2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nathan_Piasco:","~Moussab_Bennehar1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luis_Roldao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dzmitry_Tsishkou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cyrille_Migniot:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pascal_Vasseur:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cédric_Demonceaux:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52733.2024.01433"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/HerauPBRTMVD24,\n  author={Quentin Herau and Nathan Piasco and Moussâb Bennehar and Luis Roldao and Dzmitry Tsishkou and Cyrille Migniot and Pascal Vasseur and Cédric Demonceaux},\n  title={SOAC: Spatio-Temporal Overlap-Aware Multi-Sensor Calibration using Neural Radiance Fields},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={15131-15140},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52733.2024.01433},\n  booktitle={CVPR},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In rapidly-evolving domains such as autonomous driving, the use of multiple sensors with different modalities is crucial to ensure high operational precision and stability. To correctly exploit the provided information by each sensor in a single common frame, it is essential for these sensors to be accurately calibrated. In this paper, we leverage the ability of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) to represent different sensors modalities in a common volumetric representation to achieve robust and accurate spatio-temporal sensor calibration. By designing a partitioning approach based on the visible part of the scene for each sensor, we formulate the calibration problem using only the overlapping areas. This strategy results in a more robust and accurate calibration that is less prone to failure. We demonstrate that our approach works on outdoor urban scenes by validating it on multiple established driving datasets. Results show that our method is able to get better accuracy and robustness compared to existing methods."},"title":{"value":"SOAC: Spatio-Temporal Overlap-Aware Multi-Sensor Calibration using Neural Radiance Fields"},"authors":{"value":["Quentin Herau","Nathan Piasco","Moussâb Bennehar","Luis Roldao","Dzmitry Tsishkou","Cyrille Migniot","Pascal Vasseur","Cédric Demonceaux"]}},"tmdate":1744730528857,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1734536560288,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Moussab_Bennehar1"],"forum":"Vv3wLkOxlp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":258547,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1744730528857,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Vv3wLkOxlp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.15803v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"herau|soac_spatiotemporal_overlapaware_multisensor_calibration_using_neural_radiance_fields"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Quentin_Herau:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nathan_Piasco:","~Moussab_Bennehar1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luis_Roldão:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dzmitry_Tsishkou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cyrille_Migniot:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pascal_Vasseur:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cédric_Demonceaux:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2311.15803"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2311-15803,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Quentin Herau and Nathan Piasco and Moussâb Bennehar and Luis Roldão and Dzmitry Tsishkou and Cyrille Migniot and Pascal Vasseur and Cédric Demonceaux},\n  title={SOAC: Spatio-Temporal Overlap-Aware Multi-Sensor Calibration using Neural Radiance Fields},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2311.15803},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2311.15803}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In rapidly-evolving domains such as autonomous driving, the use of multiple sensors with different modalities is crucial to ensure high operational precision and stability. To correctly exploit the provided information by each sensor in a single common frame, it is essential for these sensors to be accurately calibrated. In this paper, we leverage the ability of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) to represent different sensors modalities in a common volumetric representation to achieve robust and accurate spatio-temporal sensor calibration. By designing a partitioning approach based on the visible part of the scene for each sensor, we formulate the calibration problem using only the overlapping areas. This strategy results in a more robust and accurate calibration that is less prone to failure. We demonstrate that our approach works on outdoor urban scenes by validating it on multiple established driving datasets. Results show that our method is able to get better accuracy and robustness compared to existing methods."},"title":{"value":"SOAC: Spatio-Temporal Overlap-Aware Multi-Sensor Calibration using Neural Radiance Fields"},"authors":{"value":["Quentin Herau","Nathan Piasco","Moussâb Bennehar","Luis Roldão","Dzmitry Tsishkou","Cyrille Migniot","Pascal Vasseur","Cédric Demonceaux"]}},"tmdate":1734536586594,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1734536560365,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Moussab_Bennehar1"],"forum":"7vdW9guF9k","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":258551,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1734536586594,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"7vdW9guF9k","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.15803v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"herau|soac_spatiotemporal_overlapaware_multisensor_calibration_using_neural_radiance_fields"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Quentin_Herau:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nathan_Piasco:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Moussâb_Bennehar:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luis_Roldão:","~Dzmitry_Tsishkou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cyrille_Migniot:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pascal_Vasseur:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cédric_Demonceaux:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2311.15803"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2311-15803,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Quentin Herau and Nathan Piasco and Moussâb Bennehar and Luis Roldão and Dzmitry Tsishkou and Cyrille Migniot and Pascal Vasseur and Cédric Demonceaux},\n  title={SOAC: Spatio-Temporal Overlap-Aware Multi-Sensor Calibration using Neural Radiance Fields},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2311.15803},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2311.15803}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In rapidly-evolving domains such as autonomous driving, the use of multiple sensors with different modalities is crucial to ensure high operational precision and stability. To correctly exploit the provided information by each sensor in a single common frame, it is essential for these sensors to be accurately calibrated. In this paper, we leverage the ability of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) to represent different sensors modalities in a common volumetric representation to achieve robust and accurate spatio-temporal sensor calibration. By designing a partitioning approach based on the visible part of the scene for each sensor, we formulate the calibration problem using only the overlapping areas. This strategy results in a more robust and accurate calibration that is less prone to failure. We demonstrate that our approach works on outdoor urban scenes by validating it on multiple established driving datasets. Results show that our method is able to get better accuracy and robustness compared to existing methods."},"title":{"value":"SOAC: Spatio-Temporal Overlap-Aware Multi-Sensor Calibration using Neural Radiance Fields"},"authors":{"value":["Quentin Herau","Nathan Piasco","Moussâb Bennehar","Luis Roldão","Dzmitry Tsishkou","Cyrille Migniot","Pascal Vasseur","Cédric Demonceaux"]}},"tmdate":1731493032303,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731490097865,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dzmitry_Tsishkou1"],"forum":"2a94quTpUX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":220690,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731493032303,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2a94quTpUX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CVPR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10654794/10654797/10655202.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CVPR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"herau|soac_spatiotemporal_overlapaware_multisensor_calibration_using_neural_radiance_fields"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Quentin_Herau:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nathan_Piasco:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Moussâb_Bennehar:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luis_Roldao:","~Dzmitry_Tsishkou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cyrille_Migniot:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pascal_Vasseur:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cédric_Demonceaux:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52733.2024.01433"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cvpr/HerauPBRTMVD24,\n  author={Quentin Herau and Nathan Piasco and Moussâb Bennehar and Luis Roldao and Dzmitry Tsishkou and Cyrille Migniot and Pascal Vasseur and Cédric Demonceaux},\n  title={SOAC: Spatio-Temporal Overlap-Aware Multi-Sensor Calibration using Neural Radiance Fields},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={15131-15140},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR52733.2024.01433},\n  booktitle={CVPR},\n  crossref={conf/cvpr/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In rapidly-evolving domains such as autonomous driving, the use of multiple sensors with different modalities is crucial to ensure high operational precision and stability. To correctly exploit the provided information by each sensor in a single common frame, it is essential for these sensors to be accurately calibrated. In this paper, we leverage the ability of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) to represent different sensors modalities in a common volumetric representation to achieve robust and accurate spatio-temporal sensor calibration. By designing a partitioning approach based on the visible part of the scene for each sensor, we formulate the calibration problem using only the overlapping areas. This strategy results in a more robust and accurate calibration that is less prone to failure. We demonstrate that our approach works on outdoor urban scenes by validating it on multiple established driving datasets. Results show that our method is able to get better accuracy and robustness compared to existing methods."},"title":{"value":"SOAC: Spatio-Temporal Overlap-Aware Multi-Sensor Calibration using Neural Radiance Fields"},"authors":{"value":["Quentin Herau","Nathan Piasco","Moussâb Bennehar","Luis Roldao","Dzmitry Tsishkou","Cyrille Migniot","Pascal Vasseur","Cédric Demonceaux"]}},"tmdate":1731493031385,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731490097837,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dzmitry_Tsishkou1"],"forum":"a3c6YNwuAB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":220686,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731493031385,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"a3c6YNwuAB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Neurocomputing 2019"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJON/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"duan|a_new_imaging_model_of_lytro_light_field_camera_and_its_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Huixian_Duan:","~Lin_Mei2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Song:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Na_Liu_0013:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2018.05.118"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijon/DuanMWSL19,\n  author={Huixian Duan and Lin Mei and Jun Wang and Lei Song and Na Liu},\n  title={A new imaging model of Lytro light field camera and its calibration},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={Neurocomputing},\n  volume={328},\n  pages={189-194},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2018.05.118}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Due to the unique light gathering and post-capture processing, light field cameras with microlens array are being paid more and more attention. Especially, commercial light field cameras, such as Lytro and Raytrix, have been released. But calibrating light field cameras is still an open and challenging problem. In this paper, we present a new imaging model and a calibration method for Lytro camera, which makes its imaging process be easy to understand. Firstly, according to the image formation for pinhole camera and the theory of light propagation, we model the imaging process of Lytro light field camera, and drive a homogenous intrinsic matrix, which represents the relationship between each pixel on the image plane and its corresponding light field. Then, based on the derived intrinsic matrix, a calibration algorithm using the checkerboard grid for Lytro light field camera is proposed. At last, extensive experimental results have demonstrated the correctness of our imaging model of Lytro light field camera and the effectiveness of our calibration algorithm."},"title":{"value":"A new imaging model of Lytro light field camera and its calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Huixian Duan","Lin Mei","Jun Wang","Lei Song","Na Liu"]}},"tmdate":1731487203292,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1731483718015,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lin_Mei2"],"forum":"ws4BG3hb4f","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":213633,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731487203292,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ws4BG3hb4f","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ME-FoMo 2024 Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/200fe83320e0990fb0747a0b1d627cc55f2d827d.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Uncertainty Calibration","Large Language Models"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/ME-FoMo"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite the significant progress made in practical applications of aligned language models (LMs), they tend to be overconfident in output answers compared to the corresponding pre-trained LMs. In this work, we systematically evaluate the impact of the alignment process on logit-based uncertainty calibration of LMs under the multiple-choice setting. We first conduct a thoughtful empirical study on how aligned LMs differ in calibration from their pre-trained counterparts. Experimental results reveal that there are two distinct uncertainties in LMs under the multiple-choice setting, which are responsible for the answer decision and the format preference of the LMs, respectively. Then, we investigate the role of these two types of uncertainty on aligned LM's calibration through fine-tuning in synthetic alignment schemes and conclude that one reason for aligned LMs' overconfidence is the alteration of their answer uncertainty. We hope our findings could provide insights into the design of more reliable alignment processes for LMs."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhe2024investigating,\ntitle={Investigating Uncertainty Calibration of Aligned Language Models under the Multiple-Choice Setting},\nauthor={Guande He and Peng Cui and Jianfei Chen and Wenbo Hu and Jun Zhu},\nbooktitle={ICLR 2024 Workshop on Mathematical and Empirical Understanding of Foundation Models},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=I1OcffsjUX}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Investigating Uncertainty Calibration of Aligned Language Models under the Multiple-Choice Setting"}},"tmdate":1714984267018,"pdate":1709534844129,"tcdate":1706713501053,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/ME-FoMo","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/ME-FoMo/Submission10/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/ME-FoMo/Submission10/Authors"],"forum":"I1OcffsjUX","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":10,"cdate":1706713501053,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/ME-FoMo/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/ME-FoMo/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/ME-FoMo/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/ME-FoMo/Submission10/-/Revision"],"mdate":1714984267018,"odate":1712079579685,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/ME-FoMo","id":"I1OcffsjUX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CSIRO"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"vidas|ad_hoc_radiometric_calibration_of_a_thermalinfrared_camera"},"abstract":{"value":"Many applications can benefit from the accurate\nsurface temperature estimates that can be made using a passive\nthermal-infrared camera. However, the process of radiometric\ncalibration which enables this can be both expensive and time\nconsuming. An ad hoc approach for performing radiometric\ncalibration is proposed which does not require specialized equip-\nment and can be completed in a fraction of the time of the\nconventional method. The proposed approach utilizes the me-\nchanical properties of the camera to estimate scene temperatures\nautomatically, and uses these target temperatures to model the\neffect of sensor temperature on the digital output. A comparison\nwith a conventional approach using a blackbody radiation source\nshows that the accuracy of the method is sufficient for many\ntasks requiring temperature estimation. Furthermore, a novel\nvisualization method is proposed for displaying the radiometri-\ncally calibrated images to human operators. The representation\nemploys an intuitive coloring scheme and allows the viewer to\nperceive a large variety of temperatures accurately."},"title":{"value":"Ad hoc radiometric calibration of a thermal-infrared camera"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Steve Vidas","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Steve%20Vidas"},{"fullname":"Peyman Moghadam","username":"~Peyman_Moghadam1"}]}},"tmdate":1791423561574,"pdate":1385596800000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/dicta.2013.6691478"],"tcdate":1791423560977,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Peyman_Moghadam1"],"forum":"gROywxh8Mo","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":115137,"cdate":1573021470165,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791423561574,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"gROywxh8Mo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["PINN","Galerkin method","ML for finance","option calibration"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/6d3f0e7001811931ea85f3c6a7837d6848da2b19.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"neurosymbolic & hybrid AI systems (physics-informed, logic & formal reasoning, etc.)"},"abstract":{"value":"Local-volatility calibration is an ill-posed inverse parabolic problem, and\nstandard pipelines typically separate surface interpolation from the pricing\noperator used downstream. We instead learn only the variance-rate coefficient\n$\\kappa=\\sigma_{\\mathrm{loc}}^2/2$ and obtain prices from the exact\nlog-moneyness Dupire equation through differentiable weighted-Galerkin layers.\nA positive parameterisation makes coefficient admissibility architectural; the\nweighted discretisation makes every implicit state matrix symmetric\npositive-definite (SPD); and reverse-mode gradients reuse the same sparse\nsolves as the forward pass. Smoothness remains a statistical regulariser rather\nthan a surrogate for either positivity or the PDE. We prove that finite soft\npenalties cannot certify membership in the admissible ellipticity set and test\nthe distinction through synthetic recovery, a mechanism-level hard-versus-soft\ncomparison, and out-of-sample calibration on an S\\&P~500 option snapshot. The\nresults show that structural guarantees can be retained without claiming\nuniversal coefficient accuracy or a complete static-arbitrage certificate."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026localvolatility,\ntitle={Local-Volatility Calibration with Hard-Constrained Differentiable Galerkin Layers},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=nFu1WZQTwS},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Local-Volatility Calibration with Hard-Constrained Differentiable Galerkin Layers"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8f2fcc8f9334f85c0d3098c5a5a761c552b5c16c.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791229557509,"tcdate":1789455681051,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission20062/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission20062/Authors"],"forum":"nFu1WZQTwS","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":20062,"cdate":1789455681051,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission20062/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791229557509,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"nFu1WZQTwS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Unsupervised Combinatorial Optimization","Diffusion Models","Transition Calibration"]},"primary_area":{"value":"learning on graphs and other geometries & topologies"},"abstract":{"value":"Diffusion models have recently shown promise for unsupervised combinatorial optimization (CO), learning to generate high-quality solutions without labeled optimal solutions. However, we find that their training signals can exhibit substantially different discriminative quality across transition directions, a phenomenon we term Constructive-Corrective Transition Asymmetry. Specifically, learned policies exhibit substantially weaker target-conditioned discrimination for constructive transitions than for corrective ones, which our analysis relates to a directional scale asymmetry in the underlying transition signals. These findings highlight a gap between effective trajectory-level credit assignment and discriminative transition-level supervision. To address this problem, we propose Constructive-Corrective Transition Calibration (CCTC), which calibrates transition-level supervision according to signal magnitude and transition direction. CCTC combines bounded calibration with direction-conditioned weighting to selectively strengthen target-favored constructive transitions. Experiments on four node- and edge-selection CO problems across eight settings show that CCTC consistently outperforms existing unsupervised state-of-the-art methods while achieving stronger constructive transition discrimination."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026cctc,\ntitle={{CCTC}: Constructive{\\textendash}Corrective Transition Calibration for Unsupervised Combinatorial Optimization},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=m1CZPvA72u},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CCTC: Constructive–Corrective Transition Calibration for Unsupervised Combinatorial Optimization"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fc9da76e0df2e1080232b06094977b4a7fcd2fd4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791219460572,"tcdate":1787213981513,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission1309/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission1309/Authors"],"forum":"m1CZPvA72u","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1309,"cdate":1787213981513,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission1309/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791219460572,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"m1CZPvA72u","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"evaluation,evaluation methodologies,automatic evaluation of datasets,benchmarking"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM Evaluation","Item Response Theory","Benchmark Analysis","Preference Modeling"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"N/A"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"N/A"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"4"},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"No"},"B5_elaboration":{"value":"4 and Appendix G"},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"4.1, 4.2, 4.3, Appendix D and E"},"B2_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix M"},"B3_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix M"},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"4 and Appendix B"},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"Yes"},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"4 and Appendix B"},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"4 and Appendix G"},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"Generative AI is used for polishing our writing and debugging experiment code."},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"N/A"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026dualeval,\ntitle={DualEval: Joint Model-Item Calibration for Unified {LLM} Evaluation},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=TFF7BeYIw0},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"DualEval: Joint Model-Item Calibration for Unified LLM Evaluation"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"Yes"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","Approaches to low-compute settings (efficiency)","Data analysis"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"N/A"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"Yes"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"N/A"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"Current LLM evaluation relies on two complementary but often disconnected signals: static benchmarks with objective correctness labels and arena-style preference data that better reflect open-ended user interactions. We introduce DualEval, a latent model-item calibration framework that represents models and evaluation items in a shared space, jointly estimating model ability together with item difficulty and sharpness. We apply DualEval across four domains: coding, math, miscellaneous domain-knowledge tasks, and generic everyday user queries. Our evaluation uses 18 frontier LLMs, static benchmark labels, and reward-model scores validated against held-out human preferences for open-ended model responses. Empirically, our framework produces reliable and balanced model rankings, and its learned item-level profiles support downstream applications such as benchmark compression for sample-efficient evaluation and anomaly detection for contamination or outlier analysis. Overall, DualEval unifies static and arena-style evaluation through joint model-item calibration, producing model rankings and item-level diagnostics that support more sample-efficient, interpretable, and auditable evaluation pipelines."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"N/A"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e39772537ea2d82ed449113519dc1b8dd616a2ee.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Resources and Evaluation"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"yes"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"N/A"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1790696937784,"tcdate":1779611421348,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission4935/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission4935/Authors"],"forum":"TFF7BeYIw0","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4935,"cdate":1779611421348,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1790696937784,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"TFF7BeYIw0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IJCNN 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11227166/11227148/11227261.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IJCNN/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"nunes|beyond_accuracy_the_role_of_calibration_in_computational_pathology"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","","","~Jaime_S._Cardoso1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IJCNN64981.2025.11227261"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ijcnn/NunesMOPZC25,\n  author={João D. Nunes and Diana Montezuma and Domingos Oliveira and Tânia Pereira and Inti Zlobec and Jaime S. Cardoso},\n  title={Beyond Accuracy: The Role of Calibration in Computational Pathology},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={1-7},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IJCNN64981.2025.11227261},\n  booktitle={IJCNN},\n  crossref={conf/ijcnn/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Deep learning in computational pathology (CPath) has rapidly advanced in recent years. Research has primarily focused on enhancing accuracy and interpretability across various histology image analysis tasks, from tile-level to slide-level foundation models and novel multiple instance learning (MIL) strategies. However, it is equally important for models to provide well-calibrated confidence estimates. Due to factors such as dataset bias, overfitting, and limited training data, existing models tend to be overly confident on test sets. Promising solutions to address this issue include temperature scaling, a post-hoc method that adjusts logits using a single scalar value. However, the role of calibration in CPath is yet to be clarified. In this study, we evaluate temperature scaling and linear temperature scaling for CPath tasks, analyzing their impact on recalibration in both in-domain and out-of-domain distributions. The results show the limitations of current probability calibration techniques and motivate future work."},"title":{"value":"Beyond Accuracy: The Role of Calibration in Computational Pathology"},"authors":{"value":["João D. Nunes","Diana Montezuma","Domingos Oliveira","Tânia Pereira","Inti Zlobec","Jaime S. Cardoso"]}},"tmdate":1772480612784,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/ijcnn/NunesMOPZC25"],"tcdate":1772480600046,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jaime_S_Cardoso1"],"forum":"jegw4XzuWJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":845992,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772480612784,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"jegw4XzuWJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.14925v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"du|automatic_spectral_calibration_of_hyperspectral_imagesmethod_dataset_and_benchmark"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhuoran_Du:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shaodi_You:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cheng_Cheng:","~Shikui_Wei1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.14925"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-14925,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhuoran Du and Shaodi You and Cheng Cheng and Shikui Wei},\n  title={Automatic Spectral Calibration of Hyperspectral Images:Method, Dataset and Benchmark},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2412.14925},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.14925}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Hyperspectral image (HSI) densely samples the world in both the space and frequency domain and therefore is more distinctive than RGB images. Usually, HSI needs to be calibrated to minimize the impact of various illumination conditions. The traditional way to calibrate HSI utilizes a physical reference, which involves manual operations, occlusions, and/or limits camera mobility. These limitations inspire this paper to automatically calibrate HSIs using a learning-based method. Towards this goal, a large-scale HSI calibration dataset is created, which has 765 high-quality HSI pairs covering diversified natural scenes and illuminations. The dataset is further expanded to 7650 pairs by combining with 10 different physically measured illuminations. A spectral illumination transformer (SIT) together with an illumination attention module is proposed. Extensive benchmarks demonstrate the SoTA performance of the proposed SIT. The benchmarks also indicate that low-light conditions are more challenging than normal conditions. The dataset and codes are available online:https://github.com/duranze/Automatic-spectral-calibration-of-HSI"},"title":{"value":"Automatic Spectral Calibration of Hyperspectral Images:Method, Dataset and Benchmark"},"authors":{"value":["Zhuoran Du","Shaodi You","Cheng Cheng","Shikui Wei"]}},"tmdate":1769058745284,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2412-14925"],"tcdate":1769058735039,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shikui_Wei1"],"forum":"PPlqKgLmJC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":786749,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769058745284,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"PPlqKgLmJC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.13872v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"faustini|empirical_calibration_and_metric_differential_privacy_in_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pedro_Faustini:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Natasha_Fernandes:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Annabelle_McIver:","~Mark_Dras1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.13872"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2503-13872,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Pedro Faustini and Natasha Fernandes and Annabelle McIver and Mark Dras},\n  title={Empirical Calibration and Metric Differential Privacy in Language Models},\n  year={2025},\n  month={March},\n  cdate={1740787200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2503.13872},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.13872}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"NLP models trained with differential privacy (DP) usually adopt the DP-SGD framework, and privacy guarantees are often reported in terms of the privacy budget $ε$. However, $ε$ does not have any intrinsic meaning, and it is generally not possible to compare across variants of the framework. Work in image processing has therefore explored how to empirically calibrate noise across frameworks using Membership Inference Attacks (MIAs). However, this kind of calibration has not been established for NLP. In this paper, we show that MIAs offer little help in calibrating privacy, whereas reconstruction attacks are more useful. As a use case, we define a novel kind of directional privacy based on the von Mises-Fisher (VMF) distribution, a metric DP mechanism that perturbs angular distance rather than adding (isotropic) Gaussian noise, and apply this to NLP architectures. We show that, even though formal guarantees are incomparable, empirical privacy calibration reveals that each mechanism has different areas of strength with respect to utility-privacy trade-offs."},"title":{"value":"Empirical Calibration and Metric Differential Privacy in Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Pedro Faustini","Natasha Fernandes","Annabelle McIver","Mark Dras"]}},"tmdate":1768965628380,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2503-13872"],"tcdate":1768965345862,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mark_Dras1"],"forum":"oB7BjWjyfp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":773164,"cdate":1740787200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768965628380,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"oB7BjWjyfp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.12987v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"he|efficient_behaviorconsistent_calibration_for_multiagent_market_simulation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tianlang_He1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Keyan_Lu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chang_Xu_0008:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Liu_0278:","~Weiqing_Liu1","~S.-H._Gary_Chan2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiang_Bian_0002:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.12987"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2307-12987,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Tianlang He and Keyan Lu and Chang Xu and Yang Liu and Weiqing Liu and S.-H. Gary Chan and Jiang Bian},\n  title={Efficient Behavior-consistent Calibration for Multi-agent Market Simulation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2307.12987},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.12987}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Order-driven market simulation mimics the trader behaviors to generate order streams to support interactive studies of financial strategies. In market simulator, the multi-agent approach is commonly adopted due to its explainability. Existing multi-agent systems employ heuristic search to generate order streams, which is inefficient for large-scale simulation. Furthermore, the search-based behavior calibration often leads to inconsistent trader actions under the same general market condition, making the simulation results unstable and difficult to interpret. We propose CaliSim, the first search-free calibration approach multi-agent market simulator which achieves large-scale efficiency and behavior consistency. CaliSim uses meta-learning and devises a surrogate trading system with a consistency loss function for the reproducibility of order stream and trader behaviors. Extensive experiments in the market replay and case studies show that CaliSim achieves state-of-the-art in terms of order stream reproduction with consistent trader behavior and can capture patterns of real markets."},"title":{"value":"Efficient Behavior-consistent Calibration for Multi-agent Market Simulation"},"authors":{"value":["Tianlang He","Keyan Lu","Chang Xu","Yang Liu","Weiqing Liu","S.-H. Gary Chan","Jiang Bian"]}},"tmdate":1767760895135,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1724627720281,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~S.-H._Chan1"],"forum":"VN8QOJcnaW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":72868,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1767760895135,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"VN8QOJcnaW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.01691v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"lee|steerpose_simultaneous_extrinsic_camera_calibration_and_matching_from_articulation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sang-Eun_Lee:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ko_Nishino:","~Shohei_Nobuhara1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2506.01691"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2506-01691,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Sang-Eun Lee and Ko Nishino and Shohei Nobuhara},\n  title={SteerPose: Simultaneous Extrinsic Camera Calibration and Matching from Articulation},\n  year={2025},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1748736000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2506.01691},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2506.01691}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Can freely moving humans or animals themselves serve as calibration targets for multi-camera systems while simultaneously estimating their correspondences across views? We humans can solve this problem by mentally rotating the observed 2D poses and aligning them with those in the target views. Inspired by this cognitive ability, we propose SteerPose, a neural network that performs this rotation of 2D poses into another view. By integrating differentiable matching, SteerPose simultaneously performs extrinsic camera calibration and correspondence search within a single unified framework. We also introduce a novel geometric consistency loss that explicitly ensures that the estimated rotation and correspondences result in a valid translation estimation. Experimental results on diverse in-the-wild datasets of humans and animals validate the effectiveness and robustness of the proposed method. Furthermore, we demonstrate that our method can reconstruct the 3D poses of novel animals in multi-camera setups by leveraging off-the-shelf 2D pose estimators and our class-agnostic model."},"title":{"value":"SteerPose: Simultaneous Extrinsic Camera Calibration and Matching from Articulation"},"authors":{"value":["Sang-Eun Lee","Ko Nishino","Shohei Nobuhara"]}},"tmdate":1766185671529,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2506-01691"],"tcdate":1766185664123,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shohei_Nobuhara2"],"forum":"LpREzijZ83","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":710729,"cdate":1748736000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1766185671529,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"LpREzijZ83","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.00661v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"kim|ekfbased_radarinertial_odometry_with_online_temporal_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Changseung_Kim:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Geun_Sik_Bae:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Woojae_Shin:","~Sen_Wang7","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hyondong_Oh:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.00661"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2502-00661,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Changseung Kim and Geun Sik Bae and Woojae Shin and Sen Wang and Hyondong Oh},\n  title={EKF-Based Radar-Inertial Odometry with Online Temporal Calibration},\n  year={2025},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1738368000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2502.00661},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.00661}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate time synchronization between heterogeneous sensors is crucial for ensuring robust state estimation in multi-sensor fusion systems. Sensor delays often cause discrepancies between the actual time when the event was captured and the time of sensor measurement, leading to temporal misalignment (time offset) between sensor measurement streams. In this paper, we propose an extended Kalman filter (EKF)-based radar-inertial odometry (RIO) framework that estimates the time offset online. The radar ego-velocity measurement model, derived from a single radar scan, is formulated to incorporate the time offset into the update. By leveraging temporal calibration, the proposed RIO enables accurate propagation and measurement updates based on a common time stream. Experiments on both simulated and real-world datasets demonstrate the accurate time offset estimation of the proposed method and its impact on RIO performance, validating the importance of sensor time synchronization. Our implementation of the EKF-RIO with online temporal calibration is available at https://github.com/spearwin/EKF-RIO-TC."},"title":{"value":"EKF-Based Radar-Inertial Odometry with Online Temporal Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Changseung Kim","Geun Sik Bae","Woojae Shin","Sen Wang","Hyondong Oh"]}},"tmdate":1765901641907,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2502-00661"],"tcdate":1765901620132,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sen_Wang7"],"forum":"e2zPGDk2hy","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":709542,"cdate":1738368000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1765901641907,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"e2zPGDk2hy","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.14028v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|classsimilarity_based_label_smoothing_for_generalized_confidence_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Chihuang_Liu1","~Joseph_F._JáJá1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.14028"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2006-14028,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Chihuang Liu and Joseph F. JáJá},\n  title={Class-Similarity Based Label Smoothing for Generalized Confidence Calibration},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2006.14028},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.14028}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Generating confidence calibrated outputs is of utmost importance for the applications of deep neural networks in safety-critical decision-making systems. The output of a neural network is a probability distribution where the scores are estimated confidences of the input belonging to the corresponding classes, and hence they represent a complete estimate of the output likelihood relative to all classes. In this paper, we propose a novel form of label smoothing to improve confidence calibration. Since different classes are of different intrinsic similarities, more similar classes should result in closer probability values in the final output. This motivates the development of a new smooth label where the label values are based on similarities with the reference class. We adopt different similarity measurements, including those that capture feature-based similarities or semantic similarity. We demonstrate through extensive experiments, on various datasets and network architectures, that our approach consistently outperforms state-of-the-art calibration techniques including uniform label smoothing."},"title":{"value":"Class-Similarity Based Label Smoothing for Generalized Confidence Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Chihuang Liu","Joseph F. JáJá"]}},"tmdate":1747338463312,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1738817791735,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chihuang_Liu1"],"forum":"GW84rQK719","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":300786,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747338463312,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"GW84rQK719","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.12987v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"he|efficient_behaviorconsistent_calibration_for_multiagent_market_simulation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianlang_He:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Keyan_Lu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chang_Xu_0008:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Liu_0278:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weiqing_Liu:","~S.-H._Gary_Chan2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiang_Bian_0002:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.12987"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2307-12987,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Tianlang He and Keyan Lu and Chang Xu and Yang Liu and Weiqing Liu and S.-H. Gary Chan and Jiang Bian},\n  title={Efficient Behavior-consistent Calibration for Multi-agent Market Simulation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2307.12987},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.12987}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Order-driven market simulation mimics the trader behaviors to generate order streams to support interactive studies of financial strategies. In market simulator, the multi-agent approach is commonly adopted due to its explainability. Existing multi-agent systems employ heuristic search to generate order streams, which is inefficient for large-scale simulation. Furthermore, the search-based behavior calibration often leads to inconsistent trader actions under the same general market condition, making the simulation results unstable and difficult to interpret. We propose CaliSim, the first search-free calibration approach multi-agent market simulator which achieves large-scale efficiency and behavior consistency. CaliSim uses meta-learning and devises a surrogate trading system with a consistency loss function for the reproducibility of order stream and trader behaviors. Extensive experiments in the market replay and case studies show that CaliSim achieves state-of-the-art in terms of order stream reproduction with consistent trader behavior and can capture patterns of real markets."},"title":{"value":"Efficient Behavior-consistent Calibration for Multi-agent Market Simulation"},"authors":{"value":["Tianlang He","Keyan Lu","Chang Xu","Yang Liu","Weiqing Liu","S.-H. Gary Chan","Jiang Bian"]}},"tmdate":1724627711691,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1724627701278,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~S.-H._Chan1"],"forum":"BnaWH35DXR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":72710,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1724627711691,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"BnaWH35DXR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.14092v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"xing|reghec_handeye_calibration_via_simultaneous_multiview_point_clouds_registration_of_arbitrary_object"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shiyu_Xing:","~Fengshui_Jing1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Min_Tan_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2304.14092"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2304-14092,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shiyu Xing and Fengshui Jing and Min Tan},\n  title={RegHEC: Hand-Eye Calibration via Simultaneous Multi-view Point Clouds Registration of Arbitrary Object},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2304.14092},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2304.14092}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"RegHEC is a registration-based hand-eye calibration technique with no need for accurate calibration rig but arbitrary available objects, applicable for both eye-in-hand and eye-to-hand cases. It tries to find the hand-eye relation which brings multi-view point clouds of arbitrary scene into simultaneous registration under a common reference frame. RegHEC first achieves initial alignment of multi-view point clouds via Bayesian optimization, where registration problem is modeled as a Gaussian process over hand-eye relation and the covariance function is modified to be compatible with distance metric in 3-D motion space SE(3), then passes the initial guess of hand-eye relation to an Anderson Accelerated ICP variant for later fine registration and accurate calibration. RegHEC has little requirement on calibration object, it is applicable with sphere, cone, cylinder and even simple plane, which can be quite challenging for correct point cloud registration and sensor motion estimation using existing methods. While suitable for most 3-D vision guided tasks, RegHEC is especially favorable for robotic 3-D reconstruction, as calibration and multi-view point clouds registration of reconstruction target are unified into a single process. Our technique is verified with extensive experiments using varieties of arbitrary objects and real hand-eye system. We release an open-source C++ implementation of RegHEC."},"title":{"value":"RegHEC: Hand-Eye Calibration via Simultaneous Multi-view Point Clouds Registration of Arbitrary Object"},"authors":{"value":["Shiyu Xing","Fengshui Jing","Min Tan"]}},"tmdate":1740981250444,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1740981239866,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fengshui_Jing1"],"forum":"rgF5MNZyVG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":344535,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740981250444,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rgF5MNZyVG","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose a reliability-aware, robust price-space SSVI calibration pipeline that turns sparse intraday SPX transaction-bar data into auditable canonical implied-volatility surfaces with improved out-of-sample economic feasibility."},"venue":{"value":"2026 Oral"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a5844b53ba87f3445bb5ae4a24475662ab27fee2.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["arbitrage","option surface","convex optimization","implied volatility surface","intraday options","transaction bars","sparse data","canonicalization","SSVI","robust calibration","Huber loss","effective sample size","reliability weighting","static arbitrage audit","SPX options"]},"venueid":{"value":"mathai.club/MathAI/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"bagantsova|calibration_under_sparse_data_robust_canonical_surface_estimation_from_transaction_bars"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ekaterina_Bagantsova1"]},"abstract":{"value":"We study intraday canonicalization and auditing of implied-volatility (IV) surfaces for S\\&P~500 index (SPX) options when the available observations are sparse, trade-based open--high--low--close--volume (OHLCV) bars. Such panels exhibit strongly non-uniform cross-sectional support---dense near at-the-money (ATM) and short maturities, and thin in the wings and long tenors---so naive fixed-grid completion can create visually smooth surfaces with unstable calibration and misleading feasibility diagnostics. We propose a reliability-aware canonicalization pipeline that (i) constructs forward and discount proxies from liquid futures strips, (ii) quantifies local information content via an effective sample size (ESS) diagnostic induced by kernel receptive fields, and (iii) calibrates a per-timestamp Surface SVI (SSVI) total-variance surface directly in price space under robust losses. Our main methodological contribution is a reliability-weighted vega-normalized robust objective that downweights weakly supported marks while retaining the interpretability and tractability of price-space calibration. We compare three calibration objectives---robust price residuals, vega-normalized residuals, and reliability-weighted vega residuals---using paired timestamp-level inference with Holm-adjusted randomization tests, and we evaluate economic plausibility with a unified static-arbitrage audit that reports both violation rates and hinge-type severity measures. Empirically, price-residual calibration minimizes price root-mean-squared error (RMSE), whereas reliability-weighted vega residuals yield the most consistent reductions in out-of-sample (OOS) arbitrage severity and in average any-rule violation rates. These results support reliability-weighted robust objectives as default canonicalizers for economically plausible intraday surfaces built from sparse transaction bars and for downstream learning tasks."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nbagantsova2026calibration,\ntitle={Calibration under Sparse Data: Robust Canonical Surface Estimation from Transaction Bars},\nauthor={Ekaterina Bagantsova},\nbooktitle={Conference of Mathematics of AI},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=nN25OpOpDF}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration under Sparse Data: Robust Canonical Surface Estimation from Transaction Bars"},"authors":{"value":["Ekaterina Bagantsova"]}},"tmdate":1774709868133,"pdate":1773547381408,"tcdate":1771357930003,"writers":["mathai.club/MathAI/2026/Conference","mathai.club/MathAI/2026/Conference/Submission115/Authors"],"signatures":["mathai.club/MathAI/2026/Conference/Submission115/Authors"],"forum":"nN25OpOpDF","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":115,"cdate":1771357930003,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["mathai.club/MathAI/2026/Conference/-/Submission","mathai.club/MathAI/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","mathai.club/MathAI/2026/Conference/-/Edit","mathai.club/MathAI/2026/Conference/Submission115/-/Revision"],"mdate":1774709868133,"odate":1771935600705,"domain":"mathai.club/MathAI/2026/Conference","id":"nN25OpOpDF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE J. Sel. Top. Appl. Earth Obs. Remote. Sens. 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/4609443/8767044/08725894.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/STAEORS/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"jacob|radiometric_characterization_of_a_waterbased_conical_blackbody_calibration_target_for_millimeterwave_remote_sensing"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Karl_Jacob:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Arne_Schröder:","~Leandro_von_Werra2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Florian_Reinhard:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Philippe_Raisin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Axel_Murk:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/JSTARS.2019.2913729"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/staeors/JacobSWRRM19,\n  author={Karl Jacob and Arne Schröder and Leandro von Werra and Florian Reinhard and Philippe Raisin and Axel Murk},\n  title={Radiometric Characterization of a Water-Based Conical Blackbody Calibration Target for Millimeter-Wave Remote Sensing},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={IEEE J. Sel. Top. Appl. Earth Obs. Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={12},\n  number={6},\n  pages={1688-1696},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/JSTARS.2019.2913729}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper, we present the design and radiometric characterization of a water-based conical blackbody calibration target to be applied as a precise reference source in laboratories and for the accurate calibration of ground-based microwave instruments. The concept of aqueous blackbody targets circumvents the conflict between the electromagnetic and thermal properties of traditional calibration target designs. The temperature controllable target consists of a conical low loss plastic shell, which has been manufactured using a stereolithography three-dimensional printer to define the water-air interface. An advanced exponential profile of the shell is used to compensate the comparatively high refractive index of water and to obtain a high emissivity. Simulations and active measurements of the coherent backscattering S11 have been performed verifying its excellent electromagnetic properties. Furthermore, radiometric measurements at 110 GHz demonstrate the outstanding thermal performance at various target temperatures between 10 and 60 °C. The differences between the brightness temperature and the water temperature are measured to be less than 40 mK and therefore significantly smaller in comparison to traditional calibration targets. In addition, the measured baseline ripples in the spectra caused by the water-based calibration target are small compared to established blackbody concepts."},"title":{"value":"Radiometric Characterization of a Water-Based Conical Blackbody Calibration Target for Millimeter-Wave Remote Sensing"},"authors":{"value":["Karl Jacob","Arne Schröder","Leandro von Werra","Florian Reinhard","Philippe Raisin","Axel Murk"]}},"tmdate":1746780271938,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1746780198590,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Leandro_Von_Werra1"],"forum":"Nd0dDtawrm","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":414052,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1746780271938,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Nd0dDtawrm","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISBI 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10635099/10635102/10635160.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISBI/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"penso|a_joint_training_and_confidence_calibration_procedure_that_is_robust_to_label_noise"},"authorids":{"value":["~Coby_Penso1",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ISBI56570.2024.10635160"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/isbi/PensoG24,\n  author={Coby Penso and Jacob Goldberger},\n  title={A Joint Training and Confidence Calibration Procedure That is Robust to Label Noise},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={1-5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ISBI56570.2024.10635160},\n  booktitle={ISBI},\n  crossref={conf/isbi/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Manually annotated medical imaging data tend to have unreliable labels due to the complexity of the medical data and the considerable variability across experts. Noisy data can pose a significant challenge when it comes to learning the model’s parameters and calibrating its predictive confidence. This study presents a joint training and confidence calibration procedure that is robust to label noise. The method is based on estimating the noise level as part of a noise-robust training procedure. The estimated noise level is then used to modify the computed network accuracy on the noisy validation set which is required by the calibration procedure. We demonstrate that, despite the unreliable labels, we can still achieve calibration results similar to those obtained by a procedure using data with noise-free labels."},"title":{"value":"A Joint Training and Confidence Calibration Procedure That is Robust to Label Noise"},"authors":{"value":["Coby Penso","Jacob Goldberger"]}},"tmdate":1772483170380,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/isbi/PensoG24"],"tcdate":1772483165282,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Coby_Penso1"],"forum":"vUdxYDaihf","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":846092,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772483170380,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"vUdxYDaihf","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"luo|combom_bayesian_exemplar_search_for_efficiently_exploring_the_accuracycalibration_pareto_frontier"},"authorids":{"value":["~Gaoxiang_Luo1","~Aryan_Deshwal1"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.1027.pdf"},"abstract":{"value":"Selecting an optimal set of exemplars is critical for good performance of in-context learning. However, prior exemplar search methods narrowly optimize for predictive accuracy, critically neglecting model calibration—a key determinant of trustworthiness and safe deployment. In this paper, we formulate exemplar selection as a multi-objective optimization problem, explicitly targeting both the maximization of predictive accuracy and the minimization of expected calibration error. We solve this problem with a sample-efficient Combinatorial Bayesian Optimization algorithm (COM-BOM) to find the Pareto-front that optimally trade-offs the two objectives of accuracy and calibration. We evaluate COM-BOM on multiple tasks from un-saturated MMLU-pro benchmark and find that COM-BOM beats or matches the baselines in jointly optimizing the two objectives, while requiring a minimal number of LLM API calls."},"title":{"value":"COM-BOM: Bayesian Exemplar Search for Efficiently Exploring the Accuracy-Calibration Pareto Frontier"},"authors":{"value":["Gaoxiang Luo","Aryan Deshwal"]}},"tmdate":1769374174834,"pdate":1762236000000,"tcdate":1769374174834,"writers":["~Gaoxiang_Luo1","~Aryan_Deshwal1"],"signatures":["~Gaoxiang_Luo1"],"forum":"ehGDr34QAt","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":42857,"cdate":1769374174834,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1769374174834,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"ehGDr34QAt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2b2f4adec2bbcadb241e8cd57bb5b368f8f5bc36.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"sergazinov|improving_model_probability_calibration_by_integration_of_large_data_sources_with_biased_labels"},"authorids":{"value":["~Renat_Sergazinov1","~Richard_Chen2","~Cheng_Ji2","~Jing_Wu5","~Daniel_Cociorva1","brunzell@amazon.com"]},"html":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/34240/36395"},"abstract":{"value":"Probability calibration transforms raw output of a classification model into empirically interpretable probability. When the model is purposed to detect rare event and only a small expensive data source has clean labels, it becomes extraordinarily challenging to obtain accurate probability calibration. Utilizing an additional large cheap data source is very helpful, however, such data sources oftentimes suffer from biased labels. To this end, we introduce an approximate expectation-maximization (EM) algorithm to extract useful information from the large data sources. For a family of calibration methods based on the logistic likelihood, we derive closed-form updates and call the resulting iterative algorithm CalEM. We show that CalEM inherits convergence guarantees from the approximate EM algorithm. We test the proposed model in simulation and on the real marketing datasets, where it shows significant performance increases."},"title":{"value":"Improving model probability calibration by integration of large data sources with biased labels"},"authors":{"value":["Renat Sergazinov","Richard Chen","Cheng Ji","Jing Wu","Daniel Cociorva","Hakan Brunzell"]}},"tmdate":1753582839765,"pdate":1742454000000,"tcdate":1753582773928,"writers":["~Renat_Sergazinov1","~Richard_Chen2","~Cheng_Ji2","~Jing_Wu5","~Daniel_Cociorva1","brunzell@amazon.com"],"signatures":["~Renat_Sergazinov1"],"forum":"znuLAsTrJG","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":38101,"cdate":1753582773928,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1753582839765,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"znuLAsTrJG","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Uncertainty; Calibration; Multimodality"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2025 February Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025mmboundary,\ntitle={{MMB}oundary: Advancing {MLLM} Knowledge Boundary Awareness through Reasoning Step Confidence Calibration},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - February 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=jfsvLv9z7P},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"MMBoundary: Advancing MLLM Knowledge Boundary Awareness through Reasoning Step Confidence Calibration"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","NLP engineering experiment","Publicly available software and/or pre-trained models","Data resources"]},"abstract":{"value":"In recent years, multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have made significant progress but continue to face inherent challenges in multimodal reasoning, which requires multi-level (e.g., perception, reasoning) and multi-granular (e.g., multi-step reasoning chain) advanced inferencing. \nPrior work on estimating model confidence tends to focus on the overall response for training and calibration, but fails to assess confidence in each reasoning step, leading to undesirable hallucination snowballing. \nIn this work, we present MMBoundary, a novel framework that advances the knowledge boundary awareness of MLLMs through reasoning step confidence calibration. \nTo achieve this, we incorporate complementary textual and cross-modal self-rewarding signals to estimate confidence at each step of the MLLM reasoning process. \nIn addition to supervised fine-tuning MLLM on this set of self-rewarded confidence estimation signal for initial confidence expression warm-up, we further introduce a reinforcement learning stage with multiple reward functions for further aligning model knowledge and calibrating confidence at each reasoning step, enhancing reasoning chain self-correction. \nEmpirical results show that MMBoundary significantly outperforms existing methods across diverse domain datasets and metrics, achieving an average of 7.5% reduction in multimodal confidence calibration errors and up to 8.3% improvement in task performance."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e1505d72fd9f647edc190ce685de4da4c8e06454.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Interpretability and Analysis of Models for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782961971738,"tcdate":1739558190173,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission2384/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission2384/Authors"],"forum":"jfsvLv9z7P","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2384,"cdate":1739558190173,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission2384/-/Blind_Submission_License_Agreement","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission2384/-/Change_Reviewer_Nomination","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782961971738,"odate":1746821355472,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February","id":"jfsvLv9z7P","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"calibration/uncertainty, free-text/natural language explanations, robustness"},"keywords":{"value":["confidence calibration","unceratinty quantification","natural language critiques"]},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 March Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026critical,\ntitle={CritiCal: Can Natural Language Critiques Help {LLM}s' Uncertainty or Confidence Calibration?},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - March 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=un63enLNke},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CritiCal: Can Natural Language Critiques Help LLMs' Uncertainty or Confidence Calibration?"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability","Data analysis"]},"abstract":{"value":"Confidence calibration is critical for safe use of Large Language Models (LLMs), where clear verbalized confidence enhances user trust. \nTraditional methods that mimic reference confidence expressions often fail to utilize the logic in model's original reasoning chain.\nWe propose natural language critique as a solution, which is ideal for confidence calibration, as precise gold confidence labels are hard to obtain and often require multiple generations but assessing whether the confidence is appropriate is easy by analyzing its internal logic and answer correctness.\nThis paper studies how natural language critiques can enhance verbalized confidence: (1) *What to critique*: uncertainty (question-focused) or confidence (answer-specific)? \nAnalysis shows confidence suits multiple-choice tasks, while uncertainty excels in open-ended scenarios. \n(2) *How to critique*: self-critique or critique calibration training? \nWe propose **Self-Critique**, enabling LLMs to critique and optimize their confidence, and **CritiCal**, using natural language **Criti**ques to train confidence **Cal**ibration.\nExperiments show that CritiCal significantly outperforms Self-Critique and other competitive baselines on reasoning-intensive questions, even surpassing its teacher, GPT-4o, in one case.\nCritiCal also shows robust generalization ability, proving its reliability."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3c27caa8d01a96a4057d4bfd05da48d17e7754f7.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Interpretability and Analysis of Models for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/Submission"}},"tmdate":1780804797841,"tcdate":1773417115944,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/Submission452/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/Submission452/Authors"],"forum":"un63enLNke","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":452,"cdate":1773417115944,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1780804797841,"odate":1780804780244,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/March","id":"un63enLNke","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM","Pruning","Efficient AI"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/954b2b200c1fa68e01be2459181204627ffecead.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"With the rapid expansion of large language models (LLMs), the demand for memory and computational resources has grown significantly. Recent advances in LLM pruning aim to reduce the size and computational cost of these models. However, existing methods often suffer from either suboptimal pruning performance or low time efficiency during the pruning process. In this work, we propose an efficient and effective pruning method that simultaneously achieves high pruning performance and fast pruning speed with calibration efficiency. Our approach: (1) introduces an activation variance-guided pruning metric: a new metric that allows for better semantic information distinction preservation in the output activations after pruning;  (2) enables model pruning with only a small sequence length of calibration dataset, while can maintain similar pruning performance as the original baselines that relies on larger sequence of calibration dataset (e.g. 2048 sequence lengths for Wanda and RIA). We conduct extensive experiments on prevalent LLMs, such as OPT, LLaMA, LLaMA-2, LLaMA-3, Qwen2.5, and MoE-based models such as Mixtral 8x7B. The experimental results show that we can achieve up to 18% decrease of perplexity and up to 63% less pruning time on WikiText-2, demonstrating the effectiveness of the proposed method."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nmi2025ace,\ntitle={{ACE}: Exploring Activation Variance for Accurate and Calibration-Efficient {LLM} Pruning},\nauthor={Zhendong Mi and Zhenglun Kong and Geng Yuan and Shaoyi Huang},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Cyh0djVq3m}\n}"},"title":{"value":"ACE: Exploring Activation Variance for Accurate and Calibration-Efficient LLM Pruning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/59773adf672f073651c5be4bfa9dc5022d83394b.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"mi|ace_exploring_activation_variance_for_accurate_and_calibrationefficient_llm_pruning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhendong_Mi1","~Zhenglun_Kong1","~Geng_Yuan1","~Shaoyi_Huang1"]},"authors":{"value":["Zhendong Mi","Zhenglun Kong","Geng Yuan","Shaoyi Huang"]}},"tmdate":1764126567340,"tcdate":1758206447431,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12224/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12224/Authors"],"forum":"Cyh0djVq3m","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":12224,"cdate":1758206447431,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission12224/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1764126567340,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"Cyh0djVq3m","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.11683v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhi|unifying_scene_representation_and_handeye_calibration_with_3d_foundation_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Weiming_Zhi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haozhan_Tang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianyi_Zhang_0014:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Matthew_Johnson-Roberson:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.11683"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2404-11683,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Weiming Zhi and Haozhan Tang and Tianyi Zhang and Matthew Johnson-Roberson},\n  title={Unifying Scene Representation and Hand-Eye Calibration with 3D Foundation Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2404.11683},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.11683}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Representing the environment is a central challenge in robotics, and is essential for effective decision-making. Traditionally, before capturing images with a manipulator-mounted camera, users need to calibrate the camera using a specific external marker, such as a checkerboard or AprilTag. However, recent advances in computer vision have led to the development of \\emph{3D foundation models}. These are large, pre-trained neural networks that can establish fast and accurate multi-view correspondences with very few images, even in the absence of rich visual features. This paper advocates for the integration of 3D foundation models into scene representation approaches for robotic systems equipped with manipulator-mounted RGB cameras. Specifically, we propose the Joint Calibration and Representation (JCR) method. JCR uses RGB images, captured by a manipulator-mounted camera, to simultaneously construct an environmental representation and calibrate the camera relative to the robot's end-effector, in the absence of specific calibration markers. The resulting 3D environment representation is aligned with the robot's coordinate frame and maintains physically accurate scales. We demonstrate that JCR can build effective scene representations using a low-cost RGB camera attached to a manipulator, without prior calibration."},"title":{"value":"Unifying Scene Representation and Hand-Eye Calibration with 3D Foundation Models"},"authors":{"value":["Weiming Zhi","Haozhan Tang","Tianyi Zhang","Matthew Johnson-Roberson"]}},"tmdate":1763254431934,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2404-11683"],"tcdate":1763254426825,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Weiming_Zhi1"],"forum":"4uaAANWGCd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":704134,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1763254431934,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4uaAANWGCd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/26632/26404"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AAAI/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|transferable_posthoc_calibration_on_pretrained_transformers_in_noisy_text_classification"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jun_Zhang24","~Wen_Yao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoqian_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ling_Feng:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v37i11.26632"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aaai/0052YCF23,\n  author={Jun Zhang and Wen Yao and Xiaoqian Chen and Ling Feng},\n  title={Transferable Post-hoc Calibration on Pretrained Transformers in Noisy Text Classification},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={13940-13948},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v37i11.26632},\n  booktitle={AAAI},\n  crossref={conf/aaai/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent work has demonstrated that pretrained transformers are overconfident in text classification tasks, which can be calibrated by the famous post-hoc calibration method temperature scaling (TS). Character or word spelling mistakes are frequently encountered in real applications and greatly threaten transformer model safety. Research on calibration under noisy settings is rare, and we focus on this direction. Based on a toy experiment, we discover that TS performs poorly when the datasets are perturbed by slight noise, such as swapping the characters, which results in distribution shift. We further utilize two metrics, predictive uncertainty and maximum mean discrepancy (MMD), to measure the distribution shift between clean and noisy datasets, based on which we propose a simple yet effective transferable TS method for calibrating models dynamically. To evaluate the performance of the proposed methods under noisy settings, we construct a benchmark consisting of four noise types and five shift intensities based on the QNLI, AG-News, and Emotion tasks. Experimental results on the noisy benchmark show that (1) the metrics are effective in measuring distribution shift and (2) transferable TS can significantly decrease the expected calibration error (ECE) compared with the competitive baseline ensemble TS by approximately 46.09%."},"title":{"value":"Transferable Post-hoc Calibration on Pretrained Transformers in Noisy Text Classification"},"authors":{"value":["Jun Zhang","Wen Yao","Xiaoqian Chen","Ling Feng"]}},"tmdate":1747716677143,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731482181573,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wen_Yao1"],"forum":"ThFOGRxEOe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":211932,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747716677143,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ThFOGRxEOe","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.00442v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"kimura|geometric_insights_into_focal_loss_reducing_curvature_for_enhanced_model_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Masanari_Kimura:","~Hiroki_Naganuma1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.00442"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2405-00442,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Masanari Kimura and Hiroki Naganuma},\n  title={Geometric Insights into Focal Loss: Reducing Curvature for Enhanced Model Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2405.00442},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.00442}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The key factor in implementing machine learning algorithms in decision-making situations is not only the accuracy of the model but also its confidence level. The confidence level of a model in a classification problem is often given by the output vector of a softmax function for convenience. However, these values are known to deviate significantly from the actual expected model confidence. This problem is called model calibration and has been studied extensively. One of the simplest techniques to tackle this task is focal loss, a generalization of cross-entropy by introducing one positive parameter. Although many related studies exist because of the simplicity of the idea and its formalization, the theoretical analysis of its behavior is still insufficient. In this study, our objective is to understand the behavior of focal loss by reinterpreting this function geometrically. Our analysis suggests that focal loss reduces the curvature of the loss surface in training the model. This indicates that curvature may be one of the essential factors in achieving model calibration. We design numerical experiments to support this conjecture to reveal the behavior of focal loss and the relationship between calibration performance and curvature."},"title":{"value":"Geometric Insights into Focal Loss: Reducing Curvature for Enhanced Model Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Masanari Kimura","Hiroki Naganuma"]}},"tmdate":1747570433995,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747570425898,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hiroki_Naganuma1"],"forum":"5Fao6EIn0n","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":521256,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747570433995,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5Fao6EIn0n","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"3D Imaging and Applications 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://library.imaging.org/admin/apis/public/api/ist/website/downloadArticle/ei/33/18/art00008"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/3DICA/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"meyer|3d_multicamera_calibration_with_a_fractal_encoded_multishape_target"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dominique_E._Meyer:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pranav_Verma:","~Falko_Kuester1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.2352/ISSN.2470-1173.2021.18.3DIA-101"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/3dica/MeyerVK21,\n  author={Dominique E. Meyer and Pranav Verma and Falko Kuester},\n  title={3D Multi-Camera Calibration with a Fractal Encoded Multi-Shape Target},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={1-9},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.2352/ISSN.2470-1173.2021.18.3DIA-101},\n  booktitle={3D Imaging and Applications},\n  crossref={conf/3dica/2021}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Robust multi-camera calibration is a fundamental task for all multi-view camera systems, leveraging discreet camera model fitting from sparse target observations. Stereo systems, photogrammetry and light-field arrays have all demonstrated the need for geometrically consistent calibrations to achieve higherlevels of sub-pixel localization accuracy for improved depth estimation. This work presents a calibration target that leverages multi-directional features to achieve improved dense calibrations of camera systems. We begin by presenting a 2D target that uses an encoded feature set, each with 12 bits of uniqueness for flexible patterning and easy identification. These features combine orthogonal sets of straight and circular binary edges, along with Gaussian peaks. Our proposed feature extraction algorithm uses steerable filters for edge localization, and an ellipsoidal peak fitting for the circle center estimation. Feature uniqueness is used for associativity across views, which is combined into a 3D pose graph for nonlinear optimization. Existing camera models are leveraged for intrinsic and extrinsic estimates, demonstrating a reduction in mean re-projection error of for stereo calibration from 0.2 pixels to 0.01 pixels when using a traditional checkerboard and the proposed target respectively."},"title":{"value":"3D Multi-Camera Calibration with a Fractal Encoded Multi-Shape Target"},"authors":{"value":["Dominique E. Meyer","Pranav Verma","Falko Kuester"]}},"tmdate":1731486255987,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1731482900521,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Falko_Kuester1"],"forum":"5vVi9ZsuwJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":212548,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731486255987,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5vVi9ZsuwJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IMSE 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://library.imaging.org/admin/apis/public/api/ist/website/downloadArticle/ei/31/9/art00012"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EI-ISS/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"tai|fisheye_camera_calibration_using_horizontal_and_vertical_laser_planes_projected_from_a_laser_level"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yen-Chou_Tai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu-Hsiang_Chiu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi-Yu_Hsieh:","~Yong-Sheng_Chen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jen-Hui_Chuang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.2352/ISSN.2470-1173.2019.9.IMSE-374"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ei-iss/TaiCHCC19,\n  author={Yen-Chou Tai and Yu-Hsiang Chiu and Yi-Yu Hsieh and Yong-Sheng Chen and Jen-Hui Chuang},\n  title={Fish-eye camera calibration using horizontal and vertical laser planes projected from a laser level},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  pages={1-6},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.2352/ISSN.2470-1173.2019.9.IMSE-374},\n  booktitle={IMSE},\n  crossref={conf/ei-iss/2019}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"A practical approach to calibrate a fish-eye camera by using horizontal and vertical laser planes projected from a laser level is proposed. The approach does not need to use camera parameters, scene information, and calibration objects in the scene. Instead, only an off-the-shelf laser level is utilized to cast the laser planes toward the scene to generate image features for the calibration based on the principle of projective geometry. With proper alignment of laser level, smooth laser curves can be obtained in the fish-eye image, and the principal center of the camera can be found by intersecting two straight laser lines in the fish-eye image. Other curved laser lines can then be used to measure the calibration data for the correction of radial distortion. Experimental results demonstrate that satisfactory calibration can be achieved by using the proposed method."},"title":{"value":"Fish-eye camera calibration using horizontal and vertical laser planes projected from a laser level"},"authors":{"value":["Yen-Chou Tai","Yu-Hsiang Chiu","Yi-Yu Hsieh","Yong-Sheng Chen","Jen-Hui Chuang"]}},"tmdate":1762255784796,"pdate":1546300800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/ei-iss/TaiCHCC19"],"tcdate":1762255770435,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yong-Sheng_Chen1"],"forum":"rzGdBOZjQz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":653126,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762255784796,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rzGdBOZjQz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.14904v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"jain|llmassisted_code_cleaning_for_training_accurate_code_generators"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Naman_Jain:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianjun_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wei-Lin_Chiang:","~Joseph_E._Gonzalez1","~Koushik_Sen2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ion_Stoica:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2311.14904"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2311-14904,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Naman Jain and Tianjun Zhang and Wei-Lin Chiang and Joseph E. Gonzalez and Koushik Sen and Ion Stoica},\n  title={LLM-Assisted Code Cleaning For Training Accurate Code Generators},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2311.14904},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2311.14904}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Natural language to code generation is an important application area of LLMs and has received wide attention from the community. The majority of relevant studies have exclusively concentrated on increasing the quantity and functional correctness of training sets while disregarding other stylistic elements of programs. More recently, data quality has garnered a lot of interest and multiple works have showcased its importance for improving performance. In this work, we investigate data quality for code and find that making the code more structured and readable leads to improved code generation performance of the system. We build a novel data-cleaning pipeline that uses these principles to transform existing programs by 1.) renaming variables, 2.) modularizing and decomposing complex code into smaller helper sub-functions, and 3.) inserting natural-language based plans via LLM based transformations. We evaluate our approach on two challenging algorithmic code generation benchmarks and find that fine-tuning CodeLLaMa-7B on our transformed modularized programs improves the performance by up to 30% compared to fine-tuning on the original dataset. Additionally, we demonstrate improved performance from using a smaller amount of higher-quality data, finding that a model fine-tuned on the entire original dataset is outperformed by a model trained on 15% of our cleaned dataset. Even in comparison to closed-source models, our models outperform the much larger AlphaCoder models."},"title":{"value":"LLM-Assisted Code Cleaning For Training Accurate Code Generators"},"authors":{"value":["Naman Jain","Tianjun Zhang","Wei-Lin Chiang","Joseph E. Gonzalez","Koushik Sen","Ion Stoica"]}},"tmdate":1792968606626,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731476163024,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Joseph_E._Gonzalez1"],"forum":"9x3CIILccH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":201086,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1792968606626,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9x3CIILccH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2018"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1806.07336v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2018"},"paperhash":{"value":"bennun|neural_code_comprehension_a_learnable_representation_of_code_semantics"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tal_Ben-Nun1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alice_Shoshana_Jakobovits:","~Torsten_Hoefler1"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1806.07336"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1806-07336,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Tal Ben-Nun and Alice Shoshana Jakobovits and Torsten Hoefler},\n  title={Neural Code Comprehension: A Learnable Representation of Code Semantics},\n  year={2018},\n  cdate={1514764800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1806.07336},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1806.07336}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With the recent success of embeddings in natural language processing, research has been conducted into applying similar methods to code analysis. Most works attempt to process the code directly or use a syntactic tree representation, treating it like sentences written in a natural language. However, none of the existing methods are sufficient to comprehend program semantics robustly, due to structural features such as function calls, branching, and interchangeable order of statements. In this paper, we propose a novel processing technique to learn code semantics, and apply it to a variety of program analysis tasks. In particular, we stipulate that a robust distributional hypothesis of code applies to both human- and machine-generated programs. Following this hypothesis, we define an embedding space, inst2vec, based on an Intermediate Representation (IR) of the code that is independent of the source programming language. We provide a novel definition of contextual flow for this IR, leveraging both the underlying data- and control-flow of the program. We then analyze the embeddings qualitatively using analogies and clustering, and evaluate the learned representation on three different high-level tasks. We show that even without fine-tuning, a single RNN architecture and fixed inst2vec embeddings outperform specialized approaches for performance prediction (compute device mapping, optimal thread coarsening); and algorithm classification from raw code (104 classes), where we set a new state-of-the-art."},"title":{"value":"Neural Code Comprehension: A Learnable Representation of Code Semantics"},"authors":{"value":["Tal Ben-Nun","Alice Shoshana Jakobovits","Torsten Hoefler"]}},"tmdate":1769624480617,"pdate":1546214400000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-1806-07336"],"tcdate":1768984411293,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Torsten_Hoefler1"],"forum":"bYsDE9N0GC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":782121,"cdate":1514764800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1769624480617,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bYsDE9N0GC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2024 poster"},"TLDR":{"value":"We highlight importance of code style for improving data quality"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Code Generation","Data Quality","Synthetic Data"]},"primary_area":{"value":"generative models"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Natural language to code generation is an important application area of LLMs and has received wide attention from the community. \nThe majority of relevant studies have exclusively concentrated on increasing the quantity and functional correctness of training sets while disregarding other stylistic elements of programs. More recently, data quality has garnered a lot of interest and multiple works have showcased its importance for improving performance. In this work, we investigate data quality for code and find that making the code more structured and readable leads to improved code generation performance of the system. We build a novel data-cleaning pipeline that uses these principles to transform existing programs by 1.) renaming variables, 2.) modularizing and decomposing complex code into smaller helper sub-functions, and 3.) inserting natural-language based planning annotations. We evaluate our approach on two challenging algorithmic code generation benchmarks and find that fine-tuning CodeLLaMa-7B on our transformed programs improves the performance by up to \\textbf{30\\%} compared to fine-tuning on the original dataset. Additionally, we demonstrate improved performance from using a smaller amount of higher-quality data, finding that a model fine-tuned on the entire original dataset is outperformed by a model trained on one-eighth of our cleaned dataset. Even in comparison to closed-source models, our models outperform the much larger AlphaCode models."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\njain2024llmassisted,\ntitle={{LLM}-Assisted Code Cleaning For Training Accurate Code Generators},\nauthor={Naman Jain and Tianjun Zhang and Wei-Lin Chiang and Joseph E. Gonzalez and Koushik Sen and Ion Stoica},\nbooktitle={The Twelfth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=maRYffiUpI}\n}"},"title":{"value":"LLM-Assisted Code Cleaning For Training Accurate Code Generators"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/abdd4c0aa83ef59ad6f792d31a4ba8cecb383198.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"jain|llmassisted_code_cleaning_for_training_accurate_code_generators"},"authorids":{"value":["~Naman_Jain2","~Tianjun_Zhang1","~Wei-Lin_Chiang1","~Joseph_E._Gonzalez1","~Koushik_Sen2","~Ion_Stoica1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Naman Jain","Tianjun Zhang","Wei-Lin Chiang","Joseph E. Gonzalez","Koushik Sen","Ion Stoica"]}},"tmdate":1713672260036,"pdate":1705410826865,"tcdate":1695104444160,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1668/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1668/Authors"],"forum":"maRYffiUpI","number":1668,"cdate":1695104444160,"mdate":1713672260036,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1668/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1668/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1668/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"maRYffiUpI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Video Generation","Controllable Generation","Agentic Generation"]},"primary_area":{"value":"generative models"},"abstract":{"value":"Video diffusion models generate realistic videos in a black box. Physics is imagined rather than computed; object placement, motion, and camera trajectories are resolved internally and only visible in the final clip. Inspired by film production, we present Code Video Model, which gives video generation an executable previs, with code as the engine. Given a prompt, Code Video Model writes a program that lays out a scene, animates objects, places a camera, and computes dynamics from equations; rendering the program yields a proxy video and a reference image. Rendering the resulting proxy video takes seconds, making it \\emph{fast} to preview; it is \\emph{grounded}, since dynamics are computed, and \\emph{directly and precisely controllable}, because code can modify each element. A pretrained video model is then conditioned on the proxy video and reference image to generate the final clip. However, we observe that existing video models tend to copy the flat appearance of the proxy video into the generated clip. To address this issue, Code Video Model further introduces early-step conditioning, a training-free schedule that attends to the proxy video only during early sampling steps when structure is established, and drops this conditioning thereafter. As a result, Code Video Model is able to generate a final clip that follows the proxy video’s structure while preserving the reference image’s appearance. We introduce CodeVideoBench to evaluate controllable video generation across diverse applications including gaming, scene editing, world-model data, physics grounding, bullet time, and 3D/4D reconstruction. On CodeVideoBench, Code Video Model with MiniMax-H3 achieves overall scores of 85.2, 82.1, and 85.8 under QwenVL-3.8-27B, GPT-5.6, and GPT-6, respectively, versus 74.3, 73.1, and 80.6 for VACE, the strongest learned baseline. The project page is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/w/CodeVideoModel-2027."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026code,\ntitle={Code Video Model},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=KIGGxNm6gG},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Code Video Model"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a82892b3fbac8bda5b40d44754dff1e00290d09b.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791228057209,"tcdate":1789368406966,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission17169/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission17169/Authors"],"forum":"KIGGxNm6gG","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":17169,"cdate":1789368406966,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission17169/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791228057209,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"KIGGxNm6gG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Software Eng. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/32/10721226/10636040.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TSE/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|learning_to_generate_structured_code_summaries_from_hybrid_code_context"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ziyi_Zhou:","~Mingchen_Li3","~Huiqun_Yu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guisheng_Fan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Penghui_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zijie_Huang_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2024.3439562"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tse/ZhouLYFYH24,\n  author={Ziyi Zhou and Mingchen Li and Huiqun Yu and Guisheng Fan and Penghui Yang and Zijie Huang},\n  title={Learning to Generate Structured Code Summaries From Hybrid Code Context},\n  year={2024},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1727740800000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Software Eng.},\n  volume={50},\n  number={10},\n  pages={2512-2528},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2024.3439562}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code summarization aims to automatically generate natural language descriptions for code, and has become a rapidly expanding research area in the past decades. Unfortunately, existing approaches mainly focus on the “one-to-one” mapping from methods to short descriptions, which hinders them from becoming practical tools: 1) The program context is ignored, so they have difficulty in predicting keywords outside the target method; 2) They are typically trained to generate brief function descriptions with only one sentence in length, and therefore have difficulty in providing specific information. These drawbacks are partially due to the limitations of public code summarization datasets. In this paper, we first build a large code summarization dataset including different code contexts and summary content annotations, and then propose a deep learning framework that learns to generate structured code summaries from hybrid program context, named StructCodeSum. It provides both an LLM-based approach and a lightweight approach which are suitable for different scenarios. Given a target method, StructCodeSum predicts its function description, return description, parameter description, and usage description through hybrid code context, and ultimately builds a Javadoc-style code summary. The hybrid code context consists of path context, class context, documentation context and call context of the target method. Extensive experimental results demonstrate: 1) The hybrid context covers more than 70% of the summary tokens in average and significantly boosts the model performance; 2) When generating function descriptions, StructCodeSum outperforms the state-of-the-art approaches by a large margin; 3) According to human evaluation, the quality of the structured summaries generated by our approach is better than the documentation generated by Code Llama."},"title":{"value":"Learning to Generate Structured Code Summaries From Hybrid Code Context"},"authors":{"value":["Ziyi Zhou","Mingchen Li","Huiqun Yu","Guisheng Fan","Penghui Yang","Zijie Huang"]}},"tmdate":1762258349527,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747238338682,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mingchen_Li3"],"forum":"CxHfFnxygH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":461792,"cdate":1727740800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762258349527,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"CxHfFnxygH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Empir. Softw. Eng. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10664-025-10731-0.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"majdinasab|deepcodeprobe_evaluating_code_representation_quality_in_models_trained_on_code"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-025-10731-0"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ese/MajdinasabNK25,\n  author={Vahid Majdinasab and Amin Nikanjam and Foutse Khomh},\n  title={DeepCodeProbe: Evaluating Code Representation Quality in Models Trained on Code},\n  year={2025},\n  month={December},\n  cdate={1764547200000},\n  journal={Empir. Softw. Eng.},\n  volume={30},\n  number={6},\n  pages={169},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-025-10731-0}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Machine Learning models trained on code and artifacts extracted from them (e.g., version control histories, code differences, etc.), provide invaluable assistance for software engineering tasks. Despite their good performance, there exists a lack of understanding about the quality of code representations used to train these models and how different representations affect their learning process. This gap limits our ability to systematically evaluate and improve representation quality in ML-based software engineering. As such, we present DeepCodeProbe, an approach for analyzing the quality of code representations and their impact on model learning. Our study of current ML models for code shows that high-quality structured representations enable effective learning of task-specific patterns without needing to understand complete programming syntax. We demonstrate how the quality of code structure representation is more important for model performance than increasing model size. Further analysis shows that selectively scaling specific parts of a model works better than expanding the entire architecture when using high-quality representations. Based on our results, we outline practical steps for improving representation quality in ML models for code-related tasks. To support future work, we release a replication package that allows for comparative analysis of representation quality across diverse code-focused ML models. This work enhances our understanding of representation quality in ML models trained on code, laying a foundation for the development of more efficient and robust tools across software engineering tasks."},"title":{"value":"DeepCodeProbe: Evaluating Code Representation Quality in Models Trained on Code"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Vahid Majdinasab","username":"~Vahid_Majdinasab1"},{"fullname":"Amin Nikanjam","username":""},{"fullname":"Foutse Khomh","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1785855302757,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/ese/MajdinasabNK25"],"tcdate":1785855298925,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Vahid_Majdinasab1"],"forum":"q3brLIdYDt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":116306,"cdate":1764547200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785855302757,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"q3brLIdYDt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.01250v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|active_code_learning_benchmarking_sampleefficient_training_of_code_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Qiang_Hu3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuejun_Guo_0001:","~Xiaofei_Xie1","~Maxime_Cordy1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Ma_0003:","~Mike_Papadakis1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yves_Le_Traon:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2306.01250"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2306-01250,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Qiang Hu and Yuejun Guo and Xiaofei Xie and Maxime Cordy and Lei Ma and Mike Papadakis and Yves Le Traon},\n  title={Active Code Learning: Benchmarking Sample-Efficient Training of Code Models},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2306.01250},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2306.01250}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The costly human effort required to prepare the training data of machine learning (ML) models hinders their practical development and usage in software engineering (ML4Code), especially for those with limited budgets. Therefore, efficiently training models of code with less human effort has become an emergent problem. Active learning is such a technique to address this issue that allows developers to train a model with reduced data while producing models with desired performance, which has been well studied in computer vision and natural language processing domains. Unfortunately, there is no such work that explores the effectiveness of active learning for code models. In this paper, we bridge this gap by building the first benchmark to study this critical problem - active code learning. Specifically, we collect 11 acquisition functions~(which are used for data selection in active learning) from existing works and adapt them for code-related tasks. Then, we conduct an empirical study to check whether these acquisition functions maintain performance for code data. The results demonstrate that feature selection highly affects active learning and using output vectors to select data is the best choice. For the code summarization task, active code learning is ineffective which produces models with over a 29.64\\% gap compared to the expected performance. Furthermore, we explore future directions of active code learning with an exploratory study. We propose to replace distance calculation methods with evaluation metrics and find a correlation between these evaluation-based distance methods and the performance of code models."},"title":{"value":"Active Code Learning: Benchmarking Sample-Efficient Training of Code Models"},"authors":{"value":["Qiang Hu","Yuejun Guo","Xiaofei Xie","Maxime Cordy","Lei Ma","Mike Papadakis","Yves Le Traon"]}},"tmdate":1767613737722,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727453815532,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Maxime_Cordy1"],"forum":"7Rtm7Fecbt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":87851,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1767613737722,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"7Rtm7Fecbt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Software Eng. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/32/10531114/10471610.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TSE/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|active_code_learning_benchmarking_sampleefficient_training_of_code_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Qiang_Hu3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuejun_Guo_0001:","~Xiaofei_Xie1","~Maxime_Cordy1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Ma_0003:","~Mike_Papadakis1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yves_Le_Traon:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2024.3376964"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tse/HuGXCMPT24,\n  author={Qiang Hu and Yuejun Guo and Xiaofei Xie and Maxime Cordy and Lei Ma and Mike Papadakis and Yves Le Traon},\n  title={Active Code Learning: Benchmarking Sample-Efficient Training of Code Models},\n  year={2024},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1714521600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Software Eng.},\n  volume={50},\n  number={5},\n  pages={1080-1095},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2024.3376964}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The costly human effort required to prepare the training data of machine learning (ML) models hinders their practical development and usage in software engineering (ML4Code), especially for those with limited budgets. Therefore, efficiently training models of code with less human effort has become an emergent problem. Active learning is such a technique to address this issue that allows developers to train a model with reduced data while producing models with desired performance, which has been well studied in computer vision and natural language processing domains. Unfortunately, there is no such work that explores the effectiveness of active learning for code models. In this paper, we bridge this gap by building the first benchmark to study this critical problem - active code learning. Specifically, we collect 11 acquisition functions (which are used for data selection in active learning) from existing works and adapt them for code-related tasks. Then, we conduct an empirical study to check whether these acquisition functions maintain performance for code data. The results demonstrate that feature selection highly affects active learning and using output vectors to select data is the best choice. For the code summarization task, active code learning is ineffective which produces models with over a 29.64% gap compared to the expected performance. Furthermore, we explore future directions of active code learning with an exploratory study. We propose to replace distance calculation methods with evaluation metrics and find a correlation between these evaluation-based distance methods and the performance of code models."},"title":{"value":"Active Code Learning: Benchmarking Sample-Efficient Training of Code Models"},"authors":{"value":["Qiang Hu","Yuejun Guo","Xiaofei Xie","Maxime Cordy","Lei Ma","Mike Papadakis","Yves Le Traon"]}},"tmdate":1767613737408,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727453815902,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Maxime_Cordy1"],"forum":"jiA2lU3fcT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":87906,"cdate":1714521600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1767613737408,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"jiA2lU3fcT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ASE 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10298258/10298286/10298520.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/KBSE/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"tian|code_difference_guided_adversarial_example_generation_for_deep_code_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhao_Tian:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junjie_Chen_0003:","~Zhi_Jin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ASE56229.2023.00149"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/kbse/TianCJ23,\n  author={Zhao Tian and Junjie Chen and Zhi Jin},\n  title={Code Difference Guided Adversarial Example Generation for Deep Code Models},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={850-862},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ASE56229.2023.00149},\n  booktitle={ASE},\n  crossref={conf/kbse/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Adversarial examples are important to test and enhance the robustness of deep code models. As source code is discrete and has to strictly stick to complex grammar and semantics constraints, the adversarial example generation techniques in other domains are hardly applicable. Moreover, the adversarial example generation techniques specific to deep code models still suffer from unsatisfactory effectiveness due to the enormous ingredient search space. In this work, we propose a novel adversarial example generation technique (i.e., CODA) for testing deep code models. Its key idea is to use code differences between the target input (i.e., a given code snippet as the model input) and reference inputs (i.e., the inputs that have small code differences but different prediction results with the target input) to guide the generation of adversarial examples. It considers both structure differences and identifier differences to preserve the original semantics. Hence, the ingredient search space can be largely reduced as the one constituted by the two kinds of code differences, and thus the testing process can be improved by designing and guiding corresponding equivalent structure transformations and identifier renaming transformations. Our experiments on 15 deep code models demonstrate the effective-ness and efficiency of CODA, the naturalness of its generated examples, and its capability of enhancing model robustness after adversarial fine-tuning. For example, CODA reveals 88.05 % and 72.51 % more faults in models than the state-of-the-art techniques (i.e., CARROT and ALERT) on average, respectively."},"title":{"value":"Code Difference Guided Adversarial Example Generation for Deep Code Models"},"authors":{"value":["Zhao Tian","Junjie Chen","Zhi Jin"]}},"tmdate":1747645556623,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1747645540244,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhi_Jin1"],"forum":"2Y8dnuMfhn","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":525784,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747645556623,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"2Y8dnuMfhn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SP 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10646615/10646598/10646683.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/SP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|srcmarker_dualchannel_source_code_watermarking_via_scalable_code_transformations"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Borui_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wei_Li:","~Liyao_Xiang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bo_Li:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/SP54263.2024.00097"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/sp/YangLXL24,\n  author={Borui Yang and Wei Li and Liyao Xiang and Bo Li},\n  title={SrcMarker: Dual-Channel Source Code Watermarking via Scalable Code Transformations},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={4088-4106},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/SP54263.2024.00097},\n  booktitle={SP},\n  crossref={conf/sp/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The expansion of the open source community and the rise of large language models have raised ethical and security concerns on the distribution of source code, such as misconduct on copyrighted code, distributions without proper licenses, or misuse of the code for malicious purposes. Hence it is important to track the ownership of source code, in which watermarking is a major technique. Yet, drastically different from natural languages, source code watermarking requires far stricter and more complicated rules to ensure the readability as well as the functionality of the source code. Hence we introduce SrcMarker, a watermarking system to unobtrusively encode ID bitstrings into source code, without affecting the usage and semantics of the code. To this end, SrcMarker performs transformations on an AST-based intermediate representation that enables unified transformations across different programming languages. The core of the system utilizes learning-based embedding and extraction modules to select rule-based transformations for watermarking. In addition, a novel feature-approximation technique is designed to tackle the inherent non-differentiability of rule selection, thus seamlessly integrating the rule-based transformations and learning-based networks into an interconnected system to enable end-to-end training. Extensive experiments demonstrate the superiority of SrcMarker over existing methods in various watermarking requirements."},"title":{"value":"SrcMarker: Dual-Channel Source Code Watermarking via Scalable Code Transformations"},"authors":{"value":["Borui Yang","Wei Li","Liyao Xiang","Bo Li"]}},"tmdate":1727614187102,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727614182251,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Liyao_Xiang1"],"forum":"FpxZsuVpwc","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":106330,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727614187102,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"FpxZsuVpwc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2102.06360v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|deeppseudo_deep_pseudocode_generation_via_transformer_and_code_feature_extraction"},"authorids":{"value":["~Guang_Yang14","~Xiang_Chen27","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ke_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chi_Yu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.06360"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2102-06360,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Guang Yang and Xiang Chen and Ke Liu and Chi Yu},\n  title={DeepPseudo: Deep Pseudo-code Generation via Transformer and Code Feature Extraction},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2102.06360},\n  url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.06360}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Pseudo-code written by natural language is helpful for novice developers' program comprehension. However, writing such pseudo-code is time-consuming and laborious. Motivated by the research advancements of sequence-to-sequence learning and code semantic learning, we propose a novel deep pseudo-code generation method DeepPseudo via code feature extraction and Transformer. In particular, DeepPseudo utilizes a Transformer encoder to perform encoding for source code and then use a code feature extractor to learn the knowledge of local features. Finally, it uses a pseudo-code generator to perform decoding, which can generate the corresponding pseudo-code. We choose two corpora (i.e., Django and SPoC) from real-world large-scale projects as our empirical subjects. We first compare DeepPseudo with seven state-of-the-art baselines from pseudo-code generation and neural machine translation domains in terms of four performance measures. Results show the competitiveness of DeepPseudo. Moreover, we also analyze the rationality of the component settings in DeepPseudo."},"title":{"value":"DeepPseudo: Deep Pseudo-code Generation via Transformer and Code Feature Extraction"},"authors":{"value":["Guang Yang","Xiang Chen","Ke Liu","Chi Yu"]}},"tmdate":1785418239952,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1727150468507,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Guang_Yang14"],"forum":"cluDFIMIgb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":83013,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1785418239952,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"cluDFIMIgb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["Code optimization","performance-aware","code-llm"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/cc72ea1837fb152f937e694e197944f11fe5ecd3.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"applications to computer vision, audio, language, and other modalities"},"abstract":{"value":"Code runtime optimization$\\textemdash$the task of rewriting a given code to a faster one$\\textemdash$remains challenging,\nas it requires reasoning about performance trade-offs involving algorithmic and structural choices.\nRecent approaches employ code-LLMs with slow-fast code pairs provided as optimization guidance, but such pair-based methods obscure the causal factors of performance gains and often lead to superficial pattern imitation rather than genuine performance reasoning.\nWe introduce ECO, a performance-aware prompting framework for code optimization.\nECO first distills runtime optimization instructions (ROIs) from reference slow-fast code pairs; \nEach ROI describes root causes of inefficiency and the rationales that drive performance improvements.\nFor a given input code, ECO in parallel employs (i) a symbolic advisor to produce a bottleneck diagnosis tailored to the code, and (ii) an ROI retriever to return related ROIs.\nThese two outputs are then composed into a performance-aware prompt, providing actionable guidance for code-LLMs.\nECO's prompts are model-agnostic, require no fine-tuning, and can be easily prepended to any code-LLM prompt.\nOur empirical studies highlight that ECO prompting significantly improves code-LLMs' ability to generate efficient code, achieving speedups of up to 7.81$\\times$ while minimizing correctness loss."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nkim2026eco,\ntitle={{ECO}: Enhanced Code Optimization via Performance-Aware Prompting for Code-{LLM}s},\nauthor={Su-Hyeon Kim and Joonghyuk Hahn and Sooyoung Cha and Yo-Sub Han},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=R5L1TD1Z58}\n}"},"title":{"value":"ECO: Enhanced Code Optimization via Performance-Aware Prompting for Code-LLMs"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/420da69fe9c0ae2b13d37453ff2cd28a7362fe07.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"kim|eco_enhanced_code_optimization_via_performanceaware_prompting_for_codellms"},"authorids":{"value":["~Su-Hyeon_Kim1","~Joonghyuk_Hahn1","~Sooyoung_Cha2","~Yo-Sub_Han1"]},"authors":{"value":["Su-Hyeon Kim","Joonghyuk Hahn","Sooyoung Cha","Yo-Sub Han"]}},"tmdate":1770805198614,"tcdate":1758364609391,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission25140/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission25140/Authors"],"forum":"R5L1TD1Z58","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":25140,"cdate":1758364609391,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission25140/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission25140/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770805198614,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"R5L1TD1Z58","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.08489v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"mahfuz|confidence_calibration_for_audio_captioning_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Rehana_Mahfuz1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yinyi_Guo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Erik_Visser:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.08489"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2409-08489,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Rehana Mahfuz and Yinyi Guo and Erik Visser},\n  title={Confidence Calibration for Audio Captioning Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2409.08489},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.08489}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"To establish the trustworthiness of systems that automatically generate text captions for audio, images and video, existing reference-free metrics rely on large pretrained models which are impractical to accommodate in resource-constrained settings. To address this, we propose some metrics to elicit the model's confidence in its own generation. To assess how well these metrics replace correctness measures that leverage reference captions, we test their calibration with correctness measures. We discuss why some of these confidence metrics align better with certain correctness measures. Further, we provide insight into why temperature scaling of confidence metrics is effective. Our main contribution is a suite of well-calibrated lightweight confidence metrics for reference-free evaluation of captions in resource-constrained settings."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration for Audio Captioning Models"},"authors":{"value":["Rehana Mahfuz","Yinyi Guo","Erik Visser"]}},"tmdate":1740008022913,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1740008016993,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rehana_Mahfuz1"],"forum":"Y5wNuTbfcS","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":335624,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1740008022913,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Y5wNuTbfcS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Image Process. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/83/10795784/11006398.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIP/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"jing|recursive_confidence_training_for_pseudolabeling_calibration_in_semisupervised_fewshot_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kunlei_Jing1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hebo_Ma:","~Chen_Zhang53","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lei_Wen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhaorui_Zhang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2025.3569196"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tip/JingMZWZ25,\n  author={Kunlei Jing and Hebo Ma and Chen Zhang and Lei Wen and Zhaorui Zhang},\n  title={Recursive Confidence Training for Pseudo-Labeling Calibration in Semi-Supervised Few-Shot Learning},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Image Process.},\n  volume={34},\n  pages={3194-3208},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIP.2025.3569196}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Semi-Supervised Few-Shot Learning (SSFSL) aims to address the data scarcity in few-shot learning by leveraging both a few labeled support data and abundant unlabeled data. In SSFSL, a classifier trained on scarce support data is often biased and thus assigns inaccurate pseudo-labels to the unlabeled data, which will mislead downstream learning tasks. To combat this issue, we introduce a novel method called Certainty-Aware Recursive Confidence Training (CARCT). CARCT hinges on the insight that selecting pseudo-labeled data based on confidence levels can yield more informative support data, which is crucial for retraining an unbiased classifier to achieve accurate pseudo-labeling—a process we term pseudo-labeling calibration. We observe that accurate pseudo-labels typically exhibit smaller certainty entropy, indicating high-confidence pseudo-labeling compared to those of inaccurate pseudo-labels. Accordingly, CARCT constructs a joint double-Gaussian model to fit the certainty entropies collected across numerous SSFSL tasks. Thereby, A semi-supervised Prior Confidence Distribution (ssPCD) is learned to aid in distinguishing between high-confidence and low-confidence pseudo-labels. During an SSFSL task, ssPCD guides the selection of both high-confidence and low-confidence pseudo-labeled data to retrain the classifier that then assigns more accurate pseudo-labels to the low-confidence pseudo-labeled data. Such recursive confidence training continues until the low-confidence ones are exhausted, terminating the pseudo-labeling calibration. The unlabeled data all receive accurate pseudo-labels to expand the few support data to generalize the downstream learning task, which in return meta-refines the classifier, named self-training, to boost the pseudo-labeling in subsequent tasks. Extensive experiments on basic and extended SSFSL setups showcase the superiority of CARCT versus state-of-the-art methods, and comprehensive ablation studies and visualizations justify our insight. The source code is available at https://github.com/Klein-JING/CARCT"},"title":{"value":"Recursive Confidence Training for Pseudo-Labeling Calibration in Semi-Supervised Few-Shot Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Kunlei Jing","Hebo Ma","Chen Zhang","Lei Wen","Zhaorui Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1771054095111,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tip/JingMZWZ25"],"tcdate":1767787104965,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chen_Jason_Zhang1"],"forum":"xMsHOVntVF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":725879,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1771054095111,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xMsHOVntVF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.22462v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"yang|ifevalcode_controlled_code_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jian_Yang_0003:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wei_Zhang_0021:","~Shukai_Liu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Linzheng_Chai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yingshui_Tan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiaheng_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ge_Zhang_0009:","~Wangchunshu_Zhou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guanglin_Niu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhoujun_Li_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Binyuan_Hui:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junyang_Lin:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.22462"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2507-22462,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jian Yang and Wei Zhang and Shukai Liu and Linzheng Chai and Yingshui Tan and Jiaheng Liu and Ge Zhang and Wangchunshu Zhou and Guanglin Niu and Zhoujun Li and Binyuan Hui and Junyang Lin},\n  title={IFEvalCode: Controlled Code Generation},\n  year={2025},\n  month={July},\n  cdate={1751328000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2507.22462},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.22462}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code large language models (Code LLMs) have made significant progress in code generation by translating natural language descriptions into functional code; however, real-world applications often demand stricter adherence to detailed requirements such as coding style, line count, and structural constraints, beyond mere correctness. To address this, the paper introduces forward and backward constraints generation to improve the instruction-following capabilities of Code LLMs in controlled code generation, ensuring outputs align more closely with human-defined guidelines. The authors further present IFEvalCode, a multilingual benchmark comprising 1.6K test samples across seven programming languages (Python, Java, JavaScript, TypeScript, Shell, C++, and C#), with each sample featuring both Chinese and English queries. Unlike existing benchmarks, IFEvalCode decouples evaluation into two metrics: correctness (Corr.) and instruction-following (Instr.), enabling a more nuanced assessment. Experiments on over 40 LLMs reveal that closed-source models outperform open-source ones in controllable code generation and highlight a significant gap between the models' ability to generate correct code versus code that precisely follows instructions."},"title":{"value":"IFEvalCode: Controlled Code Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Jian Yang","Wei Zhang","Shukai Liu","Linzheng Chai","Yingshui Tan","Jiaheng Liu","Ge Zhang","Wangchunshu Zhou","Guanglin Niu","Zhoujun Li","Binyuan Hui","Junyang Lin"]}},"tmdate":1791623419094,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2507-22462"],"tcdate":1769138923174,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wangchunshu_Zhou1"],"forum":"SshzKHWz5e","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":790515,"cdate":1751328000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1791623419094,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SshzKHWz5e","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce a Selective Classification framework that provides guarantees on specific error metrics (like false negative rate), enabling domain-aware trade-offs beyond accuracy."},"venue":{"value":"Calibration for Modern AI @ AISTATS 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/4a0598b8760e3347d27f9a9a7a4ce925b5fab7c2.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Selective Prediction","Abstention","Reliable Machine Learning","Metric-Specific Guarantees","Medical Imaging"]},"venueid":{"value":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI"},"paperhash":{"value":"jemelen|beyond_accuracy_controlling_broad_error_types_in_selective_classification"},"authorids":{"value":["~Emilien_Jemelen2","~Sandrine_Katsahian1","~Francisco_Orchard1","~Agathe_Guilloux1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Selective prediction allows classifiers to abstain on uncertain inputs, improving reliability in high-stakes applications. Existing frameworks, such as Selection with Guaranteed Risk (SGR), provide tight statistical guarantees on overall misclassification risk, but this is inadequate for many applications—particularly in medicine—where guarantees on specific error types, such as false positives, false negatives, or positive predictive value (PPV), are required. We propose a general framework for selective binary classification with metric-specific guarantees. Our theory extends risk control from 0/1 loss to arbitrary binary losses and derives new high-probability bounds for the corresponding metrics under abstention. We instantiate the framework with neural networks and validate it on public imaging datasets, showing that our metric-aware selective classifiers better capture domain-specific trade-offs than accuracy-based approaches. Code and reproducibility artifacts are released at https://github.com/EmilienJemelen/selective-classification."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\njemelen2026beyond,\ntitle={Beyond Accuracy: Controlling Broad Error Types in Selective Classification},\nauthor={Emilien Jemelen and Sandrine Katsahian and Francisco Orchard and Agathe Guilloux},\nbooktitle={Towards Trustworthy Predictions: Theory and Applications of Calibration for Modern AI},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=geLfXcpkd0}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Beyond Accuracy: Controlling Broad Error Types in Selective Classification"},"authors":{"value":["Emilien Jemelen","Sandrine Katsahian","Francisco Orchard","Agathe Guilloux"]}},"tmdate":1776068197966,"pdate":1776068197425,"tcdate":1771249255264,"writers":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission6/Authors"],"signatures":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission6/Authors"],"forum":"geLfXcpkd0","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6,"cdate":1771249255264,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Post_Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission6/-/Revision","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Edit"],"mdate":1776068197966,"odate":1776068197425,"domain":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","id":"geLfXcpkd0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Trans. Assoc. Comput. Linguistics 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://direct.mit.edu/tacl/article-pdf/doi/10.1162/tacl_a_00636/2334456/tacl_a_00636.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TACL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"jiang|addressing_the_binning_problem_in_calibration_assessment_through_scalar_annotations"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhengping_Jiang:","~Anqi_Liu1","~Benjamin_Van_Durme1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1162/tacl_a_00636"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tacl/JiangLD24,\n  author={Zhengping Jiang and Anqi Liu and Benjamin Van Durme},\n  title={Addressing the Binning Problem in Calibration Assessment through Scalar Annotations},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={Trans. Assoc. Comput. Linguistics},\n  volume={12},\n  pages={120-136},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1162/tacl_a_00636}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Computational linguistics models commonly target the prediction of discrete—categorical—labels. When assessing how well-calibrated these model predictions are, popular evaluation schemes require practitioners to manually determine a binning scheme: grouping labels into bins to approximate true label posterior. The problem is that these metrics are sensitive to binning decisions. We consider two solutions to the binning problem that apply at the stage of data annotation: collecting either distributed (redundant) labels or direct scalar value assignment.In this paper, we show that although both approaches address the binning problem by evaluating instance-level calibration, direct scalar assignment is significantly more cost-effective. We provide theoretical analysis and empirical evidence to support our proposal for dataset creators to adopt scalar annotation protocols to enable a higher-quality assessment of model calibration."},"title":{"value":"Addressing the Binning Problem in Calibration Assessment through Scalar Annotations"},"authors":{"value":["Zhengping Jiang","Anqi Liu","Benjamin Van Durme"]}},"tmdate":1768992143226,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731505589607,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Anqi_Liu2"],"forum":"jcIcNEpojB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":228096,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768992143226,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"jcIcNEpojB","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 1999"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6243/16780/00772537.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/1999"},"paperhash":{"value":"kopparapu|the_effect_of_measurement_noise_on_intrinsic_camera_calibration_parameters"},"authorids":{"value":["~Sunil_Kumar_Kopparapu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peter_I._Corke:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.1999.772537"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/KopparapuC99,\n  author={Sunil Kumar Kopparapu and Peter I. Corke},\n  title={The Effect of Measurement Noise on Intrinsic Camera Calibration Parameters},\n  year={1999},\n  cdate={915148800000},\n  pages={1281-1286},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.1999.772537},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/1999}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The camera calibration matrix captures the transformation of a 3D point onto a 2D image plane as occurs in the camera being used for imaging. Given the world coordinates of a number of precisely placed points in a 3D space, camera calibration requires the measurement of the 2D projection of those scene points on the image plane. While the coordinates of the points in 3D space can be measured precisely, the image coordinates that are determined from the digital image are not precise mainly because of the measurement noise. In this paper, we derive analytical relationships between the errors in the intrinsic camera parameters (ICPs) due to error in measurements. We show that the errors in the ICPs are Gaussian distributed when the measurement noise is Gaussian. We verify these results experimentally."},"title":{"value":"The Effect of Measurement Noise on Intrinsic Camera Calibration Parameters"},"authors":{"value":["Sunil Kumar Kopparapu","Peter I. Corke"]}},"tmdate":1746437589762,"pdate":915148800000,"tcdate":1746437509929,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sunil_Kumar_Kopparapu1"],"forum":"GSXW2AwgcN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":410451,"cdate":915148800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1746437589762,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"GSXW2AwgcN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MICCAI (1) 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-319-10404-1_57.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MICCAI/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"lourenço|continuous_zoom_calibration_by_tracking_salient_points_in_endoscopic_video"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Miguel_Lourenço:","~João_Pedro_Barreto1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fernando_Fonseca:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hélder_Ferreira:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rui_M._Duarte:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jorge_Correia-Pinto:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-10404-1_57"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/miccai/LourencoBFFDC14,\n  author={Miguel Lourenço and João Pedro Barreto and Fernando Fonseca and Hélder Ferreira and Rui M. Duarte and Jorge Correia-Pinto},\n  title={Continuous Zoom Calibration by Tracking Salient Points in Endoscopic Video},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={456-463},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-10404-1_57},\n  booktitle={MICCAI (1)},\n  crossref={conf/miccai/2014-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Many image-based systems for aiding the surgeon during minimally invasive surgery require the endoscopic camera to be calibrated at all times. This article proposes a method for accomplishing this goal whenever the camera has optical zoom and the focal length changes during the procedure. Our solution for online calibration builds on recent developments in tracking salient points using differential image alignment, is well suited for continuous operation, and makes no assumptions about the camera motion or scene rigidity. Experimental validation using both a phantom model and in vivo data shows that the method enables accurate estimation of focal length when the zoom varies, avoiding the need to explicitly recalibrate during surgery. To the best of our knowledge this the first work proposing a practical solution for online zoom calibration in the operation room."},"title":{"value":"Continuous Zoom Calibration by Tracking Salient Points in Endoscopic Video"},"authors":{"value":["Miguel Lourenço","João Pedro Barreto","Fernando Fonseca","Hélder Ferreira","Rui M. Duarte","Jorge Correia-Pinto"]}},"tmdate":1729846805604,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1729846786612,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Joao_Barreto1"],"forum":"wUpU6wyLtp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":160449,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1729846805604,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"wUpU6wyLtp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MICCAI (1) 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-319-10404-1_57.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MICCAI/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"lourenço|continuous_zoom_calibration_by_tracking_salient_points_in_endoscopic_video"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Miguel_Lourenço:","~João_Pedro_Barreto1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fernando_Fonseca:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hélder_Ferreira:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rui_M._Duarte:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jorge_Correia-Pinto:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-10404-1_57"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/miccai/LourencoBFFDC14,\n  author={Miguel Lourenço and João Pedro Barreto and Fernando Fonseca and Hélder Ferreira and Rui M. Duarte and Jorge Correia-Pinto},\n  title={Continuous Zoom Calibration by Tracking Salient Points in Endoscopic Video},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={456-463},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-10404-1_57},\n  booktitle={MICCAI (1)},\n  crossref={conf/miccai/2014-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Many image-based systems for aiding the surgeon during minimally invasive surgery require the endoscopic camera to be calibrated at all times. This article proposes a method for accomplishing this goal whenever the camera has optical zoom and the focal length changes during the procedure. Our solution for online calibration builds on recent developments in tracking salient points using differential image alignment, is well suited for continuous operation, and makes no assumptions about the camera motion or scene rigidity. Experimental validation using both a phantom model and in vivo data shows that the method enables accurate estimation of focal length when the zoom varies, avoiding the need to explicitly recalibrate during surgery. To the best of our knowledge this the first work proposing a practical solution for online zoom calibration in the operation room."},"title":{"value":"Continuous Zoom Calibration by Tracking Salient Points in Endoscopic Video"},"authors":{"value":["Miguel Lourenço","João Pedro Barreto","Fernando Fonseca","Hélder Ferreira","Rui M. Duarte","Jorge Correia-Pinto"]}},"tmdate":1729846799057,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1729846781574,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Joao_Barreto1"],"forum":"rrfxBu1QPz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":160440,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1729846799057,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rrfxBu1QPz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MICCAI (1) 2014"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-319-10404-1_57.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MICCAI/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"lourenço|continuous_zoom_calibration_by_tracking_salient_points_in_endoscopic_video"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Miguel_Lourenço:","~João_Pedro_Barreto1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fernando_Fonseca:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hélder_Ferreira:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rui_M._Duarte:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jorge_Correia-Pinto:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-10404-1_57"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/miccai/LourencoBFFDC14,\n  author={Miguel Lourenço and João Pedro Barreto and Fernando Fonseca and Hélder Ferreira and Rui M. Duarte and Jorge Correia-Pinto},\n  title={Continuous Zoom Calibration by Tracking Salient Points in Endoscopic Video},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={456-463},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-10404-1_57},\n  booktitle={MICCAI (1)},\n  crossref={conf/miccai/2014-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Many image-based systems for aiding the surgeon during minimally invasive surgery require the endoscopic camera to be calibrated at all times. This article proposes a method for accomplishing this goal whenever the camera has optical zoom and the focal length changes during the procedure. Our solution for online calibration builds on recent developments in tracking salient points using differential image alignment, is well suited for continuous operation, and makes no assumptions about the camera motion or scene rigidity. Experimental validation using both a phantom model and in vivo data shows that the method enables accurate estimation of focal length when the zoom varies, avoiding the need to explicitly recalibrate during surgery. To the best of our knowledge this the first work proposing a practical solution for online zoom calibration in the operation room."},"title":{"value":"Continuous Zoom Calibration by Tracking Salient Points in Endoscopic Video"},"authors":{"value":["Miguel Lourenço","João Pedro Barreto","Fernando Fonseca","Hélder Ferreira","Rui M. Duarte","Jorge Correia-Pinto"]}},"tmdate":1729846785562,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1729846770953,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Joao_Barreto1"],"forum":"ODI7FujzQR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":160414,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1729846785562,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ODI7FujzQR","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"The goal of the paper is to explore the feasibility of finding algorithm implementations from code"},"venue":{"value":"DL4C @ ICLR 2025"},"keywords":{"value":["Code Search","Self-Supervised Learning"]},"abstract":{"value":"Identifying algorithm implementations in source code is crucial for code comprehension, reference retrieval, and program synthesis. This paper presents PC2SC, a novel framework for mapping pseudo-code to source code without manual annotations. We introduce p-language, a structured representation that encodes control flow, mathematical expressions, and natural language descriptions of algorithms. A static analyzer extracts these features, converting pseudo-code into p-code, then embedded into a shared vector space with source code using self-supervised learning for retrieval.\nGiven pseudo-code as input, PC2SC returns a ranked list of matching code snippets. Evaluations on the Stony Brook Algorithm Repository and GitHub projects demonstrate that PC2SC outperforms state-of-the-art code search tools in both C and Java. It successfully retrieves correct implementations within the top 25, 10, and 1 ranked results for 98.5\\%, 93.8\\%, and 66.2\\% of queries, respectively. In GitHub projects, it identified 74 algorithm implementations out of 87 queries.\nPC2SC bridges the gap between algorithmic descriptions and executable implementations, offering a scalable, language-independent solution for algorithm retrieval and paving the way for future advancements in cross-language code search and automated synthesis."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nkulkarni2025from,\ntitle={From Pseudo-Code to Source Code: A Self-Supervised Search Approach},\nauthor={Adithya Kulkarni and Mohna Chakraborty and Yonas Afewerki Sium and Sai Charishma Valluri and Wei Le and Qi Li},\nbooktitle={ICLR 2025 Third Workshop on Deep Learning for Code},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=s0et2A33uM}\n}"},"title":{"value":"From Pseudo-Code to Source Code: A Self-Supervised Search Approach"},"Anonymization":{"value":"This submission has been anonymized for double-blind review via the removal of identifying information such as names, affiliations, and identifying URLs."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f66040cae5688f41e985df30c7df9d92b7fa62fe.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C"},"paperhash":{"value":"kulkarni|from_pseudocode_to_source_code_a_selfsupervised_search_approach"},"authorids":{"value":["~Adithya_Kulkarni1","~Mohna_Chakraborty1","~Yonas_Afewerki_Sium1","svalluri@iastate.edu","~Wei_Le1","~Qi_Li14"]},"Track":{"value":"long paper (up to 9 pages)"},"authors":{"value":["Adithya Kulkarni","Mohna Chakraborty","Yonas Afewerki Sium","Sai Charishma Valluri","Wei Le","Qi Li"]}},"tmdate":1745072952302,"pdate":1741234621082,"tcdate":1739440822398,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission57/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission57/Authors"],"forum":"s0et2A33uM","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":57,"cdate":1739440822398,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C/Submission57/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1745072952302,"odate":1741234621082,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/DL4C","id":"s0et2A33uM","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce Action-Conditioned Feedback Distribution (ACFD) drift and propose CRC, which adapts frozen RL-based traffic signal controllers to evolving test-time traffic dynamics through credible residual calibration using test-time feedback."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Traffic Signal Control","Test-Time Adaptation","Credible Calibration","Intelligent Transportation"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/ac7956aeb831c16606c5b160ec752a8d8792a855.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"applications to robotics, autonomy, planning"},"abstract":{"value":"Traffic signal control (TSC) plays a critical role in improving urban traffic efficiency. Although reinforcement learning (RL) has achieved remarkable progress in TSC, RL-based controllers are typically trained under specific traffic conditions and remain frozen during testing. In practice, environmental feedback under dynamic traffic conditions may change after a signal action is applied, leading to performance degradation. Existing solutions mainly rely on adaptation capabilities acquired before testing and remain limited in exploiting feedback changes that emerge at test time. In this paper, we introduce Action-Conditioned Feedback Distribution (ACFD) drift to characterize changes in the feedback distribution over next states and rewards conditioned on the current state and action, and propose Credible Residual Calibration CRC), a plug-in test-time adaptation framework for TSC. CRC freezes the pretrained base controller and attaches a residual calibrator to its outputs, enabling adaptation through test-time feedback. CRC uses the test-time Bellman inconsistency of the frozen base controller as an observable value-relevant signal of ACFD drift and maintains calibration-state statistics from stepwise feedback. Based on the calibration state, the residual calibrator learns action-wise candidate residual corrections, while action-wise credibility controls their release for decision making. CRC further uses calibration-state-guided action routing to adjust the decision mode and dynamic replay to update the residual calibrator from informative test-time transitions. Extensive experiments on real-world datasets and multiple base TSC controllers demonstrate that CRC consistently improves test-time control performance."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026crc,\ntitle={{CRC}: Credible Residual Calibration for Traffic Signal Control under Test-Time Drift},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=a8mbI21iTz},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CRC: Credible Residual Calibration for Traffic Signal Control under Test-Time Drift"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a1fa20f931bae8c8a950a4a0a5b2ff0596750a7b.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791232111560,"tcdate":1789702267972,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission36520/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission36520/Authors"],"forum":"a8mbI21iTz","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":36520,"cdate":1789702267972,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission36520/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791232111560,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"a8mbI21iTz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Post-Training Quantization","Robustness"]},"primary_area":{"value":"unsupervised, self-supervised, semi-supervised, and supervised representation learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Post-training quantization (PTQ) enables efficient deployment of pretrained models using only a small calibration set, but its robustness under distribution shifts remains underexplored.\nWe observe that quantized models retain substantially less of their full-precision performance on corrupted inputs than on clean data, particularly under low-bit quantization.\nWe study this problem from the perspective of calibration data and show that different transformations of the same fixed-budget calibration set lead to markedly different corruption robustness.\nOur analysis reveals that effective transformations tend to reduce activation energy, exhibiting a characteristic energy shift similar to that observed under common corruptions.\nWe associate this reduction with two changes in self-attention: lower attention concentration and weaker directional alignment among value vectors associated with different tokens.\nMotivated by these observations, we introduce *spatial entropy of difference energy*, a model-independent score that measures how broadly a transformation distributes its changes across an image.\nThis score strongly correlates with both activation-energy reduction and post-quantization corruption robustness, enabling transformation selection without model inference.\nBased on this proxy, we propose **Entropy-$K$**, which selects multiple high-scoring transformations while preserving the original calibration budget.\nEntropy-$K$ requires neither corrupted target-domain samples nor modification of the underlying PTQ algorithm.\nExperiments across multiple Vision Transformer architectures and low-bit PTQ settings on ImageNet and ImageNet-C demonstrate consistent improvements in corruption robustness while maintaining competitive clean accuracy."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026corruption,\ntitle={Corruption Robustness in Post-Training Quantization of Vision Transformers: A Calibration Perspective},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Tb8ZKaLFR9},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Corruption Robustness in Post-Training Quantization of Vision Transformers: A Calibration Perspective"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/49aa283c46db3b4e1380340fb6a53f7a909efedf.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791220153715,"tcdate":1787644588211,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission4403/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission4403/Authors"],"forum":"Tb8ZKaLFR9","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4403,"cdate":1787644588211,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission4403/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791220153715,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"Tb8ZKaLFR9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Medical Imaging 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/42/10336247/10189221.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TMI/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"shi|progressive_pretraining_network_for_3d_system_matrix_calibration_in_magnetic_particle_imaging"},"authorids":{"value":["~Gen_Shi2","~Lin_Yin1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu_An:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guanghui_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liwen_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhongwei_Bian:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ziwei_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haoran_Zhang:","~Hui_Hui2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Tian_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TMI.2023.3297173"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tmi/ShiYALZBCZHT23,\n  author={Gen Shi and Lin Yin and Yu An and Guanghui Li and Liwen Zhang and Zhongwei Bian and Ziwei Chen and Haoran Zhang and Hui Hui and Jie Tian},\n  title={Progressive Pretraining Network for 3D System Matrix Calibration in Magnetic Particle Imaging},\n  year={2023},\n  month={December},\n  cdate={1701388800000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Medical Imaging},\n  volume={42},\n  number={12},\n  pages={3639-3650},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TMI.2023.3297173}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Magnetic particle imaging (MPI) is an emerging technique for determining magnetic nanoparticle distributions in biological tissues. Although system-matrix (SM)-based image reconstruction offers higher image quality than the X-space-based approach, the SM calibration measurement is time-consuming. Additionally, the SM should be recalibrated if the tracer’s characteristics or the magnetic field environment change, and repeated SM measurement further increase the required labor and time. Therefore, fast SM calibration is essential for MPI. Existing calibration methods commonly treat each row of the SM as independent of the others, but the rows are inherently related through the coil channel and frequency index. As these two elements can be regarded as additional multimodal information, we leverage the transformer architecture with a self-attention mechanism to encode them. Although the transformer has shown superiority in multimodal fusion learning across several fields, its high complexity may lead to overfitting when labeled data are scarce. Compared with labeled SM (i.e., full size), low-resolution SM data can be easily obtained, and fully using such data may alleviate overfitting. Accordingly, we propose a pseudo-label-based progressive pretraining strategy to leverage unlabeled data. Our method outperforms existing calibration methods on a public real-world OpenMPI dataset and simulation dataset. Moreover, our method improves the resolution of two in-house MPI scanners without requiring full-size SM measurements. Ablation studies confirm the contributions of modeling SM inter-row relations and the proposed pretraining strategy."},"title":{"value":"Progressive Pretraining Network for 3D System Matrix Calibration in Magnetic Particle Imaging"},"authors":{"value":["Gen Shi","Lin Yin","Yu An","Guanghui Li","Liwen Zhang","Zhongwei Bian","Ziwei Chen","Haoran Zhang","Hui Hui","Jie Tian"]}},"tmdate":1784550711346,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1729951348125,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~gen_Shi1"],"forum":"bqfym64jTx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":161112,"cdate":1701388800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784550711346,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bqfym64jTx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Image Vis. Comput. 2018"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IVC/2018"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|calibration_free_userindependent_gaze_estimation_with_tensor_analysis"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nanxiang_Li:","~Carlos_Busso1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.imavis.2018.04.001"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ivc/LiB18,\n  author={Nanxiang Li and Carlos Busso},\n  title={Calibration free, user-independent gaze estimation with tensor analysis},\n  year={2018},\n  cdate={1514764800000},\n  journal={Image Vis. Comput.},\n  volume={74},\n  pages={10-20},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.imavis.2018.04.001}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•Tensor analysis framework for user-independent gaze detection without calibration•Decomposes high dimension gaze data into factors selected by LASSO regression•Evaluation includes individual, gaze, user-screen distances and session differences.•Reduce performance gap between user-dependent and user-independent conditions•Effective solution for gaze-aware multimodal interfaces"},"title":{"value":"Calibration free, user-independent gaze estimation with tensor analysis"},"authors":{"value":["Nanxiang Li","Carlos Busso"]}},"tmdate":1747060586005,"pdate":1514764800000,"tcdate":1747060549017,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Carlos_Busso1"],"forum":"pTOGoAIsoq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":418193,"cdate":1514764800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747060586005,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"pTOGoAIsoq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2025"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Code Generation","Preference Optimization"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/e2f536e5a1526a56f3255f6fddb2261aedc3f763.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"generative models"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Code generation models have shown significant potential for programming tasks. \nHowever, existing training methods like supervised fine-tuning face key limitations: they do not effectively teach models to prioritize correct over incorrect solutions in ambiguous situations, nor do they effectively optimize the runtime efficiency of the generated code. \nTo address these challenges, we propose CodeDPO, a framework that integrates preference learning into code generation to improve two key code preference factors: code correctness and efficiency.\nCodeDPO employs a novel dataset construction method, utilizing a self-generation-and-validation mechanism that simultaneously generates and evaluates code and test cases. \nThe underlying assumption is that test cases executable by multiple code snippets provide more reliable validation, and code that passes more tests is more likely to be correct. \nThrough this self-validation process, our PageRank-inspired algorithm iteratively updates the ranking score of each code snippet, ultimately creating a code preference optimization dataset based on correctness and efficiency.\nCodeDPO is flexible and scalable, generating diverse preference optimization data without depending on external resources. \nThrough comprehensive evaluations of five widely used benchmarks, CodeDPO demonstrates significant improvements in correctness and efficiency compared to existing methods. \nOur experiments prove that CodeDPO enhances the capabilities of LLMs in code generation and provides a robust foundation for conducting code preference optimization in more complex and challenging real-world scenarios.\nCode and additional details are available: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/CodeDPO."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nzhang2025codedpo,\ntitle={Code{DPO}: Aligning Code Models with Self Generated and Verified Source Code},\nauthor={Kechi Zhang and Ge Li and Yihong Dong and Jingjing Xu and Jun Zhang and Jing Su and Yongfei Liu and Zhi Jin},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=U5TebOVpfd}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CodeDPO: Aligning Code Models with Self Generated and Verified Source Code"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f36f4d2cebcf0c069022883fa97ea76edbc66b18.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|codedpo_aligning_code_models_with_self_generated_and_verified_source_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kechi_Zhang1","~Ge_Li4","~Yihong_Dong1","~Jingjing_Xu1","~Jun_Zhang27","~Jing_Su2","~Yongfei_Liu1","~Zhi_Jin1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Kechi Zhang","Ge Li","Yihong Dong","Jingjing Xu","Jun Zhang","Jing Su","Yongfei Liu","Zhi Jin"]}},"tmdate":1738735627709,"tcdate":1726475373011,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission1066/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission1066/Authors"],"forum":"U5TebOVpfd","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1066,"cdate":1726475373011,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission1066/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission1066/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738735627709,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"U5TebOVpfd","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"multilingual, confidence estimation, calibration"},"keywords":{"value":["multilingual","confidence estimation","calibration"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"Multilingual"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"N/A"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"Yes"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3.1, 4.1, Appendix A.1.3, E.1"},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"N/A"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_elaboration":{"value":"Ethics Statement Section"},"B5_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3.1 and Appendix A.1.3, A.2, A.3"},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"No"},"A2_elaboration":{"value":"Ethics Statement Section"},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3.2, 4.2, Appendix A.2, A.3"},"B2_elaboration":{"value":"Ethics Statement Section"},"B3_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3.1 and Ethics Statement Section"},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3.1, 4.1"},"C4_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix C.1"},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"Yes"},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3.1, Appendix A.1.2, E.1"},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3.1, 5, Appendix A.1.2"},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"Ethics Statement Section"},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"N/A"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026beyond,\ntitle={Beyond the Final Layer: Intermediate Representations for Better Multilingual Calibration in Large Language Models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=T711933uQE},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Beyond the Final Layer: Intermediate Representations for Better Multilingual Calibration in Large Language Models"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"Yes"},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"Yes"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"Yes"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"Confidence calibration, the alignment of a model's predicted confidence with its actual accuracy, is critical for the reliable deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs), yet remains under-explored in multilingual contexts. We conduct the first large-scale systematic study of multilingual calibration across eight model families and over 100 languages, finding that non-English languages are worse-calibrated than English (e.g., LLaMA3's average Expected Calibration Error is 23.1% on non-English against 4.6% on English). To diagnose this, we investigate the model's internal representations and find that the final layer, biased by English-centric training, provides a poor signal for multilingual confidence, whereas late-intermediate layers offer a better-calibrated signal. Building on this, we introduce a suite of training-free methods, including Language-Aware Confidence Ensemble (LACE), which adaptively selects an ensemble of layers for each language. Both the multilingual calibration gap and the late-intermediate peak persist beyond MCQA, in open-ended QA, chain-of-thought reasoning, and alternative MCQA scoring protocols. Our study highlights the costs of English-centric alignment and offers a path toward more globally equitable LLMs."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"N/A"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/72af8eff8c015dfea7bea2c52d0c04b135af72ef.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Multilingualism and Cross-Lingual NLP"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"no"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"N/A"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1790696968409,"tcdate":1779715386645,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission8058/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission8058/Authors"],"forum":"T711933uQE","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":8058,"cdate":1779715386645,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1790696968409,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"T711933uQE","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"On 15 frozen-feature settings (CLIP, DINOv2, CNNs), calibration-aware linear probing reveals a clean representation-family split: the best training-time objective depends on the backbone, and no single loss wins universally."},"venue":{"value":"CTB@ICML 2026"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["calibration","linear probing","foundation models","expected calibration error","temperature scaling","training-time calibration objectives","CLIP","DINOv2"]},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration objectives for deep classifiers have historically been designed under end-to-end training. Foundation models, however, are increasingly used through frozen-feature adaptation, and full fine-tuning to recalibrate is often infeasible. Post-hoc temperature scaling is cheap but limited to a scalar transform. We ask whether calibration-aware linear probing—relearning only the head under a calibration objective—can occupy the middle ground. Across 15 dataset–model settings spanning CLIP, DINOv2, same-domain CNNs, and cross-domain CNN transfer, the answer is a clean representation-family split rather than a universal winning loss. CLIP gains, when present, come from a direct confidence–accuracy penalty. DINOv2 leaves little reliable headroom beyond temperature scaling. Same-domain CNNs favor confidence- and margin-sensitive reweighting, including a new diagnostic V-family introduced here. Calibration-aware probing therefore serves both as a lightweight recalibration tool and as a diagnostic that exposes how frozen representations encode confidence. Objective choice is part of evaluating uncertainty on frozen foundation-model features, not a minor implementation detail."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nchoi2026choosing,\ntitle={Choosing Training-Time Calibration Objectives for Frozen Foundation-Model Features: A Linear-Probing Benchmark},\nauthor={Heejin Choi},\nbooktitle={ICML 2026 Workshop on Combining Theory and Benchmarks: Towards A Virtuous Cycle to Understand and Guarantee Foundation Model Performance},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=OXYRHjtEG3}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Choosing Training-Time Calibration Objectives for Frozen Foundation-Model Features: A Linear-Probing Benchmark"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/df6dcef87b641f916d0ba96f5844fbc064c7d6d8.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB"},"paperhash":{"value":"choi|choosing_trainingtime_calibration_objectives_for_frozen_foundationmodel_features_a_linearprobing_benchmark"},"authorids":{"value":["~Heejin_Choi1"]},"authors":{"value":["Heejin Choi"]}},"tmdate":1781619217623,"pdate":1779714420438,"tcdate":1778310085539,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/Submission143/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/Submission143/Authors"],"forum":"OXYRHjtEG3","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":143,"cdate":1778310085539,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Submission_Release","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/Submission143/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB/-/Edit"],"mdate":1781619217623,"odate":1779714420438,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/CTB","id":"OXYRHjtEG3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IJCAI2025 workshop Causal Learning for Recommendation Systems"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/89150dd76646c2c80d299fa489c70f08d76c71c1.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Debiased recommendation","propensity learning","calibration","balancing"]},"venueid":{"value":"ijcai.org/IJCAI/2025/Workshop/Causal_Learning_RecSys"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|cbpl_a_unified_calibration_and_balancing_propensity_learning_framework_in_causal_recommendation_for_debiasing"},"authorids":{"value":["~Shufeng_Zhang2","~Tianyu_Xia3"]},"abstract":{"value":"In recommender systems, observed data always suffer from Missing-Not-At-Random (MNAR) issue: users rate only a non-random subset of items, leading to a biased recommendation if the model is trained on such biased data directly. One type of popular debiasing method is to learn an accurate propensity score (the probability a rating is observed) and then reweight the observed sample to achieve unbiased rating prediction. While calibration metric and balancing metric are widely adopted as constraints to learn a high-quality propensity model, existing methods optimize these objectives in an isolated manner, neglecting their inherent connections. To bridge this gap, we first decompose the balancing constraint, making the balancing loss and the calibration loss have a similar form. Then we propose a unified Calibration and Balancing Propensity Learning (CBPL) framework that minimizes calibration loss and balancing loss simultaneously. In addition, we provide a theoretical analysis showing that our method has a variance reduction property. Experimental results on three real-world recommendation datasets demonstrate that our method can outperform the state-of-the-art baselines."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhang2025cbpl,\ntitle={{CBPL}: A Unified Calibration and Balancing Propensity Learning Framework in Causal Recommendation for Debiasing},\nauthor={Shufeng Zhang and Tianyu Xia},\nbooktitle={IJCAI2025 workshop Causal Learning for Recommendation Systems},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=IO57cbGb6R}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CBPL: A Unified Calibration and Balancing Propensity Learning Framework in Causal Recommendation for Debiasing"},"authors":{"value":["Shufeng Zhang","Tianyu Xia"]}},"tmdate":1755564710258,"pdate":1750526120701,"tcdate":1748370848376,"writers":["ijcai.org/IJCAI/2025/Workshop/Causal_Learning_RecSys","ijcai.org/IJCAI/2025/Workshop/Causal_Learning_RecSys/Submission17/Authors"],"signatures":["ijcai.org/IJCAI/2025/Workshop/Causal_Learning_RecSys/Submission17/Authors"],"forum":"IO57cbGb6R","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":17,"cdate":1748370848376,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ijcai.org/IJCAI/2025/Workshop/Causal_Learning_RecSys/-/Submission","ijcai.org/IJCAI/2025/Workshop/Causal_Learning_RecSys/-/Post_Submission","ijcai.org/IJCAI/2025/Workshop/Causal_Learning_RecSys/-/Edit","ijcai.org/IJCAI/2025/Workshop/Causal_Learning_RecSys/Submission17/-/Camera-Ready_Revisions"],"mdate":1755564710258,"odate":1755564710215,"domain":"ijcai.org/IJCAI/2025/Workshop/Causal_Learning_RecSys","id":"IO57cbGb6R","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.19804v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|compliancetocode_enhancing_financial_compliance_checking_via_code_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Siyuan_Li:","~Jian_Chen18","~Rui_Yao1","~Xuming_Hu1","~Peilin_Zhou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weihua_Qiu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Simin_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chucheng_Dong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhiyao_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qipeng_Xie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zixuan_Yuan:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.19804"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-19804,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Siyuan Li and Jian Chen and Rui Yao and Xuming Hu and Peilin Zhou and Weihua Qiu and Simin Zhang and Chucheng Dong and Zhiyao Li and Qipeng Xie and Zixuan Yuan},\n  title={Compliance-to-Code: Enhancing Financial Compliance Checking via Code Generation},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2505.19804},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.19804}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Nowadays, regulatory compliance has become a cornerstone of corporate governance, ensuring adherence to systematic legal frameworks. At its core, financial regulations often comprise highly intricate provisions, layered logical structures, and numerous exceptions, which inevitably result in labor-intensive or comprehension challenges. To mitigate this, recent Regulatory Technology (RegTech) and Large Language Models (LLMs) have gained significant attention in automating the conversion of regulatory text into executable compliance logic. However, their performance remains suboptimal particularly when applied to Chinese-language financial regulations, due to three key limitations: (1) incomplete domain-specific knowledge representation, (2) insufficient hierarchical reasoning capabilities, and (3) failure to maintain temporal and logical coherence. One promising solution is to develop a domain specific and code-oriented datasets for model training. Existing datasets such as LexGLUE, LegalBench, and CODE-ACCORD are often English-focused, domain-mismatched, or lack fine-grained granularity for compliance code generation. To fill these gaps, we present Compliance-to-Code, the first large-scale Chinese dataset dedicated to financial regulatory compliance. Covering 1,159 annotated clauses from 361 regulations across ten categories, each clause is modularly structured with four logical elements-subject, condition, constraint, and contextual information-along with regulation relations. We provide deterministic Python code mappings, detailed code reasoning, and code explanations to facilitate automated auditing. To demonstrate utility, we present FinCheck: a pipeline for regulation structuring, code generation, and report generation."},"title":{"value":"Compliance-to-Code: Enhancing Financial Compliance Checking via Code Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Siyuan Li","Jian Chen","Rui Yao","Xuming Hu","Peilin Zhou","Weihua Qiu","Simin Zhang","Chucheng Dong","Zhiyao Li","Qipeng Xie","Zixuan Yuan"]}},"tmdate":1779718350151,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2505-19804"],"tcdate":1754008648835,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rui_Yao1"],"forum":"r4byMpUw5x","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":606698,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1779718350151,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"r4byMpUw5x","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.12596v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|refining_chatgptgenerated_code_characterizing_and_mitigating_code_quality_issues"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yue_Liu17","~Thanh_Le-Cong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ratnadira_Widyasari:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chakkrit_Tantithamthavorn:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Li_Li_0029:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuan-Bach_Dinh_Le_0001:","~David_Lo1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.12596"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2307-12596,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yue Liu and Thanh Le-Cong and Ratnadira Widyasari and Chakkrit Tantithamthavorn and Li Li and Xuan-Bach Dinh Le and David Lo},\n  title={Refining ChatGPT-Generated Code: Characterizing and Mitigating Code Quality Issues},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2307.12596},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2307.12596}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We systematically study the quality of 4,066 ChatGPT-generated code implemented in two popular programming languages, i.e., Java and Python, for 2,033 programming tasks. The goal of this work is three folds. First, we analyze the correctness of ChatGPT on code generation tasks and uncover the factors that influence its effectiveness, including task difficulty, programming language, time that tasks are introduced, and program size. Second, we identify and characterize potential issues with the quality of ChatGPT-generated code. Last, we provide insights into how these issues can be mitigated. Experiments highlight that out of 4,066 programs generated by ChatGPT, 2,756 programs are deemed correct, 1,082 programs provide wrong outputs, and 177 programs contain compilation or runtime errors. Additionally, we further analyze other characteristics of the generated code through static analysis tools, such as code style and maintainability, and find that 1,930 ChatGPT-generated code snippets suffer from maintainability issues. Subsequently, we investigate ChatGPT's self-repairing ability and its interaction with static analysis tools to fix the errors uncovered in the previous step. Experiments suggest that ChatGPT can partially address these challenges, improving code quality by more than 20%, but there are still limitations and opportunities for improvement. Overall, our study provides valuable insights into the current limitations of ChatGPT and offers a roadmap for future research and development efforts to enhance the code generation capabilities of AI models like ChatGPT."},"title":{"value":"Refining ChatGPT-Generated Code: Characterizing and Mitigating Code Quality Issues"},"authors":{"value":["Yue Liu","Thanh Le-Cong","Ratnadira Widyasari","Chakkrit Tantithamthavorn","Li Li","Xuan-Bach Dinh Le","David Lo"]}},"tmdate":1744863850567,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1734956621414,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yue_Liu17"],"forum":"tmeW3lR7rk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":259211,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1744863850567,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"tmeW3lR7rk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ECAI 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"noah|codecloak_a_method_for_mitigating_code_leakage_by_llm_code_assistants"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3233/FAIA251340"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ecai/NoahSBMMES25,\n  author={Amit Finkman Noah and Avishag Shapira and Eden Bar-Kochva and Inbar Maimon and Dudu Mimran and Yuval Elovici and Asaf Shabtai},\n  title={CodeCloak: A Method for Mitigating Code Leakage by LLM Code Assistants},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={4418-4427},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3233/FAIA251340},\n  booktitle={ECAI},\n  crossref={conf/ecai/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language model (LLM)-based code assistants are increasingly popular among developers. These tools help improve developers’ coding efficiency and reduce errors by providing real-time suggestions based on the developer’s codebase. While beneficial, the use of these tools can inadvertently expose the developer’s proprietary code to the code assistant service provider during the development process. In this work, we propose a method aimed at mitigating the risk of code leakage when using LLM-based code assistants. CodeCloak is a novel, real-time, deep reinforcement learning agent that manipulates the prompts before sending them to the code assistant model. CodeCloak aims to achieve the following two contradictory objectives: (i) minimizing code leakage, while (ii) preserving relevant and useful suggestions for the developer. Our evaluation performed on multiple code assistant models, demonstrates CodeCloak’s effectiveness on a diverse set of code repositories of varying sizes, as well as its transferability across different models. We validate our approach through human judgment of suggestion quality and testing on complete repositories simulating real development scenarios.The source code is available at: https://github.com/AmitFinkman/CodeCloak"},"title":{"value":"CodeCloak: A Method for Mitigating Code Leakage by LLM Code Assistants"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Amit Finkman Noah","username":""},{"fullname":"Avishag Shapira","username":""},{"fullname":"Eden Bar-Kochva","username":""},{"fullname":"Inbar Maimon","username":"~Inbar_Maimon1"},{"fullname":"Dudu Mimran","username":""},{"fullname":"Yuval Elovici","username":""},{"fullname":"Asaf Shabtai","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1779688493205,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/ecai/NoahSBMMES25"],"tcdate":1779688490392,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Inbar_Maimon1"],"forum":"Yfy7GIlj7H","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":21258,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1779688493205,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"Yfy7GIlj7H","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Spiking neural networks","ANN-to-SNN conversion","post-training calibration","low-latency inference","shared calibration policies","spike-budget optimization"]},"primary_area":{"value":"unsupervised, self-supervised, semi-supervised, and supervised representation learning"},"abstract":{"value":"Converting pretrained artificial neural networks (ANNs) into spiking neural networks (SNNs) aims to reuse learned representations while addressing severe clipping, quantization, and temporal mismatch errors at short simulation steps. To mitigate these errors, existing calibration methods commonly reconstruct ANN activations through layer-wise parameter adjustment. Consequently, these methods fail to preserve network-wide feature alignment across heterogeneous stages and varying inference budgets. In this paper, we find that isolated parameter fitting inherently overlooks the complex interactions of temporal conversion errors. Effective calibration cannot treat neuron operating parameters as independent variables. Instead, it must govern them as a globally coordinated system anchored by shared network statistics. In light of this, we propose STAGECAL, a novel shared neuron calibration framework for plug-and-play ANN-to-SNN conversion that keeps pretrained ANN backbone weights strictly fixed. Our framework brings two key technical contributions. Firstly, we introduce a shared calibration network. It utilizes channel-wise activation statistics, normalized stage depth, and target spike budgets to jointly predict threshold adjustments and initial membrane potentials. On top, we then introduce a structured residual correction function. This function dynamically predicts mixing coefficients that combine three fixed residual bases from an initial pilot conversion into bounded stage-wise current corrections. These corrections are theoretically linked through an exact membrane-potential balance identity. Extensive experiments on classification tasks demonstrate that STAGECAL even exceeds the original ANN by 5.71% with only 0.17x energy and few simulation time steps on ImageNet. It also reduces hidden-layer synaptic operations by 4.24% relative to percentile-based conversion. This enables highly efficient SNN inference without running the source ANN and calibration network."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026stagecal,\ntitle={StageCal: Shared Neuron Calibration for {ANN}-to-{SNN} Conversion with Few-Step Inference},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=eSETJYHDId},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"StageCal: Shared Neuron Calibration for ANN-to-SNN Conversion with Few-Step Inference"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/b9bd7676d3261659cba4be8f6e84216ce5e24d38.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791232916948,"tcdate":1789724696610,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission40697/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission40697/Authors"],"forum":"eSETJYHDId","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":40697,"cdate":1789724696610,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission40697/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791232916948,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"eSETJYHDId","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Computational Imaging"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/6745852/10398876/10720339.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"güngör|calibfpa_a_focal_plane_array_imaging_system_based_on_online_deeplearning_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TCI.2024.3477312"},"abstract":{"value":"Compressive focal plane arrays (FPA) enable cost-effective high-resolution (HR) imaging by acquisition of several multiplexed measurements on a low-resolution (LR) sensor. Multiplexed encoding of the visual scene is often attained via electronically controllable spatial light modulators (SLM). To capture system non-idealities such as optical aberrations, a system matrix is measured via additional offline scans, where the system response is recorded for a point source at each spatial location on the imaging grid. An HR image can then be reconstructed by solving an inverse problem that involves encoded measurements and the calibration matrix. However, this offline calibration framework faces limitations due to challenges in encoding single HR grid locations with a fixed coded aperture, lengthy calibration scans repeated to account for system drifts, and computational burden of reconstructions based on dense system matrices. Here, we propose a novel compressive FPA system based on online deep-learning calibration of multiplexed LR measurements (CalibFPA). To acquire multiplexed measurements, we devise an optical setup where a piezo-stage locomotes a pre-printed fixed coded aperture. We introduce a physics-driven deep-learning method to correct for the influences of optical aberrations in multiplexed measurements without the need for offline calibration scans. The corrected measurement matrix is of block-diagonal form, so it can be processed efficiently to recover HR images with a user-preferred reconstruction algorithm including least-squares, plug-and-play, or unrolled techniques. On simulated and experimental datasets, we demonstrate that CalibFPA outperforms state-of-the-art compressive FPA methods. We also report analyses to validate the design elements in CalibFPA and assess computational complexity."},"title":{"value":"CalibFPA: A Focal Plane Array Imaging System Based on Online Deep-Learning Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Alper Güngör"},{"fullname":"M. Umut Bahceci"},{"fullname":"Yasin Ergen"},{"fullname":"Ahmet Sözak"},{"fullname":"O. Oner Ekiz"},{"fullname":"Tolga Yelboga"},{"fullname":"Tolga Çukur","username":"~Tolga_Çukur1"}]}},"tmdate":1789093219761,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tci.2024.3477312"],"tcdate":1762172023641,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Tolga_Cukur1"],"forum":"1kdWmyYMiL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":2575,"cdate":1732824211693,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789093219761,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"1kdWmyYMiL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans Autom. Sci. Eng. 2015"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8856/7079441/06834832.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TASE/2015"},"paperhash":{"value":"wu|a_minimal_poebased_model_for_robotic_kinematic_calibration_with_only_position_measurements"},"authorids":{"value":["~Liao_Wu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiangdong_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ken_Chen_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongliang_Ren_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TASE.2014.2328652"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tase/WuYCR15,\n  author={Liao Wu and Xiangdong Yang and Ken Chen and Hongliang Ren},\n  title={A Minimal POE-Based Model for Robotic Kinematic Calibration With Only Position Measurements},\n  year={2015},\n  cdate={1420070400000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans Autom. Sci. Eng.},\n  volume={12},\n  number={2},\n  pages={758-763},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TASE.2014.2328652}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper proposes an algorithm for robotic kinematic calibration based on a minimal product of exponentials (POE)-based model for the applications where only position measurements are required. Both joint zero-offset errors and initial frame twist error can be involved in this model. Analysis of the identifiability of these errors shows that at most six elements of these parameters can be identified. It also suggests that at least three noncollinear points on the end-effector should be measured to maximize the identifiability. Compared with the traditional POE-based model with full pose (position and orientation) measurements, the minimal model with only position measurements outperforms in terms of convenience, efficiency, and accuracy. Note to Practitioners-Kinematic calibration is pivotal to improve the position accuracy of a robot. To avoid the disadvantages of measuring the orientation of the end-effector during calibration, an algorithm using only position measurements is presented, with which one needs only position measurements of several points fixed on the end-effector without orientation information during the whole calibration process. This will greatly facilitate the scheme design as well as the practical operations. The identifiability of the parameters is then analyzed with two conclusions: 1) at most six elements of the joint zero-offsets and the initial frame twist in total can be identified simultaneously and 2) at least three points on the end-effector which are not collinear should be measured so as to make the identifiability maximum. According to these conclusions, one should carefully select parameters to formulate the error model and measure sufficient points on the end-effector during the calibration procedure."},"title":{"value":"A Minimal POE-Based Model for Robotic Kinematic Calibration With Only Position Measurements"},"authors":{"value":["Liao Wu","Xiangdong Yang","Ken Chen","Hongliang Ren"]}},"tmdate":1747096523280,"pdate":1420070400000,"tcdate":1747096488362,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Liao_Wu1"],"forum":"QcCwDpsMML","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":429326,"cdate":1420070400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747096523280,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"QcCwDpsMML","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Code Generation, Large Language Models, Post training"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2025 February Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025codedpo,\ntitle={Code{DPO}: Aligning Code Models with Self Generated and Verified Source Code},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - February 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=fY7ueMXmBh},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CodeDPO: Aligning Code Models with Self Generated and Verified Source Code"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Publicly available software and/or pre-trained models"]},"abstract":{"value":"Code generation models have shown significant potential for programming tasks. \nHowever, existing training methods like supervised fine-tuning face key limitations: they do not effectively teach models to prioritize correct over incorrect solutions in ambiguous situations, nor do they effectively optimize the runtime efficiency of the generated code. \nTo address these challenges, we propose CodeDPO, a framework that integrates preference learning into code generation to improve two key code preference factors: code correctness and efficiency.\nCodeDPO employs a novel dataset construction method, utilizing a self-generation-and-validation mechanism that simultaneously generates and evaluates code and test cases. \nThe underlying assumption is that test cases executable by multiple code snippets provide more reliable validation, and code that passes more tests is more likely to be correct. \nThrough this self-validation process, our PageRank-inspired algorithm iteratively updates the ranking score of each code snippet, ultimately creating a code preference optimization dataset based on correctness and efficiency.\nCodeDPO is flexible and scalable, generating diverse preference optimization data without depending on powerful models such as GPT-4. \nThrough comprehensive evaluations of five widely used benchmarks, CodeDPO demonstrates significant improvements in correctness and efficiency compared to existing methods. \nOur experiments prove that CodeDPO enhances the capabilities of LLMs in code generation and provides a robust foundation for conducting code preference optimization in more complex and challenging real-world scenarios."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7c49c7d68662f0745988779a03b2973e2a64eb7a.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"NLP Applications"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782961910341,"tcdate":1738468054540,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission51/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission51/Authors"],"forum":"fY7ueMXmBh","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":51,"cdate":1738468054540,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission51/-/Change_Reviewer_Nomination","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/Submission51/-/Blind_Submission_License_Agreement","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Desk_Reject_Verification_Flag","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782961910341,"odate":1746821355472,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/February","id":"fY7ueMXmBh","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.771.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|codedpo_aligning_code_models_with_self_generated_and_verified_source_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kechi_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ge_Li_0001:","~Yihong_Dong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jingjing_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jing_Su:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yongfei_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhi_Jin_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.771/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/Zhang0DXZSLJ25,\n  author={Kechi Zhang and Ge Li and Yihong Dong and Jingjing Xu and Jun Zhang and Jing Su and Yongfei Liu and Zhi Jin},\n  title={CodeDPO: Aligning Code Models with Self Generated and Verified Source Code},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={15854-15871},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.771/},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2025-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code generation models have shown significant potential for programming tasks. However, existing training methods like supervised fine-tuning face key limitations: they do not effectively teach models to prioritize correct over incorrect solutions in ambiguous situations, nor do they effectively optimize the runtime efficiency of the generated code. To address these challenges, we propose CodeDPO, a framework that integrates preference learning into code generation to improve two key code preference factors: code correctness and efficiency. CodeDPO employs a novel dataset construction method, utilizing a self-generation-and-validation mechanism that simultaneously generates and evaluates code and test cases. The underlying assumption is that test cases executable by multiple code snippets provide more reliable validation, and code that passes more tests is more likely to be correct. Through this self-validation process, our PageRank-inspired algorithm iteratively updates the ranking score of each code snippet, ultimately creating a code preference optimization dataset based on correctness and efficiency. CodeDPO is flexible and scalable, generating diverse preference optimization data without depending on powerful models such as GPT-4. Through comprehensive evaluations of five widely used benchmarks, CodeDPO demonstrates significant improvements in correctness and efficiency compared to existing methods. Our experiments prove that CodeDPO enhances the capabilities of LLMs in code generation and provides a robust foundation for conducting code preference optimization in more complex and challenging real-world scenarios."},"title":{"value":"CodeDPO: Aligning Code Models with Self Generated and Verified Source Code"},"authors":{"value":["Kechi Zhang","Ge Li","Yihong Dong","Jingjing Xu","Jun Zhang","Jing Su","Yongfei Liu","Zhi Jin"]}},"tmdate":1760537801013,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/Zhang0DXZSLJ25"],"tcdate":1760537791736,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yihong_Dong1"],"forum":"XwUteLQvNH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":645025,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1760537801013,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"XwUteLQvNH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.05605v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|codedpo_aligning_code_models_with_self_generated_and_verified_source_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kechi_Zhang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ge_Li_0001:","~Yihong_Dong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jingjing_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Zhang:","~Jing_Su2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yongfei_Liu:","~Zhi_Jin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.05605"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2410-05605,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Kechi Zhang and Ge Li and Yihong Dong and Jingjing Xu and Jun Zhang and Jing Su and Yongfei Liu and Zhi Jin},\n  title={CodeDPO: Aligning Code Models with Self Generated and Verified Source Code},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2410.05605},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2410.05605}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code generation models have shown significant potential for programming tasks. However, existing training methods like supervised fine-tuning face key limitations: they do not effectively teach models to prioritize correct over incorrect solutions in ambiguous situations, nor do they effectively optimize the runtime efficiency of the generated code. To address these challenges, we propose CodeDPO, a framework that integrates preference learning into code generation to improve two key code preference factors: code correctness and efficiency. CodeDPO employs a novel dataset construction method, utilizing a self-generation-and-validation mechanism that simultaneously generates and evaluates code and test cases. The underlying assumption is that test cases executable by multiple code snippets provide more reliable validation, and code that passes more tests is more likely to be correct. Through this self-validation process, our PageRank-inspired algorithm iteratively updates the ranking score of each code snippet, ultimately creating a code preference optimization dataset based on correctness and efficiency. CodeDPO is flexible and scalable, generating diverse preference optimization data without depending on external resources. Through comprehensive evaluations of five widely used benchmarks, CodeDPO demonstrates significant improvements in correctness and efficiency compared to existing methods. Our experiments prove that CodeDPO enhances the capabilities of LLMs in code generation and provides a robust foundation for conducting code preference optimization in more complex and challenging real-world scenarios."},"title":{"value":"CodeDPO: Aligning Code Models with Self Generated and Verified Source Code"},"authors":{"value":["Kechi Zhang","Ge Li","Yihong Dong","Jingjing Xu","Jun Zhang","Jing Su","Yongfei Liu","Zhi Jin"]}},"tmdate":1760537792300,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1739949780765,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jing_Su2"],"forum":"b9NbZB8XX5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":329064,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1760537792300,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"b9NbZB8XX5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"J. Intell. Manuf. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10845-023-02098-0.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/JIM/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"rozanec|active_learning_and_novel_model_calibration_measurements_for_automated_visual_inspection_in_manufacturing"},"authorids":{"value":["~Joze_M._Rozanec1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luka_Bizjak:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Elena_Trajkova:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Patrik_Zajec:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jelle_Keizer:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Blaz_Fortuna:","~Dunja_Mladenic2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10845-023-02098-0"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/jim/RozanecBTZKFM24,\n  author={Joze M. Rozanec and Luka Bizjak and Elena Trajkova and Patrik Zajec and Jelle Keizer and Blaz Fortuna and Dunja Mladenic},\n  title={Active learning and novel model calibration measurements for automated visual inspection in manufacturing},\n  year={2024},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1717200000000},\n  journal={J. Intell. Manuf.},\n  volume={35},\n  number={5},\n  pages={1963-1984},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s10845-023-02098-0}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Quality control is a crucial activity performed by manufacturing enterprises to ensure that their products meet quality standards and avoid potential damage to the brand’s reputation. The decreased cost of sensors and connectivity enabled increasing digitalization of manufacturing. In addition, artificial intelligence enables higher degrees of automation, reducing overall costs and time required for defect inspection. This research compares three active learning approaches, having single and multiple oracles, to visual inspection. Six new metrics are proposed to assess the quality of calibration without the need for ground truth. Furthermore, this research explores whether existing calibrators can improve performance by leveraging an approximate ground truth to enlarge the calibration set. The experiments were performed on real-world data provided by Philips Consumer Lifestyle BV. Our results show that the explored active learning settings can reduce the data labeling effort by between three and four percent without detriment to the overall quality goals, considering a threshold of p = 0.95. Furthermore, the results show that the proposed calibration metrics successfully capture relevant information otherwise available to metrics used up to date only through ground truth data. Therefore, the proposed metrics can be used to estimate the quality of models’ probability calibration without committing to a labeling effort to obtain ground truth data."},"title":{"value":"Active learning and novel model calibration measurements for automated visual inspection in manufacturing"},"authors":{"value":["Joze M. Rozanec","Luka Bizjak","Elena Trajkova","Patrik Zajec","Jelle Keizer","Blaz Fortuna","Dunja Mladenic"]}},"tmdate":1783158775437,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/jim/RozanecBTZKFM24"],"tcdate":1771880314018,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dunja_Mladenic2"],"forum":"KUnYimI0vi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":826864,"cdate":1717200000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1783158775437,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"KUnYimI0vi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.11392v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"allegro|multicamera_handeye_calibration_for_humanrobot_collaboration_in_industrial_robotic_workcells"},"authorids":{"value":["~Davide_Allegro1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Matteo_Terreran:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Stefano_Ghidoni:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2406.11392"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2406-11392,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Davide Allegro and Matteo Terreran and Stefano Ghidoni},\n  title={Multi-Camera Hand-Eye Calibration for Human-Robot Collaboration in Industrial Robotic Workcells},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2406.11392},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2406.11392}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In industrial scenarios, effective human-robot collaboration relies on multi-camera systems to robustly monitor human operators despite the occlusions that typically show up in a robotic workcell. In this scenario, precise localization of the person in the robot coordinate system is essential, making the hand-eye calibration of the camera network critical. This process presents significant challenges when high calibration accuracy should be achieved in short time to minimize production downtime, and when dealing with extensive camera networks used for monitoring wide areas, such as industrial robotic workcells. Our paper introduces an innovative and robust multi-camera hand-eye calibration method, designed to optimize each camera's pose relative to both the robot's base and to each other camera. This optimization integrates two types of key constraints: i) a single board-to-end-effector transformation, and ii) the relative camera-to-camera transformations. We demonstrate the superior performance of our method through comprehensive experiments employing the METRIC dataset and real-world data collected on industrial scenarios, showing notable advancements over state-of-the-art techniques even using less than 10 images. Additionally, we release an open-source version of our multi-camera hand-eye calibration algorithm at https://github.com/davidea97/Multi-Camera-Hand-Eye-Calibration.git."},"title":{"value":"Multi-Camera Hand-Eye Calibration for Human-Robot Collaboration in Industrial Robotic Workcells"},"authors":{"value":["Davide Allegro","Matteo Terreran","Stefano Ghidoni"]}},"tmdate":1727811951272,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727811947582,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Davide_Allegro1"],"forum":"Rr80dNLnMt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":133875,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727811951272,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Rr80dNLnMt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"npj Digit. Medicine 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-025-01623-0.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/NPJDM/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"schurz|simulating_mismatch_between_calibration_and_target_population_in_ai_for_mammography_the_retrospective_vaib_study"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haiko_Schurz:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Klara_Solander:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Davida_Åström:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fernando_Cossío:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Taeyang_Choi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Magnus_Dustler:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Claes_Lundström:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Håkan_Gustafsson:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sophia_Zackrisson:","~Fredrik_Strand1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-01623-0"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/npjdm/SchurzSACCDLGZS25,\n  author={Haiko Schurz and Klara Solander and Davida Åström and Fernando Cossío and Taeyang Choi and Magnus Dustler and Claes Lundström and Håkan Gustafsson and Sophia Zackrisson and Fredrik Strand},\n  title={Simulating mismatch between calibration and target population in AI for mammography the retrospective VAIB study},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={npj Digit. Medicine},\n  volume={8},\n  number={1},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-01623-0}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"AI cancer detection models require calibration to attain the desired balance between cancer detection rate (CDR) and false positive rate. In this study, we simulate the impact of six types of mismatches between the calibration population and the clinical target population, by creating purposefully non-representative datasets to calibrate AI for clinical settings. Mismatching the acquisition year between healthy and cancer-diagnosed screening participants led to a distortion in CDR between −3% to +19%. Mismatching age led to a distortion in CDR between −0.2% to +27%. Mismatching breast density distribution led to a distortion in CDR between +1% to 16%. Mismatching mammography vendors lead to a distortion in CDR between −32% to + 33%. Mismatches between calibration population and target clinical population lead to clinically important deviations. It is vital for safe clinical AI integration to ensure that important aspects of the calibration population are representative of the target population."},"title":{"value":"Simulating mismatch between calibration and target population in AI for mammography the retrospective VAIB study"},"authors":{"value":["Haiko Schurz","Klara Solander","Davida Åström","Fernando Cossío","Taeyang Choi","Magnus Dustler","Claes Lundström","Håkan Gustafsson","Sophia Zackrisson","Fredrik Strand"]}},"tmdate":1762527391452,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/npjdm/SchurzSACCDLGZS25"],"tcdate":1762527374083,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fredrik_Strand1"],"forum":"9JW6iH9BtW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":676678,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762527391452,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9JW6iH9BtW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICASSP 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10887540/10887541/10887895.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICASSP/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|an_automatic_extrinsic_calibration_method_for_lidarcamera_fusion_via_combining_semantic_and_geometric_features"},"authorids":{"value":["~Minqian_Wang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Libo_Weng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fei_Gao_0014:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP49660.2025.10887895"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icassp/WangW025a,\n  author={Minqian Wang and Libo Weng and Fei Gao},\n  title={An Automatic Extrinsic Calibration Method for LiDAR-Camera Fusion via Combining Semantic and Geometric Features},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={1-5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP49660.2025.10887895},\n  booktitle={ICASSP},\n  crossref={conf/icassp/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Precise extrinsic calibration is one of the key techniques for LiDAR-camera fusion system. In current methods, the extrinsic calibration is usually not automatic. To address this, an automatic calibration method via combining semantic and geometric features is proposed, which is not dependent on any specific calibration object. First, extrinsics are automatically initialized; semantic objects are utilized to formulate the edge constraints and projection boundary constraints. Then, an efficient global optimization algorithm that synergizes the Jacobian matrix and the stochastic strategy of simulated annealing is put forward to calculate precise extrinsics. A feedback mechanism is designed to evaluate the reliability of the proposed method. Experiments on the KITTI dataset show that the proposed method achieves a rotation error of 0.14°and a translation error of 4.5cm, outperforming most current methods. Besides, the experiment on the proposed optimization algorithm is also conducted to verify its effectiveness and efficiency."},"title":{"value":"An Automatic Extrinsic Calibration Method for LiDAR-Camera Fusion via Combining Semantic and Geometric Features"},"authors":{"value":["Minqian Wang","Libo Weng","Fei Gao"]}},"tmdate":1762404713778,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/icassp/WangW025a"],"tcdate":1762404711418,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Minqian_Wang1"],"forum":"nnSvF01rEk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":661826,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762404713778,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"nnSvF01rEk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"PLOS ONE"},"pdf":{"value":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article/file?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0302333&type=printable"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"quradaa|a_novel_code_representation_for_detecting_java_code_clones_using_highlevel_and_abstract_compiled_code_representations"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0302333"},"abstract":{"value":"In software development, it’s common to reuse existing source code by copying and pasting, resulting in the proliferation of numerous code clones—similar or identical code fragments—that detrimentally affect software quality and maintainability. Although several techniques for code clone detection exist, many encounter challenges in effectively identifying semantic clones due to their inability to extract syntax and semantics information. Fewer techniques leverage low-level source code representations like bytecode or assembly for clone detection. This work introduces a novel code representation for identifying syntactic and semantic clones in Java source code. It integrates high-level features extracted from the Abstract Syntax Tree with low-level features derived from intermediate representations generated by static analysis tools, like the Soot framework. Leveraging this combined representation, fifteen machine-learning models are trained to effectively detect code clones. Evaluation on a large dataset demonstrates the models’ efficacy in accurately identifying semantic clones. Among these classifiers, ensemble classifiers, such as the LightGBM classifier, exhibit exceptional accuracy. Linearly combining features enhances the effectiveness of the models compared to multiplication and distance combination techniques. The experimental findings indicate that the proposed method can outperform the current clone detection techniques in detecting semantic clones."},"title":{"value":"A novel code representation for detecting Java code clones using high-level and abstract compiled code representations"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Fahmi H. Quradaa","username":"~Fahmi_H._Quradaa1"},{"fullname":"Unil Yun"},{"fullname":"Sara Shahzad"},{"fullname":"Rashad Saeed"},{"fullname":"Mubarak M. Sufyan"}]}},"tmdate":1789090628388,"pdate":1715299200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0302333"],"tcdate":1772591353877,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Fahmi_H._Quradaa1"],"forum":"jex8o29mnK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":52850,"cdate":1715365047604,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789090628388,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"jex8o29mnK","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"code language models, dpo, sft, language models, optimisation, python"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2024 April Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2024codeoptimise,\ntitle={Code-Optimise: Optimising Code Language Models for Functional Correctness and Efficiency},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - April 2024},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=qwD7a6QgsU},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Code-Optimise: Optimising Code Language Models for Functional Correctness and Efficiency"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment","Approaches low compute settings-efficiency","Publicly available software and/or pre-trained models","Data resources"]},"abstract":{"value":"Code Language Models are capable of generating solutions that are fairly functionally correct and efficient. However, previous work has focused on improving either functional correctness or efficiency, usually at the expense of the other. To this end, we introduce Code-Optimise, a lightweight optimisation for Code Language Models that incorporates learning signals for correctness (pass, fail) as well as code efficiency (fast, slow). During training, Code-Optimise dynamically selects solutions from our self-generated code preference data to reduce overfitting. Code-Optimise achieves significant improvements in $pass@k$ while decreasing average runtime by up to 6\\% for cheaper code execution. It also reduces the average length of generated solutions by up to 23\\% for HumanEval and up to 48\\% for MBPP for faster response / inference times while demonstrating the fastest overall (single) solutions ($best@k$)."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7b8497cd763f685378f78964a872615de2d6660a.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"NLP Applications"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission"}},"tmdate":1794063139246,"tcdate":1713265190056,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission760/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission760/Authors"],"forum":"qwD7a6QgsU","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":760,"cdate":1713265190056,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/Submission760/-/Blind_Submission_License_Agreement","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1794063139246,"odate":1713279540366,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/April","id":"qwD7a6QgsU","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISOCC 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10031262/10031265/10031579.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISOCC/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"kim|high_detection_rate_bch_code_with_crc_code_for_memory_application"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Minseo_Kim:","~Jongsun_Park1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ISOCC56007.2022.10031579"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/isocc/KimP22c,\n  author={Minseo Kim and Jongsun Park},\n  title={High Detection Rate BCH Code with CRC Code for Memory Application},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={35-36},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ISOCC56007.2022.10031579},\n  booktitle={ISOCC},\n  crossref={conf/isocc/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With the aggressive scaling of DRAM process technology, the reliability degradation has become a critical issue, which inevitably increases the demand for more powerful error correction code (ECC) than conventional Hamming code. Although double error correction (DEC) BCH code needs less parity bits than other DEC codes, it cannot detect the errors larger than 3 bits. In this paper, we present a novel BCH code combined with cyclic redundancy check (CRC) code which can effectively detect multi-bit errors. By employing the CRC code, the proposed BCH code corrects double errors with a 3-bit error detection probability of 84%. In addition, by reusing the BCH encoder as CRC encoder and decoder in the proposed BCH, the area overhead of the proposed BCH is only 7.8<sup>%</sup> compared to the conventional BCH code."},"title":{"value":"High Detection Rate BCH Code with CRC Code for Memory Application"},"authors":{"value":["Minseo Kim","Jongsun Park"]}},"tmdate":1748222863878,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1748222854183,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jongsun_Park1"],"forum":"vHtyT0iFeJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":551105,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1748222863878,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"vHtyT0iFeJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEICE Trans. Commun. 2013"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/transcom/E96.B/7/E96.B_1764/_pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IEICET/2013"},"paperhash":{"value":"chong|steppingrandom_code_a_rateless_erasure_code_for_shortlength_messages"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zan-Kai_Chong:","~Bok-Min_Goi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hiroyuki_Ohsaki:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cheng-Kuan_Bryan_Ng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hong-Tat_Ewe:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1587/transcom.E96.B.1764"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ieicet/ChongGONE13,\n  author={Zan-Kai Chong and Bok-Min Goi and Hiroyuki Ohsaki and Cheng-Kuan Bryan Ng and Hong-Tat Ewe},\n  title={Stepping-Random Code: A Rateless Erasure Code for Short-Length Messages},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  journal={IEICE Trans. Commun.},\n  volume={96-B},\n  number={7},\n  pages={1764-1771},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1587/transcom.E96.B.1764}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Rateless erasure code is an error correction code that is able to encode a message of k uncoded symbols into an infinite number of coded symbols. One may reconstruct the original message from any k(1+ε) coded symbols, where ε denotes the decoding inefficiency. This paper proposes a hybrid code that combines the stepping code and random code and name it as Stepping-Random (SR) code. The Part I (first k) coded symbols of SR code are generated with stepping code. The rest of the coded symbols are generated with random code and denoted as Part II coded symbols. The numerical results show that the new hybrid code is able to achieve a complete decoding with no extra coded symbol (ε=0) if all the Part I coded symbols are received without loss. However, if only a portion of Part I coded symbols are received, a high probability of complete decoding is still achievable with k+10 coded symbols from the combination of Part I and II. SR code has a decoding complexity of O(k) in the former and O((βk)3) in the latter, where β∈R for 0≤β≤1, is the fraction of uncoded symbols that fails to be reconstructed from Part I coded symbols."},"title":{"value":"Stepping-Random Code: A Rateless Erasure Code for Short-Length Messages"},"authors":{"value":["Zan-Kai Chong","Bok-Min Goi","Hiroyuki Ohsaki","Cheng-Kuan Bryan Ng","Hong-Tat Ewe"]}},"tmdate":1769470385035,"pdate":1388448000000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/ieicet/ChongGONE13"],"tcdate":1769470248525,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bok-Min_Goi1"],"forum":"ZOqiw3zMRw","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":804991,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769470385035,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ZOqiw3zMRw","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Bildverarbeitung für die Medizin 2006"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/3-540-32137-3_85.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/BILDMED/2006"},"paperhash":{"value":"wengert|fully_automatic_endoscope_calibration_for_intraoperative_use"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Christian_Wengert:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mireille_Reeff:","~Philippe_C._Cattin1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gábor_Székely:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-32137-3_85"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/bildmed/WengertRCS06,\n  author={Christian Wengert and Mireille Reeff and Philippe C. Cattin and Gábor Székely},\n  title={Fully Automatic Endoscope Calibration for Intraoperative Use},\n  year={2006},\n  cdate={1136073600000},\n  pages={419-423},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-32137-3_85},\n  booktitle={Bildverarbeitung für die Medizin},\n  crossref={conf/bildmed/2006}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As of today endoscopes have been only used as a keyhole to look inside the human body. Our goal is to enhance the endoscope to a full imaging device providing better quantitative and qualitative data. Possible applications for such an enhanced endoscope are referencing, navigation and 3D visualization during endoscopic surgery. To obtain accurate results, a reliable and fully automatic calibration method for the endoscopic camera has been developed which can be used within the operating room (OR). Special care has been taken to ensure robustness against inevitable distortions and inhomogeneous illumination."},"title":{"value":"Fully Automatic Endoscope Calibration for Intraoperative Use"},"authors":{"value":["Christian Wengert","Mireille Reeff","Philippe C. Cattin","Gábor Székely"]}},"tmdate":1758176392724,"pdate":1136073600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/bildmed/WengertRCS06"],"tcdate":1758176214838,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Philippe_C._Cattin1"],"forum":"mjeNWmc2W9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":624338,"cdate":1136073600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1758176392724,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"mjeNWmc2W9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACC 2012"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6297579/6314593/06314972.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AMCC/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"borra|autonomous_calibration_algorithms_for_networks_of_cameras"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Domenica_Borra:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Enrico_Lovisari:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ruggero_Carli:","~Fabio_Fagnani1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sandro_Zampieri:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACC.2012.6314972"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/amcc/BorraLCFZ12,\n  author={Domenica Borra and Enrico Lovisari and Ruggero Carli and Fabio Fagnani and Sandro Zampieri},\n  title={Autonomous calibration algorithms for networks of cameras},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  pages={5126-5131},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ACC.2012.6314972},\n  booktitle={ACC},\n  crossref={conf/amcc/2012}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We deal with the important applicative problem of distributed cameras calibration. We model a network of N cameras as an undirected graph in which communicating cameras can measure their relative orientation in a noisy way. These measures can be used in order to minimize a suitable cost function. The shape of this cost function depends on a vector of integers K. We propose two algorithms which in a distributive way estimate such K, comparing advantages and disadvantages of both. Simulations are run on a grid network to prove effectiveness of the algorithms."},"title":{"value":"Autonomous calibration algorithms for networks of cameras"},"authors":{"value":["Domenica Borra","Enrico Lovisari","Ruggero Carli","Fabio Fagnani","Sandro Zampieri"]}},"tmdate":1753365366520,"pdate":1325376000000,"tcdate":1753365335403,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fabio_Fagnani1"],"forum":"EQTiv4rYfR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":586479,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753365366520,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"EQTiv4rYfR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"shu|a_spatiotemporal_handeye_calibration_for_trajectory_alignment_in_visualinertial_odometry_evaluation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zichao_Shu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lijun_Li:","~Rui_Wang12","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zetao_Chen:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.14894"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2404-14894,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zichao Shu and Lijun Li and Rui Wang and Zetao Chen},\n  title={A Spatiotemporal Hand-Eye Calibration for Trajectory Alignment in Visual(-Inertial) Odometry Evaluation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2404.14894},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2404.14894}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"A common prerequisite for evaluating a visual(-inertial) odometry (VO/VIO) algorithm is to align the timestamps and the reference frame of its estimated trajectory with a reference ground-truth derived from a system of superior precision, such as a motion capture system. The trajectory-based alignment, typically modeled as a classic hand-eye calibration, significantly influences the accuracy of evaluation metrics. However, traditional calibration methods are susceptible to the quality of the input poses. Few studies have taken this into account when evaluating VO/VIO trajectories that usually suffer from noise and drift. To fill this gap, we propose a novel spatiotemporal hand-eye calibration algorithm that fully leverages multiple constraints from screw theory for enhanced accuracy and robustness. Experimental results show that our algorithm has better performance and is less noise-prone than state-of-the-art methods."},"title":{"value":"A Spatiotemporal Hand-Eye Calibration for Trajectory Alignment in Visual(-Inertial) Odometry Evaluation"},"authors":{"value":["Zichao Shu","Lijun Li","Rui Wang","Zetao Chen"]}},"tmdate":1762954925073,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2404-14894"],"tcdate":1762954922210,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rui_Wang12"],"forum":"b9Z2BqXmDP","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":696232,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762954925073,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"b9Z2BqXmDP","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7083369/10504377/10496172.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"shu|a_spatiotemporal_handeye_calibration_for_trajectory_alignment_in_visualinertial_odometry_evaluation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zichao_Shu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lijun_Li:","~Rui_Wang12","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zetao_Chen:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3387143"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/ShuLWC24,\n  author={Zichao Shu and Lijun Li and Rui Wang and Zetao Chen},\n  title={A Spatiotemporal Hand-Eye Calibration for Trajectory Alignment in Visual(-Inertial) Odometry Evaluation},\n  year={2024},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1717200000000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={9},\n  number={6},\n  pages={5134-5141},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3387143}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"A common prerequisite for evaluating a visual (-inertial) odometry (VO/VIO) algorithm is to align the timestamps and the reference frame of its estimated trajectory with a reference ground-truth derived from a system of superior precision, such as a motion capture system. The trajectory-based alignment, typically modeled as a classic hand-eye calibration, significantly influences the accuracy of evaluation metrics. However, traditional calibration methods are susceptible to the quality of the input poses. Few studies have taken this into account when evaluating VO/VIO trajectories that usually suffer from noise and drift. To fill this gap, we propose a novel spatiotemporal hand-eye calibration algorithm that fully leverages multiple constraints from screw theory for enhanced accuracy and robustness. Experimental results show that our algorithm has better performance and is less noise-prone than state-of-the-art methods."},"title":{"value":"A Spatiotemporal Hand-Eye Calibration for Trajectory Alignment in Visual(-Inertial) Odometry Evaluation"},"authors":{"value":["Zichao Shu","Lijun Li","Rui Wang","Zetao Chen"]}},"tmdate":1762954924633,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/ral/ShuLWC24"],"tcdate":1762954922232,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Rui_Wang12"],"forum":"S9lS1An4KE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":696234,"cdate":1717200000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762954924633,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"S9lS1An4KE","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"A simple yet effective dynamic regularization method that improves previous regularization-based calibration approaches by informing the model what it should and should not know."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2024 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["calibration","regularization"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/24c0d706b5f49d4474fdcef6a48858704cb176af.pdf"},"primary_area":{"value":"general machine learning (i.e., none of the above)"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Miscalibration in deep learning refers to the confidence of the model does not match the performance. This problem usually arises due to the overfitting issue in deep learning models, resulting in overly confident predictions during testing. Existing methods typically prevent overfitting and mitigate miscalibration by adding a maximum-entropy regularizer to the objective function. The objective of these method can be understood as seeking a model that not only fits the ground-truth labels by increasing the confidence but also maximizes the entropy of predicted probabilities by decreasing the confidence. However, previous methods cannot provide clear guidance on when to increase the confidence (known knowns) or decrease the confidence (known unknowns), leading to the two conflicting optimization objectives (increasing but also decreasing confidence). In this work, we propose a simple yet effective method called dynamic regularization calibration (drc), to address this trade-off by exploring outlier samples within the training set, resulting in a reliable model that can admit it knows somethings and does not know others. drc effectively fits the labels for in-distribution samples while applying regularization to potential outliers dynamically, thereby obtaining robust calibrated model. Both theoretical and empirical analyses demonstrate the superiority of drc compared with previous methods."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nhan2024provable,\ntitle={Provable Dynamic Regularization Calibration},\nauthor={Zongbo Han and Yifeng Yang and Changqing Zhang and Linjun Zhang and Joey Tianyi Zhou and Qinghua Hu and Huaxiu Yao},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ktVzWL9BAQ}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Provable Dynamic Regularization Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ef7076314da18c8be3b89ff36d00f11cbcbd7a1e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"han|provable_dynamic_regularization_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zongbo_Han1","~Yifeng_Yang1","~Changqing_Zhang1","~Linjun_Zhang1","~Joey_Tianyi_Zhou1","~Qinghua_Hu1","~Huaxiu_Yao1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Zongbo Han","Yifeng Yang","Changqing Zhang","Linjun Zhang","Joey Tianyi Zhou","Qinghua Hu","Huaxiu Yao"]}},"tmdate":1711385405417,"tcdate":1695104623912,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1672/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1672/Authors"],"forum":"ktVzWL9BAQ","number":1672,"cdate":1695104623912,"mdate":1711385405417,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission1672/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"ktVzWL9BAQ","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Bayesian posterior sampling for uncertainty estimation and calibration in nnU-Net."},"venue":{"value":"MIDL 2025 - Short Papers"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d16226ea45d1a093bd480f1692fd2ee70df5710a.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["nnU-Net","Model Calibration","Uncertainty Estimation","Ischemic Stroke"]},"venueid":{"value":"MIDL.io/2025/Short_Papers"},"paperhash":{"value":"heylen|uncertainty_estimation_and_calibration_in_nnunetbased_ischemic_stroke_lesion_segmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ewout_Heylen1","jelle.demeestere@uzleuven.be","anke.wouters@uzleuven.be","pierre.seners@gmail.com","sorench@gmail.com","nyuen@stanford.edu","mmlynash@stanford.edu","skemp@stanford.edu","jheit@stanford.edu","albers@stanford.edu","seenad@stanford.edu","robin.lemmens@uzleuven.be","lansberg@stanford.edu","~Frederik_Maes1"]},"abstract":{"value":"nnU-Net has become widely recognized as a state-of-the-art semantic segmentation framework. However, deep learning models are often poorly calibrated, resulting in unreliable probability estimates. Additionally, they lack meaningful uncertainty quantification. We trained an nnU-Net model to segment ischemic stroke lesions on acute-phase Diffusion-Weighted Imaging (DWI) MRI and applied a Bayesian posterior sampling approach to estimate uncertainty and improve model calibration. Our findings show that the Bayesian posterior sampling approach yields better calibration compared to a conventional nnU-Net, while providing uncertainty estimates and maintaining comparable segmentation performance."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nheylen2025uncertainty,\ntitle={Uncertainty Estimation and Calibration in nnU-Net-Based Ischemic Stroke Lesion Segmentation},\nauthor={Ewout Heylen and Jelle Demeestere and Anke Wouters and Pierre Seners and Soren Christensen and Nicole Yuen and Michael Mlynash and Stephanie Kemp and Jeremy J. Heit and Gregory W. Albers and Seena Dehkharghani and Robin Lemmens and Maarten Lansberg and Frederik Maes},\nbooktitle={Medical Imaging with Deep Learning - Short Papers},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=BXCmwipKRb}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Uncertainty Estimation and Calibration in nnU-Net-Based Ischemic Stroke Lesion Segmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Ewout Heylen","Jelle Demeestere","Anke Wouters","Pierre Seners","Soren Christensen","Nicole Yuen","Michael Mlynash","Stephanie Kemp","Jeremy J. Heit","Gregory W. Albers","Seena Dehkharghani","Robin Lemmens","Maarten Lansberg","Frederik Maes"]}},"tmdate":1748838732896,"pdate":1746113376726,"tcdate":1744449542810,"writers":["MIDL.io/2025/Short_Papers","MIDL.io/2025/Short_Papers/Submission110/Authors"],"signatures":["MIDL.io/2025/Short_Papers/Submission110/Authors"],"forum":"BXCmwipKRb","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":110,"cdate":1744449542810,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["MIDL.io/2025/Short_Papers/-/Submission","MIDL.io/2025/Short_Papers/-/Post_Submission","MIDL.io/2025/Short_Papers/-/Edit","MIDL.io/2025/Short_Papers/Submission110/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1748838732896,"odate":1744460940844,"domain":"MIDL.io/2025/Short_Papers","id":"BXCmwipKRb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote. Sens. 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/36/10807682/10855605.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TGRS/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|radiometric_calibration_using_artificial_intelligence_constituting_uniform_observing_systems_for_infrared_satellites"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Boyang_Chen_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Aiqun_Wu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wen_Hui:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peng_Rao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuan_Feng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fansheng_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Changpei_Han:","~Qichao_Ying1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yapeng_Wu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Miao_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Damian_Moss:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhenxing_Qian:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2025.3534794"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tgrs/ChenWHRFCHYWLMQ25,\n  author={Boyang Chen and Aiqun Wu and Wen Hui and Peng Rao and Xuan Feng and Fansheng Chen and Changpei Han and Qichao Ying and Yapeng Wu and Miao Liu and Damian Moss and Zhenxing Qian},\n  title={Radiometric Calibration Using Artificial Intelligence: Constituting Uniform Observing Systems for Infrared Satellites},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={63},\n  pages={1-16},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2025.3534794}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Radiometric calibration (RC) is a critical process in aerospace infrared remote sensing that establishes the relationship between the radiation energy of observed objects and the digital number (DN) output from sensors, which is fundamental for ensuring high-precision applications of infrared remote sensing data. At present, source-based RC (SBRC) is the predominant method, relying on a variety of radiometric sources (RSs) including in-orbit blackbodies, or natural targets such as lakes and oceans. This approach, while effective, imposes constraints on remote sensing systems such as space & weight allocation for RS and additional observation time for RC. Moreover, the reliance on physical calibration sources can introduce uncertainties due to factors such as imperfect emissivity of in-orbit blackbodies, lack of data consistency due to varied RS types, and variations in environmental conditions. In this article, we propose a novel RC method named artificial intelligence RC (AIRC), which directly generates RC coefficients for the in-orbit remote sensing satellites using the physical and environmental parameters of the sensor. We first theoretically prove that RC coefficients can be derived as functions of the sensor states. Next, we propose our neural networks for infrared RC (RCNN), to learn this relationship based on historical high-accuracy calibration data, enabling a shift from reference traceability (RT) to states traceability (ST). Then, to verify the feasibility of the proposed scheme, we train and test a multilayered perceptron (MLP) as a simple implementation of RCNN based on our long-term well-curated RC data from our FengYun-4A Advanced Geosynchronous Radiation Imager (FY-4A AGRI), and the experiments show that the proposed method achieves high-accuracy RC comparable with the official RC method applied on FY-4A AGRI that uses an in-orbit blackbody. Our study showcases how to conduct RC using the “reason (the states of sensor)–results (calibration coefficient)” logic, as supplement to the existing “result (observation to RS)–reason (calibration coefficient)” logic, which promotes constituting a uniform observing system for cross-platform infrared satellites."},"title":{"value":"Radiometric Calibration Using Artificial Intelligence: Constituting Uniform Observing Systems for Infrared Satellites"},"authors":{"value":["Boyang Chen","Aiqun Wu","Wen Hui","Peng Rao","Xuan Feng","Fansheng Chen","Changpei Han","Qichao Ying","Yapeng Wu","Miao Liu","Damian Moss","Zhenxing Qian"]}},"tmdate":1741141227874,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1741141226311,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Qichao_Ying1"],"forum":"HoD7eSVuqh","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":351451,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1741141227874,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"HoD7eSVuqh","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Crossref"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"ahmed|art_of_code_review"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18122/td.2388.boisestate"},"abstract":{"value":"Code Review is crucial for advancing computer science. It ensures high-quality software, contributes to the reliability of artificial intelligence (AI) systems, and addresses security vulnerabilities. Effective peer code review requires helpful comments and supportive automated tools in collaborative development. It is important to ensure these Code Review comments serve their purposes. This dissertation reflects the evolution of research on the usefulness of Code Review comments. First, I examine papers that define the usefulness of Code Review comments, mine and annotate datasets, study developers’ perceptions, analyze factors from different aspects, and use classifiers to predict the usefulness of Code Review comments automatically. Second, I investigate the usefulness of Code Review comments through novel textual feature-based and untapped featureless approaches. Third, I present the generalization performance of the benchmark models and datasets, subsequently providing their explanations. Fourth, I dive into the utility of Code Review comments by scrutinizing the non-verbal cues that often carry emotive or instructive weight for developers. Finally, I investigate the utility of Code Review comments in the unexplored scientific software domain. My investigation into scientific software extends findings from Code Review comment usefulness research on general-purpose software, benefiting developers, scientists, and software engineering researchers in both the general and scientific software communities."},"title":{"value":"Art of Code Review"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Sharif Ahmed","username":"~Sharif_Ahmed1"}]}},"tmdate":1782828558165,"pdate":1754006400000,"externalIds":["doi:10.18122/td.2388.boisestate"],"tcdate":1782828554773,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Sharif_Ahmed1"],"forum":"aVVUKaGfvb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":79199,"cdate":1770851498667,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1782828558165,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"aVVUKaGfvb","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Topology changes after PTQ calibration can systematically amplify or attenuate numerical error in GNNs, with the direction and magnitude determined by the graph and message-passing operator."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["graph neural networks","post-training quantization","topology drift","low-precision inference","graph robustness","structural distribution shift"]},"primary_area":{"value":"learning on graphs and other geometries & topologies"},"abstract":{"value":"Post-training quantization (PTQ) is attractive for graph neural networks (GNNs), yet it is commonly evaluated on the same topology used for calibration. We study whether post-calibration topology drift changes the numerical discrepancy between a quantized GNN and its full-precision counterpart beyond the error already present on the calibration graph. We introduce Component-Preserving Degree-Preserving Rewiring (CP-DPR), a controlled intervention that changes neighborhood composition while preserving the node set, features, labels, model weights, edge count, exact node-wise degree sequence, and connected-component partition. We measure the baseline-adjusted excess logit divergence \\(EQD_Z = QD_Z(A_\\delta) - QD_Z(A_0)\\). Experiments cover Cora, CiteSeer, PubMed, and Roman-empire; GCN and GraphSAGE; W8A8 and W4A4 simulated PTQ; five training seeds; and ten perturbation seeds per severity. At the strongest perturbation level, 10 of 12 external dataset–operator–precision settings exhibit positive excess divergence, with substantially larger absolute effects under W4A4. Roman-empire with GCN provides a consistent negative case that persists after validation-only model refinement. Activation diagnostics show negligible additional clipping in the dominant positive regimes and a stronger association with rounding mismatch. GraphSAGE further exposes an observer-blindness failure mode in which min–max calibration parameters remain unchanged despite internal activation-distribution motion. These results provide evidence that topology drift induces operator-dependent numerical sensitivity not captured by calibration-graph accuracy alone, while the evidence for operator dependence currently rests on one heterophilous benchmark."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026postcalibration,\ntitle={Post-Calibration Topology Drift Reveals Operator-Dependent Numerical Sensitivity in Quantized Graph Neural Networks},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=BN9TNxcB9N},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Post-Calibration Topology Drift Reveals Operator-Dependent Numerical Sensitivity in Quantized Graph Neural Networks"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/23ac86c85ada6fee70b0c1c92eef9edf3316705e.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791235030020,"tcdate":1789798268319,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55441/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55441/Authors"],"forum":"BN9TNxcB9N","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":55441,"cdate":1789798268319,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55441/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791235030020,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"BN9TNxcB9N","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Desk Rejected Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["moderation","semantic calibration"]},"primary_area":{"value":"other topics in machine learning (i.e., none of the above)"},"abstract":{"value":"Current generative models can produce synthetic media that is visually indistinguishable from real content. As a result, traditional detection methods rely mostly on subtle artifacts introduced during generation. However, we show that such methods could eventually become ineffective. Anticipating this, we suggest that the main risk lies not in whether a media sample is synthetic or real, but in whether its semantic content is deceiving, that is, whether it distorts the information distribution in a way that misrepresents reality. To capture this, we formally introduce the notion of deception in the context of online media streams. Complementing standard detection approaches, we introduce semantic calibration to mitigate deception directly by processing semantic content using captioning and large language models, rather than relying on artifacts introduced by generative models. Our method is explainable, transparent, and modality agnostic, providing a rigorous foundation for developing new tools to combat online misinformation. We offer both theoretical justification and empirical evidence for its effectiveness."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nanonymous2026semantic,\ntitle={Semantic Calibration in Media Streams},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=6YTEDVd80T}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Semantic Calibration in Media Streams"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/bd36da35b8709b906dafc8f295b5e593f0ff76f4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Desk_Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"berdoz|semantic_calibration_in_media_streams"},"authorids":{"value":["~Frédéric_Berdoz1","~Luca_A_Lanzendörfer1","~Antonis_Asonitis1","~Roger_Wattenhofer1"]},"authors":{"value":["Frédéric Berdoz","Luca A Lanzendörfer","Antonis Asonitis","Roger Wattenhofer"]}},"tmdate":1770907921389,"tcdate":1758285622978,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission18248/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission18248/Authors"],"forum":"6YTEDVd80T","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":18248,"cdate":1758285622978,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission18248/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission18248/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Desk_Rejected_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770907921389,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"6YTEDVd80T","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE J. Sel. Top. Appl. Earth Obs. Remote. Sens. 2018"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/4609443/8351996/08340844.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/STAEORS/2018"},"paperhash":{"value":"ravi|simultaneous_system_calibration_of_a_multilidar_multicamera_mobile_mapping_platform"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Radhika_Ravi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yun-Jou_Lin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Magdy_Elbahnasawy:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tamer_Shamseldin:","~Ayman_Habib1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/JSTARS.2018.2812796"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/staeors/RaviLESH18,\n  author={Radhika Ravi and Yun-Jou Lin and Magdy Elbahnasawy and Tamer Shamseldin and Ayman Habib},\n  title={Simultaneous System Calibration of a Multi-LiDAR Multicamera Mobile Mapping Platform},\n  year={2018},\n  cdate={1514764800000},\n  journal={IEEE J. Sel. Top. Appl. Earth Obs. Remote. Sens.},\n  volume={11},\n  number={5},\n  pages={1694-1714},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/JSTARS.2018.2812796}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Mobile light detection and ranging (LiDAR) systems are widely used to generate precise 3-D spatial information, which in turn aids a variety of applications such as digital building model generation, transportation corridor asset management, telecommunications, precision agriculture, and infrastructure monitoring. Integrating such systems with one or more cameras would allow forward and backward projection between imagery and LiDAR data, thus facilitating several other high-level data processing activities, such as reliable feature extraction and colorization of point cloudsv. To increase the registration accuracy of point clouds derived from LiDAR data and imagery, along with their accuracy with respect to the ground truth, a simultaneous estimation of the mounting parameters relating the different laser scanners and cameras to the onboard global navigation satellite system (GNSS)/inertial navigation system (INS) unit is vital. This paper proposes a calibration procedure that directly estimates the mounting parameters for several spinning multibeam laser scanners and cameras onboard an airborne or terrestrial mobile platform. This procedure is based on the use of conjugate points and linear/planar features in overlapping images and point clouds derived from different drive-runs/flight lines. In order to increase the efficiency of semi-automatic conjugate feature extraction from the LiDAR data, specifically designed calibration boards covered by highly reflective surfaces that could be easily deployed and set up within an outdoor environment are used in this study. An experimental setup is used to evaluate the performance of the proposed calibration procedure for both airborne and terrestrial mobile mapping systems through the a posteriori variance factor of the least squares adjustment procedure and the quality of fit of the adjusted LiDAR point cloud and image points to linear/planar surfaces before and after the calibration process."},"title":{"value":"Simultaneous System Calibration of a Multi-LiDAR Multicamera Mobile Mapping Platform"},"authors":{"value":["Radhika Ravi","Yun-Jou Lin","Magdy Elbahnasawy","Tamer Shamseldin","Ayman Habib"]}},"tmdate":1731600255679,"pdate":1514764800000,"tcdate":1731600243973,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ayman_Habib1"],"forum":"A7LobFEhcg","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":244069,"cdate":1514764800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731600255679,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"A7LobFEhcg","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICPC 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11025805/11025854/11025933.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IWPC/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"he|clcosum_curriculum_learningbased_code_summarization_for_code_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Hongkui_He1","~Jiexin_Wang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liuwen_Cao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Cai_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPC66645.2025.00035"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iwpc/HeWC025,\n  author={Hongkui He and Jiexin Wang and Liuwen Cao and Yi Cai},\n  title={CLCoSum: Curriculum Learning-Based Code Summarization for Code Language Models},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={1-13},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPC66645.2025.00035},\n  booktitle={ICPC},\n  crossref={conf/iwpc/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The code summarization task aims to automatically generate natural language descriptions for code snippets. Recently, pre-trained code language models (CLMs) have demonstrated outstanding performance on code summarization. Additionally, researchers have shown that there is a strong correlation between code function names and summaries, and poorly defined function names lead to worse summaries generated by models. To mitigate this issue, in this paper, we propose CLCoSum, a curriculum learning-based code summarization method for CLMs that improves their performance in poorly named function scenarios. CLCoSum helps CLMs avoid overreliance on function names when they are poorly defined. First, CLCoSum employs data augmentation operators on function names to generate semantically equivalent poorly named codes, which are considered harder data and assist in reducing the model's reliance on unclear function names. Subsequently, CLCoSum uses a curriculum learning paradigm to allow the model to learn these harder codes in an organized way during finetuning. This approach enables CLMs to progress from easier to more difficult training data, similar to the human learning process. Extensive experiments on two existing datasets for Java and Python demonstrate that CLCoSum boosts the performance of various CLMs in code summarization. Specifically, the improvements in BLEU-4 score range from approximately 5% to $\\mathbf{2 0. 8 \\%}$. The fine-tuning speed of CLCoSum on the augmented dataset is also competitive. Our code and data are available at https://github.com/KuiH/CLCoSum."},"title":{"value":"CLCoSum: Curriculum Learning-Based Code Summarization for Code Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Hongkui He","Jiexin Wang","Liuwen Cao","Yi Cai"]}},"tmdate":1784185003158,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iwpc/HeWC025"],"tcdate":1767664984595,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jiexin_Wang1"],"forum":"SSLMGTbfDz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":717591,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784185003158,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SSLMGTbfDz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ASE 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/KBSE/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|attributionguided_adversarial_code_prompt_generation_for_code_completion_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xueyang_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guozhu_Meng:","~Shangqing_Liu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lu_Xiang:","~Kun_Sun6","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kai_Chen_0012:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiapu_Luo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Liu_0003:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3691620.3695517"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/kbse/LiMLXS0L024,\n  author={Xueyang Li and Guozhu Meng and Shangqing Liu and Lu Xiang and Kun Sun and Kai Chen and Xiapu Luo and Yang Liu},\n  title={Attribution-guided Adversarial Code Prompt Generation for Code Completion Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={1460-1471},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3691620.3695517},\n  booktitle={ASE},\n  crossref={conf/kbse/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models have made significant progress in code completion, which may further remodel future software development. However, these code completion models are found to be highly risky as they may introduce vulnerabilities unintentionally or be induced by a special input, i.e., adversarial code prompt. Prior studies mainly focus on the robustness of these models, but their security has not been fully analyzed.In this paper, we propose a novel approach AdvPro that can automatically generate adversarial code prompts for these code completion models. AdvPro incorporates 14 code mutation strategies at the granularity of five levels. The mutation strategies are ensured to make no modifications to code semantics, which should be insensitive to the models. Moreover, we leverage gradient attribution to localize the important code as mutation points and speed up adversarial prompt generation. Extensive experiments are conducted on 13 state-of-the-art models belonging to 7 families. The results show that our approach can effectively generate adversarial prompts, with an increased rate of 69.6% beyond the baseline ALERT. By comparing the results of attribution-guided localization, we find that the recognition results of important tokens in input codes are almost identical among different models. This finding reduces the limitation of using open-source alternative models to guide adversarial attacks against closed-source models. The results of the ablation study on the components of AdvPro show that CCMs focus on variable names, but other structures are equally crucial."},"title":{"value":"Attribution-guided Adversarial Code Prompt Generation for Code Completion Models"},"authors":{"value":["Xueyang Li","Guozhu Meng","Shangqing Liu","Lu Xiang","Kun Sun","Kai Chen","Xiapu Luo","Yang Liu"]}},"tmdate":1747225007990,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1736341460679,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kun_Sun6"],"forum":"7nrPPXkJf6","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":260684,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747225007990,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"7nrPPXkJf6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/7083369/10409903/10414357.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|lidarlink_observabilityaware_probabilistic_planebased_extrinsic_calibration_for_nonoverlapping_solidstate_lidars"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jie_Xu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Song_Huang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuxin_Qiu:","~Lijun_Zhao3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenlu_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mingxing_Fang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Minhang_Wang:","~Ruifeng_Li2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3355733"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/XuHQ0YFWL24,\n  author={Jie Xu and Song Huang and Shuxin Qiu and Lijun Zhao and Wenlu Yu and Mingxing Fang and Minhang Wang and Ruifeng Li},\n  title={LiDAR-Link: Observability-Aware Probabilistic Plane-Based Extrinsic Calibration for Non-Overlapping Solid-State LiDARs},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={9},\n  number={3},\n  pages={2590-2597},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3355733}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"As solid-state LiDAR technology advances, mobile robotics and autonomous driving increasingly rely on multiple solid-state LiDARs for perception. However, limited or non-overlapping fields of view (FoV) among these sensors pose significant challenges for extrinsic calibration. Moreover, there are no quantitative indicators to evaluate calibration quality currently. To tackle these challenges, we introduce LiDAR-Link, a novel approach for calibration and evaluation, consisting of LiDAR-Bridge and LiDAR-Align. LiDAR-Bridge uses a wide-angle LiDAR to act as an intermediary, aligning point clouds from small-angle solid-state LiDARs. These small LiDARs have limited or non-overlapping FoVs, allowing us to indirectly determine the extrinsics between them. LiDAR-Align adaptively constructs a Voxel Probabilistic Plane Map to efficiently match point clouds and differentiate the contributions of various high-quality planar features. To align these point clouds, Iterated Extended Kalman Filter (IEKF) is utilized, which is based on point-to-plane residuals. The resulting covariance verifies whether point cloud alignment constraints are met and assesses calibration reliability. Furthermore, LiDAR-Align supports multi-scene joint calibration to overcome the limitations of fewer constraints caused by smaller LiDAR FoVs. We validate the accuracy of our method and the significance of observability analysis through a comprehensive set of experiments."},"title":{"value":"LiDAR-Link: Observability-Aware Probabilistic Plane-Based Extrinsic Calibration for Non-Overlapping Solid-State LiDARs"},"authors":{"value":["Jie Xu","Song Huang","Shuxin Qiu","Lijun Zhao","Wenlu Yu","Mingxing Fang","Minhang Wang","Ruifeng Li"]}},"tmdate":1784539422387,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1741407996052,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~lijun_zhao2"],"forum":"VBlOEAiEGi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":366724,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784539422387,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"VBlOEAiEGi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Instrumentation and Measurement"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/19/4407674/11495237.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|easycalib_a_novel_target_for_highaccuracy_fullyautomatic_extrinsic_calibration_of_camera_and_lidar"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIM.2026.3687975"},"abstract":{"value":"Precise extrinsic calibration is a fundamental requirement for enabling robust camera–LiDAR fusion applications. Existing target-based methods suffer from practical limitations: they require manual annotation, precise target dimensions and initial extrinsic estimates, demand complex target placement, and have limited applicability across different sensor types. To the best of our knowledge, we are not aware of a prior approach that simultaneously achieves full automation, avoids reliance on precise target dimensions and accurate initialization, and generalizes across both mechanical and non-repetitive-scan solid-state LiDARs. In this paper, we propose EasyCalib, a novel artificial target for high-accuracy fully-automatic extrinsic calibration for almost all types of camera-LiDAR systems. Specifically, we design dedicated point cloud segmenters for mechanical and solid-state LiDARs to automatically extract EasyCalib. By leveraging the co-visible corner points and central edge features of EasyCalib in both the image and point cloud domains, we achieve a coarse-to-fine, initialization-free, and highly accurate extrinsic calibration. Moreover, since the proposed calibration pipeline does not require the target to be strictly orthogonal and tolerates moderate assembly misalignment, and since the target dimensions are not involved in the calibration computation, EasyCalib requires neither complex manufacturing procedures nor precise prior knowledge of its size."},"title":{"value":"EasyCalib: A Novel Target for High-Accuracy Fully-Automatic Extrinsic Calibration of Camera and LiDAR"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhiyu Zhou","username":"~Zhiyu_Zhou3"},{"fullname":"Zhi Gao","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Zhi%20Gao"},{"fullname":"Min Cao","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Min%20Cao"},{"fullname":"Jingshi Wang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Jingshi%20Wang"},{"fullname":"Yong Li","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Yong%20Li"},{"fullname":"Zhipeng Lin","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Zhipeng%20Lin"},{"fullname":"Wenbin Yang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Wenbin%20Yang"},{"fullname":"Xiaonan Wang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Xiaonan%20Wang"},{"fullname":"Qiyuan Zhang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Qiyuan%20Zhang"}]}},"tmdate":1778143390523,"pdate":1767225600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tim.2026.3687975"],"tcdate":1778143388357,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Zhiyu_Zhou3"],"forum":"xGZzMTQkTy","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":68552,"cdate":1777410029077,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1778143390523,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"xGZzMTQkTy","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Circuits Syst. II Express Briefs 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/8920/11104806/11037460.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TCASII/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"peng|a_neural_networkenhanced_digital_background_calibration_algorithm_for_residue_amplifier_nonlinearity_in_pipelined_adcs"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yutao_Peng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ziwei_Lai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hu_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongbing_Fu:","~Yabo_Ni1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tao_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhifei_Lu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xizhu_Peng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:He_Tang_0003:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TCSII.2025.3580062"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tcasII/PengLWZFNLLPT25,\n  author={Yutao Peng and Ziwei Lai and Hu Wang and Jun Zhang and Dongbing Fu and Yabo Ni and Tao Liu and Zhifei Lu and Xizhu Peng and He Tang},\n  title={A Neural Network-Enhanced Digital Background Calibration Algorithm for Residue Amplifier Nonlinearity in Pipelined ADCs},\n  year={2025},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1754006400000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Circuits Syst. II Express Briefs},\n  volume={72},\n  number={8},\n  pages={1008-1012},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TCSII.2025.3580062}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This brief proposes a neural network-enhanced digital background calibration scheme for calibrating the linear and the third-order nonlinear gain errors of the residue amplifier (RA) in pipelined ADCs. A customized convolutional neural network (CNN) is designed to extract the information of the linear and the third-order nonlinear gain errors of RA with dither injection. Compared to traditional correlation-based calibration algorithms, the proposed method can significantly improve convergence speed and robustness against dither capacitor mismatch. Compared with previous neural network-based calibration techniques which are commonly used for foreground calibration, the proposed method can operate in background to follow error variations without any risk of signal fidelity problems. Off-chip validation with a silicon-proven 14-bit 1.3 GS/s pipelined ADC shows that, after calibration, the SNDR and SFDR are improved from 46.6 dB and 55.2 dB to 63.1 dB and 80.4 dB, respectively. Moreover, the proposed method takes only 75K samples to reach convergence, whereas traditional algorithms require several to hundreds of millions of samples to achieve convergence ( $10{^{{2}}} \\sim 10{^{{4}}}$  times faster). The implementation result shows that the power consumption of the proposed calibrator is 34.8 mW at 1.3 GHz clock frequency."},"title":{"value":"A Neural Network-Enhanced Digital Background Calibration Algorithm for Residue Amplifier Nonlinearity in Pipelined ADCs"},"authors":{"value":["Yutao Peng","Ziwei Lai","Hu Wang","Jun Zhang","Dongbing Fu","Yabo Ni","Tao Liu","Zhifei Lu","Xizhu Peng","He Tang"]}},"tmdate":1768447756245,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tcasII/PengLWZFNLLPT25"],"tcdate":1768447660662,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yabo_Ni1"],"forum":"eNV9Td4kXz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":754327,"cdate":1754006400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768447756245,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"eNV9Td4kXz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"TA 2010"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1474667015343433/pdf?md5=7ea07fccea093bc6b4139a5231ce5cbc&pid=1-s2.0-S1474667015343433-main.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/TA/2010"},"paperhash":{"value":"lemmer|calibration_of_a_carlike_mobile_robot_with_a_highprecision_positioning_system"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:László_Lemmer:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Robin_Heß:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Markus_Krauss:","~Klaus_Schilling1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3182/20101005-4-RO-2018.00052"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ta/LemmerHKS10,\n  author={László Lemmer and Robin Heß and Markus Krauss and Klaus Schilling},\n  title={Calibration of a Car-Like Mobile Robot with a High-Precision Positioning System},\n  year={2010},\n  cdate={1262304000000},\n  pages={174-179},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3182/20101005-4-RO-2018.00052},\n  booktitle={TA},\n  crossref={conf/ta/2010}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents the calibration procedure of a car-like mobile robot with a high-precision positioning system. The mobile robot, which was calibrated, is a small outdoor vehicle designed for teleoperated missions. The aim of the calibration is the achievement of an accurate locomotion, therefore the incremental propulsion sensor, the gyroscope, the propulsion actuator and the steering actuator were analyzed with the help of an active optical high-precision positioning system."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of a Car-Like Mobile Robot with a High-Precision Positioning System"},"authors":{"value":["László Lemmer","Robin Heß","Markus Krauss","Klaus Schilling"]}},"tmdate":1731595262651,"pdate":1262304000000,"tcdate":1731595150896,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Klaus_Schilling1"],"forum":"lo3C89Na4m","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":243455,"cdate":1262304000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731595262651,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"lo3C89Na4m","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Empir. Softw. Eng. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10664-023-10319-6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ESE/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"mi|a_graphbased_code_representation_method_to_improve_code_readability_classification"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qing_Mi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Zhan:","~Han_Weng2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qinghang_Bao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Longjie_Cui:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wei_Ma:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-023-10319-6"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ese/MiZWBCM23,\n  author={Qing Mi and Yi Zhan and Han Weng and Qinghang Bao and Longjie Cui and Wei Ma},\n  title={A graph-based code representation method to improve code readability classification},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={Empir. Softw. Eng.},\n  volume={28},\n  number={4},\n  pages={87},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-023-10319-6}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code readability is crucial for developers since it is closely related to code maintenance and affects developers’ work efficiency. Code readability classification refers to the source code being classified as pre-defined certain levels according to its readability. So far, many code readability classification models have been proposed in existing studies, including deep learning networks that have achieved relatively high accuracy and good performance."},"title":{"value":"A graph-based code representation method to improve code readability classification"},"authors":{"value":["Qing Mi","Yi Zhan","Han Weng","Qinghang Bao","Longjie Cui","Wei Ma"]}},"tmdate":1747534449275,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1747534446125,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~han_weng1"],"forum":"eFfJtvou7y","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":520388,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747534449275,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"eFfJtvou7y","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce difficulty calibration across two time axes: training time (RMC) and diffusion time (TSC), to isolate the true membership signal from image difficulty, achieving SOTA membership inference across diverse parameterizations."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Diffusion Models","MIA","Loss Trajectory"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Modern diffusion models are trained on massive datasets that often include copyrighted or private images. Membership inference attacks (MIAs) address this by testing whether a given image was in a model's training set. The standard approach scores an image by its reconstruction loss and flags low-loss images as members. This often fails because some images are simply easier to reconstruct than others, so a simple unseen image can score a lower loss than a complex training image. Difficulty calibration separates memorization from how difficult an image is to begin with. We show how to calibrate along two axes of time: training time and diffusion time. First, across training time, we introduce Reference-Model Calibration (RMC), which compares a fine-tuned model's loss against an earlier checkpoint. This cleanly isolates newly memorized data and gives near-perfect auditing, even without text captions. Since earlier checkpoints are not always available, we next present Temporal Self-Calibration (TSC), which calibrates across diffusion time using only a single model. Our key observation is that an image's difficulty persists across noise levels, whereas memorization is localized in time. By comparing the loss at a target timestep against neighboring timesteps from the same model, TSC attenuates image difficulty using the model's own trajectory. Empirically, our methods beat the state of the art by a wide margin, and unlike most existing attacks, generalize across different model parameterizations such as  $x$-, $v$-, and $\\epsilon$-prediction models."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026difficulty,\ntitle={Difficulty Calibration Across Time for Membership Inference in Diffusion Models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=0kefRefTrI},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Difficulty Calibration Across Time for Membership Inference in Diffusion Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/4ecf7469ed2d3fdc14056e74f6ca1f02dec868c7.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791228536976,"tcdate":1789389374160,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission18042/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission18042/Authors"],"forum":"0kefRefTrI","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":18042,"cdate":1789389374160,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission18042/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791228536976,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"0kefRefTrI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISMIS 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-16564-1_28.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISMIS/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|improve_calibration_robustness_of_temperature_scaling_by_penalizing_output_entropy"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Wen_Yao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaoqian_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ling_Feng:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-16564-1_28"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/ismis/ZhangYCF22,\n  author={Jun Zhang and Wen Yao and Xiaoqian Chen and Ling Feng},\n  title={Improve Calibration Robustness of Temperature Scaling by Penalizing Output Entropy},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={294-304},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-16564-1_28},\n  booktitle={ISMIS},\n  crossref={conf/ismis/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Data corruption is usually encountered in captured images due to the light condition, weather condition, or the quality of devices. Calibrating deep neural network (DNN) based image classifiers under data corruption is crucial, especially in safety-critical applications. A recent study shows that the widespread post-hoc calibration method temperature scaling (TS) performs poorly under corrupted shifts because it is easy to overfit the validation set. Three issues need to be addressed when improving the calibration robustness of TS: (1) How to measure the data shifts due to corruption? (2) How to reformulate TS with measured metrics? (3) How to improve the robustness of TS? Observing that output entropy increases with data shift intensity caused by corruption, we incorporate an entropy term into TS’ optimizing Negative Log-likelihood (NLL) problem (Q1). Since the two terms of loss function are mutually exclusive, we reformulate TS as a multi-objective optimization (MOO) problem (Q2). By solving the MOO problem, a set of scaling parameters can thus be obtained and integrated to improve the calibration robustness of TS (Q3). We propose a novel TS method named MOO-ETS based on the above solutions, consisting of two integration strategies. Experimental results on the corrupted versions of CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and TinyImageNet demonstrate that: (1) entropy can measure corrupted data shifts accurately; (2) MOO-ETS can achieve competitive performance under corrupted shifts compared with the state-of-the-art method Deep Ensemble and beat the TS family baselines remarkably."},"title":{"value":"Improve Calibration Robustness of Temperature Scaling by Penalizing Output Entropy"},"authors":{"value":["Jun Zhang","Wen Yao","Xiaoqian Chen","Ling Feng"]}},"tmdate":1780138983944,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731482180204,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wen_Yao1"],"forum":"rQ6E4gEoOd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":211917,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1780138983944,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"rQ6E4gEoOd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2208.06164v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"sheng|joint_optimization_of_ranking_and_calibration_with_contextualized_hybrid_model"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xiang-Rong_Sheng1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jingyue_Gao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yueyao_Cheng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Siran_Yang:","~Shuguang_Han1","~Hongbo_Deng1","~Yuning_Jiang4","~Jian_Xu8","~Bo_Zheng5"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2208.06164"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2208-06164,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Xiang-Rong Sheng and Jingyue Gao and Yueyao Cheng and Siran Yang and Shuguang Han and Hongbo Deng and Yuning Jiang and Jian Xu and Bo Zheng},\n  title={Joint Optimization of Ranking and Calibration with Contextualized Hybrid Model},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2208.06164},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2208.06164}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite the development of ranking optimization techniques, pointwise loss remains the dominating approach for click-through rate prediction. It can be attributed to the calibration ability of the pointwise loss since the prediction can be viewed as the click probability. In practice, a CTR prediction model is also commonly assessed with the ranking ability. To optimize the ranking ability, ranking loss (e.g., pairwise or listwise loss) can be adopted as they usually achieve better rankings than pointwise loss. Previous studies have experimented with a direct combination of the two losses to obtain the benefit from both losses and observed an improved performance. However, previous studies break the meaning of output logit as the click-through rate, which may lead to sub-optimal solutions. To address this issue, we propose an approach that can Jointly optimize the Ranking and Calibration abilities (JRC for short). JRC improves the ranking ability by contrasting the logit value for the sample with different labels and constrains the predicted probability to be a function of the logit subtraction. We further show that JRC consolidates the interpretation of logits, where the logits model the joint distribution. With such an interpretation, we prove that JRC approximately optimizes the contextualized hybrid discriminative-generative objective. Experiments on public and industrial datasets and online A/B testing show that our approach improves both ranking and calibration abilities. Since May 2022, JRC has been deployed on the display advertising platform of Alibaba and has obtained significant performance improvements."},"title":{"value":"Joint Optimization of Ranking and Calibration with Contextualized Hybrid Model"},"authors":{"value":["Xiang-Rong Sheng","Jingyue Gao","Yueyao Cheng","Siran Yang","Shuguang Han","Hongbo Deng","Yuning Jiang","Jian Xu","Bo Zheng"]}},"tmdate":1768973855948,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731488704987,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bo_Zheng5"],"forum":"iH11M5GkhH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":219431,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768973855948,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"iH11M5GkhH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"KDD 2023"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/KDD/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"sheng|joint_optimization_of_ranking_and_calibration_with_contextualized_hybrid_model"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xiang-Rong_Sheng1","~Jingyue_Gao3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yueyao_Cheng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Siran_Yang:","~Shuguang_Han1","~Hongbo_Deng1","~Yuning_Jiang4","~Jian_Xu8","~Bo_Zheng5"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3580305.3599851"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/kdd/ShengGCYHDJXZ23,\n  author={Xiang-Rong Sheng and Jingyue Gao and Yueyao Cheng and Siran Yang and Shuguang Han and Hongbo Deng and Yuning Jiang and Jian Xu and Bo Zheng},\n  title={Joint Optimization of Ranking and Calibration with Contextualized Hybrid Model},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={4813-4822},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3580305.3599851},\n  booktitle={KDD},\n  crossref={conf/kdd/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite the development of ranking optimization techniques, pointwise loss remains the dominating approach for click-through rate prediction. It can be attributed to the calibration ability of the pointwise loss since the prediction can be viewed as the click probability. In practice, a CTR prediction model is also commonly assessed with the ranking ability. To optimize the ranking ability, ranking loss (e.g., pairwise or listwise loss) can be adopted as they usually achieve better rankings than pointwise loss. Previous studies have experimented with a direct combination of the two losses to obtain the benefit from both losses and observed an improved performance. However, previous studies break the meaning of output logit as the click-through rate, which may lead to sub-optimal solutions. To address this issue, we propose an approach that can Jointly optimize the Ranking and Calibration abilities (JRC for short). JRC improves the ranking ability by contrasting the logit value for the sample with different labels and constrains the predicted probability to be a function of the logit subtraction. We further show that JRC consolidates the interpretation of logits, where the logits model the joint distribution. With such an interpretation, we prove that JRC approximately optimizes the contextualized hybrid discriminative-generative objective. Experiments on public and industrial datasets and online A/B testing show that our approach improves both ranking and calibration abilities. Since May 2022, JRC has been deployed on the display advertising platform of Alibaba and has obtained significant performance improvements."},"title":{"value":"Joint Optimization of Ranking and Calibration with Contextualized Hybrid Model"},"authors":{"value":["Xiang-Rong Sheng","Jingyue Gao","Yueyao Cheng","Siran Yang","Shuguang Han","Hongbo Deng","Yuning Jiang","Jian Xu","Bo Zheng"]}},"tmdate":1768558297249,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1731488696871,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bo_Zheng5"],"forum":"TM1Qzfpe1V","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":219347,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1768558297249,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"TM1Qzfpe1V","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Biomed. Eng. 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10/9119878/08895996.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TBE/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|adaptive_calibration_of_electrode_array_shifts_enables_robust_myoelectric_control"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xu_Zhang_0002:","~Le_Wu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bin_Yu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiang_Chen_0004:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xun_Chen_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TBME.2019.2952890"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tbe/ZhangWYCC20,\n  author={Xu Zhang and Le Wu and Bin Yu and Xiang Chen and Xun Chen},\n  title={Adaptive Calibration of Electrode Array Shifts Enables Robust Myoelectric Control},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Biomed. Eng.},\n  volume={67},\n  number={7},\n  pages={1947-1957},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TBME.2019.2952890}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Objective: The objective of this work is to develop a novel method for adaptive calibration of the electrode array shifts toward achieving robust myoelectric pattern-recognition control. Methods: This work is inspired by the idea of image object detection when high-density surface electromyogram signals recorded from a two-dimensional electrode array carry rich spatial information and can serve as a muscular activation image. A convolutional neural network involving transfer learning (from a network for image recognition) is used to learn muscular activity patterns at an original/baseline position of an electrode array. The shift of an electrode array can be estimated by identifying and matching partially overlapped regions between the training images of muscular activation and the testing images at any other position, in an unsupervised manner. Given the calibration of an electrode array shift, the identification of muscular activity patterns is implemented accordingly. The performance of the proposed method was evaluated with data recorded by a 10 × 10 electrode array placed over the forearm extensors of 10 subjects performing 6 wrist and finger extension tasks. Results: The proposed method achieved high task classification accuracies around 95% and outperformed five traditional methods under conditions with multiple designated shifts in offline experiments and a random shift in online testing. Conclusion: The proposed method is demonstrated to be a promising solution for the automatic and adaptive calibration of electrode array shifts. Significance: This work will enhance the robustness of myoelectric control systems."},"title":{"value":"Adaptive Calibration of Electrode Array Shifts Enables Robust Myoelectric Control"},"authors":{"value":["Xu Zhang","Le Wu","Bin Yu","Xiang Chen","Xun Chen"]}},"tmdate":1768274544234,"pdate":1577836800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tbe/ZhangWYCC20"],"tcdate":1768274538869,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Le_Wu1"],"forum":"3KLYvFarVA","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":746721,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768274544234,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"3KLYvFarVA","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Secure Code Generation","LLM Agent"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language model (LLM) agents have shown strong potential in automating real-world software engineering tasks. However, their deployment introduces new security risks, particularly their susceptibility to adversarial attacks that can manipulate agents into generating vulnerable or malicious code. Detecting such adversarially induced behavior remains a significant challenge. Existing detection approaches include guardrail model-based methods and static analysis-based methods. Guardrail models struggle in long-context settings due to degraded reasoning, while static analyzers depend on predefined security checks whose coverage may not extend to obfuscated or task-dependent malicious behavior. To address these limitations, we propose \\tool, a graph-based detector for adversarially manipulated prompt-and-patch pairs. Inspired by semantic alignment between natural language and code, \\tool first decomposes prompts into structured intent blocks and generated code into code blocks, enabling fine-grained modeling in long-context settings. It then treats these blocks as nodes and constructs an intent-code graph that captures their semantic correspondences and structural relationships. A graph neural network learns from semantic node representations and their connectivity using benign and synthetically augmented graphs, without training on examples from the evaluated attack algorithms. Experiments across diverse benchmarks, attack methods, and agent-LLM configurations show that \\tool generally outperforms the evaluated baselines under attacks with code obfuscation, while static checkers' performance varies substantially across settings despite their advantage on non-obfuscated runs. These results support structural misalignment as a promising detection signal beyond predefined vulnerability checks. Our implementation and generated prompt-and-patch data are publicly available."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026securing,\ntitle={Securing Agentic Code Generation with Intent Code  Graph Learning},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=o7WvHD1nBF},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Securing Agentic Code Generation with Intent Code  Graph Learning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/01c2c02e4deb672aec3d5342d33f4ad74b269e8a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791233784238,"tcdate":1789749802947,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission46084/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission46084/Authors"],"forum":"o7WvHD1nBF","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":46084,"cdate":1789749802947,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission46084/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791233784238,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"o7WvHD1nBF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Neurocomputing 2016"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IJON/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"pregowska|temporal_code_versus_rate_code_for_binary_information_sources"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Agnieszka_Pregowska:","~Janusz_Szczepanski1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Eligiusz_Wajnryb:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2016.08.034"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ijon/PregowskaSW16,\n  author={Agnieszka Pregowska and Janusz Szczepanski and Eligiusz Wajnryb},\n  title={Temporal code versus rate code for binary Information Sources},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  journal={Neurocomputing},\n  volume={216},\n  pages={756-762},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2016.08.034}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Highlights•Temporal code and firing rate code are compared for two types of Information Sources.•Coefficient combining information with energy was proposed.•Parameter which determines relation between both codes was found.•Large spike train variability leads to qualitative difference between the codes."},"title":{"value":"Temporal code versus rate code for binary Information Sources"},"authors":{"value":["Agnieszka Pregowska","Janusz Szczepanski","Eligiusz Wajnryb"]}},"tmdate":1752829050788,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1752829043344,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Janusz_Szczepanski1"],"forum":"sC03CBVgt8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":573270,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1752829050788,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"sC03CBVgt8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"COLING 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.482.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/COLING/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|finegrained_featuresbased_code_search_for_precise_querycode_matching"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinting_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mengqiu_Cheng:","~Mengzhen_Wang2","","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiayuan_Xie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Cai_0001:","~Qing_Li5"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.482/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/coling/ZhangCWGXCL25,\n  author={Xinting Zhang and Mengqiu Cheng and Mengzhen Wang and Songwen Gong and Jiayuan Xie and Yi Cai and Qing Li},\n  title={Fine-Grained Features-based Code Search for Precise Query-Code Matching},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={7229-7238},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.482/},\n  booktitle={COLING},\n  crossref={conf/coling/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code search aims to quickly locate target code snippets from databases using natural language queries, which promotes code reusability. Existing methods can effectively obtain aligned token-level and query word-level features. However, these studies usually represent the semantics of code and query by averaging the features of each token and word respectively, which makes it difficult to accurately capture the code details that are closely related to the query. To address this issue, we propose a fine-grained code search model that consists of a cross-modal encoder, a mapping layer, and a classification layer. Specifically, we utilize a pre-trained model, GraphCodeBERT, in the cross-modal encoder to align features. In the mapping layer, we introduce a co-attention network to capture the fine-grained interactions between code and query, ensuring a model can precisely identify key code segments relevant to the query. Finally, in the classification layer, we incorporate instruction learning techniques that leverage contextual reasoning to improve the accuracy of query-code matching. Experimental results show that our proposed model significantly outperforms existing methods across multiple programming language datasets."},"title":{"value":"Fine-Grained Features-based Code Search for Precise Query-Code Matching"},"authors":{"value":["Xinting Zhang","Mengqiu Cheng","Mengzhen Wang","Songwen Gong","Jiayuan Xie","Yi Cai","Qing Li"]}},"tmdate":1782309902686,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1753334397600,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Songwen_Gong1"],"forum":"UfTJbZQ5Pg","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":585409,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1782309902686,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UfTJbZQ5Pg","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"COLING 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.482.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/COLING/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|finegrained_featuresbased_code_search_for_precise_querycode_matching"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xinting_Zhang3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mengqiu_Cheng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mengzhen_Wang:","~Songwen_Gong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiayuan_Xie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Cai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qing_Li:"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.482/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/coling/ZhangCWGXCL25,\n  author={Xinting Zhang and Mengqiu Cheng and Mengzhen Wang and Songwen Gong and Jiayuan Xie and Yi Cai and Qing Li},\n  title={Fine-Grained Features-based Code Search for Precise Query-Code Matching},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={7229-7238},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.482/},\n  booktitle={COLING},\n  crossref={conf/coling/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code search aims to quickly locate target code snippets from databases using natural language queries, which promotes code reusability. Existing methods can effectively obtain aligned token-level and query word-level features. However, these studies usually represent the semantics of code and query by averaging the features of each token and word respectively, which makes it difficult to accurately capture the code details that are closely related to the query. To address this issue, we propose a fine-grained code search model that consists of a cross-modal encoder, a mapping layer, and a classification layer. Specifically, we utilize a pre-trained model, GraphCodeBERT, in the cross-modal encoder to align features. In the mapping layer, we introduce a co-attention network to capture the fine-grained interactions between code and query, ensuring a model can precisely identify key code segments relevant to the query. Finally, in the classification layer, we incorporate instruction learning techniques that leverage contextual reasoning to improve the accuracy of query-code matching. Experimental results show that our proposed model significantly outperforms existing methods across multiple programming language datasets."},"title":{"value":"Fine-Grained Features-based Code Search for Precise Query-Code Matching"},"authors":{"value":["Xinting Zhang","Mengqiu Cheng","Mengzhen Wang","Songwen Gong","Jiayuan Xie","Yi Cai","Qing Li"]}},"tmdate":1747881303919,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1745149681889,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~xinting_zhang2"],"forum":"daKkV2uMPR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":404911,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747881303919,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"daKkV2uMPR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.14485v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"qi|intersphere_consistencybased_method_for_cameraprojector_pair_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2304.14485"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2304-14485,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhaoshuai Qi and Jingqi Pang and Yifeng Hao and Yanning Zhang},\n  title={Inter-sphere consistency-based method for camera-projector pair calibration},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2304.14485},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2304.14485}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We construct constraints from consistency between estimated parameters from different spheres, termed inter-sphere consistency. It facilitates more flexible calibration using only two spheres, which has been considered a challenging and not well addressed ill-posed problem."},"title":{"value":"Inter-sphere consistency-based method for camera-projector pair calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhaoshuai Qi","username":"~Zhaoshuai_Qi1"},{"fullname":"Jingqi Pang","username":""},{"fullname":"Yifeng Hao","username":""},{"fullname":"Yanning Zhang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1783568525236,"pdate":1703980800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2304-14485"],"tcdate":1783568518766,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Zhaoshuai_Qi1"],"forum":"fMQZ0z1dIQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":50840,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1783568525236,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"fMQZ0z1dIQ","version":2},{"content":{"checklist_submissions":{"value":["I confirm that the submission is anonymous."]},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to WiML @ NeurIPS 2026"},"outreach_channel":{"value":["WiML google group mail / Email accouncement"]},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"home_institution":{"value":"Western Europe"},"keywords":{"value":["Structured Pruning","Uncertainty","Calibration","Confidence Reliability"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nmitra2026beyond,\ntitle={Beyond Accuracy: Calibration-Aware Structured Pruning for Reliable Deep Neural Networks},\nauthor={Pallavi Mitra},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=QML2A73j26}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Beyond Accuracy: Calibration-Aware Structured Pruning for Reliable Deep Neural Networks"},"confirm_age":{"value":"Yes"},"public_preprint":{"value":"NA"},"limit_of_paper":{"value":"Yes"},"paperhash":{"value":"mitra|beyond_accuracy_calibrationaware_structured_pruning_for_reliable_deep_neural_networks"},"email_sharing_with_WiML_consent":{"value":"Yes"},"residence":{"value":["Germany"]},"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"We move structured pruning beyond accuracy by making calibration a joint objective for reliable compression and confidence-based selective inference."},"WiML_Code_of_Conduct":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"We develop calibration-aware structured pruning methods that improve prediction reliability alongside computational efficiency, and extend them to confidence-based cascades for reliable selective inference."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/59650ac521ffa00de841b89546943b0953fe5ee7.pdf"},"anonymized_and_aggregated_information_consent":{"value":"Yes"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/Rejected_Submission"},"Funding_acknowledgement":{"value":"Yes"},"screening_acknowledgement":{"value":"Yes"},"preferred_presentation_hub":{"value":"Paris, France"},"confirm_original_research":{"value":"Yes"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Pallavi_Mitra1","fullname":"Pallavi Mitra","institutions":[{"name":"Berliner Hochschule für Technik","domain":"bht-berlin.de","country":"DE"}]}]}},"tmdate":1790773281850,"tcdate":1786977472948,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/Submission247/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/Submission247/Authors"],"forum":"QML2A73j26","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":247,"cdate":1786977472948,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/-/Submission_Release","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML/-/Edit"],"mdate":1790773281850,"odate":1790773197596,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/WiML","id":"QML2A73j26","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.emnlp-main.68.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|codet5_open_code_large_language_models_for_code_understanding_and_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yue_Wang_0034:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hung_Le:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Akhilesh_Gotmare:","~Nghi_D._Q._Bui1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junnan_Li_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Steven_C._H._Hoi:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.emnlp-main.68"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/WangLGB0H23,\n  author={Yue Wang and Hung Le and Akhilesh Gotmare and Nghi D. Q. Bui and Junnan Li and Steven C. H. Hoi},\n  title={CodeT5+: Open Code Large Language Models for Code Understanding and Generation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1069-1088},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.emnlp-main.68},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) pretrained on vast source code have achieved prominent progress in code intelligence. However, existing code LLMs have two main limitations. First, they often adopt a specific architecture (encoder-only or decoder-only) or rely on a unified encoder-decoder network for different downstream tasks, lacking the flexibility to operate in the optimal architecture for a specific task. Secondly, they often employ a limited set of pretraining objectives which might not be relevant to some tasks and hence result in substantial performance degrade. To address these limitations, we propose “CodeT5+”, a family of encoder-decoder LLMs for code in which component modules can be flexibly combined to suit a wide range of code tasks. Such flexibility is enabled by our proposed mixture of pretraining objectives, which cover span denoising, contrastive learning, text-code matching, and causal LM pretraining tasks, on both unimodal and bimodal multilingual code corpora. Furthermore, we propose to initialize CodeT5+ with frozen off-the-shelf LLMs without training from scratch to efficiently scale up our models, and explore instruction-tuning to align with natural language instructions. We extensively evaluate CodeT5+ on over 20 code-related benchmarks in different settings, including zero-shot, finetuning, and instruction-tuning. We observe state-of-the-art (SoTA) performance on various code-related tasks, and our instruction-tuned CodeT5+ 16B achieves new SoTA results of 35.0% pass@1 and 54.5% pass@10 on the HumanEval code generation task against other open code LLMs, even surpassing the OpenAI code-cushman-001 model."},"title":{"value":"CodeT5+: Open Code Large Language Models for Code Understanding and Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Yue Wang","Hung Le","Akhilesh Gotmare","Nghi D. Q. Bui","Junnan Li","Steven C. H. Hoi"]}},"tmdate":1727727176835,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727727173431,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Nghi_D._Q._Bui1"],"forum":"5LdmtfcHEn","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":123513,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727727176835,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5LdmtfcHEn","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We propose CodeT5+, a new family of open code LLMs with a dynamic architecture that can flexibly operate in different modes (encoder-only, decoder-only, and encoder-decoder) to support a wide range of code understanding and generation tasks."},"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2023 Main"},"Submission_Track_2":{"value":"NLP Applications"},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Model","Code Intelligence","Code Understanding and Generation"]},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) pretrained on vast source code have achieved prominent progress in code intelligence. However, existing code LLMs have two main limitations. First, they often adopt a specific architecture (encoder-only or decoder-only) or rely on a  unified encoder-decoder network for different downstream tasks, lacking the flexibility to operate in the optimal architecture for a specific task. Secondly, they often employ a limited set of pretraining objectives which might not be relevant to some  tasks and hence result in substantial performance degrade. To address these limitations, we propose “CodeT5+”, a family of encoder-decoder LLMs for code in which component modules can be flexibly combined to suit a wide range of  code tasks.  Such flexibility is enabled by our proposed  mixture of pretraining objectives, which cover span denoising, contrastive learning, text-code matching, and causal LM pretraining tasks, on both unimodal and bimodal multilingual code corpora. Furthermore, we propose to initialize CodeT5+ with frozen off-the-shelf LLMs without training from scratch to efficiently scale up our models, and explore instruction-tuning to align with natural language instructions. We extensively evaluate CodeT5+ on over 20 code-related benchmarks in different settings, including zero-shot, finetuning, and instruction-tuning. We observe state-of-the-art (SoTA)  performance on various code-related tasks, and our instruction-tuned CodeT5+ 16B achieves new SoTA results of 35.0% pass@1 and 54.5% pass@10 on the HumanEval code generation task against other open code LLMs, even surpassing the OpenAI code-cushman-001 model."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nwang2023codet,\ntitle={CodeT5+: Open Code Large Language Models for Code Understanding and Generation},\nauthor={Yue Wang and Hung Le and Akhilesh Deepak Gotmare and Nghi D. Q. Bui and Junnan Li and Steven Hoi},\nbooktitle={The 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=uu6Oq7MN7g}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CodeT5+: Open Code Large Language Models for Code Understanding and Generation"},"Submission_Type":{"value":"Regular Long Paper"},"pdf":{"value":"/attachment/873693977709a6e9f377b4c48d6f3b88dd5d5b45.pdf"},"Submission_Track":{"value":"Language Modeling and Analysis of Language Models"},"venueid":{"value":"EMNLP/2023/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|codet5_open_code_large_language_models_for_code_understanding_and_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yue_Wang19","~Hung_Le2","~Akhilesh_Deepak_Gotmare1","~Nghi_D._Q._Bui1","~Junnan_Li2","~Steven_Hoi2"]},"authors":{"value":["Yue Wang","Hung Le","Akhilesh Deepak Gotmare","Nghi D. Q. Bui","Junnan Li","Steven Hoi"]}},"tmdate":1701459718631,"pdate":1696709242997,"tcdate":1686708297529,"writers":["EMNLP/2023/Conference","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission851/Authors"],"signatures":["EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission851/Authors"],"forum":"uu6Oq7MN7g","number":851,"cdate":1686708297529,"mdate":1701459718631,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Submission","EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Post_Submission","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission851/-/Revision","EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Edit","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission851/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"odate":1701459718616,"domain":"EMNLP/2023/Conference","id":"uu6Oq7MN7g","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MSR 2014"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MSR/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"krutz|a_code_clone_oracle"},"authorids":{"value":["~Daniel_E._Krutz1","~Wei_Le1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2597073.2597127"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/msr/KrutzL14,\n  author={Daniel E. Krutz and Wei Le},\n  title={A code clone oracle},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={388-391},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/2597073.2597127},\n  booktitle={MSR},\n  crossref={conf/msr/2014}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code clones are functionally equivalent code segments. De- tecting code clones is important for determining bugs, fixes and software reuse. Code clone detection is also essential for developing fast and precise code search algorithms. How- ever, the challenge of such research is to evaluate that the clones detected are indeed functionally equivalent, consider- ing the majority of clones are not textual or even syntacti- cally identical. The goal of this work is to generate a set of method level code clones with a high confidence to help to evaluate future code clone detection and code search tools to evaluate their techniques. We selected three open source programs, Apache, Python and PostgreSQL, and randomly sampled a total of 1536 function pairs. To confirm whether or not these function pairs indicate a clone and what types of clones they belong to, we recruited three experts who have experience in code clone research and four students who have experience in programming for manual inspection. For confi- dence of the data, the experts consulted multiple code clone detection tools to make the consensus. To assist manual inspection, we built a tool to automatically load function pairs of interest and record the manual inspection results. We found that none of the 66 pairs are textual identical type- 1 clones, and 9 pairs are type-4 clones. Our data is available at: http://phd.gccis.rit.edu/weile/data/cloneoracle/."},"title":{"value":"A code clone oracle"},"authors":{"value":["Daniel E. Krutz","Wei Le"]}},"tmdate":1747237039787,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1727894273296,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wei_Le1"],"forum":"RwEINVxlu0","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":137546,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747237039787,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"RwEINVxlu0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"APGV 2005"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/APGV/2005"},"paperhash":{"value":"wuerger|a_display_calibration_technique_based_on_invariant_human_colour_mechanisms"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sophie_M._Wuerger:","~Dimosthenis_Karatzas1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Georg_F._Meyer:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1080402.1080454"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/apgv/WuergerKM05,\n  author={Sophie M. Wuerger and Dimosthenis Karatzas and Georg F. Meyer},\n  title={A display calibration technique based on invariant human colour mechanisms},\n  year={2005},\n  cdate={1104537600000},\n  pages={171},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/1080402.1080454},\n  booktitle={APGV},\n  crossref={conf/apgv/2005}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"When human observers are asked to adjust a coloured light such that it appears neither red nor green, or such that it appears neither yellow nor blue, most colour-normal observers have no difficulty in making these adjustments. Furthermore, these colour appearance judgements are not significantly influenced by language or age [Saunders and van Brakel 1997] and individual differences in colour sensitivity are not reflected in the unique hues settings [Webster et al. 2000]. The human colour system seems to be able to calibrate itself so that there is a remarkable agreement across observers in relation to these unique hue judgements. Here we show how we can use the invariance of these unique hue judgements to develop a colour calibration technique for display devices which eliminates the need for an external calibration standard or a measurement device."},"title":{"value":"A display calibration technique based on invariant human colour mechanisms"},"authors":{"value":["Sophie M. Wuerger","Dimosthenis Karatzas","Georg F. Meyer"]}},"tmdate":1763110915674,"pdate":1104537600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/apgv/WuergerKM05"],"tcdate":1763110891005,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dimosthenis_Karatzas1"],"forum":"juIKzWH11y","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":702677,"cdate":1104537600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1763110915674,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"juIKzWH11y","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"DCOSS 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8270697/8271927/08271952.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/DCOSS/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"simonjan|distributed_visual_sensor_network_calibration_based_on_joint_object_detections"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jennifer_Simonjan:","~Bernhard_Rinner2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/DCOSS.2017.17"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/dcoss/SimonjanR17,\n  author={Jennifer Simonjan and Bernhard Rinner},\n  title={Distributed Visual Sensor Network Calibration Based on Joint Object Detections},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  pages={109-116},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/DCOSS.2017.17},\n  booktitle={DCOSS},\n  crossref={conf/dcoss/2017}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In this paper we present a distributed, autonomous network calibration algorithm, which enables visual sensor networks to gather knowledge about the network topology. A calibrated sensor network provides the basis for more robust applications, since nodes are aware of their spatial neighbors. In our approach, sensor nodes estimate relative positions and orientations of nodes with overlapping fields of view based on jointly detected objects and geometric relations. Distance and angle measurements are the only information required to be exchanged between nodes. The process works iteratively, first calibrating camera neighbors in a pairwise manner and then spreading the calibration information through the network. Further, each node operates within its local coordinate system avoiding the need for any global coordinates. While existing methods mostly exploit computer vision algorithms to relate nodes to each other based on their images, we solely rely on geometric constraints."},"title":{"value":"Distributed Visual Sensor Network Calibration Based on Joint Object Detections"},"authors":{"value":["Jennifer Simonjan","Bernhard Rinner"]}},"tmdate":1731564485592,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1731564459434,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bernhard_Rinner2"],"forum":"pUGSce5mgW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":238482,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731564485592,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"pUGSce5mgW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EUSIPCO 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9908519/9909515/09909678.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EUSIPCO/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"tegler|sensor_node_calibration_in_presence_of_a_dominant_reflective_plane"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Erik_Tegler:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Martin_Larsson:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Magnus_Oskarsson:","~Kalle_Åström1"]},"html":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9909678"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/eusipco/TeglerLOA22,\n  author={Erik Tegler and Martin Larsson and Magnus Oskarsson and Kalle Åström},\n  title={Sensor node calibration in presence of a dominant reflective plane},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={1941-1945},\n  url={https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9909678},\n  booktitle={EUSIPCO},\n  crossref={conf/eusipco/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent advances in simultaneous estimation of both receiver and sender positions in ad-hoc sensor networks have made it possible to automatically calibrate node positions - a prerequisite for many applications. In man-made environments there are often large planar reflective surfaces that give significant reverberations. In this paper, we study geometric problems of receiver-sender node calibration in the presence of such reflective planes. We establish a rank-1 factorization problem that can be used to simplify the estimation. We also show how to estimate offsets, in the Time difference of arrival case, using only the rank constraint. Finally, we present a new solver for the minimal cases of sender-receiver position estimation. These contributions result in a powerful stratified approach for the node calibration problem, given a reflective plane. The methods are verified with both synthetic and real data."},"title":{"value":"Sensor node calibration in presence of a dominant reflective plane"},"authors":{"value":["Erik Tegler","Martin Larsson","Magnus Oskarsson","Kalle Åström"]}},"tmdate":1731487489618,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731484195570,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kalle_Åström1"],"forum":"zX1OCrVDBS","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":214005,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731487489618,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zX1OCrVDBS","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2024 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Natural Language Processing","Adversarial Learning","Unsupervised Learning"]},"primary_area":{"value":"unsupervised, self-supervised, semi-supervised, and supervised representation learning"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Recently, there has been a significant increase in the use of large-scale Question-Answering (QA) models. However, these models have started to reveal some limitations, such as generating incorrect information, which are holding back their progress. Most of the current calibration methods have their own problems, like the high cost of collecting fine-tuning data, limited interpretability and high intrusiveness, making them less suitable for wider use. To tackle this challenge, we introduce a new machine learning paradigm called \"Generalized Adversarial Learning\" (GAL) to improve the calibration of large QA models without the need for supervision. We explain the core principles and ideas behind GAL and present empirical evidence demonstrating its effectiveness, interpretability, and non-intrusive nature, achieving performance surpassing the state-of-the-art in some metrics even within the field of supervised learning."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nchen2024generalized,\ntitle={Generalized Adversarial Learning--An Innovative Unsupervised Paradigm In {LLM}'s Calibration},\nauthor={ZiXuan Chen and Jinxiu Liu and Hanlin Wang and Nuo Chen and Qi Liu},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=vmlboYfvpf}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Generalized Adversarial Learning--An Innovative Unsupervised Paradigm In LLM's Calibration"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2d9191a89fcefa334b509d04e3ea24d029fda549.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|generalized_adversarial_learningan_innovative_unsupervised_paradigm_in_llms_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~ZiXuan_Chen7","~Jinxiu_Liu1","~Hanlin_Wang3","~Nuo_Chen7","~Qi_Liu19"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["ZiXuan Chen","Jinxiu Liu","Hanlin Wang","Nuo Chen","Qi Liu"]}},"tmdate":1711385361920,"tcdate":1695402382866,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5933/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5933/Authors"],"forum":"vmlboYfvpf","number":5933,"cdate":1695402382866,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission5933/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1711385361920,"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"vmlboYfvpf","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We use unlabeled data to improve deep ensemble diversity and calibration for small to medium-sized training sets."},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2024"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["deep ensembles","calibration","uncertainty","diversity","PAC-Bayes"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/0adf8fa67f1a179ce70dc8554d5851ada0641314.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"societal considerations including fairness, safety, privacy"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"We present a method to improve the calibration of deep ensembles in the small data regime in the presence of unlabeled data. Our approach, which we name $\\nu$-ensembles, is extremely easy to implement: given an unlabeled set, for each unlabeled data point, we simply fit a different randomly selected label with each ensemble member. We provide a theoretical analysis based on a PAC-Bayes bound which guarantees that for such a labeling we obtain low negative log-likelihood and high ensemble diversity on testing samples. Empirically, through detailed experiments, we find that for low to moderately-sized training sets, $\\nu$-ensembles are more diverse and provide better calibration than standard ensembles, sometimes significantly."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\npitas2024nuensembles,\ntitle={\\${\\textbackslash}nu\\$-ensembles: Improving deep ensemble calibration in the small data regime},\nauthor={Konstantinos Pitas and Julyan Arbel},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=wHgu98u8Sc}\n}"},"title":{"value":"$\\nu$-ensembles: Improving deep ensemble calibration in the small data regime"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/9044277220ff251fc90d26dea121800d5a6d30b2.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"pitas|\\nuensembles_improving_deep_ensemble_calibration_in_the_small_data_regime"},"authorids":{"value":["~Konstantinos_Pitas1","~Julyan_Arbel1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors' identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Konstantinos Pitas","Julyan Arbel"]}},"tmdate":1707625758940,"tcdate":1695494318474,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission8035/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission8035/Authors"],"forum":"wHgu98u8Sc","number":8035,"cdate":1695494318474,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission8035/-/Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/Submission8035/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2024/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1707625758940,"odate":1697213872796,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Conference","id":"wHgu98u8Sc","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Regularize, not rescale: entropy + reverse-KL guidance prevent gradient vanishing in classifier-guided diffusion, improving FID without retraining."},"venue":{"value":"SPIGM @ NeurIPS"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d55d3ad739bbf99d0b340e68baba4c8dd2a9b1d5.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Diffusion models","classifier guidance","conditional image generation","entropy regularization","reverse-KL guidance","tilted loss"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SPIGM"},"paperhash":{"value":"javid|enhancing_diffusion_model_guidance_through_calibration_and_regularization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Alireza_Javid1","~Amirhossein_Bagheri1","ngprelcic@ucsd.edu"]},"abstract":{"value":"Classifier-guided diffusion models have emerged as a powerful approach for conditional image generation, but they suffer from overconfident predictions during early denoising steps, causing the guidance gradient to vanish. This paper presents two complementary contributions to enhance classifier-guided diffusion models. First, we introduce a differentiable Smooth Expected Calibration Error (Smooth ECE) loss that improves classifier calibration with minimal fine-tuning, achieving approximately a 3\\% improvement in Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) scores. Second, we propose enhanced sampling guidance methods that operate on off-the-shelf classifiers without requiring retraining. Our approach includes: (1) tilted sampling that leverages batch-level information to control outlier influence, (2) adaptive entropy-regularized sampling to maintain diversity, and (3) a novel divergence-regularized sampling method that adds a class-aware, mode-covering correction that strengthens movement toward the target class while maintaining exploration. Theoretical analysis reveals that our methods effectively combine enhanced\ntarget direction guidance with controlled diversity exploration, mitigating gradient vanishing. Experimental results on ImageNet demonstrate that our best divergence-guided sampling achieves an FID of 2.13 while maintaining competitive precision and recall metrics. Our methods provide a practical solution for improving conditional generation quality without the computational overhead of classifier and diffusion model retraining."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\njavid2025enhancing,\ntitle={Enhancing Diffusion Model Guidance through Calibration and Regularization},\nauthor={Alireza Javid and Amirhossein Bagheri and Nuria Gonzalez-Prelcic},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Structured Probabilistic Inference {\\&} Generative Modeling},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ytT6MWyYKR}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Enhancing Diffusion Model Guidance through Calibration and Regularization"},"authors":{"value":["Alireza Javid","Amirhossein Bagheri","Nuria Gonzalez-Prelcic"]}},"tmdate":1766519741626,"pdate":1758591840463,"tcdate":1755882121684,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SPIGM","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SPIGM/Submission28/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SPIGM/Submission28/Authors"],"forum":"ytT6MWyYKR","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":28,"cdate":1755882121684,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SPIGM/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SPIGM/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SPIGM/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SPIGM/Submission28/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1766519741626,"odate":1765718426826,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/SPIGM","id":"ytT6MWyYKR","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Image Vis. Comput. 2009"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IVC/2009"},"paperhash":{"value":"marinakis|selfcalibration_of_a_visionbased_sensor_network"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dimitri_Marinakis:","~Gregory_Dudek1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.imavis.2006.06.009"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ivc/MarinakisD09,\n  author={Dimitri Marinakis and Gregory Dudek},\n  title={Self-calibration of a vision-based sensor network},\n  year={2009},\n  cdate={1230768000000},\n  journal={Image Vis. Comput.},\n  volume={27},\n  number={1-2},\n  pages={116-130},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.imavis.2006.06.009}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"When a network of vision-based sensors is emplaced in an environment for applications such as surveillance or monitoring the spatial relationships between the sensing units must be inferred or computed for self-calibration purposes. In this paper we describe a technique to solve one aspect of this self-calibration problem: automatically determining the topology and connectivity information of a network of cameras based on a statistical analysis of observed motion in the environment. While the technique can use labels from reliable cameras systems, the algorithm is powerful enough to function using ambiguous tracking data. The method requires no prior knowledge of the relative locations of the cameras and operates under very weak environmental assumptions. Our approach stochastically samples plausible agent trajectories based on a delay model that allows for transitions to and from sources and sinks in the environment. The technique demonstrates considerable robustness both to sensor error and non-trivial patterns of agent motion. The output of the method is a Markov model describing the behavior of agents in the system and the underlying traffic patterns. The concept is demonstrated with simulation data for systems containing up to 10 agents and verified with experiments conducted on a six camera sensor network."},"title":{"value":"Self-calibration of a vision-based sensor network"},"authors":{"value":["Dimitri Marinakis","Gregory Dudek"]}},"tmdate":1762524391349,"pdate":1230768000000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/ivc/MarinakisD09"],"tcdate":1762524258415,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Gregory_Dudek1"],"forum":"m1VOn4tHNt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":676238,"cdate":1230768000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762524391349,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"m1VOn4tHNt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"2026 IEEE International Conference on Data Mining"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f96dab56c866148c77c64ae8636e7141c5d9b2bd.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"binli|too_sure_to_be_safe_model_calibration_for_reliable_log_anomaly_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["~BinLi3","~Dongdong_Wang1","~Siyang_Lu1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Online log anomaly detection is critical for maintaining the reliability of large-scale computing systems. Although recent language model-based log anomaly detectors achieve strong detection performance, their confidence estimates remain poorly calibrated. We show that these detectors frequently assign excessive confidence to incorrect predictions, particularly for anomalous logs under severe class imbalance. Moreover, confidence on erroneous predictions remains persistently high even when conventional calibration metrics indicate good calibration, creating a critical reliability gap for operational monitoring systems. To address this issue, we propose Log Reconstruction and Distance (LoRD), a lightweight post-hoc calibration framework for reliable log anomaly detection. LoRD learns prediction-route-specific reliability models from latent representations of correctly classified validation samples and estimates prediction reliability through route-wise reconstruction distances. Based on the estimated reliability, LoRD selectively recalibrates high-risk predictions to suppress overconfident errors while preserving reliable predictions. Extensive experiments on four large-scale log benchmark datasets and multiple language model-based detectors demonstrate that LoRD consistently improves confidence reliability and substantially reduces overconfident anomaly-related errors without sacrificing anomaly detection performance."},"title":{"value":"Too Sure to Be Safe: Model Calibration for Reliable Log Anomaly Detection"},"authors":{"value":["BinLi","Dongdong Wang","Siyang Lu"]}},"tmdate":1790630089435,"pdate":1790308800000,"tcdate":1790630089435,"writers":["~BinLi3","~Dongdong_Wang1","~Siyang_Lu1"],"signatures":["~Dongdong_Wang1"],"forum":"cYhTDf0c3x","license":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license","number":55070,"cdate":1790630089435,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1790630089435,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"cYhTDf0c3x","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/7083369/10683798/10669166.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|edgecalib_multiframe_weighted_edge_features_for_automatic_targetless_lidarcamera_calibration"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3455895"},"abstract":{"value":"In multimodal perception systems, achieving precise extrinsic calibration between LiDAR and camera is of critical importance. However, the pre-calibrated extrinsic parameters may gradually drift during operation, leading to a decrease in the accuracy of the perception system. It is challenging to address this issue using methods based on artificial targets. In this article, we introduce an edge-based approach for automatic targetless calibration of LiDARs and cameras in real-world scenarios. The edge features, which are prevalent in various environments, are used to establish reliable correspondences in images and point clouds. Specifically, we leverage the Segment Anything Model to facilitate the extraction of stable and reliable image edge features. Then a multi-frame weighting strategy is used for feature filtering while alleviating the dependence on the environment. Finally, we estimate accurate extrinsic parameters based on edge correspondence constraints. Our method achieves a mean rotation error of $0.069^{\\circ }$ and a mean translation error of $\\mathbf 1.037\\ \\text{cm}$ on the KITTI dataset, outperforming existing edge-based calibration methods and demonstrating strong robustness, accuracy, and generalization capabilities."},"title":{"value":"EdgeCalib: Multi-Frame Weighted Edge Features for Automatic Targetless LiDAR-Camera Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Xingchen Li","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Xingchen%20Li"},{"fullname":"Yifan Duan","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Yifan%20Duan"},{"fullname":"Beibei Wang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Beibei%20Wang"},{"fullname":"Haojie Ren","username":"~Haojie_Ren2"},{"fullname":"Guoliang You","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Guoliang%20You"},{"fullname":"Yu Sheng","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Yu%20Sheng"},{"fullname":"Jianmin Ji","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Jianmin%20Ji"},{"fullname":"Yanyong Zhang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Yanyong%20Zhang"}]}},"tmdate":1778207513689,"pdate":1730419200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/lra.2024.3455895"],"tcdate":1778207510911,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Haojie_Ren2"],"forum":"p7JFrYKXYL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":69150,"cdate":1763794896799,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1778207513689,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"p7JFrYKXYL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CIFEr 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10772730/10772750/10772909.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CIFER/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"hoshisashi|whackamole_learning_physics_informed_deep_calibration_for_implied_volatility_surface"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kentaro_Hoshisashi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Carolyn_E._Phelan:","~Paolo_Barucca1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CIFEr62890.2024.10772909"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/cifer/HoshisashiPB24,\n  author={Kentaro Hoshisashi and Carolyn E. Phelan and Paolo Barucca},\n  title={Whack-a-mole Learning: Physics- Informed Deep Calibration for Implied Volatility Surface},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={1-8},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CIFEr62890.2024.10772909},\n  booktitle={CIFEr},\n  crossref={conf/cifer/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Calibrating the Implied Volatility Surface (IVS) using sparse market data is an essential task for option pricing in quantitative finance. The calibrated values must provide a solution to a specified partial differential equation (PDE) in addition to obeying no-arbitrage conditions modelled by individual differential inequalities. However, this leads to a multi-objective optimization problem, which emerges in Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) as well as in our generalized framework. In order to address this problem, we propose a novel calibration algorithm called Whack-a-mole Learning (WamL), which integrates self-adaptive and auto-balancing processes for each loss term. The developed algorithm realizes efficient reweighting mechanisms for each objective function, ensuring alignment with constraints of price derivatives to achieve smooth surface fitting while satisfying PDE and no-arbitrage conditions. In our tests, this approach enables the straightforward implementation of a deep calibration method that incorporates no-arbitrage constraints, providing an appropriate fit for uneven and sparse market data. WamL also enhances the representation of risk profiles for option prices, offering a robust and efficient solution for IVS calibration."},"title":{"value":"Whack-a-mole Learning: Physics- Informed Deep Calibration for Implied Volatility Surface"},"authors":{"value":["Kentaro Hoshisashi","Carolyn E. Phelan","Paolo Barucca"]}},"tmdate":1777890655617,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1741350260969,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kentaro_Hoshisashi1"],"forum":"GIVwB6VCmJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":364511,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1777890655617,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"GIVwB6VCmJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.08766v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhuang|sauc_sparsityaware_uncertainty_calibration_for_spatiotemporal_prediction_with_graph_neural_networks"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dingyi_Zhuang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuheng_Bu:","~Guang_Wang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shenhao_Wang:","~Jinhua_Zhao2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.08766"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2409-08766,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Dingyi Zhuang and Yuheng Bu and Guang Wang and Shenhao Wang and Jinhua Zhao},\n  title={SAUC: Sparsity-Aware Uncertainty Calibration for Spatiotemporal Prediction with Graph Neural Networks},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2409.08766},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.08766}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Quantifying uncertainty is crucial for robust and reliable predictions. However, existing spatiotemporal deep learning mostly focuses on deterministic prediction, overlooking the inherent uncertainty in such prediction. Particularly, highly-granular spatiotemporal datasets are often sparse, posing extra challenges in prediction and uncertainty quantification. To address these issues, this paper introduces a novel post-hoc Sparsity-awar Uncertainty Calibration (SAUC) framework, which calibrates uncertainty in both zero and non-zero values. To develop SAUC, we firstly modify the state-of-the-art deterministic spatiotemporal Graph Neural Networks (ST-GNNs) to probabilistic ones in the pre-calibration phase. Then we calibrate the probabilistic ST-GNNs for zero and non-zero values using quantile approaches.Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate that SAUC can effectively fit the variance of sparse data and generalize across two real-world spatiotemporal datasets at various granularities. Specifically, our empirical experiments show a 20\\% reduction in calibration errors in zero entries on the sparse traffic accident and urban crime prediction. Overall, this work demonstrates the theoretical and empirical values of the SAUC framework, thus bridging a significant gap between uncertainty quantification and spatiotemporal prediction."},"title":{"value":"SAUC: Sparsity-Aware Uncertainty Calibration for Spatiotemporal Prediction with Graph Neural Networks"},"authors":{"value":["Dingyi Zhuang","Yuheng Bu","Guang Wang","Shenhao Wang","Jinhua Zhao"]}},"tmdate":1777580655701,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747751238810,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jinhua_Zhao2"],"forum":"h8wSQxkdE8","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":539939,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1777580655701,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"h8wSQxkdE8","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"J. Comput. Soc. Sci. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s42001-024-00302-6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/JOCSS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"shigenaka|masbench_a_benchmarking_for_parameter_calibration_of_multiagent_crowd_simulation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shusuke_Shigenaka:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shunki_Takami:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuki_Tanigaki:","~Shuhei_Watanabe1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Masaki_Onishi:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s42001-024-00302-6"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/jocss/ShigenakaTTWO24,\n  author={Shusuke Shigenaka and Shunki Takami and Yuki Tanigaki and Shuhei Watanabe and Masaki Onishi},\n  title={MAS-Bench: a benchmarking for parameter calibration of multi-agent crowd simulation},\n  year={2024},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1727740800000},\n  journal={J. Comput. Soc. Sci.},\n  volume={7},\n  number={2},\n  pages={2121-2145},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s42001-024-00302-6}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-agent simulation (MAS) has attracted significant attention for the prevention of pedestrian accidents and the spread of infectious diseases caused by overcrowding in recent years. In the MAS paradigm, each pedestrian is represented by a single agent. Control parameters for each agent need to be calibrated based on pedestrian traffic data to reproduce phenomena of interest accurately. Furthermore, observing all pedestrian traffic at large-scale events such as festivals and sports games is difficult. In such cases, parameter optimization is essential so that the appropriate parameters can be determined by solving an error minimization problem between the simulation results and incomplete observed pedestrian traffic data. We propose a benchmark problem, namely MAS-Bench, to discuss the performance of MAS parameter calibration methods uniformly. Numerical experiments demonstrate the baseline performance of four well-known optimization methods on six different error minimization problems that are defined on MAS-Bench. Moreover, we investigate the validity of the error function in the calibration by evaluating the correlation between the calibration and estimation scores. These scores are error functions relating to the available and unavailable observations, respectively."},"title":{"value":"MAS-Bench: a benchmarking for parameter calibration of multi-agent crowd simulation"},"authors":{"value":["Shusuke Shigenaka","Shunki Takami","Yuki Tanigaki","Shuhei Watanabe","Masaki Onishi"]}},"tmdate":1769009930431,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/jocss/ShigenakaTTWO24"],"tcdate":1769009926912,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Shuhei_Watanabe1"],"forum":"qTbzcsuOhF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":785245,"cdate":1727740800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769009930431,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"qTbzcsuOhF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICASSP 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10445798/10445803/10446466.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICASSP/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"hao|multidimensional_geometric_featurebased_calibration_method_for_lidar_and_camera_fusion"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yuhan_Hao2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xin_Jin_0002:","~Dongyu_Du1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP48485.2024.10446466"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icassp/Hao0D24,\n  author={Yuhan Hao and Xin Jin and Dongyu Du},\n  title={Multi-Dimensional Geometric Feature-Based Calibration Method for LiDAR and Camera Fusion},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={8406-8410},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP48485.2024.10446466},\n  booktitle={ICASSP},\n  crossref={conf/icassp/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Extrinsic calibration between LiDAR and camera has become an indispensable task across diverse domains, including autonomous vehicles, robotics, and surveillance systems. However, existing methods suffer from limited precision due to the inaccurate and insufficient detected features caused by the sparsity of point-clouds and the inherent ranging errors of LiDAR. In this paper, we propose a multi-dimensional geometric feature-based calibration method between LiDAR and camera. First, a 3D structured calibration target is proposed with multi-normal surfaces, edges in different directions, and distinctive corner features. Secondly, a point-plane and angle error-based point-cloud feature detection method is designed to establish 3D-2D feature point pairs with image features. Finally, a Perspective-n-Point (PnP) problem is solved to estimate the extrinsic parameters. The experimental results show that the proposed method reduces Mean Reprojection Error (MRE) by 0.05 pixels and achieves a 70% reduction in Normalized Reprojection Error (NRE) compared with state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods under the conditions of smaller training size, larger test size, and more repeat times."},"title":{"value":"Multi-Dimensional Geometric Feature-Based Calibration Method for LiDAR and Camera Fusion"},"authors":{"value":["Yuhan Hao","Xin Jin","Dongyu Du"]}},"tmdate":1747039403938,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1743775985044,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dongyu_Du1"],"forum":"3wVSb2nSzQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":375994,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747039403938,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"3wVSb2nSzQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett. 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/7083369/10683798/10669166.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/RAL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|edgecalib_multiframe_weighted_edge_features_for_automatic_targetless_lidarcamera_calibration"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xingchen_Li4","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yifan_Duan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Beibei_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haojie_Ren:","~Guoliang_You1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yu_Sheng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianmin_Ji:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yanyong_Zhang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3455895"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ral/LiDWRYSJZ24,\n  author={Xingchen Li and Yifan Duan and Beibei Wang and Haojie Ren and Guoliang You and Yu Sheng and Jianmin Ji and Yanyong Zhang},\n  title={EdgeCalib: Multi-Frame Weighted Edge Features for Automatic Targetless LiDAR-Camera Calibration},\n  year={2024},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1730419200000},\n  journal={IEEE Robotics Autom. Lett.},\n  volume={9},\n  number={11},\n  pages={10073-10080},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2024.3455895}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In multimodal perception systems, achieving precise extrinsic calibration between LiDAR and camera is of critical importance. However, the pre-calibrated extrinsic parameters may gradually drift during operation, leading to a decrease in the accuracy of the perception system. It is challenging to address this issue using methods based on artificial targets. In this article, we introduce an edge-based approach for automatic targetless calibration of LiDARs and cameras in real-world scenarios. The edge features, which are prevalent in various environments, are used to establish reliable correspondences in images and point clouds. Specifically, we leverage the Segment Anything Model to facilitate the extraction of stable and reliable image edge features. Then a multi-frame weighting strategy is used for feature filtering while alleviating the dependence on the environment. Finally, we estimate accurate extrinsic parameters based on edge correspondence constraints. Our method achieves a mean rotation error of $0.069^{\\circ }$ and a mean translation error of $\\mathbf 1.037\\ \\text{cm}$ on the KITTI dataset, outperforming existing edge-based calibration methods and demonstrating strong robustness, accuracy, and generalization capabilities."},"title":{"value":"EdgeCalib: Multi-Frame Weighted Edge Features for Automatic Targetless LiDAR-Camera Calibration"},"authors":{"value":["Xingchen Li","Yifan Duan","Beibei Wang","Haojie Ren","Guoliang You","Yu Sheng","Jianmin Ji","Yanyong Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1744359742499,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731481541029,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Guoliang_You1"],"forum":"cMtfkpYM9D","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":210490,"cdate":1730419200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1744359742499,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"cMtfkpYM9D","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"The SAUC method effectively quantifies uncertainty in sparse spatiotemporal data, reducing calibration errors by 20% in datasets like traffic and crime."},"venue":{"value":"TGL Workshop 2023 LongPaper"},"keywords":{"value":["Uncertainty calibration","Spatiotemporal deep learning","Graph Neural Networks"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/625b338795e0bc80ce45835eeabb53d46a8ec044.pdf"},"format":{"value":"Long paper, up to 8 pages. If the reviewers recommend it to be changed to a short paper, I would be willing to revise my paper to fit within 4 pages."},"abstract":{"value":"Quantifying uncertainty is essential for achieving robust and reliable predictions. However, existing spatiotemporal models predominantly predict deterministic values, often overlooking the uncertainty in their forecasts. Particularly, high-resolution spatiotemporal datasets are rich in zeros, posing further challenges in quantifying the uncertainty of such sparse and asymmetrically distributed data. This paper introduces a novel post-hoc Sparsity-aware Uncertainty Calibration (SAUC) method, calibrating the uncertainty in both zero and non-zero values. We modify the state-of-the-art deterministic spatiotemporal Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) to probabilistic ones as the synthetic models in the pre-calibration phase. Applied to two real-world spatiotemporal datasets of varied granularities, extensive experiments demonstrate SAUC's capacity to adeptly calibrate uncertainty, effectively fitting the variance of zero values and exhibiting robust generalizability. Specifically, our empirical experiments show a 20\\% of reduction in calibration errors in zero entries on the sparse traffic accident and urban crime prediction. The results validate our method's theoretical and empirical values, demonstrating calibrated results that provide reliable safety guidance, thereby bridging a significant gap in uncertainty quantification (UQ) for sparse spatiotemporal data."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nzhuang2023sauc,\ntitle={{SAUC}: Sparsity-Aware Uncertainty Calibration for Spatiotemporal Prediction with Graph Neural Networks},\nauthor={Dingyi Zhuang and Yuheng Bu and Guang Wang and Shenhao Wang and Jinhua Zhao},\nbooktitle={Temporal Graph Learning Workshop @ NeurIPS 2023},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=W9QOkbGccr}\n}"},"title":{"value":"SAUC: Sparsity-Aware Uncertainty Calibration for Spatiotemporal Prediction with Graph Neural Networks"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/b51febf647856d8adeb6ca62524e460295d934e4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/TGL"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhuang|sauc_sparsityaware_uncertainty_calibration_for_spatiotemporal_prediction_with_graph_neural_networks"},"authorids":{"value":["~Dingyi_Zhuang1","~Yuheng_Bu1","~Guang_Wang1","shenhaowang@ufl.edu","jinhua@mit.edu"]},"authors":{"value":["Dingyi Zhuang","Yuheng Bu","Guang Wang","Shenhao Wang","Jinhua Zhao"]}},"tmdate":1699966595011,"pdate":1697841683333,"tcdate":1696256406163,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/TGL","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/TGL/Submission24/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/TGL/Submission24/Authors"],"forum":"W9QOkbGccr","number":24,"cdate":1696256406163,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/TGL/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/TGL/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/TGL/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/TGL/Submission24/-/Camera-Ready"],"mdate":1699966595011,"odate":1699216801406,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2023/Workshop/TGL","id":"W9QOkbGccr","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NIER@ICSE 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3639476.3639763"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICSE/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"tu|beyond_a_joke_dead_code_elimination_can_delete_live_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haoxin_Tu:","~Lingxiao_Jiang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Debin_Gao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:He_Jiang_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3639476.3639763"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icse/TuJG024,\n  author={Haoxin Tu and Lingxiao Jiang and Debin Gao and He Jiang},\n  title={Beyond a Joke: Dead Code Elimination Can Delete Live Code},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={32-36},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3639476.3639763},\n  booktitle={NIER@ICSE},\n  crossref={conf/icse/2024nier}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Dead Code Elimination (DCE) is a fundamental compiler optimization technique that removes dead code (e.g., unreachable or reachable but whose results are unused) in the program to produce smaller or faster executables. However, since compiler optimizations are typically aggressively performed and there are complex relationships/interplay among a vast number of compiler optimizations (including DCE), it is not known whether DCE is indeed correctly performed and will only delete dead code in practice. In this study, we open a new research problem to investigate: can DCE happen to erroneously delete live code? To tackle this problem, we design a new approach named Xdead, which leverages differential testing, static binary analysis, and dynamic symbolic execution techniques, to detect miscompilation bugs caused by the erroneously deleted live code. Preliminary evaluation shows that Xdead can identify many divergent portions indicating erroneously deleted live code and finally detect two such miscompilation bugs in LLVM compilers. Our findings call for more attention to the potential issues in existing DCE implementations and more conservative strategies when designing new DCE-related compiler optimizations."},"title":{"value":"Beyond a Joke: Dead Code Elimination Can Delete Live Code"},"authors":{"value":["Haoxin Tu","Lingxiao Jiang","Debin Gao","He Jiang"]}},"tmdate":1718349099647,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1718349090399,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lingxiao_Jiang1"],"forum":"uZedEvXF7I","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":28717,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1718349099647,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"uZedEvXF7I","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ASE 2014"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/KBSE/2014"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|active_code_search_incorporating_user_feedback_to_improve_code_search_relevance"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shaowei_Wang_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:David_Lo_0001:","~Lingxiao_Jiang1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2642937.2642947"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/kbse/WangLJ14,\n  author={Shaowei Wang and David Lo and Lingxiao Jiang},\n  title={Active code search: incorporating user feedback to improve code search relevance},\n  year={2014},\n  cdate={1388534400000},\n  pages={677-682},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/2642937.2642947},\n  booktitle={ASE},\n  crossref={conf/kbse/2014}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code search techniques return relevant code fragments given a user query. They typically work in a passive mode: given a user query, a static list of code fragments sorted by the relevance scores decided by a code search technique is returned to the user. A user will go through the sorted list of returned code fragments from top to bottom. As the user checks each code fragment one by one, he or she will naturally form an opinion about the true relevance of the code fragment. In an active model, those opinions will be taken as feedbacks to the search engine for refining result lists. In this work, we incorporate users' opinion on the results from a code search engine to refine result lists: as a user forms an opinion about one result, our technique takes this opinion as feedback and leverages it to re-order the results to make truly relevant results appear earlier in the list. The refinement results can also be cached to potentially improve future code search tasks. We have built our active refinement technique on top of a state-of-the-art code search engine---Portfolio. Our technique improves Portfolio in terms of Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG) by more than 11.3%, from 0.738 to 0.821."},"title":{"value":"Active code search: incorporating user feedback to improve code search relevance"},"authors":{"value":["Shaowei Wang","David Lo","Lingxiao Jiang"]}},"tmdate":1718349113248,"pdate":1388534400000,"tcdate":1718349091460,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Lingxiao_Jiang1"],"forum":"eHFqhblKdL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":28768,"cdate":1388534400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1718349113248,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"eHFqhblKdL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.07922v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|codet5_open_code_large_language_models_for_code_understanding_and_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yue_Wang_0034:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hung_Le:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Akhilesh_Deepak_Gotmare:","~Nghi_D._Q._Bui1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junnan_Li_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Steven_C._H._Hoi:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2305.07922"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2305-07922,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yue Wang and Hung Le and Akhilesh Deepak Gotmare and Nghi D. Q. Bui and Junnan Li and Steven C. H. Hoi},\n  title={CodeT5+: Open Code Large Language Models for Code Understanding and Generation},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2305.07922},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2305.07922}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) pretrained on vast source code have achieved prominent progress in code intelligence. However, existing code LLMs have two main limitations in terms of architecture and pretraining tasks. First, they often adopt a specific architecture (encoder-only or decoder-only) or rely on a unified encoder-decoder network for different downstream tasks. The former paradigm is limited by inflexibility in applications while in the latter, the model is treated as a single system for all tasks, leading to suboptimal performance on a subset of tasks. Secondly, they often employ a limited set of pretraining objectives which might not be relevant to some downstream tasks and hence result in substantial performance degrade. To address these limitations, we propose ``CodeT5+'', a family of encoder-decoder LLMs for code in which component modules can be flexibly combined to suit a wide range of downstream code tasks. Such flexibility is enabled by our proposed mixture of pretraining objectives to mitigate the pretrain-finetune discrepancy. These objectives cover span denoising, contrastive learning, text-code matching, and causal LM pretraining tasks, on both unimodal and bimodal multilingual code corpora. Furthermore, we propose to initialize CodeT5+ with frozen off-the-shelf LLMs without training from scratch to efficiently scale up our models, and explore instruction-tuning to align with natural language instructions. We extensively evaluate CodeT5+ on over 20 code-related benchmarks in different settings, including zero-shot, finetuning, and instruction-tuning. We observe state-of-the-art (SoTA) model performance on various code-related tasks, such as code generation and completion, math programming, and text-to-code retrieval tasks. Particularly, our instruction-tuned CodeT5+ 16B achieves new SoTA results on HumanEval code generation task against other open code LLMs."},"title":{"value":"CodeT5+: Open Code Large Language Models for Code Understanding and Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Yue Wang","Hung Le","Akhilesh Deepak Gotmare","Nghi D. Q. Bui","Junnan Li","Steven C. H. Hoi"]}},"tmdate":1727727187082,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727727173635,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Nghi_D._Q._Bui1"],"forum":"XYtLyDKBD9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":123528,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727727187082,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"XYtLyDKBD9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/23/5/2391/pdf?version=1677145520"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"yan|online_calibration_study_of_noncontact_current_sensors_for_threephase_fourwire_power_cables"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Peiwu_Yan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenbin_Zhang_0006:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Le_Yang:","","~Hao_Yu10","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rujin_Huang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junyu_Zhu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xi_Liu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s23052391"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/YanZYZYHZL23,\n  author={Peiwu Yan and Wenbin Zhang and Le Yang and Wenying Zhang and Hao Yu and Rujin Huang and Junyu Zhu and Xi Liu},\n  title={Online Calibration Study of Non-Contact Current Sensors for Three-Phase Four-Wire Power Cables},\n  year={2023},\n  month={March},\n  cdate={1677628800000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={23},\n  number={5},\n  pages={2391},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s23052391}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Three-phase four-wire power cables are a primary kind of power transmission method in low-voltage distribution networks. This paper addresses the problem that calibration currents are not easily electrified during the transporting of three-phase four-wire power cable measurements, and proposes a method for obtaining the magnetic field strength distribution in the tangential direction around the cable, finally enabling online self-calibration. The simulation and experimental results show that this method can self-calibrate the sensor arrays and reconstruct the phase current waveforms in three-phase four-wire power cables without calibration currents, and this method is not affected by disturbances such as wire diameter, current amplitudes, and high-frequency harmonics. This study reduces the time and equipment costs required to calibrate the sensing module compared to related studies using calibration currents. This research offers the possibility of fusing sensing modules directly with running primary equipment, and the development of hand-held measurement devices."},"title":{"value":"Online Calibration Study of Non-Contact Current Sensors for Three-Phase Four-Wire Power Cables"},"authors":{"value":["Peiwu Yan","Wenbin Zhang","Le Yang","Wenying Zhang","Hao Yu","Rujin Huang","Junyu Zhu","Xi Liu"]}},"tmdate":1754236941846,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1739946237904,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wenying_Zhang1"],"forum":"xLB87BDbPw","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":328223,"cdate":1677628800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1754236941846,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xLB87BDbPw","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.16803v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"utpala|language_agnostic_code_embeddings"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Saiteja_Utpala:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alex_Gu:","~Pin-Yu_Chen3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.16803"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2310-16803,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Saiteja Utpala and Alex Gu and Pin-Yu Chen},\n  title={Language Agnostic Code Embeddings},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2310.16803},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2310.16803}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recently, code language models have achieved notable advancements in addressing a diverse array of essential code comprehension and generation tasks. Yet, the field lacks a comprehensive deep dive and understanding of the code embeddings of multilingual code models. In this paper, we present a comprehensive study on multilingual code embeddings, focusing on the cross-lingual capabilities of these embeddings across different programming languages. Through probing experiments, we demonstrate that code embeddings comprise two distinct components: one deeply tied to the nuances and syntax of a specific language, and the other remaining agnostic to these details, primarily focusing on semantics. Further, we show that when we isolate and eliminate this language-specific component, we witness significant improvements in downstream code retrieval tasks, leading to an absolute increase of up to +17 in the Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR)."},"title":{"value":"Language Agnostic Code Embeddings"},"authors":{"value":["Saiteja Utpala","Alex Gu","Pin-Yu Chen"]}},"tmdate":1727465351599,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727459451839,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Pin-Yu_Chen1"],"forum":"0Fmg3JqVbs","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":91927,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727465351599,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0Fmg3JqVbs","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NAACL-HLT 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.naacl-long.38.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/NAACL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"utpala|language_agnostic_code_embeddings"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Saiteja_Utpala:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alex_Gu:","~Pin-Yu_Chen3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.naacl-long.38"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/naacl/UtpalaGC24,\n  author={Saiteja Utpala and Alex Gu and Pin-Yu Chen},\n  title={Language Agnostic Code Embeddings},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={678-691},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.naacl-long.38},\n  booktitle={NAACL-HLT},\n  crossref={conf/naacl/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recently, code language models have achieved notable advancements in addressing a diverse array of essential code comprehension and generation tasks. Yet, the field lacks a comprehensive deep dive and understanding of the code embeddings of multilingual code models. In this paper, we present a comprehensive study on multilingual code embeddings, focusing on the cross-lingual capabilities of these embeddings across different programming languages. Through probing experiments, we demonstrate that code embeddings comprise two distinct components: one deeply tied to the nuances and syntax of a specific language, and the other remaining agnostic to these details, primarily focusing on semantics. Further, we show that when we isolate and eliminate this language-specific component, we witness significant improvements in downstream code retrieval tasks, leading to an absolute increase of up to +17 in the Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR)."},"title":{"value":"Language Agnostic Code Embeddings"},"authors":{"value":["Saiteja Utpala","Alex Gu","Pin-Yu Chen"]}},"tmdate":1727462728261,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727459452221,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Pin-Yu_Chen1"],"forum":"5qDPTQLjlC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":91955,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727462728261,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"5qDPTQLjlC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EACL (Findings) 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-eacl.207.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"kim|marking_code_without_breaking_it_code_watermarking_for_detecting_llmgenerated_code"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.findings-eacl.207"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/eacl/KimPH26,\n  author={Jungin Kim and Shinwoo Park and Yo-Sub Han},\n  title={Marking Code Without Breaking It: Code Watermarking for Detecting LLM-Generated Code},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={3990-4002},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.findings-eacl.207},\n  booktitle={EACL (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/eacl/2026f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Identifying LLM-generated code through watermarking poses a challenge in preserving functional correctness. Previous methods rely on the assumption that watermarking high-entropy tokens effectively maintains output quality. Our analysis reveals a fundamental limitation of this assumption: syntax-critical tokens such as keywords often exhibit the highest entropy, making existing approaches vulnerable to logic corruption. We present STONE, a syntax-aware watermarking method that embeds watermarks only in non-syntactic tokens and preserves code integrity. For rigorous evaluation, we also introduce STEM, a comprehensive metric that balances three critical dimensions: correctness, detectability, and imperceptibility. Across Python, C++, and Java, STONE preserves correctness, sustains strong detectability, and achieves balanced performance with minimal computational overhead. Our implementation is available at https://github.com/inistory/STONE-watermarking."},"title":{"value":"Marking Code Without Breaking It: Code Watermarking for Detecting LLM-Generated Code"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jungin Kim","username":"~Jungin_Kim1"},{"fullname":"Shinwoo Park","username":""},{"fullname":"Yo-Sub Han","username":"~Yo-Sub_Han1"}]}},"tmdate":1791450367665,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/eacl/KimPH26"],"tcdate":1784130306790,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yo-Sub_Han1"],"forum":"JJ9UYFyjhI","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":57847,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1791450367665,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"JJ9UYFyjhI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.17429v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"liang|condor_a_code_discriminator_integrating_general_semantics_with_code_details"},"authorids":{"value":["~Qingyuan_Liang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhao_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chen_Liu:","~Zeyu_Sun2","~Wenjie_Zhang2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yizhou_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zixiao_Zhao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Qi_Luo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wentao_Wang:","~Yanjie_Jiang1","~Yingfei_Xiong1","~Lu_Zhang11"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.17429"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-17429,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Qingyuan Liang and Zhao Zhang and Chen Liu and Zeyu Sun and Wenjie Zhang and Yizhou Chen and Zixiao Zhao and Qi Luo and Wentao Wang and Yanjie Jiang and Yingfei Xiong and Lu Zhang},\n  title={Condor: A Code Discriminator Integrating General Semantics with Code Details},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2412.17429},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.17429}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"LLMs demonstrate significant potential across various software engineering tasks. However, they still face challenges in generating correct code on the first attempt when addressing complex requirements. Introducing a discriminator to select reliable outputs from multiple generated results is an effective way to enhance their reliability and stability. Currently, these discriminators fall into two categories: execution-based discriminators and non-execution-based discriminators. Execution-based discriminators face flexibility challenges due to difficulties in obtaining test cases and security concerns, while non-execution-based discriminators, although more flexible, struggle to capture subtle differences in code details. To maintain flexibility while improving the model's ability to capture fine-grained code details, this paper proposes Condor. We first design contrastive learning to optimize the code representations of the base model, enabling it to reflect differences in code details. Then, we leverage intermediate data from the code modification process to further enrich the discriminator's training data, enhancing its ability to discern code details. Experimental results indicate that on the subtle code difference dataset (i.e., CodeNanoFix), Condor significantly outperforms other discriminators in discriminative performance: Condor (1.3B) improves the discriminative F1 score of DeepSeek-Coder (1.3B) from 67% to 73%. In discriminating LLM-generated outputs, Condor (1.3B) and Condor (110M) raise the Pass@1 score of Meta-Llama-3.1-Instruct (70B) on the CodeNanoFix dataset from 52.64% to 62.63% and 59.64%, respectively. Moreover, Condor demonstrates strong generalization capabilities on the MBPP and APPS datasets. For example, Condor (1.3B) improves the Pass@1 of Meta-Llama-3.1-Instruct (70B) on the APPS dataset by 147.05%."},"title":{"value":"Condor: A Code Discriminator Integrating General Semantics with Code Details"},"authors":{"value":["Qingyuan Liang","Zhao Zhang","Chen Liu","Zeyu Sun","Wenjie Zhang","Yizhou Chen","Zixiao Zhao","Qi Luo","Wentao Wang","Yanjie Jiang","Yingfei Xiong","Lu Zhang"]}},"tmdate":1790005315687,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1739943903889,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Qingyuan_Liang1"],"forum":"fnsOvkJ7hJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":327538,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1790005315687,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"fnsOvkJ7hJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/32/11298241/11201032.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"liang|condor_a_code_discriminator_integrating_general_semantics_with_code_details"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2025.3620145"},"abstract":{"value":"LLMs demonstrate significant potential across various software engineering tasks. However, they still face challenges in generating correct code on the first attempt when addressing complex requirements. Introducing a discriminator to select reliable outputs from multiple generated results is an effective way to enhance their reliability and stability. Currently, these discriminators fall into two categories: execution-based discriminators and non-execution-based discriminators. Execution-based discriminators face flexibility challenges due to difficulties in obtaining test cases and security concerns, while non-execution-based discriminators, although more flexible, struggle to capture subtle differences in code details. To maintain flexibility while improving the model’s ability to capture fine-grained code details, this paper proposes Condor. We first design contrastive learning to optimize the code representations of the base model, enabling it to reflect differences in code details. Then, we leverage intermediate data from the code modification process to further enrich the discriminator’s training data, enhancing its ability to discern code details. Experimental results indicate that on the subtle code difference dataset (i.e., CodeNanoFix), Condor significantly outperforms other discriminators in discriminative performance: Condor (1.3B) improves the discriminative F1 score of DeepSeek-Coder (1.3B) from 67% to 73%. In discriminating LLM-generated outputs, Condor (1.3B) and Condor (110M) raise the Pass@1 score of Llama-3.1-Instruct (70B) on the CodeNanoFix dataset from 52.64% to 62.63% and 59.64%, respectively. Moreover, Condor demonstrates strong generalization capabilities on the APPS, MBPP, and LiveCodeBench datasets. For example, Condor (1.3B) improves the Pass@1 of Llama-3.1-Instruct (70B) on the APPS dataset by 147.05%."},"title":{"value":"Condor: A Code Discriminator Integrating General Semantics With Code Details"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Qingyuan Liang"},{"fullname":"Zhao Zhang"},{"fullname":"Chen Liu"},{"fullname":"Zeyu Sun","username":"~Zeyu_Sun2"},{"fullname":"Wenjie Zhang"},{"fullname":"Yizhou Chen"},{"fullname":"Zixiao Zhao"},{"fullname":"Qi Luo"},{"fullname":"Wentao Wang"},{"fullname":"Yanjie Jiang"},{"fullname":"Yingfei Xiong"},{"fullname":"Lu Zhang"}]}},"tmdate":1789092949530,"pdate":1764547200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1109/tse.2025.3620145"],"tcdate":1768979342457,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Zeyu_Sun2"],"forum":"6ElvxHd1iI","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":33242,"cdate":1765490286017,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789092949530,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"6ElvxHd1iI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"TLDR":{"value":"We use task vector to guide calibration-free quantization."},"keywords":{"value":["calibration-free quantization","PTQ"]},"primary_area":{"value":"applications to computer vision, audio, language, and other modalities"},"abstract":{"value":"Fine-tuning foundation models has become the standard way to create domain-specific expert models. \nWhile calibration-free quantization offers an efficient deployment solution without requiring downstream data, existing methods often suffer significant performance drop at low bit-widths. \nThese methods typically neglect the task-specific information encoded in the task vector, which is defined as the parameter difference between the fine-tuned and pre-trained weights.\nIn this work, we propose **T**ask-aware bas**I**s to **G**uid**E** calibration-f**R**ee **Q**uantization (TIGER-Q). \nTIGER-Q extracts a task-aware basis from the task vector to serve as a data-free proxy for the Hessian matrix. This basis establishes a task-sensitive subspace that guides the quantization process to minimize error along dimensions critical to the target task. \nTo efficiently solve this objective, we introduce a decoupled continuous-discrete refinement pipeline.\nIt computes closed-form analytical solutions for continuous quantization scales, while employing a local search for discrete weights, coupled with a row-wise acceptance mechanism to maintain monotonic error reduction.\nExtensive experiments show that TIGER-Q achieves superior performance over state-of-the-art calibration-free methods on both language and vision models."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026finetuning,\ntitle={Fine-Tuning Leaves Clues: Calibration-Free Quantization via Task-Aware Basis},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=PTL1u5Zn05},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Fine-Tuning Leaves Clues: Calibration-Free Quantization via Task-Aware Basis"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/1616d385018eeccd1aa675fc88368c9399b280ba.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791219285216,"tcdate":1787191777897,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission279/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission279/Authors"],"forum":"PTL1u5Zn05","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":279,"cdate":1787191777897,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission279/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791219285216,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"PTL1u5Zn05","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IROS 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6034548/6094399/06095106.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IROS/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"miura|slambased_online_calibration_of_asynchronous_microphone_array_for_robot_audition"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","~Kazuhiro_Nakadai1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.2011.6095106"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iros/MiuraYNN11,\n  author={Hiroaki Miura and Takami Yoshida and Keisuke Nakamura and Kazuhiro Nakadai},\n  title={SLAM-based online calibration of asynchronous microphone array for robot audition},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={524-529},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.2011.6095106},\n  booktitle={IROS},\n  crossref={conf/iros/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper addresses the online calibration of an asynchronous microphone array for robots. Conventional microphone array technologies require a lot of measurements of transfer functions to calibrate microphone locations, and a multi-channel A/D converter for inter-microphone synchronization. We solve these two problems using a framework combining Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) and beamforming in an online manner. To do this, we assume that estimations of microphone locations, a sound source location, and microphone clock difference correspond to mapping, self-localization, observation errors in SLAM, respectively. In our framework, the SLAM process calibrates locations and clock differences of microphones every time a microphone array observes a sound like a human's clapping, and a beamforming process works as a cost function to decide the convergence of calibration by localizing the sound with the estimated locations and clock differences. After calibration, beamforming is used for sound source localization. We implemented a prototype system using Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) based SLAM and Delay-and-Sum Beamforming (DS-BF). The experimental results showed that microphone locations and clock differences were estimated properly with 10–15 sound events (handclaps), and the error of sound source localization with the estimated information was less than the grid size of beamforming, that is, the lowest error was theoretically attained."},"title":{"value":"SLAM-based online calibration of asynchronous microphone array for robot audition"},"authors":{"value":["Hiroaki Miura","Takami Yoshida","Keisuke Nakamura","Kazuhiro Nakadai"]}},"tmdate":1772259526365,"pdate":1325289600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iros/MiuraYNN11"],"tcdate":1772259369207,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Kazuhiro_Nakadai1"],"forum":"1AiQHM8I3r","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":838065,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772259526365,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"1AiQHM8I3r","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Medical Imaging 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/42/9273282/09130729.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TMI/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"mehrtash|confidence_calibration_and_predictive_uncertainty_estimation_for_deep_medical_image_segmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alireza_Mehrtash:","~William_M._Wells_III1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Clare_M._Tempany:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Purang_Abolmaesumi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tina_Kapur:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TMI.2020.3006437"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tmi/MehrtashWTAK20,\n  author={Alireza Mehrtash and William M. Wells III and Clare M. Tempany and Purang Abolmaesumi and Tina Kapur},\n  title={Confidence Calibration and Predictive Uncertainty Estimation for Deep Medical Image Segmentation},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Medical Imaging},\n  volume={39},\n  number={12},\n  pages={3868-3878},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TMI.2020.3006437}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Fully convolutional neural networks (FCNs), and in particular U-Nets, have achieved state-of-the-art results in semantic segmentation for numerous medical imaging applications. Moreover, batch normalization and Dice loss have been used successfully to stabilize and accelerate training. However, these networks are poorly calibrated i.e. they tend to produce overconfident predictions for both correct and erroneous classifications, making them unreliable and hard to interpret. In this paper, we study predictive uncertainty estimation in FCNs for medical image segmentation. We make the following contributions: 1) We systematically compare cross-entropy loss with Dice loss in terms of segmentation quality and uncertainty estimation of FCNs; 2) We propose model ensembling for confidence calibration of the FCNs trained with batch normalization and Dice loss; 3) We assess the ability of calibrated FCNs to predict segmentation quality of structures and detect out-of-distribution test examples. We conduct extensive experiments across three medical image segmentation applications of the brain, the heart, and the prostate to evaluate our contributions. The results of this study offer considerable insight into the predictive uncertainty estimation and out-of-distribution detection in medical image segmentation and provide practical recipes for confidence calibration. Moreover, we consistently demonstrate that model ensembling improves confidence calibration."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration and Predictive Uncertainty Estimation for Deep Medical Image Segmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Alireza Mehrtash","William M. Wells III","Clare M. Tempany","Purang Abolmaesumi","Tina Kapur"]}},"tmdate":1747166731638,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1747166663366,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~William_M_Wells1"],"forum":"V1IdlHYpju","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":450851,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747166731638,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"V1IdlHYpju","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1911.13273v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"mehrtash|confidence_calibration_and_predictive_uncertainty_estimation_for_deep_medical_image_segmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alireza_Mehrtash:","~William_M._Wells_III1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Clare_M._Tempany:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Purang_Abolmaesumi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tina_Kapur:"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1911.13273"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1911-13273,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Alireza Mehrtash and William M. Wells III and Clare M. Tempany and Purang Abolmaesumi and Tina Kapur},\n  title={Confidence Calibration and Predictive Uncertainty Estimation for Deep Medical Image Segmentation},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1911.13273},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1911.13273}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Fully convolutional neural networks (FCNs), and in particular U-Nets, have achieved state-of-the-art results in semantic segmentation for numerous medical imaging applications. Moreover, batch normalization and Dice loss have been used successfully to stabilize and accelerate training. However, these networks are poorly calibrated i.e. they tend to produce overconfident predictions both in correct and erroneous classifications, making them unreliable and hard to interpret. In this paper, we study predictive uncertainty estimation in FCNs for medical image segmentation. We make the following contributions: 1) We systematically compare cross entropy loss with Dice loss in terms of segmentation quality and uncertainty estimation of FCNs; 2) We propose model ensembling for confidence calibration of the FCNs trained with batch normalization and Dice loss; 3) We assess the ability of calibrated FCNs to predict segmentation quality of structures and detect out-of-distribution test examples. We conduct extensive experiments across three medical image segmentation applications of the brain, the heart, and the prostate to evaluate our contributions. The results of this study offer considerable insight into the predictive uncertainty estimation and out-of-distribution detection in medical image segmentation and provide practical recipes for confidence calibration. Moreover, we consistently demonstrate that model ensembling improves confidence calibration."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration and Predictive Uncertainty Estimation for Deep Medical Image Segmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Alireza Mehrtash","William M. Wells III","Clare M. Tempany","Purang Abolmaesumi","Tina Kapur"]}},"tmdate":1747166718764,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1747166663823,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~William_M_Wells1"],"forum":"AydfncjZtZ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":450876,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747166718764,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"AydfncjZtZ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"DSAA 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/10722027/10722766/10722831.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/DSAA/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|an_online_calibration_method_for_robust_multimodality_3d_object_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xingyu_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yige_Yao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jianming_Hu:","~Zhidong_Deng1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/DSAA61799.2024.10722831"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/dsaa/LiYHD24,\n  author={Xingyu Li and Yige Yao and Jianming Hu and Zhidong Deng},\n  title={An Online Calibration Method for Robust Multi-Modality 3D Object Detection},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={1-10},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/DSAA61799.2024.10722831},\n  booktitle={DSAA},\n  crossref={conf/dsaa/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-modality sensor fusion for 3D perception is a significant part for autonomous driving perception, which enables a comprehensive integration of different sensors and obtains a holistic understanding of the surrounding environment to improve accuracy, stability and reliability. Nevertheless, vibrations, collisions, and acceleration/deceleration in motion may result in a minor disturbance to the position of sensors, leading to offsets in sensor calibration. To address the limitations of the original method demanding considerable manual effort and time for hand-annotating checkerboards, we introduce an online Camera-LiDAR calibration method, which can perform calculations during the operation of autonomous vehicles to eliminate sensor biases. Different from the previous CNN-based online calibration algorithm, our approach differs in utilizing multi-scale features to accomplish alignment with a foundational ResNet and FPN Backbone alongside an attention-based multi-scale feature fusion module. Moreover, in the design of the loss function, we incorporate depth loss and point cloud loss in addition to the original smooth L1 norm loss to facilitate network backpropagation. Ultimately, our proposed method achieves an average translation error of 0.88 cm and a rotation error of 0.073° on the KITTI odometry dataset, which can significantly limit the discrepancies between sensors and thus enhance detection accuracy."},"title":{"value":"An Online Calibration Method for Robust Multi-Modality 3D Object Detection"},"authors":{"value":["Xingyu Li","Yige Yao","Jianming Hu","Zhidong Deng"]}},"tmdate":1731482677997,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731479697998,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zhidong_Deng1"],"forum":"A6MM6SgdXF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":208001,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731482677997,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"A6MM6SgdXF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICRA 2008"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/4534525/4543169/04543397.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICRA/2008"},"paperhash":{"value":"sagawa|accurate_calibration_of_intrinsic_camera_parameters_by_observing_parallel_light_pairs"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ryusuke_Sagawa1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yasushi_Yagi:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2008.4543397"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icra/SagawaY08,\n  author={Ryusuke Sagawa and Yasushi Yagi},\n  title={Accurate calibration of intrinsic camera parameters by observing parallel light pairs},\n  year={2008},\n  cdate={1199145600000},\n  pages={1390-1397},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ROBOT.2008.4543397},\n  booktitle={ICRA},\n  crossref={conf/icra/2008}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This study describes a method of estimating the intrinsic parameters of a perspective camera. In previous calibration methods for perspective cameras, the intrinsic and extrinsic parameters are estimated simultaneously during calibration. Thus, the intrinsic parameters depend on the estimation of the extrinsic parameters, which is inconsistent with the fact that intrinsic parameters are independent of extrinsic ones. Moreover, in a situation where the extrinsic parameters are not used, only the intrinsic parameters need to be estimated. In this case, an intrinsic parameter, such as focal length, is not sufficiently robust to combat the image processing noise, that is absorbed by both parameter types, during calibration. We therefore propose a new method that allows the estimation of intrinsic parameters without estimating the extrinsic parameters. In order to calibrate the intrinsic parameters, the proposed method observes parallel light pairs that are projected on different points. This is accomplished by applying the constraint that the relative angle of two parallel rays is constant irrespective of where the rays are projected. This method focuses only on intrinsic parameters and the calibrations are sufficiently robust as demonstrated in this study. Moreover, our method can visualize the error of the calibrated result and the degeneracy of the input data."},"title":{"value":"Accurate calibration of intrinsic camera parameters by observing parallel light pairs"},"authors":{"value":["Ryusuke Sagawa","Yasushi Yagi"]}},"tmdate":1731464158570,"pdate":1199145600000,"tcdate":1731462537324,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ryusuke_Sagawa1"],"forum":"9oItmPOxfn","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":188244,"cdate":1199145600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731464158570,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"9oItmPOxfn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Autom. Softw. Eng. 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10515-025-00561-1.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"lin|decomposition_then_watermarking_enhancing_code_traceability_with_dualchannel_code_watermarking"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10515-025-00561-1"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ase/LinLJPZWL26,\n  author={Haibo Lin and Zhong Li and Ruihua Ji and Minxue Pan and Tian Zhang and Nan Wu and Xuandong Li},\n  title={Decomposition then watermarking: Enhancing code traceability with dual-channel code watermarking},\n  year={2026},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1780272000000},\n  journal={Autom. Softw. Eng.},\n  volume={33},\n  number={1},\n  pages={16},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/s10515-025-00561-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Code watermarking has gained increasing attention for tracing the provenance of code with the rapid growth of the open-source community. Existing work on code watermarking has shown promising results yet still falls short, especially when a multi-bit watermark for encoding diverse information is required. In this paper, we propose DWC, a novel code watermarking method with highly watermark capacity. The key idea of DWC is to first decompose the code into natural and formal channels, then embed the watermark separately into each channel based solely on its respective information. As such, DWC reduces the mutual interference between these two channels and the impacts of irrelevant information within the code, thus enabling more effective transformations for embedding watermarks with higher capacity and robustness. Our extensive experiments on source code snippets in four programming languages (C, C++, Java, and Python) demonstrate the effectiveness, efficiency, and capability of DWC in embedding multi-bit watermarks, as well as the utility and robustness of the watermarked code it generates."},"title":{"value":"Decomposition then watermarking: Enhancing code traceability with dual-channel code watermarking"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Haibo Lin","username":"~Haibo_Lin2"},{"fullname":"Zhong Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Ruihua Ji","username":""},{"fullname":"Minxue Pan","username":""},{"fullname":"Tian Zhang","username":"~Tian_Zhang8"},{"fullname":"Nan Wu","username":""},{"fullname":"Xuandong Li","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1778201172645,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/ase/LinLJPZWL26"],"tcdate":1777957319161,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Haibo_Lin2"],"forum":"sbWjmzzGPk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":5550,"cdate":1780272000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1778201172645,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"sbWjmzzGPk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.00700v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"molison|is_llmgenerated_code_more_maintainable_reliable_than_humanwritten_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alfred_Santa_Molison:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marcia_Moraes:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Glaucia_Melo_0001:","~Fabio_Santos1","~Wesley_K._G._Assunção1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.00700"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2508-00700,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Alfred Santa Molison and Marcia Moraes and Glaucia Melo and Fabio Santos and Wesley K. G. Assunção},\n  title={Is LLM-Generated Code More Maintainable & Reliable than Human-Written Code?},\n  year={2025},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1754006400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2508.00700},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.00700}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Background: The rise of Large Language Models (LLMs) in software development has opened new possibilities for code generation. Despite the widespread use of this technology, it remains unclear how well LLMs generate code solutions in terms of software quality and how they compare to human-written code. Aims: This study compares the internal quality attributes of LLM-generated and human-written code. Method: Our empirical study integrates datasets of coding tasks, three LLM configurations (zero-shot, few-shot, and fine-tuning), and SonarQube to assess software quality. The dataset comprises Python code solutions across three difficulty levels: introductory, interview, and competition. We analyzed key code quality metrics, including maintainability and reliability, and the estimated effort required to resolve code issues. Results: Our analysis shows that LLM-generated code has fewer bugs and requires less effort to fix them overall. Interestingly, fine-tuned models reduced the prevalence of high-severity issues, such as blocker and critical bugs, and shifted them to lower-severity categories, but decreased the model's performance. In competition-level problems, the LLM solutions sometimes introduce structural issues that are not present in human-written code. Conclusion: Our findings provide valuable insights into the quality of LLM-generated code; however, the introduction of critical issues in more complex scenarios highlights the need for a systematic evaluation and validation of LLM solutions. Our work deepens the understanding of the strengths and limitations of LLMs for code generation."},"title":{"value":"Is LLM-Generated Code More Maintainable & Reliable than Human-Written Code?"},"authors":{"value":["Alfred Santa Molison","Marcia Moraes","Glaucia Melo","Fabio Santos","Wesley K. G. Assunção"]}},"tmdate":1784277228705,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2508-00700"],"tcdate":1770831583915,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fabio_Santos1"],"forum":"1htOSxp8gn","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":822293,"cdate":1754006400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784277228705,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"1htOSxp8gn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ESEM 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11322809/11323251/11323370.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ESEM/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"molison|is_llmgenerated_code_more_maintainable_reliable_than_humanwritten_code"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alfred_Santa_Molison:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marcia_Moraes:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Glaucia_Melo_0001:","~Fabio_Santos1","~Wesley_K._G._Assunção1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ESEM64174.2025.00036"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/esem/MolisonMMSA25,\n  author={Alfred Santa Molison and Marcia Moraes and Glaucia Melo and Fabio Santos and Wesley K. G. Assunção},\n  title={Is LLM-Generated Code More Maintainable & Reliable Than Human-Written Code?},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={151-162},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ESEM64174.2025.00036},\n  booktitle={ESEM},\n  crossref={conf/esem/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Background: The rise of Large Language Models (LLMs) in software development has opened new possibilities for code generation. Despite the widespread use of this technology, it remains unclear how well LLMs generate code solutions in terms of software quality and how they compare to humanwritten code. Aims: This study compares the internal quality attributes of LLM-generated and human-written code. Method: Our empirical study integrates datasets of coding tasks, three LLM configurations (zero-shot, few-shot, and fine-tuning), and SonarQube to assess software quality. The dataset comprises Python code solutions across three difficulty levels: introductory, interview, and competition. We analyzed key code quality metrics, including maintainability and reliability, and the estimated effort required to resolve code issues. Results: Our analysis shows that LLM-generated code has fewer bugs and requires less effort to fix them overall. Interestingly, fine-tuned models reduced the prevalence of high-severity issues, such as blocker and critical bugs, and shifted them to lower-severity categories, but decreased the model's performance. In competition-level problems, the LLM solutions sometimes introduce structural issues that are not present in human-written code. Conclusion: Our findings provide valuable insights into the quality of LLM-generated code; however, the introduction of critical issues in more complex scenarios highlights the need for a systematic evaluation and validation of LLM solutions. Our work deepens the understanding of the strengths and limitations of LLMs for code generation."},"title":{"value":"Is LLM-Generated Code More Maintainable & Reliable Than Human-Written Code?"},"authors":{"value":["Alfred Santa Molison","Marcia Moraes","Glaucia Melo","Fabio Santos","Wesley K. G. Assunção"]}},"tmdate":1784277228096,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/esem/MolisonMMSA25"],"tcdate":1770831583964,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Fabio_Santos1"],"forum":"hLQ3vpHzfj","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":822309,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784277228096,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"hLQ3vpHzfj","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MIDL 2024 Short Papers"},"keywords":{"value":["Uncertainty estimation","self-supervised models","chest X-ray pathology classification","calibration"]},"abstract":{"value":"Uncertainty quantification is crucial for the safe deployment of AI systems in clinical radiology. We analyze the calibration of CheXzero (Tiu et al., 2022), a high-performance self-supervised model for chest X-ray pathology detection, on two external datasets and evaluate the effectiveness of two common uncertainty estimation methods: Maximum Softmax Probabilities (MSP) and Monte Carlo Dropout. Our analysis reveals poor calibration on both external datasets, with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) scores ranging from 0.12 to 0.41. Furthermore, we find that the model’s prediction accuracy does not correlate with the uncertainty scores derived from MSP and Monte Carlo Dropout. These findings highlight the need for more robust uncertainty quantification methods to ensure the trustworthiness of AI-assisted clinical decision-making."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nxu2024calibration,\ntitle={Calibration and Uncertainty Estimation Challenges in Self-Supervised Chest X-ray Pathology Classification Models},\nauthor={Jenny Xu and Pranav Rajpurkar},\nbooktitle={Medical Imaging with Deep Learning},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=PfCY5BLNHc}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibration and Uncertainty Estimation Challenges in Self-Supervised Chest X-ray Pathology Classification Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3fe6447a4f689f8ea1031215c0d039f3016527b6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"MIDL.io/2024/Short_Papers"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|calibration_and_uncertainty_estimation_challenges_in_selfsupervised_chest_xray_pathology_classification_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jenny_Xu1","~Pranav_Rajpurkar1"]},"authors":{"value":["Jenny Xu","Pranav Rajpurkar"]}},"tmdate":1717208423357,"pdate":1714208724602,"tcdate":1712815639149,"writers":["MIDL.io/2024/Short_Papers","MIDL.io/2024/Short_Papers/Submission160/Authors"],"signatures":["MIDL.io/2024/Short_Papers/Submission160/Authors"],"forum":"PfCY5BLNHc","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":160,"cdate":1712815639149,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["MIDL.io/2024/Short_Papers/-/Submission","MIDL.io/2024/Short_Papers/-/Post_Submission","MIDL.io/2024/Short_Papers/-/Edit","MIDL.io/2024/Short_Papers/Submission160/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1717208423357,"odate":1714208724602,"domain":"MIDL.io/2024/Short_Papers","id":"PfCY5BLNHc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ISOCC 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9332802/9332909/09333091.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ISOCC/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"tavares|calibration_of_mchannel_timeinterleaved_analogtodigital_converters_based_on_curve_fitting"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yang_Azevedo_Tavares:","~Sewon_Lee1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Seunghyun_Kim:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Minjae_Lee_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ISOCC50952.2020.9333091"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/isocc/TavaresLKL20,\n  author={Yang Azevedo Tavares and Sewon Lee and Seunghyun Kim and Minjae Lee},\n  title={Calibration of M-Channel Time-Interleaved Analog-to-Digital Converters Based on Curve Fitting},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={252-253},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ISOCC50952.2020.9333091},\n  booktitle={ISOCC},\n  crossref={conf/isocc/2020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents a foreground calibration for time-interleaved analog-to-digital converters (TI-ADCs) based on curve fitting for any TI-ADC interleaving order. It outperforms the cited methods by utilizing a hardware efficient digital correction scheme while obtaining an accurate estimation of the channels mismatches. A commercial 12-bit 3.6 GS/s TI-ADC is employed to validate the proposed calibration."},"title":{"value":"Calibration of M-Channel Time-Interleaved Analog-to-Digital Converters Based on Curve Fitting"},"authors":{"value":["Yang Azevedo Tavares","Sewon Lee","Seunghyun Kim","Minjae Lee"]}},"tmdate":1769412961396,"pdate":1609372800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/isocc/TavaresLKL20"],"tcdate":1769412959375,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Sewon_Lee1"],"forum":"3w7SVU3SRU","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":801229,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769412961396,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"3w7SVU3SRU","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We identify a role-dependent fidelity-coverage trade-off in CIL: high-fidelity exemplars support representation rehearsal, while sensitivity-guided compressed samples provide broader coverage for classifier-only calibration."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Continual Learning","Class-Incremental Learning","Memory-Constrained Continual Learning","Classifier Alignment","Data Compression","Task-Aware Compression"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/87a49a2114ca41b1c8eac7b3ce5daa5b41f71542.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"transfer learning, meta learning, and lifelong learning"},"abstract":{"value":"In Class-Incremental Learning (CIL), limited replay can preserve separable features for historical classes, yet provide insufficient distribution coverage to align the classifier with the historical class distributions in feature space. We observe that representation rehearsal and classifier calibration have different input fidelity requirements: with backbone frozen, calibration can trade input detail for broader historical coverage, provided that compression retains information relevant to classification in feature space. To this end, we propose a role-decoupled memory framework: using high-fidelity exemplars for representation learning, and compressed low-fidelity samples exclusively for subsequent classifier calibration. Drawing on the concept of Average Gradient Outer Product (AGOP), we propose two novel compression schemes, AGOP-PCA and AGOP-guided transform coding, to achieve broad feature coverage and preserve information useful for classification. Experiments across diverse CIL benchmarks show consistent improvements over strong replay, compressed replay and classifier-alignment baselines."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026fidelity,\ntitle={Fidelity for Rehearsal, Coverage for Calibration: Role-Decoupled Memory for Class-Incremental Learning},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=HPDPhkD8w9},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Fidelity for Rehearsal, Coverage for Calibration: Role-Decoupled Memory for Class-Incremental Learning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/4fef34524d1248c20a9da3d87e25e8083816924d.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791233286395,"tcdate":1789737056052,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission43050/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission43050/Authors"],"forum":"HPDPhkD8w9","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":43050,"cdate":1789737056052,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission43050/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791233286395,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"HPDPhkD8w9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/21314/21063"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AAAI/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"gong|confidence_calibration_for_intent_detection_via_hyperspherical_space_and_rebalanced_accuracyuncertainty_loss"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yantao_Gong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cao_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fan_Yang_0087:","~Xunliang_Cai1","~Guanglu_Wan1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiansong_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weipeng_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Houfeng_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v36i10.21314"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aaai/GongLYCWCZW22,\n  author={Yantao Gong and Cao Liu and Fan Yang and Xunliang Cai and Guanglu Wan and Jiansong Chen and Weipeng Zhang and Houfeng Wang},\n  title={Confidence Calibration for Intent Detection via Hyperspherical Space and Rebalanced Accuracy-Uncertainty Loss},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={10690-10698},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v36i10.21314},\n  booktitle={AAAI},\n  crossref={conf/aaai/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Data-driven methods have achieved notable performance on intent detection, which is a task to comprehend user queries. Nonetheless, they are controversial for over-confident predictions. In some scenarios, users do not only care about the accuracy but also the confidence of model. Unfortunately, mainstream neural networks are poorly calibrated, with a large gap between accuracy and confidence. To handle this problem defined as confidence calibration, we propose a model using the hyperspherical space and rebalanced accuracy-uncertainty loss. Specifically, we project the label vector onto hyperspherical space uniformly to generate a dense label representation matrix, which mitigates over-confident predictions due to overfitting sparse one-hot label matrix. Besides, we rebalance samples of different accuracy and uncertainty to better guide model training. Experiments on the open datasets verify that our model outperforms the existing calibration methods and achieves a significant improvement on the calibration metric."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration for Intent Detection via Hyperspherical Space and Rebalanced Accuracy-Uncertainty Loss"},"authors":{"value":["Yantao Gong","Cao Liu","Fan Yang","Xunliang Cai","Guanglu Wan","Jiansong Chen","Weipeng Zhang","Houfeng Wang"]}},"tmdate":1767748873719,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731927468916,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xunliang_Cai1"],"forum":"kI6tTXu84U","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":249920,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1767748873719,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"kI6tTXu84U","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.09278v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"gong|confidence_calibration_for_intent_detection_via_hyperspherical_space_and_rebalanced_accuracyuncertainty_loss"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yantao_Gong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cao_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fan_Yang_0087:","~Xunliang_Cai1","~Guanglu_Wan1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiansong_Chen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Weipeng_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Houfeng_Wang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2203.09278"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2203-09278,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yantao Gong and Cao Liu and Fan Yang and Xunliang Cai and Guanglu Wan and Jiansong Chen and Weipeng Zhang and Houfeng Wang},\n  title={Confidence Calibration for Intent Detection via Hyperspherical Space and Rebalanced Accuracy-Uncertainty Loss},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2203.09278},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2203.09278}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Data-driven methods have achieved notable performance on intent detection, which is a task to comprehend user queries. Nonetheless, they are controversial for over-confident predictions. In some scenarios, users do not only care about the accuracy but also the confidence of model. Unfortunately, mainstream neural networks are poorly calibrated, with a large gap between accuracy and confidence. To handle this problem defined as confidence calibration, we propose a model using the hyperspherical space and rebalanced accuracy-uncertainty loss. Specifically, we project the label vector onto hyperspherical space uniformly to generate a dense label representation matrix, which mitigates over-confident predictions due to overfitting sparce one-hot label matrix. Besides, we rebalance samples of different accuracy and uncertainty to better guide model training. Experiments on the open datasets verify that our model outperforms the existing calibration methods and achieves a significant improvement on the calibration metric."},"title":{"value":"Confidence Calibration for Intent Detection via Hyperspherical Space and Rebalanced Accuracy-Uncertainty Loss"},"authors":{"value":["Yantao Gong","Cao Liu","Fan Yang","Xunliang Cai","Guanglu Wan","Jiansong Chen","Weipeng Zhang","Houfeng Wang"]}},"tmdate":1767748873244,"pdate":1640995200000,"tcdate":1731927469749,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xunliang_Cai1"],"forum":"UC7rbBPA5P","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":249935,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1767748873244,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"UC7rbBPA5P","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Multi-camera multi-object tracking; Self-supervised representation learning; Feature disentanglement; Surveillance video analytics"]},"primary_area":{"value":"applications to computer vision, audio, language, and other modalities"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-camera multi-object tracking (MCMOT) faces significant challenges in maintaining consistent object identities across varying camera perspectives, particularly when precise calibration and extensive annotations are required. In this paper, we present CALIBFREE, a self-supervised representation learning framework that does not need any calibration or manual labeling for the MCMOT task. By disentangling view-agnostic and view-specific features through single-view distillation and cross-view reconstruction, our method adapts to complex, dynamic scenarios with minimal overhead. Experiments on the MMP-MvMHAT dataset show a 3% improvement in overall accuracy and a 7. 5% increase in the average F1 score over state-of-the-art approaches, confirming the effectiveness of our calibration-free design. Moreover, on the more diverse MvMHAT dataset, our approach demonstrates superior over-time tracking and strong cross-view performance, highlighting its adaptability to a wide range of camera configurations."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nxian2025calibfree,\ntitle={{CALIBFREE}: Self-Supervised Feature Disentanglement for Calibration-Free Multi-Camera Multi-Object Tracking},\nauthor={Ruiqi Xian and Iain Melvin and Deep Anil Patel and Sanjoy Kundu and Martin Renqiang Min and Dinesh Manocha},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=4kb6vectZ3}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CALIBFREE: Self-Supervised Feature Disentanglement for Calibration-Free Multi-Camera Multi-Object Tracking"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8828134e86422d4a78b78810805ff26f02947789.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"xian|calibfree_selfsupervised_feature_disentanglement_for_calibrationfree_multicamera_multiobject_tracking"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ruiqi_Xian1","~Iain_Melvin1","~Deep_Anil_Patel1","~Sanjoy_Kundu1","~Martin_Renqiang_Min1","~Dinesh_Manocha3"]},"authors":{"value":["Ruiqi Xian","Iain Melvin","Deep Anil Patel","Sanjoy Kundu","Martin Renqiang Min","Dinesh Manocha"]}},"tmdate":1763084420189,"tcdate":1758301948607,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission20056/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission20056/Authors"],"forum":"4kb6vectZ3","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":20056,"cdate":1758301948607,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission20056/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1763084420189,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"4kb6vectZ3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ITSC 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/6069628/6082792/06082899.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ITSC/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|3d_triangulation_based_extrinsic_calibration_between_a_stereo_vision_system_and_a_lidar"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:You_Li_0005:","~Yassine_Ruichek2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cindy_Cappelle:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC.2011.6082899"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/itsc/LiRC11,\n  author={You Li and Yassine Ruichek and Cindy Cappelle},\n  title={3D triangulation based extrinsic calibration between a stereo vision system and a LIDAR},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={797-802},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ITSC.2011.6082899},\n  booktitle={ITSC},\n  crossref={conf/itsc/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper presents a novel extrinsic calibration algorithm between a binocular stereo vision system and a 2D LIDAR (laser range finder). Extrinsic calibration of these heterogeneous sensors is required to fuse information obtained separately by vision sensor and LIDAR in the context of intelligent vehicle. By placing a planar chessboard at different positions and orientations in front of the sensors, the proposed method solves the problem based on 3D reconstruction of the chessboard and geometric constraints between views from the stereovision system and the LIDAR. The three principle steps of the approach are: 3D corner points triangulation, 3D plane least-squares estimation, solving extrinsic parameters by applying a non-linear optimization algorithm based on the geometric constraints. To evaluate the performance of the algorithm, experiments based on computer simulation and real data are performed. The proposed approach is also compared with a popular calibration method to show its advantages."},"title":{"value":"3D triangulation based extrinsic calibration between a stereo vision system and a LIDAR"},"authors":{"value":["You Li","Yassine Ruichek","Cindy Cappelle"]}},"tmdate":1752847911116,"pdate":1293840000000,"tcdate":1752847816580,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yassine_RUICHEK1"],"forum":"ykctfi7BkJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":574788,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1752847911116,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ykctfi7BkJ","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"A 12-seed rerun shows active sensing's apparent edge over random and fixed-grid sampling for wildfire calibration is scenario-dependent, not general, and a 5-seed pilot alone would have said otherwise."},"venue":{"value":"RL4XS Workshop Poster"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/1824a74ac424840fd31b6620b05d383fead925ed.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["active experimental design","statistical power","physical-parameter calibration","wildfire simulation","active learning","reproducibility","paired significance testing"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/RL4XS"},"paperhash":{"value":"senan|paired_significance_testing_for_active_experimental_design_comparisons_in_wildfire_model_calibration"},"abstract":{"value":"Autonomous and adaptive experimentation systems are increasingly proposed for the physical sciences, often on the intuition that choosing which measurement to take next, rather than measuring at random or on a fixed schedule, should make better use of a limited observation budget. This project tests this intuition directly on a physical-parameter calibration problem: a stochastic wildfire simulator with three unknown physical parameters, calibrated from field observations under a fixed budget. This project compares an active, uncertainty-seeking sensing policy against random sampling and a fixed monitoring-grid sweep, evaluating all three on held-out calibration loss, burned-area agreement with the true fire, and parameter-recovery error. An initial run with 5 independent fire draws suggested active sensing had a real edge. A properly-powered rerun with 12 independent fire draws did not reproduce it: none of the three strategies are statistically distinguishable on any of the three metrics (paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test, $p \\geq 0.456$ pooled over the full budget sweep). A second scenario with a different wind direction, ignition point, and terrain map does show a significant active-sensing advantage on calibration loss ($p=0.0009$), so the effect is not universally absent, it is scenario-dependent and easy to mistake for a general property of the method from a single underpowered pilot. This project argues that claims about adaptive experimental design improving physical-parameter calibration should be treated as empirical, checkable questions specific to a deployment scenario, not assumed a prior, and it reports the initial false positive as a worked example of why."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nsenan2026paired,\ntitle={Paired Significance Testing for Active Experimental Design Comparisons in Wildfire Model Calibration},\nauthor={Rhea Senan},\nbooktitle={Reinforcement Learning for Experimental Sciences Workshop},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=bU7TbyCQO7}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Paired Significance Testing for Active Experimental Design Comparisons in Wildfire Model Calibration"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Rhea_Senan1","fullname":"Rhea Senan","institutions":[{"name":"California Institute of Technology","domain":"caltech.edu","country":"US"}]}]}},"tmdate":1790872387049,"pdate":1790872385075,"tcdate":1788357214058,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/RL4XS","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/RL4XS/Submission52/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/RL4XS/Submission52/Authors"],"forum":"bU7TbyCQO7","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":52,"cdate":1788357214058,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/RL4XS/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/RL4XS/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/RL4XS/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/RL4XS/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1790872387049,"odate":1790872385075,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/RL4XS","id":"bU7TbyCQO7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Sensors 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/20/23/6717/pdf?version=1606700134"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/SENSORS/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"santos|multisensor_extrinsic_calibration_using_an_extended_set_of_pairwise_geometric_transformations"},"authorids":{"value":["~Vítor_M._F._Santos2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daniela_Rato:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Paulo_Dias:","~Miguel_Oliveira2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s20236717"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/sensors/SantosRDO20,\n  author={Vítor M. F. Santos and Daniela Rato and Paulo Dias and Miguel Oliveira},\n  title={Multi-Sensor Extrinsic Calibration Using an Extended Set of Pairwise Geometric Transformations},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={Sensors},\n  volume={20},\n  number={23},\n  pages={6717},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/s20236717}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Systems composed of multiple sensors for exteroceptive perception are becoming increasingly common, such as mobile robots or highly monitored spaces. However, to combine and fuse those sensors to create a larger and more robust representation of the perceived scene, the sensors need to be properly registered among them, that is, all relative geometric transformations must be known. This calibration procedure is challenging as, traditionally, human intervention is required in variate extents. This paper proposes a nearly automatic method where the best set of geometric transformations among any number of sensors is obtained by processing and combining the individual pairwise transformations obtained from an experimental method. Besides eliminating some experimental outliers with a standard criterion, the method exploits the possibility of obtaining better geometric transformations between all pairs of sensors by combining them within some restrictions to obtain a more precise transformation, and thus a better calibration. Although other data sources are possible, in this approach, 3D point clouds are obtained by each sensor, which correspond to the successive centers of a moving ball its field of view. The method can be applied to any sensors able to detect the ball and the 3D position of its center, namely, LIDARs, mono cameras (visual or infrared), stereo cameras, and TOF cameras. Results demonstrate that calibration is improved when compared to methods in previous works that do not address the outliers problem and, depending on the context, as explained in the results section, the multi-pairwise technique can be used in two different methodologies to reduce uncertainty in the calibration process."},"title":{"value":"Multi-Sensor Extrinsic Calibration Using an Extended Set of Pairwise Geometric Transformations"},"authors":{"value":["Vítor M. F. Santos","Daniela Rato","Paulo Dias","Miguel Oliveira"]}},"tmdate":1753454279950,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1753450773685,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Miguel_Riem_Oliveira1"],"forum":"TuJj1UMOZJ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":588879,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1753454279950,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"TuJj1UMOZJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMBC 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/10339936/10339939/10340381.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMBC/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"furukawa|single_and_multiframe_autocalibration_for_3d_endoscopy_with_differential_rendering"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ryo_Furukawa1","~Ryusuke_Sagawa1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shiro_Oka:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shinji_Tanaka:","~Hiroshi_Kawasaki1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/EMBC40787.2023.10340381"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/embc/0001SOTK23,\n  author={Ryo Furukawa and Ryusuke Sagawa and Shiro Oka and Shinji Tanaka and Hiroshi Kawasaki},\n  title={Single and multi-frame auto-calibration for 3D endoscopy with differential rendering},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={1-5},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/EMBC40787.2023.10340381},\n  booktitle={EMBC},\n  crossref={conf/embc/2023}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The use of 3D measurement in endoscopic images offers practicality in cancer diagnosis, computer-assisted interventions, and making annotations for machine learning training data. An effective approach is the implementation of an active stereo system, using a micro-sized pattern projector and an endoscope camera, which has been intensively developed. One open problem for such a system is the necessity of strict and complex calibration of the projector-camera system to precisely recover the shapes. Moreover, since the head of an endoscope should have enough elasticity to avoid harming target objects, the positions of the pattern projector cannot be tightly fixed to the head, resulting in limited accuracy. A straightforward approach to the problem is applying auto-calibration. However, it requires special markers in the pattern or a highly accurate initial position for stable calibration, which is impractical for real operation. In the paper, we propose a novel auto-calibration method based on differential rendering techniques, which are recently proposed and drawing wide attention. To apply the method to an endoscopic system, where a diffractive optical element (DOE) is used, we propose a technique to simultaneously estimate the focal length of the DOE as well as the extrinsic parameters between a projector and a camera. We also propose a multi-frame optimization algorithm to jointly optimize the intrinsic and extrinsic parameters, relative pose between frames, and the entire shape.Clinical relevance— One-shot endoscopic measurement of depth information is a practical solution for cancer diagnosis, computer-assisted interventions, and making annotations for machine learning training data."},"title":{"value":"Single and multi-frame auto-calibration for 3D endoscopy with differential rendering"},"authors":{"value":["Ryo Furukawa","Ryusuke Sagawa","Shiro Oka","Shinji Tanaka","Hiroshi Kawasaki"]}},"tmdate":1731462535370,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727683012799,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ryo_Furukawa1"],"forum":"HwkK2Bs1mY","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":109341,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1731462535370,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"HwkK2Bs1mY","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Software Eng. 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/32/9378892/08651396.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TSE/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"scalabrino|automatically_assessing_code_understandability"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Simone_Scalabrino:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gabriele_Bavota:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Christopher_Vendome:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mario_Linares-Vásquez:","~Denys_Poshyvanyk1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rocco_Oliveto:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2019.2901468"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tse/ScalabrinoBVLPO21,\n  author={Simone Scalabrino and Gabriele Bavota and Christopher Vendome and Mario Linares-Vásquez and Denys Poshyvanyk and Rocco Oliveto},\n  title={Automatically Assessing Code Understandability},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Software Eng.},\n  volume={47},\n  number={3},\n  pages={595-613},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2019.2901468}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Understanding software is an inherent requirement for many maintenance and evolution tasks. Without a thorough understanding of the code, developers would not be able to fix bugs or add new features timely. Measuring code understandability might be useful to guide developers in writing better code, and could also help in estimating the effort required to modify code components. Unfortunately, there are no metrics designed to assess the understandability of code snippets. In this work, we perform an extensive evaluation of 121 existing as well as new code-related, documentation-related, and developer-related metrics. We try to (i) correlate each metric with understandability and (ii) build models combining metrics to assess understandability. To do this, we use 444 human evaluations from 63 developers and we obtained a bold negative result: none of the 121 experimented metrics is able to capture code understandability, not even the ones assumed to assess quality attributes apparently related, such as code readability and complexity. While we observed some improvements while combining metrics in models, their effectiveness is still far from making them suitable for practical applications. Finally, we conducted interviews with five professional developers to understand the factors that influence their ability to understand code snippets, aiming at identifying possible new metrics."},"title":{"value":"Automatically Assessing Code Understandability"},"authors":{"value":["Simone Scalabrino","Gabriele Bavota","Christopher Vendome","Mario Linares-Vásquez","Denys Poshyvanyk","Rocco Oliveto"]}},"tmdate":1753718358049,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1753718340917,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Denys_Poshyvanyk1"],"forum":"fi3lLem7Vw","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":597816,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753718358049,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"fi3lLem7Vw","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP (Findings) 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-emnlp.482.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|recal_samplerelation_guided_confidence_calibration_over_tabular_data"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.findings-emnlp.482"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/WangZHWCBW23,\n  author={Haotian Wang and Zhen Zhang and Mengting Hu and Qichao Wang and Liang Chen and Yatao Bian and Bingzhe Wu},\n  title={RECAL: Sample-Relation Guided Confidence Calibration over Tabular Data},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={7246-7257},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.findings-emnlp.482},\n  booktitle={EMNLP (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2023f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Tabular-format data is widely adopted in various real-world applications. Various machine learning models have achieved remarkable success in both industrial applications and data-science competitions. Despite these successes, most current machine learning methods for tabular data lack accurate confidence estimation, which is needed by some high-risk sensitive applications such as credit modeling and financial fraud detection. In this paper, we study the confidence estimation of machine learning models applied to tabular data. The key finding of our paper is that a real-world tabular dataset typically contains implicit sample relations, and this can further help to obtain a more accurate estimation. To this end, we introduce a general post-training confidence calibration framework named RECAL to calibrate the predictive confidence of current machine learning models by employing graph neural networks to model the relations between different samples. We perform extensive experiments on tabular datasets with both implicit and explicit graph structures and show that RECAL can significantly improve the calibration quality compared to the conventional method without considering the sample relations."},"title":{"value":"RECAL: Sample-Relation Guided Confidence Calibration over Tabular Data"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Haotian Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Zhen Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Mengting Hu","username":""},{"fullname":"Qichao Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Liang Chen","username":"~Liang_Chen7"},{"fullname":"Yatao Bian","username":""},{"fullname":"Bingzhe Wu","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1793078049739,"pdate":1703980800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/WangZHWCBW23"],"tcdate":1793078046041,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Liang_Chen7"],"forum":"CIv46YIqOW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":215572,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1793078049739,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"CIv46YIqOW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICONIP (4) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-981-95-4384-7_27.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wen|domain_generalization_with_clipbased_multimodal_calibration_distillation"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-95-4384-7_27"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/iconip/WenZZHLXM25,\n  author={Zhiyu Wen and Pei Zhang and Ming Zhang and Xiaohong Huang and Dandan Li and Kun Xie and Yan Ma},\n  title={Domain Generalization with CLIP-Based Multi-modal Calibration Distillation},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={384-395},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-95-4384-7_27},\n  booktitle={ICONIP (4)},\n  crossref={conf/iconip/2025-4}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Domain generalization aims to enhance the performance of neural network models on unseen target domains by addressing the distribution shift between source and target domains. The rise of large model technologies, particularly the emergence of vision-language models (VLMs), has provided new solutions for domain generalization. Through knowledge distillation, the powerful generalization capabilities of VLMs can be transferred to compact student models, making them adaptable for resource-constrained scenarios. Nevertheless, current studies on visual-language knowledge distillation often overlook the potential inconsistency between visual and textual encodings, leading to limited generalization of student models. To this end, we propose the CLIP-based multi-modal calibration distillation (termed CMCD). The framework firstly fuses visual and textual encodings according to their cross-modal similarity (referred to as encoding calibration), then distills the calibrated encodings into the student model. Additionally, we apply normalization to eliminate domain-specific style features in visual encodings, thus enhancing the generalization capability of the student model. Extensive experiments on multiple domain generalization benchmarks verify that CMCD outperforms the state-of-the-art methods, demonstrating an average classification accuracy improvement of 4.67%."},"title":{"value":"Domain Generalization with CLIP-Based Multi-modal Calibration Distillation"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhiyu Wen","username":""},{"fullname":"Pei Zhang","username":"~Pei_Zhang17"},{"fullname":"Ming Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Xiaohong Huang","username":""},{"fullname":"Dandan Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Kun Xie","username":""},{"fullname":"Yan Ma","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784646755490,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/iconip/WenZZHLXM25"],"tcdate":1784646745704,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Pei_Zhang17"],"forum":"x1L6Y6N03v","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":85005,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784646755490,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"x1L6Y6N03v","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.06715v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"yun|sensor_calibration_model_balancing_accuracy_realtime_and_efficiency"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinyong_Yun:","~Hyungjin_Kim1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Seokho_Ahn:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Euijong_Lee:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Young-Duk_Seo:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.06715"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2511-06715,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jinyong Yun and Hyungjin Kim and Seokho Ahn and Euijong Lee and Young-Duk Seo},\n  title={Sensor Calibration Model Balancing Accuracy, Real-time, and Efficiency},\n  year={2025},\n  month={November},\n  cdate={1761955200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2511.06715},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.06715}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Most on-device sensor calibration studies benchmark models only against three macroscopic requirements (i.e., accuracy, real-time, and resource efficiency), thereby hiding deployment bottlenecks such as instantaneous error and worst-case latency. We therefore decompose this triad into eight microscopic requirements and introduce Scare (Sensor Calibration model balancing Accuracy, Real-time, and Efficiency), an ultra-compressed transformer that fulfills them all. SCARE comprises three core components: (1) Sequence Lens Projector (SLP) that logarithmically compresses time-series data while preserving boundary information across bins, (2) Efficient Bitwise Attention (EBA) module that replaces costly multiplications with bitwise operations via binary hash codes, and (3) Hash optimization strategy that ensures stable training without auxiliary loss terms. Together, these components minimize computational overhead while maintaining high accuracy and compatibility with microcontroller units (MCUs). Extensive experiments on large-scale air-quality datasets and real microcontroller deployments demonstrate that Scare outperforms existing linear, hybrid, and deep-learning baselines, making Scare, to the best of our knowledge, the first model to meet all eight microscopic requirements simultaneously."},"title":{"value":"Sensor Calibration Model Balancing Accuracy, Real-time, and Efficiency"},"authors":{"value":["Jinyong Yun","Hyungjin Kim","Seokho Ahn","Euijong Lee","Young-Duk Seo"]}},"tmdate":1771452817814,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2511-06715"],"tcdate":1771452807443,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hyungjin_Kim1"],"forum":"4bhy1IIZhL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":825443,"cdate":1761955200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1771452817814,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4bhy1IIZhL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP (Findings) 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-emnlp.482.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/EMNLP/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|recal_samplerelation_guided_confidence_calibration_over_tabular_data"},"authorids":{"value":["~Haotian_Wang7","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhen_Zhang:","~Mengting_Hu1","~Qichao_Wang1","~Liang_Chen28","~Yatao_Bian1","~Bingzhe_Wu1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.findings-emnlp.482"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/WangZHWCBW23,\n  author={Haotian Wang and Zhen Zhang and Mengting Hu and Qichao Wang and Liang Chen and Yatao Bian and Bingzhe Wu},\n  title={RECAL: Sample-Relation Guided Confidence Calibration over Tabular Data},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  pages={7246-7257},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.findings-emnlp.482},\n  booktitle={EMNLP (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2023f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Tabular-format data is widely adopted in various real-world applications. Various machine learning models have achieved remarkable success in both industrial applications and data-science competitions. Despite these successes, most current machine learning methods for tabular data lack accurate confidence estimation, which is needed by some high-risk sensitive applications such as credit modeling and financial fraud detection. In this paper, we study the confidence estimation of machine learning models applied to tabular data. The key finding of our paper is that a real-world tabular dataset typically contains implicit sample relations, and this can further help to obtain a more accurate estimation. To this end, we introduce a general post-training confidence calibration framework named RECAL to calibrate the predictive confidence of current machine learning models by employing graph neural networks to model the relations between different samples. We perform extensive experiments on tabular datasets with both implicit and explicit graph structures and show that RECAL can significantly improve the calibration quality compared to the conventional method without considering the sample relations."},"title":{"value":"RECAL: Sample-Relation Guided Confidence Calibration over Tabular Data"},"authors":{"value":["Haotian Wang","Zhen Zhang","Mengting Hu","Qichao Wang","Liang Chen","Yatao Bian","Bingzhe Wu"]}},"tmdate":1762551107985,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1727454055858,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yatao_Bian1"],"forum":"SzCucWDuop","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":88136,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1762551107985,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SzCucWDuop","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.01203v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|pseudolabel_calibration_semisupervised_multimodal_entity_alignment"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luyao_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pengnian_Qi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xigang_Bao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chunlai_Zhou:","~Biao_Qin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.01203"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2403-01203,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Luyao Wang and Pengnian Qi and Xigang Bao and Chunlai Zhou and Biao Qin},\n  title={Pseudo-Label Calibration Semi-supervised Multi-Modal Entity Alignment},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2403.01203},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.01203}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-modal entity alignment (MMEA) aims to identify equivalent entities between two multi-modal knowledge graphs for integration. Unfortunately, prior arts have attempted to improve the interaction and fusion of multi-modal information, which have overlooked the influence of modal-specific noise and the usage of labeled and unlabeled data in semi-supervised settings. In this work, we introduce a Pseudo-label Calibration Multi-modal Entity Alignment (PCMEA) in a semi-supervised way. Specifically, in order to generate holistic entity representations, we first devise various embedding modules and attention mechanisms to extract visual, structural, relational, and attribute features. Different from the prior direct fusion methods, we next propose to exploit mutual information maximization to filter the modal-specific noise and to augment modal-invariant commonality. Then, we combine pseudo-label calibration with momentum-based contrastive learning to make full use of the labeled and unlabeled data, which improves the quality of pseudo-label and pulls aligned entities closer. Finally, extensive experiments on two MMEA datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of our PCMEA, which yields state-of-the-art performance."},"title":{"value":"Pseudo-Label Calibration Semi-supervised Multi-Modal Entity Alignment"},"authors":{"value":["Luyao Wang","Pengnian Qi","Xigang Bao","Chunlai Zhou","Biao Qin"]}},"tmdate":1747751143529,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747751139884,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Biao_Qin2"],"forum":"Qu8XuswwyI","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":539827,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747751143529,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Qu8XuswwyI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"AAAI 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/28762/29463"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/AAAI/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|pseudolabel_calibration_semisupervised_multimodal_entity_alignment"},"authorids":{"value":["~Luyao_Wang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pengnian_Qi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xigang_Bao:","~Chunlai_Zhou1","~Biao_Qin1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v38i8.28762"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/aaai/WangQBZQ24,\n  author={Luyao Wang and Pengnian Qi and Xigang Bao and Chunlai Zhou and Biao Qin},\n  title={Pseudo-Label Calibration Semi-supervised Multi-Modal Entity Alignment},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={9116-9124},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v38i8.28762},\n  booktitle={AAAI},\n  crossref={conf/aaai/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-modal entity alignment (MMEA) aims to identify equivalent entities between two multi-modal knowledge graphs for integration. Unfortunately, prior arts have attempted to improve the interaction and fusion of multi-modal information, which have overlooked the influence of modal-specific noise and the usage of labeled and unlabeled data in semi-supervised settings. In this work, we introduce a Pseudo-label Calibration Multi-modal Entity Alignment (PCMEA) in a semi-supervised way. Specifically, in order to generate holistic entity representations, we first devise various embedding modules and attention mechanisms to extract visual, structural, relational, and attribute features. Different from the prior direct fusion methods, we next propose to exploit mutual information maximization to filter the modal-specific noise and to augment modal-invariant commonality. Then, we combine pseudo-label calibration with momentum-based contrastive learning to make full use of the labeled and unlabeled data, which improves the quality of pseudo-label and pulls aligned entities closer. Finally, extensive experiments on two MMEA datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of our PCMEA, which yields state-of-the-art performance."},"title":{"value":"Pseudo-Label Calibration Semi-supervised Multi-Modal Entity Alignment"},"authors":{"value":["Luyao Wang","Pengnian Qi","Xigang Bao","Chunlai Zhou","Biao Qin"]}},"tmdate":1747751141910,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1713996734388,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chunlai_Zhou1"],"forum":"0mvYIxGYB6","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":3565,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747751141910,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0mvYIxGYB6","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACML 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"http://proceedings.mlr.press/v129/zha20a/zha20a.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACML/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"zha|efficient_attention_calibration_network_for_realtime_semantic_segmentation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hengfeng_Zha:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rui_Liu_0015:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dongsheng_Zhou:","~Xin_Yang8","~Qiang_Zhang13","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiaopeng_Wei:"]},"html":{"value":"http://proceedings.mlr.press/v129/zha20a.html"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acml/ZhaLZYZW20,\n  author={Hengfeng Zha and Rui Liu and Dongsheng Zhou and Xin Yang and Qiang Zhang and Xiaopeng Wei},\n  title={Efficient Attention Calibration Network for Real-Time Semantic Segmentation},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={337-352},\n  url={http://proceedings.mlr.press/v129/zha20a.html},\n  booktitle={ACML},\n  crossref={conf/acml/2020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In recent years, the attention mechanism has been widely used in computer vision. Semantic segmentation, as one of the fundamental tasks of computer vision, has been subject to tremendous development as a result. But because of its huge computing overhead, attention-based approaches are difficult to use for real-time applications such as self-driving. In this paper, we propose a self-calibration method baesd on self-attentiion that successfully applies the attention mechanism to real-time semantic segmentation. Specifically, a spatial attention module to adjust the edges of the coarse segmentation results which gained from the real-time semantic segmentation backbone network, and obtain more granular segmentation results. We refer to this method as the Efficient Attentional Calibration Network (EACNet). Experiments on the Cityscapes dataset validate the efficiency and performance of the method. With the high-resolution input and without any post-processing, EACNet achieved 72.4% mIoU of accuracy while running at 116.9 FPS. Compared to other state-of-the-art methods for real-time semantic segmentation, our network gained a better balance between performance and speed."},"title":{"value":"Efficient Attention Calibration Network for Real-Time Semantic Segmentation"},"authors":{"value":["Hengfeng Zha","Rui Liu","Dongsheng Zhou","Xin Yang","Qiang Zhang","Xiaopeng Wei"]}},"tmdate":1747379428689,"pdate":1577836800000,"tcdate":1731490987308,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Qiang_Zhang13"],"forum":"8FbE3pfHlb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":221409,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747379428689,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"8FbE3pfHlb","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Inf. Theory 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/18/8694135/08550743.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TIT/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|blind_gain_and_phase_calibration_via_sparse_spectral_methods"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yanjun_Li_0001:","~Kiryung_Lee1","~Yoram_Bresler1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.2018.2883623"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tit/LiLB19,\n  author={Yanjun Li and Kiryung Lee and Yoram Bresler},\n  title={Blind Gain and Phase Calibration via Sparse Spectral Methods},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Inf. Theory},\n  volume={65},\n  number={5},\n  pages={3097-3123},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.2018.2883623}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Blind gain and phase calibration (BGPC) is a bilinear inverse problem involving the determination of unknown gains and phases of the sensing system, and the unknown signal, jointly. BGPC arises in numerous applications, e.g., blind albedo estimation in inverse rendering, synthetic aperture radar autofocus, and sensor array auto-calibration. In some cases, sparse structure in the unknown signal alleviates the ill-posedness of BGPC. Recently, there has been renewed interest in solutions to BGPC with careful analysis of error bounds. In this paper, we formulate BGPC as an eigenvalue/eigenvector problem and propose to solve it via power iteration, or in the sparsity or joint sparsity case, via truncated power iteration. Under certain assumptions, the unknown gains, phases, and the unknown signal can be recovered simultaneously. Numerical experiments show that power iteration algorithms work not only in the regime predicted by our main results, but also in regimes where theoretical analysis is limited. We also show that our power iteration algorithms for BGPC compare favorably with competing algorithms in adversarial conditions, e.g., with noisy measurement or with a bad initial estimate."},"title":{"value":"Blind Gain and Phase Calibration via Sparse Spectral Methods"},"authors":{"value":["Yanjun Li","Kiryung Lee","Yoram Bresler"]}},"tmdate":1747368210933,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1741218222774,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yoram_Bresler1"],"forum":"Jit8KwvvHK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":356126,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747368210933,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Jit8KwvvHK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1712.00111v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|blind_gain_and_phase_calibration_via_sparse_spectral_methods"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yanjun_Li_0001:","~Kiryung_Lee1","~Yoram_Bresler1"]},"html":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1712.00111"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1712-00111,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yanjun Li and Kiryung Lee and Yoram Bresler},\n  title={Blind Gain and Phase Calibration via Sparse Spectral Methods},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/1712.00111},\n  url={http://arxiv.org/abs/1712.00111}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Blind gain and phase calibration (BGPC) is a bilinear inverse problem involving the determination of unknown gains and phases of the sensing system, and the unknown signal, jointly. BGPC arises in numerous applications, e.g., blind albedo estimation in inverse rendering, synthetic aperture radar autofocus, and sensor array auto-calibration. In some cases, sparse structure in the unknown signal alleviates the ill-posedness of BGPC. Recently there has been renewed interest in solutions to BGPC with careful analysis of error bounds. In this paper, we formulate BGPC as an eigenvalue/eigenvector problem, and propose to solve it via power iteration, or in the sparsity or joint sparsity case, via truncated power iteration. Under certain assumptions, the unknown gains, phases, and the unknown signal can be recovered simultaneously. Numerical experiments show that power iteration algorithms work not only in the regime predicted by our main results, but also in regimes where theoretical analysis is limited. We also show that our power iteration algorithms for BGPC compare favorably with competing algorithms in adversarial conditions, e.g., with noisy measurement or with a bad initial estimate."},"title":{"value":"Blind Gain and Phase Calibration via Sparse Spectral Methods"},"authors":{"value":["Yanjun Li","Kiryung Lee","Yoram Bresler"]}},"tmdate":1747368210803,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1741218221838,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yoram_Bresler1"],"forum":"HjA19Zn9yd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":356094,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747368210803,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"HjA19Zn9yd","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2023 Findings"},"TLDR":{"value":"A general post-training framework, RECAL, calibrates the confidence of ML models. It models relations between table samples, using an element-wise scaling approach to improve confidence."},"Submission_Track_2":{"value":"Interpretability, Interactivity, and Analysis of Models for NLP"},"keywords":{"value":["Confidence Calibration","Tabular Data","Element-Wise Temperature Scaling"]},"abstract":{"value":"Tabular-format data is widely adopted in various real-world applications. Various machine learning models have achieved remarkable success in both industrial applications and data-science competitions. Despite these successes, most current machine learning methods for tabular data lack accurate confidence estimation, which is needed by some high-risk sensitive applications such as credit modeling and financial fraud detection. In this paper, we study the confidence estimation of machine learning models applied to tabular data. The key finding of our paper is that a real-world tabular dataset typically contains implicit sample relations, and this can further help to obtain a more accurate estimation. To this end, we introduce a general post-training confidence calibration framework named RECAL to calibrate the predictive confidence of current machine learning models by employing graph neural networks to model the relations between different samples. We perform extensive experiments on tabular datasets with both implicit and explicit graph structures and show that RECAL can significantly improve the calibration quality compared to the conventional method without considering the sample relations."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nhaotian2023recal,\ntitle={{RECAL}: Sample-Relation Guided Confidence Calibration over Tabular Data},\nauthor={Wang HaoTian and Zhen Zhang and Mengting Hu and Qichao Wang and Liang Chen and Yatao Bian and Bingzhe Wu},\nbooktitle={The 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing},\nyear={2023},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=YMlYb8cWgE}\n}"},"title":{"value":"RECAL: Sample-Relation Guided Confidence Calibration over Tabular Data"},"Submission_Type":{"value":"Regular Long Paper"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/467b4260f058b924931e2eef3c56c6045d229811.pdf"},"Submission_Track":{"value":"Machine Learning for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"EMNLP/2023/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"haotian|recal_samplerelation_guided_confidence_calibration_over_tabular_data"},"authorids":{"value":["~Wang_HaoTian2","~Zhen_Zhang30","~Mengting_Hu1","~Qichao_Wang1","~Liang_Chen17","~Yatao_Bian1","~Bingzhe_Wu1"]},"authors":{"value":["Wang HaoTian","Zhen Zhang","Mengting Hu","Qichao Wang","Liang Chen","Yatao Bian","Bingzhe Wu"]}},"tmdate":1701459717476,"pdate":1696709241955,"tcdate":1686676714011,"writers":["EMNLP/2023/Conference","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission789/Authors"],"signatures":["EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission789/Authors"],"forum":"YMlYb8cWgE","number":789,"cdate":1686676714011,"mdate":1701459717476,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Submission","EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Post_Submission","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission789/-/Revision","EMNLP/2023/Conference/-/Edit","EMNLP/2023/Conference/Submission789/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"odate":1701459717459,"domain":"EMNLP/2023/Conference","id":"YMlYb8cWgE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"2022 IEEE International Conference on Software Analysis, Evolution and Reengineering"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"cai|cssam_code_search_via_attention_matching_of_code_semantics_and_structures"},"authorids":{"value":["~Bo_Cai2","~Yaoxiang_Yu1","csehuyi@whu.edu.cn"]},"html":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/stamp/stamp.jsp?tp=&arnumber=9825891"},"abstract":{"value":"The proper names of software engineering functions and methods can greatly assist developers in understanding and maintaining the code. Most researchers convert the method name generation task into the text summarization task. They take the token sequence and the abstract syntax tree (AST) of source code as input, and generate method names with a decoder. However, most proposed models learn semantic and structural features of the source code separately, resulting in poor performance in the method name generation task. Actually, each token in source code must have a corresponding node in its AST. Inspired by this observation, we propose SGMNG, a structure-guided method name generation model that learns the representation of two combined features. Additionally, we build a code graph called code relation graph (CRG) to describe the code structure clearly. CRG retains the structure of the AST of source code and contains data flows and control flows. SGMNG captures the semantic features of the code by encoding the token sequence and captures the structural features of the code by encoding the CRG. Then, SGMNG matches tokens in the sequence and nodes in the CRG to construct the combination of two features. We demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed approach on the public dataset Java-Small with 700K samples, which indicates that our approach achieves significant improvement over the state-of-the-art baseline models in the ROUGE metric."},"title":{"value":"CSSAM: Code Search via Attention Matching of Code Semantics and Structures"},"authors":{"value":["Bo Cai","Yaoxiang Yu","Yi Hu"]}},"tmdate":1749381710557,"pdate":1647273600000,"tcdate":1749381710557,"writers":["~Bo_Cai2","~Yaoxiang_Yu1","csehuyi@whu.edu.cn"],"signatures":["~Bo_Cai2"],"forum":"SP4srNTTbD","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","number":37354,"cdate":1749381710557,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1749381710557,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"SP4srNTTbD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.02856v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"hayoun|physics_and_semantic_informed_multisensor_calibration_via_optimization_theory_and_selfsupervised_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shmuel_Y._Hayoun:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Meir_Halachmi:","~Doron_Serebro1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kfir_Twizer:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Elinor_Medezinski:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Liron_Korkidi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Moshik_Cohen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Itai_Orr:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2206.02856"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2206-02856,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shmuel Y. Hayoun and Meir Halachmi and Doron Serebro and Kfir Twizer and Elinor Medezinski and Liron Korkidi and Moshik Cohen and Itai Orr},\n  title={Physics and semantic informed multi-sensor calibration via optimization theory and self-supervised learning},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2206.02856},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2206.02856}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Achieving safe and reliable autonomous driving relies greatly on the ability to achieve an accurate and robust perception system; however, this cannot be fully realized without precisely calibrated sensors. Environmental and operational conditions as well as improper maintenance can produce calibration errors inhibiting sensor fusion and, consequently, degrading the perception performance. Traditionally, sensor calibration is performed in a controlled environment with one or more known targets. Such a procedure can only be carried out in between drives and requires manual operation; a tedious task if needed to be conducted on a regular basis. This sparked a recent interest in online targetless methods, capable of yielding a set of geometric transformations based on perceived environmental features, however, the required redundancy in sensing modalities makes this task even more challenging, as the features captured by each modality and their distinctiveness may vary. We present a holistic approach to performing joint calibration of a camera-lidar-radar trio. Leveraging prior knowledge and physical properties of these sensing modalities together with semantic information, we propose two targetless calibration methods within a cost minimization framework once via direct online optimization, and second via self-supervised learning (SSL)."},"title":{"value":"Physics and semantic informed multi-sensor calibration via optimization theory and self-supervised learning"},"authors":{"value":["Shmuel Y. Hayoun","Meir Halachmi","Doron Serebro","Kfir Twizer","Elinor Medezinski","Liron Korkidi","Moshik Cohen","Itai Orr"]}},"tmdate":1769197248939,"pdate":1672444800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2206-02856"],"tcdate":1769197246063,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Doron_Serebro1"],"forum":"4UIhXwexLX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":793332,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769197248939,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"4UIhXwexLX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.04324v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"mishra|granite_code_models_a_family_of_open_foundation_models_for_code_intelligence"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mayank_Mishra:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Matt_Stallone:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gaoyuan_Zhang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yikang_Shen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Aditya_Prasad:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Adriana_Meza_Soria:","~Michele_Merler1","~Parameswaran_Selvam1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Saptha_Surendran:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shivdeep_Singh:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Manish_Sethi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuan-Hong_Dang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pengyuan_Li_0013:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kun-Lung_Wu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Syed_Zawad:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andrew_Coleman:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Matthew_White:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mark_Lewis:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Raju_Pavuluri:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yan_Koyfman:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Boris_Lublinsky:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Maximilien_de_Bayser:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ibrahim_Abdelaziz:","~Kinjal_Basu2","~Mayank_Agarwal1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yi_Zhou_0015:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chris_Johnson:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Aanchal_Goyal:","~Hima_Patel1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:S._Yousaf_Shah:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Petros_Zerfos:","~Heiko_Ludwig1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Asim_Munawar:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Maxwell_Crouse:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pavan_Kapanipathi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shweta_Salaria:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bob_Calio:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sophia_Wen:","~Seetharami_Seelam1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Brian_Belgodere:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Carlos_A._Fonseca:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Amith_Singhee:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nirmit_Desai:","~David_D._Cox1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ruchir_Puri:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rameswar_Panda:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.04324"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2405-04324,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Mayank Mishra and Matt Stallone and Gaoyuan Zhang and Yikang Shen and Aditya Prasad and Adriana Meza Soria and Michele Merler and Parameswaran Selvam and Saptha Surendran and Shivdeep Singh and Manish Sethi and Xuan-Hong Dang and Pengyuan Li and Kun-Lung Wu and Syed Zawad and Andrew Coleman and Matthew White and Mark Lewis and Raju Pavuluri and Yan Koyfman and Boris Lublinsky and Maximilien de Bayser and Ibrahim Abdelaziz and Kinjal Basu and Mayank Agarwal and Yi Zhou and Chris Johnson and Aanchal Goyal and Hima Patel and S. Yousaf Shah and Petros Zerfos and Heiko Ludwig and Asim Munawar and Maxwell Crouse and Pavan Kapanipathi and Shweta Salaria and Bob Calio and Sophia Wen and Seetharami Seelam and Brian Belgodere and Carlos A. Fonseca and Amith Singhee and Nirmit Desai and David D. Cox and Ruchir Puri and Rameswar Panda},\n  title={Granite Code Models: A Family of Open Foundation Models for Code Intelligence},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2405.04324},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.04324}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) trained on code are revolutionizing the software development process. Increasingly, code LLMs are being integrated into software development environments to improve the productivity of human programmers, and LLM-based agents are beginning to show promise for handling complex tasks autonomously. Realizing the full potential of code LLMs requires a wide range of capabilities, including code generation, fixing bugs, explaining and documenting code, maintaining repositories, and more. In this work, we introduce the Granite series of decoder-only code models for code generative tasks, trained with code written in 116 programming languages. The Granite Code models family consists of models ranging in size from 3 to 34 billion parameters, suitable for applications ranging from complex application modernization tasks to on-device memory-constrained use cases. Evaluation on a comprehensive set of tasks demonstrates that Granite Code models consistently reaches state-of-the-art performance among available open-source code LLMs. The Granite Code model family was optimized for enterprise software development workflows and performs well across a range of coding tasks (e.g. code generation, fixing and explanation), making it a versatile all around code model. We release all our Granite Code models under an Apache 2.0 license for both research and commercial use."},"title":{"value":"Granite Code Models: A Family of Open Foundation Models for Code Intelligence"},"authors":{"value":["Mayank Mishra","Matt Stallone","Gaoyuan Zhang","Yikang Shen","Aditya Prasad","Adriana Meza Soria","Michele Merler","Parameswaran Selvam","Saptha Surendran","Shivdeep Singh","Manish Sethi","Xuan-Hong Dang","Pengyuan Li","Kun-Lung Wu","Syed Zawad","Andrew Coleman","Matthew White","Mark Lewis","Raju Pavuluri","Yan Koyfman","Boris Lublinsky","Maximilien de Bayser","Ibrahim Abdelaziz","Kinjal Basu","Mayank Agarwal","Yi Zhou","Chris Johnson","Aanchal Goyal","Hima Patel","S. Yousaf Shah","Petros Zerfos","Heiko Ludwig","Asim Munawar","Maxwell Crouse","Pavan Kapanipathi","Shweta Salaria","Bob Calio","Sophia Wen","Seetharami Seelam","Brian Belgodere","Carlos A. Fonseca","Amith Singhee","Nirmit Desai","David D. Cox","Ruchir Puri","Rameswar Panda"]}},"tmdate":1792170578763,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1718943656095,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hima_Patel1"],"forum":"xviitwRwwD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":38509,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1792170578763,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xviitwRwwD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"FAccT ’22: Proceedings of the 2022 ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and Transparency, pp. 2026-2034"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"loi|is_calibration_a_fairness_requirement_an_argument_from_the_point_of_view_of_moral_philosophy_and_decision_theory"},"authorids":{"value":["~Michele_Loi2","~Christoph_Heitz1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3531146.3533245"},"abstract":{"value":"This paper evaluates whether calibration between groups is morally required for fair machine-learning systems. It compares calibration with equality of false positive and false negative rates, treating fairness as a question of wrongful discrimination. The paper clarifies when calibration has moral force and when other fairness criteria may better capture the relevant discriminatory harm."},"title":{"value":"Is Calibration a Fairness Requirement?: An Argument from the Point of View of Moral Philosophy and Decision Theory"},"authors":{"value":["Michele Loi","Christoph Heitz"]}},"tmdate":1778228429305,"pdate":1622930400000,"tcdate":1778228429305,"writers":["~Michele_Loi2","~Christoph_Heitz1"],"signatures":["~Michele_Loi2"],"forum":"qP3bHy51PZ","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":50213,"cdate":1778228429305,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1778228429305,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"qP3bHy51PZ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Pattern Recognit. Lett. 1995"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/PRL/1995"},"paperhash":{"value":"schettini|colorimetric_calibration_of_color_scanners_by_backpropagation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Raimondo_Schettini3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bruno_Barolo:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Elisabetta_Boldrin:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0167-8655(95)00037-H"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/prl/SchettiniBB95,\n  author={Raimondo Schettini and Bruno Barolo and Elisabetta Boldrin},\n  title={Colorimetric calibration of color scanners by back-propagation},\n  year={1995},\n  cdate={788918400000},\n  journal={Pattern Recognit. Lett.},\n  volume={16},\n  number={10},\n  pages={1051-1056},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/0167-8655(95)00037-H}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Colorimetric calibration makes it possible to define a mapping function between the color coordinate system of a particular device and a CIE (Commision Internationale de Eclairage) standard representation of color. A simple method for calibrating a color scanner is described here. It employs a multilayer feed-forward neural network trained by back-propagation, using a standard color set as the training set. Experimental results appear to confirm the feasibility of the method."},"title":{"value":"Colorimetric calibration of color scanners by back-propagation"},"authors":{"value":["Raimondo Schettini","Bruno Barolo","Elisabetta Boldrin"]}},"tmdate":1762331811536,"pdate":788918400000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/prl/SchettiniBB95"],"tcdate":1762331790855,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Raimondo_Schettini3"],"forum":"pulxOLfNsz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":657826,"cdate":788918400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1762331811536,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"pulxOLfNsz","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We study how visual token pruning affects confidence calibration in MLLMs and show that moderate coverage-based pruning can improve calibration without hurting accuracy."},"venue":{"value":"MMRAgI Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6726a50aea799eca76f7dd994f3535a4dd8e784e.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["multimodal reasoning","MLLMs","visual token pruning","calibration","confidence estimation","efficient inference"]},"venueid":{"value":"thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/MMRAgI"},"paperhash":{"value":"tan|does_visual_token_pruning_improve_calibration_an_empirical_study_on_confidence_in_mllms"},"authorids":{"value":["~Kaizhen_Tan1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Visual token pruning is a widely used strategy for efficient inference in multimodal large language models (MLLMs), but existing work mainly evaluates it with task accuracy. In this paper, we study how visual token pruning affects model calibration, that is, whether predicted confidence matches actual correctness. Using LLaVA-1.5-7B on POPE and ScienceQA-IMG, we evaluate Expected Calibration Error (ECE), Brier score, and AURC under several pruning strategies, including SCOPE with different saliency weights, saliency-only pruning, FastV, and random pruning, across multiple token budgets. Our results show that pruning does not simply trade reliability for efficiency. On POPE, a pure-coverage setting in SCOPE achieves substantially lower ECE than the full unpruned model while maintaining similar accuracy. An internal α-sweep further shows a consistent trend: reducing the saliency weight improves calibration at all tested token budgets, while accuracy changes only slightly. In contrast, saliency-based pruning leads to worse calibration, and real FastV causes severe performance degradation in our setting. On ScienceQA-IMG, pruning also reduces ECE, with accuracy remaining stable or slightly improving. We additionally study the gap power exponent in coverage-based selection and find that its default setting is not always optimal. Overall, our results suggest that visual token pruning should be evaluated not only by accuracy, but also by confidence quality, especially for multimodal systems that need reliable decisions."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\ntan2026does,\ntitle={Does Visual Token Pruning Improve Calibration? An Empirical Study on Confidence in {MLLM}s},\nauthor={Kaizhen Tan},\nbooktitle={The 2nd Workshop on Multi-Modal Reasoning for Agentic Intelligence},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=VFthcjyrvd}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Does Visual Token Pruning Improve Calibration? An Empirical Study on Confidence in MLLMs"},"authors":{"value":["Kaizhen Tan"]}},"tmdate":1790802663620,"pdate":1779086807863,"tcdate":1776044172837,"writers":["thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/MMRAgI","thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/MMRAgI/Submission21/Authors"],"signatures":["thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/MMRAgI/Submission21/Authors"],"forum":"VFthcjyrvd","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":21,"cdate":1776044172837,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/MMRAgI/-/Submission","thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/MMRAgI/-/Post_Submission","thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/MMRAgI/-/Edit"],"mdate":1790802663620,"odate":1790802663598,"domain":"thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/MMRAgI","id":"VFthcjyrvd","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Multi-feature SAE ablation passes the random-K significance test on refusal rate, but a Claude-as-judge audit shows the effect is hallucination induction, not calibration."},"venue":{"value":"Mech Interp Workshop ICML 2026 Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Sparse Autoencoders","Steering","Probing"]},"abstract":{"value":"We replicate Ferrando et al.'s (2024) entity-recognition SAE feature pattern on a 27B reasoning-tuned model (Qwen3.6-27B) using a paper-grade Top-$K$ Sparse Autoencoder, then test whether the feature is causally usable for hallucination calibration. The single best SAE latent at layer 31 reaches AUROC $0.814$ (95% bootstrap CI $[0.731, 0.898]$) on cross-type known-vs-unknown classification, with the entity-recognition signal peaking in the mid-stack as Ferrando reported on Gemma-2-2B-IT (peak around layer 9 in their 26-layer model). The SAE latent is statistically indistinguishable from a layer-32 L2 logistic regression probe ($0.887$ $[0.823, 0.941]$) and a diff-of-means probe ($0.859$): the contribution is interpretability, not raw classification performance. Single-feature steering produces a null effect on refusal calibration. Multi-feature top-$K$ ablation at small $K{=}200$ ($0.3$% of the dictionary) produces a $4$–$8\\sigma$ effect against a random-$K$ null — in contrast with prior large-$K$ work where the random control nullifies the signal (Al-Qurashi 2025) — but a Claude-as-judge correctness audit reveals the effect is *induced confabulation*: known-entity incorrect-answer rate rises from $62$% to $77$% while correct-answer rate drops from $8$% to $0$%. Statistical significance against random-$K$ is necessary but not sufficient evidence for a calibration mechanism: the intervention identifies a hallucination-induction circuit, not a calibration knob."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nvicentino2026hallucinationinduction,\ntitle={Hallucination-Induction, Not Calibration: When Multi-Feature {SAE} Steering Looks Like It Works},\nauthor={Caio Sanford Guimar{\\~a}es Vicentino},\nbooktitle={Mechanistic Interpretability Workshop at ICML 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=8HVIWyekTJ}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Hallucination-Induction, Not Calibration: When Multi-Feature SAE Steering Looks Like It Works"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/cd0787ff7f992ba0ef501c039d6baff29c522dc4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp"},"paperhash":{"value":"vicentino|hallucinationinduction_not_calibration_when_multifeature_sae_steering_looks_like_it_works"},"authorids":{"value":["~Caio_Sanford_Guimarães_Vicentino1"]},"authors":{"value":["Caio Sanford Guimarães Vicentino"]}},"tmdate":1781186335600,"pdate":1781186329999,"tcdate":1777502298875,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/Submission73/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/Submission73/Authors"],"forum":"8HVIWyekTJ","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":73,"cdate":1777502298875,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp/-/Submission_Release"],"mdate":1781186335600,"odate":1781186329999,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/Mech_Interp","id":"8HVIWyekTJ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.19813v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|slambased_joint_calibration_of_multiple_asynchronous_microphone_arrays_and_sound_source_localization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiang_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuanzheng_He:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daobilige_Su:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Katsutoshi_Itoyama:","~Kazuhiro_Nakadai1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junfeng_Wu_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shoudong_Huang:","~Youfu_Li1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:He_Kong:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.19813"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2405-19813,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jiang Wang and Yuanzheng He and Daobilige Su and Katsutoshi Itoyama and Kazuhiro Nakadai and Junfeng Wu and Shoudong Huang and Youfu Li and He Kong},\n  title={SLAM-based Joint Calibration of Multiple Asynchronous Microphone Arrays and Sound Source Localization},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2405.19813},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2405.19813}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Robot audition systems with multiple microphone arrays have many applications in practice. However, accurate calibration of multiple microphone arrays remains challenging because there are many unknown parameters to be identified, including the relative transforms (i.e., orientation, translation) and asynchronous factors (i.e., initial time offset and sampling clock difference) between microphone arrays. To tackle these challenges, in this paper, we adopt batch simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) for joint calibration of multiple asynchronous microphone arrays and sound source localization. Using the Fisher information matrix (FIM) approach, we first conduct the observability analysis (i.e., parameter identifiability) of the above-mentioned calibration problem and establish necessary/sufficient conditions under which the FIM and the Jacobian matrix have full column rank, which implies the identifiability of the unknown parameters. We also discover several scenarios where the unknown parameters are not uniquely identifiable. Subsequently, we propose an effective framework to initialize the unknown parameters, which is used as the initial guess in batch SLAM for multiple microphone arrays calibration, aiming to further enhance optimization accuracy and convergence. Extensive numerical simulations and real experiments have been conducted to verify the performance of the proposed method. The experiment results show that the proposed pipeline achieves higher accuracy with fast convergence in comparison to methods that use the noise-corrupted ground truth of the unknown parameters as the initial guess in the optimization and other existing frameworks."},"title":{"value":"SLAM-based Joint Calibration of Multiple Asynchronous Microphone Arrays and Sound Source Localization"},"authors":{"value":["Jiang Wang","Yuanzheng He","Daobilige Su","Katsutoshi Itoyama","Kazuhiro Nakadai","Junfeng Wu","Shoudong Huang","Youfu Li","He Kong"]}},"tmdate":1772259365419,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747450047738,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Youfu_Li1"],"forum":"TaXTYpkdtD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":518489,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772259365419,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"TaXTYpkdtD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Robotics 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/8860/10365208/10551479.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TROB/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|slambased_joint_calibration_of_multiple_asynchronous_microphone_arrays_and_sound_source_localization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiang_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuanzheng_He:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daobilige_Su:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Katsutoshi_Itoyama:","~Kazuhiro_Nakadai1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junfeng_Wu_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shoudong_Huang:","~Youfu_Li1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:He_Kong:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TRO.2024.3410456"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/trob/WangHSIN0H0K24,\n  author={Jiang Wang and Yuanzheng He and Daobilige Su and Katsutoshi Itoyama and Kazuhiro Nakadai and Junfeng Wu and Shoudong Huang and Youfu Li and He Kong},\n  title={SLAM-Based Joint Calibration of Multiple Asynchronous Microphone Arrays and Sound Source Localization},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Robotics},\n  volume={40},\n  pages={4024-4044},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TRO.2024.3410456}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Robot audition systems with multiple microphone arrays have many applications in practice. However, the accurate calibration of multiple microphone arrays remains challenging because there are many unknown parameters to be identified, including the relative transforms (i.e., orientation and translation) and asynchronous factors (i.e., initial time offset and sampling clock difference) between microphone arrays. To tackle these challenges, in this article, we adopt batch simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) for joint calibration of multiple asynchronous microphone arrays and sound source localization. Using the Fisher information matrix (FIM) approach, we first conduct the observability analysis (i.e., parameter identifiability) of the abovementioned calibration problem and establish necessary/sufficient conditions under which the FIM and the Jacobian matrix have full column rank, which implies the identifiability of the unknown parameters. We also discover several scenarios where the unknown parameters are not uniquely identifiable. Subsequently, we propose an effective framework to initialize the unknown parameters, which is used as the initial guess in batch SLAM for multiple microphone array calibration, aiming to further enhance optimization accuracy and convergence. Extensive numerical simulations and real experiments have been conducted to verify the performance of the proposed method. The experimental results show that the proposed pipeline achieves higher accuracy with fast convergence in comparison to methods that use the noise-corrupted ground truth of the unknown parameters as the initial guess in the optimization and other existing frameworks."},"title":{"value":"SLAM-Based Joint Calibration of Multiple Asynchronous Microphone Arrays and Sound Source Localization"},"authors":{"value":["Jiang Wang","Yuanzheng He","Daobilige Su","Katsutoshi Itoyama","Kazuhiro Nakadai","Junfeng Wu","Shoudong Huang","Youfu Li","He Kong"]}},"tmdate":1772259363444,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747450203275,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Youfu_Li1"],"forum":"bntDnIdnTW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":518645,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1772259363444,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bntDnIdnTW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEEE Trans. Robotics 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/8860/10365208/10551479.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TROB/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|slambased_joint_calibration_of_multiple_asynchronous_microphone_arrays_and_sound_source_localization"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiang_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuanzheng_He:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Daobilige_Su:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Katsutoshi_Itoyama:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kazuhiro_Nakadai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Junfeng_Wu_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shoudong_Huang:","~Youfu_Li1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:He_Kong:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TRO.2024.3410456"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/trob/WangHSIN0H0K24,\n  author={Jiang Wang and Yuanzheng He and Daobilige Su and Katsutoshi Itoyama and Kazuhiro Nakadai and Junfeng Wu and Shoudong Huang and Youfu Li and He Kong},\n  title={SLAM-Based Joint Calibration of Multiple Asynchronous Microphone Arrays and Sound Source Localization},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={IEEE Trans. Robotics},\n  volume={40},\n  pages={4024-4044},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/TRO.2024.3410456}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Robot audition systems with multiple microphone arrays have many applications in practice. However, the accurate calibration of multiple microphone arrays remains challenging because there are many unknown parameters to be identified, including the relative transforms (i.e., orientation and translation) and asynchronous factors (i.e., initial time offset and sampling clock difference) between microphone arrays. To tackle these challenges, in this article, we adopt batch simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) for joint calibration of multiple asynchronous microphone arrays and sound source localization. Using the Fisher information matrix (FIM) approach, we first conduct the observability analysis (i.e., parameter identifiability) of the abovementioned calibration problem and establish necessary/sufficient conditions under which the FIM and the Jacobian matrix have full column rank, which implies the identifiability of the unknown parameters. We also discover several scenarios where the unknown parameters are not uniquely identifiable. Subsequently, we propose an effective framework to initialize the unknown parameters, which is used as the initial guess in batch SLAM for multiple microphone array calibration, aiming to further enhance optimization accuracy and convergence. Extensive numerical simulations and real experiments have been conducted to verify the performance of the proposed method. The experimental results show that the proposed pipeline achieves higher accuracy with fast convergence in comparison to methods that use the noise-corrupted ground truth of the unknown parameters as the initial guess in the optimization and other existing frameworks."},"title":{"value":"SLAM-Based Joint Calibration of Multiple Asynchronous Microphone Arrays and Sound Source Localization"},"authors":{"value":["Jiang Wang","Yuanzheng He","Daobilige Su","Katsutoshi Itoyama","Kazuhiro Nakadai","Junfeng Wu","Shoudong Huang","Youfu Li","He Kong"]}},"tmdate":1747450051917,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747450047465,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Youfu_Li1"],"forum":"F9W59tYwEO","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":518484,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747450051917,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"F9W59tYwEO","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["Federated Learning","Graph Learning","Robustness","Geometric Learning"]},"primary_area":{"value":"learning on graphs and other geometries & topologies"},"abstract":{"value":"Training highly generalizable server model necessitates requires data from multiple sources in Federated Graph Learning. However, noisy labels are increasingly undermining federated system due to the propagation of erroneous information between nodes. Compounding this issue, significant variations in data distribution among clients make noise node detection more challenging. In our work, we propose an effective structural and semantic calibration framework for Robust Federated Graph Learning, AURA. We observe that spectral discrepancies across different clients adversely affect noise detection. To address this, we employs SVD for self-supervision, compelling the model to learn an intrinsic and consistent structural representation of the data, thereby effectively attenuating local high-frequency perturbations induced by noisy nodes. We introduce two metrics, namely \"Depth Influence\" and \"Breadth Influence\". Based on these metrics, the framework judiciously selects and aggregates the most consensual knowledge from the class prototypes uploaded by each client. Concurrently, clients perform knowledge distillation by minimizing the KL divergence between their local model's output distribution and that of the global model, which markedly enhances the model's generalization performance and convergence stability in heterogeneous data environments. AURA demonstrates remarkable robustness across multiple datasets, for instance, achieving a $7.6\\%$ $\\uparrow$ F1-macro score under a 20\\%-uniform noise on Cora. The code is available for anonymous access at \\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/AURA-F351/}."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nshang2026aura,\ntitle={{AURA}: Structural and Semantic Calibration for Robust Federated Graph Learning},\nauthor={Xiaoran Shang and Guancheng Wan and Chao Huang and Wenwen He and Yuxin Wu and Wenke Huang},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=KTjAeX6u2a}\n}"},"title":{"value":"AURA: Structural and Semantic Calibration for Robust Federated Graph Learning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f6b9cf9f61199790456c2fa3f784385f781d1828.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"shang|aura_structural_and_semantic_calibration_for_robust_federated_graph_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xiaoran_Shang2","~Guancheng_Wan1","~Chao_Huang22","~Wenwen_He1","~Yuxin_Wu3","~Wenke_Huang1"]},"authors":{"value":["Xiaoran Shang","Guancheng Wan","Chao Huang","Wenwen He","Yuxin Wu","Wenke Huang"]}},"tmdate":1770804797272,"tcdate":1756745302001,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission542/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission542/Authors"],"forum":"KTjAeX6u2a","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":542,"cdate":1756745302001,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission542/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770804797272,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"KTjAeX6u2a","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["CyberSecurity","Code Generation","Agent","Secure Code Generation"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/9fe0a3d739cbf79bca97a12640aa2a930c76b595.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"datasets and benchmarks"},"abstract":{"value":"AI coding agents now write much of the code that ships, yet whether that code is secure remains unclear. We introduce the Last Secure Code Benchmark (LSCBench), a comprehensive evaluation of secure code generation by coding agents. Built from 150 real vulnerabilities, its 450 tasks span seven widely used languages (Java, C, C++, JavaScript, Python, PHP, and Go), 24 CWEs including 21 of the 2025 CWE Top 25 and 8 of the Top 10 KEV weaknesses, more than any benchmark we compare with, and three vibe-coding scenarios: filling in a function, writing a file, and building a repository's source tree from scratch. Every task asks only for functionality, through a specification that never mentions security, and hidden functional and security tests grade the result. Across six frontier agents, 73.6\\% to 91.3\\% of tasks pass functionally, yet even the strongest, Claude Fable 5, produces code that is both functional and secure for only 25.1\\% of tasks, and the others for 16.4\\% to 24.7\\%. The best model only passes 12\\% of the vulnerability scenarios across all vibe coding setting, posing strong concern on the security of the code written by vibe coding."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026last,\ntitle={Last Secure Code Benchmark: Is the Code You Vibe Secure?},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=MfNPixduM2},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Last Secure Code Benchmark: Is the Code You Vibe Secure?"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/80a12fff785f5b8c1a1375ad14621eaf7b887576.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791231402674,"tcdate":1789650824901,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission31576/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission31576/Authors"],"forum":"MfNPixduM2","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":31576,"cdate":1789650824901,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission31576/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791231402674,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"MfNPixduM2","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.05398v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"cao|bayesian_model_calibration_for_diblock_copolymer_thin_film_selfassembly_using_power_spectrum_of_microscopy_data"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lianghao_Cao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Keyi_Wu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:J._Tinsley_Oden:","~Peng_Chen1","~Omar_Ghattas1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2306.05398"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2306-05398,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Lianghao Cao and Keyi Wu and J. Tinsley Oden and Peng Chen and Omar Ghattas},\n  title={Bayesian model calibration for diblock copolymer thin film self-assembly using power spectrum of microscopy data},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2306.05398},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2306.05398}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Identifying parameters of computational models from experimental data, or model calibration, is fundamental for assessing and improving the predictability and reliability of computer simulations. In this work, we propose a method for Bayesian calibration of models that predict morphological patterns of diblock copolymer (Di-BCP) thin film self-assembly while accounting for various sources of uncertainties in pattern formation and data acquisition. This method extracts the azimuthally-averaged power spectrum (AAPS) of the top-down microscopy characterization of Di-BCP thin film patterns as summary statistics for Bayesian inference of model parameters via the pseudo-marginal method. We derive the analytical and approximate form of a conditional likelihood for the AAPS of image data. We demonstrate that AAPS-based image data reduction retains the mutual information, particularly on important length scales, between image data and model parameters while being relatively agnostic to the aleatoric uncertainties associated with the random long-range disorder of Di-BCP patterns. Additionally, we propose a phase-informed prior distribution for Bayesian model calibration. Furthermore, reducing image data to AAPS enables us to efficiently build surrogate models to accelerate the proposed Bayesian model calibration procedure. We present the formulation and training of two multi-layer perceptrons for approximating the parameter-to-spectrum map, which enables fast integrated likelihood evaluations. We validate the proposed Bayesian model calibration method through numerical examples, for which the neural network surrogate delivers a fivefold reduction of the number of model simulations performed for a single calibration task."},"title":{"value":"Bayesian model calibration for diblock copolymer thin film self-assembly using power spectrum of microscopy data"},"authors":{"value":["Lianghao Cao","Keyi Wu","J. Tinsley Oden","Peng Chen","Omar Ghattas"]}},"tmdate":1747254022963,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1747252582519,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Peng_Chen1"],"forum":"oW6rDIiFxN","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":465846,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747254022963,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"oW6rDIiFxN","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SmartCity360° 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-030-76063-2_30.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/SMARTCITY360/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"fang|are_neural_networks_really_the_holy_grail_a_comparison_of_multivariate_calibration_for_lowcost_environmental_sensors"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xinwei_Fang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Iain_Bate:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:David_Griffin_0002:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-76063-2_30"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/smartcity360/FangB020,\n  author={Xinwei Fang and Iain Bate and David Griffin},\n  title={Are Neural Networks Really the Holy Grail? A Comparison of Multivariate Calibration for Low-Cost Environmental Sensors},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  pages={442-461},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-76063-2_30},\n  booktitle={SmartCity360°},\n  crossref={conf/smartcity360/2020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Data obtained from low-cost environmental sensors can have various issues such as low precision and accuracy and incompleteness. A calibration process is often applied to address such issues. With the recent advances in artificial intelligence, we have seen an increased number of applications that starts to use an artificial neural network (ANN) to calibrate the sensors, and their results are promising. In this work, we used a six-months worth of real hourly data to demonstrate that the ANN may not always be the best choice of a calibration method. Our evaluation compares an ANN-based method with a simple regression-based method in various aspects. The result shows that the ANN-based method does not consistently outperform the regression-based method. More interestingly, in the comparison, our results suggest that the performance of a calibration can be more sensitive to some of the factors (e.g. training and testing data, model parameters) than the use of different calibration methods. Even though the results may not be generalised in other sensors or datasets, our evaluation provides evidence showing that inappropriate use of a calibration method can compromise the calibration result, and the use of the ANN will not magically solve that problem."},"title":{"value":"Are Neural Networks Really the Holy Grail? A Comparison of Multivariate Calibration for Low-Cost Environmental Sensors"},"authors":{"value":["Xinwei Fang","Iain Bate","David Griffin"]}},"tmdate":1759523986415,"pdate":1577836800000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/smartcity360/FangB020"],"tcdate":1759523975286,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xinwei_Fang1"],"forum":"lwBDBygUDE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":634592,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1759523986415,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"lwBDBygUDE","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Modern Pathology"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"ji|physical_color_calibration_of_digital_pathology_scanners_for_robust_artificial_intelligenceassisted_cancer_diagnosis"},"html":{"value":"https://researchportal.tuni.fi/en/publications/8047df5f-3f11-4950-b89d-5c434ab171c1"},"abstract":{"value":"The potential of artificial intelligence (AI) in digital pathology is limited by technical inconsistencies in the production of whole slide images (WSIs). This causes degraded AI performance and poses a challenge for widespread clinical application, as fine-tuning algorithms for each site is impractical. Changes in the imaging workflow can also compromise diagnostic accuracy and patient safety. Physical color calibration of scanners, relying on a biomaterial-based calibrant slide and a spectrophotometric reference measurement, has been proposed for standardizing WSI appearance, but its impact on AI performance has not been investigated. We evaluated whether physical color calibration can enable robust AI performance. We trained fully supervised and foundation model–based AI systems for detecting and Gleason grading prostate cancer using WSIs of prostate biopsies from the STHLM3 clinical trial (n = 3651) and evaluated their performance in 3 external cohorts (n = 1161) with and without calibration. With physical color calibration, the fully supervised system's concordance with pathologists’ grading (Cohen linearly weighted κ) improved from 0.439 to 0.619 in the Stavanger University Hospital cohort (n = 860), from 0.354 to 0.738 in the Karolinska University Hospital cohort (n = 229), and from 0.423 to 0.452 in the Aarhus University Hospital cohort (n = 72). The foundation model's concordance improved as follows: from 0.739 to 0.760 (Karolinska), from 0.424 to 0.459 (Aarhus), and from 0.547 to 0.670 (Stavanger). This study demonstrated that physical color calibration provides a potential solution to the variation introduced by different scanners, making AI-based cancer diagnostics more reliable and applicable in diverse clinical settings."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{8047df5f3f114950b89d5c434ab171c1,\n  title     = \"Physical Color Calibration of Digital Pathology Scanners for Robust Artificial Intelligence–Assisted Cancer Diagnosis\",\n  abstract  = \"The potential of artificial intelligence (AI) in digital pathology is limited by technical inconsistencies in the production of whole slide images (WSIs). This causes degraded AI performance and poses a challenge for widespread clinical application, as fine-tuning algorithms for each site is impractical. Changes in the imaging workflow can also compromise diagnostic accuracy and patient safety. Physical color calibration of scanners, relying on a biomaterial-based calibrant slide and a spectrophotometric reference measurement, has been proposed for standardizing WSI appearance, but its impact on AI performance has not been investigated. We evaluated whether physical color calibration can enable robust AI performance. We trained fully supervised and foundation model–based AI systems for detecting and Gleason grading prostate cancer using WSIs of prostate biopsies from the STHLM3 clinical trial (n = 3651) and evaluated their performance in 3 external cohorts (n = 1161) with and without calibration. With physical color calibration, the fully supervised system's concordance with pathologists{\\textquoteright} grading (Cohen linearly weighted κ) improved from 0.439 to 0.619 in the Stavanger University Hospital cohort (n = 860), from 0.354 to 0.738 in the Karolinska University Hospital cohort (n = 229), and from 0.423 to 0.452 in the Aarhus University Hospital cohort (n = 72). The foundation model's concordance improved as follows: from 0.739 to 0.760 (Karolinska), from 0.424 to 0.459 (Aarhus), and from 0.547 to 0.670 (Stavanger). This study demonstrated that physical color calibration provides a potential solution to the variation introduced by different scanners, making AI-based cancer diagnostics more reliable and applicable in diverse clinical settings.\",\n  keywords  = \"artificial intelligence, color calibration, computational pathology, foundation model, prostate cancer, whole slide scanning\",\n  author    = \"Xiaoyi Ji and Richard Salmon and Nita Mulliqi and Umair Khan and Yinxi Wang and Anders Blilie and Henrik Olsson and Pedersen, \\{Bodil Ginnerup\\} and S{\\o}rensen, \\{Karina Dalsgaard\\} and Ulh{\\o}i, \\{Benedicte Parm\\} and Kjosavik, \\{Svein R.\\} and Janssen, \\{Emilius A.M.\\} and Mattias Rantalainen and Lars Egevad and Pekka Ruusuvuori and Martin Eklund and Kimmo Kartasalo\",\n  note      = \"Publisher Copyright: {\\textcopyright} 2025 The Authors\",\n  year      = \"2025\",\n  month     = may,\n  doi       = \"10.1016/j.modpat.2025.100715\",\n  language  = \"English\",\n  volume    = \"38\",\n  journal   = \"Modern Pathology\",\n  issn      = \"0893-3952\",\n  publisher = \"Elsevier B.V.\",\n  number    = \"5\",\n}"},"title":{"value":"Physical Color Calibration of Digital Pathology Scanners for Robust Artificial Intelligence–Assisted Cancer Diagnosis"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Xiaoyi Ji","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Xiaoyi%20Ji"},{"fullname":"Richard Salmon","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Richard%20Salmon"},{"fullname":"Nita Mulliqi","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Nita%20Mulliqi"},{"fullname":"Umair Khan","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Umair%20Khan"},{"fullname":"Yinxi Wang","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Yinxi%20Wang"},{"fullname":"Anders Blilie","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Anders%20Blilie"},{"fullname":"Henrik Olsson","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Henrik%20Olsson"},{"fullname":"Bodil Ginnerup Pedersen","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Bodil%20Ginnerup%20Pedersen"},{"fullname":"Karina Dalsgaard Sørensen","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Karina%20Dalsgaard%20S%C3%B8rensen"},{"fullname":"Benedicte Parm Ulhøi","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Benedicte%20Parm%20Ulh%C3%B8i"},{"fullname":"Svein R. Kjosavik","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Svein%20R.%20Kjosavik"},{"fullname":"Emilius A.M. Janssen","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Emilius%20A.M.%20Janssen"},{"fullname":"Mattias Rantalainen","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Mattias%20Rantalainen"},{"fullname":"Lars Egevad","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Lars%20Egevad"},{"fullname":"Pekka Ruusuvuori","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Pekka%20Ruusuvuori"},{"fullname":"Martin Eklund","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Martin%20Eklund"},{"fullname":"Kimmo Kartasalo","username":"~Kimmo_Kartasalo1"}]}},"tmdate":1787925216080,"pdate":1746057600000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1016/j.modpat.2025.100715"],"tcdate":1787925215596,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Kimmo_Kartasalo1"],"forum":"2HtFxkyy3h","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":104938,"cdate":1742600055126,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1787925216080,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"2HtFxkyy3h","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"IEICE Trans. Fundam. Electron. Commun. Comput. Sci. 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/transfun/E106.A/5/E106.A_2022EAL2038/_pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/IEICETA/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"zeng|novel_autocalibration_method_for_7elements_hexagonal_array_with_mutual_coupling"},"authorids":{"value":["","~Xin_Qiu3","~Jinhai_Li1","","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1587/transfun.2022eal2038"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/ieiceta/ZengQLLSC23,\n  author={Fankun Zeng and Xin Qiu and Jinhai Li and Biqi Long and Wuhai Su and Xiaoran Chen},\n  title={Novel Auto-Calibration Method for 7-Elements Hexagonal Array with Mutual Coupling},\n  year={2023},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1682899200000},\n  journal={IEICE Trans. Fundam. Electron. Commun. Comput. Sci.},\n  volume={106},\n  number={5},\n  pages={858-862},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1587/transfun.2022eal2038}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Mutual coupling between antenna array elements will significantly degrade the performance of the array signal processing methods. Due to the Toeplitz structure of mutual coupling matrix (MCM), there exist some mutual coupling calibration algorithms for the uniform linear array (ULA) or uniform circular array (UCA). But few methods for other arrays. In this letter, we derive a new transformation formula for the MCM of the 7-elements hexagonal array (HA-7). Further, we extend two mutual coupling auto-calibration methods from UCA to HA by the transformation formula. Simulation results demonstrate the validity of the proposed two methods."},"title":{"value":"Novel Auto-Calibration Method for 7-Elements Hexagonal Array with Mutual Coupling"},"authors":{"value":["Fankun Zeng","Xin Qiu","Jinhai Li","Biqi Long","Wuhai Su","Xiaoran Chen"]}},"tmdate":1775400406962,"pdate":1703980800000,"tcdate":1775400068766,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jinhai_Li1"],"forum":"epbiN1CPqo","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":864658,"cdate":1682899200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1775400406962,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"epbiN1CPqo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CCA 2007"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/4389190/4389191/04389447.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/IEEECCA/2007"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|quaternionbased_visual_servo_control_in_the_presence_of_camera_calibration_error"},"authorids":{"value":["~Guoqiang_Hu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nicholas_R._Gans:","~Warren_E._Dixon1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CCA.2007.4389447"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/IEEEcca/HuGD07,\n  author={Guoqiang Hu and Nicholas R. Gans and Warren E. Dixon},\n  title={Quaternion-Based Visual Servo Control in the Presence of Camera Calibration Error},\n  year={2007},\n  cdate={1167609600000},\n  pages={1492-1497},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/CCA.2007.4389447},\n  booktitle={CCA},\n  crossref={conf/IEEEcca/2007}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"One important issue that has limited the robustness of vision-based control systems is a dependence on exact camera calibration to relate the image-space to the Euclidean-space. If the calibration is not exactly known, then performance degradation and potential unpredictable response from the system may occur. The focus of this paper is the development of a quaternion-based camera rotation estimate that is related to the actual rotation error computed from a homography that relates corresponding images, the development of a new closed-loop error system, and a new Lyapunov-based analysis that demonstrates the stability of the quaternion error system."},"title":{"value":"Quaternion-Based Visual Servo Control in the Presence of Camera Calibration Error"},"authors":{"value":["Guoqiang Hu","Nicholas R. Gans","Warren E. Dixon"]}},"tmdate":1731511498759,"pdate":1167609600000,"tcdate":1715699791517,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Warren_Dixon1"],"forum":"SloQs5PL9G","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":11115,"cdate":1167609600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1731511498759,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SloQs5PL9G","version":2}],"count":10000}